/* ACE POINT · JEE Main chemistry PYQs 2019–2025 (added 20 Nov 2026). Merged into CHAPTERS.jeem[*].jpyq by index.html. */
window.JEEM_PYQ_EXTRA = {"m05-soln":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":54,"q":"Which of the following graph correctly represents the plots of K_H at 1 bar for gases in water versus temperature?","options":["Graph (A)","Graph (B)","Graph (C)","Graph (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) right shape, wrong gas order (O₂ > N₂ > He). (C) straight lines through origin, wrong shape and order. (D) U-shaped curves, wrong trend. (B) has the correct order He > N₂ > CH₄.\nWorking: over the usual temperature range K_H of these gases rises with temperature (gases are less soluble in hot water) and then passes through a maximum; a larger K_H means a less soluble gas. He is the least soluble and CH₄ the most, so from top to bottom the curves are He > N₂ > CH₄, as in graph (B).\nKey idea: Highest K_H = least soluble gas, so He sits on top and CH₄ at the bottom.","traps":["Order of Henry's constants of gases in water: Large K_H means low solubility; He is the least soluble, so it has the largest K_H (NCERT Table 1.2: He 144.97 kbar, N₂ 76.48, CH₄ 0.413).","Larger K_H read as greater solubility: x = p/K_H: the least soluble gas has the highest curve."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C04-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The Molarity (M) of an aqueous solution containing 5.85 g of NaCl in 500 mL water is : (Given : Molar Mass Na : 23 and Cl : 35.5 g mol⁻¹)","options":["20","4","2","0.2"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 2: slips a factor of 10 in the mL → L conversion. (A) 20 / (B) 4: divide grams or use mL directly.\nWorking: 5.85/58.5 = 0.1 mol; 0.1 mol / 0.5 L = 0.2 M (solution volume taken as 500 mL).\nKey idea: 0.1 mol in 0.5 L → 0.2 M.","traps":["mL not converted to L: Always convert to litres.","Solvent volume vs solution volume: At JEE Main level take it as solution volume."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"2.5 g of a non-volatile, non-electrolyte is dissolved in 100 g of water at 25°C. The solution showed a boiling point elevation by 2°C. Assuming the solute concentration is negligible with respect to the solvent concentration, the vapor pressure of the resulting aqueous solution is ______ mm of Hg (nearest integer)\n[Given : Molal boiling point elevation constant of water (K_b) = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹, 1 atm pressure = 760 mm of Hg, molar mass of water = 18 g mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"707","range":[707.0,707.0],"explain":"Common slips: ~760 × (1 − 0.0646) = 711 if x = n/(n+N) is used instead of the stated approximation; using 25°C vapour pressure (~23.8 mm) gives ~22; 2.5 g used to compute molar mass wastes time.\nWorking: m = ΔT_b/K_b = 2/0.52 = 3.846 mol kg⁻¹ → 0.3846 mol in 100 g; x_solute ≈ n/N = 0.3846/5.556 = 0.0692; p = 760 (1 − 0.0692) = 707.4 → 707. (The stem's '25°C' with p° = 760 mm is inconsistent – p° of water is 760 mm only at 100°C – but the intended answer uses 760.)\nKey idea: Molality from ΔT_b directly gives moles of solute per 100 g water; RLVP ≈ n/N.","traps":["Using mass 2.5 g: Moles come straight from molality × 0.1 kg.","25°C vs 760 mm: Use the data given; 1 atm = 760 mm is intended p°."],"topics":["Lowering of vapour pressure","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"2.7 kg of each of water and acetic acid are mixed. The freezing point of the solution will be −x °C. Consider the acetic acid does not dimerise in water, nor dissociates in water. x = ______ (nearest integer)\n[Given: Molar mass of water = 18 g mol⁻¹, acetic acid = 60 g mol⁻¹; Kf H₂O: 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹; Kf acetic acid: 3.90 K kg mol⁻¹; freezing point: H₂O = 273 K, acetic acid = 290 K]","type":"num","answer":"31","range":[31.0,31.0],"explain":"Common slips: 200 (using acetic acid as solvent: 150/2.7 × 3.9 = 217 → 290 − 217 = 73 K, i.e. −200 °C); 13 or 26 (using Kf of acetic acid or wrong molality).\nWorking: water is the solvent (150 mol vs 45 mol acetic acid). m = 45 mol / 2.7 kg = 16.67 mol kg⁻¹; ΔTf = 1.86 × 16.67 = 31.0 K, so freezing point ≈ −31 °C, x = 31. (Taking acetic acid as solvent would give an unphysical −200 °C.)\nKey idea: Solvent = component in larger mole amount (water); use only its Kf.","traps":["Acetic acid taken as solvent: Larger mole count → solvent (water).","Dilute-solution formula at high m: Question explicitly wants ΔTf = Kf m."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"An artificial cell is made by encapsulating 0.2 M glucose solution within a semipermeable membrane. The osmotic pressure developed when the artificial cell is placed within a 0.05 M solution of NaCl at 300 K is ____ ×10⁻¹ bar. (nearest integer).\n[Given: R = 0.083 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]\nAssume complete dissociation of NaCl","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50: only the glucose solution used (0.2 × 0.083 × 300 = 4.98 bar). 37: NaCl taken without i (0.2 − 0.05 = 0.15). 75: concentrations added (0.3). Note the NaCl solution alone (0.1 particle M) also gives 24.9, so a student using the outer solution alone gets the right number by wrong reasoning.\nWorking: inside 0.2 M particles; outside 0.05 × 2 = 0.1 M; Δπ = (0.2 − 0.1)(0.083)(300) = 2.49 bar = 24.9 ×10⁻¹ ≈ 25.\nKey idea: Net π = (ΣiC_inside − ΣiC_outside)RT.","traps":["i omitted for NaCl: NaCl → 2 ions, i = 2.","Only inner solution used: Pressure developed depends on concentration difference."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Considering acetic acid dissociates in water, its dissociation constant is 6.25 × 10⁻⁵. If 5 mL of acetic acid is dissolved in 1 litre water, the solution will freeze at −x × 10⁻² °C, provided pure water freezes at 0 °C.\nx = ______ (Nearest integer)\nGiven: (K_f)water = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹; density of acetic acid is 1.2 g mL⁻¹; molar mass of water = 18 g mol⁻¹; molar mass of acetic acid = 60 g mol⁻¹; density of water = 1 g cm⁻³. Acetic acid dissociates as CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 37 (i = 2 assumed); 0 or 0.19 (power-of-ten slip). Ignoring dissociation gives 18.6 → 19, so the i correction barely matters here.\nWorking: 5 mL × 1.2 = 6 g = 0.1 mol in 1 kg water → m = 0.1; α = √(Ka/C) = √(6.25×10⁻⁴) = 0.025; i = 1.025; ΔTf = 1.86 × 0.1 × 1.025 = 0.1907 → 19 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: i = 1 + α; α from Ostwald dilution.","traps":["Full dissociation assumed: Weak acid: i = 1 + α.","Volume used as mass: Use density 1.2 g mL⁻¹."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The density of 'x' M solution ('x' molar) of NaOH is 1.12 g mL⁻¹, while in molality, the concentration of the solution is 3 m (3 molal). Then x is\n(Given : Molar mass of NaOH is 40 g/mol)","options":["3.8","3.0","3.5","2.8"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): student divides 3 mol by mass of water ÷ density or uses 1120 g as solvent. (D): rounding of a mis-step.\nWorking: 3 mol NaOH (120 g) in 1000 g water → 1120 g solution → V = 1120/1.12 = 1000 mL → M = 3.0.\nKey idea: Mass of solution = 1000 + 3×40 = 1120 g; with d = 1.12 the volume is exactly 1 L.","traps":["Solvent mass used as solution volume: Volume = (mass of solvent + solute)/density."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Consider the dissociation of the weak acid HX as given below\nHX(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq), Ka = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵\n[Ka : dissociation constant]\nThe osmotic pressure of 0.03M aqueous solution of HX at 300 K is ______ × 10⁻² bar (nearest integer).\n[Given : R = 0.083 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"76","range":[76.0,76.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 75 (taking i = 1, π = 0.747), 149 (i = 2, full dissociation).\nWorking: α = √(Ka/C) = √(1.2×10⁻⁵/0.03) = 0.02; i = 1 + α = 1.02; π = iCRT = 1.02 × 0.03 × 0.083 × 300 = 0.762 bar = 76 × 10⁻² bar.\nKey idea: Weak acid: α small, i = 1 + α ≈ 1.02.","traps":["i taken as 1 or 2: Compute α from Ka."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Molality (m) of 3M aqueous solution of NaCl is : (Given : Density of solution = 1.25 g mL⁻¹, Molar mass in g mol⁻¹ : Na – 23, Cl – 35.5)","options":["2.90 m","3.85 m","1.90 m","2.79 m"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2.90: subtracts wrong solute mass or rounds poorly. (B) 3.85: divides by wrong mass. (C) 1.90: arithmetic slip. Using 1250 g as solvent gives 2.40 (not listed).\nWorking: 1 L solution = 1250 g; NaCl = 3 × 58.5 = 175.5 g; water = 1074.5 g; m = 3/1.0745 = 2.79 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: Solvent mass = solution mass − solute mass.","traps":["Solution mass used as solvent mass: Subtract 175.5 g NaCl before dividing."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"When 'x' × 10⁻² mL methanol (molar mass = 32 g, density = 0.792 g/cm³) is added to 100 mL water (density = 1 g/cm³), the following diagram is obtained.\nx = ______ (nearest integer).\n[Given : Molal freezing point depression constant of water at 273.15 K is 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"543","range":[543.0,543.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 430 (mass in g × 100, density not used), 341 (volume using density 1.26 by inversion), 54 (decimal slip).\nWorking: ΔTf = 2.5 K; m = 2.5/1.86 = 1.344 mol kg⁻¹; in 0.100 kg water n = 0.1344 mol; mass = 4.30 g; volume = 4.30/0.792 = 5.43 mL = 543 × 10⁻² mL.\nKey idea: Graph gives ΔTf = 273.15 − 270.65 = 2.5 K.","traps":["Mass reported instead of volume: Divide grams by 0.792 g/mL.","Methanol volatility worried about: Question treats it as freezing point depression solute only."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr06S2C23-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A solution containing 10 g of an electrolyte AB₂ in 100 g of water boils at 100.52°C. The degree of ionization of the electrolyte (α) is ________ × 10⁻¹. (nearest integer)\n[Given : Molar mass of AB₂ = 200 g mol⁻¹, K_b (molal boiling point elevation const. of water) = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹, boiling point of water = 100°C; AB₂ ionises as AB₂ → A²⁺ + 2B⁻]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 (α = 1 from i = 2 with n = 2), 3 (using i = 1 + 3α), 2 (reporting i).\nWorking: m = (10/200)/0.1 = 0.5 mol kg⁻¹; i = 0.52/(0.52 × 0.5) = 2; i = 1 + 2α → α = 0.5 = 5 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: AB₂ gives 3 ions: i = 1 + 2α.","traps":["Wrong (n−1) factor: i = 1 + (n − 1)α, n = 3."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"A solution is prepared by adding 1 mole ethyl alcohol in 9 mole water. The mass percent of solute in the solution is ______ (Integer answer) (Given : Molar mass in g mol⁻¹ Ethyl alcohol : 46 water : 18)","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 10 (mole %); 28 (mass of solute/mass of solvent).\nWorking: 46/(46 + 162) × 100 = 22.1 ≈ 22.\nKey idea: Use total mass in denominator.","traps":["Mole % vs mass %: Convert moles to grams."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Molality of an aqueous solution of urea is 4.44 m. Mole fraction of urea in solution is x × 10⁻³. Value of x is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"74","range":[74.0,74.0],"explain":"Common slips: 80 (4.44/55.56, solvent-only denominator); 7.4 scale error.\nWorking: 4.44 mol urea in 1000 g (55.56 mol) water; x = 4.44/(55.56 + 4.44) = 0.0740 → 74.\nKey idea: Denominator is total moles.","traps":["Denominator only solvent moles: Add solute moles to total."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"0.05 M CuSO₄ when treated with 0.01 M K₂Cr₂O₇ gives green colour solution of Cu₂Cr₂O₇. The two solutions are separated by a semi permeable membrane (SPM): side X contains the K₂Cr₂O₇ solution and side Y contains the CuSO₄ solution.\nDue to osmosis:","options":["Molarity of K₂Cr₂O₇ solution is lowered.","Green colour formation observed on side Y.","Molarity of CuSO₄ solution is lowered.","Green colour formation observed on side X."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) compares nominal molarity only, or reverses flow direction. (B)/(D) assume ions cross the membrane and react to give the green compound.\nWorking: effective concentration of CuSO₄ = 0.05×2 = 0.10 M, of K₂Cr₂O₇ = 0.01×3 = 0.03 M; water moves from X to Y, diluting CuSO₄. Ions cannot cross the SPM, so no green colour forms.\nKey idea: Only solvent crosses; it moves toward the side with more dissolved particles (CuSO₄, 0.10 M particles vs 0.03 M).","traps":["Colour reaction across membrane: An SPM lets only solvent pass; solutes never meet.","Ignoring van't Hoff factor: Compare i×C on each side."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Molarity (M) of an aqueous solution containing x g of anhyd. CuSO₄ in 500 mL solution at 32 °C is 2 × 10⁻¹ M. Its molality will be ____ × 10⁻³ m. (nearest integer). [Given density of the solution = 1.25 g/mL]","type":"num","answer":"164","range":[164.0,164.0],"explain":"Common slips: 160 if solute mass is not subtracted (0.2/1.25); 128 if density misapplied; using 500 mL wrongly halves values.\nWorking: per litre, solution mass = 1250 g, CuSO₄ = 0.2 × 159.5 = 31.9 g, water = 1218.1 g; m = 0.2/1.2181 = 0.1642 → 164.\nKey idea: m = 1000M / (1000d − M × molar mass).","traps":["Solvent mass not corrected for solute: Subtract solute mass (31.9 g).","Irrelevant data: Molarity and density suffice."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The vapor pressure of pure benzene and methyl benzene at 27°C is given as 80 Torr and 24 Torr, respectively. The mole fraction of methyl benzene in vapor phase, in equilibrium with an equimolar mixture of those two liquids (ideal solution) at the same temperature is ______ × 10⁻² (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"23","range":[23.0,23.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50 if liquid mole fraction is reported; 30 if 24/80 used; 77 if benzene fraction given.\nWorking: p(benzene) = 40, p(toluene) = 12, total 52 Torr; y(toluene) = 12/52 = 0.231 → 23.\nKey idea: y = partial pressure ÷ total pressure.","traps":["Liquid vs vapour mole fraction: Vapour is richer in the more volatile component.","Reporting the wrong component: Question asks for methyl benzene."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"We have three aqueous solutions of NaCl labelled as 'A', 'B' and 'C' with concentration 0.1 M, 0.01 M and 0.001 M, respectively. The value of van 't Hoff factor (i) for these solutions will be in the order :","options":["i_A < i_C < i_B","i_A < i_B < i_C","i_A > i_B > i_C","i_A = i_B = i_C"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): assumes NaCl always i = 2 (complete dissociation). (C): reverses trend (thinks more solute, more ions per formula). (A): random.\nWorking: Dilution reduces interionic attraction, so apparent dissociation (i) rises towards 2 as concentration falls: 0.1 M < 0.01 M < 0.001 M.\nKey idea: Lower concentration → less ion pairing → i closer to ideal value.","traps":["Ideal i = 2 assumed for all concentrations: Measured i rises towards 2 on dilution.","Trend reversed: i is per formula unit, not total ions."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Mass of ethylene glycol (antifreeze) to be added to 18.6 kg of water to protect the freezing point at −24 °C is ______ kg (Molar mass in g mol⁻¹ for ethylene glycol 62, Kf of water = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 14880 (left in grams), about 0.8 (forgetting to multiply by 18.6 kg), 12.9 (molality reported).\nWorking: ΔTf = 24 K; m = 24/1.86 = 12.90 mol kg⁻¹; moles = 12.90 × 18.6 = 240 mol; mass = 240 × 62 = 14 880 g ≈ 14.88 kg → 15.\nKey idea: moles = ΔTf × w(kg)/Kf = 24 × 10 = 240; × 62 g = 14.88 kg.","traps":["Grams vs kilograms in answer: Convert 14 880 g to 14.88 kg.","Solvent mass ignored: Moles = molality × kg of solvent."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal & non-ideal solutions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"A solution of two miscible liquids showing negative deviation from Raoult's law will have :","options":["increased vapour pressure, increased boiling point","increased vapour pressure, decreased boiling point","decreased vapour pressure, decreased boiling point","decreased vapour pressure, increased boiling point"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) mixes a VP rise with a bp rise (inconsistent). (B) is positive deviation. (C) inconsistent pair: lower VP must give higher bp.\nWorking: stronger A–B attractions lower escaping tendency, so vapour pressure falls below the Raoult value and the boiling point rises.\nKey idea: Vapour pressure and boiling point move in opposite directions.","traps":["Swapping positive and negative deviation: Negative deviation = stronger A–B attraction = lower VP."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The quantity which changes with temperature is :","options":["Molality","Molarity","Mole fraction","Mass percentage"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) depend only on masses or moles, which do not change with temperature.\nWorking: molarity uses solution volume, which depends on temperature; the others are mass/mole based.\nKey idea: Volume-based terms change with temperature.","traps":["Molality confused with molarity: Molality is per kg solvent - mass based."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Volume of 3M NaOH (formula weight 40 g mol⁻¹) which can be prepared from 84 g of NaOH is ________ ×10⁻¹ dm³.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.7 entered unscaled (rounds to 1), 6.3 (multiplying 2.1 × 3), 700 (mL).\nWorking: 84/40 = 2.1 mol; V = 2.1/3 = 0.7 dm³ = 7 ×10⁻¹ dm³.\nKey idea: V = n/M.","traps":["Unit scaling: Convert 0.7 dm³ to 7 ×10⁻¹ dm³."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A solution of H₂SO₄ is 31.4% H₂SO₄ by mass and has a density of 1.25 g/mL. The molarity of the H₂SO₄ solution is _________ M (nearest integer)\n[Given molar mass of H₂SO₄ = 98 g mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 100 g as 100 mL (ignoring density) gives 3.2 → 3; computing molality instead (31.4 g H₂SO₄ in 68.6 g water) gives ≈ 4.67 → 5.\nWorking: M = (31.4 × 1.25 × 10)/98 = 392.5/98 = 4.005 ≈ 4 M.\nKey idea: M = 10 × d × (w/w %)/Mₘ.","traps":["Density ignored: Volume of 100 g solution = 100/1.25 = 80 mL.","Molality computed instead: Molarity divides by litres of solution, not kg solvent."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The osmotic pressure of a dilute solution is 7 × 10⁵ Pa at 273 K. Osmotic pressure of the same solution at 283 K is _________ × 10⁴ N m⁻².","type":"num","answer":"73","range":[73.0,73.0],"explain":"Common slips: 70 if T change ignored; 7 or 726 if the ×10⁴ scale is misapplied; 72 if truncated.\nWorking: π ∝ T at constant C: π = 7 × 10⁵ × 283/273 = 7.256 × 10⁵ Pa = 72.56 × 10⁴ N m⁻² ≈ 73 (nearest integer).\nKey idea: Same solution → C fixed → π₂/π₁ = T₂/T₁.","traps":["Scale misread: 7.256 × 10⁵ = 72.56 × 10⁴."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Molality of 0.8 M H₂SO₄ solution (density 1.06 g cm⁻³) is ________ × 10⁻³ m.","type":"num","answer":"815","range":[815.0,815.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 755 (divides by 1.06 kg solution), 800 (assumes molality = molarity).\nWorking: 1 L solution = 1060 g; H₂SO₄ = 0.8 × 98 = 78.4 g; water = 981.6 g; m = 0.8/0.9816 = 0.815 = 815 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: kg of solvent = (1000·d − M·Mₘ)/1000.","traps":["Solution mass used as solvent mass: Subtract solute mass (78.4 g)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"What happens to freezing point of benzene when small quantity of naphthalene is added to benzene?","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains unchanged","First decreases and then increases"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Increases: confusing with boiling point elevation. (C) Unchanged: thinking a non-electrolyte has no effect. (D) No such behaviour for a dilute solution.\nWorking: naphthalene is a non-volatile solute; it lowers the vapour pressure of benzene and so depresses its freezing point (ΔTf = Kf·m).\nKey idea: Solute → freezing point down, boiling point up.","traps":["Freezing point confused with boiling point: Solute raises bp but lowers fp."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The mass of sodium acetate (CH₃COONa) required to prepare 250 mL of 0.35 M aqueous solution is _______ g. (Molar mass of CH₃COONa is 82.02 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7177 or 28.7 (no mL→L or wrong volume use), 0.0875 (moles reported), 287 (multiplying by 1000/250 wrongly).\nWorking: n = 0.35 × 0.250 = 0.0875 mol; m = 0.0875 × 82.02 = 7.18 g ≈ 7.\nKey idea: mass = M × V(L) × molar mass.","traps":["mL not converted to L: Divide mL by 1000 before multiplying."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The solution from the following with highest depression in freezing point/lowest freezing point is","options":["180 g of acetic acid dissolved in benzene","180 g of acetic acid dissolved in water","180 g of benzoic acid dissolved in benzene","180 g of glucose dissolved in water"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores dimerisation, or picks it because Kf(benzene) is larger. (C) benzoic acid: fewer moles (180/122) and also dimerises. (D) glucose: only 1 mol, non-electrolyte.\nWorking: by 'lowest freezing point' B is lowest: 3 mol acetic acid, i ≈ 1 (slight ionisation) in water, ΔTf ≈ 5.6 K per kg → about −5.6 °C, while benzene solutions start from 5.5 °C and acetic/benzoic acid dimerise there (i ≈ ½). Caveat: solvent mass is not given, and on 'highest depression' alone A (3 × ½ × 5.12 ≈ 7.7 K per kg) would beat B, so the stem's two criteria conflict; the key follows the lowest freezing point/intended 'association in benzene lowers i' idea.\nKey idea: Equal mass → compare moles (acetic acid 3 mol > benzoic 1.5 > glucose 1); association in benzene halves the particle count; water also has the lower pure-solvent freezing point.","traps":["Equal mass but unequal moles: Convert to moles first: glucose 1 mol, acetic acid 3 mol.","Dimerisation in benzene ignored: Use i ≈ 0.5 for carboxylic acids in benzene."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"If a substance 'A' dissolves in solution of a mixture of 'B' and 'C' with their respective number of moles as n_A, n_B and n_C. Mole fraction of C in the solution is","options":["n_C/(n_A × n_B × n_C)","n_B/(n_A + n_B)","n_C/(n_A + n_B + n_C)","n_C/(n_A − n_B − n_C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) product instead of sum. (B) wrong component and omits C. (D) differences instead of sum.\nWorking: mole fraction = moles of C / total moles of all components.\nKey idea: Denominator is the sum of moles of every component.","traps":["Leaving one component out of the total: Include every component in the denominator."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal & non-ideal solutions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Identify the mixture that shows positive deviations from Raoult's Law","options":["(CH₃)₂CO + CS₂","(CH₃)₂CO + C₆H₅NH₂","CHCl₃ + C₆H₆","CHCl₃ + (CH₃)₂CO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): chloroform–acetone is NCERT's classic negative-deviation pair (C–H···O H-bond). (B): acetone–aniline forms H-bond → negative. (C): chloroform–benzene has CH···π interaction → negative; student may think 'non-polar + polar' means positive.\nWorking: CS₂ breaks dipole–dipole interactions of acetone and forms weaker A–B interactions → positive deviation. Acetone–aniline, chloroform–benzene and chloroform–acetone have stronger A–B interactions (H-bonding/dipole) → negative deviation.\nKey idea: Positive deviation when A–B interactions are weaker than A–A/B–B; acetone + CS₂ is the NCERT case.","traps":["Chloroform–acetone mistaken as positive: Remember: CHCl₃ H-bonds to acetone O → negative.","Polar + non-polar assumed positive: CHCl₃ C–H interacts with π cloud → negative."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The molarity of 1 L orthophosphoric acid (H₃PO₄) having 70% purity by weight (specific gravity 1.54 g cm⁻³) is ______ M. (Molar mass of H₃PO₄ = 98 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7.14 (density ignored, 70% w/v); 15.7 (purity ignored).\nWorking: 1000 mL × 1.54 g mL⁻¹ × 0.70 / 98 = 11.0 mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: M = 10 × d × %w / M_r.","traps":["Purity factor omitted: Multiply solution mass by 0.70."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Mass of Urea (NH₂CONH₂) required to be dissolved in 1000 g of water in order to reduce the vapour pressure of water by 25% is ______ g. (Nearest integer)\nGiven: Molar mass of N, C, O and H are 14, 12, 16 and 1 g mol⁻¹ respectively","type":"num","answer":"1111","range":[1111.0,1111.0],"explain":"Common slips: 833: uses n₂/n₁ = 0.25 (dilute approximation). 278/0.25-type slips: uses mass ratio. 1000+: confuses 25% lowering with p = 25% of p°.\nWorking: Δp/p° = x(urea) = 0.25 = n/(n + 55.56) → n = 18.52 mol → 18.52 × 60 = 1111 g. (Dilute approximation n/n₁ would give 833 g.)\nKey idea: With a big lowering, keep n₂/(n₁ + n₂).","traps":["Dilute approximation used: Check: 25% lowering is not dilute; use n₂/(n₁+n₂).","'Reduce by 25%' read as 'reduce to 25%': Δp/p° = 0.25."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Consider the following pairs of solution which will be isotonic at the same temperature. The number of pairs of solutions is / are ________.\nA. 1 M aq. NaCl and 2 M aq. urea\nB. 1 M aq. CaCl₂ and 1.5 M aq. KCl\nC. 1.5 M aq. AlCl₃ and 2 M aq. Na₂SO₄\nD. 2.5 M aq. KCl and 1 M aq. Al₂(SO₄)₃","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if Al₂(SO₄)₃ is given i = 3 or AlCl₃ i = 3; lower counts if i is ignored (only concentrations compared).\nWorking: iC values – A 2 = 2; B 3 = 3; C 6 = 6; D 5 = 5 (complete dissociation assumed). All four pairs isotonic.\nKey idea: π ∝ iC; compare iC for each pair.","traps":["Ions per formula miscounted: Write the dissociation for each salt."],"topics":["Osmotic pressure","van't Hoff factor"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The vapour pressure vs. temperature curve for a solution solvent system is shown below.\nThe boiling point of the solvent is ______ °C.","type":"num","answer":"82","range":[82.0,82.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 83 (takes the solution curve), 80 or 81 (reads where curves start instead of where they meet 1 atm).\nWorking: pure solvent has the higher vapour pressure at every temperature (left curve), so it reaches 1 atm first, at 82 °C; the solution boils at 83 °C (elevation 1 °C).\nKey idea: Boiling point = temperature where VP = 1 atm; solvent curve lies higher.","traps":["Curve start read as boiling point: Boiling point is where curve meets the 1 atm line.","Solvent and solution curves swapped: Solute lowers VP, so solution curve is lower/right."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Apr08S1C21-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"If the boiling points of two solvents X and Y (having same molecular weights) are in the ratio 2 : 1 and their enthalpy of vaporizations are in the ratio 1 : 2, then the boiling point elevation constant of X is m times the boiling point elevation constant of Y. The value of m is ______ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (ΔvapH ratio ignored, or Tb not squared: 2 × 2), 2 (ΔvapH placed in the numerator: 2² × ½), 1 (both slips together).\nWorking: Kb = R·M·Tb²/(1000·ΔvapH); Kb(X)/Kb(Y) = (2)² × (2/1) = 8.\nKey idea: Kb ∝ Tb²/ΔvapH: square the boiling-point ratio and invert the enthalpy ratio.","traps":["Tb not squared: Kb = RM Tb²/(1000 ΔvapH).","Enthalpy ratio applied directly: ΔvapH is in the denominator: ratio 1:2 doubles Kb(X)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Solution of 12 g of non-electrolyte (A) prepared by dissolving it in 1000 mL of water exerts the same osmotic pressure as that of 0.05 M glucose solution at the same temperature. The empirical formula of A is CH₂O. The molecular mass of A is ________ g. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"240","range":[240.0,240.0],"explain":"Common slips: 30: reports the empirical formula mass. 180: assumes A is glucose. 0.6 or 600: volume/unit slip.\nWorking: isotonic → same molarity 0.05 M; 12 g/M = 0.05 mol (in ≈1 L) → M = 240 g mol⁻¹ (= 8 × CH₂O, C₈H₁₆O₈).\nKey idea: Equal π at equal T ⇒ equal concentration ⇒ moles = 0.05.","traps":["Empirical formula used as the answer: Molar mass comes from π; empirical formula is just a cross-check."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"25 mL of an aqueous solution of KCl was found to require 20 mL of 1 M AgNO₃ solution when titrated using K₂CrO₄ as an indicator. What is the depression in freezing point of KCl solution of the given concentration? ______ (Nearest integer).\n(Given : K_f = 2.0 K kg mol⁻¹)\nAssume 1) 100% ionization and 2) density of the aqueous solution as 1 g mL⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using molarity 0.8 M as molality gives 3.2 (still 3). Forgetting i = 2 gives 1.7 → 2. Using solution mass as solvent mass gives 3.2. Neglecting KCl mass is harmless here.\nWorking: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl, so n(KCl) = n(AgNO₃) = 0.020 L × 1 M = 0.020 mol in 25 mL (25 g) of solution.\nKey idea: n(KCl) = 0.020 mol in 25 g solution; water = 25 − 1.49 = 23.51 g; m = 0.851; ΔT_f = 2 × 2.0 × 0.851 = 3.40 → 3.","traps":["van't Hoff factor omitted: KCl → K⁺ + Cl⁻, i = 2.","Molarity used as molality: Subtract solute mass from solution mass."],"topics":["Depression of freezing point","van't Hoff factor"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The density of 3M solution of NaCl is 1.0 g mL⁻¹. Molality of the solution is ______ × 10⁻² m. (Nearest integer).\nGiven: Molar mass of Na and Cl is 23 and 35.5 g mol⁻¹ respectively.","type":"num","answer":"364","range":[364.0,364.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking m = M (300) or using solution mass 1000 g as solvent (300) are the main slips; rounding 363.8 to 363.\nWorking: take 1 L of solution = 1000 g (density 1.0 g mL⁻¹); it contains 3 mol NaCl = 3 × 58.5 = 175.5 g.\nKey idea: Solvent = 1000 − 175.5 = 824.5 g; m = 3/0.8245 = 3.64 → 364.","traps":["Molarity equated to molality: Subtract solute mass."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"20% of acetic acid is dissociated when its 5 g is added to 500 mL of water. The depression in freezing point of such water is ________ × 10⁻³ °C. Atomic mass of C, H and O are 12, 1 and 16 a.m.u. respectively. [Given : Molal depression constant and density of water are 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹ and 1 g cm⁻³ respectively.]","type":"num","answer":"372","range":[372.0,372.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 310 (i = 1), 248 or 186 (association picture i < 1), 3720 (molality with 50 g instead of 500 g).\nWorking: n = 5/60 = 0.0833 mol in 0.500 kg water → m = 0.1667 mol kg⁻¹; i = 1 + 0.2 = 1.2; ΔTf = 1.2 × 1.86 × 0.1667 = 0.372 °C = 372 × 10⁻³ °C.\nKey idea: Acetic acid dissociates in water (i = 1.2), unlike its dimerisation in benzene.","traps":["Association idea carried over from benzene: In water acetic acid ionises: i = 1 + α.","i omitted: Include i whenever α is given."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The molality of a 10%(v/v) solution of di-bromine solution in CCl₄ (carbon tetrachloride) is 'x'. x = ________ × 10⁻² m. (Nearest integer) [Given : molar mass of Br₂ = 160 g mol⁻¹; atomic mass of C = 12 g mol⁻¹; atomic mass of Cl = 35.5 g mol⁻¹; density of dibromine = 3.2 g cm⁻³; density of CCl₄ = 1.6 g cm⁻³]","type":"num","answer":"139","range":[139.0,139.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 125 (taking 100 mL CCl₄ = 160 g as solvent), 69 (using Br₂ molar mass 80), 113 (using solution mass instead of solvent mass, 0.2/0.176).\nWorking: 10 mL Br₂ = 32 g = 0.2 mol; 90 mL CCl₄ = 144 g; m = 0.2/0.144 = 1.389 → 139 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: 10 mL Br₂ in 100 mL solution leaves 90 mL CCl₄ as solvent.","traps":["Solvent volume taken as 100 mL: v/v is per 100 mL of solution; solvent = 90 mL.","Atomic masses of C and Cl used unnecessarily: Molality needs only solvent mass, not its moles."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"In the depression of freezing point experiment\nA. Vapour pressure of the solution is less than that of pure solvent\nB. Vapour pressure of the solution is more than that of pure solvent\nC. Only solute molecules solidify at the freezing point\nD. Only solvent molecules solidify at the freezing point\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["A and C only","A only","A and D only","B and C only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) includes C: solute is thought to freeze out. (B) A only: misses that D is also true. (D) B and C: both reversed.\nWorking: a non-volatile solute lowers vapour pressure (A true, B false); on freezing, pure solid solvent separates (D true, C false).\nKey idea: Solid that forms is pure solvent; solution's lower vapour pressure meets the solid's curve at a lower T.","traps":["Who freezes out: Freezing removes pure solvent; solute stays in solution."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"5 g of NaOH was dissolved in deionized water to prepare a 450 mL stock solution. What volume (in mL) of this solution would be required to prepare 500 mL of 0.1 M solution? _____________\nGiven : Molar Mass of Na, O and H is 23, 16 and 1 g mol⁻¹ respectively","type":"num","answer":"180","range":[180.0,180.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 400 (treating 450 mL as solvent volume and mis-scaling), 18 or 1800 (unit slip), 200 (using 0.25 M after rounding).\nWorking: n = 5/40 = 0.125 mol in 0.450 L → 0.2778 M. Needed 0.05 mol → V = 0.05/0.2778 L = 0.180 L = 180 mL.\nKey idea: Moles needed / stock molarity = volume.","traps":["mL/L slip: Keep volumes in L until the end."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The number of units, which are used to express concentration of solutions from the following is ________. Mass percent, Mole, Mole fraction, Molarity, ppm, Molality","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6: counting 'mole'; 4: wrongly excluding ppm.\nWorking: all except 'mole' (an amount, not a concentration).\nKey idea: Mole is an amount of substance, not amount per solution.","traps":["Mole vs mole fraction: Concentration is a ratio."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The total pressure observed by mixing two liquids A and B is 350 mm Hg when their mole fractions are 0.7 and 0.3 respectively. The total pressure becomes 410 mm Hg if the mole fractions are changed to 0.2 and 0.8 respectively for A and B. The vapour pressure of pure A is ________ mm Hg. (Nearest integer) Consider the liquids and solutions behave ideally.","type":"num","answer":"314","range":[314.0,314.0],"explain":"Common slips: 434 (p_B instead of p_A); 313 (truncating 313.9 instead of rounding).\nWorking: 0.7p_A + 0.3p_B = 350; 0.2p_A + 0.8p_B = 410 → p_A ≈ 314 mm Hg (p_B ≈ 434 mm Hg).\nKey idea: Write p_total for each composition and eliminate p_B.","traps":["Which component asked: Re-read: pure A.","Rounding: Round to nearest integer."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The osmotic pressure of solutions of PVC in cyclohexanone at 300 K are plotted on the graph shown below. The molar mass of PVC is ________ g mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer) (Given : R = 0.083 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"41500","range":[41500.0,41500.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 41 or 41.5 if g vs kg confused; 3.4 × 10⁻⁶-type answers if slope is taken as M/RT; 1.5 × 10⁻² if slope is multiplied by RT.\nWorking: for π = CRT/M the plot of π/C vs C should be a horizontal line with intercept RT/M; the graph as drawn (line through origin with slope) does not match the law, so the value 6 × 10⁻⁴ must be read as RT/M: M = RT/(6 × 10⁻⁴) = 0.083 × 300/(6 × 10⁻⁴) = 41500 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: π/C (with C in g/L) = RT/M; set the given 6 × 10⁻⁴ equal to RT/M.","traps":["Slope vs intercept of π/C plot: Use π/C = RT/M and take the given number as RT/M.","Inverting RT/M: M = RT ÷ (π/C); a polymer must have a large M."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan25S1C17-p.webp"],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"What is the mass ratio of ethylene glycol (C₂H₆O₂, molar mass = 62 g/mol) required for making 500 g of 0.25 molal aqueous solution and 250 mL of 0.25 molar aqueous solution?","options":["1 : 2","1 : 1","2 : 1","3 : 1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) inverts the ratio; (B) assumes 0.25 molal and 0.25 molar need the same mass regardless of amount; (D) has no basis (arithmetic slip).\nWorking: n = 0.25(500 – 62n)/1000 → n ≈ 0.123 mol ≈ 7.6 g; 250 mL of 0.25 M: 0.0625 mol = 3.875 g; ratio ≈ 1.97 ≈ 2 : 1.\nKey idea: 0.25 m in ~0.5 kg water needs ~0.125 mol; 0.25 M in 0.25 L needs 0.0625 mol → about 2 : 1.","traps":["Solution mass vs solvent mass: Do the exact or approximate calculation and pick the nearest ratio.","Order of the ratio: Write the ratio in the order the question lists the solutions."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The number of pairs of the solutions having the same value of the osmotic pressure from the following is ______. (Assume 100% ionization) A. 0.500 M C₂H₅OH(aq) and 0.25 M KBr(aq); B. 0.100 M K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) and 0.100 M FeSO₄(NH₄)₂SO₄(aq); C. 0.05 M K₄[Fe(CN)₆](aq) and 0.25 M NaCl(aq); D. 0.15 M NaCl(aq) and 0.1 M BaCl₂(aq); E. 0.02 M KCl·MgCl₂·6H₂O(aq) and 0.05 M KCl(aq)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (treating Mohr's salt or carnallite as i = 2 or 3, or counting crystal water); 5 (taking K₄[Fe(CN)₆] i = 11 or counting C as equal); 2 (missing both double salts).\nWorking: A 0.50/0.50; B 0.50/0.50 (Mohr's salt i = 5); C 0.25/0.50 ✗; D 0.30/0.30; E 0.10/0.10 (carnallite i = 5) → 4 pairs.\nKey idea: Double salts dissociate fully into simple ions (FeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄ → 5 ions; KCl·MgCl₂ → 5 ions); complexes do not ([Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ stays as one ion, i = 5).","traps":["Double salt vs complex: Double salts give all constituent ions; complex ions stay intact.","Water of crystallisation: Hydrate water does not add particles."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Solid Lead nitrate is dissolved in 1 litre of water. The solution was found to boil at 100.15 °C. When 0.2 mol of NaCl is added to the resulting solution, it was observed that the solution froze at −0.8 °C. The solubility product of PbCl₂ formed is __________ × 10⁻⁶ at 298 K. (Nearest integer)\nGiven : K_b = 0.5 K kg mol⁻¹ and K_f = 1.8 K kg mol⁻¹. Assume molality to be equal to molarity in all cases.","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using i = 1 for lead nitrate (m = 0.3); ignoring precipitation and using 0.7 M ions; taking Ksp = s² or s·(2s) with common ion; using 0.444 directly as solubility.\nWorking: boiling: 0.15 = 3 × 0.5 × m → m(Pb(NO₃)₂) = 0.1. Adding 0.2 mol NaCl precipitates PbCl₂. Freezing: total particles = 0.8/1.8 = 0.444 m; spectators Na⁺ (0.2) + NO₃⁻ (0.2) = 0.4, so dissolved PbCl₂ gives 0.0444 m = 3s → s = 0.0148 M; Ksp = 4s³ = 1.3 × 10⁻⁵ = 13 × 10⁻⁶.\nKey idea: Freezing-point depression counts all dissolved particles; subtract spectator Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ to get PbCl₂'s share.","traps":["i = 3 for Pb(NO₃)₂: Count ions: Pb²⁺ + 2NO₃⁻.","Spectator ions remain in solution: Subtract 0.2 Na⁺ + 0.2 NO₃⁻ from total."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I: A. van't Hoff factor, i; B. k_f; C. Solutions with same osmotic pressure; D. Azeotropes\nList II: I. Cryoscopic constant; II. Isotonic solutions; III. Normal molar mass / Abnormal molar mass; IV. Solutions with same composition of vapour above it\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps C and D (isotonic vs azeotrope); (C) swaps B and C; (D) swaps A and B. Each moves one or two pairs.\nWorking: i = normal molar mass/abnormal molar mass (A-III); K_f is the cryoscopic constant (B-I); solutions with the same osmotic pressure are isotonic (C-II); an azeotrope boils with the same composition in liquid and vapour (D-IV).\nKey idea: i = normal/abnormal molar mass (ratio > 1 for dissociation).","traps":["i ratio direction: For molar mass, i = normal ÷ abnormal."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Azeotropes","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Some amount of dichloromethane (CH₂Cl₂) is added to 671.141 mL of chloroform (CHCl₃) to prepare 2.6 × 10⁻³ M solution of CH₂Cl₂ (DCM). The concentration of DCM is ______ ppm (by mass). Given: atomic mass: C = 12, H = 1, Cl = 35.5; density of CHCl₃ = 1.49 g cm⁻³","type":"num","answer":"148","range":[148.0,148.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 221 (taking 1 L solution), 0.148 (grams, not ppm), 1745 (using moles ×10⁶ without molar mass), 99 (dividing by 1.49 instead of multiplying).\nWorking: moles DCM = 2.6×10⁻³ × 0.671141 = 1.745×10⁻³; mass = 1.745×10⁻³ × 85 = 0.1483 g; CHCl₃ mass = 671.141 × 1.49 = 1000.0 g; ppm = 0.1483/1000 × 10⁶ ≈ 148.\nKey idea: 671.141 mL × 1.49 g mL⁻¹ = 1000 g; mass of DCM = 2.6×10⁻³ × 0.671 × 85 = 0.148 g, so ≈148 ppm.","traps":["Solution volume taken as 1 L: Use the actual volume (0.671 L, solute volume neglected).","Density direction: mass = density × volume."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A solution containing 2 g of a non-volatile solute in 20 g of water boils at 373.52 K. The molecular mass of the solute is ______ g mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) Given, water boils at 373 K, K_b for water = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.1 or 10 (grams-to-kg slip), 1000.\nWorking: ΔT_b = 373.52 − 373 = 0.52 K; M = K_b × w × 1000 / (ΔT_b × W) = 0.52 × 2 × 1000 / (0.52 × 20) = 100 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: ΔT_b = 0.52 K equals K_b, so molality is 1 mol kg⁻¹: 0.1 mol in 1 kg-basis gives M = 100.","traps":["Grams vs kilograms of solvent: Convert 20 g to 0.020 kg (the 1000 factor)."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Lead storage battery contains 38% by weight solution of H₂SO₄. The van't Hoff factor is 2.67 at this concentration. The temperature in Kelvin at which the solution in the battery will freeze is ____. (Nearest integer). Given K_f = 1.8 K kg mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"243","range":[243.0,243.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 262 (i ignored), 254 (molality per 100 g solution instead of solvent), 230 (using 38/100 kg solvent).\nWorking: in 100 g solution, 38 g H₂SO₄ and 62 g water; m = (38/98)/0.062 = 6.25 mol kg⁻¹; ΔTf = 2.67 × 1.8 × 6.25 ≈ 30.0 K; Tf = 273 − 30 = 243 K.\nKey idea: Use 62 g water as solvent per 100 g solution.","traps":["Solvent mass is 62 g: Molality uses kg of solvent: 100 − 38 = 62 g.","van't Hoff factor applied: Multiply by i for electrolytes."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"At 27°C, a solution containing 2.5 g of solute in 250.0 mL of solution exerts an osmotic pressure of 400 Pa. The molar mass of the solute is ________ g mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer) (Given : R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"62250","range":[62250.0,62250.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 62.25 or 6225 (missing or partial unit conversion), 0.06 (Pa treated as bar), 249 000 (using 1 atm-type conversion mistakes).\nWorking: π = 400 Pa = 0.004 bar; T = 300 K; M = wRT/(πV) = 2.5 × 0.083 × 300/(0.004 × 0.250) = 62250 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa so 400 Pa = 4 × 10⁻³ bar.","traps":["Pa not converted to bar: Convert 400 Pa = 0.004 bar before substituting.","Volume in mL: Use V = 0.250 L."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Evaluate the following statements for their correctness. A. The elevation in boiling point temperature of water will be same for 0.1 M NaCl and 0.1 M urea. B. Azeotropic mixtures boil without change in their composition. C. Osmosis always takes place from hypertonic to hypotonic solution. D. The density of 32% H₂SO₄ solution having molarity 4.09 M is approximately 1.26 g mL⁻¹. E. A negatively charged sol is obtained when KI solution is added to silver nitrate solution. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, B and D only","A and C only","B and D only","B, D and E only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts A, ignoring i = 2 for NaCl. (B) accepts C, reversing direction of osmosis. (D) accepts E, assuming I⁻ is adsorbed though Ag⁺ is in excess.\nWorking: i = 2 for NaCl; B true; C false: solvent moves hypotonic → hypertonic; D: d = 4.09×98/(10×32) = 1.25 ≈ 1.26, taken true; E false: KI into excess AgNO₃ gives AgI/Ag⁺, a positive sol.\nKey idea: The sol takes the charge of the ion in excess: KI added to AgNO₃ means Ag⁺ excess → positive sol.","traps":["Order of mixing decides sol charge: The reagent in the flask is in excess; its ion is adsorbed.","Same molarity, same ΔTb: Multiply by i: NaCl i ≈ 2, urea i = 1."],"topics":["Elevation of boiling point","Azeotropes","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The depression in freezing point observed for a formic acid solution of concentration 0.5 mL L⁻¹ is 0.0405 °C. Density of formic acid is 1.05 g mL⁻¹. The van't Hoff factor of the formic acid solution is nearly: (Given for water K_f = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹)","options":["0.8","1.1","1.9","2.4"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1.1 if mass is taken as 0.5 g without density and molar mass slightly off, or weak-acid intuition (i slightly above 1); (A) 0.8 suggests association (as for acetic acid in benzene); (D) 2.4 from dropping density or a factor slip.\nWorking: 0.5 mL × 1.05 = 0.525 g HCOOH = 0.0114 mol per litre ≈ 0.0114 mol kg⁻¹ (dilute); i = 0.0405/(1.86 × 0.0114) ≈ 1.9.\nKey idea: 0.525 g/46 g mol⁻¹ ≈ 0.0114 mol; i ≈ 1.9 (the number is chemically odd for a weak acid, but follows from the data).","traps":["Volume-of-solute concentration: Convert mL → g with density, then g → mol.","Expecting i ≈ 1 for a weak acid: Compute from the data; do not judge from expected dissociation."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Two solutions A and B are prepared by dissolving 1 g of non-volatile solutes X and Y, respectively, in 1 kg of water. The ratio of depression in freezing points for A and B is found to be 1 : 4. The ratio of molar masses of X and Y is:","options":["1 : 4","1 : 0.25","1 : 0.20","1 : 5"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1 : 4 copies the ΔTf ratio directly (forgetting the inverse relation); (C)/(D) are arbitrary near values.\nWorking: ΔTf = Kf·(w/M)/W; same w and W so ΔTf ∝ 1/M; M_X : M_Y = 4 : 1 = 1 : 0.25.\nKey idea: Larger ΔTf means more moles per gram, i.e. a smaller molar mass.","traps":["Direct instead of inverse ratio: Write ΔTf = Kf·w/(M·W) and cancel common terms."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Chlorophyll extracted from the crushed green leaves was dissolved in water to make 2 L solution of Mg of concentration 48 ppm. The number of atoms of Mg in this solution is x × 10²⁰ atoms. The value of x is ______. (Nearest Integer)\n(Given: Atomic mass of Mg is 24 g mol⁻¹, N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 (using 1 L), 2 (forgetting the 10²⁰ scaling: 2.4 × 10²¹), 240 (power-of-ten slip).\nWorking: 2 L ≈ 2000 g solution (density ≈ 1); Mg = 48 × 10⁻⁶ × 2000 = 0.096 g = 0.004 mol → 0.004 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 24.08 × 10²⁰ → 24.\nKey idea: ppm × mass of solution = mass of solute.","traps":["Power of ten in x × 10²⁰: Write 2.408 × 10²¹ = 24.08 × 10²⁰.","Density not given: Dilute aqueous solution: 1 mL ≈ 1 g."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"When 800 mL of 0.5 M nitric acid is heated in a beaker, its volume is reduced to half and 11.5 g of nitric acid is evaporated. The molarity of the remaining nitric acid solution is x × 10⁻² M. (Nearest Integer)\n(Molar mass of nitric acid is 63 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"54","range":[54.0,54.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 100 (ignoring solute loss), 27 (using 800 mL as final volume), 50 (ignoring volume change).\nWorking: initial HNO₃ = 0.8 × 0.5 = 0.4 mol; lost 11.5/63 = 0.1825 mol; left 0.2175 mol in 0.400 L → 0.544 M = 54.4 × 10⁻² → 54.\nKey idea: New M = (initial mol − lost mol) / new volume.","traps":["Ignoring evaporated acid: Subtract the 0.1825 mol lost."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The elevation in boiling point for 1 molal solution of non-volatile solute A is 3 K. The depression in freezing point for 2 molal solution of A in the same solvent is 6 K. The ratio of K_b and K_f i.e., K_b/K_f is 1 : X. The value of X is [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Ignoring molality and dividing 3 by 6 gives X = 2.\nWorking: K_b = 3/1 = 3 K kg mol⁻¹; K_f = 6/2 = 3 K kg mol⁻¹; K_b/K_f = 1 → X = 1.\nKey idea: Divide each ΔT by its own molality before taking the ratio.","traps":["Different molalities: K = ΔT/m for each case."],"topics":["Elevation of boiling point","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"250 g solution of D-glucose in water contains 10.8% of carbon by weight. The molality of the solution is nearest to (Given: Atomic Weights are H, 1u ; C, 12u ; O, 16u)","options":["1.03","2.06","3.09","5.40"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1.03: half value (e.g. using 360 or taking carbon fraction wrong); (C) 3.09 / (D) 5.40: using 250 g as solvent, or treating 10.8% as glucose % or mixing up carbon mass with glucose mass.\nWorking: carbon = 0.108 × 250 = 27 g; glucose C₆H₁₂O₆ is 72/180 = 40% C, so glucose = 67.5 g = 0.375 mol; water = 250 − 67.5 = 182.5 g; m = 0.375/0.1825 = 2.05 ≈ 2.06 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: Glucose is 40% carbon by mass, so solute mass = 27/0.4; subtract it from 250 g to get the solvent.","traps":["Solution mass used as solvent mass: Subtract solute mass from solution mass for molality.","% carbon read as % glucose: Convert carbon mass to glucose mass with 72/180."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Boiling point of a 2% aqueous solution of a non-volatile solute A is equal to the boiling point of 8% aqueous solution of a non-volatile solute B. The relation between molecular weights of A and B is.","options":["M_A = 4M_B","M_B = 4M_A","M_A = 8M_B","M_B = 8M_A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): ratio inverted (larger % → larger M missed); (C)/(D): using 8 instead of 8/2 = 4.\nWorking: equal ΔTb → equal molality: (2/M_A)/98 = (8/M_B)/92 → M_B = 4.26 M_A ≈ 4M_A (exactly 4 if both are taken per the same mass of water, the intended approximation).\nKey idea: Same ΔTb, same solvent → same moles per kg; 4 times the mass needs 4 times the molar mass.","traps":["Ratio inverted: More mass for the same moles means higher molar mass.","Solvent mass difference ignored: Take the closest option; intended approximation is 4."],"topics":["Elevation of boiling point","Molar mass from colligative properties"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"When a certain amount of solid A is dissolved in 100 g of water at 25°C to make a dilute solution, the vapour pressure of the solution is reduced to one-half of that of pure water. The vapour pressure of pure water is 23.76 mmHg. The number of moles of solute A added is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (exact mole-fraction form; the official key uses the dilute approximation); 0 or 12 (arithmetic slips); using 23.76 mmHg needlessly.\nWorking: using the dilute-solution form (p° − p)/p° = n₂/n₁: 0.5 = n₂/(100/18) → n₂ = 2.78 ≈ 3. The exact Raoult form n₂/(n₁ + n₂) = 0.5 gives n₂ = n₁ = 5.56 ≈ 6; the word 'dilute' points to the approximation (though a 50% lowering is not really dilute).\nKey idea: Under the dilute approximation Δp/p° ≈ n₂/n₁.","traps":["Exact vs approximate RLVP: Follow the \"dilute solution\" cue: use n₂/n₁.","Redundant vapour pressure value: Only the ratio p/p° matters."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"150 g of acetic acid was contaminated with 10.2 g ascorbic acid (C₆H₈O₆) to lower down its freezing point by (x × 10⁻¹)°C. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) [Given Kf = 3.9 K kg mol⁻¹; Molar mass of ascorbic acid = 176 g mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.15 or 1.5 if the ×10⁻¹ scaling is misread; using total mass (160.2 g) in place of solvent mass gives 14.\nWorking: n = 10.2/176 = 0.0580 mol; m = 0.0580/0.150 = 0.386 mol kg⁻¹; ΔTf = 3.9 × 0.386 = 1.51 °C = 15 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Molality uses kg of solvent only.","traps":["Solvent vs solution mass: Use 0.150 kg acetic acid."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A gaseous mixture of two substances A and B, under a total pressure of 0.8 atm is in equilibrium with an ideal liquid solution. The mole fraction of substance A is 0.5 in the vapour phase and 0.2 in the liquid phase. The vapour pressure of pure liquid A is ____ atm. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 0 (swapping mole fractions: 0.5 p° = 0.2 × 0.8 = 0.16 → p° = 0.32 atm).\nWorking: p_A = y_A P = 0.5 × 0.8 = 0.4 atm = x_A p°_A = 0.2 p°_A → p°_A = 2 atm.\nKey idea: Partial pressure from vapour, Raoult from liquid.","traps":["Which mole fraction where: y for Dalton, x for Raoult."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"If O₂ gas is bubbled through water at 303 K, the number of millimoles of O₂ gas that dissolve in 1 litre of water is _______. (Nearest Integer) (Given : Henry's Law constant for O₂ at 303 K is 46.82 k bar and partial pressure of O₂ = 0.920 bar) (Assume solubility of O₂ in water is too small, nearly negligible)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (stopping at mole fraction ~2×10⁻⁵); 20 (using x × 10⁶); 2 (rounding error).\nWorking: x(O₂) = p/K_H = 0.920/46820 = 1.965 × 10⁻⁵; n(O₂) ≈ x × n(H₂O) = 1.965×10⁻⁵ × 55.5 = 1.09 × 10⁻³ mol ≈ 1 mmol.\nKey idea: Multiply mole fraction by 55.5 mol water per litre.","traps":["Mole fraction reported as moles: n(gas) ≈ x × 55.5 mol."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"1.80 g of solute A was dissolved in 62.5 cm³ of ethanol and freezing point of the solution was found to be 155.1 K. The molar mass of solute A is ___ g mol⁻¹. [Given: Freezing point of ethanol is 156.0 K. Density of ethanol is 0.80 g cm⁻³. Freezing point depression constant of ethanol is 2.00 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"80","range":[80.0,80.0],"explain":"Common slips: 64 (using 62.5 cm³ as 62.5 g); 800 or 8 (g vs kg slip).\nWorking: mass of ethanol = 50 g; ΔTf = 0.9 K = 2.00 × (1.80/M)/0.050 → M = 80 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Solvent mass = 62.5 × 0.80 = 50 g.","traps":["Volume used as mass: Mass = volume × density."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The osmotic pressure of blood is 7.47 bar at 300 K. To inject glucose to a patient intravenously, it has to be isotonic with blood. The concentration of glucose solution in g L⁻¹ is ____. (Molar mass of glucose = 180 g mol⁻¹, R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹) (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"54","range":[54.0,54.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 0.3 (answer in mol L⁻¹ instead of g L⁻¹); 27 or 108 (wrong R or applying i).\nWorking: C = π/RT = 7.47/(0.083 × 300) = 0.30 mol L⁻¹; × 180 = 54 g L⁻¹.\nKey idea: Glucose is a non-electrolyte, i = 1.","traps":["Molar instead of mass concentration: Multiply by molar mass 180."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Solute A associates in water. When 0.7 g of solute A is dissolved in 42.0 g of water, it depresses the freezing point by 0.2°C. The percentage association of solute A in water, is [Given: Molar mass of A = 93 g mol⁻¹. Molal depression constant of water is 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹]","options":["50 %","60 %","70 %","80 %"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 60 % = taking i (0.6) itself as the percentage; (A) 50 % or (C) 70 % come from arithmetic slips (using i = 1 − α without the ½ factor gives 40 %, which is not an option).\nWorking: m = (0.7/93)/0.042 = 0.1792 mol kg⁻¹; Kf·m = 0.3333 K; i = 0.2/0.3333 = 0.60. For dimerisation i = 1 − α/2 ⇒ α = 0.80 = 80 %. (The question does not state the degree of association n; dimerisation is the intended assumption.)\nKey idea: For 2A ⇌ A₂, i = 1 − α/2; i < 1 signals association.","traps":["Assumed association number: Default to dimerisation: i = 1 − α/2.","Mass of solvent in kg: Convert 42 g to 0.042 kg."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"A commercially sold conc. HCl is 35% HCl by mass. If the density of this commercial acid is 1.46 g/mL, the molarity of this solution is : (Atomic mass : Cl = 35.5 amu, H = 1 amu)","options":["10.2 M","12.5 M","14.0 M","18.2 M"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 10.2 M – dividing by 1.46 instead of multiplying (or using 35 g per 100 mL with wrong density use); (B) 12.5 M – arithmetic slip / using M = 40; (D) 18.2 M – using 1.46 × 1000 × 0.35 / 28 or confusing with molality-type working.\nWorking: 1 L of acid weighs 1460 g, of which 35% = 511 g is HCl; 511/36.5 = 14.0 mol, so 14.0 M = (C).\nKey idea: Take 1 L: mass = 1000 × density; solute mass = mass % of that; divide by molar mass.","traps":["Density used as divisor: Write mass of 1000 mL = 1000 × d explicitly.","Molality instead of molarity: Molarity needs litres of solution, not kg of solvent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A 0.5 percent solution of potassium chloride was found to freeze at –0.24°C. The percentage dissociation of potassium chloride is ______. (Nearest integer) (Molal depression constant for water is 1.80 K kg mol⁻¹ and molar mass of KCl is 74.6 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"99 or 98","ranges":[[99.0,99.0],[98.0,98.0]],"range":[99.0,99.0],"explain":"Common slips: 49–50 – taking α = (i–1)/2 or forgetting i; 199 – reporting i × 100; 98 vs 99 – solvent mass basis (both accepted).\nWorking: 0.5 g KCl in 100 g water: m = (0.5/74.6)/0.1 = 0.0670; ΔTf(expected) = 1.8 × 0.0670 = 0.1206 K; i = 0.24/0.1206 = 1.99; α = i – 1 = 0.99 → 99%. Taking 99.5 g water (0.5 g in 100 g solution) gives i = 1.98 → 98%; both answers were accepted.\nKey idea: For KCl (n = 2), α = (i – 1)/(n – 1) = i – 1.","traps":["i reported as answer: Convert i to α = (i–1)/(n–1).","Solvent mass basis: State the basis; both accepted here."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The osmotic pressure exerted by a solution prepared by dissolving 2.0 g of protein of molar mass 60 kg mol⁻¹ in 200 mL of water at 27°C is ______ Pa. [integer value] (use R = 0.083 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"415","range":[415.0,415.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.415 or 4 (left in kPa / bar); 415000 (molar mass taken as 60 g); 369 (T = 27 K not converted correctly or R = 0.0821 misuse).\nWorking: n = 2/60000 = 3.33 × 10⁻⁵ mol; c = 1.667 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹; π = cRT = 1.667 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.083 × 300 = 4.15 × 10⁻³ bar = 415 Pa.\nKey idea: Watch kg mol⁻¹ and bar → Pa (×10⁵).","traps":["Molar mass in kg: Convert to 60 000 g mol⁻¹ before finding moles.","bar to Pa: 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Assertion (A): At 10°C, the density of a 5 M solution of KCl [atomic masses of K and Cl are 39 and 35.5 g mol⁻¹] is 'x' g mL⁻¹. The solution is cooled to −21°C. The molality of the solution will remain unchanged. Reason (R): The molality of a solution does not change with temperature as mass remains unaffected with temperature.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – thinking R is a general statement unrelated to A; (C) – believing cooling changes molality via density change; (D) – confusing molality with molarity, which does change with temperature.\nWorking: molality = moles of solute per kg of solvent; both are mass-based and do not change on cooling, so the molality stays the same (A true) and R gives exactly that reason. The density value is irrelevant to the conclusion. (Strictly, 5 M KCl cooled to −21°C would deposit ice/KCl, but the question intends the idealised mass-based argument.)\nKey idea: Molality uses only masses (solute moles and solvent kg), so temperature cannot change it; the given data are decoys.","traps":["Molarity vs molality swap: Ask whether the term uses volume (molarity) or mass (molality).","R judged not to explain A: R gives the exact cause of A, so it explains it."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"2 g of a non-volatile non-electrolyte solute is dissolved in 200 g of two different solvents A and B whose ebullioscopic constants are in the ratio of 1 : 8. The elevation in boiling points of A and B are in the ratio x/y (x : y). The value of y is ______ (Nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 – reports x instead of y; 0.125 or 4 – inverts or halves the ratio; trying to compute molality with an unknown molar mass.\nWorking: the same solute mass in the same solvent mass gives the same molality, so ΔTb ∝ Kb; ratio = 1 : 8 → y = 8.\nKey idea: Equal molality cancels: ΔTb(A)/ΔTb(B) = Kb(A)/Kb(B).","traps":["Unused masses: Same solute and solvent masses → molality cancels."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A solution containing 2.5 × 10⁻³ kg of a solute dissolved in 75 × 10⁻³ kg of water boils at 373.535 K. The molar mass of the solute is ______ g mol⁻¹. [nearest integer] (Given: K_b(H₂O) = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹, boiling point of water = 373.15 K)","type":"num","answer":"45","range":[45.0,45.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 0.045 or 45000 (kg/g mix-up); 4 or 450 (decimal slip in 0.385).\nWorking: ΔT_b = 0.385 K; M = K_b × w₂ × 1000 / (ΔT_b × w₁) = 0.52 × 2.5 × 1000 / (0.385 × 75) = 45.0 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Convert both masses to g (2.5 g, 75 g) before using the 1000 factor.","traps":["kg vs g in the formula: Convert to grams, or use the formula consistently in kg."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The vapour pressures of two volatile liquids A and B at 25 °C are 50 Torr and 100 Torr, respectively. If the liquid mixture contains 0.3 mole fraction of A, then the mole fraction of liquid B in the vapour phase is x/17. The value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (y_A numerator), 12 (using 0.3 for B), 7 (liquid mole fraction).\nWorking: p_A = 0.3 × 50 = 15, p_B = 0.7 × 100 = 70, total 85 Torr; y_B = 70/85 = 14/17, so x = 14.\nKey idea: Vapour is richer in the more volatile component: y_B = p_B/P.","traps":["Answering for A: Question asks about B in vapour."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"2.5 g of protein containing only glycine (C₂H₅NO₂) is dissolved in water to make 500 mL of solution. The osmotic pressure of this solution at 300 K is found to be 5.03 × 10⁻³ bar. The total number of glycine units present in the protein is ____ (Given: R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"330","range":[330.0,330.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 434 (dividing by residue mass 57 – chemically sounder but not the key); 165 (forgetting the 0.5 L volume).\nWorking: C = π/RT = 5.03×10⁻³/(0.083×300) = 2.02×10⁻⁴ M; n = 1.01×10⁻⁴ mol; M = 2.5/1.01×10⁻⁴ ≈ 24 750 g mol⁻¹; 24 750/75 = 330. Note: strictly, a glycine residue in a peptide is C₂H₃NO (57 g mol⁻¹), which would give ≈ 434; the key divides by the free-glycine mass given in the stem.\nKey idea: π = (w/M)RT/V, then units = M/75.","traps":["Free amino acid vs residue mass: Follow the formula the stem gives (C₂H₅NO₂, 75).","Volume in litres: n = CV with V = 0.5 L."],"topics":["Osmotic pressure","Molar mass from colligative properties"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"1.2 mL of acetic acid is dissolved in water to make 2.0 L of solution. The depression in freezing point observed for this strength of acid is 0.0198°C. The percentage of dissociation of the acid is ________. (Nearest integer) [Given : Density of acetic acid is 1.02 g mL⁻¹, Molar mass of acetic acid is 60 g mol⁻¹, K_f (H₂O) = 1.85 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking m = 0.0204 (forgetting the 2 L) gives i < 1; ignoring density gives 1.2 g → α ≈ 3 %; reporting i (1) instead of α.\nWorking: mass = 1.224 g = 0.0204 mol in 2 L → ≈ 0.0102 mol kg⁻¹ (dilute); i = 0.0198/(1.85 × 0.0102) = 1.049; α = i – 1 = 0.049 ≈ 5 %.\nKey idea: For HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, i = 1 + α.","traps":["Volume to molality: Divide moles by 2 (dilute: M ≈ m).","i vs α: α = (i – 1)/(n – 1) with n = 2."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Elevation in boiling point for 1.5 molal solution of glucose in water is 4 K. The depression in freezing point for 4.5 molal solution of glucose in water is 4 K. The ratio of molal elevation constant to molal depression constant (Kb/Kf) is ____.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.33 from inverting the ratio.\nWorking: Kb = 4/1.5, Kf = 4/4.5; Kb/Kf = 4.5/1.5 = 3.\nKey idea: Equal ΔT means K is inversely proportional to m.","traps":["Ratio inverted: K = ΔT/m."],"topics":["Elevation of boiling point","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Of the following four aqueous solutions, total number of those solutions whose freezing point is lower than that of 0.10 M C₂H₅OH is ______. (Integer answer) (i) 0.10 M Ba₃(PO₄)₂ (ii) 0.10 M Na₂SO₄ (iii) 0.10 M KCl (iv) 0.10 M Li₃PO₄","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if Ba₃(PO₄)₂ is excluded as insoluble; lower counts if i is miscounted.\nWorking: ethanol i = 1; Ba₃(PO₄)₂ i = 5, Na₂SO₄ 3, KCl 2, Li₃PO₄ 4 (ideal full dissociation). All four have larger ΔTf, so 4.\nKey idea: Any electrolyte at the same molarity depresses the freezing point more than a non-electrolyte.","traps":["All four electrolytes have i > 1: Treat each as completely dissociated as the question intends: i = 5, 3, 2, 4.","Lower freezing point = larger ΔTf: Bigger i·m → bigger depression → lower freezing point."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"An aqueous KCl solution of density 1.20 g mL⁻¹ has a molality of 3.30 mol kg⁻¹. The molarity of the solution in mol L⁻¹ is ______ (Nearest integer) [Molar mass of KCl = 74.5]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (forgetting the solute mass in the solution volume, 3.96); 2.75 type slips from using 1.2 L directly.\nWorking: 1 kg water + 3.30 mol KCl (245.85 g) = 1245.85 g solution; V = 1245.85/1.20 = 1038.2 mL; M = 3.30/1.0382 = 3.18 → 3.\nKey idea: Base on 1 kg solvent; add solute mass; divide by density for volume.","traps":["Solution mass includes the solute: Mass of solution = 1000 + 3.30×74.5 = 1245.85 g → V = 1038 mL.","Rounding: Carry three figures before rounding."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"100 mL of Na₃PO₄ solution contains 3.45 g of sodium. The molarity of the solution is ____ × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Atomic Masses – Na: 23.0 u, O: 16.0 u, P: 31.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 150 (moles of Na taken as moles of salt), 21 (dividing 3.45 g by M(Na₃PO₄) = 164), 5 (volume slip).\nWorking: n(Na) = 3.45/23 = 0.15 mol → n(Na₃PO₄) = 0.05 mol in 0.100 L → 0.50 M = 50 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Divide moles of Na by 3 to get moles of Na₃PO₄.","traps":["Moles of Na ÷ 3: Use the formula ratio.","Mass is of sodium, not of salt: Read which species the mass belongs to."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"83 g of ethylene glycol dissolved in 625 g of water. The freezing point of the solution is ____ K. (Nearest integer) [Use: Molal freezing point depression constant of water = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹; Freezing point of water = 273 K; Atomic masses: C: 12.0 u, O: 16.0 u, H: 1.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"269","range":[269.0,269.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (ΔTf instead of Tf), 270 (rounding ΔTf to 3 early), 270 also if glycerol's molar mass 92 is used.\nWorking: n = 83/62 = 1.339 mol; m = 1.339/0.625 = 2.142 mol kg⁻¹; ΔTf = 1.86 × 2.142 = 3.98 K; Tf = 273 – 3.98 = 269.0 ≈ 269 K.\nKey idea: Ethylene glycol C₂H₆O₂ = 62 g mol⁻¹; subtract ΔTf from 273.","traps":["Answer is Tf, not ΔTf: Subtract from 273 K.","Molar mass of ethylene glycol: HOCH₂CH₂OH = 62."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"1 kg of 0.75 molal aqueous solution of sucrose can be cooled up to −4 °C before freezing. The amount of ice (in g) that will be separated out is ______. (Nearest integer) [Given: K_f(H₂O) = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"518","range":[518.0,518.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 651 (taking 1 kg as the water mass: 1000 − 348.8); 0 or small values (using molality 0.75 directly); 278 (water left instead of ice).\nWorking: 0.75 mol sucrose (256.5 g) per 1000 g water → 1256.5 g solution; 1000 g solution has 795.9 g water and 0.597 mol sucrose. At −4 °C, m = 4/1.86 = 2.151 mol kg⁻¹, so water left = 0.597/2.151 = 0.2776 kg. Ice = 795.9 − 277.6 = 518.3 ≈ 518 g.\nKey idea: As ice forms, solute moles are fixed and molality rises; find the water that gives m = 4/1.86.","traps":["Solution mass taken as solvent mass: Subtract sucrose mass: solution = water + 342m g.","Water left vs ice formed: Ice = initial water − remaining water."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"40 g of glucose (Molar mass = 180) is mixed with 200 mL of water. The freezing point of solution is _____ K. (Nearest integer) [Given: K_f = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹; Density of water = 1.00 g cm⁻³; Freezing point of water = 273.15 K]","type":"num","answer":"271","range":[271.0,271.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (reporting ΔT_f); 270/272 from rounding or from using 240 g solution mass; 273 if molarity misused with wrong volume.\nWorking: m = (40/180)/0.200 = 1.111 mol kg⁻¹; ΔT_f = 1.86 × 1.111 = 2.07 K; T_f = 273.15 − 2.07 = 271.08 K → 271.\nKey idea: Mass of solvent = 200 g (density 1); subtract ΔT_f from 273.15.","traps":["Depression vs freezing point: Answer = 273.15 − ΔT_f.","Solvent vs solution mass: Molality uses kg of solvent only."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which one of the following 0.10 M aqueous solutions will exhibit the largest freezing point depression?","options":["hydrazine","glucose","glycine","KHSO₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hydrazine – weak base, i barely above 1; (B) glucose – non-electrolyte; (C) glycine – zwitterion, still one particle.\nWorking: ΔTf = iKfm. KHSO₄ → K⁺ + HSO₄⁻, and HSO₄⁻ partly ionises further, so i > 2; hydrazine (weak base), glucose and glycine (zwitterion, net neutral) have i ≈ 1. Largest depression: KHSO₄ (D).\nKey idea: Only the ionic salt gives multiple particles.","traps":["Glycine as ion pair: A zwitterion is one particle, i ≈ 1."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The molarity of the solution prepared by dissolving 6.3 g of oxalic acid (H₂C₂O₄·2H₂O) in 250 mL of water in mol L⁻¹ is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass: H: 1.0, C: 12.0, O: 16.0]","type":"num","answer":"20","range":[20.0,20.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 90 g mol⁻¹ (anhydrous) gives 28; forgetting the 250 mL → L conversion gives 5 × 10⁻² etc.\nWorking: M(H₂C₂O₄·2H₂O) = 126 g mol⁻¹; 6.3/126 = 0.050 mol; 0.050/0.250 L = 0.20 M = 20 × 10⁻² → 20 (taking the solution volume as 250 mL).\nKey idea: Oxalic acid dihydrate M = 126.","traps":["Anhydrous molar mass used: Include 2H₂O: 90 + 36 = 126.","Water volume vs solution volume: Take 250 mL as the solution volume (dilute)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"1.22 g of an organic acid is separately dissolved in 100 g of benzene (K_b = 2.6 K kg mol⁻¹) and 100 g of acetone (K_b = 1.7 K kg mol⁻¹). The acid is known to dimerize in benzene but remain as a monomer in acetone. The boiling point of the solution in acetone increases by 0.17 °C. The increase in boiling point of solution in benzene in °C is x × 10⁻². The value of x is _______. (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass: C = 12.0, H = 1.0, O = 16.0]","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 26 (ignores dimerisation, i = 1), 52 (i = 2), 17 (uses acetone Kb).\nWorking: acetone: 0.17 = 1.7 × (1.22/M)/0.1 → M = 122 g/mol (benzoic acid). Benzene, complete dimerisation i = 0.5: ΔTb = 0.5 × 2.6 × (0.01/0.1) = 0.13 °C → x = 13.\nKey idea: Dimerisation halves the particle count: i = ½.","traps":["Ignoring dimerisation: Association → i = 1/n; for dimer i = 0.5.","i inverted to 2: Fewer particles than formula units → i < 1."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"When 9.45 g of ClCH₂COOH is added to 500 mL of water, its freezing point drops by 0.5 °C. The dissociation constant of ClCH₂COOH is x × 10⁻³. The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [K_f(H₂O) = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"36","range":[36.0,36.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 344 (α reported); 24 (Ka = cα², 1−α ignored); 18 (m taken as 0.1); 72 (c taken as 0.4).\nWorking: M = 94.5, n = 0.1 mol in 0.5 kg → m = 0.2; i = 0.5/(1.86 × 0.2) = 1.344 → α = 0.344; Ka = cα²/(1−α) = 0.2 × 0.1183/0.656 = 0.0361 = 36 × 10⁻³ (taking molarity ≈ molality).\nKey idea: i = 1 + α for HA; Ka = cα²/(1−α).","traps":["1 − α dropped: α is not small here; keep (1 − α).","Mass of water vs solution: Molality = mol per kg solvent = 0.1/0.5."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"4.5 g of compound A (MW = 90) was used to make 250 mL of its aqueous solution. The molarity of the solution in M is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.2 (not converted to ×10⁻¹ form); 5 (mL not converted); 20 (unit slip).\nWorking: n = 4.5/90 = 0.05 mol; M = 0.05/0.25 = 0.2 M = 2 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: M = (mass/M)/V(L).","traps":["Answer format slip: Match the given ×10⁻¹ form."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"C₆H₆ freezes at 5.5 °C. The temperature at which a solution of 10 g of C₄H₁₀ in 200 g of C₆H₆ freezes is ____ °C. (The molal freezing point depression constant of C₆H₆ is 5.12 °C/m.)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (reporting ΔTf), 10 (adding ΔTf).\nWorking: m = (10/58)/0.200 = 0.862 mol kg⁻¹; ΔTf = 5.12 × 0.862 = 4.41 °C; Tf = 5.5 − 4.41 ≈ 1.09 → 1.\nKey idea: New Tf = Tf° − ΔTf.","traps":["Reporting ΔTf: Subtract from 5.5 °C."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"1 molal aqueous solution of an electrolyte A₂B₃ is 60% ionised. The boiling point of the solution at 1 atm is ______ K. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [Given Kb for (H₂O) = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"375","range":[375.0,375.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 374 (using i = 1 + α = 1.6 → ΔTb 0.83, 373.98 → 374), 373 (reporting ΔTb only or ignoring i), 376 (using i = 5).\nWorking: A₂B₃ → 2A³⁺ + 3B²⁻ (n = 5); i = 1 + α(n − 1) = 1 + 0.6 × 4 = 3.4. ΔTb = iKbm = 3.4 × 0.52 × 1 = 1.768 K; Tb = 373.15 + 1.77 = 374.9 ≈ 375 K.\nKey idea: i = 1 + (n − 1)α with n = 5 for A₂B₃; add ΔTb to 373 K.","traps":["Wrong number of ions: Count 2 + 3 = 5 ions.","Reporting ΔTb instead of Tb: Add to 373.15 K."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"If a compound AB dissociates to the extent of 75% in an aqueous solution, the molality of the solution which shows a 2.5 K rise in the boiling point of the solution is ______ molal. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [K_b = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (i ignored, 4.8); 2 (i = 2 taken, 2.4); rounding 2.75 down to 2.\nWorking: i = 1 + α = 1.75; m = ΔTb/(i·Kb) = 2.5/(1.75 × 0.52) = 2.75 → 3.\nKey idea: i = 1 + (n − 1)α with n = 2.","traps":["i not applied: AB → A⁺ + B⁻ raises particle count.","Full dissociation assumed: Use α = 0.75."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"224 mL of SO₂(g) at 298 K and 1 atm is passed through 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH solution. The non-volatile solute produced is dissolved in 36 g of water. The lowering of vapour pressure of solution (assuming the solution is dilute) (P°(H₂O) = 24 mm of Hg) is x × 10⁻² mm of Hg, the value of x is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 (i ignored), 18 (Na₂SO₃, 0.005 mol, i = 3), 23 (strict mole count at 298 K).\nWorking: taking 224 mL as 0.01 mol SO₂ (STP-like), SO₂ + NaOH → NaHSO₃ (0.01 mol, i = 2); ΔP = 24 × (2×0.01)/2 = 0.24 mm → 24. Strictly at 298 K, n(SO₂) = 0.00916 mol, giving a NaHSO₃/Na₂SO₃ mixture and ≈ 23, so the data are loosely set.\nKey idea: Equimolar SO₂ and NaOH → NaHSO₃ (i = 2).","traps":["Na₂SO₃ assumed: Check mole ratio 1:1 → bisulphite.","i omitted: NaHSO₃ → Na⁺ + HSO₃⁻, i = 2."],"topics":["Lowering of vapour pressure","van't Hoff factor"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"When 12.2 g of benzoic acid is dissolved in 100 g of water, the freezing point of solution was found to be –0.93 °C (K_f(H₂O) = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹). The number (n) of benzoic acid molecules associated (assuming 100% association) is ____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 – i computed as 1 by misreading ΔT; 0.5 entered as n (i instead of n); 4 – inverted relation.\nWorking: molality (as monomer) = (12.2/122)/0.1 = 1.0 m; ΔT_f = 0.93 = i × 1.86 × 1.0 → i = 0.5 = 1/n → n = 2 (dimer).\nKey idea: For complete association, i = 1/n.","traps":["i reported instead of n: n = 1/i for 100% association."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"At 20°C, the vapour pressure of benzene is 70 torr and that of methyl benzene is 20 torr. The mole fraction of benzene in the vapour phase at 20°C above an equimolar mixture of benzene and methyl benzene is ________ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"78","range":[78.0,78.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50 (taking liquid mole fraction); 22 (toluene fraction).\nWorking: p(benzene) = 0.5 × 70 = 35 torr, p(toluene) = 0.5 × 20 = 10 torr, total 45 torr; y(benzene) = 35/45 = 0.778 → 78 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Raoult for partial pressures, Dalton for vapour fraction; vapour is richer in the more volatile component.","traps":["Liquid fraction reported: Vapour fraction = p_i/p_total.","Wrong component: Answer for benzene, the more volatile one."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The vapour pressures of A and B at 25°C are 90 mm Hg and 15 mm Hg respectively. If A and B are mixed such that the mole fraction of A in the mixture is 0.6, then the mole fraction of B in the vapour phase is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 4 (liquid mole fraction of B), 9 (vapour mole fraction of A).\nWorking: p_A = 0.6 × 90 = 54, p_B = 0.4 × 15 = 6, total 60 mm Hg; y_B = 6/60 = 0.1 = 1 × 10⁻¹ → 1.\nKey idea: The vapour is enriched in the more volatile component; B drops from 0.4 in liquid to 0.1 in vapour.","traps":["Liquid vs vapour composition: Use y_B = x_B p°_B / p_total.","Wrong component: Re-read which component is asked."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The water having more dissolved O₂ is :","options":["boiling water","water at 80°C","polluted water","water at 4°C"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) hot water – expels dissolved gas; (C) polluted water – microbes use up O₂ (low DO), tempting only if pollution is confused with aeration.\nWorking: gas solubility in water falls as temperature rises (dissolution of O₂ is exothermic), and organic pollution consumes O₂ (high BOD); the coldest, clean sample (4°C) holds the most O₂.\nKey idea: Colder, cleaner water dissolves more O₂.","traps":["Hot water thought to dissolve more: Gases dissolve less on heating."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which one of the following 0.06 M aqueous solutions has lowest freezing point ?","options":["Al₂(SO₄)₃","C₆H₁₂O₆","KI","K₂SO₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) chosen if \"lowest\" is read as smallest depression; (D) if Al₂(SO₄)₃ ions are miscounted.\nWorking: ΔTf ∝ i·m; i = 5 (Al₂(SO₄)₃), 1 (glucose), 2 (KI), 3 (K₂SO₄); largest i → lowest freezing point.\nKey idea: Lowest FP = largest depression = largest i.","traps":["Lowest FP vs least depression: Lowest freezing point = largest ΔTf.","Ion count: i = 5 for Al₂(SO₄)₃."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"If the concentration of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) in blood is 0.72 g L⁻¹, the molarity of glucose in blood is ______ × 10⁻³ M. (Nearest integer) [Given : Atomic mass of C = 12, H = 1, O = 16 u]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.004 (not scaling to ×10⁻³), 40 (decimal slip).\nWorking: M(glucose) = 180; 0.72/180 = 4.0 × 10⁻³ M → 4.\nKey idea: Molarity = (g/L)/M.","traps":["Scale factor: Report 4 for 4 × 10⁻³."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"CO₂ gas is bubbled through water during a soft drink manufacturing process at 298 K. If CO₂ exerts a partial pressure of 0.835 bar then x m mol of CO₂ would dissolve in 0.9 L of water. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) (Henry's law constant for CO₂ at 298 K is 1.67 × 10³ bar)","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.5 (stopping at mole fraction ×10³), 0.025 (forgetting mmol), 0.45 (using 0.9 L as 0.9 mol water).\nWorking: x(CO₂) = p/K_H = 0.835/1670 = 5.0×10⁻⁴; water = 900 g/18 = 50 mol; n(CO₂) ≈ 5.0×10⁻⁴ × 50 = 0.025 mol = 25 mmol.\nKey idea: For a dilute gas, n(gas) ≈ x × n(water).","traps":["mmol conversion: Multiply moles by 1000.","Henry constant form: Check units: x = p/K_H must be dimensionless."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"When 3.00 g of a substance 'X' is dissolved in 100 g of CCl₄, it raises the boiling point by 0.60 K. The molar mass of the substance 'X' is ______ g mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer). [Given Kb for CCl₄ is 5.0 K kg mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"250","range":[250.0,250.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.25 or 25 (solvent mass not converted to kg / factor of 10 slip).\nWorking: ΔTb = Kb·m → 0.60 = 5.0 × (3.00/M)/0.100 → M = 250 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Use solvent mass in kg.","traps":["Solvent mass in kg: Divide by 0.100 kg."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The density of NaOH solution is 1.2 g cm⁻³. The molality of this solution is ________ m. (Round off to the Nearest Integer) [Use: Atomic masses: Na: 23.0 u, O: 16.0 u, H: 1.0 u; Density of H₂O: 1.0 g cm⁻³]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (using 1200 g of NaOH per 1000 g water or mixing up mass of solution and solvent), 4.17 → 4 (dividing 5 mol by 1.2 kg solution), or giving up.\nWorking: the stem gives no concentration. Taking the solution volume equal to the water volume (NaOH adds mass, not volume), 1 L solution = 1200 g containing 1000 g water and 200 g NaOH = 5 mol → 5 m. Without that assumption the question cannot be solved; it is data-deficient.\nKey idea: Equal volumes of solution and water: mass difference per litre (200 g) is the NaOH.","traps":["Solvent mass, not solution mass: Molality uses kg of water only.","Hidden assumption in incomplete data: Use the given water density: equal volumes."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"1.46 g of a biopolymer dissolved in a 100 mL water at 300 K exerted an osmotic pressure of 2.42 × 10⁻³ bar. The molar mass of the biopolymer is _____ × 10⁴ g mol⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer) [Use: R = 0.083 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 150 000 entered unscaled, 1.5 or 1502 (volume left in mL → ×1000 error).\nWorking: M = wRT/(πV) = 1.46 × 0.083 × 300 / (2.42×10⁻³ × 0.100) = 1.50 × 10⁵ g mol⁻¹ = 15 × 10⁴ → 15.\nKey idea: π = (w/M)RT/V with V in L.","traps":["mL to L: Use V = 0.100 L with R in L bar."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In a solvent 50% of an acid HA dimerizes and the rest dissociates. The van't Hoff factor of the acid is _____ × 10⁻². (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"125","range":[125.0,125.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 75 (association only), 150 (dissociation only), 100 (cancelling).\nWorking: per mole HA: 0.5 → 0.25 (HA)₂; 0.5 → 0.5 H⁺ + 0.5 A⁻; total particles = 0.25 + 1.0 = 1.25 → i = 125 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Add particles from both processes.","traps":["Dimer gives half the moles: Divide associated moles by 2."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"AB₂ is 10% dissociated in water to A²⁺ and B⁻. The boiling point of a 10.0 molal aqueous solution of AB₂ is _______ °C. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Molal elevation constant of water K_b = 0.5 K kg mol⁻¹; boiling point of pure water = 100 °C]","type":"num","answer":"106","range":[106.0,106.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 105 (i = 1), 115 (i = 3, full dissociation), 101 (using 1 molal).\nWorking: i = 1 + (3 − 1)(0.1) = 1.2; ΔTb = 1.2 × 0.5 × 10 = 6 K → 106 °C.\nKey idea: i = 1 + α(n − 1) with n = 3 ions for AB₂.","traps":["n taken as 2: Count ions: A²⁺ + 2B⁻ = 3.","Dissociation ignored: Apply i = 1.2."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A 6.50 molal solution of KOH (aq.) has a density of 1.89 g cm⁻³. The molarity of the solution is ________ mol dm⁻³. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Atomic masses: K: 39.0 u; O: 16.0 u; H: 1.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 (using 1000 g as solution mass: 6.5 × 1.89), 6 or 7 (volume taken as 1 L of water).\nWorking: 1000 g water + 6.5 × 56 = 364 g KOH → 1364 g solution; V = 1364/1.89 = 721.7 cm³; M = 6.5/0.7217 = 9.0.\nKey idea: Mass of solution = 1000 + (moles × molar mass); volume = mass/density.","traps":["Solution mass taken as 1 kg: Add 364 g of KOH before dividing by density."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"At 363 K, the vapour pressure of A is 21 kPa and that of B is 18 kPa. One mole of A and 2 moles of B are mixed. Assuming that this solution is ideal, the vapour pressure of the mixture is ______ kPa. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 20 (mole fractions swapped: 14 + 6), 19.5 (simple average), 39 (pressures added).\nWorking: x_A = 1/3, x_B = 2/3; p_total = 7 + 12 = 19 kPa.\nKey idea: Weight each p° by its own mole fraction.","traps":["Mole fractions swapped: Write x_A and x_B explicitly before substituting."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The mole fraction of a solute in a 100 molal aqueous solution _______ × 10⁻². (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Atomic masses: H: 1.0 u, O: 16.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"64","range":[64.0,64.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 180 or 1.8 (x = 100/55.56, forgetting to add solute moles), 10 (mass-based ratio).\nWorking: 100 mol solute per 1000 g water (55.56 mol); x = 100/155.56 = 0.643 → 64 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: x(solute) = m/(m + 1000/M_solvent).","traps":["Solute moles not added to total: Total moles = solute + solvent."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A 1 molal K₄Fe(CN)₆ solution has a degree of dissociation of 0.4. Its boiling point is equal to that of another solution which contains 18.1 weight percent of a non electrolytic solute A. The molar mass of A is_____ u. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n[Density of water = 1.0 g cm⁻³]","type":"num","answer":"85","range":[85.0,85.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 70 (using 100 g water instead of 81.9 g → 69.6), 74 (i = 3 from 1 + 0.4×5 → 73.7), 221 (ignoring i).\nWorking: i = 1 + α(n – 1) = 1 + 0.4 × 4 = 2.6 → effective molality 2.6. For A: 18.1 g in 81.9 g water, (18.1/M)/0.0819 = 2.6 → M = 18.1/(2.6 × 0.0819) = 85.0 → 85.\nKey idea: K₄[Fe(CN)₆] → 5 ions, so i = 1 + 4α; 18.1 wt% means 18.1 g solute per 81.9 g solvent.","traps":["Solvent mass from weight percent: Solvent = 100 – 18.1 = 81.9 g.","Number of ions n: K₄[Fe(CN)₆] → 4K⁺ + [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ = 5 particles."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"2 molal solution of a weak acid HA has a freezing point of −3.885 °C. The degree of dissociation of this acid is _____ × 10⁻³. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Molal depression constant of water = 1.85 K kg mol⁻¹, Freezing point of pure water = 0 °C]","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (α as 0.005), 1050 (reporting i × 10³), 105.\nWorking: ΔTf = 3.885 = i × 1.85 × 2 → i = 1.05; for HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, i = 1 + α → α = 0.05 = 50 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: i = 1 + (n − 1)α with n = 2.","traps":["Reporting i instead of α: α = i − 1 for a 1:1 electrolyte.","Missing minus sign: Freezing point of a solution is below 0 °C."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"A solute A dimerizes in water. The boiling point of a 2 molar solution of A is 100.52 °C. The percentage association of A is ____. (Round off to the Nearest integer) [Use : K_b for water = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹, Boiling point of water = 100 °C]","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 50 (using α = 1 − i), 0 (taking i = 1), 200 (i = 1 − α with α = 0.5 doubled).\nWorking: ΔTb = 0.52 = i × 0.52 × 2 (taking 2 M ≈ 2 m) → i = 0.5; for dimerisation i = 1 − α/2 → α = 1, i.e. 100 % association.\nKey idea: For dimerisation i = 1 − α/2.","traps":["i–α relation for dimer: For nA → Aₙ, i = 1 − α(1 − 1/n).","Molar vs molal: Dilute aqueous: take m ≈ M."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"If 80 g of copper sulphate CuSO₄·5H₂O is dissolved in deionised water to make 5 L of solution, the concentration of the copper sulphate solution is x × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹. The value of x is ______. [Atomic masses Cu: 63.54 u, S: 32 u, O: 16 u, H: 1 u]","type":"num","answer":"64","range":[64.0,64.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 100 (anhydrous molar mass 159.5); 321 (not dividing by volume); 13 (dividing by 5 twice).\nWorking: M(CuSO₄·5H₂O) = 63.54 + 32 + 64 + 90 = 249.54; 80/249.54 = 0.3206 mol in 5 L = 0.0641 M → x ≈ 64.\nKey idea: n = 80/249.54 = 0.3206 mol; M = 0.3206/5 = 0.0641 mol L⁻¹ = 64 × 10⁻³.","traps":["Water of crystallisation ignored: Molar mass of the pentahydrate = 249.54 g mol⁻¹.","Volume not divided: Divide 0.3206 mol by 5 L."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal & non-ideal solutions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"At 35°C, the vapour pressure of CS₂ is 512 mm Hg and that of acetone is 344 mm Hg. A solution of CS₂ in acetone has a total vapour pressure of 600 mm Hg. The false statement amongst the following is :","options":["heat must be absorbed in order to produce the solution at 35°C","Raoult's law is not obeyed by this system","a mixture of 100 mL CS₂ and 100 mL acetone has a volume < 200 mL","CS₂ and acetone are less attracted to each other than to themselves"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student thinks dissolution releases heat. (B): true since deviation exists. (D): true, the definition of positive deviation.\nWorking: total VP 600 mm exceeds both pure VPs, so the system shows positive deviation: Δ_mixH > 0, Δ_mixV > 0, weaker A–B attraction. Volume < 200 mL is false.\nKey idea: P(total) above both pure components ⇒ positive deviation ⇒ ΔH_mix > 0 and ΔV_mix > 0.","traps":["ΔV sign for positive deviation: Positive deviation: molecules escape more easily, ΔH > 0 and ΔV > 0.","Asked for the false statement: Mark each statement T/F before choosing."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Two open beakers one containing a solvent and the other containing a mixture of that solvent with a non volatile solute are together sealed in a container. Over time -","options":["The volume of the solution does not change and the volume of the solvent decreases","The volume of the solution decrease and the volume of the solvent increases","The volume of the solution increase and the volume of the solvent decreases","The volume of the solution and the solvent does not change"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats both phases as in equilibrium with no net transfer to solution; (B) reverses direction; (D) assumes a single common equilibrium is reached immediately.\nWorking: the solution has the lower vapour pressure, so solvent evaporates from the pure-solvent beaker and condenses into the solution until (ideally) all solvent has transferred.\nKey idea: Solvent always moves from higher to lower vapour pressure through the vapour.","traps":["Transfer direction reversed: Vapour condenses where vapour pressure is lower."],"topics":["Lowering of vapour pressure","Vapour pressure"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Vapour pressure","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"A graph of vapour pressure and temperature for three different liquids X, Y and Z is shown below: [vapour pressure (mm Hg: 200, 400, 500, 800) vs temperature (293, 313, 333, 353 K); X rises first (lowest temperature), then Y, then Z]. The following inferences are made: (A) X has higher intermolecular interactions compared to Y. (B) X has lower intermolecular interactions compared to Y. (C) Z has lower intermolecular interactions compared to Y. The correct inference(s) is/are :","options":["(A)","(C)","(B)","(A) and (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) reverse the relation (higher VP taken as stronger forces); (B) misreads Z.\nWorking: at any T, VP(X) > VP(Y) > VP(Z), so intermolecular forces X < Y < Z; only inference (B) is right, i.e. option (C).\nKey idea: Curve that rises at lower T = more volatile = weaker forces.","traps":["Higher VP read as stronger forces: More volatile ⇒ weaker attractions."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Ferrous sulphate heptahydrate is used to fortify foods with iron. The amount (in grams) of the salt required to achieve 10 ppm of iron in 100 kg of wheat is ______. Atomic weight : Fe = 55.85 ; S = 32.0 ; O = 16.00","type":"num","answer":"4.95 to 4.97","range":[4.95,4.97],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1.0 (Fe only), 2.72 (anhydrous FeSO₄), 0.0497 (unit slip).\nWorking: 10 ppm of 10⁵ g = 1.0 g Fe; salt = 1.0 × 277.85/55.85 = 4.97 g (with H = 1.008, M = 277.96 → 4.98 g, just outside the accepted range).\nKey idea: Mass of salt = mass of Fe × M(salt)/A(Fe), using the hydrate.","traps":["Anhydrous molar mass used: Include 7 H₂O.","ppm to grams conversion: 1 ppm of 100 kg = 0.1 g."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The molarity of HNO₃ in a sample which has density 1.4 g/mL and mass percentage of 63% is ______. (Molecular weight of HNO₃ = 63)","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: Forgetting density gives 10; using 100 g of water as volume gives 10.\nWorking: M = (1000 × 1.4 × 0.63)/63 = 14 M.\nKey idea: M = 10 × d × (w/w%)/M_solute.","traps":["Density ignored: Volume = mass/density."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"How much amount of NaCl should be added to 600 g of water (ρ = 1.00 g/mL) to decrease the freezing point of water to −0.2 °C? ______. (The freezing point depression constant for water = 2 K kg mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"1.74 to 1.76 or 0.03","ranges":[[1.74,1.76],[0.03,0.03]],"range":[1.74,1.76],"explain":"Common slips: Without i: 3.51 g / 0.06 mol; forgetting ×0.6 kg gives 2.93 g.\nWorking: ΔTf = i·Kf·m → 0.2 = 2 × 2 × m → m = 0.05 mol kg⁻¹; 0.6 kg → 0.03 mol NaCl = 1.755 g. NTA accepts 1.74–1.76 (grams) or 0.03 (moles) because 'amount' is ambiguous.\nKey idea: Use i = 2 for NaCl and mass of solvent in kg.","traps":["van't Hoff factor omitted: NaCl gives two ions, i = 2.","\"Amount\" ambiguity: NTA accepted both; give mass unless moles asked."],"topics":["Depression of freezing point","van't Hoff factor"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A cylinder containing an ideal gas (0.1 mol of 1.0 dm³) is in thermal equilibrium with a large volume of 0.5 molal aqueous solution of ethylene glycol at its freezing point. The gas is held under a frictionless piston by stoppers S₁ and S₂, and the cylinder stands in the solution. If the stoppers S₁ and S₂ are suddenly withdrawn, the volume of the gas in litres after equilibrium is achieved will be ____. (Given, Kf (water) = 2.0 K kg mol⁻¹, R = 0.08 dm³ atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"2.17 to 2.23","range":[2.17,2.23],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1.0 (gas assumed not to expand), 0.46 (Boyle step inverted, V = 1/2.176), 2.24 (gas taken to STP at 273 K, 1 atm with 0.1 mol); using 273 K instead of 272 K still falls inside the accepted range (2.18).\nWorking: ΔTf = 2.0 × 0.5 = 1 K, T = 272 K; P = 0.1 × 0.08 × 272/1 = 2.176 atm; isothermal expansion against 1 atm gives V = 2.176 L ≈ 2.18.\nKey idea: Gas stays at 272 K; final P = 1 atm, so V = nRT/1.","traps":["Final pressure unclear: Withdrawn stoppers let the piston move until gas pressure = 1 atm."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"10.30 mg of O₂ is dissolved into a liter of sea water of density 1.03 g/mL. The concentration of O₂ in ppm is ____.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10.3 (taking 1 L = 1000 g), 0.01 (mg/g confusion), 10300.\nWorking: mass of 1 L sea water = 1030 g; ppm = (10.30 × 10⁻³ g / 1030 g) × 10⁶ = 10.\nKey idea: ppm (mass) = mg solute per kg solution: 10.30/1.03 = 10.","traps":["Density ignored: Sea water mass = 1.03 × 1000 g."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The size of a raw mango shrinks to a much smaller size when kept in a concentrated salt solution. Which one of the following processes can explain this ?","options":["Diffusion","Dialysis","Osmosis","Reverse osmosis"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) diffusion is solute/solvent mixing without membrane; (B) dialysis moves solutes across a membrane; (D) reverse osmosis needs applied external pressure.\nWorking: water flows out of the mango cells through the semipermeable membrane into the hypertonic salt solution – osmosis (exosmosis).\nKey idea: Solvent flows from dilute to concentrated side through a membrane.","traps":["Reverse osmosis chosen: RO needs pressure greater than π."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Henry's constant (in kbar) for four gases α, β, γ and δ in water at 298 K is given below:\nGas | α | β | γ | δ\nK_H (kbar) | 50 | 2 | 2 × 10⁻⁵ | 0.5\n(density of water = 10³ kg m⁻³ at 298 K)\nThis table implies that:","options":["The pressure of a 55.5 molal solution of γ is 1 bar","The pressure of a 55.5 molal solution of δ is 250 bar","Solubility of γ at 308 K is lower than at 298 K","α has the highest solubility in water at a given pressure"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats x ≈ 1 or loses a factor of 100; (C) is generally true for gases but not implied by single-temperature data; (D) reverses K_H–solubility relation.\nWorking: 55.5 molal in water (55.5 mol water/kg) → x = 0.5. p = K_H·x: δ → 0.5 kbar × 0.5 = 0.25 kbar = 250 bar. γ → 10⁻⁵ kbar = 0.01 bar, not 1 bar. (C) cannot be inferred from 298 K data alone. (D) α has the largest K_H, so lowest solubility.\nKey idea: 55.5 mol/kg of solute in 55.5 mol of water gives x = 0.5.","traps":["Higher K_H read as higher solubility: Solubility ∝ 1/K_H at fixed p.","Generally true but not implied by data: Ask what the table alone shows."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The mole fraction of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) in an aqueous binary solution is 0.1. The mass percentage of water in it, to the nearest integer, is ______.","type":"num","answer":"47","range":[47.0,47.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 53 (glucose % instead of water), 90 (mole % of water), 10.\nWorking: 0.1 mol glucose = 18 g, 0.9 mol water = 16.2 g; % water = 16.2/34.2 × 100 = 47.4 → 47.\nKey idea: Take 1 mol total and convert each to grams.","traps":["Solute percentage reported: Re-read which component is asked."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"6.023 × 10²² molecules are present in 10 g of a substance 'x'. The molarity of a solution containing 5 g of substance 'x' in 2 L solution is ______ × 10⁻³.","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50: forgetting the 2 L; 250: power-of-ten slip; 2.5: answering in 10⁻² units.\nWorking: 0.1 mol in 10 g → M = 100 g/mol; 5 g = 0.05 mol in 2 L → 0.025 M = 25 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: Moles = N/N_A; molarity = moles/volume (L).","traps":["Volume not divided: Divide by 2 L."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"If 250 cm³ of an aqueous solution containing 0.73 g of a protein A is isotonic with one litre of another aqueous solution containing 1.65 g of a protein B, at 298 K, the ratio of the molecular masses of A and B is ______ × 10⁻² (to the nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"177","range":[177.0,177.0],"explain":"Common slips: 44: forgetting the 0.25 L volume (0.73/1.65); 56: inverted ratio; 1.77 entered without ×10⁻² scaling.\nWorking: isotonic → equal molarity: (0.73/M_A)/0.25 = (1.65/M_B)/1 → M_A/M_B = 0.73/(0.25 × 1.65) = 1.77 → 177.\nKey idea: Isotonic = same c (non-electrolytes); keep each volume.","traps":["Volume dropped: Concentration = (w/M)/V for each solution.","Ratio inverted: Solve for M_A/M_B explicitly."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"At 300 K, the vapour pressure of a solution containing 1 mole of n-hexane and 3 moles of n-heptane is 550 mm of Hg. At the same temperature, if one more mole of n-heptane is added to this solution, the vapour pressure of the solution increases by 10 mm of Hg. What is the vapour pressure in mm Hg of n-heptane in its pure state ________ ?","type":"num","answer":"600","range":[600.0,600.0],"explain":"Common slips: 400 (p° of hexane picked); 560 or 550 (solution VP taken as pure VP).\nWorking: 550 = (p_hex + 3p_hep)/4 and 560 = (p_hex + 4p_hep)/5 → p_hex + 3p_hep = 2200, p_hex + 4p_hep = 2800 → p°(heptane) = 600 mm Hg (p°hexane = 400). Note: physically hexane is the more volatile, so the data are artificial, but the arithmetic is fixed.\nKey idea: Mole fractions change from 3/4 to 4/5 heptane.","traps":["Reporting the other component: Check which liquid is asked: n-heptane.","Real-world volatility clash: Use the given data; Raoult arithmetic decides."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A 100 mL solution was made by adding 1.43 g of Na₂CO₃·xH₂O. The normality of the solution is 0.1 N. The value of x is ______. (The atomic mass of Na is 23 g/mol)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: x ≈ 2 if n-factor 1 is used (M = 143 → x ≈ 2.06); x = 0 / non-integer from forgetting the 100 mL volume.\nWorking: n-factor of Na₂CO₃ = 2 → molarity 0.05 M → 0.005 mol in 0.1 L; M = 1.43/0.005 = 286 = 106 + 18x → x = 10.\nKey idea: N = n-factor × M; for Na₂CO₃ (total cation charge) n = 2.","traps":["n-factor taken as 1: Na₂CO₃ has total cationic charge 2 → n = 2.","Volume not converted: Moles = N × V(L) / n-factor."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The osmotic pressure of a solution of NaCl is 0.10 atm and that of a glucose solution is 0.20 atm. The osmotic pressure of a solution formed by mixing 1 L of the sodium chloride solution with 2 L of the glucose solution is x × 10⁻³ atm. x is ______. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"167","range":[167.0,167.0],"explain":"Common slips: 200 or 150 (simple average or sum without dilution); 300 (adding π values); 200 also if i = 2 is applied again to NaCl.\nWorking: π is proportional to total particle concentration; π_mix = (0.10 × 1 + 0.20 × 2)/3 = 0.1667 atm = 166.7 × 10⁻³ → 167.\nKey idea: π contributions dilute by volume: π_mix = Σπᵢ Vᵢ / V_total.","traps":["Applying i again to NaCl: Given π already includes dissociation.","Simple average or sum: Weight each π by its volume fraction."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A soft drink was bottled with a partial pressure of CO₂ of 3 bar over the liquid at room temperature. The partial pressure of CO₂ over the solution approaches a value of 30 bar when 44 g of CO₂ is dissolved in 1 kg of water at room temperature. The approximate pH of the soft drink is ______ × 10⁻¹. (First dissociation constant of H₂CO₃ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁷; log 2 = 0.3; density of the soft drink = 1 g mL⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"37","range":[37.0,37.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 32 (using 1 M), 74 or 54 (forgetting the square root/½ factor), 43 (log 2 sign slip).\nWorking: Henry's law: 1 mol CO₂/kg ↔ 30 bar, so 3 bar → 0.1 mol/kg ≈ 0.1 M; [H⁺] = √(4×10⁻⁷ × 0.1) = 2×10⁻⁴ M; pH = 4 – 0.3 = 3.7 = 37 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Solubility scales linearly with p; then pH = ½(pKa – log c).","traps":["Reference concentration used directly: Scale to 3 bar: 0.1 mol.","Square root missed: [H⁺] = √(Ka c)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"A solution of two components containing n₁ moles of the 1st component and n₂ moles of the 2nd component is prepared. M₁ and M₂ are the molecular weights of component 1 and 2 respectively. If d is the density of the solution in g mL⁻¹, C₂ is the molarity and x₂ is the mole fraction of the 2nd component, then C₂ can be expressed as :","options":["C₂ = 1000x₂ / [M₁ + x₂(M₂ − M₁)]","C₂ = dx₂ / [M₂ + x₂(M₂ − M₁)]","C₂ = dx₁ / [M₂ + x₂(M₂ − M₁)]","C₂ = 1000dx₂ / [M₁ + x₂(M₂ − M₁)]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) drops density d (mass taken as volume); (B) drops the factor 1000 (mL→L) and uses M₂ for the base term; (C) uses x₁ in the numerator.\nWorking: take 1 mol total; mass = x₁M₁ + x₂M₂ = M₁ + x₂(M₂ − M₁) g; volume = mass/d mL; C₂ = x₂ × 1000d / [M₁ + x₂(M₂ − M₁)].\nKey idea: Basis 1 mol total: average molar mass M₁ + x₂(M₂ − M₁) gives the mass of solution.","traps":["Factor 1000 or density dropped: Track units: g ÷ (g/mL) = mL, ×1000 to per litre.","Wrong component in numerator: Molarity of component 2 needs moles of 2 (x₂)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The elevation of boiling point of 0.10 m aqueous CrCl₃·xNH₃ solution is two times that of 0.05 m aqueous CaCl₂ solution. The value of x is ____.\n[Assume 100% ionisation of the complex and CaCl₂, coordination number of Cr as 6, and that all NH₃ molecules are present inside the coordination sphere]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (taking i = 2 or forgetting CaCl₂ has i = 3), 6 (assuming i = 4, all Cl⁻ outside), 3.\nWorking: i(complex) × 0.10 = 2 × 3 × 0.05 → i = 3 → [Cr(NH₃)₅Cl]²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → x = 5.\nKey idea: i = 3 → two Cl⁻ outside → one Cl inside → five NH₃ fill CN 6.","traps":["i of CaCl₂ taken as 1: CaCl₂ → 3 ions, i = 3.","Coordination sphere count: x + (inner Cl) = 6; inner Cl = 3 − (i − 1)."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Elevation of boiling point"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"A set of solutions is prepared using 180 g of water as a solvent and 10 g of different non-volatile solutes A, B and C. The relative lowering of vapour pressure in the presence of these solutes are in the order [Given, molar mass of A = 100 g mol⁻¹; B = 200 g mol⁻¹; C = 10,000 g mol⁻¹]","options":["A > B > C","A > C > B","C > B > A","B > C > A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) reverses the order (taking RLVP proportional to molar mass); (B) and (D) are mixed orders.\nWorking: moles of solute 0.1, 0.05, 0.001; RLVP = mole fraction of solute, so A > B > C.\nKey idea: Equal mass: RLVP ∝ 1/M.","traps":["RLVP proportional to molar mass: Colligative = number of particles, so smaller M wins."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The vapour pressures of pure liquids A and B are 400 and 600 mmHg, respectively at 298 K. On mixing the two liquids, the sum of their initial volumes is equal to the volume of the final mixture. The mole fraction of liquid B is 0.5 in the mixture. The vapour pressure of the final solution, the mole fraction of components A and B in vapour phase, respectively are –","options":["500 mmHg, 0.5, 0.5","450 mmHg, 0.4, 0.6","450 mmHg, 0.5, 0.5","500 mmHg, 0.4, 0.6"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.5, 0.5 copies liquid-phase mole fractions into the vapour; (B)/(C) 450 mmHg from averaging wrongly or treating as non-ideal negative deviation.\nWorking: no volume change ⇒ ideal solution; P = 0.5×400 + 0.5×600 = 500 mmHg; y_A = 200/500 = 0.4, y_B = 0.6.\nKey idea: Vapour is richer in the more volatile component: y_B = 300/500.","traps":["Vapour composition taken equal to liquid composition: Compute y_i = x_i p_i°/P_total.","Missing that ΔV_mix = 0 means ideal: Link ΔV_mix = 0 to Raoult's law."],"topics":["Raoult's law","Ideal & non-ideal solutions"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"For the solution of the gases w, x, y and z in water at 298 K, the Henry's law constants (K_H) are 0.5, 2, 35 and 40 kbar, respectively. The correct plot for the given data is: [Four graphs of partial pressure (y-axis) vs mole fraction of water (x-axis), each with lines labelled w, x, y, z.]","options":["Lines rising with mole fraction of water, starting at different positive intercepts, z highest then y, x, w","Lines rising slightly with mole fraction of water from different intercepts, z top, w bottom","Lines falling with mole fraction of water from intercepts on the y-axis, slope magnitude z > y > x > w (w almost flat)","Lines falling with mole fraction of water, but z and y with shallow slopes and x, w with steeper slopes"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) rising lines fit the NCERT gas-fraction axis; (D) wrong ordering of slopes.\nWorking: p = K_H·x_gas = K_H(1 − x_water); p falls linearly with x_water, intercept K_H and slope −K_H, so z (40 kbar) is highest and steepest, w (0.5 kbar) nearly flat.\nKey idea: x_gas = 1 − x_water ⇒ negative slope = −K_H.","traps":["Axis variable misread: Rewrite p in terms of x_water.","Slope ordering reversed: Higher K_H ⇒ steeper line, lower solubility."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The osmotic pressure of a dilute solution of an ionic compound XY in water is four times that of a solution of 0.01 M BaCl₂ in water. Assuming complete dissociation of the given ionic compounds in water, the concentration of XY (in mol L⁻¹) in solution is :","options":["6 × 10⁻²","4 × 10⁻⁴","16 × 10⁻⁴","4 × 10⁻²"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 4 × 10⁻² ignores i of both (4 × 0.01); (B)/(C) arise from inverting the ratio or squaring factors.\nWorking: π ∝ iC. i(BaCl₂) = 3, i(XY) = 2. 2C = 4 × 3 × 0.01 ⇒ C = 0.06 M = 6 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Equate i₁C₁ = 4 i₂C₂ with i(BaCl₂) = 3, i(XY) = 2.","traps":["van't Hoff factor ignored: Include i = 3 for BaCl₂ and i = 2 for XY.","Ratio inverted: Write π(XY) = 4π(BaCl₂) explicitly."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Osmotic pressure"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Liquid 'M' and liquid 'N' form an ideal solution. The vapour pressures of pure liquids 'M' and 'N' are 450 and 700 mmHg, respectively, at the same temperature. Then correct statement is: (x_M = mole fraction of 'M' in solution; x_N = mole fraction of 'N' in solution; y_M = mole fraction of 'M' in vapour phase; y_N = mole fraction of 'N' in vapour phase)","options":["(x_M − y_M) < (x_N − y_N)","x_M/x_N < y_M/y_N","x_M/x_N > y_M/y_N","x_M/x_N = y_M/y_N"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the sign relation (x_M − y_M is positive); (B) assumes vapour richer in M; (D) assumes vapour composition equals liquid.\nWorking: y_M/y_N = (x_M/x_N)(450/700) < x_M/x_N. Also vapour is richer in N, so x_M − y_M > 0 > x_N − y_N, making (A) false.\nKey idea: y_M/y_N = (p°_M/p°_N)(x_M/x_N), and p°_M < p°_N.","traps":["Vapour taken richer in less volatile M: More volatile component is enriched in vapour.","Vapour composition equal to liquid: Ideal ≠ azeotrope."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Molal depression constant for a solvent is 4.0 K kg mol⁻¹. The depression in the freezing point of the solvent for 0.03 mol kg⁻¹ solution of K₂SO₄ is : (Assume complete dissociation of the electrolyte)","options":["0.12 K","0.36 K","0.18 K","0.24 K"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.12 K uses i = 1; (D) 0.24 K uses i = 2; (C) 0.18 K arithmetic slip / i = 1.5.\nWorking: K₂SO₄ → 2K⁺ + SO₄²⁻, i = 3; ΔTf = i Kf m = 3 × 4.0 × 0.03 = 0.36 K.\nKey idea: Count ions: 2 K⁺ + 1 SO₄²⁻ = 3.","traps":["Ion count for K₂SO₄: Write the dissociation equation."],"topics":["Depression of freezing point","van't Hoff factor"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"What would be the molality of 20% (mass/mass) aqueous solution of KI? (molar mass of KI = 166 g mol⁻¹)","options":["1.08","1.48","1.51","1.35"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1.08 / (D) 1.35 from rounding or wrong mass; using 100 g solution as solvent gives 1.20 (absent).\nWorking: 20 g KI in 80 g water; moles = 20/166 = 0.1205; m = 0.1205/0.080 = 1.506 ≈ 1.51 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: Solvent mass = 100 – 20 = 80 g.","traps":["Solution mass used as solvent mass: Subtract solute mass first."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lowering of vapour pressure","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"At room temperature, a dilute solution of urea is prepared by dissolving 0.60 g of urea in 360 g of water. If the vapour pressure of pure water at this temperature is 35 mmHg, lowering of vapour pressure will be (molar mass of urea = 60 g mol⁻¹):","options":["0.027 mmHg","0.028 mmHg","0.017 mmHg","0.031 mmHg"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) come from arithmetic slips (e.g. using 0.0008 or mass ratios); (D) from using wrong moles of water.\nWorking: n(urea) = 0.01 mol, n(water) = 20 mol; Δp = p°·x_solute = 35 × 0.01/20.01 ≈ 0.0175 → 0.017 mmHg.\nKey idea: Δp = p° x₂ with x₂ ≈ n₂/n₁ for dilute solutions.","traps":["Mass ratio used instead of mole ratio: Convert both to moles first.","Relative lowering reported as absolute: Multiply by p° = 35 mmHg."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"1 g of non-volatile non-electrolyte solute is dissolved in 100 g of two different solvents A and B whose ebullioscopic constants are in the ratio of 1 : 5. The ratio of the elevation in their boiling points, ΔT_b(A)/ΔT_b(B), is:","options":["5 : 1","10 : 1","1 : 5","1 : 0.2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) invert the ratio; (B) adds a spurious factor.\nWorking: same solute mass and solvent mass give the same molality; ΔT_b = K_b·m, so ratio = 1 : 5.\nKey idea: Equal molality → ratio of K_b.","traps":["Ratio inverted: ΔT_b ∝ K_b directly."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The mole fraction of a solvent in aqueous solution of a solute is 0.8. The molality (in mol kg⁻¹) of the aqueous solution is","options":["13.88 × 10⁻¹","13.88 × 10⁻²","13.88","13.88 × 10⁻³"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are power-of-ten slips from using grams instead of kg or wrong molar mass handling.\nWorking: take 0.8 mol water (14.4 g) and 0.2 mol solute; m = 0.2/0.0144 = 13.89 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: m = 1000 x₂ / (x₁ × 18).","traps":["g vs kg of solvent: Divide by 0.0144 kg, not 14.4 g."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Osmotic pressure","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"A solution is prepared by dissolving 0.6 g of urea (molar mass = 60 g mol⁻¹) and 1.8 g of glucose (molar mass = 180 g mol⁻¹) in 100 mL of water at 27 °C. The osmotic pressure of the solution is : (R = 0.08206 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":["4.92 atm","1.64 atm","2.46 atm","8.2 atm"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 2.46 atm uses only one solute (0.1 M); (B) 1.64 and (D) 8.2 come from temperature/volume slips (e.g. 200 K or 0.06 L).\nWorking: total solute = 0.01 + 0.01 = 0.02 mol in 0.1 L → 0.2 M; π = CRT = 0.2 × 0.08206 × 300 = 4.92 atm.\nKey idea: Colligative properties depend on total particles: add moles of urea and glucose.","traps":["Only one solute counted: Sum moles of all non-volatile solutes.","Volume not converted to litres or °C used: Use V = 0.1 L, T = 300 K."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Henry's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which one of the following statements regarding Henry's law is not correct ?","options":["The value of K_H increases with increase of temperature and K_H is function of the nature of the gas","Higher the value of K_H at a given pressure, higher is the solubility of the gas in the liquids.","The partial pressure of the gas in vapour phase is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas in the solution.","Different gases have different K_H (Henry's law constant) values at the same temperature."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student thinks K_H falls with T because solubility falls. (C): wording of the law itself, true. (D): NCERT sentence, true.\nWorking: p = K_H·x, so at a given pressure a higher K_H means a lower mole fraction (lower solubility); statement (B) reverses this. (A), (C), (D) are NCERT statements.\nKey idea: K_H sits in the numerator of p = K_H x, so a big K_H means a poorly soluble gas.","traps":["K_H read as a solubility constant: Write p = K_H·x: for fixed p, larger K_H gives smaller x.","K_H rising with T thought false: Solubility down ⇔ K_H up as T rises (NCERT N₂/O₂ data)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"A solution of sodium sulfate contains 92 g of Na⁺ ions per kilogram of water. The molality of Na⁺ ions in that solution in mol kg⁻¹ is:","options":["16","8","4","12"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): halves for Na₂SO₄. (A): doubles. (D): random.\nWorking: 92/23 = 4 mol Na⁺ per kg water → 4 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: Molality of Na⁺ = mol Na⁺ / kg water.","traps":["Mass of Na₂SO₄ computed: Question asks molality of Na⁺, given mass of Na⁺.","Factor of 2 applied: 92 g Na⁺ / 23 = 4 mol directly; no factor needed."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A solution containing 62 g ethylene glycol in 250 g water is cooled to –10 °C. If Kf for water is 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹, the amount of water (in g) separated as ice is:","options":["32","48","16","64"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) arithmetic or using 250 g as final mass; 186 g (not an option) is the liquid left, not the ice.\nWorking: 1 mol glycol; at –10 °C, 10 = 1.86 × 1/W → W = 0.186 kg liquid water left; ice = 250 – 186 = 64 g.\nKey idea: Ice that separates is pure water; the remaining solution must have m = 10/1.86.","traps":["Answer is remaining water, not ice: Ice = initial water – remaining water."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Raoult's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Liquids A and B form an ideal solution in the entire composition range. At 350 K, the vapor pressures of pure A and pure B are 7 × 10³ Pa and 12 × 10³ Pa, respectively. The composition of the vapor in equilibrium with a solution containing 40 mole percent of A at this temperature is :","options":["x_A = 0.37; x_B = 0.63","x_A = 0.28; x_B = 0.72","x_A = 0.76; x_B = 0.24","x_A = 0.4; x_B = 0.6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) repeats the liquid composition. (C) swaps A and B. (A) arithmetic slip / uses wrong mole fraction.\nWorking: p_A = 0.4 × 7 = 2.8 kPa, p_B = 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 kPa, total 10 kPa → y_A = 0.28, y_B = 0.72.\nKey idea: The vapour is richer in the more volatile component (B).","traps":["Liquid composition copied to vapour: Vapour is enriched in the more volatile B.","Labels swapped: Write p_A = x_A p°_A explicitly."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elevation of boiling point","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Elevation in the boiling point for 1 molal solution of glucose is 2 K. The depression in the freezing point of 2 molal solution of glucose in the same solvent is 2 K. The relation between K_b and K_f is:","options":["K_b = 0.5 K_f","K_b = 2 K_f","K_b = 1.5 K_f","K_b = K_f"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.5: inverts the ratio. (D) equal: ignores different molalities. (C) arbitrary.\nWorking: K_b = 2/1 = 2 K kg mol⁻¹; K_f = 2/2 = 1 K kg mol⁻¹; K_b = 2K_f.\nKey idea: Same ΔT at double molality means K_f is half of K_b.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Compute each K separately, then compare."],"topics":["Elevation of boiling point","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Depression of freezing point","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The freezing point of a diluted milk sample is found to be −0.2 °C, while it should have been −0.5 °C for pure milk. How much water has been added to pure milk to make the diluted sample?","options":["2 cups of water to 3 cups of pure milk","1 cup of water to 3 cups of pure milk","3 cups of water to 2 cups of pure milk","1 cup of water to 2 cups of pure milk"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) inverts the ratio (3:2 milk:water). (D)/(B) small additions.\nWorking: ΔTf ∝ concentration; 0.2/0.5 = 2/5, so volume grows 5/2 times: 2 cups milk + 3 cups water.\nKey idea: Solute fixed; concentration × volume constant.","traps":["Ratio inversion: Total/original = 5/2 → water added = 3/2 of milk."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"K₂HgI₄ is 40% ionised in aqueous solution. The value of its van't Hoff factor (i) is :-","options":["1.8","2.2","2.0","1.6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2.2: n = 4 (complex ion wrongly split) gives 1 + 3(0.4). (C) 2.0 and (D) 1.6: near-value fillers (2.0 would need α = 0.5 with n = 3).\nWorking: K₂[HgI₄] → 2K⁺ + [HgI₄]²⁻ gives n = 3 ions; i = 1 + (n – 1)α = 1 + 2 × 0.4 = 1.8.\nKey idea: The complex ion [HgI₄]²⁻ is one particle.","traps":["Complex ion broken into parts: Write the dissociation: only K⁺ separate; [HgI₄]²⁻ is one ion."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass from colligative properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Freezing point of a 4% aqueous solution of X is equal to freezing point of 12% aqueous solution of Y. If molecular weight of X is A, then molecular weight of Y is :","options":["A","3A","4A","2A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A: assumes equal freezing point means equal molar mass. (C) 4A / (D) 2A: arithmetic slips with percentages.\nWorking: equal ΔTf ⇒ equal molality. Approximating with equal solvent mass, 4/A = 12/M ⇒ M = 3A. (Strictly, with 96 g and 88 g water, M = 3.27A; 3A is the closest option.)\nKey idea: Same ΔTf ⇒ same m ⇒ w/M equal per kg solvent.","traps":["Exact solvent mass ignored: Take nearest option; note approximation."],"topics":["Molar mass from colligative properties","Depression of freezing point"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"8 g of NaOH is dissolved in 18 g of H₂O. Mole fraction of NaOH in solution and molality (in mol kg⁻¹) of the solution respectively are :","options":["0.167, 11.11","0.2, 22.20","0.2, 11.11","0.167, 22.20"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 0.2 = moles of NaOH taken as the mole fraction (forgetting to divide by total moles); 22.20 = molality with the mass of solution or a slip by 2. (C) right molality, wrong mole fraction (0.2/1 using only water in the denominator). (D) right mole fraction, doubled molality.\nWorking: n(NaOH)=8/40=0.2, n(H₂O)=1; x=0.2/1.2=0.167; m=0.2/0.018 kg=11.11 mol kg⁻¹.\nKey idea: Mole fraction uses total moles (1.2); molality uses kg of solvent only (0.018 kg).","traps":["Mole fraction denominator uses only solvent moles: Denominator = n(solute)+n(solvent)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"van't Hoff factor","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Molecules of benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH) dimerise in benzene. 'w' g of the acid dissolved in 30 g of benzene shows a depression in freezing point equal to 2 K. If the percentage association of the acid to form dimer in the solution is 80, then w is :\n(Given that K_f = 5 K kg mol⁻¹, Molar mass of benzoic acid = 122 g mol⁻¹)","options":["1.8 g","2.4 g","1.0 g","1.5 g"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 1.5 g: association ignored (i = 1 gives 1.46 g). (C) 1.0 g: i = 1.4 (1 + α/2), as if the acid dissociated instead of associating. (A) 1.8 g: i = 0.8, from using 1 − α/4 instead of 1 − α/2.\nWorking: i = 1 − α/2 = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6; 2 = 0.6 × 5 × (w/122)/0.030 → w/122 = 0.02 → w = 2.44 g ≈ 2.4 g.\nKey idea: For dimerisation i = 1 − α + α/2 = 1 − α/2.","traps":["Ignoring association (i = 1): Use i = 1 − α/2 = 0.6.","Wrong particle count for association: Each pair of molecules becomes one particle: α/2 particles added back."],"topics":["van't Hoff factor","Depression of freezing point"]}],"m06-equil":[{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base theories","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"What will be the decreasing order of basic strength of the following conjugate bases?\n⁻OH, RO⁻, CH₃COO⁻, Cl⁻","options":["Cl⁻ > RO⁻ > ⁻OH > CH₃COO⁻","⁻OH > RO⁻ > CH₃COO⁻ > Cl⁻","Cl⁻ > ⁻OH > RO⁻ > CH₃COO⁻","RO⁻ > ⁻OH > CH₃COO⁻ > Cl⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): student thinks OH⁻ strongest (water more acidic misjudged). (A)/(C): reverse the rule and put Cl⁻ (from strong acid) first.\nWorking: conjugate acid strengths HCl > CH₃COOH > H₂O > ROH (alcohols slightly weaker acids than water), so base strength RO⁻ > OH⁻ > CH₃COO⁻ > Cl⁻.\nKey idea: Weakest acid → strongest conjugate base; alcohols are weaker acids than water.","traps":["RO⁻ vs OH⁻ order: Remember ROH is a weaker acid than H₂O (alkyl +I).","Strong acid gives weak conjugate base: Rank conjugate acids first, then reverse."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The equilibrium constant for the reaction SO₃(g) ⇌ SO₂(g) + ½O₂(g) is Kc = 4.9 × 10⁻². The value of Kc for the reaction given below 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) is:","options":["49","416","41.6","4.9"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 41.6 – decimal slip. (A) 49 – multiplying instead of inverting. (D) 4.9 – leaving Kc unchanged except exponent.\nWorking: reversing gives 1/Kc; doubling squares it: Kc′ = 1/(4.9 × 10⁻²)² = 1/0.002401 ≈ 416.\nKey idea: Reverse → 1/K; ×2 → K².","traps":["Only reversal applied: Square after inverting.","Decimal slip: (4.9×10⁻²)² = 2.4×10⁻³."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The following reaction occurs in the Blast furnace where iron ore is reduced to iron metal\nFe₂O₃(s) + 3CO(g) ⇌ Fe(l) + 3CO₂(g)\nUsing the Le Chatelier's principle, predict which one of the following will not disturb the equilibrium.","options":["Addition of CO₂","Removal of CO","Addition of Fe₂O₃","Removal of CO₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(D): changing gaseous concentrations does shift equilibrium; student may think removal of a product never \"disturbs\".\nWorking: Fe₂O₃ is a pure solid, its activity is constant and it does not appear in Kc = [CO₂]³/[CO]³; adding it does not shift the equilibrium. (The equation as given needs 2Fe(l) to balance Fe; this does not affect the answer.)\nKey idea: Adding/removing a pure solid does not change Q, so no shift.","traps":["Adding any reactant shifts equilibrium: Pure solids have constant activity; they are absent from Q."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: On passing HCl(g) through a saturated solution of BaCl₂, at room temperature white turbidity appears.\nStatement II: When HCl gas is passed through a saturated solution of NaCl, sodium chloride is precipitated due to common ion effect.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): thinks BaCl₂ is soluble so no turbidity, or confuses with BaSO₄ test needing sulphate. (D)/(C): doubts common ion effect applies to soluble salts.\nWorking: extra Cl⁻ from HCl pushes Qsp above Ksp for saturated BaCl₂ → BaCl₂ separates (white turbidity); NCERT states the same for NaCl.\nKey idea: Any saturated chloride solution will deposit salt when Cl⁻ is raised by HCl gas.","traps":["Soluble salt assumed unaffected: NCERT applies it to saturated NaCl too."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"At −20°C and 1 atm pressure, a cylinder is filled with equal number of H₂, I₂ and HI molecules for the reaction H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), the Kp for the process is x × 10⁻¹.\nx = ______.\n[Given : R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","options":["2","1","10","0.01"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1: student finds Kp = 1 and forgets the ×10⁻¹ scaling. (A)/(D): student uses (RT)^Δn with a wrong Δn or plugs in R and T.\nWorking: equal molecules → equal partial pressures p each; Kp = p²/(p·p) = 1 = 10 × 10⁻¹, so x = 10 (Δn = 0, R and T irrelevant). (Strictly, I₂ would be solid at −20 °C; the question treats all three as gases.)\nKey idea: Δn = 0 makes Kp dimensionless and independent of R, T; equal amounts give Kp = 1 → x = 10.","traps":["Kp = 1 read directly as x: Write 1 = 10 × 10⁻¹.","R and T used although Δn = 0: Check Δn first."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The ratio Kp/Kc for the reaction :\nCO(g) + ½O₂(g) ⇌ CO₂(g) is :","options":["1","(RT)^(1/2)","RT","1/√(RT)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): sign of Δn reversed. (A): Δn taken as zero. (C): Δn = 1.\nWorking: Δn_g = 1 − 1.5 = −½, so Kp/Kc = (RT)^(−½).\nKey idea: Δn = products − reactants (gases only).","traps":["Δn sign reversed: Δn = n(products) − n(reactants)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"For the given hypothetical reactions, the equilibrium constants are as follows :\nX ⇌ Y; K₁ = 1.0\nY ⇌ Z; K₂ = 2.0\nZ ⇌ W; K₃ = 4.0\nThe equilibrium constant for the reaction X ⇌ W is","options":["12.0","8.0","6.0","7.0"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 7.0 = sum of K values; (A) 12.0 and (C) 6.0 other arithmetic slips.\nWorking: adding reactions multiplies K: 1.0 × 2.0 × 4.0 = 8.0.\nKey idea: Adding equations → multiply K.","traps":["Adding K values: K multiplies when equations add."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : A Buffer solution is the mixture of a salt and an acid or a base mixed in any particular quantities\nStatement (II) : Blood is a naturally occurring buffer solution whose pH is maintained by H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ concentrations.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): accepts Statement I by not noticing \"any acid/base, any quantities\". (D)/(A): student unaware of the H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ blood buffer.\nWorking: a buffer needs a weak acid (or base) with its conjugate salt in comparable amounts, not any acid/base in any quantity; blood pH is held by the H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ pair.\nKey idea: Buffer = weak acid + its salt (or weak base + its salt) in comparable ratio; strong acid + salt is not a buffer.","traps":["\"Any quantities\" accepted: Check the weak-acid requirement and ratio near 1."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"For a sparingly soluble salt AB₂, the equilibrium concentrations of A²⁺ ions and B⁻ ions are 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ M and 0.24 × 10⁻³ M, respectively. The solubility product of AB₂ is :","options":["0.069 × 10⁻¹²","0.276 × 10⁻¹²","6.91 × 10⁻¹²","27.65 × 10⁻¹²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) skip the square or misplace powers of ten. (D) uses 4s³ with s taken as the B⁻ value (double counting the 2).\nWorking: Ksp = [A²⁺][B⁻]² = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ × (2.4 × 10⁻⁴)² = 6.91 × 10⁻¹².\nKey idea: Use given [B⁻] directly squared; do not multiply by 2 again.","traps":["Double counting the factor 2: Given [B⁻] already includes the 2.","Power-of-ten slip: Rewrite as 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ first."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Kₐ for CH₃COOH is 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ and K_b for NH₄OH is 1.8 × 10⁻⁵. The pH of ammonium acetate solution will be _________.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: answers dependent on concentration, or 7.005 rounding.\nWorking: pH = 7 + ½(pKₐ − pK_b) = 7 + 0 = 7.\nKey idea: Equal Kₐ and K_b → neutral, independent of concentration.","traps":["Concentration thought to be needed: pH of weak-weak salt is concentration-independent."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Solubility of calcium phosphate (molecular mass, M) in water is W g per 100 mL at 25 °C. Its solubility product at 25 °C will be approximately.","options":["10⁷ (W/M)³","10³ (W/M)⁵","10⁷ (W/M)⁵","10⁵ (W/M)⁵"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses wrong power 3. (B) forgets 108 (only 10⁵·10⁻²) or forgets the ×10 volume factor and 108. (D) uses the ×10⁵ from the volume conversion but forgets the 108 ≈ 10² factor.\nWorking: S = 10W/M mol L⁻¹; Ksp = 108 S⁵ = 108 × 10⁵ (W/M)⁵ ≈ 10⁷ (W/M)⁵.\nKey idea: Molar solubility = 10W/M; Ksp = 108 S⁵ ≈ 10² × 10⁵ (W/M)⁵.","traps":["Per 100 mL not per litre: Convert to mol L⁻¹ first: multiply by 10.","Dropping 108 coefficient: Write ion concentrations 3S and 2S explicitly."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Aqueous solution of ammonium carbonate is basic.\nStatement (II) : Acidic/basic nature of salt solution of a salt of weak acid and weak base depends on Ka and Kb value of acid and the base forming it.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): accepts I but rejects the NCERT rule. (C)/(D): assume ammonium salts are always acidic (true for NH₄Cl) or that weak-acid/weak-base salts are neutral (true only for CH₃COONH₄).\nWorking: pH = 7 + ½(pKa − pKb); for (NH₄)₂CO₃ the acid is HCO₃⁻ (pKa ≈ 10.3) and the base NH₃ (pKb ≈ 4.75), so pH > 7 (basic); II is the NCERT rule.\nKey idea: Carbonate (from very weak HCO₃⁻) hydrolyses more than NH₄⁺, so solution is basic.","traps":["Ammonium salts assumed acidic: Compare Ka(HCO₃⁻) with Kb(NH₃): Kb is far larger, so basic.","Weak acid–weak base salt assumed neutral: Neutral only when pKa = pKb."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For the reaction N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), Kp = 0.492 atm at 300 K. Kc for the reaction at same temperature is _________ × 10⁻².\n(Given : R = 0.082 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Multiplying instead of dividing gives 12.1 (1210 × 10⁻²); using R = 8.314 gives a meaningless value.\nWorking: Δn = 1; Kc = Kp/(RT) = 0.492/(0.082 × 300) = 0.492/24.6 = 0.02 = 2 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Kc = Kp/(RT)^Δn with Δn = 2 − 1 = 1.","traps":["Kc = Kp(RT): Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn, so divide."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The following concentrations were observed at 500 K for the formation of NH₃ from N₂ and H₂. At equilibrium; [N₂] = 2 × 10⁻² M, [H₂] = 3 × 10⁻² M and [NH₃] = 1.5 × 10⁻² M. Equilibrium constant for the reaction is ________.","type":"num","answer":"417","range":[417.0,417.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 0.375 (forgets the cube on [H₂]), 0.0024 (inverts the expression).\nWorking: Kc = [NH₃]²/([N₂][H₂]³) = (1.5×10⁻²)²/(2×10⁻² × 27×10⁻⁶) = 2.25×10⁻⁴/5.4×10⁻⁷ = 416.7 ≈ 417.\nKey idea: Use stoichiometric powers: square NH₃, cube H₂.","traps":["Powers omitted: Write the balanced equation first: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃.","Reverse reaction K: Formation of NH₃: product on top."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Precipitation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The pH at which Mg(OH)₂ [Ksp = 1 × 10⁻¹¹] begins to precipitate from a solution containing 0.10 M Mg²⁺ ions is _______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (reporting pOH), 10 (forgetting square root), 4.5/9.5 (taking [OH⁻]² wrongly).\nWorking: [OH⁻]² = Ksp/[Mg²⁺] = 10⁻¹¹/0.1 = 10⁻¹⁰ → [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵ M; pOH = 5; pH = 9.\nKey idea: Q = Ksp at onset; take √ for [OH⁻].","traps":["pOH reported as pH: pH = 14 − pOH.","Square root missed: [OH⁻]² = 10⁻¹⁰ → [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The pH of an aqueous solution containing 1M benzoic acid (pKa = 4.20) and 1M sodium benzoate is 4.5. The volume of benzoic acid solution in 300 mL of this buffer solution is _________ mL. (given : log 2 = 0.3)","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 200 (ratio inverted, acid = 2 parts), 150 (taking equal volumes), 50.\nWorking: 4.5 = 4.2 + log([salt]/[acid]) ⇒ ratio 2; both stock solutions 1 M, so V_salt : V_acid = 2 : 1; V_acid = 300/3 = 100 mL.\nKey idea: Same molarity stocks ⇒ mole ratio = volume ratio.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Salt (conjugate base) is in the numerator."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"For the given reaction, choose the correct expression of K_C from the following :-\nFe³⁺(aq) + SCN⁻(aq) ⇌ (FeSCN)²⁺(aq)","options":["K_C = [Fe³⁺][SCN⁻]/[FeSCN²⁺]","K_C = [FeSCN²⁺]/([Fe³⁺][SCN⁻])","K_C = [FeSCN²⁺]²/([Fe³⁺][SCN⁻])","K_C = [FeSCN²⁺]/([Fe³⁺]²[SCN⁻]²)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): reciprocal. (C)/(D): wrong exponents (charge 2+ misread as coefficient).\nWorking: all coefficients 1; products over reactants.\nKey idea: Exponents = stoichiometric coefficients, not ionic charges.","traps":["Charge read as coefficient: Use coefficients only."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"A(g) ⇌ B(g) + C/2 (g). The correct relationship between K_P, α and equilibrium pressure P is","options":["K_P = α^(1/2) P^(3/2) / (2+α)^(3/2)","K_P = α^(3/2) P^(1/2) / [(2+α)^(1/2) (1−α)]","K_P = α^(1/2) P^(1/2) / (2+α)^(3/2)","K_P = α^(1/2) P^(1/2) / (2+α)^(1/2)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) all drop the (1−α) denominator and/or invert the powers of α and (2+α); (A) keeps P^(3/2) by adding exponents instead of Δn = ½; (D) is what one gets by cancelling (2+α) wrongly.\nWorking: moles 1−α, α, α/2; total (2+α)/2. K_P = (2αP/(2+α))·(αP/(2+α))^(1/2) / (2(1−α)P/(2+α)) = α^(3/2)P^(1/2)/[(2+α)^(1/2)(1−α)].\nKey idea: Δn = ½ so P appears as P^(1/2); total moles 1+α/2 gives the (2+α) factor.","traps":["Total moles miscounted: Write moles: 1−α, α, α/2; total = 1+α/2.","Reactant term dropped: Always divide by p_A = (1−α)·P/total."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Weak acid ionisation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"For a concentrated solution of a weak electrolyte (K_eq = equilibrium constant) A₂B₃ of concentration 'c', the degree of dissociation 'α' is :","options":["(K_eq/25c²)^(1/5)","(K_eq/108c⁴)^(1/5)","(K_eq/5c⁴)^(1/5)","(K_eq/6c⁵)^(1/5)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 25c²: uses (2+3)² and wrong c power. (C) 5c⁴: adds coefficients instead of 2²·3³. (D) 6c⁵: multiplies coefficients and forgets to cancel one c from the denominator.\nWorking: A₂B₃ ⇌ 2A³⁺ + 3B²⁻; K = (2cα)²(3cα)³ / c(1−α) ≈ 108c⁴α⁵ for α ≪ 1, so α = (K/108c⁴)^(1/5).\nKey idea: K = 2²·3³·c⁵α⁵/c = 108c⁴α⁵.","traps":["Coefficients not raised to powers: Write each ion concentration as (ν·cα) and raise to ν.","c from denominator not cancelled: Simplify c⁵/c = c⁴ before choosing."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The equilibrium composition for the reaction PCl₃ + Cl₂ ⇌ PCl₅ at 298 K is given below:\n[PCl₃]eq = 0.2 mol L⁻¹, [Cl₂]eq = 0.1 mol L⁻¹, [PCl₅]eq = 0.40 mol L⁻¹\nIf 0.2 mol of Cl₂ is added at the same temperature, the equilibrium concentration of PCl₅ is ________ × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹\nGiven : Kc for the reaction at 298 K is 20","type":"num","answer":"49","range":[49.0,49.0],"explain":"Common slips: 86 – from the other root x ≈ 0.46, which is impossible because x cannot exceed [PCl₃] = 0.2; forgetting x on the numerator side or adding Cl₂ without shifting gives 40; using 0.2 mol as a concentration in an unstated volume is assumed 1 L.\nWorking: (0.4 + x)/[(0.2 − x)(0.3 − x)] = 20 ⇒ 20x² − 11x + 0.8 = 0 ⇒ x = 0.0862; [PCl₅] = 0.486 ≈ 49 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹. Volume is not stated – 1 L is implied.\nKey idea: Set up ICE from the old equilibrium plus added Cl₂; pick root x < 0.2.","traps":["Wrong root of quadratic chosen: x must be less than [PCl₃] = 0.2.","Volume not stated: Take 1 L consistent with given data."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The solubility product of BaSO₄ is 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ at 298 K. The solubility of BaSO₄ in 0.1 M K₂SO₄(aq) solution is ______ × 10⁻⁹ g L⁻¹ (nearest integer). Given: Molar mass of BaSO₄ is 233 g mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"233","range":[233.0,233.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (answer left in mol L⁻¹), 2330 or 23 (power-of-ten slip), 117 (taking [SO₄²⁻] = 0.2 M from K₂SO₄ stoichiometry confusion).\nWorking: [SO₄²⁻] ≈ 0.1 M, s = Ksp/0.1 = 1 × 10⁻⁹ mol L⁻¹ = 233 × 10⁻⁹ g L⁻¹.\nKey idea: K₂SO₄ gives 0.1 M sulphate (one SO₄²⁻ per formula unit), so s = 10⁻⁹ M.","traps":["Answer left in mol L⁻¹: Units asked are g L⁻¹.","Sulphate doubled: K₂SO₄ gives 2K⁺ but only one SO₄²⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Precipitation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"25.0 mL of 0.050 M Ba(NO₃)₂ is mixed with 25.0 mL of 0.020 M NaF. K_sp of BaF₂ is 0.5 × 10⁻⁶ at 298 K. The ratio of [Ba²⁺][F⁻]² and K_sp is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 40: forgets dilution on mixing (0.05 × 0.02² = 2 × 10⁻⁵). 20 or 10: dilutes only one of the two ions. 500: forgets to square [F⁻] (0.025 × 0.010/0.5 × 10⁻⁶).\nWorking: after mixing (dilution ×½), [Ba²⁺] = 0.025 M, [F⁻] = 0.010 M; Q = 0.025 × (0.010)² = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶; Q/K_sp = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶/0.5 × 10⁻⁶ = 5 (precipitation occurs).\nKey idea: Halve both concentrations on mixing equal volumes, then square [F⁻].","traps":["Dilution on mixing ignored: Equal volumes mixed → each concentration halves.","Square on [F⁻] missed: Follow the stoichiometric exponent."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"(i) X(g) ⇌ Y(g) + Z(g) K_p1 = 3\n(ii) A(g) ⇌ 2B(g) K_p2 = 1\nIf the degree of dissociation and initial concentration of both the reactants X(g) and A(g) are equal, then the ratio of the total pressure at equilibrium (p₁/p₂) is equal to x : 1. The value of x is ______ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Forgetting the 4 (2² from 2B) gives 3. Inverting gives 1/12.\nWorking: (i) moles 1 − α, α, α (total 1 + α) → K_p1 = α²p₁/(1 − α²). (ii) moles 1 − α, 2α (total 1 + α) → K_p2 = 4α²p₂/(1 − α²). Dividing: 3/1 = p₁/(4p₂) → p₁/p₂ = 12.\nKey idea: Dividing: 3/1 = P₁/(4P₂) → P₁/P₂ = 12.","traps":["Square of 2 missed: Write mole fractions carefully."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The dissociation constant of acetic acid is x × 10⁻⁵. When 25 mL of 0.2 M CH₃COONa solution is mixed with 25 mL of 0.02 M CH₃COOH solution, the pH of the resultant solution is found to be equal to 5. The value of x is ____________","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.1 (ratio inverted, pKₐ = 6 → Kₐ = 10⁻⁶ = 0.1 × 10⁻⁵), 1 (writing Kₐ = 10⁻⁵ from pH = pKₐ), 100 (scaling slip).\nWorking: after mixing [salt] = 0.1 M, [acid] = 0.01 M; pH = pKₐ + log(0.1/0.01) = pKₐ + 1 = 5 → pKₐ = 4 → Kₐ = 1 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 × 10⁻⁵.\nKey idea: Salt:acid = 10:1 → pH = pKₐ + 1.","traps":["Inverted ratio: log([A⁻]/[HA]) – salt on top.","Expressing in ×10⁻⁵: 1 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 × 10⁻⁵."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"If the pKa of lactic acid is 5, then the pH of 0.005 M calcium lactate solution at 25°C is ________ × 10⁻¹ (Nearest integer). Lactic acid: CH₃–CH(OH)–COOH.","type":"num","answer":"85","range":[85.0,85.0],"explain":"Common slips: 83 or 84 (using c = 0.005 M: pH = 7 + ½(5 − 2.3) = 8.35); 15 (computing pOH or acid pH).\nWorking: Ca(lactate)₂ gives [lactate⁻] = 0.01 M; pH = 7 + ½(pKa + log c) = 7 + ½(5 − 2) = 8.5 = 85 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Use [A⁻] = 2 × 0.005 = 0.01 M; Ca²⁺ does not hydrolyse.","traps":["Anion concentration: Count anions per formula unit."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A litre of buffer solution contains 0.1 mole of each of NH₃ and NH₄Cl. On the addition of 0.02 mole of HCl by dissolving gaseous HCl, the pH of the solution is found to be ________ × 10⁻³ (Nearest integer) [Given : pK_b(NH₃) = 4.745, log 2 = 0.301, log 3 = 0.477, T = 298 K]","type":"num","answer":"9079","range":[9079.0,9079.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 9255 (original pH, ignoring HCl); 9431 if the salt/base ratio is inverted; 4921 if pOH is reported as pH; 9 if the ×10⁻³ scaling is missed.\nWorking: NH₃ 0.08, NH₄⁺ 0.12; pOH = 4.745 + log(0.12/0.08) = 4.745 + 0.176 = 4.921; pH = 9.079 → 9079.\nKey idea: H⁺ converts NH₃ to NH₄⁺: base falls by 0.02, salt rises by 0.02; log 1.5 = log 3 − log 2.","traps":["Ratio direction in basic buffer: For pOH use log([salt]/[base]); salt increased so pOH rises and pH falls.","pOH reported instead of pH: Finish with pH = 14 − pOH."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"When the hydrogen ion concentration [H⁺] changes by a factor of 1000, the value of pH of the solution ______","options":["decreases by 2 units","increases by 2 units","decreases by 3 units","increases by 1000 units"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) use 2 units (miscounting zeros or log 100); (B) also reverses the direction; (D) treats pH as linear in [H⁺].\nWorking: log 1000 = 3, so pH changes by 3 units; since 'increases by 3 units' is not offered, 'changes' is read as [H⁺] increasing 1000 times, so pH decreases by 3.\nKey idea: Δ(pH) = –log(factor) = –3 when [H⁺] rises 1000 times.","traps":["Direction of pH change: Write pH₂ – pH₁ = –log([H⁺]₂/[H⁺]₁) and keep the sign.","Linear vs log scale: pH changes by the log of the factor, never by the factor itself."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Millimoles of calcium hydroxide required to produce 100 mL of the aqueous solution of pH 12 is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ___________ (Nearest integer).\nAssume complete dissociation.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 10 (forgets 2 OH⁻ per formula unit); 50 (uses 1 L or mixes mol/mmol); 0.5 written instead of x.\nWorking: pH 12 → pOH 2 → [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M; in 100 mL, OH⁻ = 1 mmol; each Ca(OH)₂ gives 2 OH⁻, so Ca(OH)₂ = 0.5 mmol = 5 × 10⁻¹ mmol.\nKey idea: Each Ca(OH)₂ gives two OH⁻.","traps":["Two OH⁻ per Ca(OH)₂: Divide [OH⁻] by 2.","mmol and ×10⁻¹ scaling: Convert carefully: 0.5 mmol = 5 × 10⁻¹."],"topics":["pH scale","Ionic product of water"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Water decomposes at 2300 K\nH₂O(g) → H₂(g) + ½O₂(g)\nThe percent of water decomposing at 2300 K and 1 bar is ___________ (Nearest integer).\nEquilibrium constant for the reaction is 2 × 10⁻³ at 2300 K.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Forgetting the ½ for O₂ (α^(3/2) = 2 × 10⁻³ → 1.6%, rounds to 2 anyway) or using α² = K (≈4.5%).\nWorking: moles H₂O, H₂, O₂ = 1 − α, α, α/2 (total 1 + α/2) at P = 1 bar. Kp = α(α/2)^(1/2)/[(1 − α)(1 + α/2)^(1/2)] ≈ α^(3/2)/√2 = 2 × 10⁻³ → α^(3/2) = 2.83 × 10⁻³ → α ≈ 0.02, i.e. 2%.\nKey idea: Moles of O₂ = α/2; with α small, Kp ≈ α^(3/2)/√2.","traps":["Half mole of O₂: Write ICE table with α/2 for O₂.","Neglecting α in denominator: α ≈ 0.02 justifies 1 − α ≈ 1."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"At 298 K\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), K₁ = 4 × 10⁵\nN₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g), K₂ = 1.6 × 10¹²\nH₂(g) + ½O₂(g) ⇌ H₂O(g), K₃ = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³\nBased on above equilibria, then equilibrium constant of the reaction, 2NH₃(g) + 5/2 O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) + 3H₂O(g) is ____________ × 10⁻³³ (Nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 64 (multiplying by K₁ instead of dividing gives 6.4 × 10⁻²²); 40 (using K₃ not cubed changes exponent); 25 (inverting fraction).\nWorking: target = (eq. 2) + 3 × (eq. 3) − (eq. 1), so K = K₂K₃³/K₁ = (1.6 × 10¹² × 1.0 × 10⁻³⁹)/(4 × 10⁵) = 0.4 × 10⁻³² = 4 × 10⁻³³.\nKey idea: Add equations → multiply K; reverse → reciprocal; scale by n → power n.","traps":["K₃ needs cube: Match coefficients first.","NH₃ on reactant side: Reverse equation → divide by K₁."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"600 mL of 0.01 M HCl is mixed with 400 mL of 0.01 M H₂SO₄. The pH of the mixture is ______ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer) [Given log 2 = 0.30, log 3 = 0.48, log 5 = 0.69, log 7 = 0.84, log 11 = 1.04]","type":"num","answer":"186","range":[186.0,186.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 200 (H₂SO₄ taken as monoprotic), 170 (adding pH values or using log 2 only), 115 (no dilution).\nWorking: [H⁺] = (6 + 8) mmol / 1000 mL = 0.014 M; pH = 2 − log 1.4 = 2 − 0.14 = 1.86.\nKey idea: Total H⁺ = 600×0.01 + 400×0.02 = 14 mmol in 1 L.","traps":["H₂SO₄ counted as monoprotic: Multiply H₂SO₄ molarity by 2 for [H⁺].","log 1.4 from given logs: log 1.4 = log 7 + log 2 − 1 = 0.14."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Consider the following equation: 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH = −190 kJ. The number of factors which will increase the yield of SO₃ at equilibrium from the following is ____. A. Increasing temperature; B. Increasing pressure; C. Adding more SO₂; D. Adding more O₂; E. Addition of catalyst","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counting catalyst), 2 (missing O₂ or SO₂ addition), 4 (counting temperature).\nWorking: B, C, D increase yield; A lowers it for an exothermic reaction; E changes only rate.\nKey idea: Exothermic with Δn = −1: low T and high P favour SO₃; adding reactants shifts forward; catalyst does not shift.","traps":["Catalyst does not raise yield: Catalyst speeds both directions equally.","Exothermic: heating lowers yield: Use ΔH sign for K change with T."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For reaction : SO₂(g) + ½O₂(g) ⇌ SO₃(g), Kp = 2 × 10¹² at 27°C and 1 atm pressure. The Kc for the same reaction is ________ × 10¹³. (Nearest integer) (Given R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (Kc = Kp/(RT)^½ = 4 × 10¹¹ → 0.04 × 10¹³), 10 (reading the result as ×10¹²), 5 (using Δn = −1).\nWorking: Δn = −½; Kc = Kp(RT)^½ = 2 × 10¹² × √24.6 = 2 × 10¹² × 4.96 = 9.92 × 10¹² ≈ 1 × 10¹³.\nKey idea: Negative Δn means Kc > Kp: Kc = Kp(RT)^½.","traps":["Sign of Δn mishandled: Rearrange Kc = Kp(RT)^(−Δn) = Kp(RT)^(+½).","Scaling the answer to ×10¹³: Express as 0.992 × 10¹³ → 1."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"At 298 K, the solubility of silver chloride in water is 1.434 × 10⁻³ g L⁻¹. The value of −log Ksp for silver chloride is ________. (Given mass of Ag is 107.9 g mol⁻¹ and mass of Cl is 35.5 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (−log S instead of Ksp), 6 (forgetting molar mass conversion then mishandling powers).\nWorking: s = 1.434 × 10⁻³ / 143.4 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ M; Ksp = s² = 10⁻¹⁰; −log Ksp = 10.\nKey idea: Molar mass 143.4 makes S exactly 10⁻⁵ M.","traps":["−log S vs −log Ksp: Square S before taking log."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"20 mL of 0.1 M NH₄OH is mixed with 40 mL of 0.05 M HCl. The pH of the mixture is nearest to: (Given: K_b(NH₄OH) = 1 × 10⁻⁵, log 2 = 0.30, log 3 = 0.48, log 5 = 0.69, log 7 = 0.84, log 11 = 1.04)","options":["3.2","4.2","5.2","6.2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 4.2 forgets the dilution sign or uses C = 0.1; (D) 6.2 adds instead of subtracts ½pKb or treats as buffer-like; (A) 3.2 treats it as excess strong acid.\nWorking: 2 mmol NH₄OH + 2 mmol HCl → 2 mmol NH₄Cl in 60 mL, C = 1/30 M; pH = 7 − ½pK_b − ½log C = 7 − 2.5 + ½(1.48) ≈ 5.24.\nKey idea: Equal mmol → pure NH₄Cl, C = 2/60 M.","traps":["Buffer vs salt hydrolysis: Compare mmol first: equal amounts give only the salt.","Dilution to total volume: Divide 2 mmol by 60 mL."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Weak acid ionisation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Ka₁, Ka₂ and Ka₃ are the respective ionization constants for the following reactions (a), (b) and (c): (a) H₂C₂O₄ ⇌ H⁺ + HC₂O₄⁻; (b) HC₂O₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + C₂O₄²⁻; (c) H₂C₂O₄ ⇌ 2H⁺ + C₂O₄²⁻. The relationship between Ka₁, Ka₂ and Ka₃ is given as","options":["Ka₃ = Ka₁ + Ka₂","Ka₃ = Ka₁ − Ka₂","Ka₃ = Ka₁ / Ka₂","Ka₃ = Ka₁ × Ka₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) adds constants (confusing with adding ΔG or pK); (B)/(C) subtract or divide (as for reversed or subtracted reactions).\nWorking: (c) = (a) + (b), so the equilibrium constants multiply: Ka₃ = Ka₁·Ka₂.\nKey idea: Equations add → K values multiply (pK values add).","traps":["Adding K instead of multiplying: Write Ka₃ expression and multiply Ka₁ and Ka₂ expressions."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"At 298 K, the equilibrium constant is 2 × 10¹⁵ for the reaction:\nCu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) ⇌ Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s)\nThe equilibrium constant for the reaction\n½Cu²⁺(aq) + Ag(s) ⇌ ½Cu(s) + Ag⁺(aq)\nis x × 10⁻⁸. The value of x is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (taking 1/K without square root, 5 × 10⁻¹⁶), 4 (halving instead of square root).\nWorking: reverse and halve → K' = √(1/K) = √(5 × 10⁻¹⁶) = 2.24 × 10⁻⁸ → 2.\nKey idea: Reverse → reciprocal; ×½ → square root.","traps":["Halving K instead of square root: Coefficient factor becomes an exponent.","Forgetting reversal: Compare which side Cu²⁺ is on."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Class XII students were asked to prepare one litre of buffer solution of pH 8.26 by their chemistry teacher. The amount of ammonium chloride to be dissolved by the student in 0.2 M ammonia solution to make one litre of the buffer is (Given pK_b (NH₃) = 4.74; Molar mass of NH₃ = 17 g mol⁻¹; Molar mass of NH₄Cl = 53.5 g mol⁻¹)","options":["53.5 g","72.3 g","107.0 g","126.0 g"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 53.5 g = 1 mol, from a slip in the log step (log ratio = 0.7); (B)/(D) from using pH instead of pOH or wrong sign of the log term; molar mass of NH₃ is a distractor.\nWorking: pOH = 14 − 8.26 = 5.74 = 4.74 + log([NH₄⁺]/0.2) → [NH₄⁺] = 2.0 M → 2 mol × 53.5 = 107 g.\nKey idea: pOH − pKb = 1 → salt/base = 10 → 2 M salt.","traps":["pH vs pOH for basic buffer: For base buffers convert pH to pOH first.","Unused NH₃ molar mass: Only the salt mass is asked."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"At 310 K, the solubility of CaF₂ in water is 2.34 × 10⁻³ g / 100 mL. The solubility product of CaF₂ is ___ × 10⁻⁸ (mol/L)³. (Given molar mass : CaF₂ = 78 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (taking 1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ as ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁸ via unit slip), 108 (if × 10⁻¹² intended), 27 (Ksp = s³ or forgetting 4).\nWorking: s = 2.34 × 10⁻² g L⁻¹ / 78 = 3.0 × 10⁻⁴ M; Ksp = 4s³ = 4 × 27 × 10⁻¹² = 1.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 0.0108 × 10⁻⁸ → nearest integer 0 (the × 10⁻⁸ scaling makes the integer answer 0).\nKey idea: Convert per 100 mL to per litre (× 10) before dividing by molar mass; then express in the asked power of ten.","traps":["Per 100 mL not converted: Multiply by 10 to get g L⁻¹.","Answer 0 looks wrong: Report 0.0108 rounded to nearest integer = 0."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The plot of pH-metric titration of weak base NH₄OH vs strong acid HCl looks like: [Four sketches of pH (with 7 marked) vs volume of acid] (A) curve starting well above 7, falling gently, then a steep drop crossing below 7, levelling at low pH; (B) curve starting low and rising (S-shape upward); (C) flat high, a vertical drop and flat low with sharp corners, the drop reaching the bottom; (D) flat low, a vertical rise, then flat high.","options":["Smooth falling S-curve from above pH 7 to low pH","Rising S-curve from low pH to high pH","Rectangular step: flat, vertical drop, flat","Rectangular step rising from low to high"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) rise with acid added (base-into-acid direction); (C) is an idealised step with no buffer region, more like strong vs strong.\nWorking: titrating a base with acid, pH must fall with added acid. A weak base starts at a moderate pH (~11), falls gently in the buffer region (NH₄OH/NH₄Cl), then drops steeply with the equivalence point below 7 (NH₄Cl is acidic) — the smooth falling curve (A).\nKey idea: Adding acid lowers pH; a weak base gives a gradual fall then a drop ending at an acidic equivalence point.","traps":["Direction of pH change: Check what is in the flask: base, so pH falls as acid is added.","Weak vs strong base curve shape: A weak base shows a buffer region, so the start is gradual, not flat-vertical."],"topics":["Buffers","Salt hydrolysis"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Weak acid ionisation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Ka for butyric acid (C₃H₇COOH) is 2 × 10⁻⁵. The pH of 0.2 M solution of butyric acid is ___ × 10⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Given log 2 = 0.30]","type":"num","answer":"27","range":[27.0,27.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 or 30 if log 2 is ignored; 23 if log 2 is added instead of subtracted.\nWorking: [H⁺] = √(Ka C) = √(4 × 10⁻⁶) = 2 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 3 − 0.30 = 2.70 = 27 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: pH = ½(pKa − log C) = ½(4.70 + 0.70) = 2.70.","traps":["Sign of log 2: pH = 3 − log 2."],"topics":["Weak acid ionisation","pH scale"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"At 600 K, 2 mol of NO are mixed with 1 mol of O₂. 2NO(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g). The reaction occurring as above comes to equilibrium under a total pressure of 1 atm. Analysis of the system shows that 0.6 mol of oxygen are present at equilibrium. The equilibrium constant for the reaction is ____. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 1 (using moles not partial pressures: 0.64/(1.44×0.6) = 0.74), 0 (mis-rounding), 3 (wrong total moles).\nWorking: O₂ used 0.4 → NO 1.2, O₂ 0.6, NO₂ 0.8; total 2.6 mol at 1 atm. Kp = (0.8/2.6)² / [(1.2/2.6)² (0.6/2.6)] = 1.93 ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Convert to mole fractions using total moles 2.6 at P = 1 atm.","traps":["Moles vs partial pressures: Use p_i = (n_i/n_total)P."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"If the solubility product of PbS is 8 × 10⁻²⁸, then the solubility of PbS in pure water at 298 K is x × 10⁻¹⁶ mol L⁻¹. The value of x is ________. (Nearest Integer) [Given √2 = 1.41]","type":"num","answer":"282","range":[282.0,282.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (s in 10⁻¹⁴ units); 28 (exponent slip); 2 (√8 dropped to 2).\nWorking: s = √Ksp = √(8×10⁻²⁸) = 2√2 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 2.82 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 282 × 10⁻¹⁶ mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: s = √Ksp; express 2.82 × 10⁻¹⁴ as 282 × 10⁻¹⁶.","traps":["Exponent rescaling: Multiply mantissa by 100 when exponent drops by 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"200 mL of 0.01 M HCl is mixed with 400 mL of 0.01 M H₂SO₄. The pH of the mixture is ____.","options":["1.14","1.78","2.34","3.02"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) from ignoring the dilution or the dibasic factor in the log step; (A) from not diluting to 600 mL.\nWorking: H⁺ = 200 × 0.01 + 400 × 0.01 × 2 = 2 + 8 = 10 mmol in 600 mL → [H⁺] = 0.0167 M; pH = 2 − log 1.67 = 1.78 (H₂SO₄ taken as fully dibasic).\nKey idea: Add millimoles of H⁺, divide by total volume.","traps":["H₂SO₄ counted as monobasic: Multiply molarity by basicity.","Volume not added: Total volume = 600 mL."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"For a reaction at equilibrium A(g) ⇌ B(g) + ½C(g), the relation between dissociation constant (K), degree of dissociation (α) and equilibrium pressure (p) is given by:","options":["K = α^(1/2) p^(3/2) / [(1 + 3α/2)^(1/2) (1 − α)]","K = α^(3/2) p^(1/2) / [(2 + α)^(1/2) (1 − α)]","K = (αp)^(3/2) / [(1 + 3α/2)^(1/2) (1 − α)]","K = (αp)^(3/2) / [(1 + α)(1 − α)^(1/2)]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) use total moles 1 + 3α/2 (adding α + α/2 + α instead of 1 − α + α + α/2); (D) uses total 1 + α (as if ½C were 1 mol) and puts the root on (1 − α).\nWorking: moles 1 − α, α, α/2; total 1 + α/2. Kp = [αp/(1+α/2)]·[(α/2)p/(1+α/2)]^½ / [(1−α)p/(1+α/2)] = α^(3/2) p^(1/2) / [(1−α)(2+α)^(1/2)].\nKey idea: Total moles = 1 + α/2; the ½ makes the denominator √(1+α/2) = √(2+α)/√2, absorbing the √½ from C.","traps":["Total moles miscounted: Sum the equilibrium column: (1−α) + α + α/2 = 1 + α/2.","Lost factor √2: Multiply numerator and denominator by √2 to reach the option form."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"PCl₅ dissociates as PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g). 5 moles of PCl₅ are placed in a 200 litre vessel which contains 2 moles of N₂ and is maintained at 600 K. The equilibrium pressure is 2.46 atm. The equilibrium constant Kp for the dissociation of PCl₅ is ____ × 10⁻³ (nearest integer). (Given: R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; assume ideal gas behaviour)","type":"num","answer":"1107","range":[1107.0,1107.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: x = 5 if N₂ is ignored (complete dissociation, Kp undefined); using mole fractions without N₂ in the denominator gives Kp ≈ 1.58 (1581); using concentrations gives Kc ≈ 0.0225.\nWorking: n(total) = pV/RT = 2.46 × 200/(0.082 × 600) = 10 mol = (5 − x) + x + x + 2 → x = 3. Partial pressures: PCl₅ 0.492, PCl₃ 0.738, Cl₂ 0.738 atm; Kp = 0.738²/0.492 = 1.107 atm = 1107 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: Inert gas adds to n_total and dilutes partial pressures but does not react.","traps":["Inert N₂ left out of total moles: Total moles from pV/RT includes all gases present, inert ones too.","Kc found instead of Kp: Kp needs partial pressures in atm."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The Ksp for bismuth sulphide (Bi₂S₃) is 1.08 × 10⁻⁷³. The solubility of Bi₂S₃ in mol L⁻¹ at 298 K is","options":["1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵","2.7 × 10⁻¹²","3.2 × 10⁻¹⁰","4.2 × 10⁻⁸"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Other options come from wrong stoichiometric powers (e.g. s² or s³ type expressions, forgetting the 2² × 3³ = 108 coefficient).\nWorking: Ksp = (2s)²(3s)³ = 108 s⁵ = 1.08 × 10⁻⁷³ ⇒ s⁵ = 10⁻⁷⁵ ⇒ s = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: Bi₂S₃ ⇌ 2Bi³⁺ + 3S²⁻: Ksp = 4s² × 27s³ = 108 s⁵.","traps":["Coefficient 108 in Ksp expression: Write (2s)²(3s)³ explicitly."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base theories","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:\nAssertion A: The amphoteric nature of water is explained by using Lewis acid/base concept.\nReason R: Water acts as an acid with NH₃ and as a base with H₂S.\nIn the light of the above statements choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) accept the Lewis wording (water does have lone pairs, so \"Lewis base\" sounds right); (C) doubts that weak H₂S can protonate water.\nWorking: the dual role of water is explained by the Brønsted–Lowry concept (proton donor to NH₃, proton acceptor from H₂S), not by the Lewis concept, so A is false; R is a correct fact.\nKey idea: Amphiprotic = proton donor and acceptor = Brønsted–Lowry; Lewis theory cannot describe water as an acid.","traps":["Lewis vs Brønsted for water: Ask whether proton transfer is involved – then Brønsted.","Weak acid H₂S still protonates water: Any acid stronger than water donates a proton to it in solution."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"50 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH is being titrated against 0.1 M NaOH. When 25 mL of NaOH has been added, the pH of the solution will be ______ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer) (Given : pKa (CH₃COOH) = 4.76; log 2 = 0.30; log 3 = 0.48; log 5 = 0.69; log 7 = 0.84; log 11 = 1.04)","type":"num","answer":"476","range":[476.0,476.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using the log table to compute something extra (e.g. 3-ish pH of the free acid, or volume-dilution corrections that cancel); answering 4.76 instead of 476.\nWorking: 5 mmol acid + 2.5 mmol NaOH → 2.5 mmol CH₃COOH + 2.5 mmol CH₃COO⁻ (half-neutralisation); pH = pKa + log 1 = 4.76 = 476 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Equal moles of acid and conjugate base → pH = pKa; dilution cancels in the ratio.","traps":["Log data used unnecessarily: Check mole ratio first – it is 1:1.","Dilution correction applied: Ratio [A⁻]/[HA] is volume-independent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"2NOCl(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) + Cl₂(g). In an experiment, 2.0 moles of NOCl was placed in a one-litre flask and the concentration of NO after equilibrium established was found to be 0.4 mol/L. The equilibrium constant at 30°C is ______ × 10⁻⁴.","type":"num","answer":"125","range":[125.0,125.0],"explain":"Common slips: 500 – forgets to square (0.4×0.2/1.6); 250 – takes [Cl₂] = 0.4; 200 – uses initial NOCl 2.0.\nWorking: [NO] = 0.4, [Cl₂] = 0.2, [NOCl] = 2.0 − 0.4 = 1.6 M. Kc = (0.4)²(0.2)/(1.6)² = 0.032/2.56 = 0.0125 = 125 × 10⁻⁴.\nKey idea: [Cl₂] is half of [NO] formed; NOCl consumed equals NO formed.","traps":["Powers in Kc dropped: Raise each concentration to its coefficient.","Initial NOCl used: Use 2.0 − 0.4 = 1.6 at equilibrium."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"pH value of 0.001 M NaOH solution is ______.","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answer: 3 (reporting pOH as pH).\nWorking: [OH⁻] = 10⁻³ M, pOH = 3, pH = 14 − 3 = 11 (at 298 K).\nKey idea: Strong base: pOH = −log[OH⁻], pH = 14 − pOH.","traps":["pOH reported as pH: Base solutions have pH > 7."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A student needs to prepare a buffer solution of propanoic acid and its sodium salt with pH 4. The ratio of [CH₃CH₂COO⁻]/[CH₃CH₂COOH] required to make the buffer is ………. Given: Kₐ(CH₃CH₂COOH) = 1.3 × 10⁻⁵","options":["0.03","0.13","0.23","0.33"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Inverting the ratio gives 7.7 (not offered); (A) 0.03 or (C) 0.23/(D) 0.33 come from mis-rounding pKₐ = 4.89 or adding instead of subtracting.\nWorking: pH = pKₐ + log([A⁻]/[HA]) → [A⁻]/[HA] = Kₐ/[H⁺] = 1.3×10⁻⁵/1×10⁻⁴ = 0.13.\nKey idea: [A⁻]/[HA] = Kₐ/[H⁺] = 1.3×10⁻⁵ / 1×10⁻⁴.","traps":["Salt/acid ratio vs acid/salt: pH < pKₐ means less salt than acid.","Log arithmetic: Use Kₐ/[H⁺] directly."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The solubility of AgCl will be maximum in which of the following ?","options":["0.01 M KCl","0.01 M HCl","0.01 M AgNO₃","Deionised water"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B) give Cl⁻; (C) gives Ag⁺ – all equally lower solubility; a student may think acid (HCl) increases solubility.\nWorking: options A–C each supply a common ion (Cl⁻ or Ag⁺) that suppresses solubility; pure water has none.\nKey idea: Any added Ag⁺ or Cl⁻ shifts AgCl ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ left.","traps":["HCl as acid raising solubility: Cl⁻ is the anion of a strong acid; common ion dominates."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"4.0 moles of argon and 5.0 moles of PCl₅ are introduced into an evacuated flask of 100 litre capacity at 610 K. The system is allowed to equilibrate. At equilibrium, the total pressure of mixture was found to be 6.0 atm. The Kp for the reaction is [Given: R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","options":["2.25","6.24","12.13","15.24"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Ignoring argon (taking 6 atm as only PCl₅/PCl₃/Cl₂ pressure) gives wrong x and large Kp values such as 6.24 or 12.13; using mole fractions without total moles also misleads.\nWorking: n_total = PV/RT = 6 × 100/(0.082 × 610) = 12.0 mol; initially 9 mol, so x = 3 mol PCl₅ dissociated → PCl₅ 2, PCl₃ 3, Cl₂ 3, Ar 4; each mole = 0.5 atm → Kp = (1.5 × 1.5)/1.0 = 2.25.\nKey idea: Find total moles from PV = nRT, subtract the inert gas, then get x from the mole increase.","traps":["Argon left out of total moles: Total pressure includes every gas present.","Mole increase = x: Δn = +1 per mole dissociated."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The OH⁻ concentration in a mixture of 5.0 mL of 0.0504 M NH₄Cl and 2 mL of 0.0210 M NH₃ solution is x × 10⁻⁶ M. The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer) [Given Kw = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ and Kb = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (using molarity ratio without volumes); 108 (ratio inverted: 1.8×10⁻⁵ × 6 = 108 × 10⁻⁶); using Kw to convert gives pH-based slips.\nWorking: n(NH₄⁺) = 0.252 mmol, n(NH₃) = 0.042 mmol; [OH⁻] = 1.8×10⁻⁵ × 0.042/0.252 = 3.0×10⁻⁶ M → x = 3.\nKey idea: Buffer ratio = ratio of moles; total volume cancels.","traps":["Use moles, not given molarities: Ratio of mmol: 0.042/0.252 = 1/6.","Basic buffer formula: [OH⁻] = Kb·[NH₃]/[NH₄⁺]; Kw is not needed."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The equilibrium constant Kc at 298 K for the reaction A + B ⇌ C + D is 100. Starting with an equimolar solution with concentrations of A, B, C and D all equal to 1 M, the equilibrium concentration of D is ____ × 10⁻² M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"182","range":[182.0,182.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 82 (reporting x instead of 1 + x), 18 (the [A] value 0.182), 91 (assuming no initial products).\nWorking: Qc = 1 < 100, so the reaction moves forward. (1 + x)²/(1 – x)² = 100 → (1 + x)/(1 – x) = 10 → x = 9/11 = 0.818. [D] = 1.818 M = 181.8 × 10⁻² ≈ 182.\nKey idea: Perfect square: take √Kc = 10 directly.","traps":["Report [D], not x: Add the shift to the starting 1 M.","Direction of shift: Compare Qc = 1 with Kc = 100."],"topics":["Law of equilibrium","Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The overall stability constant of the complex ion [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is 2.1 × 10¹³. The overall dissociation constant is y × 10⁻¹⁴. Then y is ____. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (truncating 4.76), 48 (exponent slip), 21 (repeating the given number).\nWorking: overall dissociation constant = 1/β₄ = 1/(2.1 × 10¹³) = 4.76 × 10⁻¹⁴ → y ≈ 5.\nKey idea: K_dissociation = 1/β; mind the exponent shift.","traps":["Exponent shift: Write 1/2.1 × 10⁻¹³ = 0.476 × 10⁻¹³ = 4.76 × 10⁻¹⁴."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The number of moles of NH₃, that must be added to 2 L of 0.80 M AgNO₃ in order to reduce the concentration of Ag⁺ ions to 5.0 × 10⁻⁸ M (K_formation for [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ = 1.0 × 10⁸) is ______. (Nearest integer) [Assume no volume change on adding NH₃]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (only free NH₃ 0.4 M × 2 L = 0.8); 3 (complexed NH₃ 1.6 M ×2 L = 3.2 without free); 2 (total concentration 2.0 M reported, not moles).\nWorking: nearly all Ag⁺ becomes complex, [Ag(NH₃)₂⁺] ≈ 0.80 M; Kf = 0.80/(5 × 10⁻⁸ × [NH₃]²) = 10⁸ → [NH₃]² = 0.16, [NH₃] = 0.40 M free. Total NH₃ = 0.40 + 2 × 0.80 = 2.0 M; in 2 L = 4 mol.\nKey idea: Total ligand = free ligand at equilibrium + 2 × complex formed.","traps":["Bound NH₃ forgotten: Add 2 NH₃ per Ag⁺ complexed.","Molarity vs moles: Multiply by 2 L."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"When 5.1 g of solid NH₄HS is introduced into a two litre evacuated flask at 27°C, 20% of the solid decomposes into gaseous ammonia and hydrogen sulphide. The K_p for the reaction at 27°C is x × 10⁻². The value of x is _______. (Integer answer) [Given R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 24 (using total pressure 0.492 squared); 25 (Kp taken as p = 0.246, not p²); 1.5 (using 10% instead of 20% or 4 L volume); including the solid in Kp.\nWorking: n(NH₄HS) = 5.1/51 = 0.1 mol; 20% → 0.02 mol each of NH₃ and H₂S. p = nRT/V = 0.02 × 0.082 × 300/2 = 0.246 atm each; K_p = p(NH₃)·p(H₂S) = 0.0605 atm² = 6.05 × 10⁻² → 6.\nKey idea: Kp = p² with p = nRT/V for each gas, solid omitted.","traps":["Solid included in Kp: Pure solids have activity 1 – leave out.","Total vs partial pressure: Each gas has half the total pressure."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A₃B₂ is a sparingly soluble salt of molar mass M (g mol⁻¹) and solubility x g L⁻¹. The solubility product satisfies Ksp = a (x/M)⁵. The value of a is ________. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"108","range":[108.0,108.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 – multiplies coefficients without powers; 72 – 3² × 2³; 1 – forgets coefficients.\nWorking: molar solubility s = x/M; A₃B₂ ⇌ 3A²⁺ + 2B³⁻; Ksp = (3s)³(2s)² = 27 × 4 s⁵ = 108 s⁵. a = 108.\nKey idea: (3s)³(2s)² = 108 s⁵.","traps":["Powers swapped: Each ion concentration (ns) is raised to its own coefficient n.","g L⁻¹ vs mol L⁻¹: Divide by M first – the question already does this."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The pH of a solution obtained by mixing 50 mL of 1 M HCl and 30 mL of 1 M NaOH is x × 10⁻⁴. The value of x is __________. (Nearest integer) [log 2.5 = 0.3979]","type":"num","answer":"6021","range":[6021.0,6021.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3979 (divides excess 20 mmol by 50 mL only: [H⁺] = 0.4 M, pH 0.398), 0 (treating the mixture as neutral).\nWorking: excess H⁺ = 50 − 30 = 20 mmol in 80 mL → [H⁺] = 0.25 M; pH = −log 0.25 = 1 − 0.3979 = 0.6021 = 6021 × 10⁻⁴.\nKey idea: Excess moles ÷ total volume.","traps":["Ignoring total volume: Divide excess mmol by 80 mL."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"At 1990 K and 1 atm pressure, there are equal number of Cl₂ molecules and Cl atoms in the reaction mixture. The value of K_P for the reaction Cl₂(g) ⇌ 2Cl(g) under the above conditions is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 (Kp = 1 by taking p = 1 for each); 2.5 (p² not divided); 1 (ratio of numbers).\nWorking: mole fractions 0.5 each, so p(Cl) = p(Cl₂) = 0.5 atm; Kp = (0.5)²/0.5 = 0.5 = 5 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Equal moles at 1 atm → 0.5 atm each.","traps":["Total pressure given to each gas: Partial pressure = x × P_total."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The stepwise formation of [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is: Cu²⁺ + NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)]²⁺ (K₁); [Cu(NH₃)]²⁺ + NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₂]²⁺ (K₂); [Cu(NH₃)₂]²⁺ + NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₃]²⁺ (K₃); [Cu(NH₃)₃]²⁺ + NH₃ ⇌ [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (K₄). The values of stability constants K₁, K₂, K₃ and K₄ are 10⁴, 1.58 × 10³, 5 × 10² and 10² respectively. The overall equilibrium constant for dissociation of [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is x × 10⁻¹². The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (β₄ itself, 7.9 × 10¹¹, misread); 0 (adding constants); 13 (mantissa slip).\nWorking: β₄ = K₁K₂K₃K₄ = 10⁴ × 1.58 × 10³ × 5 × 10² × 10² = 7.9 × 10¹¹; dissociation constant = 1/β₄ = 1.27 × 10⁻¹² → x = 1.\nKey idea: Dissociation constant = 1/(K₁K₂K₃K₄).","traps":["Not inverted: Dissociation is the reverse of formation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The solubility product of PbI₂ is 8.0 × 10⁻⁹. The solubility of lead iodide in 0.1 molar solution of lead nitrate is x × 10⁻⁶ mol/L. The value of x is ____. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: √2 = 1.41]","type":"num","answer":"141","range":[141.0,141.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 283 (forgetting factor 2 → s = √(8 × 10⁻⁸) = 2.83 × 10⁻⁴), 126 (pure-water solubility ∛(Ksp/4) = 1.26 × 10⁻³ in wrong units).\nWorking: Ksp = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² = 0.1 × (2s)² → 0.4 s² = 8 × 10⁻⁹ → s = √2 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.41 × 10⁻⁴ = 141 × 10⁻⁶ M.\nKey idea: [I⁻] = 2s; [Pb²⁺] ≈ 0.1.","traps":["[I⁻] taken as s: Use stoichiometry of PbI₂."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The solubility of AgCN in a buffer solution of pH = 3 is x. The value of x is: [Assume: No cyano complex is formed; Ksp(AgCN) = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ and Ka(HCN) = 6.2 × 10⁻¹⁰]","options":["0.625 × 10⁻⁶","1.9 × 10⁻⁵","2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶","1.6 × 10⁻⁶"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) quotes Ksp itself; (D)/(A) come from wrong combinations (Ka ratio misuse or square-root slips); √Ksp = 1.5×10⁻⁸ in water.\nWorking: s = [Ag⁺] = [CN⁻] + [HCN] = [CN⁻](1 + [H⁺]/Ka). Ksp = s[CN⁻] = s²/(1 + [H⁺]/Ka). [H⁺]/Ka = 10⁻³/6.2×10⁻¹⁰ = 1.61×10⁶, so s² = 2.2×10⁻¹⁶ × 1.61×10⁶ = 3.55×10⁻¹⁰, s = 1.88×10⁻⁵ ≈ 1.9×10⁻⁵ M.\nKey idea: At pH ≪ pKa almost all CN⁻ is protonated: s ≈ √(Ksp·[H⁺]/Ka).","traps":["HCN formation ignored: In acid, CN⁻ is removed as HCN, raising solubility.","Ka ratio inverted: Check that solubility increases in acid."],"topics":["Solubility product","Weak acid ionisation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The solubility of Ca(OH)₂ in water is: [Given: the solubility product of Ca(OH)₂ in water = 5.5 × 10⁻⁶]","options":["1.77 × 10⁻⁶","1.11 × 10⁻⁶","1.11 × 10⁻²","1.77 × 10⁻²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 1.77 × 10⁻² = ∛(5.5×10⁻⁶) (factor 4 dropped); (A)/(B) take square root type or exponent slip.\nWorking: Ksp = s(2s)² = 4s³ → s³ = 1.375 × 10⁻⁶ → s = 1.11 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: AB₂: s = ∛(Ksp/4).","traps":["Factor 4 omitted: Write [OH⁻] = 2s.","Exponent slip in cube root: Cube root of 10⁻⁶ is 10⁻²."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A homogeneous ideal gaseous reaction AB₂(g) ⇌ A(g) + 2B(g) is carried out in a 25 litre flask at 27°C. The initial amount of AB₂ was 1 mole and the equilibrium pressure was 1.9 atm. The value of Kp is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"73","range":[73.0,73.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 36 (forgetting the 2 in 2αP₀), 18 or 145 (squaring errors), about 66 if the initial pressure is wrongly taken as 1 atm.\nWorking: P₀ = 1×0.0821×300/25 = 0.985 atm; total (1+2α)P₀ = 1.9 → α = 0.464; Kp = (αP₀)(2αP₀)²/((1−α)P₀) = 0.457×0.837/0.528 ≈ 0.725 → 72.5 ≈ 73.\nKey idea: Find P₀ from nRT/V first; then 1 + 2α = Pₑq/P₀.","traps":["Coefficient 2 not applied to B: B = 2α; square (2αP₀).","Initial pressure assumed 1 atm: Compute P₀ = nRT/V."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The pH of ammonium phosphate solution, if pKa of phosphoric acid and pKb of ammonium hydroxide are 5.23 and 4.75 respectively, is ______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6.76 – sign reversed ½(pKb – pKa); 9.99/12 – formula for salt of strong base used; 7.24 entered unrounded.\nWorking: salt of weak acid and weak base: pH = 7 + ½(pKa – pKb) = 7 + ½(5.23 – 4.75) = 7.24; key 7 is the nearest integer (question gives no rounding instruction).\nKey idea: Weak–weak salt pH depends only on pKa – pKb.","traps":["pKa and pKb swapped: Acid pKa larger → pH > 7.","Polyprotic acid worry: Use the single given pKa as the question intends."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g). In an equilibrium mixture, the partial pressures are P(SO₃) = 43 kPa; P(O₂) = 530 Pa and P(SO₂) = 45 kPa. The equilibrium constant K_P = ________ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"172","range":[172.0,172.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1723 or 17 (Pa/kPa slip for O₂); 17228 (using bar); 0.0017 (using Pa throughout).\nWorking: K_P = P(SO₃)²/[P(SO₂)²·P(O₂)] = 43²/(45² × 0.53) kPa⁻¹ = 1849/1073.25 = 1.723 kPa⁻¹ = 172 × 10⁻². (In bar the same data give 172 bar⁻¹, so the key assumes kPa units; the unit is not stated.)\nKey idea: Convert all pressures to one unit (kPa here) before substituting; the expected unit is kPa⁻¹.","traps":["Pa vs kPa for O₂: Convert 530 Pa = 0.53 kPa.","Unit of K_P unstated: Use the units the question uses (kPa)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Precipitation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"A solution is 0.1 M in Cl⁻ and 0.001 M in CrO₄²⁻. Solid AgNO₃ is gradually added to it. Assuming that the addition does not change in volume and Ksp(AgCl) = 1.7 × 10⁻¹⁰ M² and Ksp(Ag₂CrO₄) = 1.9 × 10⁻¹² M³. Select correct statement from the following :","options":["AgCl precipitates first because its Ksp is high.","Ag₂CrO₄ precipitates first as its Ksp is low.","Ag₂CrO₄ precipitates first because the amount of Ag⁺ needed is low.","AgCl will precipitate first as the amount of Ag⁺ needed to precipitate is low."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) compares Ksp of salts with different formula types; (A) right conclusion, wrong reason; (C) wrong arithmetic or ignoring the square root.\nWorking: [Ag⁺] needed for AgCl = 1.7×10⁻¹⁰/0.1 = 1.7×10⁻⁹ M; for Ag₂CrO₄ = √(1.9×10⁻¹²/10⁻³) ≈ 4.4×10⁻⁵ M; AgCl needs far less Ag⁺ and precipitates first.\nKey idea: Compute the threshold [Ag⁺] for each salt; the smaller threshold precipitates first.","traps":["Raw Ksp comparison: Ksp in M² vs M³ cannot be compared; compute [Ag⁺] needed.","Right answer, wrong reason: Check the 'because' clause, not only the conclusion."],"topics":["Precipitation","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Value of K_P for the equilibrium reaction N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) at 288 K is 47.9. The K_C for this reaction at same temperature is _______. (Nearest integer) (R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1145 (multiplying by RT instead of dividing), 48 (Δn taken as 0).\nWorking: Kc = Kp/(RT)^Δn = 47.9/(0.083 × 288)¹ = 47.9/23.9 = 2.0 → 2.\nKey idea: Δn = +1, so Kc = Kp/RT.","traps":["Multiply vs divide by RT: Kc = Kp/(RT)^Δn."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"For the reaction A + B ⇌ 2C the value of equilibrium constant is 100 at 298 K. If the initial concentration of all the three species is 1 M each, then the equilibrium concentration of C is x × 10⁻¹ M. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 17 or 18 (ignoring initial C: 2y/(1−y) = 10), 10 (taking √K as [C]).\nWorking: Q = 1 < K, so the reaction moves forward; (1+2y)²/(1−y)² = 100 → (1+2y)/(1−y) = 10 → y = 0.75; [C] = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5 M = 25 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Taking the square root makes the quadratic linear: (1+2y)/(1−y) = 10.","traps":["Initial product ignored: Include all initial concentrations.","Stoichiometric 2 for C: Use coefficient: [C] = 1 + 2y."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic product of water","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Assuming that Ba(OH)₂ is completely ionised in aqueous solution under the given conditions the concentration of H₃O⁺ ions in 0.005 M aqueous solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 298 K is ______ × 10⁻¹² mol L⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 2 (using [OH⁻] = 0.005).\nWorking: [OH⁻] = 2 × 0.005 = 0.01 M; [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻¹⁴/10⁻² = 1 × 10⁻¹² M.\nKey idea: Ba(OH)₂ gives 2 OH⁻.","traps":["Factor 2 for OH⁻: Multiply by number of OH⁻ per formula."],"topics":["Ionic product of water","pH scale"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃ + Cl₂; Kc = 1.844. 3.0 moles of PCl₅ is introduced in a 1 L closed reaction vessel at 380 K. The number of moles of PCl₅ at equilibrium is ___ × 10⁻³. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"1396","range":[1396.0,1396.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 1604 (reporting x instead of 3 − x), approximations giving ~2352 (x ≈ √(1.844×3)), rounding slips (1395/1397).\nWorking: x²/(3 − x) = 1.844 → x² + 1.844x − 5.532 = 0 → x = 1.604; PCl₅ left = 3 − 1.604 = 1.396 mol = 1396 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: Kc is not small; solve exactly and subtract from 3.","traps":["Report PCl₅, not x: Re-read what is asked.","No small-x shortcut: Use the quadratic."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The equilibrium constant for the reaction A(s) ⇌ M(s) + ½O₂(g) is Kp = 4. At equilibrium, the partial pressure of O₂ is ___ atm. (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (taking p = √4), 4 (ignoring the exponent), 8.\nWorking: solids omitted, Kp = √(p_O₂) = 4 → p_O₂ = 16 atm.\nKey idea: Square, don't root: p = Kp².","traps":["Exponent ½ means square Kp: Write Kp = √p and solve for p."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Two salts A₂X and MX have the same value of solubility product of 4.0 × 10⁻¹². The ratio of their molar solubilities i.e. S(A₂X)/S(MX) = _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (equal Ksp → equal solubility), 0.02 inverse ratio, 63 (using S³ without the 4).\nWorking: A₂X: 4S³ = 4 × 10⁻¹² → S = 10⁻⁴; MX: S² = 4 × 10⁻¹² → S = 2 × 10⁻⁶; ratio = 50.\nKey idea: Equal Ksp does not mean equal solubility when stoichiometry differs.","traps":["Equal Ksp = equal solubility: Write Ksp in terms of S for each formula."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Weak acid ionisation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Sulphurous acid (H₂SO₃) has Ka₁ = 1.7 × 10⁻² and Ka₂ = 6.4 × 10⁻⁸. The pH of 0.588 M H₂SO₃ is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (treating H₂SO₃ as a strong dibasic acid: [H⁺] = 1.176 M, pH ≈ −0.07), 2 (rounding/log slip), 4 (using Ka₂).\nWorking: second ionisation negligible; solving the quadratic gives [H⁺] = 0.0918 M, pH = 1.04 (the approximation x = √(KaC) = 0.100 M gives pH 1.00); either way the integer is 1.\nKey idea: Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ → treat as monoprotic; Ka₁C ≈ 0.01 → [H⁺] ≈ 0.1 M.","traps":["Both protons assumed fully released: Compare Ka values; use only Ka₁."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base theories","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which of the following compound CANNOT act as a Lewis base?","options":["NF₃","PCl₅","SF₄","ClF₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NF₃ – weak base but has a lone pair; (C) SF₄ – one lone pair; (D) ClF₃ – two lone pairs.\nWorking: P in PCl₅ has no lone pair (5 bond pairs) so it cannot donate an electron pair; NF₃, SF₄, ClF₃ have lone pairs on the central atom (1, 1, 2).\nKey idea: No lone pair on central atom → cannot act as Lewis base (PCl₅ is in fact a Lewis acid).","traps":["NF₃ thought non-basic: Weak base ≠ cannot donate.","Lone pairs miscounted: Count valence electrons minus bonds."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"0.01 moles of a weak acid HA (Ka = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶) is dissolved in 1.0 L of 0.1 M HCl solution. The degree of dissociation of HA is ________ × 10⁻⁵ (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Neglect volume change on adding HA. Assume degree of dissociation << 1]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1414 (α = √(Ka/c) without HCl ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻²), 0.2 or 20 (power-of-ten slips).\nWorking: [H⁺] ≈ 0.1 M from HCl; Ka = [H⁺]·α/(1−α) ≈ 0.1α → α = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ / 0.1 = 2 × 10⁻⁵.\nKey idea: With a large common-ion concentration, α = Ka/[H⁺], independent of acid concentration.","traps":["Ostwald dilution formula used: H⁺ comes from HCl.","HA concentration used: α = Ka/[H⁺] only."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Weak acid ionisation"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Consider the reaction N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g). The temperature at which K_C = 20.4 and K_P = 600.1, is_____K. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n[Assume all gases are ideal and R = 0.0831 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"354","range":[354.0,354.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 18.8 (√ taken, Δn = 2 assumed), 0.0028 (inverse), 4.25 × 10⁵ (multiplying).\nWorking: Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn with Δn = 1 → T = Kp/(Kc R) = 600.1/(20.4 × 0.0831) = 600.1/1.6952 = 354.0 → 354.\nKey idea: Δn = 2 – 1 = 1, so T = Kp/(Kc·R).","traps":["Δn miscount: Δn = moles gaseous products – reactants = 1."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"In order to prepare a buffer solution of pH 5.74, sodium acetate is added to acetic acid. If the concentration of acetic acid in the buffer is 1.0 M, the concentration of sodium acetate in the buffer is _____ M. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: pKa (acetic acid) = 4.74]","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.1 (ratio inverted), 1 (forgetting the log), 5.74 or 4.74 (plugging pH).\nWorking: pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) → 5.74 = 4.74 + log([salt]/1.0) → [salt] = 10 M.\nKey idea: pH − pKa = 1 → salt/acid = 10.","traps":["Ratio inverted: pH > pKa means more salt than acid."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The solubility of CdSO₄ in water is 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹. Its solubility in 0.01 M H₂SO₄ solution is ______ × 10⁻⁶ mol L⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest integer) (Assume that solubility is much less than 0.01 M)","type":"num","answer":"64","range":[64.0,64.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 32 (taking [SO₄²⁻] = 0.02 M), 800 (ignoring the common ion), 0.064 (power-of-ten slip).\nWorking: Ksp = (8.0 × 10⁻⁴)² = 6.4 × 10⁻⁷; with [SO₄²⁻] = 0.01 M, s = 6.4 × 10⁻⁷/0.01 = 6.4 × 10⁻⁵ = 64 × 10⁻⁶ mol L⁻¹ (treating H₂SO₄ as giving 0.01 M sulphate).\nKey idea: One H₂SO₄ gives one SO₄²⁻: [SO₄²⁻] = 0.01 M.","traps":["Doubling sulphate: Only one sulphate per acid molecule.","Ignoring HSO₄⁻ equilibrium: The question tells you to take 0.01 M."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The molar solubility of Zn(OH)₂ in 0.1 M NaOH solution is x × 10⁻¹⁸ M. The value of x is ______ (Nearest integer). (Given: The solubility product of Zn(OH)₂ is 2 × 10⁻²⁰)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (forgetting the square, s = 2 × 10⁻¹⁹); 20 or 200 (power-of-ten slips); 1.7 × 10⁻⁷ (pure-water solubility).\nWorking: s = Ksp/[OH⁻]² = 2 × 10⁻²⁰/(0.1)² = 2 × 10⁻¹⁸ M, so x = 2.\nKey idea: s = Ksp/[OH⁻]² = 2 × 10⁻²⁰/0.01 = 2 × 10⁻¹⁸ M.","traps":["OH⁻ not squared: Ksp = s(0.1)² → s = 2 × 10⁻²⁰/10⁻².","2s added to 0.1: 2s is negligible against 0.1 M."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Two solutions A and B, each of 100 L was made by dissolving 4 g of NaOH and 9.8 g of H₂SO₄ in water, respectively. The pH of the resultant solution obtained from mixing 40 L of solution A and 10 L of solution B is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"10.6","range":[10.6,10.6],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10.78 (forgets H₂SO₄ gives 2 H⁺ → excess 0.03 mol/50 L = 6×10⁻⁴ M); 10.70 (divides excess by 40 L only); 7 (assumes complete neutralisation); 3.40 (reports pOH).\nWorking: A = 10⁻³ M NaOH, B = 10⁻³ M H₂SO₄ (2×10⁻³ M H⁺). 40 L A → 0.04 mol OH⁻; 10 L B → 0.02 mol H⁺; excess OH⁻ 0.02 mol in 50 L = 4×10⁻⁴ M; pOH = 3.40, pH = 10.60.\nKey idea: Count equivalents: OH⁻ 0.04 mol vs H⁺ 0.02 mol; divide excess by 50 L.","traps":["H₂SO₄ treated as monoprotic: Multiply H₂SO₄ moles by 2.","Excess divided by one solution's volume: Use 40 + 10 = 50 L."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"3 g of acetic acid is added to 250 mL of 0.1 M HCl and the solution made up to 500 mL. To 20 mL of this solution ½ mL of 5 M NaOH is added. The pH of the solution is ______.\n[Given : pKa of acetic acid = 4.75, molar mass of acetic acid = 60 g/mol, log 3 = 0.4771]\nNeglect any changes in volume","type":"num","answer":"5.22 to 5.24","range":[5.22,5.24],"explain":"Common slips: Ignoring HCl gives 2.5 mmol NaOH > 2 mmol acid (strong-base excess, pH ≫ 7); using 2:0.5 ratio gives 5.35; using log(1/3) gives 4.27.\nWorking: 20 mL contains 2 mmol CH₃COOH and 1 mmol HCl; 2.5 mmol NaOH neutralises HCl (1) and then 1.5 mmol CH₃COOH → 1.5 mmol CH₃COO⁻, 0.5 mmol acid left; pH = 4.75 + log 3 = 5.227.\nKey idea: NaOH reacts with HCl first; the remainder makes the acetate buffer.","traps":["HCl not consumed first: Strong acid is neutralised before the weak acid.","Ratio inverted: Use log([salt]/[acid])."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionic product of water","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"For the following Assertion and Reason, the correct option is : Assertion : The pH of water increases with increase in temperature. Reason : The dissociation of water into H⁺ and OH⁻ is an exothermic reaction.","options":["Both assertion and reason are true, but the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion.","Both assertion and reason are false.","Assertion is not true, but reason is true.","Both assertion and reason are true, and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(A) accept that pH increases (confusing \"neutral\" with pH 7 always); (C) accepts exothermic ionisation (bond breaking is endothermic).\nWorking: self-ionisation of water is endothermic, so Kw rises with temperature, [H⁺] rises and pH falls (water stays neutral). Both statements are false.\nKey idea: Bond breaking in H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻ is endothermic; hotter water has more H⁺ so lower pH, yet is still neutral.","traps":["Neutral water assumed always pH 7: Neutral means [H⁺] = [OH⁻]; pH 7 only at 298 K.","Ionisation of water taken as exothermic: Neutralisation is exothermic, so ionisation is endothermic."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Precipitation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The Ksp for the following dissociation is 1.6 × 10⁻⁵: PbCl₂(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq). Which of the following choices is correct for a mixture of 300 mL 0.134 M Pb(NO₃)₂ and 100 mL 0.4 M NaCl?","options":["Q < Ksp","Q > Ksp","Q = Ksp","Not enough data provided"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) arises if [Cl⁻] is not squared and numbers are mishandled; (C) has no basis; (D) tempts those who think volumes are missing.\nWorking: in 400 mL, [Pb²⁺] = 40.2/400 = 0.1005 M, [Cl⁻] = 40/400 = 0.100 M; Q = 0.1005 × (0.1)² = 1.0 × 10⁻³ > 1.6 × 10⁻⁵.\nKey idea: Dilute both ions to the total volume, then Q = [Pb²⁺][Cl⁻]².","traps":["Not diluting on mixing: Use total volume 400 mL.","Not squaring [Cl⁻]: Q uses [Cl⁻]²."],"topics":["Precipitation","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"In the figure shown below reactant A (represented by square) is in equilibrium with product B (represented by circle). The equilibrium constant is:","options":["2","1","8","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1 if counts are rushed; (C) and (D) would need a different stoichiometry or miscount.\nWorking: assuming A ⇌ B, K = [B]/[A] = 11/6 ≈ 1.8, closest to 2. Note: the reaction is not stated and 11/6 is not exactly 2, so the official key takes the nearest option, (A).\nKey idea: K = N(B)/N(A) = 11/6 ≈ 2 (same volume cancels).","traps":["Miscounting shapes: Count row by row; mark each counted shape.","Reaction stoichiometry not stated: Take A ⇌ B as stated by the wording."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Jan09S2C11-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The solubility product of Cr(OH)₃ at 298 K is 6.0 × 10⁻³¹. The concentration of hydroxide ions in a saturated solution of Cr(OH)₃ will be:","options":["⁴√(18 × 10⁻³¹)","⁴√(2.22 × 10⁻³¹)","⁴√(4.86 × 10⁻²⁹)","√(18 × 10⁻³¹)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 6/27 = 0.222 → gives s, not [OH⁻]; (C) 6 × 81 = 486 → 81Ksp mistakes (3s)⁴ factor; (D) wrong root.\nWorking: Ksp = s(3s)³ = 27s⁴; [OH⁻] = 3s = 3·⁴√(Ksp/27) = ⁴√(3Ksp) = ⁴√(18 × 10⁻³¹).\nKey idea: [OH⁻]⁴ = 81s⁴ = 3 Ksp.","traps":["Solubility s reported instead of [OH⁻]: Multiply s by 3 before answering.","Factor 81 instead of 3: Write [OH⁻]⁴ = 81 × Ksp/27."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"For the following Assertion and Reason, the correct option is: Assertion (A): When Cu(II) and sulphide ions are mixed, they react together extremely quickly to give a solid. Reason (R): The equilibrium constant of Cu²⁺(aq) + S²⁻(aq) ⇌ CuS(s) is high because the solubility product is low.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the explanation for (A)","Both (A) and (R) are false","(A) is false and (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the explanation for (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the main trap: both statements are true and seem linked; (B)/(C) catch students who think a reaction with large K cannot be fast or doubt K = 1/Ksp.\nWorking: precipitation of CuS is fast (A true) and K = 1/Ksp is large (R true), but K tells only the extent, not the speed, so R does not explain A.\nKey idea: Large K means a reaction goes far, not that it goes fast.","traps":["Large K taken as fast reaction: Ask: does the reason talk about rate or extent?.","Doubt that K = 1/Ksp: Reverse reaction has reciprocal K."],"topics":["Law of equilibrium","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"An acidic buffer is obtained on mixing:","options":["100 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH and 200 mL of 0.1 M NaOH","100 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COOH and 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH","100 mL of 0.1 M HCl and 200 mL of 0.1 M CH₃COONa","100 mL of 0.1 M HCl and 200 mL of 0.1 M NaCl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) complete neutralisation leaves only salt; (A) excess strong base; (D) no weak acid.\nWorking: 10 mmol HCl + 20 mmol CH₃COONa → 10 mmol CH₃COOH + 10 mmol CH₃COONa, an acidic buffer. (A) excess NaOH; (B) only salt; (D) strong acid + neutral salt.\nKey idea: Count mmol and see what remains after reaction.","traps":["Strong acid ruled out at sight: React first, then judge the mixture.","Equivalence point mistaken for buffer: Check which species are left over."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"100 mL of 0.1 M HCl is taken in a beaker and to it 100 mL of 0.1 M NaOH is added in steps of 2 mL and the pH is continuously measured. Which of the following graphs correctly depicts the change in pH?","options":["pH vs vol. of NaOH: starts flat near 7 and falls in an S-shape (decreasing curve)","pH vs vol. of NaOH: straight line rising through pH 7","pH vs vol. of NaOH: S-shaped rise from low pH, with the steep part crossing the dotted pH 7 line midway","pH vs vol. of NaOH: S-shaped rise from low pH levelling off at the dotted pH 7 line at the top"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) falling curve – reversed (base titrated with acid); (B) linear – ignores log scale; (D) curve plateaus at 7 – assumes pH never exceeds 7 (true only exactly at endpoint).\nWorking: in a strong acid–strong base titration the pH starts near 1, rises slowly while acid is in excess, then jumps steeply through pH 7 at the equivalence point (100 mL) – the sigmoidal rise crossing the pH 7 line is graph (C). (D) wrongly caps the curve at 7, (A) falls, (B) is linear.\nKey idea: pH changes slowly while excess acid remains, then jumps sharply at the equivalence point.","traps":["Curve assumed to stop at pH 7: The S-curve shape is generic; the steep part straddles pH 7.","Linear pH change: Equal NaOH steps change [H⁺] by small fractions until near equivalence."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Physical & dynamic equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"For the equilibrium A ⇌ B, the variation of the rate of the forward (a) and reverse (b) reaction with time is given by (four rate vs time sketches).","options":["a falls then rises again, b rises then falls; they do not meet but show a gap marked 'equilibrium'","a falls and levels off, b rises and levels off at a lower value; gap between them marked 'equilibrium'","a falls, b rises from zero, and the two curves merge to a single common constant value marked 'equilibrium'","a and b cross each other and then level off at different values"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) leave a gap – rates unequal at equilibrium; (D) curves cross and diverge – confuses concentrations crossing with rates.\nWorking: at the start forward rate is maximum and reverse is zero; forward falls, reverse rises, and at equilibrium both become equal and constant – the curves meet and run together.\nKey idea: At equilibrium r_f = r_b.","traps":["Rates unequal at equilibrium: Rates must become equal; only concentrations can differ."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"If the equilibrium constant for A ⇌ B + C is K⁽¹⁾eq and that of B + C ⇌ P is K⁽²⁾eq, the equilibrium constant for A ⇌ P is :","options":["K⁽²⁾eq − K⁽¹⁾eq","K⁽¹⁾eq·K⁽²⁾eq","K⁽¹⁾eq / K⁽²⁾eq","K⁽¹⁾eq + K⁽²⁾eq"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) add/subtract K as if they were energies (ΔG adds, K multiplies); (C) divides, which is the rule for subtracting equations.\nWorking: adding the two equations gives A ⇌ P, so K = K⁽¹⁾·K⁽²⁾ (the [B][C] terms cancel).\nKey idea: Add equations → multiply K; subtract → divide; reverse → reciprocal; multiply by n → power n.","traps":["K added instead of multiplied: Write K expressions and multiply; ΔG° (not K) is additive."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Consider the following reaction\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) ; ΔH° = +58 kJ\nFor each of the following cases (a, b), the direction in which the equilibrium shifts is:\n(a) Temperature is decreased\n(b) Pressure is increased by adding N₂ at constant T","options":["(a) towards reactant, (b) no change","(a) towards product, (b) towards reactant","(a) towards product, (b) no change","(a) towards reactant, (b) towards product"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the temperature effect and treats added N₂ as a pressure increase favouring fewer moles; (C) wrong temperature direction; (D) wrong for both.\nWorking: forward reaction is endothermic, so cooling favours N₂O₄; adding inert N₂ (pressure rises at constant volume) leaves partial pressures unchanged, so no shift.\nKey idea: Only partial pressures of reacting gases matter; inert gas at constant V changes total pressure only.","traps":["Inert gas taken as a pressure increase: Check whether partial pressures of reacting species change.","Temperature direction reversed: Cooling favours the exothermic direction – here the reverse."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For a reaction X + Y ⇌ 2Z, 1.0 mol of X, 1.5 mol of Y and 0.5 mol of Z were taken in a 1 L vessel and allowed to react. At equilibrium, the concentration of Z was 1.0 mol L⁻¹. The equilibrium constant of the reaction is x/15. The value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 30 (change in Z taken as 1.0 or X, Y taken to fall by 0.5 each → [X] = 0.5, [Y] = 1.0, Kc = 2 = 30/15); 15 (Kc taken as 1 by reading [Z] = 1 only); 10 (X, Y left at initial values: 1/(1 × 1.5) = 10/15).\nWorking: Z rises by 0.5 M, so X and Y each fall by 0.25 M → [X] = 0.75, [Y] = 1.25; Kc = 1²/(0.75 × 1.25) = 16/15 → x = 16.\nKey idea: Change in Z = 1.0 − 0.5 = 0.5; X and Y each change by half of that.","traps":["Initial Z ignored: Δ[Z] = final − initial = 0.5 M.","Stoichiometric ratio 1:1:2: Use Δ[X] = Δ[Z]/2."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"For the reaction :\nFe₂N(s) + (3/2)H₂(g) ⇌ 2Fe(s) + NH₃(g)","options":["K_C = K_P(RT)","K_C = K_P/√(RT)","K_C = K_P/[(RT)√(RT)]","K_C = K_P√(RT)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) results if Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn is not inverted, or if the solids are counted (Δn = +1/2); (C) uses Δn = −3/2 (only H₂ counted); (A) uses a whole-number Δn.\nWorking: only gases count, Δn_g = 1 − 3/2 = −1/2; K_P = K_C/√(RT) so K_C = K_P√(RT).\nKey idea: Ignore solids; Kc = Kp(RT)^(−Δn).","traps":["Kp–Kc formula not inverted: Write Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn first, then solve for Kc.","Solids counted in Δn: Only gaseous species enter Δn."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Arrange the following solutions in the decreasing order of pOH :\n(A) 0.01 M HCl\n(B) 0.01 M NaOH\n(C) 0.01 M CH₃COONa\n(D) 0.01 M NaCl","options":["(B) > (C) > (D) > (A)","(A) > (C) > (D) > (B)","(B) > (D) > (C) > (A)","(A) > (D) > (C) > (B)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the decreasing pH order (read as pOH); (B) swaps C and D (treats acetate as acidic); (C) pH order with C/D swapped.\nWorking: pOH: HCl 12; NaCl 7; CH₃COONa slightly basic (pOH < 7, ≈ 5.6); NaOH 2. Decreasing: A > D > C > B.\nKey idea: pOH order is the reverse of pH order: acid has the highest pOH.","traps":["pH order given for pOH: Highest pOH = most acidic solution.","Acetate taken as acidic/neutral: Weak-acid anion + strong-base cation → basic, so pOH < 7."],"topics":["Salt hydrolysis","pH scale"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The value of K_C is 64 at 800 K for the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). The value of K_C for the following reaction is: NH₃(g) ⇌ ½N₂(g) + (3/2)H₂(g)","options":["1/4","1/8","8","1/64"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 1/64 – reversed but not square-rooted; (C) 8 – square-rooted but not inverted; (A) 1/4 – cube root or 64 divided by 16 type slip.\nWorking: reversing gives 1/64; halving the coefficients takes the square root, so K = √(1/64) = 1/8.\nKey idea: K(new) = Kⁿ with n = −½ here.","traps":["Only reversal applied: Check both direction and multiplier of the equation.","Only square root applied: Reactants and products swapped means invert K."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"If the solubility product of AB₂ is 3.20 × 10⁻¹¹ M³, then the solubility of AB₂ in pure water is ______ × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹. [Assuming that neither kind of ion reacts with water]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3.17 (s = ∛Ksp, factor 4 ignored); 2.52 (Ksp taken as 2s³); 0.2 or 20 (exponent slip).\nWorking: Ksp = s(2s)² = 4s³ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ → s³ = 8 × 10⁻¹² → s = 2 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: AB₂: Ksp = 4s³.","traps":["Stoichiometric factor dropped: Concentration of B⁻ is 2s, squared gives 4s²."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Solubility product","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"If solubility product of Zr₃(PO₄)₄ is denoted by K_sp and its molar solubility is denoted by S, then which of the following relation between S and K_sp is correct","options":["S = ⁹√(K_sp/929)","S = ⁷√(K_sp/216)","S = ⁶√(K_sp/144)","S = ⁷√(K_sp/6912)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong exponent sum and constant; (B) 216 = 6³, ignores coefficient powers properly; (C) 144 = 3²×4², wrong powers and exponent 1/6.\nWorking: Zr₃(PO₄)₄ ⇌ 3Zr⁴⁺ + 4PO₄³⁻; K_sp = (3S)³(4S)⁴ = 27 × 256 S⁷ = 6912 S⁷.\nKey idea: K_sp = xˣ·yʸ·S⁽ˣ⁺ʸ⁾ for A_xB_y.","traps":["Forgetting to raise coefficients to powers: Use xˣ·yʸ; 3³·4⁴ = 6912."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"For the following reactions, equilibrium constants are given: S(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ SO₂(g); K₁ = 10⁵². 2S(s) + 3O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g); K₂ = 10¹²⁹. The equilibrium constant for the reaction 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) is:","options":["10¹⁸¹","10¹⁵⁴","10²⁵","10⁷⁷"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 10¹⁸¹ multiplies K₁ and K₂; (B) 10¹⁵⁴ = 10¹²⁹ × 10²⁵-type mixing or adds exponents wrongly; (D) 10⁷⁷ = K₂/K₁ (forgets to square K₁).\nWorking: target = (eq 2) − 2×(eq 1), so K = K₂/K₁² = 10¹²⁹/10¹⁰⁴ = 10²⁵.\nKey idea: Add equations ⇒ multiply K; doubling ⇒ square; reversing ⇒ reciprocal.","traps":["K₁ not squared: Match coefficients first: 2SO₂ needs 2×eq 1.","Ks added instead of multiplied: Write exponents: 129 − 2×52."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"pH scale","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Consider the following statements: (a) The pH of a mixture containing 400 mL of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ and 400 mL of 0.1 M NaOH will be approximately 1.3. (b) Ionic product of water is temperature dependent. (c) A monobasic acid with Kₐ = 10⁻⁵ has a pH = 5. The degree of dissociation of this acid is 50%. (d) The Le Chatelier's principle is not applicable to common-ion effect. The correct statements are :","options":["(a), (b) and (d)","(a), (b) and (c)","(a) and (b)","(b) and (c)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts (d) – thinks common-ion effect is a separate law; (C) rejects (c) by using α = √(Kₐ/c) without c; (D) rejects (a) by treating H₂SO₄ as monobasic (gives pH 7).\nWorking: (a) H⁺ = 80 mmol, OH⁻ = 40 mmol, excess 40 mmol in 800 mL ⇒ 0.05 M ⇒ pH 1.3 ✓; (b) Kw rises with T ✓; (c) [H⁺] = Kₐ ⇒ [A⁻] = [HA] ⇒ α = 50% ✓; (d) common-ion effect is a Le Chatelier shift ✗.\nKey idea: pH = pKa means half-dissociated; H₂SO₄ gives 2 H⁺.","traps":["H₂SO₄ treated as monobasic: Multiply by the n-factor 2 for H₂SO₄.","α from Ostwald dilution without concentration: Use Kₐ = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]: [H⁺] = Kₐ ⇒ ratio 1."],"topics":["pH scale","Ionic product of water","Weak acid ionisation"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction quotient & Le Chatelier's principle","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"For the reaction, 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH = –57.2 kJ mol⁻¹ and K_c = 1.7 × 10¹⁶. Which of the following statement is INCORRECT?","options":["The equilibrium constant is large suggestive of reaction going to completion and so no catalyst is required.","The equilibrium will shift in forward direction as the pressure increase.","The equilibrium constant decreases as the temperature increases.","The addition of inert gas at constant volume will not affect the equilibrium constant."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are true Le Chatelier statements; (D) may tempt those who think inert gas always shifts equilibrium.\nWorking: K says nothing about rate; SO₂ oxidation is very slow and needs Pt or V₂O₅. (B) Δn_g = –1, pressure favours forward; (C) exothermic, K falls with T; (D) inert gas at constant V changes nothing.\nKey idea: Thermodynamics (K) and kinetics (rate) are independent.","traps":["Large K taken to mean fast reaction: K decides extent, catalyst decides speed.","Inert gas effect at constant V: At constant V partial pressures unchanged; K never changes."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Salt hydrolysis","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The pH of a 0.02 M NH₄Cl solution will be [given K_b(NH₄OH) = 10⁻⁵ and log 2 = 0.301]","options":["4.65","5.35","4.35","2.65"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4.65 from adding ½log C wrongly (4.5 + 0.15) or sign slip; (C) 4.35 from sign error on log 0.02; (D) 2.65 treating salt as strong acid.\nWorking: pH = 7 – ½pK_b – ½log C = 7 – 2.5 – ½(–1.699) = 5.35.\nKey idea: log 0.02 = –1.699, so –½log C = +0.85.","traps":["Sign of ½log C: Write log 0.02 = –1.70 explicitly."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"What is the molar solubility of Al(OH)₃ in 0.2 M NaOH solution ? Given that, solubility product of Al(OH)₃ = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴ :","options":["12 × 10⁻²³","12 × 10⁻²¹","3 × 10⁻¹⁹","3 × 10⁻²²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are power-of-ten distractors that catch slips in handling 0.2³ = 8 × 10⁻³ (e.g. not cubing, writing 0.2³ as 8 × 10⁻⁴ or 8 × 10⁻²) or misplacing the exponent when dividing; none follows from a correct Ksp expression.\nWorking: Ksp = s(0.2)³ (OH⁻ from Al(OH)₃ negligible) ⇒ s = 2.4 × 10⁻²⁴ / 8 × 10⁻³ = 3 × 10⁻²² M.\nKey idea: Ksp = [Al³⁺][OH⁻]³ with [OH⁻] ≈ 0.2 M: s = Ksp/0.008.","traps":["Forgetting to cube [OH⁻]: Write Ksp with exponents before substituting.","Exponent slip with 0.2³: Common ion fixes [OH⁻]; do not use 27s⁴."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The molar solubility of Cd(OH)₂ is 1.84 × 10⁻⁵ M in water. The expected solubility of Cd(OH)₂ in a buffer solution of pH = 12 is :","options":["6.23 × 10⁻¹¹ M","1.84 × 10⁻⁹ M","(2.49/1.84) × 10⁻⁹ M","2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ M"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 6.23 × 10⁻¹¹ uses s³ without the factor 4 (Ksp taken as s³); (B) 1.84 × 10⁻⁹ divides s by 10⁴ as if solubility scales linearly; (C) a garbled ratio form built from both numbers.\nWorking: Ksp = 4s³ = 4(1.84 × 10⁻⁵)³ = 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁴; at pH 12, [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M, so s′ = Ksp/[OH⁻]² = 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ M.\nKey idea: In a buffer [OH⁻] is fixed, so s′ = Ksp/[OH⁻]², with Ksp = 4s³.","traps":["Ksp written as s³ instead of 4s³: Write [Cd²⁺]=s, [OH⁻]=2s before cubing.","[OH⁻] not squared or pH used as [H⁺]: Convert pH 12 to [OH⁻] = 10⁻² M, then square."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"In which one of the following equilibria, Kp ≠ Kc ?","options":["NO₂(g) + SO₂(g) ⇌ NO(g) + SO₃(g)","2HI(g) ⇌ H₂(g) + I₂(g)","2NO(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + O₂(g)","2C(s) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)–(C) all have Δn(gas) = 0, so Kp = Kc for them; in (D) the solid C is not counted.\nWorking: Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn; only (D) has Δn(gas) = 2 − 1 = 1 (solid C not counted).\nKey idea: Count only gaseous species for Δn.","traps":["Solid counted in Δn: Exclude solids and liquids."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Buffers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"20 mL of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ solution is added to 30 mL of 0.2 M NH₄OH solution. The pH of the resultant mixture is : [pK_b of NH₄OH = 4.7].","options":["9.4","5.0","9.0","5.2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): 9.4 from taking 2 mmol H⁺ (ratio 2/4 inverted) or pKb ± log2 confusion. (B): pOH given as pH. (D): 5.2 from pKb + log 3.\nWorking: H⁺ = 2 × 2 = 4 mmol; NH₄OH = 6 mmol → 2 mmol NH₄OH left + 4 mmol NH₄⁺; pOH = 4.7 + log(4/2) = 5.0, pH = 9.0.\nKey idea: Count H⁺ (×2 for H₂SO₄), find leftover base and salt, then Henderson for pOH.","traps":["H₂SO₄ taken as monobasic: 2 mmol H₂SO₄ give 4 mmol H⁺.","pOH reported as pH: pH = 14 − pOH."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Consider the following reversible chemical reactions: A₂(g) + B₂(g) ⇌ 2AB(g) (K₁) .....(1); 6AB(g) ⇌ 3A₂(g) + 3B₂(g) (K₂) .....(2). The relation between K₁ and K₂ is:","options":["K₂ = K₁³","K₂ = K₁⁻³","K₁K₂ = 3","K₁K₂ = 1/3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) K₁³: forgot reversal. (C)/(D): multiplying K by the coefficient instead of raising to a power.\nWorking: Equation (2) is equation (1) reversed and multiplied by 3, so K₂ = (1/K₁)³ = K₁⁻³.\nKey idea: Reverse → reciprocal; ×n → power n.","traps":["Coefficient multiplies K: Scaling the equation raises K to that power.","Reversal missed: Products and reactants are swapped: take reciprocal."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kp–Kc relation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The value of Kp/Kc for the following reactions at 300 K are, respectively : (At 300 K, RT = 24.62 dm³ atm mol⁻¹) N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g); N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g); N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)","options":["1, 24.62 dm³ atm mol⁻¹, 606.0 dm⁶ atm² mol⁻²","1, 4.1 × 10⁻² dm⁻³ atm⁻¹ mol⁻¹, 606.0 dm⁶ atm² mol⁻²","606.0 dm⁶ atm² mol⁻², 1.65 × 10⁻³ dm³ atm⁻² mol⁻¹","1, 24.62 dm³ atm mol⁻¹, 1.65 × 10⁻³ dm⁻⁶ atm⁻² mol²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): uses (RT)⁺² instead of (RT)⁻² for NH₃. (B): uses 1/RT for Δn = +1. (C): wrong for the first two reactions and swaps signs.\nWorking: Kp/Kc = (RT)^Δn; Δn = 0, +1, −2 → 1, 24.62 dm³ atm mol⁻¹, 1/24.62² = 1.65 × 10⁻³ dm⁻⁶ atm⁻² mol².\nKey idea: For ammonia synthesis Δn = −2, so the ratio is 1/(RT)², a small number with inverse units.","traps":["Δn sign reversed for NH₃ synthesis: Δn = 2 − 4 = −2 → divide by (RT)².","Units of (RT)^Δn: Write units as (dm³ atm mol⁻¹)^Δn."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"5.1 g NH₄SH is introduced in 3.0 L evacuated flask at 327 °C. 30% of the solid NH₄SH decomposed to NH₃ and H₂S as gases. The Kp of the reaction at 327 °C is (R = 0.082 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, Molar mass of S = 32 g mol⁻¹, molar mass of N = 14 g mol⁻¹)","options":["1 × 10⁻⁴ atm²","4.9 × 10⁻³ atm²","0.242 atm²","0.242 × 10⁻⁴ atm²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1 × 10⁻⁴: Kc = (0.01 M)², concentrations used instead of partial pressures. (B) 4.9 × 10⁻³: (0.07)² – uses the undecomposed 70% of the solid. (D) 0.242 × 10⁻⁴: right digits with a spurious power of ten (unit mix-up).\nWorking: n(NH₄SH) = 5.1/51 = 0.1 mol; 0.03 mol each of NH₃ and H₂S; p = nRT/V = 0.03 × 0.082 × 600/3 = 0.492 atm each; Kp = (0.492)² = 0.242 atm².\nKey idea: Kp = p(NH₃)·p(H₂S); solid omitted; T must be in kelvin.","traps":["Kc vs Kp: Convert each gas to p = nRT/V before multiplying.","Undecomposed solid used in K: Pure solid is omitted from K; use only the 0.03 mol of each gas."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Consider the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). The equilibrium constant of the above reaction is K_P. If pure ammonia is left to dissociate, the partial pressure of ammonia at equilibrium is given by (assume that P_NH₃ ≪ P_total at equilibrium)","options":["3√3 · √K_P · P² / 4","3√3 · √K_P · P² / 16","√K_P · P² / 16","√K_P · P² / 4"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong denominator (√256=16 vs 4). (C)/(D) drop the 3³ from p(H₂)³.\nWorking: with NH₃ negligible, p(N₂) = P/4, p(H₂) = 3P/4; K_P = p(NH₃)²/[(P/4)(3P/4)³] → p(NH₃) = √(27 K_P P⁴/256) = 3√3 · √K_P · P²/16.\nKey idea: N₂ : H₂ = 1 : 3 by stoichiometry, so they share P as P/4 and 3P/4.","traps":["Dropping the 3³ factor: p(H₂) = 3P/4, cubed.","Square-root of 256: 4⁴ = 256, √256 = 16."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Law of equilibrium","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In a chemical reaction, A + 2B ⇌ 2C + D (equilibrium constant K), the initial concentration of B was 1.5 times of the concentration of A, but the equilibrium concentrations of A and B were found to be equal. The equilibrium constant (K) for the aforesaid chemical reaction is :","options":["16","4","1","1/4"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 16: forgetting to square or wrong power on B. (C) 1: taking x with 1:1 consumption. (D) 1/4: inverting K.\nWorking: a − x = 1.5a − 2x ⇒ x = 0.5a; [A]=[B]=0.5a, [C]=a, [D]=0.5a; K = a²(0.5a)/(0.5a·0.25a²) = 4.\nKey idea: Use the condition [A]eq = [B]eq to find x in terms of a; a cancels because Δn = 0.","traps":["B consumed at same rate as A: Write 1.5a − 2x for B.","K inverted: K = products over reactants."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kc & Kp","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Two solids dissociate as follows: A(s) ⇌ B(g) + C(g); Kp₁ = x atm²; D(s) ⇌ C(g) + E(g); Kp₂ = y atm². The total pressure when both the solids dissociate simultaneously is :","options":["(x + y) atm","x² + y² atm","2√(x + y) atm","√(x + y) atm"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) adds Kp values directly. (B) squares instead of rooting. (D) gives p_C only, forgets B and E.\nWorking: p_B = p₁, p_E = p₂, p_C = p₁ + p₂; p₁(p₁+p₂) = x, p₂(p₁+p₂) = y ⇒ (p₁+p₂)² = x + y; P_total = 2(p₁+p₂) = 2√(x+y).\nKey idea: Add the two Kp expressions: (p₁+p₂)² = x + y; total = p_B + p_C + p_E = 2(p₁+p₂).","traps":["Total pressure taken as p_C only: Total = sum of B, C and E partial pressures.","Treating each solid independently: C pressure appears in both Kp; solve together."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Common ion effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"If K_sp of Ag₂CO₃ is 8 × 10⁻¹², the molar solubility of Ag₂CO₃ in 0.1 M AgNO₃ is :","options":["8 × 10⁻¹² M","8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M","8 × 10⁻¹¹ M","8 × 10⁻¹³ M"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 8×10⁻¹¹: [Ag⁺] not squared (K_sp/0.1). (A) 8×10⁻¹²: K_sp itself. (D) 8×10⁻¹³: divided by 10 too many times.\nWorking: [Ag⁺] ≈ 0.1 M, so s = [CO₃²⁻] = K_sp/[Ag⁺]² = 8×10⁻¹²/10⁻² = 8×10⁻¹⁰ M.\nKey idea: K_sp = [Ag⁺]²[CO₃²⁻]; with [Ag⁺] = 0.1, s = K_sp/0.01.","traps":["Forgetting the square on Ag⁺: Write K_sp expression with the stoichiometric power."],"topics":["Common ion effect","Solubility product"]}],"m07-redox":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"Consider the following electrochemical cell at standard condition.\nAu(s) | QH₂, Q | NH₄X(0.01 M) || Ag⁺(1 M) | Ag(s)  E_cell = +0.4 V\nThe couple QH₂/Q represents quinhydrone electrode, the half cell reaction is given below (Q and QH₂ shown below):\nQ + 2e⁻ + 2H⁺ → QH₂  E°(Q/QH₂) = +0.7 V\n[Given : E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = +0.8 V and 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V]\nThe pK_b value of the ammonium halide salt (NH₄X) used here is ________. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (log C term dropped), 8 (pK_a reported), 10 (from a 0.03 pH slope), and a negative or meaningless pH if the electrode potential is written with the wrong sign.\nWorking: E_cell = 0.8 − (0.7 − 0.06 pH) = 0.1 + 0.06 pH = 0.4, so pH = 5. For the salt NH₄X, pH = 7 − pK_b/2 − ½ log C, so 5 = 7 − pK_b/2 + 1 and pK_b = 6.\nKey idea: The cell EMF fixes the pH at the quinhydrone electrode, and that pH is the hydrolysis pH of 0.01 M NH₄X.","traps":["Anode and cathode swapped in E°cell calculation: E_cell = E(cathode, right) − E(anode, left), with each electrode potential written as a reduction potential: E(Q/QH₂) = 0.7 − 0.06 pH.","Reaction quotient written upside down or exponent dropped: (0.06/2) log(1/[H⁺]²) = 0.06 pH; the 2 in the electron count cancels the square."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C21-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":55,"q":"40 mL of a mixture of CH₃COOH and HCl (aqueous solution) is titrated against 0.1 M NaOH solution conductometrically. The titration curve is shown below. Which of the following statement is correct?","options":["CH₃COOH is neutralised first followed by neutralisation of HCl","The concentration of HCl in the original mixture is 0.005 M","The concentration of CH₃COOH in the original mixture is 0.005 M","Point ‘C’ indicates the complete neutralisation of HCl"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong: the order is reversed. (B) correct: 0.1 × 2.0/40 = 0.005 M HCl. (C) wrong: it gives the HCl value to acetic acid; the actual value is 0.1 × 3.0/40 = 0.0075 M. (D) wrong: C is the end point for both acids, so it marks CH₃COOH being neutralised, not HCl.\nKey idea: In the conductance curve the strong acid's H⁺ is removed first (steep fall); the weak acid then gives a gentle rise until the final excess-base break.","traps":["Order of neutralisation of strong and weak acid reversed: OH⁻ reacts first with free H⁺ from HCl; conductance falls as fast H⁺ is replaced by Na⁺. Acetic acid reacts only after that.","Titration break point assigned to wrong acid: B is the HCl end point; the B-to-C volume (3 mL) belongs to CH₃COOH; C marks complete neutralisation of both acids."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S2C05-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"1 Faraday electricity was passed through Cu²⁺ (1.5 M, 1 L) /Cu and 0.1 Faraday was passed through Ag⁺ (0.2 M, 1 L)/Ag electrolytic cells. After this the two cells were connected through a salt bridge, with the Cu and Ag electrodes joined externally through a meter, to make an electrochemical cell. The emf of the cell thus formed at 298 K is ________ mV (nearest integer)\nGiven : E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.34 V; E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = 0.8 V; 2.303RT/F = 0.06V","type":"num","answer":"400","range":[400.0,400.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 413 (electrolysis ignored), 409 (1 F taken as 1 mol Cu), 430 (Ag⁺ not squared), 0.4 (unit), and 520 (Q inverted: 0.46 + 0.06).\nWorking: Cu²⁺ left = 1.5 − 0.5 = 1.0 M; Ag⁺ left = 0.2 − 0.1 = 0.1 M; E°cell = 0.80 − 0.34 = 0.46 V; E = 0.46 − (0.06/2) log(1.0/0.1²) = 0.46 − 0.06 = 0.40 V = 400 mV.\nKey idea: Electrolysis first changes the concentrations; only then apply Nernst with n = 2 and Q = [Cu²⁺]/[Ag⁺]².","traps":["Electrolysis step skipped before Nernst calculation: Subtract the moles deposited first: Cu²⁺ 1.5 − 0.5 = 1.0 M; Ag⁺ 0.2 − 0.1 = 0.1 M.","Faraday n-factor ignored for divalent ion: Cu²⁺ needs 2 F per mole, so 1 F deposits 0.5 mol."],"topics":["Nernst equation","Faraday's laws"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Types of redox reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":56,"q":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II.\nLIST-I (Redox Reaction) | LIST-II (Type of Redox Reaction)\nA. CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) —Δ→ CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) | I. Disproportionation reaction\nB. 2NaH(s) —Δ→ 2Na(s) + H₂(g) | II. Combination reaction\nC. V₂O₅(s) + 5Ca(s) —Δ→ 2V(s) + 5CaO(s) | III. Decomposition reaction\nD. 2H₂O₂(aq) —Δ→ 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g) | IV. Displacement reaction\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II: all four wrong. (B) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III: all four wrong. (D) has A and B right but swaps C and D. (C) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I is correct.\nWorking: A – CH₄ combines with O₂ (combination); B – NaH breaks into Na and H₂ (decomposition); C – Ca displaces V from V₂O₅ (displacement); D – O in H₂O₂ (−1) goes to −2 and 0 (disproportionation).\nKey idea: Classify by what happens to oxidation numbers and reactant count: one element going both up and down is disproportionation.","traps":["Disproportionation mistaken for decomposition: In H₂O₂ oxygen (−1) goes both to −2 and to 0, which is disproportionation. NCERT uses it as its disproportionation example.","Combustion classified as displacement or decomposition: NCERT lists this combustion as a combination redox reaction (elements or compounds combining with O₂)."],"topics":["Types of redox reactions","Disproportionation"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Given below is the plot of the molar conductivity vs √concentration for KCl in aqueous solution. If, for the higher concentration of KCl solution, the resistance of the conductivity cell is 100 Ω, then the resistance of the same cell with the dilute solution is 'x' Ω. The value of x is _______ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"150","range":[150.0,150.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 100 (√C used as C), 67 (R ∝ 1/Λm, or points swapped), 44 (√C and Λm combined wrongly).\nWorking: C = 0.1² = 0.01 and 0.15² = 0.0225 mol L⁻¹; κ ∝ Λm × C: 150 × 0.01 = 1.5 (dilute) and 100 × 0.0225 = 2.25 (concentrated). Same cell, so R ∝ 1/κ: x = 100 × 2.25/1.5 = 150 Ω.\nKey idea: Same cell, so R ∝ 1/κ, and κ = Λm × C, with C obtained by squaring the axis value.","traps":["Square root of concentration used as concentration: Square the axis values: C = 0.01 and 0.0225 mol L⁻¹.","Molar conductivity treated as proportional to conductance: For the same cell R depends on κ = Λm C, not on Λm alone."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S1C23-p.webp"],"topics":["Conductivity vs concentration","Molar conductivity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrode potential & SHE","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"One of the commonly used electrode is calomel electrode. Under which of the following categories, calomel electrode comes?","options":["Metal ion - Metal electrodes","Oxidation - Reduction electrodes","Metal - Insoluble Salt - Anion electrodes","Gas - Ion electrodes"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): Hg metal present, so 'metal–metal ion'. (B): redox electrodes like Pt/Fe³⁺,Fe²⁺. (D): SHE-type gas electrode.\nWorking: calomel electrode is Hg | Hg₂Cl₂(s) | Cl⁻ – a metal / insoluble salt / anion electrode.\nKey idea: Calomel = Hg in contact with Hg₂Cl₂ (insoluble) and KCl (anion).","traps":["Metal–metal ion chosen: Hg₂²⁺ is fixed by sparingly soluble Hg₂Cl₂ and Cl⁻ – the anion controls potential."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"What pressure (bar) of H₂ would be required to make emf of hydrogen electrode zero in pure water at 25°C ?","options":["0.5","10⁻¹⁴","1","10⁻⁷"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 10⁻⁷: forgets the square on [H⁺]. (C) 1: thinks standard pressure always gives zero. (A) 0.5: random.\nWorking: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂; E = 0 − (0.059/2) log(p_H₂/[H⁺]²) = 0 → p_H₂ = [H⁺]² = (10⁻⁷)² = 10⁻¹⁴ bar.\nKey idea: E = 0 when Q = 1, i.e. p_H₂ = [H⁺]².","traps":["[H⁺] not squared: Write the balanced half-reaction before Q.","1 bar assumed to give E = 0: SHE needs [H⁺] = 1 M too."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Only 2 mL of KMnO₄ solution of unknown molarity is required to reach the end point of a titration of 20 mL of oxalic acid (2M) in acidic medium. The molarity of KMnO₄ solution should be ______ M.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 20: uses 1:1 ratio. 50: mole ratio inverted (5 : 2 instead of 2 : 5). 80: mL slip.\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5H₂C₂O₄ → … ; mol oxalic = 0.040 → mol KMnO₄ = 0.040 × 2/5 = 0.016 in 2 mL → 8 M.\nKey idea: n-factors 5 (MnO₄⁻) and 2 (oxalic acid) → 2:5 mole ratio.","traps":["1:1 mole ratio: Balance: 2MnO₄⁻ : 5C₂O₄²⁻.","Unrealistic 8 M doubted: Answer what the numbers give."],"topics":["Redox titrations","Equivalent concept & n-factor"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Fuel cells","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Fuel cell, using hydrogen and oxygen as fuels,\nA. has been used in spaceship\nB. has an efficiency of 40% to produce electricity\nC. uses aluminum as catalysts\nD. is eco-friendly\nE. is actually a type of Galvanic cell only\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, D, E only","A, B, C only","A, B, D only","A, B, D, E only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (B), (C) and (D) all contain statement B (40 % is the thermal-plant figure, not the fuel-cell one), so they fail; option (B) also contains statement C (aluminium catalyst). (D) tempts students who accept 40 %. (C) drops E for those who think a fuel cell is not galvanic because reactants are fed continuously.\nWorking: H₂–O₂ cell was used in the Apollo programme (A true); its efficiency is about 70 %, 40 % is the thermal-plant figure (B false); the electrodes carry finely divided Pt or Pd, not Al (C false); product is water, pollution free (D true); it converts chemical energy of the cell reaction into electricity, i.e. a galvanic cell (E true).\nKey idea: Fuel cell efficiency ≈ 70 %; catalyst Pt/Pd; it is a galvanic cell with continuous feed.","traps":["40 % efficiency accepted: Fuel cell ~70 %; 40 % belongs to thermal plants.","Fuel cell not seen as galvanic: Any cell turning reaction energy into electricity is galvanic."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"For a strong electrolyte, a plot of molar conductivity against (concentration)¹ᐟ² is a straight line, with a negative slope, the correct unit for the slope is","options":["S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L","S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L⁻¹ᐟ²","S cm² mol⁻¹ L¹ᐟ²","S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L¹ᐟ²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) inverts the L exponent (L in denominator). (A) drops the ½ on L. (C) forgets to combine mol exponents (−1 − ½).\nWorking: Λm = Λ°m − A c¹ᐟ²; slope unit = (S cm² mol⁻¹)/(mol L⁻¹)¹ᐟ² = S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L¹ᐟ².\nKey idea: Divide Λm units by c¹ᐟ² units: mol⁻¹ × mol⁻¹ᐟ² and L⁺¹ᐟ².","traps":["L exponent sign flipped: 1/(L⁻¹)¹ᐟ² = L⁺¹ᐟ².","mol exponents not combined: mol⁻¹ × mol⁻¹ᐟ² = mol⁻³ᐟ²."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kohlrausch's law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Molar ionic conductivities of divalent cation and anion are 57 S cm² mol⁻¹ and 73 S cm² mol⁻¹ respectively. The molar conductivity of solution of an electrolyte with the above cation and anion will be:","options":["187 S cm² mol⁻¹","260 S cm² mol⁻¹","65 S cm² mol⁻¹","130 S cm² mol⁻¹"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 260: both λ doubled because ions are \"divalent\". (C) 65: averaged. (A) 187: 2×57 + 73 (M₂X-type mis-stoichiometry).\nWorking: M²⁺ and X²⁻ combine 1:1 (MX), so by Kohlrausch Λ = 57 + 73 = 130 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Multiply λ by number of ions per formula unit, not by charge.","traps":["λ multiplied by charge: Use ions per formula unit; MX has one of each."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Batteries","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The reaction at cathode in the cells commonly used in clocks involves.","options":["oxidation of Mn from +2 to +7","reduction of Mn from +4 to +3","oxidation of Mn from +3 to +4","reduction of Mn from +7 to +2"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): oxidation at cathode – sign convention error. (D): KMnO₄ reduction in acid (+7 → +2) recalled instead.\nWorking: clocks use the dry (Leclanché) cell; cathode: MnO₂ + NH₄⁺ + e⁻ → MnO(OH) + NH₃, Mn +4 → +3.\nKey idea: Cathode = reduction; dry cell MnO₂ (+4) → MnO(OH) (+3).","traps":["Oxidation placed at cathode: Cathode always reduction.","Permanganate change recalled: Dry cell uses MnO₂, not MnO₄⁻."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The quantity of silver deposited when one coulomb charge is passed through AgNO₃ solution:","options":["0.1 g atom of silver","1 chemical equivalent of silver","1 g of silver","1 electrochemical equivalent of silver"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) chemical equivalent needs 1 F (96500 C), not 1 C.\nWorking: m = Z·Q; for Q = 1 C, m = Z (electrochemical equivalent).\nKey idea: 1 C deposits Z grams; 1 F deposits one gram-equivalent.","traps":["Coulomb vs faraday: One gram-equivalent needs 96500 C."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"For the electrochemical cell M | M²⁺ || X | X²⁻\nIf E°(M²⁺/M) = 0.46 V and E°(X/X²⁻) = 0.34 V.\nWhich of the following is correct?","options":["E_cell = 0.80 V","M + X → M²⁺ + X²⁻ is a spontaneous reaction","E_cell = −0.80 V","M²⁺ + X²⁻ → M + X is a spontaneous reaction"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) add the potentials (±0.80). (B) assumes the cell as written always runs.\nWorking: E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = 0.34 − 0.46 = −0.12 V, so the written reaction is non-spontaneous and its reverse is spontaneous.\nKey idea: Subtract reduction potentials; negative E° means reverse reaction is spontaneous.","traps":["Potentials added: E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with reduction potentials.","Written cell assumed spontaneous: Check sign of E° first."],"topics":["Galvanic cells","EMF & Gibbs energy"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"A conductivity cell with two electrodes (dark side) are half filled with infinitely dilute aqueous solution of a weak electrolyte. (The cell is a 1 cm × 1 cm × 1 cm cube; two opposite faces are the electrodes.) If volume is doubled by adding more water at constant temperature, the molar conductivity of the cell will -","options":["depend upon type of electrolyte","increase sharply","decrease sharply","remain same or can not be measured accurately"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) increase sharply: student applies 'Λm of weak electrolyte rises steeply on dilution' without noticing the solution is already infinitely dilute. (C): confuses molar conductivity with conductivity κ, which falls on dilution. (A): vague hedge for students unsure of the limit.\nWorking: the solution is already at infinite dilution, so Λm has reached its limiting value Λ°m; further dilution cannot raise it (and at such low conductance it cannot be measured accurately).\nKey idea: At infinite dilution Λm = Λ°m, a constant; doubling the volume changes nothing measurable.","traps":["Sharp rise of weak-electrolyte Λm applied blindly: Note 'infinitely dilute' — the limit is already reached.","Conductivity κ confused with molar conductivity Λm: κ falls on dilution, Λm rises (or stays at limit)."],"topics":["Conductivity vs concentration","Molar conductivity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which of the following material is not a semiconductor.","options":["Copper oxide","Graphite","Silicon","Germanium"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Copper oxide: students think metal oxides are insulators or conductors; (C)/(D) unlikely.\nWorking: graphite is an electronic conductor; CuO, Si and Ge are listed as semiconductors.\nKey idea: Graphite conducts like a metal (delocalised π electrons).","traps":["CuO assumed not a semiconductor: NCERT Table 2.2 lists CuO under semiconductors."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Types of redox reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Reaction): A. N₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g); B. 2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) → 2PbO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g); C. 2Na(s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H₂(g); D. 2NO₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq) → NO₂⁻(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)\nList II (Type of redox reaction): I. Decomposition; II. Displacement; III. Disproportionation; IV. Combination\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are cyclic shifts; each mislabels at least two reactions.\nWorking: A combination; B thermal decomposition; C Na displaces H₂ from water; D N(+4) goes to +3 and +5 – disproportionation.\nKey idea: Check oxidation numbers in D: one N element goes both up and down.","traps":["Decomposition with gas release called displacement: Single reactant breaking up = decomposition."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"How can an electrochemical cell be converted into an electrolytic cell ?","options":["Applying an external opposite potential greater than E⁰cell","Exchanging the electrodes at anode and cathode.","Applying an external opposite potential lower than E⁰cell","Reversing the flow of ions in salt bridge."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): opposite potential lower than E⁰ only reduces current. (B)/(D): physical swaps do not drive non-spontaneous reaction.\nWorking: when E_ext exceeds the cell potential the reaction and current reverse and the cell works as an electrolytic cell.\nKey idea: Electrolytic means driven: need E_ext > E_cell.","traps":["Lower opposing voltage thought enough: Below E_cell the galvanic reaction still runs."],"topics":["Galvanic cells","Electrolysis"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Thiosulphate reacts differently with iodine and bromine in the reactions given below:\n2S₂O₃²⁻ + I₂ → S₄O₆²⁻ + 2I⁻\nS₂O₃²⁻ + 5Br₂ + 5H₂O → 2SO₄²⁻ + 4Br⁻ + 10H⁺\nWhich of the following statements justifies the above dual behaviour of thiosulphate?","options":["Thiosulphate undergoes oxidation by bromine and reduction by iodine in these reactions","Bromine is a weaker oxidant than iodine","Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine","Bromine undergoes oxidation and iodine undergoes reduction in these reactions"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) says thiosulphate is reduced by iodine – it is oxidised in both; (B) reverses halogen oxidising order; (D) says bromine is oxidised – both halogens are reduced.\nWorking: S goes +2 → +2.5 with I₂ but +2 → +6 with Br₂; the stronger oxidant Br₂ takes S to the higher state. Both halogens are reduced; thiosulphate is oxidised in both. (The Br₂ equation in the question is not balanced – the balanced form is S₂O₃²⁻ + 4Br₂ + 5H₂O → 2SO₄²⁻ + 8Br⁻ + 10H⁺; this does not affect the answer.)\nKey idea: Stronger oxidant (Br₂) pushes S to +6; weaker I₂ only to +2.5.","traps":["Thiosulphate thought to be reduced by I₂: S oxidation number rises in both reactions.","Halogen oxidising order reversed: Oxidising power F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂."],"topics":["Oxidation & reduction","Oxidation number"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Among the following halogens\nF₂, Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂\nWhich can undergo disproportionation reactions?","options":["F₂ and Cl₂","Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂","Only I₂","F₂, Cl₂ and Br₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) include F₂, the NCERT exception; (C) only I₂ is too restrictive.\nWorking: Cl, Br, I show positive oxidation states and disproportionate in alkali; F, the most electronegative element, has no positive state and cannot disproportionate.\nKey idea: Fluorine has only −1 and 0 states, so it cannot be both oxidised and reduced.","traps":["Including F₂: F cannot have a positive oxidation state."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The emf of cell Tl | Tl⁺ (0.001 M) || Cu²⁺ (0.01 M) | Cu is 0.83 V at 298 K. It could be increased by :","options":["increasing concentration of Tl⁺ ions","increasing concentration of Cu²⁺ ions","increasing concentration of both Tl⁺ and Cu²⁺ ions","decreasing concentration of both Tl⁺ and Cu²⁺ ions"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) raises product ion → E falls. (C) both raised: [Tl⁺]² rises faster than [Cu²⁺] so E falls. (D) both lowered: [Tl⁺]² falls faster, so E also rises – (D) is arguably correct too, but the official answer is (B).\nWorking: cell reaction 2Tl + Cu²⁺ → 2Tl⁺ + Cu, E = E° − (0.059/2) log([Tl⁺]²/[Cu²⁺]); raising [Cu²⁺] raises E. Strictly, lowering both by the same factor also lowers Q and raises E, so (D) is arguably correct too; (B) is the intended single answer.\nKey idea: Increase cathode ion, decrease anode ion to increase emf.","traps":["Changing both concentrations: Write Q = [Tl⁺]²/[Cu²⁺] before judging.","Anode ion increase thought to help: Tl⁺ is a product."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The reaction; ½H₂(g) + AgCl(s) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) + Ag(s) occurs in which of the following galvanic cell :","options":["Pt | H₂(g) | HCl(soln.) || AgNO₃(aq) | Ag","Ag | AgCl(s) | KCl(soln.) || AgNO₃(aq) | Ag","Pt | H₂(g) | KCl(soln.) || AgCl(s) | Ag","Pt | H₂(g) | HCl(soln.) || AgCl(s) | Ag"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses Ag⁺/Ag cathode, not AgCl. (B) has no hydrogen electrode. (C) uses KCl at the anode side, but H⁺ must be in solution with the H₂ electrode.\nWorking: anode H₂ → H⁺ (hydrogen electrode in HCl), cathode AgCl + e⁻ → Ag + Cl⁻ (silver–silver chloride electrode); products H⁺ and Cl⁻ are HCl.\nKey idea: AgCl must appear as a solid on the cathode side; H₂ electrode needs H⁺ (HCl).","traps":["Ag⁺/Ag vs Ag/AgCl cathode: AgCl(s) is in the reaction, so the AgCl electrode is used.","KCl vs HCl electrolyte: H⁺ is a product: hydrogen electrode needs acid."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The molar conductivity for electrolytes A and B are plotted against C¹ᐟ² as shown below. Electrolytes A and B respectively are:","options":["A – weak electrolyte, B – weak electrolyte","A – weak electrolyte, B – strong electrolyte","A – strong electrolyte, B – strong electrolyte","A – strong electrolyte, B – weak electrolyte"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reverses the assignment (higher Λm at low C mistaken for strong). (A)/(C) assume both curves are the same type.\nWorking: a linear, gentle fall of Λm with √C is a strong electrolyte (B); a steep rise at low C is a weak electrolyte (A).\nKey idea: Straight line (Debye–Hückel–Onsager) = strong; steep hyperbola near zero = weak.","traps":["Higher Λm read as stronger electrolyte: Judge by shape, not by height."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr09S1C02-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The standard reduction potentials at 298 K for the following half cells are given below:\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O, E° = 1.33 V\nFe³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ → Fe, E° = −0.04 V\nNi²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Ni, E° = −0.25 V\nAg⁺(aq) + e⁻ → Ag, E° = 0.80 V\nAu³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ → Au, E° = 1.40 V\nConsider the given electrochemical reactions. The number of metal(s) which will be oxidized by Cr₂O₇²⁻, in aqueous solution is ____.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 if Au is included (1.40 vs 1.33 misread); 2 if Ag is excluded because its E° is positive.\nWorking: metals with E° below 1.33 V (Fe, Ni, Ag) give positive E°cell with dichromate; Au (1.40 V) is not oxidised. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Oxidant with higher E° oxidises any metal whose couple has lower E°.","traps":["Positive E° metals excluded: Compare with 1.33 V, not with zero.","Au close to dichromate: Au couple is higher, so Au is not oxidised."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Batteries","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Cell): A. Leclanche cell B. Ni–Cd cell C. Fuel cell D. Mercury cell\nLIST II (Use/Property/Reaction): I. Converts energy of combustion into electrical energy II. Does not involve any ion in solution and is used in hearing aids III. Rechargeable IV. Reaction at anode Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives Leclanche the rechargeable tag. (B) shifts every pair by one. (D) puts Leclanche with hearing aids.\nWorking: Leclanche anode Zn → Zn²⁺; Ni–Cd is rechargeable; fuel cell converts combustion energy; mercury cell (no ions in solution, hearing aids).\nKey idea: Only the mercury cell has no ion concentration change; Ni–Cd is the secondary cell.","traps":["Leclanche vs mercury cell: Mercury cell: no ions in solution, hearing aids."],"topics":["Batteries","Fuel cells"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which out of the following is a correct equation to show change in molar conductivity with respect to concentration for a weak electrolyte, if the symbols carry their usual meaning :","options":["Λm − Λm° + AC¹ᐟ² = 0","Λm²C + KaΛm°² − KaΛmΛm° = 0","Λm − Λm° − AC¹ᐟ² = 0","Λm²C − KaΛm°² + KaΛmΛm° = 0"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the strong-electrolyte (Kohlrausch √c) law. (C) has the wrong sign even for strong electrolytes. (B) has both Ka terms with wrong signs.\nWorking: Ka = cα²/(1−α) with α = Λm/Λm° gives Ka = cΛm²/[Λm°(Λm° − Λm)], i.e. cΛm² − KaΛm°² + KaΛmΛm° = 0.\nKey idea: Start from Ka = cα²/(1−α), substitute α = Λm/Λm°, multiply out.","traps":["Strong vs weak electrolyte equation: √c law only for strong electrolytes.","Sign slip after cross-multiplying: Expand Ka·Λm°(Λm° − Λm) = cΛm² carefully."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which of the following reactions are disproportionation reactions?\n(A) Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Cu\n(B) 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O\n(C) 2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂\n(D) 2MnO₄⁻ + 3Mn²⁺ + 2H₂O → 5MnO₂ + 4H⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (B)","(A), (D)","(B), (C), (D)","(A), (B), (C)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) (A),(D): takes the comproportionation of MnO₄⁻ and Mn²⁺ (one product from two states) as disproportionation. (C) (B),(C),(D): misses Cu⁺ and counts every Mn redox reaction. (D) (A),(B),(C): sees Mn going to two states in KMnO₄ decomposition and ignores that O, not Mn, is oxidised.\nWorking: In (A) Cu(+1) goes to +2 and 0; in (B) Mn(+6) goes to +7 and +4 – both disproportionation. (C) is a decomposition where Mn is reduced (+7 → +6, +4) but O is oxidised (−2 → 0): different elements, not disproportionation. (D) is the reverse (comproportionation: +7 and +2 → +4).\nKey idea: Disproportionation needs the SAME element in ONE oxidation state to go both up and down.","traps":["KMnO₄ decomposition taken as disproportionation: Check which element is oxidised: here it is O, Mn is only reduced.","Comproportionation confused with disproportionation: Disproportionation starts from one oxidation state; (D) starts from two."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The potential for the given half cell at 298 K is (−) _______ × 10⁻² V (Nearest integer)\n2H⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → H₂(g)\n[H⁺] = 1 M, P_H₂ = 2 atm\n(Given : 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V, log 2 = 0.3)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 2 (uses n = 1), 0 (rounds 0.9 down), 9 (unit slip ×10⁻³).\nWorking: E = 0 − (0.06/2) log(P_H₂/[H⁺]²) = −0.03 × 0.3 = −0.009 V = −0.9 × 10⁻² V → 1.\nKey idea: Only pressure term changes; [H⁺]=1.","traps":["n = 2 forgotten: Write the balanced half reaction: 2e⁻.","Answer −0.9 rounded wrongly: 0.9 × 10⁻² → nearest integer 1."],"topics":["Nernst equation","Electrode potential & SHE"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The lowest oxidation number of an atom in a compound A₂B is −2 . The number of electrons in its valence shell is _______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 2 (takes |ON| as electrons), 8 or 4.\nWorking: Lowest oxidation number = (group valence electrons − 8) = −2 → 6 valence electrons (Group 16, e.g. B in A₂B like H₂O, Na₂S).\nKey idea: Valence electrons = 8 + lowest oxidation number.","traps":["Oxidation number taken as electron count: Atom gains 2 electrons to complete octet → it had 6."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The amount of electricity in Coulomb required for the oxidation of 1 mol of H₂O to O₂ is ______ × 10⁵ C.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (per mol O₂, i.e. 3.86 × 10⁵), 1 (one electron per H₂O).\nWorking: 2 mol e⁻ per mol H₂O = 2 × 96 500 = 1.93 × 10⁵ C ≈ 2\nKey idea: Each O(−2) in water loses 2 e⁻ to become O(0).","traps":["Per mol O₂ vs per mol H₂O: Divide 4 e⁻ by 2 H₂O."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Consider the following redox reaction :\nMnO₄⁻ + H⁺ + H₂C₂O₄ ⇌ Mn²⁺ + H₂O + CO₂\nThe standard reduction potentials are given as below (E°red) :\nE°(MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺) = +1.51 V\nE°(CO₂/H₂C₂O₄) = −0.49 V\nIf the equilibrium constant of the above reaction is given as Keq = 10ˣ, then the value of x = ______ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"338","range":[338.0,338.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 34 (n = 1), 68 (n = 2), 169 (n = 5), 339 (0.059 used), 333 (0.06 used).\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 6H⁺ + 5H₂C₂O₄ → 2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 10CO₂, n = 10; E°cell = 2.00 V; log K = 10 × 2.00/0.0591 = 338.4 → 338. With 0.059 V it would be 339, so the answer depends on the constant used\nKey idea: Full balanced equation transfers 10 electrons.","traps":["n taken from one half-reaction: Use the LCM: n = 10 for the balanced equation.","0.059 vs 0.0591: Use 0.0591 V when nearest integer is asked."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The mass of silver (Molar mass of Ag : 108 g mol⁻¹) displaced by a quantity of electricity which displaces 5600 mL of O₂ at S.T.P. will be ______ g.","type":"num","answer":"108","range":[108.0,108.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 54 (taking 2e⁻ per O₂, i.e. treating O as the unit), 27 (1 mol e⁻ per O₂ ÷ 4 confusion), 216 (8 e⁻), 432 (using 22.4 L ↔ 1 F per mole O₂ incorrectly).\nWorking: 5600 mL O₂ at STP = 0.25 mol; 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ so charge = 1 F; Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag gives 1 mol = 108 g.\nKey idea: 1 mol O₂ needs 4 F; 0.25 mol O₂ → 1 F → 1 mol Ag.","traps":["Electrons per O₂ taken as 2: Each O₂ needs 4 e⁻ (two O atoms × 2).","mL not converted to mol correctly: 5600/22400 = 0.25 mol."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"From the given list, the number of compounds with +4 oxidation state of Sulphur ________.\nSO₃, H₂SO₃, SOCl₂, SF₄, BaSO₄, H₂S₂O₇","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (miscounting SOCl₂ as +6 or SF₄ as +6), 4 (treating H₂S₂O₇ as +4 because of two S atoms, or SO₃ as +4).\nWorking: S is +6 in SO₃, BaSO₄, H₂S₂O₇ and +4 in H₂SO₃, SOCl₂, SF₄ → 3.\nKey idea: Pyrosulphuric acid has S +6 despite two S atoms; SOCl₂ and SF₄ are S(IV).","traps":["Two S atoms halve the charge in H₂S₂O₇: 2x + 2 − 14 = 0 → x = +6.","SO₂/SO₃ oxidation states mixed: Compute each: SO₃ +6, H₂SO₃ +4."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Corrosion","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following statements is not correct about rusting of iron?","options":["Rusting of iron is envisaged as setting up of electrochemical cell on the surface of iron object.","Dissolved acidic oxides SO₂, NO₂ in water act as catalyst in the process of rusting.","Coating of iron surface by tin prevents rusting, even if the tin coating is peeling off.","When pH lies above 9 or 10, rusting of iron does not take place."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is true (NCERT exercise 2.15 wording). (B) true in substance - acidic oxides supply H⁺ that speeds rusting. (D) true - in strongly alkaline medium rusting is suppressed.\nWorking: tin is less reactive than iron; once the coating breaks, iron becomes the anode and rusts faster. Only zinc (galvanising) protects after damage.\nKey idea: Tin is only a barrier; zinc is sacrificial.","traps":["Tin treated like zinc: Compare E°: Sn is above Fe, so Fe becomes anode once exposed.","High pH statement doubted: Cathode reaction needs H⁺; alkaline medium slows rusting."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following cannot function as an oxidising agent?","options":["SO₄²⁻","MnO₄⁻","N³⁻","BrO₃⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SO₄²⁻ may look inert, but S is +6 (maximum) and can be reduced (conc. H₂SO₄ oxidises). (B) MnO₄⁻ classic oxidant. (D) BrO₃⁻ oxidant (Br +5).\nWorking: N in N³⁻ is at its lowest oxidation state (−3) and cannot be reduced; S(+6), Mn(+7), Br(+5) can all be reduced.\nKey idea: Lowest oxidation state → only reductant.","traps":["Sulphate thought non-oxidising: S at +6 can be reduced; only the lowest oxidation state cannot."],"topics":["Oxidation & reduction","Oxidation number"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The hydrogen electrode is dipped in a solution of pH = 3 at 25°C. The potential of the electrode will be ________ ×10⁻² V. (2.303RT/F = 0.059 V)","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −18 vs 18 sign confusion; 17 (truncating 17.7); 59 or 6 (forgetting pH factor or units).\nWorking: E(H⁺/H₂) = −0.059 × pH = −0.177 V = −17.7 ×10⁻² V; the answer 18 is the magnitude (or oxidation potential +0.177 V); as a reduction potential the value is −18.\nKey idea: E = −0.059 pH at 1 bar H₂; magnitude 0.177 V.","traps":["Sign of reduction potential: Reduction potential is −0.177 V; the oxidation potential is +0.177 V.","Rounding to nearest integer: Round 17.7 to 18."],"topics":["Nernst equation","Electrode potential & SHE"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Chlorine undergoes disproportionation in alkaline medium as shown below :\naCl₂(g) + bOH⁻(aq) → cClO⁻(aq) + dCl⁻(aq) + eH₂O(l)\nThe values of a, b, c and d in a balanced redox reaction are respectively :","options":["3, 4, 4 and 2","1, 2, 1 and 1","2, 4, 1 and 3","2, 2, 1 and 3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) mixes in chlorate-type ratio; (C)/(D) do not balance Cl or charge.\nWorking: Cl₂ + 2OH⁻ → ClO⁻ + Cl⁻ + H₂O (one Cl 0→+1, the other 0→−1).\nKey idea: Electrons lost (0→+1) = electrons gained (0→−1), so ClO⁻ : Cl⁻ = 1 : 1.","traps":["Confusing with hot-alkali chlorate equation: Product given is ClO⁻ (+1), so ratio is 1:1."],"topics":["Disproportionation","Balancing: oxidation-number method"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The mass of zinc produced by the electrolysis of zinc sulphate solution with a steady current of 0.015 A for 15 minutes is _________ × 10⁻⁴ g.\n(Atomic mass of zinc = 65.4 amu)","type":"num","answer":"46","range":[46.0,46.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using n = 1 gives ≈91; forgetting minutes→seconds gives ≈0.76 (×10⁻⁴ g); dropping the scale gives 0.0046.\nWorking: Q = 0.015 × 900 = 13.5 C; mol Zn = 13.5/(2 × 96500) = 6.99 × 10⁻⁵; mass = 4.57 × 10⁻³ g = 45.7 × 10⁻⁴ g ≈ 46.\nKey idea: m = (I t / nF) × M with n = 2 for Zn²⁺.","traps":["n = 1 used for Zn²⁺: Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn: divide by 2F.","Time not converted / scale misread: Use t = 900 s; then express 4.57 × 10⁻³ g as 45.7 × 10⁻⁴."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A constant current was passed through a solution of AuCl₄⁻ ion between gold electrodes. After a period of 10.0 minutes, the increase in mass of cathode was 1.314 g. The total charge passed through the solution is _______ × 10⁻² F.\n(Given atomic mass of Au = 197)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 0.67 (n = 1), 1.33 (n = 2), using time to find current then mis-converting.\nWorking: AuCl₄⁻ has Au³⁺; n(Au) = 1.314/197 = 6.67×10⁻³ mol; charge = 3 × 6.67×10⁻³ = 0.020 F = 2 × 10⁻² F.\nKey idea: Au(+3) → 3 F per mole of Au; time not needed.","traps":["Electrons per Au misjudged: Oxidation state: x − 4 = −1 → x = +3.","Time given but not needed: Question asks total charge in F only."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"2MnO₄⁻ + bI⁻ + cH₂O → xI₂ + yMnO₂ + zOH⁻\nIf the above equation is balanced with integer coefficients, the value of z is _______.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (H₂O coefficient), 6 (b), 3 (x), 2 (y); 16 if using 4 MnO₄⁻.\nWorking: Mn +7→+4 gains 3e⁻ each (6e⁻ for 2 Mn); 2I⁻→I₂ loses 2e⁻, so 6I⁻ → 3I₂; charge: −2 −6 = −8 → 8OH⁻; H: 4H₂O. 2MnO₄⁻ + 6I⁻ + 4H₂O → 3I₂ + 2MnO₂ + 8OH⁻.\nKey idea: Electron balance 2×3 = 6 → 6I⁻; then balance charge with OH⁻.","traps":["Coefficient for water taken for z: Write the full balanced equation before reading z."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Reduction potential of ions are given below:\nClO₄⁻: E° = 1.19 V; IO₄⁻: E° = 1.65 V; BrO₄⁻: E° = 1.74 V\nThe correct order of their oxidising power is :","options":["IO₄⁻ > BrO₄⁻ > ClO₄⁻","BrO₄⁻ > ClO₄⁻ > IO₄⁻","ClO₄⁻ > IO₄⁻ > BrO₄⁻","BrO₄⁻ > IO₄⁻ > ClO₄⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) follow imagined group trends instead of the data. (B) assumes Cl above I by electronegativity.\nWorking: higher standard reduction potential = stronger oxidising agent; 1.74 > 1.65 > 1.19 V.\nKey idea: Read the numbers: oxidising power follows E°(red). Perbromate's anomaly is the hidden point.","traps":["Applying a group trend instead of E° data: Rank by the E° given."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Total number of species from the following which can undergo disproportionation reaction is ________.\nH₂O₂, ClO₃⁻, P₄, Cl₂, Ag, Cu⁺¹, F₂, NO₂, K⁺","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (missing Cu⁺ or NO₂), 7 (including F₂ or Ag), 4 (excluding ClO₃⁻ thinking +5 is high).\nWorking: H₂O₂ (O −1 → 0, −2), ClO₃⁻ (Cl +5 → +7, −1), P₄ (0 → −3, +1), Cl₂ (0 → +1, −1), Cu⁺ (→ Cu²⁺ + Cu) and NO₂ (+4 → +3, +5) can; Ag (lowest state 0), F₂ (no positive state) and K⁺ cannot. Count 6.\nKey idea: Need an element with accessible higher and lower oxidation states; F, metals in lowest state and K⁺ fail.","traps":["F₂ treated like other halogens: F has no positive oxidation state.","Cu⁺ disproportionation forgotten: 2Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Cu in water."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Identify the factor from the following that does not affect electrolytic conductance of a solution.","options":["The nature of solvent used.","The nature of the electrolyte added.","The nature of the electrode used.","Concentration of the electrolyte."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) are all NCERT-listed factors; a student who misreads 'does not' picks one of them.\nWorking: NCERT lists nature of electrolyte, ion size/solvation, nature and viscosity of solvent, concentration and temperature; the electrode material is not a factor.\nKey idea: Electrolytic conductance depends on the solution, not the electrode material.","traps":["'does not' missed: Underline the negative word."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"One Faraday of electricity liberates x × 10⁻¹ gram atom of copper from copper sulphate. x is ________.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 10 (n = 1 assumed).\nWorking: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu; 1 F deposits 0.5 g atom = 5 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: 1 F = 1 mol e⁻; Cu²⁺ needs 2.","traps":["n-factor ignored: Divide by charge on ion."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I : S₈ solid undergoes disproportionation reaction under alkaline conditions to form S²⁻ and S₂O₃²⁻. Statement II : ClO₄⁻ can undergo disproportionation reaction under acidic condition. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct","Both statement I and statement II are correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D): believing ClO₄⁻ can disproportionate like ClO⁻, ClO₂⁻, ClO₃⁻. (B): forgetting S₈ disproportionation in alkali.\nWorking: S₈ + 12OH⁻ → 4S²⁻ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ + 6H₂O (NCERT eq.); Cl in ClO₄⁻ is +7, highest state, so it cannot disproportionate.\nKey idea: A species in its highest (or lowest) oxidation state cannot disproportionate.","traps":["ClO₄⁻ grouped with other oxoanions: Check oxidation state: +7 is maximum for Cl."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The values of conductivity of some materials at 298.15 K in S m⁻¹ are 2.1 × 10³, 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶, 1.2 × 10, 3.91, 1.5 × 10⁻², 1 × 10⁻⁷, 1.0 × 10³. The number of conductors among the materials is ______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (excludes HCl solution, using only the 'Conductors' column); 5 (adds Si 1.5×10⁻²); 2 (only values ≥ 10³).\nWorking: from NCERT Table 2.2 these are Na (2.1×10³), glass (10⁻¹⁶), graphite (12), 0.1 M HCl (3.91), Si (1.5×10⁻²), CuO (10⁻⁷), Fe (10³). Conductors: Na, graphite, Fe and the 0.1 M HCl solution (electrolytic conductor) → 4. (Counting only the 'Conductors' column of the table would give 3.)\nKey idea: Values are NCERT Table 2.2 entries; 0.1 M HCl conducts electrolytically.","traps":["Electrolyte solution counted or not: Count the HCl solution as a conductor (answer 4).","Semiconductor cut-off: Semiconductors 10⁻⁶–10⁴ range per NCERT; Si 1.5×10⁻² is a semiconductor."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Number of moles of H⁺ ions required by 1 mole of MnO₄⁻ to oxidise oxalate ion to CO₂ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 16 (reads equation coefficient directly); 5 (oxalate coefficient).\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O; 16/2 = 8 per MnO₄⁻.\nKey idea: Divide the 16 H⁺ by 2 MnO₄⁻.","traps":["Coefficient not normalised: Scale to 1 mol MnO₄⁻."],"topics":["Balancing: ion–electron method","Redox titrations"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Types of redox reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following options are correct for the reaction\n2[Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + Zn(s) → 2Au(s) + [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻(aq)\nA. Redox reaction\nB. Displacement reaction\nC. Decomposition reaction\nD. Combination reaction\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and B only","C and D only","A only","A and D only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) A only: student misses that it is also displacement because of the complex ions. (D): products merge Zn with CN⁻, mistaken for combination. (B): ignores oxidation-number change.\nWorking: Au goes +1 → 0 and Zn 0 → +2 (redox); the more reactive Zn displaces Au from its complex (metal displacement). Not decomposition or combination.\nKey idea: One metal replacing another from its compound is displacement and always redox.","traps":["Complex formation read as combination: Look for one element replacing another – that is displacement.","Displacement missed: Track Au and Zn oxidation numbers separately."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"In ammonium-phosphomolybdate, the oxidation state of Mo is + ______","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5: confuses with P (+5). 3 or 4: wrong formula (e.g. treating it as (NH₄)₂MoO₄ ratio wrongly).\nWorking: (NH₄)₃PO₄·12MoO₃ (or (NH₄)₃[PMo₁₂O₄₀]): 3(+1) + 5 + 12x − 80 = 0 → x = +6.\nKey idea: It is a phosphate with 12 MoO₃ units: Mo stays +6 as in molybdate.","traps":["Mo confused with P oxidation state: MoO₃ units: Mo = +6."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The standard reduction potentials at 298 K for the following half cells are given below:\nNO₃⁻ + 4H⁺ + 3e⁻ → NO(g) + 2H₂O E° = 0.97 V\nV²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → V E° = −1.19 V\nFe³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ → Fe E° = −0.04 V\nAg⁺(aq) + e⁻ → Ag(s) E° = 0.80 V\nAu³⁺(aq) + 3e⁻ → Au(s) E° = 1.40 V\nThe number of metal(s) which will be oxidized by NO₃⁻ in aqueous solution is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if Ag (positive E°) is wrongly excluded because 'only negative-E° metals dissolve'; 4 if Au is included.\nWorking: metals with E° below 0.97 V are oxidised – V (−1.19), Fe (−0.04), Ag (0.80) = 3; Au (1.40) is not.\nKey idea: Ecell = 0.97 − E°(M) > 0 for V, Fe, Ag.","traps":["Only metals below hydrogen counted: The oxidant here is NO₃⁻ (0.97 V), not H⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Products of electrolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"The product, which is not obtained during the electrolysis of brine solution is :","options":["NaOH","Cl₂","H₂","HCl"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are the three products; a student imagining H₂ + Cl₂ recombining might doubt one of them.\nWorking: electrolysis of aqueous NaCl gives H₂ at the cathode, Cl₂ at the anode and NaOH in solution; HCl is not formed.\nKey idea: Brine → NaOH + H₂ + Cl₂.","traps":["H₂ and Cl₂ assumed to combine: Gases are evolved at separate electrodes."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: oxidation-number method","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"2IO₃⁻ + xI⁻ + 12H⁺ → 6I₂ + 6H₂O\nWhat is the value of x ?","options":["10","2","12","6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): student copies the iodate coefficient. (C): student sets x = 12 by matching H⁺ or total I atoms. (D): student matches I₂ coefficient.\nWorking: iodine balance 2 + x = 12 → x = 10; charge: −2 − 10 + 12 = 0 ✓; electrons: 2 I(+5→0) gain 10 e⁻, 10 I⁻ lose 10 e⁻ ✓.\nKey idea: Iodine atom balance alone fixes x: 2 + x = 12.","traps":["Total I counted as x: Subtract the 2 I already from IO₃⁻.","Coefficient copied from I₂: Count atoms, not molecules."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The reaction\n½H₂(g) + AgCl(s) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) + Ag(s)\noccurs in which of the given galvanic cell.","options":["Ag|AgCl(s)|KCl(solⁿ)|AgNO₃|Ag","Pt|H₂(g)|HCl(solⁿ)|AgCl(s)|Ag","Pt|H₂(g)|HCl(solⁿ)|AgNO₃(solⁿ)|Ag","Pt|H₂(g)|KCl(solⁿ)|AgCl(s)|Ag"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): no H₂ electrode at all. (C): uses Ag⁺/Ag cathode instead of AgCl/Ag, so Cl⁻ not produced. (D): KCl electrolyte gives no H⁺ in solution as in the equation.\nWorking: anode H₂ → 2H⁺ (Pt|H₂|H⁺), cathode AgCl + e⁻ → Ag + Cl⁻; both H⁺ and Cl⁻ in one HCl electrolyte → Pt|H₂|HCl|AgCl|Ag.\nKey idea: Products H⁺ and Cl⁻ together = HCl solution; cathode must be AgCl|Ag.","traps":["Electrolyte ignored: Electrolyte must hold the ions in the equation (H⁺, Cl⁻).","AgCl/Ag vs Ag⁺/Ag electrode: Reaction uses AgCl(s), so cathode is Ag|AgCl|Cl⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: In redox titration, the indicators used are sensitive to change in pH of the solution. Statement II: In acid-base titration, the indicators used are sensitive to change in oxidation potential. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) catch students who check only one statement; (D) catches a careless read of familiar wording.\nWorking: the two statements swap the indicator types; redox indicators respond to oxidation potential, acid–base indicators to pH.\nKey idea: Swap the words back: redox ↔ potential, acid–base ↔ pH.","traps":["Swapped words not noticed: Read which titration is paired with which property."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The sum of oxidation state of the metals in Fe(CO)₅, VO²⁺ and WO₃ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 or 15 (giving Fe a positive value in Fe(CO)₅), 8 (taking VO²⁺ as V(+2)).\nWorking: Fe(CO)₅ 0, VO²⁺ +4, WO₃ +6 → sum 10.\nKey idea: Metal carbonyls have M(0); in VO²⁺ count oxygen: x − 2 = +2 → +4.","traps":["VO²⁺ read as V²⁺: Subtract oxygen: V = +4 (vanadyl)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The number of incorrect statements from the following is ______. A. The electrical work that a reaction can perform at constant pressure and temperature is equal to the reaction Gibbs energy. B. E°cell is dependent on the pressure. C. dE°cell/dT = ΔrS°/nF. D. A cell is operating reversibly if the cell potential is exactly balanced by an opposing source of potential difference.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (marking A wrong because of the missing minus sign, or marking C wrong since it is not in NCERT), 0 (accepting B because E depends on pressure for gas electrodes).\nWorking: A correct (maximum non-expansion/electrical work = −ΔrG, taken as equal in magnitude); B incorrect (E° refers to fixed standard pressure, so it does not depend on pressure); C correct (from ΔrG° = −nFE° and ∂ΔG/∂T = −ΔS); D correct (reversible operation). One incorrect statement.\nKey idea: E depends on pressure through the Nernst term, but E° is fixed at standard pressure.","traps":["E vs E° confused: E° is defined at 1 bar; pressure enters only through Q in the Nernst equation.","Sign in statement A judged wrong: The statement concerns magnitude of maximum electrical work; accepted as correct."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: oxidation-number method","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"KMnO₄ is titrated with ferrous ammonium sulphate hexahydrate in presence of dilute H₂SO₄. Number of water molecules produced for 2 molecules of KMnO₄ is ________.","type":"num","answer":"68","range":[68.0,68.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8: only the ionic-equation water (4 × 2). 4: water for one MnO₄⁻. 60: only crystal water counted. 34: per one KMnO₄.\nWorking: 2KMnO₄ + 10FeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄·6H₂O + 8H₂SO₄ → K₂SO₄ + 2MnSO₄ + 5Fe₂(SO₄)₃ + 10(NH₄)₂SO₄ + 68H₂O. Redox water = 8 (4 per MnO₄⁻) plus 60 water of crystallisation released from 10 Mohr's salt units.\nKey idea: Write the full molecular equation: 10 Mohr's salt for 2 KMnO₄ → 60 + 8 = 68 H₂O.","traps":["Water of crystallisation ignored: Note 'hexahydrate' in the stem and add 6 per Fe.","Per-molecule scaling slip: Scale the whole equation to 2 MnO₄⁻."],"topics":["Balancing: oxidation-number method","Redox titrations"]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A metal surface of 100 cm² area has to be coated with nickel layer of thickness 0.001 mm. A current of 2 A was passed through a solution of Ni(NO₃)₂ for 'x' seconds to coat the desired layer. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) (ρ_Ni (density of Nickel) is 10 g mL⁻¹, Molar mass of Nickel is 60 g mol⁻¹, F = 96500 C mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"161","range":[161.0,161.0],"explain":"Common slips: 80 or 80.4: forgets n = 2 for Ni²⁺. 1608: thickness taken as 0.001 cm (mm not converted to cm). 16: mm → cm conversion slip the other way.\nWorking: V = 100 cm² × 10⁻⁴ cm = 0.01 cm³; m = 0.1 g; n(Ni) = 1/600 mol; Q = 2 × 96500/600 = 321.7 C; t = 321.7/2 = 160.8 s ≈ 161.\nKey idea: Mass = area × thickness × density; then Q = n_e F.","traps":["Electrons per Ni²⁺ forgotten: Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni.","Thickness unit conversion: Convert to cm before multiplying by cm²."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"At what pH, given half cell MnO₄⁻(0.1 M) | Mn²⁺(0.001 M) will have electrode potential of 1.282 V? ______ (Nearest Integer)\nGiven E°(MnO₄⁻|Mn²⁺) = 1.54 V, 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Ignoring H⁺ gives no pH solution. Using n = 1 or [H⁺]¹ gives absurd pH. Inverting the quotient gives negative pH.\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O; E = E° − (0.059/5) log([Mn²⁺]/([MnO₄⁻][H⁺]⁸)) with [Mn²⁺] = 10⁻³ M, [MnO₄⁻] = 10⁻¹ M and −log[H⁺] = pH.\nKey idea: 1.282 = 1.54 − (0.059/5)[log(10⁻³/10⁻¹) + 8pH] → 8pH − 2 = 21.86 → pH ≈ 2.98 ≈ 3.","traps":["H⁺ exponent dropped: Write the balanced half-reaction first.","Quotient inverted for reduction: Q = [Mn²⁺]/([MnO₄⁻][H⁺]⁸)."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Sum of oxidation states of bromine in bromic acid and perbromic acid is ______.","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking bromic as HBrO₂ (+3) or perbromic as HBrO₃ gives 10 or 8.\nWorking: bromic acid HBrO₃: +1 + x − 6 = 0 → Br = +5; perbromic acid HBrO₄: +1 + x − 8 = 0 → Br = +7; sum = 12.\nKey idea: HBrO₃: +5; HBrO₄: +7; sum 12.","traps":["-ic / per-ic names mixed up: -ous = +3, -ic = +5, per-ic = +7 for Cl/Br."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Equivalent concept & n-factor","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"When Fe₀.₉₃O is heated in presence of oxygen, it converts to Fe₂O₃. The number of correct statement/s from the following is ________\nA. The equivalent weight of Fe₀.₉₃O is Molecular weight / 0.79\nB. The number of moles of Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ in 1 mole of Fe₀.₉₃O is 0.79 and 0.14 respectively\nC. Fe₀.₉₃O is metal deficient with lattice comprising of cubic closed packed arrangement of O²⁻ ions\nD. The % composition of Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ in Fe₀.₉₃O is 85% and 15% respectively","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (A rejected by using n-factor 0.93 or 1, or C rejected as 'metal excess'), 2 (D % taken per total O, not per Fe).\nWorking: x + y = 0.93 and 2x + 3y = 2 give Fe²⁺ = 0.79, Fe³⁺ = 0.14 (B). On oxidation only Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺, n-factor 0.79 (A). 0.79/0.93 = 85%, 0.14/0.93 = 15% (D). FeO has rock-salt structure (ccp O²⁻) and Fe₀.₉₃O is metal deficient (C). All 4 correct.\nKey idea: Only Fe²⁺ changes on oxidation, so n-factor = moles of Fe²⁺ = 0.79.","traps":["n-factor taken as total Fe: Fe³⁺ is already oxidised; count only Fe²⁺ electrons.","Metal deficient vs metal excess: Fe < 1 per O means vacancies at metal sites = metal deficient."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"At 298 K, a 1 litre solution containing 10 mmol of Cr₂O₇²⁻ and 100 mmol of Cr³⁺ shows a pH of 3.0.\nGiven : Cr₂O₇²⁻ → Cr³⁺ ; E° = 1.330 V\nand 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V\nThe potential for the half cell reaction is x × 10⁻³ V. The value of x is __________","type":"num","answer":"917","range":[917.0,917.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 504 (using n = 3: 1.330 − 0.826), ≈ 907 (forgetting the square on [Cr³⁺]), 1330 (ignoring H⁺, so log Q = 0), ≈ 1743 (sign of the log term reversed).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O; E = 1.330 − (0.059/6) log{(0.1)² / [(0.01)(10⁻³)¹⁴]} = 1.330 − (0.059/6)(42) = 1.330 − 0.413 = 0.917 V.\nKey idea: log Q = log(10⁻²) − log(10⁻²·10⁻⁴²) = 42.","traps":["Electrons and H⁺ count: Balance first: 6e⁻, 14H⁺, 2Cr³⁺.","mmol in 1 L: 10 mmol/L = 0.01 M; 100 mmol/L = 0.1 M."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Choose the correct representation of conductometric titration of benzoic acid vs sodium hydroxide. (Each option: graph of Conductance (y) vs V_NaOH (x).)","options":["V-shape: conductance falls steeply to a minimum, then rises steeply","Conductance flat (constant) then rises linearly after equivalence","Small initial dip, then gradual linear rise, then steeper rise after equivalence","Inverted V: conductance rises to a maximum, then falls"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): strong acid–strong base curve (H⁺ replaced by Na⁺). (B): ignores rise from salt formation before end point. (D): no physical basis.\nWorking: benzoic acid is weak, so the initial conductance is low; the first NaOH gives a slight dip, then conductance rises gradually as fully ionised sodium benzoate replaces the weakly ionised acid; after the equivalence point excess, highly mobile OH⁻ makes it rise steeply – option (C).\nKey idea: Before end point, weakly ionised acid is replaced by fully ionised sodium benzoate → slow rise (slight initial dip from common-ion suppression); after end point free OH⁻ gives steep rise.","traps":["Strong vs weak acid curve: Weak acid has low initial conductance; it rises before end point.","Initial dip: Small dip then rise is characteristic of weak acid."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"In which of the following reactions the hydrogen peroxide acts as a reducing agent?","options":["Mn²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Mn⁴⁺ + 2OH⁻","HOCl + H₂O₂ → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ + O₂","PbS + 4H₂O₂ → PbSO₄ + 4H₂O","2Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2OH⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D): H₂O₂ oxidises Mn²⁺, S²⁻ and Fe²⁺ (O → −2), i.e. acts as oxidant.\nWorking: only in (B) is O of H₂O₂ oxidised (−1 → 0, O₂ evolved); elsewhere O goes −1 → −2.\nKey idea: H₂O₂ acts as reductant whenever O₂ is evolved.","traps":["O₂ evolution marker: Look for O₂ as product of H₂O₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Consider the cell Pt(s)|H₂(g) (1 atm)|H⁺(aq, [H⁺] = 1)||Fe³⁺(aq), Fe²⁺(aq)|Pt(s). Given E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 0.771 V and E°(H⁺/½H₂) = 0 V, T = 298 K. If the potential of the cell is 0.712 V, the ratio of concentration of Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ is ________ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 0 or 0.1 if Q is inverted (Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺); answers with n = 2 give ratio ≈ 100 (3.16 if square root).\nWorking: 0.712 = 0.771 − 0.059 log([Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺]) → log ratio = 1.0 → ratio = 10.\nKey idea: Cell reaction: ½H₂ + Fe³⁺ → H⁺ + Fe²⁺, n = 1, Q = [Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺] (H⁺ and H₂ at unit activity).","traps":["Q inverted: Q = products/reactants of the reduction Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺.","n taken as 2: Balance with ½H₂: one electron per Fe³⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Pt(s) | H₂(g)(1 bar) | H⁺(aq)(1 M) || M³⁺(aq), M⁺(aq) | Pt(s). The E_cell for the given cell is 0.1115 V at 298 K when [M⁺(aq)]/[M³⁺(aq)] = 10ᵃ. The value of a is ______. Given: E°(M³⁺/M⁺) = 0.2 V; 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.5 (n = 1 used); –3 (quotient inverted); 6 (n = 4 used).\nWorking: E = 0.2 – (0.059/2)·a = 0.1115 → a = 0.0885/0.0295 = 3.\nKey idea: Overall: H₂ + M³⁺ → 2H⁺ + M⁺, n = 2, Q = [M⁺]/[M³⁺] with [H⁺] = 1 and p(H₂) = 1.","traps":["Electrons transferred: n = difference in oxidation states = 2.","Quotient orientation: Write the cell reaction and put products in the numerator."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"Which one among the following metals is the weakest reducing agent?","options":["K","Na","Li","Rb"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Li chosen because it has the highest ionisation enthalpy; (A)/(D) chosen by assuming reducing power falls down the group.\nWorking: Li –3.05, K –2.93, Rb ≈ –2.98, Na –2.71 V; Na least negative.\nKey idea: Most negative E° = strongest reductant; Li (hydration) is strongest, Na is weakest.","traps":["Li as strongest reducing agent: Use E° (includes hydration enthalpy), not IE alone.","Na anomaly: Na has the least negative E° among alkali metals."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Following figure shows dependence of molar conductance of two electrolytes on concentration. Λ°m is the limiting molar conductivity.\nThe number of incorrect statement(s) from the following is ___________\n(A) Λ°m for electrolyte A is obtained by extrapolation\n(B) For electrolyte B, Λm vs √c graph is a straight line with intercept equal to Λ°m\n(C) At infinite dilution, the value of degree of dissociation approaches zero for electrolyte B.\n(D) Λ°m for any electrolyte A and B can be calculated using λ° for individual ions","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (accepting extrapolation for weak electrolyte A); 3 (treating Kohlrausch law as only for strong electrolytes).\nWorking: curve A (steep) is the weak electrolyte – its Λ°m cannot be found by extrapolation, so (A) is incorrect. (B) is correct for the strong electrolyte B. (C) is incorrect – at infinite dilution α approaches 1, not zero. (D) is correct – Kohlrausch's law applies to both. Incorrect statements = 2.\nKey idea: Steep curve = weak electrolyte; Kohlrausch's law works for both.","traps":["Which curve is the weak electrolyte: Steep rise at low c = weak electrolyte.","Counting incorrect statements: Mark each statement T/F, then count F."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan29S1C16-p.webp"],"topics":["Conductivity vs concentration","Kohlrausch's law"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The equilibrium constant for the reaction Zn(s) + Sn²⁺(aq) ⇌ Zn²⁺(aq) + Sn(s) is 1 × 10²⁰ at 298 K. The magnitude of standard electrode potential of Sn/Sn²⁺ if E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V is __________ × 10⁻² V. (Nearest integer)\nGiven : 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V","type":"num","answer":"17","range":[17.0,17.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 42 (taking n = 1: 1.18 − 0.76); 135 (adding instead of subtracting: 0.59 + 0.76); 59 (reporting E°cell).\nWorking: E°cell = (0.059/2) log K = 0.0295 × 20 = 0.59 V; E°cell = E°(Sn²⁺/Sn) − (−0.76) → E°(Sn²⁺/Sn) = 0.59 − 0.76 = −0.17 V; magnitude = 17 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Zn is anode; E°cell = E°cathode(Sn) − E°anode(Zn).","traps":["n = 2 electrons: Read n from Zn → Zn²⁺.","Sign while solving: Substitute −0.76 with sign."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the cell Pt(s) | H₂(g, 1 atm) | H⁺(aq, 1 M) || Fe³⁺(aq), Fe²⁺(aq) | Pt(s). When the potential of the cell is 0.712 V at 298 K, the ratio [Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺] is ______. (Nearest integer) Given: Fe³⁺ + e⁻ = Fe²⁺, E°(Fe³⁺, Fe²⁺ | Pt) = 0.771; 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (taking [Fe³⁺]/[Fe²⁺] = 0.104), 93 (n = 2 without squaring the ratio: log ratio = 2 × 0.059/0.06 ≈ 1.97), 9 (truncating 9.6).\nWorking: 0.712 = 0.771 − 0.06 log([Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺]) → log ratio = 0.983 → ratio ≈ 9.6 → 10.\nKey idea: With n = 1 (or n = 2 and squared ratio) log([Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺]) = 0.059/0.06 ≈ 0.98.","traps":["Inverted concentration ratio: Write the cell reaction H₂ + 2Fe³⁺ → 2H⁺ + 2Fe²⁺ and form Q from it.","n = 2 without squaring: If n = 2, Q has squared Fe terms, which cancels the 2."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The number of electrons involved in the reduction of permanganate to manganese dioxide in acidic medium is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (reduction to Mn²⁺ in acid), 1 (to MnO₄²⁻), 4.\nWorking: Mn +7 → +4, so 3 electrons per MnO₄⁻.\nKey idea: Electrons = change in oxidation number, +7 → +4 = 3, regardless of medium.","traps":["Acidic medium cue: The product is stated (MnO₂); use +7 → +4."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The electrode potential of the following half cell at 298 K: X | X²⁺(0.001 M) || Y²⁺(0.01 M) | Y is ____ × 10⁻² V (Nearest integer). Given: E°(X²⁺|X) = −2.36 V; E°(Y²⁺|Y) = +0.36 V; 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V","type":"num","answer":"275","range":[275.0,275.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 269 (Q inverted), 278 (n = 1), 272 (ignoring concentration term), 200 (using 0.36−2.36 = −2.00 magnitude).\nWorking: E°cell = 0.36 − (−2.36) = 2.72 V; E = 2.72 − (0.06/2) log(0.001/0.01) = 2.72 + 0.03 = 2.75 V. The question says 'half cell', but the value asked for is the EMF of the whole cell.\nKey idea: Q = [X²⁺]/[Y²⁺] = 0.1 so the log term adds 0.03 V.","traps":["Q = [anode ion]/[cathode ion]: Write X + Y²⁺ → X²⁺ + Y; Q = [X²⁺]/[Y²⁺].","n = 2 electrons: Divide 0.06 by 2."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Products of electrolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which one of the following statements is correct for electrolysis of brine solution?","options":["O₂ is formed at cathode","H₂ is formed at anode","Cl₂ is formed at cathode","OH⁻ is formed at cathode"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) O₂ is an oxidation product (anode) and in brine Cl₂ is preferred by overpotential. (B) H₂ comes from reduction at the cathode. (C) Cl₂ forms at the anode.\nWorking: at the cathode water is reduced (2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻); at the anode Cl⁻ is oxidised to Cl₂.\nKey idea: Cathode: 2H₂O + 2e⁻ → H₂ + 2OH⁻; so OH⁻ is generated at the cathode.","traps":["Anode/cathode swapped: Reduction at cathode (H₂, OH⁻); oxidation at anode (Cl₂).","OH⁻ seen as an anode species: Here OH⁻ is produced at the cathode as H₂O is reduced."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The logarithm of equilibrium constant for the reaction Pd²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ PdCl₄²⁻ is ________ (Nearest integer) Given : 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V; Pd²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Pd(s), E° = 0.83 V; PdCl₄²⁻(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Pd(s) + 4Cl⁻(aq), E° = 0.65 V","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 49 (adding 0.83 + 0.65 = 1.48 V); −6 (subtracting the wrong way); 3 (using n = 1); 28 or 22 (using only one couple).\nWorking: E° = 0.83 − 0.65 = 0.18 V, n = 2; log K = nE°/0.06 = 2 × 0.18/0.06 = 6\nKey idea: Subtract the potentials: reaction = (Pd²⁺/Pd) − (PdCl₄²⁻/Pd); n = 2.","traps":["Adding instead of subtracting E°: Reverse the second half-reaction: E°overall = 0.83 − 0.65.","Dropping n = 2: Electrons cancel but each half-reaction transfers 2."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"H₂O₂ acts as a reducing agent in :","options":["Mn²⁺ + 2H₂O₂ → MnO₂ + 2H₂O","2Fe²⁺ + 2H⁺ + H₂O₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2H₂O","Na₂S + 4H₂O₂ → Na₂SO₄ + 4H₂O","2NaOCl + H₂O₂ → 2NaCl + H₂O + O₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C): H₂O₂ oxidises Mn²⁺, Fe²⁺ and S²⁻ and is itself reduced to H₂O, so it acts as oxidising agent.\nWorking: only in D does O of H₂O₂ go from −1 to 0 (O₂); in A–C it goes to −2 in H₂O, i.e. H₂O₂ is reduced\nKey idea: H₂O₂ is a reducing agent when O₂ is evolved.","traps":["Any reaction with H₂O₂ seen as reduction: Look for O₂ among products: that marks H₂O₂ as reductant."],"topics":["Oxidation & reduction","Oxidation number"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The resistivity of a 0.8 M solution of an electrolyte is 5 × 10⁻³ Ω cm. Its molar conductivity is ________ × 10⁴ Ω⁻¹ cm² mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 0 – dropping the factor 1000 gives 250 S cm² mol⁻¹ = 0.025 × 10⁴, and using ρ in place of κ gives 1000 × 5 × 10⁻³/0.8 = 6.25 S cm² mol⁻¹; both round to 0 in units of 10⁴.\nWorking: κ = 1/ρ = 200 S cm⁻¹; Λm = 1000κ/M = 1000 × 200/0.8 = 2.5 × 10⁵ = 25 × 10⁴ S cm² mol⁻¹\nKey idea: κ = 1/ρ; then Λm = 1000κ/M in S cm² mol⁻¹.","traps":["Missing factor 1000: Use Λm = κ × 1000/M when M is in mol L⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A sample of a metal oxide has formula M₀.₈₃O₁.₀₀. The metal M can exist in two oxidation states +2 and +3. In the sample of M₀.₈₃O₁.₀₀, the percentage of metal ions existing in +2 oxidation state is ________ %. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"59","range":[59.0,59.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 41 (percentage of +3 instead), 49 (reporting x×100 without dividing by 0.83), 34 (reporting the +3 fraction y × 100 without dividing by 0.83).\nWorking: x + y = 0.83; 2x + 3y = 2 → y = 0.34, x = 0.49; 0.49/0.83 = 59.0%\nKey idea: Charge neutrality: 2x + 3(0.83 − x) = 2.","traps":["Reporting +3 fraction: Re-read which oxidation state is asked.","Dividing by total metal: Percentage = x/0.83 × 100."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The cell potential for Zn|Zn²⁺(aq)||Snˣ⁺|Sn is 0.801 V at 298 K. The reaction quotient for the above reaction is 10⁻². The number of electrons involved in the given electrochemical cell reaction is ____. (Given: E°(Zn²⁺|Zn) = −0.763 V, E°(Snˣ⁺|Sn) = +0.008 V and 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (assuming Zn²⁺/Sn²⁺ without solving); 1; 8 (sign of log Q mishandled).\nWorking: E°cell = 0.008 − (−0.763) = 0.771 V; 0.801 = 0.771 − (0.06/n) log 10⁻² = 0.771 + 0.12/n → n = 4. Assuming Sn²⁺ (n = 2) would give 0.831 V, which does not match the data.\nKey idea: log Q = −2 makes E > E°: 0.03 = 0.12/n.","traps":["Assuming n = 2: Use the data: 0.030 V = 0.12/n gives n = 4 (Sn⁴⁺).","Sign of log Q: Write E − E° = −(0.06/n)(−2)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The molar conductivity of a conductivity cell filled with 10 moles of 20 mL NaCl solution is Λm1 and that of 20 moles in another identical cell having 80 mL NaCl solution is Λm2. The conductivities exhibited by these two cells are same. The relationship between Λm2 and Λm1 is","options":["Λm2 = 2Λm1","Λm2 = Λm1/2","Λm2 = Λm1","Λm2 = 4Λm1"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) inverts the ratio (Λm ∝ c); (C) assumes equal κ means equal Λm; (D) uses volume ratio 4 alone, ignoring the doubled moles.\nWorking: Λm = κ/c; c1 = 10/20 = 0.5 mol mL⁻¹, c2 = 20/80 = 0.25 mol mL⁻¹; equal κ so Λm2/Λm1 = c1/c2 = 2.\nKey idea: Only the concentration ratio matters: c1/c2 = 0.5/0.25 = 2.","traps":["Volume ratio used alone: Concentration = moles/volume; both change.","Λm taken proportional to c: At fixed κ, Λm = κ/c falls as c rises."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which of the given reactions is not an example of disproportionation reaction?","options":["2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂","2NO₂ + H₂O → HNO₃ + HNO₂","MnO₄⁻ + 4H⁺ + 3e⁻ → MnO₂ + 2H₂O","3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) each have one element going both up and down; a student who sees MnO₂ in both (C) and (D) may pick (D).\nWorking: (C) is only a reduction half-reaction (Mn +7 → +4). (A) O −1 → −2 and 0; (B) N +4 → +5 and +3; (D) Mn +6 → +7 and +4 – all disproportionations.\nKey idea: A half-reaction with electrons on one side cannot be a disproportionation.","traps":["MnO₂ in both (C) and (D): Check whether Mn also goes up: in (D) MnO₄⁻ forms.","\"Not\" in stem: Underline \"not\"."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The dark purple colour of KMnO₄ disappears in the titration with oxalic acid in acidic medium. The overall change in the oxidation number of manganese in the reaction is:","options":["5","1","7","2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 7 is the initial oxidation number, not the change; (D) 2 is the final oxidation number; (B) 1 is the change to manganate (+6), not acidic.\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ (Mn +7) → Mn²⁺ (+2) in acid; change = 5.\nKey idea: Acid medium: MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺, n = 5.","traps":["Final value given instead of change: Change = 7 − 2.","Medium-dependent product: Acid → Mn²⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The amount of charge in F (Faraday) required to obtain one mole of iron from Fe₃O₄ is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (per mole Fe₃O₄), 2 (taking Fe²⁺ only), 3 from Fe³⁺ only (right number, wrong reason).\nWorking: Fe₃O₄ → 3Fe needs 8 e⁻ (average Fe oxidation state +8/3); per mole Fe = 8/3 = 2.67 F → 3.\nKey idea: Fe₃O₄ = FeO·Fe₂O₃: 8 electrons for 3 Fe.","traps":["Per formula unit vs per Fe: Divide by 3 Fe atoms.","Fe taken as +2 or +3: Average oxidation state +8/3."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"20 mL of 0.02 M hypo solution is used for the titration of 10 mL of copper sulphate solution, in the presence of excess of KI using starch as an indicator. The molarity of Cu²⁺ is found to be _____ × 10⁻² M [nearest integer]\nGiven : 2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → Cu₂I₂ + I₂\nI₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking Cu²⁺ : S₂O₃²⁻ as 1:2 gives 2; as 2:1 gives 8.\nWorking: S₂O₃²⁻ = 20 × 0.02 = 0.4 mmol; 2Cu²⁺ ≡ I₂ ≡ 2S₂O₃²⁻ so Cu²⁺ = 0.4 mmol in 10 mL = 0.04 M = 4 × 10⁻² M.\nKey idea: Net 1 Cu²⁺ ≡ 1 S₂O₃²⁻.","traps":["Mole ratio through I₂: Write the chain 2Cu²⁺ → I₂ → 2S₂O₃²⁻."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"20 mL of 0.02 M K₂Cr₂O₇ solution is used for the titration of 10 mL of Fe²⁺ solution in the acidic medium. The molarity of Fe²⁺ solution is ______ × 10⁻² M. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (ratio 1:1), 8 (ratio 1:2 or n = 2), 12 (half the volume ratio).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6Fe²⁺ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O; mmol Fe²⁺ = 6 × 20 × 0.02 = 2.4; M = 2.4/10 = 0.24 M = 24 × 10⁻² M.\nKey idea: Each Cr₂O₇²⁻ takes 6 electrons; each Fe²⁺ gives 1.","traps":["Missing factor 6: Use n-factor 6 for Cr₂O₇²⁻ and 1 for Fe²⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: For KI, molar conductivity increases steeply with dilution. Statement II: For carbonic acid, molar conductivity increases slowly with dilution.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts both swapped statements; (C)/(D) accept one of the swapped statements.\nWorking: KI is a strong electrolyte — Λm rises only slowly (Λm = Λ°m − A√c); carbonic acid is a weak electrolyte — Λm rises steeply at high dilution as ionisation increases. Both statements are the reverse, so both false.\nKey idea: Strong electrolytes: slow, nearly linear rise vs √c; weak electrolytes: steep rise on dilution.","traps":["Strong vs weak behaviour swapped: Identify KI as strong and H₂CO₃ as weak before judging."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Batteries","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Cd(s) + 2Ni(OH)₃(s) → CdO(s) + 2Ni(OH)₂(s) + H₂O(l); (B) Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) → ZnO(s) + Hg(l); (C) 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 2H₂SO₄(aq); (D) 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l). List-II: (I) Primary battery; (II) Discharging of secondary battery; (III) Fuel cell; (IV) Charging of secondary battery. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) – (I), (B) – (II), (C) – (III), (D) – (IV)","(A) – (IV), (B) – (I), (C) – (II), (D) – (III)","(A) – (II), (B) – (I), (C) – (IV), (D) – (III)","(A) – (II), (B) – (I), (C) – (III), (D) – (IV)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) keeps A–II and B–I but swaps the last two pairs (lead equation as fuel cell, H₂–O₂ as charging); (A) simply pairs in list order; (B) takes the Ni–Cd equation as charging and the lead equation as discharging.\nWorking: Ni–Cd discharge (A–II); mercury cell is primary (B–I); PbSO₄ → Pb + PbO₂ is the reverse of lead-storage discharge, i.e. charging (C–IV); H₂–O₂ is the fuel cell (D–III).\nKey idea: Pb + PbO₂ + H₂SO₄ on the product side means the cell is being recharged.","traps":["Direction of lead-storage reaction: Check which side holds PbSO₄: PbSO₄ as reactant = charging.","Ni–Cd cell less familiar: Metal Cd being oxidised means the cell is delivering current (discharge)."],"topics":["Batteries","Fuel cells"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"On reaction with stronger oxidizing agent like KIO₄, hydrogen peroxide oxidizes with the evolution of O₂. The oxidation number of I in KIO₄ changes to ____.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 7 (no change), 1 or −1 (I⁻ or I₂ assumed), 0.\nWorking: KIO₄ + H₂O₂ → KIO₃ + H₂O + O₂; I goes from +7 to +5 (H₂O₂ acts as a reducing agent).\nKey idea: H₂O₂ reduces periodate to iodate (+5).","traps":["Product of periodate reduction: Recall IO₄⁻ → IO₃⁻ with H₂O₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Resistance of a conductivity cell (cell constant 129 m⁻¹) filled with 74.5 ppm solution of KCl is 100 Ω (labelled as solution 1). When the same cell is filled with KCl solution of 149 ppm, the resistance is 50 Ω (labelled as solution 2). The ratio of molar conductivity of solution 1 and solution 2 i.e. Λ₁/Λ₂ = x × 10⁻³. The value of x is _____. (Nearest integer) Given, molar mass of KCl is 74.5 g mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"1000","range":[1000.0,1000.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (forgetting ×10⁻³ scaling); 500 or 2000 (using only κ ratio or only c ratio); 4000 (ratio inverted with both).\nWorking: κ₁ = 129/100 = 1.29 S m⁻¹, c₁ = 74.5 mg L⁻¹ = 1×10⁻³ M; κ₂ = 2.58 S m⁻¹, c₂ = 2×10⁻³ M. Λ₁/Λ₂ = (1.29/1)/(2.58/2) = 1 = 1000 × 10⁻³.\nKey idea: Doubling c while halving R doubles κ, so Λm is unchanged → ratio 1.","traps":["Only κ ratio used: Λm = κ/c; divide by each concentration.","Answer scale: Match the requested power of ten."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For a cell, Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(0.001 M) || Ag⁺(0.01 M) | Ag(s), the cell potential is found to be 0.43 V at 298 K. The magnitude of standard electrode potential for Cu²⁺/Cu is ___ × 10⁻² V. [Given: E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = 0.80 V and 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V]","type":"num","answer":"34","range":[34.0,34.0],"explain":"Common slips: 31 or 37 (Q without the square on [Ag⁺], or sign of log term reversed); 46 (reporting E°cell).\nWorking: Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag; E = E° − (0.06/2) log(10⁻³/10⁻⁴) = E° − 0.03 → E° = 0.46 V; E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.80 − 0.46 = 0.34 V = 34 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Q = 10⁻³/(10⁻²)² = 10.","traps":["[Ag⁺] not squared: Write the balanced cell reaction first.","E°cell reported: E°cell = E°(Ag) − E°(Cu)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The cell potential for the following cell Pt | H₂(g) | H⁺(aq) || Cu²⁺(0.01 M) | Cu(s) is 0.576 V at 298 K. The pH of the solution is ____. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 (forgetting the square on [H⁺]); 4 or 6 (sign error in Q or dropping the 0.01 M Cu²⁺); pH obtained with n = 1.\nWorking: H₂ + Cu²⁺ → 2H⁺ + Cu, E° = 0.34 V (standard Cu²⁺/Cu value, not given in the question). 0.576 = 0.34 − (0.059/2) log([H⁺]²/0.01) → log([H⁺]²/0.01) = −8 → [H⁺]² = 10⁻¹⁰ → pH = 5 (taking p(H₂) = 1 bar).\nKey idea: Q = [H⁺]²/[Cu²⁺]; with n = 2 the squares cancel neatly to log[H⁺] = −5.","traps":["E° not supplied: Recall E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V.","Square on [H⁺] dropped: Write the balanced cell reaction before Q."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The resistance of conductivity cell containing 0.01 M KCl solution at 298 K is 1750 Ω. If the conductivity of 0.01 M KCl solution at 298 K is 0.152 × 10⁻³ S cm⁻¹, then the cell constant of the conductivity cell is ____ × 10⁻³ cm⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"266","range":[266.0,266.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 87 (dividing κ by R or R by κ); 0.266 (unit 10⁻³ ignored).\nWorking: G* = κ × R = 0.152 × 10⁻³ S cm⁻¹ × 1750 Ω = 0.266 cm⁻¹ = 266 × 10⁻³ cm⁻¹.\nKey idea: G* = l/A = κR.","traps":["κ divided by R: κ = G*/R, so G* = κR."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In a cell, the following reactions take place: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻, E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 0.77 V; 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻, E°(I₂/I⁻) = 0.54 V. The standard electrode potential for the spontaneous reaction in the cell is x × 10⁻² V at 298 K. The value of x is ____. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"23","range":[23.0,23.0],"explain":"Common slips: −23 – non-spontaneous direction; 131 – adds potentials; −31 – doubles the I₂/I⁻ potential (0.77 − 2 × 0.54 = −0.31).\nWorking: spontaneous reaction is 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ (higher reduction potential is the cathode). E°cell = 0.77 − 0.54 = 0.23 V = 23 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Higher E°red (Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) is reduced; E° = 0.23 V.","traps":["Multiplying E° by coefficients: E° is intensive; never scale it.","Direction from written half-reactions: Pick the direction with positive E°cell."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of reduction potentials of the following pairs is\nA. Cl₂/Cl⁻ B. I₂/I⁻ C. Ag⁺/Ag D. Na⁺/Na E. Li⁺/Li\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["A > C > B > D > E","A > B > C > D > E","A > C > B > E > D","A > B > C > E > D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) put I₂/I⁻ above Ag⁺/Ag (halogens assumed always higher); (C)/(D) put Li above Na, following reactivity-with-water intuition or size trend.\nWorking: E° (V): Cl₂/Cl⁻ +1.36 > Ag⁺/Ag +0.80 > I₂/I⁻ +0.54 > Na⁺/Na −2.71 > Li⁺/Li −3.05.\nKey idea: Li⁺/Li has the most negative E° (high hydration enthalpy); Ag⁺/Ag (+0.80) beats I₂/I⁻ (+0.54).","traps":["Li vs Na reduction potential: Li has the most negative E° because of its large hydration enthalpy.","Ag⁺/Ag vs I₂/I⁻: Iodine is a weak oxidant: +0.54 V < +0.80 V."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A solution of Fe₂(SO₄)₃ is electrolyzed for 'x' min with a current of 1.5 A to deposit 0.3482 g of Fe. The value of x is ________. [nearest integer]\nGiven: 1 F = 96500 C mol⁻¹; Atomic mass of Fe = 56 g mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"20","range":[20.0,20.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 13 (using n = 2 as for Fe²⁺), 1200 (answer in seconds), 40 (n = 3 counted per Fe₂ unit, i.e. 6).\nWorking: Fe³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Fe; Q = (0.3482/56) × 3 × 96500 = 1800 C; t = 1800/1.5 = 1200 s = 20 min.\nKey idea: Charge per Fe atom comes from Fe³⁺ in Fe₂(SO₄)₃.","traps":["n-factor for Fe in Fe₂(SO₄)₃: Fe is +3 in Fe₂(SO₄)₃; each Fe needs 3 e⁻.","Seconds vs minutes: Divide seconds by 60."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrode potential & SHE","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The (∂E/∂T)ₚ of different types of half cells are as follows: A: 1 × 10⁻⁴; B: 2 × 10⁻⁴; C: 0.1 × 10⁻⁴; D: 0.2 × 10⁻⁴ (Where E is the electromotive force). Which of the above half cells would be preferred to be used as reference electrode ?","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) largest coefficient – picks the most \"responsive\" electrode; (A)/(D) intermediate values chosen by misreading 0.1 × 10⁻⁴ vs 1 × 10⁻⁴.\nWorking: a reference electrode should have a potential that hardly changes with temperature, i.e. the smallest temperature coefficient; C (0.1 × 10⁻⁴) is smallest = (C).\nKey idea: Reference = constant potential, so pick the half-cell whose E changes least with T.","traps":["Largest value picked: Reference must not respond to conditions.","Scientific notation misread: Convert all to the same power of ten."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the following is an example of disproportionation reaction?","options":["3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O","MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → MnO₂ + 2H₂O","10I⁻ + 2MnO₄⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 5I₂","8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O → 8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – a half-reaction showing only reduction of Mn(+6); (C) – ordinary redox (I⁻ oxidised, Mn reduced); (D) – S oxidised, Mn reduced.\nWorking: in (A) Mn(+6) goes to both +7 (MnO₄⁻) and +4 (MnO₂) – the same element in one oxidation state is both oxidised and reduced. (B) is a half-reaction (reduction only); (C) and (D) are ordinary redox reactions between two species.\nKey idea: Disproportionation: one species, one element, goes both up and down.","traps":["Half-reaction mistaken for disproportionation: A half-reaction with free electrons shows only one change; disproportionation needs two products with higher and lower oxidation states."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Cu(s) + Sn²⁺ (0.001 M) → Cu²⁺ (0.01 M) + Sn(s). The Gibbs free energy change for the above reaction at 298 K is x × 10⁻¹ kJ mol⁻¹; The value of x is ______. [nearest integer] [Given: E⊖(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.34 V; E⊖(Sn²⁺/Sn) = –0.14 V; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"983","range":[983.0,983.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 926 (E⊖ only, 92.6 kJ); 869 (Q inverted or sign of the log term reversed, E = –0.4505 V); –983 (sign of ΔG lost for a non-spontaneous cell).\nWorking: E⊖cell = –0.14 – 0.34 = –0.48 V; E = –0.48 – (0.0591/2) log(0.01/0.001) = –0.5096 V; ΔG = –nFE = –2 × 96500 × (–0.5096) = 98.3 kJ mol⁻¹ = 983 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Q = [Cu²⁺]/[Sn²⁺] = 10; E falls further below E⊖.","traps":["Ignoring the concentration term: Apply Nernst: E = E⊖ – (0.0591/n) log Q.","Q inverted: Q = [products]/[reactants] = [Cu²⁺]/[Sn²⁺] = 10."],"topics":["EMF & Gibbs energy","Nernst equation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kohlrausch's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The limiting molar conductivities of NaI, NaNO₃ and AgNO₃ are 12.7, 12.0 and 13.3 mS m² mol⁻¹, respectively (all at 25°C). The limiting molar conductivity of AgI at this temperature is ______ mS m² mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: 12 or 11 – wrong combination (e.g. NaI + NaNO₃ − AgNO₃ = 11.4); 38 – adds all three.\nWorking: Λ°(AgI) = Λ°(AgNO₃) + Λ°(NaI) − Λ°(NaNO₃) = 13.3 + 12.7 − 12.0 = 14.0 mS m² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Keep Ag⁺ and I⁻, cancel Na⁺ and NO₃⁻.","traps":["Wrong salt subtracted: Write ions: Ag⁺NO₃⁻ + Na⁺I⁻ − Na⁺NO₃⁻."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"For the reaction taking place in the cell: Pt(s) | H₂(g) | H⁺(aq) || Ag⁺(aq) | Ag(s), E°_cell = +0.5332 V. The value of Δ_fG° is ________ kJ mol⁻¹. (in nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"51","range":[51.0,51.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: −103 or 103 (ΔrG° of the cell reaction as written); −51 (sign lost); 51.4 rounded up to 52.\nWorking: cell reaction H₂ + 2Ag⁺ → 2H⁺ + 2Ag, ΔrG° = −2 × 96500 × 0.5332 = −102.9 kJ. With Δ_fG°(H⁺) = Δ_fG°(H₂) = Δ_fG°(Ag) = 0, ΔrG° = −2Δ_fG°(Ag⁺), so Δ_fG°(Ag⁺) = +51.45 ≈ 51 kJ mol⁻¹. (103 kJ would be |ΔrG°| of the whole two-electron cell reaction, not a formation Gibbs energy.)\nKey idea: ΔrG° = Σ products − Σ reactants; with H⁺, H₂, Ag all zero, only Ag⁺ remains, twice, on the reactant side.","traps":["Reaction vs formation Gibbs energy: Write ΔrG° = 2Δ_fG°(H⁺) + 2Δ_fG°(Ag) − 2Δ_fG°(Ag⁺) − Δ_fG°(H₂).","Sign of Δ_fG°(Ag⁺): Ag⁺ is on the reactant side, so its Δ_fG° is positive."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A 2.0 g sample containing MnO₂ is treated with HCl liberating Cl₂. The Cl₂ gas is passed into a solution of KI and 60.0 mL of 0.1 M Na₂S₂O₃ is required to titrate the liberated iodine. The percentage of MnO₂ in the sample is ______. (Nearest integer) [Atomic masses (in u) Mn = 55; Cl = 35.5; O = 16, I = 127, Na = 23, K = 39, S = 32]","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 26 (taking I₂ = thiosulphate moles, 1:1 instead of 1:2), 7 (halving twice), 4–5 (using Mn atomic mass 55 instead of MnO₂ 87).\nWorking: thiosulphate = 6.0 mmol → I₂ = 3.0 mmol → Cl₂ = 3.0 mmol → MnO₂ = 3.0 mmol (MnO₂ + 4HCl → MnCl₂ + Cl₂ + 2H₂O) = 3.0 × 10⁻³ × 87 = 0.261 g → 13.05 % ≈ 13.\nKey idea: MnO₂ : Cl₂ : I₂ : S₂O₃²⁻ = 1 : 1 : 1 : 2.","traps":["I₂ : thiosulphate ratio: Write the titration equation before dividing.","Molar mass of MnO₂: MnO₂ = 55 + 32 = 87 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The solubility product of a sparingly soluble salt A₂X₃ is 1.1 × 10⁻²³. If specific conductance of the solution is 3 × 10⁻⁵ S m⁻¹, the limiting molar conductivity of the solution is x × 10⁻³ S m² mol⁻¹. The value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3000 or 0.003 (L vs m³ slip), wrong s from Ksp = s⁵ or 4s³.\nWorking: Ksp = (2s)²(3s)³ = 108 s⁵ = 1.1 × 10⁻²³ → s⁵ ≈ 1.02 × 10⁻²⁵ → s ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹ = 1.0 × 10⁻² mol m⁻³; Λ°m ≈ κ/s = 3 × 10⁻⁵ / 10⁻² = 3 × 10⁻³ S m² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: For AₓBᵧ, Ksp = xˣyʸ s^(x+y); convert mol L⁻¹ to mol m⁻³ (× 1000).","traps":["mol L⁻¹ vs mol m⁻³: Multiply mol L⁻¹ by 1000 before dividing.","Ksp expression for A₂X₃: Ksp = 2² × 3³ × s⁵ = 108 s⁵."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The quantity of electricity in Faraday needed to reduce 1 mol of Cr₂O₇²⁻ to Cr³⁺ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 3 (per Cr instead of per Cr₂O₇²⁻).\nWorking: Cr goes from +6 to +3 (3 e⁻ each) and there are 2 Cr per ion: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O, so 6 F.\nKey idea: n-factor of dichromate = 6.","traps":["Per Cr vs per dichromate: Multiply by 2 Cr atoms."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For the given reactions Sn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Sn and Sn⁴⁺ + 4e⁻ → Sn, the electrode potentials are E°(Sn²⁺/Sn) = −0.140 V and E°(Sn⁴⁺/Sn) = 0.010 V. The magnitude of standard electrode potential for Sn⁴⁺/Sn²⁺, i.e. E°(Sn⁴⁺/Sn²⁺), is ______ × 10⁻² V. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 15 (simple subtraction 0.010 − (−0.140)); 5 (averaging); 32 (forgetting to divide by n = 2).\nWorking: ΔG° adds, not E°: 2E°(Sn⁴⁺/Sn²⁺) = 4(0.010) − 2(−0.140) = 0.040 + 0.280 = 0.320 → E° = 0.16 V = 16 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Weight each E° by its n before adding; divide by the n of the target half-reaction.","traps":["Adding E° directly: Convert each to nE° (∝ ΔG) first.","Final division by n: Divide by 2 electrons for Sn⁴⁺ → Sn²⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the following reactions indicates the reducing ability of hydrogen peroxide in basic medium ?","options":["HOCl + H₂O₂ → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ + O₂","PbS + 4H₂O₂ → PbSO₄ + 4H₂O","2MnO₄⁻ + 3H₂O₂ → 2MnO₂ + 3O₂ + 2H₂O + 2OH⁻","Mn²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Mn⁴⁺ + 2OH⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) H₂O₂ is a reductant but medium is acidic (H₃O⁺); (B) oxidises S²⁻ to SO₄²⁻; (D) oxidises Mn²⁺ in basic medium.\nWorking: in (C) H₂O₂ is oxidised to O₂ (reductant) and OH⁻ shows basic medium; (A) is reducing but in acidic medium; (B) and (D) show H₂O₂ as oxidant.\nKey idea: Reductant H₂O₂ always releases O₂; then check H⁺ vs OH⁻ on the product side.","traps":["Medium ignored: Look for OH⁻ vs H₃O⁺ in the equation.","Basic medium but oxidant: Mn²⁺→Mn⁴⁺ means H₂O₂ is reduced, so it is the oxidant."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A dilute solution of sulphuric acid is electrolysed using a current of 0.10 A for 2 hours to produce hydrogen and oxygen gas. The total volume of gases produced at STP is ________ cm³. (Nearest integer) [Given : Faraday constant F = 96500 C mol⁻¹, at STP molar volume of an ideal gas is 22.7 L mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"127","range":[127.0,127.0],"explain":"Common slips: Only H₂: 85 cm³; only O₂: 42 cm³; using 22.4 L: 125 cm³; time in hours not converted.\nWorking: Q = 720 C = 7.46 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻; H₂ = 3.73 × 10⁻³ mol, O₂ = 1.865 × 10⁻³ mol; total 5.60 × 10⁻³ mol × 22700 cm³ = 127 cm³.\nKey idea: Moles of gas = Q/F × (1/2 + 1/4).","traps":["Only one gas counted: Add H₂ and O₂.","22.4 vs 22.7 L: Use the given 22.7 L mol⁻¹."],"topics":["Faraday's laws","Products of electrolysis"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The cell potential for the given cell at 298 K Pt | H₂(g, 1 bar) | H⁺(aq) || Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s) is 0.31 V. The pH of the acidic solution is found to be 3, whereas the concentration of Cu²⁺ is 10⁻ˣ M. The value of x is ____. (Given: E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.34 V and 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V)","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (sign of log term reversed), 4 or 8 (using [H⁺] not [H⁺]², or n = 1).\nWorking: H₂ + Cu²⁺ → 2H⁺ + Cu; E = 0.34 − (0.06/2) log([H⁺]²/[Cu²⁺]) = 0.34 − 0.03 log(10⁻⁶/10⁻ˣ) = 0.34 − 0.03(x − 6) = 0.31 → x = 7.\nKey idea: Q = [H⁺]²/[Cu²⁺]; n = 2.","traps":["[H⁺] not squared: Balance the cell reaction first.","Q inverted: Q = [products]/[reactants] for the cell reaction."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: The limiting molar conductivity of KCl (strong electrolyte) is higher compared to that of CH₃COOH (weak electrolyte). Statement II: Molar conductivity decreases with decrease in concentration of electrolyte. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts I by assuming strong electrolytes always have higher Λ; (B)/(C) accept the reversed dilution rule.\nWorking: Λ°m(KCl) = 73.5 + 76.3 ≈ 149.8 S cm² mol⁻¹, while Λ°m(CH₃COOH) = 390.5 S cm² mol⁻¹, so I is false. Molar conductivity increases as concentration decreases, so II is false.\nKey idea: Limiting molar conductivity depends on ion mobility (H⁺ is very fast), not on electrolyte strength.","traps":["Λ°m of acetic acid is larger: At infinite dilution acetic acid (390.5) exceeds KCl (~149.9) thanks to H⁺.","Molar conductivity rises on dilution: κ falls on dilution, Λm rises."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"For the galvanic cell, Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(0.02 M) → Zn²⁺(0.04 M) + Cu(s), E_cell = ____ × 10⁻² V. (Nearest integer) [Use: E°(Cu/Cu²⁺) = –0.34 V, E°(Zn/Zn²⁺) = +0.76 V, 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V]","type":"num","answer":"109","range":[109.0,109.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 111 (inverting the quotient), 108 (n = 1 or wrong log), –42 or 42 (subtracting the given potentials directly).\nWorking: E° = 0.76 + 0.34 = 1.10 V (from the oxidation potentials given). E = 1.10 – (0.059/2) log(0.04/0.02) = 1.10 – 0.0295 × 0.301 = 1.091 V = 109 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Given values are oxidation potentials: E°cell = E°ox(anode) + E°red(cathode) = 0.76 + 0.34.","traps":["Oxidation vs reduction potentials: Convert both to reduction potentials first.","Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺]: Product ion in numerator."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"When 10 mL of an aqueous solution of KMnO₄ was titrated in acidic medium, equal volume of 0.1 M of an aqueous solution of ferrous sulphate was required for complete discharge of colour. The strength of KMnO₄ in grams per litre is ______ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass of K = 39, Mn = 55, O = 16]","type":"num","answer":"316","range":[316.0,316.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1580 (n-factor of 5 ignored); 527 (n-factor 3 as in neutral medium).\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O; M(KMnO₄) × 5 = 0.1 × 1 (equal volumes) → 0.02 M; × 158 g mol⁻¹ = 3.16 g L⁻¹ = 316 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: 1 MnO₄⁻ oxidises 5 Fe²⁺ in acid.","traps":["n-factor ignored: Use 5 : 1 electron ratio.","Medium-dependent n-factor: Acidic medium → Mn²⁺, 5 electrons."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The resistance of a conductivity cell with cell constant 1.14 cm⁻¹, containing 0.001 M KCl at 298 K is 1500 Ω. The molar conductivity of 0.001 M KCl solution at 298 K in S cm² mol⁻¹ is _______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"760","range":[760.0,760.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.76 (omitting the 1000 factor); 7.6 × 10⁻⁴ reported as κ; 1316 from inverting cell constant (R/G*).\nWorking: κ = G*/R = 1.14/1500 = 7.6 × 10⁻⁴ S cm⁻¹; Λm = κ × 1000/c = 7.6 × 10⁻⁴ × 1000/0.001 = 760 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Λm (S cm² mol⁻¹) = 1000 κ / c(mol L⁻¹).","traps":["Missing 1000 factor: Convert L to cm³: multiply κ by 1000/c."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Consider the following cell reaction: Cd(s) + Hg₂SO₄(s) + (9/5) H₂O(l) ⇌ CdSO₄·(9/5)H₂O(s) + 2Hg(l). The value of E°cell is 4.315 V at 25°C. If ΔH° = −825.2 kJ mol⁻¹, the standard entropy change ΔS° in J K⁻¹ is ______. (Nearest integer) [Given: Faraday constant = 96487 C mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using n = 1 gives ≈ −1372; forgetting kJ→J gives nonsense; using T = 25 instead of 298 K gives ~299; sign slip gives −25.\nWorking: n = 2 (Cd → Cd²⁺, Hg₂²⁺ → 2Hg). ΔG° = −nFE° = −2 × 96487 × 4.315 = −832 683 J mol⁻¹. ΔS° = (ΔH° − ΔG°)/T = (−825 200 + 832 683)/298 = 7483/298 ≈ 25.1 → 25. (The real Weston cell E° is about 1.02 V; the given 4.315 V is unrealistic, but the calculation still gives the expected answer.)\nKey idea: ΔS = (ΔH − ΔG)/T with both in J; n = 2.","traps":["n for the cell: Cd → Cd²⁺ loses 2 e⁻.","kJ vs J and °C vs K: Convert ΔH to J and T to 298 K."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I (Parameter) with List-II (Unit): (a) Cell constant; (b) Molar conductivity; (c) Conductivity; (d) Degree of dissociation of electrolyte. List-II: (i) S cm² mol⁻¹; (ii) Dimensionless; (iii) m⁻¹; (iv) Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps conductivity and α; (C) gives cell constant molar-conductivity units; (D) makes cell constant dimensionless and conductivity m⁻¹.\nWorking: G* = l/A (m⁻¹); Λm in S cm² mol⁻¹; κ in S m⁻¹ = Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹; α is a ratio (dimensionless).\nKey idea: κ = G* × conductance: m⁻¹ × S = S m⁻¹.","traps":["Cell constant vs conductivity units: Cell constant is pure geometry (l/A): only m⁻¹."],"topics":["Conductivity","Molar conductivity"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"(A) HOCl + H₂O₂ → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻ + O₂ (B) I₂ + H₂O₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2I⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂. Choose the correct option.","options":["H₂O₂ acts as reducing and oxidising agent respectively in equation (A) and (B)","H₂O₂ acts as oxidising agent in equation (A) and (B)","H₂O₂ acts as reducing agent in equation (A) and (B)","H₂O₂ act as oxidizing and reducing agent respectively in equation (A) and (B)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) – assume H₂O₂ changes role between acidic and basic media; (B) – H₂O₂ is usually remembered as an oxidant.\nWorking: in both equations oxygen in H₂O₂ goes from −1 to 0 (O₂); Cl goes +1 → −1 in (A) and I goes 0 → −1 in (B), so H₂O₂ is the reductant in both.\nKey idea: Whenever H₂O₂ gives O₂, it has been oxidised → reducing agent.","traps":["H₂O₂ always an oxidant: Check product: O₂ means H₂O₂ was oxidised.","Medium decides role: Track oxidation numbers in each equation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: oxidation-number method","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The reaction of sulphur in alkaline medium is given below: S₈(s) + a OH⁻(aq) → b S²⁻(aq) + c S₂O₃²⁻(aq) + d H₂O(l). The value of 'a' is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (d reported or halved equation); 8 (balanced only S); 24 (doubled).\nWorking: S₈ + 12OH⁻ → 4S²⁻ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ + 6H₂O (S 8 = 4 + 4; charge −12 = −8 −4; O 12 = 6 + 6; H 12 = 12).\nKey idea: Use oxidation-number change: 0 → −2 and 0 → +2 in 2:1 S-atom ratio.","traps":["Wrong coefficient reported: Read which letter is asked (a = OH⁻)."],"topics":["Balancing: oxidation-number method","Disproportionation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The magnitude of the change in oxidising power of the MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ couple is x × 10⁻⁴ V, if the H⁺ concentration is decreased from 1 M to 10⁻⁴ M at 25 °C. (Assume concentration of MnO₄⁻ and Mn²⁺ to be same on change in H⁺ concentration). The value of x is ____. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: 2.303RT/F = 0.059]","type":"num","answer":"3776","range":[3776.0,3776.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 472 (power 8 ignored, ×4 only → 0.0472 V), 2360 (n = 8 confusion), 18880 (n = 1).\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O; E = E° − (0.059/5) log(1/[H⁺]⁸); ΔE = (0.059/5) × 8 × 4 = 0.3776 V.\nKey idea: ΔE = (0.059/n) × (H⁺ power) × Δlog[H⁺].","traps":["H⁺ exponent dropped: Write the balanced half-reaction first."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following equation depicts the oxidizing nature of H₂O₂?","options":["KIO₄ + H₂O₂ → KIO₃ + H₂O + O₂","2I⁻ + H₂O₂ + 2H⁺ → I₂ + 2H₂O","I₂ + H₂O₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2I⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂","Cl₂ + H₂O₂ → 2HCl + O₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D): O₂ is evolved, so H₂O₂ is oxidised (reducing role); students pick (C) because I₂/I⁻ appears as in (B).\nWorking: in (B) H₂O₂ oxidises I⁻ to I₂ and itself goes O(−1) → O(−2) in H₂O. In (A), (C), (D) H₂O₂ is oxidised to O₂ (O −1 → 0), i.e. acts as a reducing agent.\nKey idea: If O₂ is liberated from H₂O₂, H₂O₂ acted as a reducing agent; if it ends as H₂O/OH⁻ only, it oxidised something.","traps":["O₂ evolution read as oxidation: Check whose oxidation number rises: O in H₂O₂ goes −1 → 0.","Iodine in both (B) and (C): Acidic: H₂O₂ oxidises I⁻; basic: it reduces I₂."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Equivalent concept & n-factor","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"In basic medium CrO₄²⁻ oxidises S₂O₃²⁻ to form SO₄²⁻ and itself changes into Cr(OH)₄⁻. The volume of 0.154 M CrO₄²⁻ required to react with 40 mL of 0.25 M S₂O₃²⁻ is ______ mL. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"173","range":[173.0,173.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 43 (taking n = 2 for S₂O₃²⁻ as in the iodine/tetrathionate reaction), 22 (n = 1), 65 (V = 10/0.154 with 1:1 mole ratio).\nWorking: Cr(+6) in CrO₄²⁻ → Cr(+3) in Cr(OH)₄⁻: 3 e⁻ per CrO₄²⁻. S(+2) in S₂O₃²⁻ → S(+6) in SO₄²⁻: 8 e⁻ per S₂O₃²⁻. Equivalents: 40 × 0.25 × 8 = 80 meq = V × 0.154 × 3 → V = 173.2 mL ≈ 173.\nKey idea: S₂O₃²⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ is an 8-electron oxidation, unlike S₂O₃²⁻ → S₄O₆²⁻ (1 e⁻ each).","traps":["Tetrathionate n-factor used: Check the product: SO₄²⁻ means S goes +2 → +6.","Only one S counted: Two S atoms per S₂O₃²⁻ → 8 e⁻."],"topics":["Equivalent concept & n-factor","Redox titrations"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Copper reduces NO₃⁻ into NO and NO₂ depending upon the concentration of HNO₃ in solution. (Assuming fixed [Cu²⁺] and P_NO = P_NO₂), the HNO₃ concentration at which the thermodynamic tendency for reduction of NO₃⁻ into NO and NO₂ by copper is same is 10ˣ M. The value of 2x is ______. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [Given, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.34 V, E°(NO₃⁻/NO) = 0.96 V, E°(NO₃⁻/NO₂) = 0.79 V and at 298 K, (RT/F)(2.303) = 0.059]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (reporting x, not 2x); sign error giving negative x; 9 (using [H⁺] only and forgetting that [NO₃⁻] also equals c: log c ≈ 4.3).\nWorking: NO₃⁻ + 4H⁺ + 3e⁻ → NO + 2H₂O, E = 0.96 + (0.059/3) log([NO₃⁻][H⁺]⁴/P_NO); NO₃⁻ + 2H⁺ + e⁻ → NO₂ + H₂O, E = 0.79 + 0.059 log([NO₃⁻][H⁺]²/P_NO₂). With [H⁺] = [NO₃⁻] = c and P = 1 bar, equal potentials: 0.96 + 0.0983 log c = 0.79 + 0.177 log c → log c = 0.17/0.0787 = 2.16 → 2x = 4.32 → 4.\nKey idea: Equal tendency ⇔ equal reduction potentials (same Cu anode); [HNO₃] enters as both H⁺ and NO₃⁻.","traps":["Reported x instead of 2x: Re-read what is asked.","Nitrate concentration ignored: HNO₃ supplies both H⁺ and NO₃⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Consider the following reaction: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O, E° = 1.51 V. The quantity of electricity required in Faraday to reduce five moles of MnO₄⁻ is ____.","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (one mole only), 1 (per mole of MnO₄⁻ as 1 F), using E° in a ΔG calculation.\nWorking: 5 F per mole of MnO₄⁻ × 5 mol = 25 F.\nKey idea: Faradays = moles × electrons per ion; E° is irrelevant.","traps":["E° value used: Charge depends only on electrons.","n-factor ignored: Multiply by 5 e⁻ per MnO₄⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Emf of the following cell at 298 K in V is x × 10⁻². Zn|Zn²⁺ (0.1 M)||Ag⁺ (0.01 M)|Ag. The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = –0.76 V; E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = +0.80 V; 2.303RT/F = 0.059]","type":"num","answer":"147","range":[147.0,147.0],"explain":"Common slips: 153 – [Ag⁺] not squared (log 10 = 1, E = 1.5305 V); 165 – sign of correction reversed; 156 – E° only.\nWorking: Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag, n = 2, E° = 1.56 V; E = 1.56 – (0.059/2) log(0.1/0.01²) = 1.56 – 0.0295×3 = 1.4715 V ≈ 147 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Ag⁺]² = 0.1/10⁻⁴ = 10³.","traps":["[Ag⁺] not squared: Balance the overall reaction first.","Q inverted: Q = products/reactants for the cell reaction."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The species given below that does NOT show disproportionation reaction is:","options":["BrO₄⁻","BrO⁻","BrO₂⁻","BrO₃⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) all have Br in intermediate states (+1, +3, +5) and do disproportionate; a student who only remembers \"BrO₃⁻ is stable\" may pick (D).\nWorking: in BrO₄⁻ bromine is in its highest oxidation state (+7), so it cannot be oxidised further and cannot disproportionate; Br in BrO⁻ (+1), BrO₂⁻ (+3) and BrO₃⁻ (+5) is in intermediate states.\nKey idea: Highest oxidation state (+7 for halogens) → can only be reduced → no disproportionation.","traps":["Halogen swapped: Same rule: +7 cannot disproportionate.","NOT-type stem: Find the species with Br in its maximum oxidation state."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Potassium chlorate is prepared by electrolysis of KCl in basic solution as shown by following equation. 6OH⁻ + Cl⁻ → ClO₃⁻ + 3H₂O + 6e⁻. A current of xA has to be passed for 10h to produce 10.0g of potassium chlorate. The value of x is _______. (Nearest integer) (Molar mass of KClO₃ = 122.6 g mol⁻¹, F = 96500 C)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 0 (forgetting the 6 electrons gives 0.22 A), 13 (10 h taken as 1 h gives 13.1 A), 79 (time in minutes).\nWorking: n(KClO₃) = 10.0/122.6 = 0.0816 mol; Q = 6 × 0.0816 × 96500 = 4.72 × 10⁴ C; I = Q/(10 × 3600) = 1.31 A → 1.\nKey idea: Electrons per formula unit = 6 (Cl: −1 → +5).","traps":["n-factor omitted: Count electrons from the half-equation: 6.","Time unit: t = 36000 s."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Assume a cell with the following reaction: Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(1 × 10⁻³ M) → Cu²⁺(0.250 M) + 2Ag(s); E°cell = 2.97 V. Ecell for the above reaction is ________ V. (Nearest integer) [Given : log 2.5 = 0.3979, T = 298 K]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Entering 2.81 instead of the nearest integer; note that rounding to 3 can hide small slips in the log term.\nWorking: Ecell = 2.97 − (0.0591/2) log(0.250/(10⁻³)²) = 2.97 − 0.02955 × 5.398 = 2.97 − 0.160 = 2.81 V → 3. (E°cell 2.97 V is not the real Cu/Ag value of 0.46 V, but the data are self-consistent.)\nKey idea: Q = [Cu²⁺]/[Ag⁺]²; n = 2.","traps":["[Ag⁺] not squared: Raise concentrations to their coefficients.","Integer rounding: Round 2.81 → 3."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the cell at 25°C: Zn | Zn²⁺(aq), (1 M) || Fe³⁺(aq), Fe²⁺(aq) | Pt(s). The fraction of total iron present as Fe³⁺ ion at the cell potential of 1.500 V is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) (Given: E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 0.77 V, E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V)","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 31 (reporting [Fe³⁺]/[Fe²⁺] = 0.31 instead of the fraction), 76 (fraction as Fe²⁺), 5 or 9 (using n = 1 or dropping the square).\nWorking: E°cell = 1.53 V; Zn + 2Fe³⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Fe²⁺; 1.500 = 1.53 − (0.059/2) log([Fe²⁺]²/[Fe³⁺]²) → log([Fe²⁺]/[Fe³⁺]) = 0.51 → ratio 3.22; fraction Fe³⁺ = 1/4.22 = 0.237 → 24.\nKey idea: With n = 2 and the squared ratio, the 0.059/2 and the square cancel to give 0.059 log ratio.","traps":["Ratio vs fraction: Fraction = [Fe³⁺]/([Fe³⁺]+[Fe²⁺]).","n and the squared term: Write the balanced equation before Q."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"When 10 mL of an aqueous solution of Fe²⁺ ions was titrated in the presence of dil H₂SO₄ using diphenylamine indicator, 15 mL of 0.02 M solution of K₂Cr₂O₇ was required to get the end point. The molarity of the solution containing Fe²⁺ ions is x × 10⁻² M. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (1:1 ratio), 6 (n-factor 2 for dichromate), 5 (inverting volumes).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6Fe²⁺ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O; mmol Cr₂O₇²⁻ = 0.3, so mmol Fe²⁺ = 1.8; M = 1.8/10 = 0.18 = 18 × 10⁻² M.\nKey idea: One dichromate accepts 6 electrons; each Fe²⁺ gives 1.","traps":["n-factor of dichromate: Balance the equation or use n-factor 6."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Identify the process in which change in the oxidation state is five:","options":["Cr₂O₇²⁻ → 2Cr³⁺","MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺","CrO₄²⁻ → Cr³⁺","C₂O₄²⁻ → 2CO₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives 6 in total (3 per Cr) – tempting if per-atom/total is muddled; (D) gives 2.\nWorking: Mn +7 → +2 (change 5). Cr₂O₇²⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ is 3 per Cr (6 total); CrO₄²⁻ → Cr³⁺ is 3; C₂O₄²⁻ → 2CO₂ is 1 per C (2 total).\nKey idea: MnO₄⁻ in acid = 5-electron change.","traps":["Per atom vs per formula: Compute per atom and total."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The conductivity of a weak acid HA of concentration 0.001 mol L⁻¹ is 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ S cm⁻¹. If Λ°m(HA) = 190 S cm² mol⁻¹, the ionization constant (Ka) of HA is equal to _________ × 10⁻⁶. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 11 (Ka = cα²), 0.02 or 20 000 (missing the 1000 factor), 105 (reporting α).\nWorking: Λm = 1000κ/c = 1000 × 2.0×10⁻⁵ / 0.001 = 20 S cm² mol⁻¹; α = 20/190 = 0.1053; Ka = cα²/(1 − α) = 1.24 × 10⁻⁵ = 12.4 × 10⁻⁶ → 12. (Using Ka ≈ cα² would give 11.)\nKey idea: Λm (S cm² mol⁻¹) = 1000 κ / c(mol L⁻¹); α ≈ 0.1 means (1 − α) changes the integer.","traps":["Factor 1000 in Λm: Convert L to cm³.","Do not drop (1 − α): Use the full Ostwald expression when rounding matters."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"10.0 mL of 0.05 M KMnO₄ solution was consumed in a titration with 10.0 mL of given oxalic acid dihydrate solution. The strength of given oxalic acid solution is ........ × 10⁻² g/L. (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1575","range":[1575.0,1575.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1125 (anhydrous M = 90), 630 (1:1 ratio), 3150.\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ ≡ 5H₂C₂O₄; mmol KMnO₄ = 0.5 → oxalic acid 1.25 mmol in 10 mL = 0.125 M; × 126 g mol⁻¹ (dihydrate) = 15.75 g/L = 1575 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: n-factors 5 and 2; M(H₂C₂O₄·2H₂O) = 126.","traps":["Dihydrate molar mass: Add 2 × 18 to 90.","2:5 mole ratio: Use n-factor 5 for MnO₄⁻, 2 for oxalate."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"For the cell Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(aq) (0.1 M) || Ag⁺(aq) (0.01 M) | Ag(s), the cell potential E₁ = 0.3095 V. For the cell Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(aq) (0.01 M) || Ag⁺(aq) (0.001 M) | Ag(s), the cell potential = ______ × 10⁻² V. (Round off to the nearest integer). [Use: 2.303RT/F = 0.059]","type":"num","answer":"28","range":[28.0,28.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25 (using [Ag⁺] not squared), 31 (unchanged), 34 (sign of correction reversed).\nWorking: Cu + 2Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2Ag, n = 2. E = E° − 0.0295 log([Cu²⁺]/[Ag⁺]²). Cell 1: log(0.1/10⁻⁴) = 3 → E° = 0.3095 + 0.0885 = 0.398 V. Cell 2: log(0.01/10⁻⁶) = 4 → E = 0.398 − 0.118 = 0.280 V = 28 × 10⁻² V.\nKey idea: Q = [Cu²⁺]/[Ag⁺]²; changes in Q change E by 0.0295 per decade.","traps":["Square the Ag⁺ concentration: Write the balanced cell reaction first."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"2MnO₄⁻ + bC₂O₄²⁻ + cH⁺ → xMn²⁺ + yCO₂ + zH₂O. If the above equation is balanced with integer coefficients, the value of c is _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (water coefficient), 10 (CO₂), 5 (oxalate).\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O; c = 16.\nKey idea: Mn gains 5e⁻, each C₂O₄²⁻ loses 2e⁻ → 2:5 ratio, then balance O with H₂O and H with H⁺.","traps":["Reading the wrong coefficient: Answer only c, the H⁺ coefficient."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A 5.0 m mol dm⁻³ aqueous solution of KCl has a conductance of 0.55 mS when measured in a cell of cell constant 1.3 cm⁻¹. The molar conductivity of this solution is ______ mS m² mol⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 143 (Λm in S cm² mol⁻¹ reported), 0.143 or 1430 (unit slips), 0 (0.0143 S m² mol⁻¹ rounded without converting to mS).\nWorking: κ = G × G* = 0.55 mS × 1.3 cm⁻¹ = 0.715 mS cm⁻¹ = 71.5 mS m⁻¹; c = 5.0 mol m⁻³; Λm = 14.3 ≈ 14 mS m² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: 5.0 mmol dm⁻³ = 5.0 mol m⁻³ exactly; 1.3 cm⁻¹ = 130 m⁻¹.","traps":["cm/m conversion slip: Convert everything to SI before dividing."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"15 mL of aqueous solution of Fe²⁺ in acidic medium completely reacted with 20 mL of 0.03 M aqueous Cr₂O₇²⁻. The molarity of the Fe²⁺ solution is _______ × 10⁻² M (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (1 : 1 ratio), 8 (1 : 2 ratio), 12 (using 3 instead of 6).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6Fe²⁺ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O; mmol dichromate = 0.6 → Fe²⁺ = 3.6 mmol; M = 3.6/15 = 0.24 M = 24 × 10⁻² M.\nKey idea: n-factor of Cr₂O₇²⁻ = 6, of Fe²⁺ = 1.","traps":["Ratio taken 1 : 1: Each Cr₂O₇²⁻ accepts 6 e⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A KCl solution of conductivity 0.14 S m⁻¹ shows a resistance of 4.19 Ω in a conductivity cell. If the same cell is filled with an HCl solution, the resistance drops to 1.03 Ω. The conductivity of the HCl solution is ____ × 10⁻² S m⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"57","range":[57.0,57.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (ratio inverted: 0.14 × 1.03/4.19 = 3.4 × 10⁻²), 0.57 or 1 (forgetting the ×10⁻² scaling).\nWorking: cell constant G* = κR = 0.14 × 4.19 = 0.5866 m⁻¹; κ(HCl) = G*/R = 0.5866/1.03 = 0.5695 S m⁻¹ = 56.95 × 10⁻² → 57.\nKey idea: Same cell → κ ∝ 1/R: κ₂ = κ₁R₁/R₂.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Lower resistance must mean higher conductivity.","Scaling ×10⁻²: Divide the result by 10⁻² before rounding."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The oxidation states of nitrogen in NO, NO₂, N₂O and NO₃⁻ are in the order of :","options":["NO₃⁻ > NO₂ > NO > N₂O","NO₂ > NO₃⁻ > NO > N₂O","N₂O > NO₂ > NO > NO₃⁻","NO > NO₂ > N₂O > NO₃⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps NO₂ and NO₃⁻ (forgetting the −1 charge or the third O); (C) and (D) reverse the order or misplace N₂O (+1, taking it as +2).\nWorking: N is +2 in NO, +4 in NO₂, +1 in N₂O and +5 in NO₃⁻, so NO₃⁻ > NO₂ > NO > N₂O.\nKey idea: N₂O has the lowest (+1) and NO₃⁻ the highest (+5) oxidation state of N.","traps":["N₂O taken as +2: Divide total by number of N atoms.","Ignoring charge on NO₃⁻: Sum of ONs = ionic charge."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"In basic medium, H₂O₂ exhibits which of the following reactions ?\n(A) Mn²⁺ → Mn⁴⁺\n(B) I₂ → I⁻\n(C) PbS → PbSO₄\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (C) only","(A) only","(B) only","(A), (B) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) includes PbS → PbSO₄, which is true for H₂O₂ but in acidic medium; (B) misses that H₂O₂ can act as a reductant (I₂ → I⁻); (C) misses the oxidising action on Mn²⁺.\nWorking: in basic medium H₂O₂ oxidises Mn²⁺ to Mn⁴⁺ (MnO₂) and reduces I₂ to I⁻ (itself going to O₂); PbS → PbSO₄ is the standard example of its oxidising action in acidic medium.\nKey idea: H₂O₂ is both oxidant and reductant in basic medium; PbS → PbSO₄ is the acidic-medium example.","traps":["PbS → PbSO₄ assumed basic: Tie each reaction to the medium in which NCERT lists it.","H₂O₂ only as oxidant: Check whether H₂O₂ gains or loses electrons in each change."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kohlrausch's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The molar conductivities at infinite dilution of barium chloride, sulphuric acid and hydrochloric acid are 280, 860 and 426 S cm² mol⁻¹ respectively. The molar conductivity at infinite dilution of barium sulphate is ________ S cm² mol⁻¹ (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"288","range":[288.0,288.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 714 (subtracting HCl once), 1566 (adding all).\nWorking: Λ°(BaSO₄) = Λ°(BaCl₂) + Λ°(H₂SO₄) − 2Λ°(HCl) = 280 + 860 − 852 = 288 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Cancel 2Cl⁻ and 2H⁺ by subtracting 2Λ°(HCl).","traps":["HCl subtracted once: Balance the ions: 2HCl needed."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Hydrogen peroxide reacts with iodine in basic medium to give:","options":["IO₄⁻","IO⁻","I⁻","IO₃⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) IO₄⁻ and (D) IO₃⁻ – treat H₂O₂ as an oxidant raising iodine’s oxidation state; (B) IO⁻ – the product of I₂ with alkali alone.\nWorking: in alkaline medium H₂O₂ reduces I₂: I₂ + H₂O₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2I⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂.\nKey idea: In base, H₂O₂ → O₂ (reducing); I₂ (0) → I⁻ (–1).","traps":["H₂O₂ assumed always an oxidant: In basic medium H₂O₂ acts as a reducing agent: I₂ + H₂O₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2I⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂.","I₂ disproportionation in base used: The question asks for the product of H₂O₂ with I₂; O₂ is the oxidation product, iodine is reduced to I⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"If the conductivity of mercury at 0 °C is 1.07 × 10⁶ S m⁻¹ and the resistance of a cell containing mercury is 0.243 Ω, then the cell constant of the cell is x × 10⁴ m⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"26","range":[26.0,26.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 440 (κ/R); 0 or 3 (power-of-ten slips).\nWorking: G* = κR = 1.07 × 10⁶ × 0.243 = 2.60 × 10⁵ m⁻¹ = 26 × 10⁴ m⁻¹; x = 26.\nKey idea: G* = 1.07 × 10⁶ × 0.243 = 2.60 × 10⁵ m⁻¹ = 26 × 10⁴ m⁻¹.","traps":["Cell constant = κ/R: G* = R × κ (since κ = G*/R).","m⁻¹ vs cm⁻¹: Keep SI throughout: S m⁻¹ × Ω = m⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given that the standard potentials (E°) of Cu²⁺/Cu and Cu⁺/Cu are 0.34 V and 0.522 V respectively, the E° of Cu²⁺/Cu⁺ is :","options":["+0.158 V","0.182 V","–0.182 V","–0.158 V"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 0.182 / (C) –0.182: direct subtraction 0.522 – 0.34 with/without sign. (D) –0.158: sign slip.\nWorking: ΔG° adds: 2(0.34) = 1(E°) + 1(0.522) → E°(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = 0.68 – 0.522 = 0.158 V.\nKey idea: Potentials are intensive; only n·E° is additive.","traps":["E° values subtracted directly: Convert each to ΔG° = –nFE° (n = 2, 1), add, then divide by new n.","Sign of the result: Check: Cu²⁺→Cu (n=2) = Cu²⁺→Cu⁺ (n=1) + Cu⁺→Cu (n=1)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Oxidation number of potassium in K₂O, K₂O₂ and KO₂, respectively, is :","options":["+1, +4 and +2","+1, +2 and +4","+1, +1 and +1","+2, +1 and +1/2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): fix O at –2 and solve for K. (D): mixes up which element changes.\nWorking: alkali metals are always +1 in compounds; the oxygen changes: oxide O²⁻ (–2), peroxide O₂²⁻ (–1 each), superoxide O₂⁻ (–½ each).\nKey idea: K is +1; it is oxygen whose oxidation number changes (–2, –1, –½).","traps":["O fixed at –2: Group 1 metal is always +1 – solve for O instead."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The redox reaction among the following is :","options":["Combination of dinitrogen with dioxygen at 2000 K","Formation of ozone from atmospheric oxygen in the presence of sunlight","Reaction of H₂SO₄ with NaOH","Reaction of [Co(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ with AgNO₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ozone formation looks like 'oxygen reacting' but O stays at 0; (C) neutralisation is a familiar non-redox reaction; (D) precipitation of AgCl from ionisable Cl⁻ is metathesis.\nWorking: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO changes N from 0 to +2 and O from 0 to −2. 3O₂ → 2O₃ keeps O at 0 (allotropic change); neutralisation and AgCl precipitation involve no change in oxidation number.\nKey idea: A reaction is redox only if some atom changes oxidation number; allotropic conversion does not count.","traps":["O₂ → O₃ taken as redox: Assign oxidation numbers: both O₂ and O₃ are 0."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kohlrausch's law","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The equation that is incorrect is -","options":["(Λ°m)NaBr − (Λ°m)NaI = (Λ°m)KBr − (Λ°m)NaBr","(Λ°m)H₂O = (Λ°m)HCl + (Λ°m)NaOH − (Λ°m)NaCl","(Λ°m)KCl − (Λ°m)NaCl = (Λ°m)KBr − (Λ°m)NaBr","(Λ°m)NaBr − (Λ°m)NaCl = (Λ°m)KBr − (Λ°m)KCl"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) looks wrong because water is a weak electrolyte, but it is the standard Kohlrausch route to Λ°(H₂O); (C) and (D) are valid differences.\nWorking: by Kohlrausch's law the left side of (A) is λ°(Br⁻) − λ°(I⁻) but the right side is λ°(K⁺) − λ°(Na⁺), which are unequal; (B), (C), (D) cancel correctly.\nKey idea: Expand each Λ° into λ°+ + λ°− and see which side fails to match.","traps":["Weak-electrolyte equation suspected: Kohlrausch law is precisely how Λ° of weak electrolytes is found."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"What would be the electrode potential for the given half cell reaction at pH = 5? ______ 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ ; E°red = 1.23 V (R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; Temp = 298 K; oxygen under std. atm. pressure of 1 bar)","type":"num","answer":"-0.94 to -0.93","range":[-0.94,-0.93],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: +0.935 (reduction potential), −1.525/+1.525 (sign of log term wrong), −1.23 + 0.0148×5 (forgot [H⁺]⁴).\nWorking: for the written oxidation, E°ox = −1.23 V; E = −1.23 − (0.059/4) log([H⁺]⁴ pO₂) = −1.23 + 0.059 × 5 = −0.935 V. Note the wording is ambiguous: the reduction potential at pH 5 would be +0.935 V; the key takes the oxidation potential for the reaction as written.\nKey idea: Write E for the reaction as written; E°ox = −E°red.","traps":["Reduction vs oxidation potential: Follow the direction written.","Power of [H⁺] dropped: Include [H⁺]⁴ with n = 4."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For an electrochemical cell Sn(s) | Sn²⁺(aq, 1M) || Pb²⁺(aq, 1M) | Pb(s) the ratio [Sn²⁺]/[Pb²⁺] when this cell attains equilibrium is ______. (Given E°(Sn²⁺|Sn) = −0.14 V, E°(Pb²⁺|Pb) = −0.13 V, 2.303RT/F = 0.06)","type":"num","answer":"2.13 to 2.16","range":[2.13,2.16],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.464 (ratio inverted, 10^(−1/3)); 1.47 (n = 1 not used correctly → 10^(1/6)); 4.64 (n = 4 used, 10^(2/3)).\nWorking: E°cell = −0.13 − (−0.14) = 0.01 V; at equilibrium Ecell = 0, so log([Sn²⁺]/[Pb²⁺]) = 2 × 0.01/0.06 = 1/3, ratio = 10^(1/3) = 2.154.\nKey idea: Ecell = 0 at equilibrium, so log K = nE°/0.06; K = [Sn²⁺]/[Pb²⁺] = 10^(1/3) ≈ 2.15.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Q = [products]/[reactants]; Sn²⁺ is produced.","n taken as 1: Write the balanced cell reaction first."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The compound that cannot act both as oxidising and reducing agent is:","options":["H₂O₂","H₂SO₃","HNO₂","H₃PO₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) H₂O₂, (B) H₂SO₃, (C) HNO₂ all have the central atom in an intermediate state, so they can do both.\nWorking: P in H₃PO₄ is +5, its maximum, so it can only be reduced (and in practice is a non-oxidising acid); O in H₂O₂ (−1), S in H₂SO₃ (+4) and N in HNO₂ (+3) are intermediate states.\nKey idea: Element at its maximum oxidation state cannot be oxidised further.","traps":["Double negative wording: Find the atom at its extreme oxidation state."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"108 g of silver (molar mass 108 g mol⁻¹) is deposited at cathode from AgNO₃(aq) solution by a certain quantity of electricity. The volume (in L) of oxygen gas produced at 273 K and 1 bar pressure from water by the same quantity of electricity is ______.","type":"num","answer":"5.66 to 5.68","range":[5.66,5.68],"explain":"Common slips: Using 22.4 L gives 5.60 (outside the range); taking 2 e⁻ per O₂ gives 11.35; 1 e⁻ gives 22.7.\nWorking: 1 mol Ag = 1 F; O₂ needs 4 F per mol, so 0.25 mol; at 273 K, 1 bar molar volume 22.7 L → 5.675 L, inside 5.66–5.68.\nKey idea: Same charge → equal equivalents; O₂ equivalent = 1/4 mol.","traps":["22.4 vs 22.7 L molar volume: At 273 K and 1 bar use 22.7 L.","Electrons per O₂: Four electrons per O₂."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Amongst the following, the form of water with the lowest ionic conductance at 298 K is:","options":["distilled water","water from a well","saline water used for intravenous injection","sea water"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) well water has dissolved minerals; (C) 0.9% NaCl; (D) sea water has the highest ion content.\nWorking: conductance depends on dissolved ions; distilled water has almost none (only H⁺/OH⁻ from self-ionisation).\nKey idea: Fewest ions → lowest conductance → distilled water.","traps":["Well water thought pure: Groundwater carries dissolved salts."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The Gibbs energy change (in J) for the given reaction at [Cu²⁺] = [Sn²⁺] = 1 M and 298 K is: Cu(s) + Sn²⁺(aq.) → Cu²⁺(aq.) + Sn(s); (E°(Sn²⁺|Sn) = –0.16 V, E°(Cu²⁺|Cu) = 0.34 V, Take F = 96500 C mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"96500","range":[96500.0,96500.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: –96500 (sign reversed), ±48250 (n = 1), ±96500 × 0.18 using E° sum instead of difference.\nWorking: Sn²⁺ is reduced (cathode), Cu oxidised (anode); E°cell = –0.16 – 0.34 = –0.50 V; with unit concentrations E = E°; ΔG = –nFE = –2 × 96500 × (–0.50) = +96500 J.\nKey idea: Write E°cell = E°(reduced species) – E°(oxidised species) as written; a negative E gives positive ΔG.","traps":["Sign of E°cell: Cathode = species reduced in the given equation (Sn²⁺).","n = 2 dropped: Cu → Cu²⁺ transfers two electrons."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The oxidation states of iron atoms in compounds (A), (B) and (C), respectively, are x, y and z. The sum of x, y and z is ____. (A) Na₄[Fe(CN)₅(NOS)]; (B) Na₄[FeO₄]; (C) [Fe₂(CO)₉]","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 or 8 (taking NOS as NO⁺ giving Fe +1/+3 confusions, or Fe as +6 from familiar FeO₄²⁻), 5 (Fe +1 in A).\nWorking: (A) NOS⁻ ligand with 5 CN⁻ and Na₄: 4 + x – 5 – 1 = 0 → x = +2; (B) 4 + y – 8 = 0 → y = +4; (C) CO neutral → z = 0. Sum = 6.\nKey idea: Carbonyls are zero-valent; Na₄FeO₄ is Fe(IV), not ferrate(VI).","traps":["Familiar ferrate(VI) assumed: Use the actual Na₄ count.","Charge on NOS ligand: Balance total charge: NOS⁻ overall."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For the disproportionation reaction 2Cu⁺(aq) ⇌ Cu(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) at 298 K, ln K (where K is the equilibrium constant) is ______ × 10⁻¹. Given (E°(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = 0.16 V, E°(Cu⁺/Cu) = 0.52 V, RT/F = 0.025)","type":"num","answer":"144","range":[144.0,144.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 288 (n = 2 used); –144 (E° subtracted wrongly); 62.5 (log₁₀ form mixed with ln).\nWorking: E°cell = E°(Cu⁺/Cu) – E°(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = 0.52 – 0.16 = 0.36 V, n = 1; ln K = nFE°/RT = 0.36/0.025 = 14.4 = 144 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Cathode couple Cu⁺/Cu, anode couple Cu²⁺/Cu⁺; one electron each.","traps":["n taken as 2: Each half-reaction transfers one electron.","Answer scaling × 10⁻¹: ln K = 14.4 → enter 144."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"An acidic solution of dichromate is electrolyzed for 8 minutes using 2A current. As per the following equation\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O\nThe amount of Cr³⁺ obtained was 0.104 g. The efficiency of the process (in %) is ______.\n(Take : F = 96000 C, At. mass of chromium = 52)","type":"num","answer":"60","range":[60.0,60.0],"explain":"Common slips: 120: using 6 e⁻ per Cr (ignoring the 2 Cr); 30: using 1.5 e⁻ per Cr; 1 or 0.6: reporting fraction not %.\nWorking: Q = 2 × 480 = 960 C = 0.01 F; 3 F per mol Cr³⁺ → 0.01/3 mol = 0.1733 g theoretical; efficiency = 0.104/0.1733 × 100 = 60%.\nKey idea: Electrons per Cr³⁺ = 6/2 = 3.","traps":["6 e⁻ per Cr³⁺ used: Divide by the 2 Cr formed.","Minutes not converted: 8 min = 480 s."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Equivalent concept & n-factor","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The volume (in mL) of 0.1 N NaOH required to neutralise 10 mL of 0.1 N phosphinic acid is ______.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: 30: treating H₃PO₂ as tribasic on top of normality; 20: dibasic (confusing with H₃PO₃).\nWorking: N₁V₁ = N₂V₂ → 0.1 × 10 = 0.1 × V → V = 10 mL (normality already accounts for basicity; H₃PO₂ is monobasic anyway).\nKey idea: With normalities, N₁V₁ = N₂V₂ directly.","traps":["Basicity applied twice: Normality already includes n-factor."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"For the cell shown (Zn rod in 1 M ZnSO₄ (–ve) | salt bridge | 1 M CuSO₄ with Cu rod (+ve), with an external source E_ext opposing the cell), E°(Cu²⁺|Cu) = +0.34 V, E°(Zn²⁺|Zn) = –0.76 V. Identify the incorrect statement from the options below for the above cell :","options":["If E_ext > 1.1 V, Zn dissolves at Zn electrode and Cu deposits at Cu electrode","If E_ext > 1.1 V, e⁻ flows from Cu to Zn","If E_ext = 1.1 V, no flow of e⁻ or current occurs","If E_ext < 1.1 V, Zn dissolves at anode and Cu deposits at cathode"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) correct – electrons are pushed from Cu to Zn; (C) correct – balance point; (D) correct – normal galvanic behaviour. Picking these comes from missing the 'incorrect' wording.\nWorking: E°cell = 1.10 V. Above 1.1 V the cell is driven as an electrolytic cell: Zn deposits and Cu dissolves, electrons go Cu → Zn externally. So (A) is incorrect; (B), (C), (D) are correct.\nKey idea: E_ext > E°cell reverses the cell reaction.","traps":["Missing reversal above 1.1 V: Above 1.1 V Zn²⁺ is reduced and Cu oxidised.","Electron direction reversed: Under E_ext > 1.1 V the flow is Cu → Zn."],"topics":["Galvanic cells","Electrolysis"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A 20.0 mL solution containing 0.2 g impure H₂O₂ reacts completely with 0.316 g of KMnO₄ in acid solution. The purity of H₂O₂ (in %) is ______ (mol. wt. of H₂O₂ = 34; mol. wt. of KMnO₄ = 158)","type":"num","answer":"85","range":[85.0,85.0],"explain":"Common slips: 34 (1:1 ratio), 13.6 (ratio inverted), 170 (forgetting impurity division).\nWorking: KMnO₄ = 2×10⁻³ mol; 2MnO₄⁻ ≡ 5H₂O₂ → H₂O₂ = 5×10⁻³ mol = 0.17 g; purity = 0.17/0.2 × 100 = 85%.\nKey idea: Equivalents: n(KMnO₄)×5 = n(H₂O₂)×2.","traps":["Mole ratio 1:1: MnO₄⁻ gains 5 e⁻; H₂O₂ loses 2 e⁻.","Volume used: Volume is irrelevant; masses are given."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Consider the following equations : 2Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → xA + yB (in basic medium); 2MnO₄⁻ + 6H⁺ + 5H₂O₂ → x′C + y′D + z′E (in acidic medium). The sum of the stoichiometric coefficients x, y, x′, y′ and z′ for products A, B, C, D and E, respectively, is ______.","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: 17 or 21 if H₂O count is wrong; 18 if OH⁻ count halved; treating Fe(OH)₃ as product changes the count.\nWorking: 2Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2OH⁻ (x = 2, y = 2); 2MnO₄⁻ + 6H⁺ + 5H₂O₂ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5O₂ + 8H₂O (x′ + y′ + z′ = 15); total 19.\nKey idea: In acidic MnO₄⁻/H₂O₂, H₂O₂ is oxidised to O₂ (5 O₂ for 2 MnO₄⁻).","traps":["Water count after H⁺ given: Balance O: 8 O from MnO₄⁻ + 10 from H₂O₂ = 10 (O₂) + 8 (H₂O).","H₂O₂ role reversed: With Fe²⁺ it oxidises; with MnO₄⁻ it reduces."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The variation of molar conductivity with concentration of an electrolyte (X) in aqueous solution is shown in the given figure.\nThe electrolyte X is:","options":["CH₃COOH","KNO₃","HCl","NaCl"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) KNO₃, (C) HCl, (D) NaCl – strong electrolytes: Λm falls only slightly and linearly with √c.\nWorking: the steep rise of Λm on dilution (non-linear, no extrapolable line) is typical of a weak electrolyte; acetic acid is the only weak electrolyte.\nKey idea: Steep, curved Λm vs √c → weak electrolyte; gentle straight line → strong electrolyte.","traps":["HCl chosen as acid: HCl is a strong electrolyte; only CH₃COOH is weak."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep05S2C05-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Redox titrations","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The volume, in mL, of 0.02 M K₂Cr₂O₇ solution required to react with 0.288 g of ferrous oxalate in acidic medium is ______. (Molar mass of Fe = 56 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 16.7 mL (only Fe²⁺ oxidised, n = 1), 33.3 mL (only oxalate, n = 2), 100 mL or 300 mL from n-factor of dichromate taken as 3 or 1.\nWorking: FeC₂O₄ M = 144 g mol⁻¹ → 0.288 g = 2 × 10⁻³ mol; each FeC₂O₄ loses 3 e⁻ (Fe²⁺→Fe³⁺: 1; C₂O₄²⁻→2CO₂: 2) → 6 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻; Cr₂O₇²⁻ takes 6 e⁻ → 1 × 10⁻³ mol needed; V = 10⁻³/0.02 L = 50 mL.\nKey idea: Ferrous oxalate gives up 3 electrons: one from Fe²⁺ and two from C₂O₄²⁻.","traps":["Oxalate oxidation ignored: Both Fe²⁺ and C₂O₄²⁻ are oxidised by acidified dichromate.","Dichromate electrons per ion: Cr₂O₇²⁻ has two Cr: 6 e⁻ per ion."],"topics":["Redox titrations","Equivalent concept & n-factor"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Potassium chlorate is prepared by the electrolysis of KCl in basic solution\n6OH⁻ + Cl⁻ → ClO₃⁻ + 3H₂O + 6e⁻\nIf only 60% of the current is utilized in the reaction, the time (rounded to the nearest hour) required to produce 10 g of KClO₃ using a current of 2 A is_____.\n(Given : F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹, molar mass of KClO₃ = 122 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (multiplying the time by 0.6 instead of dividing: 3.96 h), 2 (taking n = 1), 7 (ignoring efficiency: 6.6 h).\nWorking: charge = (10/122) × 6 × 96500 = 4.746 × 10⁴ C; effective current 0.6 × 2 = 1.2 A; t = 3.955 × 10⁴ s ≈ 10.99 h → 11.\nKey idea: Required charge ÷ (efficiency × I) = time.","traps":["Efficiency applied the wrong way: Less useful current → more time needed.","Electron count dropped: Use n = 6 per ClO₃⁻."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"For the given cell; Cu(s)|Cu²⁺(C₁ M)||Cu²⁺(C₂ M)|Cu(s), change in Gibbs energy (ΔG) is negative, if:","options":["C₁ = 2C₂","C₂ = C₁/√2","C₁ = C₂","C₂ = √2 C₁"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) gives E = 0, ΔG = 0; (A) and (B) have C₂ < C₁, so E < 0 and ΔG > 0.\nWorking: E°cell = 0; Ecell = (0.059/2) log(C₂/C₁); ΔG = −nFE < 0 needs C₂ > C₁, only (D).\nKey idea: In a concentration cell current flows so as to equalise concentrations: cathode side must be more concentrated.","traps":["Anode side taken as more concentrated: Q = [Cu²⁺]anode/[Cu²⁺]cathode = C₁/C₂.","E° = 0 taken as no reaction: E depends on concentration ratio even when E° = 0."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Equivalent concept & n-factor","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In order to oxidise a mixture one mole of each of FeC₂O₄, Fe₂(C₂O₄)₃, FeSO₄ and Fe₂(SO₄)₃ in acidic medium, the number of moles of KMnO₄ required is –","options":["3","2","1","1.5"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3 counts only Fe-containing species or misses oxalate electrons; (C) 1 counts only one oxalate; (D) 1.5 forgets oxalate in Fe₂(C₂O₄)₃ or adds Fe³⁺ wrongly.\nWorking: electrons lost – FeC₂O₄ 1 (Fe²⁺) + 2 (C₂O₄²⁻) = 3; Fe₂(C₂O₄)₃ 3 × 2 = 6; FeSO₄ 1; Fe₂(SO₄)₃ 0; total 10 e⁻; MnO₄⁻ takes 5 e⁻ ⇒ 2 mol.\nKey idea: Fe³⁺ and SO₄²⁻ are already fully oxidised; Fe²⁺ gives 1 e⁻, each C₂O₄²⁻ gives 2 e⁻.","traps":["Oxalate in ferric oxalate ignored: Check anion and cation separately.","Fe₂(SO₄)₃ counted as reducing agent: Only species below their highest state consume oxidant."],"topics":["Equivalent concept & n-factor","Redox titrations"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Given that : E°(O₂/H₂O) = +1.23 V, E°(S₂O₈²⁻/SO₄²⁻) = +2.05 V, E°(Br₂/Br⁻) = +1.09 V, E°(Au³⁺/Au) = +1.4 V. The strongest oxidizing agent is –","options":["O₂","Br₂","S₂O₈²⁻","Au³⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Au³⁺ – recalled as 'noble'; (A) O₂ familiar; (B) Br₂ lowest.\nWorking: highest reduction potential (+2.05 V) ⇒ S₂O₈²⁻ is the strongest oxidant.\nKey idea: Largest positive E°_red = strongest oxidant.","traps":["Picking a familiar strong oxidant: Compare numbers only."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrode potential & SHE","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Calculate the standard cell potential (in V) of the cell in which the following reaction takes place: Fe²⁺(aq) + Ag⁺(aq) → Fe³⁺(aq) + Ag(s). Given that E°(Ag⁺/Ag) = x V, E°(Fe²⁺/Fe) = y V, E°(Fe³⁺/Fe) = z V.","options":["x + 2y − 3z","x − z","x − y","x + y − z"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) x − z uses E°(Fe³⁺/Fe) directly as the anode couple; (C) x − y uses E°(Fe²⁺/Fe); (D) x + y − z subtracts potentials without weighting by electrons.\nWorking: Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺ = (Fe³⁺→Fe) − (Fe²⁺→Fe) in ΔG terms: −1·F·E° = −3Fz + 2Fy, so E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 3z − 2y. E°cell = x − (3z − 2y) = x + 2y − 3z.\nKey idea: Potentials are not additive; ΔG° = −nFE° is. E°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 3z − 2y.","traps":["Potentials added without electron weighting: Convert each to ΔG° = −nFE°, add, then divide by n.","Wrong couple chosen for the anode: Write the actual half-reaction Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻."],"topics":["Electrode potential & SHE","EMF & Gibbs energy"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation number","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct order of the oxidation states of nitrogen in NO, N₂O, NO₂ and N₂O₃ is :","options":["NO₂ < N₂O₃ < NO < N₂O","NO₂ < NO < N₂O₃ < N₂O","N₂O < N₂O₃ < NO < NO₂","N₂O < NO < N₂O₃ < NO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) are decreasing orders (reverse sign of the sequence); (C) swaps NO (+2) and N₂O₃ (+3).\nWorking: N₂O +1, NO +2, N₂O₃ +3, NO₂ +4, so increasing order N₂O < NO < N₂O₃ < NO₂.\nKey idea: Oxidation state = 2×(number of O)/(number of N).","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing order read the wrong way: Write each value and check the '<' direction.","N₂O₃ placed below NO: Divide total O charge by number of N atoms."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The standard Gibbs energy for the given cell reaction in kJ mol⁻¹ at 298 K is : Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), E° = 2 V at 298 K (Faraday's constant, F = 96000 C mol⁻¹)","options":["−384","−192","192","384"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) −192 uses n = 1; (C)/(D) positive sign.\nWorking: ΔG° = −nFE° = −2 × 96000 × 2 = −384000 J = −384 kJ mol⁻¹. (E° = 2 V is a hypothetical value set by the paper; the real Zn–Cu cell is about 1.1 V.)\nKey idea: Spontaneous cell (E° > 0) ⇒ ΔG° negative; n = 2.","traps":["n taken as 1: Count electrons: Zn → Zn²⁺ gives 2.","Sign dropped: Positive E° means negative ΔG°."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A solution of Ni(NO₃)₂ is electrolysed between platinum electrodes using 0.1 Faraday electricity. How many mole of Ni will be deposited at the cathode?","options":["0.20","0.05","0.10","0.15"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 0.10 ignores the 2 e⁻ per Ni; (A) 0.20 multiplies by 2.\nWorking: Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni; 0.1 F deposits 0.1/2 = 0.05 mol Ni.\nKey idea: Moles deposited = F passed / electrons per ion.","traps":["Electrons per ion ignored: Ni²⁺ needs 2 F per mole."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Consider the statements S1 and S2 : S1 : Conductivity always increases with decrease in the concentration of electrolyte. S2 : Molar conductivity always increases with decrease in the concentration of electrolyte. The correct option among the following is :","options":["Both S1 and S2 are correct","S1 is wrong and S2 is correct","S1 is correct and S2 is wrong","Both S1 and S2 are wrong"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts S1 by carrying the Λm trend over to κ; (C) swaps the two trends; (D) rejects S2 by applying the κ trend to Λm.\nWorking: conductivity κ falls on dilution (fewer ions per unit volume), molar conductivity Λm = κ/c rises on dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes.\nKey idea: κ ↓ on dilution, Λm ↑ on dilution.","traps":["Conductivity and molar conductivity trends swapped: κ counts ions per volume; Λm counts per mole."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity vs concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which one of the following graphs between molar conductivity (Λm) versus √C is correct?","options":["Graph (A)","Graph (B)","Graph (C)","Graph (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) right shape but NaCl above KCl (assuming smaller Na⁺ moves faster); (A)/(C) crossing lines imply different slopes, wrong for same valence type.\nWorking: both are strong 1:1 electrolytes, so Λm falls linearly with √C with nearly equal slopes (Debye–Hückel–Onsager A depends on valence type); Λ°m(KCl) > Λ°m(NaCl) because K⁺ has higher ionic conductivity than Na⁺.\nKey idea: Same valence type → same slope; K⁺ more mobile (less hydrated) than Na⁺.","traps":["Smaller Na⁺ assumed more mobile: Na⁺ is more hydrated, so K⁺ conducts better.","Slopes assumed different: A depends only on electrolyte type (1:1 for both)."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Apr10S2C19-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given : Co³⁺ + e⁻ → Co²⁺ ; E° = +1.81 V; Pb⁴⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb²⁺ ; E° = +1.67 V; Ce⁴⁺ + e⁻ → Ce³⁺ ; E° = +1.61 V; Bi³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Bi ; E° = +0.20 V. Oxidizing power of the species will increase in the order :","options":["Ce⁴⁺ < Pb⁴⁺ < Bi³⁺ < Co³⁺","Co³⁺ < Pb⁴⁺ < Ce⁴⁺ < Bi³⁺","Co³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺ < Bi³⁺ < Pb⁴⁺","Bi³⁺ < Ce⁴⁺ < Pb⁴⁺ < Co³⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the order; (A)/(C) misplace Bi³⁺ (e.g. using nE° so Bi³⁺ = 0.60 is still lowest, or misreading).\nWorking: higher standard reduction potential = stronger oxidising agent; 0.20 < 1.61 < 1.67 < 1.81. Number of electrons does not matter.\nKey idea: E° is intensive: compare values directly.","traps":["Multiplying E° by n: E° is intensive; compare as given.","Order reversed: Write values beside each species."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disproportionation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"An example of a disproportionation reaction is :","options":["2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂","2MnO₄⁻ + 10I⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5I₂ + 8H₂O","2CuBr → CuBr₂ + Cu","2NaBr + Cl₂ → 2NaCl + Br₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Mn goes to two products but both are reductions (O is oxidised): decomposition; (B) ordinary intermolecular redox; (D) halogen displacement.\nWorking: Cu(+1) goes to Cu(+2) and Cu(0) – same element, same oxidation state, oxidised and reduced. In (A) Mn is only reduced (+7 → +6, +4) while O is oxidised; (B) is ordinary redox between Mn and I; (D) is a displacement.\nKey idea: Disproportionation needs the same element both up and down.","traps":["Two products of one element ≠ disproportionation: Check that one product is higher and one lower than the start."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conductivity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The decreasing order of electrical conductivity of the following aqueous solutions is : 0.1 M Formic acid (A), 0.1 M Acetic acid (B), 0.1 M Benzoic acid (C)","options":["C > B > A","A > B > C","A > C > B","C > A > B"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts benzoic weakest (thinking aryl donates); (A)/(D) assume benzoic is strongest.\nWorking: at equal concentration, conductivity follows degree of ionisation, i.e. acid strength: HCOOH (pKa 3.75) > C₆H₅COOH (4.20) > CH₃COOH (4.76).\nKey idea: Acid strength: HCOOH > PhCOOH > CH₃COOH.","traps":["Benzoic acid placed weakest: sp² phenyl is –I relative to CH₃.","Larger acid assumed stronger: Compare pKa values."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Faraday's laws","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The anodic half-cell of lead-acid battery is recharged using electricity of 0.05 Faraday. The amount of PbSO₄ electrolyzed in g during the process is :\n(Molar mass of PbSO₄ = 303 g mol⁻¹)","options":["22.8","15.2","7.6","11.4"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): 15.2 g counting both plates (or 1 F per mol). (A)/(D): 3× and 1.5× multiples.\nWorking: at the (discharge) anode half-cell, PbSO₄ + 2e⁻ → Pb + SO₄²⁻ on charging, so 0.05 F converts 0.025 mol PbSO₄ = 0.025 × 303 = 7.6 g.\nKey idea: Half-cell only: 2 F per mole PbSO₄.","traps":["Both electrodes counted: Question asks only the anodic half-cell: 2 F per mol PbSO₄.","1 F per mol PbSO₄: Pb²⁺ ⇌ Pb⁰/Pb⁴⁺ involves 2 e⁻."],"topics":["Faraday's laws","Batteries"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"If the standard electrode potential for a cell is 2 V at 300 K, the equilibrium constant (K) for the reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) ⇌ Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) at 300 K is approximately. (R = 8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, F = 96000 C mol⁻¹)","options":["e¹⁶⁰","e³²⁰","e⁻¹⁶⁰","e⁻⁸⁰"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) e³²⁰: n doubled. (C) e⁻¹⁶⁰: sign error. (D) e⁻⁸⁰: n = 1 and sign error.\nWorking: ln K = nFE°/RT = 2 × 96000 × 2/(8 × 300) = 160, K = e¹⁶⁰.\nKey idea: Positive E° gives K > 1; ln K = nFE°/RT.","traps":["Sign of ln K: Both expressions have a minus sign; they cancel.","n mis-set: n = 2 for Zn/Cu²⁺."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation & reduction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The chemical nature of hydrogen peroxide is :-","options":["Oxidising and reducing agent in acidic medium, but not in basic medium.","Oxidising and reducing agent in both acidic and basic medium","Reducing agent in basic medium, but not in acidic medium","Oxidising agent in acidic medium, but not in basic medium."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) each add a false restriction (\"but not in …\" medium) to a half-true statement.\nWorking: O in H₂O₂ is −1, between −2 and 0, so it can be reduced (to H₂O/OH⁻) or oxidised (to O₂) in both acidic and basic media.\nKey idea: Intermediate oxidation state (−1) of O means H₂O₂ can go up or down; the medium only changes the balancing species (H⁺ or OH⁻).","traps":["Medium restriction added to a true behaviour: Recall H₂O₂ oxidises (e.g. Fe²⁺, PbS) and reduces (e.g. MnO₄⁻, Ag₂O) in both media."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the following reduction processes : Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn(s); E° = −0.76 V; Ca²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ca(s); E° = −2.87 V; Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mg(s); E° = −2.36 V; Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni(s); E° = −0.25 V. The reducing power of the metals increases in the order :","options":["Ca < Zn < Mg < Ni","Ni < Zn < Mg < Ca","Zn < Mg < Ni < Ca","Ca < Mg < Zn < Ni"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): decreasing order (reversed). (A)/(C): partly scrambled orders.\nWorking: more negative E° (reduction) → stronger reducing metal: Ni (−0.25) < Zn (−0.76) < Mg (−2.36) < Ca (−2.87).\nKey idea: Reducing power increases as E°(Mⁿ⁺/M) becomes more negative.","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing order: Most negative E° = strongest reductant = last in an increasing list."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nernst equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In the cell : Pt(s)|H₂(g, 1 bar)|HCl(aq)|AgCl(s)|Ag(s)|Pt(s) the cell potential is 0.92 V when a 10⁻⁶ molal HCl solution is used. The standard electrode potential of (AgCl/Ag, Cl⁻) electrode is : [Given: 2.303RT/F = 0.06 V at 298 K]","options":["0.20 V","0.76 V","0.40 V","0.94 V"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 0.76 V: the familiar |E°(Zn²⁺/Zn)| value, not obtained from this cell. (C) 0.40 V and (D) 0.94 V: come from partial or mis-signed concentration corrections (e.g. using one ion only or the wrong sign of log Q). A student who keeps only [H⁺] would get 0.56 V, not offered, which signals both ions must be used.\nWorking: H₂ + 2AgCl → 2H⁺ + 2Cl⁻ + 2Ag; E = E° − (0.06/2) log([H⁺]²[Cl⁻]²) = E° − 0.03 log(10⁻²⁴) = E° + 0.72; 0.92 = E° + 0.72, E° = 0.20 V.\nKey idea: Q = [H⁺]²[Cl⁻]² = 10⁻²⁴; correction is +0.72 V.","traps":["Counting both ions in Q: Write the full cell reaction; both ions appear as products.","Sign of log term: E = E° − (0.06/n)log Q; small Q raises E."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Balancing: ion–electron method","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"In the reaction of oxalate with permanganate in acidic medium, the number of electrons involved in producing one molecule of CO₂ is :","options":["10","2","1","5"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 10: total electrons for the balanced equation. (B) 2: per oxalate ion. (D) 5: per MnO₄⁻.\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O; 10 e⁻ for 10 CO₂, i.e. 1 e⁻ per CO₂ (C: +3 → +4).\nKey idea: C goes from +3 to +4: one electron per CO₂.","traps":["Per oxalate vs per CO₂: Divide by the number of CO₂ formed.","Electrons per MnO₄⁻: Read what is asked: per CO₂."],"topics":["Balancing: ion–electron method","Equivalent concept & n-factor"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Galvanic cells","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"For the cell Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) || Mˣ⁺(aq) | M(s), different half cells and their standard electrode potentials are given below:\nMˣ⁺(aq)/M(s): Au³⁺(aq)/Au(s) 1.40 V; Ag⁺(aq)/Ag(s) 0.80 V; Fe³⁺(aq)/Fe²⁺(aq) 0.77 V; Fe²⁺(aq)/Fe(s) −0.44 V.\nIf E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V, which cathode will give a maximum value of E°cell per electron transferred?","options":["Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺","Ag⁺/Ag","Au³⁺/Au","Fe²⁺/Fe"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Au³⁺/Au has the largest raw E°cell (2.16 V) – chosen if n is ignored. (A) Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ (1.53 V, n=1) is only 0.03 V behind Ag – chosen by a rounding or anode-sign slip. (D) Fe²⁺/Fe gives the smallest value.\nWorking: E°cell = E°cathode + 0.76 gives 2.16 (Au, n=3), 1.56 (Ag, n=1), 1.53 (Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺, n=1), 0.32 V (Fe²⁺/Fe, n=2); E°cell/n is largest for Ag⁺/Ag (1.56). Note: E°cell itself is intensive, so 'per electron' is read here as E°cell ÷ n.\nKey idea: Divide E°cell by electrons per cathode ion: Au 0.72, Ag 1.56, Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ 1.53, Fe²⁺/Fe 0.16 V.","traps":["Taking largest raw E°cell (Au): Apply the stated division by n = 3 for Au³⁺.","Thinking E°cell scales with n: Follow the question's operational definition: E°cell ÷ n."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"EMF & K","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given the equilibrium constant Kc of the reaction Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s) is 10 × 10¹⁵, calculate the E°cell of this reaction at 298 K. [2.303 RT/F at 298 K = 0.059 V]","options":["0.04736 V","0.4736 V","0.4736 mV","0.04736 mV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.04736 V: forgets log K = 16 or misplaces a decimal. (C)/(D): mV unit trap.\nWorking: E° = (0.059/n) log K = (0.059/2) × log(10¹⁶) = 0.0295 × 16 = 0.472 V ≈ 0.4736 V (the option value corresponds to 0.0592 V). Closest option (B).\nKey idea: Rewrite K = 10¹⁶; log K = 16; n = 2.","traps":["K written as 10 × 10¹⁵: Convert to 10¹⁶ before taking log.","n omitted: Two electrons transfer: divide by 2."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"EMF & Gibbs energy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The standard electrode potential E° and its temperature coefficient (dE°/dT) for a cell are 2 V and −5 × 10⁻⁴ V K⁻¹ at 300 K respectively. The cell reaction is Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s). The standard reaction enthalpy (ΔrH°) at 300 K in kJ mol⁻¹ is, [Use R = 8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and F = 96,000 C mol⁻¹]","options":["−412.8","−384.0","206.4","192.0"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) −384.0: ΔG taken as ΔH. (C)/(D): sign or n errors (n = 1 halves values).\nWorking: ΔG° = −2×96000×2 = −384 kJ; ΔS° = nF(dE°/dT) = −96 J K⁻¹; ΔH° = ΔG° + TΔS° = −384 − 28.8 = −412.8 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: ΔS from dE/dT; then Gibbs–Helmholtz combination.","traps":["ΔG reported as ΔH: Add TΔS term using ΔS = nF dE/dT.","n = 1 used: Zn → Zn²⁺: n = 2."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Kohlrausch's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Λ°m for NaCl, HCl and NaA are 126.4, 425.9 and 100.5 S cm² mol⁻¹, respectively. If the conductivity of 0.001 M HA is 5 × 10⁻⁵ S cm⁻¹, degree of dissociation of HA is :","options":["0.75","0.125","0.25","0.50"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 0.50 / (C) 0.25: slips of factor 2 or 4 in κ→Λm unit conversion; (A) 0.75: Λ° added wrongly (e.g. using NaCl instead of subtracting).\nWorking: Λ°(HA) = 425.9 + 100.5 − 126.4 = 400; Λm = 1000κ/c = 1000 × 5×10⁻⁵/0.001 = 50; α = 50/400 = 0.125.\nKey idea: Λ°(HA) = Λ°(HCl) + Λ°(NaA) − Λ°(NaCl); α = Λm/Λ°m.","traps":["Unit factor 1000 in Λm: Λm (S cm² mol⁻¹) = 1000 κ / c."]}],"m18-nitro":[{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"Identify [A], [B] and [C], respectively in the following reaction sequence:\n[A] —[NaNO₂, HCl, 273–278 K]→ C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[KI]→ [B] —[2Na, Dry ether]→ [C]","options":["[A] C₆H₅NH₂, [B] C₆H₅Cl, [C] C₆H₆","[A] C₆H₅NH₂, [B] C₆H₅Cl, [C] C₆H₅I","[A] C₆H₅NO₂, [B] C₆H₅I, [C] C₆H₅–C₆H₅","[A] C₆H₅NH₂, [B] C₆H₅I, [C] C₆H₅–C₆H₅"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chlorobenzene from counter-ion and benzene from 'reduction' by Na. (B) chlorobenzene then iodobenzene (nonsense halogen swap). (C) right B and C but A = nitrobenzene, not diazotisable. (D) correct.\nKey idea: Diazotisation needs ArNH₂; KI gives ArI; 2Na/ether couples two ArX to Ar–Ar.","traps":["Counter-ion chloride taken as the substituent introduced: The nucleophile that replaces N₂ is the one added in that step: KI gives ArI; Cl⁻ needs CuCl (Sandmeyer).","Fittig coupling product of haloarene with sodium: Two ArX + 2Na give Ar–Ar (Fittig); the 2Na in the arrow signals coupling of two aryl groups."]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"In the following reactions, which one is NOT correct?","options":["C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[EtOH]→ C₆H₅OEt","C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[H₃PO₂ / H₂O]→ C₆H₆","C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[CuCN / KCN]→ C₆H₅CN","C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[Potassium Iodide]→ C₆H₅I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong product: EtOH gives benzene, not phenetole (answer). (B) correct: H₃PO₂ reduction to benzene. (C) correct: Sandmeyer cyanation. (D) correct: KI gives iodobenzene without Cu.\nKey idea: EtOH and H₃PO₂ are both reducing agents for ArN₂⁺, giving ArH.","traps":["Ethanol reduces diazonium to arene, not ether or phenol: NCERT: mild reducing agents H₃PO₂ and CH₃CH₂OH reduce diazonium salts to arenes; ethanol is oxidised to ethanal.","Question asks for the NOT correct reaction: Mark each as right/wrong, then pick the wrong one."]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"The sequence from the following that would result in giving predominantly 3,4,5-Tribromoaniline is:","options":["Aniline —[Br₂, water]→","Nitrobenzene —[(i) Br₂, acetic acid; (ii) Sn, HCl]→","4-Nitroaniline —[(i) Br₂ (excess), acetic acid; (ii) NaNO₂, HCl, CuBr; (iii) Sn, HCl]→","Benzene —[(i) Br₂, AlBr₃; (ii) NH₃]→"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives 2,4,6-tribromoaniline. (B) gives 3-bromoaniline at best. (C) correct: 2,6-dibromo-4-nitroaniline, then NH₂ replaced by Br (1,2,3-tribromo-5-nitrobenzene), then NO₂ reduced to NH₂ at the position meta to the outer bromines. (D) bromobenzene does not aminate with NH₃ and has one Br.\nKey idea: Use NH₂ as a temporary director, then remove it via diazonium; the nitro group becomes the final NH₂.","traps":["Direct bromination of aniline gives 2,4,6 pattern: Bromine water on aniline gives 2,4,6-tribromoaniline (Br ortho/para to NH₂), not 3,4,5.","Directing groups decide bromination position: Nitro deactivates the ring, so Br₂/AcOH gives at most monobromination; it cannot give three Br at 3,4,5."],"topics":["Diazonium in synthesis","Aniline substitution"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"Which of the following amine(s) show(s) positive carbylamine test?\nA. (structure A shown below)\nB. (CH₃)₂NH\nC. CH₃NH₂\nD. (CH₃)₃N\nE. (structure E shown below)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["C Only","B, C and D Only","A and C Only","A and E Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Compound A, aniline (1°): yes; B, (CH₃)₂NH (2°): no; C, CH₃NH₂ (1°): yes; D, (CH₃)₃N (3°): no; E, N-methylaniline (2°): no. Option A omits aniline; option B picks 2°/3° amines; option C is correct; option D includes the secondary aryl amine.\nKey idea: Count H atoms on N: only –NH₂ gives an isocyanide.","traps":["Secondary or tertiary amine credited with carbylamine test: Carbylamine needs two N–H on the same N (RNH₂); secondary amines with one N–H do not form isocyanides.","Aromatic primary amine thought not to give carbylamine: NCERT: both aliphatic and aromatic primary amines give the carbylamine test."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C19-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"The descending order of basicity of following amines is:\n(A), (B), (C) – shown below\n(D) CH₃NH₂\n(E) (CH₃)₂NH\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["E > D > B > A > C","E > D > A > B > C","E > A > D > C > B","B > E > D > A > C"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option A is correct. Option B puts OCH₃-aniline below aniline (inductive-only view). Option C puts aniline above methylamine and nitroaniline above anisidine. Option D puts anisidine above the aliphatic amines.\nKey idea: Aliphatic >> aryl amines; within anilines, para donors raise and para acceptors lower basicity.","traps":["Aliphatic amines stronger bases than aryl amines: Aryl amines lose lone-pair availability to the ring; even p-anisidine (pKb ~8.7) is far weaker than CH₃NH₂ (pK_b 3.4).","Para substituent effect on aniline basicity: At para, +R of OCH₃ dominates, raising basicity above aniline; para NO₂ (−R) lowers it."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S2C19-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"Match the compounds (List-I) with the appropriate catalyst/reagents (List-II) for their reduction into corresponding amines.\nList-I (Compound) | List-II (Catalyst/Reagent)\nA. R–CO–NH₂ | I. NaOH (aq)\nB. C₆H₅–NO₂ | II. H₂/Ni\nC. R–C≡N | III. LiAlH₄, H₂O\nD. N-alkylphthalimide (shown below) | IV. Sn, HCl\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option A swaps compounds A and C: H₂/Ni does not reduce amides readily. Option B is correct. Option C swaps compounds B and C: Sn/HCl does not reduce nitriles to amines. Option D pairs B with NaOH and D with Sn/HCl, both wrong.\nKey idea: Start from the unique pairs (amide–LiAlH₄, phthalimide–NaOH) and the rest falls out.","traps":["Interchangeable reducing agents in list match: Find the unique fits: amides need LiAlH₄, Sn/HCl is the nitro reagent, which leaves H₂/Ni for the nitrile.","Gabriel phthalimide step is hydrolysis not reduction: N-alkylphthalimide releases RNH₂ by alkaline hydrolysis, so NaOH(aq) matches D."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan22S2C19-s.webp"],"topics":["Amine preparation by reduction","Gabriel synthesis"]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Consider the following sequence of reactions.\n4-Ethoxyaniline —[NaNO₂, HCl, 0–5 °C]→ (A) —[(i) phenol, NaOH; (ii) HCl dil.]→ (B) (Molecular formula C₁₄H₁₄N₂O₂) —[(i) NaOH; (ii) H₃CCH₂Br]→ (C) (Molecular formula C₁₆H₁₈N₂O₂)\nTotal number of sp³ hybridised carbon atoms in the major product C formed is ______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 2 (forgetting the second ethylation), 3 (counting one CH₂ as sp²), 0 or 12/16 (counting ring carbons).\nKey idea: Use the formula change (+C₂H₄) to confirm the second ethyl group, then count only alkyl carbons.","traps":["Williamson ethylation step adds a second ethoxy group: C₂H₄ increase from B to C means one more ethyl group; NaOH/EtBr ethylates the phenolic OH.","Hybridisation count of aromatic and azo carbons: Only the ethyl carbons (CH₂ and CH₃ of each OEt) are sp³: 2 × 2 = 4; all ring carbons are sp²."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan23S2C25-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Consider the following sequence of reactions:\nChlorobenzene —[(i) Mg, dry ether; (ii) CO₂, H₃O⁺; (iii) NH₃, Δ]→ A —[Br₂, NaOH]→ B\n11.25 mg of chlorobenzene will produce ---- ×10⁻¹ mg of product B. (Consider the reactions result in complete conversion.)\n[Given molar mass of C, H, O, N and Cl as 12, 1, 16, 14 and 35.5 g mol⁻¹ respectively]","type":"num","answer":"93","range":[93.0,93.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 121 (stopping at benzamide), 122 (benzoic acid), 107 (benzylamine, carbon not lost), 9 or 9.3 (ignoring the × 10⁻¹ scaling).\nKey idea: Every step is 1:1 and Hoffmann loses one carbon, so moles of aniline = moles of chlorobenzene.","traps":["Hoffmann bromamide product keeps all carbons: Hoffmann removes the carbonyl carbon: PhCONH₂ → PhNH₂ (M = 93).","Power-of-ten scaling in answer blank: 9.3 mg = 93 × 10⁻¹ mg."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S1C25-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction with nitrous acid","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"X g of ethylamine is subjected to reaction with NaNO₂/HCl followed by water; evolved dinitrogen gas which occupied 2.24 L volume at STP. X is ______ × 10⁻¹ g.","type":"num","answer":"45","range":[45.0,45.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4.5 or 0.45: answer box scaling (×10⁻¹) misread. 90: assumes 2 N per N₂ means 2 amines.\nWorking: C₂H₅NH₂ + HNO₂ → C₂H₅OH + N₂ + H₂O (1:1); 2.24 L at STP = 0.1 mol N₂ → 0.1 mol amine × 45 g mol⁻¹ = 4.5 g = 45 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: One RNH₂ gives one N₂ – the two N atoms come from the amine and nitrite.","traps":["Two amines per N₂: One N from amine, one from HNO₂.","Scale factor ×10⁻¹: Divide grams by 10⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Phthalimide is made to undergo following sequence of reactions.\nPhthalimide —(i) KOH (ii) Benzylchloride→ 'P'\nTotal number of π bonds present in product 'P' is/are ______.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (thinking P is benzylamine after hydrolysis, which has only 3 π bonds); 5 (forgetting the benzyl ring); 16 (counting π electrons).\nWorking: P = N-benzylphthalimide: phthalimide benzene ring 3 π + 2 C=O = 5; benzyl ring 3 π; total 8.\nKey idea: Without hydrolysis, P keeps both C=O and two benzene rings.","traps":["Hydrolysis step assumed: Only KOH and RX given.","π bonds vs electrons: Count bonds: Kekulé 3 per ring."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Sandmeyer & Gattermann","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The number of halobenzenes from the following that can be prepared by Sandmeyer's reaction is ____\n[Structures: I fluorobenzene, II chlorobenzene, III bromobenzene, IV iodobenzene, V astatobenzene]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3: iodobenzene counted (made from diazonium salt, but with KI, not CuI). 4: F counted too. 5: all.\nWorking: Sandmeyer (CuCl/HCl, CuBr/HBr) gives chlorobenzene and bromobenzene only; iodobenzene uses KI (no Cu), fluorobenzene uses HBF₄ (Balz–Schiemann), At not via Sandmeyer → 2.\nKey idea: Sandmeyer = Cu(I) halide → only Cl and Br (plus CN).","traps":["Iodination by KI counted as Sandmeyer: Sandmeyer needs Cu(I); KI route is separate.","Fluoro via Balz-Schiemann counted: F via HBF₄/heat, not Cu salt."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"9.3 g of pure aniline is treated with bromine water at room temperature to give a white precipitate of the product 'P'. The mass of product 'P' obtained is 26.4 g. The percentage yield is ____ %.","type":"num","answer":"80","range":[80.0,80.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using monobromoaniline (M = 172): 26.4/17.2 > 100% – a check that flags the error; using M with Br = 79.9 gives 329.7 → 80.1% (rounds to 80).\nWorking: aniline 93 g mol⁻¹ → 0.1 mol → 0.1 mol 2,4,6-tribromoaniline (M = 330); theoretical 33.0 g; yield = 26.4/33.0 = 80%.\nKey idea: Bromine water → tribromo product; 1:1 mole ratio.","traps":["Monobromination assumed: –NH₂ activates o/p fully → tribromo.","Molar mass of product: C₆H₄Br₃N: 93 − 3 + 240 = 330."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reaction with nitrous acid","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"X g of ethanamine was subjected to reaction with NaNO₂/HCl followed by hydrolysis to liberate N₂ and HCl. The HCl generated was completely neutralised by 0.2 moles of NaOH. X is ______ g.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4.5 (2 HCl per amine), 18 (½ HCl), 9.2 (ethanol molar mass 46).\nWorking: C₂H₅NH₂ → C₂H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ → (H₂O) C₂H₅OH + N₂ + HCl; 1 HCl per amine; 0.2 mol × 45 g mol⁻¹ = 9 g.\nKey idea: Diazonium chloride hydrolysis releases one HCl per amine.","traps":["HCl consumed vs released: Count only HCl from hydrolysis as stated.","Wrong molar mass: C₂H₅NH₂ = 45."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"9.3 g of pure aniline upon diazotisation followed by coupling with phenol gives an orange dye. The mass of orange dye produced (assume 100% yield/conversion) is ______ g. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"20","range":[20.0,20.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 19 (truncating 19.8), 18 (wrong formula/molar mass), 10 (using aniline mass ratio wrongly).\nWorking: 9.3 g aniline = 0.1 mol → 0.1 mol p-hydroxyazobenzene (C₁₂H₁₀N₂O, M = 198) = 19.8 g ≈ 20.\nKey idea: Aniline : dye = 1 : 1; M(dye) = 198 g mol⁻¹.","traps":["Dye formula miscounted: C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄–OH = C₁₂H₁₀N₂O.","Rounding 19.8: Round 19.8 to 20."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sandmeyer & Gattermann","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Identify the product A in the following reaction.\nAniline (C₆H₅NH₂) →[(i) NaNO₂ + HCl (ii) Cu₂Cl₂ (iii) NaOH, 623 K, 300 atm (iv) H⁺] A","options":["4-chloroaniline","phenol (C₆H₅OH)","4-chlorophenol","4-aminophenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D): assume ring chlorination or extra substitution at para; none occurs.\nWorking: diazotisation gives C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻; Sandmeyer (Cu₂Cl₂) gives chlorobenzene; NaOH at 623 K/300 atm (Dow) gives sodium phenoxide; acidification gives phenol.\nKey idea: Every step acts at the same carbon: NH₂ → N₂⁺ → Cl → OH.","traps":["Cu₂Cl₂ taken as ring chlorinating agent: Sandmeyer replaces N₂⁺ only; no ring substitution."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"An amine (X) is prepared by ammonolysis of benzyl chloride. On adding p-toluenesulphonyl chloride to it the solution remains clear. Molar mass of the amine (X) formed is ______ g mol⁻¹.\n(Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹ C : 12, H : 1, O : 16, N : 14)","type":"num","answer":"287","range":[287.0,287.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 107 (benzylamine – primary, sulphonamide dissolves in alkali), 197 (dibenzylamine), 288/322 (quaternary ion or salt).\nWorking: no reaction with the Hinsberg-type reagent → tertiary amine; ammonolysis with excess benzyl chloride gives tribenzylamine (C₆H₅CH₂)₃N, C₂₁H₂₁N = 252 + 21 + 14 = 287. (Wording is loose: a primary amine also gives a clear alkaline solution of its sulphonamide; the intended answer follows the 'no reaction' reading.)\nKey idea: No reaction with sulphonyl chloride = tertiary amine = tribenzylamine.","traps":["Clear solution read as soluble sulphonamide: Here \"remains clear\" means nothing formed – no reaction.","Formula of tribenzylamine miscounted: (C₇H₇)₃N = C₂₁H₂₁N."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Ammonolysis of alkyl halides"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"If 279 g of aniline is reacted with one equivalent of benzenediazonium chloride, the maximum amount of aniline yellow formed will be ________ g. (nearest integer)\n(consider complete conversion).","type":"num","answer":"591","range":[591.0,591.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 588/594 from rounded molar masses; 701 if HCl is not lost (C₁₂H₁₂N₃Cl, M ≈ 233.5); 197 if moles of aniline ignored.\nWorking: C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + C₆H₅NH₂ → p-H₂N–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅ (C₁₂H₁₁N₃, M = 197) + HCl; 279/93 = 3 mol → 3 × 197 = 591 g.\nKey idea: Aniline yellow C₁₂H₁₁N₃ = 197 g mol⁻¹; 1:1 with aniline.","traps":["Not recognising aniline yellow: Aniline yellow = p-aminoazobenzene.","Molar mass slip: Write C₁₂H₁₁N₃ carefully."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Number of amine compounds from the following giving solids which are soluble in NaOH upon reaction with Hinsberg's reagent is ________.\n1) aniline (C₆H₅NH₂); 2) benzamide (C₆H₅CONH₂); 3) semicarbazide (H₂NNH–CO–NH₂); 4) N-methylaniline (C₆H₅NH–CH₃); 5) C₆H₅–NH–(six-membered ring drawn with two C=C)–NH₂ (4-aminodiphenylamine type, terminal primary NH₂); 6) urea (H₂N–CO–NH₂); 7) 2-methoxyaniline (o-anisidine); 8) propan-1-amine (CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂); 9) triethylamine; 10) N-ethyl-N-methyl-cyclohexylmethanamine (tertiary); 11) cyclohexylamine","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (including benzamide or N-methylaniline), 7–8 (including urea/semicarbazide), 4 (missing compound 5 or o-anisidine).\nWorking: primary amines give N-substituted sulphonamides with acidic N–H, soluble in NaOH: aniline, the NH₂-bearing diphenylamine derivative, o-anisidine, propylamine, cyclohexylamine = 5. 2° (N-methylaniline) gives insoluble product; 3° do not react; amides, urea, semicarbazide are not amines.\nKey idea: Only 1° amine N gives an alkali-soluble sulphonamide.","traps":["Amides/urea counted as amines: –CO–NH₂ is an amide, not an amine.","Secondary amine counted: 2° sulphonamide has no N–H, insoluble in alkali."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : All the following compounds react with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride.\nC₆H₅NH₂  (C₆H₅)₂NH  (C₆H₅)₃N\nStatement (II) : Their products in the above reaction are soluble in aqueous NaOH.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): student assumes the 3° amine also reacts. (A): student thinks only the reaction claim fails but all products dissolve in NaOH.\nWorking: triphenylamine (3°) does not react, so I is false; the sulphonamide from diphenylamine has no N–H and is insoluble in NaOH, so II is false.\nKey idea: Only sulphonamides with an N–H (from 1° amines) dissolve in alkali; 3° amines give no sulphonamide.","traps":["Tertiary amine assumed to react: No N–H on 3° amine → no sulphonamide.","All sulphonamides taken as alkali-soluble: 2° sulphonamide has no acidic H."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Correct order of basic strength of Pyrrole (five-membered aromatic ring with N–H), Pyridine (six-membered aromatic ring with N) and Piperidine (saturated six-membered ring with N–H) is:","options":["Pyridine > Piperidine > Pyrrole","Pyrrole > Piperidine > Pyridine","Pyrrole > Pyridine > Piperidine","Piperidine > Pyridine > Pyrrole"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses that sp² N is less basic than sp³ N. (B)/(C) place pyrrole first, ignoring that its lone pair is in the aromatic sextet.\nWorking: piperidine (sp³ N lone pair, pKaH ≈ 11) > pyridine (sp² N lone pair not in the ring π-system, ≈ 5.2) > pyrrole (lone pair part of aromatic sextet, ≈ −4).\nKey idea: Pyrrole would lose aromaticity on protonating N, so it is hardly basic.","traps":["Pyrrole N–H looks like a 2° amine: Check whether the lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet.","sp² vs sp³ nitrogen: More s-character holds lone pair tighter."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : The NH₂ group in Aniline is ortho and para directing and a powerful activating group.\nStatement (II) : Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Craft's reaction (alkylation and acylation).\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): student thinks a powerful activator must undergo FC readily. (D): remembers anilinium ion is meta-directing and doubts Statement I.\nWorking: –NH₂ is a strong o/p activator; aniline forms a salt with AlCl₃ (Lewis acid–base), the N⁺ deactivates the ring, so no Friedel–Crafts.\nKey idea: AlCl₃ complexes the N lone pair; activation is lost.","traps":["Activator assumed to give Friedel–Crafts: Lewis acid catalyst reacts with the basic NH₂ first."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amine classification & naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Aminobenzene and aniline are same organic compounds.\nStatement (II) : Aminobenzene and aniline are different organic compounds.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): thinks 'amino' prefix means a different compound. (A)/(D): logical contradiction, picked only by misreading.\nWorking: Aminobenzene, aniline and benzenamine are all C₆H₅NH₂.\nKey idea: Same structure, different names.","traps":["Unfamiliar name taken as different compound: Draw the structure from the name."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of compounds which give reaction with Hinsberg's reagent is ______.\nCompounds (drawn): (1) benzenediazonium chloride C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻; (2) dibenzamide C₆H₅CO–NH–COC₆H₅; (3) aniline C₆H₅NH₂; (4) N-phenylpiperidine; (5) N-methylbenzylamine C₆H₅CH₂–NH–CH₃; (6) trimethylamine (CH₃)₃N; (7) ethane-1,2-diamine H₂N–CH₂CH₂–NH₂; (8) piperidine (cyclic N–H); (9) pyridine; (10) propan-1-amine CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂; (11) urea H₂N–CO–NH₂.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (counting urea and imide N–H), 6 (counting one of them), 4 (missing the diamine or cyclic piperidine).\nWorking: aniline, N-methylbenzylamine, ethane-1,2-diamine, piperidine, propan-1-amine = 5; diazonium salt, imide, urea, 3° amines and pyridine do not.\nKey idea: React only if N is an amine nitrogen bearing at least one H; amides/imides/urea are too weakly nucleophilic.","traps":["Any N–H counted: Amide N lone pair is delocalised; no Hinsberg reaction.","Cyclic 2° amine missed: Ring N–H is a 2° amine."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following is strongest Bronsted base?","options":["Aniline [C₆H₅NH₂]","Diphenylamine [(C₆H₅)₂NH]","Pyrrole [five-membered aromatic ring with N–H, lone pair shown on N]","Pyrrolidine [saturated five-membered ring with N–H]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Aniline: lone pair delocalised into one ring (pKb 9.4). (B) Diphenylamine: delocalised into two rings, even weaker. (C) Pyrrole: lone pair used in aromaticity; protonation destroys aromaticity, extremely weak base — the lone pair drawn on N tempts students.\nWorking: pyrrolidine is a sec-alkyl amine with a localised sp³ lone pair (pKb ≈ 2.7); aniline and diphenylamine have lone pairs delocalised into rings, and pyrrole’s lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet.\nKey idea: Localised sp³ lone pair on a saturated amine is most available for a proton.","traps":["Drawn lone pair on pyrrole N looks available: Check if the lone pair is part of an aromatic 6π system.","Two aryl groups thought to donate electrons: Aryl groups withdraw the lone pair by resonance."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ammonolysis of alkyl halides","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Identify B formed in the reaction. Cl–(CH₂)₄–Cl —(excess NH₃)→ A —(NaOH)→ B + H₂O + NaCl","options":["Pyrrolidine (five-membered saturated ring containing NH)","1,1′-Bipyrrolidine (two pyrrolidine rings joined through an N–N bond)","Cl⁻ H₃N⁺–(CH₂)₄–N⁺H₃ Cl⁻","H₂N–(CH₂)₄–NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) N–N bonded dimer: no reasonable route. (C) is a diammonium salt (A-like), not the NaOH product, and NaOH would remove it. (D) the diamine would need 2 NaCl + 2 H₂O, not the single NaCl shown.\nWorking: ammonolysis of one C–Cl gives 4-chlorobutan-1-amine, which cyclises intramolecularly (5-membered ring) to pyrrolidinium chloride (A); NaOH liberates pyrrolidine with one H₂O and one NaCl, matching the stoichiometry shown.\nKey idea: Count by-products: one NaCl and one H₂O means a single ammonium salt was neutralised; a 4-carbon chain closes to a stable 5-membered ring.","traps":["Both Cl replaced by NH₂: Check by-products: one NaCl only; 5-membered ring formation is fast.","Salt taken as final product: NaOH converts any ammonium salt into the free amine."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The arenium ion which is not involved in the bromination of Aniline is __________.\n[Four σ-complex structures; the original paper shows the charge symbol as ⊖ though arenium ions are cations]","options":["para-attack ion: C4 sp³ bearing H and Br, charge on the iminium N (=NH₂ with charge sign)","ortho-attack ion: C2 sp³ bearing H and Br, NH₂ on C1, charge on C3","ortho-attack ion: C2 sp³ bearing H and Br, iminium =NH₂ on C1 with charge sign","meta-attack ion: C3 sp³ bearing H and Br, NH₂ on C1, charge on C2"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) para-attack iminium form and (C) ortho-attack iminium form look odd because charge is on N, but they are the key stabilising forms; (B) charge at C3 may look 'meta', but it is a valid ortho-attack form.\nWorking: –NH₂ is o/p-directing; aniline brominates at 2,4,6 positions, so the σ-complex from attack at the meta carbon (D) is not involved. (B) is a valid ortho-attack canonical form (charge at C3 adjacent to C2).\nKey idea: Judge each ion by where Br/H sits (sp³ carbon), not by where the charge is drawn.","traps":["Charge on 'meta' carbon taken as meta attack: Find the carbon bearing H and Br; that tells the attack position.","⊖ sign on arenium ions in the original paper: Treat the charge as positive; arenium ions are carbocations."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sandmeyer & Gattermann","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The product A formed in the following reaction is\n[aniline (C₆H₅NH₂) —(NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C; then Cu₂Cl₂)→ A]","options":["2-chloroaniline","anilinium chloride (C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻)","chlorobenzene","1,2-dichlorobenzene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ring chlorination with NH₂ retained. (B) only salt formation with HCl. (D) double chlorination.\nWorking: diazotisation gives C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻; Sandmeyer with Cu₂Cl₂/HCl replaces N₂⁺ by Cl.\nKey idea: N₂⁺ is replaced; the ring is not further substituted.","traps":["Salt formation mistaken as product: NaNO₂/HCl at 0 °C gives the diazonium salt, then Cu(I) substitution."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The final product A, formed in the following multistep reaction sequence is :\nBromobenzene (C₆H₅Br) —(i) Mg, ether, then CO₂, H⁺; (ii) NH₃, Δ; (iii) Br₂, NaOH→ A","options":["Benzoyl bromide, C₆H₅COBr","Benzoic acid, C₆H₅COOH","Aniline, C₆H₅NH₂","Benzamide, C₆H₅CONH₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Benzoyl bromide: Br₂ read as brominating the acyl group. (B) Benzoic acid: stopping after step (i). (D) Benzamide: stopping after step (ii), missing Hoffmann degradation.\nWorking: PhBr → PhMgBr → (CO₂, H⁺) PhCOOH → (NH₃, Δ) PhCOO⁻NH₄⁺ → PhCONH₂ → (Br₂/NaOH, Hoffmann bromamide) PhNH₂.\nKey idea: Br₂/NaOH on a primary amide = Hoffmann: RCONH₂ → RNH₂ (one C less).","traps":["Stopping at the amide: Br₂ + NaOH on RCONH₂ always means Hoffmann bromamide.","Br₂ taken as acyl bromination: Acyl halides come from PCl₅/SOCl₂, not Br₂/NaOH."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Following is a confirmatory test for aromatic primary amines. Identify reagent (A) and (B).\nAniline (C₆H₅NH₂) —(A)→ benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) —(B), NaOH→ Scarlet red dye","options":["A = NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5°C ; B = 2-naphthol (β-naphthol; OH at C-2 of naphthalene)","A = NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5°C ; B = aniline (C₆H₅NH₂)","A = NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5°C ; B = 1-naphthol (α-naphthol; OH at C-1 of naphthalene)","A = HNO₃/H₂SO₄ ; B = phenol (C₆H₅OH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Aniline coupling gives yellow p-aminoazobenzene, not the confirmatory scarlet dye. (C) 1-Naphthol: wrong isomer for the standard test. (D) HNO₃/H₂SO₄ nitrates, does not diazotise.\nWorking: diazotisation with NaNO₂/HCl at 0–5 °C, then coupling with alkaline β-naphthol gives the orange-red/scarlet azo dye (1-phenylazo-2-naphthol) – the dye test for aromatic primary amines.\nKey idea: Dye test: ArNH₂ → ArN₂⁺Cl⁻ (NaNO₂/HCl, 0–5 °C) + alkaline 2-naphthol → scarlet dye.","traps":["α- vs β-naphthol: Standard reagent is β-naphthol (OH at C-2, next to β carbon).","Coupling partner taken as aniline: Scarlet/orange-red dye comes from β-naphthol."],"topics":["Coupling reactions","Tests for amines"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The products A and B formed in the following reaction scheme are respectively\nBenzene —[i) conc. HNO₃/conc. H₂SO₄, 323–333 K; ii) Sn/HCl]→ A —[i) NaNO₂, HCl, 273–278 K; ii) Phenol]→ B","options":["Aniline, and 2-(phenylazo)phenol (o-hydroxyazobenzene: Ph–N=N–C₆H₄–OH with OH ortho to the azo group)","Aniline, and 4-(phenylazo)phenol (p-hydroxyazobenzene: HO–C₆H₄–N=N–Ph, para)","1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene (m-chloronitrobenzene), and a biphenyl: 3'-nitro-[1,1'-biphenyl]-4-ol type structure (HO-C₆H₄–C₆H₄–NO₂, no azo link)","Aniline, and an amino-hydroxy biphenyl (2-amino-4'-hydroxybiphenyl: C–C linked rings, no azo link)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) coupling ortho instead of para. (C) treats Sn/HCl wrongly and forms C–C biaryl. (D) C–C coupling instead of N=N azo linkage.\nWorking: nitration then Sn/HCl reduction gives aniline (A); diazotisation at 273–278 K and coupling with phenol (alkaline) at the para position gives p-hydroxyazobenzene, an orange dye (B).\nKey idea: Diazonium ion is a weak electrophile; it attacks the para position of phenoxide and the N=N is retained.","traps":["Ortho vs para coupling: Coupling goes para (ortho only if para is blocked).","Azo link replaced by biaryl bond: Diazonium coupling keeps N=N; only Sandmeyer/Gattermann lose N₂."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The azo-dye (Y) formed in the following reactions is: Sulphanilic acid + NaNO₂ + CH₃COOH → X. X + [1-naphthylamine (naphthalene with NH₂ at C-1)] → Y","options":["[Naphthalene core with two HO₃S– groups and two –N=N– links, each azo link to a 4-aminonaphthyl group]","[HSO₃–C₆H₄–N=N– on one naphthalene ring and –N=N–C₆H₄–SO₃H on the other ring (bis-azo naphthalene, no NH₂)]","[4-(HSO₃)C₆H₄–N=N– at C-4 of 1-naphthylamine; NH₂ and azo group para to each other on the same ring: 4-amino-1-naphthyl azo benzenesulphonic acid]","[4-(HSO₃)C₆H₄–N=N– on one naphthalene ring with NH₂ on the other ring]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B): bis-azo products / wrong placement of SO₃H. (D): coupling on the other ring – ignores activation of the ring bearing NH₂ and para selectivity.\nWorking: diazotised sulphanilic acid couples para to NH₂ of 1-naphthylamine (C-4, same ring). 'HSO₃–' in the options is just another way of writing –SO₃H.\nKey idea: Coupling occurs para to the activating –NH₂ on the same ring.","traps":["Coupling on wrong ring: Coupling goes to the activated ring, para to NH₂.","Double coupling assumed: One mole diazonium, one coupling site para to NH₂."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement I : Aniline reacts with con. H₂SO₄, followed by heating at 453 − 473 K gives p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, which gives blood red colour in the 'Lassaigne's test'. Statement II : In Friedel - Craft's alkylation and acylation reactions, aniline forms salt with the AlCl₃ catalyst. Due to this, nitrogen of aniline acquires a positive charge and acts as deactivating group. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both statement I and statement II are true","Statement I is true but statement II is false","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): doubting Statement II. (C)/(D): thinking blood-red needs only Fe/SCN reagent not N+S, or expecting Prussian blue.\nWorking: sulphonation of aniline at 453–473 K gives sulphanilic acid; with N and S both present, Lassaigne extract gives NaSCN → blood red with Fe³⁺. Statement II is the NCERT reason aniline fails Friedel–Crafts.\nKey idea: N and S together in the compound → SCN⁻ → blood red with Fe³⁺.","traps":["Prussian blue vs blood red: When both N and S are present, NaSCN forms → blood red."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A compound (x) with molar mass 108 g mol⁻¹ undergoes acetylation to give product with molar mass 192 g mol⁻¹. The number of amino groups in the compound (x) is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.95 (not a whole number) from using 43 (adds CH₃CO without removing H); 3 if using 28 per group.\nWorking: each acetylation of –NH₂ replaces H by COCH₃, adding 42 g mol⁻¹; (192 − 108)/42 = 2 (e.g. phenylenediamine, M = 108).\nKey idea: Each acetyl adds 43 − 1 = 42.","traps":["Mass added per acetylation: Net increase = 42 per NH₂."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The major products A and B from the following reactions are:\nAcetanilide (C₆H₅NHCOCH₃) —Br₂/AcOH→ A; Acetanilide —LiAlH₄→ B","options":["A = 2,4,6-tribromoacetanilide; B = C₆H₅NH–CH(OH)CH₃ (N-(1-hydroxyethyl)aniline)","A = 4-bromoacetanilide (p-Br-C₆H₄-NHCOCH₃); B = N-ethylaniline (C₆H₅NHCH₂CH₃)","A = 2,4,6-tribromoacetanilide; B = aniline (C₆H₅NH₂)","A = 4-bromoacetanilide; B = C₆H₅NH–CH(OH)CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): tribromo product copies the aniline + bromine water result. (A)/(D): carbinolamine B assumes LiAlH₄ stops at C–OH as for ketones. (C): aniline B assumes reductive cleavage/hydrolysis of the amide.\nWorking: the acetyl group moderates –NH activation, so Br₂/AcOH gives mainly the para monobromo product (ortho blocked sterically). LiAlH₄ reduces the amide C=O fully to CH₂: C₆H₅NHCOCH₃ → C₆H₅NHCH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: Acetylation tames –NH₂ so only para-monobromination occurs; LiAlH₄ converts –CONH– to –CH₂NH–.","traps":["Acetanilide treated like aniline: Acetyl lone-pair delocalisation lowers activation → mono para.","LiAlH₄ stops at alcohol: Amide C=O is reduced completely to CH₂."],"topics":["Aniline substitution","Amine preparation by reduction"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is\nBenzene ring carrying –CH₂CONH₂ and, ortho to it, –COOCH₃ (methyl 2-(carbamoylmethyl)benzoate) —Br₂/NaOH, Δ→ ?","options":["tetralin-1,3-dione: benzo-fused six-membered carbocycle Ar–C(=O)–CH₂–C(=O)–CH₂–Ar (two C=O, at C1 and C3)","homophthalic anhydride (benzo-fused six-membered cyclic anhydride, isochroman-1,3-dione)","homophthalimide (benzo-fused six-membered imide, isoquinoline-1,3-dione with NH)","isoindolin-1-one: benzo-fused five-membered lactam, ring = Ar–CH₂–NH–C(=O)–Ar"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) keeps the amide carbon (no degradation) and forms an imide. (B) assumes base hydrolysis of both groups then anhydride formation. (A) assumes a carbon–carbon (Dieckmann/Claisen-type) closure. All three are six-membered: the one-carbon loss is the key discriminator.\nWorking: Hoffmann bromamide converts –CH₂CONH₂ to –CH₂NH₂ (one carbon lost). The new benzylic amine attacks the ortho ester intramolecularly, eliminating CH₃OH and closing the five-membered lactam (isoindolin-1-one).\nKey idea: Hoffmann removes the carbonyl carbon; the remaining CH₂NH₂ + ortho CO₂Me close a 5-membered lactam.","traps":["Carbon loss ignored: Count carbons: Hoffmann product has one less C.","Ring size miscounted: Count ring atoms: C(aryl)–CH₂–N–C(=O)–C(aryl) = 5."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Compound P (M.F. C₁₄H₁₃ON) —HCl, Δ→ Filter → Residue Q + Filtrate; Filtrate —NaOH→ Oily liquid R.\nCompound P is neutral, Q gives effervescence with NaHCO₃ while R reacts with Hinsberg's reagent to give solid soluble in NaOH. Compound P is","options":["N-methyl-N-phenylbenzamide, C₆H₅CON(CH₃)C₆H₅","4-methyl-N-phenylbenzamide, p-CH₃C₆H₄CONHC₆H₅","(3-(methylamino)phenyl)(phenyl)methanone, C₆H₅CO–C₆H₄–NHCH₃ (meta)","3-methyl-5-phenylbenzamide (biphenyl carboxamide with CH₃), primary amide –CONH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) also hydrolyses to an acid + amine, but N-methylaniline is 2° (Hinsberg product insoluble in NaOH). (C) is a ketone/amine – not neutral and no acid formed. (D) is a primary amide giving NH₃, not an oily amine.\nWorking: acid hydrolysis of the neutral anilide gives p-toluic acid (insoluble solid residue Q, fizzes with NaHCO₃) and anilinium chloride in the filtrate; NaOH liberates aniline (oily R), a 1° amine whose Hinsberg sulphonamide is NaOH-soluble. Formula check C₁₄H₁₃NO fits.\nKey idea: NaOH-soluble Hinsberg product ⇒ 1° amine; acid residue ⇒ amide linkage ⇒ ArCONHAr'.","traps":["1° vs 2° amine from Hinsberg solubility: Only 1° sulphonamide has acidic N–H, so it dissolves in NaOH.","Neutral compound with amine group: A free amine is basic, not neutral; only amides are neutral."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Number of isomeric aromatic amines with molecular formula C₈H₁₁N, which can be synthesized by Gabriel Phthalimide synthesis is ________.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if the enantiomers of 1-phenylethanamine are not counted; 14 or more if aryl amines (ethylanilines, dimethylanilines) are included; 3 if only benzylamines are counted.\nWorking: Gabriel gives only primary amines with NH₂ on an sp³ carbon: 2-phenylethanamine, 1-phenylethanamine, o-, m-, p-methylbenzylamine = 5 constitutional isomers; 1-phenylethanamine is chiral (R and S), giving 6. Aniline-type (ArNH₂) isomers such as ethylanilines/xylidines cannot be made (aryl halides do not undergo SN2).\nKey idea: Only C₆H₅–C–NH₂ (benzylic/homobenzylic) types qualify; one is chiral, giving 5 + 1 = 6.","traps":["Aryl amines included: Phthalimide anion cannot displace aryl halide.","Enantiomers not counted: Check each structure for a stereocentre; 1-phenylethanamine is chiral."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sandmeyer & Gattermann","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List I with List II:\nBenzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂Cl) is reacted with reagents in List I to form products in List II.\nLIST I (Reagent): A. Aniline (C₆H₅NH₂); B. HBF₄, Δ; C. Cu, HCl; D. CuCN/KCN\nLIST II (Product): I. Fluorobenzene (C₆H₅F); II. Benzonitrile (C₆H₅CN); III. 4-Aminoazobenzene, C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄–NH₂ (para); IV. Chlorobenzene (C₆H₅Cl)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): CuCl-type product swapped with CN product (C-II, D-IV) – Sandmeyer cyanide vs Gattermann chloride mixed. (C)/(D): aniline matched to fluoro product and HBF₄ to azo dye.\nWorking: aniline coupling → p-aminoazobenzene (III); HBF₄ then heat → fluorobenzene, Balz–Schiemann (I); Cu/HCl → chlorobenzene, Gattermann (IV); CuCN/KCN → benzonitrile, Sandmeyer (II).\nKey idea: Cu/HCl gives Ar–Cl (Gattermann); CuCN gives Ar–CN; HBF₄/Δ gives Ar–F; aniline couples.","traps":["Gattermann vs Sandmeyer swapped: Product follows the anion supplied: HCl → Cl, CN⁻ → CN.","Fluorination reagent: Fluoroborate on heating gives Ar–F."],"topics":["Sandmeyer & Gattermann","Coupling reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The product (P) formed from the following multistep reaction is: [4-nitrotoluene (CH₃ and NO₂ para) → (i) Br₂ (ii) H₂/Pd (iii) NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C (iv) H₃PO₂ → (P)]","options":["2-Bromotoluene (Br and CH₃ ortho)","2-Bromo-4-methylphenol (OH; Br ortho to OH; CH₃ para to OH)","3-Bromotoluene (Br and CH₃ meta)","3-Bromo-4-methylphenol (OH; Br meta to OH and ortho to CH₃; CH₃ para to OH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) assumes Br goes ortho to the amino group (after reduction) or meta to CH₃; (D) is exactly the product if the diazonium group were hydrolysed to OH (warm water) instead of being removed by H₃PO₂; (B) additionally misplaces Br ortho to the N-derived group.\nWorking: CH₃ (o/p) and NO₂ (meta) both direct Br to C-2 (ortho to CH₃) → 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene; H₂/Pd reduces NO₂ to NH₂; diazotisation then H₃PO₂ replaces N₂⁺ by H → 2-bromotoluene.\nKey idea: H₃PO₂ removes the diazo group completely, so the NO₂ only served to steer bromine ortho to CH₃.","traps":["Diazonium converted to phenol: H₃PO₂ / ethanol replace N₂⁺ by H.","Bromination position misjudged: Bromination occurs on nitrotoluene; both groups direct to C-2."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In the following reaction, 'A' is\nCompound (shown below) + EtO–CO–OEt (diethyl carbonate) → 'A' (Major product)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) iminoether ring would need loss of water/ethanol in a different way; not formed from carbonate. (C) the open-chain carbamate is the intermediate that cyclises by losing EtOH. (D) wrong carbon skeleton (no C–C bond formation possible).\nKey idea: Both ethoxy groups are displaced: N first (more nucleophilic), then the OH intramolecularly, giving a stable five-membered oxazolidin-2-one.","traps":["Stopping at the intermediate: Check for a second nucleophile placed for 5-ring closure."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S1C08-s.webp","img/jee/JM23Feb01S1C08-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ammonolysis of alkyl halides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A) : α-halocarboxylic acid on reaction with dil. NH₃ gives good yield of α-amino carboxylic acid whereas the yield of amines is very low when prepared from alkyl halides. Reason (R) : Amino acids exist in zwitter ion form in aqueous medium. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) is the common pick: both facts known but the link (protonated N is not a nucleophile) missed. (A)/(B) reject one true statement.\nKey idea: In the zwitterion H₃N⁺–CHR–COO⁻ the nitrogen has no lone pair free, so it cannot attack another halo-acid; hence good yield.","traps":["True facts but link not seen: Ask: what stops the product amine reacting again? Protonated –NH₃⁺.","Assertion doubted because ammonolysis 'always gives mixtures': The exception arises because the product is internally protonated."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Choose the correct colour of the product for the following reaction. p-HO₃S–C₆H₄–N=N–OOCCH₃ (diazonium acetate of sulphanilic acid) + 1-Naphthyl amine →","options":["White","Yellow","Red","Blue"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Yellow: confuses with aniline yellow / methyl orange colour. (A) White and (D) Blue: unrelated.\nKey idea: Sulphanilic acid diazonium + 1-naphthylamine gives a red azo dye (used to detect NO₂⁻).","traps":["Yellow vs red azo dye: Naphthylamine-coupled sulphanilic dye is red (nitrite test)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties of amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I : Pure Aniline and other arylamines are usually colourless. Statement II : Arylamines get coloured on storage due to atmospheric reduction. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): misses the oxidation→reduction swap. (C)/(D): doubts the colourless statement.\nKey idea: Air (O₂) oxidises arylamines; atmosphere cannot reduce them.","traps":["Oxidation vs reduction: Air is an oxidant."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Identify the product formed (A and E): 4-nitrotoluene —Br₂→ A —Sn/HCl→ B —NaNO₂/HCl, 273–278 K→ C —H₃PO₂/H₂O→ D —(i) KMnO₄/KOH; (ii) H₃O⁺→ E","options":["A = 2,5-dibromo-4-nitrotoluene (Br ortho to Me and Br ortho to NO₂, on opposite sides); E = 2,5-dibromobenzoic acid","A = 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene; E = 2-bromotoluene","A = 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene; E = 2-bromobenzoic acid","A = 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene; E = 2-bromo-4-hydroxybenzoic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes dibromination. (B) stops before the KMnO₄ step (E shown as the toluene D). (D) treats H₃PO₂ as if it gave phenol (confuses with warm water hydrolysis).\nKey idea: Both substituents send Br to C2; the NO₂ is only a temporary director – it becomes NH₂, then N₂⁺, then H.","traps":["H₃PO₂ confused with H₂O/warm (phenol formation): H₃PO₂ or ethanol → H; warm water → OH.","Last oxidation step skipped: Track the product letter asked for (E, after KMnO₄)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct order in aqueous medium of basic strength in case of methyl substituted amines is :","options":["NH₃ > Me₃N > MeNH₂ > Me₂NH","Me₃N > Me₂NH > MeNH₂ > NH₃","Me₂NH > MeNH₂ > Me₃N > NH₃","Me₂NH > Me₃N > MeNH₂ > NH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the gas-phase (inductive only) order. (D) is the ethyl-series pattern (2° > 3° > 1°) applied to methyl. (A) reverses everything.\nKey idea: Methyl series: 2° > 1° > 3° > NH₃ (solvation beats +I for 3°); ethyl series: 2° > 3° > 1° > NH₃.","traps":["Ethyl-series order used for methyl amines: Me₃N is below MeNH₂ in water; Et₃N is above EtNH₂.","Gas-phase order used: 'Aqueous medium' brings in solvation and H-bonding of the ammonium ions."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List I (Amines) with List II (pK_b). List I: A. Aniline; B. Ethanamine; C. N-Ethylethanamine; D. N,N-Diethylethanamine. List II: I. 3.25; II. 3.00; III. 9.38; IV. 3.29. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps the 1° and 3° values (expects 3° most basic only after 2°, and 1° = 3.25); (B) gives the 2° amine the 1° value and the 1° amine the lowest pKb; (D) puts aniline at 3.25 and the tertiary amine at 9.38 (aryl and alkyl confused).\nKey idea: In water for ethyl series: (C₂H₅)₂NH (3.00) > (C₂H₅)₃N (3.25) > C₂H₅NH₂ (3.29) ≫ C₆H₅NH₂ (9.38); lower pKb = stronger base.","traps":["Tertiary amine not the strongest in water: For ethyl groups in water use 2° > 3° > 1°; for methyl groups 2° > 1° > 3°.","3.25 vs 3.29: Memorise the NCERT pK_b values pairwise: 1° ethyl 3.29, 3° ethyl 3.25."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The major product 'P' for the following sequence of reactions is : Ph–CO–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CO–NH₂ (5-oxo-5-phenylpentanamide) —[1) Zn/Hg, HCl; 2) LiAlH₄; 3) H₃O⁺]→ 'P' (major product).","options":["Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CH(OH)–NH₂","Ph–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂–NH₂ (5-phenylpentan-1-amine)","Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂–NH₂ (5-amino-1-phenylpentan-1-ol)","Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂–OH (1-phenylpentane-1,5-diol)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes both C=O groups become CH–OH (hydride-type reduction everywhere); (C) keeps the ketone as alcohol, i.e. ignores Clemmensen and treats LiAlH₄ as reducing ketone to alcohol; (D) treats amide as giving alcohol (like ester reduction).\nKey idea: Zn-Hg/HCl turns C=O of ketone into CH₂; LiAlH₄ turns –CONH₂ into –CH₂NH₂, not an alcohol.","traps":["Which group Clemmensen reduces: Clemmensen acts on aldehyde/ketone C=O only, giving CH₂.","Amide + LiAlH₄ gives amine, not alcohol: Amide reduction keeps N: RCONH₂ → RCH₂NH₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Reaction of propanamide with Br₂/KOH(aq) produces :","options":["Propanenitrile","Propylamine","Ethylnitrile","Ethylamine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) propylamine keeps all three carbons (amide reduction with LiAlH₄); (A) propanenitrile is the dehydration product (P₂O₅); (C) \"ethylnitrile\" is a name-mixing distractor.\nKey idea: Hoffmann bromamide = step-down: RCONH₂ → RNH₂.","traps":["Carbon count after Hoffmann: Hoffmann loses the C=O carbon as CO₃²⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Benzyl isocyanide can be obtained by: A. C₆H₅CH₂Br —[AgCN]→ ; B. C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ —[CHCl₃, aq. KOH]→ ; C. C₆H₅CH₂–NHCH₃ —[CHCl₃, aq. KOH]→ ; D. C₆H₅CH₂OTs —[KCN]→ . Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B and C","A and D","A and B","Only B"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"C: a secondary amine cannot give an isocyanide. D: ionic KCN attacks through C to give benzyl cyanide (nitrile). Option D 'Only B' misses the AgCN route.\nKey idea: AgCN (largely covalent) reacts through N to give RNC; 1° amine + CHCl₃/KOH gives RNC.","traps":["KCN vs AgCN: KCN (ionic) gives nitriles; AgCN (covalent Ag–C) gives isocyanides.","Secondary amine in carbylamine test: Only primary amines (RNH₂) give the isocyanide."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In the conversion of compound (X) [4-nitrotoluene] to product (Y) [3,5-dibromotoluene], the sequence of reagents to be used will be:","options":["(i) Br₂, Fe (ii) Fe, H⁺ (iii) LiAlH₄","(i) Br₂(aq) (ii) LiAlH₄ (iii) H₃O⁺","(i) Fe, H⁺ (ii) Br₂(aq) (iii) HNO₂ (iv) H₃PO₂","(i) Fe, H⁺ (ii) Br₂(aq) (iii) HNO₂ (iv) CuBr"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): LiAlH₄ does not remove a nitro/amino group from the ring, and direct bromination of nitrotoluene goes ortho to CH₃; (D): CuBr would put a third Br where NH₂ was (3,4,5-tribromotoluene).\nKey idea: Br must enter meta to CH₃, which only NH₂ (para to CH₃) can direct; the NH₂ is then removed by diazotisation and H₃PO₂.","traps":["CuBr vs H₃PO₂ last step: Count Br in target: only two, so N₂⁺ must be replaced by H.","Br₂(aq) gives ortho to NH₂ only: With para blocked, Br₂ water gives 2,6-dibromo product."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Nitrobenzene —[H₂/Pd, C₂H₅OH]→ [A] —[(CH₃CO)₂O, Pyridine]→ [B]. Consider the above reaction and identify the product B.","options":["C₆H₅–NH–CO–CH₃ (acetanilide)","C₆H₅–NH–CH₂–COOH (N-phenylglycine)","C₆H₅–CH₂–CO–NH₂ (2-phenylacetamide)","C₆H₅–CO–CH₂NH₂ (2-amino-1-phenylethanone)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) N-alkylated glycine would need a haloacid, not an anhydride. (C) isomeric amide with CH₂ between ring and C=O (from phenylacetic acid). (D) amino ketone isomer; anhydride does not form C–C bonds.\nKey idea: H₂/Pd gives aniline; Ac₂O/pyridine acetylates N to give acetanilide.","traps":["Isomeric amide structures: Acylation puts CH₃CO on N; ring–N bond is kept."],"topics":["Alkylation & acylation","Amine preparation by reduction"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"How many of the transformations given below would result in aromatic amines? 1) Benzamide (C₆H₅CONH₂) + Br₂ + NaOH →; 2) Potassium phthalimide (drawn as a benzo-fused cyclic imide with N–K) + chlorobenzene →; 3) Nitrobenzene —[H₂, Pd/C]→; 4) C₆H₅–NH–COCH₃ (acetanilide) —[dil. H₂SO₄, Δ]→","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counting Gabriel with chlorobenzene), 2 (also dropping the acid hydrolysis because the product is the anilinium salt).\nWorking: (1) Hoffmann bromamide degradation of benzamide gives aniline; (3) H₂, Pd/C reduces nitrobenzene to aniline; (4) acid hydrolysis of acetanilide gives aniline (as its salt). (2) fails because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Gabriel works only for primary alkyl amines.","traps":["Gabriel with aryl halide counted: Aryl C–Cl does not undergo SN2; Gabriel gives only aliphatic primary amines.","Acid hydrolysis gives salt: The transformation still produces an aromatic amine (freed on basification)."],"topics":["Amine preparation by reduction","Gabriel synthesis","Hoffmann bromamide"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"An organic compound [A] (C₄H₁₁N), shows optical activity and gives N₂ gas on treatment with HNO₂. The compound [A] reacts with PhSO₂Cl producing a compound which is soluble in KOH. The structure of A is: [options are skeletal structures]","options":["N-methylpropan-1-amine, CH₃CH₂CH₂–NH–CH₃ (drawn with N lone pair)","butan-2-amine, CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)NH₂","N,N-dimethylethanamine, CH₃CH₂–N(CH₃)₂","butan-1-amine, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2° amine: no N₂, Hinsberg product insoluble in KOH, achiral. (C) 3° amine: no Hinsberg reaction. (D) 1° amine passes both tests but is achiral.\nKey idea: Only a 1° amine with a stereocentre fits; C₄H₁₁N gives butan-2-amine.","traps":["Optical activity condition ignored: Check for a carbon with four different groups.","Secondary amine sulphonamide solubility: Only the 1° sulphonamide has acidic N–H → KOH-soluble."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Reaction with nitrous acid"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"An organic compound 'A' on reaction with NH₃ followed by heating gives compound B, which on further strong heating gives compound C (C₈H₅NO₂). Compound C on sequential reaction with ethanolic KOH, alkyl chloride and hydrolysis with alkali gives a primary amine. The compound A is:","options":["benzene-1,2-dicarbaldehyde (phthalaldehyde)","2-formylbenzoic acid","benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid (phthalic acid)","2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) contain aldehyde groups that would not form an imide on heating with NH₃; (D) salicylaldehyde has no second carbonyl.\nWorking: C₈H₅NO₂ is phthalimide (Gabriel reagent). Phthalic acid + NH₃ → ammonium phthalate (B) → on strong heating phthalimide.\nKey idea: Imide needs two –COOH groups ortho on the ring.","traps":["Precursor of phthalimide: An imide –CO–NH–CO– needs two acid groups."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct stability order of the following diazonium salt is\n(A) p-methoxybenzenediazonium chloride (B) p-nitrobenzenediazonium chloride (C) benzenediazonium chloride (D) p-cyanobenzenediazonium chloride","options":["(A) > (B) > (C) > (D)","(A) > (C) > (D) > (B)","(C) > (A) > (D) > (B)","(C) > (D) > (B) > (A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats NO₂ as stabilising; (C) puts the unsubstituted salt above p-OMe; (D) reverses the whole order.\nWorking: electron donation into the ring stabilises the –N₂⁺ group; p-OCH₃ (+R) most stable, then H, then p-CN, and p-NO₂ (strongest −R/−I) least stable.\nKey idea: Diazonium is a cation: EDG stabilises, EWG destabilises; NO₂ > CN in withdrawal.","traps":["EWG stabilising by delocalisation: Diazonium is a cation – needs electron donation.","NO₂ vs CN order: NO₂ is the stronger withdrawing group."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Experimental reaction of CH₃Cl with aniline and anhydrous AlCl₃ does not give o and p-methylaniline.\nReason (R): The —NH₂ group of aniline becomes deactivating because of salt formation with anhydrous AlCl₃ and hence yields m-methyl aniline as the product.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true, but (R) is false.","(A) is false, but (R) is true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): accepts R because its first half is the NCERT reason; (B): same, but thinks it does not explain; (D): doubts the assertion.\nWorking: aniline does not undergo Friedel–Crafts alkylation (A true). The Lewis acid–base salt deactivates the ring, but no m-methylaniline is formed – the reaction does not occur at all, so R is false.\nKey idea: Deactivated –NH₂·AlCl₃ complex stops Friedel–Crafts entirely; no meta product.","traps":["Half-true Reason: Judge every clause of R; the meta-product claim is false.","Nitration analogy: Friedel–Crafts on a strongly deactivated ring gives nothing."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct sequential order of the reagents for the given reaction is :\n4-nitroaniline (O₂N–C₆H₄–NH₂, para) → 4-iodophenol (HO–C₆H₄–I, para)","options":["HNO₂, Fe/H⁺, HNO₂, KI, H₂O/H⁺","HNO₂, KI, Fe/H⁺, HNO₂, H₂O/warm","HNO₂, KI, HNO₂, Fe/H⁺, H₂O/H⁺","HNO₂, Fe/H⁺, KI, HNO₂, H₂O/warm"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) reduce NO₂ while the first diazonium is present (or before using it), losing positional control; (C) applies HNO₂ twice before any new NH₂ exists.\nWorking: diazotise NH₂ (HNO₂) → KI gives 4-iodonitrobenzene → Fe/H⁺ reduces NO₂ to NH₂ → diazotise again → warm water gives the phenol; I ends up where NH₂ was and OH where NO₂ was.\nKey idea: Use the existing NH₂ first (→ I), then create the second NH₂ from NO₂ (→ OH).","traps":["Order of reduction and diazotisation: Each HNO₂ must follow the creation of an NH₂.","Position of I vs OH: Map I to the original NH₂, OH to the original NO₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I: A. Benzenesulphonyl chloride; B. Hoffmann bromamide reaction; C. Carbylamine reaction; D. Hoffmann orientation\nList II: I. Test for primary amines; II. Anti Saytzeff; III. Hinsberg reagent; IV. Known reaction of Isocyanates.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D): benzenesulphonyl chloride matched to isocyanates; Hoffmann bromamide confused with Hofmann orientation.\nWorking: C₆H₅SO₂Cl = Hinsberg reagent; Hoffmann bromamide proceeds via an isocyanate; carbylamine = test for 1° amines; Hofmann elimination gives the less substituted (anti-Saytzeff) alkene: A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nKey idea: Two different \"Hoffmann\" items: bromamide (via R–N=C=O) vs elimination (anti-Saytzeff).","traps":["Hoffmann bromamide vs Hofmann elimination: Bromamide → amine via isocyanate; elimination → least substituted alkene.","Hinsberg reagent identity: Benzenesulphonyl chloride is Hinsberg's reagent."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Hoffmann bromamide"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"An organic compound 'A' contains nitrogen and chlorine. It dissolves readily in water to give a solution that turns litmus red. Titration of compound 'A' with standard base indicates that the molecular weight of 'A' is 131 ± 2. When a sample of 'A' is treated with aq. NaOH, a liquid separates which contains N but not Cl. Treatment of the obtained liquid with nitrous acid followed by phenol gives orange precipitate. The compound 'A' is: (A) a non-aromatic cyclohexa-2,5-dien-1-yl ring (two C=C, sp³ C-1) bearing –NH₃⁺Cl⁻; (B) 2-chloroaniline (o-ClC₆H₄NH₂); (C) a cyclopenta-2,4-diene ring (two C=C, sp³ C-1) bearing –CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻; (D) anilinium chloride C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻.","options":["Cyclohexa-2,5-dien-1-ylammonium chloride (non-aromatic ring)","2-Chloroaniline","(Cyclopenta-2,4-dien-1-yl)methylammonium chloride (non-aromatic diene ring)","Anilinium chloride, C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) are aliphatic (non-aromatic) amine salts that cannot give a stable diazonium/azo dye; (B) is a free amine with Cl on the ring (not acidic in water and the liberated liquid would contain Cl).\nWorking: an acidic, water-soluble salt containing N and Cl that liberates a chlorine-free amine with NaOH is an amine hydrochloride; diazotisation then coupling with phenol (orange azo dye) needs a primary aromatic amine. C₆H₅NH₃Cl has M = 129.5 (within 131 ± 2). 2-chloroaniline is not a salt and its liberated liquid would contain Cl.\nKey idea: Only an aromatic primary amine gives a coloured azo dye with phenol; the Cl is the counter-ion.","traps":["Cl in the ring vs counter-ion: NaOH released liquid has no Cl, so Cl was ionic.","Aromatic vs non-aromatic ring: Count the double bonds; only a benzene ring forms stable arenediazonium salts."],"topics":["Coupling reactions","Diazonium preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Aniline on nitration yields ortho, meta & para nitro derivatives of aniline. Reason R: Nitrating mixture is a strong acidic mixture. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats strong acidity as unrelated, though it is the cause; (C)/(D) reject true NCERT lines.\nWorking: in the strongly acidic nitrating mixture aniline is protonated to anilinium (meta-directing), so a significant meta product forms along with o and p (and oxidation products); R explains A.\nKey idea: Anilinium (–NH₃⁺) is meta-directing, so meta product appears.","traps":["Why R explains A: Strong acid → protonation → meta-directing group."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which among the following is the strongest Brønsted base ?","options":["Triethylamine, (C₂H₅)₃N:","Diethylamine, (C₂H₅)₂NH","1-Methyl-2,3-dihydropyrrole (N-methyl-2-pyrroline; N lone pair conjugated with the ring C=C)","Quinuclidine (1-azabicyclo[2.2.2]octane)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Et₃N – steric crowding hinders solvation of R₃NH⁺; (B) Et₂NH – strongest among open-chain but quinuclidine's lone pair is more available; (C) enamine – lone pair in conjugation.\nWorking: in quinuclidine the alkyl groups are tied back in a cage, so the N lone pair is fully exposed and the protonated cation is well solvated (pKa of conjugate acid ≈ 11.0, about equal to or slightly above Et₂NH ≈ 11.0 and above Et₃N ≈ 10.7). (C) is an enamine – lone pair delocalised into C=C, weakest base.\nKey idea: Tying back the alkyl groups (quinuclidine) removes steric hindrance while keeping +I, giving maximum basicity.","traps":["More alkyl groups = more basic: In water, add solvation and steric effects.","Enamine lone pair: Check if N is attached to a C=C (lone pair delocalised)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"'A' (C₈H₆Cl₂O) —NH₃→ C₈H₈ClNO —Br₂/NaOH→ product: 4-chloro-3-methylaniline (benzene ring with NH₂; Cl para to NH₂ and CH₃ meta to NH₂, next to the Cl). Consider the above reaction, the compound 'A' is :","options":["4-Chloro-2-methylbenzoyl chloride","3-Chloro-4-methylbenzoyl chloride","4-Chloro-3-methylbenzoyl chloride","4-Chloro-3-(chloromethyl)benzaldehyde"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives 4-chloro-2-methylaniline; (B) gives 3-chloro-4-methylaniline (Cl/CH₃ swapped); (D) aldehyde does not form an amide.\nWorking: ArCOCl + NH₃ → ArCONH₂ (C₈H₈ClNO); Hoffmann bromamide removes the carbonyl carbon → ArNH₂ with NH₂ where COCl was. Product has Cl para and CH₃ meta to NH₂ → A = 4-chloro-3-methylbenzoyl chloride. (D) cannot give an amide with NH₃.\nKey idea: NH₂ appears exactly at the carbon that carried COCl.","traps":["Cl and CH₃ positions swapped: Map each substituent relative to the C–N carbon."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) + 'A' —NaOH→ Product (orange-red dye). Which among the following represent reagent 'A'?","options":["Naphthalen-2-ol (β-naphthol)","Naphthalen-1-ol (α-naphthol)","Aniline","Naphthalen-1-amine"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) α-naphthol – also couples but not the classic orange-red dye; (C) aniline – yellow dye, acid medium; (D) naphthylamine – amine coupling, not in NaOH.\nWorking: coupling of benzenediazonium chloride with β-naphthol in alkali gives 1-phenylazo-2-naphthol, the orange-red dye (classic test). α-Naphthol gives a different (brown-violet) azo dye; aniline gives yellow p-aminoazobenzene in mildly acidic medium.\nKey idea: β-Naphthol + PhN₂⁺ (alkaline) → orange-red azo dye (also the lab test for 1° aromatic amines).","traps":["α vs β naphthol: Remember the test reagent: β-naphthol (2-naphthol)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The Hinsberg reagent is:","options":["C₆H₅SO₂Cl (benzenesulphonyl chloride)","C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (benzenediazonium chloride)","potassium phthalimide","4-hydroxyazobenzene (HO–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₅)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) diazonium salt (coupling); (C) Gabriel synthesis reagent; (D) azo dye (coupling product).\nWorking: Hinsberg's reagent is benzenesulphonyl chloride.\nKey idea: Hinsberg = PhSO₂Cl.","traps":["Named reagents mixed: Link each reagent to its reaction."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The conversion of propan-1-ol to n-butylamine involves the sequential addition of reagents. The correct sequential order of reagents is","options":["(i) SOCl₂ (ii) KCN (iii) H₂/Ni, Na(Hg)/C₂H₅OH","(i) HCl (ii) H₂/Ni, Na(Hg)/C₂H₅OH","(i) SOCl₂ (ii) KCN (iii) CH₃NH₂","(i) HCl (ii) CH₃NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – no carbon added (and H₂/Ni does not reduce an alkyl chloride to amine); (C) – CH₃NH₂ on a nitrile does not give the amine; (D) – gives N-methylpropylamine, a secondary amine with 3-carbon chain.\nWorking: CH₃CH₂CH₂OH → (SOCl₂) CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl → (KCN) CH₃CH₂CH₂CN → (H₂/Ni or Na(Hg)/C₂H₅OH) CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂; nitrile adds the extra carbon.\nKey idea: Product has one more carbon → introduce CN, then reduce.","traps":["Carbon count not checked: C3 → C4 needs a C–C forming step (cyanide).","Methylamine thought to supply the extra carbon: Alkylation by CH₃NH₂ gives a secondary amine; nitrile must be reduced."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The reaction of R–CO–NH₂ with bromine and KOH gives RNH₂ as the end product. Which one of the following is the intermediate product formed in this reaction?","options":["R–CO–NH–Br","R–NH–Br","R–N=C=O","R–CO–NBr₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) N-bromoamide – true intermediate (ambiguity); (B) R–NHBr – after decarbonylation, not formed; (D) dibromoamide – over-bromination.\nWorking: the Hoffmann bromamide mechanism goes amide → N-bromoamide RCONHBr → N-bromoamide anion → (R migrates, Br⁻ lost) isocyanate R–N=C=O → carbamic acid → RNH₂ + CO₂. The isocyanate (C) is the key intermediate; note that RCONHBr (option A) is also a genuine intermediate, but the isocyanate is the intended answer.\nKey idea: Alkyl migration from C to N creates R–N=C=O, which hydrolyses and loses CO₂.","traps":["Two genuine intermediates offered: The isocyanate is taken as 'the' intermediate.","Loss of carbon before migration: Carbon is lost as CO₂ only after the isocyanate is hydrolysed."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which statement is NOT correct for p-toluenesulphonyl chloride?","options":["It is known as Hinsberg's reagent.","It is used to distinguish primary and secondary amines.","On treatment with secondary amine, it leads to a product, that is soluble in alkali.","It doesn't react with tertiary amines."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – may look false because NCERT names benzenesulphonyl chloride as Hinsberg's reagent; (B), (D) – true NCERT statements.\nWorking: a secondary amine gives an N,N-dialkyl sulphonamide with no N–H, so it is not acidic and is insoluble in alkali; (C) is false. (A) is loosely true – NCERT names benzenesulphonyl chloride as Hinsberg's reagent and says it is now replaced by p-toluenesulphonyl chloride.\nKey idea: Only primary amine sulphonamides keep an acidic N–H and dissolve in alkali.","traps":["Name of Hinsberg reagent: NCERT also says p-toluenesulphonyl chloride is now used; both are called Hinsberg's reagent.","Primary vs secondary sulphonamide solubility swapped: Acidic N–H (from primary amine) → soluble in alkali; secondary gives no N–H → insoluble."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The final product 'C' in the following series of reactions is: nitrobenzene —Sn/HCl→ A —NaNO₂/HCl→ B —β-naphthol/NaOH→ C","options":["phenylazo group on C-1 of naphthalene with OH meta to it on the same ring (4-(phenylazo)naphthalen-2-ol type)","phenylazo on one ring (C-5) and OH on the other ring (C-2): 5-(phenylazo)naphthalen-2-ol","1-(phenylazo)naphthalen-2-ol (azo at C-1, OH at adjacent C-2)","phenylazo at a β-carbon (C-6) of one ring and OH at the α-carbon C-1 of the other ring: 6-(phenylazo)naphthalen-1-ol (azo and OH on different rings, not peri)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) – azo and OH at wrong relative positions; (D) chosen by those who place the azo para-like on the far ring.\nWorking: nitrobenzene → aniline (A) → benzenediazonium chloride (B) → coupling with β-naphthol (2-naphthol) at the activated C-1 gives the orange-red dye 1-phenylazo-2-naphthol.\nKey idea: In 2-naphthol, C-1 (α, ortho to OH) is the most activated site.","traps":["Para-coupling rule applied blindly: In 2-naphthol the para position lies across the ring junction; coupling goes to C-1.","Reading fused-ring positions: Locate OH first, then check the azo is on the adjacent α-carbon."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Statement I: In Hofmann degradation reaction, the migration of only an alkyl group takes place from carbonyl carbon of the amide to the nitrogen atom. Statement II: The group is migrated in Hofmann degradation reaction to electron deficient atom.","options":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – misses \"only\"; (C) – accepts I and doubts the mechanism; (B) – rejects II because NCERT does not mention electron deficiency.\nWorking: NCERT says an alkyl or aryl group migrates (benzamide gives aniline), so \"only an alkyl group\" is false. The group migrates to the electron-deficient (nitrene-like) N of the acyl nitrene/N-bromoamide anion – II true → (D).\nKey idea: Aryl groups migrate too (benzamide → aniline); migration always goes to an electron-poor N.","traps":["\"Only alkyl\" overlooked: Read qualifiers like \"only\", \"always\".","Electron-deficient N doubted: The N that receives the group has lost Br⁻ and is electron deficient."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction with nitrous acid","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"A primary aliphatic amine on reaction with nitrous acid in cold (273 K) and there after raising temperature of reaction mixture to room temperature (298 K), gives a/an","options":["nitrile","alcohol","diazonium salt","secondary amine"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) applies the aromatic case (stable at 273 K) to an aliphatic amine; (A) and (D) are unrelated products.\nWorking: RNH₂ + HNO₂ → [RN₂⁺] (unstable aliphatic diazonium) → ROH + N₂ on warming.\nKey idea: Aliphatic diazonium salts decompose at once to alcohol + N₂.","traps":["Cold condition suggests diazonium salt: Note \"aliphatic\" and the later warming to 298 K."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"With respect to the following reaction, consider the given statements: aniline —HNO₃ / H₂SO₄, 288 K→ products. (A) o-Nitroaniline and p-nitroaniline are the predominant products. (B) p-Nitroaniline and m-nitroaniline are the predominant products. (C) HNO₃ acts as an acid. (D) H₂SO₄ acts as an acid.","options":["(A) and (C) are correct statements.","(A) and (D) are correct statements.","(B) and (D) are correct statements.","(B) and (C) are correct statements."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) assume –NH₂ stays o/p-directing; (D) says HNO₃ acts as an acid – it accepts a proton from H₂SO₄.\nWorking: in strong acid aniline is protonated to anilinium (meta-directing), so p- (~51%) and m- (~47%) nitroanilines dominate; H₂SO₄ protonates HNO₃ (HNO₃ acts as a base) to form NO₂⁺.\nKey idea: Strong acid converts –NH₂ to –NH₃⁺; in mixed acid, HNO₃ is the base.","traps":["Anilinium is meta-directing: Ask what aniline becomes in conc. acid.","HNO₃ acts as a base: H₂SO₄ + HNO₃ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Aniline —[Br₂(excess)/H₂O]→ A (major product). Aniline —[(i) (CH₃CO)₂O, (ii) Br₂, CH₃COOH, (iii) HCl]→ B (major product). Consider the above reaction, the product A and product B respectively are","options":["4-bromoaniline and 4-bromoaniline","2,4,6-tribromoaniline and 2,4,6-tribromoaniline","2,4,6-tribromoaniline and 4-bromoaniline","4-bromoaniline and 2,4,6-tribromoaniline"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores polybromination; (B) ignores the moderating acetyl group; (D) swaps the two routes.\nWorking: free –NH₂ activates strongly → tribromination in bromine water; acetylation moderates activation, Br₂/AcOH gives p-bromoacetanilide; HCl hydrolysis gives p-bromoaniline.\nKey idea: Protect –NH₂ as amide to stop at mono (para) substitution, then hydrolyse.","traps":["Products swapped: Free amine = tri; protected = mono."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In Friedel-Crafts alkylation of aniline, one gets:","options":["alkylated product with ortho and para substitution.","secondary amine after acidic treatment.","an amide product.","positively charged nitrogen at benzene ring."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) expects normal o/p activation by –NH₂; (B) confuses with N-alkylation; (C) confuses with acetylation by acyl chloride without AlCl₃.\nWorking: the Lewis base –NH₂ forms a salt with AlCl₃, so nitrogen becomes positively charged and strongly deactivates the ring; no alkylation occurs.\nKey idea: –NH₂ + AlCl₃ → –NH₂⁺–AlCl₃⁻: ring deactivated.","traps":["–NH₂ as o/p activator: Lewis acid catalysts react with the amine first.","Acylation vs alkylation: Question is alkylation with AlCl₃."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"[Structure: 3-(aminomethyl)benzamide – benzene ring with CH₂NH₂ and, meta to it, CONH₂] —(CH₃CO)₂O→ P (Major product). The major product in the above reaction is:","options":["3-(acetamidomethyl)-N-acetylbenzamide: CH₂NHCOCH₃ and C(=O)NHCOCH₃ on the ring","CH₂NH₂ retained, amide N-acetylated: C(=O)NHCOCH₃","CH₂NH₃⁺ CH₃COO⁻ (salt) with C(=O)NHCOCH₃","N-[3-carbamoylbenzyl]acetamide: CH₂NHCOCH₃ with CONH₂ unchanged"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats both NH₂ as amines (double acetylation); (B) reverses the reactivity; (C) imagines an acid–base salt with the amine while the amide reacts.\nWorking: the benzylic primary amine is a good nucleophile and is acetylated; the amide N lone pair is delocalised into C=O and is not acylated by acetic anhydride under these conditions.\nKey idea: Amide N lone pair is tied up in resonance; only the amine acylates.","traps":["Amide NH₂ is not nucleophilic: Check what is attached: C=O next to N kills nucleophilicity.","Benzylic amine behaves as aliphatic amine: Look one atom away from N."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–CH₂–COCl —(i) alcoholic NH₃; (ii) NaOH, Br₂; (iii) NaNO₂, HCl; (iv) H₂O→ Major product","options":["CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(Br)–CH₂OH","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂OH","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂OH","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) keeps all carbons – forgets the carbon lost in Hofmann; (A) adds Br to the chain – misreads Br₂ as brominating; (D) gives chloride – takes HCl as the nucleophile.\nWorking: NH₃ gives the amide (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂CONH₂; Hofmann bromamide removes one carbon giving (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂NH₂; HNO₂ then water converts the 1° aliphatic amine to the alcohol (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂OH (3-methylbutan-1-ol). Rearrangement of the primary carbocation is ignored by the question.\nKey idea: Hofmann bromamide shortens the chain by one carbon; HNO₂ on a 1° aliphatic amine gives the alcohol.","traps":["Carbon lost in Hofmann: Amide C=O carbon leaves as CO₃²⁻.","Br₂ as brominating agent: In Hofmann, Br₂/NaOH acts on N–H, not on the chain."],"topics":["Hoffmann bromamide","Reaction with nitrous acid"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties of amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following is not a correct statement for primary aliphatic amines?","options":["The intermolecular association in primary amines is less than the intermolecular association in secondary amines.","Primary amines on treating with nitrous acid solution form corresponding alcohols except methyl amine.","Primary amines are less basic than the secondary amines.","Primary amines can be prepared by the Gabriel phthalimide synthesis."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) looks doubtful because of the 'except methyl amine' clause; (C) may be judged false by students who remember the gas-phase or aromatic order.\nWorking: (A) is false – primary amines have two N–H hydrogens and so more intermolecular H-bonding than secondary amines. (B) methylamine gives mainly other products (e.g. methyl nitrite/ether) rather than methanol, as commonly stated; (C) true for aliphatic amines in solution; (D) true.\nKey idea: Two N–H hydrogens in RNH₂ → stronger association than in R₂NH (one N–H).","traps":["Comparison word reversed: Count N–H bonds available for H-bonding.","Methylamine exception distracts: An odd-sounding exception is not automatically false."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The correct structures of A and B formed in the following reactions are: p-nitrophenol —H₂/Pd, C₂H₅OH→ A —acetic anhydride (CH₃CO)₂O, 1.0 eq.→ B (Major product)","options":["A: 4-aminophenol; B: 2-acetyl-4-aminophenol (ring C-acetylated ortho to OH)","A: 4-aminophenyl acetate; B: 4-acetamidophenyl acetate (O- and N-acetylated)","A: 4-aminophenol; B: 4-aminophenyl acetate (O-acetylated)","A: 4-aminophenol; B: N-(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetamide (paracetamol, N-acetylated)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) O-acylation – assumes phenol OH reacts first; (B) acylates the wrong group in A and then both in B (needs 2 eq.); (A) Friedel–Crafts C-acylation – needs a Lewis acid.\nWorking: H₂/Pd reduces –NO₂ to –NH₂ (A = 4-aminophenol). With one equivalent of acetic anhydride the more nucleophilic –NH₂ is acylated in preference to the phenolic –OH, giving paracetamol (B).\nKey idea: NH₂ is a better nucleophile than ArOH: one equivalent of Ac₂O → amide (paracetamol).","traps":["O- vs N-acylation: Amine N is far more nucleophilic than phenolic O; 1 eq. goes to N.","Equivalents ignored: 1.0 eq. anhydride → only one acyl group added."],"topics":["Alkylation & acylation","Amine preparation by reduction"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The total number of reagents from those given below, that can convert nitrobenzene into aniline is ______. (Integer answer) I. Sn – HCl; II. Sn – NH₄OH; III. Fe – HCl; IV. Zn – HCl; V. H₂ – Pd; VI. H₂ – Raney Nickel","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 – accepts Sn–NH₄OH; 4 – rejects Zn–HCl or Raney Ni as unfamiliar; 3 – counts only metal/acid.\nWorking: metal/acid (Sn–HCl, Fe–HCl, Zn–HCl) and catalytic hydrogenation (H₂/Pd, H₂/Raney Ni) all reduce –NO₂ to –NH₂. Sn with NH₄OH (basic, no acid) does not give aniline. Count = 5.\nKey idea: Metal reductions of NO₂ to NH₂ need acid; NH₄OH is basic.","traps":["Sn with NH₄OH: Acid medium required; basic medium gives other products.","Raney Ni not in NCERT list: Raney Ni is finely divided nickel – works."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The major products A and B formed in the following reaction sequence are: Aniline —(CH₃CO)₂O (acetic anhydride)→ A —Br₂, CH₃COOH, room temperature→ B","options":["A = acetanilide (C₆H₅NHCOCH₃), B = 3-bromoacetanilide (meta-Br)","A = acetanilide, B = 4-bromoacetanilide (para-Br)","A = 4-aminoacetophenone (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–COCH₃), B = 4-amino-3-bromoacetophenone","A = 4-aminoacetophenone, B = 4-amino-3,5-dibromoacetophenone"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) meta product (treats amide as deactivating meta-director); (C)/(D) Friedel–Crafts C-acylation of aniline, which does not occur; (D) also overbrominates as if free NH₂.\nWorking: acetic anhydride N-acetylates aniline (no Friedel–Crafts on the ring) to acetanilide; the moderately activating o/p-directing –NHCOCH₃ group gives mainly para-monobromination (steric hindrance at ortho).\nKey idea: –NHCOCH₃ is still o/p-directing but tamer than –NH₂, giving mono para product.","traps":["C-acylation of aniline: Aniline N is the nucleophile; no AlCl₃ here, and NH₂ would complex it anyway.","Amide treated as meta director: N lone pair attached to ring still donates: o/p director."],"topics":["Aniline substitution","Alkylation & acylation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"'A' and 'B' in the following reactions are: Aniline (C₆H₅NH₂) —NaNO₂/HCl, then KCN→ (A) —SnCl₂/HCl, then H₃O⁺→ (B)","options":["(A): C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (benzenediazonium chloride); (B): C₆H₅CHO","(A): C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻; (B): C₆H₅Cl","(A): C₆H₅CN (benzonitrile); (B): C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde)","(A): C₆H₅CN; (B): C₆H₅Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – treat KCN as not reacting so A stays the diazonium salt; (B)/(D) – B as chlorobenzene treats SnCl₂/HCl as a Cl source (Sandmeyer with Cl).\nWorking: diazotisation then cyanide replaces N₂ giving benzonitrile (A); SnCl₂/HCl reduces the nitrile to the imine salt which hydrolyses to benzaldehyde (Stephen reaction) (B).\nKey idea: SnCl₂/HCl then H₃O⁺ on a nitrile = Stephen → aldehyde.","traps":["Chloride from HCl assumed: SnCl₂/HCl is a reducing system, not a chlorinating one."],"topics":["Diazonium in synthesis","Sandmeyer & Gattermann"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In the following reaction the reason why meta-nitro product also formed is: Aniline —conc. HNO₃/conc. H₂SO₄, 288 K→ p-nitroaniline [A] 51% + m-nitroaniline [B] 47% + o-nitroaniline [C] 2%","options":["low temperature","–NH₂ group is highly meta-directive","Formation of anilinium ion","–NO₂ substitution always takes place at meta-position"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – –NH₂ is o/p-directing, not meta; (D) – over-generalises the meta direction of –NO₂; (A) – temperature is not the cause.\nWorking: in strongly acidic medium aniline is protonated to C₆H₅NH₃⁺, which is deactivating and meta-directing, so a large amount of m-nitroaniline forms.\nKey idea: Acid converts –NH₂ (o/p) to –NH₃⁺ (meta).","traps":["–NH₂ called meta director: Free –NH₂ is o/p-directing; protonated form is meta."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The diazonium salt of which of the following compounds will form a coloured dye on reaction with β-naphthol in NaOH? (1) benzylamine C₆H₅CH₂NH₂; (2) N,N-dimethylaniline C₆H₅N(CH₃)₂; (3) aniline C₆H₅NH₂; (4) N-methylaniline C₆H₅NHCH₃.","options":["C₆H₅CH₂NH₂","C₆H₅N(CH₃)₂","C₆H₅NH₂","C₆H₅NHCH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) benzylamine – has a benzene ring but NH₂ is on an sp³ carbon; (B), (D) – 2°/3° amines give N-nitroso compounds instead.\nWorking: only a primary aromatic amine gives a stable arenediazonium salt that couples to an azo dye; benzylamine is aliphatic (unstable diazonium), 2° and 3° amines do not form diazonium salts.\nKey idea: Need Ar–NH₂ (N directly on the ring).","traps":["Benzylamine treated as aromatic amine: Check whether N is attached to the ring carbon."],"topics":["Diazonium preparation","Coupling reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The total number of amines among the following which can be synthesized by Gabriel synthesis is ____. (A) (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–NH₂; (B) CH₃CH₂NH₂; (C) C₆H₅CH₂–NH₂ (benzylamine); (D) C₆H₅NH₂ (aniline).","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counting aniline), 2 (excluding benzylamine as \"aromatic\").\nWorking: Gabriel gives primary amines from alkyl halides by SN2 on potassium phthalimide; A, B, C have NH₂ on an sp³ CH₂; aniline cannot be made (aryl halides do not undergo SN2).\nKey idea: Primary amines with NH₂ on sp³ carbon only.","traps":["Aniline counted: Aryl halides do not undergo SN2 with phthalimide anion.","Benzylamine excluded: Benzyl halide reacts readily by SN2."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following reaction/s will not give p-aminoazobenzene? (A) Nitrobenzene: (i) Sn/HCl (ii) HNO₂ (iii) Aniline. (B) Nitrobenzene: (i) NaBH₄ (ii) NaOH (iii) Aniline. (C) Aniline: (i) HNO₂ (ii) Aniline, HCl.","options":["A only","B only","C only","A and B"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrongly doubts Sn/HCl route; (C) wrongly thinks coupling in HCl fails; (D) adds A by assuming the Sn/HCl product is an anilinium salt that cannot diazotise.\nWorking: (A) Sn/HCl reduces PhNO₂ to aniline → diazotisation → coupling with aniline gives p-aminoazobenzene. (B) NaBH₄ does not reduce an aromatic nitro group, so no aniline/diazonium forms → no azo dye. (C) aniline → benzenediazonium chloride → coupling with aniline (HCl) gives p-aminoazobenzene (via diazoamino rearrangement in acid). So only B fails.\nKey idea: NaBH₄ reduces C=O, not Ar–NO₂; no amine → no diazonium → no azo dye.","traps":["NaBH₄ treated as general reducer: NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor for aldehydes/ketones only.","Negative wording missed: Underline \"not\" before scanning schemes."],"topics":["Coupling reactions","Diazonium preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Carbylamine test is used to detect the presence of primary amino group in an organic compound. Which of the following compound is formed when this test is performed with aniline?","options":["C₆H₅–NHCH₃ (N-methylaniline)","C₆H₅–CONH₂ (benzamide)","C₆H₅–CN (benzonitrile)","C₆H₅–NC (phenyl isocyanide)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) –CN is the nitrile (C-bonded) – the look-alike; (A) N-methylaniline would need methylation; (B) benzamide is unrelated (amide).\nWorking: aniline + CHCl₃ + alcoholic KOH gives phenyl isocyanide (C₆H₅N≡C), the foul-smelling carbylamine.\nKey idea: Carbylamine = isocyanide, N bonded to the ring: Ar–N≡C.","traps":["Nitrile vs isocyanide: In isocyanide the ring is bonded to N."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Aniline is treated with HNO₃, H₂SO₄ at 288 K giving p-nitroaniline (A), m-nitroaniline (B) and o-nitroaniline (C). Correct statement about the given chemical reaction is:","options":["–NH₂ group is ortho and para directive, so product (B) is not possible.","Reaction is possible and compound (B) will be the major product.","The reaction will form sulphonated product instead of nitration.","Reaction is possible and compound (A) will be major product."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores anilinium formation; (B) overestimates meta – it is large but second; (C) sulphonation needs conc. H₂SO₄ heating, nitration dominates here.\nWorking: in strong acid much aniline is protonated to anilinium (meta director), so a large amount of meta product forms (≈47 %), but para (≈51 %) is still the major product; ortho is ≈2 %.\nKey idea: Significant meta, but para is still major.","traps":["Meta taken as major: Remember the actual split p 51 : m 47 : o 2.","Meta thought impossible: In acid, anilinium ion is present."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"An amine on reaction with benzenesulphonyl chloride produces a compound insoluble in alkaline solution. This amine can be prepared by ammonolysis of ethyl chloride. The correct structure of amine is:","options":["C₆H₅–NH–CH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂NH₂","CH₃CH₂CH₂NHCH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂–NH–CH₂CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ethylamine – primary, sulphonamide is alkali-soluble; (C) secondary but would need a methyl halide; (A) aryl secondary amine not from ethyl chloride.\nWorking: alkali-insoluble sulphonamide → secondary amine; of the 2° amines, only N-ethylpropan-1-amine carries an N-ethyl group that arises from ethyl chloride alkylating (propyl)amine.\nKey idea: Alkali-insoluble Hinsberg product = 2° amine; the ethyl group on N must come from ethyl chloride.","traps":["Primary amine chosen: Primary sulphonamide dissolves in alkali.","Any secondary amine accepted: N-substituent must be ethyl."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Ammonolysis of alkyl halides"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A. Phenyl methanamine B. N,N-Dimethylaniline C. N-Methyl aniline D. Benzenamine. Choose the correct order of basic nature of the above amines.","options":["A > C > B > D","D > C > B > A","D > B > C > A","A > B > C > D"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts N-methylaniline above N,N-dimethylaniline – applies the aqueous 2°>3° rule of alkylamines to anilines; (B)/(C) reverse the order (aniline most basic).\nWorking: benzylamine (lone pair not delocalised, pKb ≈ 4.7) is most basic; among the anilines +I of methyl groups raises basicity: N,N-dimethylaniline (pKb 8.92) > N-methylaniline (9.30) > aniline (9.38).\nKey idea: In anilines, steric inhibition of resonance and +I make the tertiary aryl amine the most basic of the three.","traps":["Aliphatic 2° > 3° rule carried to anilines: From pK_b data: PhNMe₂ pKb 8.92 < PhNH₂ 9.38.","Benzylamine treated as an arylamine: CH₂ separates N from ring – no delocalisation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sandmeyer & Gattermann","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —Cu₂Cl₂→ C₆H₅Cl + N₂ (b) C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —Cu, HCl→ C₆H₅Cl + N₂ (c) 2CH₃CH₂Cl + 2Na —ether→ C₂H₅–C₂H₅ + 2NaCl (d) 2C₆H₅Cl + 2Na —ether→ C₆H₅–C₆H₅ + 2NaCl. List-II: (i) Wurtz reaction (ii) Sandmeyer reaction (iii) Fittig reaction (iv) Gatterman reaction.","options":["(a) → (iii), (b) → (i), (c) → (iv), (d) → (ii)","(a) → (ii), (b) → (i), (c) → (iv), (d) → (iii)","(a) → (ii), (b) → (iv), (c) → (i), (d) → (iii)","(a) → (iii), (b) → (iv), (c) → (i), (d) → (ii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(A) – Fittig assigned to diazonium reaction (a) by name confusion; (B) – Wurtz given to (b).\nWorking: Cu₂Cl₂/HCl – Sandmeyer; Cu powder/HCl – Gattermann; alkyl halide + Na – Wurtz; aryl halide + Na – Fittig.\nKey idea: Cu(I) salt = Sandmeyer; Cu metal + HX = Gattermann.","traps":["Sandmeyer vs Gattermann reagent: Cu₂Cl₂ → Sandmeyer; Cu/HCl → Gattermann.","Wurtz vs Fittig: Alkyl–alkyl = Wurtz; aryl–aryl = Fittig."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Compound A is converted to B on reaction with CHCl₃ and KOH. The compound B is toxic and can be decomposed by C. A, B and C respectively are:","options":["primary amine, nitrile compound, conc. HCl","secondary amine, isonitrile compound, conc. NaOH","primary amine, isonitrile compound, conc. HCl","secondary amine, nitrile compound, conc. NaOH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) nitrile instead of isonitrile – look-alike name; (B) secondary amine does not give the test and isocyanides are not decomposed by alkali.\nWorking: carbylamine reaction – a primary amine with CHCl₃/KOH gives the foul-smelling, toxic isocyanide (isonitrile); isocyanides are hydrolysed by acid (conc. HCl) to the primary amine and formic acid, but are stable to alkali.\nKey idea: Primary amine → isocyanide (R–N≡C); isocyanides hydrolyse only in acid.","traps":["Nitrile vs isonitrile: Carbylamine = isocyanide, R–NC.","Acid vs alkali for hydrolysis: Isocyanides are stable to base, hydrolysed by acid."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"3-Bromobenzamide (m-Br–C₆H₄–CONH₂) —KOBr→ A (major product); 3-bromobenzamide —(i) LiAlH₄ (ii) H₃O⁺→ B (major product). In the above reactions, product A and product B respectively are : [options drawn as structures]","options":["aniline, 3-bromobenzylamine","3-bromoaniline, benzylamine","aniline, benzylamine","3-bromoaniline, 3-bromobenzylamine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) assume Br is removed (reductive or substitution) in one or both steps; (B) removes Br only in the LiAlH₄ route.\nWorking: KOBr (Br₂/KOH) causes Hoffmann bromamide degradation – loss of the carbonyl carbon – giving 3-bromoaniline; LiAlH₄ reduces –CONH₂ to –CH₂NH₂ giving 3-bromobenzylamine; the aryl C–Br survives both.\nKey idea: KOBr = Hoffmann (lose C=O carbon, ArNH₂); LiAlH₄ = reduction (keep carbon, ArCH₂NH₂); Ar–Br untouched.","traps":["KOBr not recognised as Hoffmann reagent: Read any hypohalite (NaOBr, KOBr, Br₂/OH⁻) on an amide as Hoffmann degradation.","Aryl bromide assumed to react: Aryl C–Br is inert to LiAlH₄ and to hypobromite under these conditions."],"topics":["Hoffmann bromamide","Amine preparation by reduction"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Sulphanilic acid (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–SO₃H) —NaNO₂, HCl, 273–278 K→ A (major product) —N,N-dimethylaniline, 273 K→ B (major product). Consider the above reaction, compound B is : [options drawn as structures]","options":["HO₃S–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₄–N(CH₃)₂ with the azo link meta to N(CH₃)₂","C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄–N(CH₃)₂ (para) – no SO₃H","p-HO₃S–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₄–N(CH₃)₂-p (methyl orange, acid form)","p-HO₃S–C₆H₄–C₆H₄–N(CH₃)₂-p (biphenyl, no azo link)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) meta coupling; (B) loses the SO₃H group; (D) loses N₂ (biaryl, as in a Gomberg-type coupling).\nWorking: diazotisation gives the p-sulphobenzenediazonium ion; it couples at the para position of N,N-dimethylaniline giving 4-[4-(dimethylamino)phenylazo]benzenesulphonic acid (methyl orange).\nKey idea: Diazonium coupling keeps N=N and goes para to the activating NMe₂ group.","traps":["Azo link lost: Coupling retains –N=N–.","Wrong ring position: Couple para to NMe₂."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) + A + H₂O → benzene (major product); benzene + B, anhyd. AlCl₃ → ethylbenzene (C₆H₅CH₂CH₃). In the chemical reactions given above A and B respectively are :","options":["H₃PO₂ and CH₃CH₂Cl","CH₃CH₂OH and H₃PO₂","H₃PO₂ and CH₃CH₂OH","CH₃CH₂Cl and H₃PO₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) H₃PO₂ correct but CH₃CH₂OH is not the FC reagent; (B) ethanol could reduce ArN₂⁺ but H₃PO₂ cannot alkylate; (D) order reversed.\nWorking: H₃PO₂ (hypophosphorous acid) reduces ArN₂⁺ to ArH; Friedel-Crafts ethylation needs CH₃CH₂Cl with AlCl₃ (ethanol is not the usual FC alkylating agent with AlCl₃).\nKey idea: Fix B first: only an alkyl halide does FC alkylation with AlCl₃.","traps":["Ethanol as FC reagent: FC alkylation uses R–Cl/AlCl₃.","Order of A and B: Match A to step 1, B to step 2."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which one of the following reactions does not occur ?","options":["Aniline + (CH₃CO)₂O/pyridine → acetanilide (C₆H₅NHCOCH₃)","Aniline + H₂SO₄ → sulphanilic acid (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–SO₃H)","Aniline + AlCl₃ + CH₃Cl → p-toluidine (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–CH₃)","Aniline + HNO₃/H₂SO₄ → p-nitroaniline (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–NO₂)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) tempting because NCERT says direct nitration gives m-product and tarry oxidation products; (B) sulphanilic acid is a valid NCERT reaction.\nWorking: aniline (Lewis base) forms a salt with AlCl₃, which deactivates the ring, so Friedel–Crafts alkylation does not occur. Note (D) as drawn is also questionable – direct nitration gives a large share of m-nitroaniline plus oxidation products – but some p-product does form, so (C) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: AlCl₃ + –NH₂ → Lewis salt; ring becomes deactivated → no FC.","traps":["Nitration chosen: Nitration still occurs (mixture); FC does not occur at all.","NOT-type stem: Tick each reaction first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is labelled as Assertion (A) and other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): Gabriel phthalimide synthesis cannot be used to prepare aromatic primary amines. Reason (R): Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution reaction. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is false but (R) is true.","Both (A) and (R) true and (R) is correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true but (R) is false."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) for students who know aryl halides with strong EWGs or under drastic conditions do undergo SNAr and so call R false; (A) for those unsure of the causal link.\nWorking: NCERT states aromatic primary amines cannot be made by Gabriel synthesis because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the phthalimide anion; (R) is the stated reason (taken as true under ordinary conditions).\nKey idea: Phthalimide anion is an SN2 nucleophile; aryl halides do not react that way, so only alkyl primary amines form.","traps":["R stated too generally: Read R in the Gabriel context (ordinary conditions, phthalimide anion)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which one of the products of the following reactions does not react with Hinsberg reagent to form sulphonamide?","options":["4-nitrobenzonitrile (p-NO₂–C₆H₄–CN) + Na/Hg, C₂H₅OH →","Benzonitrile (C₆H₅CN) + SnCl₂ + HCl →","4-formylbenzonitrile (p-OHC–C₆H₄–CN) + LiAlH₄, then H₃O⁺ →","3-methylbenzonitrile (m-CH₃–C₆H₄–CN) + H₂/Ni →"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) students worry that Na/Hg only reduces NO₂; both groups give amines; (C) students think LiAlH₄ reduces only CHO; (D) H₂/Ni is the standard nitrile → primary amine route.\nWorking: SnCl₂/HCl (Stephen reduction) converts C₆H₅CN to the imine, hydrolysed to benzaldehyde – no amine, so no sulphonamide; (A) Na/Hg–EtOH (Mendius) gives ArCH₂NH₂ (nitro also reduced), (C) LiAlH₄ gives ArCH₂NH₂ (CHO → CH₂OH), (D) H₂/Ni gives m-CH₃C₆H₄CH₂NH₂ – all primary amines that react with Hinsberg reagent.\nKey idea: SnCl₂/HCl stops at the imine and hydrolysis gives an aldehyde, so there is no N–H for the sulphonyl chloride.","traps":["SnCl₂/HCl mistaken for Sn/HCl: Nitrile + SnCl₂/HCl → aldehyde (Stephen).","NOT-type stem: Mark each product as amine / not amine first."],"topics":["Amine preparation by reduction","Tests for amines"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which one of the following compounds will liberate CO₂, when treated with NaHCO₃?","options":["(CH₃)₃N⁺H Cl⁻","(CH₃)₄N⁺ OH⁻","CH₃–CO–NH₂","CH₃NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) has charges and looks salt-like but is a strong base; (C) amide N–H is not acidic enough; (D) is a base.\nWorking: trimethylammonium chloride is the only acidic species (conjugate acid of an amine); (B) is a strong base, (C) is neutral, (D) is basic. Strictly, (CH₃)₃NH⁺ (pKa ≈ 9.8) is a weaker acid than H₂CO₃ (pKa₁ ≈ 6.4), so appreciable CO₂ evolution is doubtful, but it is the only possible choice.\nKey idea: Only an acid (proton donor) can release CO₂ from HCO₃⁻; the protonated amine is the only proton donor offered.","traps":["Charged species assumed acidic: Quaternary ammonium hydroxide has no N–H and contains OH⁻ – strong base.","Relative acidity vs carbonic acid: Choose the only acid present; note R₃NH⁺ is a weak acid."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"C₆H₅NO₂ —(Sn + HCl)→ \"A\" —(C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻, H⁺)→ P (yellow coloured compound). Consider the above reaction, the product \"P\" is:","options":["2-aminoazobenzene (Ph–N=N–C₆H₄–NH₂, NH₂ ortho to azo)","4-aminoazobenzene (Ph–N=N–C₆H₄–NH₂, NH₂ para to azo)","diphenylamine (Ph–NH–Ph)","diazoaminobenzene (Ph–N=N–NH–Ph)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the N-coupled triazene (real intermediate in weakly acidic medium); (A) is the ortho isomer; (C) no azo group.\nWorking: Sn/HCl reduces nitrobenzene to aniline (A); coupling with benzenediazonium chloride in acid gives para-coupling to p-aminoazobenzene (aniline yellow).\nKey idea: Diazo coupling with aniline goes para to NH₂, giving aniline yellow.","traps":["N-coupling vs C-coupling: Final yellow dye is the C-para azo product.","Ortho vs para: Coupling is para (steric)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reaction with nitrous acid","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"[Reactant: (1-methylcycloheptyl)methanamine – cycloheptane ring with C-1 bearing CH₃ and CH₂NH₂]\n(i) NaNO₂, HCl, 278 K; (ii) H₂O → P (major product). What is the major product \"P\" of the following reaction?","options":["1-ethyl-1-methylcycloheptane","1-(chloromethyl)-1-methylcycloheptane","1-(diazoniomethyl)cycloheptan-1-ol chloride (OH and CH₂N₂⁺Cl⁻ on C-1)","(1-methylcycloheptyl)methanol (CH₃ and CH₂OH on C-1)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) treats the aliphatic diazonium salt as stable (true only for aryl diazonium); (B) chloride capture; (A) reduction product.\nWorking: a primary aliphatic amine with HNO₂ gives an unstable diazonium salt that loses N₂ and is attacked by water to give the alcohol – (D). Strictly, this neopentyl-type primary cation would rearrange (ring expansion/Demjanov or methyl shift) so the real major product is a rearranged alcohol; no rearranged product is offered, so the intended NCERT-level answer is (D).\nKey idea: Aliphatic RN₂⁺ is unstable → N₂ + alcohol (at NCERT level).","traps":["Aliphatic vs aromatic diazonium stability: Only arenediazonium salts survive at 273–278 K.","Carbocation rearrangement: Answer within the options; note the real chemistry."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Aniline is less basic than acetamide.\nStatement II: In aniline, the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom is delocalised over benzene ring due to resonance and hence less available to a proton.\nChoose the most appropriate option;","options":["Statement I is true but statement II is false.","Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Both statement I and statement II are true.","Both statement I and statement II are false."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the truth of both; (C) accepts Statement I because \"aniline is weakly basic\" is remembered; (D) rejects the true resonance statement.\nWorking: acetamide's N lone pair is delocalised onto the strongly electron-withdrawing C=O, so it is far less basic than aniline (Statement I false); Statement II is the NCERT explanation of aniline's weak basicity (true). Option (B).\nKey idea: Delocalisation into C=O (amide) is much stronger than into a benzene ring, so amides are almost neutral.","traps":["Amide is weaker base than aniline: Compare where the lone pair goes: ring vs C=O."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amine structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In gaseous triethyl amine the \"–C–N–C–\" bond angle is ________ degree.","type":"num","answer":"108","range":[108.0,108.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 109 or 109.5 (ideal tetrahedral), 107 (NH₃ value), 120 (planar).\nWorking: NCERT states the C–N–C angle in the pyramidal amine is 108° (its example is trimethylamine). Note: the question names triethylamine, for which NCERT gives no value; the expected answer simply transfers NCERT's 108°.\nKey idea: Lone pair compresses C–N–C below 109.5°; NCERT value 108°.","traps":["NH₃ 107° vs amine 108°: Alkyl groups widen slightly to 108°.","Tetrahedral default: Lone pair repulsion reduces angle."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"What is A in the following reaction? Benzyl bromide (C₆H₅CH₂Br) —(i) potassium phthalimide; (ii) OH⁻/H₂O→ A (Major Product)","options":["C₆H₅CONHCH₂C₆H₅ (N-benzylbenzamide)","C₆H₅CH₂OH","Phthalimide","C₆H₅CH₂NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes OH⁻ just substitutes Br; (C) is the by-product skeleton; (A) is a mis-assembled amide.\nWorking: Gabriel synthesis – SN2 N-alkylation to N-benzylphthalimide, then basic hydrolysis releases benzylamine (and phthalate).\nKey idea: Gabriel → only 1° amines, free of 2°/3°.","traps":["Hydroxide hydrolyses the imide, not the C–Br: Step (i) has already consumed the bromide."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The correct sequence of correct reagents for the following transformation is: nitrobenzene → 3-chlorophenol (OH and Cl meta to each other)","options":["(i) Fe, HCl (ii) Cl₂, HCl (iii) NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C (iv) H₂O/H⁺","(i) Fe, HCl (ii) NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C (iii) H₂O/H⁺ (iv) Cl₂, FeCl₃","(i) Cl₂, FeCl₃ (ii) Fe, HCl (iii) NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C (iv) H₂O/H⁺","(i) Cl₂, FeCl₃ (ii) NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C (iii) Fe, HCl (iv) H₂O/H⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chlorinates aniline (o/p, polychlorination); (B) chlorinates phenol (o/p); (D) tries to diazotise before an NH₂ exists.\nWorking: chlorinate while NO₂ (meta director) is present → m-chloronitrobenzene; reduce to m-chloroaniline; diazotise; warm with water → 3-chlorophenol.\nKey idea: Introduce Cl while the meta director is still NO₂.","traps":["Order decides orientation: Place substituent when NO₂ is present for meta.","Diazotisation needs ArNH₂: Reduce before NaNO₂/HCl."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Aniline —[NaNO₂, HCl, 273–278 K]→ \"X\" —[\"A\"]→ phenol (major product). In the above chemical reaction, intermediate \"X\" and reagent/condition \"A\" are:","options":["X – benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻); A – H₂O/NaOH","X – nitrobenzene; A – H₂O/Δ","X – benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻); A – H₂O/Δ","X – nitrobenzene; A – H₂O/NaOH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – NaOH (alkaline medium) is the coupling condition, not hydrolysis; (B)/(D) – NaNO₂/HCl does not nitrate the ring.\nWorking: diazotisation at 0–5 °C gives C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻; warming with water hydrolyses it to phenol + N₂ + HCl.\nKey idea: ArN₂⁺Cl⁻ + H₂O (warm) → ArOH + N₂ + HCl.","traps":["NaOH vs heat: Hydrolysis to phenol needs warming in water (acidic).","Nitrite thought to nitrate: NaNO₂/HCl makes HNO₂ → diazotises ArNH₂."],"topics":["Diazonium in synthesis","Diazonium preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ammonolysis of alkyl halides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Ammonolysis of alkyl halides followed by the treatment with NaOH solution can be used to prepare primary, secondary and tertiary amines. The purpose of NaOH in the reaction is:","options":["to remove basic impurities","to activate NH₃ used in the reaction","to remove acidic impurities","to increase the reactivity of alkyl halide"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NaOH cannot remove a base; (B) NH₃ needs no activation – it is the nucleophile; (D) NaOH does not activate the alkyl halide (it would rather cause substitution/elimination).\nWorking: ammonolysis gives the amine as its ammonium halide salt (RNH₃⁺X⁻); NaOH neutralises the HX and liberates the free amine – the option wording 'remove acidic impurities' is the closest description.\nKey idea: Ammonolysis gives R–NH₃⁺X⁻; base deprotonates it to R–NH₂.","traps":["Base thought to activate NH₃: Ask what the product actually is: a salt that needs neutralising."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which of the following is least basic?","options":["(CH₃CO)N̈HC₂H₅","(C₂H₅)₃N̈","(CH₃CO)₂N̈H","(C₂H₅)₂N̈H"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) alkylamines – most basic (+I alkyl groups); (A) mono-amide – weakly basic but one acyl only, so more basic than the diacyl compound.\nWorking: in diacetamide (an imide) the N lone pair is delocalised into two C=O groups, so it is far less basic than the mono-amide; the two alkylamines are strongly basic.\nKey idea: Each C=O conjugated to N pulls the lone pair away; two acyls → least basic.","traps":["Tertiary amine thought least basic in water: Resonance with C=O lowers basicity far more than solvation effects."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Hoffmann bromamide degradation of benzamide gives product A, which upon heating with CHCl₃ and NaOH gives product B. The structures of A and B are:","options":["A – 4-bromoaniline; B – 2-amino-5-bromobenzaldehyde","A – aniline; B – phenyl isocyanide (C₆H₅NC)","A – aniline; B – 2-aminobenzaldehyde","A – 4-bromobenzamide; B – 4-bromo-2-formylbenzamide"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (A) – Reimer-Tiemann formylation (phenol reaction) wrongly applied to aniline; (A)/(D) – Br from Br₂ wrongly retained on the ring.\nWorking: C₆H₅CONH₂ + Br₂/NaOH → C₆H₅NH₂ (one carbon less); aniline + CHCl₃/NaOH (heat) → carbylamine reaction → C₆H₅NC.\nKey idea: CHCl₃/base with a primary amine → isocyanide; with phenol → salicylaldehyde.","traps":["Reimer-Tiemann applied to amine: Primary amine → carbylamine.","Bromine retained in product: Br₂ only forms N-bromoamide; ring not brominated."],"topics":["Hoffmann bromamide","Tests for amines"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ammonolysis of alkyl halides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following reaction is an example of ammonolysis?","options":["C₆H₅COCl + C₆H₅NH₂ → C₆H₅CONHC₆H₅","C₆H₅CH₂CN —[H]→ C₆H₅CH₂CH₂NH₂","C₆H₅NH₂ —HCl→ C₆H₅NH₃⁺Cl⁻","C₆H₅CH₂Cl + NH₃ → C₆H₅CH₂NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) acylation (benzoylation) of aniline; (B) reduction of nitrile; (C) salt formation.\nWorking: ammonolysis = cleavage of C–X by NH₃; benzyl chloride + NH₃ → benzylamine.\nKey idea: Ammonolysis requires NH₃ as the nucleophile breaking a C–X bond.","traps":["Any N-acylation called ammonolysis: Ammonolysis uses NH₃ itself."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Primary, secondary and tertiary amines can be separated using :-","options":["Para-Toluene sulphonyl chloride","Chloroform and KOH","Benzene sulphonic acid","Acetyl amide"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) CHCl₃/KOH is the carbylamine test (1° only, a test not a separation); (C) the sulphonic acid lacks the reactive S–Cl; (D) not a reagent for this.\nWorking: an arylsulphonyl chloride (Hinsberg-type reagent; p-toluenesulphonyl chloride works like benzenesulphonyl chloride) gives an alkali-soluble sulphonamide with 1°, an insoluble one with 2°, and no reaction with 3° amines.\nKey idea: Sulphonyl CHLORIDE (benzene- or p-toluene-) is needed; sulphonic acid does not react.","traps":["Sulphonic acid vs sulphonyl chloride: The reagent must have the S–Cl bond.","Carbylamine test confused with separation: Carbylamine detects 1° amines only; it cannot separate three classes."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"C₇H₇N₂OCl (A) + C₂H₅OH → anisole (C₆H₅OCH₃) + N₂ + \"X\" + \"Y\"\nIn the above reaction, the structural formula of (A), \"X\" and \"Y\" respectively are :","options":["p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–N₂⁺Cl⁻ (4-methoxybenzenediazonium chloride), CH₃CHO, HCl","p-Cl–C₆H₄–N₂⁺ ⁻OCH₃ (4-chlorobenzenediazonium methoxide), ethylene oxide, HCl","p-Cl–C₆H₄–N₂⁺ ⁻OCH₃ (4-chlorobenzenediazonium methoxide), CH₃CHO, H₂O","p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–N₂⁺Cl⁻ (4-methoxybenzenediazonium chloride), ethylene oxide, H₂O"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): Cl on the ring with methoxide counter-ion would give chlorobenzene, not anisole; (D)/(B): ethylene oxide is not an oxidation product of ethanol; H₂O instead of HCl ignores the chloride.\nWorking: A = 4-methoxybenzenediazonium chloride (C₇H₇N₂OCl); ethanol reduces ArN₂⁺ to ArH (anisole), being oxidised to ethanal; the chloride leaves as HCl: ArN₂⁺Cl⁻ + CH₃CH₂OH → ArH + N₂ + CH₃CHO + HCl.\nKey idea: Ethanol acts as a reductant: ArN₂⁺Cl⁻ → ArH + N₂, ethanol → ethanal, chloride → HCl.","traps":["Ring substituent vs counter-ion: The OCH₃ stays on the ring in the product anisole, so it must be on the ring in A.","Oxidation product of ethanol: Ethanol loses 2H → ethanal."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Aniline (C₆H₅NH₂) is treated with NaNO₂, HCl at 273–278 K to give X (major product); X then reacts with N,N-dimethylaniline (C₆H₅N(CH₃)₂) to give Y (major product). Considering the above reaction, X and Y respectively are:","options":["Benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) and 2-(phenylazo)-N,N-dimethylaniline (azo group ortho to NMe₂)","Benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻) and 4-(phenylazo)-N,N-dimethylaniline, C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄–N(CH₃)₂ (para; methyl yellow / butter yellow)","Chlorobenzene and 4-(phenylazo)-N,N-dimethylaniline (para)","Chlorobenzene and 2-(phenylazo)-N,N-dimethylaniline (azo group ortho to NMe₂, same Y as option A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ortho coupling – ignores the para preference of azo coupling; (C) chlorobenzene – confuses diazotisation with Sandmeyer; (D) chlorobenzene plus ortho coupling – wrong on both counts.\nWorking: diazotisation at 0–5 °C gives C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ (not chlorobenzene – that needs CuCl/Sandmeyer); the diazonium ion is a weak electrophile and couples at the para position of the strongly activated N,N-dimethylaniline (p-dimethylaminoazobenzene).\nKey idea: Diazonium salts couple para to a strong activating group (–NMe₂, –OH, –NH₂).","traps":["Ortho vs para coupling: Coupling goes para unless para is blocked.","Diazonium salt vs aryl chloride: Cl⁻ only replaces N₂ with Cu(I) (Sandmeyer); cold NaNO₂/HCl stops at the diazonium salt."],"topics":["Coupling reactions","Diazonium preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In the reaction of hypobromite with amide, the carbonyl carbon is lost as :","options":["CO₃²⁻","HCO₃⁻","CO₂","CO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) CO₂ is the neutral form students expect from the isocyanate hydrolysis; (B) HCO₃⁻ ignores the excess NaOH; (D) CO is a decarbonylation guess.\nWorking: in the Hoffmann bromamide degradation RCONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → RNH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O; the carbonyl carbon leaves as carbonate in the strongly basic medium.\nKey idea: Isocyanate hydrolysis gives CO₂, which in 4 equivalents of NaOH ends as CO₃²⁻.","traps":["CO₂ vs carbonate: In excess NaOH, CO₂ is trapped as CO₃²⁻ – read the balanced equation."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"An organic compound \"A\" on treatment with benzene sulphonyl chloride gives compound B. B is soluble in dil. NaOH solution. Compound A is :","options":["C₆H₅–N–(CH₃)₂","C₆H₅–NHCH₂CH₃","C₆H₅–CH₂NHCH₃","C₆H₅–CH(CH₃)–NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is tertiary (no reaction); (B) and (C) are secondary – sulphonamide forms but is insoluble in NaOH; (C) is written with a CH₂ to look like a primary benzylamine.\nWorking: only a primary amine gives an N-alkylbenzenesulphonamide with an acidic N–H that dissolves in NaOH; (D) is the only primary amine (1-phenylethanamine).\nKey idea: Alkali-soluble sulphonamide ⇒ N–H left after sulphonylation ⇒ primary amine.","traps":["Benzylic CH₂ mistaken for primary amine: Count H atoms on N, not on carbon.","Primary vs secondary amine products: Only the 1° product has an acidic N–H."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Aniline —(HNO₃, H₂SO₄, 288 K)→ A (o-nitroaniline) + B (m-nitroaniline) + C (p-nitroaniline).\nConsider the given reaction, percentage yield of :","options":["C > A > B","B > C > A","A > C > B","C > B > A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) follow the simple o/p-director rule (ortho second or first); (B) overstates meta above para.\nWorking: direct nitration in strong acid protonates much of the aniline to meta-directing anilinium, giving roughly p 51 %, m 47 %, o 2 %; so para > meta > ortho.\nKey idea: Anilinium ion makes meta substantial (47 %); ortho is only ~2 %.","traps":["–NH₂ taken as only o/p director: In H₂SO₄ much of it is –NH₃⁺, a meta director.","Ortho assumed larger than meta: Recall NCERT yields: o only 2 %."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Identify A in the following reaction: aniline (C₆H₅NH₂) —K₂Cr₂O₇→ A","options":["p-Benzoquinone (cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione)","p-Aminophenoxide drawn as 4-H₂N–C₆H₄–OK (potassium 4-aminophenoxide)","Nitrobenzene, C₆H₅NO₂","Nitrocyclohexane, C₆H₁₁NO₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) potassium aminophenoxide – a hydroxylation product with no oxidation of the ring to quinone; (C) nitrobenzene – needs peroxy acids, not dichromate; (D) nitrocyclohexane – ring reduction is impossible with an oxidant.\nWorking: acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ oxidises aniline (via p-aminophenol/quinone-imine, hydrolysed) to p-benzoquinone.\nKey idea: Strongly activated arenes (phenol, aniline) are oxidised by chromic acid to p-benzoquinone.","traps":["NH₂ oxidised only to NO₂: Acidic dichromate oxidises the activated ring of aniline; the product is p-benzoquinone.","Analogy with phenol oxidation missed: Phenol and aniline both oxidise with chromic acid to p-benzoquinone."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which one of the following gives the most stable Diazonium salt?","options":["CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NH₂ (propan-1-amine)","4-Methylaniline (p-toluidine), 4-CH₃–C₆H₄–NH₂","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–NH₂ (propan-2-amine)","N-Methylaniline, C₆H₅–NHCH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) primary aliphatic amines – unstable alkyldiazonium ions; (D) secondary aromatic amine – forms N-nitroso compound.\nWorking: only p-toluidine is a primary aromatic amine; its arenediazonium ion is resonance-stabilised. Aliphatic diazonium ions decompose; N-methylaniline gives an N-nitrosamine.\nKey idea: Ar–NH₂ (1°) → ArN₂⁺, resonance-stabilised.","traps":["Secondary aromatic amine chosen: Secondary amines give N-nitrosamines with HNO₂, not diazonium salts.","Aliphatic diazonium thought stable: Alkyldiazonium salts lose N₂ at once; only arenediazonium salts are stabilised by resonance."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The increasing order of pK_b for the following compounds will be : NH₂–CH=NH (A); [bicyclic guanidine: 1,5,7-triazabicyclo[4.4.0]dec-5-ene, two fused six-membered rings sharing a C=N carbon with ring N, NH and =N] (B); CH₃NHCH₃ (C)","options":["(A) < (B) < (C)","(C) < (A) < (B)","(B) < (A) < (C)","(B) < (C) < (A)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): reverse order from reading pK_b as base strength. (D): ranks dimethylamine above amidine using +I effect only.\nWorking: lowest pK_b = strongest base. Guanidine (B) gives a conjugate acid with three equivalent resonance forms (strongest); amidine (A) has two-form resonance-stabilised amidinium; dimethylamine (C) has no such resonance, so pK_b: B < A < C.\nKey idea: Protonation at the imine N gives a delocalised cation (amidinium/guanidinium); more equivalent resonance forms → stronger base → lower pK_b.","traps":["pK_b order read as basicity order: Lower pK_b = stronger base; write basicity first, then reverse.","sp² imine N assumed weaker than sp³ amine N: Check the conjugate acid: resonance delocalisation of + charge outweighs hybridisation."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the following reaction : C₆H₅–N(CH₃)₂ + p-(Na⁺ ⁻O₃S)–C₆H₄–N₂⁺Cl⁻ —OH⁻→ 'X'. The product 'X' is used :","options":["in acid base titration as an indicator","in protein estimation as an alternative to ninhydrin","in laboratory test for phenols","as food grade colourant"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): azo dyes are colourants, but methyl orange is not food grade. (C): diazonium coupling is a phenol test, not the product's use. (B): unrelated.\nWorking: azo coupling of diazotised sulphanilic acid (sodium salt) with N,N-dimethylaniline at the para position gives methyl orange, an acid-base indicator.\nKey idea: Me₂N–C₆H₄–N=N–C₆H₄–SO₃Na = methyl orange (indicator).","traps":["Azo dye equated with food colour: Recognise the sulphonated dimethylamino azo compound as methyl orange.","Coupling used as phenol test: The question asks the product's use, not the reaction's use."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In the following reaction sequence\np-toluidine (4-methylaniline) —Ac₂O→ A —Br₂/AcOH→ B\nthe major products B is -","options":["N-(2-bromo-4-methylphenyl)acetamide (Br ortho to NHCOCH₃, CH₃ para)","N-(2-bromo-4-(bromomethyl)phenyl)acetamide (Br ortho to NHCOCH₃, CH₂Br para)","N-(3-bromo-4-methylphenyl)acetamide (Br ortho to CH₃)","N-(2-acetyl-4-methylphenyl)acetamide (COCH₃ ortho to NHCOCH₃)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) brominates the methyl group (free-radical condition not present); (C) follows the CH₃ director; (D) treats Ac₂O as a Friedel–Crafts acylating agent on the ring.\nWorking: Ac₂O gives 4-methylacetanilide (A); NHCOCH₃ is a stronger o/p director than CH₃ and its para position is blocked, so Br enters ortho to NHCOCH₃ (monobromination because acetylation moderates activation).\nKey idea: The stronger activator (–NHCOCH₃) decides the position: ortho to it, since para is occupied.","traps":["CH₃ allowed to direct: Lone-pair (+R) donor beats hyperconjugation.","Side-chain bromination: Benzylic bromination needs light/heat radical conditions, not Br₂/AcOH."],"topics":["Aniline substitution","Alkylation & acylation"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Diazonium in synthesis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The major product Z obtained in the following reaction scheme is: 3-bromoaniline —NaNO₂/HCl, 273–278 K→ X —Cu₂Br₂→ Y —HNO₃/H₂SO₄→ Z","options":["1,3-dibromo-2-nitrobenzene (NO₂ between the two Br)","2,4-dibromo-1-nitrobenzene (NO₂ ortho to one Br and para to the other)","1,3-dibromo-2,4-dinitrobenzene (four consecutive ring positions NO₂, Br, NO₂, Br; two NO₂ groups)","1,3-dibromo-5-nitrobenzene (NO₂ meta to both Br)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) nitrates between the Br (sterically blocked); (D) treats Br as meta director; (C) assumes double nitration.\nWorking: diazotisation then Sandmeyer gives 1,3-dibromobenzene (Y). Both Br are o/p directors; C-4 is ortho to one Br and para to the other and less hindered than C-2, so mononitration gives 2,4-dibromo-1-nitrobenzene.\nKey idea: Halogens are deactivating but o/p directing; reinforced, less crowded position wins.","traps":["Halogen treated as meta director: Halogens direct o/p despite deactivation.","Ignoring steric hindrance at C-2: Prefer C-4 (ortho + para, less crowded)."],"topics":["Diazonium in synthesis","Sandmeyer & Gattermann"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The decreasing order of basicity of the following amines is: (I) aniline, C₆H₅NH₂; (II) pyridine; (III) cyclohexylamine; (IV) pyrrole","options":["(I) > (III) > (IV) > (II)","(III) > (I) > (II) > (IV)","(III) > (II) > (I) > (IV)","(II) > (III) > (IV) > (I)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts aniline above pyridine; (A) puts aniline first; (D) puts pyridine above cyclohexylamine and aniline last instead of pyrrole.\nWorking: cyclohexylamine (sp³ N, localised lone pair, pKaH ≈ 10.6) > pyridine (sp² N, pKaH ≈ 5.2) > aniline (lone pair delocalised, pKaH ≈ 4.6) > pyrrole (lone pair part of aromatic sextet, essentially non-basic).\nKey idea: Pyrrole N lone pair is in the aromatic sextet → least basic; pyridine lone pair is not delocalised → beats aniline.","traps":["Pyridine assumed weaker than aniline: Pyridine lone pair is not in the ring π system; pKaH 5.2 > 4.6.","Pyrrole treated like a secondary amine: Pyrrole lone pair is used for aromaticity."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Consider the following reactions: [P] —(i) NaNO₂/HCl, 0–5 °C; (ii) β-naphthol/NaOH→ coloured solid; [P] —Br₂/H₂O→ C₇H₆NBr₃. The compound [P] is:","options":["o-toluidine (2-methylaniline)","m-toluidine (3-methylaniline)","p-toluidine (4-methylaniline)","N-methyl-m-toluidine (3-methyl-N-methylaniline)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) o-toluidine has only 4,6 free → C₇H₇NBr₂; (C) p-toluidine has only 2,6 free; (D) secondary amine → N-nitrosamine, no azo dye, and formula C₈.\nWorking: azo dye formation needs a primary aromatic amine; tribromination needs positions 2, 4, 6 (o/p to NH₂) all free, which is only true for m-toluidine; o- and p-toluidine give dibromo products; option (D) is secondary and would give C₈ product.\nKey idea: Formula C₇H₆NBr₃ ⇒ 3 Br on ring ⇒ CH₃ must be meta to NH₂.","traps":["p-Toluidine chosen as typical coupling amine: Para blocked by CH₃ → only two Br.","Molecular formula not checked: Count C in the given product formula."],"topics":["Coupling reactions","Aniline substitution"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Three isomers A, B and C (mol. formula C₈H₁₁N) give the following results :\nA and C —(Diazotization)→ P + Q —(i) Hydrolysis (ii) oxidation (KMnO₄ + H⁺)→ R (product of A) + S (product of C)\nR has lower boiling point than S\nB —(C₆H₅SO₂Cl)→ alkali-insoluble product\nA, B and C, respectively are :","options":["2-ethylaniline; 1-ethyl-3-methylbenzene (no N); 3-ethylaniline","2-ethylaniline; N-methylbenzylamine (C₆H₅CH₂NHCH₃); 4-ethylaniline","4-ethylaniline; N-ethylaniline (C₆H₅NHCH₂CH₃); 2-ethylaniline","3-ethylaniline; N-methylbenzylamine (C₆H₅CH₂NHCH₃); 2-ethylaniline"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B is a hydrocarbon (no N) – cannot react with C₆H₅SO₂Cl; (C) A para / C ortho reverses the b.p. clue; (D) A meta / C ortho – R (meta acid) would boil higher than salicylic acid.\nWorking: A and C are primary arylamines (diazotised, hydrolysed to phenols, side chain oxidised to –COOH → hydroxybenzoic acids). R has lower b.p. → salicylic acid (intramolecular H-bond) → A = 2-ethylaniline; S = 4-hydroxybenzoic acid → C = 4-ethylaniline. B gives an alkali-insoluble sulphonamide → secondary amine (N-methylbenzylamine). Only (B) fits.\nKey idea: Ortho-hydroxy acid (intramolecular H-bond) is more volatile than its para isomer; secondary amine → alkali-insoluble Hinsberg product.","traps":["Ortho/para assignment swapped: Intramolecular H-bond (ortho) lowers b.p., so the lower-boiling R comes from the ortho amine.","Secondary amine Hinsberg product thought soluble: 1° sulphonamide has acidic N–H (alkali-soluble); 2° does not."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Reaction with nitrous acid"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following will react with CHCl₃ + alc. KOH ?","options":["Adenine and lysine","Adenine and thymine","Adenine and proline","Thymine and proline"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) thymine has only ring NH (imide) – no primary NH₂; (C) proline is a secondary (ring) amine; (D) both fail.\nWorking: carbylamine reaction needs a primary amino group; adenine (6-aminopurine) and lysine (two –NH₂) have one; thymine has no –NH₂ (only ring N–H amides) and proline is a secondary amine.\nKey idea: Look for a free –NH₂: adenine (C-6 NH₂) and lysine (α and ε NH₂).","traps":["Proline taken as primary amine: Proline's N is inside a pyrrolidine ring – secondary.","Thymine thought to carry NH₂: Thymine/uracil have C=O and ring NH, no NH₂."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The most appropriate reagent for conversion of C₂H₅CN into CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ is :","options":["Na(CN)BH₃","LiAlH₄","NaBH₄","CaH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NaBH₃CN – used for reductive amination of imines; (C) NaBH₄ – reduces aldehydes/ketones, not nitriles; (D) CaH₂ – base/drying agent.\nWorking: nitriles are reduced to primary amines by LiAlH₄ (or H₂/Ni, Na/EtOH); NaBH₄ and NaBH₃CN do not reduce nitriles; CaH₂ is a drying agent.\nKey idea: LiAlH₄ reduces C≡N to CH₂NH₂.","traps":["NaBH₄ assumed to reduce nitriles: NaBH₄ is mild – C=O only."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aniline substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The final major product of the following reaction is:\n[3-methylaniline (m-toluidine)] —(i) Ac₂O/pyridine; (ii) Br₂, FeCl₃; (iii) OH⁻/Δ→","options":["4-bromo-3-methylaniline (Br para to NH₂, ortho to CH₃)","2-bromo-5-methylaniline (Br ortho to NH₂, para to CH₃)","2-bromo-3-methylaniline (Br between NH₂ and CH₃)","3-bromo-5-methylaniline (Br meta to NH₂)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ortho to NHAc / para to CH₃ – the CH₃ director wins only if NHAc's dominance and its bulk are ignored; (C) the crowded 2-position between two groups; (D) meta to NH₂ – wrong orientation for an o/p director.\nWorking: acetylation gives the acetanilide (moderate o/p director, bulky); bromination goes para to –NHCOCH₃ (C-4, also ortho to CH₃); alkaline hydrolysis restores –NH₂ → 4-bromo-3-methylaniline.\nKey idea: The stronger activator (–NHCOCH₃) controls orientation and its bulk favours para.","traps":["Weaker director allowed to control: Let the stronger activator decide; para to NHAc is favoured sterically.","Deprotection step forgotten: Hydrolysis removes the acetyl group to give free NH₂."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The increasing order of pK_b values of the following compounds is -\nI: 4-methoxy-N,N-dimethylaniline; II: N,N-dimethylaniline; III: 3-cyano-N-methylaniline; IV: 3-hydroxy-N-methylaniline","options":["I < II < IV < III","II < IV < III < I","II < I < III < IV","I < II < III < IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps III and IV (treats OH as more withdrawing than CN); (B)/(C) put II above I, ignoring the +M of para-OCH₃.\nWorking: p-OCH₃ (+M) raises basicity of I above II; meta-CN and meta-OH act only by −I, CN much stronger, so III is the weakest base. Basicity I > II > IV > III, so pK_b I < II < IV < III.\nKey idea: Meta groups act by −I only: CN (strong −I) > OH (weak −I).","traps":["OH treated as donating at meta: At meta, OH has no resonance link to N; only −I acts.","pKb vs basicity direction reversed: Lower pKb = stronger base."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following compounds can be prepared in good yield by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis?","options":["C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ (benzylamine)","C₆H₅NH₂ (aniline)","C₆H₅CH₂CONH₂ (phenylacetamide)","CH₃–CH₂–NHCH₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aniline – primary but aromatic, aryl halide will not react; (C) amide – not an amine; (D) secondary amine – Gabriel gives only 1°.\nWorking: Gabriel gives primary aliphatic amines; benzyl bromide undergoes SN2 with phthalimide anion. Aryl halides do not (no aniline); secondary amines and amides are not formed.\nKey idea: Benzylamine has NH₂ on an sp³ carbon, so it counts as a primary \"aliphatic-type\" amine.","traps":["Any primary amine accepted: Check the halide needed: aryl halide does not undergo SN2.","Benzylamine thought aromatic: NH₂ is on CH₂ (sp³), not on the ring."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In the following compounds, the decreasing order of basic strength will be – (C₂H₅)₂NH, C₂H₅NH₂, NH₃","options":["(C₂H₅)₂NH > C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃","(C₂H₅)₂NH > NH₃ > C₂H₅NH₂","NH₃ > C₂H₅NH₂ > (C₂H₅)₂NH","C₂H₅NH₂ > NH₃ > (C₂H₅)₂NH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts NH₃ above ethylamine; (C) reverses the order; (D) places diethylamine weakest.\nWorking: +I effect and solvation both favour 2° > 1° > NH₃ for ethyl amines in water.\nKey idea: Alkyl groups raise basicity; 2° ethylamine strongest of the three.","traps":["Recalling solvation anomaly where it does not apply: Without 3° amine, 2° > 1° > NH₃ is straightforward."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Coupling of benzene diazonium chloride with 1-naphthol in alkaline medium will give [options are structures of phenylazo-1-naphthol isomers]","options":["3-(phenylazo)naphthalen-1-ol [N=N at C-3 of 1-naphthol]","2-(phenylazo)naphthalen-1-ol [N=N ortho to OH at C-2]","4-(phenylazo)naphthalen-1-ol [N=N para to OH at C-4]","5-(phenylazo)naphthalen-1-ol [OH and N=N on different rings]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) meta position, not activated; (B) ortho C-2 coupling – minor/alternative; (D) coupling on the other ring.\nWorking: phenoxide-type activation; coupling occurs at C-4 (para to OH) of 1-naphthol, giving 4-(phenylazo)-1-naphthol (orange dye).\nKey idea: Coupling goes para to OH when para is free.","traps":["Choosing ortho coupling: Para (C-4) is free so it is preferred."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following amines can be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide reaction ?","options":["Neo-pentylamine","n-butylamine","triethylamine","t-butylamine"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) neopentyl is 1° but SN2-inert; (C) 3° amine; (D) t-butyl halide eliminates.\nWorking: Gabriel synthesis needs SN2 on a primary unhindered halide; n-butyl bromide works. Neopentyl is too hindered, t-butyl is tertiary, triethylamine is 3°.\nKey idea: Gabriel = SN2 on unhindered 1° alkyl halide.","traps":["Neopentylamine accepted as 1°: Check SN2 feasibility of the halide."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The major product obtained in the following reaction is: 1-(2-amino-6-cyanophenyl)ethan-1-one [benzene with NH₂, C(=O)CH₃ and CN on adjacent positions, acetyl in the middle] —(i) CHCl₃/KOH; (ii) Pd/C, H₂→ ?","options":["Ring bearing NHCH₃, CH(OH)CH₃ and CH₂NH₂ (all three groups reduced)","Ring bearing NHCH₃, C(=O)CH₃ and CN","Ring bearing NHCH₃, CH(OH)CH₃ and CN","Ring bearing NHCHCl₂, CH(OH)CH₃ and CN"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reduces only the isocyanide; (C) leaves CN untouched; (D) treats CHCl₃ as a dichloromethylating agent of NH₂.\nWorking: carbylamine reaction converts ArNH₂ to ArN≡C; H₂/Pd-C then reduces the isocyanide to ArNHCH₃, the nitrile to CH₂NH₂ and the aryl ketone to the secondary alcohol. Selective survival of CN in (C) is not justified.\nKey idea: H₂/Pd-C reduces N≡C, C≡N and C=O alike here.","traps":["Partial reduction assumed: List every reducible group and apply H₂/Pd-C to each.","Carbylamine product misdrawn: Dichlorocarbene gives R–N≡C, which reduces to N–CH₃."],"topics":["Tests for amines","Amine preparation by reduction"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Aniline dissolved in dilute HCl is reacted with sodium nitrite at 0°C. This solution was added dropwise to a solution containing equimolar mixture of aniline and phenol in dil. HCl. The structure of the major product is :","options":["p-aminoazobenzene (C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄-p-NH₂)","p-hydroxyazobenzene (C₆H₅–N=N–C₆H₄-p-OH)","diazoaminobenzene (C₆H₅–N=N–NH–C₆H₅)","C₆H₅–N=N–O–C₆H₅ (O-coupled diazo ether)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) p-hydroxyazobenzene requires alkaline medium (phenoxide); (C) N-coupling product (kinetic, rearranges in acid); (D) O-coupling is not observed.\nWorking: in acidic (dil. HCl) medium phenol is not converted to phenoxide and couples poorly, whereas aniline (free-amine fraction) undergoes C-coupling at para position; major product p-aminoazobenzene. N-coupled diazoaminobenzene forms in weakly acidic/neutral conditions but rearranges in acid.\nKey idea: Phenol couples as phenoxide (alkaline); aniline couples in mildly acidic medium.","traps":["Phenol assumed more reactive coupling partner: Note the medium: no phenoxide in dil. HCl.","N-coupling product chosen: C-para coupling is the stable product in acid."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"2-(2-Iodoethyl)cyclohexan-1-one —KCN/DMSO→ [A] —H₂/Pd→ [B]. The major products A and B are, respectively: (1) A = 1-hydroxy-2-(2-iodoethyl)cyclohexane-1-carbonitrile (cyanohydrin, I retained); B = 1-(aminomethyl)-2-(2-iodoethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol; (2) A = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanenitrile; B = 2-(3-aminopropyl)cyclohexan-1-ol; (3) A = cyanohydrin with I retained (as in 1); B = 1-(aminomethyl)-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol (C–I reduced to C–H); (4) A = 3-(2-oxocyclohexyl)propanenitrile; B = 2-(3-aminopropyl)cyclohexan-1-one.","options":["cyanohydrin (HO, CN on C-1; CH₂CH₂I kept) ; HO/CH₂NH₂ on C-1 with CH₂CH₂I kept","2-(CH₂CH₂CN)cyclohexanone ; 2-(CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂)cyclohexanol","cyanohydrin (HO, CN on C-1; CH₂CH₂I kept) ; HO/CH₂NH₂ on C-1 with CH₂CH₃","2-(CH₂CH₂CN)cyclohexanone ; 2-(CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂)cyclohexanone"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) assume KCN adds to C=O as a cyanohydrin; (C) also wrongly reduces C–I to C–H; (D) leaves the ketone untouched by H₂/Pd.\nWorking: KCN in DMSO (polar aprotic) does S_N2 at the primary C–I, not cyanohydrin formation (no acid/HCN), giving the nitrile; H₂/Pd reduces C≡N to CH₂NH₂ and, as NCERT states for catalytic hydrogenation, the ketone to a secondary alcohol, so (B). Note: in practice Pd/H₂ reduces aliphatic ketones slowly, which is why (D) is a common objection.\nKey idea: Polar aprotic KCN substitutes the iodide; catalytic H₂ reduces both C≡N and C=O.","traps":["Cyanohydrin vs S_N2: No acid/HCN; good leaving group I with CN⁻ in DMSO → S_N2.","Ketone thought inert to H₂/Pd: NCERT lists catalytic hydrogenation for C=O reduction too."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Hinsberg's reagent is :","options":["C₆H₅SO₂Cl","C₆H₅COCl","SOCl₂","(COCl)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) benzoyl chloride (benzoylation reagent); (C) thionyl chloride; (D) oxalyl chloride – all acid chlorides.\nWorking: Hinsberg's reagent is benzenesulphonyl chloride, C₆H₅SO₂Cl.\nKey idea: Hinsberg = sulphonyl (SO₂) chloride.","traps":["Benzoyl vs benzenesulphonyl chloride: Hinsberg has SO₂."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂NH₂ (4-aminobutan-2-ol) —[ethyl formate (1 equiv.), triethylamine]→ ?","options":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂NHCHO","CH₃CH=CH–CH₂NH₂","CH₃CH(O–CHO)CH₂CH₂NH₂ (O-formyl ester, 4-aminobutan-2-yl formate)","CH₃–CH(OH)–CH=CH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) are elimination products – no dehydrating/base-strong conditions; (C) O-formylation assumes OH is the better nucleophile.\nWorking: with only 1 equiv. of the acylating ester, the more nucleophilic –NH₂ is acylated (aminolysis of ethyl formate) to give the N-formamide; OH stays free.\nKey idea: With a limited acylating agent, NH₂ reacts before OH.","traps":["O-acylation chosen over N-acylation: Compare nucleophilicity: N lone pair is more available than O.","Triethylamine read as an elimination base: No leaving group is present; Et₃N only scavenges acid."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gabriel synthesis","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Ethylamine (C₂H₅NH₂) can be obtained from N-ethylphthalimide on treatment with :","options":["NaBH₄","CaH₂","H₂O","NH₂NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NaBH₄ reduces carbonyls but does not free the amine; (B) CaH₂ is a drying agent/base; (C) neutral water is too weak to cleave the imide (NCERT uses aqueous alkali).\nWorking: hydrazinolysis (Ing–Manske) of N-alkylphthalimide releases the primary amine and phthalhydrazide (NCERT uses alkaline hydrolysis; hydrazine is the standard alternative).\nKey idea: NH₂NH₂ cleaves N-alkylphthalimide to RNH₂ + phthalhydrazide.","traps":["Plain water mistaken for the hydrolysis step: Hydrolysis needs alkali; hydrazine is the usual reagent.","Hydride reagent chosen to \"reduce\" to amine: Gabriel cleavage is nucleophilic acyl substitution, not reduction."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amine preparation by reduction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a correct method of the preparation of benzylamine from cyanobenzene?","options":["(i) HCl/H₂O (ii) NaBH₄","(i) LiAlH₄ (ii) H₃O⁺","(i) SnCl₂ + HCl(gas) (ii) NaBH₄","H₂/Ni"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) are the NCERT methods; (C) looks wrong because Stephen reduction is taught as an aldehyde route, but the imine intermediate is reduced by NaBH₄ to the amine.\nWorking: HCl/H₂O hydrolyses PhCN to PhCOOH, which NaBH₄ does not reduce, so no amine forms. LiAlH₄ and H₂/Ni reduce C≡N to CH₂NH₂; SnCl₂/HCl gives the aldimine (Stephen), which NaBH₄ reduces to PhCH₂NH₂.\nKey idea: Acid hydrolysis converts the nitrile to benzoic acid; NaBH₄ cannot reduce a carboxylic acid.","traps":["Stephen reagent assumed to give only aldehyde: The intermediate aldimine is reducible by NaBH₄ to the amine.","NaBH₄ thought to reduce carboxylic acids: NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes, ketones and imines, not COOH."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The increasing order of the pK_b of the following compound is : (A) 1-(4-fluorophenyl)-3-phenylthiourea; (B) 1-(4-methoxyphenyl)-3-phenylthiourea; (C) 1-(4-nitrophenyl)-3-phenylthiourea; (D) 1-(4-methylphenyl)-3-phenylthiourea [each Ar–NH–C(=S)–NH–Ph]","options":["(A) < (C) < (D) < (B)","(B) < (D) < (A) < (C)","(C) < (A) < (D) < (B)","(B) < (D) < (C) < (A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option C reverses (pK_b vs K_b confusion); option A puts F most basic; option D misplaces NO₂ above F.\nWorking: basicity order OCH₃ (strong +R) > CH₃ (+I/hyperconjugation) > F (−I > +R) > NO₂ (−R, −I); lower pK_b = stronger base, so pK_b: B < D < A < C.\nKey idea: Stronger base ⇒ smaller pK_b; OMe > Me > F > NO₂ in donating power.","traps":["pK_b vs basic strength: Write basicity order first, then reverse for pK_b only if needed.","Halogen effect misjudged: For halogens −I dominates; still better than NO₂."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coupling reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Benzene diazonium chloride on reaction with aniline in the presence of dilute hydrochloric acid gives :","options":["o-aminoazobenzene, Ph–N=N–C₆H₄–NH₂ (ortho)","4-aminobiphenyl, Ph–C₆H₄–NH₂","p-aminoazobenzene, Ph–N=N–C₆H₄–NH₂ (para)","diazoaminobenzene, Ph–N=N–NH–Ph"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) diazoaminobenzene forms by N-coupling in neutral medium; (A) ortho product; (B) loses N₂ (Gomberg-type) product.\nWorking: in mildly acidic medium the diazonium ion couples at the para position of aniline (C-coupling), giving p-aminoazobenzene.\nKey idea: Coupling is para electrophilic substitution under mildly acidic conditions.","traps":["N-coupling product chosen: Acidic medium favours C-coupling at para.","Ortho coupling: Para preferred sterically."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Major product of the following reaction is :\n[Scheme: 2-chloroacryloyl chloride CH₂=C(Cl)–COCl + glycinamide H₂N–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂ —(1) Et₃N, (2) Free radical polymerisation→ ?]","options":["Polymer –[CH₂–C(Cl)(C(=O)NH–C(=O)CH₂NH₂)]ₙ– (acylation at the amide nitrogen; imide side chain)","Polymer with backbone carbons bearing –COCl and –NH–C(=O)CH₂NH₂ (amine added to the C=C, acid chloride intact)","Polymer with backbone carbons bearing –COCl and –NH–CH₂C(=O)NH₂ (conjugate addition of amine, acid chloride intact)","Polymer –[CH₂–C(Cl)(C(=O)NH–CH₂–C(=O)NH₂)]ₙ– (amide formed at the amine nitrogen, then vinyl polymerisation)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): acylation at amide N. (B)/(C): amine adds across C=C and COCl survives, which is chemically unlikely.\nWorking: the more nucleophilic amine NH₂ of glycinamide (not the amide NH₂, whose lone pair is delocalised) is acylated by the acid chloride (Et₃N takes up HCl) to give CH₂=C(Cl)–CO–NH–CH₂–CONH₂; free-radical polymerisation through C=C gives –[CH₂–C(Cl)(CONHCH₂CONH₂)]ₙ–, i.e. option (D).\nKey idea: Acid chloride reacts with the most nucleophilic N; the C=C is kept for polymerisation.","traps":["Amide N taken as the nucleophile: Amide N lone pair is delocalised into C=O; the amine N reacts.","Conjugate addition instead of acylation: Acid chloride is far more reactive than the C=C toward an amine with Et₃N."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Arrange the following amines in the decreasing order of basicity:\n(I) pyridine (II) pyrrole (1H-pyrrole) (III) piperidine","options":["I > II > III","III > II > I","I > III > II","III > I > II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): pyridine first, piperidine last. (B): pyrrole above pyridine. (C): pyridine above piperidine.\nWorking: piperidine (sp³ N, localised lone pair) > pyridine (sp² N, lone pair not in the π system) > pyrrole (lone pair part of the aromatic sextet) → III > I > II.\nKey idea: Lone pair in aromatic sextet (pyrrole) = almost non-basic.","traps":["Pyridine put above piperidine: sp² N holds its lone pair more tightly than sp³ N.","Pyrrole treated as an ordinary 2° amine: Pyrrole N lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Basicity of amines","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The increasing basicity order of the following compounds is: (A) CH₃CH₂NH₂ (B) (CH₃CH₂)₂NH (C) (CH₃)₃N (D) C₆H₅NHCH₃","options":["(D) < (C) < (A) < (B)","(A) < (B) < (D) < (C)","(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(D) < (C) < (B) < (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option B: ordering by inductive effect alone puts the 3° amine most basic. Option C makes N-methylaniline the most basic. Option D reverses the aliphatic order.\nWorking: N-methylaniline least basic (lone pair delocalised); in water (CH₃)₃N (pKb 4.22) < C₂H₅NH₂ (3.29) < (C₂H₅)₂NH (3.00).\nKey idea: In water, 3° methylamine is less basic than 1° ethylamine because of poor solvation; arylamine weakest.","traps":["Gas-phase order used in water: In water, solvation and sterics make 3° amines weaker."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The major product obtained in the following reaction is: C₆H₅–CH(OH)–CH(NH₂)–CH₃ —(CH₃CO)₂O / pyridine (1 eqv.), room temp→ ?","options":["o-Acetyl ring product: 2-(CH₃CO)–C₆H₄–CH(OH)–CH(NH₂)–CH₃","O-acetyl: C₆H₅–CH(OCOCH₃)–CH(NH₂)–CH₃","N-acetyl: C₆H₅–CH(OH)–CH(NHCOCH₃)–CH₃","Enamide alkene: C₆H₅–C(stub)=C(CH₃)–NHCOCH₃ (dehydrated N-acetyl product; the benzylic carbon carries a short unlabelled stub, read as CH₃ or H)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Friedel–Crafts on the ring – no Lewis acid. (B) O-acylation – OH is less nucleophilic than NH₂. (D) Elimination – no dehydrating conditions.\nWorking: with only one equivalent of anhydride at room temperature the more nucleophilic NH₂ is acylated selectively, giving the N-acetyl amide.\nKey idea: Amine N is a better nucleophile than alcohol O; 1 equiv reagent acylates N only.","traps":["O-acylation chosen: With one equivalent, the stronger nucleophile (NH₂) reacts."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"An aromatic compound 'A' having molecular formula C₇H₆O₂ on treating with aqueous ammonia and heating forms compound 'B'. The compound 'B' on reaction with molecular bromine and potassium hydroxide provides compound 'C' having molecular formula C₆H₇N. The structure of 'A' is :","options":["3-(2-furyl)prop-2-enal (furan ring with –CH=CH–CHO)","salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde)","benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH)","4-hydroxybenzaldehyde"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) furylacrolein: C₇H₆O₂ but not aromatic benzene-type and gives no amide. (B) and (D) hydroxybenzaldehydes: same formula but aldehydes do not form amides with NH₃.\nWorking: C₆H₅COOH + NH₃ → C₆H₅COO⁻NH₄⁺ → (Δ) C₆H₅CONH₂ (B); Hoffmann bromamide (Br₂/KOH) → C₆H₅NH₂ (C₆H₇N, aniline).\nKey idea: Only a carboxylic acid gives an amide with NH₃/Δ; Br₂/KOH removes one carbon.","traps":["Isomers of C₇H₆O₂: Need –COOH to make an amide for Hoffmann."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hoffmann bromamide","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A compound 'X' on treatment with Br₂/NaOH, provided C₃H₉N, which gives positive carbylamine test. Compound 'X' is :-","options":["CH₃COCH₂NHCH₃","CH₃CH₂COCH₂NH₂","CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂","CH₃CON(CH₃)₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B): amino ketones, not amides – no Hoffmann reaction. (D) tertiary amide (no N–H) cannot undergo Hoffmann.\nWorking: Hoffmann bromamide degradation converts RCONH₂ to RNH₂ with one carbon less; butanamide → propan-1-amine C₃H₉N (1° amine, positive carbylamine).\nKey idea: Primary amide with n+1 carbons → 1° amine with n carbons.","traps":["Amide with N-substituents: Hoffmann needs –CONH₂ (two N–H).","Carbon loss forgotten: Hoffmann removes the C=O carbon."],"topics":["Hoffmann bromamide","Tests for amines"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkylation & acylation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The increasing order of reactivity of the following compounds towards reaction with alkyl halides directly is : (A) Benzamide, C₆H₅CONH₂; (B) Phthalimide (isoindoline-1,3-dione); (C) 2-Aminobenzonitrile (o-cyanoaniline); (D) Aniline","options":["(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)","(A) < (C) < (D) < (B)","(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts o-cyanoaniline above aniline. (D) imide above amide. (C) imide most reactive (confusing with Gabriel, which needs KOH first).\nWorking: nucleophilicity follows lone-pair availability: imide N (two C=O) < amide N (one C=O) < aniline with o-CN (−I/−R) < aniline.\nKey idea: More C=O groups on N ⇒ less nucleophilic; EWG on ring lowers aniline nucleophilicity.","traps":["Phthalimide thought reactive: Gabriel needs KOH to form the imide anion first; neutral imide is weakest.","CN effect ignored: −I/−R of CN lowers lone-pair availability."]}],"m10-pblock":[{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: In group 13, the stability of +1 oxidation state increases down the group.\nStatement II: The atomic size of gallium is greater than that of aluminium.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): student applies \"size increases down group\" to Ga. (A)/(D): student thinks +3 becomes more stable down the group.\nWorking: inert pair effect makes +1 more stable down group 13 (Tl⁺ most stable); Ga (≈135 pm) is smaller than Al (≈143 pm) because poorly shielding 3d electrons raise Z_eff.\nKey idea: Ga < Al in size due to 10 poorly shielding 3d electrons; +1 stabilises down group via inert pair.","traps":["Size assumed to increase Al → Ga: Remember the d-contraction anomaly: Ga is smaller than Al.","Inert pair trend reversed: Down group 13, +1 gets more stable (Tl⁺)."],"topics":["Group 13","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The number of neutrons present in the more abundant isotope of boron is 'x'. Amorphous boron upon heating with air forms a product, in which the oxidation state of boron is 'y'. The value of x + y is ____.","options":["9","6","4","3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 6: x alone (forgetting y), or ¹⁰B neutrons 5 + 1. Using ¹⁰B gives 5 + 3 = 8 (not offered), which pushes a student to re-check. (D) 3: y alone. (C) 4: neutrons miscounted.\nWorking: ¹¹B (~80%) has 6 neutrons (x = 6); amorphous B burns in air to B₂O₃ (and BN), boron +3 (y = 3); x + y = 9.\nKey idea: ¹¹B is ≈ 80% abundant → 6 neutrons; B₂O₃ → +3.","traps":["¹⁰B taken as more abundant: Atomic mass 10.81 is closer to 11, so ¹¹B dominates.","Neutron count from mass vs atomic number: 11 − 5 = 6, then add y."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct statements from the following are:\n(A) The decreasing order of atomic radii of group 13 elements is Tl > In > Ga > Al > B.\n(B) Down the group 13 electronegativity decreases from top to bottom.\n(C) Al dissolves in dil. HCl and liberates H₂ but conc. HNO₃ renders Al passive by forming a protective oxide layer on the surface.\n(D) All elements of group 13 exhibits highly stable +1 oxidation state.\n(E) Hybridisation of Al in [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ ion is sp³d².\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (B), (C) and (E) only","(C) and (E) only","(A), (C) and (E) only","(A) and (C) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) include (A) – the smooth radius order ignoring Ga anomaly. (A) also includes (B), assuming a simple downward EN trend.\nWorking: (A) false – Ga (135 pm) < Al (143 pm); (B) false – EN falls B→Al then rises slightly; (C) true; (D) false – +1 stable only for Tl (In, Ga less so); (E) true (octahedral, sp³d² in VBT).\nKey idea: Ga is smaller than Al; EN is not monotonic in group 13; only heavier members favour +1.","traps":["Smooth radius order assumed: d-block contraction makes Ga < Al.","EN assumed to decrease down group: EN: B > Tl > In > Ga > Al roughly; not a steady fall."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Gallium is used in the manufacturing of thermometers.\nStatement II : A thermometer containing gallium is useful for measuring the freezing point (256 K) of brine solution.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student knows Ga is used in thermometers and assumes any thermometric use. (C)/(D): student unaware Ga is used in thermometers.\nWorking: Ga (m.p. 303 K, b.p. ≈ 2676 K) is used in high-temperature thermometers; at 256 K gallium is solid, so it cannot measure the freezing point of brine.\nKey idea: Ga is liquid only above 303 K; useful for high, not sub-zero, temperatures.","traps":["Ga assumed liquid at low temperature: Ga m.p. 303 K > 256 K → solid."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The number of ions from the following that are expected to behave as oxidising agent is :\nSn⁴⁺, Sn²⁺, Pb²⁺, Tl³⁺, Pb⁴⁺, Tl⁺","options":["3","4","2","1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3: Sn⁴⁺ counted as oxidising because it is a high oxidation state. (B) 4: Sn⁴⁺ and Pb²⁺ (or Tl⁺) added through \"positive ion = oxidiser\" thinking. (D) 1: only Pb⁴⁺ counted, Tl³⁺ missed.\nWorking: inert pair effect makes the lower states stable for Pb (+2) and Tl (+1), so Pb⁴⁺ and Tl³⁺ are oxidising. For Sn, +4 is the stable state, so Sn⁴⁺ is not an oxidising agent (Sn²⁺ is a reducing agent); Pb²⁺ and Tl⁺ are stable. Count = 2.\nKey idea: Only the heavier element (Pb, Tl) has a stable lower state; for Sn the higher state is stable, so Sn⁴⁺ is not oxidising.","traps":["Sn⁴⁺ assumed oxidising like Pb⁴⁺: Inert pair effect is strong only at Pb; Sn(IV) is the stable state.","Every high-charge ion taken as oxidiser: Ask whether the ion can gain electrons to reach a more stable state."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 13","Group 14"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Evaluate the following statements related to group 14 elements for their correctness.\n(A) Covalent radius decreases down the group from C to Pb in a regular manner.\n(B) Electronegativity decreases from C to Pb down the group gradually.\n(C) Maximum covalency of C is 4 whereas other elements can expand their covalency due to presence of d orbitals.\n(D) Heavier elements do not form pπ – pπ bonds.\n(E) Carbon can exhibit negative oxidation states.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (B) and (C) Only","(A) and (B) Only","(C), (D) and (E) Only","(C) and (D) Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): accept the altered radius or EN statements. (D): miss that carbon shows negative oxidation states (e.g. CH₄, −4).\nWorking: radius increases down the group (A false); EN falls from C to Si and then stays nearly constant, not gradual (B false); C, D, E are standard NCERT facts.\nKey idea: Radius increases down a group; EN of Si–Pb is nearly constant.","traps":["\"Gradually\" accepted for EN: Si, Ge, Sn, Pb have nearly equal EN.","Negative oxidation state of C forgotten: CH₄ has C in −4."],"topics":["Group 14","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:\nAssertion A: The stability order of +1 oxidation state of Ga, In and Tl is Ga < In < Tl.\nReason R: The inert pair effect stabilizes the lower oxidation state down the group.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is true but R is false.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) need one statement to be false – both are standard facts; (B) tempts students unsure whether inert pair is the cause.\nWorking: +1 state grows more stable down Group 13 (Ga < In < Tl) because the ns² pair becomes increasingly inert; R explains A.\nKey idea: Inert pair effect is the reason Tl⁺ is more stable than Tl³⁺.","traps":["Doubting R explains A: Inert pair effect is the textbook cause of this order."],"topics":["Group 13","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Identify the incorrect statements about group 15 elements :\n(A) Dinitrogen is a diatomic gas which acts like an inert gas at room temperature.\n(B) The common oxidation states of these elements are −3, +3 and +5.\n(C) Nitrogen has unique ability to form pπ−pπ multiple bonds.\n(D) The stability of +5 oxidation states increases down the group.\n(E) Nitrogen shows a maximum covalency of 6.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (C), (E) only","(D) and (E) only","(A), (B), (D) only","(B), (D), (E) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options A, C and D each include a correct statement – (A), (B) or (C) – as incorrect; statement (B) about −3/+3/+5 can look too general but is true.\nWorking: +5 stability decreases down the group (inert pair), and N max covalency is 4; A, B, C are correct.\nKey idea: Inert pair: +3 more stable than +5 down the group; N has no d orbitals → covalency 4.","traps":["Trend of +5 stability: p-block: lower state more stable down the group.","Maximum covalency of N: N limited to 4 (no d orbitals)."],"topics":["Group 15","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Both rhombic and monoclinic sulphur exist as S₈ while oxygen exists as O₂.\nReason (R): Oxygen forms pπ–pπ multiple bonds with itself and other elements having small size and high electronegativity like C, N, which is not possible for sulphur.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) sees both true but misses the causal link. (B) rejects R thinking S forms pπ–pπ bonds (e.g. CS₂). (C) doubts that monoclinic sulphur is also S₈.\nWorking: both allotropes are S₈ rings; O₂ forms a strong pπ–pπ double bond which S cannot, so sulphur catenates into S₈ — R explains A.\nKey idea: Weak pπ–pπ overlap for S favours σ-bonded chains/rings (S₈).","traps":["True statements, explanation link: Ask whether R causes A: no S=S double bond → S₈.","Monoclinic sulphur molecular form: Both rhombic and monoclinic contain S₈ rings."],"topics":["Group 16","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"On reaction of Lead Sulphide with dilute nitric acid which of the following is not formed?","options":["Sulphur","Nitric oxide","Lead nitrate","Nitrous oxide"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) students expect PbSO₄/SO₂ rather than S. (B) may be rejected by those who think NO₂ forms. (C) forgotten as the salt product.\nWorking: 3PbS + 8HNO₃(dil) → 3Pb(NO₃)₂ + 3S + 2NO + 4H₂O; N₂O is not formed.\nKey idea: Dilute HNO₃ is reduced to NO; sulphide is oxidised only to elemental S.","traps":["NO vs N₂O as reduction product: Dilute HNO₃ with most reductants gives NO.","NOT-type selection: Write the balanced equation first."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I: A. Melting Point [K] B. Ionic Radius [M³⁺/pm] C. Δ_iH₁ [kJ mol⁻¹] D. Atomic Radius [pm]\nLIST II: I. Tl > In > Ga > Al > B II. B > Tl > Al ≈ Ga > In III. Tl > In > Al > Ga > B IV. B > Al > Tl > In > Ga\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats the regular ionic-radius order as melting point. (C) gives the atomic-radius order (III) to melting point and the melting-point order (IV) to ionic radius. (D) swaps IE and m.p. orders.\nWorking: m.p. B > Al > Tl > In > Ga (Ga lowest); M³⁺ radius rises steadily B → Tl; ΔiH₁ B (801) > Tl (589) > Al (577) ≈ Ga (579) > In (558); atomic radius Ga < Al because of d-contraction.\nKey idea: Ga is the odd one: lowest m.p. and smaller atomic radius than Al; Tl has higher IE than Al/Ga (poor shielding by 4f/5d).","traps":["Atomic vs ionic radius order: Ionic M³⁺ radii increase regularly; atomic radius has Ga < Al.","Lowest melting point of Ga: Ga (303 K) is lowest; B highest."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of oxygen atoms present in chemical formula of fuming sulphuric acid is ______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (H₂SO₄ taken as fuming acid) or 5 (H₂SO₅, Caro's acid) or 8 (H₂S₂O₈, Marshall's acid).\nWorking: fuming sulphuric acid (oleum) is H₂S₂O₇, which has 7 O atoms.\nKey idea: Oleum = H₂SO₄ + SO₃ = H₂S₂O₇.","traps":["Oleum vs peroxy acids: Fuming acid = H₂SO₄·SO₃."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): PH₃ has lower boiling point than NH₃.\nReason (R): In liquid state NH₃ molecules are associated through van der Waals forces, but PH₃ molecules are associated through hydrogen bonding.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): reads R quickly and does not notice the swap. (B): believes heavier PH₃ boils higher.\nWorking: NH₃ is H-bonded, PH₃ is not (P not electronegative enough); the Reason swaps the two, so A true, R false.\nKey idea: Only N, O, F form strong H-bonds.","traps":["Species swapped in Reason: Read which molecule is linked to which force.","Molar mass trend overriding H-bonding: H-bonding raises NH₃ boiling point."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Among the following oxides of p-block elements, number of oxides having amphoteric nature is _______.\nCl₂O₇, CO, PbO₂, N₂O, NO, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, N₂O₅, SnO₂","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (forgets PbO₂), 4 or 5 (counts neutral oxides CO/NO/N₂O as amphoteric).\nWorking: Amphoteric – PbO₂, Al₂O₃, SnO₂. Neutral – CO, N₂O, NO. Acidic – Cl₂O₇, SiO₂, N₂O₅.\nKey idea: Neutral ≠ amphoteric; Group 14 heavier dioxides are amphoteric.","traps":["Neutral oxides counted as amphoteric: Neutral oxides react with neither acid nor base.","PbO₂ nature: GeO₂ acidic; SnO₂ and PbO₂ amphoteric."],"topics":["Group 14","Group 13","Group 15"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : SiO₂ and GeO₂ are acidic while SnO and PbO are amphoteric in nature.\nStatement (II) : Allotropic forms of carbon are due to property of catenation and pπ–dπ bond formation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) judges Statement I false (GeO₂ taken as amphoteric) and accepts the wrong pπ–dπ claim. (B) rejects Statement I because GeO₂ is thought amphoteric. (C) misses that Statement II wrongly says pπ–dπ instead of pπ–pπ.\nWorking: CO₂, SiO₂ and GeO₂ are acidic while SnO and PbO are amphoteric, so Statement I is true; carbon allotropy arises from catenation and pπ–pπ (not pπ–dπ) bonding, so Statement II is false.\nKey idea: Carbon has no d orbitals; its multiple bonds are pπ–pπ.","traps":["pπ–dπ vs pπ–pπ: Carbon has no accessible d orbitals.","GeO₂ nature: NCERT: CO₂, SiO₂, GeO₂ acidic; SnO₂, PbO₂ amphoteric."],"topics":["Group 14","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The strongest reducing agent among the following is :","options":["SbH₃","NH₃","BiH₃","PH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SbH₃ is second strongest. (B) NH₃ chosen from basicity, which runs the other way. (D) PH₃ is a weaker reducing agent than SbH₃ and BiH₃.\nWorking: E–H bond enthalpy falls down Group 15, so reducing character rises NH₃ < PH₃ < AsH₃ < SbH₃ < BiH₃.\nKey idea: Weakest E–H bond → strongest reducing hydride → BiH₃.","traps":["Basicity trend mixed with reducing trend: Reducing power follows falling bond enthalpy, opposite to basicity."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Element not showing variable oxidation state is :","options":["Bromine","Iodine","Chlorine","Fluorine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C): Br, I, Cl all show +1, +3, +5, +7 in oxoacids and interhalogens, so they do show variable states.\nWorking: F is the most electronegative element and has no d-orbitals in its valence shell, so it shows only −1 in compounds; Cl, Br, I show −1 to +7.\nKey idea: Highest electronegativity and no vacant d orbitals → F only −1.","traps":["Assuming all halogens behave alike: Remember F is the only halogen without positive oxidation states."],"topics":["Group 17","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Melting point of Boron (2453 K) is unusually high in group 13 elements.\nReason (R) : Solid Boron has very strong crystalline lattice.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below ;","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) Is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): doubts the causal link though NCERT states it directly. (C): rejects the lattice reason. (D): rejects the given melting point.\nWorking: boron forms a covalent network of B₁₂ icosahedra; this very strong lattice explains its very high melting point compared with Al–Tl.\nKey idea: Covalent icosahedral network → very high mp; other Group 13 elements are metallic with low mp (Ga 303 K).","traps":["Correct reason judged \"not the explanation\": If R is the textbook cause stated in the same sentence, it explains A."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement (I) : Oxygen being the first member of group 16 exhibits only −2 oxidation state. Statement (II) : Down the group 16 stability of +4 oxidation state decreases and +6 oxidation state increases. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts \"only −2\" (forgets OF₂, O₂F₂, peroxides). (C) accepts the reversed inert pair trend. (D) accepts both alterations.\nWorking: O also shows +2 (OF₂), +1 (O₂F₂) and −1 (peroxides), so I is false; down the group the +4 state becomes more stable and +6 less stable (inert pair effect), so II is reversed.\nKey idea: Inert pair effect stabilises the lower (+4) state down Group 16; O has positive states with F.","traps":["\"Only −2\" accepted for oxygen: Recall OF₂ (+2), O₂F₂ (+1), H₂O₂ (−1).","Inert pair trend reversed: Down the group the lower oxidation state (+4) gains stability."],"topics":["Group 16","Anomalous behaviour of first members","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"1 mole of PbS is oxidised by \"X\" moles of O₃ to get \"Y\" moles of O₂. X + Y = ________.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (only X counted), 5 or 7 (wrong O balance), 1.33 (using all three O atoms of each O₃: 4/3 O₃ with no O₂).\nWorking: PbS + 4O₃ → PbSO₄ + 4O₂; X = 4, Y = 4, X + Y = 8.\nKey idea: O₃ → O₂ + [O]; four oxygen atoms needed for SO₄²⁻.","traps":["Ozone treated as delivering three O atoms: Ozone oxidises as O₃ → O₂ + [O]."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Identify the incorrect pair from the following :","options":["Fluoroapatite – 3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·CaF₂","Carnallite – KCl·MgCl₂·6H₂O","Cryolite – Na₃AlF₆","Fluorspar – BF₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) fluoroapatite formula looks complex and may be suspected; (B) carnallite hydrate count may seem odd; (C) cryolite may be confused with AlF₃. Only (D) is wrong: fluorspar is CaF₂.\nWorking: fluorspar is CaF₂; BF₃ is a covalent gas, not a mineral. The other three formulas are correct.\nKey idea: Fluorspar = CaF₂ (the 'spar' is a calcium mineral); boron does not form a fluoride mineral of this name.","traps":["Complex formula assumed wrong: Check the simple-looking pair first; fluorspar must contain Ca.","Fluorspar linked to boron fluoride: Remember fluorspar = CaF₂ as the main F source."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : The electronegativity of group 14 elements from Si to Pb, gradually decreases.\nStatement II : Group 14 contains non-metallic, metallic, as well as metalloid elements.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) accept the general down-group EN decrease; (C)/(D) forget that Si/Ge are metalloids.\nWorking: Pauling values Si 1.8, Ge 1.8, Sn 1.8, Pb 1.9 – no gradual decrease from Si to Pb (I false); C non-metal, Si/Ge metalloids, Sn/Pb metals (II true).\nKey idea: Below carbon, group 14 EN stays ≈1.8–1.9 because of poor shielding by d/f electrons.","traps":["General downward EN trend applied: Recall group 14 values Si–Pb are nearly equal.","Metalloids not remembered: C non-metal; Si, Ge metalloids; Sn, Pb metals."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Aluminium chloride in acidified aqueous solution forms an ion having geometry","options":["Square planar","Octahedral","Trigonal bipyramidal","Tetrahedral"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Square planar: geometry of d⁸ complexes, not of Al³⁺. (C) Trigonal bipyramidal: sp³d five-coordinate, e.g. PCl₅. (D) Tetrahedral: [Al(OH)₄]⁻ formed in alkaline/neutral hydrolysis, or Al₂Cl₆ units – the main distractor.\nWorking: in acidified water Al³⁺ is hydrated as [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺; six aqua ligands using vacant 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals give an octahedral ion.\nKey idea: Acidified water keeps Al as the hexa-aqua cation [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ → six ligands → octahedral.","traps":["Tetrahedral [Al(OH)₄]⁻ confused with hexa-aqua ion: Acid medium → aqua ion (6 H₂O); alkaline/hydrolysis → hydroxo (4 OH⁻).","Al₂Cl₆ dimer geometry recalled: The question is about the species in water, not anhydrous AlCl₃."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P. However from As to Bi only a small increase in covalent radius is observed.\nReason (R) : Covalent and ionic radii in a particular oxidation state increases down the group.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) chosen because R is the general trend and sounds explanatory. (A)/(D) reject one of two NCERT sentences.\nWorking: both are NCERT lines; the small increase from As to Bi is explained by poor shielding of completely filled d and/or f orbitals, not by the general down-group increase stated in R.\nKey idea: R states the trend; A needs the d/f poor-shielding reason.","traps":["General trend taken as explanation: A is about the size of the increase; only poor d/f shielding explains it."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Choose the correct statements about the hydrides of group 15 elements.\nA. The stability of the hydrides decreases in the order NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃ > BiH₃.\nB. The reducing ability of the hydride increases in the order NH₃ < PH₃ < AsH₃ < SbH₃ < BiH₃.\nC. Among the hydrides, NH₃ is strong reducing agent while BiH₃ is mild reducing agent.\nD. The basicity of the hydrides increases in the order NH₃ < PH₃ < AsH₃ < SbH₃ < BiH₃.\nChoose the most appropriate from the options given below :","options":["C and D only","A and D only","A and B only","B and C only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option A accepts statement C (reducing trend reversed) and statement D (basicity trend reversed). Option B accepts D, which is false. Option D accepts C, which is false.\nWorking: E–H bond strength falls down the group, so thermal stability decreases (A true) and reducing character increases (B true); C reverses this (NH₃ mild, BiH₃ strongest); basicity decreases NH₃ > … > BiH₃, so D is false.\nKey idea: Weaker E–H bond down the group ⇒ less stable and more reducing; lone pair more diffuse ⇒ less basic.","traps":["Basicity trend reversed: Basicity falls down the group (NH₃ most basic).","Reducing strength reversed in statement C: BiH₃ is the strongest reducing agent."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:\nAssertion A: H₂Te is more acidic than H₂S.\nReason R: Bond dissociation enthalpy of H₂Te is lower than H₂S.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is false but R is true.","A is true but R is false."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) thinks electronegativity/ion stability is the real reason. (C) uses electronegativity to say H₂S is more acidic. (D) thinks bond enthalpy rises with size.\nWorking: acidity of H₂E increases H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te because the H–E bond enthalpy decreases down the group; R is the reason.\nKey idea: Down a group, bond strength (size) dominates over electronegativity for acidity of hydrides.","traps":["Electronegativity argument for acidity: Within a group, weaker H–E bond ⇒ stronger acid.","Doubting R as the explanation: NCERT gives decrease in bond enthalpy as the reason."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Consider the oxides of group 14 elements SiO₂, GeO₂, SnO₂, PbO₂, CO and GeO. The amphoteric oxides are","options":["SnO₂, CO","SiO₂, GeO₂","SnO₂, PbO₂","GeO, GeO₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): CO taken as amphoteric (it is neutral). (B): SiO₂/GeO₂ – both acidic; student may think Ge (metalloid) gives amphoteric oxide. (D): GeO thought amphoteric by analogy with SnO/PbO.\nWorking: CO is neutral; SiO₂, GeO₂ are acidic and GeO is distinctly acidic; SnO₂ and PbO₂ (and SnO, PbO) are amphoteric.\nKey idea: Amphoteric character starts at Sn: SnO, SnO₂, PbO, PbO₂ are amphoteric; Ge oxides are acidic.","traps":["GeO assumed amphoteric like SnO: Recall NCERT: GeO is distinctly acidic.","CO treated as amphoteric: CO is a neutral oxide."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement - I: Noble gases have very high boiling points.\nStatement - II: Noble gases are monoatomic gases. They are held together by strong dispersion forces. Because of this they are liquefied at very low temperature. Hence, they have very high boiling points.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): student remembers 'liquefied at very low temperature' sentence and reads the rest as true. (C): accepts II because most of its words are NCERT's.\nWorking: noble gases have very low boiling points because only weak dispersion forces hold the monoatomic atoms; Statement II says 'strong' forces and 'very high' b.p. (and is internally inconsistent), so both are false.\nKey idea: Weak dispersion forces → very low b.p.; Statement II contradicts itself.","traps":["NCERT sentence with qualifiers flipped: Check every adjective against the known trend."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I : Group 13 trivalent halides get easily hydrolyzed by water due to their covalent nature. Statement II : AlCl₃ upon hydrolysis in acidified aqueous solution forms octahedral [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ ion. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are true","Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is true but statement II is false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): doubting the covalent nature of AlCl₃. (D): thinking AlCl₃ gives tetrahedral [Al(OH)₄]⁻ in water (true only in alkali).\nWorking: trihalides of Group 13 are covalent and hydrolyse readily; AlCl₃ in acidified water gives octahedral [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺.\nKey idea: Acidified solution → hexaaqua cation (octahedral); alkali → tetrahedral [Al(OH)₄]⁻.","traps":["Acid vs alkali aluminium species: Acidic medium → [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺; basic → [Al(OH)₄]⁻."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Choose the correct statements from the following: A. All group 16 elements form oxides of general formula EO₂ and EO₃, where E = S, Se, Te and Po. Both the types of oxides are acidic in nature. B. TeO₂ is an oxidising agent while SO₂ is reducing in nature. C. The reducing property decreases from H₂S to H₂Te down the group. D. The ozone molecule contains five lone pairs of electrons. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and B only","C and D only","A and D only","B and C only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (D): accept C (direction reversed). (B), (C): accept D – O₃ has 2+1+3 = 6 lone pairs, not 5.\nWorking: A and B are NCERT (old edition) statements; C is reversed (reducing power increases H₂S → H₂Te); O₃ has 6 lone pairs.\nKey idea: H–E bond weakens down the group so H₂Te is the strongest reductant.","traps":["Reducing trend reversed: Weaker H–E bond → stronger reductant: H₂S < H₂Te.","Lone pairs in ozone: Count 1 + 2 + 3 = 6."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Oxide): A. N₂O₄ B. NO₂ C. N₂O₅ D. N₂O\nLIST II (Type of bond): I. 1 N = O bond II. 1 N – O – N bond III. 1 N – N bond IV. 1 N=N / N≡N bond\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): N₂O₄ and N₂O₅ swapped (both are dimers of NO₂-type units, one N–N linked, one O-bridged). (C): N₂O₅ given N=N and N₂O given N–O–N, confusing the linear N–N–O skeleton.\nWorking: N₂O₄ has an N–N bond joining two NO₂ units; NO₂ has one N=O (plus one N–O in a resonance form); N₂O₅ is O₂N–O–NO₂ with one bridging N–O–N; N₂O is N=N=O ↔ N≡N–O.\nKey idea: N₂O₄ = O₂N–NO₂ (N–N), N₂O₅ = O₂N–O–NO₂ (N–O–N).","traps":["N₂O₄ vs N₂O₅ linkage swapped: N₂O₄ is a direct NO₂ dimer (N–N); N₂O₅ needs a bridging O.","N₂O skeleton as N–O–N: N₂O is linear N–N–O, not O-centred."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which one of the following elements will remain as liquid inside pure boiling water?","options":["Li","Br","Cs","Ga"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Cs has m.p. 302 K like Ga but reacts violently with water; (B) Br₂ is liquid at room temperature but boils at 59 °C; (A) Li reacts with water.\nWorking: Ga melts at ~303 K and boils above 2500 K, and does not react with water, so it stays liquid at 373 K; Br₂ (b.p. 332 K) boils off, Li and Cs react with water.\nKey idea: Need low m.p., high b.p. and no reaction with water – only Ga satisfies all three.","traps":["Caesium chosen for low melting point: Check reactivity with water: Cs reacts explosively.","Bromine chosen as the liquid element: Its b.p. (~332 K) is below 373 K."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Group-13 elements react with O₂ in amorphous form to form oxides of type M₂O₃ (M = element). Which among the following is the most basic oxide?","options":["Al₂O₃","Tl₂O₃","B₂O₃","Ga₂O₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) B₂O₃ is acidic (opposite end); (A)/(D) amphoteric oxides tempt students unsure of the Tl position.\nWorking: basic character of Group 13 oxides increases down the group (B₂O₃ acidic, Al₂O₃ and Ga₂O₃ amphoteric, In₂O₃ and Tl₂O₃ basic); Tl₂O₃ is the most basic of those given.\nKey idea: Metallic character, hence oxide basicity, rises down the group.","traps":["Inert pair effect assumed to reduce basicity: Compare like with like: M₂O₃ basicity still increases down the group."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":82,"q":"XeF₄ reacts with SbF₅ to form [XeF_m]ⁿ⁺[SbF_y]ᶻ⁻.\nm + n + y + z = ______","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: 12 (writes XeF₄²⁺/SbF₇²⁻-type), 13 (counts XeF₅⁺ from XeF₆), 9 (forms XeF₂-type product).\nWorking: XeF₄ + SbF₅ → [XeF₃]⁺[SbF₆]⁻; m = 3, n = 1, y = 6, z = 1 → 11.\nKey idea: One F⁻ moves from XeF₄ to SbF₅: XeF₃⁺ + SbF₆⁻.","traps":["XeF₆ reaction recalled instead: Single F⁻ transfer from the given fluoride."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The number of species from the following carrying a single lone pair on central atom Xenon is ______. XeF₅⁺, XeO₃, XeO₂F₂, XeF₅⁻, XeO₃F₂, XeOF₄, XeF₄","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (missing XeF₅⁺ or XeO₂F₂), 5 (counting XeF₅⁻ as one lone pair), 6 (including XeF₄ / XeO₃F₂).\nWorking: lone pairs on Xe — XeF₅⁺ 1, XeO₃ 1, XeO₂F₂ 1, XeF₅⁻ 2, XeO₃F₂ 0, XeOF₄ 1, XeF₄ 2 → four species with one lone pair.\nKey idea: Lone pairs = (8 − charge-adjusted valence electrons used: 1 per F, 2 per O)/2.","traps":["XeF₅⁻ given one lone pair: Anion adds an electron: 9 − 5 = 4 → 2 lone pairs (pentagonal planar).","Xe=O counted as using one electron: Each Xe=O uses two Xe electrons."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"The ratio of sigma and π bonds present in pyrophosphoric acid is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (both bridge P–O σ bonds missed, 10 σ : 2 π), 3 (four P=O bonds assumed, 12 σ : 4 π).\nWorking: H₄P₂O₇ = (HO)₂P(=O)–O–P(=O)(OH)₂: σ bonds = 4 O–H + 4 P–OH + 2 P–O(bridge) + 2 P=O σ = 12; π bonds = 2 (P=O); ratio 12 : 2 = 6.\nKey idea: Each P is tetrahedral with one P=O; two P–O bonds to the bridge oxygen.","traps":["Bridge P–O–P bonds counted once: Bridging oxygen makes two σ bonds."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"ClF₅ at room temperature is a:","options":["Colourless liquid with square pyramidal geometry","Colourless gas with square pyramidal geometry","Colourless gas with trigonal bipyramidal geometry.","Colourless liquid with trigonal bipyramidal geometry"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): physically correct state (bp ≈ −13 °C) but against the NCERT table that the official answer follows. (C)/(D): AX₅ treated as trigonal bipyramidal, ignoring the lone pair on Cl.\nWorking: ClF₅ has 5 bp + 1 lp around Cl (sp³d²) → square pyramidal; the old NCERT interhalogen table describes ClF₅ as a colourless liquid. Note: its boiling point is about −13 °C, so strictly it is a gas at 25 °C; the official answer follows the NCERT table.\nKey idea: AX₅E type → square pyramidal; textbook state = colourless liquid.","traps":["Lone pair on Cl ignored: Count 7 valence e⁻ of Cl: 5 bonds + 1 lone pair → square pyramidal.","Physical state gas vs liquid: For the exam answer, follow the NCERT table entry."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"The incorrect statement from the following for borazine is:","options":["It can react with water.","It is a cyclic compound.","It has electronic delocalization.","It contains banana bonds."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): 'inorganic benzene' suggests inertness like benzene, but borazine is hydrolysed. (B)/(C): true – cyclic and delocalised; a student may doubt delocalisation because B–N bonds are polar.\nWorking: borazine B₃N₃H₆ is a planar six-membered ring ('inorganic benzene') with π-delocalisation (N lone pairs into B p orbitals); it is hydrolysed by water (more reactive than benzene). Banana (3c–2e B–H–B) bonds belong to diborane, not borazine.\nKey idea: Banana bonds = diborane; borazine = benzene-like delocalised ring.","traps":["Borazine assumed inert like benzene: Polar B–N bonds make borazine add/hydrolyse readily.","Diborane and borazine bonding mixed up: Diborane: 3c–2e B–H–B bridges; borazine: normal 2c–2e σ + delocalised π."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"The correct order of bond enthalpy (kJ mol⁻¹) is :","options":["C–C > Si–Si > Sn–Sn > Ge–Ge","Si–Si > C–C > Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn","C–C > Si–Si > Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn","Si–Si > C–C > Sn–Sn > Ge–Ge"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ge and Sn (options differ by one pair). (B)/(D) put Si–Si above C–C, confusing with Si–O strength or with the halogen-type anomaly.\nWorking: E–E bond enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): C–C 348, Si–Si 297, Ge–Ge 260, Sn–Sn 240, so C–C > Si–Si > Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn.\nKey idea: Group 14 E–E bond enthalpy falls steadily down the group with no anomaly; carbon catenates best.","traps":["Halogen-style anomaly applied to carbon: No lone-pair repulsion issue for C–C; C–C is strongest.","Ge and Sn order swapped: Trend is regular down the group: Ge–Ge > Sn–Sn."],"topics":["Group 14","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Which of the Phosphorus oxoacid can create silver mirror from AgNO₃ solution?","options":["H₄P₂O₅","H₄P₂O₇","(HPO₃)ₙ","H₄P₂O₆"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) pyrophosphoric and (C) metaphosphoric acids have P(+5), no P–H. (D) hypophosphoric acid has P(+4) and a P–P bond but no P–H – the trap for students who equate lower oxidation state with reducing power.\nWorking: pyrophosphorous acid H₄P₂O₅ has two P–H bonds (P in +3) and reduces Ag⁺ to Ag; H₄P₂O₇, (HPO₃)ₙ (P +5) and H₄P₂O₆ (P +4, P–P bond, no P–H) do not.\nKey idea: Reducing oxoacid of P ⇔ P–H bond present (P oxidation state +1 or +3).","traps":["Hypophosphoric vs pyrophosphorous: Find oxidation state: +4 in H₄P₂O₆ (no P–H), +3 in H₄P₂O₅ (P–H present)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Sum of π-bonds present in peroxodisulphuric acid and pyrosulphuric acid is ________","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 or 7: drawing peroxo link wrongly or taking H₂SO₅ instead; 4: counting only one acid.\nWorking: H₂S₂O₈ (HO₃S–O–O–SO₃H) has 4 S=O; H₂S₂O₇ (HO₃S–O–SO₃H) has 4 S=O; total 8.\nKey idea: Each SO₃ unit (two S=O + OH + bridge) contributes 2 π; both acids have two such units.","traps":["Peroxodisulphuric vs peroxomonosulphuric: Di = two S atoms."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Reaction of thionyl chloride with white phosphorus forms a compound [A], which on hydrolysis gives [B], a dibasic acid. [A] and [B] are respectively :","options":["P₄O₆ and H₃PO₃","POCl₃ and H₃PO₄","PCl₅ and H₃PO₄","PCl₃ and H₃PO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) give H₃PO₄, which is tribasic. (A) P₄O₆ is not formed with SOCl₂ (it is the oxide from limited O₂), though it also gives H₃PO₃.\nWorking: P₄ + 8SOCl₂ → 4PCl₃ + 4SO₂ + 2S₂Cl₂ (A = PCl₃); PCl₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₃ + 3HCl (B = H₃PO₃, dibasic).\nKey idea: 'Dibasic' points to H₃PO₃ (one P–H bond); the chloride that hydrolyses to it is PCl₃.","traps":["Basicity read from number of H atoms: Only O–H hydrogens are acidic; H₃PO₃ has one P–H.","P₄O₆ also gives H₃PO₃: Check both A and B: SOCl₂ is a chlorinating agent, so A is a chloride."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"Assertion A: Carbon forms two important oxides – CO and CO₂. CO is neutral whereas CO₂ is acidic in nature. Reason R: CO₂ can combine with water in a limited way to form carbonic acid, while CO is sparingly soluble in water. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats 'sparingly soluble' as an irrelevant extra fact; (A)/(D) reject one of two true statements.\nWorking: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ (only to a limited extent), so CO₂ is acidic; CO is only sparingly soluble and forms no acid, so it is neutral. Both statements are true and R explains A.\nKey idea: Acidic oxide = gives acid with water; CO₂ does (H₂CO₃), CO does not.","traps":["Explanation vs extra fact: Ask whether R states why A happens: forming H₂CO₃ is why CO₂ is acidic."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The bond dissociation energy is highest for","options":["Cl₂","I₂","Br₂","F₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) F₂ chosen by assuming smallest atom = strongest bond; (C) Br₂ and (B) I₂ follow the downward trend and are weaker.\nWorking: bond dissociation enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): Cl₂ 242.6, Br₂ 192.8, F₂ 158.8, I₂ 151.1. F₂ is unexpectedly low because of repulsion between lone pairs on the small F atoms, so Cl₂ is highest.\nKey idea: Order Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂.","traps":["F–F bond is weak: Remember the lone-pair repulsion anomaly of F₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"\"A\" obtained by Ostwald's method involving air oxidation of NH₃, upon further air oxidation produces \"B\", \"B\" on hydration forms an oxoacid of Nitrogen along with evolution of \"A\". The oxoacid also produces \"A\" and gives positive brown ring test.\nIdentify A and B, respectively :","options":["N₂O₃, NO₂","NO₂, N₂O₅","NO₂, N₂O₄","NO, NO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) N₂O₃ is not the Pt-catalysed product; (B)/(C) start from NO₂, missing that the first oxidation gives NO.\nWorking: Ostwald process 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → (Pt) 4NO + 6H₂O, so A = NO; 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂, so B = NO₂; 3NO₂ + H₂O → 2HNO₃ + NO (A is evolved); HNO₃ gives the brown ring test and is itself reduced to NO.\nKey idea: NO₂ disproportionates in water to HNO₃ + NO.","traps":["First oxidation product of NH₃: Pt-catalysed NH₃ oxidation gives NO."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Bond dissociation energy of \"E–H\" bond of the \"H₂E\" hydrides of group 16 elements (given below), follows order. A. O; B. S; C. Se; D. Te. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A > B > C > D","D > C > B > A","B > A > C > D","A > B > D > C"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): reverse trend (confusing with acidic strength order); (C): places S above O (confusing with catenation or electron gain enthalpy anomaly); (D): Te/Se swap.\nWorking: the E–H bond gets longer as E grows from O to Te, so the bond dissociation enthalpy falls: H₂O > H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te, i.e. A > B > C > D.\nKey idea: Larger E atom → longer, weaker E–H bond: H₂O > H₂S > H₂Se > H₂Te.","traps":["Bond strength vs acid strength: Weaker bond ⇒ stronger acid: the two orders are opposite.","O anomaly misapplied: Bond enthalpy has no O anomaly here; small O gives strongest bond."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The oxidation state of phosphorus in hypophosphoric acid is + ________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (hypophosphorous acid H₃PO₂), 3 (phosphorous acid H₃PO₃), 5 (pyrophosphoric/orthophosphoric acid).\nWorking: H₄P₂O₆: 4(+1) + 2x + 6(−2) = 0 → x = +4\nKey idea: Hypophosphoric = H₄P₂O₆ with a P–P bond, P = +4.","traps":["Hypophosphoric vs hypophosphorous: -ic acid H₄P₂O₆ (+4); -ous acid H₃PO₂ (+1)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"The Lewis acid character of boron tri halides follows the order:","options":["BI₃ > BBr₃ > BCl₃ > BF₃","BCl₃ > BF₃ > BBr₃ > BI₃","BF₃ > BCl₃ > BBr₃ > BI₃","BBr₃ > BI₃ > BCl₃ > BF₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) follows electronegativity of halogen (reverse of truth). (B) and (D) random mixes.\nWorking: back-donation from a filled halogen p orbital into the empty 2p orbital of B is strongest for F (2p–2p overlap) and weakest for I, so electron deficiency and Lewis acidity increase BF₃ < BCl₃ < BBr₃ < BI₃.\nKey idea: Back donation from F 2p to B 2p is most effective (same size orbitals), so BF₃ is least electron-deficient.","traps":["Electronegativity argument: Back-bonding dominates: BF₃ weakest."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The geometry around boron in the product 'B' formed from the following reaction is: BF₃ + NaH —(450 K)→ A + NaF; A + NMe₃ → B","options":["trigonal planar","tetrahedral","pyramidal","square planar"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) trigonal planar is boron in BF₃/BH₃ before adduct formation; (C) pyramidal confuses with the N of NMe₃; (D) square planar irrelevant.\nWorking: 2BF₃ + 6NaH → B₂H₆ (A) + 6NaF; B₂H₆ + 2NMe₃ → 2BH₃·NMe₃ (B), boron sp³ tetrahedral.\nKey idea: BH₃ + Lewis base → four-coordinate boron.","traps":["Forgetting adduct formation: After NMe₃ donates a pair, boron has 4 bond pairs."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The interhalogen compound formed from the reaction of bromine with excess of fluorine is a:","options":["hypohalite","halate","perhalate","halite"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) perhalate (+7) if IF₇-type is assumed; (D) halite (+3) if BrF₃ is taken as the product; (A) hypohalite (+1) for BrF.\nWorking: Br₂ + 5F₂ (excess) → 2BrF₅; Br is +5, the same oxidation state as in a halate (XO₃⁻), so BrF₅ is the \"halate\" type.\nKey idea: Excess F₂ gives BrF₅, Br(+5) ↔ BrO₃⁻ (halate).","traps":["BrF₃ vs BrF₅ with excess F₂: \"Excess fluorine\" pushes to BrF₅.","Oxoanion name ↔ oxidation state: hypo- +1, -ite +3, -ate +5, per-…-ate +7."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Match List I with List II: List-I (molecule): A. XeO₃, B. XeF₂, C. XeOF₄, D. XeF₆. List-II (hybridization; shape): I. sp³d; linear, II. sp³; pyramidal, III. sp³d³; distorted octahedral, IV. sp³d²; square pyramidal. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) pair XeF₂ with sp³d² square pyramidal or XeO₃ with square pyramidal (ignoring lone pairs); (D) gives XeOF₄ sp³d linear (confusing with XeF₂).\nWorking: XeO₃ (3 bp + 1 lp) sp³ pyramidal; XeF₂ (2 bp + 3 lp) sp³d linear; XeOF₄ (5 bp + 1 lp) sp³d² square pyramidal; XeF₆ (6 bp + 1 lp) sp³d³ distorted octahedral.\nKey idea: Steric number = bonded atoms + lone pairs on Xe: XeO₃ 4, XeF₂ 5, XeOF₄ 6, XeF₆ 7.","traps":["Lone pairs on Xe ignored: Valence electrons of Xe (8) minus bonding electrons, then halve.","XeOF₄ and XeF₆ shapes swapped: 6 electron domains = sp³d² (square pyramidal); 7 = sp³d³."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The sum of number of lone pairs of electrons present on the central atoms of XeO₃, XeOF₄ and XeF₆ is _____________","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 or 9 (counting lone pairs on O/F too, or treating Xe=O as single bonds); 2 (missing XeF₆ lone pair).\nWorking: each has one lone pair on Xe – XeO₃ (8 − 6 = 2 e⁻), XeOF₄ (8 − 2 − 4 = 2 e⁻), XeF₆ (8 − 6 = 2 e⁻); sum = 3.\nKey idea: Lone pairs on Xe = (8 − electrons used in bonding)/2; each Xe=O uses 2.","traps":["Lone pairs on all atoms counted: Only the central Xe is asked.","XeF₆ lone pair missed: 7 electron pairs: 6 bp + 1 lp."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Borazine, also known as inorganic benzene, can be prepared by the reaction of 3-equivalents of \"X\" with 6-equivalents of \"Y\". \"X\" and \"Y\", respectively are:","options":["B(OH)₃ and NH₃","B₂H₆ and NH₃","B₂H₆ and HN₃","NH₃ and B₂O₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) boric acid + NH₃ – boron source confused; (C) HN₃ (hydrazoic acid) looks like NH₃; (D) order reversed and wrong boron compound.\nWorking: 3B₂H₆ + 6NH₃ → 3[BH₂(NH₃)₂]⁺[BH₄]⁻ → (heat) 2B₃N₃H₆ + 12H₂.\nKey idea: Borazine = diborane + ammonia (1:2), then heat.","traps":["NH₃ vs HN₃: Read the formula: HN₃ is hydrazoic acid.","Order of X and Y: B₂H₆ : NH₃ = 1 : 2 → X = B₂H₆."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The metal that has very low melting point and its periodic position is closer to a metalloid is :","options":["Al","Ga","Se","In"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Se is not a metal; (D) In is low-melting (430 K) but higher than Ga; (A) Al is a high-melting metal.\nWorking: gallium melts at about 303 K and lies just below Al, next to metalloid Ge; Se is a non-metal, Al and In melt much higher.\nKey idea: Ga (303 K) is the textbook low-melting metal, bordering Ge.","traps":["Ga vs In melting point: Ga melts near body temperature; In around 430 K.","Se listed as a \"metal\": Check metal/non-metal first."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Boric acid is a weak acid\nReason R : Boric acid is not able to release H⁺ ion on its own. It receives OH⁻ ion from water and releases H⁺ ion.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who see Lewis acidity as unrelated to strength; (C) for those who believe boric acid donates its own H⁺.\nWorking: B(OH)₃ + 2H₂O → [B(OH)₄]⁻ + H₃O⁺; it is a weak monobasic Lewis acid, and this mode of ionisation explains its weakness.\nKey idea: Boric acid ionises only by accepting OH⁻ – an indirect, limited route, so it is weak.","traps":["Lewis vs protonic acid: Boric acid accepts OH⁻; it is monobasic.","R explains A?: Weak because H⁺ is only released indirectly via OH⁻ uptake."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of non-ionisable protons present in the product B obtained from the following reaction is ___.\nC₂H₅OH + PCl₃ → C₂H₅Cl + A\nA + PCl₃ → B","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Stopping at A = H₃PO₃ gives 1; counting all H in H₄P₂O₅ gives 4; note that H₄P₂O₅ also has 2 ionisable H, so make sure you count the P–H hydrogens, not the O–H ones.\nWorking: A = H₃PO₃ (3C₂H₅OH + PCl₃ → 3C₂H₅Cl + H₃PO₃); H₃PO₃ with PCl₃ gives pyrophosphorous acid H₄P₂O₅ (5H₃PO₃ + PCl₃ → 3H₄P₂O₅ + 3HCl), which has two P–H bonds → 2 non-ionisable H.\nKey idea: Each P(III) in these acids carries one P–H; H₄P₂O₅ has two P(III) → 2.","traps":["Product A vs B: Count for B = H₄P₂O₅.","Ionisable vs non-ionisable H: Only H on O ionise; P–H do not."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which oxoacid of phosphorous has the highest number of oxygen atoms present in its chemical formula?","options":["Pyrophosphorous acid","Hypophosphoric acid","Phosphoric acid","Pyrophosphoric acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): confused with hypophosphorous H₃PO₂ or thought to have most O; (A): pyrophosphorous vs pyrophosphoric suffix mix-up; (C): highest oxidation state taken as most oxygen.\nWorking: H₄P₂O₅ (pyrophosphorous, 5 O), H₄P₂O₆ (hypophosphoric, 6 O), H₃PO₄ (4 O), H₄P₂O₇ (pyrophosphoric, 7 O).\nKey idea: Pyro = two units minus one water; -ic acid of P(V) → H₄P₂O₇ has 7 O.","traps":["-ous vs -ic: -ous → P(III), -ic → P(V); more O with -ic.","Hypophosphoric vs hypophosphorous: Hypophosphoric H₄P₂O₆ has a P–P bond, 6 O."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Assertion (A): Boron is unable to form BF₆³⁻. Reason (R): Size of B is very small.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts small size as the explanation; (C) wrongly calls the true size statement false; (D) rejects the true assertion.\nWorking: B cannot exceed covalency 4 because it has no d orbitals (only 2s, 2p available), so BF₆³⁻ is impossible; B is indeed small, but the textbook reason for the maximum covalency of 4 is the absence of d orbitals, so R is not the correct explanation.\nKey idea: Maximum covalency of B is 4 because of the absence of d orbitals.","traps":["Small size taken as the reason: Covalency limit comes from the available orbitals (no 2d)."],"topics":["Group 13","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List-I with List-II, match the gas evolved during each reaction. List-I: (A) (NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇ —Δ→; (B) KMnO₄ + HCl →; (C) Al + NaOH + H₂O →; (D) NaNO₃ —Δ→. List-II: (I) H₂; (II) N₂; (III) O₂; (IV) Cl₂. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) – (II), (B) – (III), (C) – (I), (D) – (IV)","(A) – (III), (B) – (I), (C) – (IV), (D) – (II)","(A) – (II), (B) – (IV), (C) – (I), (D) – (III)","(A) – (III), (B) – (IV), (C) – (I), (D) – (II)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) give O₂ for ammonium dichromate (dichromate is an oxidant, but the gas is N₂); (B) also gives H₂ for KMnO₄ + HCl; (A) gives O₂ for KMnO₄ + HCl and Cl₂ for NaNO₃.\nWorking: (NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇ → N₂ + Cr₂O₃ + 4H₂O; KMnO₄ oxidises HCl to Cl₂; Al + NaOH + H₂O → Na[Al(OH)₄] + H₂; 2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂.\nKey idea: In ammonium dichromate, NH₄⁺ is oxidised to N₂ internally.","traps":["Oxygen from dichromate: NH₄⁺ is the reductant: N(−3) → N₂(0).","Alkali metal nitrate gives NO₂?: NaNO₃ → NaNO₂ + O₂ only."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of interhalogens from the following having square pyramidal structure is: ClF₃, IF₇, BrF₅, BrF₃, I₂Cl₆, IF₅, ClF, ClF₅","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 if I₂Cl₆ (planar, bridged) is counted; 5 if T-shaped AX₃ are counted.\nWorking: XF₅ type (5 bond pairs + 1 lone pair, sp³d²) are square pyramidal: BrF₅, IF₅, ClF₅ = 3. ClF₃/BrF₃ T-shaped, IF₇ pentagonal bipyramidal, I₂Cl₆ planar dimer, ClF linear.\nKey idea: Only the AX₅ type has 5 bp + 1 lp.","traps":["I₂Cl₆ geometry: I₂Cl₆ is planar with two bridging Cl."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"White phosphorus reacts with thionyl chloride to give","options":["PCl₅, SO₂ and S₂Cl₂","PCl₃, SO₂ and S₂Cl₂","PCl₃, SO₂ and Cl₂","PCl₅, SO₂ and Cl₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) give PCl₅ (formed with SO₂Cl₂ or excess Cl₂, not SOCl₂); (C) gives Cl₂ instead of S₂Cl₂ (S of SOCl₂ is reduced).\nWorking: P₄ + 8SOCl₂ → 4PCl₃ + 4SO₂ + 2S₂Cl₂.\nKey idea: SOCl₂ chlorinates P₄ only to PCl₃; the sulfur ends up as S₂Cl₂ and SO₂.","traps":["PCl₅ vs PCl₃: Memorise: SOCl₂ + P₄ → PCl₃; SO₂Cl₂ + P₄ → PCl₅.","S₂Cl₂ vs Cl₂: Balance S atoms: they must go somewhere."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Concentrated HNO₃ reacts with Iodine to give","options":["HI, NO₂ and H₂O","HIO₂, N₂O and H₂O","HIO₃, NO₂ and H₂O","HIO₄, N₂O and H₂O"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) shows iodine reduced (impossible with an oxidant); (B)/(D) give N₂O, which is not the product of conc. HNO₃.\nWorking: I₂ + 10HNO₃(conc.) → 2HIO₃ + 10NO₂ + 4H₂O; conc. HNO₃ is reduced to NO₂ and iodine is oxidised to iodic acid.\nKey idea: Conc. HNO₃ → NO₂; non-metals go to their highest common oxoacid (C → CO₂, S → H₂SO₄, P → H₃PO₄, I₂ → HIO₃).","traps":["Reduction product of conc. HNO₃: Conc. HNO₃ gives NO₂.","HIO₃ vs HIO₄: NCERT product is iodic acid HIO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Dinitrogen and dioxygen, the main constituents of air, do not react with each other in atmosphere to form oxides of nitrogen because","options":["N₂ is unreactive in the condition of atmosphere.","Oxides of nitrogen are unstable.","Reaction between them can occur in the presence of a catalyst.","The reaction is endothermic and require very high temperature."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is partly true but not the root cause; (B) is false as a reason (NO forms readily at high T); (C) is irrelevant (no catalyst exists in air).\nWorking: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO is strongly endothermic (ΔH ≈ +180 kJ mol⁻¹) and needs ~2000 K (electric arc, lightning); this is the best reason.\nKey idea: High N≡N bond enthalpy makes NO formation endothermic, so it needs very high temperature.","traps":["Vague \"unreactive\" option: Pick the option that gives the thermodynamic cause."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"When borax is heated with CoO on a platinum loop, blue coloured bead formed is largely due to:","options":["B₂O₃","Co(BO₂)₂","CoB₄O₇","Co[B₄O₅(OH)₄]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B₂O₃ is the colourless glass, not the coloured species; (C) tetraborate formula from borax anion; (D) hydrated borax-anion form.\nWorking: borax on heating gives NaBO₂ + B₂O₃; B₂O₃ combines with CoO to give cobalt metaborate Co(BO₂)₂, which is blue.\nKey idea: Coloured bead = metal metaborate M(BO₂)₂.","traps":["Metaborate vs tetraborate: Bead species is metaborate."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Consider the following sulphur based oxoacids: H₂SO₃, H₂SO₄, H₂S₂O₈ and H₂S₂O₇. Amongst these oxoacids, the number of those with peroxo (O–O) bond is ______.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (counting H₂S₂O₇ – S–O–S bridge mistaken for peroxo); 0 (H₂S₂O₈ not known).\nWorking: only peroxodisulphuric acid H₂S₂O₈ has an –O–O– bridge; H₂S₂O₇ (pyrosulphuric) has an S–O–S bridge.\nKey idea: Peroxo only in H₂S₂O₈.","traps":["Pyrosulphuric acid counted: Check for O bonded to O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Identify the correct statement for B₂H₆ from those given below. (A) In B₂H₆, all B–H bonds are equivalent. (B) In B₂H₆ there are four 3-centre-2-electron bonds. (C) B₂H₆ is a Lewis acid. (D) B₂H₆ can be synthesized from both BF₃ and NaBH₄. (E) B₂H₆ is a planar molecule. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) and (E) only","(B), (C) and (E) only","(C) and (D) only","(C) and (E) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option B accepts statement (B) (the four bridging B–H bonds counted as four 3c–2e bonds); options A, B and D accept statement (E) (planarity) – the terminal BH₂ groups lie in one plane, but the bridging H atoms are above and below it.\nWorking: (A) false – four terminal 2c–2e and four bridging B–H bonds differ; (B) false – only two 3c–2e (banana) bonds; (C) true – electron-deficient, forms adducts (e.g. with NH₃, H⁻); (D) true – 4BF₃ + 3NaBH₄ → 2B₂H₆ + 3NaBF₄ (and NaBH₄ + I₂ also gives B₂H₆); (E) false – bridging H atoms lie above and below the plane.\nKey idea: Two banana bonds, each B–H–B spanning three centres with two electrons.","traps":["Four bridging bonds counted as four 3c–2e bonds: Each 3c–2e bond spans B–H–B; two bridges = two 3c–2e bonds.","Planarity assumed: Bridging H lie above and below that plane."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The most stable trihalide of nitrogen is:","options":["NF₃","NCl₃","NBr₃","NI₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D): heavier halogens give weaker, more strained N–X bonds; (D) NI₃ is the least stable (explosive).\nWorking: only NF₃ is thermodynamically stable (strong N–F bonds, small F fits around small N); NCl₃ is explosive, NBr₃ and NI₃ are unstable (NI₃·NH₃ is a contact explosive).\nKey idea: Small N + small F → strong bonds and little steric strain.","traps":["Bond polarity confused with stability: Bond strength, not polarity, governs stability here; N–F is the strongest."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"PCl₅ is well known, but NCl₅ is not. Because,","options":["nitrogen is less reactive than phosphorous.","nitrogen doesn't have d-orbitals in its valence shell.","catenation tendency is weaker in nitrogen than phosphorous.","size of phosphorous is larger than nitrogen."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – N₂ is less reactive, but that is a property of N≡N, not of N atom covalency; (C) – true but irrelevant; (D) – true, size helps, but the textbook reason is lack of d orbitals.\nWorking: N (n = 2) has no 2d orbitals, so it cannot expand its octet to form five bonds; P can use 3d (expanded valence).\nKey idea: Expanded octet needs vacant d orbitals in the valence shell; second-period N has none.","traps":["True fact chosen as the reason: Ask which fact limits the number of bonds: availability of d orbitals."],"topics":["Group 15","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The number of bridged oxygen atoms present in compound B formed from the following reactions is\nPb(NO₃)₂ —(673 K)→ A + PbO + O₂\nA —(Dimerise)→ B","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) confuses N₂O₄ with N₂O₅ (O₂N–O–NO₂, one bridging O) or assumes an O-bridged ONONO₂ dimer; (C)/(D) borrow bridging-O counts from phosphorus oxides such as P₄O₆.\nWorking: 2Pb(NO₃)₂ → 2PbO + 4NO₂ + O₂; A = NO₂, B = N₂O₄ (O₂N–NO₂, planar, N–N bonded) with no bridging oxygen.\nKey idea: NO₂ dimerises through the odd electron on N, giving an N–N bond; N₂O₅ (not N₂O₄) has the bridging O.","traps":["N₂O₄ vs N₂O₅ bridging: N₂O₄ is O₂N–NO₂; N₂O₅ is O₂N–O–NO₂.","Identity of A: Balance: lead nitrate gives NO₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the following reactions:\nPCl₃ + H₂O → A + HCl\nA + H₂O → B + HCl\nnumber of ionisable protons present in the product B ________.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counting all three H of H₃PO₃), 1 (taking B as partially hydrolysed HOPCl₂ or similar from literal two-step balance).\nWorking: PCl₃ hydrolyses completely (PCl₃ + 3H₂O → H₃PO₃ + 3HCl); the intended B is phosphorous acid H₃PO₃, HP(O)(OH)₂, with two P–OH groups → 2 ionisable protons (P–H is not ionisable). The two-step scheme is not balanced literally (two steps would leave one Cl), but the intended product is clearly H₃PO₃.\nKey idea: Count only O–H hydrogens for basicity of P oxoacids.","traps":["H₃PO₃ is dibasic, not tribasic: One H is bonded directly to P.","Literal two-step balance: Read it as complete hydrolysis to H₃PO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Choose the correct stability order of group 13 elements in their +1 oxidation state.","options":["Al < Ga < In < Tl","Tl < In < Ga < Al","Al < Ga < Tl < In","Al < Tl < Ga < In"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the trend (+3 stability order); (C)/(D) break the smooth trend for Tl/In.\nWorking: inert pair effect grows down the group, so +1 becomes steadily more stable: Al < Ga < In < Tl = (A).\nKey idea: Lower oxidation state stability rises down a p-block group (inert pair effect).","traps":["Trend reversed: +1 stable for heavy elements (Tl⁺).","Ga anomaly overextended: Inert pair effect order is smooth Al→Tl."],"topics":["Group 13","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The correct order of melting points of hydrides of group 16 elements is :","options":["H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂O","H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te","H₂S < H₂Te < H₂Se < H₂O","H₂Se < H₂S < H₂Te < H₂O"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ignores H-bonding of water; (C) swaps Se/Te; (D) puts H₂Se below H₂S.\nWorking: m.p. (K) H₂O 273, H₂S 188, H₂Se 208, H₂Te 222; H₂O highest by H-bonding, rest rise with molar mass: H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te < H₂O = (A).\nKey idea: Water is anomalous; for H₂S→H₂Te dispersion forces rise with molar mass.","traps":["Water placed lowest: H-bonding dominates for H₂O.","Se/Te swapped: m.p. rises with molar mass after H₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Consider the following reaction : A + alkali → B (Major Product). If B is an oxoacid of phosphorus with no P–H bond, then A is :","options":["White P₄","Red P₄","P₂O₃","H₃PO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) white P₄ – best-known P + NaOH reaction, but gives hypophosphite (P–H); (C) P₂O₃ – anhydride of H₃PO₃ (P–H); (D) H₃PO₃ – gives phosphite salts (P–H), its disproportionation to H₃PO₄ needs heat, not alkali.\nWorking: white P₄ + alkali disproportionates to PH₃ + H₂PO₂⁻ (hypophosphite, two P–H bonds); P₂O₃ and H₃PO₃ with alkali give phosphite (one P–H bond). That leaves red P₄. Red P + alkali is not described in NCERT, so this question is best answered by elimination; red phosphorus oxidised in alkaline medium can give hypophosphate (H₄P₂O₆, P–P bond, no P–H bond).\nKey idea: Hypophosphite and phosphite both keep P–H bonds; only a phosphate-type product has none.","traps":["Familiar white P + NaOH chosen: Check P–H bonds in H₃PO₂ (two).","H₃PO₃ disproportionation recalled: Alkali gives phosphite salt, still P–H."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List-I (Si-compounds) with List-II (Si-polymeric/other products). List-I: (A) (CH₃)₄Si, (B) (CH₃)Si(OH)₃, (C) (CH₃)₂Si(OH)₂, (D) (CH₃)₃Si(OH). List-II: (I) Chain silicone, (II) Dimeric silicone, (III) Silane, (IV) 2D-silicone.","options":["(A)–(III), (B)–(II), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(I), (C)–(II), (D)–(III)","(A)–(II), (B)–(I), (C)–(IV), (D)–(III)","(A)–(III), (B)–(IV), (C)–(I), (D)–(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – swaps the trifunctional (2D) and monofunctional (dimer) silanols; (B)/(C) – give the tetramethyl compound a silicone structure though it has no OH to condense.\nWorking: (CH₃)₄Si has no OH, it is a (tetramethyl)silane; CH₃Si(OH)₃ has three OH and condenses into a cross-linked 2D/3D network; (CH₃)₂Si(OH)₂ (two OH) gives linear chain silicones; (CH₃)₃SiOH (one OH) can only dimerise to (CH₃)₃Si–O–Si(CH₃)₃ → option (D).\nKey idea: One OH → dimer (chain stopper), two OH → linear chain, three OH → cross-linked sheet/network, no OH → silane.","traps":["Mono- vs tri-silanol swapped: Count Si–OH: condensation sites set how far the polymer can grow.","(CH₃)₄Si treated as a silicone: No Si–OH → no condensation → it stays a silane."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Heating white phosphorus with conc. NaOH solution gives mainly","options":["Na₃P and H₂O","H₃PO and NaH","P(OH)₃ and NaH₂PO₄","PH₃ and NaH₂PO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – sodium phosphide needs Na metal, not NaOH; (B) – NaH cannot form in water; (C) – phosphate (P +5) and P(OH)₃ give no reduced product, so electrons do not balance.\nWorking: P₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → PH₃ + 3NaH₂PO₂ (disproportionation of P(0) into P(−3) and P(+1)) → (D).\nKey idea: P(0) splits into P(−3) in PH₃ and P(+1) in H₂PO₂⁻ – a disproportionation.","traps":["Hypophosphite vs phosphate product: A reduced product (PH₃) must pair with a low oxidised state (+1)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The gas produced by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite is","options":["NH₃","N₂","N₂O","Cl₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NH₃ comes from NH₄Cl with a base, not nitrite; (C) N₂O comes from heating NH₄NO₃; (D) Cl₂ is not formed (Cl⁻ stays as NaCl).\nWorking: NH₄Cl(aq) + NaNO₂(aq) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + NaCl(aq) (via NH₄NO₂ decomposition).\nKey idea: Ammonium nitrite decomposes to N₂ and water.","traps":["NH₄NO₂ vs NH₄NO₃ decomposition: Nitrite → N₂; nitrate → N₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Assertion A: Fluorine forms one oxoacid. Reason R: Fluorine has smallest size amongst all halogens and is highly electronegative. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) tempts those who think the \"real\" reason is absence of d orbitals; (C)/(D) reject a true statement.\nWorking: F forms only HOF; the textbook reason is its small size and high electronegativity (absence of d orbitals is also often cited, but R as given is the NCERT explanation).\nKey idea: NCERT itself gives size and electronegativity as the cause.","traps":["Absence of d orbitals as the \"real\" reason: Follow NCERT: small size and high EN explain HOF only."],"topics":["Group 17","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Nitrogen gas is obtained by thermal decomposition of","options":["Ba(NO₃)₂","Ba(N₃)₂","NaNO₂","NaNO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ba(NO₃)₂ gives NO₂/O₂; (C) NaNO₂ gives N₂ only with NH₄Cl; (D) NaNO₃ gives NaNO₂ + O₂.\nWorking: Ba(N₃)₂ → Ba + 3N₂ (very pure N₂). Ba(NO₃)₂ gives BaO + NO₂ + O₂; NaNO₃ gives NaNO₂ + O₂; NaNO₂ alone does not give N₂ (NH₄Cl + NaNO₂ does).\nKey idea: Azides (N₃⁻) decompose to metal + N₂; nitrates release O₂/NO₂.","traps":["NaNO₂ + NH₄Cl memory: NaNO₂ needs NH₄Cl; it does not give N₂ on its own heating.","Nitrate vs azide: Read N₃ vs NO₃ carefully."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: The pentavalent oxide of group-15 element, E₂O₅, is less acidic than trivalent oxide, E₂O₃, of the same element. Statement II: The acidic character of trivalent oxide of group 15 elements, E₂O₃, decreases down the group. In light of the above statements, choose most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I true, but statement II is false.","Statement I is false but statement II is true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) accept the reversed Statement I; (B) rejects the correct down-group trend.\nWorking: the higher oxidation state oxide is more acidic, so E₂O₅ is more acidic than E₂O₃ (I false); E₂O₃ goes from acidic (N, P) through amphoteric (As, Sb) to basic (Bi) down the group (II true).\nKey idea: Higher oxidation state → more covalent, more acidic oxide; metallic character rises down the group.","traps":["Reversed oxidation-state rule: Recall N₂O₅/HNO₃ vs N₂O₃/HNO₂ acidity."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Among the given oxides of nitrogen, N₂O, N₂O₃, N₂O₄ and N₂O₅, the number of compound(s) having an N–N bond is:","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) assumes all N₂Oₓ have N–N; (B) misses N₂O (linear N–N–O); (A) counts only N₂O₄.\nWorking: N₂O (N≡N⁺–O⁻), N₂O₃ (ON–NO₂) and N₂O₄ (O₂N–NO₂) have N–N bonds; N₂O₅ is O₂N–O–NO₂ with an N–O–N bridge.\nKey idea: N₂O₅ is the anhydride O₂N–O–NO₂.","traps":["N₂O₅ has N–O–N: Draw N₂O₅ as anhydride of HNO₃.","N₂O is N–N–O, not N–O–N: N₂O is isoelectronic with CO₂, N central."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which of the following oxoacids of sulphur contains \"S\" in two different oxidation states?","options":["H₂S₂O₃","H₂S₂O₆","H₂S₂O₇","H₂S₂O₈"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) H₂S₂O₈: average +7 looks odd, but each S is +6 with a peroxide link; (C) both +6; (B) both +5 (S–S bond between equal S).\nWorking: in thiosulphuric acid the central S and the terminal (thio) S are in different states (+5 and −1 by structure; conventionally +6 and −2); H₂S₂O₆ has both S at +5, H₂S₂O₇ and H₂S₂O₈ have both S at +6.\nKey idea: Average oxidation number hides unequal atoms; only an S=S/S–S between unlike S (thio S) gives two values.","traps":["Peroxodisulphuric acid +7 trap: Count the peroxide O as −1; each S is +6.","Dithionic S–S bond: Symmetric S–S → both S identical (+5)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match the List-I with List-II. List-I (Metal): (A) Cs (B) Ga (C) B (D) Si. List-II (Application): (I) High temperature thermometer (II) Water repellent sprays (III) Photoelectric cells (IV) Bullet proof vest. Choose the most appropriate answer from the option given below:","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) mix Ga with photoelectric cells (confusing with Cs) and Si with thermometers.\nWorking: Cs – photoelectric cells (low IE); Ga – high-temperature thermometers (liquid over a wide range); B – bullet-proof vests (boron carbide/boron fibres); Si – water-repellent sprays (silicones).\nKey idea: Low IE → photoemission (Cs); wide liquid range → thermometer (Ga); hard B fibres/B₄C → armour; silicones → water repellent.","traps":["Si itself vs silicones: Read \"Si\" as silicon-based polymers (silicones).","Ga vs Cs: Photoemission needs lowest IE – Cs."],"topics":["Group 13","Group 14"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The oxoacid of phosphorus that is easily obtained from a reaction of alkali and white phosphorus and has two P–H bonds, is :","options":["Phosphonic acid","Phosphinic acid","Pyrophosphorus acid","Hypophosphoric acid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) phosphonic acid = H₃PO₃ (one P–H); (D) hypophosphoric acid H₄P₂O₆ looks like \"hypophosphorous\"; (C) pyrophosphorous acid H₄P₂O₅ has two P–H but is not made from P₄ + alkali.\nWorking: P₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → PH₃ + 3NaH₂PO₂; H₃PO₂ (hypophosphorous acid, IUPAC phosphinic acid) has two P–H bonds. Phosphonic acid (H₃PO₃) has one P–H; hypophosphoric acid H₄P₂O₆ has none.\nKey idea: H₃PO₂: one OH, two P–H – IUPAC phosphinic acid; H₃PO₃: phosphonic acid.","traps":["Hypophosphorous vs hypophosphoric: Hypophosphoric = H₄P₂O₆ (P–P bond, no P–H).","Phosphinic vs phosphonic: \"-inic\" = two P–H (H₃PO₂); \"-onic\" = one P–H (H₃PO₃)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Aqueous solution of which of the following boron compounds will be strongly basic in nature?","options":["NaBH₄","LiBH₄","B₂H₆","Na₂B₄O₇"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): BH₄⁻ salts look like \"hydride = base\", though their aqueous solutions are not described as strongly basic; (C) B₂H₆ hydrolyses to boric acid (weak acid) and H₂.\nWorking: borax hydrolyses, Na₂B₄O₇ + 7H₂O → 2NaOH + 4H₃BO₃, giving NaOH (strong base) with weak boric acid, so the solution is alkaline; B₂H₆ gives boric acid (acidic); BH₄⁻ salts are only slowly hydrolysed and are not the textbook basic example.\nKey idea: Borax is a salt of strong base NaOH and weak acid H₃BO₃ → alkaline solution.","traps":["Borohydride taken as strong base: NaBH₄ is a reducing agent, stable enough in water; borax is the hydrolysing salt.","Diborane hydrolysis product: Gives H₃BO₃ (acidic) + H₂."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The incorrect statement is:","options":["Cl₂ is more reactive than ClF.","F₂ is more reactive than ClF.","On hydrolysis ClF forms HOCl and HF.","F₂ is a stronger oxidizing agent than Cl₂ in aqueous solution."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) true (F₂ is the exception); (C) true hydrolysis; (D) true – F₂ has the highest E°.\nWorking: interhalogens (except with F–F comparison) are more reactive than the parent halogens because the X–X′ bond is weaker than X–X, so 'Cl₂ more reactive than ClF' is incorrect. F₂ is more reactive than ClF; ClF + H₂O → HOCl + HF; F₂ is the stronger oxidant in water.\nKey idea: X–X′ bonds are weaker than X–X (except F–F), so interhalogens out-react their parent halogens other than F₂.","traps":["Interhalogens more reactive than halogens: NCERT: interhalogens are generally more reactive than halogens except fluorine.","Hydrolysis products of XX′: The larger, less electronegative halogen ends up in the oxoacid (HOCl), the smaller as HF."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"AB₃ is an interhalogen T-shaped molecule. The number of lone pairs of electrons on A is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (confusing with trigonal pyramidal AB₃E); 0 (trigonal planar); 3 (linear AB₂E₃).\nWorking: AB₃ interhalogen (ClF₃, BrF₃, IF₃): A has 7 valence electrons, 3 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs → sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry, T-shaped. Answer 2.\nKey idea: T-shaped = 3 bp + 2 lp around the central atom.","traps":["T-shape = AB₃E₂: T-shaped means 5 electron pairs with 2 lone pairs equatorial (ClF₃, BrF₃).","Lone pairs on A only: The question asks for the central atom A."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The number of non-ionisable hydrogen atoms present in the final product obtained from the hydrolysis of PCl₅ is:","options":["0","2","1","3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 1 is from H₃PO₃ (one P–H) – stopping at the wrong acid; (B) 2 from H₃PO₂; (D) 3 counts all H as non-ionisable.\nWorking: PCl₅ + 4H₂O → H₃PO₄ + 5HCl (via POCl₃). H₃PO₄ has all three H on O (P–OH), so all are ionisable; non-ionisable (P–H) H = 0.\nKey idea: Only P–H hydrogens are non-ionisable; H₃PO₄ has none.","traps":["Final product is H₃PO₄, not H₃PO₃: Match oxidation state: P(+5) → H₃PO₄.","Ionisable vs non-ionisable H: Only O–H hydrogens ionise."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Chalcogen group elements are:","options":["Se, Tb and Pu.","Se, Te and Po.","S, Te and Pm.","O, Ti and Po."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Tb (terbium) and Pu look like Te/Po; (C) Pm resembles Po; (D) Ti resembles Te.\nWorking: Group 16 (chalcogens) = O, S, Se, Te, Po; options (A), (C), (D) each contain a non-Group-16 element (Tb, Pu, Pm, Ti).\nKey idea: Chalcogens: O, S, Se, Te, Po (Lv).","traps":["Symbol look-alikes: Write the group column first, then match."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List-I (Species) with List-II (Number of lone pairs of electrons on the central atom): (a) XeF₂, (b) XeO₂F₂, (c) XeO₃F₂, (d) XeF₄; (i) 0, (ii) 1, (iii) 2, (iv) 3. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)–(iv), (b)–(i), (c)–(ii), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(ii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(ii), (c)–(iv), (d)–(i)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(ii), (c)–(i), (d)–(iii)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps the oxyfluorides (XeO₂F₂ 0, XeO₃F₂ 1); (B) and (C) give XeF₂ 2 and XeF₄ 0 – confusing bond pairs with lone pairs.\nWorking: Xe has 8 valence electrons; lone pairs = (8 − 2×O − F)/2: XeF₂ (8−2)/2 = 3; XeO₂F₂ (8−4−2)/2 = 1; XeO₃F₂ (8−6−2)/2 = 0; XeF₄ (8−4)/2 = 2.\nKey idea: Each Xe=O uses 2 Xe electrons, each Xe–F uses 1; the rest pair up as lone pairs.","traps":["Oxygen uses two Xe electrons: Subtract 2 per O and 1 per F from 8.","Lone pairs vs bond pairs: Lone pairs = (8 − electrons used in bonding)/2."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In which one of the following molecules strongest back donation of an electron pair from halide to boron is expected?","options":["BCl₃","BF₃","BBr₃","BI₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) BI₃ / (C) BBr₃ – chosen by those who think larger, more polarisable halides donate more; (A) BCl₃ – middle choice when unsure.\nWorking: back donation is pπ–pπ from a filled halogen p orbital into the empty 2p of B; overlap is best for the 2p–2p match in BF₃, and falls off for 3p, 4p, 5p (Cl, Br, I).\nKey idea: Back donation needs similar-sized p orbitals: 2p(F)–2p(B) overlaps best.","traps":["Lewis acidity order reversed: Weakest Lewis acid = strongest back donation.","Electronegativity reasoning: Orbital overlap, not electronegativity, decides π-donation."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In polythionic acid, H₂SₓO₆ (x = 3 to 5), the oxidation state(s) of sulphur is/are:","options":["+5 only","+6 only","+3 and +5 only","0 and +5 only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) +5 only – ignores the central S atoms; (B) +6 – assumes sulphate-like S; (C) +3 – uses an averaged value (e.g. for x = 4, average = +2.5).\nWorking: structure HO₃S–(S)ₓ₋₂–SO₃H; each terminal S bonded to three O (one OH) and one S is +5; the bridging S atoms bonded only to S are 0.\nKey idea: S–S bonds contribute zero; only S–O bonds raise the oxidation state.","traps":["Average oxidation state: Draw the structure and assign each S separately.","Central S forgotten: Chain S atoms bonded only to S are 0."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which one of the following is used to remove most of plutonium from spent nuclear fuel?","options":["ClF₃","O₂F₂","I₂O₅","BrO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ClF₃ – used to make UF₆ (uranium), not to remove Pu; (C) I₂O₅ – estimation of CO; (D) \"BrO₃\" – unstable bromine oxide, no such use.\nWorking: dioxygen difluoride oxidises plutonium to volatile PuF₆, which is how most Pu is removed from spent fuel. ClF₃/BrF₃ are used for UF₆ in uranium enrichment; I₂O₅ is used to estimate CO.\nKey idea: Pu → PuF₆ by O₂F₂; U → UF₆ by ClF₃/BrF₃.","traps":["ClF₃ nuclear use confused with O₂F₂: ClF₃/BrF₃ → UF₆ (enrichment); O₂F₂ → PuF₆ (spent fuel)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which one of the following is formed (mainly) when red phosphorus is heated in a sealed tube at 803 K?","options":["White phosphorus","Yellow phosphorus","β-Black phosphorus","α-Black phosphorus"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) β-black P comes from white P at 473 K under pressure; (A)/(B) white/yellow P – red P does not revert to these on heating in a sealed tube.\nWorking: red P heated in a sealed tube at 803 K gives α-black phosphorus; β-black phosphorus is made by heating white P at 473 K under high pressure.\nKey idea: Red P + 803 K sealed tube → α; white P + 473 K high pressure → β.","traps":["α vs β black phosphorus: Pair red→α (803 K), white→β (473 K, high pressure)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of halogen(s) forming halic (V) acid is __________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 – includes fluorine; 2 – forgets HBrO₃ or HIO₃.\nWorking: halic(V) acids HXO₃ are HClO₃, HBrO₃ and HIO₃; fluorine (most electronegative, no d-orbitals, only HOF known) forms none. Count = 3.\nKey idea: F shows only −1 (HOF is the sole F oxoacid).","traps":["Fluorine included: F has no positive oxidation states in oxoacids (only HOF)."],"topics":["Group 17","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which one of the following correctly represents the order of stability of oxides, X₂O; (X = halogen)?","options":["Br > Cl > I","Br > I > Cl","Cl > I > Br","I > Cl > Br"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) put Br first; (C) assumes Cl (most familiar oxide Cl₂O) most stable.\nWorking: old NCERT states that iodine oxides are the most stable and bromine oxides the least stable among halogen oxides (I > Cl > Br), the Br anomaly arising from middle-row (poor 4p–2p overlap) effects. Strictly, I₂O is not a well-known compound; the question applies the general halogen-oxide order.\nKey idea: Halogen oxide stability: I > Cl > Br (Br is the anomaly).","traps":["Expecting a monotonic trend: Recall the Br anomaly: bromine oxides least stable."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The number of S=O bonds present in sulphurous acid, peroxodisulphuric acid and pyrosulphuric acid, respectively are:","options":["2, 3 and 4","1, 4 and 3","2, 4 and 3","1, 4 and 4"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) give H₂SO₃ two S=O; (B)/(C) give pyrosulphuric acid only 3 S=O (forgetting one SO₂ unit per S).\nWorking: H₂SO₃ = OS(OH)₂ → 1 S=O; H₂S₂O₈ = HO₃S–O–O–SO₃H → 2 + 2 = 4; H₂S₂O₇ = HO₃S–O–SO₃H → 2 + 2 = 4.\nKey idea: Each S(VI) in H₂S₂O₇/H₂S₂O₈ carries two S=O; S(IV) in H₂SO₃ has one.","traps":["H₂SO₃ drawn with two S=O: H₂SO₃ = O=S(OH)₂ with a lone pair on S.","Bridging O counted as S=O: Only terminal non-OH oxygens are double-bonded."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Among the following allotropic forms of sulphur, the number of allotropic forms, which will show paramagnetism is ____. (A) α-sulphur; (B) β-sulphur; (C) S₂-form","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (all sulphur taken diamagnetic), 3.\nWorking: α- and β-sulphur are S₈ rings with all electrons paired; S₂ vapour has two unpaired π* electrons like O₂ and is paramagnetic.\nKey idea: S₂ is isoelectronic in valence with O₂ → two unpaired electrons.","traps":["Missing S₂ paramagnetism: Apply MO picture of O₂ to S₂."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The correct statement about B₂H₆ is:","options":["Terminal B–H bonds have less p-character when compared to bridging bonds.","The two B–H–B bonds are not of same length.","All B–H–B angles are of 120°.","Its fragment, BH₃, behaves as a Lewis base."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) the two bridges are equivalent (symmetric molecule); (C) 120° taken from planar BH₃; (D) electron-deficient BH₃ is a Lewis acid, not base.\nWorking: terminal B–H are normal 2c–2e bonds (shorter, ~119 pm) using orbitals of higher s-character; bridging 3c–2e bonds are longer (~133 pm) with more p-character. (B) both bridges are equal; (C) B–H–B angle ≈ 97°, not 120°; (D) BH₃ is a Lewis acid.\nKey idea: Shorter bond ⇒ more s-character; terminal B–H are shorter than bridging B–H.","traps":["120° from trigonal BH₃: B is roughly sp³ in B₂H₆; B–H–B ≈ 97°.","s/p character direction reversed: Bent's rule: more s-character → shorter, stronger bond."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Among the following, the number of halide(s) which is/are inert to hydrolysis is ______. (A) BF₃ (B) SiCl₄ (C) PCl₅ (D) SF₆","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (taking BF₃ as inert because B–F is strong/back-bonded), 0 (thinking SF₆ hydrolyses like other S halides), 3.\nWorking: only SF₆ is inert to hydrolysis (S is coordinatively saturated and sterically shielded by six F; no vacant low-energy orbital usable). BF₃ hydrolyses to H₃BO₃ + HBF₄, SiCl₄ to Si(OH)₄/SiO₂ + HCl, PCl₅ to POCl₃/H₃PO₄ + HCl.\nKey idea: Hydrolysis needs a site for water attack: vacant p (B), vacant d/expandable (Si, P); SF₆ is saturated and shielded.","traps":["BF₃ judged inert: BF₃ still hydrolyses giving H₃BO₃ and HBF₄.","SF₆ treated like SCl₂/SF₄: SF₆ is kinetically inert (steric shielding)."],"topics":["Group 16","Group 13","Group 14"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of bond dissociation enthalpy of halogens is:","options":["Cl₂ > F₂ > Br₂ > I₂","I₂ > Br₂ > Cl₂ > F₂","Cl₂ > Br₂ > F₂ > I₂","F₂ > Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) simple down-group trend ignoring F₂ anomaly; (B) reversed trend; (A) puts F₂ above Br₂ (F₂ is below Br₂).\nWorking: bond enthalpies ≈ Cl₂ 242, Br₂ 192, F₂ 158, I₂ 151 kJ mol⁻¹; F₂ is anomalously low because of lone-pair repulsion in the small F₂ molecule.\nKey idea: F₂ sits between Br₂ and I₂.","traps":["Simple group trend applied: Remember lone-pair repulsion weakens F–F.","F₂ placed just below Cl₂: F₂ (158) < Br₂ (192)."],"topics":["Group 17","Anomalous behaviour of first members"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement-I: α and β forms of sulphur can change reversibly between themselves with slow heating or slow cooling. Statement-II: At room temperature the stable crystalline form of sulphur is monoclinic sulphur. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts monoclinic as room-temperature form; (A)/(D) doubt reversible interconversion.\nWorking: rhombic (α) and monoclinic (β) sulphur interconvert at the transition temperature 369 K on slow heating/cooling, so I is true; at room temperature the stable form is rhombic (α), so II is false.\nKey idea: α = rhombic, stable below 369 K; β = monoclinic, stable above.","traps":["Monoclinic vs rhombic swapped: Rhombic (yellow) is the ordinary room-temperature form."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Compound A used as a strong oxidizing agent is amphoteric in nature. It is the part of lead storage batteries. Compound A is:","options":["PbO₂","PbO","PbSO₄","Pb₃O₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) PbO – amphoteric but not a strong oxidant; (C) PbSO₄ – formed on discharge, not an oxidising agent; (D) Pb₃O₄ – mixed oxide 2PbO·PbO₂, not used in battery.\nWorking: PbO₂ (Pb +4) is a strong oxidant (inert pair effect), amphoteric, and is the cathode material of the lead storage battery.\nKey idea: Pb(IV) is unstable (inert pair) → PbO₂ oxidises; it is the battery cathode.","traps":["PbSO₄ chosen: PbSO₄ is the discharge product, not an oxidant.","PbO chosen for amphoterism: Pb(II) is stable, so PbO is not a strong oxidant."],"topics":["Group 14","Inert pair effect & catenation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Assertion A: In TlI₃, isomorphous to CsI₃, the metal is present in +1 oxidation state. Reason R: Tl metal has fourteen f electrons in the electronic configuration. Choose the most appropriate answer.","options":["A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – student thinks Tl lacks 14 f electrons (forgets 4f¹⁴ before 5d¹⁰); (B) – f-electrons' poor shielding is part of the inert pair argument, so R looks like the explanation; (C) – formula read as Tl³⁺ + 3I⁻.\nWorking: TlI₃ is Tl⁺[I₃]⁻ like Cs⁺[I₃]⁻, so A is true; Tl ([Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰6s²6p¹) does have 14 f electrons, so R is true, but the +1 state is due to the inert pair effect (and I₃⁻ formation since Tl³⁺ oxidises I⁻), not the f-electron count itself.\nKey idea: TlI₃ = Tl⁺ I₃⁻; Tl³⁺ cannot coexist with I⁻ (it oxidises it).","traps":["Formula taken at face value as Tl³⁺: Compare with CsI₃: Cs can only be +1, so I₃⁻ is present.","Poor f-shielding treated as the explanation: R only states a count; the cause is inert pair stability plus Tl³⁺/I⁻ redox."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 13"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The set in which compounds have different nature is:","options":["B(OH)₃ and H₃PO₃","B(OH)₃ and Al(OH)₃","NaOH and Ca(OH)₂","Be(OH)₂ and Al(OH)₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) both acidic – tempting if H₃PO₃ is thought of as a base-forming compound; (C) both strong/medium bases; (D) both amphoteric – students who forget the Be–Al diagonal relation pick it.\nWorking: B(OH)₃ is a weak (Lewis) acid while Al(OH)₃ is amphoteric – different nature. B(OH)₃ and H₃PO₃ are both acidic; NaOH and Ca(OH)₂ are both basic; Be(OH)₂ and Al(OH)₃ are both amphoteric (diagonal relationship).\nKey idea: Down Group 13 the hydroxide changes from acidic (B) to amphoteric (Al); Be(OH)₂ mirrors Al(OH)₃.","traps":["Diagonal relationship pair: Diagonal pair Be–Al: both amphoteric.","Same group assumed same nature: Group 13 shows acidic → amphoteric change from B to Al."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Chemical nature of the nitrogen oxide compound obtained from a reaction of concentrated nitric acid and P₄O₁₀ (in 4 : 1 ratio) is:","options":["acidic","basic","amphoteric","neutral"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) neutral – students who think of NO/N₂O; (C) amphoteric – no nitrogen oxide is amphoteric; (B) basic – never for a non-metal oxide.\nWorking: 4HNO₃ + P₄O₁₀ → 4HPO₃ + 2N₂O₅; P₄O₁₀ dehydrates nitric acid to its anhydride N₂O₅, which is acidic (gives HNO₃ with water).\nKey idea: P₄O₁₀ removes water from HNO₃ → N₂O₅ (anhydride of HNO₃) → acidic.","traps":["Wrong oxide identified: P₄O₁₀ is a dehydrating agent → acid anhydride N₂O₅."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are the statements about diborane (a) Diborane is prepared by the oxidation of NaBH₄ with I₂ (b) Each boron atom is in sp² hybridized state (c) Diborane has one bridged 3 centre-2-electron bond (d) Diborane is a planar molecule. The option with correct statement(s) is -","options":["(c) and (d) only","(a) only","(c) only","(a) and (b) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(A) catch students who miss that there are two 3c–2e bonds; (D) catches those who take B as sp² (as in BH₃).\nWorking: 2NaBH₄ + I₂ → B₂H₆ + 2NaI + H₂ is the lab preparation (a true); B is sp³ (b false); there are TWO B–H–B 3c–2e bridges (c false); the bridging H atoms lie above and below the plane, so the molecule is non-planar (d false).\nKey idea: B₂H₆: sp³ B, two banana bonds, non-planar; made from NaBH₄ + I₂.","traps":["One vs two banana bonds: B₂H₆ has two B–H–B bridges.","BH₃-like sp² assumed: In B₂H₆ each B has four bonds → sp³."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following group-15 hydride is the strongest reducing agent ?","options":["AsH₃","BiH₃","PH₃","SbH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) PH₃ chosen by students who confuse reducing power with basicity/stability; (D) SbH₃ is second.\nWorking: E–H bond strength falls down the group (NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃ > BiH₃), so BiH₃ releases H most easily and is the strongest reducing agent.\nKey idea: Weakest E–H bond → strongest reductant → BiH₃.","traps":["Trend direction: Reducing power increases down the group."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following compounds of Group–14 elements is not known?","options":["[GeCl₆]²⁻","[Sn(OH)₆]²⁻","[SiCl₆]²⁻","[SiF₆]²⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) [SiF₆]²⁻ is chosen by students who think Si cannot exceed covalence 4; (A) and (B) are heavier-element species that are known.\nWorking: six large Cl atoms cannot fit around the small Si atom (and Si–Cl lone-pair interaction is weak), so [SiCl₆]²⁻ is unknown; [SiF₆]²⁻, [GeCl₆]²⁻ and [Sn(OH)₆]²⁻ are known.\nKey idea: Small F fits six around Si; large Cl does not.","traps":["Si can exceed covalence four: Si uses d orbitals with small F; the limit is steric.","NOT-type stem: Mark each known/unknown first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The product obtained from the electrolytic oxidation of acidified sulphate solutions, is:","options":["HSO₄⁻","HO₃SOOSO₃H","HO₂SOSO₂H","HO₃SOSO₃H"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) the starting ion itself; (C) H₂S₂O₄-type (dithionous) skeleton; (D) H₂S₂O₇ pyrosulphuric acid – S–O–S without peroxide, the closest look-alike.\nWorking: anodic oxidation of HSO₄⁻ in acid gives peroxodisulphuric acid H₂S₂O₈, i.e. HO₃S–O–O–SO₃H (contains a peroxide link), option (B).\nKey idea: Electrolytic oxidation couples two HSO₄⁻ through an O–O bond: 2HSO₄⁻ → H₂S₂O₈ + 2e⁻.","traps":["Peroxo vs pyro linkage: Oxidation joins via O–O: look for \"OO\".","Electrolysis product not just the ion: Anode oxidation must raise O-content/link."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The oxidation states of 'P' in H₄P₂O₇, H₄P₂O₅ and H₄P₂O₆, respectively, are:","options":["7, 5 and 6","5, 4 and 3","5, 3 and 4","6, 4 and 5"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) forgets to divide by 2 P atoms (or adds 2); (B) right values in wrong order; (D) each off by +1 (miscounting H).\nWorking: 4 + 2x − 14 = 0 → +5; 4 + 2x − 10 = 0 → +3; 4 + 2x − 12 = 0 → +4; so 5, 3, 4, option (C).\nKey idea: With 2 P atoms, each extra O raises each P by +1.","traps":["Two P atoms share the charge: Write 2x explicitly.","Order of species: Match values to the listed order."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Number of Cl=O bonds in chlorous acid, chloric acid and perchloric acid respectively are","options":["3, 1 and 1","4, 1 and 0","1, 1 and 3","1, 2 and 3"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses; (B) counts O atoms of perchlorate as chlorous; (C) mixes chloric and chlorous.\nWorking: HOClO (one Cl=O), HOClO₂ (two), HOClO₃ (three); each has one Cl–OH.\nKey idea: Number of Cl=O = number of O − 1 (one O is in OH).","traps":["Chlorous vs chloric names: Per- > -ic > -ous > hypo- in O count."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I with List-II: List-I (Industrial process): (a) Haber's process (b) Ostwald's process (c) Contact process (d) Hall-Heroult process. List-II (Application): (i) HNO₃ synthesis (ii) Aluminium extraction (iii) NH₃ synthesis (iv) H₂SO₄ synthesis. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – swaps Ostwald and Contact; (A)/(D) – shift Haber to Al/H₂SO₄.\nWorking: Haber – NH₃; Ostwald – HNO₃; Contact – H₂SO₄; Hall–Héroult – electrolytic extraction of Al.\nKey idea: Haber NH₃ → Ostwald oxidises NH₃ to HNO₃; Contact gives SO₃/H₂SO₄.","traps":["Ostwald vs Contact: Ostwald oxidises NH₃ (N acid); Contact oxidises SO₂ (S acid)."],"topics":["Group 15","Group 16"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Match List-I with List-II: List-I (Name of oxo acid): (a) Hypophosphorous acid (b) Orthophosphoric acid (c) Hypophosphoric acid (d) Orthophosphorous acid. List-II (Oxidation state of 'P'): (i) +5 (ii) +4 (iii) +3 (iv) +2 (v) +1. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(v), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(v), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(v), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) – give hypophosphorous +2 (the unused decoy); (C) also gives orthophosphoric +1; (D) gives orthophosphoric +4.\nWorking: H₃PO₂ +1; H₃PO₄ +5; H₄P₂O₆ +4; H₃PO₃ +3.\nKey idea: Hypophosphorous H₃PO₂ (+1) ≠ hypophosphoric H₄P₂O₆ (+4).","traps":["Hypophosphorous vs hypophosphoric: -ous = H₃PO₂ (+1); -ic = H₄P₂O₆ (+4).","Extra List-II entry +2: Compute each oxidation number; one entry is unused."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A group 15 element, which is a metal and forms a hydride with strongest reducing power among group 15 hydrides. The element is:","options":["Sb","P","As","Bi"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) – metalloids with weaker reducing hydrides; (B) – non-metal.\nWorking: Bi is the only true metal in Group 15; E–H bond strength falls down the group, so BiH₃ is the strongest reducing agent (and least stable hydride).\nKey idea: Weakest E–H bond (BiH₃) → strongest reducing agent.","traps":["Sb as metal: Only Bi is a typical metal; Sb is a metalloid."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The INCORRECT statement regarding the structure of C₆₀ is:","options":["The six-membered rings are fused to both six and five-membered rings.","Each carbon atom forms three sigma bonds.","The five-membered rings are fused only to six-membered rings.","It contains 12 six-membered rings and 24 five-membered rings."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are true NCERT statements; (D) changes the counts (12 and 24 instead of 20 and 12).\nWorking: C₆₀ (buckminsterfullerene) has 20 six-membered and 12 five-membered rings; each sp² carbon makes three σ bonds; each pentagon is surrounded only by hexagons, while each hexagon touches both hexagons and pentagons.\nKey idea: Euler: 12 pentagons always; for C₆₀, hexagons = 20.","traps":["Ring counts misremembered: Pentagons are always 12; hexagons in C₆₀ are 20.","Incorrect-statement wording: Pick the one that is false."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The exact volumes of 1 M NaOH solution required to neutralise 50 mL of 1 M H₃PO₃ solution and 100 mL of 2 M H₃PO₂ solution, respectively, are:","options":["100 mL and 100 mL","100 mL and 50 mL","100 mL and 200 mL","50 mL and 50 mL"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) second value 100 mL – 2 M concentration of H₃PO₂ ignored (100 mmol taken); (B) 50 mL – H₃PO₂ amount quartered (slip in M×V); (D) H₃PO₃ taken as monobasic and H₃PO₂ concentration ignored. Treating both as tribasic gives 150 mL and 600 mL (not offered).\nWorking: H₃PO₃ is dibasic (two P–OH) → 50 mmol × 2 = 100 mmol NaOH = 100 mL; H₃PO₂ is monobasic (one P–OH) → 200 mmol × 1 = 200 mL.\nKey idea: Only O–H hydrogens are acidic: H₃PO₃ = 2, H₃PO₂ = 1.","traps":["All three H taken as acidic: Draw the structure: count P–OH, not H.","Concentration ignored for second solution: mmol = M × V for each solution."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The reaction of white phosphorus on boiling with alkali in inert atmosphere resulted in the formation of product 'A'. The reaction 1 mol of 'A' with excess of AgNO₃ in aqueous medium gives ________ mol(s) of Ag. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (taking A = PH₃), 2 (assuming P +1 → +3), 12 (treating per P₄).\nWorking: P₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → PH₃ + 3NaH₂PO₂; H₂PO₂⁻ + 4Ag⁺ + 2H₂O → H₃PO₄ + 4Ag + 3H⁺ (P +1 → +5, 4 e⁻) → 4 mol Ag. Here 'A' is the oxoanion salt NaH₂PO₂ (formed alongside PH₃), which reduces Ag⁺; the gas PH₃ is not 'A'.\nKey idea: H₂PO₂⁻ is a strong reducing agent: P(+1) → P(+5) releases 4 electrons.","traps":["A taken as PH₃: Product used with AgNO₃ is the oxoanion salt NaH₂PO₂.","P oxidised only to +3: Excess Ag⁺ oxidises to phosphate (+5)."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The set that represents the pair of neutral oxides of nitrogen is :","options":["NO and N₂O","N₂O and N₂O₃","N₂O and NO₂","NO and NO₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Each wrong option pairs one neutral oxide with an acidic one (N₂O₃ or NO₂).\nWorking: N₂O and NO are neutral; N₂O₃ and NO₂ (and N₂O₅) are acidic.\nKey idea: Low oxidation states (+1, +2) of N give neutral oxides N₂O and NO.","traps":["NO₂ thought neutral: NO₂ dimerises and gives HNO₃/HNO₂ with water – acidic."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The number of ionisable hydrogens present in the product obtained from a reaction of phosphorus trichloride and phosphonic acid is:","options":["3","0","2","1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3 – basicity of H₃PO₄ confused; (B) 0; (D) 1 – basicity of H₃PO₂; also 4 if all H counted.\nWorking: PCl₃ + 5H₃PO₃ → 3H₄P₂O₅ + 3HCl; pyrophosphorous (diphosphonic) acid H₄P₂O₅ = (HO)(H)P(O)–O–P(O)(H)(OH) has 2 P–OH (ionisable) and 2 P–H (non-ionisable): 2.\nKey idea: Ionisable H = P–OH only; P–H hydrogens are not acidic.","traps":["P–H counted as ionisable: Draw the structure; count only O–H on P.","Identity of the product: PCl₃ + H₃PO₃ condenses to H₄P₂O₅."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A xenon compound 'A' upon partial hydrolysis gives XeO₂F₂. The number of lone pair of electrons present in compound A is _______ (Round off to the Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (only Xe lone pair), 18 (only F), 14 (taking A as XeF₄: 2 on Xe + 12 on F).\nWorking: XeF₆ + 2H₂O → XeO₂F₂ + 4HF, so A = XeF₆; lone pairs = 1 on Xe + 6 × 3 on F = 19.\nKey idea: “Lone pairs in compound” includes all terminal F atoms.","traps":["Only central-atom lone pairs: Read “in compound” – include F lone pairs.","A taken as XeF₄: Partial hydrolysis to XeO₂F₂ is a XeF₆ reaction."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The oxide without nitrogen-nitrogen bond is:","options":["N₂O","N₂O₄","N₂O₃","N₂O₅"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) N₂O – N–N–O; (B) N₂O₄ – O₂N–NO₂ (long N–N); (C) N₂O₃ – ON–NO₂.\nWorking: N₂O₅ is O₂N–O–NO₂ with no N–N bond; N₂O, N₂O₃ and N₂O₄ each have an N–N bond.\nKey idea: Only N₂O₅ is bridged through oxygen.","traps":["N₂O₅ drawn with N–N bond: N₂O₅ is O₂N–O–NO₂ (N–O–N bridge); N₂O, N₂O₃, N₂O₄ all contain N–N.","N₂O written N–O–N: Nitrous oxide is linear N=N=O / N≡N⁺–O⁻."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Chlorine reacts with hot and concentrated NaOH and produces compounds (X) and (Y). Compound (X) gives white precipitate with silver nitrate solution. The average bond order between Cl and O atoms in (Y) is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"1.66 to 1.67","range":[1.66,1.67],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (thinks Y = NaOCl, cold dilute product); 1.75 (uses ClO₄⁻); 2 (counts Cl=O double bonds only); 1.5 (ClO₂⁻).\nWorking: 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH (hot, conc.) → 5NaCl (X, white AgCl) + NaClO₃ (Y) + 3H₂O. ClO₃⁻ has 3 Cl–O σ bonds + 2 π bonds spread by resonance over 3 Cl–O bonds: bond order = 5/3 = 1.67.\nKey idea: Hot conc. alkali → chlorate; bond order in ClO₃⁻ = (3 σ + 2 π)/3 = 5/3.","traps":["Hot vs cold alkali product: Hot concentrated → NaClO₃; cold dilute → NaOCl.","Bond order counted per drawn bond: Average total bonds (5) over 3 equivalent Cl–O links."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"In the following reactions products (A) and (B), respectively, are :\nNaOH + Cl₂ → (A) + side products (hot and conc.)\nCa(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → (B) + side products (dry)","options":["NaClO₃ and Ca(OCl)₂","NaOCl and Ca(ClO₃)₂","NaClO₃ and Ca(ClO₃)₂","NaOCl and Ca(OCl)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) give hypochlorite for hot conc. NaOH (cold dilute product); (B)/(C) give chlorate for dry lime.\nWorking: 6NaOH (hot, conc.) + 3Cl₂ → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O; dry slaked lime + Cl₂ gives bleaching powder Ca(OCl)₂ (with CaCl₂).\nKey idea: Hot concentrated alkali → chlorate; cold dilute alkali or dry lime → hypochlorite.","traps":["Hot/cold condition swapped: Tie 'hot conc.' to ClO₃⁻ and 'cold dil./dry lime' to OCl⁻."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The number of bonds between sulphur and oxygen atoms in S₂O₈²⁻ and the number of bonds between sulphur and sulphur atoms in rhombic sulphur, respectively, are :","options":["4 and 8","4 and 6","8 and 8","8 and 6"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) count only 4 S–O (one S) or count σ only per S; (D)/(B) give 6 S–S (confusing with monoclinic/S₆).\nWorking: in peroxodisulphate each S is bonded to four O (3 terminal + 1 bridging peroxo O) → 8 S–O linkages; S₈ crown ring has 8 S–S bonds.\nKey idea: Count bonded atom-pairs: each S in S₂O₈²⁻ has 4 O neighbours; S₈ ring has 8 edges.","traps":["Counting double bonds twice: Count S–O connections (bonded atom pairs), not separate σ and π bonds.","S₈ ring miscounted: Ring of n atoms has n bonds."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"White Phosphorus on reaction with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO₂ gives phosphine and compound (X). (X) on acidification with HCl gives compound (Y). The basicity of compound (Y) is :","options":["4","1","2","3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 3 counts all H in H₃PO₂; (C) 2 is H₃PO₃ (phosphorous acid); (A) 4 has no basis (P₄ atoms).\nWorking: P₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → PH₃ + 3NaH₂PO₂ (disproportionation); NaH₂PO₂ + HCl → H₃PO₂ (hypophosphorous acid), which has one P–OH and two P–H, so basicity 1.\nKey idea: Only H on oxygen is ionisable; H₃PO₂ = H₂P(O)OH, basicity 1.","traps":["All three H counted as acidic: Draw structure; P–H hydrogens are not ionisable.","X taken as phosphite: P₄ + NaOH gives hypophosphite NaH₂PO₂."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The reaction of H₃N₃B₃Cl₃ (A) with LiBH₄ in tetrahydrofuran gives inorganic benzene (B). Further, the reaction of (A) with (C) leads to H₃N₃B₃(Me)₃. Compounds (B) and (C) respectively, are:","options":["Boron nitride and MeBr","Borazine and MeMgBr","Borazine and MeBr","Diborane and MeMgBr"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) boron nitride is \"inorganic graphite\"; (C) MeBr cannot transfer CH₃⁻; (D) diborane is not inorganic benzene.\nWorking: hydride replaces B–Cl to give borazine B₃N₃H₆; a carbanion source (Grignard MeMgBr) replaces B–Cl by B–Me. MeBr is an electrophile and cannot methylate boron.\nKey idea: Inorganic benzene = borazine; B–Cl + MeMgBr → B–Me.","traps":["Methyl bromide chosen as methylating agent: B is electrophilic; it needs a nucleophilic CH₃⁻ source (Grignard).","Inorganic benzene vs inorganic graphite: Borazine = benzene analogue; hexagonal BN = graphite analogue."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"On heating compound (A) gives a gas (B) which is a constituent of air. This gas when treated with H₂ in the presence of a catalyst gives another gas (C) which is basic in nature. (A) should not be:","options":["(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇","Pb(NO₃)₂","NaN₃","NH₄NO₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are all textbook N₂ sources; a student who forgets the azide route may pick (C).\nWorking: C is NH₃, so B is N₂ (Haber process). (NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇, NaN₃ and NH₄NO₂ all give N₂ on heating; Pb(NO₃)₂ gives PbO + NO₂ + O₂ (O₂ + H₂ gives water, not a basic gas).\nKey idea: Basic gas from H₂ + air gas = NH₃ from N₂; only lead nitrate does not give N₂.","traps":["Forgetting azide decomposition gives N₂: Azides decompose to metal + N₂ (air-bag chemistry).","O₂ also a constituent of air: Follow the chain: H₂ + gas must give a basic gas, so B must be N₂."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The shape/structure of [XeF₅]⁻ and XeO₃F₂, respectively, are :","options":["pentagonal planar and trigonal bipyramidal","trigonal bipyramidal and pentagonal planar","octahedral and square pyramidal","trigonal bipyramidal and trigonal bipyramidal"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the two shapes; (C) uses electron count without lone pairs placed correctly; (D) ignores the two lone pairs of [XeF₅]⁻.\nWorking: [XeF₅]⁻ has 5 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs (sp³d³, pentagonal bipyramidal electron geometry, lone pairs axial) → pentagonal planar. XeO₃F₂ has 5 σ bond pairs, no lone pair (sp³d) → trigonal bipyramidal (F axial, O equatorial).\nKey idea: Count steric number: [XeF₅]⁻ = 7 (2 lp axial → pentagonal planar); XeO₃F₂ = 5 (no lp → TBP).","traps":["Lone pairs of [XeF₅]⁻ ignored: Xe 8 e + 5 F + 1 charge → 2 lone pairs.","Shapes swapped between species: Write steric number for each in order."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In a molecule of pyrophosphoric acid, the number of P–OH, P=O and P–O–P bonds/moiety(ies) respectively are:","options":["3, 3 and 3","2, 4 and 1","4, 2 and 0","4, 2 and 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) forgets the bridging oxygen; (B) mixes up OH and P=O counts; (A) random.\nWorking: H₄P₂O₇ = (HO)₂P(=O)–O–P(=O)(OH)₂: 4 P–OH, 2 P=O, 1 P–O–P.\nKey idea: Tetrabasic acid → 4 OH; two P=O; one bridging O.","traps":["Bridging O forgotten: Draw the P–O–P link explicitly."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Aqua regia is used for dissolving noble metals (Au, Pt, etc). The gas evolved in this process is:","options":["N₂","N₂O₃","NO","N₂O₅"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) are acidic oxides not evolved; (A) N₂ is the over-reduction guess.\nWorking: Au + 4H⁺ + NO₃⁻ + 4Cl⁻ → AuCl₄⁻ + NO + 2H₂O.\nKey idea: Nitrate is reduced to NO.","traps":["NO₂ habit from conc. HNO₃: Aqua regia equation gives NO."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"On heating, lead(II) nitrate gives a brown gas (A). The gas (A) on cooling changes to a colourless solid/liquid (B). (B) on heating with NO changes to a blue solid (C). The oxidation number of nitrogen in solid (C) is :","options":["+5","+2","+4","+3"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) +5: N in the nitrate/N₂O₅; (B) +2: N in NO, the reagent added; (C) +4: N in NO₂/N₂O₄ (A or B) – stopping one step early.\nWorking: Pb(NO₃)₂ → PbO + NO₂ (A, brown) + O₂; 2NO₂ ⇌ N₂O₄ (B, colourless) on cooling; N₂O₄ + 2NO → 2N₂O₃ (C, blue solid); N in N₂O₃ is +3.\nKey idea: Blue solid = N₂O₃ (NO + NO₂ combined), N average +3.","traps":["Stopping at N₂O₄ (B) instead of C: Track each step: the last product is N₂O₃.","Taking oxidation state of NO reagent: Combine NO (+2) and NO₂ (+4): average +3."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The structure of PCl₅ in the solid state is","options":["square pyramidal","tetrahedral [PCl₄]⁺ and octahedral [PCl₆]⁻","square planar [PCl₄]⁺ and octahedral [PCl₆]⁻","trigonal bipyramidal"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) not a PCl₅ structure; (C) [PCl₄]⁺ is 4 bp, no lone pair → tetrahedral, not square planar; (D) TBP is gas/liquid phase.\nWorking: solid PCl₅ is ionic [PCl₄]⁺[PCl₆]⁻ – sp³ tetrahedral cation and sp³d² octahedral anion; TBP is the gas/liquid structure.\nKey idea: Solid PCl₅ autoionises to [PCl₄]⁺[PCl₆]⁻.","traps":["Gas-phase TBP structure chosen: Note \"solid state\" in stem.","[PCl₄]⁺ shape misassigned: 4 bond pairs, 0 lone pairs → tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Reaction of ammonia with excess Cl₂ gives:","options":["NH₄Cl and N₂","NCl₃ and NH₄Cl","NH₄Cl and HCl","NCl₃ and HCl"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NH₄Cl and N₂ – products with excess NH₃; (B) and (C) – mixtures not formed (HCl would react with NH₃ only when NH₃ is in excess).\nWorking: with excess Cl₂, NH₃ + 3Cl₂ → NCl₃ + 3HCl; with excess NH₃, 8NH₃ + 3Cl₂ → 6NH₄Cl + N₂.\nKey idea: Excess Cl₂ → NCl₃ + HCl; excess NH₃ → NH₄Cl + N₂.","traps":["Excess condition swapped: Tie the excess reagent to the product: excess Cl₂ chlorinates N fully."],"topics":["Group 17","Group 15"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct statement with respect to dinitrogen is :","options":["liquid dinitrogen is not used in cryosurgery.","it can be used as an inert diluent for reactive chemicals.","it can combine with dioxygen at 25°C","N₂ is paramagnetic in nature."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) negates a true NCERT use; (C) N₂ + O₂ → 2NO needs ~2000 K/electric arc; (D) MO: N₂ has no unpaired electrons.\nWorking: N≡N (very high bond enthalpy) makes N₂ inert at room temperature, so it is used as an inert atmosphere/diluent; liquid N₂ IS used in cryosurgery; N₂ reacts with O₂ only at very high temperature (electric arc); N₂ has all electrons paired (diamagnetic).\nKey idea: High N≡N bond enthalpy → inertness at room temperature → used as inert diluent/atmosphere.","traps":["NCERT use negated: Read for 'not' inserted into a known NCERT line.","Room-temperature N₂ + O₂ reaction assumed: NO forms only at very high temperature."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The number of Cl = O bonds in perchloric acid is, \"_________\"","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (all four O as Cl=O, forgetting the OH), 2 (confusing with chloric acid HClO₃).\nWorking: HClO₄ = HO–ClO₃: one Cl–OH and three Cl=O.\nKey idea: Oxoacid H is on O: HClO₄ = HO–Cl(=O)₃.","traps":["OH oxygen counted as Cl=O: Acidic H sits on one O → one Cl–OH."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Reaction of an inorganic sulphite X with dilute H₂SO₄ generates compound Y. Reaction of Y with NaOH gives X. Further, the reaction of X with Y and water affords compound Z. Y and Z, respectively, are:","options":["S and Na₂SO₃","SO₂ and NaHSO₃","SO₃ and NaHSO₃","SO₂ and Na₂SO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) stops at Na₂SO₃ (that is X, not Z); (C) SO₃ – sulphite gives SO₂ not SO₃ (no change in oxidation state); (A) S – would need reduction.\nWorking: X = Na₂SO₃; Na₂SO₃ + H₂SO₄(dil) → Na₂SO₄ + H₂O + SO₂ (Y); SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O; Na₂SO₃ + SO₂ + H₂O → 2NaHSO₃ (Z).\nKey idea: Sulphite + acid → SO₂ (S stays +4); excess SO₂ with sulphite → bisulphite.","traps":["Z taken as Na₂SO₃: Z forms from X + Y, so it must differ from X: bisulphite.","SO₃ from sulphite: Dilute acid only displaces SO₂; S remains +4."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The reaction of NO with N₂O₄ at 250 K gives:","options":["N₂O₅","NO₂","N₂O","N₂O₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NO₂ – N₂O₄ dissociation product, tempting but not the combined product; (A) N₂O₅ – needs oxidation (+5); (C) N₂O – needs reduction (+1).\nWorking: 2NO + N₂O₄ → 2N₂O₃ at 250 K (oxidation-state averaging: +2 and +4 give +3).\nKey idea: Mixing N(+2) and N(+4) oxides at low temperature gives N(+3) oxide.","traps":["NO₂ chosen from N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂: Average the oxidation states of reactants: (+2 + +4)/2 = +3."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Diborane (B₂H₆) reacts independently with O₂ and H₂O to produce, respectively","options":["HBO₂ and H₃BO₃","H₃BO₃ and B₂O₃","B₂O₃ and H₃BO₃","B₂O₃ and [BH₄]⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HBO₂ is metaboric acid formed on heating H₃BO₃; (B) order reversed; (D) BH₄⁻ forms with hydride donors, not water.\nWorking: B₂H₆ + 3O₂ → B₂O₃ + 3H₂O; B₂H₆ + 6H₂O → 2B(OH)₃ + 6H₂.\nKey idea: O₂ → oxide B₂O₃; H₂O → boric acid + H₂.","traps":["Order of products reversed: Match 'respectively' carefully."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"C₆₀, an allotrope of carbon contains :","options":["20 hexagons and 12 pentagons.","12 hexagons and 20 pentagons.","18 hexagons and 14 pentagons.","16 hexagons and 16 pentagons."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the two counts; (C)/(D) arbitrary totals of 32 faces.\nWorking: buckminsterfullerene has 20 six-membered and 12 five-membered rings (Euler: always 12 pentagons for a closed fullerene).\nKey idea: Fullerene always has 12 pentagons; C₆₀ has 20 hexagons.","traps":["Hexagon and pentagon counts swapped: Recall: 12 pentagons in every closed fullerene."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The amorphous form of silica is :","options":["quartz","kieselguhr","cristobalite","tridymite"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) quartz, (C) cristobalite, (D) tridymite are the three crystalline polymorphs listed alongside.\nWorking: quartz, cristobalite and tridymite are crystalline forms of SiO₂; kieselguhr is amorphous.\nKey idea: Kieselguhr (diatomaceous earth) is the amorphous form.","traps":["Unfamiliar polymorph names: Learn the three crystalline forms as a set."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The correct statements among I to III regarding group 13 element oxides are, (I) Boron trioxide is acidic. (II) Oxides of aluminium and gallium are amphoteric. (III) Oxides of indium and thallium are basic.","options":["(I), (II) and (III)","(II) and (III) only","(I) and (III) only","(I) and (II) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) doubts B₂O₃ being acidic; (C) doubts Ga₂O₃ amphoteric; (D) doubts Tl/In oxides basic.\nWorking: B₂O₃ acidic, Al₂O₃ and Ga₂O₃ amphoteric, In₂O₃ and Tl₂O₃ basic — all three true.\nKey idea: Metallic (basic) character increases down the group; Al and Ga sit in the amphoteric middle.","traps":["Gallium oxide nature: Al and Ga oxides are both amphoteric."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct order of catenation is :","options":["C > Si > Ge ≈ Sn","C > Sn > Si ≈ Ge","Ge > Sn > Si > C","Si > Sn > C > Ge"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts Sn above Si (ignores bond-strength trend); (C) and (D) reverse the trend, possibly confusing with metallic character.\nWorking: catenation falls down group 14 as E–E bond enthalpy falls (C–C ≫ Si–Si > Ge–Ge ≈ Sn–Sn); old NCERT gives C ≫ Si > Ge ≈ Sn.\nKey idea: Catenation follows E–E bond strength: C ≫ Si > Ge ≈ Sn.","traps":["Down-group trend reversed: Tie catenation to E–E bond enthalpy."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 14"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 16","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The oxoacid of sulphur that does not contain bond between sulphur atoms is :","options":["H₂S₄O₆","H₂S₂O₇","H₂S₂O₃","H₂S₂O₄"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tetrathionic acid has an S–S–S–S chain; (C) thiosulphuric acid has a terminal S bonded to S; (D) dithionous acid has a direct S–S bond – all chosen if formulas with two S are assumed to be bridged by O.\nWorking: H₂S₂O₇ (pyrosulphuric acid/oleum) has HO₃S–O–SO₃H with an S–O–S bridge; H₂S₄O₆ (tetrathionic, S–S–S–S chain), H₂S₂O₃ (thiosulphuric, S=S) and H₂S₂O₄ (dithionous, S–S) all have S–S bonds.\nKey idea: Pyro acids (H₂S₂O₇) are linked through O; thio/thionic/dithionous acids have S–S.","traps":["Assuming every S₂ acid has an S–O–S bridge: Draw each: dithionous acid is HO–S(O)–S(O)–OH.","Thiosulphate S treated as bridging: Thio S replaces a terminal =O on S."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The number of pentagons in C₆₀ and trigons (triangles) in white phosphorus, respectively, are:","options":["12 and 3","20 and 4","12 and 4","20 and 3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) use 20 (hexagon count); (A)/(D) use 3 (bonds per P or edges per face) instead of 4 faces.\nWorking: C₆₀ has 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons; P₄ is a tetrahedron with 4 triangular faces.\nKey idea: 12 pentagons, 4 triangular faces.","traps":["Hexagons vs pentagons in C₆₀: Pentagons are 12, hexagons are 20."],"topics":["Group 14","Group 15"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The correct statement among the following is","options":["(SiH₃)₃N is pyramidal and more basic than (CH₃)₃N","(SiH₃)₃N is planar and more basic than (CH₃)₃N","(SiH₃)₃N is pyramidal and less basic than (CH₃)₃N","(SiH₃)₃N is planar and less basic than (CH₃)₃N"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats SiH₃ like an electron-releasing alkyl; (B) correct geometry but assumes more basic; (C) correct basicity but keeps pyramidal shape.\nWorking: in trisilylamine the N lone pair is delocalised into vacant Si orbitals (pπ–dπ / N→Si σ* back-donation), so N is sp²-like and planar and the lone pair is far less available: weaker base than pyramidal (CH₃)₃N.\nKey idea: Delocalisation that flattens N also removes its lone pair from basic use – geometry and basicity go together.","traps":["SiH₃ treated as an alkyl donor: Back-bonding into Si outweighs the inductive effect.","Geometry and basicity judged separately: One cause (lone-pair delocalisation) fixes both planarity and low basicity."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The basic structural unit of feldspar, zeolites, mica, and asbestos is :","options":["(SiO₃)²⁻","SiO₂","(SiO₄)⁴⁻","–(SiR₂–O)ₙ– (R = Me)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SiO₃²⁻ is the repeat unit of chain/cyclic silicates; (B) silica; (D) silicone.\nWorking: all silicates are built from SiO₄⁴⁻ tetrahedra linked by shared corners.\nKey idea: All silicates = SiO₄⁴⁻ tetrahedra.","traps":["Repeat unit vs basic unit: Basic unit is always the tetrahedron."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The C–C bond length is maximum in","options":["graphite","C₇₀","diamond","C₆₀"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) graphite has bond order ~1.33; (B)/(D) fullerenes contain C=C and C–C.\nWorking: diamond has only sp³ C–C single bonds (154 pm); graphite (141.5 pm) and fullerenes (138/145 pm) have partial double-bond character.\nKey idea: Pure single bonds (sp³) are longest.","traps":["Larger fullerene assumed to have longer bonds: Bond length depends on bond order, not molecule size."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Correct statements among a to d regarding silicones are :\n(a) They are polymers with hydrophobic character\n(b) They are biocompatible.\n(c) In general, they have high thermal stability and low dielectric strength.\n(d) Usually, they are resistant to oxidation and used as greases.","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), and (b) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a), (b) and (d) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): accept (c). (B): reject (d) as unfamiliar.\nWorking: (a), (b), (d) true; (c) false because silicones have high dielectric strength (good insulators) though thermally stable.\nKey idea: Silicones: hydrophobic, thermally stable, good insulators, inert, biocompatible.","traps":["Statement (c) accepted: Check every clause: silicones are good electrical insulators (high dielectric strength).","Grease use doubted: NCERT: used as sealants, greases, electrical insulators."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Aluminium is usually found in +3 oxidation state. In contrast, thallium exists in +1 and +3 oxidation states. This is due to :","options":["lanthanoid contraction","lattice effect","diagonal relationship","inert pair effect"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): lanthanoid contraction, related but not the reason. (B)/(C): unrelated concepts.\nWorking: poor shielding by 4f/5d electrons holds the 6s² pair of Tl, making +1 stable (inert pair effect).\nKey idea: Heavy p-block → lower oxidation state by 2 is stabilised.","traps":["Lanthanoid contraction chosen: Stable lower oxidation state down group 13 = inert pair effect."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 13"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Good reducing nature of H₃PO₂ is attributed to the presence of:","options":["One P–OH bond","One P–H bond","Two P–H bonds","Two P–OH bonds"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) One P–OH: true count but P–OH gives acidity, not reducing power. (B) One P–H: that is H₃PO₃. (D) Two P–OH: H₃PO₃ count, again acidity-related.\nWorking: H₃PO₂ is H–P(=O)(H)–OH; its two P–H bonds make it (and its salts) strong reducing agents.\nKey idea: P–H bonds = reducing power; P–OH bonds = basicity. H₃PO₂: 2 P–H, 1 P–OH.","traps":["H₃PO₂ vs H₃PO₃ P–H count: Write structures: H₃PO₂ monobasic so 2 H on P; H₃PO₃ dibasic so 1 H on P.","Acidic H mistaken as reducing site: Reducing power comes from H attached directly to P."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 18","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The type of hybridisation and number of lone pair(s) of electrons of Xe in XeOF₄, respectively, are :","options":["sp³d and 1","sp³d and 2","sp³d² and 1","sp³d² and 2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) sp³d: counts the Xe=O as two domains wrongly or forgets one. (D) 2 lone pairs: treats O as not bonded or uses XeF₄ count.\nWorking: Xe (8 valence e⁻) in XeOF₄: 5 σ bonds + 1 lone pair = 6 electron domains → sp³d², square pyramidal.\nKey idea: Double bond counts as one domain: 5 σ + 1 lp = 6 → sp³d², 1 lone pair.","traps":["Carrying over XeF₄ lone pairs: Oxygen uses two Xe electrons; recount: 8 − 4 − 2 = 2 e⁻ → 1 lp."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 13","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The number of 2-centre-2-electron and 3-centre-2-electron bonds in B₂H₆, respectively, are :","options":["2 and 4","2 and 1","2 and 2","4 and 2"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2 and 4: swaps the counts. (B) 2 and 1 / (C) 2 and 2: count only one terminal B–H per B or think of a single bridge.\nWorking: diborane has 4 terminal B–H (2c–2e) bonds and 2 bridging B–H–B (3c–2e, banana) bonds.\nKey idea: Each B has two terminal H; the two bridging H each make one 3c–2e bond.","traps":["Order of the two counts reversed: Read the order: terminal (2c–2e) first, bridging (3c–2e) second."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 15","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The pair that contains two P–H bonds in each of the oxoacids is :","options":["H₃PO₂ and H₄P₂O₅","H₄P₂O₅ and H₄P₂O₆","H₃PO₃ and H₃PO₂","H₄P₂O₅ and H₃PO₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) H₄P₂O₆ has no P–H (P–P bond). (C) H₃PO₃ has only one P–H. (D) H₃PO₃ again only one P–H.\nWorking: H₃PO₂ (hypophosphorous) has 2 P–H; H₄P₂O₅ (pyrophosphorous/diphosphonic) has one P–H on each P = 2; H₃PO₃ has 1; H₄P₂O₆ has 0.\nKey idea: Each P keeps its H count separately: H₃PO₂ = 1 P–OH + 2 P–H; H₄P₂O₅ = 2 P–OH + one P–H on each P; H₃PO₃ = 2 P–OH + 1 P–H; H₄P₂O₆ = 4 P–OH, no P–H.","traps":["Total H mistaken for P–OH: Draw each structure: count H on P separately.","Hypophosphoric acid thought to have P–H: H₄P₂O₆ has P–P bond and four P–OH, no P–H."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 14","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The chloride that CANNOT get hydrolysed is:","options":["SiCl₄","SnCl₄","PbCl₄","CCl₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) PbCl₄ – unstable (inert pair), but it does hydrolyse. (A)/(B) readily hydrolysed.\nWorking: carbon has no vacant d orbitals (and is sterically shielded), so CCl₄ cannot accept a lone pair from water; SiCl₄, SnCl₄, PbCl₄ hydrolyse.\nKey idea: Hydrolysis needs a vacant orbital on the central atom.","traps":["PbCl₄ instability: Instability ≠ resistance to hydrolysis.","'CANNOT' wording: Mark the inert one."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The relative stability of +1 oxidation state of group 13 elements follows the order :-","options":["Al < Ga < Tl < In","Tl < In < Ga < Al","Al < Ga < In < Tl","Ga < Al < In < Tl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Tl and In. (B) reverses the order. (D) swaps Al and Ga.\nWorking: inert pair effect increases down Group 13, so +1 becomes more stable: Al < Ga < In < Tl.\nKey idea: Lower oxidation state gets more stable down the group (inert pair effect).","traps":["Order reversed: +1 stability rises down the group.","Al/Ga anomaly carried over: No anomaly in the +1 stability order."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 13"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Iodine reacts with concentrated HNO₃ to yield Y along with other products. The oxidation state of iodine in Y is :","options":["5","3","1","7"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 3: HIO₂-type acid, not formed. (C) 1: HOI, formed with water/alkali not conc. HNO₃. (D) 7: periodic acid, needs stronger oxidant; conc. HNO₃ stops at +5.\nWorking: I₂ + 10HNO₃(conc) → 2HIO₃ + 10NO₂ + 4H₂O; Y = iodic acid HIO₃, I is +5.\nKey idea: Conc. HNO₃ oxidises I₂ to HIO₃ (iodine +5).","traps":["Assuming highest oxidation state +7: Remember conc. HNO₃ gives iodic acid HIO₃, not HIO₄."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Inert pair effect & catenation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The element that does NOT show catenation is:","options":["Sn","Ge","Si","Pb"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Sn shows weak catenation (Ge ≈ Sn); (B) Ge and (C) Si show catenation decreasingly.\nWorking: catenation falls C ≫ Si > Ge ≈ Sn; lead does not show catenation (weak Pb–Pb bond).\nKey idea: Catenation ∝ M–M bond strength, which falls down the group; Pb does not catenate.","traps":["Tin assumed non-catenating: NCERT order puts Sn ≈ Ge; only Pb is excluded."],"topics":["Inert pair effect & catenation","Group 14"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 17","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Chlorine on reaction with hot and concentrated sodium hydroxide gives :","options":["Cl⁻ and ClO₂⁻","Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻","Cl⁻ and ClO⁻","ClO₃⁻ and ClO₂⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Cl⁻ and ClO⁻: cold dilute alkali product. (A) chlorite – not formed directly. (D) no reduced product – impossible for disproportionation.\nWorking: 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH (hot, conc.) → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O (disproportionation to Cl⁻ and ClO₃⁻).\nKey idea: Hot conc. → chlorate; cold dil. → hypochlorite.","traps":["Cold-dilute product chosen: Hot + concentrated → ClO₃⁻."]}],"m20-prac":[{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"Identify A, B and C in the given below reaction sequence\n[A] —[HNO₃]→ Pb(NO₃)₂ —[H₂SO₄]→ [B] —[(1) Ammonium acetate, (2) Acetic acid, (3) K₂CrO₄]→ [C] (Yellow ppt)","options":["PbCl₂, PbSO₄, PbCrO₄","PbCl₂, Pb(SO₄)₂, PbCrO₄","PbS, PbSO₄, Pb(CH₃COO)₂","PbS, PbSO₄, PbCrO₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) PbCl₂ start: wrong group route. (B) Pb(SO₄)₂ is a wrong formula. (C) Lead acetate is not a yellow precipitate.\nWorking: PbS (group II sulphide) dissolves in hot dil. HNO₃ to give Pb(NO₃)₂; dil. H₂SO₄ then precipitates white PbSO₄ (compound B); PbSO₄ dissolves in ammonium acetate, and after acidifying with acetic acid, K₂CrO₄ gives yellow PbCrO₄ (compound C).\nKey idea: Lead reaches Pb(NO₃)₂ from HNO₃ only via the group II sulphide, and the yellow product is always PbCrO₄.","traps":["Group I chloride chosen as starting lead compound: HNO₃ dissolving the precipitate to Pb(NO₃)₂ is the group II step for sulphides; PbCl₂ is dissolved in hot water, not HNO₃.","Wrong lead sulphate formula: Pb²⁺ with SO₄²⁻ gives PbSO₄."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"In the precipitation of the iron group (III) in qualitative analysis, ammonium chloride is added before adding ammonium hydroxide to :","options":["decrease concentration of ⁻OH ions","increase concentration of Cl⁻ ions","prevent interference by phosphate ions","increase concentration of NH₄⁺ ions"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): true as a step but not the purpose. (B): Cl⁻ is irrelevant. (C): phosphate interference is a separate issue.\nWorking: NH₄⁺ (common ion) suppresses NH₄OH ionisation, lowering [OH⁻] so only Group III hydroxides (low Ksp) precipitate, not Group IV/V/Mg(OH)₂.\nKey idea: The aim is to lower [OH⁻] so ionic product exceeds Ksp only for Fe(OH)₃, Al(OH)₃, Cr(OH)₃.","traps":["Mechanism step chosen over purpose: Ask why: the effect of extra NH₄⁺ is to lower [OH⁻]."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"When MnO₂ and H₂SO₄ are added to a salt (A), a greenish yellow gas is liberated. The salt (A) is:","options":["NH₄Cl","CaI₂","KNO₃","NaBr"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) NaBr also gives a halogen but reddish-brown Br₂. (B) CaI₂ gives violet I₂. (C) KNO₃ gives brown NO₂ only with Cu, not greenish yellow.\nWorking: chloride + MnO₂ + conc. H₂SO₄ gives Cl₂ (greenish yellow). Bromide gives reddish-brown Br₂, iodide violet I₂ vapour; nitrate gives no coloured halogen.\nKey idea: Greenish-yellow gas = Cl₂ → chloride.","traps":["Any halide accepted: Match colour: Cl₂ greenish yellow, Br₂ red-brown, I₂ violet."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"In a borax bead test under hot condition, a metal salt (one from the given) is heated at point B of the flame (shown below), resulted in green colour salt bead. The spin-only magnetic moment value of the salt is ____ BM (Nearest integer) [Given atomic number of Cu = 29, Ni = 28, Mn = 25, Fe = 26]","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (Cu²⁺, if B read as oxidising zone – Cu hot oxidising bead is green); 5 (Fe²⁺ d⁶); 3 (Ni²⁺ d⁸ = 2.83); 6 also = Mn²⁺ d⁵ (but Mn bead in reducing flame is colourless).\nWorking: B lies in the inner reducing zone; a green bead in the reducing flame (hot) points to iron (Fe²⁺, bottle green). The answer takes the salt as an Fe(III) salt, d⁵, 5 unpaired → √35 = 5.92 ≈ 6 BM. If the salt were Fe(II) the value would be 4.90 ≈ 5, so the item relies on the iron salt being ferric; Cu (green in oxidising flame) would give 1.73 ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Point B = reducing flame; green there = Fe; ferric salt d⁵ → 5.92 ≈ 6.","traps":["Flame zone misread: Inner zone near the burner = reducing; outer tip = oxidising.","Fe(II) vs Fe(III) salt: The answer counts the original (ferric) salt: d⁵."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr05S1C23-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Mohr's salt","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"While preparing crystals of Mohr's salt, dil H₂SO₄ is added to a mixture of ferrous sulphate and ammonium sulphate, before dissolving this mixture in water, dil H₂SO₄ is added here to:","options":["prevent the hydrolysis of ferrous sulphate","prevent the hydrolysis of ammonium sulphate","increase the rate of formation of crystals","make the medium strongly acidic"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ammonium sulphate hydrolysis is negligible here. (D) 'strongly acidic' overstates the small dilute addition. (C) acid does not speed crystal growth.\nWorking: Fe²⁺ hydrolyses to basic salts/Fe(OH)₂ (and oxidises); a little dilute acid suppresses this.\nKey idea: The weak-base cation Fe²⁺ is the one that hydrolyses.","traps":["Which salt hydrolyses: Fe²⁺ hydrolysis is much stronger and also aids oxidation."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Precipitating reagent and conditions): A. NH₄Cl + NH₄OH B. NH₄OH + Na₂CO₃ C. NH₄OH + NH₄Cl + H₂S gas D. dilute HCl\nList II (Cation): I. Mn²⁺ II. Pb²⁺ III. Al³⁺ IV. Sr²⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) swap Mn²⁺ and Pb²⁺ (both form sulphides; Pb also in group II with H₂S in acid). (C)/(D) swap Al³⁺ and Sr²⁺ (hydroxide vs carbonate).\nWorking: group III (NH₄Cl/NH₄OH) – Al³⁺; group V (carbonate in NH₄OH) – Sr²⁺; group IV (H₂S in ammoniacal medium) – Mn²⁺; group I (dil. HCl) – Pb²⁺. (The standard Group V reagent is (NH₄)₂CO₃; the question writes Na₂CO₃.)\nKey idea: Dil. HCl → Pb²⁺ (group I); NH₄Cl/NH₄OH → hydroxides (Al³⁺); H₂S in NH₄OH → MnS; carbonate → Sr²⁺.","traps":["Pb²⁺ placed in H₂S group: Pb²⁺ is first precipitated as PbCl₂ by dil. HCl.","Na₂CO₃ instead of (NH₄)₂CO₃: Recognise carbonate = group V (Ba, Sr, Ca)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"During the detection of acidic radical present in a salt, a student gets a pale yellow precipitate soluble with difficulty in NH₄OH solution when sodium carbonate extract was first acidified with dil. HNO₃ and then AgNO₃ solution was added. This indicates presence of :","options":["I⁻","CO₃²⁻","Cl⁻","Br⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) I⁻: yellow ppt, but AgI is insoluble in NH₄OH. (B) CO₃²⁻: destroyed by HNO₃ acidification. (C) Cl⁻: AgCl is white and readily soluble.\nWorking: AgBr is pale yellow and only sparingly soluble in NH₄OH; AgCl is white and readily soluble, AgI is yellow and insoluble; carbonate is removed on acidification with HNO₃.\nKey idea: Pale yellow + partly soluble in NH₄OH = AgBr.","traps":["Pale yellow vs yellow confused: Use NH₄OH solubility: AgBr slightly soluble, AgI insoluble.","Carbonate kept as possible: Acidifying with dil. HNO₃ expels CO₂ first."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Consider the following reactions\nNiS + HNO₃ + HCl → A + NO + S + H₂O\nA + NH₄OH + 2 H₃C–C(=N–OH)–C(=N–OH)–CH₃ (dimethylglyoxime) → B + NH₄Cl + H₂O\nThe number of protons that do not involve in hydrogen bonding in the product B is ______.","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: 14 (all H counted), 16 (complex with two neutral dmgH₂ ligands), 10 or 6 (methyls miscounted).\nWorking: A = NiCl₂; B = bis(dimethylglyoximato)nickel(II), [Ni(dmgH)₂]. It has 4 CH₃ (12 H) and 2 O–H…O protons in intramolecular H-bonds; protons not in H-bonding = 12.\nKey idea: Each dmgH⁻ loses one OH proton; the two remaining OH protons form O–H…O bridges.","traps":["All 14 protons counted: Exclude the 2 bridging O–H protons.","dmg taken as neutral ligand: Ni²⁺ bonded to two dmgH⁻ (mono-anions)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Name of the test): A. Borax bead test B. Charcoal cavity test C. Cobalt nitrate test D. Flame test\nLIST II (Reaction sequence involved) [M is metal]:\nI. MCO₃ → MO →(Co(NO₃)₂, +Δ) CoO·MO\nII. MCO₃ → MCl₂ → M²⁺\nIII. MSO₄ →(Na₂B₄O₇, Δ) M(BO₂)₂ → MBO₂ → M\nIV. MSO₄ →(Na₂CO₃, Δ) MCO₃ → MO → M\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) swap charcoal cavity and cobalt nitrate sequences (both use MO); (C) swaps flame test and cobalt nitrate.\nWorking: borax bead forms metaborates (III); charcoal cavity with Na₂CO₃ gives carbonate → oxide → metal (IV); cobalt nitrate gives CoO·MO coloured mass (I); flame test uses chloride paste (II).\nKey idea: Only the cobalt nitrate test uses Co(NO₃)₂; only flame test needs volatile chloride.","traps":["Charcoal cavity vs cobalt nitrate: Look for the named reagent in each sequence."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Test): A. Baeyer's test B. Ceric ammonium nitrate test C. Phthalein dye test D. Schiff's test\nLIST II (Identification): I. Phenol II. Aldehyde III. Alcoholic-OH group IV. Unsaturation\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): CAN to phenol (CAN also responds to phenols with a brown colour). (B)/(C): Baeyer swapped.\nWorking: Baeyer → unsaturation; CAN → alcoholic OH; phthalein dye (with phthalic anhydride) → phenol; Schiff → aldehyde.\nKey idea: Phthalein = phenol; CAN = alcohol (red colour).","traps":["CAN vs phthalein test: Phthalein dye test is specific to phenols."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Identify the incorrect statements regarding primary standard of titrimetric analysis.\n(A) It should be purely available in dry form.\n(B) It should not undergo chemical change in air.\n(C) It should be hygroscopic and should react with another chemical instantaneously and stoichiometrically.\n(D) It should be readily soluble in water.\n(E) KMnO₄ & NaOH can be used as primary standard.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(C) and (E) only","(A) and (B) only","(B) and (E) only","(C) and (D) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) picks correct statements (reverses NOT). (C) includes (B), a true property. (D) includes solubility, a true property.\nWorking: a primary standard must be non-hygroscopic (C false); KMnO₄ and NaOH are secondary standards (E false).\nKey idea: NaOH absorbs moisture/CO₂ and KMnO₄ is not obtained pure and decomposes, so both need standardisation.","traps":["Selecting correct instead of incorrect statements: Mark each true/false, then pick the false ones.","KMnO₄ and NaOH as primary standards: They are standardised against oxalic acid / Mohr's salt."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Test): A. Br₂ water test B. Ceric ammonium nitrate test C. Ferric chloride test D. 2,4-DNP test\nLIST II (Observation): I. Yellow orange or orange red precipitate formed II. Reddish orange colour disappears III. Red colour appears IV. Blue, Green, Violet or Red colour appears\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) shifts every observation by one place (e.g. CAN is given FeCl₃'s observation). (C) gives bromine water the precipitate. (D) mixes all four.\nWorking: bromine water decolourises (unsaturation); CAN gives red colour with alcohols; neutral FeCl₃ gives blue/green/violet/red with phenols; 2,4-DNP gives yellow-orange-red precipitate with carbonyls.\nKey idea: Precipitate only for 2,4-DNP; colour loss only for Br₂ water.","traps":["CAN red vs FeCl₃ violet: CAN → red (alcohol); FeCl₃ → violet range (phenol)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Consider the following test for a group-IV cation.\nM²⁺ + H₂S → A (Black precipitate) + byproduct\nA + aqua regia → B + NOCl + S + H₂O\nB + KNO₂ + CH₃COOH → C + byproduct\nThe spin-only magnetic moment value of the metal complex C is ______ BM (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Choosing Ni gives 3 (paramagnetic Ni²⁺, 2 unpaired); keeping Co as Co²⁺ gives 4 (3 unpaired, √15) or 2 for low-spin d⁷ (1 unpaired, 1.73).\nWorking: black CoS dissolves in aqua regia to CoCl₂; KNO₂/acetic acid gives yellow K₃[Co(NO₂)₆], low-spin Co³⁺ d⁶ (t₂g⁶) → μ = 0.\nKey idea: NO₂⁻ oxidises Co²⁺ to Co³⁺ and is a strong-field ligand → d⁶ low spin, diamagnetic.","traps":["Co(II) vs Co(III): KNO₂ in acid oxidises Co²⁺ to Co³⁺.","Ni vs Co in group IV: KNO₂/CH₃COOH is the Co test; Ni uses dimethylglyoxime."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Potassium hydrogen phthalate is a primary standard for standardisation of sodium hydroxide solution.\nStatement (II) : In this titration phenolphthalein can be used as indicator.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): thinks methyl orange is needed. (B)/(C): does not know KHP as a primary standard (often only oxalic acid is taught).\nWorking: KHP is a classic non-hygroscopic primary standard for NaOH; the weak acid–strong base end point is basic (pH ≈ 8–9), so phenolphthalein suits it.\nKey idea: KHP + NaOH is weak acid vs strong base → phenolphthalein.","traps":["KHP unfamiliar as primary standard: Primary standards: pure, stable, non-hygroscopic, high molar mass – KHP fits.","Indicator choice by end-point pH: Weak acid–strong base → basic end point → phenolphthalein."],"topics":["Acid–base titration","Indicators"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of white coloured salts, among the following is\n(a) SrSO₄\n(b) Mg(NH₄)PO₄\n(c) BaCrO₄\n(d) Mn(OH)₂\n(e) PbSO₄\n(f) PbCrO₄\n(g) AgBr\n(h) PbI₂\n(i) CaC₂O₄\n(j) [Fe(OH)₂(CH₃COO)]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong counts: 4 (Mn(OH)₂ excluded as it turns brown), 6 (AgBr counted as white), 3.\nWorking: White – SrSO₄, Mg(NH₄)PO₄, Mn(OH)₂ (white when fresh, browns in air), PbSO₄, CaC₂O₄ = 5. Coloured – BaCrO₄ (yellow), PbCrO₄ (yellow), AgBr (pale yellow), PbI₂ (yellow), basic ferric acetate (brown-red).\nKey idea: Chromates, iodides and Fe(III) basic acetate are coloured; sulphates/oxalate/phosphate of s-block and Pb are white.","traps":["Mn(OH)₂ colour: Freshly precipitated Mn(OH)₂ is white.","AgBr pale yellow vs white: Only AgCl among silver halides is white."],"topics":["Cation analysis","Anion analysis"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Dimethyl glyoxime forms a six-membered covalent chelate when treated with NiCl₂ solution in presence of NH₄OH.\nStatement (II) : Prussian blue precipitate contains iron both in (+2) and (+3) oxidation states.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) accept 'six-membered' (confusing with the H-bonded ring). (D) rejects II by thinking Prussian blue has only Fe(III) or Fe(II).\nWorking: Statement I is false and Statement II is true (Ni(dmg)₂ has five-membered Ni–N=C–C=N chelate rings; the six-membered rings are O–H···O hydrogen-bonded rings, not chelate rings. Prussian blue Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ has Fe(III) and Fe(II))\nKey idea: Each dmg binds Ni through two N atoms: ring Ni–N–C–C–N = 5 members.","traps":["Chelate ring vs H-bonded ring: Count atoms in the ring containing Ni and both donor N.","Mixed valence of Prussian blue: Outer Fe³⁺, inner [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ (Fe²⁺)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Yellow compound of lead chromate gets dissolved on treatment with hot NaOH solution. The product of lead formed is a :","options":["Tetraanionic complex with coordination number six","Neutral complex with coordination number four","Dianionic complex with coordination number six","Dianionic complex with coordination number four"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes [Pb(OH)₆]⁴⁻ (Pb(IV)-like CN 6). (B) assumes neutral Pb(OH)₂ as a \"complex\". (C) assumes [Pb(OH)₆]²⁻ (would need Pb(IV)).\nWorking: PbCrO₄ + 4OH⁻ → [Pb(OH)₄]²⁻ + CrO₄²⁻; tetrahydroxidoplumbate(II) is a dianion with CN 4.\nKey idea: Pb(II) amphoteric: dissolves as [Pb(OH)₄]²⁻ (plumbite).","traps":["CN 6 assumed for all hydroxido complexes: Charge balance: Pb²⁺ + 4OH⁻ gives 2– with CN 4; 6OH⁻ would give 4–."],"topics":["Cation analysis","Anion analysis"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"NaCl reacts with conc. H₂SO₄ and K₂Cr₂O₇ to give reddish fumes (B), which react with NaOH to give yellow solution (C). (B) and (C) respectively are ;","options":["CrO₂Cl₂, Na₂CrO₄","Na₂CrO₄, CrO₂Cl₂","CrO₂Cl₂, KHSO₄","CrO₂Cl₂, Na₂Cr₂O₇"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the order. (C) KHSO₄ is a by-product, colourless. (D) Na₂Cr₂O₇ is orange and forms in acid, not in NaOH.\nWorking: chromyl chloride test — 4NaCl + K₂Cr₂O₇ + 6H₂SO₄ → 2CrO₂Cl₂ (red-orange fumes) + …; CrO₂Cl₂ + 4NaOH → Na₂CrO₄ (yellow) + 2NaCl + 2H₂O.\nKey idea: In alkali, Cr(VI) is yellow chromate; in acid, orange dichromate.","traps":["Dichromate chosen for yellow solution: NaOH (alkaline) gives CrO₄²⁻ (yellow); dichromate is orange."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Phenolic group can be identified by a positive:","options":["Tollen's test","Phthalein dye test","Carbylamine test","Lucas test"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Tollens' - aldehydes. (C) carbylamine - primary amines. (D) Lucas - classifies alcohols.\nWorking: phenols condense with phthalic anhydride (conc. H₂SO₄) to give a phthalein dye (pink/red in alkali); the other tests are for aldehydes, primary amines and alcohols.\nKey idea: Phthalein dye test (and neutral FeCl₃) identify phenols.","traps":["Phenol mistaken for alcohol test: Lucas distinguishes 1°/2°/3° alcohols, not phenols."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"On passing a gas, 'X', through Nessler's reagent, a brown precipitate is obtained. The gas 'X' is","options":["Cl₂","CO₂","NH₃","H₂S"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cl₂: chosen by students who link any coloured result to a halogen. (B) CO₂: confused with the lime-water test. (D) H₂S: confused with lead-acetate/Group II sulphide precipitates (black, not brown).\nWorking: Nessler's reagent (alkaline K₂HgI₄) gives a brown precipitate (iodide of Millon's base) only with ammonia/NH₄⁺.\nKey idea: Brown precipitate with Nessler's reagent is specific to ammonia.","traps":["Brown/black precipitate tests mixed up: Remember the pair: Nessler's reagent ↔ NH₃ (brown); lead acetate paper ↔ H₂S (black)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"A reagent which gives brilliant red precipitate with Nickel ions in basic medium is","options":["dimethyl glyoxime","sodium nitroprusside","meta-dinitrobenzene","neutral FeCl₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) nitroprusside: test for sulphide (violet). (C) m-dinitrobenzene: Zimmermann-type tests, not metal ions. (D) neutral FeCl₃: phenol test (violet).\nWorking: Ni²⁺ + 2 dmgH in ammoniacal medium gives rosy-red Ni(dmgH)₂.\nKey idea: DMG → red Ni chelate.","traps":["Colour tests mixed: Pair reagents with their target: DMG–Ni²⁺, nitroprusside–S²⁻, FeCl₃–phenol."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The oxidation number of iron in the compound formed during brown ring test for NO₃⁻ ion is ________.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 2 (Fe²⁺ from FeSO₄, NO treated as neutral), 3 (Fe³⁺ thought formed by oxidation).\nWorking: brown ring complex [Fe(H₂O)₅(NO)]²⁺ contains NO⁺, so Fe is +1 (x + 1 = +2).\nKey idea: Charge transfer Fe²⁺ + NO → Fe⁺–NO⁺ makes Fe +1.","traps":["NO taken as neutral: In the brown ring complex NO binds as NO⁺; Fe is +1."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : The gas liberated on warming a salt with dil H₂SO₄, turns a piece of paper dipped in lead acetate into black, it is a confirmatory test for sulphide ion.\nStatement (II) : In statement-I the colour of paper turns black because of formation of lead sulphite.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement II error: PbS is lead sulphide; \"lead sulphite\" (PbSO₃) is white. (C) chosen by those who miss the -ite. (A)/(D) chosen by those who think lead acetate test is only preliminary.\nWorking: S²⁻ + dil H₂SO₄ → H₂S, which blackens lead acetate paper as PbS (lead sulphide, not sulphite), so II is false. (Strictly, the lab manual treats the lead acetate test as the dilute-acid group test and sodium nitroprusside as confirmatory, but the official answer treats I as true.)\nKey idea: H₂S + Pb(CH₃COO)₂ → PbS (black) + 2CH₃COOH.","traps":["Sulphite read as sulphide: Black product = PbS, lead sulphide.","Preliminary vs confirmatory test: Follow the official answer: lead acetate blackening is accepted as identification of S²⁻."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Number of metal ions characterized by flame test among the following is ________.\nSr²⁺, Ba²⁺, Ca²⁺, Cu²⁺, Zn²⁺, Co²⁺, Fe²⁺","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (only Group V alkaline earth ions, forgetting Cu²⁺), 5 (including Co²⁺ because of the blue cobalt-glass/borax bead association).\nWorking: Sr²⁺ (crimson red), Ba²⁺ (apple/grassy green), Ca²⁺ (brick red) and Cu²⁺ (green/bluish-green) give characteristic flame colours; Zn²⁺, Co²⁺, Fe²⁺ are not identified by flame test.\nKey idea: Alkaline earth Ca/Sr/Ba plus Cu give flame colours; Co is identified by borax bead, not flame.","traps":["Cu²⁺ forgotten: Copper gives a green flame (also Cu halide blue-green).","Borax bead colour confused with flame colour: Separate bead test ions (Co, Ni, Mn, Cr) from flame test ions."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The compound that is white in color is","options":["ammonium sulphide","ammonium arsenomolybdate","lead iodide","lead sulphate"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): yellow (NH₄)₂S solution remembered as colourless/white. (B): yellow ammonium arsenomolybdate confused with white. (C): PbI₂ is bright yellow – student mixes up with PbCl₂ (white).\nWorking: PbSO₄ is a white precipitate (Pb²⁺ confirmatory test); PbI₂ is yellow; ammonium arsenomolybdate is a yellow precipitate (AsO₄³⁻/PO₄³⁻ type test); ammonium sulphide solution is yellow.\nKey idea: Of the lead salts, chloride and sulphate are white, iodide and chromate are yellow.","traps":["PbI₂ vs PbCl₂ colour: PbCl₂, PbSO₄ white; PbI₂, PbCrO₄ yellow.","Molybdate precipitate colour: Remember 'canary yellow' for arsenomolybdate/phosphomolybdate."],"topics":["Cation analysis","Anion analysis"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Molar mass of the salt from NaBr, NaNO₃, KI and CaF₂ which does not evolve coloured vapours on heating with concentrated H₂SO₄ is ________ g mol⁻¹.\n(Molar mass in g mol⁻¹ : Na : 23, N : 14, K : 39, O : 16, Br : 80, I : 127, F : 19, Ca : 40)","type":"num","answer":"78","range":[78.0,78.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 85 (NaNO₃, if nitrate assumed colourless), 103 (NaBr), 166 (KI).\nWorking: NaBr → reddish-brown Br₂; NaNO₃ → brown NO₂ on heating; KI → violet I₂; CaF₂ → colourless HF. M(CaF₂) = 40 + 38 = 78.\nKey idea: Fluoride gives only colourless HF.","traps":["Nitrate thought colourless: Nitrate + conc. H₂SO₄ on heating → brown NO₂."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Formation of which complex, among the following, is not a confirmatory test of Pb²⁺ ions :","options":["lead chromate","lead iodide","lead sulphate","lead nitrate"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) are the standard yellow precipitates; (C) PbSO₄ may be wrongly rejected by students who know only the KI/K₂CrO₄ tests, though it is also a white precipitate used to confirm Pb²⁺.\nWorking: Pb²⁺ is confirmed by yellow PbCrO₄ (K₂CrO₄), yellow PbI₂ (KI) and white PbSO₄ (dil. H₂SO₄); Pb(NO₃)₂ is soluble, gives no precipitate and is not a test. (The stem loosely calls these precipitates 'complexes'.)\nKey idea: A confirmatory test needs a characteristic precipitate; Pb(NO₃)₂ is soluble (it is how lead is brought into solution).","traps":["Lead sulphate rejected as a test: Remember white PbSO₄ with dil. H₂SO₄ (soluble in ammonium acetate) also confirms Pb²⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Element not present in Nessler's reagent is :","options":["I","N","K","Hg"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) I, (C) K, (D) Hg are all in K₂[HgI₄]; a student linking the reagent with ammonia might think N is part of it and so avoid (B).\nWorking: Nessler's reagent is alkaline K₂[HgI₄] (K, Hg, I, plus KOH); it contains no nitrogen – it is the reagent FOR NH₄⁺/NH₃.\nKey idea: Nessler's reagent detects nitrogen (NH₃/NH₄⁺) but contains none.","traps":["Reagent confused with the species it detects: Write K₂[HgI₄] + KOH and list its elements."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Match List I with List II:\nLIST I (Reagents used): A. Alkaline solution of copper sulphate and sodium citrate; B. Neutral FeCl₃; C. Alkaline chloroform solution; D. Potassium iodide and sodium hypochlorite\nLIST II (Compound with functional group detected): I. 1-Phenylethanol, C₆H₅CH(OH)CH₃; II. Propan-2-amine (isopropylamine), (CH₃)₂CH–NH₂; III. Phenylacetaldehyde, C₆H₅CH₂CHO; IV. 3-Ethylphenol (m-ethylphenol)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): Benedict's matched to phenol or amine. (B): carbylamine matched to the alcohol and iodoform to the amine – CH(OH)CH₃ group missed.\nWorking: Benedict's reagent (alkaline CuSO₄ + sodium citrate) → aldehyde III; neutral FeCl₃ → phenol IV; CHCl₃/alkali (carbylamine) → 1° amine II; KI + NaOCl (→ NaOI, iodoform) → CH₃CH(OH)– group of I.\nKey idea: KI + NaOCl generates NaOI → iodoform with CH₃CH(OH)–; alkaline Cu²⁺/citrate is Benedict's.","traps":["KI + NaOCl not recognised as iodoform test: NaOCl oxidises I⁻ to OI⁻ → iodoform reagent.","Alcohol not seen as iodoform-positive: CH₃CH(OH)– is oxidised to CH₃CO– first."],"topics":["Functional group tests","Iodoform"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The titration curve of weak acid vs. strong base (with phenolphthalein as indicator) is shown below. The K_phenolphthalein = 4 × 10⁻¹⁰.\nGiven: log 2 = 0.3\nThe number of following statement/s which is/are correct about phenolphthalein is ______\nA. It can be used as an indicator for the titration of weak acid with weak base.\nB. It begins to change colour at pH = 8.4\nC. It is a weak organic base\nD. It is colourless in acidic medium","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (accepts C – 'organic indicator' assumed base, or accepts A), 1 (rejects B by taking colour change at pK_In = 9.4).\nWorking: pK_In = −log(4 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 10 − 0.6 = 9.4; colour change starts at pK_In − 1 = 8.4 (B true). D true. A false (no sharp jump in weak acid–weak base titration). C false (phenolphthalein is a weak acid). Count = 2.\nKey idea: Indicator works over pK_In ± 1: 8.4–10.4 for phenolphthalein; it is a weak acid.","traps":["Change start at pK_In instead of pK_In − 1: Visible change begins one pH unit before pK_In.","Phenolphthalein called a base: It is a weak acid HIn."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Apr08S1C18-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Methyl orange is a weak acid. Statement II: The benzenoid form of methyl orange is more intense/deeply coloured than the quinonoid form. In the light of the above statement, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) accepts \"weak acid\" (methyl orange is sold as the sodium sulphonate salt, so it looks acidic); (B)/(C) accept the reversed colour claim.\nWorking: in Ostwald's theory methyl orange is treated as a weak base (phenolphthalein is the weak acid), and the quinonoid (red, acid) form is more deeply coloured than the benzenoid (yellow) form.\nKey idea: Phenolphthalein = weak acid, methyl orange = weak base; quinonoid form is always the deeper-coloured one.","traps":["Methyl orange called a weak acid: Ostwald: methyl orange acts as a weak base.","Benzenoid vs quinonoid intensity reversed: Quinonoid form has extended conjugation and is deeper coloured."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"20 mL of calcium hydroxide was consumed when it was reacted with 10 mL of unknown solution of H₂SO₄. Also 20 mL standard solution of 0.5 M HCl containing 2 drops of phenolphthalein was titrated with calcium hydroxide, the mixture showed pink colour when burette displayed the value of 35.5 mL whereas the burette showed 25.5 mL initially. The concentration of H₂SO₄ is ________ M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2: forgets that Ca(OH)₂ is diacidic (uses 1 M base). 0.5: ignores dibasic H₂SO₄/1:1 ratio errors. 3 or 4: uses 35.5 mL as titre.\nWorking: titre = 35.5 − 25.5 = 10.0 mL Ca(OH)₂ for 20 mL × 0.5 M HCl = 10 mmol H⁺ → 2 × M × 10 = 10 → [Ca(OH)₂] = 0.5 M. Then 20 mL × 0.5 M = 10 mmol Ca(OH)₂ reacts 1:1 with H₂SO₄ in 10 mL → 1 M.\nKey idea: Ca(OH)₂ and H₂SO₄ are both di-functional: they react 1:1 in moles.","traps":["Final burette reading taken as titre: Titre = final − initial.","Acidity/basicity factors misapplied: Use equivalents or balanced equations for each step."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Test): A. Molisch's Test; B. Biuret Test; C. Carbylamine Test; D. Schiff's Test\nList II (Functional group / Class of Compound): I. Peptide; II. Carbohydrate; III. Primary amine; IV. Aldehyde\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) - III, (B) - IV, (C) - II, (D) - I","(A) - II, (B) - I, (C) - III, (D) - IV","(A) - III, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D) - II","(A) - I, (B) - II, (C) - III, (D) - IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) put carbylamine on carbohydrate/peptide. (D) swaps Molisch and biuret, a common mix-up of the two protein/carbohydrate tests.\nWorking: Molisch – carbohydrates (II); biuret – peptides (I); carbylamine – primary amines (III); Schiff's – aldehydes (IV).\nKey idea: Molisch = any carbohydrate (violet ring); biuret = two or more peptide bonds.","traps":["Molisch and biuret swapped: Biuret is named after the -CO-NH- linkage of urea dimer: peptides."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"A solution of FeCl₃ when treated with K₄[Fe(CN)₆] gives a Prussian blue precipitate due to the formation of :","options":["Fe[Fe(CN)₆]","Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃","Fe₃[Fe(CN)₆]₂","K[Fe₂(CN)₆]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) charge-unbalanced 1:1. (C) Fe₃[Fe(CN)₆]₂ is the Turnbull-type ratio for Fe²⁺ with ferricyanide. (D) invented formula.\nWorking: 4Fe³⁺ + 3[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ → Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃, Prussian blue.\nKey idea: Four Fe³⁺ (+12) balance three [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ (−12).","traps":["Turnbull blue ratio used: Balance charges: Fe³⁺ with [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ → 4:3."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which element is not present in Nessler's reagent?","options":["Mercury","Iodine","Potassium","Oxygen"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are all in K₂[HgI₄]; a student who remembers 'alkaline' (KOH) may doubt D and pick another.\nWorking: Nessler's reagent is K₂[HgI₄], which has no oxygen; strictly the reagent is made alkaline with KOH, so the wording is loose, but the intended answer is (D).\nKey idea: Nessler's reagent = K₂HgI₄ (with KOH); the complex has only K, Hg and I.","traps":["KOH in the reagent makes oxygen 'present': Answer by the formula of the active compound, K₂[HgI₄]."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A) : An aqueous solution of KOH when used for volumetric analysis, its concentration should be checked before the use. Reason (R) : On aging, KOH solution absorbs atmospheric CO₂. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) for those who know both facts but think the check is due to hygroscopic nature only. (A)/(B) reject one true statement.\nWorking: KOH solution absorbs CO₂ from air (2KOH + CO₂ → K₂CO₃ + H₂O), so its OH⁻ concentration falls on standing and it must be standardised before use. Both are true and R explains A.\nKey idea: Absorption of CO₂ converts some KOH to K₂CO₃, lowering the hydroxide concentration - hence standardise.","traps":["Hygroscopic nature taken as the only reason: Any change in composition on standing explains the need to standardise; CO₂ uptake is one."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"An ammoniacal metal salt solution gives a brilliant red precipitate on addition of dimethylglyoxime. The metal ion is :","options":["Co²⁺","Fe²⁺","Ni²⁺","Cu²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Co²⁺: same group IV as Ni²⁺, confirmed by other tests. (B) Fe²⁺: DMG gives red colour only in special conditions, not the standard test. (D) Cu²⁺: blue ammine complex, not a DMG precipitate.\nWorking: Ni²⁺ forms red bis(dimethylglyoximato)nickel(II) in ammoniacal medium.\nKey idea: Rosy-red ppt with DMG in NH₃ = Ni²⁺.","traps":["Co²⁺ vs Ni²⁺ in group IV: Ni²⁺ → red with DMG; Co²⁺ → blue with NH₄SCN/amyl alcohol."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"K₂Cr₂O₇ paper acidified with dilute H₂SO₄ turns green when exposed to :","options":["Sulphur dioxide","Sulphur trioxide","Hydrogen sulphide","Carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) SO₃: S already +6, cannot reduce. (C) H₂S: also a reductant but its standard test is lead acetate paper; tempting. (D) CO₂: not a reductant (lime-water test).\nWorking: SO₂ reduces orange Cr₂O₇²⁻ to green Cr³⁺. H₂S also reduces dichromate, but it gives a milky sulphur deposit and is identified by lead acetate paper; SO₂ is the standard answer.\nKey idea: SO₂ reduces dichromate to Cr³⁺ (green) and is oxidised to SO₄²⁻.","traps":["SO₂ vs SO₃: Only the lower oxidation state can reduce.","H₂S also reduces: Match each gas to its standard lab test."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List I (Elements) with List II (Colour imparted to the flame). A. K B. Ca C. Sr D. Ba ; I. Brick red II. Violet III. Apple green IV. Crimson red. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ca and Sr (brick red vs crimson). (C) swaps Sr and Ba. (D) shifts every pair.\nWorking: K – violet (II); Ca – brick red (I); Sr – crimson red (IV); Ba – apple green (III).\nKey idea: Ca brick red, Sr crimson, Ba apple green, K violet.","traps":["Brick red vs crimson red: Ca = brick red (orange-red); Sr = crimson (deep red).","Ba vs Sr colour: Ba = apple green (also seen in the Group V confirmatory test)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match the List-I (Cations) with List-II (Group reagents): A. Pb²⁺, Cu²⁺ B. Al³⁺, Fe³⁺ C. Co²⁺, Ni²⁺ D. Ba²⁺, Ca²⁺ ; i) H₂S gas in presence of dilute HCl ii) (NH₄)₂CO₃ in presence of NH₄OH iii) NH₄OH in presence of NH₄Cl iv) H₂S in presence of NH₄OH. Correct match is -","options":["A → i, B → iii, C → iv, D → ii","A → i, B → iii, C → ii, D → iv","A → iii, B → i, C → iv, D → ii","A → iv, B → ii, C → iii, D → i"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps Groups IV and V reagents. (C) swaps Groups II and III. (D) shifts every pair.\nWorking: Group II – H₂S in dil. HCl (Pb²⁺, Cu²⁺); Group III – NH₄OH with NH₄Cl (Al³⁺, Fe³⁺); Group IV – H₂S in NH₄OH (Co²⁺, Ni²⁺); Group V – (NH₄)₂CO₃ in NH₄OH (Ba²⁺, Ca²⁺).\nKey idea: Acidic H₂S → Group II; NH₄OH/NH₄Cl → Group III hydroxides; alkaline H₂S → Group IV sulphides; carbonate → Group V.","traps":["Acidic vs alkaline H₂S: Low [S²⁻] (acid) precipitates only very insoluble sulphides (Group II); high [S²⁻] (NH₄OH) gives Group IV.","Group IV and V reagents swapped: Ba²⁺/Ca²⁺ are precipitated as carbonates (Group V)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"A chloride salt solution acidified with dil. HNO₃ gives a curdy white precipitate, [A], on addition of AgNO₃. [A] on treatment with NH₄OH gives a clear solution B. A and B are respectively:","options":["H[AgCl₃] & (NH₄)[Ag(OH)₂]","AgCl & [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl","H[AgCl₃] & [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl","AgCl & (NH₄)[Ag(OH)₂]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) invent a chloro-acid H[AgCl₃] as the precipitate; (A)/(D) give a hydroxido complex instead of the ammine complex.\nWorking: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl, curdy white (A); AgCl + 2NH₃ → [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl, soluble (B).\nKey idea: Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl↓ (white, curdy); AgCl + 2NH₃ → [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl (soluble).","traps":["Ammine vs hydroxido complex: NH₄OH acts as a source of NH₃ ligand for Ag⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Number of compounds giving (i) red colouration with ceric ammonium nitrate and also (ii) positive iodoform test from the following is ______: acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃); 1-phenylpropan-2-ol (C₆H₅CH₂CH(OH)CH₃); ethanal (CH₃CHO); butan-2-ol (CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃); methoxyethane (CH₃OCH₂CH₃); 2-phenylethan-1-ol (C₆H₅CH₂CH₂OH); 1-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol (C₆H₁₁CH₂CH(OH)CH₃); 2-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol (C₆H₁₁C(OH)(CH₃)₂)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (counting acetophenone and ethanal, which pass iodoform only); 4 (counting the tertiary 2-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol because it has two CH₃ on the carbinol carbon); 2 (missing the long-chain 1-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol).\nWorking: both tests are positive for 1-phenylpropan-2-ol, butan-2-ol and 1-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol; acetophenone and ethanal give iodoform but no alcohol for CAN; 2-phenylethanol and the 3° alcohol give CAN but no iodoform; ether gives neither. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Need an alcohol (CAN) whose carbinol carbon is CH(OH)CH₃ (iodoform): secondary methyl carbinols only.","traps":["Both tests, not either: Tick CAN and iodoform separately; count only double ticks.","Tertiary methyl carbinol: Iodoform needs CH₃CH(OH)–: a carbinol H is required for oxidation."],"topics":["Functional group tests","Iodoform"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"During the borax bead test with CuSO₄, a blue green colour of the bead was observed in oxidising flame due to the formation of :","options":["CuO","Cu(BO₂)₂","Cu₃B₂","Cu"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CuO is the intermediate, not the coloured bead; (C), (D) relate to reducing flame (Cu, red/opaque).\nWorking: in the oxidising flame CuSO₄ and B₂O₃ (from borax) form copper(II) metaborate Cu(BO₂)₂, which is blue-green.\nKey idea: Bead colour comes from the metal metaborate.","traps":["Oxidising vs reducing flame product: Oxidising flame gives metaborate; reducing gives metal."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Kinetics experiments","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"An indicator 'X' is used for studying the effect of variation in concentration of iodide on the rate of reaction of iodide ion with H₂O₂ at room temp. The indicator 'X' forms blue coloured complex with compound 'A' present in the solution. The indicator 'X' and compound 'A' respectively are :","options":["Methyl orange and H₂O₂","Methyl orange and iodine","Starch and H₂O₂","Starch and iodine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) methyl orange is an acid–base indicator; (C) starch does not complex H₂O₂.\nWorking: H₂O₂ oxidises I⁻ to I₂, and I₂ forms a blue complex with starch (the time for the blue colour to appear is used to follow the rate). So X = starch and A = iodine.\nKey idea: Blue colour = starch–I₂ (triiodide) complex.","traps":["Which species gives blue: Blue complex forms with iodine only."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In the wet tests for identification of various cations by precipitation, which transition element cation doesn't belong to group IV in qualitative inorganic analysis?","options":["Co²⁺","Ni²⁺","Zn²⁺","Fe³⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Co²⁺ and Ni²⁺ are classic Group IV (black sulphides). Zn²⁺ is Group IV (white ZnS) but may be picked because Zn is often said not to be a true transition element.\nWorking: Group IV (H₂S in ammoniacal medium) precipitates Co²⁺, Ni²⁺ and Zn²⁺ (and Mn²⁺) as sulphides; Fe³⁺ is precipitated earlier, in Group III, as Fe(OH)₃.\nKey idea: Fe³⁺ is precipitated earlier as Fe(OH)₃ in Group III, so it is the odd one out.","traps":["Zn²⁺ picked as 'not transition': Answer the group question asked; Zn²⁺ is a Group IV cation.","Fe in Group III, not IV: Fe³⁺, Al³⁺, Cr³⁺ are Group III hydroxides."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"During the qualitative analysis of SO₃²⁻ using dilute H₂SO₄, SO₂ gas is evolved which turns K₂Cr₂O₇ solution (acidified with dilute H₂SO₄):","options":["blue","black","red","green"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Blue: confusion with CrO₅ (peroxide test) or starch-iodine. Black: confusion with H₂S and lead acetate. Red: chromyl chloride / dichromate colour.\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₂ + 2H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻ + H₂O; orange dichromate turns green (Cr³⁺).\nKey idea: Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange) + SO₂ → Cr³⁺ (green) + SO₄²⁻.","traps":["Blue from dichromate-peroxide test: Blue CrO₅ needs H₂O₂; SO₂ is a reducing agent giving Cr³⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Formulae for Nessler's reagent is :","options":["KHgI₃","HgI₂","KHg₂I₂","K₂HgI₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) KHgI₃: wrong stoichiometry, a tri-iodo complex that is not the reagent; (B) HgI₂: the red precipitate that dissolves in excess KI to make the reagent; (C) KHg₂I₂: invented formula with Hg in wrong ratio.\nWorking: HgI₂ dissolves in excess KI: HgI₂ + 2KI → K₂[HgI₄]; an alkaline (KOH) solution of K₂HgI₄ is Nessler's reagent.\nKey idea: HgI₂ + 2KI → K₂[HgI₄]; the alkaline solution of this complex is Nessler's reagent.","traps":["HgI₂ versus K₂[HgI₄]: Remember HgI₂ dissolves in excess KI to give the soluble tetraiodo complex."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Upon heating a borax bead dipped in cupric sulphate in a luminous flame, the colour of the bead becomes green. Statement II: The green colour observed is due to the formation of copper(I) metaborate. In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts green colour in luminous flame. (C) accepts Cu(I) metaborate as green (it is colourless). (D) accepts green in luminous flame but rejects the cause.\nWorking: in the luminous (reducing) flame Cu(BO₂)₂ is reduced to colourless CuBO₂ and red opaque Cu, so the bead is not green (I false); copper(I) metaborate is colourless (II false). The blue-green colour is from Cu(BO₂)₂ in the oxidising flame.\nKey idea: Luminous flame is reducing: Cu(II) goes to colourless CuBO₂ / red Cu, so no green bead.","traps":["Luminous = reducing flame: Luminous (yellow) flame reduces; non-luminous oxidises.","Cu(I) vs Cu(II) metaborate colour: Cu(I) is d¹⁰ → colourless CuBO₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The incorrect statement for the use of indicators in acid-base titration is:","options":["Phenolphthalein is a suitable indicator for a weak acid vs strong base titration.","Methyl orange may be used for a weak acid vs weak base titration.","Methyl orange is a suitable indicator for a strong acid vs weak base titration.","Phenolphthalein may be used for a strong acid vs strong base titration."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true: endpoint basic. (C) true: endpoint acidic. (D) true: steep jump covers both indicators.\nWorking: a weak acid–weak base titration has no sharp pH jump at the equivalence point, so methyl orange cannot be used – (B) is incorrect; (A), (C) and (D) are true.\nKey idea: Weak acid–weak base titration has no sharp pH jump, so no simple indicator works.","traps":["Finding the false statement: Mark each true/false; pick the false.","Weak–weak titration: No sharp end point for weak acid vs weak base."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"In base vs. acid titration, at the end point methyl orange is present as","options":["quinonoid form","heterocyclic form","phenolic form","benzenoid form"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) benzenoid is the yellow alkaline form (present before the end point); (B)/(C) are not methyl orange forms.\nWorking: titrating a base with acid, the end point is on the acid side (pH ≈ 3.1–4.4) where methyl orange is protonated and red, in the quinonoid form; the yellow basic form is benzenoid (azo).\nKey idea: Methyl orange: yellow benzenoid (base) → red quinonoid (acid).","traps":["Benzenoid vs quinonoid: End point of base vs acid is acidic → red, protonated quinonoid form."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Phenolphthalein is a pH dependent indicator, remains colourless in acidic solution and gives pink colour in basic medium\nReason R : Phenolphthalein is a weak acid. It doesn't dissociate in basic medium.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) if the student reads only \"weak acid\"; (D) if the student doubts the colour behaviour in A.\nWorking: phenolphthalein (HIn) is a weak acid that ionises in basic medium to the pink In⁻ form, so the second sentence of R is false.\nKey idea: Base removes H⁺ from HIn → pink In⁻; so it DOES dissociate in base.","traps":["Dissociation in base: OH⁻ pulls HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻ to the right.","Half-true reason: Judge every clause of R."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄ titrations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"In the titration of KMnO₄ and oxalic acid in acidic medium, the change in oxidation number of carbon at the end point is_____","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (per oxalate ion), 5 (Mn change).\nWorking: C in C₂O₄²⁻ is +3 and in CO₂ is +4 → change of 1 per carbon atom (2 per oxalate).\nKey idea: C: +3 → +4 is one unit per carbon.","traps":["Per oxalate vs per carbon: Read \"change in oxidation number of carbon\" — per atom."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Fe³⁺ cation gives a prussian blue precipitate on addition of potassium ferrocyanide solution due to the formation of:","options":["[Fe(H₂O)₆]₂[Fe(CN)₆]","Fe₂[Fe(CN)₆]₂","Fe₃[Fe(OH)₂(CN)₄]₂","Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) are charge-unbalanced or invented formulas; (B) mimics a 1:1 ratio.\nWorking: 4Fe³⁺ + 3[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ → Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃, iron(III) hexacyanidoferrate(II) (Prussian blue).\nKey idea: Balance 4 × (+3) with 3 × (−4).","traps":["Charge balance of the precipitate: Check 4 Fe³⁺ = +12 balancing 3 [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ = −12."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kinetics experiments","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"For kinetic study of the reaction of iodide ion with H₂O₂ at room temperature: (A) Always use freshly prepared starch solution. (B) Always keep the concentration of sodium thiosulphate solution less than that of KI solution. (C) Record the time immediately after the appearance of blue colour. (D) Record the time immediately before the appearance of blue colour. (E) Always keep the concentration of sodium thiosulphate solution more than that of KI solution. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (B), (C) only","(A), (D), (E) only","(D), (E) only","(A), (B), (E) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(B) pick \"more thiosulphate\", which would stop the blue colour appearing; (B)/(C) pick \"before the blue colour\", which cannot be observed.\nWorking: fresh starch is needed for a sharp blue; thiosulphate must be the limiting reagent (less than iodide) so that I₂ appears once it is used up; the time is noted as soon as the blue colour appears. (D) and (E) are the opposites.\nKey idea: Thiosulphate is the timer; it must run out first, so it is kept small.","traps":["Amount of thiosulphate: Blue appears only after all thiosulphate is consumed.","When to stop the clock: The end-point is the first visible blue."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A compound 'X' is a weak acid and it exhibits colour change at pH close to the equivalence point during neutralization of NaOH with CH₃COOH. Compound 'X' exists in ionized form in basic medium. The compound 'X' is:","options":["methyl orange","methyl red","phenolphthalein","eriochrome Black T"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) change in acidic range (suit strong acid–weak base); (D) complexometric (EDTA) indicator.\nWorking: weak acid–strong base titration has an equivalence point near pH 8–9 (acetate hydrolysis); phenolphthalein (pH range 8.2–10) is a weak acid that is pink in its ionised form in base.\nKey idea: Equivalence pH > 7 → phenolphthalein.","traps":["Methyl orange for all titrations: Match indicator range to equivalence pH."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"During the qualitative analysis of salt with cation y²⁺, addition of a reagent (X) to alkaline solution of the salt gives a bright red precipitate. The reagent (X) and the cation (y²⁺) present respectively are:","options":["Dimethylglyoxime and Ni²⁺","Dimethylglyoxime and Co²⁺","Nessler's reagent and Hg²⁺","Nessler's reagent and Ni²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Co²⁺ is the same group as Ni²⁺ (Group IV) and confused with it; (C)/(D) Nessler's reagent is for ammonium.\nWorking: Ni²⁺ in ammoniacal/alkaline solution gives a rosy-red precipitate of bis(dimethylglyoximato)nickel(II) with DMG. Nessler's reagent detects NH₄⁺ (brown precipitate).\nKey idea: DMG + Ni²⁺ (NH₃) → rosy-red Ni(dmg)₂.","traps":["Co²⁺ vs Ni²⁺ in Group IV: DMG is specific for Ni²⁺; Co²⁺ is confirmed with KNO₂/α-nitroso-β-naphthol."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"In the flame test of a mixture of salts, a green flame with blue centre was observed. Which one of the following cations may be present?","options":["Cu²⁺","Sr²⁺","Ba²⁺","Ca²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Ba²⁺ – also green (apple/grassy green) but without blue centre; (B) Sr²⁺ – crimson; (D) Ca²⁺ – brick red.\nWorking: copper salts give a green flame with a blue centre (bluish-green); Ba²⁺ gives apple/yellowish green, Sr²⁺ crimson, Ca²⁺ brick red.\nKey idea: Green with blue centre = copper; plain apple green = barium.","traps":["Ba²⁺ apple-green flame chosen: Blue centre / bluish-green → Cu²⁺; apple green → Ba²⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"White precipitate of AgCl dissolves in aqueous ammonia solution due to formation of:","options":["[Ag(NH₃)₄]Cl₂","[Ag(Cl)₂(NH₃)₂]","[Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl","[Ag(NH₃)Cl]Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes CN 4 and Ag²⁺; (B) neutral complex would not dissolve as ions; (D) wrong ligand count.\nWorking: AgCl + 2NH₃ → [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl, soluble diamminesilver(I) chloride (Ag⁺, linear, CN 2) – the confirmatory test for chloride.\nKey idea: Ag⁺ forms linear two-coordinate complexes: [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺.","traps":["Coordination number of Ag⁺: Ag(I) is linear, CN 2 (also in Tollens' reagent)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"How many of the given compounds will give a positive Biuret test ______ ? Glycine, Glycylalanine, Tripeptide, Biuret","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counting the dipeptide), 1 (excluding biuret itself), 4 (counting glycine as an amino acid \"protein test\").\nWorking: the biuret test (alkaline CuSO₄, violet colour) needs at least two –CO–NH– linkages close together: tripeptide (two peptide bonds) and biuret itself (H₂N–CO–NH–CO–NH₂) are positive; glycine (none) and the dipeptide glycylalanine (one) are negative → 2.\nKey idea: Biuret test is positive for compounds with two or more adjacent amide (peptide) bonds, including biuret itself.","traps":["Dipeptide gives negative biuret: Need at least two peptide bonds.","Biuret itself is positive: Biuret has two –CO–NH– units."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which statement is not true with respect to nitrate ion test ? (A) A dark brown ring is formed at the junction of two solutions. (B) Ring is formed due to nitroferrous sulphate complex. (C) The brown complex is [Fe(H₂O)₅(NO)]SO₄. (D) Heating the nitrate salt with conc. H₂SO₄, light brown fumes are evolved.","options":["A dark brown ring is formed at the junction of two solutions.","Ring is formed due to nitroferrous sulphate complex.","The brown complex is [Fe(H₂O)₅(NO)]SO₄.","Heating the nitrate salt with conc. H₂SO₄, light brown fumes are evolved."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) are textbook-true; (D) \"light brown\" fumes may look wrong to students expecting \"dark brown\" but is accepted as true.\nWorking: the brown ring is the nitroso-ferrous sulphate complex [Fe(H₂O)₅(NO)]SO₄, formed at the junction of the layers; heating a nitrate with conc. H₂SO₄ gives brown NO₂ fumes. \"Nitroferrous\" (nitro instead of nitroso) makes (B) the untrue statement.\nKey idea: The ligand is NO (nitrosyl): nitroso-ferrous sulphate.","traps":["Nitro vs nitroso: NO ligand = nitrosyl/nitroso.","Colour wording of fumes: NO₂ fumes are brown; the item hinges on the name."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I (Anion): A. CO₃²⁻; B. S²⁻; C. SO₃²⁻; D. NO₂⁻. List II (Gas evolved on reaction with dil. H₂SO₄): I. Colourless gas which turns lead acetate paper black; II. Colourless gas which turns acidified potassium dichromate solution green; III. Brown fumes which turns acidified KI solution containing starch blue; IV. Colourless gas evolved with brisk effervescence, which turns lime water milky. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps SO₃²⁻ and NO₂⁻; (B) matches CO₃²⁻ with dichromate (SO₂ test); (A) gives CO₃²⁻ brown fumes.\nWorking: CO₃²⁻ → CO₂ (effervescence, lime water milky); S²⁻ → H₂S (lead acetate black, PbS); SO₃²⁻ → SO₂ (turns acidified dichromate green, Cr³⁺); NO₂⁻ → NO/NO₂ brown fumes which liberate I₂ from acidified KI (starch blue).\nKey idea: SO₂ and CO₂ both turn lime water milky, but only SO₂ reduces dichromate to green Cr³⁺.","traps":["SO₂ vs CO₂ tests: Dichromate green is specific to the reducing gas SO₂.","Sulphite vs nitrite swap: Brown fumes = nitrogen oxides from nitrite."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄ titrations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"0.01 M KMnO₄ solution was added to 20.0 mL of 0.05 M Mohr's salt solution through a burette. The initial reading of the 50 mL burette is zero. The volume of KMnO₄ solution left in the burette after the end point is _______ mL. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"30","range":[30.0,30.0],"explain":"Common slips: 20 (volume used, not left); 0 or negative (taking 1:1 ratio → 100 mL needed); 4 (dividing by 5 the wrong way).\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺: mmol Fe²⁺ = 20 × 0.05 = 1.0; mmol MnO₄⁻ = 0.2 → V = 0.2/0.01 = 20 mL used; 50 − 20 = 30 mL left.\nKey idea: Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ (1 e⁻), MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ (5 e⁻).","traps":["Used vs left in burette: Subtract from 50 mL.","1:5 mole ratio: Only Fe²⁺ reacts, 5 per MnO₄⁻."],"topics":["KMnO₄ titrations","Mohr's salt"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A white precipitate was formed when BaCl₂ was added to water extract of an inorganic salt. Further, a gas 'X' with characteristic odour was released when the formed white precipitate was dissolved in dilute HCl. The anion present in the inorganic salt is:","options":["I⁻","SO₃²⁻","S²⁻","NO₂⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) S²⁻ also gives a smelly gas (H₂S) with acid but no white Ba precipitate; (A), (D) give no BaCl₂ precipitate.\nWorking: SO₃²⁻ + Ba²⁺ → BaSO₃ (white), which dissolves in dilute HCl releasing SO₂ (pungent, burning-sulphur odour); BaSO₄ would not dissolve, and I⁻, S²⁻, NO₂⁻ give no white Ba precipitate.\nKey idea: White Ba salt soluble in dilute HCl with pungent gas → sulphite; insoluble → sulphate.","traps":["Odour of gas confused with H₂S: S²⁻ is not precipitated by BaCl₂."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Detecting extra elements","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which one of the following complexes is violet in colour?","options":["[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻","[Fe(SCN)₆]⁴⁻","Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃·H₂O","[Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) pale yellow ferrocyanide; (B) thiocyanato-iron complex – suggests the blood-red Fe³⁺/SCN⁻ test; (C) Prussian blue from the nitrogen test.\nWorking: sulphide ion adds to nitroprusside [Fe(CN)₅NO]²⁻ to give the violet [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻ (sulphur test). [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is pale yellow, Prussian blue is blue, and the thiocyanato iron complexes are blood red.\nKey idea: Violet in Lassaigne's test always means S²⁻ + nitroprusside → [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻.","traps":["Violet = nitroprusside + sulphide: Link each complex to its test: Prussian blue → N; blood red → SCN⁻; violet → S²⁻.","Hexacyanoferrate(II) is pale yellow: Recall K₄[Fe(CN)₆] is pale yellow."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Indicators","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: In the titration between strong acid and weak base methyl orange is suitable as an indicator. Statement II: For titration of acetic acid with NaOH phenolphthalein is not a suitable indicator. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) rejects methyl orange for weak base titrations; (C) accepts the negated Statement II; (D) rejects both.\nWorking: strong acid–weak base gives an acidic end point, matched by methyl orange (I true). Acetic acid–NaOH gives a basic end point, for which phenolphthalein is the suitable indicator (II false).\nKey idea: Acidic end point → methyl orange; basic end point → phenolphthalein.","traps":["Phenolphthalein suits weak acid–strong base: The equivalence point is basic (pH ~8–9), inside phenolphthalein's 8.2–10 range.","Methyl orange for strong acid–weak base: Strong acid + weak base → acidic equivalence point → methyl orange (3.1–4.4)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Mohr's salt","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The ratio of number of water molecules in Mohr's salt and potash alum is ______ × 10⁻¹. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2.5 (using 24 H₂O alum) – non-integer; 20 (inverted ratio).\nWorking: Mohr's salt FeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄·6H₂O (6 H₂O); potash alum KAl(SO₄)₂·12H₂O (12 H₂O); 6/12 = 0.5 = 5 × 10⁻¹. (With the doubled alum formula, 24 H₂O, the ratio would be 2.5, not an integer – the answer follows NCERT's formula.)\nKey idea: Use NCERT's formulas: 6 and 12 water molecules.","traps":["Formula of potash alum: The answer uses KAl(SO₄)₂·12H₂O (NCERT form): 6/12 = 0.5 = 5×10⁻¹.","Order of ratio: Keep the order given in the stem."],"topics":["Mohr's salt","Potash alum"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which one of the following phenols does not give colour when condensed with phthalic anhydride in presence of conc. H₂SO₄? [Structures: (1) phenol, C₆H₅OH; (2) p-cresol (4-methylphenol), OH and CH₃ para; (3) resorcinol (benzene-1,3-diol), OH groups meta; (4) catechol (benzene-1,2-diol), OH groups ortho]","options":["Phenol (C₆H₅OH)","p-Cresol (4-methylphenol)","Resorcinol (benzene-1,3-diol)","Catechol (benzene-1,2-diol)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) phenol is the standard positive (phenolphthalein); (C) resorcinol gives the strongly fluorescent fluorescein – a well-known positive; (D) catechol looks \"crowded\" but still has a free para position, tempting those who think any extra substituent blocks the test.\nWorking: the phthalein condensation is an electrophilic attack para to OH; in p-cresol the para position is blocked by CH₃, so no phthalein dye forms. Phenol gives phenolphthalein, resorcinol gives fluorescein, catechol also condenses (para to one OH is free).\nKey idea: Phthalic anhydride attacks para to the phenolic OH; block that position and no dye forms.","traps":["Para position must be free: Check the position para to each OH.","Resorcinol gives fluorescein, not 'no colour': Any coloured/fluorescent condensation product counts as colour."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Acidic ferric chloride solution on treatment with excess of potassium ferrocyanide gives a Prussian blue coloured colloidal species. It is:","options":["Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃","K₅Fe[Fe(CN)₆]₂","HFe[Fe(CN)₆]","KFe[Fe(CN)₆]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ – the NCERT/Lassaigne formula, insoluble Prussian blue; (B) K₅Fe[Fe(CN)₆]₂ – charge-balanced-looking but not a real species; (C) HFe[Fe(CN)₆] – acid form suggested by 'acidic' medium.\nWorking: with excess K₄[Fe(CN)₆], Fe³⁺ gives 'soluble' Prussian blue KFe[Fe(CN)₆] (KFeᴵᴵᴵ[Feᴵᴵ(CN)₆]), which stays as a colloidal dispersion; Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ is the insoluble form obtained with excess Fe³⁺.\nKey idea: Excess ferrocyanide → K⁺ incorporated → KFe[Fe(CN)₆] (soluble/colloidal Prussian blue).","traps":["NCERT formula chosen: Read the condition: excess ferrocyanide and colloidal → KFe[Fe(CN)₆].","'Acidic' suggests H⁺ in formula: Acid only prevents Fe(OH)₃ precipitation."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The addition of dilute NaOH to Cr³⁺ salt solution will give:","options":["a solution of [Cr(OH)₄]⁻","precipitate of Cr₂O₃(H₂O)ₙ","precipitate of [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻","precipitate of Cr(OH)₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is what forms in EXCESS NaOH (a solution, not with dilute alkali); (C) is an anionic complex and cannot be a precipitate; (D) is the conventional formula of the same precipitate – tempting and only rejected because the hydrated oxide is the better description.\nWorking: dilute (not excess) NaOH gives a green gelatinous precipitate of hydrated chromium(III) oxide, Cr₂O₃·nH₂O; excess NaOH would dissolve it as [Cr(OH)₄]⁻/[Cr(OH)₆]³⁻ (a solution, not a precipitate). Option (D) Cr(OH)₃ is the common textbook formula for the same precipitate, so (B) and (D) describe nearly the same thing; (B) is preferred as the more accurate formulation.\nKey idea: \"Dilute NaOH\" means a precipitate; Cr(III) \"hydroxide\" is really hydrated Cr₂O₃.","traps":["Dilute vs excess NaOH: Dilute/limited alkali → precipitate; excess → soluble hydroxo complex.","Cr(OH)₃ vs hydrated Cr₂O₃: The true solid is Cr₂O₃·nH₂O; pick the hydrated-oxide option when both appear."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which one of the following tests used for the identification of functional groups in organic compounds does not use copper reagent?","options":["Barfoed's test","Seliwanoff's test","Benedict's test","Biuret test for peptide bond"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Barfoed – less familiar, but also Cu²⁺ acetate; (C) Benedict – Cu²⁺ citrate; (D) biuret – CuSO₄ violet complex with peptides.\nWorking: Barfoed (copper(II) acetate in acetic acid), Benedict (alkaline Cu²⁺ citrate) and biuret (alkaline CuSO₄) all use Cu²⁺; Seliwanoff uses resorcinol in conc. HCl (ketose test).\nKey idea: Seliwanoff = resorcinol + HCl (dehydration to furfural derivative), no metal.","traps":["Barfoed reagent unknown: Barfoed = copper(II) acetate – monosaccharide vs disaccharide test.","NOT-type selection: Mark each test copper/no copper before choosing."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Consider the sulphides HgS, PbS, CuS, Sb₂S₃, As₂S₃ and CdS. Number of these sulphides soluble in 50% HNO₃ is ________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 – counts only PbS, CuS, CdS (treats As₂S₃ as group IIB-only, soluble only in yellow ammonium sulphide); 5 – adds Sb₂S₃; 6 – includes HgS.\nWorking: in Group II analysis the Cu-group sulphides PbS, CuS and CdS dissolve in hot 50% HNO₃ (oxidised to S and nitrates), and As₂S₃ is oxidised to soluble H₃AsO₄; HgS does not dissolve (needs aqua regia) and Sb₂S₃ is converted to insoluble hydrated antimony oxide rather than dissolving. Count = 4 (PbS, CuS, As₂S₃, CdS). The As₂S₃/Sb₂S₃ split depends on detailed lab behaviour beyond NCERT.\nKey idea: HgS is the classic sulphide that resists 50% HNO₃; Sb₂S₃ gives an insoluble oxide.","traps":["HgS solubility: HgS dissolves only in aqua regia.","IIB sulphides in HNO₃: As₂S₃ is still oxidised by HNO₃; Sb₂S₃ forms insoluble oxide."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List-I (Metal Ion) with List-II (Group in Qualitative analysis): (a) Mn²⁺; (b) As³⁺; (c) Cu²⁺; (d) Al³⁺. List-II: (i) Group-III; (ii) Group-IIA; (iii) Group-IV; (iv) Group-IIB. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Mn²⁺ in III and Al³⁺ in IIB; (C) swaps Al³⁺ to IV; (D) puts Mn²⁺ in IIB.\nWorking: Mn²⁺ – Group IV (MnS with H₂S in ammoniacal medium); As³⁺ – Group IIB (sulphide soluble in yellow ammonium sulphide); Cu²⁺ – Group IIA (copper group); Al³⁺ – Group III (Al(OH)₃ with NH₄Cl/NH₄OH).\nKey idea: As/Sb/Sn = IIB (dissolve in yellow ammonium sulphide); Cu/Pb/Bi/Cd = IIA.","traps":["IIA vs IIB: IIB sulphides (As, Sb, Sn) dissolve in yellow ammonium sulphide.","Mn²⁺ in Group III: NH₄Cl suppresses OH⁻; Mn²⁺ precipitates as MnS in Group IV."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Statement I: Colourless cupric metaborate is reduced to cuprous metaborate in a luminous flame. Statement II: Cuprous metaborate is obtained by heating boric anhydride and copper sulphate in a non-luminous flame. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – misses that cupric metaborate is not colourless; (C)/(D) – misses the cupric/cuprous swap in II.\nWorking: in the oxidising (non-luminous) flame CuSO₄ + B₂O₃ give blue-green cupric metaborate Cu(BO₂)₂, so II (which says cuprous) is false; in the luminous (reducing) flame Cu(BO₂)₂ is reduced to colourless cuprous metaborate CuBO₂ and Cu, so I is false because cupric metaborate is blue, not colourless.\nKey idea: Non-luminous (oxidising) flame → Cu(II) metaborate (blue); luminous (reducing) flame → Cu(I)/Cu.","traps":["Colour word altered: Cu(BO₂)₂ is blue; CuBO₂ is colourless.","Oxidation state swapped: Oxidising flame keeps Cu²⁺."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which of the following compound gives pink colour on reaction with phthalic anhydride in conc. H₂SO₄ followed by treatment with NaOH?","options":["2-Propylphenol (OH ortho to an n-propyl chain; position para to OH free)","A catechol (two adjacent OH) carrying an n-propyl group and a CH₃ group, each para to one OH","A resorcinol (benzene-1,3-diol): OH groups at C-1 and C-3 (meta), CH₃ at C-6 and n-propyl at C-4, i.e. 4-methyl-6-propylbenzene-1,3-diol; every position para to an OH is substituted","4-Propylphenol (OH para to an n-propyl chain)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – para blocked though it is a simple phenol; (B) catechol / (C) resorcinol – two OH groups look 'more phenolic' but every position para to an OH is substituted (an unblocked resorcinol would give fluorescein, a green fluorescence, not the pink phthalein).\nWorking: the phthalein test needs a phenol with a free position para to OH (condensation with phthalic anhydride occurs para to OH, giving a phenolphthalein-type dye that is pink in alkali). Only (A) has a free para position; in (B), (C) and (D) every para position is blocked.\nKey idea: Phthalein formation = electrophilic substitution para to OH; blocked para → no dye.","traps":["Para position blocked: Check the carbon para to each OH.","More OH assumed better: Only free para positions matter."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"0.4 g mixture of NaOH, Na₂CO₃ and some inert impurities was first titrated with N/10 HCl using phenolphthalein as an indicator; 17.5 mL of HCl was required at the end point. After this methyl orange was added and titrated. 1.5 mL of same HCl was required for the next end point. The weight percentage of Na₂CO₃ in the mixture is ______. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (doubling the second titre, i.e. using 2 × 1.5 mL for Na₂CO₃), 2 (half of carbonate only with factor 53).\nWorking: phenolphthalein end point: NaOH + ½Na₂CO₃ (to HCO₃⁻) = 17.5 mL; methyl orange further 1.5 mL = HCO₃⁻ → CO₂, i.e. ½ of carbonate. Na₂CO₃ = 1.5 × 0.1 = 0.15 mmol = 15.9 mg; % = 0.0159/0.4 × 100 = 3.98 ≈ 4.\nKey idea: Methyl-orange titre after phenolphthalein = moles of Na₂CO₃ (HCO₃⁻ → H₂CO₃).","traps":["Carbonate titre doubled: Second titre alone equals mmol of Na₂CO₃.","Phenolphthalein stops at CO₂: Phenolphthalein end point = CO₃²⁻ → HCO₃⁻ only."],"topics":["Acid–base titration","Indicators"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The identification of Ni²⁺ is carried out by dimethyl glyoxime in the presence of NH₄OH. Statement II: The dimethyl glyoxime is a bidentate neutral ligand. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) accepts 'neutral' because free DMG is neutral; (A)/(B) doubt the Ni²⁺ test itself.\nWorking: Statement I is true (red Ni(dmgH)₂ precipitate in ammoniacal solution). Statement II is taken as false because DMG binds Ni²⁺ as the monoanion dmgH⁻ (a bidentate uninegative ligand) after losing one oxime H; the free DMG molecule itself is neutral, so the statement is debatable, but the accepted answer reflects the ligand as it acts in the complex.\nKey idea: In Ni(dmgH)₂ each DMG has lost one H⁺: bidentate, uninegative.","traps":["Free molecule vs bound ligand: Check the ligand's charge in the complex Ni(dmgH)₂."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Consider titration of NaOH solution versus 1.25 M oxalic acid solution. At the end point following burette readings were obtained: (i) 4.5 mL (ii) 4.5 mL (iii) 4.4 mL (iv) 4.4 mL (v) 4.4 mL. If the volume of oxalic acid taken was 10.0 mL then the molarity of the NaOH solution is ______ M. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (basicity 2 ignored, 2.84); average 4.44 mL gives 5.63 – same rounding; 5 (truncation).\nWorking: concordant reading = 4.4 mL (three equal readings). Oxalic acid is dibasic: M(NaOH) × 4.4 = 2 × 1.25 × 10.0 → M = 25/4.4 = 5.68 → 6.\nKey idea: Use the repeated (concordant) value and n₁M₁V₁ = n₂M₂V₂.","traps":["Oxalic acid treated as monobasic: H₂C₂O₄ + 2NaOH.","Average of all readings: Take the reading repeated most."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"On treating a compound with warm dil. H₂SO₄, gas X is evolved which turns K₂Cr₂O₇ paper acidified with dil. H₂SO₄ to a green compound Y. X and Y respectively are –","options":["X = SO₂, Y = Cr₂O₃","X = SO₃, Y = Cr₂O₃","X = SO₂, Y = Cr₂(SO₄)₃","X = SO₃, Y = Cr₂(SO₄)₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) SO₃ – not evolved by dil. acid and cannot reduce Cr(VI) (S already +6); (A) Cr₂O₃ – an oxide does not form in acidic aqueous medium.\nWorking: sulphite + dil. H₂SO₄ → SO₂; K₂Cr₂O₇ + H₂SO₄ + 3SO₂ → K₂SO₄ + Cr₂(SO₄)₃ (green) + H₂O.\nKey idea: The reducing gas must be SO₂ (S +4 → +6); in H₂SO₄ Cr³⁺ appears as Cr₂(SO₄)₃.","traps":["SO₃ chosen as gas: Only a reducing gas turns orange Cr(VI) green.","Green product as Cr₂O₃: In acid solution Cr(III) stays as sulphate."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Seliwanoff test and Xanthoproteic test are used for the identification of _______ and _______ respectively.","options":["Aldoses, ketoses","Proteins, ketoses","Ketoses, proteins","Ketoses, aldoses"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – Seliwanoff right but xanthoproteic misattributed to aldoses; (A) – Seliwanoff thought to detect aldoses; (B) – order reversed.\nWorking: Seliwanoff's reagent (resorcinol/HCl) gives a rapid cherry-red colour with ketoses; xanthoproteic test (conc. HNO₃, yellow) detects aromatic amino-acid residues in proteins.\nKey idea: Seliwanoff → ketose (fast dehydration to furfural derivative); xanthoproteic → nitration of aromatic rings in proteins.","traps":["Order of blanks reversed: Match each test to its blank in order.","Seliwanoff thought to detect aldoses: Seliwanoff: ketoses react fast."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"2,4-DNP test can be used to identify:","options":["Amine","Aldehyde","Ether","Halogens"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) amine – hydrazine has N, so 'amine' looks related; (C)/(D) – no reaction with ethers or halides.\nWorking: 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine condenses with carbonyl compounds (aldehydes/ketones) to give yellow-orange 2,4-DNP hydrazones.\nKey idea: 2,4-DNP reacts with C=O.","traps":["Nitrogen reagent linked to amines: DNP is a carbonyl reagent (condensation)."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Ceric ammonium nitrate and CHCl₃ / alc. KOH are used for the identification of functional groups present in ______ and ______ respectively.","options":["Alcohol, phenol","Amine, alcohol","Alcohol, amine","Amine, phenol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – order reversed; (A)/(D) – CHCl₃/alc. KOH confused with Reimer–Tiemann (phenol, but needs aq. NaOH).\nWorking: ceric ammonium nitrate gives a red colour with alcohols; CHCl₃/alc. KOH is the carbylamine test for primary amines.\nKey idea: CHCl₃ + alc. KOH + RNH₂ → RNC (carbylamine).","traps":["Chloroform/base linked to Reimer–Tiemann: Alcoholic KOH + CHCl₃ as a test = carbylamine.","Blanks reversed: Keep the order of reagents."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"An inorganic Compound 'X' on treatment with concentrated H₂SO₄ produces brown fumes and gives dark brown ring with FeSO₄ in presence of concentrated H₂SO₄. Also Compound 'X' gives precipitate 'Y', when its solution in dilute HCl is treated with H₂S gas. The precipitate 'Y' on treatment with concentrated HNO₃ followed by excess of NH₄OH further gives deep blue coloured solution. Compound 'X' is:","options":["Co(NO₃)₂","Pb(NO₂)₂","Cu(NO₃)₂","Pb(NO₃)₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Co²⁺ is not precipitated in Group II (it is Group IV); (B) nitrite also gives brown fumes but Pb gives no blue ammine; (D) Pb²⁺ is Group II and nitrate fits, but PbS/Pb²⁺ gives a white precipitate, not deep blue, with NH₄OH.\nWorking: brown NO₂ fumes with conc. H₂SO₄ and the brown ring test show nitrate; precipitation by H₂S in dilute HCl puts the cation in Group II (Cu²⁺ or Pb²⁺); CuS dissolves in conc. HNO₃ and excess NH₄OH gives deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺. So X = Cu(NO₃)₂.\nKey idea: Deep blue with excess NH₄OH after Group II precipitation = Cu²⁺.","traps":["Pb²⁺ also in Group II: The deep blue ammine complex singles out Cu²⁺.","Nitrite vs nitrate: Nitrite gives brown fumes even with dilute acid; check both tests together."],"topics":["Cation analysis","Anion analysis"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"To an aqueous solution containing ions such as Al³⁺, Zn²⁺, Ca²⁺, Fe³⁺, Ni²⁺, Ba²⁺ and Cu²⁺ was added conc. HCl, followed by H₂S. The total number of cations precipitated during this reaction is/are:","options":["1","3","4","2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 2 adds Zn²⁺ or Ni²⁺ (sulphides seen in group IV); (B)/(C) also count group III/IV ions or Fe³⁺ (forgetting HCl suppresses S²⁻).\nWorking: in strongly acidic medium only group-II cations (very low Ksp sulphides) precipitate: Cu²⁺ as CuS. Zn²⁺, Ni²⁺ need ammoniacal H₂S; Al³⁺/Fe³⁺ are group III; Ca²⁺/Ba²⁺ group V. (Fe³⁺ is reduced by H₂S, giving colloidal S, but no cation precipitates.)\nKey idea: Acid suppresses [S²⁻]; only very insoluble sulphides (Cu, Pb, Cd, Hg, Bi, As, Sb, Sn) come down.","traps":["Acidic H₂S precipitates only group II: Group IV needs NH₄OH + H₂S.","Fe³⁺ with H₂S: Sulphur is not a cation precipitate."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anion analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Reagent, 1-naphthylamine and sulphanilic acid in acetic acid is used for the detection of","options":["N₂O","NO₃⁻","NO","NO₂⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NO₃⁻ – confused with brown-ring test or with nitrate after reduction; (A), (C) gaseous oxides – not detected by this reagent.\nWorking: this is the Griess–Ilosvay test; nitrite in acid diazotises sulphanilic acid, which couples with 1-naphthylamine to give a red azo dye – specific to NO₂⁻.\nKey idea: Sulphanilic acid + 1-naphthylamine is a diazotisation–coupling test, so it needs nitrous acid, i.e. NO₂⁻.","traps":["Nitrite vs nitrate test: Diazotisation needs HNO₂ → only nitrite reacts directly."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"10.0 ml of Na₂CO₃ solution is titrated against 0.2 M HCl solution. The following titre values were obtained in 5 readings.\n4.8 ml, 4.9 ml, 5.0 ml, 5.0 ml and 5.0 ml\nBased on these readings, and convention of titrimetric estimation of concentration of Na₂CO₃ solution is ________ mM. (Round off to the Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: 49 (averaging all five readings, 4.94 mL), 100 (ignoring the factor 2), 98.\nWorking: concordant reading 5.0 mL; Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂, so M × 10.0 × 2 = 0.2 × 5.0 → M = 0.05 M = 50 mM.\nKey idea: Use the concordant value 5.0 mL, and 2 mol HCl per mol Na₂CO₃.","traps":["Averaging all readings: Titration convention uses concordant readings.","Missing factor 2: Balance the equation first."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cation analysis","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The potassium ferrocyanide solution gives a Prussian blue colour, when added to:","options":["CoCl₃","FeCl₂","CoCl₂","FeCl₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CoCl₃ and (C) CoCl₂ – cobalt salts give green/brown cobalt ferrocyanides; (B) FeCl₂ – gives white K₂Fe[Fe(CN)₆] turning blue only on air oxidation.\nWorking: ferrocyanide gives Prussian blue Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ with Fe³⁺ (FeCl₃).\nKey idea: Fe³⁺ + [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ → Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ (Prussian blue).","traps":["Fe²⁺ vs Fe³⁺: Prussian blue = Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ needs Fe³⁺ with ferrocyanide; Fe²⁺ gives a white/pale-blue precipitate.","Ferro/ferri-cyanide swapped: Ferrocyanide [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ pairs with Fe³⁺; ferricyanide pairs with Fe²⁺."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The strength of an aqueous NaOH solution is most accurately determined by titrating : (Note : consider that an appropriate indicator is used)","options":["Aq. NaOH in a volumetric flask and concentrated H₂SO₄ in a conical flask","Aq. NaOH in a pipette and aqueous oxalic acid in a burette","Aq. NaOH in a burette and concentrated H₂SO₄ in a conical flask","Aq. NaOH in a burette and aqueous oxalic acid in a conical flask"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) use concentrated H₂SO₄, not a primary standard; (B) pipettes NaOH, which is unsafe and less accurate.\nWorking: NaOH is standardised against the primary standard oxalic acid (pipetted into the conical flask) with NaOH in the burette; conc. H₂SO₄ is not a primary standard, and NaOH should not be pipetted (caustic, absorbs CO₂).\nKey idea: Use a primary standard (oxalic acid) in the flask; titrant NaOH in the burette.","traps":["Strong acid assumed better than weak acid: Accuracy needs a primary standard, not acid strength.","Apparatus placement swapped: NaOH goes in the burette."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"While titrating dilute HCl solution with aqueous NaOH, which of the following will not be required?","options":["Clamp and phenolphthalein","Pipette and distilled water","Burette and porcelain tile","Bunsen burner and measuring cylinder"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) list standard titration items; a student may think a measuring cylinder is used to add acid and hesitate on (D).\nWorking: an acid–base titration needs a burette, clamp/stand, pipette, indicator (phenolphthalein), distilled water and a white tile; no heating is needed, so a Bunsen burner (and a measuring cylinder, as the pipette is used) is not required.\nKey idea: Strong acid–strong base titration at room temperature needs no heating.","traps":["Heating confused with KMnO₄ titration: HCl/NaOH titration is done cold."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"An organic compound 'X' showing the following solubility profile is – [Scheme: 'X' with water → insoluble; 5% HCl → insoluble; 10% NaOH → soluble; 10% NaHCO₃ → insoluble]","options":["m-Cresol","Oleic acid","o-Toluidine","Benzamide"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) oleic acid is a carboxylic acid – would dissolve in NaHCO₃; (C) o-toluidine is basic – dissolves in HCl; (D) benzamide neutral – insoluble in NaOH too.\nWorking: soluble in NaOH but not in NaHCO₃ ⇒ weak acid (phenol); insoluble in HCl ⇒ not an amine; m-cresol fits. Oleic acid dissolves in NaHCO₃; o-toluidine dissolves in HCl; benzamide is neutral.\nKey idea: NaOH-soluble but NaHCO₃-insoluble = phenol.","traps":["Carboxylic acid chosen for NaOH solubility: Check NaHCO₃: only acids stronger than H₂CO₃ dissolve."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The organic compound that gives following qualitative analysis is : Test / Inference — (a) Dil. HCl : Insoluble ; (b) NaOH solution : soluble ; (c) Br₂/water : Decolourization","options":["phenol (C₆H₅OH)","aniline (C₆H₅NH₂)","cyclohexylamine (C₆H₁₁NH₂)","cyclohexanol (C₆H₁₁OH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aniline dissolves in dil. HCl and not in NaOH; (C) cyclohexylamine is basic and does not decolourise Br₂ water; (D) cyclohexanol is neither soluble in dilute NaOH nor reactive to Br₂ water.\nWorking: insoluble in acid (not basic) and soluble in NaOH (acidic) means phenol; phenol also decolourises bromine water giving 2,4,6-tribromophenol.\nKey idea: Acidic + activated aromatic ring ⇒ phenol.","traps":["Aniline chosen for bromine-water test: Use the HCl/NaOH solubility pair first.","Cyclohexanol assumed acidic enough for NaOH: Only phenol (resonance-stabilised phenoxide) dissolves in aqueous NaOH."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The peptide that gives positive ceric ammonium nitrate and carbylamine tests is :","options":["Lys-Asp","Ser-Lys","Gln-Asp","Asp-Gln"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Lys-Asp has amine but no –OH; (C)/(D) Gln-Asp have amide/acid only, plus N-terminal NH₂ for carbylamine but no –OH.\nWorking: CAN test needs an alcoholic –OH (serine side chain CH₂OH); carbylamine needs a free primary amine (lysine ε-NH₂; also the N-terminal NH₂). Only Ser-Lys has an –OH.\nKey idea: CAN is the deciding test: only serine brings an alcoholic –OH.","traps":["Every peptide has an N-terminal NH₂: Use the CAN (–OH) test to decide.","Gln amide mistaken for amine: Amides do not give carbylamine test."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid–base titration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"In an acid-base titration, 0.1 M HCl solution was added to the NaOH solution of unknown strength. Which of the following correctly shows the change of pH of the titration mixture in this experiment? [(A) pH starts high, stays nearly flat, drops steeply near equivalence, then levels low; (B) pH starts low and rises steeply (base added to acid); (C) like A but with a small bump/upturn after the drop; (D) pH falls gradually in a smooth concave curve with no sharp jump.]","options":["Curve (A)","Curve (C)","Curve (D)","Curve (B)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (D) [curve (B)] is base added to acid; option (B) [curve (C)] adds an artefact bump; option (C) [curve (D)] shows no sharp equivalence change.\nWorking: strong acid added to strong base: pH starts high (~13), changes slowly, falls sharply at equivalence, then levels at low pH – curve (A). Curve (B) is base-into-acid, curve (C) has an unphysical rise, curve (D) lacks the equivalence jump.\nKey idea: Titrant HCl lowers pH; strong–strong gives a steep vertical drop at equivalence.","traps":["Direction of titration: Read which solution is in the flask."],"topics":["Acid–base titration","Indicators"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The correct match between item 'I' and item 'II' is : Item 'I' (compound): (A) Lysine (B) Furfural (C) Benzyl alcohol (D) Styrene; Item 'II' (reagent): (P) 1-naphthol (Q) ninhydrin (R) KMnO₄ (S) Ceric ammonium nitrate","options":["(A)→(Q), (B)→(P), (C)→(S), (D)→(R)","(A)→(Q), (B)→(R), (C)→(S), (D)→(P)","(A)→(Q), (B)→(P), (C)→(R), (D)→(S)","(A)→(R), (B)→(P), (C)→(Q), (D)→(S)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps furfural and styrene (KMnO₄ vs 1-naphthol). (C) swaps benzyl alcohol and styrene (both 'oxidisable'). (D) lysine with KMnO₄ – wrong.\nWorking: lysine (α-amino acid) – ninhydrin; furfural – 1-naphthol (Molisch colour, furfural is the species that condenses); benzyl alcohol – ceric ammonium nitrate (alcohol test, red colour); styrene – KMnO₄ (Baeyer test for C=C).\nKey idea: Ninhydrin–amino acid and KMnO₄–alkene are anchors; CAN is the alcohol test.","traps":["Benzyl alcohol with KMnO₄: Use the specific test: CAN for –OH; KMnO₄ decolourisation for C=C.","Molisch reagent and furfural: Molisch colour arises from furfural derivatives + 1-naphthol."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional group tests","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct match between Item I and Item II is :- Item I: (A) Ester test, (B) Carbylamine test, (C) Phthalein dye test. Item II: (P) Tyr, (Q) Asp, (R) Ser, (S) Lys.","options":["(A)→(Q); (B)→(S); (C)→(P)","(A)→(R); (B)→(Q); (C)→(P)","(A)→(Q); (B)→(S); (C)→(R)","(A)→(R); (B)→(S); (C)→(Q)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) A→Ser: ester test is for –COOH, Ser has –OH; B→Asp wrong (no free extra NH₂). (C) C→Ser: alcoholic OH does not give phthalein dye. (D) A→Ser and C→Asp wrong.\nWorking: ester test detects an extra –COOH → Asp (aspartic acid, side-chain CH₂COOH); carbylamine test detects a free primary –NH₂ → Lys (side-chain (CH₂)₄NH₂); phthalein dye test detects phenolic –OH → Tyr. Ser (alcoholic –OH) gives none of these.\nKey idea: Ask which side chain carries COOH, NH₂, phenolic OH: Asp, Lys, Tyr.","traps":["Ser alcoholic OH treated as phenolic: Phthalein dye needs phenolic OH (aromatic ring) – only Tyr.","Every amino acid gives carbylamine: Distinguish by side-chain group; Lys has the second amino group."]}],"m04-thermo":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":53,"q":"Two vessels A and B are connected via stopcock. The vessel A is filled with a gas at a certain pressure. The entire assembly is immersed in water (stirred, with a thermometer dipping in it) and is allowed to come to thermal equilibrium with water. After opening the stopcock the gas from vessel A expands into vessel B and no change in temperature is observed in the thermometer. Which of the following statement is true ?","options":["dw≠0","dq≠0","dU≠0","The pressure in the vessel B before opening the stopcock is zero"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) false: p_ext = 0 so w = 0. (B) false: no temperature change of the bath, q = 0. (C) false: q = w = 0 so dU = 0 (ideal gas at constant T). (D) true: B must be evacuated for free expansion.\nKey idea: Expansion into vacuum means p_ext = 0, so no work, no heat and no change in internal energy.","traps":["Free expansion assumed to do work: Work is −p_ext dV and p_ext = 0 when expanding into vacuum, so w = 0.","Heat flow assumed in water-bath experiment: No temperature change of the bath is observed, so q = 0."],"topics":["First law","Work & heat"]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"System & state functions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":53,"q":"Which of the following properties will change when system containing solution 1 will become solution 2?\nSolution 1: 10 mol of solute x + 10 L of water\n→ Solution 2: 1 L of solution 1 + 1 mol of solute x + 1 L of water","options":["Gibbs free energy","Molar heat capacity","Density","Concentration"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Gibbs free energy: extensive, total amount changes, correct. (B) Molar heat capacity: intensive (per mole). (C) Density: intensive and composition unchanged. (D) Concentration: 10 mol/10 L vs 2 mol/2 L, unchanged.\nKey idea: Check the ratio of solute to solvent first; if it is unchanged only extensive properties change.","traps":["Extensive vs intensive property confused: Solution 2 = 2 mol in about 2 L, same ratio as 10 mol in 10 L, so intensive properties are unchanged; only the total G changes.","Molar quantity mistaken for extensive: Any per-mole quantity is intensive."]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"A perfect gas (0.1 mol) having C̄v = 1.50R (independent of temperature) undergoes the transformation shown below from point 1 to point 4. If each step is reversible, the total work done (w) while going from point 1 to point 4 is (–) ______ J (nearest integer)\n[Given: R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"304","range":[304.0,304.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 101 (using 1 atm); 3 (no conversion to J); 0.304 or 304000 (cm³ slips); 203 (net cycle area idea, subtracting a 1 atm step). Correct 304.\nWorking: steps 1→2 and 3→4 are at constant volume (w = 0); step 2→3 is at constant p = 3.00 atm with ΔV = 2000 − 1000 cm³ = 1.00 L, so w = −pΔV = −3.00 L atm = −3.00 × 101.3 J ≈ −304 J.\nKey idea: Only the horizontal step on a P-V diagram does work; w = −p ΔV with consistent units.","traps":["Irrelevant given data used in calculation: Work on a P-V diagram is the area under the path; isochoric steps contribute zero.","kJ and J mixed in final answer: 1000 cm³ = 1 L; 3 L atm × 101.3 = 304 J."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S2C21-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"System & state functions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":52,"q":"An ideal gas undergoes a cyclic transformation starting from the point A and coming back to the same point by tracing the path A→B→C→D→A as shown in the three cases below.\nChoose the correct option regarding ΔU :","options":["ΔU(Case-I) > ΔU(Case-II) > ΔU(Case-III)","ΔU(Case-III) > ΔU(Case-II) > ΔU(Case-I)","ΔU(Case-I) > ΔU(Case-III) > ΔU(Case-II)","ΔU(Case-I) = ΔU(Case-II) = ΔU(Case-III)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)-(C) rank by enclosed area (work), wrong. (D) correct: ΔU = 0 for all three cycles.\nKey idea: Any state function returns to its initial value over a cycle.","traps":["State function zero for cyclic process: Area gives work, a path function; ΔU over any cycle is 0.","Work area confused with internal energy: The question is about ΔU, not w; the graph data is irrelevant."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C02-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The enthalpy of formation of ethane (C₂H₆) from ethylene by addition of hydrogen where the bond-energies of C–H, C–C, C=C, H–H are 414 kJ, 347 kJ, 615 kJ and 435 kJ respectively is – ______ kJ","type":"num","answer":"125","range":[125.0,125.0],"explain":"Common slips: −539 or other values if all bonds of both molecules counted wrongly (e.g. 4 C–H on ethene vs 6 C–H on ethane mismatched); +125 sign reversed; +268 if only C=C and C–C are compared.\nWorking: bonds broken C=C + H–H = 615 + 435 = 1050; formed C–C + 2 C–H = 347 + 828 = 1175; ΔH = 1050 − 1175 = −125 kJ.\nKey idea: Only C=C and H–H break; C–C and two new C–H form.","traps":["Products minus reactants: With bond enthalpies use reactants − products.","New C–H bonds miscounted: Only two new C–H bonds form."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Three moles of an ideal gas are compressed isothermally from 60 L to 20 L using constant pressure of 5 atm. Heat exchange Q for the compression is − ______ Lit. atm.","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: −200 entered (double minus); 3 × value (using moles); a reversible-work attempt with nRT ln(Vf/Vi) (T not given, so it stalls); 400 from a wrong ΔV.\nWorking: isothermal ideal gas, ΔU = 0. w = −pₑₓ(V_f − V_i) = −5(20 − 60) = +200 L atm; q = −w = −200 L atm, so the blank is 200.\nKey idea: Constant external pressure ⇒ irreversible; q = pₑₓΔV = −200 L atm.","traps":["Reversible formula used: Constant external pressure means irreversible work.","Sign handled twice: Enter magnitude only."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Enthalpy of neutralisation of strong monobasic acid with strong monoacidic base is always −57 kJ mol⁻¹.\nReason (R): Enthalpy of neutralisation is the amount of heat liberated when one mole of H⁺ ions furnished by acid combine with one mole of ⁻OH ions furnished by base to form one mole of water.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): student rejects A because of \"always\"/rounded value, though the constancy is the very point. (C): student accepts both but misses that the common net ionic reaction (R) is why the value is constant. (A): student doubts the definition in R.\nWorking: for any strong acid–strong base pair both are fully ionised, so the only net change is H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l); hence ΔH is essentially constant (≈ −57 kJ mol⁻¹) – A true, and R (heat per mole of water from H⁺ + OH⁻) is exactly why – B. Wording caveat: \"always\" and the rounded −57 (actual ≈ −57.1 to −57.3, slightly condition-dependent) could tempt (D).\nKey idea: Strong acid + strong base always reduces to H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O, so ΔH_neut is constant (≈ −57 kJ mol⁻¹); R explains A.","traps":["\"Always\" treated as automatically false: For strong acid–strong base the net reaction is identical, so the value really is constant (≈ −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹).","Reason not linked to assertion: The common net ionic reaction in R is precisely why the enthalpy is the same for every pair."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The heat of combustion of solid benzoic acid at constant volume is −321.30 kJ at 27 °C. The heat of combustion at constant pressure is (−321.30 − xR) kJ, the value of x is ____.","type":"num","answer":"150","range":[150.0,150.0],"explain":"Common slips: −150 or 0.5: sign or factor slips. −750: H₂O counted as gas (Δn_g = 10 − 7.5 = +2.5). 13.5: T taken as 27 instead of 300 K.\nWorking: C₆H₅COOH(s) + 15/2 O₂(g) → 7CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l); Δn_g = 7 − 7.5 = −0.5; ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT = −321.30 + (−0.5)(300)R = −321.30 − 150R → x = 150.\nKey idea: Only gases count: Δn_g = −1/2; T = 300 K.","traps":["Water counted as gas: At 27 °C water is liquid.","Temperature in °C: Convert to 300 K."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Combustion of 1 mole of benzene is expressed as C₆H₆(l) + 15/2 O₂(g) → 6CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l).\nThe standard enthalpy of combustion of 2 mol of benzene is −'x' kJ. x = ______.\nGiven:\n1. standard enthalpy of formation of 1 mol of C₆H₆(l), for the reaction 6C(graphite) + 3H₂(g) → C₆H₆(l) is 48.5 kJ mol⁻¹.\n2. Standard enthalpy of formation of 1 mol of CO₂(g), for the reaction C(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) is −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹.\n3. Standard enthalpy of formation of 1 mol of H₂O(l), for the reaction H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → H₂O(l) is −286 kJ mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"6535","range":[6535.0,6535.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3267.5 (per mole), 6341 (sign of 48.5 flipped: −3170.5 × 2).\nWorking: ΔcH = 6(−393.5) + 3(−286) − 48.5 = −3267.5 kJ mol⁻¹; for 2 mol −6535 kJ.\nKey idea: ΔH = ΣΔfH(products) − ΣΔfH(reactants), then ×2.","traps":["Per-mole answer given: Question asks for 2 mol.","Sign of benzene formation enthalpy: Subtract +48.5 (makes value more negative)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"An ideal gas, C̄v = (5/2)R, is expanded adiabatically against a constant pressure of 1 atm until it doubles in volume. If the initial temperature and pressure is 298 K and 5 atm, respectively then the final temperature is ______ K (nearest integer).\n[c̄v is the molar heat capacity at constant volume]","type":"num","answer":"274","range":[274.0,274.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 226 (using reversible TV^(γ−1) = const with γ = 7/5: 298 × 2^(−0.4)), 238/250 (using P₁ instead of P_ext).\nWorking: irreversible adiabatic: nC̄v(T₂ − T₁) = −P_ext(V₂ − V₁) = −P_ext·V₁ = −1 × nR(298)/5; (5/2)(T₂ − 298) = −59.6 → T₂ = 274.2 K ≈ 274.\nKey idea: For constant external pressure, use ΔU = −P_ext ΔV, not PV^γ relations.","traps":["Reversible adiabatic formula applied: Constant P_ext ⇒ irreversible ⇒ energy balance.","Initial pressure used as external pressure: Work uses P_ext = 1 atm."],"topics":["First law","Types of processes"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For the reaction at 298 K, 2A + B → C, ΔH = 400 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = 0.2 kJ mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. The reaction will become spontaneous above ______ K.","type":"num","answer":"2000","range":[2000.0,2000.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (kJ/J mismatch if ΔS taken in J), 80 or 0.5 (inverted ratio), 298 (given T used).\nWorking: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0 when T > ΔH/ΔS = 400/0.2 = 2000 K.\nKey idea: T = ΔH/ΔS when both in kJ.","traps":["Given 298 K used as answer: 298 K is irrelevant; solve ΔG = 0.","kJ/J mixed: Both values here are in kJ."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Consider the figure provided below.\n1 mol of an ideal gas is kept in a cylinder, fitted with a piston, at the position A, at 18°C. If the piston is moved to position B, keeping the temperature unchanged, then 'x' L atm work is done in this reversible process.\nx = ________ L atm. (nearest integer)\n[Given : Absolute temperature = °C + 273.15, R = 0.08206 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"55","range":[55.0,55.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 52 (using V₂ = 90 L), −55 (sign), 24 (forgetting ln factor).\nWorking: V₁ = 10 L, V₂ = 10 + 90 = 100 L, T = 291.15 K; |w| = 2.303 × 1 × 0.08206 × 291.15 × log 10 = 55.0 L atm (work done by gas; w = −55).\nKey idea: Final volume = 10 + 90 = 100 L, so log term = 1.","traps":["Using 90 L as final volume: Add the initial 10 L.","Sign of work: Report magnitude as asked."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr08S1C25-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Δ_vapH° for water is +40.79 kJ mol⁻¹ at 1 bar and 100°C. Change in internal energy for this vapourisation under same condition is ______ kJ mol⁻¹. (Integer answer) (Given R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"38","range":[38.0,38.0],"explain":"Common slips: 40.79 + 3.10 = 43.89 → 44 (sign error); using T = 100 gives 40.79 − 0.83 → 40; forgetting /1000 gives a negative value.\nWorking: ΔU = ΔH − Δn_gRT = 40.79 − (1)(8.3 × 373)/1000 = 40.79 − 3.10 = 37.69 ≈ 38.\nKey idea: Only the gas formed counts: Δn_g = 1.","traps":["Sign of Δn_gRT: ΔU = ΔH − Δn_gRT for gas formation.","°C used in RT: Convert to 373 K and J to kJ."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"When equal volume of 1 M HCl and 1 M H₂SO₄ are separately neutralised by excess volume of 1 M NaOH solution, x and y kJ of heat is liberated respectively. The value of y/x is ____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 if one assumes heat of neutralisation is the same per mole of acid.\nWorking: H₂SO₄ supplies twice the H⁺ per litre; heat ∝ moles of water formed, so y/x = 2.\nKey idea: Strong acid–strong base: ~57 kJ per mole of water; H₂SO₄ forms 2 mol water per mole.","traps":["Equal heat for equal molarity: Count H⁺, not acid molecules."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The heat of solution of anhydrous CuSO₄ and CuSO₄·5H₂O are −70 kJ mol⁻¹ and +12 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively.\nThe heat of hydration of CuSO₄ to CuSO₄·5H₂O is −x kJ. The value of x is ____. (nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"82","range":[82.0,82.0],"explain":"Common slips: 58 if signs are mishandled (−70 + 12); −82 (writing ΔH itself instead of x).\nWorking: ΔhydH = ΔsolH(anhydrous) − ΔsolH(hydrate) = −70 − 12 = −82 kJ, x = 82.\nKey idea: Anhydrous → hydrate → solution equals anhydrous → solution directly.","traps":["Sign of hydrate solution enthalpy: ΔH(hyd) = ΔH₁ − ΔH₂ with signs."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"When ΔH_vap = 30 kJ/mol and ΔS_vap = 75 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, then the temperature of vapour, at one atmosphere is ______ K.","type":"num","answer":"400","range":[400.0,400.0],"explain":"Common slips: Forgetting kJ → J gives 0.4.\nWorking: at the boiling point ΔG = 0, so T = ΔH/ΔS = 30000/75 = 400 K.\nKey idea: Convert units, then divide.","traps":["kJ vs J: Convert 30 kJ to 30000 J."],"topics":["Gibbs energy","Entropy"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Choose the correct option for free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition from the following :","options":["q = 0, ΔT = 0, w = 0","q = 0, ΔT ≠ 0, w = 0","q ≠ 0, ΔT = 0, w = 0","q = 0, ΔT < 0, w ≠ 0"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): thinks expansion must cool or change temperature. (C): forgets adiabatic means q = 0. (D): applies reversible adiabatic expansion (work done, gas cools).\nWorking: Adiabatic → q = 0; free expansion (pₑₓ = 0) → w = 0; so ΔU = 0 and for an ideal gas U depends only on T, so ΔT = 0.\nKey idea: q = 0 and w = 0 give ΔU = 0; ideal gas U = f(T) only, so ΔT = 0.","traps":["Free expansion confused with reversible adiabatic expansion: Ask whether there is any opposing pressure; pₑₓ = 0 means no work.","Assuming expansion changes temperature: For an ideal gas, ΔU = 0 means ΔT = 0."],"topics":["First law","Types of processes"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"For a certain reaction at 300 K, K = 10, then ΔG° for the same reaction is −______ × 10⁻¹ kJ mol⁻¹.\n(Given R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"57","range":[57.0,57.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25 (using log without 2.303 factor: 2.49 kJ), 5744 or 5.7 (wrong scaling), 6 (rounded kJ).\nWorking: ΔG° = −2.303 × 8.314 × 300 × log 10 = −5744 J = −5.744 kJ = −57.44 × 10⁻¹ kJ → 57.\nKey idea: ln 10 = 2.303, so ΔG° = −2.303 × 8.314 × 300 J ≈ −5.74 kJ.","traps":["Dropping 2.303 with log: ln K = 2.303 log K.","×10⁻¹ kJ scaling: −5.744 kJ = −57.44 × 10⁻¹ kJ."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"If three moles of an ideal gas at 300 K expand isothermally from 30 dm³ to 45 dm³ against a constant opposing pressure of 80 kPa, then the amount of heat transferred is _______ J.","type":"num","answer":"1200","range":[1200.0,1200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: ≈3034 J (using reversible 2.303nRT log(45/30)), −1200 (sign of q), 1.2 (kPa·dm³ = J slip → kJ), 1200000 (unit conversion error).\nWorking: irreversible: w = −p_ex ΔV = −80×10³ Pa × 15×10⁻³ m³ = −1200 J; ideal gas, isothermal → ΔU = 0, so q = +1200 J.\nKey idea: Constant external pressure means irreversible work: only p_ex and ΔV matter.","traps":["Reversible formula used: Constant opposing pressure → w = −p_ex ΔV.","kPa × dm³ unit slip: 1 kPa·dm³ = 1 J."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which of the following is not correct?","options":["ΔG is positive for a spontaneous reaction","ΔG is positive for a non-spontaneous reaction","ΔG is zero for a reversible reaction","ΔG is negative for a spontaneous reaction"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are all true statements; a student reading 'correct' instead of 'not correct' picks (D).\nWorking: spontaneous ⇒ ΔG < 0; (A) is false. ΔG = 0 at equilibrium/reversible condition.\nKey idea: Spontaneous: ΔG < 0.","traps":["Choosing a correct statement: Underline 'not' before reading options."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Standard enthalpy of vapourisation for CCl₄ is 30.5 kJ mol⁻¹. Heat required for vapourisation of 284 g of CCl₄ at constant temperature is ________ kJ.\n(Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹; C = 12, Cl = 35.5)","type":"num","answer":"56","range":[56.0,56.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 30.5 (ignores mass), 8662 (multiplies by grams), 57 (rounding up 56.25).\nWorking: M(CCl₄) = 154 g/mol; n = 284/154 = 1.844 mol; q = 1.844 × 30.5 = 56.25 kJ ≈ 56.\nKey idea: Heat = (mass / molar mass) × ΔvapH.","traps":["Grams used instead of moles: Convert 284 g to 1.844 mol first."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"An ideal gas undergoes a cyclic transformation starting from the point A and coming back to the same point by tracing the path A → B → C → A as shown in the diagram below. The total work done in the process is _______ J.","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −200 (sign convention as work by gas), 400 (only B→C), 600 or 800 (adding areas instead of subtracting), 0 (assuming cyclic work is zero).\nWorking: A→B w = −PΔV = −10 kPa × 20 dm³ = −200 J; B→C (straight line, mean P 20 kPa, ΔV = −20 dm³) w = +400 J; C→A isochoric w = 0. Net w (on gas) = +200 J = triangle area ½ × 20 × 20 kPa·dm³.\nKey idea: For a cycle, |w| = enclosed area = ½ × 20 kPa × 20 dm³ = 200 J.","traps":["Axes interchanged: Read each point as (P, V) carefully; compute ∫P dV per step.","Cyclic work assumed zero: Only state functions vanish over a cycle; work is a path function."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Jan30S1C27-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Two reactions are given below:\n2Fe(s) + 3/2 O₂(g) → Fe₂O₃(s), ΔH° = −822 kJ/mol\nC(s) + 1/2 O₂(g) → CO(g), ΔH° = −110 kJ/mol\nThen enthalpy change for following reaction 3C(s) + Fe₂O₃(s) → 2Fe(s) + 3CO(g) is _______ kJ/mol.","type":"num","answer":"492","range":[492.0,492.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −492 (sign error when reversing), 712 (forgetting ×3), −1152 (adding both).\nWorking: ΔH = 3(−110) − (−822) = −330 + 822 = +492 kJ/mol.\nKey idea: Reverse the Fe₂O₃ formation (+822) and add 3 × CO formation (−330).","traps":["Sign not changed on reversal: Reversed equation changes sign of ΔH."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Consider the following reaction at 298 K: (3/2)O₂(g) ⇌ O₃(g); K_P = 2.47 × 10⁻²⁹. ΔrG⊖ for the reaction is ________ kJ. (Given R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"163","range":[163.0,163.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −163 (sign dropped), 70.9 (log₁₀ used without 2.303), 163198 (J not converted).\nWorking: ΔrG° = −RT ln K = −8.314 × 298 × ln(2.47 × 10⁻²⁹) = −2477.6 × (−65.87) ≈ 163.2 kJ → 163.\nKey idea: Very small K ⇒ large positive ΔG°.","traps":["Sign of ΔG°: K < 1 ⇒ ΔG° > 0.","log vs ln / J vs kJ: Use 2.303 log; divide by 1000."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"If 5 moles of an ideal gas expands from 10 L to a volume of 100 L at 300 K under isothermal and reversible condition then work, w, is −x J. The value of x is ______. (Given R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"28721","range":[28721.0,28721.0],"explain":"Common slips: 28716 (ln 10 exactly – the accepted answer uses 2.303); 12471 (log term dropped); 2872 (wrong n).\nWorking: w = −2.303 nRT log(V₂/V₁) = −2.303 × 5 × 8.314 × 300 × 1 = −28721 J (−28716 J with ln 10 exactly).\nKey idea: log(100/10) = 1, so x = 2.303 nRT.","traps":["ln vs 2.303 log rounding: Use 2.303 as NCERT does."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Consider the graph of Gibbs free energy G vs Extent of reaction shown below. The number of statement/s from the following which are true with respect to points (a), (b) and (c) is ______\nA. Reaction is spontaneous at (a) and (b)\nB. Reaction is at equilibrium at point (b) and non-spontaneous at point (c)\nC. Reaction is spontaneous at (a) and non-spontaneous at (c)\nD. Reaction is non-spontaneous at (a) and (b)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3: accepts A by treating (b) as still spontaneous. 1: rejects C or B because (c) G is lower than at (a). 0: confuses value of G with sign of its slope.\nWorking: at (a) slope dG/dξ < 0 → forward spontaneous; (b) is the minimum → equilibrium (not spontaneous); at (c) slope > 0 → forward non-spontaneous. B and C true → 2.\nKey idea: Spontaneity at a point is set by the slope dG/dξ there, not by how high G is.","traps":["Equilibrium point called spontaneous: At the minimum slope is zero → equilibrium.","G value vs slope: Use the slope sign at each point."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Apr06S1C17-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The value of log K for the reaction A ⇌ B at 298 K is ______. (Nearest integer)\nGiven: ΔH° = −54.07 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔS° = 10 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n(Take 2.303 × 8.314 × 298 = 5705)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9: ignores TΔS (54070/5705 ≈ 9.48). Large or tiny values: forgets kJ → J conversion. Negative: sign slip in ΔG° = −2.303RT log K.\nWorking: ΔG° = −54070 − 298×10 = −57050 J mol⁻¹; log K = 57050/5705 = 10.\nKey idea: Convert ΔH to J, then log K = −ΔG°/5705.","traps":["Units of ΔH and ΔS mixed: Convert ΔH° to J before combining.","Sign of log K: −ΔG°/2.303RT is positive for ΔG° < 0."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the following data\nHeat of combustion of H₂(g) = −241.8 kJ mol⁻¹\nHeat of combustion of C(s) = −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nHeat of combustion of C₂H₅OH(l) = −1234.7 kJ mol⁻¹\nThe heat of formation of C₂H₅OH(l) is (−) ________ kJ mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"278","range":[278.0,278.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 2 H₂ instead of 3 gives ~36; forgetting to subtract ethanol's combustion gives ~1512; sign reversal gives +278.\nWorking: ΔfH = 2(−393.5) + 3(−241.8) − (−1234.7) = −787.0 − 725.4 + 1234.7 = −277.7 ≈ −278 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: ΔfH = Σ ΔcH(elements) − ΔcH(compound).","traps":["Hydrogen multiplier miscounted: Balance 2C + 3H₂ + ½O₂ → C₂H₅OH first.","Reactant/product sign reversal: Elements minus compound for combustion data."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"When a 60 W electric heater is immersed in a gas for 100 s in a constant volume container with adiabatic walls, the temperature of the gas rises by 5°C. The heat capacity of the given gas is ______ J K⁻¹ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1200","range":[1200.0,1200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 (forgets to multiply by time, uses 60/5), 6000 (reports q), 22 (ΔT wrongly converted to 278 K: 6000/278 ≈ 21.6).\nWorking: q = P·t = 60 × 100 = 6000 J; C = q/ΔT = 6000/5 = 1200 J K⁻¹ (ΔT of 5°C = 5 K).\nKey idea: Electrical energy Pt all goes into the gas at constant volume: C = Pt/ΔT.","traps":["°C to K conversion for ΔT: A change of 5 °C equals 5 K.","Power used as energy: Energy = power × time."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"For complete combustion of ethene, C₂H₄(g) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), the amount of heat produced as measured in bomb calorimeter is 1406 kJ mol⁻¹ at 300 K. The minimum value of TΔS needed to reach equilibrium is (−) ______ kJ. (Nearest integer) Given: R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"1411","range":[1411.0,1411.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1406 (taking bomb value as ΔH), 1401 (sign of ΔngRT wrong), 1416 (Δng taken as −4); counting water as gas gives Δng = 0 and 1406.\nWorking: bomb calorimeter gives ΔU = −1406 kJ; Δng = 2 − 4 = −2; ΔH = ΔU + ΔngRT = −1406 − 2(8.3 × 300)/1000 = −1410.98 kJ; at equilibrium ΔG = 0 so TΔS = ΔH ≈ −1411 kJ.\nKey idea: Bomb = constant volume = ΔU; only gases count in Δng; at equilibrium TΔS = ΔH.","traps":["Bomb value taken as ΔH: Bomb calorimeter is constant volume → ΔU.","Δng sign or count wrong: Δng = 2 − (1 + 3) = −2; ΔH more negative than ΔU."],"topics":["Gibbs energy","Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"At 25°C, the enthalpy of the following processes are given :\nH₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2OH(g) ΔH° = 78 kJ mol⁻¹\nH₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → H₂O(g) ΔH° = −242 kJ mol⁻¹\nH₂(g) → 2H(g) ΔH° = 436 kJ mol⁻¹\n½O₂(g) → O(g) ΔH° = 249 kJ mol⁻¹\nWhat would be the value of X for the following reaction ? ______ (Nearest integer)\nH₂O(g) → H(g) + OH(g) ΔH° = X kJ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"499","range":[499.0,499.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using O(g) data gives values like 748 or 926 (full atomisation). Forgetting the ½ factors gives 760 or so.\nWorking: H₂O(g) → H₂(g) + ½O₂(g), +242; ½H₂(g) → H(g), ½ × 436 = +218; ½H₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → OH(g), ½ × 78 = +39. Adding: X = 242 + 218 + 39 = 499 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: X = 242 + 218 + 39 = 499 kJ mol⁻¹; the O-atomisation value is not needed.","traps":["O atomisation data used: Build the target equation and use only what cancels.","Halving missed: Scale each equation to the target stoichiometry."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"0.3 g of ethane undergoes combustion at 27 °C in a bomb calorimeter. The temperature of calorimeter system (including the water) is found to rise by 0.5 °C. The heat evolved during combustion of ethane at constant pressure is ________ kJ mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Given : The heat capacity of the calorimeter system is 20 kJ K⁻¹, R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Assume ideal gas behaviour. Atomic mass of C and H are 12 and 1 g mol⁻¹ respectively]","type":"num","answer":"1006","range":[1006.0,1006.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1000 (reporting ΔU), 994 (Δng sign reversed), 999 (water taken as gas, Δng = +0.5).\nWorking: q_V = 20 × 0.5 = 10 kJ for 0.01 mol → ΔU = −1000 kJ mol⁻¹; C₂H₆ + 7/2 O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O(l), Δng = −2.5; ΔH = −1000 − 2.5 × 8.3 × 300 × 10⁻³ = −1006.2 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Δng = 2 − (1 + 3.5) = −2.5 (water liquid at 27 °C), so |ΔH| > |ΔU| by ≈ 6 kJ.","traps":["ΔU reported as heat at constant pressure: Bomb gives q_V; convert with Δng RT.","Water counted as gas in Δng: At 300 K water is liquid; Δng = −2.5."],"topics":["Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","Heat capacity & calorimetry"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"For independent processes at 300 K\nProcess | ΔH/kJ mol⁻¹ | ΔS/J K⁻¹\nA | −25 | −80\nB | −22 | 40\nC | 25 | −50\nD | 22 | 20\nThe number of non-spontaneous processes from the following is __________","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (A judged non-spontaneous by mixing kJ and J, or by thinking ΔS<0 always loses), 3 (A counted as non-spontaneous since ΔG is only −1), 0 or 4 from forgetting the ×10⁻³ factor.\nWorking: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS: A −25 + 24 = −1 kJ (spontaneous); B −22 − 12 = −34 (spontaneous); C 25 + 15 = +40 (non-spontaneous); D 22 − 6 = +16 (non-spontaneous). Count = 2.\nKey idea: Convert ΔS to kJ: TΔS = 0.3 × ΔS(J K⁻¹) kJ.","traps":["J vs kJ mismatch: Multiply ΔS by 10⁻³ before using T.","Borderline case A: Any negative ΔG means spontaneous."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is subjected to changes as shown in the graph. The magnitude of the work done (by the system or on the system) is ________ J (nearest integer). Graph (P in bar vs V in L): 1 (20 L, 1.0 bar) → 2 (40 L, 1.0 bar) horizontal; 2 → 3 (40 L, 0.5 bar) vertical; 3 → 1 along a curve (isotherm) back to (20 L, 1.0 bar). Given: log 2 = 0.3, ln 10 = 2.3.","type":"num","answer":"620","range":[620.0,620.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2000 (only the isobaric leg); 1380 (only the isotherm); 3380 (adding magnitudes instead of signed works); 1386 (using ln 2 = 0.693 not given values).\nWorking: W(1→2) by gas = 1 bar × 20 L = 2000 J; W(2→3) = 0; 3→1 isothermal compression (PV = 20 L bar) work on gas = 20 × 2.3 × 0.3 = 13.8 L bar = 1380 J; net |W| = 2000 − 1380 = 620 J.\nKey idea: Net work = area enclosed: rectangle-type top (2000 J) minus area under isotherm (nRT ln2 = 1380 J).","traps":["Signs of leg works: Net work = algebraic sum (enclosed area).","L bar to J: 1 L bar = 100 J."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"An athlete is given 100 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) for energy. This is equivalent to 1800 kJ of energy. The 50% of this energy gained is utilized by the athlete for sports activities at the event. In order to avoid storage of energy, the weight of extra water he would need to perspire is ________ g (Nearest integer). Assume that there is no other way of consuming stored energy. Given : The enthalpy of evaporation of water is 45 kJ mol⁻¹. Molar mass of C, H & O are 12, 1 and 16 g mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"360","range":[360.0,360.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 720 if the full 1800 kJ is used; 20 if moles reported; working via moles of glucose is unnecessary.\nWorking: energy left = 900 kJ; 900/45 = 20 mol H₂O = 20 × 18 = 360 g.\nKey idea: Only half the energy (900 kJ) must be removed by sweating; 45 kJ per 18 g.","traps":["Full energy used instead of the unused half: Energy to remove = energy not used = 900 kJ.","Molar masses of C, H, O tempt a glucose mole calculation: The energy value is already given for 100 g."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"28.0 L of CO₂ is produced on complete combustion of 16.8 L gaseous mixture of ethene and methane at 25 °C and 1 atm. Heat evolved during the combustion process is ______ kJ. Given: ΔH_c(CH₄) = –900 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔH_c(C₂H₄) = –1400 kJ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"847","range":[847.0,847.0],"explain":"Common slips: 925 kJ (using 22.4 L mol⁻¹); 15680 or similar (multiplying litres directly by ΔH); 700/225 (only one component counted).\nWorking: x + y = 16.8, 2x + y = 28.0 → ethene 11.2 L, methane 5.6 L; at 25 °C, 1 atm V_m = RT/P ≈ 24.47 L → 0.458 mol and 0.229 mol; heat = 0.458×1400 + 0.229×900 ≈ 847 kJ.\nKey idea: Volumes give the mole ratio; the stated 25 °C forces n = PV/RT, not V/22.4.","traps":["22.4 L only at STP: Read the temperature: at 298 K, 1 atm use PV = nRT (≈24.5 L mol⁻¹).","CO₂ from ethene is two per molecule: Write CH₄ → CO₂, C₂H₄ → 2CO₂ before setting up equations."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Consider the following reaction approaching equilibrium at 27 °C and 1 atm pressure\nA + B ⇌ C + D (k_f = 10³, k_r = 10²)\nThe standard Gibb's energy change (Δ_rG°) at 27 °C is (−) ___________ kJ mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer).\n(Given : R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and ln 10 = 2.3)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 17 or 11 (using 10³ or 10² alone as K); 5727 (forgetting J→kJ); 2 (using log not ln: 8.3 × 300 × 1 = 2490 J).\nWorking: K = k_f/k_r = 10³/10² = 10; Δ_rG° = −RT ln K = −8.3 × 300 × 2.3 × 1 = −5727 J mol⁻¹ ≈ −5.7 kJ mol⁻¹ → 6.\nKey idea: At equilibrium rates are equal, so K = k_f/k_r.","traps":["Kelvin and kJ conversion: Use 300 K and divide by 1000.","K from rate constants: K = kf/kr."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following relations are correct?\n(A) ΔU = q + pΔV\n(B) ΔG = ΔH − TΔS\n(C) ΔS = q_rev/T\n(D) ΔH = ΔU − ΔnRT\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["A and B only","B and C only","C and D only","B and D only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"A wrong: with w = −p_extΔV, ΔU = q − pΔV. D wrong: ΔH = ΔU + ΔngRT. Options (A), (C), (D) each pair one correct relation with one sign-flipped one.\nKey idea: IUPAC sign convention: work done on the system is positive, so expansion work is −pΔV; enthalpy adds pΔV.","traps":["Sign of pV work: Write w = −p_extΔV, then ΔU = q + w.","ΔH vs ΔU relation sign: H = U + pV, so ΔH is larger when Δng > 0."],"topics":["First law","Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","Gibbs energy"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"When 2 litre of ideal gas expands isothermally into vacuum to a total volume of 6 litre, the change in internal energy is ______ J. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −405 or −400 (p ΔV with 1 atm), −nRT ln 3 type values.\nWorking: U of an ideal gas depends only on T; the process is isothermal, so ΔU = 0 (also p_ext = 0, so w = 0 and q = 0).\nKey idea: Internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on T; isothermal means ΔU = 0 whatever the path.","traps":["Volumes invite a work calculation: Expansion into vacuum: pex = 0, and ideal gas at constant T has ΔU = 0."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"1 mole of ideal gas is allowed to expand reversibly and adiabatically from a temperature of 27 °C. The work done is 3 kJ mol⁻¹. The final temperature of the gas is ____ K (Nearest integer). Given Cᵥ = 20 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"150","range":[150.0,150.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 450 (adding ΔT – sign error), 123 (subtracting from 273), 255/345 using Cₚ.\nWorking: q = 0 ⇒ ΔU = w = −3000 J = nCᵥΔT ⇒ ΔT = −150 K ⇒ T = 300 − 150 = 150 K.\nKey idea: Expansion work by the gas lowers U; adiabatic so T falls by w/Cᵥ.","traps":["Expansion lowers temperature: Adiabatic expansion: gas does work at the cost of internal energy.","Celsius to Kelvin: Convert 27 °C to 300 K first."],"topics":["First law","Types of processes"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The enthalpy change for the conversion of ½Cl₂(g) to Cl⁻(aq) is (−) ________ kJ mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer) Given : ΔdisH°(Cl₂(g)) = 240 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔegH°(Cl(g)) = −350 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔhydH°(Cl⁻(g)) = −380 kJ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"610","range":[610.0,610.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 490 (using full 240 instead of ½); 850 or 970 (sign slips); 230 (forgetting hydration).\nWorking: ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g) +120; Cl(g) → Cl⁻(g) −350; Cl⁻(g) → Cl⁻(aq) −380; total = 120 − 350 − 380 = −610 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Only ½ mol Cl₂ is broken: +120 kJ.","traps":["Full bond enthalpy used for ½Cl₂: Multiply by ½ for one Cl atom.","Answer field already has a minus sign: Enter the magnitude 610."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Enthalpies of formation of CCl₄(g), H₂O(g), CO₂(g) and HCl(g) are −105, −242, −394 and −92 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively. The magnitude of enthalpy of the reaction given below is ________ kJ mol⁻¹. (nearest integer) CCl₄(g) + 2H₂O(g) → CO₂(g) + 4HCl(g)","type":"num","answer":"173","range":[173.0,173.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 103 (forgetting ×4 for HCl), 139 (forgetting ×2 for water and ×4 for HCl), 1351 (adding instead of subtracting).\nWorking: ΔH = [−394 + 4(−92)] − [−105 + 2(−242)] = −762 + 589 = −173 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Products minus reactants with coefficients; answer asked as magnitude.","traps":["Coefficient dropped: Multiply each ΔfH by its coefficient.","Sign vs magnitude: Report |ΔH| as asked."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The enthalpy of combustion of propane, graphite and dihydrogen at 298 K are: −2220.0 kJ mol⁻¹, −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹ and −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively. The magnitude of enthalpy of formation of propane (C₃H₈) is ____ kJ mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"104","range":[104.0,104.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1247 (8 H₂ used); 2220-type values (sign error); 104 negative entered.\nWorking: ΔfH = 3(−393.5) + 4(−285.8) − (−2220.0) = −1180.5 − 1143.2 + 2220.0 = −103.7 → magnitude 104.\nKey idea: ΔfH = ΣΔcH(elements) − ΔcH(compound).","traps":["Moles of H₂: Write 3C + 4H₂ → C₃H₈ first.","Reactants minus products for combustion data: ΔH = Σ ΔcH(reactants) − Σ ΔcH(products)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"While performing a thermodynamics experiment, a student made the following observations: HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O, ΔH = −57.3 kJ mol⁻¹; CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O, ΔH = −55.3 kJ mol⁻¹. The enthalpy of ionization of CH₃COOH as calculated by the student is ______ kJ mol⁻¹. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: −2 (sign reversed – ionisation is endothermic); 112.6 (adding the two values).\nWorking: ΔH_ion = ΔH_neut(weak) − ΔH_neut(strong) = −55.3 − (−57.3) = +2.0 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Weak acid releases less heat because part goes into ionising it.","traps":["Sign of ionisation enthalpy: Ionisation absorbs heat: ΔH_ion > 0."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"2.4 g coal is burnt in a bomb calorimeter in excess of oxygen at 298 K and 1 atm pressure. The temperature of the calorimeter rises from 298 K to 300 K. The enthalpy change during the combustion of coal is –x kJ mol⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Nearest Integer)\n(Given: Heat capacity of bomb calorimeter 20.0 kJ K⁻¹. Assume coal to be pure carbon)","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 40 (heat for 2.4 g, not per mole), 20 (using 1 K), 400 (using 0.1 mol).\nWorking: q = 20.0 × 2 = 40 kJ for 2.4/12 = 0.2 mol C → ΔU = −200 kJ mol⁻¹; C(s) + O₂ → CO₂ has Δn_g = 0, so ΔH = ΔU = −200 → x = 200.\nKey idea: q_v = CΔT, divide by moles; Δn_g = 0 so ΔH = ΔU.","traps":["Not converting to per mole: Divide by 0.2 mol C.","ΔU vs ΔH correction: Δn_g = 1 − 1 = 0."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For the reaction H₂F₂(g) → H₂(g) + F₂(g), ΔU = −59.6 kJ mol⁻¹ at 27°C. The enthalpy change for the above reaction is (−) ___ kJ mol⁻¹ [nearest integer] Given : R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"57","range":[57.0,57.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking Δn_g = −1 gives 62; forgetting kJ conversion gives a huge number; using 27 instead of 300 K gives ≈ 59.\nWorking: Δn_g = 2 − 1 = 1; ΔH = −59.6 + (1 × 8.314 × 300)/1000 = −59.6 + 2.49 = −57.1 → 57.\nKey idea: Δn_g = +1, so ΔH is less negative than ΔU by RT ≈ 2.5 kJ.","traps":["Δn_g sign: Δn_g = products − reactants (gas only).","Kelvin and kJ: T = 300 K; divide RT by 1000."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The molar heat capacity for an ideal gas at constant pressure is 20.785 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. The change in internal energy is 5000 J upon heating it from 300 K to 500 K. The number of moles of the gas at constant volume is ___ [Nearest integer] (Given: R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (using Cp: 5000/(20.785 × 200) = 1.2), 3 (arithmetic).\nWorking: Cv = Cp − R = 20.785 − 8.314 = 12.471 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; n = ΔU/(Cv ΔT) = 5000/(12.471 × 200) = 2.00.\nKey idea: Internal energy always uses Cv, even if the process is not at constant volume.","traps":["Cp used for ΔU: ΔU = nCvΔT; get Cv = Cp − R."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A gas (Molar mass = 280 g mol⁻¹) was burnt in excess O₂ in a constant volume calorimeter and during combustion the temperature of calorimeter increased from 298.0 K to 298.45 K. If the heat capacity of calorimeter is 2.5 kJ K⁻¹ and enthalpy of combustion of gas is 9 kJ mol⁻¹ then amount of gas burnt is _______ g. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"35","range":[35.0,35.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.125 (stops at moles); 1125 or 1.125 (stops at heat); answers using ΔU/ΔH correction (not intended).\nWorking: heat released = 2.5 × 0.45 = 1.125 kJ; moles = 1.125/9 = 0.125 mol; mass = 0.125 × 280 = 35 g (the question treats the given combustion value as the heat per mole measured in the bomb, ignoring ΔH vs ΔU).\nKey idea: Heat absorbed by calorimeter equals heat released by the fuel.","traps":["Stopping at moles: Multiply moles by 280 g mol⁻¹.","ΔH vs ΔU at constant volume: The question intends direct use of the given value; Δn_g is unknown anyway."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following relation is not correct?","options":["ΔH = ΔU − PΔV","ΔU = q + W","ΔS_sys + ΔS_surr ≥ 0","ΔG = ΔH − TΔS"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) can look wrong to students who use the older convention ΔU = q − W (W = work by system); (C) may look wrong because of \"≥\" instead of \">\", but equality holds at equilibrium.\nWorking: H = U + PV, so at constant pressure ΔH = ΔU + PΔV; the minus sign makes (A) wrong. (B) is the first law with W = work done on the system, (C) the second law, (D) the Gibbs relation.\nKey idea: Enthalpy adds PV to U, never subtracts it.","traps":["Work sign convention: NCERT uses w = work done on the system, so ΔU = q + w.","Sign in H = U + PV: Write H = U + PV and differentiate at constant P."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"System & state functions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Among the following the number of state variable is ____. Internal energy (U); Volume (V); Heat (q); Enthalpy (H).","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 4 (taking q as state function), 2.\nWorking: U, V and H are state functions; q depends on path. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Heat and work are path functions.","traps":["Heat as state function: q and w depend on path."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"When 600 mL of 0.2 M HNO₃ is mixed with 400 mL of 0.1 M NaOH solution in a flask, the rise in temperature of the flask is _______ × 10⁻² °C. (Enthalpy of neutralisation = 57 kJ mol⁻¹ and Specific heat of water = 4.2 J K⁻¹ g⁻¹) (Neglect heat capacity of flask)","type":"num","answer":"54","range":[54.0,54.0],"explain":"Common slips: 163 (heat from 0.12 mol acid); 136 (mass taken as 400 g); 90 (mass taken as 600 g).\nWorking: HNO₃ 0.12 mol, NaOH 0.04 mol → 0.04 mol water formed; q = 0.04 × 57000 = 2280 J; mass 1000 g; ΔT = 2280/(1000 × 4.2) = 0.543 °C = 54 × 10⁻² °C.\nKey idea: Heat released depends on moles of water formed = limiting NaOH (0.04 mol).","traps":["Excess acid used: Only the limiting reagent neutralises.","Wrong mass of solution: Total volume 1000 mL ≈ 1000 g."],"topics":["Heat capacity & calorimetry","Thermochemistry"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"C(s) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 400 kJ; C(s) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g) + 100 kJ. When coal of purity 60% is allowed to burn in presence of insufficient oxygen, 60% of carbon is converted into 'CO' and the remaining is converted into 'CO₂'. The heat generated when 0.6 kg of coal is burnt is ______.","options":["1600 kJ","3200 kJ","4400 kJ","6600 kJ"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 4400 kJ = purity ignored with swapped split or other slip; (B) 3200 kJ / (A) 1600 kJ = partial combustion only or wrong mass basis.\nWorking: carbon = 0.6 × 600 g = 360 g = 30 mol. CO: 18 mol × 100 = 1800 kJ; CO₂: 12 mol × 400 = 4800 kJ; total 6600 kJ.\nKey idea: Moles of C = 30; split 18 : 12 between CO and CO₂.","traps":["Purity not applied: Multiply by purity first.","Split percentages swapped: Read which product gets 60%."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"2O₃(g) ⇌ 3O₂(g). At 300 K, ozone is fifty percent dissociated. The standard free energy change at this temperature and 1 atm pressure is (−) ____ J mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Given: ln 1.35 = 0.3 and R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"747","range":[747.0,747.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.75³/0.5² = 1.6875 used with moles instead of partial pressures; starting with 2 mol O₃ and 50 % (same Kp, but some take 1 mol dissociated → O₂ 1.5 and forget total); using log instead of ln (≈ 324).\nWorking: start 1 mol O₃; 0.5 mol dissociates → O₃ 0.5, O₂ 0.75, total 1.25. At 1 atm pO₃ = 0.4, pO₂ = 0.6. Kp = 0.6³/0.4² = 1.35. ΔG° = −RT ln K = −8.3 × 300 × 0.3 = −747 J mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Given ln 1.35 confirms Kp = 1.35 – check your set-up against it.","traps":["Moles used instead of partial pressures: Divide by total moles 1.25 and multiply by P = 1 atm.","O₂ formed miscounted: Multiply O₃ lost by 3/2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"At 25 °C and 1 atm pressure, the enthalpies of combustion are as given below: ΔcH° (kJ mol⁻¹): H₂ −286.0; C (graphite) −394.0; C₂H₆(g) −1560.0. The enthalpy of formation of ethane is","options":["+54.0 kJ mol⁻¹","−68.0 kJ mol⁻¹","−86.0 kJ mol⁻¹","+97.0 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) +54 / (D) +97 – sign reversed or wrong coefficient (using 2H₂ or 1C); (B) −68 – coefficient slip.\nWorking: 2C + 3H₂ → C₂H₆; ΔfH = 2(−394) + 3(−286) − (−1560) = −788 − 858 + 1560 = −86 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: With combustion enthalpies, ΔrH = Σ ΔcH(reactants) − Σ ΔcH(products).","traps":["Products minus reactants used with combustion data: For combustion data reverse it: reactants − products.","Coefficients of C and H₂ missed: Write the balanced formation equation first."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The standard entropy change for the reaction 4Fe(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Fe₂O₃(s) is −550 J K⁻¹ at 298 K. [Given: The standard enthalpy change for the reaction is −165 kJ mol⁻¹]. The temperature in K at which the reaction attains equilibrium is ____. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"300","range":[300.0,300.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.3 – kJ/J mismatch; 298 – reads the given temperature.\nWorking: at equilibrium ΔG = 0, T = ΔH/ΔS = (−165 000)/(−550) = 300 K.\nKey idea: T_eq = ΔH/ΔS with consistent units.","traps":["Units of ΔH vs ΔS: Convert ΔH to J.","Given 298 K taken as answer: 298 K is where ΔS was measured, not equilibrium."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The standard free energy change (ΔG°) for 50% dissociation of N₂O₄ into NO₂ at 27°C and 1 atm pressure is −x J mol⁻¹. The value of x is ____. (Nearest Integer) [Given: R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, log 1.33 = 0.1239, ln 10 = 2.3]","type":"num","answer":"710","range":[710.0,710.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1728 – Kp taken as 2 (moles used instead of partial pressures); 309 – log instead of ln; 0 – assumes 50% means K = 1.\nWorking: N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂, α = 0.5: moles 0.5 and 1.0, total 1.5; at 1 atm p(N₂O₄) = 1/3, p(NO₂) = 2/3; Kp = (4/9)/(1/3) = 4/3 = 1.33. ΔG° = −RT ln K = −8.31 × 300 × 2.3 × 0.1239 = −710 J mol⁻¹ → x = 710.\nKey idea: With α = 0.5 at 1 atm, Kp = 4α²/(1 − α²) = 4/3.","traps":["Kp from moles not partial pressures: Use mole fractions × 1 atm.","log vs ln: Multiply log by 2.3 to get ln."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"At 25°C and 1 atm pressure, the enthalpy of combustion of benzene (l) and acetylene (g) are –3268 kJ mol⁻¹ and –1300 kJ mol⁻¹, respectively. The change in enthalpy for the reaction 3 C₂H₂(g) → C₆H₆(l), is","options":["+324 kJ mol⁻¹","+632 kJ mol⁻¹","–632 kJ mol⁻¹","–732 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) +632: products minus reactants (sign reversed, as if using formation-enthalpy rule); (A)/(D) arithmetic slips or using 1300 without multiplying by 3 properly.\nWorking: ΔH = Σ ΔcH(reactants) − Σ ΔcH(products) = 3(−1300) − (−3268) = −3900 + 3268 = −632 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: For combustion data the rule is reactants minus products (opposite of formation data).","traps":["Reactants minus products for combustion: Write the combustion equations and add them; or remember ΔcH rule is reactants − products.","Stoichiometric factor 3: Multiply ΔcH by the coefficient."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For complete combustion of methanol CH₃OH(l) + 3/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), the amount of heat produced as measured by bomb calorimeter is 726 kJ mol⁻¹ at 27°C. The enthalpy of combustion for the reaction is –x kJ mol⁻¹, where x is ______. (Nearest integer) (Given : R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"727","range":[727.0,727.0],"explain":"Common slips: 725 – sign of Δn_gRT reversed; 726 – ignoring the correction; 722/731 – counting H₂O(l) as gas (Δn_g = +1.5) or using T = 27 K.\nWorking: bomb calorimeter gives ΔU = –726 kJ/mol; Δn_g = 1 – 1.5 = –0.5; ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT = –726 + (–0.5)(8.3 × 300)/1000 = –727.2 kJ/mol, so x = 727.\nKey idea: Count only gaseous moles: Δn_g = 1 – 3/2 = –1/2, so |ΔH| exceeds |ΔU| slightly.","traps":["Liquid water counted in Δn_g: Only gases enter Δn_g.","Correction sign reversed: Write ΔU = –726 then add Δn_gRT."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A fish swimming in water body when taken out from the water body is covered with a film of water of weight 36 g. When it is subjected to cooking at 100°C, then the internal energy for vaporization in kJ mol⁻¹ is ______. [nearest integer] [Assume steam to be an ideal gas. Given ΔvapH⊖ for water at 373 K and 1 bar is 41.1 kJ mol⁻¹; R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"38","range":[38.0,38.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 76 (multiplying by 2 mol for 36 g); 44 (adding RT); 41 (ignoring ΔngRT).\nWorking: ΔvapU = ΔvapH – ΔngRT = 41.1 – (1)(8.31 × 373)/1000 = 41.1 – 3.10 = 38.0 kJ mol⁻¹; the 36 g mass is irrelevant to a per-mole answer.\nKey idea: Per mole: ΔU = ΔH – RT with Δng = 1.","traps":["Mass of water used: The answer is asked in kJ mol⁻¹, so the amount does not matter.","Sign of ΔngRT: ΔU = ΔH – ΔngRT; gas produced, Δng = +1."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"40% of HI undergoes decomposition to H₂ and I₂ at 300 K. ΔG⊖ for this decomposition reaction at one atmosphere pressure is ______ J mol⁻¹. [nearest integer] (Use R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; log 2 = 0.3010, ln 10 = 2.3, log 3 = 0.477)","type":"num","answer":"2735","range":[2735.0,2735.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5470 (2HI → H₂ + I₂ basis); –2735 (sign of ln K); 1114 (using α instead of K).\nWorking: per mole of HI, HI → ½H₂ + ½I₂: from 1 mol, HI 0.6, H₂ 0.2, I₂ 0.2; K = (0.2 × 0.2)^½/0.6 = 1/3; ΔG⊖ = –RT ln K = 8.31 × 300 × 2.3 × 0.477 = 2735 J mol⁻¹. Written as 2HI → H₂ + I₂ (K = 1/9) the value doubles to 5470; the accepted answer uses the one-mole-HI equation.\nKey idea: K < 1 gives positive ΔG⊖; ln 3 = 2.3 × 0.477.","traps":["Choice of balanced equation: The answer is per mole of HI; state the basis and check against the answer form.","Sign of ΔG⊖: K = 1/3 < 1 → ln K negative → ΔG⊖ positive."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Spontaneous process, (B) Process with ΔP = 0, ΔT = 0, (C) ΔH(reaction), (D) Exothermic process. List-II: (I) ΔH < 0, (II) ΔG(T,P) < 0, (III) Isothermal and isobaric process, (IV) [Bond energies of molecules in reactants] − [Bond energies of product molecules].","options":["(A)–(III), (B)–(II), (C)–(IV), (D)–(I)","(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(IV), (D)–(I)","(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(II), (B)–(I), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – links spontaneity to isothermal/isobaric (conditions, not criterion); (C) – gives ΔH < 0 for ΔHreaction in general and bond-energy difference for exothermic; (D) – pairs ΔP = 0, ΔT = 0 with ΔH < 0.\nWorking: spontaneity at constant T and P ⇔ ΔG < 0; ΔP = 0 and ΔT = 0 means isobaric and isothermal; ΔrH = Σ bond enthalpies of reactants − Σ bond enthalpies of products; exothermic ⇔ ΔH < 0 → option (B).\nKey idea: ΔG < 0 is the spontaneity criterion; bond-enthalpy form is reactants minus products (energy taken in minus energy given out).","traps":["Bond-enthalpy subtraction order: Bond enthalpies: reactants − products.","Spontaneous = exothermic: Only ΔG < 0 is the criterion at constant T, P."],"topics":["Spontaneity","Thermochemistry","Types of processes"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"When 5 moles of He gas expand isothermally and reversibly at 300 K from 10 litre to 20 litre, the magnitude of the maximum work obtained is ____ J. [nearest integer] (Given: R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and log 2 = 0.3010)","type":"num","answer":"8630","range":[8630.0,8630.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 3748 (log 2 used without 2.303); 0 (taking ΔU = 0 as w = 0); 5000 (irreversible p·ΔV with an assumed pressure).\nWorking: |w| = 2.303 nRT log(V₂/V₁) = 2.303 × 5 × 8.3 × 300 × 0.3010 = 8630.4 J → 8630.\nKey idea: Reversible isothermal work = nRT ln(V₂/V₁); use 2.303 with log₁₀.","traps":["Missing 2.303: ln x = 2.303 log x."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"4.0 L of an ideal gas is allowed to expand isothermally into vacuum until the total volume is 20 L. The amount of heat absorbed in this expansion is ______ L atm.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 16 (pΔV with p = 1 atm), 6.4 or similar from nRT ln(V₂/V₁).\nWorking: expansion into vacuum means p_ext = 0, so w = 0; isothermal ideal gas → ΔU = 0; hence q = 0.\nKey idea: Into vacuum → no work; isothermal ideal gas → no ΔU; so no heat.","traps":["Using the given volumes: Check p_ext first: vacuum means zero."],"topics":["First law","Work & heat"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For combustion of one mole of magnesium in an open container at 300 K and 1 bar pressure, Δ_cH° = −601.70 kJ mol⁻¹. The magnitude of change in internal energy for the reaction is _____ kJ. (Nearest integer) (Given: R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"600","range":[600.0,600.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 603 (sign of Δn_g reversed); 599 (Δn_g taken as −1); 602 (no correction).\nWorking: Mg(s) + ½O₂(g) → MgO(s), Δn_g = −½; ΔU = ΔH − Δn_gRT = −601.70 + 0.5 × 8.3 × 300 × 10⁻³ = −601.70 + 1.245 = −600.455 kJ → |ΔU| ≈ 600.\nKey idea: Only O₂ is gaseous; Δn_g = 0 − ½.","traps":["Sign of Δn_g: ΔU = ΔH − Δn_gRT; with Δn_g < 0, |ΔU| < |ΔH|.","Counting solids in Δn_g: Count gas moles only."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"17.0 g of NH₃ completely vapourises at –33.42°C and 1 bar pressure and the enthalpy change in the process is 23.4 kJ mol⁻¹. The enthalpy change for the vapourisation of 85 g of NH₃ under the same conditions is ________ kJ.","type":"num","answer":"117","range":[117.0,117.0],"explain":"Common slips: 23.4 (molar value not scaled); 4.68 (dividing by moles instead of multiplying); 1989 (multiplying by grams, 85 × 23.4).\nWorking: 85 g = 5 mol; ΔH = 5 × 23.4 = 117 kJ.\nKey idea: ΔH scales with moles: n = 85/17 = 5.","traps":["Extensive vs intensive: Multiply by number of moles."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"2.2 g of nitrous oxide (N₂O) gas is cooled at a constant pressure of 1 atm from 310 K to 270 K causing the compression of the gas from 217.1 mL to 167.75 mL. The change in internal energy of the process, ΔU is '–x' J. The value of 'x' is ____. [nearest integer] (Given: atomic mass of N = 14 g mol⁻¹ and of O = 16 g mol⁻¹. Molar heat capacity of N₂O is 100 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"195","range":[195.0,195.0],"explain":"Common slips: 205 (sign of work wrong), 200 (work ignored), 4935-type values from mL atm not converted.\nWorking: n = 2.2/44 = 0.05 mol; q = nC_pΔT = 0.05 × 100 × (−40) = −200 J; w = −PΔV = −1 atm × (−0.04935 L) = +0.04935 L atm = +5.0 J; ΔU = q + w = −195 J → x = 195.\nKey idea: Compression → work done on gas is positive; 1 L atm = 101.3 J.","traps":["Sign of work on compression: w = −PΔV, ΔV < 0 → w > 0.","mL atm to J: mL → L, then × 101.3."],"topics":["First law","Work & heat"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The Born-Haber cycle for KCl is evaluated with the following data: ΔfH° for KCl = −436.7 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔsubH° for K = 89.2 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔionizationH° for K = 419.0 kJ mol⁻¹; Δelectron gainH° for Cl(g) = −348.6 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔbondH° for Cl₂ = 243.0 kJ mol⁻¹. The magnitude of lattice enthalpy of KCl in kJ mol⁻¹ is ______ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"718","range":[718.0,718.0],"explain":"Common slips: 839 (full Cl₂ bond enthalpy); 21 or 1115 (sign errors in ΔegH or ΔfH).\nWorking: ΔlatticeH = 89.2 + 419.0 + 121.5 − 348.6 + 436.7 = 717.8 ≈ 718 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Per mole KCl: ½ΔbondH(Cl₂).","traps":["Half the bond enthalpy of Cl₂: Only ½Cl₂ is needed per KCl.","Sign of electron gain enthalpy: Keep signs; lattice enthalpy magnitude = 717.8."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For water ΔvapH = 41 kJ mol⁻¹ at 373 K and 1 bar pressure. Assuming that water vapour is an ideal gas that occupies a much larger volume than liquid water, the internal energy change during evaporation of water is ____ kJ mol⁻¹. [Use: R = 8.3 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"38","range":[38.0,38.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 44 (adding ΔngRT), 41 (ignoring work), –3 or 3110 (unit slip, J vs kJ).\nWorking: ΔU = ΔH – ΔngRT = 41 – (1)(8.3 × 10⁻³)(373) = 41 – 3.10 = 37.9 ≈ 38 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Δng = +1 for liquid → vapour; ΔU is smaller than ΔH.","traps":["Subtract ΔngRT: Expansion work done by system lowers ΔU.","R in kJ: Convert 8.3 J to 8.3 × 10⁻³ kJ."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"200 mL of 0.2 M HCl is mixed with 300 mL of 0.1 M NaOH. The molar heat of neutralization of this reaction is −57.1 kJ. The increase in temperature in °C of the system on mixing is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer) [Given: Specific heat of water = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, Density of water = 1.00 g cm⁻³] (Assume no volume change on mixing)","type":"num","answer":"82","range":[82.0,82.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 109 (using HCl 0.04 mol); 137 (using only 300 g of water).\nWorking: HCl 0.04 mol, NaOH 0.03 mol (limiting); heat = 0.03 × 57.1 kJ = 1713 J; mass 500 g; ΔT = 1713/(500 × 4.18) = 0.8196 °C = 81.96 × 10⁻² ≈ 82.\nKey idea: Only the limiting reagent's moles (NaOH, 0.03 mol) release heat; the whole 500 g is heated.","traps":["Excess acid moles used: Neutralisation stops when NaOH is used up.","Mass of one solution only: Total mass = 500 g after mixing."],"topics":["Heat capacity & calorimetry","Thermochemistry"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Data given for the following reaction is as follows: FeO(s) + C(graphite) → Fe(s) + CO(g). ΔH° (kJ mol⁻¹) / ΔS° (J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹): FeO(s) −266.3 / 57.49; C(graphite) 0 / 5.74; Fe(s) 0 / 27.28; CO(g) −110.5 / 197.6. The minimum temperature in K at which the reaction becomes spontaneous is _______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"964","range":[964.0,964.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.96 (kJ/J mismatch); ~1150 (ignoring S of C or FeO); 684 (using absolute S of CO only); negative answers if signs reversed.\nWorking: ΔH° = −110.5 − (−266.3) = +155.8 kJ; ΔS° = (27.28 + 197.6) − (57.49 + 5.74) = +161.65 J K⁻¹; T = ΔH°/ΔS° = 155800/161.65 = 963.8 K → 964.\nKey idea: T_min = ΔH°/ΔS° with both in joules.","traps":["kJ vs J: Convert ΔH to 155800 J before dividing.","Elements given non-zero S°: Absolute entropies of elements must be included."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The incorrect expression among the following is: (1) ΔG_system/ΔS_total = −T (at constant P); (2) ln K = (ΔH° − TΔS°)/RT; (3) K = e^(−ΔG°/RT); (4) For isothermal process w_reversible = −nRT ln(V_f/V_i)","options":["ΔG_system/ΔS_total = −T (at constant P)","ln K = (ΔH° − TΔS°)/RT","K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)","For isothermal process w_reversible = −nRT ln(V_f/V_i)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks odd but is ΔG_sys = −TΔS_total rearranged; (C) and (D) are textbook forms that students may doubt if unfamiliar.\nWorking: ΔG° = −RT ln K so ln K = −(ΔH° − TΔS°)/RT; (B) lacks the minus sign. (A) follows from ΔG_sys = −TΔS_total at constant T, P; (C) and (D) are standard.\nKey idea: ln K = −ΔG°/RT; any version without the minus is wrong.","traps":["Unfamiliar but correct expression: Use ΔG_sys = −TΔS_total at constant T, P.","Sign dropped in ln K: Re-derive from ΔG° = −RT ln K."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"For the reaction A(g) → B(g), the value of the equilibrium constant at 300 K and 1 atm is equal to 100.0. The value of ΔrG for the reaction at 300 K and 1 atm in J mol⁻¹ is −xR, where x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ and ln 10 = 2.3)","type":"num","answer":"1380","range":[1380.0,1380.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 600 (log instead of ln); 11468 (R multiplied in); 0 (ΔG = 0 at equilibrium, wording trap).\nWorking: ΔrG° = −RT ln K = −R × 300 × 2 × 2.3 = −1380R, so x = 1380. Strictly, ΔrG at equilibrium is 0; the question means the standard Gibbs energy change at 1 atm.\nKey idea: ln 100 = 2 × 2.3 = 4.6; x = 300 × 4.6.","traps":["Log base slip: ln K = 2.3 log K.","R multiplied out: Keep R symbolic."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Assuming ideal behaviour, the magnitude of log K for the following reaction at 25 °C is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ____. (Integer answer) 3HC≡CH(g) ⇌ C₆H₆(l). [Given: ΔfG°(HC≡CH) = −2.04 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹; ΔfG°(C₆H₆) = −1.24 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹; R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"855","range":[855.0,855.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 372 (ln K/ln10 slip – ΔG/RT = 197 then wrong division), 1970 (ln K × 10), 140 (forgetting factor 3: ΔrG° = 0.8 × 10⁵ J → |log K| = 14.0).\nWorking: ΔrG° = −1.24 × 10⁵ − 3(−2.04 × 10⁵) = 4.88 × 10⁵ J; |log K| = 4.88 × 10⁵/(2.303 × 8.314 × 298) = 85.5 → x = 855.\nKey idea: Multiply ΔfG° of acetylene by 3; use 2.303RT ≈ 5706 J mol⁻¹.","traps":["Stoichiometric 3 missed: ΔrG° = ΣνΔfG°(products) − ΣνΔfG°(reactants).","ln K instead of log K: Use 2.303RT for log₁₀."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The reaction of cyanamide, NH₂CN(s), with oxygen was run in a bomb calorimeter and ΔU was found to be −742.24 kJ mol⁻¹. The magnitude of ΔH₂₉₈ for the reaction NH₂CN(s) + 3/2 O₂(g) → N₂(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l) is ______ kJ. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Assume ideal gases and R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"741","range":[741.0,741.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 743 (Δn_g sign reversed), 744 (Δn_g = 1.5 counting only O₂ wrongly), 1981 (forgetting J → kJ).\nWorking: Δn_g = (1 + 1) − 3/2 = +0.5 (products N₂ and CO₂). ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT = −742.24 + 0.5 × 8.314 × 298 × 10⁻³ = −742.24 + 1.24 = −741.0 kJ mol⁻¹; magnitude 741.\nKey idea: Count only gaseous species; Δn_g = 2 − 1.5 = 0.5.","traps":["Δn_g sign reversed: Δn_g = n(gaseous products) − n(gaseous reactants).","R in J not converted: Multiply by 10⁻³ to get kJ."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The ionization enthalpy of Na⁺ formation from Na(g) is 495.8 kJ mol⁻¹, while the electron gain enthalpy of Br is −325.0 kJ mol⁻¹. Given the lattice enthalpy of NaBr is −728.4 kJ mol⁻¹. The energy for the formation of NaBr ionic solid is (−) ______ × 10⁻¹ kJ mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"5576","range":[5576.0,5576.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8992 (495.8 − 325.0 + 728.4 = 899.2, treating lattice enthalpy as endothermic), 15492 (495.8 + 325.0 + 728.4 = 1549.2, ignoring signs), 2326 (495.8 − 728.4 = −232.6, missing electron gain).\nWorking: Na(g) → Na⁺(g) +495.8; Br(g) → Br⁻(g) −325.0; Na⁺(g) + Br⁻(g) → NaBr(s) −728.4. Sum = −557.6 kJ mol⁻¹ = −5576 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Here lattice enthalpy is given negative (formation from ions), so simply add.","traps":["Lattice enthalpy sign: Use the sign given in the question (−728.4, formation).","Adding sublimation/dissociation: Data start from gaseous atoms; no extra steps."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Five moles of an ideal gas at 293 K is expanded isothermally from an initial pressure of 2.1 MPa to 1.3 MPa against at constant external pressure 4.3 MPa. The heat transferred in this process is ______ kJ mol⁻¹. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [Use R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (divided by 5 mol because of 'per mol'); reversible formula nRT ln(p₁/p₂) ≈ 5.8; sign errors (−15).\nWorking: isothermal ideal gas, ΔU = 0, q = −w = p_ext(V₂ − V₁) = p_ext·nRT(1/p₂ − 1/p₁) = 4.3 × 5 × 8.314 × 293 × (1/1.3 − 1/2.1) J = 15.35 kJ → 15. Note: the physical set-up is inconsistent (gas cannot expand against p_ext 4.3 MPa greater than its own pressure), and the unit 'kJ mol⁻¹' is loose – 15 kJ is the total heat for 5 mol.\nKey idea: q = p_ext·nRT(1/p₂ − 1/p₁); ΔU = 0.","traps":["Reversible formula used: Constant p_ext means irreversible.","Per-mole division: The answer is the total heat for 5 mol."],"topics":["Work & heat","First law"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For a chemical reaction A + B ⇌ C + D (ΔrH° = 80 kJ mol⁻¹) the entropy change ΔrS° depends on the temperature T (in K) as ΔrS° = 2T (J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹). Minimum temperature at which it will become spontaneous is ______ K. (Integer)","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 40000 (forgetting to take the square root); 40 (using 80 kJ without converting to J: 80 = 2T² → ~6, or 80/2); 0.2 (kJ/J mismatch).\nWorking: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 80000 − T(2T) ≤ 0 → T² ≥ 40000 → T = 200 K.\nKey idea: Set ΔH = T·ΔS(T): 80000 = 2T².","traps":["kJ and J mixed: Convert 80 kJ to 80000 J.","T·ΔS taken as 2T: TΔS = T × 2T = 2T²."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The average S–F bond energy in kJ mol⁻¹ of SF₆ is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: the values of standard enthalpy of formation of SF₆(g), S(g) and F(g) are –1100, 275 and 80 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively.]","type":"num","answer":"309","range":[309.0,309.0],"explain":"Common slips: −58 – SF₆ sign wrong ((275 + 480 − 1100)/6 = −57.5); 243 – F counted once (275+80+1100)/6; 1855 – not divided by 6.\nWorking: SF₆(g) → S(g) + 6F(g): ΔH = 275 + 6(80) – (–1100) = 1855 kJ; average S–F = 1855/6 = 309.2 ≈ 309.\nKey idea: Atomisation enthalpy = ΣΔfH(atoms) – ΔfH(molecule).","traps":["F(g) enthalpy taken once: Multiply ΔfH(F) by 6.","Sign of ΔfH(SF₆) mishandled: Products minus reactants."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"An average person needs about 10000 kJ energy per day. The amount of glucose (molar mass = 180.0 g mol⁻¹) needed to meet this energy requirement is ________ g. (Use: Δ_cH(glucose) = −2700 kJ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"667","range":[667.0,667.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3.7 (moles instead of grams); 666 (truncating instead of rounding); 486000 (multiplying instead of dividing).\nWorking: moles = 10000/2700 = 3.704 mol; mass = 3.704 × 180.0 = 666.7 g ≈ 667 g.\nKey idea: mass = (energy / |Δ_cH|) × M.","traps":["Rounding: Round to nearest integer: 667."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For a given chemical reaction A → B at 300 K the free energy change is –49.4 kJ mol⁻¹ and the enthalpy of reaction is 51.4 kJ mol⁻¹. The entropy change of the reaction is ___ J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"336","range":[336.0,336.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (or −7) from (51.4 − 49.4)/300 ignoring the sign of ΔG; 0.336 if left in kJ.\nWorking: ΔS = (ΔH − ΔG)/T = (51.4 + 49.4) × 10³/300 = 100800/300 = 336 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Spontaneous endothermic reaction → large positive ΔS; subtract a negative ΔG carefully.","traps":["Sign of ΔG dropped: Write ΔH – ΔG = 51.4 – (–49.4) = 100.8 kJ.","kJ not converted: Multiply by 1000 before dividing by T."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"If the standard molar enthalpy change for combustion of graphite powder is −2.48 × 10² kJ mol⁻¹, the amount of heat generated on combustion of 1 g of graphite powder is _______ kJ. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"21","range":[21.0,21.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 248 (forgetting to divide by 12), −21 (sign, though \"heat generated\" is positive), 20 (truncation).\nWorking: 248 kJ per 12 g → 248/12 = 20.7 → 21 kJ. (Real ΔcH° of graphite is −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹; the given value is treated as data.)\nKey idea: Divide by molar mass 12.","traps":["Per-mole value reported: Heat for 1 g = ΔH/12."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"At 298 K, the enthalpy of fusion of a solid (X) is 2.8 kJ mol⁻¹ and the enthalpy of vaporisation of the liquid (X) is 98.2 kJ mol⁻¹. The enthalpy of sublimation of the substance (X) in kJ mol⁻¹ is ________. (in nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"101","range":[101.0,101.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 95 or 96 (subtracting), 275 (mixing units).\nWorking: ΔsubH = ΔfusH + ΔvapH = 2.8 + 98.2 = 101 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Solid → liquid → gas: add the two enthalpies.","traps":["Subtracting instead of adding: Draw the path solid → liquid → vapour."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A system does 200 J of work and at the same time absorbs 150 J of heat. The magnitude of the change in internal energy is ______ J. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 350 (adding 200 and 150), −50 if sign kept (asked magnitude).\nWorking: ΔU = q + w = +150 + (−200) = −50 J; magnitude 50.\nKey idea: Work done by system is negative; heat absorbed is positive.","traps":["Sign of w: Write signs before adding."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For water at 100 °C and 1 bar, ΔvapH − ΔvapU = ________ × 10² J mol⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer) [Use: R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹] [Assume volume of H₂O(l) is much smaller than volume of H₂O(g). Assume H₂O(g) treated as an ideal gas]","type":"num","answer":"31","range":[31.0,31.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 8 (using 100 °C), 3100 entered unscaled, 0 (forgetting that gas forms).\nWorking: ΔH − ΔU = Δn_g RT = 1 × 8.31 × 373 = 3.10 × 10³ J mol⁻¹ = 31 × 10².\nKey idea: Δn_g = 1 for H₂O(l) → H₂O(g).","traps":["Kelvin, not Celsius: Add 273."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For the reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 495 K, ΔrG° = −9.478 kJ mol⁻¹. If we start the reaction in a closed container at 495 K with 22 millimoles of A, the amount of B in the equilibrium mixture is _______ millimoles. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; ln 10 = 2.303]","type":"num","answer":"20","range":[20.0,20.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (x = 22/11, ratio inverted), 10 (K itself), 22 (complete conversion).\nWorking: ln K = 9478/(8.314 × 495) = 2.303 → K = 10; x/(22 − x) = 10 → x = 20 mmol.\nKey idea: ln K = 2.303 → K = 10; for A ⇌ B, B = 22 × K/(1 + K).","traps":["K inverted: Negative ΔG° → K > 1, products favoured.","kJ vs J: Convert 9.478 kJ to 9478 J."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"At 25°C, 50 g of iron reacts with HCl to form FeCl₂. The evolved hydrogen gas expands against a constant pressure of 1 bar. The work done by the gas during this expansion is ______ J. (Round off to the Nearest Integer) [Given: R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Assume, hydrogen is an ideal gas] [Atomic mass of Fe is 55.85 u]","type":"num","answer":"2218","range":[2218.0,2218.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −2218 (sign of work on system used, though 'by the gas' asked), 4436 (2 mol H₂ per Fe), 186 (T = 25 instead of 298 K), 2219 (298.15 K – rounding).\nWorking: Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂; n(H₂) = 0.8953 mol; work done by the gas = nRT = 2218 J at 298 K (2219 J with 298.15 K; the accepted answer is 2218).\nKey idea: Work by gas = nRT; 1 mol H₂ per mol Fe.","traps":["Sign convention flipped: 'Work done by the gas' is reported positive.","°C used for T: Always convert to kelvin."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The standard enthalpies of formation of Al₂O₃ and CaO are −1675 kJ mol⁻¹ and −635 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively. For the reaction 3CaO + 2Al → 3Ca + Al₂O₃ the standard reaction enthalpy ΔrH° = _______ kJ. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"230","range":[230.0,230.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −230 (reactants − products), −1040 (coefficient 3 dropped), 1040.\nWorking: ΔrH° = ΔfH°(Al₂O₃) − 3ΔfH°(CaO) = −1675 − 3(−635) = +230 kJ.\nKey idea: Elements have ΔfH° = 0; multiply CaO by 3.","traps":["Coefficient dropped: Multiply by stoichiometric coefficient.","Sign reversed: Products − reactants."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Entropy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"During which of the following processes, does entropy decrease ?\n(A) Freezing of water to ice at 0°C\n(B) Freezing of water to ice at –10°C\n(C) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g)\n(D) Adsorption of CO(g) on lead surface\n(E) Dissolution of NaCl in water","options":["(A), (B), (C) and (D) only","(B) and (C) only","(A) and (E) only","(A), (C) and (E) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) omits freezing at 0°C and adsorption – a student may think ΔS = 0 at equilibrium (0°C) or confuse total with system entropy; (C)/(D) include NaCl dissolution, which increases entropy.\nWorking: system entropy falls on freezing (at any temperature), when 4 mol gas gives 2 mol gas, and when gas molecules are fixed on a surface (adsorption); dissolving NaCl increases entropy.\nKey idea: Ask only \"is the system more ordered afterwards?\" – freezing, fewer gas moles, adsorption all yes.","traps":["ΔS(system) vs ΔS(total) at 0°C: The question means the system: ice is more ordered than water.","Adsorption entropy: Gas molecules fixed on a surface lose freedom → ΔS < 0."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For the reaction C₂H₆ → C₂H₄ + H₂ the reaction enthalpy ΔrH = ______ kJ mol⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Bond enthalpies in kJ mol⁻¹: C–C: 347, C=C: 611, C–H: 414, H–H: 436]","type":"num","answer":"128","range":[128.0,128.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −128 (sign reversed – products minus reactants), 564 (forgetting H–H), wrong C–H counts.\nWorking: bonds broken: C–C 347 + 6 C–H 2484 = 2831; bonds formed: C=C 611 + 4 C–H 1656 + H–H 436 = 2703; ΔH = 2831 − 2703 = +128 kJ mol⁻¹ (net: C–C + 2 C–H − C=C − H–H).\nKey idea: Net change: break 1 C–C + 2 C–H, form 1 C=C + 1 H–H.","traps":["Sign reversed: Reactants − products for bond enthalpies.","Missed H–H bond formed: List every bond in products."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For the reaction 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g), when ΔS = –176.0 J K⁻¹ and ΔH = –57.8 kJ mol⁻¹, the magnitude of ΔG at 298 K for the reaction is ______ kJ mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 110 (sign error, –57.8 – 52.4); 52390 (no J→kJ conversion); 6 (rounding 5.35 upwards).\nWorking: ΔG = –57.8 – 298 × (–0.1760) = –57.8 + 52.45 = –5.35 kJ mol⁻¹; magnitude ≈ 5.\nKey idea: ΔG = –57.8 – 298(–0.176) = –5.35 kJ mol⁻¹ → magnitude 5.","traps":["J/kJ mismatch: Convert ΔS to –0.176 kJ K⁻¹ before multiplying by T.","Sign of –TΔS: ΔG = ΔH – TΔS = –57.8 + 52.4 = –5.35 kJ."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For the reaction ; A(l) → 2B(g); ΔU = 2.1 kcal, ΔS = 20 cal K⁻¹ at 300 K. Hence ΔG in kcal is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"-2.71 to -2.7","range":[-2.71,-2.7],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: –3.9 (uses ΔU directly: 2.1 – 6); –3.3 or +3.3 (forgets TΔS or sign); Δn_g taken as 1 → –3.3; cal/kcal slip → large numbers.\nWorking: Δn_g = 2; ΔH = 2.1 + 2(2×10⁻³)(300) = 3.3 kcal; ΔG = 3.3 – 300(0.020) = –2.70 kcal (–2.71 with R = 1.987).\nKey idea: Only gaseous moles count: Δn_g = 2 – 0 = 2.","traps":["ΔU used in place of ΔH: Convert first: ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT.","Units cal vs kcal: Convert TΔS = 6000 cal = 6.0 kcal."],"topics":["Gibbs energy","Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The standard heat of formation (Δ_fH°₂₉₈) of ethane in (kJ/mol), if the heat of combustion of ethane, hydrogen and graphite are –1560, –393.5 and –286 kJ/mol, respectively is ______.","type":"num","answer":"-192.5 or -85","ranges":[[-192.5,-192.5],[-85.0,-85.0]],"range":[-192.5,-192.5],"explain":"Common slips: Sign slip gives +85; using 2 mol H₂ instead of 3 gives +201; adding instead of subtracting ΔcH(ethane) gives −3205; the literal reading of the swapped data gives −192.5 (also accepted by NTA).\nWorking: with the intended data (graphite −393.5, H₂ −286): Δ_fH = 2(−393.5) + 3(−286) − (−1560) = −85 kJ/mol. The paper prints the H₂ and graphite values in swapped order; taken literally, 2(−286) + 3(−393.5) + 1560 = −192.5. NTA accepted both.\nKey idea: Δ_fH = Σ ΔcH(elements) − ΔcH(compound).","traps":["Swapped combustion data: Graphite's ΔcH is −393.5 (CO₂); H₂'s is −286 (H₂O).","Sign of compound term: Reactants' combustion minus products' combustion."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"At constant volume, 4 mol of an ideal gas when heated from 300 K to 500 K changes its internal energy by 5000 J. The molar heat capacity at constant volume is _______.","type":"num","answer":"6.25","range":[6.25,6.25],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25 (moles ignored), 1.25 (ΔT taken as 1000 or using total T), 4.17 (using T₁ = 300 K in place of ΔT).\nWorking: ΔU = nCv,mΔT → Cv,m = 5000/(4 × 200) = 6.25 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nKey idea: Divide ΔU by both n and ΔT.","traps":["Moles not divided out: Molar quantity: divide by n = 4."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct order of heat of combustion for the following alkadienes is: (a), (b), (c) [skeletal structures of hexa-2,4-diene: (a) both double bonds trans (2E,4E); (b) one trans, one cis (2E,4Z); (c) both cis (2Z,4Z)].","options":["(a) < (b) < (c)","(b) < (c) < (a)","(c) < (b) < (a)","(a) < (c) < (b)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) reverses the relation (treats heat of combustion as proportional to stability); (B) and (D) misread one drawing's geometry.\nWorking: all three are C₆H₁₀ isomers, so the less stable one releases more heat. Stability trans,trans > trans,cis > cis,cis (cis CH₃/chain steric clash), so heat of combustion (a) < (b) < (c).\nKey idea: For isomers, a larger heat of combustion means a less stable (higher-energy) isomer; each cis double bond adds strain.","traps":["Heat of combustion vs stability reversed: Less stable isomer releases more heat.","Mis-assigning cis/trans in skeletal drawing: Check substituents on each C=C separately."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"If enthalpy of atomisation for Br₂(l) is x kJ/mol and bond enthalpy for Br₂ is y kJ/mol, the relation between them:","options":["is x = y","is x < y","does not exist","is x > y"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) x = y holds only for gaseous diatomics like H₂(g); (B) reverses the sign; (C) is a non-answer.\nWorking: atomisation of liquid Br₂ = vaporisation + bond dissociation, so x = ΔvapH + y > y.\nKey idea: Atomisation starts from the standard state (liquid), bond enthalpy from the gas.","traps":["Equating atomisation and bond enthalpy: Check the physical state given."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Entropy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The true statement amongst the following is:","options":["Both ΔS and S are functions of temperature.","S is not a function of temperature but ΔS is a function of temperature.","Both S and ΔS are not functions of temperature.","S is a function of temperature but ΔS is not a function of temperature."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reflects the usual numerical approximation that ΔS is constant; (B), (C) deny that S rises with T.\nWorking: S increases with T (dS = Cp dT/T) and ΔS of a reaction also varies with T via ΔCp; NCERT third law also treats S as T-dependent.\nKey idea: Strictly both S and ΔS depend on T.","traps":["Approximation taken as fact: ΔS changes with T whenever ΔCp ≠ 0."],"topics":["Entropy","Second & third law"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The internal energy change (in J) when 90 g of water undergoes complete evaporation at 100 °C is ____. (Given: ΔH_vap for water at 373 K = 41 kJ/mol, R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"189000 to 190000","range":[189000.0,190000.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 37899 (1 mol only), 220506 (adding RTΔn), 200843 (using T in °C), 205000 (ignoring Δn_g).\nWorking: n = 5 mol; ΔH = 5 × 41000 = 205000 J; Δn_g RT = 5 × 8.314 × 373 = 15505.6 J; ΔU = ΔH – Δn_gRT = 189494.4 J, inside the accepted range.\nKey idea: For a liquid → vapour change, Δn_g equals the moles of vapour formed.","traps":["Sign of Δn_gRT: ΔU is smaller than ΔH when gas is formed.","Moles not scaled: Convert 90 g to 5 mol first."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The heat of combustion of ethanol into carbon dioxide and water is –327 kcal at constant pressure. The heat evolved (in cal) at constant volume and 27°C (if all gases behave ideally) is (R = 2 cal mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"-326400","range":[-326400.0,-326400.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 327600 / –327600 (sign of Δn_g reversed); 326400 vs –326400 (sign convention); using Δn_g = +2 if H₂O taken as gas.\nWorking: C₂H₅OH(l) + 3O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g) + 3H₂O(l), Δn_g = –1. ΔU = ΔH – Δn_gRT = –327000 + 2 × 300 = –326400 cal, so 326400 cal is evolved. The official answer is –326400, i.e. the signed ΔU, although 'heat evolved' would normally be quoted as a positive number.\nKey idea: Count only gaseous moles; liquid ethanol and water do not enter Δn_g.","traps":["Δn_g sign or water phase: At 27 °C water is liquid: Δn_g = 2 – 3 = –1.","Heat evolved vs ΔU sign: Note the sign convention; the official answer is the signed ΔU (–326400)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"For one mole of an ideal gas, which of these statements must be true ? (a) U and H each depends only on temperature (b) Compressibility factor z is not equal to 1 (c) C_P,m – C_V,m = R (d) dU = C_V dT for any process","options":["(a), (c) and (d)","(b), (c) and (d)","(c) and (d)","(a) and (c)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) drops (d) by thinking dU = CvdT only at constant V; (C) drops (a); (B) wrongly includes z ≠ 1.\nWorking: for an ideal gas U and H are functions of T only; z = 1; Cp – Cv = R; dU = Cv dT holds for any process because U depends only on T.\nKey idea: For ideal gas U = U(T) only, so dU = CvdT always.","traps":["dU = CvdT only at constant V: For an ideal gas U depends only on T, so it holds for any path.","z ≠ 1 for ideal gas: Ideal gas: z = 1 exactly."],"topics":["Heat capacity & calorimetry","First law"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Five moles of an ideal gas at 1 bar and 298 K is expanded into vacuum to double the volume. The work done is :","options":["C_V(T₂ − T₁)","−RT ln V₂/V₁","−RT(V₂ − V₁)","zero"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is reversible isothermal work (per mole); (C) is irreversible work against constant pressure written wrongly; (A) is ΔU for an adiabatic change.\nWorking: expansion into vacuum (free expansion) has p_ext = 0, so w = −p_ext ΔV = 0.\nKey idea: p_ext = 0 → w = 0 regardless of n, T or volume change.","traps":["Using reversible isothermal formula: Check p_ext first; vacuum means no opposing pressure."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Lattice enthalpy and enthalpy of solution of NaCl are 788 kJ mol⁻¹ and 4 kJ mol⁻¹, respectively. The hydration enthalpy of NaCl is:","options":["−780 kJ mol⁻¹","−784 kJ mol⁻¹","780 kJ mol⁻¹","784 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) −780 – added 4 instead of subtracting; (C) and (D) – positive hydration enthalpy (sign lost).\nWorking: ΔsolH = ΔlatticeH + ΔhydH → 4 = 788 + ΔhydH → ΔhydH = −784 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Hydration is exothermic; ΔhydH = ΔsolH − ΔlatticeH.","traps":["Sign of hydration enthalpy: Hydration releases energy: negative.","4 added instead of subtracted: ΔhydH = 4 − 788."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For a dimerization reaction, 2A(g) → A₂(g), at 298 K, ΔU° = −20 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔS° = −30 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, then the ΔG° will be ______ J.","type":"num","answer":"-13540 to -13537","range":[-13540.0,-13537.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −11060 J (ΔU used as ΔH: −20000 + 8940), −8582 J (Δng taken as +1), −28940 J (sign of TΔS term wrong), and kJ answers like −13.54 entered when J is asked.\nWorking: Δng = −1; ΔH° = ΔU° + ΔngRT = −20000 − 8.314 × 298 = −22477.6 J; ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS° = −22477.6 + 298 × 30 = −13537.6 J, inside the NTA range −13540 to −13537.\nKey idea: Gas moles fall from 2 to 1, so ΔH is more negative than ΔU by RT.","traps":["ΔU used directly as ΔH: Correct with ΔngRT first.","Sign of Δng: Δng = n(products) − n(reactants) = 1 − 2 = −1."],"topics":["Gibbs energy","Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The variation of equilibrium constant with temperature is given below :\nTemperature: T₁ = 25°C, K₁ = 10; T₂ = 100°C, K₂ = 100\nThe values of ΔH°, ΔG° at T₁ and ΔG° at T₂ (in kJ mol⁻¹) respectively, are close to\n[Use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","options":["0.64, −5.71 and −14.29","28.4, −7.14 and −5.71","28.4, −5.71 and −14.29","0.64, −7.14 and −5.71"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.64 comes from a units/log slip in ΔH°; (B)/(D) swap ΔG° values or use T in °C (e.g. −7.14 from wrong T/K pairing).\nWorking: log(K₂/K₁) = ΔH°/2.303R (1/T₁ − 1/T₂) → 1 = ΔH°/(19.15) × 6.75×10⁻⁴ → ΔH° ≈ 28.4 kJ; ΔG°₁ = −2.303 × 8.314 × 298 × 1 = −5.71 kJ; ΔG°₂ = −2.303 × 8.314 × 373 × 2 = −14.29 kJ.\nKey idea: Two K values at two T give ΔH°; each K with its own T gives ΔG°.","traps":["Celsius used in place of kelvin: Convert: 298 K and 373 K.","ΔG° values paired with wrong temperature: Compute ΔG° for each (T, K) pair separately."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the following equations does not correctly represent the first law of thermodynamics for the given processes involving an ideal gas ? (Assume non-expansion work is zero)","options":["Cyclic process : q = – w","Isothermal process : q = – w","Adiabatic process : ΔU = – w","Isochoric process : ΔU = q"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) cyclic ΔU = 0 ⇒ q = –w (correct); (B) isothermal ideal gas ΔU = 0 (correct); (D) isochoric w = 0 ⇒ ΔU = q (correct).\nWorking: with ΔU = q + w, adiabatic (q = 0) gives ΔU = w, not –w; the other three are correct.\nKey idea: IUPAC convention: w is work done on the system.","traps":["Using physics sign convention: Fix the convention: ΔU = q + w.","Selecting a true statement in a NOT question: Mark each option T/F first."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"For silver, C_p (J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹) = 23 + 0.01T. If the temperature (T) of 3 moles of silver is raised from 300 K to 1000 K at 1 atm pressure, the value of ΔH will be close to","options":["21 kJ","16 kJ","13 kJ","62 kJ"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 21 kJ = per mole only; (B) 16 kJ = per mole with constant C_p = 23; (C) 13 kJ = arithmetic slip.\nWorking: ΔH = 3∫(23 + 0.01T)dT = 3[23 × 700 + 0.005(1000² – 300²)] = 3[16100 + 4550] = 61950 J ≈ 62 kJ.\nKey idea: Integrate, then multiply by n = 3.","traps":["Forgetting the 3 moles: Multiply by n at the end.","Ignoring the 0.01T term: Integrate C_p dT."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"5 moles of an ideal gas at 100 K are allowed to undergo reversible compression till its temperature becomes 200 K. If C_V = 28 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, calculate ΔU and Δ(pV) for this process. (R = 8.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":["ΔU = 14 kJ; Δ(pV) = 4 kJ","ΔU = 14 kJ; Δ(pV) = 18 kJ","ΔU = 2.8 kJ; Δ(pV) = 0.8 kJ","ΔU = 14 kJ; Δ(pV) = 0.8 kJ"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 18 kJ is ΔH (nCpΔT) put as Δ(pV); (C)/(D) drop n = 5 in one or both terms.\nWorking: ΔU = nC_VΔT = 5 × 28 × 100 = 14 kJ; Δ(pV) = nRΔT = 5 × 8 × 100 = 4 kJ.\nKey idea: For an ideal gas both ΔU and Δ(pV) depend only on ΔT.","traps":["Δ(pV) confused with ΔH: Δ(pV) = nRΔT only.","Moles dropped: Multiply by n = 5."],"topics":["Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","Heat capacity & calorimetry"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"System & state functions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Among the following, the set of parameters that represents path function, is : (A) q + w ; (B) q ; (C) w ; (D) H − TS","options":["(A) and (D)","(B), (C) and (D)","(B) and (C)","(A), (B) and (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (A) and (D) include q + w (state function ΔU); option (B) includes H − TS (= G, state function).\nWorking: q and w individually depend on path; q + w = ΔU and H − TS = G are state functions.\nKey idea: Sum q + w = ΔU, a state function, though each part is path-dependent.","traps":["q + w treated as path function: Recognise q + w = ΔU.","H − TS not recognised as G: Rewrite H − TS as G."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"First law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"During compression of a spring the work done is 10 kJ and 2 kJ escaped to the surroundings as heat. The change in internal energy, ΔU (in kJ) is:","options":["8","12","– 12","–8"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 12 adds magnitudes ignoring heat-loss sign; (C) –12 and (D) –8 treat compression work as done by the system.\nWorking: work done on the system w = +10 kJ, heat lost q = –2 kJ, ΔU = q + w = +8 kJ.\nKey idea: Compression = work on the system (+); heat escaping (–).","traps":["Sign of q and w swapped: Work on system +, heat lost –."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"A process will be spontaneous at all temperatures if :","options":["ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0","ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0","ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0","ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) never spontaneous; (C) spontaneous only at high T; (D) only at low T.\nWorking: ΔG = ΔH – TΔS is negative at every T only when ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0.\nKey idea: Both terms favourable ⇒ always spontaneous.","traps":["Temperature-dependent case chosen: Check that both –ΔH and +TΔS make ΔG negative."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enthalpy (ΔH = ΔU + ΔnRT)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The difference between ΔH and ΔU (ΔH – ΔU), when the combustion of one mole of heptane (l) is carried out at a temperature T, is equal to:","options":["3RT","–3RT","–4RT","4RT"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) +RT values from wrong sign or counting heptane as gas (Δn = 7 – 12 = –5 is not offered) or water as gas (Δn = 15 – 11 = +4 → (D)); (B) –3RT from an arithmetic slip in O₂.\nWorking: C₇H₁₆(l) + 11 O₂(g) → 7 CO₂(g) + 8 H₂O(l); Δn_g = 7 – 11 = –4, so ΔH – ΔU = –4RT.\nKey idea: Water is liquid and heptane is liquid; only O₂ and CO₂ count.","traps":["Water counted as gas: At temperature T in a calorimeter water is liquid as stated by NCERT convention; count only gases."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"An ideal gas is allowed to expand from 1 L to 10 L against a constant external pressure of 1 bar. The work done in kJ is :","options":["−9.0","+10.0","−0.9","−2.0"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) −9.0 forgets L bar → kJ conversion factor 0.1; (B) sign and ΔV wrong; (D) unrelated.\nWorking: w = −p_ex ΔV = −1 bar × 9 L = −9 L bar = −900 J = −0.9 kJ.\nKey idea: 1 L bar = 100 J.","traps":["L bar to kJ: 1 L bar = 10⁻³ m³ × 10⁵ Pa = 100 J.","Sign of expansion work: IUPAC sign: expansion work is negative."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Thermochemistry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Enthalpy of sublimation of iodine is 24 cal g⁻¹ at 200°C. If specific heat of I₂(s) and I₂(vap) are 0.055 and 0.031 cal g⁻¹ K⁻¹ respectively, then enthalpy of sublimation of iodine at 250°C in cal g⁻¹ is :","options":["2.85","11.4","5.7","22.8"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(B) are fractions of 22.8 from dividing by 8, 4 or 2 (e.g. confusing per gram with per mole of I atoms); adding instead of subtracting gives 25.2 (not offered).\nWorking: Kirchhoff: ΔH₂ = ΔH₁ + ΔC_p ΔT = 24 + (0.031 − 0.055)(50) = 24 − 1.2 = 22.8 cal g⁻¹.\nKey idea: ΔC_p = C_p(vap) − C_p(solid) is negative, so ΔH_sub falls with T.","traps":["Sign of ΔC_p: ΔC_p = products − reactants.","Distractors as fractions of the answer: Units are per gram throughout; no molar conversion needed."],"topics":["Thermochemistry","Heat capacity & calorimetry"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The INCORRECT match in the following is :","options":["ΔG° < 0, K < 1","ΔG° = 0, K = 1","ΔG° > 0, K < 1","ΔG° < 0, K > 1"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)–(D) are all correct pairings; a student who reverses the sign of the relation picks (C) or (D).\nWorking: ΔG° = −RT ln K; ΔG° < 0 requires ln K > 0, i.e. K > 1.\nKey idea: ΔG° negative ⇔ K > 1.","traps":["Sign of –RT ln K reversed: Test K = 10: ln K > 0 so ΔG° < 0.","Selecting a correct pair: Underline INCORRECT before checking."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Consider the reversible isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a closed system at two different temperatures T₁ and T₂ (T₁ < T₂). The correct graphical depiction of the dependence of work done (w) on the final volume (V) is:","options":["Graph (A)","Graph (B)","Graph (C)","Graph (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): T₁ line starts above the axis (|w| ≠ 0 at the start of expansion). (C): both lines start above the axis. (D): tempting because it shows a common start at the origin, but that assumes both expansions began from the same Vᵢ (= 1), which is not given.\nWorking: |w| = nRT ln(V/Vᵢ) = nRT ln V − nRT ln Vᵢ, a straight line against ln V with slope nRT (steeper for T₂) that must start from |w| = 0 at V = Vᵢ, i.e. on the ln V axis. (A) and (C) have a line beginning above the axis, so they are wrong. (B) shows both lines starting on the axis, T₂ steeper, at different Vᵢ – valid because the question does not fix a common initial volume. (D) would also be valid only if both expansions began from the same Vᵢ (lnVᵢ ≈ 0); since that is not stated, (B) is the general depiction.\nKey idea: Each line starts at |w| = 0 on the ln V axis (at ln Vᵢ); slope nRT, so the T₂ line is steeper and may cross the T₁ line if Vᵢ differs.","traps":["Assuming a common starting volume: Only T differs in the stem; Vᵢ is not fixed, so the lines can start at different points on the ln V axis.","Line starting above the axis: |w| = 0 when V = Vᵢ, so every line must begin on the ln V axis."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Jan09S1C13-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A process has ΔH = 200 J mol⁻¹ and ΔS = 40 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Out of the values given below, choose the minimum temperature above which the process will be spontaneous :","options":["5 K","4 K","20 K","12 K"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 4 K: below threshold (ΔG > 0). (C)/(D): spontaneous but not the minimum threshold.\nWorking: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0 when T > ΔH/ΔS = 200/40 = 5 K; 5 K is the threshold above which the process is spontaneous.\nKey idea: T* = ΔH/ΔS = 5 K.","traps":["Picking a higher spontaneous value: The question asks the threshold \"above which\" – that is 5 K."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Work & heat","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"An ideal gas undergoes isothermal compression from 5 m³ to 1 m³ against a constant external pressure of 4 N m⁻². Heat released in this process is used to increase the temperature of 1 mole of Al. If molar heat capacity of Al is 24 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, the temperature of Al increases by :","options":["3/2 K","2/3 K","1 K","2 K"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3/2 K: inverts the ratio (24/16). (C) 1 K: guesses or uses wrong work value (e.g. 24 J). (D) 2 K: takes work as 48 J or uses V in wrong combination (4×(5+1)×2).\nWorking: w = −p_ext(V₂−V₁) = −4(1−5) = +16 J on the gas; isothermal ideal gas so ΔU = 0 and q = −16 J (released). 16 = 1 × 24 × ΔT gives ΔT = 2/3 K.\nKey idea: Isothermal ideal gas: all work done on it leaves as heat; that heat sets ΔT of the metal.","traps":["Sign of work in compression: Use magnitude 16 J for heat released, then divide by nC.","Inverting ΔT = q/(nC): Write q = nCΔT explicitly before dividing."],"topics":["Work & heat","Heat capacity & calorimetry"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Entropy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The process with negative entropy change is :","options":["Dissolution of iodine in water","Synthesis of ammonia from N₂ and H₂","Dissociation of CaSO₄(s) to CaO(s) and SO₃(g)","Sublimation of dry ice"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) dissolution of I₂: disorder increases. (C) solid → solid + gas: ΔS > 0. (D) sublimation: solid → gas, large ΔS > 0.\nWorking: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) has Δn_g = −2, so ΔS < 0; dissolution, decomposition giving gas and sublimation all increase entropy.\nKey idea: Fewer gas moles → lower entropy.","traps":["Exothermic synthesis thought favourable in entropy too: Judge ΔS by Δn_g, not by spontaneity."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Entropy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Two blocks of the same metal having same mass and at temperature T₁ and T₂, respectively, are brought in contact with each other and allowed to attain thermal equilibrium at constant pressure. The change in entropy, ΔS, for this process is:","options":["2C_P ln[(T₁ + T₂)/(4T₁T₂)]","2C_P ln[(T₁ + T₂)^(1/2)/(T₁T₂)]","C_P ln[(T₁ + T₂)²/(4T₁T₂)]","2C_P ln[(T₁ + T₂)/(2T₁T₂)]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) are dimensionally wrong (argument has units K⁻¹). (B) wrong exponent placement.\nWorking: final T = (T₁+T₂)/2; ΔS = C_P ln(T_f/T₁) + C_P ln(T_f/T₂) = C_P ln[(T₁+T₂)²/(4T₁T₂)].\nKey idea: Log arguments must be dimensionless – only (C) passes the check.","traps":["Dimensional check skipped: ln of a dimensionless ratio only.","Final temperature: Heat balance gives arithmetic mean."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For the chemical reaction X ⇌ Y, the standard reaction Gibbs energy depends on temperature T (in K) as ΔrG° (in kJ mol⁻¹) = 120 − (3/8)T. The major component of the reaction mixture at T is:","options":["X if T = 315 K","X if T = 350 K","Y if T = 300 K","Y if T = 280 K"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) X at 350 K – above crossover, Y favoured. (C)/(D) Y below crossover – sign reversed.\nWorking: ΔrG° = 0 at T = 320 K; below 320 K ΔrG° > 0, K < 1, X predominates; above 320 K Y predominates. Only (A) is consistent.\nKey idea: Find T where ΔG° = 0 (320 K) and decide sides.","traps":["Sign of ΔG° vs K: ΔG° > 0 → K < 1 → reactant major."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spontaneity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The reaction, MgO(s) + C(s) → Mg(s) + CO(g), for which ΔrH° = +491.1 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔrS° = 198.0 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, is not feasible at 298 K. Temperature above which reaction will be feasible is :-","options":["1890.0 K","2480.3 K","2040.5 K","2380.5 K"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are near-value distractors; using kJ with J gives 2.48 K (not offered).\nWorking: ΔG° = 0 at T = ΔH°/ΔS° = 491100 / 198.0 = 2480.3 K; above this ΔG° < 0.\nKey idea: T* = ΔH/ΔS with both in J.","traps":["kJ vs J mismatch: Convert ΔH to J before dividing."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Gibbs energy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The standard reaction Gibbs energy for a chemical reaction at an absolute temperature T is given by ΔrG° = A – BT, where A and B are non-zero constants. Which of the following is TRUE about this reaction ?","options":["Exothermic if B < 0","Exothermic if A > 0 and B < 0","Endothermic if A < 0 and B > 0","Endothermic if A > 0"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses B (= ΔS) to judge exo/endothermic. (B) A > 0 means endothermic, not exothermic. (C) A < 0 means exothermic.\nWorking: comparing with ΔG° = ΔH° – TΔS° gives A = ΔH° and B = ΔS°. The reaction is endothermic when A > 0, whatever B is.\nKey idea: Heat change is set by A (= ΔH) alone; B only affects spontaneity.","traps":["Sign of ΔS used for heat: Only ΔH decides exo/endo.","A > 0 read as exothermic: ΔH > 0 = endothermic."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"ΔG° and K","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"For the equilibrium, 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻, the value of ΔG° at 298 K is approximately :-","options":["–80 kJ mol⁻¹","–100 kJ mol⁻¹","100 kJ mol⁻¹","80 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) –80: sign error. (B) –100 / (C) 100: wrong log K or rounding.\nWorking: K = Kw = 10⁻¹⁴; ΔG° = –2.303RT log K = 2.303 × 8.314 × 298 × 14 ≈ 79.9 kJ mol⁻¹ ≈ +80.\nKey idea: K < 1 → ΔG° > 0; log Kw = –14.","traps":["Sign of ΔG°: K ≪ 1 means ΔG° positive.","K taken as [H₃O⁺][OH⁻]/[H₂O]²: Pure liquid water has activity 1, K = Kw."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heat capacity & calorimetry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"For diatomic ideal gas in a closed system, which of the following plots does not correctly describe the relation between various thermodynamic quantities ?","options":["Cv vs T: flat, then S-shaped rise, then flat at a higher value","Cp vs P: straight line rising with P","Cv vs V: horizontal line","U vs T: increasing line with a steeper segment in the middle"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks odd but is correct (degrees of freedom unfreeze). (D) U rising with T is correct. (C) Cv independent of V is correct.\nWorking: for an ideal gas Cp depends only on T, not on pressure, so a rising Cp–P line is wrong; Cv rises with T as rotational/vibrational modes become active; Cv independent of V; U increases with T.\nKey idea: Ideal-gas Cp, Cv and U are functions of T only.","traps":["Rejecting the S-shaped Cv curve: Cv steps up as vibrational modes activate at high T.","NOT wording: Look for the one plot that is wrong."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hess's law","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given :\n(i) C(graphite) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g); ΔrH° = x kJ mol⁻¹\n(ii) C(graphite) + ½O₂(g) → CO(g); ΔrH° = y kJ mol⁻¹\n(iii) CO(g) + ½O₂(g) → CO₂(g); ΔrH° = z kJ mol⁻¹\nBased on the above thermochemical equations, find out which one of the following algebraic relationships is correct ?","options":["z = x + y","x = y − z","x = y + z","y = 2z − x"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) subtract instead of add or mix up which reaction is the overall one; (D) arbitrary combination with a factor 2.\nWorking: (ii) + (iii) = (i), so x = y + z (Hess's law).\nKey idea: Step (ii) followed by step (iii) gives the overall reaction (i).","traps":["Sign slip in Hess cycle: Add the equations, then compare with the target."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Types of processes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The combination of plots which does not represent isothermal expansion of an ideal gas is:\n(A) P vs 1/V_m: straight line through origin with positive slope\n(B) P vs V_m: straight line with negative slope\n(C) PV_m vs P: horizontal line\n(D) U vs V_m: straight line with positive slope (non-zero intercept)","options":["(A) and (C)","(A) and (D)","(B) and (D)","(B) and (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) picks the two correct graphs (misreads 'does not'). (B) and (D) each keep one correct and one wrong graph – catches students who think U rises with V or that P–V is linear.\nWorking: isothermal ideal gas has P ∝ 1/V_m (A correct), PV_m constant (C correct), P vs V_m a hyperbola not a straight line (B wrong), and U constant because U depends only on T (D wrong).\nKey idea: Isothermal ideal gas: PV = const and U = const.","traps":["Picking the correct plots in a 'does not' item: Mark each plot correct/incorrect, then answer the negation.","U thought to increase on expansion: Ideal-gas U depends only on T."]}],"m11-dblock":[{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The element which shows only one oxidation state other than its elemental form is :","options":["Nickel","Titanium","Cobalt","Scandium"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ni: +2, +3, +4. (B) Ti: +2, +3, +4. (C) Co: +2, +3.\nWorking: Sc shows only +3 (3d¹4s² → Sc³⁺ noble-gas core).\nKey idea: Sc → only +3; Zn → only +2.","traps":["Zn/Sc both single-state: Recall Sc³⁺ only."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Consider the following reaction\nMnO₂ + KOH + O₂ → A + H₂O.\nProduct 'A' in neutral or acidic medium disproportionate to give products 'B' and 'C' along with water. The sum of spin-only magnetic moment values of B and C is ______ BM. (nearest integer) (Given atomic number of Mn is 25)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 or 7: adds manganate (d¹, 1.73) instead of MnO₂; 5–6 if Mn²⁺ assumed; 3: truncates 3.87.\nWorking: A = K₂MnO₄; 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O. B/C = MnO₄⁻ (Mn⁷⁺, d⁰, 0 BM) and MnO₂ (Mn⁴⁺, d³, √15 = 3.87 BM); sum ≈ 3.87 → 4.\nKey idea: MnO₄⁻ is d⁰ (diamagnetic); MnO₂ is d³ → 3.87 BM.","traps":["MnO₂ product forgotten: Write the balanced disproportionation: 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂.","Rounding 3.87: 3.87 rounds to 4."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A first row transition metal in its +2 oxidation state has a spin-only magnetic moment value of 3.86 BM. The atomic number of the metal is","options":["22","23","26","25"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 22 – Ti²⁺ is d², 2.83 BM. (C) 26 – Fe²⁺ d⁶, 4.90. (D) 25 – Mn²⁺ d⁵, 5.92.\nWorking: μ = √(n(n+2)) = 3.87 → n = 3; M²⁺ with d³ is V²⁺ (Z = 23). Ti²⁺ d² (2.83), Fe²⁺ d⁶ (4.90), Mn²⁺ d⁵ (5.92).\nKey idea: 3.87 BM ↔ 3 unpaired; d³ M²⁺ ⇒ V.","traps":["Z vs d-count mismatch: M²⁺ loses 4s²: d-count = Z − 20."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A first row transition metal with highest enthalpy of atomisation, upon reaction with oxygen at high temperature forms oxides of formula M₂Oₙ (where n = 3, 4, 5). The 'spin-only' magnetic moment value of the amphoteric oxide from the above oxides is ______ BM (near integer)\n(Given atomic number: Sc: 21, Ti: 22, V: 23, Cr: 24, Mn: 25, Fe: 26, Co: 27, Ni: 28, Cu: 29, Zn: 30)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (choosing V₂O₄, V⁴⁺ d¹, 1.73 → 2); 3 (V₂O₃, d², 2.83); 4 or 5 (choosing Cr with ΔaH° assumed highest, Cr₂O₃ d³ 3.87).\nWorking: V has the highest ΔaH° (515 kJ mol⁻¹) in the 3d series; oxides V₂O₃, V₂O₄, V₂O₅. NCERT calls V₂O₅ amphoteric (mainly acidic); V⁵⁺ is d⁰ → 0 BM.\nKey idea: V₂O₅ amphoteric, V⁵⁺ d⁰ ⇒ μ = 0.","traps":["V₂O₄ taken as amphoteric: NCERT: V₂O₅ amphoteric, V₂O₄ less basic.","Cr taken as highest ΔaH°: Table: V 515 > Fe/Ni/Co; Cr only 397."],"topics":["Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","Magnetic moment","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The metal that shows highest and maximum number of oxidation states is:","options":["Co","Mn","Fe","Ti"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Fe: student recalls ferrate(VI). (A) Co, (D) Ti: fewer states.\nWorking: Mn shows +2 to +7 (six states, highest +7); Fe up to +6, Co up to +4/+5, Ti +2 to +4.\nKey idea: Mn: 3d⁵4s² → all 7 electrons usable, +2 to +7.","traps":["Fe chosen for \"many states\": Count states in Table 4.3: Mn has the most up to +7."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one of the following complexes will exhibit the least paramagnetic behaviour? [Atomic number, Cr = 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27]","options":["[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Mn²⁺ is the most paramagnetic (reverse reading). (A)/(D) both 4 unpaired; a student who pairs electrons (low spin) in Fe²⁺ would wrongly pick (A) as diamagnetic.\nWorking: H₂O weak field, high spin: Fe²⁺ d⁶ 4 unpaired, Mn²⁺ d⁵ 5, Co²⁺ d⁷ 3, Cr²⁺ d⁴ 4 → Co²⁺ least paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Weak field H₂O → high spin; fewest unpaired = d⁷ (C).","traps":["Fe²⁺ taken as low spin: H₂O is weak field → high spin d⁶ has 4 unpaired.","Least vs most: Read \"least\"."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The spin-only magnetic moment value of the ion among Ti²⁺, V²⁺, Co³⁺ and Cr²⁺, that acts as strong oxidising agent in aqueous solution is ____ BM (Near integer).\n(Given atomic numbers: Ti: 22, V: 23, Cr: 24, Co: 27)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 if Co³⁺ treated as low spin; 4.90 for Cr²⁺ (d⁴) also rounds to 5 – a student who picks Cr²⁺ thinking it oxidising also gets 5 by luck; Ti²⁺ 2.83 → 3, V²⁺ 3.87 → 4.\nWorking: Ti²⁺, V²⁺, Cr²⁺ are strong reducing agents; Co³⁺ is the strong oxidant. Co³⁺ = 3d⁶, taken as free ion/high spin: 4 unpaired, μ = √24 = 4.90 ≈ 5 BM. (Note: real aqueous [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is low-spin and diamagnetic; the expected answer uses the free-ion count.)\nKey idea: Co³⁺ (aq) is the oxidant; d⁶ high-spin → 4 unpaired → 4.90 BM.","traps":["Reducing vs oxidising ions confused: Low oxidation states (+2 early 3d) are reductants; Co³⁺ is oxidant.","Low-spin Co³⁺ applied: Spin-only question with no ligand field data → count free-ion d⁶."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of ions from the following that have the ability to liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid is ______.\nTi²⁺, Cr²⁺ and V²⁺","options":["1","2","3","0"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): student recalls only Cr²⁺ (most familiar reducing agent).\nWorking: all three are strong reducing agents (E°(M³⁺/M²⁺) negative) and reduce H⁺ to H₂.\nKey idea: Negative E°(M³⁺/M²⁺) → M²⁺ reduces H⁺.","traps":["Only Cr²⁺ remembered: All three are named together in NCERT."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The fusion of chromite ore with sodium carbonate in the presence of air leads to the formation of products A and B along with the evolution of CO₂. The sum of spin-only magnetic moment values of A and B is ______ B.M. (Nearest integer)\n[Given atomic number: C: 6, Na: 11, O: 8, Fe: 26, Cr: 24]","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (Fe taken as Fe²⁺, d⁶, √24 = 4.90, with chromate 0); 10 (Cr taken as Cr³⁺, d³: 3.87 + 5.92 = 9.79); 9 (both slips: 4.90 + 3.87 = 8.77).\nWorking: 4FeCr₂O₄ + 8Na₂CO₃ + 7O₂ → 8Na₂CrO₄ + 2Fe₂O₃ + 8CO₂. Na₂CrO₄ Cr(VI) d⁰ → 0; Fe₂O₃ Fe³⁺ d⁵ → √35 = 5.92 BM; sum ≈ 6.\nKey idea: Chromate is d⁰ (diamagnetic); ferric oxide has five unpaired electrons.","traps":["Iron oxidation state: Air oxidises Fe to Fe₂O₃ (Fe³⁺).","Chromium taken as Cr³⁺: Product chromate has Cr(VI), d⁰."],"topics":["K₂Cr₂O₇","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The number of elements from the following that do not belong to lanthanoids is Eu, Cm, Er, Tb, Yb and Lu","options":["3","1","4","5"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3 / (C) 4 / (D) 5: student unsure of Lu (end member) or Eu/Yb/Tb and counts them as non-lanthanoids; also confusing Cm as a lanthanoid gives 0 (not offered).\nWorking: Eu (63), Er (68), Tb (65), Yb (70) and Lu (71) are lanthanoids (Ce–Lu); only Cm (96) is an actinoid → 1.\nKey idea: Lanthanoids run Ce(58)–Lu(71); Cm is an actinoid (Z = 96).","traps":["Lu wrongly excluded from lanthanoids: Lanthanoid series in NCERT ends at lutetium.","Cm taken as a lanthanoid: Remember Cm = curium, 5f series."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Actinoids"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The difference in the 'spin-only' magnetic moment values of KMnO₄ and the manganese product formed during titration of KMnO₄ against oxalic acid in acidic medium is ______ BM. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (Mn product taken as MnO₂, d³ → 3.87), 5 (rounding/unpaired electrons count), 2 (MnO₄²⁻).\nWorking: Mn(VII) in KMnO₄ is d⁰ → 0 BM; product Mn²⁺ (d⁵, 5 unpaired) → √35 = 5.92 BM; difference ≈ 6.\nKey idea: Acidic medium → Mn²⁺ (d⁵, high-spin, 5.92 BM); MnO₄⁻ is d⁰.","traps":["Product taken as MnO₂: Acidic medium → Mn²⁺.","Number of unpaired electrons given instead of moment: Use √(n(n+2))."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Among CrO, Cr₂O₃ and CrO₃, the sum of spin-only magnetic moment values of basic and amphoteric oxides is ______ 10⁻² BM (nearest integer).\n(Given atomic number of Cr is 24 )","type":"num","answer":"877","range":[877.0,877.0],"explain":"Common slips: 490 or 387 (only one oxide counted because oxide nature is misjudged); 979 (Cr²⁺ taken as d⁵: 5.92 + 3.87 = 9.79).\nWorking: CrO basic (Cr²⁺, d⁴, √24 = 4.90 BM); Cr₂O₃ amphoteric (Cr³⁺, d³, √15 = 3.87 BM); CrO₃ acidic excluded. Sum = 8.77 BM = 877 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Lower oxidation state → more basic; CrO₃ (d⁰) acidic.","traps":["Cr²⁺ d-count wrong: Cr²⁺ = 3d⁴.","Oxide nature misassigned: Basic ↔ low OS; acidic ↔ high OS."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Arrange the following elements in the increasing order of number of unpaired electrons in it.\n(A) Sc (B) Cr (C) V (D) Ti (E) Mn\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) < (D) < (C) < (B) < (E)","(A) < (D) < (C) < (E) < (B)","(B) < (C) < (D) < (E) < (A)","(C) < (E) < (B) < (A) < (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): Cr counted as 4 (3d⁴4s²) or Mn above Cr. (C)/(D): random or reversed orders.\nWorking: Sc 3d¹4s² (1), Ti 3d²4s² (2), V 3d³4s² (3), Mn 3d⁵4s² (5), Cr 3d⁵4s¹ (6).\nKey idea: Cr is 3d⁵4s¹ → 6 unpaired, more than Mn.","traps":["Cr taken as 3d⁴4s²: Remember Cr and Cu exceptions."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Among VO₂⁺, MnO₄⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻, the spin-only magnetic moment value of the species with least oxidising ability is ______ BM (Nearest integer).\n(Given atomic number V = 23, Mn = 25, Cr = 24)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (V taken as +4, d¹ → 1.73), 3 or 4 (Mn/Cr ion chosen with wrong d count).\nWorking: oxidising power VO₂⁺ < Cr₂O₇²⁻ < MnO₄⁻; VO₂⁺ has V(+5), 3d⁰ → 0 BM (all three are d⁰ anyway).\nKey idea: All three are d⁰; least oxidising VO₂⁺ gives 0.","traps":["VO₂⁺ confused with VO²⁺: VO₂⁺: x − 4 = +1 → V(V), d⁰."],"topics":["E° trends","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Iron (III) catalyses the reaction between iodide and persulphate ions, in which\nA. Fe³⁺ oxidises the iodide ion\nB. Fe³⁺ oxidises the persulphate ion\nC. Fe²⁺ reduces the iodide ion\nD. Fe²⁺ reduces the persulphate ion\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["B and C only","A only","A and D only","B only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B and C reverse both roles; (B) A only misses the regeneration step; (D) B only is chemically impossible.\nWorking: 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂ (A true); 2Fe²⁺ + S₂O₈²⁻ → 2Fe³⁺ + 2SO₄²⁻ (D true); B and C impossible (I⁻ cannot be reduced further, persulphate is not oxidised).\nKey idea: Catalyst cycles: Fe³⁺ oxidises I⁻, Fe²⁺ formed reduces S₂O₈²⁻ back to Fe³⁺.","traps":["Forgetting the regeneration step: A catalyst must be regenerated – write both steps.","Roles reversed: Check which species can gain/lose electrons."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The 'spin only' magnetic moment value of MO₄²⁻ is ________ BM. (Where M is a metal having least metallic radii among Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Mn and Zn).\n(Given atomic number: Sc = 21, Ti = 22, V = 23, Cr = 24, Mn = 25 and Zn = 30)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.73 if MnO₄²⁻ (Mn, d¹) chosen; 2.83 or 3.87 if Cr oxidation state misread; Zn assumed smallest.\nWorking: metallic radii (pm) Sc 164, Ti 147, V 135, Cr 129, Mn 137, Zn 137 → Cr; CrO₄²⁻ has Cr(VI), d⁰ → μ = 0 BM.\nKey idea: Cr is smallest (129 pm); CrO₄²⁻ is d⁰.","traps":["Mn assumed smallest: Mn (137) is larger than Cr (129).","d-count of Cr(VI): Cr⁶⁺ is 3d⁰."],"topics":["Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The equilibrium Cr₂O₇²⁻ ⇌ 2CrO₄²⁻ is shifted to the right in :","options":["a weakly acidic medium","a basic medium","a neutral medium","an acidic medium"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): acid converts chromate to dichromate (left). (C): neutral gives no specific push.\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 2OH⁻ → 2CrO₄²⁻ + H₂O; base converts dichromate to chromate.\nKey idea: OH⁻ removes H⁺ → chromate (yellow).","traps":["Direction of pH effect: Acid → dichromate (orange); base → chromate (yellow)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Fusion of MnO₂ with KOH and an oxidising agent gives dark green K₂MnO₄.\nStatement (II) : Manganate ion on electrolytic oxidation in alkaline medium gives permanganate ion.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C): only if a student doubts the colour or the electrolytic step.\nWorking: both are the NCERT preparation steps of KMnO₄.\nKey idea: Two-stage route: alkaline oxidative fusion, then oxidation of MnO₄²⁻.","traps":["Doubting electrolytic oxidation: NCERT gives both disproportionation and electrolytic routes."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The electronic configuration of Cu(II) is 3d⁹ whereas that of Cu(I) is 3d¹⁰. Which of the following is correct?","options":["Cu(II) is more stable","Cu(II) is less stable","Cu(I) and Cu(II) are equally stable","Stability of Cu(I) and Cu(II) depends on nature of copper salts"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) follows the d¹⁰ stability argument. (C) is a hedge. (D) is partly defensible (CuI, Cu₂O are stable) but not the NCERT-intended answer.\nWorking: in aqueous solution Cu²⁺ is more stable because its much more negative hydration enthalpy outweighs the second ionisation enthalpy; Cu⁺ disproportionates.\nKey idea: Hydration enthalpy, not configuration, decides aqueous stability: Cu²⁺(aq) > Cu⁺(aq).","traps":["Filled d¹⁰ assumed most stable: Apply hydration enthalpy argument for aqueous ions."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Number of colourless lanthanoid ions among the following is ____.\nEu³⁺, Lu³⁺, Nd³⁺, La³⁺, Sm³⁺","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if Eu³⁺ (4f⁶, faintly coloured) is counted colourless; 1 if Lu³⁺ is missed.\nWorking: La³⁺ (4f⁰) and Lu³⁺ (4f¹⁴) are colourless = 2.\nKey idea: Only f⁰ and f¹⁴ ions lack f–f transitions.","traps":["Half-filled/near-half-filled as colourless: Only f⁰ and f¹⁴ are colourless."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Colour of ions"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Actinoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The electronic configuration of Einsteinium is : (Given atomic number of Einsteinium = 99)","options":["[Rn]5f¹³6d⁰7s²","[Rn]5f¹²6d⁰7s²","[Rn]5f¹¹6d⁰7s²","[Rn]5f¹⁰6d⁰7s²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5f¹³ forgets the 7s² electrons. (B)/(D) off by one from miscounting or placing one electron in 6d.\nWorking: 99 − 86 = 13 electrons beyond Rn; 7s² leaves 11 in 5f (no 6d electron for Es).\nKey idea: 13 electrons after [Rn]: 7s² + 5f¹¹.","traps":["Forgetting 7s² in the count: Subtract 2 for 7s first."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : The higher oxidation states are more stable down the group among transition elements unlike p-block elements.\nStatement II : Copper can not liberate hydrogen from weak acids.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats Statement II as false because of the \"weak acid\" wording. (B)/(D) carry over the p-block inert pair trend to the d-block.\nWorking: in d-block groups heavier members favour higher states (Mo(VI), W(VI) > Cr(VI)), opposite to p-block inert pair effect; Cu has positive E° and cannot liberate H₂ from non-oxidising acids.\nKey idea: d-block: heavier = higher states stable; Cu E° = +0.34 V.","traps":["Carrying inert pair trend to d-block: Remember Mo(VI)/W(VI) more stable than Cr(VI).","\"Weak acids\" qualifier: If Cu cannot reduce H⁺ from strong acids, weak acids fail too."],"topics":["Oxidation states","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A transition metal 'M' among Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Mn and Fe has the highest second ionisation enthalpy. The spin-only magnetic moment value of M⁺ ion is ______ BM (Nearest integer)\n(Given atomic number Sc : 21, Ti : 22, V : 23, Cr : 24, Mn : 25, Fe : 26)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: choosing Mn (Mn⁺ 3d⁵4s¹, 6 unpaired) gives 7; reporting the number of unpaired electrons (5) instead of μ gives 5.\nWorking: Cr has the highest ΔiH₂ (Cr⁺ is stable 3d⁵); Cr⁺ = 3d⁵, n = 5, μ = √35 = 5.92 ≈ 6 BM.\nKey idea: Cr⁺ is 3d⁵: removing a second electron breaks a half-filled shell.","traps":["Mn as highest IE₂: IE₂ is high when M⁺ is d⁵ (Cr⁺), not M²⁺.","Configuration of Cr⁺: Cr⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵."],"topics":["Ionisation enthalpy","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In acidic medium, K₂Cr₂O₇ shows oxidising action as represented in the half reaction:\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + XH⁺ + Ye⁻ → 2A + ZH₂O\nX, Y, Z and A are respectively :","options":["14, 7, 6 and Cr³⁺","14, 6, 7 and Cr³⁺","8, 4, 6 and Cr₂O₃","8, 6, 4 and Cr₂O₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): swaps electron and water coefficients (6 vs 7). (C)/(D): thinks Cr₂O₃ forms (as in thermal decomposition of ammonium dichromate).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O (Cr +6 → +3, two Cr → 6 e⁻; 7 O → 7 H₂O → 14 H⁺).\nKey idea: Two Cr each gain 3 e⁻; O count sets water.","traps":["Y and Z swapped: Electrons = 2 × 3; water = 7 O atoms.","Cr₂O₃ as product: In acidic solution Cr stays as Cr³⁺(aq)."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The transition metal having highest 3rd ionisation enthalpy is :","options":["Mn","Fe","Cr","V"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Fe picked by increasing nuclear charge. (C) Cr picked by confusing with second IE (Cr⁺ d⁵). (D) V no special reason.\nWorking: the third IE removes an electron from M²⁺. Mn²⁺ is 3d⁵, a stable half-filled set, so its third IE is the highest; Fe²⁺ (3d⁶) loses its paired electron easily.\nKey idea: Mn²⁺ is 3d⁵; removing the third electron breaks a half-filled set.","traps":["2nd vs 3rd IE exceptional element: Write configuration of M²⁺ for third IE.","Steady increase with Z: Fe²⁺ d⁶ loses its paired electron easily."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : In aqueous solutions Cr²⁺ is reducing while Mn³⁺ is oxidising in nature.\nReason (R) : Extra stability to half filled electronic configuration is observed than incompletely filled electronic configuration.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) rejects R by thinking stability is about fully filled only. (C) feels R does not cover Cr³⁺ (d³ is not half-filled d set, but t₂g³ is half-filled). (D) wrongly reverses Cr²⁺/Mn³⁺ roles.\nWorking: Cr²⁺ (d⁴) → Cr³⁺ (d³, half-filled t₂g), so Cr²⁺ is reducing; Mn³⁺ (d⁴) → Mn²⁺ (d⁵, half-filled d set), so Mn³⁺ is oxidising. Both changes are driven by half-filled stability, so R explains A.\nKey idea: Cr³⁺ has half-filled t₂g³ and Mn²⁺ has half-filled d⁵; both products are extra stable.","traps":["Cr³⁺ d³ seen as not half-filled: In octahedral field t₂g³ is half-filled.","Both are d⁴ so same behaviour expected: Look at the product configuration of each change."],"topics":["E° trends","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following compounds show colour due to d-d transition?","options":["K₂Cr₂O₇","CuSO₄·5H₂O","KMnO₄","K₂CrO₄"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are intensely coloured but d⁰ (Cr⁶⁺, Mn⁷⁺) so colour is charge transfer; a student equating 'coloured' with d–d picks them.\nWorking: Cu²⁺ in CuSO₄·5H₂O is d⁹, so a d–d transition is possible; Cr(VI) in K₂Cr₂O₇ and K₂CrO₄ and Mn(VII) in KMnO₄ are d⁰ and are coloured by ligand-to-metal charge transfer.\nKey idea: No d electrons → no d–d transition.","traps":["Coloured means d–d: Count d electrons: Cr(VI), Mn(VII) are d⁰."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following electronic configuration would be associated with the highest magnetic moment?","options":["[Ar]3d⁷","[Ar]3d⁸","[Ar]3d³","[Ar]3d⁶"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) d⁷: 3 unpaired, 3.87 BM. (B) d⁸: 2 unpaired. (C) d³: 3 unpaired — students who think \"fewer electrons = more unpaired\" pick it.\nWorking: unpaired electrons (free ion, Hund) d⁷ = 3, d⁸ = 2, d³ = 3, d⁶ = 4; μ = √(n(n+2)) is highest for d⁶ (4.90 BM).\nKey idea: Unpaired count peaks at d⁵ and falls symmetrically; d⁶ (D) beats d³ (C) and d⁷ (A).","traps":["Equating more d electrons with more unpaired: Fill five orbitals singly first, then pair: d⁶→4, d⁷→3, d⁸→2."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The electronic configuration for Neodymium is:\n[Atomic Number for Neodymium 60]","options":["[Xe]4f⁴6s²","[Xe]5f⁴7s²","[Xe]4f⁶6s²","[Xe]4f¹5d¹6s²"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 5f/7s belong to actinoids. (C) 4f⁶ counts 8 electrons beyond Xe. (D) 4f¹5d¹ is the Ce pattern (La, Ce, Gd have 5d¹).\nWorking: Z = 60 = 54 (Xe) + 6 → 4f⁴6s²; Nd has no 5d electron.\nKey idea: Only La, Ce, Gd (and Lu) keep a 5d¹ electron; Nd is 4f⁴6s².","traps":["5d¹ pattern applied to all lanthanoids: Remember the 5d¹ exceptions: La, Ce, Gd, Lu.","Electron count off by two: 60 − 54 = 6 electrons: 6s² + 4f⁴."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Identify the incorrect pair from the following :","options":["Haber process - Iron","Polythene preparation - TiCl₄, Al(CH₃)₃","Photography - AgBr","Wacker process - PtCl₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Haber-Fe is correct (finely divided iron). (B) Ziegler catalyst TiCl₄ + Al(CH₃)₃ is the exact NCERT pair, though students who remember Al(C₂H₅)₃ may suspect it. (C) AgBr in photography is correct (light-sensitive).\nWorking: the Wacker process (ethyne/ethene to ethanal) uses PdCl₂, not PtCl₂; the other three pairs are correct.\nKey idea: Wacker = Pd; the only altered entry is a one-letter metal swap.","traps":["Pd and Pt confused in Wacker catalyst: Memorise the NCERT pairs: Wacker-PdCl₂, Ziegler-TiCl₄/Al(CH₃)₃.","Ziegler catalyst alkyl doubted: NCERT text gives Al(CH₃)₃; it is a correct pairing here."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement (I) : In the Lanthanoids, the formation of Ce⁺⁴ is favoured by its noble gas configuration. Statement (II) : Ce⁺⁴ is a strong oxidant reverting to the common +3 state. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) arise if a student thinks a noble-gas configuration ion must be stable and so cannot be a strong oxidant.\nWorking: Ce⁴⁺ is [Xe] (4f⁰) and is a strong oxidant that reverts to Ce³⁺ (E° ≈ +1.74 V).\nKey idea: Noble-gas configuration explains formation; the +3 state is still the most stable, so Ce⁴⁺ oxidises.","traps":["Stable configuration vs strong oxidant: Formation can be favoured while +3 remains thermodynamically preferred."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Choose the correct option having all the elements with d¹⁰ electronic configuration from the following :","options":["⁴⁶Pd, ²⁸Ni, ²⁶Fe, ²⁴Cr","²⁹Cu, ³⁰Zn, ⁴⁸Cd, ⁴⁷Ag","²⁷Co, ²⁸Ni, ²⁶Fe, ²⁴Cr","²⁸Ni, ²⁴Cr, ²⁶Fe, ²⁹Cu"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Pd is 4d¹⁰5s⁰ (d¹⁰) but Ni d⁸, Fe d⁶, Cr d⁵. (C) none are d¹⁰. (D) only Cu is d¹⁰.\nWorking: Cu 3d¹⁰4s¹, Zn 3d¹⁰4s², Ag 4d¹⁰5s¹, Cd 4d¹⁰5s² all have d¹⁰.\nKey idea: Group 11 and 12 atoms are all d¹⁰ thanks to the Cu/Ag exception.","traps":["Pd d¹⁰ pulls toward option A: Every element in the set must be d¹⁰.","Cu counted as d⁹: Cu is 3d¹⁰4s¹ (full d-subshell stability)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In chromyl chloride test for confirmation of Cl⁻ ion, a yellow solution is obtained. Acidification of the solution and addition of amyl alcohol and 10% H₂O₂ turns organic layer blue indicating formation of chromium pentoxide. The oxidation state of chromium in that is","options":["+6","+5","+3","+10"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) +10 assumes all five O are −2; (B) +5 splits the difference; (C) +3 is chromium's common stable state.\nWorking: CrO₅ has one oxo O and two peroxo (O₂²⁻) groups: x − 2 − 4 = 0 → x = +6.\nKey idea: CrO₅ = CrO(O₂)₂; peroxo oxygens are −1 each, so Cr is +6.","traps":["All oxygens taken as −2: Max oxidation state of Cr is +6; look for peroxo groups."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"KMnO₄ decomposes on heating at 513 K to form O₂ along with","options":["K₂MnO₄ & Mn","MnO₂ & K₂O₂","K₂MnO₄ & MnO₂","Mn & KO₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) metallic Mn – impossible thermal products; (B) peroxide not formed.\nWorking: 2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂ at 513 K.\nKey idea: Mn(+7) → Mn(+6) in K₂MnO₄ and Mn(+4) in MnO₂; O is oxidised to O₂.","traps":["Partial recall of products: Both manganate and MnO₂ form together."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"In alkaline medium, MnO₄⁻ oxidises I⁻ to","options":["I₂","IO₃⁻","IO⁻","IO₄⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) I₂ is the product in acidic medium; (C)/(D) other iodine oxoanions.\nWorking: in neutral or faintly alkaline solution 2MnO₄⁻ + H₂O + I⁻ → 2MnO₂ + 2OH⁻ + IO₃⁻.\nKey idea: Acid: I⁻ → I₂; neutral/alkaline: I⁻ → IO₃⁻.","traps":["Acidic-medium product recalled: Check the medium: alkaline gives iodate."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following acts as a strong reducing agent? (Atomic number: Ce = 58, Eu = 63, Gd = 64, Lu = 71)","options":["Eu²⁺","Gd³⁺","Lu³⁺","Ce⁴⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Gd³⁺: half-filled 4f⁷ — very stable, no tendency to be oxidised. (C) Lu³⁺: full 4f¹⁴, stable. (D) Ce⁴⁺: well-known strong oxidising agent, chosen by students who swap oxidant/reductant.\nWorking: Eu²⁺ (4f⁷) readily goes to the common +3 state, so it is a strong reductant; Gd³⁺ (4f⁷) and Lu³⁺ (4f¹⁴) are stable; Ce⁴⁺ is an oxidant.\nKey idea: An ion below the common +3 state wants to lose an electron, so it reduces others.","traps":["Ce⁴⁺ oxidant taken as reductant: Ion above +3 → oxidant; ion below +3 → reductant.","Half-filled f⁷ makes Eu²⁺ seem stable: Stability of configuration does not cancel the drive to reach the common +3 state."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which of the following statements are correct about Zn, Cd and Hg?\nA. They exhibit high enthalpy of atomization as the d-subshell is full.\nB. Zn and Cd do not show variable oxidation state while Hg shows +I and +II.\nC. Compounds of Zn, Cd and Hg are paramagnetic in nature.\nD. Zn, Cd and Hg are called soft metals.\nChoose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["C, D only","B, C only","A, D only","B, D only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C, D: accepts paramagnetism. (B) B, C: same error with C. (C) A, D: accepts the reversed atomisation statement.\nWorking: A false (full d¹⁰ gives weak metallic bonding, low enthalpy of atomisation); B true (Hg forms Hg₂²⁺, +I); C false (d¹⁰, diamagnetic); D true (soft, low-melting metals).\nKey idea: d¹⁰ means no unpaired d electrons: weak metallic bonding (soft, low ΔaH) and diamagnetic compounds.","traps":["Full d-subshell linked to high atomisation enthalpy: Atomisation enthalpy needs unpaired electrons for metallic bonding; Zn is lowest.","Hg(I) overlooked: Mercurous ion Hg₂²⁺ (Hg–Hg bonded) gives Hg +1."],"topics":["Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","Oxidation states","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Species): (A) Cr²⁺ (B) Mn⁺ (C) Ni²⁺ (D) V⁺\nList II (Electronic distribution): (I) 3d⁸ (II) 3d³4s¹ (III) 3d⁴ (IV) 3d⁵4s¹\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps Cr²⁺ and Mn⁺ (taking Cr²⁺ as 3d⁵4s¹-like). (B) and (D) misplace almost all pairs.\nWorking: Cr²⁺ 3d⁴; Mn⁺ 3d⁵4s¹; Ni²⁺ 3d⁸; V⁺ (V 3d³4s² minus one 4s electron) 3d³4s¹ as intended. (Spectroscopically V⁺ is 3d⁴, but 3d⁴ is already used for Cr²⁺, so the intended match is clear.)\nKey idea: Remove 4s electrons first: M⁺ removes one 4s, M²⁺ removes both.","traps":["Electrons removed from 3d first: Ions lose 4s electrons before 3d.","Mn⁺ written as 3d⁶: Mn⁺ keeps one 4s: 3d⁵4s¹."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Diamagnetic Lanthanoid ions are :","options":["La³⁺ & Ce⁴⁺","Nd³⁺ & Ce⁴⁺","Lu³⁺ & Eu³⁺","Nd³⁺ & Eu³⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Nd³⁺ 4f³ paramagnetic. (C) Lu³⁺ correct but Eu³⁺ 4f⁶ paramagnetic. (D) Both paramagnetic.\nWorking: La³⁺ and Ce⁴⁺ are 4f⁰ → no unpaired electrons; Nd³⁺ (4f³) and Eu³⁺ (4f⁶) are paramagnetic; Lu³⁺ (4f¹⁴) is diamagnetic but paired with Eu³⁺.\nKey idea: f⁰ and f¹⁴ ions only are diamagnetic.","traps":["One correct ion in the pair: Check both ions; Eu³⁺ is 4f⁶.","Ce⁴⁺ f-count: Remove all four valence electrons."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"A and B formed in the following reactions are:\nCrO₂Cl₂ + 4NaOH → A + 2NaCl + 2H₂O,\nA + 2HCl + 2H₂O₂ → B + 3H₂O","options":["A = Na₂Cr₂O₇, B = CrO₅","A = Na₂CrO₄, B = CrO₅","A = Na₂Cr₂O₄, B = CrO₄","A = Na₂Cr₂O₇, B = CrO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) Na₂Cr₂O₇ would need two Cr on the left (atom balance fails) and dichromate does not form in excess NaOH. (C) Na₂Cr₂O₄ is a non-species; CrO₄ wrong formula. (D) CrO₃ is not formed with H₂O₂.\nWorking: CrO₂Cl₂ + 4NaOH → Na₂CrO₄ + 2NaCl + 2H₂O (balanced: 1 Cr); Na₂CrO₄ + 2HCl + 2H₂O₂ → CrO₅ + 2NaCl + 3H₂O gives blue chromium peroxide CrO(O₂)₂ (Cr +6).\nKey idea: Balance atoms: one Cr in CrO₂Cl₂ ⇒ chromate (one Cr) in alkali; H₂O₂/H⁺ gives deep-blue CrO₅ (Cr stays +6, peroxide ligands).","traps":["Dichromate chosen in alkaline medium: In basic solution Cr(VI) exists as CrO₄²⁻; count Cr atoms.","CrO₅ formula looks like Cr(+10): CrO₅ = CrO(O₂)₂: Cr +6 with two peroxo groups."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Alkaline oxidative fusion of MnO₂ gives \"A\" which on electrolytic oxidation in alkaline solution produces B. A and B respectively are","options":["Mn₂O₃ and MnO₄²⁻","Mn₂O₇ and MnO₄⁻","MnO₄²⁻ and MnO₄⁻","MnO₄²⁻ and Mn₂O₇"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Mn₂O₃ is reduction, not oxidation. (B)/(D) Mn₂O₇ is an acid anhydride formed with conc. H₂SO₄, not in alkali.\nWorking: 2MnO₂ + 4KOH + O₂ → 2K₂MnO₄ + 2H₂O (manganate, green); electrolytic oxidation of MnO₄²⁻ in alkali gives MnO₄⁻ (permanganate).\nKey idea: Oxidation of Mn: +4 (MnO₂) → +6 (MnO₄²⁻) → +7 (MnO₄⁻).","traps":["Mn₂O₇ as the +7 product: In alkaline electrolysis the +7 species is the anion MnO₄⁻."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The orange colour of K₂Cr₂O₇ and purple colour of KMnO₄ is due to","options":["Charge transfer transition in both.","d → d transitions in KMnO₄ and charge transfer transitions in K₂Cr₂O₇.","d → d transitions in both","d → d transitions in K₂Cr₂O₇ and charge transfer transitions in KMnO₄."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) one of the two treated as d–d (wrong oxidation state count). (C) applies the NCERT d–d rule blindly.\nWorking: Cr(VI) and Mn(VII) are d⁰, so no d–d transition is possible; the colour is from ligand(O)-to-metal charge transfer.\nKey idea: Count d-electrons: Cr⁶⁺ = 3d⁰, Mn⁷⁺ = 3d⁰ ⇒ colour must be charge transfer.","traps":["All TM colour from d–d: Check d-electron count first; d⁰ cannot show d–d.","Treating Mn(VII) differently from Cr(VI): Both are d⁰ oxoanions."],"topics":["Colour of ions","K₂Cr₂O₇","KMnO₄"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Identify correct statements from below:\nA. The chromate ion is square planar.\nB. Dichromates are generally prepared from chromates.\nC. The green manganate ion is diamagnetic.\nD. Dark green coloured K₂MnO₄ disproportionates in a neutral or acidic medium to give permanganate.\nE. With increasing oxidation number of transition metal, ionic character of the oxides decreases.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B, D, E only","A, B, C only","A, D, E only","B, C, D only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): accepts 'square planar' chromate. (B)/(D): accepts diamagnetic manganate (confuses with permanganate, which is diamagnetic).\nWorking: A false (CrO₄²⁻ tetrahedral); B true; C false (MnO₄²⁻ is d¹, paramagnetic); D true (3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O); E true.\nKey idea: Manganate Mn(VI) d¹ paramagnetic; permanganate Mn(VII) d⁰ diamagnetic.","traps":["Manganate vs permanganate magnetism: Count d electrons: Mn(VI) d¹.","Chromate shape: Oxoanions MO₄ⁿ⁻ are tetrahedral."],"topics":["K₂Cr₂O₇","KMnO₄"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Interstitial compounds & alloys","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The metals that are employed in the battery industries are\nA. Fe\nB. Mn\nC. Ni\nD. Cr\nE. Cd\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, B, C, D and E","A, B, C and D only","B, D and E only","B, C and E only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): Fe included because of iron-based cells (Edison Ni–Fe) or general use; (C): Cr included instead of Ni.\nWorking: NCERT 4.7 – MnO₂ for dry cells, and the battery industry requires Zn and Ni/Cd → Mn, Ni, Cd.\nKey idea: NCERT names only Mn (MnO₂), Zn and Ni/Cd for batteries.","traps":["Fe/Cr assumed battery metals: Stick to NCERT list: MnO₂, Zn, Ni/Cd."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Choose the correct statements from the following: A. Mn₂O₇ is an oil at room temperature. B. V₂O₄ reacts with acid to give VO₂²⁺. C. CrO is a basic oxide. D. V₂O₅ does not react with acid. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A, B and D only","A, B and C only","A and C only","B and C only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) includes D, missing that V₂O₅ is amphoteric. (B) accepts statement B by reading VO₂²⁺ as the NCERT VO²⁺ (vanadyl) ion. (D) omits A (Mn₂O₇ oil).\nWorking: A true (Mn₂O₇ green oil); C true (CrO basic); D false (V₂O₅ amphoteric, reacts with acids); B is false – V₂O₄ in acid gives VO²⁺ (vanadyl), not VO₂²⁺.\nKey idea: Check each oxide fact exactly: V₂O₄ → VO²⁺ (not VO₂²⁺); V₂O₅ amphoteric (VO₂⁺ in acid, VO₄³⁻ in alkali); CrO basic; Mn₂O₇ green oil.","traps":["V₂O₅ thought purely acidic: NCERT: V₂O₅ amphoteric, though mainly acidic.","Vanadyl ion formula VO²⁺ vs VO₂²⁺: Match ion formulae exactly: V(IV) → VO²⁺, V(V) → VO₂⁺."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"In the reaction of potassium dichromate, potassium chloride and sulfuric acid (conc.), the oxidation state of the chromium in the product is (+) ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (assumes Cr reduced to Cr³⁺ as in redox with KCl); 12 (sums both Cr).\nWorking: K₂Cr₂O₇ + 4KCl + 6H₂SO₄ → 2CrO₂Cl₂ + 6KHSO₄ + 3H₂O; Cr in chromyl chloride is +6.\nKey idea: Chromyl chloride is not a redox product; Cr stays +6.","traps":["Assuming dichromate reduced: Cl⁻ is not oxidised here; product CrO₂Cl₂ keeps +6."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The standard electrode potential of M⁺/M in aqueous solution does not depend on","options":["Ionisation of a gaseous metal atom","Sublimation of a solid metal","Ionisation of a solid metal atom","Hydration of a gaseous metal ion"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are all genuine steps of the cycle; a student who only remembers 'ionisation enthalpy' may reject (A) by confusing it with (C), which differs only in 'solid' vs 'gaseous'.\nWorking: E°(M⁺/M) follows from the cycle M(s) → M(g) (sublimation) → M⁺(g) (ionisation of gaseous atom) → M⁺(aq) (hydration). Atoms are never ionised directly from the solid in this cycle.\nKey idea: Ionisation enthalpy is defined only for isolated gaseous atoms; the solid must first be sublimed.","traps":["Gaseous vs solid ionisation: Ionisation enthalpy always refers to gaseous atoms.","'Does not depend' reversal: Pick the step that is NOT part of the cycle."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Strong reducing and oxidizing agents among the following, respectively, are :","options":["Ce⁴⁺ and Eu²⁺","Eu²⁺ and Ce⁴⁺","Ce⁴⁺ and Tb⁴⁺","Ce³⁺ and Ce⁴⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the order. (C) gives two oxidants. (D) Ce³⁺ is the stable state, not a strong reductant.\nWorking: Eu²⁺ (4f⁷) readily goes to Eu³⁺ – strong reductant; Ce⁴⁺ (E° Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺ = +1.74 V) – strong oxidant.\nKey idea: Lanthanoids revert to +3: Ln²⁺ reduces, Ln⁴⁺ oxidises.","traps":["Respective order reversed: Read 'reducing, oxidizing respectively' and map in order.","Ce³⁺ taken as reductant: +3 is the stable state; it is not a strong reductant."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as \"Assertion A\" and the other is labelled as \"Reason R\"\nAssertion A : In the complex Ni(CO)₄ and Fe(CO)₅, the metals have zero oxidation state.\nReason R : Low oxidation states are found when a complex has ligands capable of π-donor character in addition to the σ-bonding.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["A is not correct but R is correct","A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) for students reading R quickly as the NCERT line; (C) if R is thought true but unrelated; (A) if oxidation state in carbonyls is miscounted.\nWorking: Ni and Fe are 0 in their carbonyls; low oxidation states are stabilised by π-ACCEPTOR ligands (CO), not π-donors, so R is false.\nKey idea: CO accepts metal d-electron density into π*; that back-donation is what stabilises electron-rich (low) oxidation states.","traps":["π-donor read as π-acceptor: Low oxidation state metal is electron-rich, so the ligand must remove (accept) density."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"During the reaction of permanganate with thiosulphate, the change in oxidation of manganese occurs by value of 3. Identify which of the below medium will favour the reaction.","options":["aqueous neutral","both aqueous acidic and faintly alkaline","both aqueous acidic and neutral","aqueous acidic"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) pairs the right faintly alkaline medium with acidic – one wrong entry; (C) pairs neutral with acidic; (D) acidic gives Mn²⁺ (change 5).\nWorking: a change of 3 (Mn +7 → +4, MnO₂) occurs in neutral or faintly alkaline medium, where thiosulphate is oxidised to sulphate (8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O → 8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻). In acid Mn goes to +2 (change 5), so every option containing 'acidic' is wrong.\nKey idea: Δ = 3 ⇒ MnO₂ ⇒ neutral/faintly alkaline; the only option without 'acidic' is (A).","traps":["Faintly alkaline option chosen despite 'acidic' partner: Every part of a combined option must be true; acidic gives Mn²⁺.","Acidic medium as default for KMnO₄: Acid: +7→+2 (Δ5); neutral: +7→+4 (Δ3); strongly alkaline: +7→+6 (Δ1)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which halogen is known to cause the reaction given below :\n2Cu²⁺ + 4X⁻ → Cu₂X₂(s) + X₂","options":["All halogens","Only Iodine","Only Bromine","Only Chlorine"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student generalises to all halides. (C)/(D): student picks a stronger oxidising halogen, confusing oxidising with reducing power of X⁻.\nWorking: Cu²⁺ can oxidise only I⁻ (the strongest reducing halide) to I₂, giving Cu₂I₂; CuX₂ of F, Cl, Br are stable, CuI₂ is unknown.\nKey idea: Only I⁻ is a strong enough reductant to be oxidised by Cu²⁺.","traps":["Oxidising vs reducing power of halides confused: The halide is oxidised here – need the best reducing halide, I⁻."],"topics":["E° trends","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In chromyl chloride, the number of d-electrons present on chromium is same as in\n(Given at. no. of Ti : 22, V : 23, Cr : 24, Mn : 25, Fe : 26)","options":["Mn (VII)","Ti (III)","Fe (III)","V (IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): student takes Cr as +5 or counts a d¹ species. (C): student uses d⁵ thinking Cr has a half-filled shell.\nWorking: CrO₂Cl₂: x − 4 − 2 = 0 → Cr(+6), d⁰. Mn(VII) d⁰; Ti(III) d¹; Fe(III) d⁵; V(IV) d¹.\nKey idea: Cr(VI) and Mn(VII) are both d⁰.","traps":["Cr(VI) taken as d¹ or d³: d-count = group number − oxidation state = 6 − 6 = 0."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The pair of lanthanides in which both elements have high third-ionization energy is:","options":["Dy, Gd","Lu, Yb","Eu, Yb","Eu, Gd"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): Gd chosen for its 4f⁷ ground state – but Gd²⁺ is 4f⁷5d¹ and loses the 5d electron easily. (B): Lu chosen for 4f¹⁴ – same 5d¹ issue.\nWorking: Eu²⁺ is 4f⁷ (half-filled) and Yb²⁺ is 4f¹⁴ (filled); removing the third electron from these stable configurations costs extra energy, so both have high third ionisation enthalpies. Gd²⁺ (4f⁷5d¹) and Lu²⁺ (4f¹⁴5d¹) lose an easy 5d electron.\nKey idea: Look at the M²⁺ configuration: f⁷ or f¹⁴ exactly (no 5d electron) → high IE₃.","traps":["Neutral-atom half-filled f shell used instead of M²⁺: Write M²⁺: Gd²⁺ 4f⁷5d¹, Lu²⁺ 4f¹⁴5d¹ lose 5d easily.","Only one element of the pair checked: Both members must satisfy the condition."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Ionisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Highest oxidation state of Mn is exhibited in Mn₂O₇. The correct statements about Mn₂O₇ are\n(A) Mn is tetrahedrally surrounded by oxygen atoms.\n(B) Mn is octahedrally surrounded by oxygen atoms.\n(C) Contains Mn-O-Mn bridge.\n(D) Contains Mn-Mn bond.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A and C only","A and D only","B and C only","B and D only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B: octahedral is wrong; Mn has only 4 O neighbours. D: no Mn–Mn bond; the Mn atoms are linked through O.\nWorking: Mn₂O₇ is O₃Mn–O–MnO₃: each Mn(+7) has three terminal O and one bridging O, so Mn is tetrahedral (A true, B false); the two MnO₄ tetrahedra share a corner through an Mn–O–Mn bridge (C true) and there is no Mn–Mn bond (D false).\nKey idea: Mn₂O₇ = O₃Mn–O–MnO₃: tetrahedral Mn with one bridging oxygen.","traps":["Octahedral assumed for transition metal: Count O around Mn: 3 terminal + 1 bridging = 4."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A) : Cu²⁺ in water is more stable than Cu⁺. Reason (R) : Enthalpy of hydration for Cu²⁺ is much less than that of Cu⁺. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) taken by those reading R as the NCERT reason without checking direction. (B) if 'less' is read as 'more negative'. (C) if Cu⁺ is thought stable because of d¹⁰.\nWorking: Cu²⁺(aq) is more stable than Cu⁺(aq) because the hydration enthalpy of Cu²⁺ is much more negative than that of Cu⁺, which more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu (A true). R says the hydration enthalpy of Cu²⁺ is much less than that of Cu⁺, which is the reverse (R false).\nKey idea: ΔhydH of Cu²⁺ is far more negative (larger) than that of Cu⁺; R as written is false.","traps":["'Less' hydration enthalpy read as 'more negative': Compare magnitudes: Cu²⁺ (smaller, higher charge) is hydrated far more strongly.","d¹⁰ Cu⁺ assumed more stable: In water hydration energy dominates; Cu⁺ disproportionates."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The magnetic moment of a transition metal compound has been calculated to be 3.87 B.M. The metal ion is","options":["Ti²⁺","V²⁺","Cr²⁺","Mn²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ti²⁺ d² → 2.83 B.M. (C) Cr²⁺ d⁴ → 4.90 B.M. (D) Mn²⁺ d⁵ → 5.92 B.M.\nWorking: μ = √(n(n+2)) = 3.87 B.M. gives n = 3; V²⁺ is 3d³ (Ti²⁺ d², Cr²⁺ d⁴, Mn²⁺ d⁵).\nKey idea: 3.87 ↔ 3 unpaired ↔ d³ (V²⁺, Cr³⁺).","traps":["Recalling Cr³⁺ for 3.87 and picking Cr: Count d electrons for the given charge: Cr²⁺ is d⁴."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one amongst the following are good oxidizing agents? A. Sm²⁺ B. Ce²⁺ C. Ce⁴⁺ D. Tb⁴⁺. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["C and D only","A and B only","D only","C only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): +2 ions (Sm²⁺, Ce²⁺) are reductants. (C)/(D): miss one of the two +4 oxidants.\nWorking: lanthanoid ions tend to return to the stable +3 state. Ce⁴⁺ (→ Ce³⁺) and Tb⁴⁺ (→ Tb³⁺) gain an electron, so they are oxidising agents; Sm²⁺ and Ce²⁺ lose an electron, so they are reducing agents. Answer: C and D only.\nKey idea: +4 ions (Ce⁴⁺, Tb⁴⁺) are oxidants; +2 ions are reductants — all revert to +3.","traps":["+2 vs +4 role: Ions above +3 oxidise; below +3 reduce.","Tb⁴⁺ forgotten: NCERT: Tb has half-filled f in +4 and is an oxidant."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"How many of the following metal ions have similar value of spin only magnetic moment in gaseous state? ________ (Given : Atomic number V, 23; Cr, 24; Fe, 26; Ni, 28) V³⁺, Cr³⁺, Fe²⁺, Ni³⁺","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if Ni³⁺ is taken as d⁸ or Fe²⁺ miscounted; 0 if pairing is assumed for d⁷; 4 if 'similar' is read loosely.\nWorking: V³⁺ 3d² (2 unpaired, 2.83 BM), Cr³⁺ 3d³ (3 unpaired, 3.87 BM), Fe²⁺ 3d⁶ (4 unpaired, 4.90 BM), Ni³⁺ 3d⁷ (3 unpaired, 3.87 BM). Only Cr³⁺ and Ni³⁺ share the same value → 2.\nKey idea: Remove 4s electrons first: Cr³⁺ 3d³ and Ni³⁺ 3d⁷ both have 3 unpaired electrons (3.87 BM).","traps":["d-count of Ni³⁺: Write configuration, remove 4s first, then 3d.","Pairing assumed in d⁷: Gaseous free ions follow Hund's rule fully."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Potassium dichromate acts as a strong oxidizing agent in acidic solution. During this process, the oxidation state changes from:","options":["+2 to +1","+6 to +2","+6 to +3","+3 to +1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) +6 to +2 (Cr²⁺ is not the product); (A)/(D) do not start from +6 at all.\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O; Cr changes from +6 (in Cr₂O₇²⁻) to +3 (Cr³⁺).\nKey idea: Cr in Cr₂O₇²⁻ is +6; acid reduction gives green Cr³⁺ (+3), 3 e⁻ per Cr.","traps":["Cr²⁺ vs Cr³⁺: Recall the green Cr³⁺ product and 6 e⁻ per Cr₂O₇²⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The standard electrode potential (M³⁺/M²⁺) for V, Cr, Mn & Co are −0.26 V, −0.41 V, +1.57 V and +1.97 V, respectively. The metal ions which can liberate H₂ from a dilute acid are :","options":["Mn²⁺ and Co²⁺","V²⁺ and Mn²⁺","V²⁺ and Cr²⁺","Cr²⁺ and Co²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) picks the two strongest oxidants (largest positive E°) – sign reversed; (B)/(D) mix one negative with one positive couple.\nWorking: M²⁺ can liberate H₂ only if it is oxidised to M³⁺ while reducing H⁺, so E°(M³⁺/M²⁺) must be below 0 V (E° of H⁺/H₂). Only V (−0.26 V) and Cr (−0.41 V) qualify, so V²⁺ and Cr²⁺ liberate H₂; Mn²⁺ and Co²⁺ (large positive E°) cannot.\nKey idea: Negative E°(M³⁺/M²⁺) → M²⁺ is a reducing agent able to reduce H⁺.","traps":["Large positive E° means oxidant, not reductant: Liberating H₂ requires M²⁺ to be oxidised; needs E° below 0."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A solution of CrO₅ in amyl alcohol has a __________ colour.","options":["Yellow","Orange-Red","Green","Blue"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) yellow = chromate CrO₄²⁻; (B) orange-red = dichromate Cr₂O₇²⁻ / chromyl chloride vapour; (C) green = Cr³⁺. Each is another chromium-species colour.\nWorking: acidified Cr₂O₇²⁻ with H₂O₂ gives CrO₅ [CrO(O₂)₂], which dissolves in amyl alcohol (or ether) with a deep blue colour.\nKey idea: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O₂ in acid gives blue CrO₅ (Cr +6, with peroxo groups), stabilised in amyl alcohol/ether.","traps":["Colours of Cr species: Link: CrO₄²⁻ yellow, Cr₂O₇²⁻ orange, Cr³⁺ green, CrO₅ blue."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The set of correct statements is :\n(i) Manganese exhibits +7 oxidation state in its oxide.\n(ii) Ruthenium and Osmium exhibit +8 oxidation state in their oxides.\n(iii) Sc shows +4 oxidation state which is oxidizing in nature.\n(iv) Cr shows oxidising nature in +6 oxidation state.","options":["(ii), (iii) and (iv)","(ii) and (iii)","(i) and (iii)","(i), (ii) and (iv)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(iii) is the false statement: Sc has only 3 valence electrons, max +3. Options (A), (B), (C) all include (iii); (D) is the only one without it.\nWorking: (i) true – Mn₂O₇ (Mn +7); (ii) true – RuO₄ and OsO₄ (+8); (iii) false – Sc (3d¹4s²) shows only +3; (iv) true – Cr(VI) (e.g. Cr₂O₇²⁻) is a strong oxidant. Correct set: (i), (ii) and (iv).\nKey idea: Maximum oxidation state cannot exceed the number of ns + (n−1)d electrons; Sc max = +3.","traps":["Sc maximum oxidation state: Count valence electrons: Sc 3d¹4s² → max +3.","+8 state exists in Ru/Os: Remember OsO₄ and RuO₄ as the +8 oxides."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"KMnO₄ oxidises I⁻ in acidic and neutral/faintly alkaline solutions, respectively, to :","options":["IO₃⁻ & IO₃⁻","I₂ & I₂","I₂ & IO₃⁻","IO₃⁻ & I₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): same product in both media; (D): media reversed.\nWorking: acidic: 2MnO₄⁻ + 10I⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5I₂ + 8H₂O (product I₂); neutral/faintly alkaline: 2MnO₄⁻ + H₂O + I⁻ → 2MnO₂ + 2OH⁻ + IO₃⁻ (product IO₃⁻).\nKey idea: Acidic MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ with I⁻ → I₂; neutral/alkaline MnO₄⁻ → MnO₂ with I⁻ → IO₃⁻.","traps":["Acidic vs neutral medium products swapped: Neutral/alkaline: iodate (with MnO₂); acidic: iodine."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"When Cu²⁺ ion is treated with KI, a white precipitate, X appears in solution. The solution is titrated with sodium thiosulphate, the compound Y is formed. X and Y respectively are :","options":["X = CuI₂, Y = Na₂S₄O₆","X = Cu₂I₂, Y = Na₂S₄O₆","X = CuI₂, Y = Na₂S₂O₃","X = Cu₂I₂, Y = Na₂S₄O₅"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CuI₂ does not exist: Cu²⁺ oxidises I⁻; Y right. (C) CuI₂ wrong and Na₂S₂O₃ is the titrant itself, not the product. (D) X right but Na₂S₄O₅ is a made-up formula one O short of tetrathionate.\nWorking: 2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → Cu₂I₂(s) (white, X) + I₂; the liberated I₂ is titrated with thiosulphate: I₂ + 2Na₂S₂O₃ → 2NaI + Na₂S₄O₆ (Y, sodium tetrathionate).\nKey idea: CuI₂ is never formed; the white solid is Cu₂I₂ (CuI) and thiosulphate is oxidised to tetrathionate S₄O₆²⁻.","traps":["CuI₂ assumed by simple ion pairing: Remember NCERT: copper(II) iodide is unknown because Cu²⁺ oxidises I⁻.","Tetrathionate formula mis-recalled: Write I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻ and count O."],"topics":["Oxidation states","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Nd²⁺ = ________","options":["4f⁴6s²","4f³","4f⁴","4f²6s²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the neutral Nd atom. (B) 4f³ is Nd³⁺. (D) 4f²6s² removes 4f electrons before 6s (and also is Nd⁴⁺-like core plus 6s²).\nWorking: Nd (Z = 60) = [Xe]4f⁴6s²; removing the two 6s electrons gives Nd²⁺ = [Xe]4f⁴.\nKey idea: Lanthanoid ions lose 6s electrons first: Nd [Xe]4f⁴6s² → Nd²⁺ [Xe]4f⁴.","traps":["Removing f before s electrons: Ionise from the outermost shell (6s) first.","Mixing up Nd²⁺ and Nd³⁺: Count: Nd has 4f⁴6s²; 2+ removes only the two 6s electrons."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct order of basicity of oxides of vanadium is :","options":["V₂O₃ > V₂O₅ > V₂O₄","V₂O₃ > V₂O₄ > V₂O₅","V₂O₅ > V₂O₄ > V₂O₃","V₂O₄ > V₂O₃ > V₂O₅"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps V₂O₄ and V₂O₅. (C) reverses the trend (acidity order). (D) puts +4 above +3.\nWorking: V₂O₃ (V +3) is basic, V₂O₄ (V +4) is less basic and V₂O₅ (V +5) is amphoteric (mainly acidic); basicity falls as the oxidation state rises, so V₂O₃ > V₂O₄ > V₂O₅.\nKey idea: Higher oxidation state → more covalent/acidic oxide; V₂O₃ (+3) most basic, V₂O₅ (+5) amphoteric.","traps":["Reversed order (acidity vs basicity): Read “basicity”; lowest oxidation state is most basic."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following elements have half-filled f-orbitals in their ground state? (Given: atomic number Sm = 62; Eu = 63; Tb = 65; Gd = 64, Pm = 61) A. Sm B. Eu C. Tb D. Gd E. Pm. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B and D only","A and B only","C and D only","A and E only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Sm is 4f⁶. (C) Tb is 4f⁹ (one more than Gd's 4f⁷5d¹ counted as 4f⁸ by some). (D) Pm 4f⁵ and Sm 4f⁶.\nWorking: Eu [Xe]4f⁷6s² and Gd [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s² have half-filled 4f; Sm 4f⁶, Pm 4f⁵ and Tb 4f⁹ do not. Answer: B and D only.\nKey idea: Gd keeps 4f⁷ by placing the extra electron in 5d; Eu is 4f⁷ 6s².","traps":["Gd written as 4f⁸: Recall Gd = [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s².","Counting f electrons from Z: Z − 56 = f electrons for La-type filling, then check exceptions."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): The first ionization enthalpy of 3d series elements is more than that of group 2 metals. Reason (R): In 3d series of elements successive filling of d-orbitals takes place. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) rejects R though d-filling is true. (C) accepts both but denies the link; NCERT links IE rise to nuclear charge as d fills. (D) rejects A by thinking of Be/Mg values.\nWorking: increasing nuclear charge as 3d fills raises IE₁. Caveat: Be and Mg have higher IE₁ than several 3d metals, so A is true only in that sense.\nKey idea: Across period 4, Z rises from Ca to the 3d metals while added 3d electrons shield poorly, so IE₁ rises.","traps":["Which group 2 metals?: Read as same-period comparison with Ca, as intended.","Explanation link: NCERT ties IE rise to nuclear charge increase during d-filling."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The reaction of H₂O₂ with potassium permanganate in acidic medium leads to the formation of mainly:","options":["Mn²⁺","Mn⁴⁺","Mn³⁺","Mn⁶⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Mn⁴⁺ (MnO₂) is the neutral/alkaline product; (D) Mn⁶⁺ (manganate) the strongly alkaline product; (C) Mn³⁺ is unstable.\nWorking: in acid MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺; H₂O₂ acts as reductant (→ O₂).\nKey idea: Acid → Mn²⁺; neutral/weak alkali → MnO₂.","traps":["Medium decides product: Acidic: Mn²⁺; neutral/faintly alkaline: MnO₂; strongly alkaline: MnO₄²⁻."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Among Co³⁺, Ti²⁺, V²⁺ and Cr²⁺ ions, one if used as a reagent cannot liberate H₂ from dilute mineral acid solution, its spin-only magnetic moment in gaseous state is ____ B.M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (treating Co³⁺ as low spin as in complexes); 3 or 4 from the Ti²⁺ or V²⁺ moments (2.83, 3.87). Cr²⁺ (d⁴, 4.90) would also round to 5, so the number alone does not prove the ion choice.\nWorking: Ti²⁺, V²⁺, Cr²⁺ are strong reductants that liberate H₂ from dilute acid; Co³⁺ is a strong oxidant. Gaseous Co³⁺ is d⁶ with 4 unpaired electrons → √24 = 4.90 ≈ 5 B.M.\nKey idea: \"Gaseous state\" means free ion → Hund's rule, high spin.","traps":["Free ion vs complex: Gaseous ion: no ligand field, so 4 unpaired d electrons."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The spin-only magnetic moment value of M³⁺ ion (in gaseous state) from the pairs Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺, Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺, Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ and Co³⁺/Co²⁺ that has negative standard electrode potential, is ____ B.M. [Nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (Cr²⁺ d⁴, μ 4.90 – using M²⁺ instead of M³⁺); 6 (Fe³⁺ d⁵, μ 5.92); 3 (number of unpaired electrons instead of μ).\nWorking: only E°(Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺) = −0.41 V is negative (Mn +1.57, Fe +0.77, Co +1.97); Cr³⁺ is 3d³, n = 3, μ = √15 = 3.87 ≈ 4 BM.\nKey idea: Cr²⁺ is a strong reductant, so Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺ has negative E°.","traps":["M²⁺ moment used: Question asks for M³⁺.","Unpaired electrons reported: Compute √(n(n+2)) = 3.87 → 4."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Processes/Reactions): (A) 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g) (B) 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g) (C) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) (D) Vegetable oil(l) + H₂ → Vegetable ghee(s)\nList-II (Catalyst): (I) Fe(s) (II) Pt(s)-Rh(s) (III) V₂O₅ (IV) Ni(s)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Fe and Pt–Rh between Ostwald and Haber; (C) and (D) put Ni on the Contact process and V₂O₅/Fe elsewhere – shifted pairs.\nWorking: contact process SO₂→SO₃ uses V₂O₅ (III); Ostwald oxidation of NH₃ uses Pt–Rh gauze (II); Haber process uses iron (I); hydrogenation of oils uses Ni (IV).\nKey idea: Contact–V₂O₅, Ostwald–Pt/Rh, Haber–Fe, hydrogenation–Ni.","traps":["Haber vs Ostwald catalyst swapped: Haber makes NH₃ on Fe; Ostwald burns NH₃ on Pt–Rh."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The products obtained from a reaction of hydrogen peroxide and acidified potassium permanganate are","options":["Mn⁴⁺, H₂O only","Mn²⁺, H₂O only","Mn⁴⁺, H₂O, O₂ only","Mn²⁺, H₂O, O₂ only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) use Mn⁴⁺ (MnO₂ forms in neutral/weakly basic medium, not acid); (B) forgets O₂ from oxidation of H₂O₂.\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5H₂O₂ + 6H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5O₂ + 8H₂O; H₂O₂ acts as reductant and is oxidised to O₂.\nKey idea: Acidic MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺; reductant H₂O₂ → O₂.","traps":["Medium decides Mn product: Acid → Mn²⁺; neutral/alkaline → MnO₂.","H₂O₂ as reducing agent: Against a stronger oxidant H₂O₂ is oxidised to O₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The spin-only magnetic moment value of the compound with strongest oxidizing ability among MnF₄, MnF₃ and MnF₂ is ______ B.M. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Choosing MnF₄ (highest oxidation state) gives 3.87 ≈ 4; choosing MnF₂ gives 5.92 ≈ 6.\nWorking: by E° values Mn³⁺ is the strongest oxidising agent (Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ ≈ +1.5 V, above MnO₂/Mn²⁺ +1.23 V; Mn³⁺ d⁴ → Mn²⁺ d⁵ half-filled), so MnF₃: d⁴, 4 unpaired, √24 = 4.90 ≈ 5 BM. Caveat: solid MnF₄ is an unstable fluorine donor and could be argued to be stronger (it would give √15 ≈ 3.87 ≈ 4), but the expected answer follows the NCERT E° reasoning.\nKey idea: Mn³⁺ → Mn²⁺ gains the stable d⁵ configuration, so MnF₃ is the strongest oxidant.","traps":["Highest oxidation state ≠ strongest oxidant: Compare E°/stability of the reduced d⁵ ion.","Rounding √24: Round to nearest integer: 5."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Iron (III) catalyst, acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ and neutral KMnO₄ have the ability to oxidise I⁻ to I₂ independently.\nStatement II : Manganate ion is paramagnetic in nature and involves pπ–pπ bonding.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options.","options":["Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): accepts both familiar-sounding lines; (C): misses the neutral-medium product; (D): misses the pπ–dπ detail but spots IO₃⁻.\nWorking: Fe³⁺ and acidified Cr₂O₇²⁻ oxidise I⁻ to I₂, but neutral/faintly alkaline MnO₄⁻ oxidises I⁻ to IO₃⁻, so I is false; MnO₄²⁻ (d¹) is paramagnetic but its π bonding is pπ(O)–dπ(Mn), not pπ–pπ, so II is false.\nKey idea: Neutral KMnO₄ takes I⁻ all the way to IO₃⁻; Mn–O π bonds use Mn d orbitals.","traps":["Neutral KMnO₄ gives I₂: Recall NCERT: neutral/faintly alkaline → IO₃⁻.","pπ–pπ vs pπ–dπ: Metal–oxygen π bonds in oxoanions use metal d orbitals."],"topics":["KMnO₄","K₂Cr₂O₇"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The total number of Mn = O bonds in Mn₂O₇ is ____","options":["4","5","6","3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): forgets one Mn or counts 2 per Mn; (B): counts the bridge as one double bond; (D): counts per Mn only.\nWorking: Mn₂O₇ is O₃Mn–O–MnO₃ (two tetrahedra sharing one O): 3 terminal Mn=O per Mn → 6.\nKey idea: Seven O, one bridging → six terminal Mn=O.","traps":["Counting only one MnO₃ unit: Draw both tetrahedra and the shared O.","Bridging O counted as double bond: Bridge = two single bonds."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In neutral or alkaline solution, MnO₄⁻ oxidises thiosulphate to:","options":["S₂O₇²⁻","S₂O₈²⁻","SO₃²⁻","SO₄²⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) are other S–S or peroxo oxoanions; (C) sulphite is an incomplete oxidation product; tetrathionate (with I₂) is a related trap.\nWorking: in neutral/faintly alkaline medium 8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O → 8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻; thiosulphate is oxidised almost quantitatively to sulphate.\nKey idea: Strong oxidant MnO₄⁻ takes S₂O₃²⁻ all the way to SO₄²⁻ (with I₂ it stops at S₄O₆²⁻).","traps":["Partial oxidation product: Only I₂ gives tetrathionate; MnO₄⁻ gives sulphate."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The oxidation state of manganese in the product obtained in a reaction of potassium permanganate and hydrogen peroxide in basic medium is ______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (acidic-medium product Mn²⁺); 6 (manganate MnO₄²⁻ in strongly alkaline medium).\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 3H₂O₂ → 2MnO₂ + 3O₂ + 2OH⁻ + 2H₂O in basic/neutral medium; Mn in MnO₂ is +4.\nKey idea: Basic/neutral: MnO₄⁻ → MnO₂ (+4).","traps":["Acidic vs basic product: Check the medium: basic gives MnO₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List-I (Reaction) with List-II (Catalyst). List-I: (A) 4NH₃(g) + 5O₂(g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g); (B) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g); (C) C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁(aq) + H₂O(l) → C₆H₁₂O₆ (Glucose) + C₆H₁₂O₆ (Fructose); (D) 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g). List-II: (I) NO(g); (II) H₂SO₄(l); (III) Pt(s); (IV) Fe(s). Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) – (II), (B) – (III), (C) – (I), (D) – (IV)","(A) – (III), (B) – (II), (C) – (I), (D) – (IV)","(A) – (III), (B) – (IV), (C) – (II), (D) – (I)","(A) – (III), (B) – (II), (C) – (IV), (D) – (I)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) put H₂SO₄ with Haber; (A) puts H₂SO₄ with Ostwald; options with D–IV take Fe for SO₂ oxidation.\nWorking: Ostwald process Pt (A–III); Haber process Fe (B–IV); acid-catalysed inversion of cane sugar H₂SO₄ (C–II); lead-chamber oxidation of SO₂ with NO (D–I).\nKey idea: Only NO is left for SO₂ oxidation once Pt, Fe and acid are placed.","traps":["Expecting V₂O₅ for SO₂ oxidation: Eliminate: Pt (Ostwald) and Fe (Haber) are certain; NO is the only gas catalyst."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following has least tendency to liberate H₂ from mineral acids?","options":["Cu","Mn","Ni","Zn"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Ni has the least negative E° among the others and may tempt students who forget Cu is positive.\nWorking: E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V is positive, so Cu cannot reduce H⁺; Mn (−1.18), Zn (−0.76) and Ni (−0.25) all can.\nKey idea: Only metals with negative E° displace H₂ from non-oxidising acids.","traps":["Ni close to hydrogen: Check sign: Cu is the only positive one."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The disproportionation of MnO₄²⁻ in acidic medium resulted in the formation of two manganese compounds A and B. If the oxidation state of Mn in B is smaller than that of A, then the spin-only magnetic moment (μ) value of B in BM is ___________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 if MnO₄⁻ (d⁰) is chosen; 6 (√35 ≈ 5.9) if Mn²⁺ is assumed; 5 (√24) for Mn³⁺.\nWorking: 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ (A, Mn +7) + MnO₂ (B, Mn +4) + 2H₂O; Mn⁴⁺ is d³, n = 3, μ = √15 = 3.87 → 4 BM.\nKey idea: B = MnO₂, d³, three unpaired electrons.","traps":["Mn(IV) d-count: Mn [Ar]3d⁵4s² → Mn⁴⁺ 3d³."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Permanganate titrations are not performed in presence of hydrochloric acid. Reason R: Chlorine is formed as a consequence of oxidation of hydrochloric acid. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) denies the causal link that NCERT itself states; (C)/(D) reject a true statement.\nWorking: MnO₄⁻ oxidises Cl⁻ of HCl to Cl₂, consuming extra permanganate and spoiling the titre; so H₂SO₄ is used. R explains A.\nKey idea: Cl⁻ is oxidised by MnO₄⁻ (E° Cl₂/Cl⁻ 1.36 V < MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ 1.51 V).","traps":["Reason judged as unrelated: Extra KMnO₄ is used for Cl⁻, so titre is wrong."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In following pairs, the one in which both transition metal ions are colourless is :","options":["Sc³⁺, Zn²⁺","Ti⁴⁺, Cu²⁺","V²⁺, Ti³⁺","Zn²⁺, Mn²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Ti⁴⁺ colourless but Cu²⁺ blue; (C) both coloured; (D) Mn²⁺ pale pink (d⁵).\nWorking: Sc³⁺ (3d⁰) and Zn²⁺ (3d¹⁰) have no d–d transitions → colourless. Cu²⁺ blue, V²⁺ violet, Ti³⁺ purple, Mn²⁺ pale pink.\nKey idea: d⁰ and d¹⁰ → no d–d transition.","traps":["Mn²⁺ thought colourless: d⁵ ions are coloured (faint pink)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"In neutral or faintly alkaline medium, KMnO₄ being a powerful oxidant can oxidize, thiosulphate almost quantitatively, to sulphate. In this reaction overall change in oxidation state of manganese will be :","options":["5","1","0","3"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5 is acid medium (Mn²⁺); (B) 1 is strongly alkaline (MnO₄²⁻); (C) no change.\nWorking: in neutral/faintly alkaline medium MnO₄⁻ → MnO₂ (+7 → +4), change 3 (8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O → 8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻).\nKey idea: Neutral/faintly alkaline → MnO₂, Δ = 3.","traps":["Acid-medium value used: Match medium: acid 5, neutral 3, strongly basic 1."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following 3d-metal ion will give the lowest enthalpy of hydration (ΔhydH) when dissolved in water?","options":["Cr²⁺","Mn²⁺","Fe²⁺","Co²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cr²⁺ – picked on size reasoning alone; (C)/(D) – picked by following the size trend (smaller ion = more negative ΔhydH) while ignoring CFSE.\nWorking: Mn²⁺ is high-spin d⁵ with zero crystal-field stabilisation in [M(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and is the largest of the four, so its hydration enthalpy is least negative (double-hump curve minimum at d⁵).\nKey idea: Zero CFSE for high-spin d⁵ Mn²⁺ → least negative ΔhydH.","traps":["Size-only reasoning: Remember CFSE adds to hydration for d¹–d⁴, d⁶–d⁹.","\"Lowest\" read as most negative: Lowest magnitude = least negative."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The difference in oxidation state of chromium in chromate and dichromate salts is ____.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 or 6 (assuming a colour change means redox); 1 (computing Cr₂O₇²⁻ as +7).\nWorking: CrO₄²⁻: x − 8 = −2 → +6; Cr₂O₇²⁻: 2x − 14 = −2 → +6. Difference 0 (the interconversion is an acid–base, not redox, equilibrium).\nKey idea: Yellow → orange is a condensation (acid–base) change, not redox.","traps":["Colour change taken as redox: Compute the oxidation numbers: both are +6."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Metals generally melt at very high temperature. Amongst the following, the metal with the highest melting point will be","options":["Hg","Ag","Ga","Cs"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Hg – heaviest atom, but liquid at room temperature; (C) Ga – melts in the hand (≈30 °C); (D) Cs – large atom, weak metallic bond (≈28 °C).\nWorking: m.p. Hg −39 °C, Cs 28 °C, Ga 30 °C, Ag 961 °C; Ag (4d, extra d-electron metallic bonding) is highest.\nKey idea: Only Ag has strong d-electron metallic bonding; the other three are famous low-melting metals.","traps":["Heavier atom assumed higher m.p: Hg is liquid (d¹⁰s², weak metallic bonding).","Ga low m.p. not known: Ga melts at about 30 °C."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Manganese (VI) has ability to disproportionate in acidic solution. The difference in oxidation states of two ions it forms in acidic solution is ____.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 – taking Mn²⁺ as the reduced product (7 − 2); 1 – difference from Mn(VI) only; \"ions\" wording may mislead since MnO₂ is not an ion.\nWorking: 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O; Mn(VI) → Mn(VII) and Mn(IV); difference 7 − 4 = 3.\nKey idea: Mn(VI) → Mn(VII) (MnO₄⁻) + Mn(IV) (MnO₂).","traps":["Mn²⁺ assumed as the lower product: Disproportionation of MnO₄²⁻ gives MnO₂ (+4).","\"Two ions\" wording: Follow the chemistry: species are MnO₄⁻ and MnO₂."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Cerium (IV) has a noble gas configuration. Which of the following is correct statement about it?","options":["It will not prefer to undergo redox reactions.","It will prefer to gain electron and act as an oxidizing agent","It will prefer to give away an electron and behave as reducing agent","It acts as both, oxidizing and reducing agent."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – noble gas configuration taken as total stability; (C) – confuses with Eu²⁺/Yb²⁺ (reducing); (D) – Ce⁴⁺ has no electrons to lose.\nWorking: Ce⁴⁺ ([Xe]) reverts to the common +3 state; E°(Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺) = +1.74 V, so it is a strong oxidant. It cannot lose further electrons.\nKey idea: For lanthanoids +3 wins: Ce⁴⁺ gains an electron (oxidant), Eu²⁺ loses one (reductant).","traps":["Noble gas core taken as redox-inert: Stability of +3 state dominates for lanthanoids.","Ce⁴⁺ vs Eu²⁺ roles swapped: Ce⁴⁺ oxidant, Eu²⁺ reductant."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Among the following, which is the strongest oxidizing agent?","options":["Mn³⁺","Fe³⁺","Ti³⁺","Cr³⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Fe³⁺ – familiar oxidant, but E° lower; (D) Cr³⁺ – very stable t₂g³; (C) Ti³⁺ is a reductant.\nWorking: E°(M³⁺/M²⁺): Mn +1.57 V, Fe +0.77 V, Cr −0.41 V, Ti −0.37 V. Mn³⁺ (d⁴) goes readily to stable d⁵ Mn²⁺, so it is the strongest oxidant.\nKey idea: M³⁺ → M²⁺ is easiest where the product is d⁵: Mn³⁺ → Mn²⁺.","traps":["Fe³⁺ chosen as familiar oxidant: Compare E°: Mn³⁺/Mn²⁺ +1.57 V > Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ +0.77 V."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The metal ion (in gaseous state) with lowest spin-only magnetic moment value is","options":["V²⁺","Ni²⁺","Cr²⁺","Fe²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) V²⁺ chosen by those who think fewer d electrons means fewer unpaired; (C)/(D) both 4 unpaired.\nWorking: V²⁺ 3d³ (3 unpaired, 3.87 BM); Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ (2 unpaired, 2.83 BM); Cr²⁺ 3d⁴ (4, 4.90 BM); Fe²⁺ 3d⁶ (4, 4.90 BM). Lowest: Ni²⁺.\nKey idea: Unpaired electrons peak at d⁵; d⁸ has only 2 unpaired.","traps":["Fewest d electrons ≠ fewest unpaired: Count unpaired: d³ → 3, d⁸ → 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The spin–only magnetic moment value of the most basic oxide of vanadium among V₂O₃, V₂O₄ and V₂O₅ is ______ B.M. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 – choosing V₂O₅ (d⁰) as most basic; 2 – V₂O₄ (V⁴⁺, d¹, 1.73 ≈ 2); 3 is correct.\nWorking: the lowest oxidation state oxide V₂O₃ (V³⁺) is most basic; V³⁺ is 3d², n = 2, μ = √(2 × 4) = 2.83 BM ≈ 3.\nKey idea: Lower oxidation state → more ionic, more basic oxide.","traps":["Highest oxide taken as most basic: Basicity falls as oxidation state rises.","d-count of V³⁺: V³⁺ = 3d²."],"topics":["Magnetic moment","Oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The most common oxidation state of Lanthanoid elements is +3. Which of the following is likely to deviate easily from +3 oxidation state?","options":["Ce (At. No. 58)","La (At. No. 57)","Lu (At. No. 71)","Gd (At. No. 64)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) La³⁺ 4f⁰; (C) Lu³⁺ 4f¹⁴; (D) Gd³⁺ 4f⁷ – all are the most stable +3 ions, so they do not deviate.\nWorking: Ce⁴⁺ (4f⁰, noble-gas configuration) is readily formed. La³⁺ (4f⁰), Gd³⁺ (4f⁷) and Lu³⁺ (4f¹⁴) are especially stable +3 ions.\nKey idea: Ce → Ce⁴⁺ gives 4f⁰; the empty/half/full f rule decides deviation.","traps":["Gd chosen for half-filled f: Gd³⁺ is itself 4f⁷, so +3 is the stable state; Ce⁴⁺ reaches 4f⁰."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of statement(s) correct from the following for copper (at. no. 29) is/are ______. (A) Cu(II) complexes are always paramagnetic. (B) Cu(I) complexes are generally colourless. (C) Cu(I) is easily oxidized. (D) In Fehling solution, the active reagent has Cu(I).","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 – accepts Cu(I) in Fehling (it is Cu(II), reduced to red Cu₂O); 2 – rejects (A) because of \"always\" (some dimeric Cu(II) compounds are antiferromagnetic, but for this question A is taken as true).\nWorking: (A) Cu²⁺ is d⁹ with one unpaired electron → paramagnetic (true as intended). (B) Cu⁺ is d¹⁰ → no d–d transition → generally colourless (true). (C) Cu⁺ is readily oxidised/disproportionates in water to Cu²⁺ (true). (D) Fehling solution contains Cu(II) tartrate complex, which is reduced to Cu₂O (false). Count = 3.\nKey idea: Fehling's blue colour is Cu²⁺; the red precipitate Cu₂O is the Cu(I) product, not the reagent.","traps":["Fehling reagent vs product: Reagent = Cu²⁺ tartrate complex; product = Cu₂O.","\"Always\" in statement A: For d⁹ with one unpaired electron it holds for ordinary complexes."],"topics":["Oxidation states","Magnetic moment","Colour of ions"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Acidified potassium permanganate solution oxidises oxalic acid. The spin-only magnetic moment of the manganese product formed from the above reaction is ______ B.M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 – takes MnO₂ (d³, 3.87) as product (neutral medium); 0 – MnO₄⁻ itself (d⁰); 5 – reports unpaired electrons instead of μ.\nWorking: 2MnO₄⁻ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 10CO₂ + 8H₂O. Mn²⁺ is d⁵ with 5 unpaired electrons: μ = √(5×7) = √35 = 5.92 ≈ 6 B.M.\nKey idea: Acidic permanganate → Mn²⁺ (d⁵, maximum unpaired) → ~5.9 B.M.","traps":["Medium-dependent product: Acid medium → Mn²⁺.","n reported instead of μ: Apply √n(n+2) and round."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In 3d series, the metal having the highest M²⁺/M standard electrode potential is","options":["Cr","Fe","Cu","Zn"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Zn tempts through its stable d¹⁰ ion; (A)/(B) are negative values.\nWorking: E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V is the only positive value in the 3d series; Cr −0.91, Fe −0.44, Zn −0.76 V.\nKey idea: Cu has positive E° because its high atomisation and ionisation enthalpies are not compensated by hydration enthalpy.","traps":["Stable Zn²⁺ assumed to mean high E°: Zn²⁺ stability gives a negative, not positive, E°."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The 'f' orbitals are half and completely filled, respectively in lanthanide ions (Given: Atomic no. Eu, 63; Sm, 62; Tm, 69; Tb, 65; Yb, 70; Dy, 66)","options":["Eu²⁺ and Tm²⁺","Sm²⁺ and Tm³⁺","Tb⁴⁺ and Yb²⁺","Dy³⁺ and Yb³⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) pairs the correct Eu²⁺ (4f⁷) with Tm²⁺ (4f¹³); (B) and (D) are one electron short of the targets.\nWorking: Tb⁴⁺ = [Xe]4f⁷ (half-filled), Yb²⁺ = [Xe]4f¹⁴ (completely filled). Eu²⁺ is 4f⁷ but Tm²⁺ is 4f¹³; Sm²⁺ 4f⁶, Tm³⁺ 4f¹²; Dy³⁺ 4f⁹, Yb³⁺ 4f¹³.\nKey idea: Ln³⁺ has (Z − 57) 4f electrons; add one electron for 2+, remove one for 4+.","traps":["Half-correct option (Eu²⁺): Check both ions: Tm²⁺ is 4f¹³, not 4f¹⁴.","Off-by-one f count: Ln³⁺: f count = Z − 57; adjust for charge."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the lanthanoids given below is the most stable in divalent form?","options":["Ce (Atomic Number 58)","Sm (Atomic Number 62)","Eu (Atomic Number 63)","Yb (Atomic Number 70)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Yb²⁺ (f¹⁴) also stable but a stronger reductant than Eu²⁺; (B) Sm²⁺ is a strong reductant; (A) Ce is known for +4.\nWorking: Eu²⁺ is 4f⁷ (half-filled) and is the most stable Ln²⁺ (E°(Eu³⁺/Eu²⁺) ≈ −0.35 V vs about −1.1 V for Yb and −1.55 V for Sm); Ce prefers +3/+4.\nKey idea: Half-filled 4f⁷ makes Eu²⁺ the most stable divalent lanthanoid ion.","traps":["Eu²⁺ vs Yb²⁺: NCERT names Eu²⁺ first; Yb²⁺ is the stronger reductant.","Ce noble-gas configuration: Ce goes to +4, not +2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The electronic configuration of Pt (atomic number 78) is:","options":["[Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d⁹ 6s¹","[Kr] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰","[Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰","[Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d⁸ 6s²"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is Ni-type 3d⁸4s² pattern; (C) is Pd-type d¹⁰s⁰ pattern; (B) has wrong core [Kr].\nWorking: Pt ground state is [Xe]4f¹⁴5d⁹6s¹ (Pd is 4d¹⁰5s⁰; Ni is 3d⁸4s²).\nKey idea: Ni d⁸s², Pd d¹⁰s⁰, Pt d⁹s¹ – each Group 10 member differs.","traps":["Group-10 pattern carried over: Memorise the three Group 10 anomalies separately."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of terminal oxygen atoms present in the product B obtained from the following reaction is ________. FeCr₂O₄ + Na₂CO₃ + O₂ → A + Fe₂O₃ + CO₂; A + H⁺ → B + H₂O + Na⁺","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 (all O counted); 8 (chromate ×2); 4 (one CrO₄ unit).\nWorking: A = Na₂CrO₄; acidification gives Na₂Cr₂O₇ (B); Cr₂O₇²⁻ has two CrO₄ tetrahedra sharing one bridging O → 6 terminal O.\nKey idea: Cr₂O₇²⁻: O₃Cr–O–CrO₃ – one bridge, six terminal.","traps":["Counting all O: Subtract the single bridging O.","B taken as chromate: B forms after acidification → dichromate."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"An acidified manganate solution undergoes disproportionation reaction. The spin-only magnetic moment value of the product having manganese in higher oxidation state is ________ B.M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.73 (MnO₄²⁻, d¹, if reactant used); 3.87 (MnO₂, Mn⁴⁺ d³, if lower state chosen).\nWorking: 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O; higher state is Mn(VII) in MnO₄⁻, d⁰ → 0 BM.\nKey idea: Mn(VII) is d⁰ → diamagnetic.","traps":["Lower-state product chosen: Higher oxidation state = +7.","Reactant used: Question asks for a product."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which one of the following when dissolved in water gives coloured solution in nitrogen atmosphere?","options":["CuCl₂","AgCl","ZnCl₂","Cu₂Cl₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) d¹⁰ and insoluble; (C) d¹⁰ colourless; (D) d¹⁰ Cu(I) – tempting if one assumes all copper salts are blue or that Cu⁺ disproportionates to Cu²⁺.\nWorking: CuCl₂ gives blue hydrated Cu²⁺ (d⁹). AgCl is insoluble and d¹⁰; ZnCl₂ is d¹⁰ colourless; Cu₂Cl₂ is d¹⁰ and sparingly soluble. N₂ is there so that Cu(I) is not oxidised by air.\nKey idea: d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions are colourless; only Cu²⁺ (d⁹) here can show d–d transitions.","traps":["Cu⁺ vs Cu²⁺ colour: Count d electrons: Cu⁺ d¹⁰ colourless, Cu²⁺ d⁹ blue.","Purpose of N₂ atmosphere: Treat N₂ as 'no oxidation by air' – the original oxidation state stays."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"These are physical properties of an element: (A) Sublimation enthalpy (B) Ionisation enthalpy (C) Hydration enthalpy (D) Electron gain enthalpy. The total number of above properties that affect the reduction potential is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 if electron gain enthalpy is included; 2 if hydration is left out (thinking only of the gaseous metal).\nWorking: E° is governed by enthalpy of sublimation/atomisation, ionisation enthalpy and hydration enthalpy → 3. Electron gain enthalpy would matter only for a non-metal couple (X₂/X⁻), not for a metal electrode, so (following NCERT) the answer is 3.\nKey idea: Mⁿ⁺(aq)/M(s) cycle = atomisation + ionisation + hydration.","traps":["Electron gain enthalpy is for X₂/X⁻: For a metal couple Mⁿ⁺/M, the cycle is sublimation + ionisation + hydration.","Hydration enthalpy counts: E° depends on the full cycle M(s) → M(g) → Mⁿ⁺(g) → Mⁿ⁺(aq)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of 4f electrons in the ground state electronic configuration of Gd²⁺ is ______. [Atomic number of Gd = 64]","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (writing Gd²⁺ as 4f⁸ by analogy with neighbours); 6 (removing 4f electrons).\nWorking: Gd [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s²; Gd²⁺ = [Xe]4f⁷5d¹ → 7 4f electrons (half-filled 4f is retained).\nKey idea: Gd holds the half-filled 4f⁷; its extra electron sits in 5d.","traps":["Gd keeps 4f⁷ 5d¹: Gd = [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s²; remove 6s² → 4f⁷5d¹.","Remove 6s first: Outermost 6s electrons leave first."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The nature of oxides V₂O₃ and CrO is indexed as 'X' and 'Y' type respectively. The correct set of X and Y is:","options":["X = basic, Y = amphoteric","X = amphoteric, Y = basic","X = acidic, Y = acidic","X = basic, Y = basic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – amphoteric belongs to V₂O₅ and Cr₂O₃, one step higher in oxidation state; (C) acidic belongs to CrO₃/Mn₂O₇.\nWorking: low oxidation state oxides are basic – V₂O₃ (+3) is basic (V₂O₄ less basic, V₂O₅ amphoteric) and CrO (+2) is basic (Cr₂O₃ amphoteric, CrO₃ acidic).\nKey idea: Lower oxidation state → more ionic → basic oxide.","traps":["Cr₂O₃ amphoteric confused with CrO: CrO (+2) basic; Cr₂O₃ (+3) amphoteric.","V₂O₅ amphoteric carried to V₂O₃: Only the highest oxide V₂O₅ is amphoteric."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Actinoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of f electrons in the ground state electronic configuration of Np (Z = 93) is ______. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (Aufbau [Rn]5f⁵7s², ignoring the 6d¹ electron); 3 (U-like 5f³).\nWorking: Np = [Rn] 5f⁴ 6d¹ 7s², so 4 f electrons.\nKey idea: Early actinoids (Pa, U, Np) keep one 6d electron: Np 5f⁴6d¹7s².","traps":["Aufbau filling assumed: Early actinoids have 6d¹; check the table."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Potassium permanganate on heating at 513 K gives a product which is:","options":["paramagnetic and colourless","diamagnetic and green","diamagnetic and colourless","paramagnetic and green"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) green but diamagnetic – confuses manganate with permanganate's diamagnetism; (A)/(C) colourless – thinks of O₂ as \"the product\".\nWorking: 2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂ at 513 K; the characteristic product K₂MnO₄ (Mn(VI), 3d¹) is green and paramagnetic (one unpaired electron).\nKey idea: MnO₄²⁻ = Mn(VI), d¹ → green, paramagnetic; MnO₄⁻ = Mn(VII), d⁰ → purple, diamagnetic.","traps":["Manganate vs permanganate magnetism: Count d electrons: Mn(VI) d¹ paramagnetic; Mn(VII) d⁰ diamagnetic.","O₂ taken as \"the product\": The question means the manganese product K₂MnO₄."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment","Colour of ions"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In the structure of the dichromate ion, there is a","options":["linear symmetrical Cr–O–Cr bond.","non-linear symmetrical Cr–O–Cr bond.","linear unsymmetrical Cr–O–Cr bond.","non-linear unsymmetrical Cr–O–Cr bond."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) linear – confuses with a sp-type bridge (e.g. some oxo-bridged complexes); (C), (D) unsymmetrical – imagines one short and one long Cr–O bridge.\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ is two CrO₄ tetrahedra sharing one corner oxygen; the bridging Cr–O–Cr is bent (126°) and both Cr–O(bridge) bonds are equal, i.e. non-linear and symmetrical (B).\nKey idea: 126° bridge angle = non-linear; identical CrO₃ halves = symmetrical.","traps":["Bridge angle linear?: NCERT gives 126°, so bent.","Symmetry of bridge: Both Cr centres are equivalent; bridge bonds equal."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which one of the following lanthanides exhibits +2 oxidation state with diamagnetic nature? (Given Z for Nd = 60, Yb = 70, La = 57, Ce = 58)","options":["Nd","Yb","La","Ce"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Nd²⁺ exists but is 4f⁴, paramagnetic; (C) La – usual state is +3 (La³⁺ diamagnetic, but not +2); (D) Ce – known for +4, Ce⁴⁺ diamagnetic but not +2.\nWorking: Yb²⁺ = [Xe]4f¹⁴ (no unpaired electrons) – diamagnetic and stable +2 state. Nd²⁺ is 4f⁴ (paramagnetic); La and Ce do not show a typical +2 (La²⁺ 5d¹, Ce²⁺ 4f² would be paramagnetic). Answer (B).\nKey idea: f¹⁴ (Yb²⁺) is the only +2 ion here with zero unpaired electrons.","traps":["La³⁺/Ce⁴⁺ diamagnetic recalled: Check both conditions: +2 AND zero unpaired electrons."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The Eu²⁺ ion is a strong reducing agent in spite of its ground state electronic configuration (outermost): [Atomic number of Eu = 63]","options":["4f⁷6s²","4f⁶","4f⁷","4f⁶6s²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) neutral Eu configuration; (B) and (D) remove an f electron (or keep 6s), mimicking an Eu³⁺-like or wrong order of removal.\nWorking: Eu = [Xe]4f⁷6s²; Eu²⁺ loses the two 6s electrons, leaving the half-filled 4f⁷. It still reduces because Eu³⁺ (the common lanthanoid state) is favoured.\nKey idea: Lanthanoid ions lose 6s electrons first; Eu²⁺ = 4f⁷ (half-filled).","traps":["Removing 4f before 6s: Outermost (6s) electrons leave first on ionisation.","Stable f⁷ yet reducing: Reducing power comes from the strong preference for the +3 state."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Interstitial compounds & alloys","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The major components in \"Gun Metal\" are:","options":["Cu, Zn and Ni","Cu, Sn and Zn","Al, Cu, Mg and Mn","Cu, Ni and Fe"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – German silver (Cu, Zn, Ni); (C) – duralumin (Al, Cu, Mg, Mn); (D) – cupronickel/monel-type.\nWorking: gun metal is about 88% Cu, 10% Sn, 2% Zn.\nKey idea: Gun metal = bronze (Cu–Sn) with a little Zn.","traps":["Alloy names mixed: German silver has Ni; gun metal has Sn."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"E° trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The electrode potential of M²⁺/M of 3d-series elements shows positive value of:","options":["Zn","Fe","Co","Cu"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) – all negative E° values.\nWorking: E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V; Zn −0.76, Fe −0.44, Co −0.28 V.\nKey idea: Cu is the only 3d metal with positive E°(M²⁺/M).","traps":["Noble-ish metals confused: Only Cu is positive in the 3d series."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"What is the correct order of the following elements with respect to their density?","options":["Cr < Zn < Co < Cu < Fe","Zn < Cu < Co < Fe < Cr","Zn < Cr < Fe < Co < Cu","Cr < Fe < Co < Cu < Zn"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts Zn highest by assuming density rises steadily with Z; (A) and (B) misplace Cr/Fe.\nWorking: NCERT values Zn 7.1, Cr 7.19, Fe 7.8, Co 8.7, Cu 8.9 g cm⁻³.\nKey idea: Density rises to Cu then drops at Zn (weak metallic bonding, full d¹⁰).","traps":["Zn assumed densest: Zn has weak metallic bonding → lower density."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The incorrect statement among the following is:","options":["VOSO₄ is a reducing agent","Cr₂O₃ is an amphoteric oxide","RuO₄ is an oxidizing agent","Red colour of ruby is due to the presence of Co³⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) V(IV) looks stable but can be oxidised; (C) RuO₄ unfamiliar but high oxidation state means oxidant; (B) amphoteric Cr₂O₃ is an NCERT line.\nWorking: ruby is Al₂O₃ with Cr³⁺ (d³), not Co³⁺; V(IV) in VOSO₄ is oxidised to V(V); Cr₂O₃ is amphoteric; RuO₄ (Ru +8) is a strong oxidant.\nKey idea: Ruby = Cr³⁺ in Al₂O₃; emerald = Cr³⁺ in beryl.","traps":["Ruby ion swapped: Remember ruby = Cr³⁺.","Asked for the incorrect one: Mark each T/F before choosing."],"topics":["Oxidation states","Colour of ions"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: CeO₂ can be used for oxidation of aldehydes and ketones. Statement II: Aqueous solution of EuSO₄ is a strong reducing agent. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but statement II is true","Statement I is true but statement II is false","Both statement I and statement II are true","Both statement I and statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes CeO₂ is too stable to oxidise (noble-gas f⁰ \"stable\" reasoning); (B) assumes Eu²⁺ is stable because of half-filled f⁷; (D) both stabilities assumed.\nWorking: Ce⁴⁺ (in CeO₂ / Ce(IV) salts) is a strong oxidant (E° Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺ = +1.74 V) that can oxidise aldehydes and ketones; Eu²⁺ (4f⁷) readily goes to the common +3 state, so aqueous EuSO₄ is a strong reducing agent. Both true.\nKey idea: Ln⁴⁺ and Ln²⁺ ions tend to return to +3: Ce⁴⁺ oxidises, Eu²⁺/Yb²⁺ reduce.","traps":["f⁷/f⁰ stability read as inertness: The common state is +3; any other state tends to revert to it.","Oxidising aldehydes and ketones seems unusual: A strong oxidant like Ce(IV) can oxidise both."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"In which of the following pairs, the outer most electronic configuration will be the same?","options":["Cr⁺ and Mn²⁺","Ni²⁺ and Cu⁺","Fe²⁺ and Co⁺","V²⁺ and Cr⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Ni²⁺ d⁸ vs Cu⁺ d¹⁰; (C) Fe²⁺ d⁶ vs Co⁺ d⁸ (3d⁷4s¹ if 3d removed wrongly); (D) V²⁺ d³ vs Cr⁺ d⁵ – chosen if Cr is written 3d⁴4s².\nWorking: Cr⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵ and Mn²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵ (same). Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ vs Cu⁺ 3d¹⁰; Fe²⁺ 3d⁶ vs Co⁺ 3d⁸ (or 3d⁷4s¹); V²⁺ 3d³ vs Cr⁺ 3d⁵.\nKey idea: Remove 4s electrons first; Cr (3d⁵4s¹) → Cr⁺ 3d⁵.","traps":["Cr anomaly forgotten: Cr is 3d⁵4s¹, so Cr⁺ = 3d⁵.","3d electrons removed before 4s: Ionisation removes 4s first."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Interstitial compounds & alloys","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The major components of German Silver are:","options":["Ge, Cu and Ag","Zn, Ni and Ag","Cu, Zn and Ni","Cu, Zn and Ag"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 'German' → Ge and 'silver' → Ag; (B) and (D) include Ag because of the name.\nWorking: German silver (nickel silver) is Cu–Zn–Ni and contains no silver.\nKey idea: German silver has no silver: Cu + Zn + Ni.","traps":["Name taken literally: Recall: nickel silver = Cu, Zn, Ni."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The spin only magnetic moment of a divalent ion in aqueous solution (atomic number 29) is ______ BM.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (truncating 1.73); 0 (Cu⁺ d¹⁰ used); 3 (wrong configuration).\nWorking: Z = 29 is Cu; Cu²⁺ is 3d⁹ with one unpaired electron; μ = √(1×3) = 1.73 BM → 2 on nearest-integer rounding.\nKey idea: Cu²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁹ → n = 1.","traps":["Truncation instead of rounding: Round to nearest: 2.","Wrong ion configuration: Remove 4s first, then one 3d."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which one of the following lanthanoids does not form MO₂? [M is lanthanoid metal]","options":["Pr","Dy","Nd","Yb"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Pr, (B) Dy, (C) Nd are exactly the NCERT list; Yb is the odd one out (+2 stable).\nWorking: Pr, Nd, Tb and Dy show +4 only in oxides MO₂; Yb shows +2 (4f¹⁴) and +3, not +4.\nKey idea: +4 in MO₂: Pr, Nd, Tb, Dy (and Ce); +2: Eu, Yb.","traps":["+2 and +4 lists confused: Yb pairs with Eu for +2 (f¹⁴ / f⁷)."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Dichromate ion is treated with base, the oxidation number of Cr in the product formed is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (assuming reduction to Cr³⁺ as in acidic oxidations).\nWorking: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 2OH⁻ → 2CrO₄²⁻ + H₂O; Cr stays +6 (no redox).\nKey idea: pH shifts CrO₄²⁻/Cr₂O₇²⁻ without changing Cr(VI).","traps":["Reduction assumed: Base only converts to chromate."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In mildly alkaline medium, thiosulphate ion is oxidized by MnO₄⁻ to \"A\". The oxidation state of sulphur in \"A\" is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2.5 – tetrathionate (I₂ reaction) oxidation state; 2 – S in S₂O₃²⁻ average; 4 – sulphite/SO₂ assumed.\nWorking: in neutral/faintly alkaline medium 8MnO₄⁻ + 3S₂O₃²⁻ + H₂O → 8MnO₂ + 6SO₄²⁻ + 2OH⁻; S in SO₄²⁻ is +6.\nKey idea: KMnO₄ (neutral/alkaline) takes S₂O₃²⁻ all the way to SO₄²⁻, unlike I₂ which gives S₄O₆²⁻.","traps":["Iodine-thiosulphate product carried over: Strong oxidant KMnO₄ gives sulphate."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Cu²⁺ salt reacts with potassium iodide to give","options":["Cu₂I₂","Cu₂I₃","CuI","Cu(I₃)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) CuI is chemically the same compound written empirically – the expected answer uses NCERT's formula Cu₂I₂; (B)/(D) invented formulas.\nWorking: 2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → Cu₂I₂(s) + I₂ (CuI₂ is not stable). Note (C) CuI is the same cuprous iodide written empirically; NCERT writes it as Cu₂I₂, which is the expected answer.\nKey idea: Cu(II) iodide does not exist – Cu²⁺ oxidises I⁻ and cuprous iodide (Cu₂I₂) precipitates with I₂.","traps":["CuI vs Cu₂I₂: Use NCERT's written formula Cu₂I₂ when both appear."],"topics":["Oxidation states","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The set having ions which are coloured and paramagnetic both is -","options":["Cu²⁺, Cr³⁺, Sc⁺","Cu²⁺, Zn²⁺, Mn⁴⁺","Sc³⁺, V⁵⁺, Ti⁴⁺","Ni²⁺, Mn⁷⁺, Hg²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Zn²⁺ d¹⁰; (C) all d⁰; (D) Mn⁷⁺ d⁰ and Hg²⁺ d¹⁰ – one bad ion spoils the set.\nWorking: Cu²⁺ (3d⁹), Cr³⁺ (3d³) and Sc⁺ (3d¹4s¹) all have unpaired d electrons → coloured and paramagnetic. Zn²⁺, Hg²⁺ (d¹⁰) and Sc³⁺, V⁵⁺, Ti⁴⁺, Mn⁷⁺ (d⁰) are colourless/diamagnetic.\nKey idea: Reject any set containing a d⁰ or d¹⁰ ion.","traps":["Sc⁺ unfamiliar: Sc⁺ = [Ar]3d¹4s¹ – unpaired."],"topics":["Colour of ions","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The correct order of following 3d metal oxides, according to their oxidation numbers is: (a) CrO₃ (b) Fe₂O₃ (c) MnO₂ (d) V₂O₅ (e) Cu₂O","options":["(d) > (a) > (b) > (c) > (e)","(a) > (c) > (d) > (b) > (e)","(a) > (d) > (c) > (b) > (e)","(c) > (a) > (d) > (e) > (b)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts V₂O₅ first (counts 5 O atoms as a high value); (B) puts MnO₂ above V₂O₅; (D) is random.\nWorking: oxidation numbers Cr +6, V +5, Mn +4, Fe +3, Cu +1 → (a) > (d) > (c) > (b) > (e).\nKey idea: Compute per metal atom: CrO₃ +6, V₂O₅ +5, MnO₂ +4, Fe₂O₃ +3, Cu₂O +1.","traps":["Counting O atoms instead of oxidation number: Divide total O charge by the number of metal atoms."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The spin only magnetic moments (in BM) for free Ti³⁺, V²⁺ and Sc³⁺ ions respectively are (At. No. Sc : 21, Ti : 22, V : 23)","options":["3.87, 1.73, 0","1.73, 3.87, 0","1.73, 0, 3.87","0, 3.87, 1.73"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ti³⁺ and V²⁺; (C)/(D) misplace Sc³⁺ (d⁰).\nWorking: Ti³⁺ 3d¹ (n=1, 1.73 BM); V²⁺ 3d³ (n=3, 3.87 BM); Sc³⁺ 3d⁰ (0).\nKey idea: Count d electrons after removing 4s first.","traps":["Order of answers: Match each ion in the stem order.","d-count: Z − 18 − charge = d count."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Number of electrons present in 4f orbital of Ho³⁺ ion is ______. (Given Atomic No. of Ho = 67)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: 11 (neutral atom count), 9 (removing three 4f electrons).\nWorking: Ho [Xe]4f¹¹6s²; Ho³⁺ loses 6s² and one 4f electron → 4f¹⁰.\nKey idea: Ln³⁺ = [Xe]4fⁿ with n = Z − 57.","traps":["5d¹ assumption: Ho has no 5d electron; Ln³⁺ has 4fⁿ with n = Z − 57."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids vs actinoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Size of Bk³⁺ ion is less than Np³⁺ ion. Reason R: The above is a consequence of the lanthanoid contraction. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – treats A as false by assuming size grows with Z; (B)/(C) – accept the Reason because 'lanthanoid contraction' is the more familiar phrase.\nWorking: Bk (Z = 97) comes after Np (Z = 93) in the 5f series, so Bk³⁺ is smaller (A true); the shrinkage is the actinoid contraction, not the lanthanoid contraction (R false).\nKey idea: Bk and Np are 5f elements: their contraction is the actinoid contraction.","traps":["Lanthanoid vs actinoid contraction: Check whether the ions are 4f or 5f elements.","Bk and Np placement: Recall Np 93, Pu 94, Am 95, Cm 96, Bk 97."],"topics":["Lanthanoids vs actinoids","Actinoids"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: The E° value of Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺ is +1.74 V. Statement II: Ce is more stable in Ce⁴⁺ state than Ce³⁺ state. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – f⁰ (noble-gas) configuration of Ce⁴⁺ taken as greater stability; (B)/(C) – doubt the NCERT value.\nWorking: NCERT gives E°(Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺) = +1.74 V; this large positive value shows Ce⁴⁺ is a strong oxidant that reverts to the common, more stable +3 state.\nKey idea: High positive E°(M⁴⁺/M³⁺) means M⁴⁺ is easily reduced, so the +3 state is more stable.","traps":["f⁰ means most stable: Use E°: +1.74 V shows Ce⁴⁺ is readily reduced.","Sign of E° misread: Positive reduction potential = oxidised form is a strong oxidant."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"FeX₂ and FeY₃ are known when x and y are:","options":["x = F, Cl, Br, I and y = F, Cl, Br","x = F, Cl, Br and y = F, Cl, Br, I","x = Cl, Br, I and y = F, Cl, Br, I","x = F, Cl, Br, I and y = F, Cl, Br, I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the sets (puts I with Fe³⁺); (C) removes FeF₂, which exists; (D) includes FeI₃.\nWorking: all four FeX₂ exist, but FeI₃ does not because Fe³⁺ oxidises I⁻ to I₂ (2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂).\nKey idea: Fe³⁺ (E° 0.77 V) oxidises I⁻ (E° 0.54 V), so FeI₃ is unstable.","traps":["FeI₃ assumed to exist: High oxidation state + reducing anion (I⁻) → redox, no compound.","Sets swapped: The higher (oxidising) state is the one that cannot hold I⁻."],"topics":["Oxidation states","E° trends"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Arrange the following metal complex/compounds in the increasing order of spin only magnetic moment. Presume all the three, high spin system. (Atomic numbers Ce = 58, Gd = 64 and Eu = 63.) (a) (NH₄)₂[Ce(NO₃)₆] (b) Gd(NO₃)₃ and (c) Eu(NO₃)₃. Answer is:","options":["(b) < (a) < (c)","(c) < (a) < (b)","(a) < (b) < (c)","(a) < (c) < (b)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats Ce in the complex as Ce³⁺ and mis-orders; (B) reverses Eu; (C) assumes Eu³⁺ (f⁶) has more unpaired electrons than Gd³⁺.\nWorking: Ce⁴⁺ is 4f⁰ (μ = 0), Eu³⁺ 4f⁶ (6 unpaired, √48 ≈ 6.93 BM), Gd³⁺ 4f⁷ (7 unpaired, √63 ≈ 7.94 BM); spin-only order (a) < (c) < (b). (Real Eu³⁺ moments are lower because of orbital/J effects, but the question asks for spin-only.)\nKey idea: Ce in (NH₄)₂[Ce(NO₃)₆] is +4 → f⁰ → diamagnetic.","traps":["Ce oxidation state misjudged: Charge balance: 2NH₄⁺ + 6NO₃⁻ → Ce⁴⁺.","Eu³⁺ given more unpaired e⁻ than Gd³⁺: f⁶ = 6 unpaired, f⁷ = 7 unpaired (maximum)."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Potassium permanganate on heating at 573 K forms potassium manganate. Statement II: Both potassium permanganate and potassium manganate are tetrahedral and paramagnetic in nature. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but statement II is false","Both statement I and statement II are true","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – assumes both ions paramagnetic; (D) – student who memorised 513 K marks I false; (C) – both errors combined.\nWorking: KMnO₄ decomposes (NCERT: at 513 K) to K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂, so it also does so at 573 K – I true. Both ions are tetrahedral, but MnO₄⁻ (Mn⁷⁺, d⁰) is diamagnetic while MnO₄²⁻ (d¹) is paramagnetic – II false.\nKey idea: Mn(VII) is d⁰ → diamagnetic; Mn(VI) is d¹ → paramagnetic.","traps":["Temperature mismatch read as false: Above the decomposition temperature the reaction still occurs.","Permanganate called paramagnetic: Count d electrons: Mn⁷⁺ d⁰."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"What is the spin-only magnetic moment value (BM) of a divalent metal ion with atomic number 25, in it's aqueous solution?","options":["5.92","5.0","zero","5.26"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 5.0 – n counted as 5 but formula not applied; (D) 5.26 – miscalculation; (C) zero – low-spin assumption.\nWorking: Mn²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵, high spin in water, n = 5, μ = √(5×7) = √35 = 5.92 BM.\nKey idea: μ = √[n(n+2)]; Mn²⁺ d⁵ high spin.","traps":["Number of electrons quoted as BM: Apply √[n(n+2)]."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The common positive oxidation states for an element with atomic number 24, are :","options":["+2 to +6","+1 and +3 to +6","+1 and +3","+1 to +6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) include +1 – tempting because Cr has a single 4s electron, but +1 is not a common state of Cr.\nWorking: Z = 24 is Cr (3d⁵4s¹); the NCERT table of 3d oxidation states lists +2, +3, +4, +5, +6 for Cr (most common +3 and +6). +1 is not listed as a common state.\nKey idea: Cr: +2 to +6 (stable +3, +6); losing only the 4s¹ electron does not give a common state.","traps":["+1 from 4s¹ configuration: In the NCERT oxidation-state table, Cr starts at +2.","\"Common\" vs \"possible\" states: Answer from the textbook table, not from exotic compounds."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"In the ground state of atomic Fe (Z = 26), the spin-only magnetic moment is ______ × 10⁻¹ BM. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n[Given : √3 = 1.73, √2 = 1.41]","type":"num","answer":"49","range":[49.0,49.0],"explain":"Common slips: 59 (Fe³⁺, 5 unpaired, √35 = 5.92), 28/29 (2 unpaired), 4.9 / 5 (forgetting ×10⁻¹).\nWorking: Fe = [Ar]3d⁶4s²; 3d⁶ has 4 unpaired electrons; μ = √(4×6) = √24 = 2√2·√3 = 2 × 1.41 × 1.73 = 4.88 BM = 48.8 × 10⁻¹ → 49.\nKey idea: Neutral Fe 3d⁶4s² has the same 4 unpaired electrons as Fe²⁺ → 4.9 BM.","traps":["Fe³⁺ value used: Atomic Fe: 3d⁶ → 4 unpaired.","×10⁻¹ BM scaling: Report 48.8 → 49."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (process): (a) Deacon's process (b) Contact process (c) Cracking of hydrocarbons (d) Hydrogenation of vegetable oils. List-II (catalyst): (i) ZSM-5 (ii) CuCl₂ (iii) Particles 'Ni' (iv) V₂O₅. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below -","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Deacon–ZSM-5, contact–Ni; (C) Deacon–Ni; (D) Deacon–V₂O₅ and contact–CuCl₂ swapped.\nWorking: Deacon (HCl + O₂ → Cl₂) – CuCl₂; Contact (SO₂ → SO₃) – V₂O₅; cracking/conversion of hydrocarbons – zeolite ZSM-5; hydrogenation of oils – finely divided Ni.\nKey idea: V₂O₅ = contact and Ni = hydrogenation are certain; only (A) has both.","traps":["Deacon vs contact catalyst: Deacon uses CuCl₂ (chloride process), contact uses V₂O₅."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The oxide that shows magnetic property is :","options":["SiO₂","Mn₃O₄","Na₂O","MgO"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are main-group oxides with all electrons paired (diamagnetic); a student may think Na₂O (alkali metal) or MgO is “magnetic” without electron reasoning.\nWorking: Mn₃O₄ (MnO·Mn₂O₃) contains Mn²⁺ (d⁵) and Mn³⁺ (d⁴) with unpaired electrons, so it is paramagnetic; SiO₂, Na₂O and MgO have no unpaired electrons.\nKey idea: Only the d-block oxide has unpaired electrons.","traps":["“Magnetic” read as ferromagnetic only: Look for unpaired electrons – only Mn ions have them."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colour of ions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"In the given chemical reaction, colors of the Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ ions, are respectively: 5Fe²⁺ + MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺","options":["Yellow, Orange","Yellow, Green","Green, Orange","Green, Yellow"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) yellow/orange – both wrong (orange is dichromate); (B) yellow/green – reversed; (C) green/orange – Fe³⁺ given dichromate colour.\nWorking: Fe²⁺ (3d⁶) is green and Fe³⁺ (3d⁵) is yellow, as in the NCERT colour table.\nKey idea: Ferrous = green (FeSO₄·7H₂O green vitriol); ferric = yellow-brown.","traps":["Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ colours swapped: Fe²⁺ (d⁶) green; Fe³⁺ (d⁵) yellow – NCERT colour table.","MnO₄⁻/dichromate colours carried over: Orange belongs to Cr₂O₇²⁻, not to any iron aqua ion."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Identify the element for which electronic configuration in +3 oxidation state is [Ar]3d⁵:","options":["Ru","Mn","Co","Fe"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ru – 4d series; (B) Mn – Mn³⁺ is 3d⁴ (d⁵ only as Mn²⁺); (C) Co – Co³⁺ is 3d⁶.\nWorking: Fe³⁺ = [Ar]3d⁵; Mn³⁺ is d⁴, Co³⁺ is d⁶, Ru³⁺ is [Kr]4d⁵.\nKey idea: Fe (3d⁶4s²) – 3e⁻ → 3d⁵.","traps":["Mn picked for d⁵: Mn³⁺ is d⁴; d⁵ in +3 state needs Z = 26 (Fe).","Group analogue Ru: Ru is a 4d element ([Kr] core)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoid contraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The atomic radius of Ag is closest to :","options":["Cu","Hg","Au","Ni"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cu: same group, so chosen by 'group similarity' though much smaller. (B) Hg: neighbour of Au, larger. (D) Ni: unrelated 3d metal.\nWorking: lanthanoid contraction makes 5d metals nearly the same size as their 4d congeners; Ag (144 pm) ≈ Au (144 pm).\nKey idea: Down a d-group: 3d < 4d ≈ 5d.","traps":["Size increases steadily down a group: After the lanthanoids, 5d radii ≈ 4d radii."],"topics":["Lanthanoid contraction","Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"K₂Cr₂O₇","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Consider the following reactions :\nNaCl + K₂Cr₂O₇ + H₂SO₄ (Conc.) → (A) + Side products\n(A) + NaOH → (B) + Side product\n(B) + H₂SO₄ (dilute) + H₂O₂ → (C) + Side product\nThe sum of the total number of atoms in one molecule each of (A), (B) and (C) is ______.","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking B as Na₂Cr₂O₇ (11 atoms) gives 22; taking C as Cr₂(SO₄)₃ or H₂CrO₄ gives other totals; counting CrO₅ as CrO₄ gives 17.\nWorking: A = CrO₂Cl₂ (chromyl chloride, 5 atoms); B = Na₂CrO₄ (7 atoms); C = CrO₅ (blue peroxide, 6 atoms); total 18.\nKey idea: A is volatile red CrO₂Cl₂; alkali gives yellow chromate; acidified H₂O₂ gives blue CrO₅.","traps":["B taken as dichromate: In NaOH, Cr(VI) is chromate.","C taken as Cr(III) product: Cold acidified H₂O₂ gives blue CrO₅ (Cr stays +6)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The third ionization enthalpy is minimum for :","options":["Fe","Ni","Co","Mn"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Mn chosen by students who think d⁵ makes it easy to ionise (it is the reverse); (B)/(C) chosen on a nuclear-charge argument.\nWorking: IE₃ removes an electron from M²⁺; Fe²⁺ (3d⁶) → Fe³⁺ (3d⁵, half-filled) is easiest; Mn²⁺ (3d⁵) is the hardest.\nKey idea: Fe²⁺ loses an electron to reach d⁵; Mn²⁺ must break d⁵.","traps":["Confusing which ion is d⁵: Write M²⁺ configurations before deciding."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The electronic configurations of bivalent europium and trivalent cerium are (atomic number: Xe = 54, Ce = 58, Eu = 63):","options":["[Xe] 4f⁴ and [Xe] 4f⁹","[Xe] 4f⁷ and [Xe] 4f¹","[Xe] 4f⁷ 6s² and [Xe] 4f² 6s²","[Xe] 4f² and [Xe] 4f⁷"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) keeps 6s electrons in ions; (A) and (D) use wrong f-counts or swap ions.\nWorking: Eu [Xe]4f⁷6s² → Eu²⁺ [Xe]4f⁷; Ce [Xe]4f¹5d¹6s² → Ce³⁺ [Xe]4f¹.\nKey idea: Lanthanoid ions lose 6s, then 5d, then 4f electrons.","traps":["Keeping 6s electrons in the ion: Ions lose 6s first."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The oxidation states of transition metal atoms in K₂Cr₂O₇, KMnO₄ and K₂FeO₄, respectively, are x, y and z. The sum of x, y and z is ____.","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: 18 (Fe taken as +5 or Mn as +6), 16 (Fe taken +3 by habit).\nWorking: Cr +6, Mn +7, Fe +6 → 19.\nKey idea: Charge balance gives Fe +6 in FeO₄²⁻.","traps":["Fe assumed +2/+3: Do the charge balance."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The incorrect statement is :","options":["In manganate and permanganate ions, the π-bonding takes place by overlap of p-orbitals of oxygen and d-orbitals of manganese","Manganate ion is green in colour and permanganate ion in purple in colour","Manganate and permanganate ions are paramagnetic","Manganate and permanganate ions are tetrahedral"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true – pπ–dπ bonding in NCERT; (B) true colours; (D) true geometry.\nWorking: MnO₄²⁻ (Mn⁶⁺, d¹) is paramagnetic but MnO₄⁻ (Mn⁷⁺, d⁰) is diamagnetic, so (C) is incorrect. Both are tetrahedral with pπ–dπ bonding; manganate green, permanganate purple.\nKey idea: Mn(VII) is d⁰ – diamagnetic despite its intense colour (charge transfer).","traps":["Coloured taken as paramagnetic: Colour of d⁰ ions is from charge transfer; d⁰ = diamagnetic.","Looking for the true statement: Mark the false one."],"topics":["KMnO₄","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The incorrect statement(s) among (a)–(c) is (are) : (a) W(VI) is more stable than Cr(VI). (b) In the presence of HCl, permanganate titrations provide satisfactory results. (c) Some lanthanoid oxides can be used as phosphors.","options":["(a) and (b) only","(a) only","(b) and (c) only","(b) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) mark (a) false – inverting the down-group trend of d-block (opposite to p-block inert pair); (C) marks the little-known phosphor use false.\nWorking: (a) true – higher oxidation states more stable down group 6; (b) false – HCl is oxidised to Cl₂ by MnO₄⁻; (c) true – Ln oxides used as phosphors in TV screens.\nKey idea: In d-block, heavier members favour higher oxidation states.","traps":["p-block inert-pair trend applied to d-block: d-block: higher oxidation states more stable down the group.","Obscure use statement doubted: NCERT mentions lanthanoid oxides being used as phosphors in television screens."],"topics":["Oxidation states","KMnO₄","Lanthanoids"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The correct electronic configuration and spin-only magnetic moment (BM) of Gd³⁺ (Z = 64), respectively, are","options":["[Xe]5f⁷ and 8.9","[Xe]4f⁷ and 7.9","[Xe]5f⁷ and 7.9","[Xe]4f⁷ and 8.9"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) 5f is actinoid; (D) 8.9 ≈ √80, i.e. n = 8 (miscounting unpaired electrons).\nWorking: Gd = [Xe]4f⁷5d¹6s²; Gd³⁺ = [Xe]4f⁷, 7 unpaired e⁻, μ = √(7×9) = √63 ≈ 7.9 BM.\nKey idea: Gd³⁺ has a half-filled 4f subshell.","traps":["5f written for a lanthanoid: Lanthanoids fill 4f.","Wrong unpaired count: Gd³⁺ loses 6s² and 5d¹."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Magnetic moment"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"d-block configuration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The set that contains atomic number of only transition element is -","options":["21, 32, 53, 64","21, 25, 42, 72","9, 17, 34, 38","37, 42, 50, 64"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 32 Ge and 53 I are p-block, 64 Gd is f-block; (C) all main-group; (D) 37 Rb s-block, 50 Sn p-block, 64 Gd f-block – Gd is tempting as an 'inner transition' element.\nWorking: 21 Sc, 25 Mn, 42 Mo and 72 Hf are all d-block (incomplete d subshell); 32 Ge, 53 I, 50 Sn, 37 Rb, 9 F, 17 Cl, 34 Se, 38 Sr are not; 64 Gd is an f-block (inner transition) element.\nKey idea: Z = 21–30, 39–48, 57/72–80 are the d-block; 72 (Hf) starts the 5d series after the lanthanoids.","traps":["Inner transition element counted as transition element: 'Transition element' here means d-block; 58–71 are f-block.","Hf (72) mistaken for a lanthanoid: Lanthanoids are 58–71; 72 is Hf, group 4."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The lanthanoid that does NOT show +4 oxidation state is","options":["Dy","Eu","Ce","Tb"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Dy: often forgotten as a +4 lanthanoid (only in DyO₂); (D) Tb: f⁷ in Tb⁴⁺ makes +4 favourable; (C) Ce: best-known +4.\nWorking: Ce (4f⁰ in Ce⁴⁺), Tb (4f⁷ in Tb⁴⁺) and Dy show +4 (Dy in the oxide); Eu shows +2 (4f⁷) and +3, not +4.\nKey idea: Half-filled f⁷ is reached by Eu²⁺ and Tb⁴⁺, so Eu goes down to +2, Tb up to +4.","traps":["Dy not remembered as a +4 lanthanoid: Learn the NCERT list: Ce, Pr, Nd, Tb, Dy show +4.","f⁷ stability applied to wrong element: Count: Eu (4f⁷6s²) reaches f⁷ by losing 2 e⁻; Tb (4f⁹6s²) by losing 4."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Interstitial compounds & alloys","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The INCORRECT statement is :","options":["bronze is an alloy of copper and tin.","brass is an alloy of copper and nickel","cast iron is used to manufacture wrought iron.","german silver is an alloy of zinc, copper and nickel"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) sounds odd ('purer iron from impure iron') but is true; (D) German silver contains no silver, which can look like the false one; (A) correct NCERT fact.\nWorking: brass is Cu–Zn, not Cu–Ni; bronze Cu–Sn, German silver Cu–Zn–Ni, and wrought iron is made from cast iron by oxidising impurities in a reverberatory furnace – all correct.\nKey idea: Brass = Cu + Zn; Cu + Ni (+Zn) is German silver/cupronickel.","traps":["Nickel swapped into brass: Brass: Cu–Zn; German silver: Cu–Zn–Ni.","German silver assumed to contain silver: German silver has no Ag; it is Cu–Zn–Ni."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Mischmetal is an alloy consisting mainly of:","options":["lanthanoid metals","actinoid metals","actinoid and transition metals","lanthanoid and actinoid metals"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) bring in actinoids, which are radioactive and not used in this common alloy; (C) plays on the 5% iron (a transition metal) but pairs it with actinoids.\nWorking: mischmetall is about 95% lanthanoid metal with about 5% iron and traces of S, C, Ca, Al.\nKey idea: Mischmetall = mixed lanthanoids (+ ~5% Fe).","traps":["Iron content suggests transition metal option: The main component is lanthanoid; actinoids are not present."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Interstitial compounds & alloys"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The lanthanide ion that would show colour is –","options":["Sm³⁺","La³⁺","Lu³⁺","Gd³⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) f⁰; (C) f¹⁴; (D) f⁷ half-filled – students assume any partly filled f is coloured.\nWorking: Sm³⁺ is 4f⁵ (coloured, yellow); La³⁺ 4f⁰ and Lu³⁺ 4f¹⁴ colourless; Gd³⁺ 4f⁷ is colourless (no f–f transitions in visible).\nKey idea: Count f electrons: f⁰, f⁷, f¹⁴ ions are colourless.","traps":["Gd³⁺ assumed coloured: f⁷ half-filled has no low-energy f–f transitions in the visible."],"topics":["Lanthanoids","Colour of ions"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Interstitial compounds & alloys","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The statement that is INCORRECT about the interstitial compounds is:","options":["They have high melting points","They are chemically reactive","They have metallic conductivity","They are very hard"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are true NCERT statements.\nWorking: interstitial compounds are chemically inert; the other three are NCERT-listed properties.\nKey idea: NCERT: 'They are chemically inert.'","traps":["Inert vs reactive flipped: Recall the four-item list.","Asking for INCORRECT missed: Underline INCORRECT."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Catalytic properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Match the catalysts (Column I) with products (Column II). Column I: (A) V₂O₅ ; (B) TiCl₄/Al(Me)₃ ; (C) PdCl₂ ; (D) Iron oxide. Column II: (i) Polyethylene ; (ii) ethanal ; (iii) H₂SO₄ ; (iv) NH₃","options":["(A)-(ii); (B)-(iii); (C)-(i); (D)-(iv)","(A)-(iii); (B)-(i); (C)-(ii); (D)-(iv)","(A)-(iii); (B)-(iv); (C)-(i); (D)-(ii)","(A)-(iv); (B)-(iii); (C)-(ii); (D)-(i)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives V₂O₅ → ethanal, Ziegler catalyst → H₂SO₄ and PdCl₂ → polyethylene; (C) and (D) shuffle NH₃ and ethanal.\nWorking: V₂O₅ – contact process (H₂SO₄); Ziegler catalyst TiCl₄/Al(CH₃)₃ – polyethylene; PdCl₂ – Wacker process (ethanal); iron (oxide) – Haber process (NH₃).\nKey idea: Contact–V₂O₅, Ziegler–polythene, Wacker–PdCl₂, Haber–Fe.","traps":["Wacker and Contact catalysts swapped: Pair V₂O₅ with SO₂ → SO₃."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Actinoids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The maximum number of possible oxidation states of actinoides are shown by","options":["berkelium (Bk) and californium (Cf)","nobelium (No) and lawrencium (Lr)","actinium (Ac) and thorium (Th)","neptunium (Np) and plutonium (Pu)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Ac/Th are early but have few states; (A) mid-late actinoids mainly +3/+4; (B) No/Lr mostly +2/+3.\nWorking: Np and Pu show +3 to +7 (five oxidation states), the widest range among actinoids; Ac only +3, Th +3/+4, later actinoids mostly +3 (No +2/+3).\nKey idea: Range peaks in the early-middle series (U, Np, Pu) and falls after Am.","traps":["Early = most states: Range peaks at Np/Pu."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Consider the hydrated ions of Ti²⁺, V²⁺, Ti³⁺ and Sc³⁺. The correct order of their spin-only magnetic moments is :","options":["Sc³⁺ < Ti³⁺ < Ti²⁺ < V²⁺","Ti³⁺ < Ti²⁺ < Sc³⁺ < V²⁺","Sc³⁺ < Ti³⁺ < V²⁺ < Ti²⁺","V²⁺ < Ti²⁺ < Ti³⁺ < Sc³⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) places Sc³⁺ above Ti ions (forgets d⁰); (C) swaps V²⁺ and Ti²⁺; (D) is the reverse order.\nWorking: Sc³⁺ d⁰ (0), Ti³⁺ d¹ (1.73 BM), Ti²⁺ d² (2.83 BM), V²⁺ d³ (3.87 BM); μ = √[n(n+2)].\nKey idea: Same d-count trend: d⁰ < d¹ < d² < d³ (all high-spin/ unpaired).","traps":["V²⁺ d-count underestimated: V²⁺ = [Ar]3d³.","Order direction reversed: Check the < sign direction."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct order of the first ionization enthalpies is:","options":["Mn < Ti < Zn < Ni","Ti < Mn < Ni < Zn","Zn < Ni < Mn < Ti","Ti < Mn < Zn < Ni"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) places Zn below Ni ignoring stable 3d¹⁰4s²; (A) puts Mn below Ti; (C) is the reverse order.\nWorking: ΔiH₁ (kJ/mol): Ti 656, Mn 717, Ni 736, Zn 906 (NCERT values).\nKey idea: General increase across the series, with Zn highest due to filled 3d¹⁰4s².","traps":["Zn placed below Ni: Filled 4s² with poorly shielding 3d¹⁰ gives Zn the highest IE₁ here."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Actinoids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The highest possible oxidation states of uranium and plutonium, respectively, are:","options":["6 and 4","7 and 6","4 and 6","6 and 7"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) assume maximum decreases after U; (B) swaps.\nWorking: in the NCERT table of actinoid oxidation states, U goes up to +6 while Np and Pu reach +7.\nKey idea: Pu also reaches +7, like Np.","traps":["Text implies only Np reaches +7: The NCERT actinoid table still lists +7 for Pu."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Thermal decomposition of a Mn compound (X) at 513 K results in compound Y, MnO₂ and a gaseous product. MnO₂ reacts with NaCl and concentrated H₂SO₄ to give a pungent gas Z. X, Y and Z, respectively, are :","options":["K₂MnO₄, KMnO₄ and SO₂","K₂MnO₄, KMnO₄ and Cl₂","K₃MnO₄, K₂MnO₄ and Cl₂","KMnO₄, K₂MnO₄ and Cl₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) swap X and Y; (A) also SO₂; (C) K₃MnO₄ (Mn(V)) instead of KMnO₄.\nWorking: 2KMnO₄ → K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂ at 513 K; MnO₂ + NaCl + conc. H₂SO₄ liberates Cl₂.\nKey idea: Only KMnO₄ disproportionates to K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ on heating.","traps":["X and Y swapped: Decomposition lowers Mn oxidation state: +7 → +6 and +4.","SO₂ from conc. H₂SO₄: MnO₂ oxidises Cl⁻; the gas is Cl₂."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoid contraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The pair that has similar atomic radii is :","options":["Sc and Ni","Ti and Hf","Mo and W","Mn and Re"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Ti–Hf and (D) Mn–Re are 3d–5d pairs; (A) Sc and Ni are across a period.\nWorking: lanthanoid contraction makes 4d and 5d members of a group nearly equal in size (Mo ≈ W). Ti (3d) is smaller than Hf; Mn (3d) smaller than Re.\nKey idea: Only 4d/5d pairs in the same group match in size.","traps":["3d–5d pair chosen: Lanthanoid contraction equalises 4d and 5d, not 3d."],"topics":["Lanthanoid contraction","Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The highest value of the calculated spin only magnetic moment (in BM) among all the transition metal complexes is :","options":["5.92","3.87","6.93","4.90"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): 6 unpaired electrons. (B)/(D): 3 and 4 unpaired.\nWorking: maximum unpaired d electrons = 5 (high-spin d⁵, e.g. Mn²⁺/Fe³⁺), μ = √35 = 5.92 BM; 6.93 BM would need 6 unpaired d electrons.\nKey idea: d⁵ high spin gives the ceiling of 5.92 BM.","traps":["Six unpaired electrons assumed possible: Max unpaired d electrons in a TM ion is 5."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic size & atomisation enthalpy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The transition element that has lowest enthalpy of atomisation, is:","options":["Zn","Cu","V","Fe"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Zn: lowest value but not a transition element (the intended trap). (C) V and (D) Fe: high atomisation enthalpy from many unpaired d electrons.\nWorking: Zn has the lowest value (126 kJ mol⁻¹) but NCERT says Zn is not regarded as a transition metal (full d¹⁰ in ground state and in Zn²⁺); among the true transition elements listed, Cu (339) < Fe (416) < V (515).\nKey idea: Read 'transition element': Zn is excluded, so the answer is the next lowest, Cu.","traps":["Zn picked from memory of 'lowest': Check the qualifier: Zn is not a transition element by NCERT definition."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoid contraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The effect of lanthanoid contraction in the lanthanoid series of elements by and large means :","options":["decrease in both atomic and ionic radii","increase in atomic radii and decrease in ionic radii","increase in both atomic and ionic radii","decrease in atomic radii and increase in ionic radii"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) split atomic and ionic trends; (C) reverses the trend.\nWorking: across the lanthanoids both atomic and Ln³⁺ ionic radii decrease (poor shielding by 4f electrons).\nKey idea: Both radii decrease steadily (atomic radii with small irregularities).","traps":["Atomic radius irregularities (Eu, Yb): \"By and large\" – the overall trend is a decrease for both."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation states","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The element that usually does not show variable oxidation states is:","options":["V","Ti","Sc","Cu"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Cu – sometimes picked as 'mostly +2', but +1/+2 exist. (A) V and (B) Ti have +2 to +5/+4.\nWorking: Sc shows only +3 (3d¹4s² → loses all three); V, Ti, Cu show several states.\nKey idea: Sc³⁺ has a noble-gas core; nothing to gain from other states.","traps":["Cu thought of as fixed +2: Cu shows +1 and +2."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"KMnO₄","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A →(4 KOH, O₂) 2B (Green) + 2H₂O; 3B →(4 HCl) 2C (Purple) + MnO₂ + 2H₂O; 2C →(H₂O, KI) 2A + 2KOH + D. In the above sequence of reactions, A and D respectively, are :-","options":["KIO₃ and MnO₂","KI and K₂MnO₄","MnO₂ and KIO₃","KI and KMnO₄"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses A and D. (B) and (D): KI is a reactant, not A; K₂MnO₄/KMnO₄ are B/C.\nWorking: A = MnO₂ (fused with KOH/O₂ → green K₂MnO₄ = B); 3K₂MnO₄ + 4HCl → 2KMnO₄ (C, purple) + MnO₂ + 2H₂O + 4KCl; then 2KMnO₄ + H₂O + KI → 2MnO₂ (A) + 2KOH + KIO₃ (D) in neutral/faintly alkaline medium (Mn +7→+4: 6 e⁻; I −1→+5: 6 e⁻).\nKey idea: Green = manganate (MnO₄²⁻), purple = permanganate (MnO₄⁻); MnO₂ is the starting oxide.","traps":["A and D order swapped: Answer in the order asked: A then D.","I⁻ → I₂ assumed: In neutral/faintly alkaline medium iodide goes to iodate."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Magnetic moment","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The pair of metal ions that can give a spin-only magnetic moment of 3.9 BM for the complex [M(H₂O)₆]Cl₂, is :","options":["Cr²⁺ and Mn²⁺","V²⁺ and Co²⁺","V²⁺ and Fe²⁺","Co²⁺ and Fe²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cr²⁺ has 4 and Mn²⁺ 5 unpaired electrons. (C) and (D) include Fe²⁺, which (high-spin d⁶) has 4.\nWorking: 3.9 BM ⇒ n = 3; H₂O weak field: V²⁺ d³ (3 unpaired), Co²⁺ d⁷ high spin (3 unpaired); Cr²⁺ 4, Mn²⁺ 5, Fe²⁺ 4.\nKey idea: n = 3 for d³ and high-spin d⁷.","traps":["Fe²⁺ counted as 3 unpaired: d⁶ high spin: 4 unpaired.","Low-spin assumed for Co²⁺: H₂O is weak field ⇒ high spin."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Lanthanoid contraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The correct order of atomic radii is :","options":["Ce > Eu > Ho > N","N > Ce > Eu > Ho","Eu > Ce > Ho > N","Ho > N > Eu > Ce"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) follows the smooth lanthanoid contraction (Ce > Eu > Ho), missing the Eu anomaly. (B) and (D) place N above lanthanoids – nonsense distractors.\nWorking: the NCERT lanthanoid radius table gives Eu 199 pm (anomalously large, metallic bonding uses only 2 electrons in 4f⁷), Ce 183 pm, Ho ~176 pm; N is far smaller (~70 pm).\nKey idea: Eu (and Yb) have abnormally large metallic radii; otherwise radius falls Ce → Ho.","traps":["Smooth lanthanoid contraction applied blindly: Eu (199 pm) is an anomalous spike in the NCERT radius table."],"topics":["Lanthanoid contraction","Lanthanoids"]}],"mx-met":[{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"Which of the following metals can be extracted through alkali leaching technique?","options":["Sn","Cu","Au","Pb"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): Cu hydrometallurgy from low-grade ores (acid leaching) recalled. (A)/(D): Sn, Pb extracted by reduction.\nWorking: gold (and silver) are leached with dilute NaCN/KCN (alkaline cyanide) in presence of air, then displaced by Zn; Sn, Cu, Pb are obtained by reduction/smelting.\nKey idea: Au: 4Au + 8CN⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂ → 4[Au(CN)₂]⁻ + 4OH⁻ (alkaline medium).","traps":["Copper hydrometallurgy: Alkaline cyanide leaching is for Au/Ag."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"In Hall-Heroult process, the following is used for reducing Al₂O₃:","options":["Magnesium","CaF₂","Graphite","Na₃AlF₆"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) cryolite and (B) CaF₂ are additives that lower melting point and raise conductivity; (A) Mg is too costly/not used.\nWorking: in electrolysis of alumina in molten cryolite, the graphite (carbon) anode is consumed, 2Al₂O₃ + 3C → 4Al + 3CO₂; carbon acts as the reducing agent. Cryolite and CaF₂ only lower the melting point and improve conductivity.\nKey idea: Overall Al₂O₃ is reduced by carbon (anode consumed), helped by electrolysis.","traps":["Cryolite chosen as reducer: Cryolite is a solvent/flux only."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Which one of the following is most likely a mismatch?","options":["Titanium – van Arkel Method","Zinc – Liquation","Nickel – Mond process","Copper – Electrolysis"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D) are correct pairs; a student unsure of van Arkel (Ti/Zr) may pick (A).\nWorking: liquation is used for low-melting metals such as tin (Sn); zinc is refined by distillation. Ti (van Arkel), Ni (Mond) and Cu (electrolytic refining) are correct.\nKey idea: Zn and Hg – distillation; Sn – liquation.","traps":["Distillation vs liquation for Zn: Low boiling → distillation (Zn, Hg); low melting → liquation (Sn)."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R\nAssertion A: In an Ellingham diagram, the oxidation of carbon to carbon monoxide shows a negative slope with respect to temperature.\nReason R: CO tends to get decomposed at higher temperature.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) accept R, but CO is more stable at higher T (that is why C reduces oxides at high T). (C) rejects A.\nWorking: for 2C + O₂ → 2CO, gas moles increase (1 → 2), so ΔS > 0 and the slope (−ΔS) is negative: A is correct. R is false: CO becomes more stable, not less, at higher temperature (that is why carbon reduces oxides at high T).\nKey idea: Negative slope means ΔG falls with T, i.e. CO formation is favoured at high T, the opposite of R.","traps":["Stability direction reversed: Negative slope → more negative ΔG at high T → CO more stable."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"Among the following compounds, the number of those present in copper matte is ________. A. CuCO₃ B. Cu₂S C. Cu₂O D. FeO","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (Cu₂O counted, it forms during roasting/bessemerisation), 3 (FeO counted as it is in the smelting step but goes to slag).\nWorking: copper matte is mainly Cu₂S with some FeS; of the given compounds only Cu₂S is present, so the count is 1.\nKey idea: Matte is a sulphide melt: Cu₂S and FeS only; FeO goes to slag as FeSiO₃.","traps":["FeO or Cu₂O counted as matte: Matte = sulphides; oxides go to slag or react in the converter."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I: A. Reverberatory furnace; B. Electrolytic cell; C. Blast furnace; D. Zone Refining furnace\nList-II: I. Pig Iron; II. Aluminium; III. Silicon; IV. Copper\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Si in the reverberatory furnace and Al in zone refining. (C)/(D) give pig iron to the reverberatory furnace.\nWorking: copper matte is smelted/roasted in a reverberatory furnace; Al by electrolysis (Hall–Héroult); pig iron from blast furnace; Si purified by zone refining.\nKey idea: Electrolytic → Al and Blast → pig iron are certain; that fixes the option.","traps":["Reverberatory vs blast furnace: Blast furnace = iron; reverberatory = copper matte."],"topics":["Extraction","Refining"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"The metal which is extracted by oxidation and subsequent reduction from its ore is :","options":["Al","Cu","Ag","Fe"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Al: electrolytic reduction only. (B) Cu: self-reduction/auto-reduction. (D) Fe: reduction by CO in blast furnace.\nWorking: Ag (and Au) is leached by NaCN/air (Ag → [Ag(CN)₂]⁻, oxidation) and then displaced by Zn (reduction).\nKey idea: Cyanide leaching first oxidises Ag to Ag(I) complex; Zn then reduces it.","traps":["Oxidation then reduction: Roasting oxidises the ore, not the metal."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Which one of the following reactions does not occur during extraction of copper?","options":["2FeS + 3O₂ → 2FeO + 2SO₂","FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃","CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃","2Cu₂S + 3O₂ → 2Cu₂O + 2SO₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) remove iron as FeSiO₃ slag – they do occur. (D) is the self-reduction step's partial oxidation – it occurs.\nWorking: in copper extraction FeS is roasted to FeO (A), FeO is removed with silica as FeSiO₃ slag (B), and Cu₂S is partly oxidised to Cu₂O before self-reduction (D). CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ is the slag reaction of the iron blast furnace, so (C) does not occur.\nKey idea: Flux in Cu extraction is silica (acidic) to remove basic FeO; CaO/limestone flux is for iron.","traps":["Blast-furnace slag reaction mixed into copper extraction: Copper: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃; Iron: CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Statement I: In froth floatation method a rotating paddle agitates the mixture to drive air out of it. Statement II: Iron pyrites are generally avoided for extraction of iron due to environmental reasons. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) misses the reversed phrase; (A)/(D) reject the pyrites statement.\nWorking: Statement I is false – the paddle draws air in to form the froth. Statement II is true – iron pyrites are avoided because roasting them releases SO₂.\nKey idea: Froth needs air bubbles – the paddle draws air in.","traps":["Air in vs out: Recall the purpose: air forms the froth that carries ore particles."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":82,"q":"The reaction representing the Mond process for metal refining is _________.","options":["ZnO + C →(Δ) Zn + CO","2K[Au(CN)₂] + Zn →(Δ) K₂[Zn(CN)₄] + 2Au","Zr + 2I₂ →(Δ) ZrI₄","Ni + 4CO →(Δ) Ni(CO)₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) van Arkel (Zr/I₂); (B) cyanide process for gold; (A) carbon reduction of ZnO.\nWorking: in the Mond process impure Ni is heated with CO (about 330–350 K) to form volatile Ni(CO)₄, which is decomposed at higher temperature (450–470 K) to give pure Ni.\nKey idea: Mond → nickel carbonyl.","traps":["Mond vs van Arkel: Mond uses CO with Ni; van Arkel uses I₂ with Zr/Ti."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"The major component of which of the following ore is sulphide based mineral?","options":["Sphalerite","Siderite","Malachite","Calamine"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) FeCO₃, (C) basic copper carbonate, (D) ZnCO₃ – all carbonates; calamine shares Zn with sphalerite.\nWorking: sphalerite (zinc blende) is ZnS, a sulphide; siderite FeCO₃, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ and calamine ZnCO₃ are carbonates.\nKey idea: Zinc ores: sphalerite (ZnS) vs calamine (ZnCO₃).","traps":["Two zinc ores: Blende/sphalerite = sulphide."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"In the extraction of copper, its sulphide ore is heated in a reverberatory furnace after mixing with silica to:","options":["remove FeO as FeSiO₃","remove calcium as CaSiO₃","decrease the temperature needed for roasting of Cu₂S","separate CuO as CuSiO₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B: calcium is not the impurity here. C: silica does not lower roasting temperature. D: copper must not be lost to slag.\nWorking: silica is the flux; the FeO formed from the iron sulphide impurity combines with it, FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ (slag), and is removed, while copper stays in the matte as Cu₂S.\nKey idea: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ (slag); copper stays in the matte.","traps":["Losing copper to slag: Flux removes the iron impurity; copper is the product."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"Statement I: During Electrolytic refining, the pure metal is made to act as anode and its impure metallic form is used as cathode. Statement II: During the Hall-Heroult electrolysis process, purified Al₂O₃ is mixed with Na₃AlF₆ to lower the melting point of the mixture. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): accept the swapped electrodes; (C): reject cryolite's role (confusing with fluorspar or with increasing conductivity only).\nWorking: Statement I is false – in electrolytic refining the impure metal is the anode and a strip of pure metal is the cathode. Statement II is true – cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) lowers the melting point of the mixture and increases its conductivity.\nKey idea: Impure metal dissolves at the anode; pure metal deposits on the cathode.","traps":["Anode/cathode swap in refining: Impure → anode (oxidised), pure → cathode (deposited)."],"topics":["Refining","Extraction"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"The methods NOT involved in concentration of ore are A. Liquation B. Leaching C. Electrolysis D. Hydraulic washing E. Froth floatation. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A and C only","C, D and E only","B, D and C only","B, D and E only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) include hydraulic washing/froth flotation, which are concentration methods. (C) includes leaching (a concentration method).\nWorking: liquation and electrolysis are refining methods; leaching, hydraulic washing and froth flotation concentrate ores. So A and C only.\nKey idea: Refining (liquation, electrolysis) comes after reduction; it is not concentration.","traps":["Refining mixed with concentration: Concentration acts on ore; liquation/electrolysis act on crude metal."],"topics":["Concentration","Refining"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","options":["Cast iron is obtained by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast.","The malleable iron is prepared from cast iron by oxidising impurities in a reverberatory furnace.","van Arkel method is used to purify tungsten.","Boron and Indium can be purified by zone refining method."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true NCERT line on cast iron. (B) true: wrought/malleable iron from cast iron in reverberatory furnace lined with haematite. (D) true: B, Ga, In are zone-refined.\nWorking: the van Arkel method purifies Zr and Ti (via volatile iodides), not tungsten, so (C) is incorrect; (A), (B) and (D) are correct statements.\nKey idea: van Arkel (iodide) process is for Zr and Ti, not W.","traps":["Metal swapped in a true-looking line: Recall van Arkel → Zr, Ti."],"topics":["Refining","Extraction"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The compound(s) that is(are) removed as slag during the extraction of copper is: (1) CaO (2) FeO (3) Al₂O₃ (4) ZnO (5) NiO. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(3), (4) Only","(1), (2), (5) Only","(1), (2) Only","(2) Only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (B) include CaO, which is the flux in the blast furnace for iron (slag is CaSiO₃); (A) Al₂O₃/ZnO are unrelated impurities.\nWorking: in copper smelting the iron (as FeO from Cu₂S/FeS roasting) is removed by silica as FeSiO₃ slag; CaO is a flux used in iron extraction, not removed here.\nKey idea: Copper metallurgy: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃.","traps":["Flux confused with slag: Slag is the product (silicate); the flux is a reagent. In Cu extraction the flux is SiO₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: Pig iron is obtained by heating cast iron with scrap iron. Statement II: Pig iron has a relatively lower carbon content than that of cast iron. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are not correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D) accept one or both swapped statements as true because the wording is familiar.\nWorking: cast iron is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke (Statement I reverses this); pig iron has about 4% C, cast iron about 3%, so pig iron has the higher carbon content (Statement II false).\nKey idea: Pig iron → (melt with scrap + coke) → cast iron; carbon falls from ~4% to ~3%.","traps":["Pig iron and cast iron swapped: Pig iron is the blast-furnace product; cast iron is made from it.","Carbon content order reversed: Pig iron ~4% C > cast iron ~3% C."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Refining using liquation method is the most suitable for metals with:","options":["Low melting point","High boiling point","High electrical conductivity","Less tendency to be soluble in melts than impurities"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) confuses with distillation (low boiling point metals like Zn, Hg); (C) confuses with electrolytic refining; (D) reverses the zone-refining idea (impurities more soluble in the melt).\nWorking: in liquation a low-melting metal (Sn, Pb, Bi) is melted and made to flow down a sloping hearth, leaving higher-melting impurities behind.\nKey idea: Liquation = melt and drain away the low-melting metal.","traps":["Liquation vs distillation vs zone refining: Liquation: m.p.; distillation: b.p.; zone refining: solubility in melt."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The metal that is not extracted from its sulphide ore is :","options":["Aluminium","Iron","Lead","Zinc"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) tempts because iron is extracted from oxide ores, but iron pyrites (FeS₂) is listed as an ore; (C)/(D) are classic sulphide-ore metals.\nWorking: Al is extracted from bauxite (oxide); Zn (zinc blende), Pb (galena) are sulphide ores; iron pyrites is a sulphide of iron (though iron is chiefly from oxides), so the only metal never from sulphide is Al.\nKey idea: Al occurs as oxide (bauxite) only; it is never extracted from a sulphide.","traps":["Iron from oxide ore: Pyrites exists as an iron sulphide ore; Al has no sulphide ore."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following methods are not used to refine any metal?\n(A) Liquation (B) Calcination (C) Electrolysis (D) Leaching (E) Distillation\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B and D only","A, B, D and E only","B, D and E only","A, C and E only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): include liquation or distillation as non-refining (forgetting Sn and Zn/Hg refining); (C): adds distillation.\nWorking: liquation (Sn), electrolysis (Cu) and distillation (Zn, Hg) are refining methods; calcination is heating an ore and leaching is concentration — neither refines a metal: B and D.\nKey idea: Refining acts on crude metal; leaching and calcination act on the ore.","traps":["Distillation as purification of ore: Zn and Hg are refined by distillation.","Leaching as refining: Leaching is concentration of ore."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Among the following ores Bauxite, Siderite, Cuprite, Calamine, Haematite, Kaolinite, Malachite, Magnetite, Sphalerite, Limonite, Cryolite, the number of principal ores of iron is _______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (missing limonite or siderite); 5 (wrongly counting pyrites-type or cuprite as iron ore).\nWorking: iron ores are siderite (FeCO₃), haematite (Fe₂O₃), magnetite (Fe₃O₄) and limonite (Fe₂O₃·3H₂O) = 4. Bauxite, kaolinite, cryolite (Al); cuprite, malachite (Cu); calamine, sphalerite (Zn).\nKey idea: Iron: haematite, magnetite, limonite, siderite.","traps":["Siderite or limonite missed: Memorise the four iron ores together."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the reaction is suitable for concentrating ore by leaching process?","options":["2Cu₂S + 3O₂ → 2Cu₂O + 2SO₂","Fe₃O₄ + CO → 3FeO + CO₂","Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2Na[Al(OH)₄]","Al₂O₃ + 6Mg → 6MgO + 4Al"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) roasting of sulphide ore; (B) partial reduction in the blast furnace; (D) metal displacement (reduction) — all later steps, not concentration.\nWorking: leaching dissolves the ore in a suitable reagent; bauxite dissolves in NaOH as aluminate (Bayer process). (A) is roasting, (B) reduction in a blast furnace, (D) a reduction (and as printed it is unbalanced: Al₂O₃ + 3Mg → 3MgO + 2Al).\nKey idea: Leaching = dissolving the ore selectively; NaOH dissolves amphoteric Al₂O₃.","traps":["Concentration vs reduction: Concentration produces a purer ore, not the metal."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: The reduction of a metal oxide is easier if the metal formed is in liquid state than solid state. Reason R: The value of ΔG° becomes more on negative side as entropy is higher in liquid state than solid state. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ignores that R is the direct reason; (C)/(D) misjudge one statement although both are textbook lines.\nWorking: for MxO(s) + reductant → xM + ..., a liquid metal product has higher entropy than solid metal, so ΔS is more positive and ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS° is more negative; reduction is easier and R explains A.\nKey idea: Higher entropy of a liquid product makes ΔS more positive, so ΔG° more negative.","traps":["Entropy of liquid vs solid: Write ΔG = ΔH − TΔS and compare ΔS."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"In metallurgy the term \"gangue\" is used for:","options":["Contamination of undesired earthy materials.","Contamination of metals, other than desired metal","Minerals which are naturally occurring in pure form","Magnetic impurities in an ore."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) describes other-metal impurities; (C) describes native metals; (D) only one kind of impurity removed by magnetic separation.\nWorking: gangue (matrix) is the unwanted earthy/siliceous material mixed with the ore.\nKey idea: Gangue = earthy, undesired materials in the ore.","traps":["Gangue vs native metal: Gangue is the impurity, not the metal."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"In liquation process used for tin (Sn), the metal:","options":["is reacted with acid","is dissolved in water","is brought to molten form which is made to flow on a slope","is fused with NaOH."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) confuses with leaching; (B) nonsense for a metal; (D) confuses with alkali fusion/leaching of ores (e.g., bauxite).\nWorking: in liquation a low-melting metal (Sn) is melted and allowed to flow down a sloping hearth, leaving higher-melting impurities behind.\nKey idea: Liquation = melt and flow away from infusible impurities.","traps":["Liquation vs leaching: Liquation uses melting; leaching uses a solvent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Sphalerite, (B) Calamine, (C) Galena, (D) Siderite. List-II: (I) FeCO₃, (II) PbS, (III) ZnCO₃, (IV) ZnS. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps calamine and siderite (both carbonates); (D) swaps sphalerite and calamine (both zinc ores); (C) swaps the sulphides/carbonates wholesale.\nWorking: sphalerite (zinc blende) ZnS; calamine ZnCO₃; galena PbS; siderite FeCO₃.\nKey idea: Two zinc ores: sphalerite = sulphide, calamine = carbonate; siderite = iron carbonate.","traps":["Zinc ores interchanged: Sphalerite/zinc blende = ZnS; calamine = ZnCO₃.","Carbonate ores interchanged: Siderite – \"sider\" = iron."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following chemical reactions represents Hall–Héroult Process?","options":["Cr₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Cr","2Al₂O₃ + 3C → 4Al + 3CO₂","FeO + CO → Fe + CO₂","2[Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + Zn(s) → 2Au(s) + [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – Goldschmidt aluminothermic reduction of Cr₂O₃; (C) – blast-furnace reduction of iron oxide by CO; (D) – zinc displacement in cyanide extraction of gold.\nWorking: electrolysis of alumina in molten cryolite with carbon anodes; overall 2Al₂O₃ + 3C → 4Al + 3CO₂.\nKey idea: Hall–Héroult = electrolysis of Al₂O₃ with carbon anode that is oxidised to CO/CO₂.","traps":["Aluminium in reaction mistaken for Al extraction: In Hall–Héroult Al is the product, from Al₂O₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"(a) Baryte, (b) Galena, (c) Zinc blende and (d) Copper pyrites. How many of these minerals are sulphide based?","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 – baryte counted because it contains sulphur (BaSO₄); 2 – copper pyrites missed (thought to be oxide/carbonate).\nWorking: galena PbS, zinc blende ZnS, copper pyrites CuFeS₂ are sulphides; baryte BaSO₄ is a sulphate. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Contains S²⁻ (sulphide) vs SO₄²⁻ (sulphate): baryte is barium sulphate.","traps":["Sulphate counted as sulphide: Baryte = BaSO₄ (sulphate); only S²⁻ minerals count."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: During electrolytic refining, blister copper deposits precious metals.\nStatement II: In the process of obtaining pure copper by electrolysis method, copper blister is used to make the anode.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) tempts students who read Statement I as saying precious metals deposit on the cathode; (C)/(B) from thinking pure copper is the anode.\nWorking: impure (blister) copper is the anode; less reactive impurities such as Ag, Au, Pt fall below it as anode mud, so the blister copper \"deposits\" the precious metals. Both true.\nKey idea: Impure copper → anode; noble metals do not dissolve and collect as anode mud.","traps":["Where precious metals collect: Less reactive metals do not oxidise; they fall as anode mud.","Anode vs cathode material: Impure = anode, pure = cathode."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement I : According to the Ellingham diagram, any metal oxide with higher ΔG° is more stable than the one with lower ΔG°. Statement II : The metal involved in the formation of oxide placed lower in the Ellingham diagram can reduce the oxide of a metal placed higher in the diagram. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts the flipped Statement I; (B)/(C) reject Statement II by reading \"lower\" as less reactive.\nWorking: an oxide with more negative (lower) ΔG°f is more stable, so Statement I is false; a metal whose oxide line lies lower can reduce an oxide lying higher (net ΔG° negative), so Statement II is true = (D).\nKey idea: Lower line = more negative ΔG° = more stable oxide = metal is the better reducing agent.","traps":["Higher ΔG° read as more stable: More negative ΔG°f = more stable.","Position vs reactivity confused: Lower line metal reduces any oxide above it."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The role of depressants in Froth Flotation method is to","options":["selectively prevent one component of the ore from coming to the froth.","reduce the consumption of oil for froth formation.","stabilize the froth.","enhance non-wettability of the mineral particles."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) role of froth stabilisers (cresols, aniline); (D) role of collectors (pine oil, xanthates); (B) invented role.\nWorking: a depressant (e.g. NaCN with ZnS/PbS ore) forms a complex/surface layer on one sulphide and stops it entering the froth, allowing selective separation.\nKey idea: Collector – makes mineral non-wettable; stabiliser – stabilises froth; depressant – keeps one mineral out of froth.","traps":["Depressant confused with collector: Collectors increase non-wettability; depressants selectively prevent one mineral from frothing.","Depressant confused with froth stabiliser: Stabilisers such as cresols and aniline do that job."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Concentration of gold ore, (B) Leaching of alumina, (C) Froth stabiliser, (D) Blister copper. List-II: (I) Aniline, (II) NaOH, (III) SO₂, (IV) NaCN.","options":["(A)–(IV), (B)–(III), (C)–(II), (D)–(I)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(II), (C)–(I), (D)–(III)","(A)–(III), (B)–(II), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(II), (B)–(IV), (C)–(III), (D)–(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – SO₂ for leaching alumina and aniline for blister copper (random); (C) – SO₂ for gold concentration; (D) – NaOH for gold and NaCN for alumina (swapped leaching agents).\nWorking: gold ore is concentrated by cyanide leaching (NaCN); bauxite is leached with NaOH; cresols/aniline are froth stabilisers; blister copper gets its blistered look from escaping SO₂ → option (B).\nKey idea: Cyanide for noble metals, alkali for amphoteric Al₂O₃; SO₂ escape causes the blisters.","traps":["Leaching agents swapped: Au needs a complexing agent (CN⁻); Al₂O₃ dissolves in alkali.","Froth stabiliser vs collector: Cresols and aniline stabilise the froth."],"topics":["Concentration","Extraction"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Statement I: Leaching of gold with cyanide ion in absence of air / O₂ leads to cyano complex of Au(III). Statement II: Zinc is oxidized during the displacement reaction carried out for gold extraction. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) accept Statement I (miss that O₂ is the oxidant and the complex is [Au(CN)₂]⁻); (B) rejects II by thinking Zn is reduced.\nWorking: leaching needs O₂ (4Au + 8CN⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂ → 4[Au(CN)₂]⁻ + 4OH⁻) and gives the Au(I) complex, so Statement I is wrong on two counts; Zn displaces Au from [Au(CN)₂]⁻ and is itself oxidised to [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻, so Statement II is right.\nKey idea: Gold needs an oxidant (O₂) to dissolve; the dicyanoaurate(I) ion is then reduced by Zn.","traps":["Au(III) vs Au(I) complex: Write [Au(CN)₂]⁻: two CN⁻ and charge −1 means Au(I).","Air requirement removed: Gold is noble; O₂ is the oxidant in leaching."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given are two statements one is labelled as Assertion A and other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Magnesium can reduce Al₂O₃ at a temperature below 1350 °C, while above 1350 °C aluminium can reduce MgO. Reason R: The melting and boiling points of magnesium are lower than those of aluminium. In light of the above statements, choose most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are correct, and R is correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct, R is not correct.","A is not correct, R is correct."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tempts those who link the boiling point of Mg to the slope change; (C) tempts those who do not know Mg boils below Al; (D) tempts those who think Al always reduces MgO.\nWorking: A is the old-NCERT Ellingham statement (Mg/MgO and Al/Al₂O₃ lines cross near 1350 °C) and R is true (Mg m.p. 650 °C, b.p. 1090 °C; Al m.p. 660 °C, b.p. 2470 °C). The crossover is a ΔG° (relative slope) argument; strictly the change in slope of the Mg line at its boiling point contributes, so (A) is arguable, but NCERT does not give R as the reason and the official answer is (B).\nKey idea: Reduction feasibility is set by which oxide has the more negative ΔG° at that temperature; m.p./b.p. alone do not decide it.","traps":["Explanation link in A-R: Ask whether R alone predicts the 1350 °C crossover; it does not.","Which metal reduces which oxide: Lower line on Ellingham diagram reduces the oxide above it."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"In the metallurgical extraction of copper, the following reaction is used: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃. FeO and FeSiO₃ respectively are:","options":["gangue and flux","flux and slag","slag and flux","gangue and slag"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) call FeSiO₃ or FeO the flux (the flux is SiO₂); (C) reverses the roles.\nWorking: FeO (from roasting iron in copper pyrites) is the impurity (gangue), SiO₂ is the flux and FeSiO₃ is the slag.\nKey idea: Impurity + flux → slag; SiO₂ is the acidic flux.","traps":["Which species is the flux: Flux is added; gangue is already present."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"In isolation of which one of the following metals from their ores, the use of cyanide salt is not commonly involved ?","options":["Zinc","Gold","Silver","Copper"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Zinc tempts students who only remember leaching of Ag/Au; (B) and (C) are classic cyanide leaching metals.\nWorking: Au and Ag are leached with NaCN/KCN; for Zn, NaCN is used as a depressant (for ZnS) in froth flotation of mixed PbS–ZnS ores; copper extraction does not commonly use cyanide.\nKey idea: Cyanide appears twice in old NCERT metallurgy: leaching of Ag/Au and depressing ZnS in flotation.","traps":["Cyanide only for Ag and Au: Recall NaCN depressant for ZnS in froth flotation.","Depressant role overlooked: List all reagent roles of NaCN."],"topics":["Extraction","Concentration"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Ore): A. Siderite; B. Malachite; C. Sphalerite; D. Calamine\nList II (Composition): I. FeCO₃; II. CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂; III. ZnS; IV. ZnCO₃\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps the two zinc ores (sphalerite ZnS vs calamine ZnCO₃); (B), (C) scramble all.\nWorking: siderite FeCO₃, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, sphalerite (zinc blende) ZnS, calamine ZnCO₃.\nKey idea: Sphalerite = zinc blende (sulphide); calamine = carbonate.","traps":["Two zinc ores swapped: \"Blende\" ores are sulphides."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The choice of reducing agents for metals extraction can be made by using Ellingham diagram, a plot of ΔG vs temperature. Statement II: The value of ΔS increases from left to right in Ellingham diagram. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts the misreading; (B)/(C) reject the standard use of the diagram.\nWorking: I is true (Ellingham ΔG°–T plots show which element can reduce a given oxide). II is false: left to right is increasing temperature; ΔS of each oxide-formation line is roughly constant (it fixes the slope, −ΔS) and changes only at phase transitions.\nKey idea: Ellingham: ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°; slope = −ΔS°, which does not 'increase' along T.","traps":["Slope, not ΔS, changes along T: Each line has slope −ΔS, roughly constant except at phase changes.","Statement I is the textbook use: A lower line can reduce the oxide of a higher line."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Sphalerite is a sulphide ore of zinc and copper glance is a sulphide ore of copper.\nStatement II: It is possible to separate two sulphide ores by adjusting proportion of oil to water or by using 'depressants' in a froth flotation method.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) for those who think flotation cannot separate two sulphides; (C) for those who confuse copper glance with copper pyrites or sphalerite with an oxide ore.\nWorking: sphalerite = ZnS, copper glance = Cu₂S; NCERT states two sulphide ores can be separated by adjusting the oil/water proportion or by depressants (e.g. NaCN depresses ZnS in a PbS–ZnS ore).\nKey idea: Depressants (NaCN) selectively stop one sulphide from frothing.","traps":["Copper glance vs copper pyrites: Glance = Cu₂S; pyrites = CuFeS₂ – both sulphides.","Depressants separate two sulphides: Recall NaCN with ZnS/PbS."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which refining process is generally used in the purification of low melting metals?","options":["Chromatographic method","Liquation","Electrolysis","Zone refining"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) zone refining – also uses melting but is for ultra-pure semiconductors (Si, Ge, Ga); (A) chromatography – for elements in minute amounts; (C) electrolysis – general for Cu, Zn.\nWorking: in liquation the low-melting metal (e.g. tin) is melted on a sloping hearth and flows away from higher-melting impurities.\nKey idea: Low melting point → let the metal melt and flow off: liquation.","traps":["Zone refining also involves melting: Zone refining = semiconductors/high purity; liquation = low-melting metals."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List-I (Name of ore/mineral) with List-II (Chemical formula): (a) Calamine (b) Malachite (c) Siderite (d) Sphalerite; (i) ZnS (ii) FeCO₃ (iii) ZnCO₃ (iv) CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps siderite and sphalerite (both start with \"S\"); (C) swaps calamine and malachite (both carbonates); (D) swaps malachite and siderite.\nWorking: calamine ZnCO₃, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, siderite FeCO₃, sphalerite (zinc blende) ZnS.\nKey idea: Two zinc ores: calamine (carbonate) and sphalerite (sulphide).","traps":["Siderite vs sphalerite: Sider- = iron (FeCO₃); sphalerite = zinc blende (ZnS)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Assertion (A): Aluminium is extracted from bauxite by the electrolysis of molten mixture of Al₂O₃ with cryolite. Reason (R): The oxidation state of Al in cryolite is +3. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true.","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) accepts R as explanation because both are true; (A) miscalculates oxidation state of Al in Na₃AlF₆; (B) thinks Al is made by carbon reduction.\nWorking: (A) true – Hall–Héroult process electrolyses purified Al₂O₃ dissolved in molten cryolite. (R) true – Na₃AlF₆ has Al +3. But cryolite is used to lower the melting point and raise conductivity, not because of Al’s oxidation state, so (R) does not explain (A): option (D).\nKey idea: Cryolite’s job: lower m.p. and increase conductivity – nothing to do with Al’s oxidation state.","traps":["True but not the explanation: Ask why cryolite is added: m.p. and conductivity."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"In the electrolytic refining of blister copper, the total number of main impurities, from the following, removed as anode mud is ______. Pb, Sb, Se, Te, Ru, Ag, Au and Pt","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (adds Pb or Ru as noble/inert), 8 (all), 3 (only Ag, Au, Pt).\nWorking: the old NCERT lists the anode mud from copper refining as antimony, selenium, tellurium, silver, gold and platinum: Sb, Se, Te, Ag, Au, Pt = 6. Pb and Ru are not in that list.\nKey idea: NCERT list: Sb, Se, Te, Ag, Au, Pt.","traps":["Ru counted as noble metal: Stick to the NCERT list.","Only noble metals counted: Sb, Se, Te also fall as mud per NCERT."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following ore is concentrated using group 1 cyanide salt?","options":["Sphalerite","Calamine","Siderite","Malachite"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Calamine and (D) Malachite – carbonate ores (calcination, not flotation); (C) Siderite – carbonate iron ore, often confused because cyanide is associated with Ag/Au leaching.\nWorking: in froth flotation of a ZnS (sphalerite) ore mixed with PbS, NaCN is used as a depressant that keeps ZnS from entering the froth; the other ores (ZnCO₃, FeCO₃, CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂) are not sulphides and are not concentrated by flotation with cyanide.\nKey idea: Cyanide in concentration = depressant in froth flotation → sulphide ore (ZnS).","traps":["Cyanide leaching of Ag/Au recalled: In concentration of sulphide ores, NaCN is a depressant.","Ore names mixed up: Sphalerite = ZnS (sulphide, flotation); calamine = ZnCO₃."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Al₂O₃ was leached with alkali to get X. The solution of X on passing of gas Y, forms Z. X, Y and Z respectively are:","options":["X = Na[Al(OH)₄], Y = SO₂, Z = Al₂O₃","X = Na[Al(OH)₄], Y = CO₂, Z = Al₂O₃·xH₂O","X = Al(OH)₃, Y = CO₂, Z = Al₂O₃","X = Al(OH)₃, Y = SO₂, Z = Al₂O₃·xH₂O"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) – SO₂ as the gas (acidic gas, but not the one used); (C)/(D) – X as Al(OH)₃ ignores that alumina dissolves as aluminate; (C) – anhydrous Al₂O₃ forms only after ignition, not on passing CO₂.\nWorking: Bayer/Hall leaching – Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2Na[Al(OH)₄]; CO₂ neutralises the aluminate and hydrated alumina Al₂O₃·xH₂O precipitates.\nKey idea: Leaching gives soluble aluminate; CO₂ precipitates hydrated alumina.","traps":["Hydrated vs anhydrous alumina: CO₂ precipitates Al₂O₃·xH₂O; heating gives Al₂O₃ later."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Metal): (a) Aluminium; (b) Iron; (c) Copper; (d) Zinc. List-II (Ores): (i) Siderite; (ii) Calamine; (iii) Kaolinite; (iv) Malachite.","options":["(a)–(iv), (b)–(iii), (c)–(ii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(i), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(i), (b)–(ii), (c)–(iii), (d)–(iv)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(i), (c)–(iv), (d)–(ii)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Distractors permute the four pairs.\nWorking: kaolinite Al₂(OH)₄Si₂O₅ (Al), siderite FeCO₃, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, calamine ZnCO₃.\nKey idea: Calamine and siderite are carbonates of Zn and Fe.","traps":["Calamine vs siderite: Calamine = Zn, siderite = Fe."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Ellingham diagram is a graphical representation of:","options":["ΔH vs T","ΔG vs T","ΔG vs P","(ΔG – TΔS) vs T"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ΔH vs T confuses with enthalpy; (C) pressure axis is wrong; (D) ΔG − TΔS = ΔH, so this is (A) in disguise.\nWorking: Ellingham diagram plots ΔG° of formation of oxides (per mol O₂) against temperature.\nKey idea: Ellingham = ΔG° (per mole O₂) against T.","traps":["(ΔG − TΔS) looks like ΔG: ΔG − TΔS simplifies to ΔH."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The method used for the purification of Indium is:","options":["van Arkel method","liquation","zone refining","vapour phase refining"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) van Arkel – Zr, Ti; (B) liquation – low-melting Sn; (D) vapour phase – Ni (Mond), also covers van Arkel.\nWorking: semiconductor-grade metals such as Si, Ge, B, Ga and In are purified by zone refining.\nKey idea: Zone refining: Si, Ge, B, Ga, In.","traps":["Refining methods mixed up: Memorise metal–method pairs."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List-I (Ore) with List-II (Element present): (a) Kernite (b) Cassiterite (c) Calamine (d) Cryolite; (i) Tin (ii) Boron (iii) Fluorine (iv) Zinc.","options":["(a)→(i), (b)→(iii), (c)→(iv), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(ii), (b)→(i), (c)→(iv), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(ii), (b)→(iv), (c)→(i), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(iii), (b)→(i), (c)→(ii), (d)→(iv)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps cassiterite/calamine; (A)/(D) put kernite on Sn or F; cryolite tempts Al, which is absent from List-II.\nWorking: kernite Na₂B₄O₇·4H₂O (B); cassiterite SnO₂ (Sn); calamine ZnCO₃ (Zn); cryolite Na₃AlF₆ (F, also Al).\nKey idea: Cryolite = Na₃AlF₆, so here it pairs with fluorine.","traps":["Cassiterite/calamine swapped: Cassiterite = SnO₂ (tin stone); calamine = ZnCO₃.","Cryolite thought only an Al ore: Na₃AlF₆ contains F."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Siderite (b) Calamine (c) Malachite (d) Cryolite. List-II: (i) Cu (ii) Ca (iii) Fe (iv) Al (v) Zn.","options":["(a)→(iii), (b)→(i), (c)→(v), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(i), (b)→(ii), (c)→(v), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(iii), (b)→(v), (c)→(i), (d)→(iv)","(a)→(i), (b)→(ii), (c)→(iii), (d)→(iv)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – calamine taken as Cu ore, malachite as Zn; (B)/(D) – siderite read as Cu, calamine as Ca (from 'calc-').\nWorking: siderite FeCO₃ (Fe), calamine ZnCO₃ (Zn), malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ (Cu), cryolite Na₃AlF₆ (Al).\nKey idea: Siderite = Fe (sideros = iron); calamine = Zn.","traps":["Calamine linked to calcium: Calamine = ZnCO₃."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The metal that can be purified economically by fractional distillation method is:","options":["Fe","Zn","Cu","Ni"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Ni – tempting because of Mond process (volatile carbonyl), which is vapour-phase refining, not distillation of the metal; (A), (C) have high boiling points.\nWorking: distillation refining suits low-boiling metals such as Zn (b.p. ≈ 907 °C) and Hg; Fe, Cu and Ni have very high boiling points (Ni is refined by the Mond process, Cu electrolytically).\nKey idea: Distillation refining = Zn, Hg (low boiling points).","traps":["Vapour-phase vs distillation: Distillation vaporises the metal itself – needs a low b.p. metal."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Consider two chemical reactions (A) and (B) that take place during metallurgical process : (A) ZnCO₃(s) —Δ→ ZnO(s) + CO₂(g) (B) 2ZnS(s) + 3O₂(g) —Δ→ 2ZnO(s) + 2SO₂(g). The correct option of names given to them respectively is :","options":["(A) is calcination and (B) is roasting","Both (A) and (B) are producing same product so both are roasting","Both (A) and (B) are producing same product so both are calcination","(A) is roasting and (B) is calcination"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps the names; (B)/(C) use the same product as a false criterion.\nWorking: heating a carbonate without air to drive off CO₂ is calcination; heating a sulphide ore in excess air to the oxide is roasting.\nKey idea: Decide by the reactant and whether O₂ is consumed, not by the product.","traps":["Names swapped: Roasting = oxygen in the equation (sulphide ores); calcination = decomposition without O₂."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Sulphide ion is soft base and its ores are common for metals. (a) Pb (b) Al (c) Ag (d) Mg. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a) and (c) only","(a) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(c) and (d) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) include Mg (hard, carbonate ores); (C) includes Al (oxide ore bauxite).\nWorking: soft acids Pb²⁺ and Ag⁺ occur as sulphides (galena PbS, argentite Ag₂S); hard Al³⁺ and Mg²⁺ occur as oxides/carbonates/silicates.\nKey idea: Soft S²⁻ pairs with soft, heavy, polarisable cations (Pb²⁺, Ag⁺, Cu⁺, Hg²⁺, Zn²⁺).","traps":["Hard cations chosen: Al³⁺, Mg²⁺ are hard → oxide/carbonate ores."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In the leaching of alumina from bauxite, the ore expected to leach out in the process by reacting with NaOH is:","options":["TiO₂","Fe₂O₃","ZnO","SiO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Fe₂O₃ and (A) TiO₂ are real bauxite impurities but do not dissolve; (C) ZnO is amphoteric and would dissolve but is not in bauxite.\nWorking: in the Bayer process the main impurities are SiO₂, Fe₂O₃ and TiO₂; SiO₂ dissolves in NaOH as sodium silicate along with sodium aluminate, while Fe₂O₃ and TiO₂ remain as red mud. ZnO is amphoteric but is not a bauxite impurity.\nKey idea: Acidic SiO₂ and amphoteric Al₂O₃ dissolve in NaOH; basic Fe₂O₃ and TiO₂ stay behind.","traps":["ZnO amphoteric distractor: Ask which oxide is actually present in bauxite.","Fe₂O₃ as the main impurity: Fe₂O₃ is basic and stays in red mud."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List I with List II (both having metallurgical terms):\n(a) Concentration of Ag ore (b) Blast furnace (c) Blister copper (d) Froth floatation method\n(i) Reverberatory furnace (ii) Pig iron (iii) Leaching with dilute NaCN solution (iv) Sulfide ores\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)–(iii), (b)–(ii), (c)–(i), (d)–(iv)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(i), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(i), (c)–(iii), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(iii), (c)–(ii), (d)–(i)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) pairs blast furnace with sulphide ores; (C)/(D) put Ag ore with sulphide ores – partly true (argentite is a sulphide) which makes them tempting.\nWorking: Ag ore is leached with dilute NaCN (cyanide process); blast furnace gives pig iron; froth floatation is for sulphide ores; blister copper by elimination goes with the reverberatory furnace (strictly, matte is made in the reverberatory furnace and blister copper in the Bessemer converter – the pairing is loose but the only consistent option).\nKey idea: Fix the certain pairs (NaCN leaching, pig iron, froth floatation–sulphide) and eliminate.","traps":["Ag ore is also a sulphide: Concentration of Ag = cyanide leaching in NCERT.","Blister copper furnace: Use elimination when one pairing is loose."],"topics":["Concentration","Extraction"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The statement that is INCORRECT about Ellingham diagram is","options":["provides idea about the reaction rate.","provides idea about free energy change.","provides idea about changes in the phases during the reaction.","provides idea about reduction of metal oxide."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are true uses; (C) may look wrong to students who forget slope breaks at melting/boiling points.\nWorking: an Ellingham diagram plots ΔG° vs T – thermodynamics only; it tells nothing about kinetics, so (A) is incorrect. Slope changes mark phase changes, so statement (C) is true.\nKey idea: ΔG° tells feasibility, not rate.","traps":["Thermodynamics is not kinetics: ΔG° plots never give rates."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The addition of silica during the extraction of copper from its sulphide ore:","options":["converts copper sulphide into copper silicate","converts iron oxide into iron silicate","reduces copper sulphide into metallic copper","reduces the melting point of the reaction mixture"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes copper is slagged; (C) confuses flux with reductant (self-reduction does that); (D) confuses with cryolite's role in Al.\nWorking: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ (slag) removes iron impurity; Cu₂S stays in the matte.\nKey idea: Acidic flux SiO₂ removes basic FeO as slag.","traps":["Flux vs reductant: Flux removes impurity as slag; Cu₂S/Cu₂O self-reduce."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The process that involves the removal of sulphur from the ores is:","options":["Smelting","Roasting","Leaching","Refining"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – smelting reduces the oxide with carbon/flux; (C) – leaching dissolves the ore chemically; (D) – refining purifies the crude metal.\nWorking: roasting heats sulphide ores in excess air, removing S as SO₂ (e.g. 2ZnS + 3O₂ → 2ZnO + 2SO₂).\nKey idea: Sulphide ore + air (roasting) → oxide + SO₂.","traps":["Roasting vs calcination/smelting: Air + sulphide ore = roasting."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following reduction reaction CANNOT be carried out with coke?","options":["Al₂O₃ → Al","ZnO → Zn","Fe₂O₃ → Fe","Cu₂O → Cu"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ZnO – reduced by coke at about 1673 K; (C) Fe₂O₃ – blast-furnace reduction by C/CO; (D) Cu₂O – reduced by coke (or self-reduction).\nWorking: on the Ellingham diagram the Al₂O₃ line lies below the C/CO line up to impractically high temperatures, so Al is extracted electrolytically; ZnO (≈1673 K), Fe₂O₃ (blast furnace) and Cu₂O are reduced by coke.\nKey idea: Highly electropositive metals (Al) have very stable oxides: electrolysis, not carbon.","traps":["NOT wording reversed: Underline CANNOT before scanning options.","ZnO thought too stable for carbon: High temperature is still practical; only Al₂O₃ is out of reach."],"topics":["Ellingham diagram","Extraction"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List-I and List-II :\nList-I: (a) Haematite (b) Bauxite (c) Magnetite (d) Malachite\nList-II: (i) Al₂O₃·xH₂O (ii) Fe₂O₃ (iii) CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ (iv) Fe₃O₄\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps haematite and magnetite (Fe₃O₄ vs Fe₂O₃); (A) and (C) misplace bauxite and malachite.\nWorking: haematite Fe₂O₃, bauxite Al₂O₃·xH₂O, magnetite Fe₃O₄, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂.\nKey idea: HaEmatite = Fe₂O₃ (red, ferric); MAGnetite = Fe₃O₄ (magnetic, mixed oxide).","traps":["Haematite and magnetite swapped: Magnetite is the magnetic mixed oxide Fe₃O₄."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The chemical that is added to reduce the melting point of the reaction mixture during the extraction of aluminium is:","options":["Cryolite","Bauxite","Calamine","Kaolinite"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) bauxite – the ore itself; (C) calamine – zinc ore; (D) kaolinite – aluminosilicate clay.\nWorking: in the Hall–Héroult process alumina is mixed with cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) (and CaF₂) to lower the melting point and raise conductivity.\nKey idea: Cryolite is the flux/solvent that lowers melting point in Hall–Héroult.","traps":["Ore vs additive: Bauxite is the ore; cryolite is the additive."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match list-I with list-II :\nList-I: (a) Mercury (b) Copper (c) Silicon (d) Nickel\nList-II: (i) Vapour phase refining (ii) Distillation refining (iii) Electrolytic refining (iv) Zone refining\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the option given below :","options":["a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii","a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps Si and Ni (vapour phase vs zone); (D) swaps Cu and Si; (A) scrambles all.\nWorking: Hg – distillation (low b.p.); Cu – electrolytic; Si – zone refining (semiconductor purity); Ni – Mond (vapour phase) process.\nKey idea: Mond process = vapour-phase refining of Ni.","traps":["Si and Ni methods swapped: Ni(CO)₄ is volatile → vapour phase."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Calamine and Malachite, respectively, are the ores of:","options":["Nickel and Aluminium","Zinc and Copper","Copper and Iron","Aluminium and Zinc"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ni/Al – unrelated metals; (C) Cu/Fe – right metal for malachite but in the wrong place, and Fe is not calamine; (D) Al/Zn – zinc placed second (reverses calamine).\nWorking: calamine is ZnCO₃ (zinc ore) and malachite is CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ (copper ore).\nKey idea: Calamine lotion contains zinc; malachite is the green copper carbonate.","traps":["Calamine (Zn) vs malachite (Cu) swapped: Calamine = ZnCO₃; malachite = CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ (green).","Order \"respectively\" ignored: Check the order of the pair against the order of the ore names."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The purest form of commercial iron is","options":["scrap iron and pig iron","wrought iron","cast iron","pig iron"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) cast iron: confused with 'refined' iron. (D)/(A): pig iron is the impure blast-furnace product.\nWorking: wrought (malleable) iron has the least carbon (~0.1–0.2%) of the commercial forms; pig iron ~4% C, cast iron ~3% C.\nKey idea: Wrought iron = purest commercial iron (lowest carbon).","traps":["Cast iron thought purer than wrought iron: Carbon: pig ~4% > cast ~3% > wrought ~0.2%."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The refining method used when the metal and the impurities have low and high melting temperatures, respectively, is -","options":["zone refining","liquation","vapour phase refining","distillation"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) zone refining relies on impurities being more soluble in the melt; (C) vapour-phase needs a volatile compound; (D) distillation is for low-boiling metals.\nWorking: in liquation a low-melting metal (e.g. Sn) is melted and made to flow down a slope away from infusible impurities.\nKey idea: Liquation separates by melting the low-melting metal away from infusible impurities.","traps":["Zone refining chosen: Zone refining is about solubility of impurities in the melt, not melting points."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Among the reactions (a) – (d), the reaction(s) that does/do not occur in the blast furnace during the extraction of iron is/are : (a) CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ (b) 3Fe₂O₃ + CO → 2Fe₃O₄ + CO₂ (c) FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ (d) FeO → Fe + ½O₂","options":["(c) and (d)","(a) and (d)","(d)","(a)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats the CaO + SiO₂ slag step as absent; (C) misses that FeSiO₃ slag belongs to copper smelting; (D) picks the genuine slag reaction as false.\nWorking: (a) slag formation from CaO (limestone) and silica, and (b) reduction of Fe₂O₃ to Fe₃O₄ by CO in the upper zone both occur. (c) FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ is the slag reaction of copper smelting, not iron; FeO is reduced by C/CO instead. (d) simple thermal decomposition of FeO does not happen (ΔG positive). So (c) and (d): option (A).\nKey idea: FeO + SiO₂ → FeSiO₃ is the copper-matte slag reaction; in iron extraction FeO is reduced, and oxides do not just decompose on heating.","traps":["FeSiO₃ slag assumed in iron furnace: Iron furnace slag is CaSiO₃; FeSiO₃ is copper smelting.","NOT-type selection reversed: Mark each reaction occurs/does not occur before matching."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Cast iron is used for the manufacture of :","options":["wrought iron and pig iron","wrought iron and steel","wrought iron, pig iron and steel","pig iron, scrap iron and steel"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C) include pig iron, which is the raw material for cast iron; (D) adds scrap iron and pig iron.\nWorking: pig iron from the blast furnace is remelted to cast iron; cast iron is then used to make wrought iron (purest commercial iron) and steel. Pig iron is the precursor, not a product.\nKey idea: Pig iron → cast iron → wrought iron and steel.","traps":["Pig iron listed as a product: Remember the order blast furnace → pig → cast."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Among statements (a)-(d), the correct ones are : (a) Lime stone is decomposed to CaO during the extraction of iron from its oxides. (b) In the extraction of silver, silver is extracted as an anionic complex. (c) Nickel is purified by Mond's process. (d) Zr and Ti are purified by Van Arkel method.","options":["(c) and (d) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(b), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) each drop a true statement; doubts usually about (a) (thinking limestone stays as CaCO₃) or (b) (thinking Ag forms a cationic complex).\nWorking: (a) CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ in the blast furnace (flux); (b) Ag leached as [Ag(CN)₂]⁻; (c) Ni → Ni(CO)₄ (Mond); (d) Zr, Ti via iodides (van Arkel). All true.\nKey idea: All four are standard NCERT metallurgy facts.","traps":["Doubting the anionic silver complex: [Ag(CN)₂]⁻ is an anion.","Limestone role in blast furnace: CaCO₃ decomposes to CaO which forms CaSiO₃ slag."],"topics":["Extraction","Refining"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The processes of calcination and roasting in metallurgical industries, respectively, can lead to :","options":["Global warming and acid rain","Photochemical smog and ozone layer depletion","Global warming and photochemical smog","Photochemical smog and global warming"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) photochemical smog needs NOx + hydrocarbons + sunlight; ozone depletion needs CFCs – neither produced here; order \"respectively\" also tested.\nWorking: calcination of carbonates releases CO₂ (greenhouse gas → global warming); roasting of sulphide ores releases SO₂ (→ acid rain).\nKey idea: CO₂ from calcination, SO₂ from roasting.","traps":["Respective order swapped: Match calcination→CO₂ first, roasting→SO₂ second."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"An Ellingham diagram provides information about :","options":["the pressure dependence of the standard electrode potentials of reduction reactions involved in the extraction of metals.","the kinetics of the reduction process.","the temperature dependence of the standard Gibbs energies of formation of some metal oxides.","the conditions of pH and potential under which a species is thermodynamically stable."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) pressure dependence of E° is not plotted; (B) old NCERT states explicitly that the diagram says nothing about kinetics; (D) Pourbaix (E–pH) diagram.\nWorking: an Ellingham diagram plots ΔfG° of oxides vs T; it says nothing about kinetics; (D) describes a Pourbaix diagram.\nKey idea: Ellingham = ΔG° vs T for oxide formation (thermodynamics only).","traps":["Pourbaix diagram described as Ellingham: Ellingham axes are ΔG° and T.","Kinetics read into thermodynamic plot: ΔG says nothing about rate."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Boron and silicon of very high purity can be obtained through:","options":["vapour phase refining","electrolytic refining","liquation","zone refining"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) vapour phase refining – used for Ni and Zr/Ti; (B) electrolytic – Cu, Zn; (C) liquation – low-melting metals like Sn.\nWorking: zone refining (impurities more soluble in the melt) gives semiconductor-grade Si, Ge, B, Ga, In. Vapour-phase refining is for Ni (Mond) and Zr/Ti (van Arkel).\nKey idea: Semiconductor-grade elements → zone refining.","traps":["Vapour phase refining for 'very high purity': van Arkel is for Zr and Ti; Si/B/Ge are zone refined."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The element that can be refined by distillation is:","options":["nickel","zinc","gallium","tin"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) nickel – refined by Mond process (volatile carbonyl, not distillation of the metal); (C) gallium – zone refining for semiconductor purity; (D) tin – liquation (low melting point, not low boiling point).\nWorking: distillation is used for low-boiling metals such as Zn and Hg; Ni is refined by the Mond process, Ga by zone refining, Sn by liquation.\nKey idea: Distillation needs a low boiling point: Zn (907 °C) and Hg.","traps":["Mond process mistaken for distillation: Mond process is vapour-phase refining via a carbonyl, a separate method.","Low melting point confused with low boiling point: Low m.p. → liquation; low b.p. → distillation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"For a reaction, 4M(s) + nO₂(g) → 2M₂Oₙ(s), the free energy change is plotted as a function of temperature. The temperature below which the oxide is stable could be inferred from the plot as the point at which:","options":["the slope changes from positive to zero","the free energy change shows a change from negative to positive value","the slope changes from negative to positive","the slope changes from positive to negative"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) refer to slope changes, which come from phase changes (melting/boiling of M) and change in ΔS, not from stability limits.\nWorking: the oxide is stable (formation spontaneous) while ΔG < 0; above the temperature where the line crosses ΔG = 0 it decomposes. Slope changes indicate phase changes, not stability.\nKey idea: Stability of a compound with respect to its elements ends where ΔfG = 0.","traps":["Slope change taken as stability limit: Kinks mark phase changes; stability needs ΔG < 0."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ellingham diagram","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"With respect to an ore, Ellingham diagram helps to predict the feasibility of its –","options":["Vapour phase refining","Zone refining","Electrolysis","Thermal reduction"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) refining methods; (C) electrolysis judged by E°, not by Ellingham.\nWorking: Ellingham ΔG°–T plots show which reductant/temperature makes thermal reduction of the oxide feasible.\nKey idea: Ellingham = ΔG° vs T for oxide formation → thermal reduction.","traps":["Mixing reduction with refining: Ellingham deals with reduction, not refining."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The Mond process is used for the","options":["extraction of Mo","Purification of Ni","Purification of Zr and Ti","Extraction of Zn"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Zr/Ti is van Arkel method; (A) 'Mo' sound-alike of Mond; (D) Zn is extracted by reduction/distillation.\nWorking: Ni is converted to volatile Ni(CO)₄ at ~330–350 K and decomposed at 450–470 K to pure Ni.\nKey idea: Mond ⇒ Ni(CO)₄ ⇒ nickel purification.","traps":["Van Arkel confused with Mond: Mond = Ni, van Arkel = Zr/Ti.","Name-sound match: Associate with CO and Ni."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The ore that contains the metal in the form of fluoride is :","options":["magnetite","sphalerite","malachite","cryolite"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) oxide; (B) sulfide; (C) basic carbonate.\nWorking: cryolite Na₃AlF₆; magnetite Fe₃O₄, sphalerite ZnS, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂.\nKey idea: Cryolite = Na₃AlF₆ (used in Hall–Héroult electrolysis).","traps":["Ore formula mix-up: Memorise ore–anion pairs."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The one that is not a carbonate is :","options":["bauxite","siderite","calamine","malachite"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) siderite, (C) calamine and (D) malachite are all carbonates; malachite is basic copper carbonate and may be mistaken for an oxide/hydroxide.\nWorking: bauxite is a hydrated aluminium oxide (AlOₓ(OH)₃₋₂ₓ); siderite FeCO₃, calamine ZnCO₃, malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂.\nKey idea: Bauxite is the only oxide (hydrated alumina) in the list.","traps":["Malachite thought not a carbonate: Malachite = CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, still a carbonate."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Assertion: For the extraction of iron, haematite ore is used. Reason: Haematite is a carbonate ore of iron.","options":["Only the reason is correct.","Both the assertion and reason are correct and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion.","Only the assertion is correct.","Both the assertion and reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) accept the reason; haematite and siderite confused; (A) rejects the true assertion.\nWorking: haematite (Fe₂O₃) is the main ore used for iron extraction, but it is an oxide ore (siderite is the carbonate).\nKey idea: Haematite = Fe₂O₃ (oxide); siderite = FeCO₃.","traps":["Haematite thought carbonate: Haematite Fe₂O₃, siderite FeCO₃."],"topics":["Extraction","Concentration"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Refining","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match the refining methods (Column I) with metals (Column II). Column I (Refining methods): (I) Liquation; (II) Zone Refining; (III) Mond Process; (IV) Van Arkel Method. Column II (Metals): (a) Zr; (b) Ni; (c) Sn; (d) Ga","options":["(I)–(b); (II)–(c); (III)–(d); (IV)–(a)","(I)–(b); (II)–(d); (III)–(a); (IV)–(c)","(I)–(c); (II)–(a); (III)–(b); (IV)–(d)","(I)–(c); (II)–(d); (III)–(b); (IV)–(a)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) give liquation to Ni; (C) gives zone refining to Zr and van Arkel to Ga – swaps of the two \"purity\" methods.\nWorking: liquation – Sn (low-melting metal); zone refining – Ga (semiconductor purity); Mond process – Ni via Ni(CO)₄; van Arkel – Zr via ZrI₄.\nKey idea: Mond = Ni(CO)₄; van Arkel = ZrI₄; zone refining = semiconductors (Ga); liquation = Sn.","traps":["Zone refining and van Arkel swapped: Zone refining is a melt/crystallise method for semiconductors; van Arkel is volatile iodide."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The correct statement is:","options":["zincite is a carbonate ore","aniline is a froth stabilizer","zone refining process is used for the refining of titanium","sodium cyanide cannot be used in the metallurgy of silver"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) zincite is ZnO; (C) zone refining is for Si, Ge, Ga, B, In (Ti uses van Arkel); (D) NaCN is used for Ag and Au leaching.\nWorking: aniline and cresols are froth stabilisers in froth flotation. Zincite is ZnO (oxide); Ti is refined by van Arkel method; NaCN leaches silver.\nKey idea: Froth stabilisers = cresols, aniline.","traps":["Refining methods mixed up: Ti and Zr → van Arkel; semiconductors → zone refining."],"topics":["Concentration","Refining"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The idea of froth floatation method came from a person X and this method is related to the process Y of ores. X and Y, respectively, are:","options":["fisher woman and concentration","washer man and reduction","washer woman and concentration","fisher man and reduction"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) fisherman/fisherwoman; (B)/(D) \"reduction\" confuses concentration with extraction.\nWorking: froth flotation, a concentration (beneficiation) method for sulphide ores, is said to have originated from a washerwoman noticing that sand-laden clothes and mineral particles behaved differently in soapy water.\nKey idea: Froth flotation = concentration; idea from a washerwoman.","traps":["Concentration vs reduction: Froth flotation separates ore from gangue: concentration."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct statement is :","options":["leaching of bauxite using concentrated NaOH solution gives sodium aluminate and sodium silicate","the blistered appearance of copper during the metallurgical process is due to the evolution of CO₂","pig iron is obtained from cast iron","the Hall-Heroult process is used for the production of aluminium and iron"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) CO₂ instead of SO₂; (C) reverses pig iron → cast iron; (D) adds iron to an aluminium-only process.\nWorking: silica impurity also dissolves in hot conc. NaOH as sodium silicate; blister copper is due to SO₂, cast iron is made from pig iron, Hall–Héroult is for Al only.\nKey idea: Hot conc. NaOH dissolves both Al₂O₃ and SiO₂ impurity.","traps":["Blister copper attributed to CO₂: Copper matte contains sulphide, so SO₂ escapes.","Pig iron and cast iron order reversed: Pig iron from blast furnace is remelted to give cast iron."],"topics":["Extraction","Concentration"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The ore that contains both iron and copper is:","options":["malachite","dolomite","azurite","copper pyrites"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): Cu-only ores. (B): Ca/Mg ore.\nWorking: copper pyrites is CuFeS₂; malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, azurite 2CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂, dolomite CaCO₃·MgCO₃.\nKey idea: Copper pyrites = CuFeS₂.","traps":["Copper carbonate ore picked: Only CuFeS₂ contains Fe."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Hall-Heroult's process is given by :","options":["Cr₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Cr","Cu²⁺(aq) + H₂(g) → Cu(s) + 2H⁺(aq)","ZnO + C —(coke, 1673 K)→ Zn + CO","2Al₂O₃ + 3C → 4Al + 3CO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) aluminothermic (Goldschmidt) reduction. (B) hydrometallurgical reduction of Cu²⁺ by H₂. (C) carbon reduction of ZnO.\nWorking: Hall–Héroult electrolysis of Al₂O₃ (in cryolite) with carbon anodes; O₂ released burns the anode, overall 2Al₂O₃ + 3C → 4Al + 3CO₂.\nKey idea: Hall–Héroult is electrolysis; carbon appears only as the consumed anode, giving CO₂.","traps":["Al present in two equations: Hall–Héroult produces Al; thermite uses Al as reductant."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match the ores (Column A) with the metals (Column B):\nColumn-A: (I) Siderite; (II) Kaolinite; (III) Malachite; (IV) Calamine\nColumn-B: (a) Zinc; (b) Copper; (c) Iron; (d) Aluminium","options":["I-b; II-c; III-d; IV-a","I-c; II-d; III-a; IV-b","I-c; II-d; III-b; IV-a","I-a; II-b; III-c; IV-d"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps malachite and calamine (both carbonates). (A)/(D) misplace siderite.\nWorking: siderite FeCO₃ (Fe); kaolinite Al₂(OH)₄Si₂O₅ (Al); malachite CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂ (Cu); calamine ZnCO₃ (Zn).\nKey idea: Malachite is green copper carbonate; calamine is zinc carbonate.","traps":["Carbonate ores swapped: Malachite = Cu (green); calamine = Zn."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The reaction that does NOT define calcination is :-","options":["ZnCO₃ →(Δ) ZnO + CO₂","Fe₂O₃·xH₂O →(Δ) Fe₂O₃ + xH₂O","CaCO₃·MgCO₃ →(Δ) CaO + MgO + 2CO₂","2Cu₂S + 3O₂ →(Δ) 2Cu₂O + 2SO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are the NCERT calcination examples (loss of CO₂ or H₂O).\nWorking: calcination is heating (in limited/no air) to drive off volatile matter such as CO₂ or H₂O; (D) heats a sulphide in excess O₂, which is roasting.\nKey idea: O₂ as a reactant → roasting; only CO₂/H₂O lost → calcination.","traps":["NOT-type reversal: Mark each as calcination or roasting first, then pick the odd one."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In the Hall-Heroult process, aluminium is formed at the cathode. The cathode is made out of :","options":["Platinum","Carbon","Pure aluminium","Copper"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) pure aluminium: confusion with electrorefining (Hoopes). (A)/(D): generic electrode metals.\nWorking: steel vessel lined with carbon acts as cathode; graphite rods are the anode.\nKey idea: Both electrodes in Hall–Héroult are carbon.","traps":["Confusing with refining cathode: Extraction cell uses carbon lining as cathode."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Extraction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The pair that does NOT require calcination is:","options":["ZnO and MgO","Fe₂O₃ and CaCO₃·MgCO₃","ZnO and Fe₂O₃·xH₂O","ZnCO₃ and CaO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) dolomite needs calcination; (C) limonite Fe₂O₃·xH₂O needs it; (D) ZnCO₃ needs it – each wrong option has one 'innocent' oxide paired with one ore that does need calcination.\nWorking: calcination removes CO₂/H₂O from carbonates and hydrated oxides; ZnO and MgO are already anhydrous oxides. Every other pair contains a carbonate or hydrated oxide.\nKey idea: Only anhydrous oxides skip calcination.","traps":["Pair judged by one member only: Check both members of the pair."]}],"mx-sb":[{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":90,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Alkali Metal): A. Li; B. Na; C. Rb; D. Cs\nList II (Emission Wavelength in nm): I. 589.2; II. 455.5; III. 670.8; IV. 780.0\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) shuffle values; (C) swaps Li and Na wavelengths.\nWorking: flame emission lines Li 670.8 nm (crimson), Na 589.2 nm (yellow), Rb 780.0 nm (red-violet), Cs 455.5 nm (blue).\nKey idea: Colour → wavelength: Na yellow ~589, Li crimson ~671, Cs blue ~455.","traps":["Wavelength order assumed to follow atomic number: Use colours: Cs blue (short λ), Rb red (long λ)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"The setting time of Cement is increased by adding :","options":["Gypsum","Limestone","Clay","Silica"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) limestone and (C) clay are raw materials; (D) silica is a constituent – none retards setting.\nWorking: 2–3% gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) is added to clinker to slow down setting.\nKey idea: Gypsum retards setting.","traps":["Raw material vs additive: Gypsum is the setting-time controller."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Ion having highest hydration enthalpy among the given alkaline earth metal ions is :","options":["Sr²⁺","Ca²⁺","Be²⁺","Ba²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Ba²⁺ for students linking 'more metallic' with 'more hydration'; (B)/(A) intermediate.\nWorking: hydration enthalpy decreases with increasing ionic size down the group; Be²⁺ (smallest) has the highest.\nKey idea: Smaller, higher charge density ion ⇒ larger hydration enthalpy.","traps":["Hydration trend reversed: Hydration depends on charge density, not reactivity."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Structures of BeCl₂ in solid state, vapour phase and at very high temperature respectively are :","options":["Polymeric, Dimeric, Monomeric","Dimeric, Polymeric, Monomeric","Polymeric, Monomeric, Dimeric","Monomeric, Dimeric, Polymeric"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps vapour and high-temperature forms; (B) swaps solid and vapour; (D) reverses the whole order.\nWorking: solid BeCl₂ is a chain polymer with Cl bridges; in the vapour it is a chloro-bridged dimer; above ~1200 K it is a linear monomer.\nKey idea: Aggregation decreases as temperature rises: solid polymer, vapour dimer, very hot monomer.","traps":["Vapour and high-T forms swapped: Dimer breaks to monomer only at very high temperature (~1200 K)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Lithium and Magnesium do not form superoxide\nStatement II : The ionic radius of Li⁺ is larger than ionic radius of Mg²⁺\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): student thinks Mg²⁺ (higher period) is larger. (A)/(B): student thinks Li forms LiO₂ like K/Rb/Cs.\nWorking: Li forms Li₂O, Mg forms MgO – neither forms a superoxide (diagonal relationship). Li⁺ (76 pm) is larger than Mg²⁺ (72 pm). Both true.\nKey idea: Li⁺ 76 pm > Mg²⁺ 72 pm – higher charge shrinks Mg²⁺ despite extra shell.","traps":["Period number used to judge size: Charge 2+ contracts Mg²⁺ to 72 pm; Li⁺ is 76 pm."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"What is the purpose of adding gypsum to cement?","options":["To facilitate the hydration of cement","To speed up the process of setting","To give a hard mass","To slow down the process of setting"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): opposite of correct role. (A)/(C): general cement properties confused with gypsum's role.\nWorking: gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O) is added to retard setting so the cement gets sufficiently hardened.\nKey idea: Gypsum is a setting retarder.","traps":["Accelerator vs retarder: Gypsum in cement slows setting."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Sodium is about 30 times as abundant as potassium in the oceans. Reason R: Potassium is bigger in size than sodium. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is tempting because ionic size seems unrelated to abundance; (B)/(D) catch students unsure of the 30× figure.\nWorking: Na⁺ is about 30 times as abundant as K⁺ in sea water; the larger K⁺ ion (lower hydration energy) is held more strongly by clay/soil and taken up by plants, so less is leached to the sea. The \"correct explanation\" link is a weak one but is the accepted answer.\nKey idea: Bigger K⁺ is adsorbed more by soils and used by plants, so the sea is enriched in Na⁺.","traps":["Size seen as unrelated to abundance: Larger K⁺ is retained by clay minerals/plants; Na⁺ is leached to the sea."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"For a good quality cement, the ratio of lime to the total of the oxides of Si, Al and Fe should be as close as possible to:","options":["3","2","1","4"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) confuse with the silica:alumina ratio (2.5–4); (C) a guess.\nWorking: old NCERT states that for good quality cement the ratio of CaO to total SiO₂ + Al₂O₃ + Fe₂O₃ should be as close as possible to 2.\nKey idea: Two ratios in NCERT: SiO₂/Al₂O₃ = 2.5–4, CaO/(SiO₂+Al₂O₃+Fe₂O₃) ≈ 2.","traps":["Two NCERT ratios mixed up: Lime ratio ≈ 2; silica/alumina 2.5–4."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Be(OH)₂ reacts with Sr(OH)₂ to yield an ionic salt.\nChoose the incorrect option related to this reaction from the following:","options":["Be is tetrahedrally coordinated in the ionic salt.","The element Be is present in the cationic part of the ionic salt.","Both Sr and Be elements are present in the ionic salt.","The reaction is an example of acid-base neutralization reaction."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): tetrahedral coordination of Be may be doubted. (D): a reaction between two hydroxides does not look like neutralisation – but Be(OH)₂ is acting as an acid. (C): obviously true.\nWorking: amphoteric Be(OH)₂ acts as an acid towards the strong base Sr(OH)₂: Be(OH)₂ + Sr(OH)₂ → Sr[Be(OH)₄] (Sr²⁺ + tetrahedral [Be(OH)₄]²⁻). Be is in the anion, so (B) is incorrect; (A), (C), (D) are correct.\nKey idea: Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric: with a strong base it forms [Be(OH)₄]²⁻.","traps":["Two bases cannot neutralise: Amphoteric Be(OH)₂ behaves as acid towards stronger base.","Metal assumed to be the cation: Be forms the complex anion [Be(OH)₄]²⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I: A. Slaked lime; B. Dead burnt plaster; C. Caustic soda; D. Washing soda\nList II: I. NaOH; II. Ca(OH)₂; III. Na₂CO₃·10H₂O; IV. CaSO₄\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) - III, (B) - IV, (C) - II, (D) - I","(A) - III, (B) - II, (C) - IV, (D) - I","(A) - II, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D) - III","(A) - I, (B) - IV, (C) - II, (D) - III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) assign washing soda formula to slaked lime. (D) swaps slaked lime and caustic soda.\nWorking: slaked lime = Ca(OH)₂ (II); dead burnt plaster = anhydrous CaSO₄ (IV); caustic soda = NaOH (I); washing soda = Na₂CO₃·10H₂O (III) → option (C).\nKey idea: Dead burnt plaster is anhydrous CaSO₄, not the hemihydrate.","traps":["Dead burnt plaster vs plaster of Paris: Dead burnt = fully dehydrated CaSO₄."],"topics":["Calcium compounds","Sodium compounds"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Choose the correct statement(s) :\nA. Beryllium oxide is purely acidic in nature.\nB. Beryllium carbonate is kept in the atmosphere of CO₂.\nC. Beryllium sulphate is readily soluble in water.\nD. Beryllium shows anomalous behaviour.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A only","B, C and D only","A, B and C only","A and B only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options with A are wrong because BeO is amphoteric. (A), (C), (D) all include A.\nWorking: BeO is amphoteric, not purely acidic (A false); BeCO₃ is unstable and is kept in an atmosphere of CO₂ (B true); BeSO₄ is readily soluble because of the high hydration enthalpy of Be²⁺ (C true); Be shows anomalous behaviour because of its small size (D true) → B, C and D only.\nKey idea: BeO is amphoteric; the other three statements are old NCERT facts.","traps":["Amphoteric changed to acidic: Recall BeO, Al₂O₃ amphoteric."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A) : Gypsum is used for making fireproof wall boards. Reason (R) : Gypsum is unstable at high temperatures. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is tempting because dehydration does absorb heat; (C) if 'unstable' is thought false; (D) if fireproof use is unknown.\nWorking: both statements are true, and the official answer is (A) (the fire resistance comes from gypsum's water of crystallisation absorbing heat as it is lost, which some would argue is the same as R, so the link is debatable)\nKey idea: Both facts hold; the official key treats the instability as not explaining the fireproof use.","traps":["Dehydration taken as the explanation: Follow the key's reading: R is a general property, not the stated reason for use."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I: A. Chlorophyll; B. Soda ash; C. Dentistry, Ornamental work; D. Used in white washing\nList-II: I. Na₂CO₃; II. CaSO₄; III. Mg²⁺; IV. Ca(OH)₂\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) give CaSO₄ to chlorophyll. (D) swaps CaSO₄ and Ca(OH)₂ uses and gives soda ash Ca(OH)₂.\nWorking: chlorophyll contains Mg²⁺; soda ash is Na₂CO₃; plaster of Paris (CaSO₄·½H₂O) is used in dentistry and ornamental work; slaked lime Ca(OH)₂ for white washing.\nKey idea: Mg in chlorophyll is the anchor pair; the rest follow.","traps":["Plaster vs slaked lime uses: Plaster of Paris (CaSO₄) for casts/ornaments; lime for white wash."],"topics":["Calcium compounds","Sodium compounds"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Reaction of BeO with ammonia and hydrogen fluoride gives A which on thermal decomposition gives BeF₂ and NH₄F. What is 'A'?","options":["(NH₄)Be₂F₅","(NH₄)₂BeF₄","(NH₄)BeF₃","H₃NBeF₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) would give BeF₂ + NH₄F in other ratios but do not reflect Be's coordination number 4. (D) neutral adduct cannot give NH₄F.\nWorking: BeO + 2NH₃ + 4HF → (NH₄)₂BeF₄ + H₂O; (NH₄)₂BeF₄ → BeF₂ + 2NH₄F on heating.\nKey idea: Be reaches coordination number 4: [BeF₄]²⁻ needs two NH₄⁺.","traps":["Coordination number of Be: Be²⁺ is tetrahedral, maximum covalency 4 → BeF₄²⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A : Beryllium has less negative value of reduction potential compared to the other alkaline earth metals. Reason R : Beryllium has large hydration energy due to small size of Be²⁺ but relatively large value of atomization enthalpy. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): thinks large hydration energy would make E° more negative, so doubts the link. (C)/(D): partial recall.\nWorking: Be has the least negative E° in group 2 (A true). The large hydration enthalpy of small Be²⁺ is outweighed by its large atomisation enthalpy, which makes E° less negative; NCERT gives exactly this as the reason, so R explains A → option (B).\nKey idea: High atomisation enthalpy outweighs high hydration energy for Be.","traps":["Hydration energy direction: NCERT cites both factors together as the cause."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Identify the correct statements about alkali metals. A. The order of standard reduction potential (M⁺ | M) for alkali metal ions is Na > Rb > Li. B. CsI is highly soluble in water. C. Lithium carbonate is highly stable to heat. D. Potassium dissolved in concentrated liquid ammonia is blue in colour and paramagnetic. E. All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["C and E only","A and E only","A, B, D only","A, B and E only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): believes D (dilute-solution property) and B. (D): believes CsI soluble. (A): believes Li₂CO₃ stable.\nWorking: E°: Na −2.71 > Rb −2.93 > Li −3.04 V (A true); CsI poorly soluble (B false); Li₂CO₃ decomposes on heating (C false); concentrated solution is bronze and diamagnetic (D false); hydrides are ionic (E true).\nKey idea: Li has the most negative E°; large-cation–large-anion salts like CsI have low solubility.","traps":["Concentrated NH₃ solution: Concentrated: bronze, diamagnetic.","Li anomaly: Li resembles Mg: carbonate decomposes."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Compound A reacts with NH₄Cl and forms a compound B. Compound B reacts with H₂O and excess of CO₂ to form compound C which on passing through or reaction with saturated NaCl solution forms sodium hydrogen carbonate. Compound A, B and C, are respectively :","options":["Ca(OH)₂, NH₄⁺, (NH₄)₂CO₃","CaCl₂, NH₄⁺, (NH₄)₂CO₃","CaCl₂, NH₃, NH₄HCO₃","Ca(OH)₂, NH₃, NH₄HCO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) stops at (NH₄)₂CO₃ (ignores 'excess CO₂') and gives NH₄⁺ as B. (B)/(C) use CaCl₂, which is the by-product, not the reagent that liberates NH₃.\nWorking: Ca(OH)₂ + 2NH₄Cl → CaCl₂ + 2NH₃ + 2H₂O (A = Ca(OH)₂, B = NH₃); NH₃ + H₂O + CO₂ (excess) → NH₄HCO₃ (C); NH₄HCO₃ + NaCl → NaHCO₃ + NH₄Cl; so option (D).\nKey idea: A base (slaked lime) frees NH₃ from NH₄Cl; excess CO₂ gives the hydrogen carbonate.","traps":["Excess CO₂ ignored: 'Excess CO₂' converts carbonate to hydrogen carbonate.","By-product CaCl₂ taken as reagent: Only a base can liberate NH₃ from NH₄Cl."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Assertion A: The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to reducing flame. Reason R: Alkali metals can be detected using flame tests. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["A is not correct but R is correct","A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) miss the changed flame type; (B) rejects the flame-test statement.\nWorking: alkali metals and their salts give their characteristic colour to the oxidising (non-luminous) flame, not the reducing flame, so A is not correct; R is correct (alkali metals are detected by flame tests) → option (A).\nKey idea: Flame colours are observed in the oxidising (non-luminous) flame.","traps":["Oxidising vs reducing flame: Remember: alkali flame colours – oxidising flame."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"The correct order of hydration enthalpies is\n(A) K⁺\n(B) Rb⁺\n(C) Mg²⁺\n(D) Cs⁺\n(E) Ca²⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["E > C > A > B > D","C > A > E > B > D","C > E > A > D > B","C > E > A > B > D"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Ca²⁺ above Mg²⁺; (B) puts K⁺ above Ca²⁺ (ignores charge); (C) swaps Rb⁺ and Cs⁺.\nWorking: doubly charged ions are hydrated much more strongly than singly charged ones, and for the same charge the smaller ion is hydrated more: Mg²⁺ > Ca²⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺, i.e. C > E > A > B > D → option (D).\nKey idea: Higher charge first, then smaller size.","traps":["Charge outweighs size: Compare charge first."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"On heating, LiNO₃ gives how many compounds among the following _________\nLi₂O, N₂, O₂, LiNO₂, NO₂","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (LiNO₂ + O₂, as for NaNO₃); 4 (adding LiNO₂ or N₂).\nWorking: 4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂, so from the list Li₂O, NO₂ and O₂ are formed = 3.\nKey idea: Diagonal relationship: Li behaves like Mg.","traps":["Li nitrate differs from Na nitrate: Li (like Mg) gives oxide + NO₂ + O₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"The alkaline earth metal sulphate(s) which are readily soluble in water is/are: A. BeSO₄ B. MgSO₄ C. CaSO₄ D. SrSO₄ E. BaSO₄. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and B","B and C","B only","A only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (B) includes CaSO₄, which is only sparingly soluble; option (C) drops BeSO₄; option (D) drops MgSO₄ (Epsom salt).\nWorking: the high hydration enthalpies of the small Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions outweigh their lattice enthalpies, so BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ are readily soluble; CaSO₄, SrSO₄ and BaSO₄ are only sparingly soluble → A and B, option (A).\nKey idea: Hydration enthalpy of Be²⁺/Mg²⁺ outweighs lattice enthalpy; heavier sulphates are insoluble.","traps":["Sparingly soluble CaSO₄: 'Readily soluble' covers only BeSO₄ and MgSO₄."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Chlorides of which metal are soluble in organic solvents :","options":["K","Be","Ca","Mg"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Mg: next most polarising, partially covalent; (A)/(C): ionic chlorides.\nWorking: the very small Be²⁺ ion has the highest polarising power, so BeCl₂ is largely covalent and dissolves in organic solvents; KCl and CaCl₂ are ionic, and MgCl₂ is much less covalent → option (B).\nKey idea: Smallest cation → greatest polarising power → covalent chloride → organic-solvent soluble.","traps":["Mg vs Be: Be²⁺ is uniquely small; its halides are covalent."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Which of the following reaction is correct?","options":["4LiNO₃ —Δ→ 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂","2LiNO₃ → 2Li + 2NO₂ + O₂","4LiNO₃ —Δ→ 2Li₂O + 2N₂O₄ + O₂","2LiNO₃ —Δ→ 2LiNO₂ + O₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): general alkali-metal nitrate → nitrite pattern; (C): N₂O₄ instead of NO₂ at high T; (B): metal formed – wrong.\nWorking: like Mg(NO₃)₂, lithium nitrate decomposes to the oxide on heating: 4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂, which is option (A). Other alkali-metal nitrates give the nitrite and O₂.\nKey idea: Li behaves like Mg (diagonal relationship): nitrate → oxide + NO₂ + O₂.","traps":["Li nitrate unlike NaNO₃: Remember Li mimics Mg: gives oxide.","NO₂ vs N₂O₄: At decomposition temperature NO₂ is the product."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"The element playing significant role in neuromuscular function and interneuronal transmission is:","options":["Be","Ca","Mg","Li"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Mg is for enzymes using ATP and chlorophyll. (A) Be is toxic, no role. (D) Li is used in mental illness treatment, not this function.\nWorking: Ca²⁺ ions are needed for neuromuscular function, interneuronal transmission, cell-membrane integrity and blood coagulation → option (B).\nKey idea: Ca²⁺ — neuromuscular function, nerve transmission, blood clotting.","traps":["Mg vs Ca roles: Mg: enzymes/chlorophyll; Ca: nerves, muscles, clotting."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"The number of alkali metal(s), from Li, K, Cs, Rb having ionization enthalpy greater than 400 kJ mol⁻¹ and forming stable super oxide is ________.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counting Li for IE or Cs for superoxide), 1 (excluding Rb thinking its IE < 400).\nWorking: Li 520, K 419, Rb 403, Cs 376 kJ mol⁻¹; superoxides: K, Rb, Cs; both conditions → K, Rb = 2.\nKey idea: Only K and Rb satisfy both IE > 400 and superoxide formation.","traps":["Rb IE near cut-off: Memorise K 419, Rb 403, Cs 376.","Li meets IE but not superoxide: Li forms only Li₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Choose the correct order of density of the alkali metals:","options":["Li < K < Na < Rb < Cs","Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs","Cs < Rb < K < Na < Li","Li < Na < K < Cs < Rb"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the simple increasing trend ignoring the anomaly; (C) the reverse trend; (D) swaps Cs and Rb.\nWorking: densities (g cm⁻³) Li 0.53, K 0.86, Na 0.97, Rb 1.53, Cs 1.90.\nKey idea: K has a larger atomic volume increase than mass increase, so it is less dense than Na.","traps":["Na/K density anomaly: Remember K (0.86) < Na (0.97)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of density is","options":["Be > Mg > Ca > Sr","Sr > Ca > Mg > Be","Sr > Be > Mg > Ca","Be > Sr > Mg > Ca"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes density rises steadily down the group; (A) assumes it falls; (D) puts Be above Sr.\nWorking: densities (g cm⁻³) Be 1.84, Mg 1.74, Ca 1.55, Sr 2.63 → Sr > Be > Mg > Ca.\nKey idea: Density falls Be → Ca, then rises sharply to Sr.","traps":["Monotonic trend assumed: Volume grows faster than mass from Be to Ca; Ca is the minimum."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Reaction of BeCl₂ with LiAlH₄ gives:\n(A) AlCl₃ (B) BeH₂ (C) LiH (D) LiCl (E) BeAlH₄\nChoose the correct answer from options given below:","options":["(A), (D) and (E)","(A), (B) and (D)","(D) and (E)","(B), (C) and (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) include the non-existent BeAlH₄ (mixing the reactant formulae); (D) includes LiH instead of AlCl₃ (wrong chloride/hydride partner).\nWorking: 2BeCl₂ + LiAlH₄ → 2BeH₂ + LiCl + AlCl₃, so AlCl₃, BeH₂ and LiCl form.\nKey idea: H⁻ goes to Be, Cl⁻ goes to Li and Al: BeH₂ + LiCl + AlCl₃.","traps":["Invented mixed hydride BeAlH₄: Balance atoms: all four H go to two Be.","LiH instead of LiCl/AlCl₃: Chloride must leave Be; it goes to the most electropositive partners."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : LiF is sparingly soluble in water.\nReason R : The ionic radius of Li⁺ ion is smallest among its group members, hence has least hydration enthalpy.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below .","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) for students who accept R because its first half (smallest radius) is true; (D) for those who think all alkali fluorides are soluble.\nWorking: LiF is sparingly soluble due to its high lattice enthalpy; Li⁺ being smallest has the HIGHEST hydration enthalpy, so R is false.\nKey idea: Smallest ion → highest charge density → largest hydration enthalpy.","traps":["Half-true reason: Check every clause of R separately.","Small ion, high hydration: Smaller ion binds water more strongly."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 1 trends"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : The chlorides of Be and Al have Cl-bridged structure. Both are soluble in organic solvents and act as Lewis bases.\nStatement II: Hydroxides of Be and Al dissolve in excess alkali to give beryllate and aluminate ions.\nIn the light of the above statements. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student reads the familiar list and misses \"bases\"; (C)/(B): student doubts amphoteric nature of Be(OH)₂.\nWorking: BeCl₂ (polymeric) and Al₂Cl₆ are Cl-bridged and soluble in organic solvents, but they are Lewis ACIDS (electron-deficient), so I is false; Be(OH)₂ → [Be(OH)₄]²⁻ and Al(OH)₃ → [Al(OH)₄]⁻ in excess alkali, so II is true.\nKey idea: Electron-deficient metal chlorides accept electron pairs → Lewis acids (AlCl₃ in Friedel–Crafts).","traps":["Lewis acid swapped to base: Check each clause; electron-deficient = acceptor = Lewis acid.","Be(OH)₂ assumed basic: Be is the amphoteric exception, like Al."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"An element A of group 1 shows similarity to an element B belonging to group 2. If A has maximum hydration enthalpy in group 1 then B is:","options":["Mg","Be","Ca","Sr"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Be is the group-2 head element (it resembles Al, not Li); (C)/(D) chosen by vertical-group thinking.\nWorking: the group-1 element with the highest hydration enthalpy is Li (smallest ion); Li shows a diagonal relationship with Mg.\nKey idea: Smallest alkali ion (Li⁺) has the highest hydration enthalpy; Li is diagonal to Mg.","traps":["Be chosen as \"first\" of group 2: Diagonal relationship links Li–Mg, Be–Al."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which of the following statement is incorrect?","options":["Low solubility of LiF in water is due to its small hydration enthalpy.","KO₂ is paramagnetic.","Solution of sodium in liquid ammonia is conducting in nature.","Sodium metal has higher density than potassium metal"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) looks false to students expecting density to rise down the group (K is the exception); (B) may be doubted if KO₂ is mistaken for a peroxide.\nWorking: LiF is poorly soluble because of its very high lattice enthalpy (small Li⁺ and F⁻); Li⁺ actually has the largest hydration enthalpy. KO₂ (O₂⁻, one unpaired electron) is paramagnetic; Na in liquid NH₃ conducts (ammoniated electrons and ions); Na (0.97) is denser than K (0.86 g cm⁻³).\nKey idea: Li⁺ has the highest hydration enthalpy of the group; LiF is insoluble because its lattice enthalpy is even larger.","traps":["Hydration vs lattice reason: Small ions → high lattice AND high hydration; LiF lattice wins.","K lighter than Na: Remember the K anomaly (larger atomic volume)."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Statement I: An alloy of lithium and magnesium is used to make aircraft plates. Statement II: The magnesium ions are important for cell-membrane integrity. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts both altered lines; (C) misses the armour/aircraft swap; (D) misses the Mg/Ca swap.\nWorking: by the old NCERT s-block text, lithium alloys with magnesium make armour plates (aircraft parts come from Li–Al alloys), so Statement I is false as worded; NCERT credits calcium (not magnesium) with cell-membrane integrity, so Statement II is false. (Li–Mg alloys do find aerospace use in reality, so the item depends on the NCERT wording.)\nKey idea: Li–Mg: armour plates; Li–Al: aircraft parts. Ca²⁺: membrane integrity and coagulation.","traps":["Armour plates vs aircraft parts: Learn: Li–Pb bearings, Li–Al aircraft, Li–Mg armour.","Mg²⁺ vs Ca²⁺ biological role: Ca²⁺: membrane integrity, coagulation, muscle; Mg²⁺: ATP enzymes, chlorophyll."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Lithium nitrate and sodium nitrate, when heated separately, respectively, give :","options":["LiNO₂ and NaNO₂","Li₂O and Na₂O","Li₂O and NaNO₂","LiNO₂ and Na₂O"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats Li like Na; (B) treats Na like Li; (D) reverses both.\nWorking: 4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂ (like Mg(NO₃)₂, anomalous Li); 2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂.\nKey idea: Li behaves like Mg (diagonal relationship): nitrate → oxide.","traps":["Lithium treated as typical alkali metal: Li is anomalous: nitrate → oxide + NO₂."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Sodium compounds"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Portland cement contains 'X' to enhance the setting time. What is 'X'?","options":["CaSO₄·½H₂O","CaSO₄·2H₂O","CaSO₄","CaCO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) plaster of Paris (sets fast – opposite role); (C) dead-burnt plaster; (D) limestone (raw material, not additive).\nWorking: gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O, about 2–3%) is added to cement clinker to slow down the setting, i.e. to increase the setting time.\nKey idea: Gypsum, the dihydrate, retards setting.","traps":["Gypsum vs plaster of Paris: Gypsum = dihydrate; PoP = hemihydrate.","\"Enhance setting time\" misread: More setting time = slower setting."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which one of the following compounds is used as a chemical in certain type of fire extinguishers?","options":["Baking Soda","Soda ash","Washing Soda","Caustic Soda"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Soda ash – anhydrous Na₂CO₃ (glass, Solvay); (C) Washing soda – Na₂CO₃·10H₂O; (D) Caustic soda – NaOH, corrosive.\nWorking: NaHCO₃ (baking soda) releases CO₂ on heating/with acid and is used in dry-chemical and soda-acid extinguishers.\nKey idea: Baking soda = NaHCO₃, gives CO₂ which smothers fire.","traps":["Soda names confused: Baking soda NaHCO₃; soda ash Na₂CO₃; washing soda Na₂CO₃·10H₂O; caustic soda NaOH."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which one of the following alkaline earth metal ions has the highest ionic mobility in its aqueous solution?","options":["Be²⁺","Mg²⁺","Ca²⁺","Sr²⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Be²⁺ – smallest bare ion taken as fastest; (B)/(C) – partial reasoning.\nWorking: hydration enthalpy falls down the group as ionic size grows, so Sr²⁺ carries the smallest hydration shell, has the smallest hydrated radius and moves fastest; Be²⁺ is most heavily hydrated and slowest.\nKey idea: Smallest bare ion → most hydrated → slowest; Sr²⁺ is the least hydrated of the four.","traps":["Bare size used instead of hydrated size: Mobility in water follows hydrated radius."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of melting point is :","options":["Be > Mg > Ca > Sr","Sr > Ca > Mg > Be","Be > Ca > Mg > Sr","Be > Ca > Sr > Mg"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes smooth decrease down the group; (B) assumes increase; (C) puts Mg above Sr.\nWorking: melting points (K) Be 1560, Ca ≈1124, Sr ≈1062, Mg 924 (Mg is anomalously low), so Be > Ca > Sr > Mg = (D).\nKey idea: Group 2 melting points do not fall smoothly – Mg has the lowest, below Ca and Sr.","traps":["Smooth trend assumed: Remember Mg (924 K) is the low outlier.","Mg placed above Sr: Mg is below Sr too."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"s-block element which cannot be qualitatively confirmed by the flame test is","options":["Li","Na","Rb","Be"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Li – distinctive crimson red; (B) Na – golden yellow; (C) Rb – red-violet (less common, may tempt).\nWorking: Be (and Mg) atoms hold electrons too tightly (small size, high IE); flame energy cannot excite them, so no flame colour. Li crimson, Na yellow, Rb red-violet.\nKey idea: Small, high-IE Be and Mg are not excited by flame energy.","traps":["Rb assumed colourless: All alkali metals colour the flame; among the options only Be fails."],"topics":["Group 2 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"BeCl₂ reacts with LiAlH₄ to give","options":["Be + Li[AlCl₄] + H₂","Be + AlH₃ + LiCl + HCl","BeH₂ + LiCl + AlCl₃","BeH₂ + Li[AlCl₄]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – free Be metal assumes LiAlH₄ reduces Be²⁺ to metal; (D) – combines LiCl + AlCl₃ into Li[AlCl₄], chemically arguable but not the NCERT equation.\nWorking: 2BeCl₂ + LiAlH₄ → 2BeH₂ + LiCl + AlCl₃ (the old NCERT equation); hydride transfer gives BeH₂. (D) is chemically similar (LiCl + AlCl₃ combine to Li[AlCl₄]), but the NCERT form is (C).\nKey idea: LiAlH₄ swaps H⁻ for Cl⁻: Be–Cl becomes Be–H; Be is not reduced to metal.","traps":["LiAlH₄ as reducing to metal: With metal halides it acts as a hydride donor.","LiCl + AlCl₃ vs Li[AlCl₄]: Go with the textbook equation unless the stem asks for complex formation."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"BeO reacts with HF in presence of ammonia to give [A] which on thermal decomposition produces [B] and ammonium fluoride. Oxidation state of Be in [A] is _______","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (counting four F as charges on Be, i.e. coordination number) or −2 (taking the complex-ion charge).\nWorking: BeO + 2NH₃ + 4HF → (NH₄)₂[BeF₄] + H₂O; (NH₄)₂[BeF₄] → BeF₂ + 2NH₄F on heating. In [BeF₄]²⁻, Be is +2.\nKey idea: Be is always +2; only its coordination number (4) changes in [BeF₄]²⁻.","traps":["Coordination number taken as oxidation state: x + 4(−1) = −2 gives x = +2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match the List-I with List-II. List-I (Metal): (A) Li (B) Na (C) Rb (D) Cs. List-II (Emitted light wavelength (nm)): (I) 670.8 (II) 589.2 (III) 780.0 (IV) 455.5. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) swap Li and Rb (both red, so wavelengths close in memory); (D) puts Cs at 670.8.\nWorking: Li crimson red 670.8 nm, Na yellow 589.2 nm, Rb red-violet 780.0 nm, Cs blue 455.5 nm (old NCERT s-block table).\nKey idea: Na 589 nm is the anchor; Cs is the only blue (short λ); Rb has the longest λ.","traps":["Li vs Rb red flames: Rb 780 nm (near IR) > Li 670.8 nm."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Amongst baking soda, caustic soda and washing soda carbonate anion is present in:","options":["washing soda only.","washing soda and caustic soda only.","washing soda and baking soda only.","baking soda, caustic soda and washing soda."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) counts HCO₃⁻ as carbonate; (B)/(D) mistake caustic soda for a carbonate.\nWorking: baking soda NaHCO₃ (hydrogencarbonate, not carbonate), caustic soda NaOH, washing soda Na₂CO₃·10H₂O; only washing soda has CO₃²⁻.\nKey idea: Baking soda is hydrogencarbonate, a different anion from carbonate.","traps":["Hydrogencarbonate counted as carbonate: HCO₃⁻ ≠ CO₃²⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"What are the products formed in sequence when excess of CO₂ is passed in slaked lime?","options":["Ca(HCO₃)₂, CaCO₃","CaCO₃, Ca(HCO₃)₂","CaO, Ca(HCO₃)₂","CaO, CaCO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reversed order; (C)/(D) CaO is not a product of this reaction.\nWorking: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O (milkiness); CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca(HCO₃)₂ (soluble, milkiness disappears).\nKey idea: Milky (CaCO₃) then clear (Ca(HCO₃)₂).","traps":["Order of products: CO₂ first precipitates CaCO₃ (milky); excess CO₂ dissolves it as Ca(HCO₃)₂.","CaO is not formed: CaO is made by heating CaCO₃, not by CO₂ on Ca(OH)₂."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Barium carbonate is insoluble in water and is highly stable.\nReason (R): The thermal stability of the carbonates increases with increasing cationic size.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is the true explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is not the true explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) for students who feel R explains only stability, not insolubility; students who pick by option letter from habit, without reading the option text, are misled; (B)/(D) for those reversing the stability trend.\nWorking: BaCO₃ is sparingly soluble and the most thermally stable Group 2 carbonate; stability rises with cation size (less polarisation of CO₃²⁻), which explains the 'highly stable' part (it does not explain insolubility, but the official answer takes R as the explanation). Note: read each option's text, not just its letter – here option (A) says R is the correct explanation.\nKey idea: Larger cation → less polarisation of carbonate → higher decomposition temperature.","traps":["Option order differs from the usual template: Read each option text fully.","R explains only half of A: Judge the link the examiner intends: stability ← cation size."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The number of water molecules in gypsum, dead burnt plaster and plaster of paris, respectively are:","options":["2, 0 and 1","0.5, 0 and 2","5, 0 and 0.5","2, 0 and 0.5"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives POP one water; (B) swaps gypsum and POP; (C) uses 5 (CuSO₄·5H₂O confusion).\nWorking: gypsum CaSO₄·2H₂O; dead burnt plaster anhydrous CaSO₄; plaster of Paris CaSO₄·½H₂O.\nKey idea: Gypsum 2 → POP ½ → dead burnt 0.","traps":["Order of the three names: Map each name in the order given.","Blue vitriol pentahydrate recalled: Gypsum is a dihydrate."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Choose the correct statement from the following:","options":["The standard enthalpy of formation for alkali metal bromides becomes less negative on descending the group.","The low solubility of CsI in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy.","Among the alkali metal halides, LiF is least soluble in water.","LiF has least negative standard enthalpy of formation among alkali metal fluorides."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) applies the fluoride trend to bromides; (B) gives LiF's reason (lattice enthalpy) for CsI; (D) reverses the fluoride trend – LiF is the most negative.\nWorking: ΔfH° of fluorides becomes less negative down the group, but for chlorides, bromides and iodides it becomes more negative, so (A) is false; CsI is poorly soluble because both large ions have small hydration enthalpies, not because of high lattice enthalpy, so (B) is false; LiF is the least soluble alkali halide (very high lattice enthalpy), so (C) is true; LiF has the MOST negative ΔfH° among the fluorides, so (D) is false.\nKey idea: Small–small (LiF) is insoluble because of lattice enthalpy; large–large (CsI) is insoluble because of weak hydration.","traps":["Fluoride trend applied to bromides: Fluorides: less negative down; Cl/Br/I: more negative down.","Lattice vs hydration as the reason: LiF – lattice enthalpy; CsI – small hydration enthalpy."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The major component/ingredient of Portland Cement is:","options":["tricalcium aluminate","tricalcium silicate","dicalcium aluminate","dicalcium silicate"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tricalcium aluminate is present but only ~11%; (C) dicalcium aluminate is not a cement phase; (D) dicalcium silicate is second (~26%).\nWorking: Portland cement is about 51% tricalcium silicate (Ca₃SiO₅), 26% dicalcium silicate and 11% tricalcium aluminate; the major component is tricalcium silicate (B).\nKey idea: Remember \"tri-silicate 51%\".","traps":["Di- vs tri-calcium silicate: Tricalcium silicate ~51% > dicalcium ~26%."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): Lithium salts are hydrated. Reason (R): Lithium has higher polarising power than other alkali metal group members. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) links the two true facts causally (both come from small Li⁺ size); (B)/(C) deny a true statement.\nWorking: both statements are true (e.g. LiCl·2H₂O; Li⁺ has the highest polarising power). The hydration of Li salts is attributed to the very high hydration enthalpy of the small Li⁺ ion, whereas polarising power explains covalent character, so R is not the explanation. (Both facts do stem from the small size of Li⁺, which is why (D) is tempting, but R does not directly cause A.)\nKey idea: Hydration ↔ hydration enthalpy; polarising power ↔ covalent character.","traps":["Shared root cause read as explanation: Ask whether R directly produces A; polarisation explains covalency, not hydration."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Number of amphoteric compound among the following is ______: (A) BeO (B) BaO (C) Be(OH)₂ (D) Sr(OH)₂","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (only BeO counted); 0 or 4 (all treated alike).\nWorking: BeO and Be(OH)₂ are amphoteric (Be's small size and high charge density, diagonal similarity with Al); BaO and Sr(OH)₂ are basic.\nKey idea: Be compounds are amphoteric; heavier group 2 oxides/hydroxides are basic.","traps":["Hydroxide forgotten: Be(OH)₂ also dissolves in acid and alkali."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Salt): (a) LiCl; (b) NaCl; (c) RbCl; (d) CsCl. List-II (Flame colour wavelength): (i) 455.5 nm; (ii) 670.8 nm; (iii) 780.0 nm; (iv) 589.2 nm. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)–(iv), (b)–(ii), (c)–(iii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(i), (c)–(iv), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(i), (b)–(iv), (c)–(ii), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(iii), (d)–(i)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Li and Na lines; (B) and (C) misplace Rb and Cs (both near the ends of the spectrum).\nWorking: Li crimson 670.8 nm, Na yellow 589.2 nm, Rb red-violet 780.0 nm, Cs blue 455.5 nm.\nKey idea: Na D-line 589 nm is the anchor; Li red 670.8; Cs blue 455.5.","traps":["Rb vs Cs wavelength: Cs = blue (short λ), Rb = red-violet (long λ)."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Among the following, number of metal/s which can be used as electrodes in the photoelectric cell is ______ (Integer answer) (A) Li (B) Na (C) Rb (D) Cs","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (all alkali metals emit electrons); 2 (Rb and Cs, lowest IE); 3.\nWorking: the old NCERT s-block text names caesium (with potassium) as used in photoelectric cells because of its very low ionisation enthalpy; of the listed metals only Cs → 1. (Physically Rb and Na also show photoemission, so the item rests on the textbook statement.)\nKey idea: Only Cs among the options is named for photoelectric cells.","traps":["All alkali metals counted: Answer from the stated use, not from IE alone.","Rb counted with Cs: Only Cs (and K) is named in NCERT."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Find A, B and C in the following reactions: NH₃ + A + CO₂ → (NH₄)₂CO₃; (NH₄)₂CO₃ + H₂O + B → NH₄HCO₃; NH₄HCO₃ + NaCl → NH₄Cl + C","options":["A – O₂ ; B – CO₂ ; C – Na₂CO₃","A – H₂O ; B – O₂ ; C – Na₂CO₃","A – H₂O ; B – O₂ ; C – NaHCO₃","A – H₂O ; B – CO₂ ; C – NaHCO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) O₂ as A – no oxidation occurs; (B)/(A) Na₂CO₃ as C – Na₂CO₃ forms only on heating NaHCO₃ later; (C) O₂ as B – CO₂ is needed to convert carbonate to bicarbonate.\nWorking: 2NH₃ + H₂O + CO₂ → (NH₄)₂CO₃; (NH₄)₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ → 2NH₄HCO₃; NH₄HCO₃ + NaCl → NH₄Cl + NaHCO₃ (sparingly soluble, precipitates).\nKey idea: The step with NaCl gives NaHCO₃ (it precipitates); Na₂CO₃ comes only after calcination.","traps":["Final product written as Na₂CO₃: Track the given step: NH₄HCO₃ + NaCl gives NaHCO₃.","O₂ inserted as reagent: No oxidation-state change in any step."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Sodium Carbonate (b) Titanium (c) Chlorine (d) Sodium hydroxide. List-II: (i) Deacon (ii) Castner-Kellner (iii) Van-Arkel (iv) Solvay.","options":["(a)→(iv), (b)→(iii), (c)→(i), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(i), (b)→(iii), (c)→(iv), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(iv), (b)→(i), (c)→(ii), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(iii), (b)→(ii), (c)→(i), (d)→(iv)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – Deacon given to Ti and Castner–Kellner to Cl₂ (Cl₂ is a by-product of that cell); (B) – Deacon for Na₂CO₃; (D) – Van Arkel for Na₂CO₃.\nWorking: Na₂CO₃ – Solvay; Ti – Van Arkel (TiI₄ decomposition); Cl₂ – Deacon (HCl + O₂ over CuCl₂); NaOH – Castner–Kellner cell.\nKey idea: Castner–Kellner's main product is NaOH even though Cl₂ is also released.","traps":["Castner–Kellner assigned to chlorine: Castner–Kellner is the NaOH process; Deacon is for Cl₂."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"A s-block element (M) reacts with oxygen to form an oxide of the formula MO₂. The oxide is pale yellow in colour and paramagnetic. The element (M) is:","options":["Mg","Na","Ca","K"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) Group 2 peroxides MO₂ have O₂²⁻ (diamagnetic); (B) Na gives Na₂O₂, not NaO₂, under normal conditions.\nWorking: K burns in excess O₂ to give the superoxide KO₂ (K⁺O₂⁻); O₂⁻ has one unpaired electron, so KO₂ is paramagnetic and coloured. Na gives mainly Na₂O₂ (diamagnetic); Mg and Ca give MO (and peroxides MO₂ that are diamagnetic).\nKey idea: Paramagnetic MO₂ means O₂⁻ (superoxide) with M⁺ → K.","traps":["MO₂ read as peroxide: Use the paramagnetic clue: O₂⁻ has an unpaired electron.","Na assumed to form superoxide: Na → peroxide; K, Rb, Cs → superoxide."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Elements): (a) Ba (b) Ca (c) Li (d) Na. List-II (Properties): (i) Organic solvent soluble compounds (ii) Outer electronic configuration 6s² (iii) Oxalate insoluble in water (iv) Formation of very strong monoacidic base. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i) and (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii) and (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv) and (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii) and (d)-(iii)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) gives Li the 6s² configuration – period mix-up; (C) assigns oxalate insolubility to Ba and organic solubility to Na; (D) swaps Ba/Ca.\nWorking: Ba is period 6 ([Xe]6s²); CaC₂O₄ is insoluble (used to detect Ca²⁺); Li compounds (LiCl) are covalent and dissolve in organic solvents; NaOH is a very strong monoacidic base.\nKey idea: Fix the unique pair first: 6s² must be Ba (period 6).","traps":["Li vs Na covalent character: Li (small, polarising) gives covalent, organic-soluble salts.","Ba vs Ca configuration: Ba is in period 6 → 6s²."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Group 2 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: None of the alkaline earth metal hydroxides dissolve in alkali. Statement II: Solubility of alkaline earth metal hydroxides in water increases down the group. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Statement I and Statement II both are incorrect.","Statement I and Statement II both are correct."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) for students who forget Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric; (A)/(C) for those who confuse the hydroxide trend with the sulphate trend (which decreases).\nWorking: Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric and dissolves in NaOH (forming [Be(OH)₄]²⁻), so Statement I is false; solubility of M(OH)₂ increases down the group (lattice enthalpy falls faster than hydration enthalpy), so Statement II is true.\nKey idea: Be is the anomalous Group 2 member: Be(OH)₂ dissolves in both acid and alkali.","traps":["Absolute word \"None\": Test absolute statements against the anomalous first member.","Hydroxide vs sulphate solubility trend: Hydroxides: solubility increases down; sulphates: decreases."],"topics":["Group 2 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List I (Elements) with List II (Properties):\n(a) Li (b) Na (c) K (d) Cs\n(i) Poor water solubility of I⁻ salt\n(ii) Most abundant element in cell fluid\n(iii) Bicarbonate salt used in fire extinguisher\n(iv) Carbonate salt decomposes easily on heating\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps Na and K (cell fluid vs fire extinguisher); (B)/(D) give Li the iodide property – tempting because LiI is unusual in being covalent/soluble in organics.\nWorking: Li₂CO₃ alone among alkali carbonates decomposes easily (Li–small cation); NaHCO₃ is the fire-extinguisher bicarbonate; K⁺ is the main cation inside cells; CsI is the least water-soluble alkali iodide (large cation, large anion, low hydration gain).\nKey idea: Li – anomalous thermal instability of carbonate; K – intracellular cation; Na – baking soda uses; Cs – low solubility of large-large salt.","traps":["Na⁺ vs K⁺ in cells: Remember K⁺ inside, Na⁺ outside cells.","Solubility of large-large salts: Small–large mismatch → soluble; large–large → less soluble."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Sodium compounds"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I: (a) NaOH (b) Be(OH)₂ (c) Ca(OH)₂ (d) B(OH)₃ (e) Al(OH)₃\nList-II: (i) Acidic (ii) Basic (iii) Amphoteric\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii), (e)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i), (e)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i), (e)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii), (e)-(iii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) call Be(OH)₂ basic and Ca(OH)₂ amphoteric (one pair shifted); (A) also calls B(OH)₃ basic; (D) makes Be(OH)₂ acidic and B(OH)₃ amphoteric.\nWorking: NaOH basic; Be(OH)₂ amphoteric (diagonal relation with Al); Ca(OH)₂ basic; B(OH)₃ acidic (Lewis acid); Al(OH)₃ amphoteric. Option (B).\nKey idea: Small, highly polarising Be²⁺ and B³⁺ make their hydroxides amphoteric/acidic.","traps":["Be(OH)₂ is amphoteric: Recall Be–Al diagonal relationship.","\"Hydroxide\" ≠ base: B(OH)₃ = H₃BO₃, a Lewis acid."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Lithium halides are some what covalent in nature.\nReason R: Lithium possess high polarisation capability.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) is the usual trap when both statements are true; (A)/(B) for those who think LiX are purely ionic or misread \"polarisation capability\".\nWorking: the very small Li⁺ ion has high polarising power, distorting the halide electron cloud and giving partial covalent character (Fajans' rule). Both true and R explains A, option (D).\nKey idea: Small cation, high charge density → polarises anion → covalent character (Fajans).","traps":["R does explain A here: Ask: does R cause A? Fajans says yes.","Polarising power vs polarisability: Small cations polarise; large anions get polarised."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I: (a) Li (b) Na (c) K (d) Cs\nList-II: (i) photoelectric cell (ii) absorbent of CO₂ (iii) coolant in fast breeder nuclear reactor (iv) treatment of cancer (v) bearings for motor engines\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(v), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(v), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(v), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives Na photoelectric cells and Cs cancer treatment; (B) gives K cancer treatment; (C) shifts every pair; (iv) lures those who recall radioactive Cs-137.\nWorking: Li–Pb alloy (white metal) for motor-engine bearings; liquid Na as fast-breeder coolant; KOH absorbs CO₂; Cs in photoelectric cells. (iv) is the spare entry.\nKey idea: Cs has lowest IE → photoelectric cells; Na high heat conductivity → reactor coolant.","traps":["Spare List-II entry: Use elimination with the surest pairs (Na coolant, Cs photocell).","K vs Na compound uses: NCERT names KOH as the CO₂ absorbent."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Both CaCl₂·6H₂O and MgCl₂·8H₂O undergo dehydration on heating. Statement II: BeO is amphoteric whereas the oxides of other elements in the same group are acidic. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are false","Both statement I and statement II are true","Statement I is true but statement II is false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – accepts I because CaCl₂·6H₂O does dehydrate; (C) – misses both alterations; (A) – remembers BeO amphoteric and misses 'acidic'.\nWorking: hydrated Ca halides lose water on heating, but MgCl₂·8H₂O hydrolyses (gives MgO/Mg(OH)Cl + HCl), so I is false; BeO is amphoteric but MgO–BaO are basic, not acidic, so II is false.\nKey idea: Mg and Be hydrated halides hydrolyse on heating; Group 2 oxides other than BeO are basic.","traps":["MgCl₂·8H₂O hydrolyses, not dehydrates: Recall Be and Mg halide hydrates give HCl + oxide/oxychloride.","Basic written as acidic: Read the second clause: MgO–BaO are basic."],"topics":["Group 2 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct order of conductivity of ions in water is:","options":["Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺","Cs⁺ > Rb⁺ > K⁺ > Na⁺","K⁺ > Na⁺ > Cs⁺ > Rb⁺","Rb⁺ > Na⁺ > K⁺ > Li⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – bare ionic radius used (smaller = faster); (C), (D) – jumbled orders.\nWorking: smaller gaseous ions are more heavily hydrated, so hydrated size decreases Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺ and ionic mobility/conductance increases Na⁺ < K⁺ < Rb⁺ < Cs⁺ (NCERT λ° values Na⁺ 50.1, K⁺ 73.5).\nKey idea: Conductance depends on hydrated radius: the largest bare ion (Cs⁺) is least hydrated and most mobile.","traps":["Bare size used: Use hydrated size."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"One of the by-products formed during the recovery of NH₃ from Solvay process is :","options":["Ca(OH)₂","NaHCO₃","CaCl₂","NH₄Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ca(OH)₂ and (D) NH₄Cl are the reactants of the recovery step; (B) NaHCO₃ is the main intermediate of the process.\nWorking: ammonia is recovered by 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → 2NH₃ + CaCl₂ + 2H₂O; CaCl₂ is the by-product.\nKey idea: NH₄Cl + lime → NH₃ (recycled) + CaCl₂ (waste).","traps":["Reactant picked as by-product: Write the recovery equation and pick what is discarded."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Ca(OCl)₂ (b) CaSO₄·½H₂O (c) CaO (d) CaCO₃. List-II: (i) Antacid (ii) Cement (iii) Bleach (iv) Plaster of paris. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ca(OCl)₂ as antacid; (B) CaSO₄·½H₂O as cement and CaO as plaster; (D) CaO as antacid and CaCO₃ as plaster.\nWorking: Ca(OCl)₂ – bleach; CaSO₄·½H₂O – plaster of Paris; CaO – a main constituent/raw material of cement; CaCO₃ – used as an antacid.\nKey idea: Hemihydrate = plaster of Paris; hypochlorite = bleach; leaves CaO/CaCO₃ for cement/antacid.","traps":["CaO vs CaCO₃ uses: CaCO₃ (mild base, insoluble) is the antacid; CaO is a cement constituent."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match list-I with list-II :\nList-I: (a) Be (b) Mg (c) Ca (d) Ra\nList-II: (i) Treatment of cancer (ii) Extraction of metals (iii) Incendiary bombs and signals (iv) Windows of X-ray tubes (v) Bearings for motor engines.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-v, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-v"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ca and Ra (cancer vs extraction); (C)/(D) give Be the Mg use and add the unused (v).\nWorking: Be – X-ray tube windows; Mg – incendiary bombs and signals; Ca – extraction of metals from oxides; Ra – radiotherapy of cancer. (v) is a distractor.\nKey idea: Be is transparent to X-rays; Ra is radioactive.","traps":["Ca and Ra uses swapped: Radioactive Ra → radiotherapy.","Unused List-II entry: Expect a spare entry in 4×5 matches."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Number of paramagnetic oxides among the following given oxides is ______. Li₂O, CaO, Na₂O₂, KO₂, MgO and K₂O","options":["1","2","3","0"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2 – counts Na₂O₂ too; (C) 3 – also counts K₂O by confusing with KO₂; (D) 0 – treats all as ionic diamagnetic oxides.\nWorking: only KO₂ contains O₂⁻ with one unpaired electron; Li₂O, CaO, MgO, K₂O (O²⁻) and Na₂O₂ (O₂²⁻) are diamagnetic – count 1.\nKey idea: Only KO₂ (O₂⁻, one unpaired electron) is paramagnetic.","traps":["Peroxide counted as paramagnetic: O₂²⁻ has all π* electrons paired (bond order 1) – diamagnetic.","Superoxide missed: O₂⁻ has one unpaired π* electron – paramagnetic (yellow)."],"topics":["Group 1 trends","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"When gypsum is heated to 393 K, it forms :","options":["Dead burnt plaster","Anhydrous CaSO₄","CaSO₄·5H₂O","CaSO₄·0.5H₂O"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) are products above 473 K; (C) is a nonexistent hydrate (confused with CuSO₄·5H₂O).\nWorking: CaSO₄·2H₂O at 393 K → CaSO₄·½H₂O (plaster of Paris); above ~473 K it gives anhydrous dead-burnt plaster.\nKey idea: 393 K → hemihydrate; >473 K → anhydrous.","traps":["Temperature thresholds confused: Remember 393 K vs 473 K."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"A metal (A) on heating in nitrogen gas gives compound B. B on treatment with H₂O gives a colourless gas which when passed through CuSO₄ solution gives a dark blue-violet coloured solution. A and B respectively, are :","options":["Mg and Mg₃N₂","Na and NaNO₃","Mg and Mg(NO₃)₂","Na and Na₃N"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Na₃N – Na does not react with N₂ (Li–Mg diagonal relationship); (B)/(C) nitrates cannot form from N₂ alone and give no NH₃.\nWorking: 3Mg + N₂ → Mg₃N₂; Mg₃N₂ + 6H₂O → 3Mg(OH)₂ + 2NH₃; NH₃ gives deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ with CuSO₄. Na does not form a nitride on heating in N₂ (only Li among alkali metals does), and nitrates are not formed from N₂.\nKey idea: Blue-violet with CuSO₄ = NH₃; NH₃ from water on a nitride; Mg (not Na) forms a nitride.","traps":["Sodium assumed to form nitride: Only Li (diagonal to Mg) forms a nitride among group 1."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Among the statements (a)–(d) the correct ones are: (a) Lithium has the highest hydration enthalpy among the alkali metals. (b) Lithium chloride is insoluble in pyridine. (c) Lithium cannot form ethynide upon its reaction with ethyne. (d) Both lithium and magnesium react slowly with H₂O.","options":["(a), (b) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(a) and (d) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) accept the false statement (b); (D) drops the true (c), which sounds wrong because other alkali metals do form ethynides.\nWorking: (a) true (smallest ion); (b) false – covalent LiCl is soluble in pyridine/organic solvents; (c) true – Li, unlike other alkali metals, does not form ethynide; (d) true – Li and Mg react slowly with water.\nKey idea: LiCl is covalent → soluble in pyridine; Li does not form ethynide.","traps":["LiCl solubility reversed: LiCl is covalent (Fajans) and dissolves in pyridine.","Li 'cannot form ethynide' judged false: Li anomaly list includes no ethynide formation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The metal mainly used in devising photoelectric cells is:","options":["Na","Rb","Li","Cs"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Rb also has low IE and is a plausible guess; (A) Na and (C) Li have higher IE.\nWorking: caesium has the lowest ionisation enthalpy/work function among the options and is the metal used in photoelectric cells.\nKey idea: Lowest IE among stable alkali metals → Cs.","traps":["Rb vs Cs: Remember the NCERT use line names caesium."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Two elements A and B have similar chemical properties. They don't form solid hydrogencarbonates, but react with nitrogen to form nitrides. A and B, respectively, are :","options":["Na and C","Li and Mg","Cs and Ba","Na and Rb"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Na and C share nothing; (C) Cs and Ba / (D) Na and Rb form solid hydrogencarbonates (alkali metals) and do not form nitrides directly.\nWorking: diagonal relationship – Li and Mg both burn in N₂ to give nitrides (Li₃N, Mg₃N₂) and neither forms a solid hydrogencarbonate.\nKey idea: Li–Mg diagonal pair: nitrides, no solid bicarbonates.","traps":["Alkali pair chosen for 'similar properties': Nitride formation is unique to Li among alkali metals."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"On combustion Li, Na and K in excess of air, the major oxides formed, respectively, are :","options":["Li₂O, Na₂O and K₂O₂","Li₂O, Na₂O₂ and K₂O","Li₂O, Na₂O₂ and KO₂","Li₂O₂, Na₂O₂ and K₂O₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) give K a peroxide or monoxide; (D) gives Li a peroxide.\nWorking: Li forms monoxide, Na peroxide, K (and heavier) superoxide in excess air.\nKey idea: Larger cation stabilises larger anion: O²⁻ (Li), O₂²⁻ (Na), O₂⁻ (K).","traps":["K forms peroxide like Na: K, Rb, Cs form superoxides."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"An alkaline earth metal 'M' readily forms water soluble sulphate and water insoluble hydroxide. Its oxide MO is very stable to heat and does not have rock-salt structure. M is :","options":["Ca","Be","Mg","Sr"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Mg satisfies the solubility clues but MgO is rock-salt; (A)/(D) sulphates are sparingly soluble and hydroxides more soluble.\nWorking: BeSO₄ (high hydration energy of Be²⁺) is soluble, Be(OH)₂ insoluble; BeO is very stable and has the wurtzite (4:4) structure, not rock salt. MgO has rock-salt structure; CaSO₄/SrSO₄ are sparingly soluble.\nKey idea: Only Be²⁺ (small, polarising) gives a non-rock-salt oxide.","traps":["Choosing Mg on solubility alone: Use the last clue: MgO is NaCl-type, BeO wurtzite."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Among the sulphates of alkaline earth metals, the solubilities of BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ in water, respectively, are:","options":["high and high","poor and poor","high and poor","poor and high"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) assumes the trend starts dropping at Mg; (B)/(D) confuse with the solubility trend of hydroxides (which increases down the group).\nWorking: the large hydration enthalpies of small Be²⁺ and Mg²⁺ outweigh lattice enthalpy, so BeSO₄ and MgSO₄ are readily soluble; CaSO₄ to BaSO₄ are sparingly soluble.\nKey idea: Group 2 sulphate solubility falls down the group: Be, Mg soluble; Ca–Ba sparingly soluble.","traps":["Sulphate and hydroxide trends mixed: Sulphates: decrease down the group (hydration dominates for small ions)."],"topics":["Group 2 trends","Anomalous Li & Be"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Calcium compounds","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Match the following compounds (Column-I) with their uses (Column-II): (I) Ca(OH)₂, (II) NaCl, (III) CaSO₄·½H₂O, (IV) CaCO₃; (A) casts of statues, (B) white wash, (C) antacid, (D) washing soda preparation.","options":["(I)-(D), (II)-(A), (III)-(C), (IV)-(B)","(I)-(B), (II)-(C), (III)-(D), (IV)-(A)","(I)-(C), (II)-(D), (III)-(B), (IV)-(A)","(I)-(B), (II)-(D), (III)-(A), (IV)-(C)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) gives NaCl as antacid and CaCO₃ for casts; (C) gives Ca(OH)₂ as antacid; (A) gives Ca(OH)₂ for washing soda (it is used in ammonia recovery in Solvay process, a plausible confusion).\nWorking: slaked lime – white wash; NaCl – raw material for Na₂CO₃ (Solvay); plaster of Paris – casts; CaCO₃ – antacid.\nKey idea: Washing soda (Na₂CO₃) needs Na – only NaCl provides it.","traps":["Ca(OH)₂ linked to Solvay process: Washing soda preparation needs the Na source: NaCl."],"topics":["Calcium compounds","Sodium compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct order of hydration enthalpies of alkali metal ions is –","options":["Li⁺ > Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺","Li⁺ > Na⁺ > K⁺ > Cs⁺ > Rb⁺","Na⁺ > Li⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺","Na⁺ > Li⁺ > K⁺ > Cs⁺ > Rb⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) swap Rb⁺/Cs⁺; (C)/(D) put Na⁺ above Li⁺.\nWorking: hydration enthalpy falls as ionic size increases: Li⁺ > Na⁺ > K⁺ > Rb⁺ > Cs⁺.\nKey idea: Smallest ion, highest charge density → most hydrated.","traps":["Li⁺/Na⁺ swapped: Hydration enthalpy follows bare ionic size."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The covalent alkaline earth metal halide (X = Cl, Br, I) is:","options":["CaX₂","SrX₂","BeX₂","MgX₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) larger cations, mainly ionic halides.\nWorking: Be²⁺ is very small with high polarising power, so BeX₂ are predominantly covalent.\nKey idea: Smallest cation ⇒ highest polarising power ⇒ covalent.","traps":["Mg chosen as 'also small': Be is the anomalous member."],"topics":["Anomalous Li & Be","Group 2 trends"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Magnesium powder burns in air to give:","options":["MgO only","MgO and Mg(NO₃)₂","MgO and Mg₃N₂","Mg(NO₃)₂ and Mg₃N₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores N₂ in air; (B)/(D) nitrate cannot form by direct combustion.\nWorking: Mg burns in air reacting with O₂ (MgO) and N₂ (Mg₃N₂).\nKey idea: Mg is reactive enough to combine with N₂ as well as O₂.","traps":["Ignoring nitrogen of air: Air is ~78% N₂; Mg (like Li) forms nitride."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The structures of beryllium chloride in the solid state and vapour phase, respectively, are :","options":["chain and dimeric","chain and chain","dimeric and dimeric","dimeric and chain"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps the two; (B)/(C) give same structure for both phases.\nWorking: solid BeCl₂ is a polymeric chain with Cl bridges; in the vapour it exists as the chloro-bridged dimer (monomer above ~1200 K).\nKey idea: Solid = polymeric chain; vapour = dimer (monomer at very high T).","traps":["Phases swapped: Solid lattices extend into chains."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The alloy used in the construction of aircrafts is :","options":["Mg–Sn","Mg–Mn","Mg–Al","Mg–Zn"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) other Mg alloys not named in NCERT for aircraft.\nWorking: Mg–Al alloys (light, strong; e.g. magnalium) are used in aircraft construction.\nKey idea: Light Mg–Al alloys for aircraft.","traps":["Any Mg alloy assumed acceptable: Recall the NCERT pairing Mg–Al."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"A hydrated solid X on heating initially gives a monohydrated compound Y. Y upon heating above 373 K leads to an anhydrous white powder Z. X and Z, respectively, are:","options":["Washing soda and soda ash.","Washing soda and dead burnt plaster.","Baking soda and dead burnt plaster.","Baking soda and soda ash."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): dead burnt plaster is anhydrous CaSO₄ but comes from gypsum via hemihydrate, not monohydrate; (C)/(D): baking soda is anhydrous NaHCO₃.\nWorking: Na₂CO₃·10H₂O → Na₂CO₃·H₂O → (above 373 K) Na₂CO₃ (soda ash, white powder). Gypsum route gives hemihydrate, not monohydrate; baking soda is not hydrated.\nKey idea: Monohydrate intermediate points to Na₂CO₃·H₂O.","traps":["Gypsum confused with washing soda: Gypsum gives hemihydrate (½H₂O), washing soda gives monohydrate."],"topics":["Sodium compounds","Calcium compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct sequence of thermal stability of the following carbonates is","options":["BaCO₃ < CaCO₃ < SrCO₃ < MgCO₃","MgCO₃ < CaCO₃ < SrCO₃ < BaCO₃","BaCO₃ < SrCO₃ < CaCO₃ < MgCO₃","MgCO₃ < SrCO₃ < CaCO₃ < BaCO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) reverse the trend (confusing with solubility of hydroxides or polarising-power reasoning applied backwards); (D) swaps Ca and Sr.\nWorking: down Group 2 the cation becomes larger and less polarising, so the carbonate ion is less distorted and decomposition temperature rises: Mg < Ca < Sr < Ba.\nKey idea: Smaller cation = more polarising = less stable carbonate.","traps":["Trend direction reversed: Link stability to low polarising power of large cations.","Middle pair swapped: Check every adjacent pair against group order."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The INCORRECT statement is :","options":["Lithium is least reactive with water among the alkali metals.","LiCl crystallises from aqueous solution as LiCl·2H₂O.","Lithium is the strongest reducing agent among the alkali metals.","LiNO₃ decomposes on heating to give LiNO₂ and O₂."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true despite highest reducing power; (C) true (most negative E°); (B) true hydrate.\nWorking: like Mg(NO₃)₂, LiNO₃ gives Li₂O, NO₂ and O₂ on heating (4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂); (A)–(C) are true.\nKey idea: Li resembles Mg: nitrate gives oxide + NO₂.","traps":["Li nitrate assumed like NaNO₃: Li is anomalous; resembles Mg.","Least reactive and strongest reductant seen as contradictory: Reactivity with water is kinetic; reducing power is E°-based."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The alkaline earth metal nitrate that does not crystallise with water molecules, is :","options":["Sr(NO₃)₂","Mg(NO₃)₂","Ca(NO₃)₂","Ba(NO₃)₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): Mg chosen by reversing trend. (A)/(C): intermediate sizes still hydrate.\nWorking: hydration of cations falls with size; Mg(NO₃)₂ crystallises as a hexahydrate while Ba(NO₃)₂ crystallises anhydrous.\nKey idea: Larger cation, lower hydration energy, anhydrous salt.","traps":["Smallest ion picked: Small, highly hydrated cations crystallise with water; big Ba²⁺ does not."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The metal that forms nitride by reacting directly with N₂ of air, is:","options":["K","Cs","Li","Rb"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) K, (B) Cs, (D) Rb: larger alkali metals form oxides/peroxides/superoxides but no nitride with N₂.\nWorking: only Li among alkali metals combines directly with N₂ (6Li + N₂ → 2Li₃N), a diagonal relationship with Mg.\nKey idea: Li behaves like Mg (diagonal relationship): Li₃N and Mg₃N₂ form directly.","traps":["Reactivity order wrongly applied: Nitride formation needs small cation with high lattice energy; only Li."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 2 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The metal used for making X-ray tube window is :","options":["Mg","Na","Ca","Be"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Mg, (C) Ca: other Group 2 metals with alloy uses. (B) Na: reactive, unsuitable.\nWorking: beryllium is used for X-ray tube windows because it has very low Z and is transparent to X-rays.\nKey idea: Low atomic number → low X-ray absorption → Be.","traps":["Mixing up Group 2 uses: Remember: Be – X-ray windows; Mg – alloys/flash powder."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Sodium metal on dissolution in liquid ammonia gives a deep blue solution due to the formation of:","options":["sodium ion-ammonia complex","sodamide","sodium-ammonia complex","ammoniated electrons"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) sodium ion–ammonia complex: present but colourless. (B) sodamide: forms only slowly on standing/with catalyst (with H₂). (C) sodium–ammonia complex: vague look-alike of (A).\nWorking: Na + (x + y)NH₃ → [Na(NH₃)ₓ]⁺ + [e(NH₃)ᵧ]⁻; the blue colour is due to the ammoniated (solvated) electron.\nKey idea: The colour comes from the solvated electron, which absorbs in the visible.","traps":["Cation complex vs solvated electron: Colour and conductivity come from the ammoniated electron."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous Li & Be","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The amphoteric hydroxide is:","options":["Ca(OH)₂","Be(OH)₂","Sr(OH)₂","Mg(OH)₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Mg(OH)₂ – weakly basic, not amphoteric.\nWorking: Be(OH)₂ dissolves in both acids and alkali (forming [Be(OH)₄]²⁻); others are basic.\nKey idea: Small, highly polarising Be²⁺ → amphoteric hydroxide (diagonal to Al).","traps":["Mg(OH)₂ weakly basic: Amphoteric means reacts with base too."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Sodium compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Match the following items in column I with the corresponding items in column II. Column I: (i) Na₂CO₃·10H₂O, (ii) Mg(HCO₃)₂, (iii) NaOH, (iv) Ca₃Al₂O₆. Column II: (P) Portland cement ingredient, (Q) Castner-Kellner process, (R) Solvay process, (S) Temporary hardness.","options":["(i)→(R); (ii)→(Q); (iii)→(S); (iv)→(P)","(i)→(R); (ii)→(S); (iii)→(Q); (iv)→(P)","(i)→(S); (ii)→(P); (iii)→(Q); (iv)→(R)","(i)→(Q); (ii)→(R); (iii)→(P); (iv)→(S)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps (ii) and (iii). (C) and (D) mismatch most pairs; (C) sends Na₂CO₃ to hardness.\nWorking: washing soda – Solvay process; Mg(HCO₃)₂ – temporary hardness; NaOH – Castner–Kellner cell; Ca₃Al₂O₆ (tricalcium aluminate) – Portland cement.\nKey idea: Solvay = soda; Castner–Kellner = NaOH; bicarbonates = temporary hardness; Ca₃Al₂O₆ = cement.","traps":["Castner–Kellner vs Solvay: Solvay makes carbonate; Castner–Kellner makes NaOH.","Codes misread: Match each item (i)–(iv) to exactly one of (P)–(S) before checking the options."],"topics":["Sodium compounds","Calcium compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A metal on combustion in excess air forms X. X upon hydrolysis with water yields H₂O₂ and O₂ along with another product. The metal is :","options":["Rb","Na","Mg","Li"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Na: peroxide gives H₂O₂ but no O₂. (D) Li: monoxide gives LiOH only. (C) Mg: MgO.\nWorking: Rb (like K, Cs) forms superoxide RbO₂; 2RbO₂ + 2H₂O → 2RbOH + H₂O₂ + O₂. Na gives peroxide (H₂O₂ only), Li and Mg give normal oxides.\nKey idea: O₂ evolution on hydrolysis ⇒ superoxide ⇒ K, Rb, Cs.","traps":["Peroxide vs superoxide: Only superoxide also releases O₂."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Group 1 trends","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct statement(s) among I to III with respect to potassium ions that are abundant within the cell fluids is/are :\nI. They activate many enzymes\nII. They participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP\nIII. Along with sodium ions, they are responsible for the transmission of nerve signals","options":["I, II and III","I and III only","III only","I and II only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D): drop II or III, catching students who think glucose oxidation or nerve signalling belongs only to Na⁺ or to Mg²⁺. (C): keeps only the most familiar role.\nWorking: all three are the textbook roles of K⁺ in cell fluid (enzyme activation, glucose oxidation to ATP, nerve signal transmission with Na⁺).\nKey idea: All three listed roles are from the same NCERT paragraph on K⁺ – none is altered.","traps":["Assigning nerve signalling only to Na⁺: Remember both ions act together in nerve signals."]}],"m17-oxy":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"Consider the following molecules:\n(p) CH₃−CH₂−CO−Cl\n(q) CH₃−CH₂−CO−O−CO−CH₃\n(r) CH₃−CH₂−CO−O−CH₂−CH₃\n(s) CH₃−CH₂−CO−NH₂\nThe correct order of rate of hydrolysis is:","options":["q > p > r > s","r > q > p > s","p > r > q > s","p > q > r > s"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) q > p: anhydride wrongly above acid chloride. (B) r > q > p: reverses the order of the top three, treating ester as most reactive. (C) p > r > q: ester and anhydride swapped. (D) correct.\nWorking: hydrolysis is a nucleophilic acyl substitution; the better the leaving group (Cl⁻ > RCOO⁻ > RO⁻ > NH₂⁻) and the weaker its lone-pair donation to C=O, the faster the reaction: acid chloride (p) > anhydride (q) > ester (r) > amide (s).\nKey idea: Hydrolysis rate of RCOL follows leaving-group ability of L⁻: Cl⁻ > RCOO⁻ > RO⁻ > NH₂⁻.","traps":["Acyl derivative reactivity order: chloride > anhydride > ester > amide: Rank by leaving group: Cl⁻ > RCOO⁻ > RO⁻ > NH₂⁻; the better the leaving group and the weaker the lone-pair donation to C=O, the faster the hydrolysis.","Mixed anhydride looks less reactive than acid chloride: Any anhydride still has a carboxylate leaving group, poorer than chloride; acid chloride is always the most reactive."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C17-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement (I): Vanillin (structure shown below) will react with NaOH and also with Tollen's reagent.\nStatement (II): Vanillin (structure shown below) will undergo self aldol condensation very easily.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I true: phenolic OH + NaOH gives phenoxide; CHO reduces Tollens'. Statement II false: no alpha-H on ArCHO, so no self aldol (with conc. alkali it would rather do Cannizzaro-type chemistry). Option (C) correct.\nWorking: vanillin (4-hydroxy-3-methoxybenzaldehyde) has a phenolic OH, which forms a phenoxide with NaOH, and a –CHO group, which reduces Tollens' reagent (I correct); the CHO is attached directly to the ring, so there is no α-H and no self aldol condensation (II incorrect).\nKey idea: Read the drawn molecule group by group: phenol (acidic), CHO (Tollens), no alpha-H (no aldol).","traps":["Alpha hydrogen presence decides aldol versus Cannizzaro: Check the carbon next to C=O: in ArCHO it is a ring carbon with no H, so no enolate and no self aldol.","Phenolic OH overlooked as acidic site reacting with NaOH: Phenols are acidic enough to form phenoxides with NaOH; vanillin's 4-OH reacts."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C18-s.webp"],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Enolates & aldol reactions"]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"The major product (P) in the following reaction is:\nPh–CO–CHO —[KOH, Δ]→ P (Major Product)","options":["Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂OH","Ph–CH(OH)–COO⁻ K⁺","Ph–CO–CH₂OH","Ph–CO–COO⁻ K⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) both C=O reduced, impossible with KOH alone. (B) correct, intramolecular disproportionation. (C) only the CHO reduced (half a Cannizzaro). (D) only the CHO oxidised (half a Cannizzaro).\nWorking: phenylglyoxal has no α-H; OH⁻ adds to the CHO and a hydride moves to the neighbouring ketone C=O within the same molecule (intramolecular Cannizzaro), giving Ph–CH(OH)–COO⁻ K⁺ (B).\nKey idea: In a 1,2-dicarbonyl with no alpha-H, the CHO is oxidised and the neighbouring C=O is reduced in the same molecule.","traps":["Intramolecular Cannizzaro of 1,2-dicarbonyl missed: With two adjacent C=O groups the hydride transfers within the molecule: CHO becomes COO⁻, C=O becomes CH(OH).","Alpha hydrogen presence decides aldol versus Cannizzaro: Phenylglyoxal has no alpha-H (Ph and CHO flank the ketone), so Cannizzaro-type redox is the only path."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S2C18-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"Which among the following compounds give yellow solid when reacted with NaOI/NaOH?\n(A) CH₃−CH(OH)−C₂H₅\n(B) CH₃−CH₂−CH₂−OH\n(C) CH₃−CO−C₂H₅\n(D) CH₃−CO−OH\n(E) CH₃−CH₂−CHO\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) and (C) Only","(A), (C) and (D) Only","(B), (C) and (E) Only","(C) and (D) Only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) yes (oxidised to methyl ketone). (B) no, primary alcohol giving propanal. (C) yes, methyl ketone. (D) no, acid. (E) no, propanal. So option (A) is correct; option (B) wrongly adds acetic acid; option (C) adds propan-1-ol and propanal and drops butan-2-ol; option (D) adds acetic acid and drops butan-2-ol.\nWorking: butan-2-ol (A) is oxidised by NaOI to butan-2-one, a methyl ketone, and butan-2-one (C) is itself a methyl ketone – both give CHI₃; propan-1-ol (B) and propanal (E) have no CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)– unit; in acetic acid (D) the CH₃CO– is part of a resonance-stabilised –COOH and does not react.\nKey idea: CH₃CO– must be a ketone or aldehyde (or a CH₃CH(OH)– precursor), not part of a COOH.","traps":["Acetic acid's CH₃CO group taken as iodoform positive: In acids the carboxylate is resonance-stabilised, so the CH₃ is not halogenated; carboxylic acids do not give iodoform.","Aldehyde other than acetaldehyde taken as iodoform positive: Propanal is CH₃CH₂CHO; it has no CH₃–CO–."]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"\"P\" is an optically active compound with molecular formula C₆H₁₂O. When 'P' is treated with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine, it gives a positive test. However, in presence of Tollens reagent, \"P\" gives a negative test.\nPredict the structure of \"P\".","options":["CH₃−CO−CH₂−CH(CH₃)−CH₃","CH₃−CO−CH(CH₃)−CH₂−CH₃","H−CO−CH₂−CH(CH₃)−CH₂−CH₃","CH₃−CO−CH₂−CH₂−CH₂−CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ketone, achiral (C4 has two CH₃). (B) correct, chiral C3. (C) chiral but an aldehyde, Tollens positive. (D) hexan-2-one, achiral.\nKey idea: Apply the tests as filters: DNP keeps carbonyls, Tollens-negative keeps ketones, optical activity needs a stereocentre.","traps":["Ketone wrongly credited with Tollens test: Negative Tollens rules out every aldehyde; only ketones remain.","Stereocentre located wrongly in branched ketone: Check the four groups on the branched carbon: in 3-methylpentan-2-one C3 has H, CH₃, C₂H₅, COCH₃ (chiral)."]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"Which one of the following reactions will not lead to the desired ether formation in major proportion?\n(iso-Bu ⇒ isobutyl, sec-Bu ⇒ sec-butyl, nPr ⇒ n-propyl, ᵗBu ⇒ tert-butyl, Et ⇒ ethyl)","options":["Cyclohexyl–O⁻ Na⁺ + CH₃Br → Cyclohexyl–O–CH₃","iso-BuO⁻ Na⁺ + sec-BuBr → sec-Bu–O–iso-Bu","C₆H₅–O⁻ Na⁺ + n-PrBr → n-Pr–O–C₆H₅","ᵗBuO⁻ Na⁺ + EtBr → ᵗBu–O–Et"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) works: methyl bromide with cyclohexoxide. (B) fails: sec-BuBr undergoes elimination (answer). (C) works: phenoxide + n-PrBr. (D) works: ᵗBuO⁻ + EtBr (primary halide).\nKey idea: Judge the halide, not the alkoxide: it must be methyl or primary.","traps":["Secondary or tertiary halide in Williamson gives elimination: Strong alkoxide base with a 2° or 3° halide favours E2; Williamson works best with methyl or 1° halides.","Bulky alkoxide blamed instead of the alkyl halide: What matters is the carbon being attacked: EtBr is primary, so SN2 by ᵗBuO⁻ still gives ᵗBu–O–Et (the NCERT route to tert-butyl ethers)."]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"When the compound shown below undergoes intramolecular aldol condensation, the major product formed is:","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 7-ring from CH₃ enolate onto CHO, slow ring size. (B) wrong skeleton. (C) correct, 5-ring with acetyl on the alkene carbon after dehydration. (D) 5-ring from aldehyde enolate onto ketone, less favoured because ketone is the poorer acceptor.\nKey idea: Make the five-membered ring with the aldehyde as the acceptor.","traps":["Choosing enolate and acceptor in intramolecular aldol: Aldehyde is the better electrophile and five-membered rings form fastest, so the internal CH₂ alpha to the ketone attacks the CHO.","Ring size counted wrongly in intramolecular reaction: Number the chain C1 (CHO) to C7 (CH₃); C5 attacking C1 gives a ring of C1–C5 (five atoms) with C6(=O)CH₃ outside."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr08S2C18-s.webp","img/jee/JM25Apr08S2C18-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"A —[(i) NaOH; (ii) H₃O⁺]→ B —[(i) EtOH; (ii) H₂SO₄, Δ]→ C\n'A' shows positive Lassaigne's test for N and its molar mass is 121.\n'B' gives effervescence with aq NaHCO₃.\n'C' gives fruity smell.\nIdentify A, B and C from the following.","options":["A = C₆H₅CONH₂, B = C₆H₅CO₂H, C = C₆H₅CO₂Et","A = C₆H₅CH₂NHNH₂, B = C₆H₅CO₂H, C = C₆H₅CO₂Et","A = p-H₂N–C₆H₄–CHO, B = p-H₂N–C₆H₄–CH(OH)–CO–C₆H₄–NH₂-p, C = p-H₂N–C₆H₄–CH(OH)–C(OH)(OEt)–C₆H₄–NH₂-p","A = o-H₂N–C₆H₄–CHO, B = o-H₂N–C₆H₄–CO₂H, C = o-EtNH–C₆H₄–CO₂Et"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) correct. (B) benzylhydrazine has M = 122 and would not hydrolyse to benzoic acid. (C) benzoin condensation needs CN⁻, product is not an acid, C is not an ester. (D) 2-aminobenzaldehyde would not become an acid with NaOH, and EtOH/H₂SO₄ does not N-ethylate.\nKey idea: NaHCO₃ effervescence means B is RCOOH; fruity smell means C is its ester; so A is the amide.","traps":["Molar mass clue fits more than one structure: Use the reaction clues: B must be a carboxylic acid formed by NaOH hydrolysis, which an aminobenzaldehyde cannot give.","Amide hydrolysis to acid with NaOH then acid: Amides are hydrolysed by aqueous alkali to carboxylate (NH₃ lost); acidification gives RCOOH."],"topics":["Acid derivatives","Carboxylic acid reactions"]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"Toluene (excess) —[(i) CrO₂Cl₂, CS₂; (ii) H₃O⁺; (iii) NaHSO₃]→ Filter → Residue (A)\nResidue (A) + HCl(dil) → Compound (B)\nStructure of residue (A) and compound (B) formed respectively is:","options":["A = C₆H₅CH(OCrOHCl₂)₂; B = C₆H₅CHO","A = C₆H₅CHO; B = C₆H₅COOH","A = C₆H₅CHO; B = C₆H₅COONa","A = C₆H₅CH(OH)SO₃Na; B = C₆H₅CHO"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Etard complex already hydrolysed in step (ii). (B) A is not filtered aldehyde and dil. HCl does not oxidise. (C) sodium benzoate cannot come from dil. HCl. (D) correct.\nKey idea: NaHSO₃ then dil. acid is the classic purify-and-regenerate cycle for aldehydes.","traps":["Bisulphite adduct is the filtered solid, not the aldehyde: Aldehydes form crystalline C₆H₅CH(OH)SO₃Na adducts; the residue after filtration is this salt.","Dilute acid regenerates carbonyl from bisulphite adduct: Dilute acid (or alkali) decomposes the adduct back to the aldehyde; nothing oxidises it."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nConsider the following reaction\nR−CO−R + H₂O ⇌ R−C(OH)₂−R (equilibrium constant K)\nStatement I: In the case of formaldehyde (H−CO−H), K is about 2280, due to small substituents, hydration is faster.\nStatement II: In the case of trichloro acetaldehyde (H−CO−CCl₃), K is about 2000 due to −I effect of −Cl.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I true (HCHO hydration K ≈ 2 × 10³). Statement II true (chloral K ≈ 2 × 10³, −I of Cl). Option (A).\nKey idea: Anything that destabilises C=O or reduces crowding pushes hydration to the gem-diol.","traps":["Steric versus electronic reason for carbonyl reactivity: Both effects increase the extent of nucleophilic addition: less crowding in HCHO, electron withdrawal in Cl₃CCHO (chloral hydrate is a stable solid).","Unfamiliar numerical data in statement: Recall that HCHO and chloral exist mostly as hydrates in water; K >> 1 for both."]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"Identify the products [A] and [B], respectively in the following reaction:\nChlorobenzene —[(i) NaOH, 623 K, 300 atm; (ii) H⁺]→ [A] —[Na₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄]→ [B]","options":["[A] benzene, [B] phenol","[A] phenol, [B] sodium phenoxide (C₆H₅O⁻Na⁺)","[A] benzene, [B] cyclohexa-2,4-dien-1-one","[A] phenol, [B] p-benzoquinone"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A = benzene wrong; oxidation of benzene to phenol not by dichromate. (B) B = phenoxide ignores the oxidant. (C) A wrong and dienone is a tautomer of phenol, not an oxidation product. (D) correct.\nKey idea: Chlorobenzene → phenol (Dow), phenol → p-benzoquinone (chromic acid).","traps":["Acidification step converts phenoxide to phenol: Step (ii) H⁺ gives phenol; dichromate/H₂SO₄ is an oxidant, it does not make the sodium salt.","Oxidation of phenol gives p-benzoquinone: NCERT: phenol with chromic acid gives benzoquinone (O at C1 and C4)."],"topics":["Phenol preparation","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"The total number of compounds from below when treated with hot KMnO₄ giving benzoic acid is:\n(Compounds (i)–(viii) are shown below.)","options":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Yes: toluene, ethylbenzene, cumene, isobutylbenzene, n-propylbenzene (5). No: tert-butylbenzene, PhC(CH₃)₂OH, 4,4'-dimethylbiphenyl. (A) 3 / (B) 4 drop long or branched chains; (D) 6 adds the biphenyl or a tertiary case.\nKey idea: Benzylic H present → one COOH per chain; check also that the product is exactly benzoic acid.","traps":["Benzylic hydrogen needed for side-chain KMnO₄ oxidation: Side-chain oxidation needs at least one benzylic H; quaternary benzylic carbon resists.","Long side chain thought to give longer acid: The entire chain is cut down to a single COOH on the ring, regardless of length."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C17-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones is responsible for Aldol reaction.\nStatement II : Reaction between benzaldehyde and ethanal will NOT give Cross - Aldol product.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): student thinks benzaldehyde, lacking α-H, cannot take part at all. (A)/(D): doubt that α-H acidity drives aldol.\nWorking: aldol needs an enolisable α-H (I correct). Benzaldehyde (no α-H) + ethanal (has α-H) gives a cross aldol product, cinnamaldehyde after dehydration (II incorrect).\nKey idea: Only one partner needs α-H; benzaldehyde acts as the electrophile.","traps":["No α-H means no aldol at all: One enolisable partner is enough for cross aldol."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Identify the product in the following reaction: 3-acetylbenzaldehyde (benzene with –CHO and –COCH₃ meta to each other) —Zn–Hg / HCl→ Product","options":["1-ethyl-3-methylbenzene","1-[3-(hydroxymethyl)phenyl]ethan-1-ol (both C=O reduced to CH–OH)","1-(3-methylphenyl)ethan-1-one (only CHO reduced to CH₃)","1-[3-(hydroxymethyl)phenyl]ethan-1-one (only CHO reduced to CH₂OH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): NaBH₄/LiAlH₄-type product. (C)/(D): assume selective reduction of one group only.\nWorking: Clemmensen reduction converts both aldehyde and ketone C=O to CH₂ → CHO → CH₃, COCH₃ → CH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: Zn–Hg/HCl reduces every aldehyde/ketone C=O to CH₂.","traps":["Clemmensen confused with hydride reduction: Clemmensen → CH₂, not CHOH.","Assumed chemoselectivity: Clemmensen reduces both."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of the correct reaction(s) among the following is ______.\n(A) Benzene + benzoyl chloride —anhyd. AlCl₃→ diphenylmethane (C₆H₅CH₂C₆H₅)\n(B) Benzoyl chloride —H₂, Pd–BaSO₄→ benzoic acid\n(C) Benzene —CO, HCl, anhyd. AlCl₃/CuCl→ benzaldehyde\n(D) Benzamide —H₃O⁺, Δ→ aniline","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2: accepts (A) (confuses acylation with alkylation/reduction) or (D) (confuses with Hofmann). 0: doubts Gattermann–Koch catalyst. 3: accepts B too.\nWorking: (A) wrong – Friedel–Crafts acylation gives benzophenone, not diphenylmethane; (B) wrong – Rosenmund gives benzaldehyde; (C) correct – Gattermann–Koch; (D) wrong – acid hydrolysis of benzamide gives benzoic acid (aniline needs Hofmann bromamide). Count = 1.\nKey idea: Check each product against the named reaction; only Gattermann–Koch is correct.","traps":["Rosenmund product: Rosenmund stops at aldehyde.","Amide hydrolysis vs Hofmann: H₃O⁺ → acid; Br₂/NaOH → amine."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br + NaOH —C₂H₅OH→ Product 'A'\nProduct A —H₂O/H⁺→ Product 'B'\nProduct A —Diborane, then H₂O/H₂O₂/OH⁻→ Product 'C'\nConsider the above reactions, identify product B and product C.","options":["B = C = 1-Propanol","B = 1-Propanol, C = 2-Propanol","B = C = 2-Propanol","B = 2-Propanol, C = 1-Propanol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes NaOH substitutes (giving propan-1-ol) and both later steps keep it. (B) reverses the regiochemistry of the two hydration methods. (C) ignores the anti-Markovnikov nature of hydroboration.\nWorking: NaOH in ethanol eliminates HBr → propene (A). Acid-catalysed hydration follows Markovnikov → propan-2-ol (B); hydroboration–oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov propan-1-ol (C).\nKey idea: H₃O⁺ hydration → Markovnikov; BH₃ then H₂O₂/OH⁻ → anti-Markovnikov.","traps":["Substitution assumed for NaOH/ethanol: Alcoholic medium + heat → elimination.","Hydroboration regiochemistry reversed: Boron goes to less substituted C → OH there."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Bromination of phenol in solvent with low polarity such as CHCl₃ or CS₂ requires Lewis acid catalyst.\nStatement II: The Lewis acid catalyst polarises the bromine to generate Br⁺.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): student carries benzene halogenation conditions over to phenol. (A)/(D): student doubts that the Lewis acid generates Br⁺ (thinks it only polarises, not forms Br⁺).\nWorking: –OH strongly activates the ring, so phenol gives monobromophenols with Br₂ in CHCl₃/CS₂ without any Lewis acid (I false); in benzene halogenation FeBr₃ polarises Br₂ to give Br⁺ (II true as a general fact).\nKey idea: Phenol is so activated that Br₂ alone reacts; Lewis acid is only for unactivated arenes.","traps":["Benzene conditions copied to phenol: Recall that –OH activates the ring; no catalyst is needed.","Polarise vs generate Br⁺ wording: Accept Br⁺ (as FeBr₄⁻·Br⁺ complex) as the standard textbook electrophile."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol physical properties","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"For the compounds:\n(A) H₃C–CH₂–O–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (ethyl propyl ether)\n(B) H₃C–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (pentane)\n(C) CH₃–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₃ (pentan-3-one)\n(D) H₃C–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (pentan-2-ol)\nThe increasing order of boiling point is:\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(D) < (C) < (A) < (B)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)","(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): ether placed below alkane (ignores ether dipole and its higher mass). (D): ketone above alcohol – overrates dipole vs H-bond. (A): order reversed (decreasing).\nWorking: comparable molar masses (72–88); alkane (only dispersion, ~36 °C) < ether (weak dipole, ~64 °C) < ketone (strong dipole, ~102 °C) < alcohol (H-bonding, ~119 °C).\nKey idea: H-bonding > dipole–dipole (C=O) > weak ether dipole > dispersion only.","traps":["Ketone placed above alcohol: Intermolecular H-bonding of –OH outweighs C=O dipole.","Increasing vs decreasing: Check direction asked: increasing."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Identify 'A' in the following reaction:\nCH₃COCH₂CH₃ (butan-2-one) —(i) N₂H₄; (ii) ethylene glycol/KOH→ 'A'","options":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (butan-2-ol)","(CH₃)₂C=N–NH₂ (acetone hydrazone)","CH₃(C₂H₅)C=N–NH₂ (butan-2-one hydrazone)","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ (butane)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): the intermediate hydrazone – student ignores step (ii). (B): hydrazone of wrong ketone. (A): reduction to alcohol (NaBH₄-type thinking).\nWorking: Wolff–Kishner reduction – hydrazone formed with N₂H₄ is decomposed by KOH in ethylene glycol on heating, C=O → CH₂, giving butane.\nKey idea: N₂H₄ then KOH/glycol, heat → C=O becomes CH₂.","traps":["Intermediate given as product: Step (ii) base/heat eliminates N₂.","Reduction to alcohol: Wolff-Kishner/Clemmensen give CH₂, not CHOH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Identify compound (Z) in the following reaction sequence.\nChlorobenzene + NaOH —623 K, 300 atm→ X —HCl→ Y —Conc. HNO₃→ Z","options":["4-nitrophenol","2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid)","2-nitrophenol","2,4-dinitrophenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): products of dilute HNO₃ at 298 K. (D): incomplete nitration.\nWorking: Dow process gives sodium phenoxide (X), acidification gives phenol (Y), and conc. HNO₃ gives 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid).\nKey idea: Conc. HNO₃ on phenol → picric acid; dil. HNO₃ → o/p-nitrophenols.","traps":["Dilute vs conc. HNO₃: Conc. HNO₃ → trinitration."],"topics":["Phenol preparation","Phenol substitution"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Consider the given chemical reaction sequence:\nPhenol —Conc. H₂SO₄→ Product A —Conc. HNO₃→ Product B\nTotal sum of oxygen atoms in Product A and Product B is ____.","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: 11: A taken as p-hydroxybenzenesulphonic acid (4 O). 13 or 10: miscounting NO₂/SO₃H oxygens.\nWorking: as in NCERT’s picric-acid preparation, conc. H₂SO₄ gives phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid (A: 1 + 3 + 3 = 7 O), and conc. HNO₃ then gives 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (B: 1 + 6 = 7 O); total 14. (Taking A as a monosulphonic acid would give 4 + 7 = 11.)\nKey idea: Phenol + conc. H₂SO₄ (for picric acid) → 2,4-disulphonic acid; both groups replaced by NO₂ plus a third NO₂.","traps":["Monosulphonation assumed: Recall NCERT: phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid route.","Oxygen count in groups: Count group by group including –OH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Consider the given chemical reaction:\nCyclohexene —[KMnO₄–H₂SO₄, Heat]→ Product \"A\"\nProduct \"A\" is:","options":["picric acid","oxalic acid","adipic acid","acetic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) small acids from over-cleavage; (A) picric acid is a nitrophenol – unrelated.\nWorking: hot acidic KMnO₄ cleaves the ring C=C; both CH carbons become COOH → HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH.\nKey idea: Ring opening of C₆ alkene gives a C₆ diacid.","traps":["Ring cleavage gives two fragments: A ring C=C cleavage gives one diacid."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"CH₃CH₂–OH —[(i) Jone's Reagent; (ii) KMnO₄; (iii) NaOH, CaO, Δ]→ P\nConsider the above reaction sequence and identify the major product P.","options":["Methanoic acid","Methoxymethane","Methane","Methanal"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) one-carbon oxygenated products from wrong cleavage. (B) dimethyl ether – unrelated.\nWorking: ethanol → acetic acid (Jones/KMnO₄); sodium acetate from NaOH, then soda-lime decarboxylation → CH₄.\nKey idea: Decarboxylation removes one carbon: C₂ acid → C₁ alkane.","traps":["Decarboxylation product misjudged: Soda-lime replaces COONa by H."],"topics":["Carboxylic acid reactions","Carboxylic acid preparation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one of the following reactions is NOT possible?","options":["Anisole —[Cl₂/AlCl₃]→ 4-chloroanisole","Chlorobenzene —[NaOH, high temp, H⁺]→ phenol","Phenol —[HCl]→ chlorobenzene","Anisole —[HBr]→ phenol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) looks impossible to students expecting bromobenzene; in fact alkyl–O breaks giving phenol + CH₃Br. (B) seems too harsh but is the Dow process.\nWorking: the aryl C–O bond of phenol has partial double-bond character and is not cleaved by HCl; the other three are standard NCERT reactions.\nKey idea: Aryl–O and aryl–X bonds resist cleavage; in anisole HBr cleaves the methyl–O bond.","traps":["Anisole + HBr thought impossible: Alkyl aryl ethers cleave at alkyl–O giving phenol.","Phenol treated like alcohol with HX: Phenolic C–O has partial double-bond character."],"topics":["Ether reactions","Phenol preparation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The product C in the following sequence of reactions has ______ π bonds.\nPropylbenzene —[KMnO₄–KOH, Δ]→ A —[H₃O⁺]→ B —[Br₂/FeBr₃]→ C","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (forgetting C=O), 5 (counting a second C=O as if a dicarboxylic product).\nWorking: side chain oxidised → potassium benzoate (A) → benzoic acid (B) → m-bromobenzoic acid (C); π bonds = 3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) = 4.\nKey idea: Any alkyl chain with benzylic H → COOH; product C₇H₅BrO₂ has 4 π bonds.","traps":["Side chain partly kept: Whole chain goes to COOH.","C=O π bond missed: Count carbonyl π too."],"topics":["Carboxylic acid preparation","Carboxylic acid reactions"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In Reimer - Tiemann reaction, phenol is converted into salicylaldehyde through an intermediate. The structure of intermediate is ______.","options":["2-(dichloromethyl)phenol [OH and CHCl₂ ortho]","sodium 2-(dichloromethyl)phenoxide [O⁻Na⁺ and CHCl₂ ortho]","sodium 2-methylphenoxide [O⁻Na⁺ and CH₃ ortho]","sodium 2-formylphenoxide [O⁻Na⁺ and CHO ortho]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): neutral phenol form – medium is strongly alkaline. (D): this is the phenoxide of the product (before acidification), not the benzal chloride intermediate. (C): methyl – wrong.\nWorking: NCERT shows the substituted benzal chloride intermediate as the phenoxide bearing –CHCl₂ ortho, hydrolysed by NaOH to the aldehyde.\nKey idea: In NaOH the phenol is a phenoxide; the intermediate carries CHCl₂.","traps":["Neutral phenol vs phenoxide: Strongly basic medium → phenoxide.","Product salt taken as intermediate: The question asks the CHCl₂ species."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which among the following aldehydes is most reactive towards nucleophilic addition reactions?","options":["C₂H₅–CHO","H–CHO","C₃H₇–CHO","CH₃–CHO"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D): larger alkyl groups – more +I and steric hindrance; only a careless student picks them.\nWorking: formaldehyde has no electron-releasing alkyl group and least steric hindrance → most electrophilic carbonyl.\nKey idea: Fewer/smaller alkyl groups → more reactive carbonyl; HCHO is the top.","traps":["Alkyl size effect reversed: +I groups reduce δ+ on C."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Picric acid is 2,4,6 - trinitrotoluene.\nStatement II : Phenol - 2,4 - disulphonic acid is treated with Conc. HNO₃ to get picric acid.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): student skims 'trinitro' and accepts Statement I. (B)/(D): student unaware of the disulphonic acid route.\nWorking: picric acid is 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (TNT is trinitrotoluene); NCERT: phenol is first sulphonated to phenol-2,4-disulphonic acid, then treated with conc. HNO₃.\nKey idea: Picric acid = 2,4,6-trinitrophenol; TNT is the toluene analogue.","traps":["Trinitrophenol vs trinitrotoluene: Picric 'acid' must have phenolic OH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Consider the given reaction, identify the major product P.\nCH₃–COOH →[(i) LiAlH₄ (ii) PCC (iii) HCN/OH⁻ (iv) H₂O/OH⁻, Δ] \"P\"","options":["CH₃–CH(OH)–COOH","CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂","CH₃–C(=O)–CH₂CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–OH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): nitrile hydrolysis stopped at amide and OH lost. (C): assumes ketone/alkylation. (D): only the LiAlH₄ step with extra carbon.\nWorking: LiAlH₄ gives ethanol; PCC gives ethanal; HCN adds to give the cyanohydrin CH₃CH(OH)CN; hydrolysis of –CN gives 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid).\nKey idea: HCN adds one carbon; full hydrolysis turns –CN into –COOH, keeping the –OH.","traps":["Hydrolysis stopped at amide: With heat, nitrile hydrolyses fully to the acid (salt).","PCC over-oxidation assumed: PCC stops at the aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Anisole (C₆H₅OCH₃) →(HNO₃, H₂SO₄) 'A' →(Br₂ (excess), Fe) 'B'.\nThe major products formed : A and B respectively are :","options":["4-nitroanisole and 2,6-dibromo-4-nitroanisole","2-nitroanisole and 4-bromo-2-nitroanisole","4-nitroanisole and 2-bromo-4-nitroanisole","2-nitroanisole and 2,4-dibromo-6-nitroanisole"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): ortho-nitro taken as major. (C): ignores \"excess\" Br₂ (mono-bromination only).\nWorking: nitration of anisole gives mainly para-nitroanisole; with excess Br₂ both positions ortho to OCH₃ (also meta to NO₂) are brominated → 2,6-dibromo-4-nitroanisole.\nKey idea: OCH₃ and NO₂ both direct Br to the two positions ortho to OCH₃.","traps":["Ortho nitro taken as major: Para is major due to sterics of OCH₃.","\"Excess\" bromine ignored: Excess Br₂ fills both activated ortho positions."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Ethoxybenzene (C₆H₅OC₂H₅) →(HNO₃, H₂SO₄) P (major product) →(2Br₂, Fe) Q (major product).\nThe ratio of number of oxygen atoms to bromine atoms in the product Q is ______ × 10⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: 30 (only one Br), 10 (O of NO₂ missed or one O counted), 5 (Br/O inverted ×10).\nWorking: P = 4-nitrophenetole; Q = 2,6-dibromo-4-nitrophenetole; O atoms = 1 (OEt) + 2 (NO₂) = 3; Br = 2; ratio 1.5 = 15 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Q has 3 O and 2 Br.","traps":["Only one Br introduced: 2Br₂ means two substitutions.","Oxygen of the ether forgotten: Count ether O too: total 3."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Identify the major products A and B respectively in the following set of reactions.\nB ←(CH₃COCl, Pyridine)– 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol –(Conc. H₂SO₄, Δ)→ A","options":["A = methylenecyclohexane; B = 2-acetyl-1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (COCH₃ on ring C-2, OH retained)","A = 1-methylcyclohexene; B = 1-methylcyclohexyl acetate (O–COCH₃ on C-1)","A = methylenecyclohexane; B = 1-acetyl-1-methylcyclohexane (COCH₃ on C-1, no O)","A = 1-methylcyclohexene; B = 4-acetyl-1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (COCH₃ on ring C-4)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) exocyclic alkene (less substituted, minor); (A)/(C)/(D) acetyl on carbon as if Friedel–Crafts acylation of a saturated ring.\nWorking: acid dehydration of the 3° alcohol gives the more substituted endocyclic alkene (Saytzeff); CH₃COCl/pyridine acylates O–H to the ester.\nKey idea: Pyridine + RCOCl acylates oxygen; dehydration gives the more substituted alkene.","traps":["Exocyclic alkene chosen: Saytzeff: trisubstituted ring alkene is major.","C-acylation instead of O-acylation: Acid chloride reacts at the O–H."],"topics":["Dehydration","Alcohol reactions"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Identify the product (P) in the following reaction:\ncyclopentanecarboxylic acid –(i) Br₂/Red P; ii) H₂O)→ (P)","options":["cyclopentanecarbonyl bromide (C₅H₉–COBr)","1-bromocyclopentane-1-carbaldehyde","2-bromocyclopentane-1-carboxylic acid (Br on ring carbon next to the COOH carbon)","1-bromocyclopentane-1-carboxylic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) stops at acyl bromide (ignores H₂O step); (B) reduces COOH to CHO; (C) halogenates β-carbon.\nWorking: HVZ brominates the α-carbon (the ring carbon bearing COOH); water hydrolyses the acyl bromide intermediate back to the acid.\nKey idea: α-carbon = ring carbon carrying COOH.","traps":["Stopping at acyl bromide: H₂O step regenerates the acid.","Wrong carbon brominated: α = carbon attached to COOH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Major product B of the following reaction has ________ π-bond.\nEthylbenzene –(KMnO₄–KOH, Δ)→ (A) –(HNO₃/H₂SO₄)→ (B)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (missing N=O π), 6 (counting both N–O as double), 7 if dinitration assumed.\nWorking: side-chain oxidation gives benzoic acid/benzoate (A); nitration gives m-nitrobenzoic acid (B): 3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (N=O) = 5 π bonds.\nKey idea: Nitro group has one N=O π bond (other N–O is single in one resonance form).","traps":["Nitro π bonds miscounted: Count one π bond per Lewis structure.","Ethyl not fully oxidised: Whole side chain → COOH."],"topics":["Carboxylic acid reactions","Carboxylic acid preparation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I (Reactions): (A) Aniline —(i) NaNO₂ + HCl, (ii) H₂O, warm→ (B) Phenol —Na₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄→ (C) Phenol —(i) CHCl₃ + aq NaOH, (ii) H⁺→ (D) Phenol —(i) NaOH, (ii) CO₂, (iii) H⁺→\nList-II (Products): (I) Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde) (II) Phenol (III) Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid) (IV) p-Benzoquinone (cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps aniline route with Reimer-Tiemann (salicylaldehyde vs phenol). (C) treats diazonium hydrolysis as giving salicylic acid. (D) mixes up Kolbe (–COOH) and Reimer-Tiemann (–CHO).\nWorking: diazotisation then warm water gives phenol; dichromate oxidises phenol to p-benzoquinone; Reimer-Tiemann gives salicylaldehyde; Kolbe gives salicylic acid.\nKey idea: CHCl₃/NaOH puts –CHO, CO₂/NaOH puts –COOH ortho to OH; dichromate gives the para-quinone.","traps":["Kolbe vs Reimer-Tiemann product swap: CHCl₃ → CHO; CO₂ → COOH.","Diazonium hydrolysis product: Warm water on ArN₂⁺ gives ArOH."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Phenol preparation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which one of the following compounds will readily react with dilute NaOH ?","options":["C₆H₅CH₂OH","C₂H₅OH","C₆H₅OH","(CH₃)₃COH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) benzyl alcohol: the phenyl ring is not on the O-bearing carbon, so no phenoxide-type resonance. (B)/(D): ordinary alcohols do not react with NaOH.\nWorking: phenol (pKa ≈ 10) is acidic enough to form sodium phenoxide with NaOH; alcohols, including benzyl alcohol, are not.\nKey idea: Only OH on an aromatic ring is acidic enough for NaOH.","traps":["Benzyl alcohol thought to be phenol-like: OH must be directly on the ring."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of carboxylic acids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The correct sequence of acidic strength of the following aliphatic acids in their decreasing order is: CH₃CH₂COOH, CH₃COOH, CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH, HCOOH","options":["HCOOH > CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH > CH₃COOH","HCOOH > CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH","CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH > CH₃COOH > HCOOH","CH₃COOH > CH₃CH₂COOH > CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH > HCOOH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D): reverse or scrambled +I trend; (D) puts formic acid last as if H were electron-donating.\nWorking: the textbook order follows the +I effect of the alkyl group (formic > acetic > propanoic > butanoic); note measured pKa of butanoic (4.82) is slightly below propanoic (4.87), but no option reflects that.\nKey idea: Larger alkyl → more +I → weaker acid; H has no +I.","traps":["Formic acid placed weakest: H has no +I, so HCOO⁻ is least destabilised.","Propanoic vs butanoic real pKa: Use the textbook +I trend in JEE."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Two moles of benzaldehyde and one mole of acetone under alkaline conditions using aqueous NaOH after heating gives x as the major product. The number of π bonds in the product x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if only one condensation is taken (benzalacetone: 3 ring + 1 C=C + 1 C=O); 8 if the C=O is ignored; 3 if benzene π bonds are forgotten.\nWorking: double Claisen-Schmidt gives dibenzalacetone PhCH=CH-CO-CH=CHPh: 6 (rings) + 2 (C=C) + 1 (C=O) = 9 π bonds.\nKey idea: Each benzaldehyde adds one CH=CH; each benzene ring has 3 π bonds.","traps":["Single vs double condensation: 2 mol aldehyde reacts at both methyl groups.","π bonds of benzene: Count 3 per ring."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Identify the product A and product B in the following set of reactions.\nCH₃–CH=CH₂ (propene) —H₂O, H⁺→ Major Product A\nCH₃–CH=CH₂ —(i) (BH₃)₂ (ii) H₂O, H₂O₂, OH⁻→ Major Product B","options":["A – CH₃CH₂CH₂–OH; B – CH₃CH(OH)CH₃","A – CH₃CH(OH)CH₃; B – CH₃CH₂CH₂–OH","A – CH₃CH₂CH₂–OH; B – CH₃CH₂CH₂–OH","A – CH₃CH₂CH₃; B – CH₃CH₂CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps the two regiochemistries. (C) assumes both routes give the primary alcohol. (D) treats both as reduction to propane (H₂O/H₂O₂ misread).\nWorking: acid-catalysed hydration follows Markovnikov (propan-2-ol); hydroboration-oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov propan-1-ol.\nKey idea: H⁺/H₂O → Markovnikov (2° alcohol); BH₃ then H₂O₂/OH⁻ → anti-Markovnikov (1° alcohol).","traps":["Swapping Markovnikov and anti-Markovnikov routes: Boron goes to the less substituted carbon, then is replaced by OH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Identify major product \"X\" formed in the following reaction:\nBenzene —CO, HCl / anhydrous AlCl₃/CuCl→ X (Major Product)","options":["Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)","Benzophenone (C₆H₅COC₆H₅)","Benzoyl chloride (C₆H₅COCl)","Benzyl chloride (C₆H₅CH₂Cl)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) treats CO + HCl as forming COCl. (B) assumes a second Friedel–Crafts step. (D) confuses with chloromethylation.\nWorking: Gattermann–Koch formylation gives benzaldehyde.\nKey idea: CO + HCl acts as a source of HCO⁺ (formyl chloride equivalent) → –CHO.","traps":["Formyl vs acyl chloride product: Gattermann–Koch puts –CHO on the ring."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Benzene + succinic anhydride (cyclic anhydride) —AlCl₃→ A —Zn–Hg/HCl→ B —Conc. H₂SO₄→ C\nWhat is the structure of C?","options":["α-Tetralone (3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one; benzene ring fused to a cyclohexanone with C=O next to the ring)","4-Phenylbutanoic acid (C₆H₅CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH)","β-Tetralone (3,4-dihydronaphthalen-2(1H)-one; C=O one carbon away from the ring)","4-Oxo-4-phenylbutanoic acid (C₆H₅COCH₂CH₂COOH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is intermediate A (stops after step 1). (B) is intermediate B (4-phenylbutanoic acid), not yet cyclised. (C) places the ring C=O at the wrong carbon.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation gives 4-oxo-4-phenylbutanoic acid (A); Clemmensen gives 4-phenylbutanoic acid (B); conc. H₂SO₄ causes intramolecular acylation to α-tetralone.\nKey idea: Clemmensen reduces only the ketone; the acid then cyclises onto the ring ortho position to give the 1-tetralone.","traps":["Stopping at an intermediate: Carry every reagent through to C.","Position of ring carbonyl: Acylation puts C=O directly on the aromatic ring."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"For the given compounds, the correct order of increasing pKa value:\n(A) Phenol (C₆H₅OH)\n(B) 4-Nitrophenol (O₂N and OH para)\n(C) 3-Methoxyphenol (OH and OCH₃ meta)\n(D) 3-Nitrophenol (OH and NO₂ meta)\n(E) 4-Methoxyphenol (HO and OCH₃ para)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(E) < (D) < (C) < (B) < (A)","(B) < (D) < (A) < (C) < (E)","(D) < (E) < (C) < (B) < (A)","(E) < (D) < (B) < (A) < (C)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) reverse the scale (treat pKa as acidity). (C) puts m-nitro below p-nitro and p-methoxy near the top. A careful solver may hunt for C < A, which no option offers.\nWorking: (B) is the intended and only defensible option (p-NO₂ < m-NO₂ < phenol < m-OMe < p-OMe). Strictly, m-methoxyphenol (pKa ≈ 9.65) is slightly more acidic than phenol (≈ 10.0) because –OCH₃ acts only by –I at meta, so the exact order B < D < C < A < E is not offered.\nKey idea: Lowest pKa = strongest acid: p-NO₂ (−R, −I) < m-NO₂ (−I only) < phenol < methoxyphenols (intended as +R donors).","traps":["pKa vs acid strength direction: Strongest acid has the lowest pKa — list it first.","meta-OCH₃ effect: At meta only –I acts; options intend +R for both."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following compounds can give positive iodoform test when treated with aqueous KOH solution followed by potassium hypoiodite.","options":["CH₃CH₂–CO–CH₂CH₃ (pentan-3-one)","CH₃CH₂–CH(–O–)CH₂ (1,2-epoxybutane; three-membered ring with O)","CH₃CH₂–CCl₂–CH₃ (2,2-dichlorobutane)","CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO (butanal)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is a ketone but has no CH₃CO–. (B) opens to butane-1,2-diol (no CH₃CH(OH)–). (D) aldehyde without CH₃CO–.\nWorking: aq KOH hydrolyses the gem-dichloride to butan-2-one (CH₃CO–), which gives CHI₃ with KOI; the others give no CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)– unit.\nKey idea: gem-dihalide + aq KOH → C=O; check for CH₃–C(=O)– after the first step.","traps":["Any ketone gives iodoform: Need CH₃–CO– specifically.","Missing hydrolysis of gem-dichloride: aq KOH converts R₂CCl₂ to R₂C=O."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following compounds will give silver mirror with ammoniacal silver nitrate?\nA. Formic acid B. Formaldehyde C. Benzaldehyde D. Acetone\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["B and C only","C and D only","A, B and C only","A only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) omits formic acid. (B) includes acetone. (D) keeps only formic acid.\nWorking: formic acid (has –CHO character), formaldehyde and benzaldehyde reduce Tollens' reagent; acetone does not.\nKey idea: HCOOH contains an H–C=O unit and is a reducing agent.","traps":["Formic acid as reducing agent: HCOOH = H–C(=O)–OH has aldehydic H."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Major product of the following reaction is\np-NC–C₆H₄–CO₂CH₃ (methyl 4-cyanobenzoate) —(i) CH₃MgBr (excess), (ii) H₃O⁺→","options":["p-NC–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)₂","p-CH₃CO–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)₂ (1-[4-(2-hydroxypropan-2-yl)phenyl]ethan-1-one)","p-CH₃CO–C₆H₄–CO₂CH₃","p-NC–C₆H₄–COCH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) leaves CN unreacted. (C) reacts only the nitrile. (D) stops the ester at the ketone and leaves CN.\nWorking: excess CH₃MgBr adds twice to the ester (→ tertiary alcohol) and once to the nitrile (imine salt), which hydrolyses to a methyl ketone.\nKey idea: Ester → 3° alcohol (2 equiv); nitrile → ketone after H₃O⁺.","traps":["Nitrile assumed inert: Excess RMgX also attacks C≡N.","Ester stops at ketone: Ketone is more reactive, so a second RMgX adds."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Identify A and B in the following sequence of reaction\nToluene (C₆H₅CH₃) —[Cl₂/hν]→ A —[H₂O, 373 K]→ B","options":["A = C₆H₅CHCl₂ (benzal chloride), B = C₆H₅COOH (benzoic acid)","A = C₆H₅CHCl₂ (benzal chloride), B = C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde)","A = C₆H₅COCl (benzoyl chloride), B = C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde)","A = C₆H₅CH₂Cl (benzyl chloride), B = C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): hydrolysis of benzotrichloride gives benzoic acid; student pairs it with the dichloride. (C): confuses side-chain chlorination with forming an acid chloride. (D): benzyl chloride hydrolyses to benzyl alcohol, not benzaldehyde.\nWorking: Side-chain chlorination in light gives benzal chloride (intended product leading to aldehyde); hydrolysis at 373 K gives the gem-diol which loses water to benzaldehyde. Note: Cl₂/hν alone could give mono/di/tri chloro products; the options fix A as C₆H₅CHCl₂.\nKey idea: gem-dichloride + water → gem-diol → aldehyde.","traps":["Number of Cl atoms decides product: Match number of Cl on side chain to oxidation level of product."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Match List - I with List - II.\nList I (Reactions):\n(A) CH₃(CH₂)₅–C(=O)–OC₂H₅ → CH₃(CH₂)₅CHO\n(B) C₆H₅COC₆H₅ → C₆H₅CH₂C₆H₅\n(C) C₆H₅CHO → C₆H₅CH(OH)CH₃\n(D) CH₃COCH₂COOC₂H₅ → CH₃CH(OH)CH₂COOC₂H₅\nList II (Reagents):\n(I) CH₃MgBr, H₂O\n(II) Zn(Hg) and conc. HCl\n(III) NaBH₄, H⁺\n(IV) DIBAL-H, H₂O\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): pairs NaBH₄ with PhCHO → but product has an extra CH₃, so needs Grignard. (A)/(D): give DIBAL-H to the ketone→CH₂ step or NaBH₄ to ester → aldehyde.\nWorking: ester → aldehyde with DIBAL-H (IV); C=O → CH₂ by Clemmensen (II); PhCHO + CH₃MgBr → secondary alcohol (I); NaBH₄ reduces the ketone but not the ester (III).\nKey idea: Watch the carbon count: extra CH₃ means Grignard.","traps":["Missing extra carbon in (C): Count carbons: product has one more C.","Which reagent stops ester at aldehyde: DIBAL-H for ester → aldehyde; NaBH₄ does not touch ester."],"topics":["Reduction of carbonyl compounds","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List - I with List - II.\nList I (Reactants): (A) Phenol, Zn/Δ (B) Phenol, CHCl₃, NaOH, HCl (C) Phenol, CO₂, NaOH, HCl (D) Phenol, Conc. HNO₃\nList II (Product): (I) Salicylaldehyde (II) Salicylic acid (III) Benzene (IV) Picric acid\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C) swap benzene/picric acid; (C),(D) swap Reimer–Tiemann and Kolbe products.\nWorking: Zn dust → benzene; Reimer–Tiemann → salicylaldehyde; Kolbe → salicylic acid; conc. HNO₃ → picric acid.\nKey idea: CHCl₃ adds –CHO, CO₂ adds –COOH, both ortho.","traps":["Reimer–Tiemann vs Kolbe: CHCl₃ → aldehyde; CO₂ → acid."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"C₂H₅Br —[alc. KOH]→ A —[Br₂/CCl₄]→ B —[KCN (excess)]→ C —[H₃O⁺ (excess)]→ D\nAcid D formed in above reaction is :","options":["Malonic acid","Oxalic acid","Succinic acid","Gluconic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) malonic acid (3 C) if only one carbon is added. (B) oxalic acid (2 C) if CN carbons are ignored. (D) gluconic acid unrelated distractor.\nWorking: alc. KOH eliminates HBr to give ethene (A); Br₂/CCl₄ gives 1,2-dibromoethane (B); excess KCN gives NC–CH₂CH₂–CN (C); hydrolysis gives HOOC–CH₂CH₂–COOH, succinic acid (D).\nKey idea: Two CN groups add two carbons: C₂ → C₄ diacid.","traps":["Carbon count after double cyanation: Count: 2 + 2 = 4 carbons.","alc. KOH gives elimination: Alcoholic KOH → alkene."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol physical properties","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which among the following has highest boiling point?","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂–OH","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO","H₅C₂–O–C₂H₅"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) butanal (72 g mol⁻¹) has a strong C=O dipole but no H-bonding. (D) diethyl ether has the same molar mass (74 g mol⁻¹) as butan-1-ol but only a weak dipole. (B) butane lowest.\nWorking: butan-1-ol has intermolecular H-bonding; butanal has only dipole–dipole attraction; the ether and butane are weaker still.\nKey idea: Intermolecular H-bonding of –OH outweighs mass and dipole effects.","traps":["Higher molar mass chosen: Compare intermolecular forces first."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : p-nitrophenol is more acidic than m-nitrophenol and o-nitrophenol.\nStatement (II) : Ethanol will give immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): accepts immediate turbidity for ethanol (true only for 3° alcohols). (C)/(D): wrongly reject I by assuming o-nitrophenol is most acidic (–I effect closest) — intramolecular H-bond makes o slightly weaker than p.\nWorking: NCERT pKa values p-nitrophenol 7.1 < o-nitrophenol 7.2 < m-nitrophenol 8.3, so p is most acidic; ethanol is a primary alcohol and gives no turbidity at room temperature with Lucas reagent (only tertiary alcohols react immediately).\nKey idea: o-nitrophenol’s intramolecular H-bond holds the proton, so p is the most acidic isomer; 1° alcohols are slowest in Lucas test.","traps":["Ortho nitrophenol assumed most acidic: Use NCERT table: p 7.1, o 7.2, m 8.3; o is weakened by intramolecular H-bond.","Lucas test class swapped: Immediate = 3°, ~5 min = 2°, none at room temperature = 1°."],"topics":["Acidity of phenols","Alcohol reactions"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The ascending order of acidity of –OH group in the following compounds is :\n(A) Bu–OH\n(B) 4-nitrophenol (O₂N–C₆H₄–OH, para)\n(C) 4-methoxyphenol (MeO–C₆H₄–OH, para)\n(D) Phenol (C₆H₅OH)\n(E) 2,4-dinitrophenol (NO₂ para and ortho to OH)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) < (D) < (C) < (B) < (E)","(C) < (A) < (D) < (B) < (E)","(C) < (D) < (B) < (A) < (E)","(A) < (C) < (D) < (B) < (E)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts p-methoxyphenol above phenol (treats OMe as –I only). (B) and (C) put p-methoxyphenol below butanol — confusing a slightly weaker phenol with an alcohol.\nWorking: butanol (pKa ~16) < p-methoxyphenol (+R of OMe, ~10.2) < phenol (10.0) < p-nitrophenol (7.1) < 2,4-dinitrophenol (~4).\nKey idea: Any phenol is far more acidic than an alcohol; EDG (OMe) lowers acidity slightly, each NO₂ at o/p raises it.","traps":["OMe treated as electron-withdrawing: At para, +R of OMe dominates; p-methoxyphenol is weaker than phenol.","EDG phenol ranked below an alcohol: Phenoxide resonance keeps all phenols far above alcohols."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Major product formed in the following reaction is a mixture of : cyclohexyl tert-butyl ether, C₆H₁₁–O–C(CH₃)₃ —HI→ Major Product","options":["Iodocyclohexane (C₆H₁₁–I) and (CH₃)₃C–I","Cyclohexanol (C₆H₁₁–OH) and (CH₃)₃COH","Iodocyclohexane (C₆H₁₁–I) and (CH₃)₃COH","Cyclohexanol (C₆H₁₁–OH) and CH₃–C(CH₃)₂–I (tert-butyl iodide)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) iodides on both sides - assumes excess HI and both C–O cleavages. (B) both alcohols - no iodide formed, ignores I⁻. (C) SN2 logic applied: I⁻ attacks the \"less hindered\" secondary carbon.\nWorking: protonated ether cleaves by SN1 to give the stable tert-butyl cation, which takes I⁻; the cyclohexyl part leaves as cyclohexanol.\nKey idea: With a 3° group, cleavage goes through the 3° carbocation: 3° iodide + other alcohol.","traps":["SN2 rule applied to tertiary ether: Check for a 3° group first: then SN1, 3° iodide forms.","Excess HI assumption: One equivalent HI: one alcohol + one iodide."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List - I with List - II. List - I (Reaction): (A) phenol → 2-hydroxybenzoic acid (salicylic acid); (B) phenol → 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde); (C) phenol → p-benzoquinone (cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione); (D) phenol → anisole (methoxybenzene). List - II (Reagent(s)): (I) Na₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄; (II) (i) NaOH, (ii) CH₃Cl; (III) (i) NaOH, CHCl₃, (ii) NaOH, (iii) HCl; (IV) (i) NaOH, (ii) CO₂, (iii) HCl. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Kolbe with Williamson and RT with oxidation. (B) puts oxidation against RT product. (D) swaps Kolbe and Williamson reagents.\nWorking: Kolbe (NaOH, CO₂, H⁺) gives salicylic acid; Reimer-Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH, then acid) gives salicylaldehyde; chromic acid oxidises phenol to benzoquinone; phenoxide + CH₃Cl (Williamson) gives anisole.\nKey idea: CO₂ → –COOH; CHCl₃ → –CHO; Cr₂O₇²⁻ → quinone; CH₃Cl → –OCH₃.","traps":["Kolbe and Reimer-Tiemann reagents swapped: Carbon source decides: CO₂ gives acid, CHCl₃ gives aldehyde.","Williamson mixed with Kolbe: Alkyl halide gives an ether, CO₂ gives carboxylic acid."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Ether preparation","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The final product A, formed in the following reaction sequence is: Ph–CH=CH₂ —[(i) BH₃; (ii) H₂O₂, ⁻OH; (iii) HBr; (iv) Mg, ether, then HCHO/H₃O⁺]→ A","options":["Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH₂OH","Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH₃","Ph–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–OH","Ph–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes Markovnikov alcohol PhCH(OH)CH₃ → branched product. (B) branched alkane (Grignard hydrolysed, Markovnikov). (D) Grignard simply protonated, HCHO ignored.\nWorking: hydroboration-oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov PhCH₂CH₂OH; HBr gives PhCH₂CH₂Br; Grignard + HCHO adds one carbon: PhCH₂CH₂CH₂OH.\nKey idea: BH₃ puts OH on terminal carbon; HCHO adds CH₂OH.","traps":["Markovnikov hydration assumed: BH₃/H₂O₂ gives anti-Markovnikov alcohol.","Formaldehyde step overlooked: Grignard adds to C=O first, giving RCH₂OH."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The major product (P) in the following reaction is\n[Scheme: 1-ethoxy-3-vinylbenzene (benzene ring with –OCH₂CH₃ and, meta to it, –CH=CH₂) —con. HBr (excess), Heat→ (P) major product]","options":["3-(2-bromoethyl)phenol: ring with –OH and meta –CH₂CH₂Br","1-bromo-3-(1-bromoethyl)benzene: ring with –Br and meta –CH(Br)CH₃","3-(1-bromoethyl)phenol: ring with –OH and meta –CH(Br)CH₃","1-bromo-3-(2-bromoethyl)benzene: ring with –Br and meta –CH₂CH₂Br"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) anti-Markovnikov (peroxide-type) addition; (B)/(D) wrongly replace phenolic –OH by Br (aryl C–O cleavage).\nWorking: alkyl aryl ether is cleaved at the ethyl–O bond to give phenol + C₂H₅Br (aryl C–O not cleaved); HBr adds to the vinyl group by Markovnikov rule via the benzylic carbocation, giving –CH(Br)CH₃.\nKey idea: Aryl–O bond has partial double-bond character and stays; the benzylic carbocation directs Br to the carbon next to the ring.","traps":["Aryl C–O cleaved to give aryl bromide: Phenols do not give aryl halides with HX; only the alkyl–O bond breaks.","Anti-Markovnikov addition: No peroxide is given; benzylic cation intermediate gives –CHBrCH₃."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The final product A formed in the following multistep reaction sequence is\n[Scheme: styrene (C₆H₅–CH=CH₂) —(i) H₂O, H⁺; (ii) CrO₃; (iii) H₂N–NH₂, KOH, Heating→ A]","options":["Ethylbenzene, C₆H₅CH₂CH₃","Phenylacetic acid, C₆H₅CH₂COOH","Hydrazone of phenylacetaldehyde, C₆H₅CH₂CH=N–NH₂","Hydrazone of acetophenone, C₆H₅C(CH₃)=N–NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) assume anti-Markovnikov hydration giving a 1° alcohol/aldehyde; (C)/(D) stop at hydrazone, ignoring KOH/heat.\nWorking: acid-catalysed hydration (Markovnikov) → 1-phenylethanol; CrO₃ → acetophenone; Wolff–Kishner (N₂H₄/KOH, heat) reduces C=O to CH₂ → ethylbenzene.\nKey idea: KOH + heat converts the hydrazone all the way to CH₂ (loss of N₂).","traps":["Hydrazone taken as final product: With KOH and heating, the hydrazone loses N₂ (Wolff-Kishner).","Anti-Markovnikov hydration: Acid hydration gives benzylic secondary alcohol."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"From the compounds given below, number of compounds which give positive Fehling's test is _________.\nBenzaldehyde, Acetaldehyde, Acetone, Acetophenone, Methanal, 4-nitrobenzaldehyde, cyclohexane carbaldehyde.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if all aldehydes counted; 2 if cyclohexanecarbaldehyde wrongly treated as aromatic.\nWorking: aliphatic aldehydes acetaldehyde, methanal and cyclohexanecarbaldehyde reduce Fehling's; aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde, 4-nitrobenzaldehyde) and ketones do not.\nKey idea: Fehling's needs an aliphatic (incl. alicyclic) aldehyde; –CHO on benzene ring fails.","traps":["All aldehydes counted: Aromatic aldehydes do not reduce Fehling's.","Cyclohexane carbaldehyde seen as aromatic: Cyclohexane is saturated, so it is an aliphatic aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Identify the reagents used for the following conversion\n[scheme: methyl ester (a cyclohexane bearing OH at one ring carbon, a CH₂CO₂CH₃ arm and, on the adjacent ring carbon, a CH₂CHO arm) —(A)→ dialdehyde (both arms now CH₂CHO, OH retained) —(B)→ bicyclic α,β-unsaturated aldehyde (hydrindane: cyclopentene ring fused to the cyclohexanol ring, CHO on the ring C=C; intramolecular aldol condensation) —(C)→ final product: bicyclic diene with a methyl on the cyclopentene C=C (CHO reduced to CH₃) and the ring OH lost as a new C=C in the six-membered ring]","options":["A = LiAlH₄, B = NaOH(aq), C = NH₂–NH₂/KOH, ethylene glycol","A = DIBAL–H, B = NaOH(aq), C = NH₂–NH₂/KOH, ethylene glycol","A = LiAlH₄, B = NaOH(alc), C = Zn/HCl","A = DIBAL–H, B = NaOH(alc), C = Zn/HCl"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) LiAlH₄ over-reduces ester to alcohol; Wolff–Kishner keeps OH. (B) DIBAL-H correct but Wolff–Kishner keeps OH. (C) LiAlH₄ wrong in step A.\nWorking: ester → aldehyde needs DIBAL-H (LiAlH₄ would give alcohol); base-catalysed intramolecular aldol condensation gives the enal; Zn(Hg)/HCl (Clemmensen) reduces CHO to CH₃ and the acid also dehydrates the secondary alcohol to the new C=C — Wolff–Kishner (basic) would leave the OH.\nKey idea: Step C removes both the CHO oxygen and the OH: only acidic Zn/HCl does both (reduction + dehydration).","traps":["LiAlH₄ for ester to aldehyde: LiAlH₄ takes esters to alcohols; DIBAL-H stops at aldehyde.","Wolff–Kishner vs Clemmensen: Look at the product: OH lost as C=C → acidic conditions needed."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Phenol treated with chloroform in presence of sodium hydroxide, which further hydrolyzed in presence of an acid results","options":["Salicylic acid","Benzene-1,2-diol","2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde","Benzene-1,3-diol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) salicylic acid: Kolbe reaction product (CO₂/NaOH) or CCl₄ variant. (B)/(D) diols: assume OH is introduced.\nWorking: Reimer–Tiemann reaction introduces –CHO ortho to OH, giving salicylaldehyde.\nKey idea: CHCl₃/NaOH → dichlorocarbene → ortho-CHO.","traps":["Kolbe product chosen: CHCl₃ → aldehyde; CO₂ (or CCl₄) → acid."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList-I (Compound): (A) Ethanol (B) Phenol (C) m-Nitrophenol (D) p-Nitrophenol\nList-II (pKa value): (I) 10.0 (II) 15.9 (III) 7.1 (IV) 8.3\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives ethanol pKa 10 and phenol 15.9 (swaps alcohol/phenol). (B) ethanol 8.3 — nonsense. (D) misplaces all.\nWorking: ethanol 15.9, phenol 10.0, m-nitrophenol 8.3, p-nitrophenol 7.1 (–R of p-NO₂ lowers pKa more).\nKey idea: p-NO₂ acts by −R and −I; m-NO₂ only by −I, so p is more acidic (lower pKa).","traps":["m vs p nitro: Resonance withdrawal operates only from o/p."],"topics":["Acidity of phenols","Acidity of alcohols"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"C₆H₅COCl (benzoyl chloride) —H₂, Pd–BaSO₄→ C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde)\nThis reduction reaction is known as:","options":["Wolff-Kishner reduction","Etard reduction","Stephen reduction","Rosenmund reduction"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Wolff-Kishner: C=O → CH₂ with NH₂NH₂/KOH. (B) Etard: toluene + CrO₂Cl₂ → benzaldehyde (an oxidation; \"Etard reduction\" does not exist). (C) Stephen: nitrile + SnCl₂/HCl then H₃O⁺ → aldehyde.\nWorking: acyl chloride + H₂ over Pd poisoned with BaSO₄ → aldehyde is the Rosenmund reduction.\nKey idea: Pd–BaSO₄ + acyl chloride = Rosenmund.","traps":["Stephen vs Rosenmund: Stephen starts from nitrile (SnCl₂/HCl); Rosenmund from acyl chloride (H₂, Pd–BaSO₄)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"In the given reactions, identify the reagent A and reagent B.\nToluene —\"A\" + (CH₃CO)₂O, 273–283 K→ [Intermediate] —H₃O⁺, Δ→ benzaldehyde\nToluene —\"B\" + CS₂→ [Intermediate] —H₃O⁺→ benzaldehyde","options":["A – CrO₃   B – CrO₃","A – CrO₃   B – CrO₂Cl₂","A – CrO₂Cl₂   B – CrO₃","A – CrO₂Cl₂   B – CrO₂Cl₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps the reagents (classic swap). (A) and (D) use one reagent for both – plausible if only one method is remembered.\nWorking: CrO₃ in acetic anhydride at 273–283 K gives benzylidene diacetate, hydrolysed to benzaldehyde; CrO₂Cl₂ in CS₂ gives the chromium complex (Etard reaction), hydrolysed to benzaldehyde.\nKey idea: Acetic anhydride goes with CrO₃; CS₂ goes with CrO₂Cl₂ (Etard).","traps":["CrO₃ and CrO₂Cl₂ swapped: Etard = CrO₂Cl₂ in CS₂; anhydride traps the aldehyde as gem-diacetate with CrO₃."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ammonia-derivative reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The compound formed by the reaction of ethanal with semicarbazide contains _______ number of nitrogen atoms.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (thinking a nitrogen is lost as NH₃, or confusing with hydrazone), 1 (oxime-like).\nWorking: CH₃CHO + H₂N–NH–CO–NH₂ → CH₃CH=N–NH–CO–NH₂ (semicarbazone) + H₂O; all three N of semicarbazide are retained.\nKey idea: Only H₂O is lost; semicarbazide has 3 N.","traps":["N lost on condensation: Condensation removes H₂O only (O from C=O, H₂ from NH₂)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Salicylaldehyde is synthesized from phenol, when reacted with","options":["HCCl₃, NaOH","CO₂, NaOH","HCOCl (formyl chloride), NaOH","CCl₄, NaOH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Kolbe's reaction → salicylic acid. (C) formyl chloride is a look-alike formylating agent (Gattermann–Koch idea), not used. (D) CCl₄/NaOH gives salicylic acid (carboxylation).\nWorking: Reimer–Tiemann reaction – phenol + CHCl₃ + NaOH (via dichlorocarbene) gives o-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde) after acidification.\nKey idea: CHCl₃ → –CHO; CO₂ or CCl₄ → –COOH.","traps":["Kolbe vs Reimer–Tiemann: CO₂ gives salicylic acid; CHCl₃ gives salicylaldehyde.","CCl₄ instead of CHCl₃: CCl₄ variant ends at acid oxidation level."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"m-chlorobenzaldehyde on treatment with 50% KOH solution yields :","options":["m-chlorobenzoate ion (3-Cl-C₆H₄-COO⁻) + m-chlorobenzyl alcohol (3-Cl-C₆H₄-CH₂OH)","m-hydroxybenzoate ion (3-HO-C₆H₄-COO⁻) + m-hydroxybenzyl alcohol (3-HO-C₆H₄-CH₂OH)","3,3'-dichlorobenzoin: 3-Cl-C₆H₄-CH(OH)-CO-C₆H₄-3-Cl","m-hydroxybenzaldehyde + m-hydroxybenzaldehyde (3-HO-C₆H₄-CHO)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) assume Ar–Cl is substituted by OH⁻ (aryl halides are unreactive). (C) benzoin condensation needs CN⁻, not KOH.\nWorking: aldehyde with no α-H undergoes Cannizzaro disproportionation in conc. alkali; the aryl C–Cl is not hydrolysed under these conditions.\nKey idea: No α-H + conc. alkali ⇒ carboxylate + alcohol; the ring Cl is a spectator.","traps":["Aryl Cl replaced by OH: Haloarenes need 623 K/high pressure for OH substitution.","Benzoin condensation: Benzoin needs cyanide catalyst."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Products A and B formed in the following set of reactions are\nB ←[B₂H₆; H₂O₂, NaOH(aq)]— 1-methylcyclohexene —[H⁺/H₂O]→ A","options":["A = cyclohexylmethanol (C₆H₁₁–CH₂OH); B = 2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol","A = 2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol; B = 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol","A = cyclohexylmethanol; B = 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol","A = 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol; B = 2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) put OH on the methyl carbon (exocyclic CH₂OH) – impossible from this alkene. (B) swaps the two products.\nWorking: acid-catalysed hydration is Markovnikov via the 3° carbocation → 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol; hydroboration–oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (syn addition) → trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (stereo not shown in options).\nKey idea: H⁺/H₂O → OH on the more substituted C; BH₃ → OH on the less substituted C.","traps":["A and B swapped: Hydroboration = anti-Markovnikov.","OH on methyl carbon: OH can only add to the two alkene carbons."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:\nAssertion A: Alcohols react both as nucleophiles and electrophiles.\nReason R: Alcohols react with active metals such as sodium, potassium and aluminum to yield corresponding alkoxides and liberate hydrogen.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): both lines are from the same NCERT paragraph so the student links them. (D): student doubts 'aluminium' in R. (A): student thinks alcohols cannot be electrophiles.\nWorking: A is NCERT verbatim (O–H cleavage as nucleophile, C–O cleavage of protonated alcohol as electrophile). R is true (NCERT acidity reaction) but shows Brønsted acidity, not dual nucleophile/electrophile behaviour.\nKey idea: R illustrates acidity (O–H cleavage), not the electrophilic C–O cleavage needed to explain A.","traps":["Adjacent NCERT lines taken as cause–effect: Ask: does R show electrophilic behaviour? No.","Aluminium doubted in R: NCERT names sodium, potassium and aluminium."],"topics":["Alcohol reactions","Acidity of alcohols"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R:\nAssertion A: pKa value of phenol is 10.0 while that of ethanol is 15.9 .\nReason R: Ethanol is stronger acid than phenol.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): student thinks larger pKa means stronger acid. (A): doubts the table values.\nWorking: NCERT table gives pKa phenol 10.0, ethanol 15.9 (A true). Lower pKa means stronger acid, so phenol is stronger; R false.\nKey idea: Lower pKa → stronger acid; phenoxide is resonance-stabilised.","traps":["Higher pKa read as stronger acid: pKa ↓ ⇒ Ka ↑ ⇒ stronger."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The product of the following reaction is P.\nCompound (shown below) —[(i) PhMgBr (1 equiv.), (ii) aq. NH₄Cl]→ P\nThe number of hydroxyl groups present in the product P is ________.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (assuming addition to CHO gives a secondary alcohol plus the phenol OH).\nWorking: the phenolic O–H is acidic and destroys the single equivalent of PhMgBr (→ benzene + ArO⁻MgBr⁺) before addition to CHO; work-up regenerates 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde, so P has 1 OH.\nKey idea: 1 equiv. Grignard is used up by the most acidic H first.","traps":["Addition assumed before acid–base: Check for acidic H and count equivalents."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Jan31S1C22-s.webp"],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Acidity of phenols"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Identify major product 'P' formed in the following reaction. Benzene + benzoyl chloride (C₆H₅COCl) —(anhydrous AlCl₃)→ 'P' (major product)","options":["3-phenoxybenzoyl chloride: C₆H₅–O–C₆H₄–COCl (meta)","Benzaldehyde, C₆H₅CHO","Benzophenone, C₆H₅COC₆H₅","Acetophenone, C₆H₅COCH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) invents an ether linkage. (B) is a Gattermann–Koch / Rosenmund product. (D) is the product with acetyl chloride, not benzoyl chloride.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation of benzene by benzoyl chloride gives benzophenone.\nKey idea: Acyl group of the acid chloride is transferred intact to the ring: Ph–CO–Ph.","traps":["Acyl group identity mixed: Copy the R of RCOCl into the ketone."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Identify the name reaction. Benzene —(CO, HCl; anhyd. AlCl₃/CuCl)→ benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)","options":["Stephen Reaction","Etard Reaction","Gatterman - Koch Reaction","Rosenmund Reduction"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Stephen: nitrile + SnCl₂/HCl. (B) Etard: toluene + CrO₂Cl₂. (D) Rosenmund: acyl chloride + H₂/Pd–BaSO₄. All also give aldehydes.\nWorking: CO + HCl with anhydrous AlCl₃/CuCl formylates benzene – Gattermann–Koch.\nKey idea: CO + HCl → Gattermann–Koch.","traps":["All options give aldehydes: Link reagent set: CO/HCl ↔ Gattermann–Koch."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Named reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Named reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Name of reaction): A. Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction B. Iodoform reaction C. Etard reaction D. Gatterman-Koch reaction\nLIST II (Reagent used): I. NaOH + I₂ II. (i) CrO₂Cl₂, CS₂ (ii) H₂O III. (i) Br₂ / red phosphorus (ii) H₂O IV. CO, HCl, anhyd. AlCl₃\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Etard and Gattermann–Koch reagents. (C)/(D) misassign iodoform and HVZ.\nWorking: HVZ – Br₂/red P then H₂O; iodoform – NaOH + I₂; Etard – CrO₂Cl₂ in CS₂ then H₂O; Gattermann–Koch – CO, HCl, anhydrous AlCl₃.\nKey idea: Etard = CrO₂Cl₂; GK = CO/HCl/AlCl₃.","traps":["Etard vs Gattermann–Koch: Etard oxidises toluene's CH₃ (CrO₂Cl₂); GK formylates benzene (CO/HCl)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Find out the major product from the following reaction.\n3-Methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one —[1) MeMgBr/CuI; 2) nPrI]→ ?","options":["3,3-dimethyl-2-propylcyclohexan-1-one","1,3-dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-ol (tertiary allylic alcohol from 1,2-addition; OH and Me on C1, ring C=C retained)","5,5-dimethyl-2-propylcyclohexan-1-one (propyl on the other α-carbon)","1,1,3-trimethyl-3-propylcyclohexane (no carbonyl)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the plain Grignard 1,2-addition product (ignores CuI). (C) alkylates the other α-carbon (thermodynamic/kinetic enolate thinking instead of the enolate formed by conjugate addition). (D) removes the carbonyl and puts both groups on carbon, mixing up addition modes.\nWorking: CuI converts MeMgBr into an organocuprate that adds 1,4 (conjugate) to the enone, putting Me on C3 (gem-dimethyl) and giving the enolate C1=C2; this specific enolate is alkylated by n-PrI at C2, giving 3,3-dimethyl-2-propylcyclohexanone.\nKey idea: R₂CuMgBr adds to the β-carbon; the enolate formed sits between C=O and the new quaternary carbon, so the electrophile enters there.","traps":["CuI ignored – 1,2 addition assumed: Cu(I) with RMgX ⇒ soft cuprate ⇒ conjugate (1,4) addition to enones.","Alkylation at the wrong α-carbon: The enolate double bond is C1=C2 formed by conjugate addition; alkylation occurs at C2."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Enolates & aldol reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The strongest acid from the following is\n(A) m-cresol (3-methylphenol) (B) m-nitrophenol (3-nitrophenol) (C) phenol (D) m-chlorophenol (3-chlorophenol)","options":["3-methylphenol","3-nitrophenol","phenol","3-chlorophenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) m-Cl is acid-strengthening but weaker –I than NO₂; (A) m-CH₃ (+I) is the weakest; (C) phenol is the reference.\nWorking: at the meta position only inductive effects act; –NO₂ (strongest –I) > –Cl (–I) > H > –CH₃ (+I). pKa: m-nitrophenol 8.3 < m-chlorophenol ~9.0 < phenol 10.0 < m-cresol 10.1.\nKey idea: At meta, compare –I only: NO₂ > Cl.","traps":["Cl judged stronger than NO₂ at meta: Group –I of NO₂ (formal + on N) exceeds that of Cl."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Among the following, the number of compounds which will give positive iodoform reaction is ________\n(a) 1-Phenylbutan-2-one\n(b) 2-Methylbutan-2-ol\n(c) 3-Methylbutan-2-ol\n(d) 1-Phenylethanol\n(e) 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-one\n(f) 1-Phenylpropan-2-ol","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if 1-phenylbutan-2-one is wrongly read as a methyl ketone (C=O at C2 but C1 bears phenyl); 3 if pinacolone is rejected because of steric bulk; 6 if tertiary alcohol (b) is counted.\nWorking: positive – (c) CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂, (d) C₆H₅CH(OH)CH₃, (e) CH₃COC(CH₃)₃ (pinacolone), (f) C₆H₅CH₂CH(OH)CH₃ = 4. Negative – (a) 1-phenylbutan-2-one is C₆H₅CH₂COCH₂CH₃ (no CH₃CO), (b) 2-methylbutan-2-ol is tertiary (cannot oxidise to a methyl ketone).\nKey idea: Need CH₃ directly on C=O or on CH(OH); check both ends of each carbonyl/carbinol carbon.","traps":["1-Phenylbutan-2-one taken as methyl ketone: C1 carries phenyl, C3 is CH₂CH₃: no CH₃CO.","Pinacolone rejected: Only the CH₃ side reacts; pinacolone gives CHI₃."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is:\n[Reactant: benzene ring with two ortho side chains: –CH₂CH₂CO₂H and –CH₂CO₂Et, i.e. 3-[2-(2-ethoxy-2-oxoethyl)phenyl]propanoic acid] —(i) LiBH₄, EtOH; (ii) H₃O⁺→ major product","options":["benzene with ortho –CH₂CH₂CH₂OH and –CH₂CH₂OH (both groups reduced; diol)","benzene with ortho –CH₂CH₂CO₂H and –CH₂CO₂H (ester hydrolysed to diacid)","benzene with ortho –CH₂CH₂CH₂OH and –CH₂CO₂Et (acid reduced, ester intact)","benzene with ortho –CH₂CH₂CO₂H and –CH₂CH₂OH (ester reduced, acid intact)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): treats LiBH₄ like LiAlH₄ (reduces both). (B): thinks acid workup only hydrolyses the ester. (C): reverses the selectivity (borane-type thinking reduces acids first).\nWorking: LiBH₄ reduces esters to primary alcohols but does not reduce carboxylic acids (acid only forms carboxylate); –CH₂CO₂Et → –CH₂CH₂OH, –CO₂H retained → 3-[2-(2-hydroxyethyl)phenyl]propanoic acid.\nKey idea: Borohydrides reduce esters (LiBH₄) but leave free COOH untouched.","traps":["LiBH₄ treated as LiAlH₄: LiBH₄ is milder: esters yes, acids no.","Selectivity reversed (borane logic): LiBH₄ ≠ BH₃; the acid just becomes carboxylate."],"topics":["Acid derivatives","Carboxylic acid reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A compound 'X' when treated with phthalic anhydride in presence of concentrated H₂SO₄ yields 'Y'. 'Y' is used as an acid/base indicator. 'X' and 'Y' are respectively","options":["Toluidine, Phenolphthalein","Carbolic acid, Phenolphthalein","Salicylaldehyde, Phenolphthalein","Anisole, methyl orange"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) toluidine is an amine (not the phenol needed); (C) salicylaldehyde is a phenol but the aldehyde does not give phenolphthalein; (D) methyl orange is an azo dye made by diazo coupling, not from phthalic anhydride.\nWorking: two phenol (carbolic acid) molecules condense with phthalic anhydride under conc. H₂SO₄ (para C-acylation/Friedel–Crafts type) to give phenolphthalein.\nKey idea: \"Phenol + phthal(ic anhydride)\" → phenolphthalein; the name itself gives the reactants.","traps":["Carbolic acid not recognised as phenol: Carbolic acid = phenol (C₆H₅OH)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of carboxylic acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The descending order of acidity for the following carboxylic acid is - A. CH₃COOH; B. F₃C–COOH; C. ClCH₂–COOH; D. FCH₂–COOH; E. BrCH₂–COOH. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B > C > D > E > A","D > B > A > E > C","B > D > C > E > A","E > D > B > A > C"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Cl above F (size/polarisability confusion); (B) and (D) put mono-halo acids above CF₃COOH or acetic acid above chloroacetic acid.\nWorking: three F atoms > one F > Cl > Br > H (–I effect order), so CF₃COOH > FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH > BrCH₂COOH > CH₃COOH.\nKey idea: –I strength: F > Cl > Br, and more halogens → stronger acid.","traps":["Chloro ranked above fluoro: For α-halo acids only –I matters: F strongest."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In the reaction given below 'A' is\nN-methylpyrrolidin-2-one (five-membered lactam: ring N bearing Me, C=O adjacent to N) —(i) NaOH, Δ; (ii) H⁺→ 'A' (major product)","options":["MeHN–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CHO (4-(methylamino)butanal)","N-methylpyrrolidine-2,2-diol (gem-diol at C-2 of the ring)","1-methyl-4,5-dihydro-1H-pyrrol-2-ol (enol form: ring C2=C3 double bond with OH on C2)","MeHN–CH₂CH₂CH₂–COOH (4-(methylamino)butanoic acid)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): reduction-level product (aldehyde) – wrong oxidation level. (B): gem-diol from nucleophilic addition without C–N cleavage (treating the C=O like a ketone). (C): enol tautomer of the lactam – no reaction at all.\nWorking: alkaline hydrolysis of the cyclic amide (lactam) opens the ring to the carboxylate and the secondary amine; acidification gives 4-(methylamino)butanoic acid (as written, ignoring zwitterion/ammonium salt formation).\nKey idea: Amide + NaOH/Δ then H⁺ → acid + amine; in a ring both ends stay in one molecule.","traps":["Lactam C=O treated like a ketone: Spot N next to C=O: it is an amide, hydrolysis breaks C–N.","Ring-opened product at the wrong oxidation level: Hydrolysis keeps the carbonyl carbon at acid level."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"For the given reaction\n'A' = (CH₃)₃C–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)₂ (2,2,4-trimethylpentan-3-ol) —(H⁺, Δ)→\nThe total number of possible products formed by tertiary carbocation of A is ________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 or 2: counts alkenes from only one of the two tertiary cations (or misses a terminal alkene). 5–6: adds alkenes from a second shift, the unrearranged 2° cation, or non-existent E/Z isomers.\nWorking: the 2° cation at C-3 reaches a 3° cation by two 1,2-shifts. Hydride shift from C-4 gives (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–C⁺(CH₃)₂ → 2,4,4-trimethylpent-2-ene and 2,4,4-trimethylpent-1-ene; methyl shift from C-2 gives (CH₃)₂C⁺–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂ → 2,3,4-trimethylpent-2-ene and 2,3,4-trimethylpent-1-ene. None shows E/Z isomerism, so 4 alkenes from the tertiary cations = 4. (A second-step hydride shift to the C-3 tertiary cation could add 2,4-dimethyl-3-methylidenepentane, but the intended single-shift count is 4.)\nKey idea: Identify every tertiary cation reachable by one 1,2-shift and list distinct alkenes from each.","traps":["Which tertiary cation is meant: List both shifts, then read the question's scope carefully.","Substitution product counted or not: Under Δ elimination dominates; state the assumption."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Decreasing order of dehydration of the following alcohols is\n(Alcohols a–d are shown below.)","options":["d > b > c > a","b > d > c > a","a > d > b > c","b > a > d > c"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts allylic d above b, missing that b gives aromatic benzene via a pentadienyl cation. (C) puts a first; a (phenol) is slowest. (D) places a second.\nWorking: b (cyclohexa-2,4-dien-1-ol) loses water to give a pentadienyl cation that becomes aromatic benzene on losing H⁺, so it dehydrates fastest; d (cyclohex-2-en-1-ol) gives a resonance-stabilised allylic cation; c (cyclohex-3-en-1-ol) gives only an ordinary secondary cation; a (phenol) does not dehydrate. Order: b > d > c > a.\nKey idea: b loses water to give a pentadienyl cation and then benzene (aromatic driving force); d gives an allylic cation; c only a secondary cation; a is least able.","traps":["Aromatic driving force ignored: Ask what product forms: b gives benzene.","Structure a misread: Either way a has no stabilised cation and is slowest."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S1C11-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol esterification & aspirin","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In a reaction, methyl salicylate (2-HO-C₆H₄-COOCH₃) ←['Y']— salicylic acid (2-HO-C₆H₄-COOH) —['X']→ acetylsalicylic acid (2-CH₃COO-C₆H₄-COOH). Reagents 'X' and 'Y' respectively are :","options":["CH₃OH/H⁺, Δ and (CH₃CO)₂O/H⁺","(CH₃CO)₂O/H⁺ and CH₃OH/H⁺, Δ","CH₃OH/H⁺, Δ and CH₃OH/H⁺, Δ","(CH₃CO)₂O/H⁺ and (CH₃CO)₂O/H⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps X and Y (arrow-direction slip). (C) and (D) use one reagent for both changes - one product would be wrong.\nWorking: X converts the phenolic –OH into –OCOCH₃ (acetylation), so X = (CH₃CO)₂O/H⁺; Y converts –COOH into –COOCH₃ (Fischer esterification), so Y = CH₃OH/H⁺, Δ.\nKey idea: Group changed tells the reagent: OH→OCOCH₃ needs an acylating agent; COOH→COOCH₃ needs an alcohol.","traps":["X and Y swapped by arrow direction: Label each arrow with the group that changes before choosing.","Same reagent assumed for both: Ask which partner supplies the acyl part: anhydride for phenol-ester, alcohol for acid-ester."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The structures of major products A, B and C in the following reaction sequence are : 2-methylpentanal (CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CHO) —[NaHSO₃, dil. HCl; NaCN, H₂O]→ [A]; [A] —[LiAlH₄]→ [B]; [A] —[HCl/H₂O, Δ]→ [C]","options":["A = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CN, B = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CH₂NH₂, C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CO₂H","A = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CN, B = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CHO, C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CO₂H","A = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)SO₃H, B = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OH, C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)SO₂Cl","A = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OSO₃Na, B = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OH, C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂Cl"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) thinks LiAlH₄ stops at aldehyde (that is DIBAL-H). (C) keeps the bisulphite adduct as product and invents SO₂Cl. (D) treats NaHSO₃ as a reducing/sulphating agent.\nWorking: the bisulphite adduct reacts with NaCN to give the cyanohydrin A; LiAlH₄ reduces C≡N to CH₂NH₂ (B); acid hydrolysis converts CN to CO₂H (C).\nKey idea: NaHSO₃ then NaCN is a safe way to add HCN; the cyanohydrin then gives amino alcohol (LiAlH₄) or α-hydroxy acid (H₃O⁺).","traps":["Bisulphite adduct taken as final A: NaCN displaces the sulphite to give the cyanohydrin.","LiAlH₄ stopping at aldehyde: LiAlH₄ reduces RCN fully to RCH₂NH₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"'A' and 'B' formed in the following set of reactions are :\n(i) 3-(hydroxymethyl)phenol [benzene ring with OH and CH₂OH meta to each other] —HBr, Δ→ A\n(ii) 3-methoxyphenol [benzene ring with OH and OCH₃ meta to each other] —HBr, Δ→ B","options":["A = 3-bromobenzyl alcohol (ring Br and CH₂OH, meta); B = 1,3-dibromobenzene","A = 3-(bromomethyl)phenol (ring OH and CH₂Br, meta); B = 3-bromoanisole (ring Br and OCH₃, meta)","A = 3-(bromomethyl)phenol (ring OH and CH₂Br, meta); B = benzene-1,3-diol (resorcinol)","A = 3-(bromomethyl)phenol (ring OH and CH₂Br, meta); B = 3-bromophenol (ring OH and Br, meta)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) replaces phenolic OH by Br (impossible) and leaves CH₂OH. (B) gives ring bromination/keeps OCH₃: confuses ether cleavage with substitution. (D) B = 3-bromophenol: cleaves the wrong bond (aryl–O instead of methyl–O).\nWorking: HBr converts the benzylic CH₂OH to CH₂Br but cannot replace phenolic OH; it cleaves the aryl methyl ether at the O–CH₃ bond to give ArOH + CH₃Br, so B = resorcinol.\nKey idea: Ar–O bonds survive HX; only sp³ C–O bonds (CH₂–OH, CH₃–OAr) break.","traps":["Phenolic OH treated like alcoholic OH: Aryl C–O has partial double-bond character; HX never replaces phenolic OH.","Wrong bond broken in aryl methyl ether: HX attacks the methyl carbon (SN2); phenol is released."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"'R' formed in the following sequence of reactions is :\n4-chloroacetophenone [p-ClC₆H₄COCH₃] —NaCN, HOAc→ 'P' —EtOH, H⁺→ 'Q' —(i) 2MeMgBr, (ii) H₃O⁺→ 'R' (major product)","options":["p-NC–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)–C(CH₃)₃ (ring CN; side chain C(OH)Me attached to CMe₃)","2-(4-cyanophenyl)propan-2-ol [p-NC–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)₂]","2-(4-chlorophenyl)-3-methylbutane-2,3-diol [p-Cl–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)–C(OH)(CH₃)₂]","2-(4-chlorophenyl)propan-2-ol [p-Cl–C₆H₄–C(OH)(CH₃)₂]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) assume CN⁻ replaces aryl Cl (aryl halides do not undergo SN with NaCN). (D) ignores the cyanohydrin and lets MeMgBr add directly to the ketone.\nWorking: P = cyanohydrin ArC(OH)(CH₃)CN; EtOH/H⁺ converts CN to the ethyl ester Q = ArC(OH)(CH₃)CO₂Et; two MeMgBr add to the ester giving ArC(OH)(CH₃)C(OH)(CH₃)₂. (Strictly a third equivalent is consumed by the free OH; the intended answer is unaffected.)\nKey idea: EtOH/H⁺ on R–CN gives R–CO₂Et; 2 RMgX on an ester → 3° alcohol with two new identical groups.","traps":["Aryl chloride replaced by cyanide: Aryl–Cl is inert to SN under these conditions; CN⁻ adds to C=O.","Fate of nitrile under EtOH/H⁺: Nitrile → ester (Pinner), then 2 MeMgBr → C(OH)Me₂."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Compound (X) undergoes following sequence of reactions to give the Lactone (Y).\nCompound (X) —(i) HCHO, KOH; (ii) KCN (alc); (iii) H₃O⁺→ Lactone (Y)\nY: five-membered lactone (γ-lactone): ring C(=O)–O–CH₂–C(CH₃)₂–CH(OH)–, i.e. 3-hydroxy-4,4-dimethyloxolan-2-one (pantolactone).\nCompound (X) is :","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–CHO (2-methylpropanal)","HOCH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CHO","HOCH₂–C(CH₃)₂–CHO","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CHO (butanal)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) is the aldol intermediate, not X (it has no α-H to react with HCHO). (B) a hydroxy-aldehyde that would give a larger, unmethylated lactone. (D) butanal would place one ethyl, not gem-dimethyl, in the ring.\nWorking: crossed aldol of isobutyraldehyde with HCHO gives HOCH₂C(CH₃)₂CHO; KCN gives the cyanohydrin; acid hydrolysis gives the γ-hydroxy α-hydroxy acid which lactonises to pantolactone.\nKey idea: Gem-dimethyl carbon next to CH₂O in the lactone = former α-carbon of isobutyraldehyde that picked up CH₂OH from HCHO.","traps":["Picking the aldol intermediate: X must still react with HCHO in step (i); (C) has no α-H.","Cannizzaro vs aldol with HCHO/KOH: With an α-H aldehyde and HCHO, aldol at the α-carbon comes first."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Find out the major products from the following reactions. Alkene (CH₃CH₂–C(CH₃)=CH₂, 2-methylbut-1-ene) → BH₃, THF; H₂O₂/OH⁻ → A. Same alkene → Hg(OAc)₂, H₂O; NaBH₄ → B.","options":["A = 2-methylbutan-1-ol [CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OH]; B = 2-methylbutan-1-ol (same primary alcohol)","A = 2-methylbutan-1-ol [CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂OH]; B = 2-methylbutan-2-ol [CH₃CH₂C(OH)(CH₃)₂]","A = 2-methylbutan-2-ol (tertiary alcohol); B = 2-methylbutan-1-ol","A = 2-methylbutan-2-ol; B = 2-methylbutan-2-ol (tertiary alcohol for both)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): treats both as anti-Markovnikov. (C): swaps the two regiochemistries. (D): treats both as Markovnikov.\nWorking: hydroboration–oxidation of 2-methylbut-1-ene gives anti-Markovnikov primary alcohol (2-methylbutan-1-ol); oxymercuration–demercuration gives Markovnikov tertiary alcohol (2-methylbutan-2-ol) without rearrangement.\nKey idea: BH₃ puts OH on the less substituted CH₂ (primary); Hg(OAc)₂/NaBH₄ puts OH on the more substituted carbon (tertiary).","traps":["Hydroboration regiochemistry: Boron goes to the less hindered carbon; OH replaces boron.","Oxymercuration unknown: Learn: oxymercuration = Markovnikov, no rearrangement."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: H₂N–CO–CH₂CH₂–CO–CH₃ (4-oxopentanamide) under Clemmensen reduction conditions will give HOOC–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃ (pentanoic acid). Statement II: (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–CH(Cl)–CO–CH₃ (3-chloro-5-methylhexan-2-one) under Wolff-Kishner reduction conditions will give (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–CH(Cl)–CH₂CH₃ (4-chloro-2-methylhexane). In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): assumes reagents touch only C=O. (B): thinks amide survives Clemmensen. (D): misses that statement I's product already shows amide hydrolysis.\nWorking: conc. HCl of Clemmensen hydrolyses the amide to –COOH while C=O → CH₂; Wolff-Kishner's hot KOH/glycol would attack the C–Cl (substitution/elimination), so the chloro product is not retained.\nKey idea: Choose Clemmensen for base-sensitive and Wolff-Kishner for acid-sensitive substrates; each reagent also acts on other groups.","traps":["Amide under conc. HCl: Hot conc. HCl hydrolyses amides to acids.","C–Cl under hot KOH: Strong base at 450 K attacks alkyl halides."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"P (n-heptane) —?→ PhCOOH (Q) + PhCH₂OH (R). The correct sequence of reagents for the preparation of Q and R is :","options":["(i) Cr₂O₃, 770 K, 20 atm; (ii) CrO₂Cl₂, H₃O⁺; (iii) NaOH; (iv) H₃O⁺","(i) CrO₂Cl₂, H₃O⁺; (ii) Cr₂O₃, 770 K, 20 atm; (iii) NaOH; (iv) H₃O⁺","(i) KMnO₄, OH⁻; (ii) Mo₂O₃, Δ; (iii) NaOH; (iv) H₃O⁺","(i) Mo₂O₃, Δ; (ii) CrO₂Cl₂, H₃O⁺; (iii) NaOH; (iv) H₃O⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) applies Etard first, on an alkane that has no benzylic CH₃ – order reversed. (C) starts with KMnO₄ oxidation of an alkane (no reaction) and has no step making the aldehyde needed for Cannizzaro. (D) uses Mo₂O₃ without the high pressure; it looks almost identical to (A) – the one-entry difference.\nWorking: n-heptane is aromatised to toluene over Cr₂O₃ at 770 K and 20 atm; the Etard reaction (CrO₂Cl₂, then H₃O⁺) converts toluene to benzaldehyde; Cannizzaro reaction with NaOH gives benzyl alcohol (R) and sodium benzoate, which H₃O⁺ converts to benzoic acid (Q). NCERT also names Mo₂O₃ as an aromatisation catalyst, so (D) is chemically very close; it differs only in giving no pressure, which is why (A) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: Only a non-enolisable aldehyde gives acid + alcohol in one go – so benzaldehyde must be made first (Etard on toluene), and toluene must come from heptane by aromatisation.","traps":["Etard used before an aromatic methyl exists: Write the intermediate after each step; CrO₂Cl₂ needs a benzylic CH₃.","Mo₂O₃ option almost the same as Cr₂O₃ option: Choose the option with the full NCERT conditions (oxide catalyst, ~773 K, 10–20 atm)."],"topics":["Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","Aldehyde & ketone preparation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In the cumene to phenol preparation in presence of air, the intermediate is","options":["Cumene (isopropylbenzene)","Isopropyl phenyl ether","Phenyl acetate","Cumene hydroperoxide (2-phenylpropan-2-yl hydroperoxide)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the starting material, not the intermediate. (B) is an ether that wrongly puts O between ring and isopropyl (looks like the rearranged species). (C) phenyl acetate mixes the two products (phenol + acetone fragment).\nWorking: air oxidises the benzylic C–H of cumene to cumene hydroperoxide, C₆H₅C(CH₃)₂–O–O–H; dilute acid then rearranges and cleaves it to phenol and acetone. So the intermediate is cumene hydroperoxide (D).\nKey idea: Air oxidises the benzylic C–H of cumene to C–OOH; acid then splits it into phenol and acetone.","traps":["Starting material chosen as intermediate: Ask which species forms after air oxidation – it must contain O–O–H.","Ether drawn to mimic the migration step: The air step adds O₂ at the benzylic carbon; the ring–O bond appears only later in the acid step."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Assertion A : Acetal / Ketal is stable in basic medium. Reason R : The high leaving tendency of alkoxide ion gives the stability to acetal/ketal in basic medium. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(B) accept R, which reverses the truth: RO⁻ is a poor leaving group. (A) rejects A, forgetting acetals are used as protecting groups under basic conditions.\nWorking: acetals/ketals are not cleaved by base, because base cannot protonate the OR oxygen and RO⁻ is a strong base, i.e. a poor leaving group. So A is true, but R ('high leaving tendency' of alkoxide) is false.\nKey idea: Base cannot protonate the OR group, and RO⁻ is a strong base and poor leaving group, so no cleavage occurs.","traps":["'High leaving tendency' of alkoxide accepted: Strong bases (RO⁻) are poor leaving groups; stability comes from the poor leaving tendency.","Acetal thought unstable in base: Hydrolysis needs acid; base leaves acetals intact."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"'A' in the given reaction is: 2,2-dimethylpropanal (pivalaldehyde, (CH₃)₃C–CHO) + 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanoic acid ((CH₃)₂C(OH)–COOH) —H⁺→ 'A' (major).","options":["Five-membered ring O–CH(t-Bu)–O–C(CH₃)₂–CH(OH)–: 2-tert-butyl-5,5-dimethyl-1,3-dioxolan-4-ol (ring carbon bearing OH instead of C=O)","Open-chain hemiacetal: (CH₃)₃C–CH(OH)–O–C(CH₃)₂–COOH","Ester HO–C(CH₃)₂–C(=O)–O–C(CH₃)₃ (tert-butyl 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanoate)","Five-membered ring O–CH(t-Bu)–O–C(=O)–C(CH₃)₂–: 2-tert-butyl-5,5-dimethyl-1,3-dioxolan-4-one"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) keeps the ring but turns the lactone C=O into CH–OH, as if the acid were reduced; (B) stops at the open hemiacetal and leaves COOH unused; (C) is a Fischer-type ester with tert-butanol, which is not a reactant (the t-Bu group belongs to the aldehyde).\nWorking: H⁺ activates the aldehyde C=O; the OH and the COOH of 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanoic acid act like the two OH groups of a diol and close a five-membered ring around the aldehyde carbon with loss of water, giving 2-tert-butyl-5,5-dimethyl-1,3-dioxolan-4-one (D).\nKey idea: Treat (CH₃)₂C(OH)COOH like ethylene glycol: two O–H groups 1,2 to each other close a five-membered acetal ring around the aldehyde carbon.","traps":["Carboxyl OH as acetal partner: Look for any two O–H groups that can reach the same carbonyl carbon and close a 5- or 6-ring.","t-Bu group mistaken for tert-butanol: Track which reactant each fragment comes from; no alcohol t-BuOH is present."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Find out the major product from the following reaction: 2,2,6-trimethylcyclohexan-1-ol (cyclohexane ring; C-1 bears OH, C-2 bears a gem-dimethyl group, C-6 bears one methyl) —H₂SO₄ (concentrated), Δ→ ?","options":["1,3,3-Trimethylcyclohex-1-ene (ring C=C bearing one methyl; gem-dimethyl carbon next to the =CH; no rearrangement)","1,5,5-Trimethylcyclohex-1-ene (ring C=C with one methyl; gem-dimethyl carbon on the far side)","1,3-Dimethyl-2-methylidenecyclohexane (exocyclic C=CH₂ flanked by two CH–CH₃ ring carbons)","1,2,3-Trimethylcyclohex-1-ene (tetrasubstituted ring C=C, each alkene carbon bearing a methyl, a third methyl on the adjacent ring carbon)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is elimination from the unrearranged 2° cation (trisubstituted, less stable); (B) puts the double bond where no cation can form; (C) is an exocyclic (less substituted) alkene from a ring-contraction/alternative shift path.\nWorking: the 2° cation at C-1 undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift from C-2 to give a 3° cation; loss of H⁺ from C-1 gives the tetrasubstituted alkene, 1,2,3-trimethylcyclohex-1-ene.\nKey idea: A 2° carbocation next to a C(CH₃)₂ group will grab a methyl to become 3°; then lose the proton that gives the most substituted C=C.","traps":["Methyl shift from quaternary carbon: Whenever a 2° cation is next to a quaternary carbon, check alkyl shifts.","Most substituted alkene: Count alkyl groups on the C=C; choose the tetrasubstituted one."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Named reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Reaction): (A) Hofmann Degradation; (B) Clemmensen reduction; (C) Cannizzaro reaction; (D) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction\nList II (Reagents): (I) Conc. KOH, Δ; (II) CHCl₃, NaOH/H₃O⁺; (III) Br₂, NaOH; (IV) Zn–Hg/HCl\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) - III, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D) - II","(A) - II, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D) - III","(A) - III, (B) - IV, (C) - II, (D) - I","(A) - II, (B) - I, (C) - III, (D) - IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) swap Hofmann (Br₂/NaOH) with Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH) – both use NaOH; (C) swaps Cannizzaro (conc. KOH) and Reimer–Tiemann.\nWorking: Hofmann degradation (amide → amine) uses Br₂/NaOH (III); Clemmensen reduction uses Zn–Hg/HCl (IV); Cannizzaro reaction uses conc. KOH, Δ (I); Reimer–Tiemann reaction uses CHCl₃, NaOH then H₃O⁺ (II).\nKey idea: Both Hofmann and Reimer–Tiemann use NaOH; the halogen source (Br₂ vs CHCl₃) separates them.","traps":["Br₂/NaOH vs CHCl₃/NaOH: Hofmann needs Br₂; Reimer–Tiemann needs CHCl₃."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The increasing order of pKₐ for the following phenols is\n(A) 2,4-Dinitrophenol\n(B) 4-Nitrophenol\n(C) 2,4,5-Trimethylphenol\n(D) Phenol\n(E) 3-Chlorophenol\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below :","options":["(A), (E), (B), (D), (C)","(C), (E), (D), (B), (A)","(C), (D), (E), (B), (A)","(A), (B), (E), (D), (C)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) places 3-chlorophenol before 4-nitrophenol (overrates –I of Cl at meta); (B)/(C) give increasing acidity, i.e. the reverse direction (pKa vs Ka confusion).\nWorking: approximate pKa values – 2,4-dinitrophenol ≈ 4.0, 4-nitrophenol ≈ 7.1, 3-chlorophenol ≈ 9.1, phenol 10.0, 2,4,5-trimethylphenol ≈ 10.6 (methyl groups donate electrons). Increasing pKa: (A) < (B) < (E) < (D) < (C).\nKey idea: Low pKa = strong acid; –R of NO₂ at o/p beats –I of Cl at meta.","traps":["pKa vs acidity direction: Write acidity order, then remember lowest pKa first.","Nitro (–R) vs meta chloro (–I): Resonance withdrawal at para outweighs –I at meta."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Find out the major product for the following reaction.\n[Reactant: cyclopentane ring carbon bearing CH₃ and a CH(OH)CH₃ group, i.e. 1-(1-methylcyclopentyl)ethan-1-ol] —H₃O⁺→ Major Product","options":["1-methyl-1-vinylcyclopentane [cyclopentane quaternary C bearing CH₃ and CH=CH₂]","1,6-dimethylcyclohex-1-ene [cyclohexene, one methyl on the alkene carbon and one on the adjacent sp³ ring carbon]","1,2-dimethylcyclohex-1-ene [tetrasubstituted ring C=C bearing both methyls]","1-methyl-2-methylenecyclohexane [exocyclic C=CH₂ next to a CH(CH₃) ring carbon]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) elimination from the unrearranged 2° cation (no ring expansion); (D) Hofmann-type exocyclic alkene from the 3° cation; (B) less substituted endocyclic alkene (trisubstituted).\nWorking: loss of water gives a 2° cation next to the quaternary ring carbon; a ring C–C bond migrates (ring expansion), giving a less strained six-membered ring with a 3° cation on the carbon bearing CH₃; loss of H⁺ from the adjacent CH(CH₃) gives the tetrasubstituted alkene 1,2-dimethylcyclohex-1-ene.\nKey idea: Ring strain relief plus 2°→3° cation drives expansion; then most substituted (tetrasubstituted) alkene forms.","traps":["Missing ring expansion: Check whether a ring bond migration gives a 3° cation and a less strained ring.","Choosing exocyclic alkene: Pick the tetrasubstituted alkene (Saytzeff)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Find out the major products from the following reaction sequence.\n4-Chloroacetophenone (p-Cl-C₆H₄-CO-CH₃) —1) NaCN; 2) EtOH, H₃O⁺→ A —MeMgBr (excess), H₃O⁺→ B","options":["A = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CO₂H; B = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-C(OH)(CH₃)₂","A = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CO₂Et; B = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-C(OH)(CH₃)₂","A = p-NC-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CN; B = p-(CH₃CH(OH))-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CH(OH)CH₃","A = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CN; B = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-C(CH₃)₂NH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) acid instead of ester – an acid would only be deprotonated by MeMgBr, not give a tertiary alcohol; (C) wrongly replaces aryl Cl by CN; (D) stops at the nitrile and has Grignard add to CN to give an amine (Grignard + nitrile gives ketone, not amine).\nWorking: NaCN adds to the ketone to give the cyanohydrin; EtOH/H₃O⁺ converts –CN into the ethyl ester, A = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-CO₂Et; excess MeMgBr adds twice to the ester, giving B = p-Cl-C₆H₄-C(OH)(CH₃)-C(OH)(CH₃)₂. The aryl C–Cl is untouched.\nKey idea: EtOH/H₃O⁺ on a nitrile gives the ethyl ester; esters take two RMgX to make 3° alcohols.","traps":["Ester vs acid after EtOH/H₃O⁺: Ethanol present → ester (Pinner-type alcoholysis).","Aryl Cl substitution by CN: Aryl halides do not undergo easy SN reactions."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"A trisubstituted compound 'A', C₁₀H₁₂O₂ gives neutral FeCl₃ test positive. Treatment of compound 'A' with NaOH and CH₃Br gives C₁₁H₁₄O₂, with hydroiodic acid gives methyl iodide and with hot conc. NaOH gives a compound B, C₁₀H₁₂O₂. Compound 'A' also decolorises alkaline KMnO₄. The number of π bond/s present in the compound 'A' is ______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (counting only the side-chain C=C), 3 (only the ring), 5 (assuming a C=O from 'O₂').\nWorking: A is eugenol (4-allyl-2-methoxyphenol): phenolic OH (FeCl₃ test, methylated by CH₃Br), OCH₃ (gives CH₃I with HI) and an allyl C=C (decolorises KMnO₄); hot conc. NaOH isomerises it to isoeugenol (B). π bonds = 3 (ring) + 1 (C=C) = 4.\nKey idea: Phenol (FeCl₃) + OCH₃ (HI → CH₃I) + C=C (KMnO₄) + isomer B = allyl → propenyl shift; Kekulé ring has 3 π bonds.","traps":["Aromatic π bonds not counted: Count 3 π bonds for each benzene ring (Kekulé form).","Hot NaOH gives isomer: Strong base shifts the terminal double bond into conjugation (eugenol → isoeugenol)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Assertion A: 4-hydroxy-4-methylpentan-2-one [(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂COCH₃] can be easily reduced using Zn–Hg/HCl to 2-methylpentan-2-ol [(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃]. Reason R: Zn–Hg/HCl is used to reduce carbonyl group to –CH₂– group. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): accept A because R is a true textbook fact; (C): reject R by confusing Clemmensen with Wolff-Kishner or with reduction to alcohol.\nWorking: the conc. HCl of the Clemmensen reagent attacks the tertiary alcohol (substitution/dehydration), and the β-hydroxy ketone can also dehydrate, so 2-methylpentan-2-ol is not obtained easily (A false); R correctly states what Zn–Hg/HCl does (R true).\nKey idea: Conc. HCl dehydrates/substitutes the tertiary alcohol (and the β-hydroxy ketone can undergo elimination), so Clemmensen is unsuitable; Wolff-Kishner would be used instead.","traps":["Clemmensen with acid-sensitive groups: Scan the substrate for acid-sensitive groups (3° OH, β-hydroxy carbonyl) before applying Zn–Hg/HCl.","Assertion judged only by reagent: Judge A on the whole molecule, not only the functional group named."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct order of pKa values for the following compounds is: (a) phenol; (b) 4-(dimethylamino)phenol; (c) 4-nitrophenol; (d) 4-isopropylphenol.","options":["a > b > c > d","c > a > d > b","b > d > a > c","b > a > d > c"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): gives acidity order instead of pKa order; (D): puts phenol above alkylphenol (ignores +I/hyperconjugation of isopropyl); (A): random order.\nWorking: –N(CH₃)₂ at para is a strong +M donor and destabilises the phenoxide most (highest pKa); isopropyl donates weakly (+I, hyperconjugation); –NO₂ at para withdraws by –R and –I (4-nitrophenol pKa 7.1 vs phenol 10.0). So b > d > a > c.\nKey idea: Strong +M donor (NMe₂) raises pKa most, alkyl raises it slightly, NO₂ lowers it: b > d > a > c.","traps":["pKa order vs acidity order: Higher pKa = weaker acid; reverse the acidity ranking.","Isopropyl donor effect is small but real: Alkyl group (+I) makes the phenoxide less stable than in phenol."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of carboxylic acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Number of compounds from the following which will not dissolve in cold NaHCO₃ and NaOH solutions but will dissolve in hot NaOH solution is ____. Compounds: 4-methylbenzoic acid; methyl benzoate; 4-methylphenyl methanoate (p-tolyl formate); 2-hydroxyacetophenone; 3-hydroxy-5-methylbenzaldehyde; 1-isopropyl-3-methoxybenzene; phenyl acetate.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (missing aryl formate as ester), 4 (counting the ether or 2-hydroxyacetophenone, thinking intramolecular H-bond blocks NaOH), 5 (adding phenols).\nWorking: 4-methylbenzoic acid dissolves even in cold NaHCO₃; 2-hydroxyacetophenone and 3-hydroxy-5-methylbenzaldehyde are phenols and dissolve in cold NaOH; 1-isopropyl-3-methoxybenzene (an ether) does not dissolve even in hot NaOH; methyl benzoate, p-tolyl formate and phenyl acetate are esters that dissolve only in hot NaOH (by hydrolysis) → 3.\nKey idea: Phenols dissolve in cold NaOH; esters need hot NaOH for hydrolysis; the anisole derivative is inert.","traps":["Aryl esters look like phenols: No free OH: it is an ester, needs hot NaOH.","Intramolecular H-bond phenol: It is still a phenol and dissolves in cold NaOH."],"topics":["Acidity of carboxylic acids","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Consider the following reaction: Propanal + Methanal —[(i) dil. NaOH; (ii) Δ; (iii) NaCN; (iv) H₃O⁺]→ Product B (C₅H₈O₃). The correct statement for product B is: it is","options":["racemic mixture and is neutral","optically active and adds one mole of bromine","racemic mixture and gives a gas with saturated NaHCO₃ solution","optically active alcohol and is neutral"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) racemic is right but the product carries COOH, not neutral. (B) the C=C would add Br₂, but a product made from achiral reagents without a chiral catalyst cannot be optically active. (D) optically active is wrong and the compound is an acid, not just an alcohol.\nWorking: propanal enolate + HCHO → 3-hydroxy-2-methylpropanal → Δ → CH₂=C(CH₃)CHO; HCN addition then hydrolysis → CH₂=C(CH₃)CH(OH)COOH, C₅H₈O₃, chiral but formed as racemate, COOH releases CO₂ with NaHCO₃. A 1,4-cyanide addition route would also give a racemic C₅H₈O₃ acid, so C holds either way.\nKey idea: A new stereocentre made from achiral starting materials gives a racemic mixture; the nitrile hydrolysis gives COOH, which reacts with NaHCO₃.","traps":["Chiral centre mistaken for optical activity: No chiral reagent or catalyst → both faces attacked equally → racemate.","Losing the acid after nitrile hydrolysis: H₃O⁺ on a cyanohydrin converts C≡N to COOH; check with formula C₅H₈O₃ (3 O)."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"An organic compound 'A' with empirical formula C₆H₆O gives sooty flame on burning. Its reaction with bromine solution in low polarity solvent results in high yield of B. B is","options":["2-bromophenol","2,3-dibromo-4-ethyltetrahydrofuran (five-membered O-ring with Br on C2 and C3, ethyl on C4)","3-(2-bromoethyl)-2,3-dihydrofuran (five-membered O-ring with one C=C and a CH₂CH₂Br side chain)","4-bromophenol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ortho isomer forms but is minor (sterics). (B) and (C) are non-aromatic ring compounds that would not burn with a sooty flame and are not bromination products of phenol.\nWorking: C₆H₆O that burns with a sooty flame (aromatic) is phenol; Br₂ in a low-polarity solvent (CHCl₃/CS₂) gives monobromination, with 4-bromophenol as the major product.\nKey idea: Low-polarity solvent → mono-bromination; para is the major product.","traps":["Ortho vs para as major product: Para is major (less steric hindrance); question asks for high yield.","Solvent polarity swap: Low-polarity solvent (CS₂/CHCl₃) → mono; water → tri."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Cyclohexylamine when treated with nitrous acid yields (P). On treating (P) with PCC results in (Q). When (Q) is heated with dil. NaOH we get (R). The final product (R) is:","options":["2-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)cyclohexan-1-one (cyclohexanone with a cyclohexenyl ring at C-2, ring C=C endocyclic)","2-cyclohexylidenecyclohexan-1-one (exocyclic C=C joining the two rings, conjugated with C=O)","2,2'-bi(cyclohexanone) (two cyclohexanone rings joined C-2 to C-2')","2-cyclohexyl-2-hydroxycyclohexan-1-one (OH and cyclohexyl on C-2 of cyclohexanone)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) β,γ-unsaturated isomer – C=C not conjugated with C=O. (C) 1,4-diketone from oxidative coupling, not aldol. (D) Puts OH on the α-carbon (wrong connectivity) and no dehydration.\nWorking: cyclohexylamine + HNO₂ gives cyclohexanol (P); PCC oxidises it to cyclohexanone (Q); dil. NaOH with heat brings about aldol condensation of two cyclohexanone molecules followed by dehydration, giving the conjugated enone 2-cyclohexylidenecyclohexan-1-one (R). In practice the β,γ-isomer (A) also forms, but the textbook aldol-condensation product is (B).\nKey idea: Aldol joins α-carbon of one ketone to C=O carbon of the other; heat removes water to give the conjugated enone.","traps":["Stopping at the aldol (ketol) stage: Heating with alkali means dehydration to the enone.","Where the C=C ends up: Elimination gives C=C conjugated with C=O (α,β)."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Oxidation of alcohols"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of molecules which gives the haloform test among the following molecules is ________. [Molecules: (i) 1-(2,4-dimethylphenyl)ethan-1-one; (ii) ethyl 3-oxopentanoate, CH₃CH₂COCH₂COOEt; (iii) acetophenone, C₆H₅COCH₃; (iv) pentan-3-ol, CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₂CH₃; (v) butan-2-ol, CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃; (vi) 2,5,5-trimethylcyclohexane-1,3-dione]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (missing butan-2-ol), 4 (counting the β-keto ester or the 2-methyl-1,3-dione as methyl ketones), 5 (counting all carbonyls).\nWorking: positive – 2,4-dimethylacetophenone and acetophenone (CH₃CO–) and butan-2-ol (CH₃CH(OH)–) = 3; negative – ethyl 3-oxopentanoate (ethyl ketone), pentan-3-ol and the cyclic 1,3-dione (no CH₃CO–).\nKey idea: Need CH₃–CO– or CH₃–CH(OH)–; an ethyl ketone or a ring C-methyl does not qualify.","traps":["Secondary alcohol giving haloform: CH₃CH(OH)R is oxidised by NaOX to CH₃COR.","Ethyl ketone mistaken for methyl ketone: Count carbons on each side of C=O."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which one of the following reactions does not represent correct combination of substrate and product under the given conditions? (all on p-tolyl substrates) (A) p-CH₃C₆H₄COCl —H₂, Pd/BaSO₄→ p-CH₃C₆H₄CHO; (B) p-CH₃C₆H₄CN —(i) DIBAL-H (ii) H₂O→ p-CH₃C₆H₄CHO; (C) p-CH₃C₆H₄CO₂C₂H₅ —(i) AlH(iBu)₂ (ii) H₂O→ p-CH₃C₆H₄CHO; (D) p-CH₃C₆H₄CH₂OH —(i) Na₂Cr₂O₇ (ii) H₂SO₄, H₂O→ p-CH₃C₆H₄CHO","options":["Rosenmund reduction of p-toluoyl chloride to p-tolualdehyde","DIBAL-H reduction of p-tolunitrile to p-tolualdehyde","AlH(iBu)₂ reduction of ethyl p-toluate to p-tolualdehyde","Na₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄ oxidation of p-methylbenzyl alcohol to p-tolualdehyde"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) uses the written-out name AlH(iBu)₂ instead of DIBAL-H, tempting those who do not recognise it; (A) and (B) are textbook-correct.\nWorking: acidified dichromate oxidises a primary alcohol through the aldehyde to the carboxylic acid (p-toluic acid); (A)–(C) are standard aldehyde preparations.\nKey idea: Primary alcohol → aldehyde needs PCC; Cr₂O₇²⁻/H⁺ gives acid.","traps":["AlH(iBu)₂ = DIBAL-H: DIBAL-H is diisobutylaluminium hydride.","Over-oxidation by dichromate: For 1° alcohols, dichromate goes to the acid; use PCC to stop at aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: On heating with KHSO₄, glycerol is dehydrated and acrolein is formed. Statement II: Acrolein has fruity odour and can be used to test glycerol's presence. Choose the correct option.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts \"fruity\" (esters are fruity); (D) and (B) doubt the dehydration reaction.\nWorking: glycerol with KHSO₄ loses two H₂O to give acrolein (CH₂=CH–CHO); acrolein has a pungent, irritating smell, not fruity, so II is false.\nKey idea: Acrolein = pungent; esters = fruity.","traps":["Odour descriptor swapped: Acrolein has a sharp, pungent smell; fruity odour belongs to esters."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In the given reaction: substrate = cyclopentane bearing, at C1, a CH₃ group and a –C(=O)OEt group (quaternary carbon) and, at the adjacent C2, a –CH₂–C(=O)–CH₃ side chain; reagent ⁻OEt → A. (Where Et is –C₂H₅). The number of chiral carbon/s in product A is ____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counting a carbonyl-flanked CH₂ or the new CH as chiral); 1 (missing the quaternary ring-fusion carbon); 4 (attack via the internal CH₂ giving a 4-membered ring with an extra stereocentre).\nWorking: ethoxide forms the enolate at the terminal CH₃ of the methyl ketone, which attacks the ester carbonyl (intramolecular Claisen/Dieckmann-type condensation) to close a six-membered ring, giving a bicyclic cyclohexane-1,3-dione fused to the cyclopentane. The two ring-fusion carbons (the quaternary C bearing CH₃ and the adjacent CH) are the only stereocentres → 2.\nKey idea: Enolate from the terminal CH₃ gives a 6-membered ring; the two original ring carbons remain the stereocentres.","traps":["Which enolate attacks: Choose the enolate that forms a 5/6-membered ring (terminal CH₃ → 6-ring).","Quaternary stereocentre: Check all four substituents; ring-fusion quaternary C is chiral."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"What is the major product of the following reaction? (CH₃)₂CH–CHO (2-methylpropanal) with ⁻OH / H₂O → ?","options":["OHC–C(CH₃)₂–CH=C(CH₃)₂ (2,2,4-trimethylpent-3-enal)","OHC–C(CH₃)₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)₂ (3-hydroxy-2,2,4-trimethylpentanal)","OHC–C(CH₃)₂–C(=O)–CH(CH₃)₂ (2,2,4-trimethyl-3-oxopentanal)","OHC–C(CH₃)=C(CH₃)₂ (2,3-dimethylbut-2-enal)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is a \"dehydrated aldol\" with the C=C placed β,γ (cannot form by aldol dehydration since the α-carbon has no H); (D) is a C6 enal that would need a different (crossed/acetone-type) aldol; (C) is an oxidised/Claisen-type β-keto aldehyde.\nWorking: 2-methylpropanal has one α-H, so it gives the aldol (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–C(CH₃)₂–CHO; the α-carbon is now quaternary, so dehydration to an α,β-unsaturated aldehyde cannot occur.\nKey idea: Dehydration needs an α-H on the aldol; here the α-carbon is C(CH₃)₂.","traps":["Automatic dehydration to enal: Check for α-H on the aldol product before dehydrating.","Cannizzaro expected: Cannizzaro needs no α-H; this aldehyde has one."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Arrange the following in decreasing acidic strength: (A) p-nitrophenol, (B) m-nitrophenol, (C) m-methoxyphenol, (D) p-methoxyphenol.","options":["A > B > C > D","B > A > C > D","D > C > A > B","D > C > B > A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts m-NO₂ above p-NO₂ (−I closer); (C)/(D) treat OCH₃ as acid-strengthening (electronegative O).\nWorking: p-NO₂ (−R, −I) > m-NO₂ (−I only) > m-OCH₃ (−I only, slightly acid-strengthening, pKa ≈ 9.6) > p-OCH₃ (+R dominates, pKa ≈ 10.2).\nKey idea: Resonance works only from o/p; at meta only the inductive effect counts.","traps":["m-OCH₃ vs p-OCH₃: At meta, OCH₃ cannot donate by resonance; its −I slightly increases acidity.","m-NO₂ above p-NO₂: p-NO₂ adds −R delocalisation of phenoxide charge."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"CH₃–CH₂–CN —(CH₃MgBr, ether)→ A —(H₃O⁺)→ B —(Zn–Hg / HCl)→ C. The correct structure of C is","options":["CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–C(=O)–CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–CH=CH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is intermediate B (stopping one step early); (C) treats Zn-Hg/HCl as reduction to alcohol; (D) an alkene from dehydration.\nWorking: Grignard adds to C≡N giving the imine magnesium salt A; hydrolysis gives butan-2-one B; Clemmensen reduction converts C=O to CH₂ giving butane C.\nKey idea: Clemmensen: C=O → CH₂, so the carbon skeleton CH₃CH₂–C–CH₃ becomes butane.","traps":["Stopping at the ketone: Read which letter is asked: C, after Clemmensen.","Clemmensen confused with alcohol reduction: Zn-Hg/conc. HCl takes C=O all the way to CH₂."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The difference in the reaction of phenol with bromine in chloroform and bromine in water medium is due to:","options":["Hyperconjugation in substrate","Polarity of solvent","Free radical formation","Electromeric effect of the substrate"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (D) are substrate effects, same in both solvents; (C) bromination of phenol is ionic, not radical.\nWorking: in a low-polarity solvent (CHCl₃/CS₂) phenol gives monobromophenols; in water, phenoxide/polarised Br₂ give 2,4,6-tribromophenol – the solvent polarity is the difference.\nKey idea: Same substrate, same reagent, different solvent → solvent polarity.","traps":["Substrate effect chosen: Ask what differs between the two experiments – only the solvent."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation of carbonyl compounds","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The products formed in the following reaction, A and B are\n4-Oxocyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde —[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ OH⁻→ A —NaBH₄→ B","options":["A = 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-one; B = 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol","A = 4-hydroxycyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde; B = 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol","A = 4-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid; B = 4-hydroxycyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid","A = 4-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid; B = 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats Tollens as a reducing agent; (B) has Tollens reducing the ketone; (D) lets NaBH₄ reduce –COOH to –CH₂OH.\nWorking: Tollens oxidises only –CHO to –COOH (ketone untouched) → A = 4-oxocyclohexanecarboxylic acid; NaBH₄ reduces the ketone to –OH but not –COOH → B = 4-hydroxycyclohexanecarboxylic acid.\nKey idea: Tollens: CHO only; NaBH₄: C=O of aldehyde/ketone only, not COOH.","traps":["NaBH₄ reduces COOH: NaBH₄ leaves acids and esters alone.","Tollens acts on ketone: Tollens oxidises aldehydes only."],"topics":["Oxidation of carbonyl compounds","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which reactant will give the following alcohol on reaction with one mole of phenyl magnesium bromide (PhMgBr) followed by acidic hydrolysis?\nPh₂C(OH)CH₃ (1,1-diphenylethanol)","options":["CH₃–C≡N","Ph–C≡N","CH₃–CO–O–Ph","Ph–CO–CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CH₃CN + PhMgBr gives acetophenone (a ketone), not the alcohol; (B) gives benzophenone; (C) phenyl acetate would first lose PhO⁻ to give acetophenone, so it reaches 1,1-diphenylethanol only with 2 mol PhMgBr, not one.\nWorking: PhMgBr adds to acetophenone to give Ph₂C(OMgBr)CH₃, hydrolysed to 1,1-diphenylethanol. Nitriles give ketones after hydrolysis; phenyl acetate needs two moles of Grignard.\nKey idea: Tertiary alcohol with one new Ph → ketone carrying the other two groups.","traps":["Nitrile gives ketone, not alcohol: Hydrolysis of the imine salt stops at a ketone.","Ester with one mole: Use the \"one mole\" condition."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Hydrolysis of which compound will give carbolic acid ?","options":["Cumene","Benzenediazonium chloride","Benzal chloride","Ethylene glycol ketal"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) cumene is the industrial source of phenol but via oxidation then acid cleavage; (C) benzal chloride hydrolyses to benzaldehyde; (D) ketal hydrolysis gives a ketone/aldehyde.\nWorking: warming C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ with water gives phenol (carbolic acid) + N₂ + HCl. Cumene needs air oxidation to the hydroperoxide first; benzal chloride gives benzaldehyde; a ketal gives a carbonyl compound and the diol.\nKey idea: Carbolic acid = phenol; ArN₂⁺ + H₂O (warm) → ArOH.","traps":["Cumene process: Cumene needs O₂ oxidation first; it is not a direct hydrolysis.","Carbolic acid name: Carbolic acid is phenol."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"EtO–C(=O)–CH₂–(cyclopent-2-ene-1,4-diyl)–CH₂CH₂CHO —(i) DiBAl-H, (ii) H₂O→ ?\nConsider the above reaction and predict the major product.\n[Substrate: a cyclopentene ring carrying CH₂COOEt on one sp³ carbon and CH₂CH₂CHO on the other sp³ carbon, both flanking the C=C.]","options":["OHC–CH₂–(cyclopentenyl)–CH₂CH₂CHO","EtO–C(=O)–CH₂–(cyclopentenyl)–CH₂CH₂CHO","EtO–C(=O)–CH₂–(cyclopentenyl)–CH₂CH₂COOH","OHC–CH₂–(cyclopentenyl)–CH₂CH₂COOH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) = no reaction; (C)/(D) invent oxidation of CHO to COOH, which a reducing agent cannot do.\nWorking: DIBAL-H reduces the ester to an aldehyde and leaves the C=C; the existing CHO is shown unchanged. Strictly, DIBAL-H would also reduce the free aldehyde to an alcohol, but no such option is given.\nKey idea: DIBAL-H: ester → aldehyde; it is a reductant, so no COOH can form.","traps":["Reduction cannot give COOH: A hydride reagent only lowers oxidation level.","C=C survives: DIBAL-H does not reduce isolated C=C."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In the following reaction: Benzene —(Oleum)→ (I) benzenesulphonic acid —(NaOH (excess), then H⁺)→ (II) phenol. The % yield for reaction I is 60% and that of reaction II is 50%. The overall yield of the complete reaction is ___% [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"30","range":[30.0,30.0],"explain":"Common slips: 55 (averaging), 110 (adding), 50 (taking last step only).\nWorking: overall yield = 0.60 × 0.50 × 100 = 30%.\nKey idea: Yields of consecutive steps multiply.","traps":["Adding or averaging yields: Multiply fractional yields."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The structure of A in the given reaction is: R–CO–CH₃ (methyl ketone) + NaOH, then CH₃CH₂Br (ethyl bromide) → A (major product).","options":["R–C(OH)(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)Br","R–C(OH)(CH₃)(CH₂CH₃)","R–CO–CH₂CH₂CH₃ (α-ethylated ketone)","R–CO–CH₂CH₂CH₂OH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) adds the halide carbon to the carbonyl and keeps Br; (B) treats ethyl bromide like a Grignard reagent adding to C=O; (D) adds an extra carbon and an OH.\nWorking: NaOH removes an acidic α-H of the methyl group to give the enolate, which does SN2 on ethyl bromide (C-alkylation): R–CO–CH₂–CH₂CH₃, i.e. R–CO–CH₂CH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: Enolate carbon (α-carbon) is the nucleophile; the C=O is retained.","traps":["Grignard-like addition: EtBr is an electrophile, not a carbanion source; the enolate attacks it.","Where the new bond forms: Base deprotonates α-C; that carbon forms the new C–C bond."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Major product 'B' of the following reaction sequence is: CH₃–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₃ —(Br₂, CH₃OH)→ A —(HI)→ B (major product).","options":["CH₃–C(OH)(CH₃)–CHBr–CH₃","CH₃–C(I)(CH₃)–CHBr–CH₃","CH₃–CBr(CH₃)–CH(OH)–CH₃","CH₃–CBr(CH₃)–CH(I)–CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) stops at an alcohol (ignores that HI converts the tertiary C–O to C–I); (C)/(D) put Br on the tertiary carbon (wrong ring-opening regiochemistry).\nWorking: Br₂ forms a bromonium ion; CH₃OH (solvent) opens it at the more substituted carbon, giving A = (CH₃)₂C(OCH₃)–CHBr–CH₃. HI cleaves this tertiary alkyl methyl ether by SN1: the tertiary carbon gets I and methanol leaves, B = (CH₃)₂C(I)–CHBr–CH₃ (2-bromo-3-iodo-3-methylbutane).\nKey idea: Nucleophile opens the bromonium at the more substituted carbon; HI then converts that tertiary C–OCH₃ to C–I.","traps":["Regiochemistry of bromonium opening: Solvent attacks the carbon that best carries positive charge (tertiary).","Which side of ether HI cleaves: Tertiary alkyl ether: SN1, tertiary halide + CH₃OH."],"topics":["Ether reactions","Ether preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Named reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) C₆H₅COCl —(H₂, Pd–BaSO₄)→ C₆H₅CHO; (B) CH₃–CN —(SnCl₂/HCl; H₃O⁺)→ CH₃–CHO; (C) C₆H₅CH₃ —(CrO₂Cl₂; H₃O⁺)→ C₆H₅CHO; (D) C₆H₆ —(CO, HCl, anhyd. AlCl₃)→ C₆H₅CHO. List-II: (I) Gatterman Koch reaction; (II) Etard reaction; (III) Stephen reaction; (IV) Rosenmund reaction.","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) shuffle the names, typically swapping Etard and Gatterman-Koch (both make benzaldehyde).\nWorking: acyl chloride + H₂/Pd–BaSO₄ = Rosenmund; nitrile + SnCl₂/HCl then hydrolysis = Stephen; toluene + chromyl chloride = Etard; benzene + CO/HCl/AlCl₃ = Gatterman-Koch.\nKey idea: Identify by substrate: acyl chloride, nitrile, toluene, benzene.","traps":["Two routes to benzaldehyde confused: Toluene → Etard; benzene + CO/HCl → Gatterman-Koch."],"topics":["Named reactions","Aldehyde & ketone preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Choose the correct option for the following reactions. B ←[(BH₃)₂; H₂O₂/OH⁻]— H₃C–C(CH₃)₂–CH=CH₂ (3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene) —[Hg(OAc)₂, H₂O; NaBH₄]→ A","options":["'A' and 'B' are both Markovnikov addition products.","'A' is Markovnikov product and 'B' is anti-Markovnikov product.","'A' and 'B' are both anti-Markovnikov products.","'B' is Markovnikov and 'A' is anti-Markovnikov product."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats hydroboration as Markovnikov because B attaches to the less substituted carbon; (D) reverses both; (C) thinks the bulky tert-butyl group forces anti-Markovnikov in both.\nWorking: oxymercuration–demercuration gives Markovnikov 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol without rearrangement (A); hydroboration–oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov 3,3-dimethylbutan-1-ol (B).\nKey idea: Hg-route: OH on the more substituted carbon; B-route: OH on the less substituted carbon.","traps":["Hydroboration regiochemistry: Judge by where OH ends up: terminal = anti-Markovnikov."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Identify the major product A and B for the below given reaction sequence. Benzene —[(1) CH₃CHClCH₃, AlCl₃; (2) O₂; (3) H⁺, H₂O]→ P; P —[Na₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄]→ A; P —[Br₂ in CS₂]→ B.","options":["phenol and 4-bromophenol","p-benzoquinone (cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione) and 4-bromophenol","p-benzoquinone and 2,4,6-tribromophenol","phenol and 2,4,6-tribromophenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) give tribromophenol (bromine water result); (A)/(D) assume dichromate leaves phenol unchanged.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts gives cumene, which via cumene hydroperoxide and acid gives phenol (P); chromic acid oxidises phenol to p-benzoquinone (A); Br₂ in low-polarity CS₂ gives mainly p-bromophenol (B).\nKey idea: Br₂/CS₂ (non-polar, cold) → monobromination; bromine water → 2,4,6-tribromophenol.","traps":["Bromine water vs Br₂ in CS₂: Polar water ionises phenol → trisubstitution; CS₂ → mono.","Oxidation of phenol: Chromic acid gives benzoquinone (NCERT)."],"topics":["Phenol preparation","Phenol substitution","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Compound I is heated with Conc. HI to give a hydroxy compound A which is further heated with Zn dust to give compound B. Identify A and B. Compound I: isopropyl phenyl ether C₆H₅–O–CH(CH₃)₂; I → (Conc. HI, Δ) → A → (Zn, Δ) → B.","options":["A = propan-2-ol (CH₃)₂CHOH, B = propane","A = phenol, B = p-benzoquinone","A = 2-isopropylphenol, B = isopropylbenzene (cumene)","A = phenol, B = benzene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) breaks the aryl–O bond instead; (B) treats Zn as an oxidant; (C) invents a Claisen/Fries-type alkyl migration.\nWorking: alkyl aryl ethers are cleaved by HI at the O–alkyl bond (aryl–O has partial double-bond character), giving phenol + 2-iodopropane; phenol heated with zinc dust gives benzene.\nKey idea: Ar–O bond does not break; phenol + Zn dust → benzene.","traps":["Aryl–O vs alkyl–O cleavage: Ar–O has partial double bond character; attack is at the alkyl carbon.","Role of Zn dust: Zn dust removes the OH (reduction) to benzene."],"topics":["Ether reactions","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Find out the major product for the reaction: 2-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)acetic acid treated with (1) I₂/NaHCO₃, (2) pyridine, Δ.","options":["bicyclic γ-lactone with the C=C at the ring-fusion bond (C3a=C7a, enol lactone): 3,4,5,6-tetrahydro-1-benzofuran-2-one type","2-(1,2-diiodocyclohexyl)acetic acid","α,β-unsaturated γ-lactone (butenolide) fused to cyclohexane: C=C between the ring-fusion carbon C3a and C3; 5,6,7,7a-tetrahydro-1-benzofuran-2(4H)-one","bicyclic γ-lactone with the C=C in the cyclohexane ring (C3a=C4)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts C=C at the ring fusion (needs loss of the H on the O-bearing carbon, not the iodide carbon); (B) is simple I₂ addition without cyclisation; (D) is the non-conjugated elimination product.\nWorking: I₂/NaHCO₃ gives iodolactonisation: iodonium forms on the ring C=C and the carboxylate attacks C-2 (5-exo, γ-lactone), leaving a tertiary iodide on C-1 (ring-fusion carbon). Pyridine/heat eliminates HI; loss of the H α to the carbonyl gives the conjugated butenolide (C), favoured over (D).\nKey idea: Lactone first (5-membered), then E2 towards the carbonyl-activated H to give conjugation.","traps":["Simple addition vs lactonisation: NaHCO₃ makes the carboxylate, which traps the iodonium ion.","Which H is eliminated: Elimination giving C=C conjugated with C=O is favoured."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ammonia-derivative reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde —Br₂→ A —NH₂OH→ B —P₂O₅, Δ→ C. Consider the above reaction sequence, the Product 'C' is :","options":["3,5-Dibromobenzonitrile (Br at 3,5; CN at 1; no OH)","3,5-Dibromo-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde","3,5-Dibromobenzaldehyde","3,5-Dibromo-4-hydroxybenzonitrile (bromoxynil)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) loses OH; (B) is only A; (C) loses OH and stops at aldehyde.\nWorking: Br₂ brominates both positions ortho to –OH (para blocked by CHO) → 3,5-dibromo-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde (A); NH₂OH → aldoxime (B); P₂O₅/Δ dehydrates the aldoxime → nitrile (C).\nKey idea: Aldoxime + P₂O₅ (dehydrating agent) → ArC≡N.","traps":["Stopping at intermediate: Carry every step to the end.","OH lost on bromination: Phenolic OH survives Br₂."],"topics":["Ammonia-derivative reactions","Phenol substitution"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"4-Aminobenzonitrile —(i) AlH(i-Bu)₂, (ii) H₂O→ 'A' —CH₃CHO, dil NaOH, Δ→ B (Major Product). The product 'B' is :","options":["OHC–C₆H₄–N=CH–CH₃ (para)","H₂N–C₆H₄–CH=CH–CHO (para)","H₂N–C₆H₄–CH₂–N=CH–CH₃ (para)","H₂N–C₆H₄–C(=O)–N=CH–CHO (para)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) imine formation at NH₂ with CHO intact (wrong site); (C) full reduction to amine; (D) amide not formed by DIBAL-H.\nWorking: DIBAL-H reduces the nitrile to the aldehyde → 4-aminobenzaldehyde (A); cross aldol (Claisen–Schmidt) with ethanal, dil NaOH, Δ → 4-aminocinnamaldehyde H₂N–C₆H₄–CH=CH–CHO.\nKey idea: DIBAL-H stops at aldehyde; ArCHO (no α-H) + CH₃CHO → ArCH=CHCHO.","traps":["DIBAL-H over-reduction: DIBAL-H at low T gives aldehyde, not amine.","Imine instead of aldol: With dil NaOH, Δ the aldol path to the conjugated enal is major."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Aldehyde & ketone preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A compound 'X' is acidic and it is soluble in NaOH solution, but insoluble in NaHCO₃ solution. Compound 'X' also gives violet colour with neutral FeCl₃ solution. The compound 'X' is :","options":["Cyclohexanol","Phenol","Benzyl alcohol","Cyclohexylmethanol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(D) aliphatic alcohols, not acidic to NaOH; (C) benzyl alcohol – OH not on ring, no FeCl₃ colour.\nWorking: phenol (pKa ≈ 10) dissolves in NaOH but is too weak to react with NaHCO₃, and gives violet colour with neutral FeCl₃; alcohols are not acidic enough for NaOH and give no FeCl₃ colour.\nKey idea: NaOH-soluble + NaHCO₃-insoluble + FeCl₃ violet = phenol.","traps":["Benzyl alcohol taken as phenolic: FeCl₃ colour needs OH directly on the ring."],"topics":["Acidity of phenols","Tests for oxygen compounds"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"When ethanol is heated with conc. H₂SO₄, a gas is produced. The compound formed, when this gas is treated with cold dilute aqueous solution of Baeyer's reagent, is:","options":["Formaldehyde","Formic acid","Glycol","Ethanoic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) products of hot/acidic KMnO₄ cleavage of ethene; (D) oxidation of ethanol itself.\nWorking: ethanol + conc. H₂SO₄ (443 K) → ethene; cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ gives syn-dihydroxylation → ethane-1,2-diol (glycol).\nKey idea: Cold dilute KMnO₄ → diol; hot acidic KMnO₄ → cleavage.","traps":["Cold dilute vs hot acidic KMnO₄: Cold dilute = glycol.","Ethanol oxidised directly: The gas (ethene) is what reacts."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties of carbonyls & acids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Two statements are given below: Statement I: The melting point of monocarboxylic acid with even number of carbon atoms is higher than that of with odd number of carbon atoms acid immediately below and above it in the series. Statement II: The solubility of monocarboxylic acids in water decreases with increase in molar mass. Choose the most appropriate option:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) rejects Statement I as unfamiliar; (C) doubts Statement II by thinking hydrogen bonding keeps all acids soluble.\nWorking: I – the even/odd alternation of melting points (even-C acids pack better in the crystal) is true; II – solubility falls as the hydrophobic chain grows, true.\nKey idea: Even-numbered chains pack more symmetrically, so their crystals melt higher.","traps":["Unfamiliar true statement rejected: Recall the zig-zag mp trend of fatty acids: even C acids are higher."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"4-Methoxybenzyl bromide (p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂Br) is treated with (i) NaCN, (ii) OH⁻, (iii) cyclohexanone, (iv) H₂, Ni → A (major product). The major product of the above reaction is (Ar = p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–; C₆H₁₁ = cyclohexyl; C(OH)(CH₂)₅ = 1-hydroxycyclohexyl):","options":["ArCH(C₆H₁₁)CH₂NH₂","ArCH(NH₂)–C(OH)(CH₂)₅","ArCH(C₆H₁₁)CONH₂","ArCH(CH₂NH₂)–C(OH)(CH₂)₅"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): reduction treated as replacing CN by NH₂ (one carbon lost); (A): OH lost as if dehydration/hydrogenolysis occurred; (C): OH⁻ step read as nitrile hydrolysis to amide.\nWorking: SN2 with CN⁻ gives ArCH₂CN; OH⁻ removes the acidic benzylic α-H (stabilised by CN and the ring); the carbanion adds to cyclohexanone giving ArCH(CN)–C(OH)(CH₂)₅; H₂/Ni reduces C≡N to CH₂NH₂ → ArCH(CH₂NH₂)–C(OH)(CH₂)₅ (venlafaxine skeleton without N-methyls).\nKey idea: CN gives the extra carbon: CH₂NH₂, not NH₂, appears after H₂/Ni.","traps":["OH⁻ read as hydrolysis: Here OH⁻ precedes a ketone: it deprotonates the acidic α-C–H.","Carbon of CN lost: Reduction of R–C≡N gives R–CH₂–NH₂, keeping the carbon."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which of the following reagents/reactions will convert 'A' to 'B'? A: 4-methyl-1-methylenecyclohexane; B: 4-methylcyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde.","options":["PCC oxidation","Ozonolysis","BH₃, H₂O₂/⁻OH followed by PCC oxidation","HBr, hydrolysis followed by oxidation by K₂Cr₂O₇"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) PCC – needs an alcohol; does not act on an alkene; (B) ozonolysis – cleaves C=CH₂ to 4-methylcyclohexanone + HCHO (loses a carbon); (D) HBr (Markovnikov) puts Br on the ring carbon → tertiary alcohol, which K₂Cr₂O₇ cannot oxidise to an aldehyde.\nWorking: hydroboration–oxidation adds H and OH anti-Markovnikov, giving the primary alcohol (4-methylcyclohexyl)methanol; PCC oxidises it to the aldehyde without going on to the acid.\nKey idea: CH₂= → CH₂OH needs anti-Markovnikov hydration (BH₃), then PCC to stop at CHO.","traps":["Markovnikov HBr used for terminal OH: HBr puts Br on the more substituted (ring) carbon → 3° alcohol, not CH₂OH.","Ozonolysis thought to give aldehyde: Ozonolysis splits the C=C – ring carbon becomes a ketone, CH₂ becomes HCHO."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Oxidation of alcohols"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Hex-4-ene-2-ol on treatment with PCC gives 'A'. 'A' on reaction with sodium hypoiodite gives 'B', which on further heating with soda lime gives 'C'. The compound 'C' is","options":["2-pentene","propionaldehyde","2-butene","4-methylpent-2-ene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2-pentene – only one carbon lost (forgot decarboxylation or haloform); (B) propanal – thinking the C=C is cleaved; (D) 4-methylpent-2-ene – wrong skeleton (branching).\nWorking: CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH=CHCH₃ → (PCC) hex-4-en-2-one → (NaOI, iodoform) CH₃CH=CHCH₂COONa + CHI₃ → (soda lime) CH₃CH=CHCH₃, but-2-ene.\nKey idea: C6 alcohol → C6 methyl ketone → C5 acid salt → C4 alkene: two carbons removed.","traps":["Only one carbon removed: Count carbons after each step: 6 → 6 → 5 → 4.","NaOI thought to attack the C=C: Hypohalite acts on the CH₃CO– group (haloform), C=C stays."],"topics":["Haloform reaction","Oxidation of alcohols"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The intermediate X, in the reaction CHCl₃ + aq. NaOH + phenol → [X] —(1) NaOH, (2) H⁺→ salicylaldehyde (o-hydroxybenzaldehyde) is:","options":["o-(trichloromethyl)phenol (OH and CCl₃ ortho)","o-(dichloromethyl)phenol (OH and CHCl₂ ortho)","sodium o-(dichloromethyl)phenoxide (O⁻Na⁺ and CHCl₂ ortho)","sodium o-(trichloromethyl)phenoxide (O⁻Na⁺ and CCl₃ ortho)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) CCl₃ would hydrolyse to COOH (salicylic acid), not CHO; (B) phenol form – ignores strongly basic medium.\nWorking: in the Reimer–Tiemann reaction phenoxide attacks dichlorocarbene at the ortho position, giving the ortho-CHCl₂ phenoxide (substituted benzal chloride); NaOH hydrolysis and acidification give salicylaldehyde. Option (C).\nKey idea: CHCl₂ → CHO on hydrolysis; in NaOH the phenol is the phenoxide.","traps":["CCl₃ vs CHCl₂: Two Cl → aldehyde, three Cl → acid.","Phenol vs phenoxide: Medium is aqueous NaOH: O⁻Na⁺ until acidification."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In the following reaction: cumene (isopropylbenzene) + O₂ → cumene hydroperoxide (C₆H₅C(CH₃)₂–O–O–H) —H⁺/H₂O→ A and B. The compounds A and B respectively are:","options":["toluene, CH₃COOH","resorcinol (benzene-1,3-diol; OH at C1 and C3), CH₃COOH","phenol, CH₃COCH₃","catechol (1,2-dihydroxybenzene), CH₃COCH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) toluene – loses the oxygen; (B)/(D) dihydroxy compounds – thinks peroxide adds two OH; acetic acid instead of acetone.\nWorking: acid-catalysed rearrangement of cumene hydroperoxide (phenyl migration to O) gives phenol and acetone. Option (C).\nKey idea: Cumene → phenol + acetone.","traps":["Peroxide gives two OH groups: The phenyl group migrates; ring gets one OH."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In the given reaction\n2A —[(i) 2 Mg, THF; (ii) Methyl benzoate; (iii) H₂O/H⁺]→ triphenylmethanol [(C₆H₅)₃C–OH]\n'A' can be","options":["benzyl bromide","bromobenzene","cyclohexyl bromide","methyl bromide"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) benzyl bromide would give PhC(OH)(CH₂Ph)₂; (C) gives dicyclohexyl phenyl carbinol; (D) gives 2-phenylpropan-2-ol.\nWorking: 2 PhBr + 2 Mg → 2 PhMgBr; first PhMgBr converts methyl benzoate to benzophenone, the second adds to give Ph₃C–O⁻MgBr⁺, hydrolysed to Ph₃COH. So A = bromobenzene.\nKey idea: Two of the three groups on the carbinol carbon come from RMgX; the third is the ester acyl group.","traps":["Benzyl vs phenyl: Benzyl adds a CH₂ between ring and carbinol carbon.","Two equivalents of Grignard on ester: Ketone is more reactive than ester and reacts again."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which of the following conditions or reaction sequence will NOT give acetophenone as the major product?","options":["(a) C₆H₅CHO + CH₃MgBr; (b) Na₂Cr₂O₇, H⁺","(a) CH₃CHO + C₆H₅MgBr; (b) PCC, DCM","C₆H₅COOC₂H₅ + 2CH₃MgBr","C₆H₅COCl + CH₃MgBr + CdCl₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) looks odd (three reagents) but is the NCERT dialkylcadmium route; (A)/(B) need checking that both form the same secondary alcohol.\nWorking: (A) and (B) both give 1-phenylethanol, oxidised to acetophenone; (D) CH₃MgBr + CdCl₂ → (CH₃)₂Cd, which converts benzoyl chloride to acetophenone; (C) the ester with two equivalents of CH₃MgBr goes on to 2-phenylpropan-2-ol (tertiary alcohol), not acetophenone.\nKey idea: Ester + 2 RMgX → tertiary alcohol; to stop at ketone use R₂Cd with acyl chloride.","traps":["Ester with two equivalents of Grignard: Ketone formed reacts faster than ester → 3° alcohol.","Role of CdCl₂: CdCl₂ + Grignard → R₂Cd, a mild ketone-forming reagent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction, is\n3-methylbutan-1-ol [(CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂OH] + 3,4-dihydro-2H-pyran —(H⁺)→","options":["tetrahydropyran ring with the 3-methylbutoxy group on C3 (one carbon away from ring O) – 3-(3-methylbutoxy)oxane","open chain: (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂–O–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH=CH–OH (enol)","open chain: (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂–O–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂–CHO (5-(3-methylbutoxy)pentanal)","tetrahydropyran ring with the 3-methylbutoxy group on C2 (next to ring O) – 2-(3-methylbutoxy)oxane (THP ether)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) anti-Markovnikov-type addition at C3 (from treating the alkene as symmetric); (B)/(C) ring-opened products from thinking the acetal hydrolyses or the ring cleaves.\nWorking: H⁺ protonates C3 of the enol ether, giving an oxocarbenium ion at C2 stabilised by the ring O; the alcohol adds at C2, giving the tetrahydropyranyl (THP) acetal, 2-(3-methylbutoxy)tetrahydropyran.\nKey idea: Protonation gives the cation next to the ring O (oxocarbenium); the alcohol adds there → acetal carbon bears two O atoms.","traps":["Regiochemistry of addition to enol ether: Positive charge sits on C2 (stabilised by O lone pair).","Ring opening vs ring retention: No water present; the alcohol adds and the ring stays intact."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ammonia-derivative reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following ketone will NOT give enamine on treatment with secondary amines? [where t-Bu is –C(CH₃)₃]","options":["C₂H₅COC₂H₅ (pentan-3-one)","C₂H₅COCH₃ (butan-2-one)","t-BuCOt-Bu (2,2,4,4-tetramethylpentan-3-one)","cyclohexanone"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) all have α-hydrogens; students may think steric hindrance in cyclohexanone or ethyl groups matters.\nWorking: enamine formation needs an α-hydrogen to lose after the iminium ion forms; di-tert-butyl ketone has no α-H (and is very hindered), so it cannot form an enamine.\nKey idea: Secondary amine → iminium ion; without α-H it cannot lose a proton to form C=C–N.","traps":["α-H requirement: Look for a ketone with no α-H.","Imine vs enamine: Secondary amine has no N–H left, so H is lost from α-C."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"BrMg–CH₂–CH₂–MgBr + CH₃–CO–CH₂–CO–CH₃ (pentane-2,4-dione) → 'A' —H₂O→ 'B' (Major Product). Consider the above reaction sequence and identify the product B.","options":["1,3-dimethylcyclopentane-1,3-diol (OH and CH₃ on each of C1 and C3 of a cyclopentane)","cyclohexane-1,4-diol","4,5-dimethylcyclopentane-1,3-diol (OH at C1 and C3, CH₃ on the two adjacent carbons C4 and C5)","4,5-dimethylcyclopentane-1,3-dione (two C=O at C1, C3 and CH₃ at C4, C5)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) six-membered ring without methyls – miscounting ring atoms and losing the CH₃ groups; (C) secondary diol with methyls on ring carbons – wrong atoms bonded; (D) diketone – forgets the addition step.\nWorking: each C–MgBr adds to one C=O of the 1,3-diketone, linking the two carbonyl carbons through the CH₂CH₂ bridge to give a five-membered ring (C(OMgBr)(CH₃)–CH₂–C(OMgBr)(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–); hydrolysis gives 1,3-dimethylcyclopentane-1,3-diol = (A). (In reality the acidic CH₂ of acetylacetone would quench the Grignard; the question ignores this.)\nKey idea: Ring = 2 bridge carbons + C–CH₂–C of the diketone = 5 atoms; each carbonyl becomes a tertiary C(OH)CH₃.","traps":["Ring size miscounted: Count: 2 (bridge) + 3 (C–CH₂–C) = 5.","Ketone methyls lost or moved: C=O carbon becomes C(OH)(CH₃) – tertiary."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Compound 'P' on nitration with dil. HNO₃ yields two isomers (A) and (B). These isomers can be separated by steam distillation. Isomers (A) and (B) show the intramolecular and intermolecular hydrogen bonding respectively. Compound (P) on reaction with conc. HNO₃ yields a yellow compound 'C', a strong acid. The number of oxygen atoms is present in compound 'C' ______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 – forgetting the phenolic O; 3 – counting nitro groups only; 9 – adding extra O.\nWorking: P = phenol; dil. HNO₃ gives o-nitrophenol (A, intramolecular H-bond, steam volatile) and p-nitrophenol (B); conc. HNO₃ gives picric acid, 2,4,6-trinitrophenol C₆H₂(NO₂)₃OH: 3 × 2 + 1 = 7 oxygen atoms.\nKey idea: Picric acid = three NO₂ (6 O) + one OH (1 O).","traps":["Phenolic oxygen missed: Count OH oxygen too."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Oxidation of toluene to Benzaldehyde can be easily carried out with which of the following reagents?","options":["CrO₃/acetic acid, H₃O⁺","CrO₃/acetic anhydride, H₃O⁺","KMnO₄/HCl, H₃O⁺","CO/HCl, anhydrous AlCl₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – no anhydride, so oxidation continues to benzoic acid; (C) – strong oxidant gives benzoic acid; (D) – Gattermann–Koch on toluene gives 4-methylbenzaldehyde (ring formylation).\nWorking: CrO₃ in acetic anhydride traps the aldehyde as benzylidene diacetate, which acid hydrolysis converts to benzaldehyde; CrO₃/AcOH and KMnO₄ over-oxidise to benzoic acid; CO/HCl/AlCl₃ (Gattermann–Koch) formylates the ring, giving p-tolualdehyde, not benzaldehyde.\nKey idea: Acetic anhydride protects the aldehyde as a gem-diacetate.","traps":["Acetic acid vs acetic anhydride: Only the anhydride forms benzylidene diacetate, which prevents further oxidation.","Gattermann–Koch thought to oxidise the methyl: Gattermann–Koch adds CHO to the ring; on toluene it gives p-tolualdehyde."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The reagent, from the following, which converts benzoic acid to benzaldehyde in one step is: C₆H₅COOH → C₆H₅CHO","options":["LiAlH₄","KMnO₄","MnO","NaBH₄"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) LiAlH₄ – reduces all the way to benzyl alcohol; (D) NaBH₄ – does not reduce –COOH; (B) KMnO₄ – oxidant, no reduction.\nWorking: vapour-phase reduction of benzoic acid (with formic acid) over MnO catalyst at about 573 K gives benzaldehyde in one step; LiAlH₄ goes on to benzyl alcohol, NaBH₄ does not reduce acids, KMnO₄ is an oxidant.\nKey idea: Free acids need a special catalyst (MnO/HCOOH, ~573 K) to stop at the aldehyde.","traps":["LiAlH₄ thought to stop at aldehyde: Aldehydes are reduced faster than acids, so LiAlH₄ gives the alcohol.","NaBH₄ assumed to reduce acids: NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes/ketones, not carboxylic acids."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The final product 'A' in the following reaction sequence: CH₃CH₂–CO–CH₃ —HCN→ ? —95% H₂SO₄, heat→ A","options":["CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–COOH","CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CN","CH₃–CH₂–C(OH)(CH₃)–COOH","CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CONH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – dehydration without nitrile hydrolysis; (C) – hydrolysis without dehydration; (D) – partial hydrolysis to amide.\nWorking: butan-2-one + HCN → cyanohydrin CH₃CH₂C(OH)(CH₃)CN; hot concentrated H₂SO₄ dehydrates it (more substituted alkene) and hydrolyses –CN (via amide) to –COOH, giving 2-methylbut-2-enoic acid. Note: with 95% acid alone the nitrile stops at the amide sulphate (as in the methacrylamide process), so (D) is chemically defensible before aqueous work-up; answer (A) assumes full hydrolysis.\nKey idea: Hot conc. H₂SO₄ does both: removes the tertiary OH and converts CN → COOH.","traps":["Stopping at the nitrile: Hot aqueous acid also hydrolyses –CN to –COOH.","Stopping at the amide: Under heating with acid the amide is hydrolysed further to the acid."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Carboxylic acid preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which of the following reactions will yield benzaldehyde as a product? (A) Benzoic acid —(i) SOCl₂, (ii) H₂/Pd–BaSO₄ (quinoline)→ ; (B) Benzyl alcohol —CrO₃/H₂SO₄→ ; (C) Methyl benzoate —(i) NaBH₄, (ii) PCC→ ; (D) Toluene —(i) CrO₃, (CH₃CO)₂O, (ii) H₃O⁺, Δ→","options":["(B) and (C)","(C) and (D)","(A) and (D)","(A) and (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – Jones (CrO₃/H₂SO₄) is a strong oxidant: gives acid, not aldehyde (PCC would stop at aldehyde); (C) – NaBH₄ does not reduce esters (LiAlH₄ would).\nWorking: (A) acid → acid chloride → Rosenmund reduction → PhCHO. (D) toluene + CrO₃/Ac₂O gives benzylidene diacetate, hydrolysed to PhCHO. (B) Jones reagent over-oxidises benzyl alcohol to benzoic acid. (C) NaBH₄ does not normally reduce esters, so there is no alcohol for PCC to oxidise → (A) and (D).\nKey idea: Stop-at-aldehyde routes need a mild/controlled step: poisoned catalyst, PCC, or trapping as gem-diacetate.","traps":["CrO₃/H₂SO₄ vs PCC: Jones reagent (aqueous acid) goes to acid; PCC stops at aldehyde.","NaBH₄ reducing esters: NaBH₄ is too weak for esters; LiAlH₄ is needed."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I (conversions of phenol): A. Phenol → salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde); B. Phenol → benzene; C. Phenol → p-benzoquinone (cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione); D. Phenol → 4-bromophenol. List II (reagents): I. Br₂ in CS₂; II. Na₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄; III. Zn; IV. CHCl₃/NaOH. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps C and D (Br₂ ↔ dichromate); (C) puts dichromate on salicylaldehyde formation; (D) swaps B and C.\nWorking: Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH) gives salicylaldehyde; Zn dust reduces phenol to benzene; chromic acid oxidises phenol to p-benzoquinone; Br₂ in CS₂ at low temperature gives monobromophenol (para major).\nKey idea: Each reagent has one NCERT product: Zn reduces, Cr(VI) oxidises, CHCl₃/NaOH formylates, Br₂/CS₂ monobrominates.","traps":["Br₂/CS₂ vs bromine water: Non-polar solvent at low T = monobromination.","Swapped pair: Fix the most certain pairs (B-III, A-IV) first."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Oxidation & reduction of phenol"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Decarboxylation of all six possible forms of diaminobenzoic acids C₆H₃(NH₂)₂COOH yields three products A, B and C. Three acids give a product 'A', two acids gives a product 'B' and one acid give a product 'C'. The melting point of product 'C' is","options":["63 °C","90 °C","104 °C","142 °C"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 63 °C is the m-isomer (product A); (C) 104 °C is the o-isomer (product B); (B) 90 °C is a filler value.\nWorking: the six acids are 2,3-, 2,4-, 2,5-, 2,6-, 3,4- and 3,5-diaminobenzoic acids. Loss of COOH gives: 2,4/2,6/3,5 → m-phenylenediamine (A, three acids); 2,3/3,4 → o-phenylenediamine (B, two); 2,5 → p-phenylenediamine (C, one). p-Phenylenediamine melts at about 140–142 °C (m ≈ 63 °C, o ≈ 102–104 °C).\nKey idea: The unique product comes from 2,5-diaminobenzoic acid: p-phenylenediamine, the most symmetric isomer with the highest m.p.","traps":["Mapping isomers to products: Delete COOH and read the 1,2/1,3/1,4 relation of the two NH₂ groups.","Symmetry and melting point: Para isomers pack best and melt highest."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The correct structure of product 'A' formed in the following reaction: PhCHO + PhCHO —NaOD in D₂O→ A + Ph–C(=O)–O⁻ (Ph is –C₆H₅)","options":["Ph–CH₂–OD","Ph–CH₂–OH","Ph–CHD–OD","Ph–CD₂–OD"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ignores D exchange of O–H with D₂O; (C) assumes D from solvent goes on carbon; (D) assumes both C–H come from solvent.\nWorking: in the Cannizzaro reaction OD⁻ adds to one PhCHO; the aldehydic H (as hydride, from carbon) is transferred to the second PhCHO, so the CH₂ carries two H (one original, one transferred) and no D; the alkoxide picks up D from D₂O, giving PhCH₂OD.\nKey idea: The new C–H bond comes from the other aldehyde molecule, never from the solvent.","traps":["Solvent D placed on carbon: Only O–H positions exchange with D₂O; the C–H comes by hydride shift.","Forgetting O–D exchange: Alkoxide is protonated by the solvent, here D₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The major product (P) of the given reaction is (where Me is –CH₃): 2,2,5-trimethylcyclohexan-1-ol (OH on C1, gem-dimethyl on adjacent C2, Me on C5) —H⁺→ P (major product).","options":["3,3,6-trimethylcyclohexene (C=C next to the gem-dimethyl carbon, no shift)","1,4,6-trimethylcyclohex-1-ene (trisubstituted C=C; Me on C1 and on the adjacent sp³ C6)","1,2,4-trimethylcyclohex-1-ene (tetrasubstituted C=C between the two Me-bearing carbons)","2,4-dimethyl-1-methylidenecyclohexane (exocyclic =CH₂)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the unrearranged product (C1=C6 alkene; C2 has no H, so no shift-free C1=C2 alkene); (B) trisubstituted alkene from the rearranged cation (Saytzeff not followed); (D) exocyclic alkene (least stable).\nWorking: protonation and loss of water give a 2° cation at C1; a 1,2-methyl shift from the gem-dimethyl C2 gives a 3° cation; loss of the C1 H gives the most substituted (tetrasubstituted) alkene, 1,2,4-trimethylcyclohexene.\nKey idea: 2° cation next to a quaternary carbon → methyl shift → 3° cation → most substituted alkene.","traps":["Missing the methyl shift: Check whether a 1,2-shift gives a 3° cation.","Saytzeff vs other alkenes: Most substituted endocyclic alkene is major."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A —(i) Cl₂, Δ; (ii) CN⁻; (iii) H₂O/H⁺→ 4-bromophenylacetic acid. In the above reaction 'A' is:","options":["1-bromo-4-ethylbenzene","bromobenzene","4-bromotoluene (1-bromo-4-methylbenzene)","4-bromostyrene (1-bromo-4-vinylbenzene)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ethyl side chain would give a 3-carbon acid chain; (B) no side chain to chlorinate (aryl C–Br does not undergo SN with CN⁻); (D) Cl₂ adds to the vinyl group.\nWorking: Cl₂/Δ chlorinates the benzylic CH₃ → BrC₆H₄CH₂Cl; CN⁻ gives BrC₆H₄CH₂CN; hydrolysis gives BrC₆H₄CH₂COOH (one extra carbon from CN).\nKey idea: –CH₂COOH = –CH₃ + one carbon from CN⁻.","traps":["Carbon count of the side chain: Product side chain CH₂COOH needs CH₃ in A."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Isobutyraldehyde on reaction with formaldehyde and K₂CO₃ gives compound 'A'. Compound 'A' reacts with KCN and yields compound 'B', which on hydrolysis gives a stable compound 'C'. The compound 'C' is:","options":["HO–CH₂–C(CH₃)₂–CH(OH)–COOH","HO–CH₂CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH(OH)–COOH","γ-lactone: 3-hydroxy-4,4-dimethyloxolan-2-one (OH on the carbon next to C=O, gem-dimethyl on the next carbon, then CH₂–O) (pantolactone)","γ-lactone: 4-hydroxy-3,3-dimethyloxolan-2-one (gem-dimethyl next to C=O, OH on the next carbon)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the open hydroxy acid – correct intermediate but not the \"stable compound\" (it lactonises); (B) wrong skeleton (no gem-dimethyl); (D) lactone with OH and gem-dimethyl positions swapped.\nWorking: crossed aldol gives HOCH₂C(CH₃)₂CHO (A); HCN/KCN adds to CHO → cyanohydrin HOCH₂C(CH₃)₂CH(OH)CN (B); hydrolysis gives the γ-hydroxy acid HOCH₂C(CH₃)₂CH(OH)COOH, which cyclises to the stable five-membered γ-lactone (pantolactone).\nKey idea: A γ-hydroxy acid closes spontaneously to a five-membered lactone; the OH α to the carboxyl stays on the ring next to C=O.","traps":["Open acid vs lactone: 1,4-hydroxy acid → γ-lactone.","Position of OH and gem-dimethyl in the ring: CN carbon becomes C=O; its OH stays on the adjacent carbon."],"topics":["Enolates & aldol reactions","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement I : The esterification of carboxylic acid with an alcohol is a nucleophilic acyl substitution. Statement II : Electron withdrawing groups in the carboxylic acid will increase the rate of esterification reaction. Choose the most appropriate option :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) tempts students who link EWG only to acidity, or who think EWG slows protonation of C=O; (D)/(B) need denying the NCERT mechanism line.\nWorking: I is the NCERT mechanism statement; II – EWG make the carbonyl carbon more electrophilic, speeding nucleophilic attack by the alcohol (the accepted answer; steric effects are not involved here).\nKey idea: More electrophilic C=O → faster nucleophilic attack.","traps":["Acidity vs reactivity: Rate depends on electrophilicity of carbonyl C."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I : Phenols are weakly acidic. Statement II : Therefore they are freely soluble in NaOH solution and are weaker acids than alcohols and water. Choose the most appropriate option:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts the reversed comparison because the first half of II (soluble in NaOH) is true; (D) denies phenols are acidic.\nWorking: phenols are weak acids (pKa ≈ 10) and dissolve in NaOH, but they are stronger, not weaker, acids than alcohols and water.\nKey idea: Phenoxide resonance makes phenol more acidic than water and alcohols.","traps":["Half-true Statement II: Check every clause of a compound statement."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"C₆H₁₂O₆ —[Zymase]→ A —[NaOI, Δ]→ B + CHI₃. The number of carbon atoms present in the product B is ________.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (taking B as acetate/ethanol); 3; 6 (carrying glucose through).\nWorking: zymase ferments glucose to ethanol (A); iodoform reaction CH₃CH₂OH + NaOI → HCOONa (B) + CHI₃; B has 1 C.\nKey idea: Haloform: R–CO–CH₃ → RCOO⁻ + CHX₃; for ethanol R = H, so B = HCOO⁻.","traps":["Carboxylate carbon count: One C leaves as CHI₃."],"topics":["Haloform reaction","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"In the given reaction: cyclohexanol (HO–C₆H₁₁) —(i) K₂Cr₂O₇; (ii) C₆H₅MgBr; (iii) H₂O; (iv) H⁺, heat→ 'X' (major product). The number of sp² hybridised carbon(s) in compound 'X' is ____.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (stopping at the alcohol, i.e. forgetting the new C=C) or 7 (miscounting ring carbons).\nWorking: cyclohexanol → cyclohexanone → (PhMgBr, H₂O) 1-phenylcyclohexan-1-ol → (H⁺, heat) 1-phenylcyclohexene (conjugated with the ring); sp² C = 6 (phenyl) + 2 (C=C) = 8.\nKey idea: Dehydration gives the alkene conjugated with phenyl; count 6 aromatic + 2 alkene carbons.","traps":["Dehydration step missed: H⁺/heat on 3° alcohol gives alkene.","Ring carbons miscounted: Count each sp² centre explicitly."],"topics":["Dehydration","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In the given reaction: cyclohexanone —(i) ⁻OH; (ii) heat→ 'P' (major product). The number of π electrons present in the product 'P' is ____.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (if only the β-hydroxy ketone is taken, no dehydration), 8 (counting O lone pairs as π electrons).\nWorking: base-catalysed aldol condensation of two cyclohexanone molecules, then dehydration on heating, gives 2-cyclohexylidenecyclohexan-1-one (one C=C and one C=O) → 2 π bonds = 4 π electrons.\nKey idea: Heat → dehydration → enone: one C=C + one C=O.","traps":["Dehydration missed: Heat converts aldol to enone.","Lone pairs counted: Count only electrons in π bonds."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The major products formed in the following reaction sequence A and B are: acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃) + Br₂/KOH → A + B","options":["A = C₆H₅COO⁻K⁺ (potassium benzoate), B = CHBr₃","A = 3-bromophenacyl bromide (3-Br-C₆H₄-CO-CH₂Br), B = 3-Br-C₆H₄-CO-CH₂OH","A = C₆H₅COCBr₃, B = C₆H₅CHO","A = 3,5-dibromoacetophenone, B = 3,5-dihydroxyacetophenone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) stops at α-monobromination and adds ring bromination; (C) takes the CBr₃ intermediate and invents benzaldehyde; (D) ring bromination/hydroxylation.\nWorking: methyl ketone + Br₂/KOH undergoes the haloform reaction: PhCOCH₃ → PhCOCBr₃ → PhCOO⁻K⁺ + CHBr₃ (bromoform).\nKey idea: Br₂ + KOH = KOBr; any CH₃CO– group gives bromoform and the carboxylate.","traps":["Base-promoted halogenation goes to CX₃: Remember haloform: CH₃CO– → CX₃CO– → RCOO⁻ + CHX₃.","Intermediate vs final product: Under KOH the tribromoketone is cleaved at once."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is: tryptophan (indol-3-yl-CH₂-CH(NH₂)-COOH) + SOCl₂, CH₃OH → major product","options":["methyl tryptophanate with both the indole N–H and the α-NH₂ as HCl salts (N-H·HCl, NH₂·HCl)","5-chloro-tryptophan with CO₂CH₃ at the carboxyl (ring-chlorinated, free NH₂)","methyl tryptophanate hydrochloride: indole N–H free, CO₂CH₃, NH₂·HCl","tryptophan acid chloride (COCl) with free NH₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) protonates the non-basic indole N; (B) invents ring chlorination; (D) stops at the acid chloride, ignoring methanol.\nWorking: SOCl₂/CH₃OH converts –COOH to –COOCH₃; the HCl generated protonates the basic α-amino group (NH₂·HCl). The indole N–H is non-basic (lone pair in the aromatic π system), so it is not protonated.\nKey idea: Amine N takes the proton; aromatic indole/pyrrole N does not.","traps":["Indole N is not basic: Indole N lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet – it stays free.","SOCl₂ in methanol = esterification: With CH₃OH as solvent the acyl chloride (or HCl formed) gives the methyl ester."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct options for the products A and B of the following reactions are: A ← Br₂ (excess), H₂O — phenol — Br₂, CS₂, < 5 °C → B","options":["A = 2,4,6-tribromophenol, B = 2,4-dibromophenol","A = 2,4,6-tribromophenol, B = 4-bromophenol","A = a tribromophenol with Br on adjacent positions (drawn as 2,3,4-pattern, not 2,4,6), B = 2,6-dibromophenol","A = 4-bromophenol, B = 4-bromophenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B as 2,4-dibromo; (C) non-2,4,6 tribromo pattern and ortho-dibromo B; (D) mono product for bromine water.\nWorking: bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt); Br₂ in CS₂ at low temperature gives monobromophenols, mainly p-bromophenol.\nKey idea: Bromine water → tribromo; Br₂/CS₂, cold → para-mono.","traps":["Solvent controls extent of bromination: Polar water ionises phenol/Br₂ → full substitution; CS₂ at low T → mono.","Para is the major mono product: Para predominates in non-polar solvent at low temperature."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Chemical Reaction): (a) CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ → CH₃CH₂OH; (b) CH₃COOCH₃ → CH₃CHO; (c) CH₃C≡N → CH₃CHO; (d) CH₃C≡N → CH₃COCH₃\nList-II (Reagent used): (i) CH₃MgBr / H₃O⁺ (1 equivalent); (ii) H₂SO₄ / H₂O; (iii) DIBAL-H / H₂O; (iv) SnCl₂, HCl / H₂O\nChoose the most appropriate match:","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps b and c (SnCl₂ on ester, DIBAL on nitrile) – DIBAL works on nitriles too, so this is the main trap; (B) puts SnCl₂ on ester hydrolysis; (D) mismatches everything.\nWorking: (a) acid hydrolysis of ethyl acetate gives ethanol (ii); (b) DIBAL-H reduces an ester to an aldehyde (iii); (c) Stephen reduction SnCl₂/HCl then H₂O gives CH₃CHO (iv); (d) CH₃MgBr then H₃O⁺ converts CH₃CN to acetone (i).\nKey idea: SnCl₂/HCl (Stephen) is nitrile-specific; DIBAL-H is the ester → aldehyde reagent here.","traps":["DIBAL-H reduces both esters and nitriles: Stephen reagent only fits nitriles, forcing DIBAL to the ester.","Grignard on nitrile gives ketone: Count carbons: 2 → 3 needs CH₃⁻."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"[Scheme: 2-methylbutanal, CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CHO —\"X\", HCN, H₂O→ cyanohydrin CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CN —LiAlH₄, H₃O⁺→ \"Y\" (Major Product)] Consider the given reaction, Identify 'X' and 'Y':","options":["X – NaOH; Y – CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)C(OH)H–NH (drawn with C bearing OH, H and NH – an imine/aminal-like fragment)","X – HNO₃; Y – CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CH₂NH₂","X – NaOH; Y – CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CH₂NH₂ (1-amino-3-methylpentan-2-ol)","X – HNO₃; Y – CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)NH₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) right Y but acid catalyst; (D) loses the nitrile carbon (C–NH₂ directly on the carbinol carbon) with wrong catalyst; (A) right catalyst but wrong reduction product.\nWorking: HCN addition is base-catalysed (NaOH generates CN⁻), giving the cyanohydrin; LiAlH₄ reduces –C≡N to –CH₂NH₂, giving the β-amino alcohol CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH(OH)CH₂NH₂.\nKey idea: CN carbon is kept: C≡N → CH₂–NH₂; HCN adds faster with base.","traps":["Base, not acid, catalyses HCN addition: Base raises [CN⁻].","Nitrile carbon is retained: C≡N → CH₂NH₂ adds a carbon to the chain."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The structure of the starting compound P used in the reaction given below is: P —(1) NaOCl; (2) H₃O⁺→ CH₃–CH=CH–COOH (but-2-enoic acid)","options":["CH₃–CH=CH–CO–CH₃ (pent-3-en-2-one)","CH₃–CH₂–CH=CH–CHO (pent-2-enal)","3,4-dihydro-2H-pyran (six-membered ring with O and one C=C)","cyclopentanone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aldehyde of the same carbon number – but NaOCl haloform needs a CH₃CO group, and an aldehyde would keep the carbon count; (C) cyclic enol ether – ring opening does not give the acid; (D) cyclopentanone – not a methyl ketone.\nWorking: NaOCl effects the haloform reaction on a methyl ketone, removing CH₃ as CHCl₃ and leaving the carboxylate (one carbon fewer); CH₃CH=CHCOOH therefore comes from CH₃CH=CHCOCH₃. The C=C is not attacked.\nKey idea: Haloform product = substrate minus one carbon, with CH₃CO– replaced by –COOH.","traps":["Carbon lost in haloform: Add one carbon (as CH₃) to the COOH carbon.","NaOCl seen as aldehyde oxidant: NaOCl here is the haloform reagent – it needs CH₃CO–."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): Synthesis of ethyl phenyl ether may be achieved by Williamson synthesis. Reason (R): Reaction of bromobenzene with sodium ethoxide yields ethyl phenyl ether. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) accept R – miss that aryl halides do not undergo SN2; (C) rejects A – thinks Williamson cannot make aryl ethers.\nWorking: A is true – sodium phenoxide + ethyl bromide (SN2 on a primary halide) gives C₆H₅OC₂H₅. R is false – aryl halides do not undergo SN2 with alkoxides because of partial C–Br double-bond character and sp² carbon.\nKey idea: For Ar–O–R, the aryl part must be the nucleophile (ArO⁻) and the alkyl part the halide.","traps":["Wrong disconnection accepted: Halide must be primary alkyl; aryl halides do not undergo SN2.","A rejected because R is wrong: Judge A on its own – phenoxide route works."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Ethyl pent-4-yn-oate on reaction with CH₃MgBr gives a 3°-alcohol. Statement II: In this reaction one mole of ethyl pent-4-yn-oate utilizes two moles of CH₃MgBr. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) counts only the ester (2 mol) and misses the alkyne H; (A)/(B) assume the alkyne quenching stops alcohol formation altogether.\nWorking: HC≡C–CH₂CH₂–COOC₂H₅ – the ester takes two CH₃MgBr (ketone, then 3° alkoxide) giving a 3° alcohol, HC≡C–CH₂CH₂–C(OH)(CH₃)₂, after work-up (Statement I true); but the terminal alkyne C–H is acidic and destroys one more mole of CH₃MgBr (CH₄ evolved), so one mole of ester uses three moles, not two (Statement II false).\nKey idea: Count every acidic H plus two per ester: 1 + 2 = 3 mol CH₃MgBr.","traps":["Terminal alkyne H ignored: Scan for acidic H (≡C–H, OH, NH, COOH) before counting RMgX.","Ester needs two equivalents: Ester → ketone → 3° alcohol; two R groups added."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the following reactions will not yield propionic acid?","options":["CH₃CH₂COCH₃ + OI⁻/H₃O⁺","CH₃CH₂CH₃ + KMnO₄ (Heat), OH⁻/H₃O⁺","CH₃CH₂CCl₃ + OH⁻/H₃O⁺","CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + Mg, CO₂ dry ether/H₃O⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks like it loses carbons wrongly – haloform removes CH₃ only, leaving C₃ acid; (C) gem-trihalide hydrolysis is unfamiliar but gives the acid; (B) is chemically weak but intended as giving propanoic acid.\nWorking: (A) iodoform reaction of butan-2-one gives CH₃CH₂COO⁻ → propionic acid; (C) gem-trichloride hydrolyses to CH₃CH₂COOH; (D) propylmagnesium bromide + CO₂ gives butanoic acid (four carbons), so it certainly does not give propionic acid. (B) is also doubtful: propane has no tertiary H and is not normally oxidised to an acid by KMnO₄ – the question treats it as giving propanoic acid. (D) is the clearest answer.\nKey idea: Grignard + CO₂ adds one carbon: C₃ halide → C₄ acid.","traps":["Grignard carboxylation adds a carbon: RMgX + CO₂ → RCOOH: acid has one more C than R.","Haloform cleavage carbon count: R–CO–CH₃ → R–COO⁻; R = C₂H₅ gives propanoate."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which one of the following is the major product of the given reaction? Substrate: 1,3-dimethyl-2-oxo-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene-7-carbonitrile (a tetralin with C=O at C-2, CH₃ at C-1 (benzylic) and C-3, and CN on the aromatic ring) treated with (i) 2CH₃MgBr, (ii) H₃O⁺, (iii) H₂SO₄, heat.","options":["Aryl methyl ketone (CH₃CO– on the aromatic ring in place of CN) with a tetrasubstituted C1=C2 double bond: 1,2,3-trimethyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalene bearing the acetyl group (alkene conjugated with the ring)","CN retained, 1,2,3-trimethyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalene (C1=C2 double bond conjugated with the ring)","CN retained, 1,2,3-trimethyl-1,4-dihydronaphthalene (C2=C3 double bond, not conjugated with the ring)","Acetyl on the ring with the C2=C3 double bond (1,2,3-trimethyl-1,4-dihydronaphthalene, not conjugated)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) keep the CN – assume only the ketone reacts despite 2 equivalents; (D) converts CN correctly but places the double bond away from the ring (less stable, unconjugated).\nWorking: two equivalents of CH₃MgBr add once to the ketone (→ 3° alkoxide) and once to the nitrile (→ imine salt); H₃O⁺ gives the 3° alcohol and converts the imine to an acetyl group (Ar–CO–CH₃). Hot H₂SO₄ dehydrates the 3° alcohol; loss of the benzylic C-1 H gives the tetrasubstituted alkene conjugated with the aromatic ring (most stable), i.e. option (A).\nKey idea: 2 equiv. RMgX = one for C=O, one for C≡N; then the alkene goes where it is most substituted and conjugated with the arene.","traps":["Nitrile left untouched: Count equivalents: 2 CH₃MgBr with two electrophilic groups → both react.","Imine hydrolysis to ketone: R–C(=NMgBr)–CH₃ + H₃O⁺ → R–CO–CH₃."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Dehydration"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The major product of the following reaction, if it occurs by SN2 mechanism is: phenol + 1-bromo-3-methylbut-2-ene (prenyl bromide, (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂Br) —K₂CO₃, acetone→","options":["(CH₃)₂C(OPh)–CH₂–CH₂–OPh (1,3-diphenoxy-3-methylbutane)","PhO–C(CH₃)₂–CH=CH₂ (2-methylbut-3-en-2-yl phenyl ether)","CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–OPh (3-methylbut-3-enyl phenyl ether)","PhO–CH₂–CH=C(CH₃)₂ (3-methylbut-2-enyl phenyl ether, prenyl phenyl ether)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the SN1/SN2′ (allylic-shift) product at the tertiary carbon; (C) moves the double bond (isomerised chain); (A) adds phenol across the alkene as well – needs acid, not base.\nWorking: K₂CO₃ generates phenoxide, which attacks the primary allylic CH₂–Br carbon directly (SN2) with no allylic shift, giving PhO–CH₂CH=C(CH₃)₂.\nKey idea: \"SN2\" = nucleophile bonds to the carbon that carried Br; the C=C stays where it was.","traps":["Allylic rearrangement product: SN2 has no carbocation – no allylic shift.","Double-bond position lost: Track the C=C: it stays between C2 and C3 of the prenyl unit."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The major products A and B in the following set of reactions are: A ←(LiAlH₄, H₃O⁺)— (CH₃)₂C(OH)CN (acetone cyanohydrin, 2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile) —(H₃O⁺, H₂SO₄)→ B","options":["A = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂OH, B = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CHO","A = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CHO, B = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CO₂H","A = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂NH₂, B = CH₂=C(CH₃)COOH","A = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂NH₂, B = (CH₃)₂C(OH)CHO"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats LiAlH₄ as converting CN to CH₂OH and hydrolysis as giving CHO; (B) A as aldehyde (DIBAL/SnCl₂ type partial reduction) and B without dehydration; (D) right A but B as aldehyde – hydrolysis stops at wrong stage.\nWorking: LiAlH₄ reduces –C≡N to –CH₂NH₂, giving A = 1-amino-2-methylpropan-2-ol. Acid hydrolysis converts CN to COOH (2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanoic acid); with H₂SO₄ the tertiary OH is dehydrated to give B = 2-methylpropenoic (methacrylic) acid. Option (C).\nKey idea: H₂SO₄ with a tertiary α-hydroxy acid → α,β-unsaturated acid (methacrylic acid).","traps":["Dehydration missed: Conc. H₂SO₄ + tertiary OH → alkene.","Nitrile hydrolysis product: Complete acid hydrolysis of CN gives COOH."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is: CH₃–C(CH₃)₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol) —(conc. H₂SO₄, a few drops)→ Major product","options":["CH₃–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₂CH₃","(CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)₂ (2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene)","(CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₃","(CH₃)₃C–CH=CH₂ (3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) direct elimination without rearrangement (Hofmann-type, less substituted); (C) loses a carbon – wrong skeleton; (A) has a rearranged (wrong) carbon skeleton.\nWorking: protonation and loss of water give a secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives the tertiary cation (CH₃)₂C⁺–CH(CH₃)₂; loss of H⁺ to give the most substituted alkene gives 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene (B).\nKey idea: Secondary cation beside a quaternary carbon → methyl shift → tetrasubstituted alkene.","traps":["No rearrangement: Check if 1,2-shift gives a more stable cation first.","Carbon count of product: Product must keep 6 carbons."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The structure of product C, formed by the following sequence of reactions is: CH₃COOH + SOCl₂ → A —(Benzene, AlCl₃)→ B —(KCN, ⁻OH)→ C","options":["C₆H₅–C(OH)(CN)–CH₃ (acetophenone cyanohydrin)","2-cyanoacetophenone (o-NC–C₆H₄–COCH₃)","C₆H₅–CH(CH₃)–COOH (2-phenylpropanoic acid)","C₆H₅–CH₂–CH₂CN (3-phenylpropanenitrile)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats CN⁻ as substituting the ring; (C) hydrolysed and reduced product; (D) chain-extended nitrile (as if alkyl halide + KCN).\nWorking: SOCl₂ converts acetic acid to acetyl chloride (A); Friedel–Crafts acylation gives acetophenone (B); cyanide with base adds to the carbonyl to give the cyanohydrin C₆H₅C(OH)(CN)CH₃ (C). Option (A).\nKey idea: KCN/OH⁻ on a ketone = cyanohydrin, not substitution.","traps":["KCN as substitution reagent: B is a ketone – CN⁻ adds to C=O.","Stopping at cyanohydrin: No H₃O⁺ step is given."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The structures of A and B formed in the following reaction are: [Ph = –C₆H₅] Benzene + succinic anhydride (cyclic, O=C–CH₂–CH₂–C(=O)–O ring) —AlCl₃ (2 eq)→ A —Zn/Hg, HCl→ B","options":["A = Ph–CO–CH₂–CH₂–COOH (4-oxo-4-phenylbutanoic acid), B = Ph–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–COOH (4-phenylbutanoic acid)","A = Ph–CO–CH₂–CH₃ (propiophenone), B = Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH₃","A = Ph–CO–CH₂–CH₂–COOH, B = Ph–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂–OH (4-phenylbutan-1-ol)","A = Ph–CO–CH₂–CH₃, B = Ph–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (propylbenzene)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) treat the anhydride as a three-carbon acyl source and lose the acid group; (B) also reduces C=O only to CHOH; (C) has Clemmensen reduce the COOH to CH₂OH as well.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation by the cyclic anhydride opens the ring to give the γ-keto acid A; Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl) reduces only the ketone C=O to CH₂, leaving COOH, so B = 4-phenylbutanoic acid.\nKey idea: Cyclic anhydride + ArH/AlCl₃ → Ar–CO–(CH₂)₂–COOH; Zn–Hg/HCl touches only aldehyde/ketone C=O.","traps":["Anhydride ring forgotten: Ring opening keeps both carbonyl carbons: one becomes the ketone, the other COOH.","Clemmensen reducing COOH: Clemmensen reduces only aldehyde/ketone carbonyls to CH₂."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"For the reaction given below: 4-(hydroxymethyl)benzaldehyde (p-HOCH₂–C₆H₄–CHO) —1. NaOH, Δ; 2. H₃O⁺→ Product. The compound which is not formed as a product in the reaction is a:","options":["compound with both alcohol and acid functional groups","monocarboxylic acid","dicarboxylic acid","diol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hydroxy acid is formed; (B) the same hydroxy acid is a monocarboxylic acid – students may reject it thinking (B) means a different product; (D) diol is the reduction product.\nWorking: the aldehyde has no α-H, so Cannizzaro occurs: half is oxidised to 4-(hydroxymethyl)benzoic acid (alcohol + acid; also a monocarboxylic acid) and half reduced to 1,4-benzenedimethanol (diol). The CH₂OH group is not oxidised, so no dicarboxylic acid forms.\nKey idea: Cannizzaro changes only the CHO: one CHO → COOH, the other → CH₂OH; the existing CH₂OH stays.","traps":["CH₂OH oxidised by NaOH: NaOH is not an oxidant; only the hydride transfer between CHO groups occurs.","Overlapping category options: Check each class against every product, not one product per class."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"What is the final product (major) 'A' in the given reaction? 1-(2-Methylcyclohexyl)ethan-1-ol [cyclohexane bearing CH₃ on C-1 and CH(OH)CH₃ on the adjacent C-2] —HCl→ 'A' (major product)","options":["1-Chloro-1-ethyl-2-methylcyclohexane (ring carbon bearing Cl and CH₂CH₃, next to the CH₃-bearing carbon)","1-Ethylidene-2-methylcyclohexane (exocyclic C=CH–CH₃)","1-Methyl-2-vinylcyclohexane (ring CH–CH=CH₂)","1-(1-Chloroethyl)-2-methylcyclohexane (direct substitution product, CH(Cl)CH₃)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – direct substitution without rearrangement; (B)/(C) – elimination products (HCl with an alcohol gives substitution as major, not alkene).\nWorking: protonation and loss of water give a secondary carbocation on the side chain; a 1,2-hydride shift from the ring carbon gives a more stable tertiary carbocation, which Cl⁻ captures to give the tertiary chloride.\nKey idea: Secondary carbocation next to a tertiary C–H → hydride shift → tertiary chloride.","traps":["Rearrangement missed: Always check for a more stable carbocation one carbon away.","Elimination chosen: HCl provides a good nucleophile; substitution dominates."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Named reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I and List-II. List-I: (a) R–CO–Cl → R–CHO; (b) R–CH₂–COOH → R–CH(Cl)–COOH; (c) R–CO–NH₂ → R–NH₂; (d) R–CO–CH₃ → R–CH₂–CH₃. List-II: (i) Br₂/NaOH; (ii) H₂/Pd–BaSO₄; (iii) Zn(Hg)/conc. HCl; (iv) Cl₂/Red P, H₂O. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)–(ii), (b)–(i), (c)–(iv), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(i), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(i), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(i), (c)–(iv), (d)–(ii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps HVZ with Hofmann (both involve halogen); (B) and (D) give acyl chloride → aldehyde to Clemmensen (both are \"reductions\").\nWorking: Rosenmund (ii), HVZ (iv), Hofmann bromamide (i), Clemmensen (iii).\nKey idea: Match each transformation to the functional-group change it makes.","traps":["Halogen reagents swapped: Br₂/NaOH removes C=O carbon; Cl₂/P halogenates α-C."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Reaction: carvone [2-methyl-5-(prop-1-en-2-yl)cyclohex-2-en-1-one] → ? → limonene [1-methyl-4-(prop-1-en-2-yl)cyclohex-1-ene]. The C=O is converted into CH₂, both C=C bonds kept. Which of the following reagent is suitable for the preparation of the product in the above reaction?","options":["NaBH₄","NH₂–NH₂ / C₂H₅O⁻Na⁺","Ni/H₂","Red P + Cl₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NaBH₄ – gives alcohol, not CH₂; (C) Ni/H₂ – hydrogenates C=C too; (D) red P + Cl₂ – α-halogenation (HVZ-type), not reduction.\nWorking: Wolff–Kishner (hydrazine, then base on heating) reduces C=O to CH₂ and leaves C=C bonds untouched; NaBH₄ gives the alcohol, Ni/H₂ also reduces C=C.\nKey idea: C=O → CH₂ means Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner; only Wolff–Kishner is offered.","traps":["NaBH₄ as full reduction: NaBH₄ stops at CH–OH.","Ni/H₂ unselective: Check that C=C survives in the product."],"topics":["Reduction of carbonyl compounds","Named reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which one of the following reactions will not form acetaldehyde?","options":["CH₃CH₂OH —Cu, 573 K→","CH₃CN —(i) DIBAL-H, (ii) H₂O→","CH₂=CH₂ + O₂ —Pd(II)/Cu(II), H₂O→","CH₃CH₂OH —CrO₃–H₂SO₄→"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cu/573 K misread as dehydration to ethene; (B) DIBAL-H thought to give amine; (C) Wacker process unfamiliar (not in NCERT) so students pick it.\nWorking: (A) Cu/573 K dehydrogenates ethanol to CH₃CHO; (B) DIBAL-H then H₂O converts CH₃CN to CH₃CHO; (C) Wacker oxidation (Pd(II)/Cu(II), H₂O, O₂) gives CH₃CHO from ethene; (D) Jones reagent (CrO₃–H₂SO₄) oxidises a primary alcohol through to CH₃COOH.\nKey idea: CrO₃–H₂SO₄ (Jones) takes 1° alcohols to acids; PCC/Cu-dehydrogenation stop at aldehyde.","traps":["Unfamiliar Wacker process picked: Eliminate the option you can positively reject (Jones → acid).","All CrO₃ reagents treated alike: Aqueous acidic CrO₃ over-oxidises 1° alcohols to acids."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The major product of the following chemical reaction is: CH₃CH₂CN —(1) H₃O⁺, Δ; (2) SOCl₂; (3) Pd/BaSO₄, H₂→ ?","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","(CH₃CH₂CO)₂O","CH₃CH₂CHO"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) over-reduction to alkane; (B) over-reduction to alcohol (poisoned catalyst prevents this); (C) anhydride from acid chloride + acid.\nWorking: CH₃CH₂CN —H₃O⁺, Δ→ CH₃CH₂COOH —SOCl₂→ CH₃CH₂COCl —H₂, Pd/BaSO₄ (Rosenmund)→ CH₃CH₂CHO (propanal).\nKey idea: Pd/BaSO₄ is poisoned so it stops at the aldehyde.","traps":["Full reduction to alcohol: BaSO₄ poisons Pd and stops at the aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Consider the following chemical reaction: CH≡CH —(1) Red hot Fe tube, 873 K; (2) CO, HCl, AlCl₃→ Product. The number of sp² hybridized carbon atom(s) present in the product is ______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (forgetting the CHO carbon), 0/2 (stopping at ethyne: sp carbons), 8 (counting O).\nWorking: ethyne through red-hot iron tube at 873 K trimerises to benzene; Gattermann–Koch (CO, HCl, anhyd. AlCl₃) gives benzaldehyde, C₆H₅CHO: 6 ring carbons + 1 carbonyl carbon = 7 sp² carbons.\nKey idea: Benzaldehyde: all 7 carbons are sp².","traps":["CHO carbon omitted: Carbonyl carbon is sp² too."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of carboxylic acids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct order of acid character of the following compounds is: I phenol, II p-nitrobenzoic acid, III benzoic acid, IV p-methylbenzoic acid (p-toluic acid).","options":["III > II > I > IV","IV > III > II > I","I > II > III > IV","II > III > IV > I"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts benzoic above nitrobenzoic; (B) reverses EWG/EDG roles; (C) puts phenol at the top.\nWorking: carboxylic acids are much stronger than phenol; among benzoic acids, p-NO₂ (−R, −I) increases acidity and p-CH₃ (+I, hyperconjugation) decreases it: II > III > IV > I.\nKey idea: COOH > OH in acidity; EWG para raises, EDG para lowers.","traps":["Phenol overestimated: pKa phenol ≈ 10 vs benzoic ≈ 4.2."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: CH₃CH₂CH=CH₂ + H₂/CO (Rh catalyst) →","options":["CH₃CH₂CH=CH–CHO","CH₃CH₂C(CHO)=CH₂","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CHO","CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) keep the C=C (H₂ not added); (D) loses a carbon (looks like ozonolysis/Wacker logic).\nWorking: hydroformylation (oxo process) adds H and CHO across C=C; with Rh catalysts the linear aldehyde, pentanal, is the major product (one carbon more than but-1-ene, no C=C left).\nKey idea: Oxo process: alkene + CO + H₂ → aldehyde with one extra carbon, mainly linear.","traps":["Carbon count wrong: CO adds one carbon.","Branched aldehyde chosen: Rh hydroformylation favours linear (anti-Markovnikov-type) product."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"What is 'X' in the given reaction? Ethylene glycol (HOCH₂–CH₂OH) + oxalic acid, 210 °C → X (major product)","options":["CH₂=CH₂","CH(OH)=CH₂","OHC–CHO","HOCH₂–CHO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) vinyl alcohol – unstable enol, would not be major; (C) glyoxal – oxidation product (not oxalic acid's role); (D) glycolaldehyde – partial oxidation.\nWorking: by analogy with glycerol + oxalic acid at high temperature (→ allyl alcohol), ethylene glycol forms the diformate (via monooxalate, loss of CO₂), which on heating loses CO₂ and H₂O from both ends to give the alkene, ethene. Caveat: this glycol–oxalic acid reaction is obscure (not in NCERT) and the textbook-documented analogue is glycerol → allyl alcohol; the accepted answer is ethene.\nKey idea: At 210–260 °C oxalic acid converts each –CH(OH)– pair into C=C via formate esters.","traps":["Oxalic acid seen as oxidant: Oxalic acid acts via formate esters/decarboxylation, not as an oxidant.","Glycerol analogy pushed to an enol: Glycol has one fewer OH: product is the plain alkene."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Identify the major products A and B respectively in the following reactions of phenol: phenol —(Br₂ in CS₂, 273 K)→ A; phenol —(i) CHCl₃, NaOH; (ii) H₃O⁺→ B.","options":["2-bromophenol and 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde","4-bromophenol and 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde)","2-bromophenol and 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde","4-bromophenol and 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) give ortho-bromo (minor in CS₂); (A)/(D) give para-CHO (Reimer–Tiemann is ortho-selective).\nWorking: Br₂ in CS₂ at low temperature gives mainly p-bromophenol (monobromination); Reimer–Tiemann gives ortho –CHO (salicylaldehyde).\nKey idea: Br₂/CS₂ → para major; CHCl₃/NaOH → ortho-CHO.","traps":["Ortho bromophenol taken as major: Para product dominates for steric reasons.","Bromine water product expected: Non-polar CS₂, 273 K → monobromination."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Named reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A (C₄H₈Cl₂) —Hydrolysis, 373 K→ B (C₄H₈O). B reacts with hydroxyl amine but does not give Tollens' test. Identify A and B.","options":["1,1-Dichlorobutane and 2-Butanone","2,2-Dichlorobutane and Butanal","1,1-Dichlorobutane and Butanal","2,2-Dichlorobutane and Butan-2-one"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) pairs a 1,1-dihalide with a ketone – inconsistent (1,1 gives aldehyde); (B) pairs 2,2 with an aldehyde – inconsistent; (C) butanal would give Tollens test.\nWorking: B forms an oxime but is Tollens-negative → ketone, butan-2-one; a gem-dihalide on hydrolysis gives the carbonyl at the same carbon → 2,2-dichlorobutane.\nKey idea: Terminal gem-dihalide → aldehyde; internal gem-dihalide → ketone.","traps":["Mismatched pair chosen: Check that the C=O forms where both Cl were.","Hydroxylamine test thought aldehyde-only: Oxime forms with both; Tollens separates them."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Identify A in the given chemical reaction: 3,3'-(1,2-phenylene)dipropanal, o-C₆H₄(CH₂CH₂CHO)₂ —[NaOH, C₂H₅OH, H₂O, Δ]→ A (major product).","options":["3,4-dihydronaphthalene-2-carbaldehyde (benzo-fused six-membered ring with CHO on the ring C=C)","o-(CH₂CH₂COOH)(CH₂CH₂CH₂OH)C₆H₄ (Cannizzaro-type acid + alcohol)","6,7-dihydro-5H-benzo[7]annulene-8-carbaldehyde (benzo-fused seven-membered ring with CHO on the ring C=C)","1,4-dihydronaphthalene-2,3-dione (benzo-fused six-membered ring bearing two C=O)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – miscounted ring as six-membered; (B) – Cannizzaro assumed (but the aldehyde has α-H); (D) – meaningless diketone from oxidation thinking.\nWorking: intramolecular aldol – the α-CH₂ of one chain attacks the other CHO, closing a 7-membered ring (Cα, CH₂, two ring carbons, CH₂, CH₂, CH); heating dehydrates to the conjugated enal with CHO on the ring double bond.\nKey idea: Ring size = atoms from the enolate carbon to the attacked carbonyl carbon along the tether: 7.","traps":["Ring size miscounted: Number every atom in the new ring.","Cannizzaro chosen with NaOH: Cannizzaro needs no α-H; here α-H present."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Identify A in the following reaction: 4-methoxybenzaldehyde —[(i) HCHO, NaOH; (ii) CH₃CH₂Br, NaH, DMF; (iii) HI, Δ]→ A","options":["4-HO–C₆H₄–COOCH₂CH₃ (ethyl 4-hydroxybenzoate)","4-CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂OH","4-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂I","4-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂OH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – stops HI cleavage at alcohol (forgets excess HI converts benzyl alcohol/ether to iodide); (B) – only step (i), ignores (ii)/(iii); (A) – ArCHO oxidised instead of reduced in crossed Cannizzaro.\nWorking: (i) crossed Cannizzaro – HCHO is oxidised, ArCHO reduced to 4-MeO-C₆H₄CH₂OH; (ii) Williamson gives 4-MeO-C₆H₄CH₂OEt; (iii) HI (excess, heat) cleaves the aryl methyl ether to phenol + CH₃I and the benzyl ethyl ether at the benzylic C–O giving ArCH₂I + EtOH → 4-HO-C₆H₄CH₂I.\nKey idea: In crossed Cannizzaro HCHO is always the one oxidised; benzylic C–O breaks with HI to give benzyl iodide.","traps":["Benzyl ether gives benzyl alcohol: Benzylic C–O cleaves via stable cation → ArCH₂I.","Wrong partner oxidised in crossed Cannizzaro: HCHO → HCOO⁻ always; ArCHO → ArCH₂OH."],"topics":["Ether reactions","Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Identify A in the given reaction: 5,6,7,8-tetrahydronaphthalene bearing a phenolic OH at C1 (aromatic ring), CH₂OH at C4 (aromatic ring) and a secondary alcohol OH at C5 (saturated ring, benzylic) —[SOCl₂]→ A (major product).","options":["Phenolic OH and ring CH–OH retained; CH₂OH → CH₂Cl","Phenolic OH retained; ring CH–OH → CH–Cl and CH₂OH → CH₂Cl","All three OH replaced: Ar–Cl, CH–Cl and CH₂Cl","Phenolic OH replaced by Cl; ring CH–OH and CH₂OH retained"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – phenolic OH also replaced; (A) – secondary OH thought unreactive; (D) – only phenol reacts (reverse).\nWorking: SOCl₂ converts alcoholic OH (primary benzylic CH₂OH and secondary benzylic CH–OH) to C–Cl; phenolic C–OH has partial double-bond character and is not replaced.\nKey idea: Phenol OH is not substituted by SOCl₂/PCl₅ in normal conditions.","traps":["Phenolic OH replaced too: Ar–OH has partial double-bond C–O; not replaced.","Secondary alcohol thought unreactive: SOCl₂ converts 1° and 2° alcohols."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which among the following compound/s does/do not form silver mirror when treated with Tollens' reagent?\n(I) (CH₃)₂CH–CHO (2-methylpropanal)\n(II) CH₂=C(OH)–CH₃ (prop-1-en-2-ol, enol of acetone)\n(III) cyclohexylidene-methanol, (CH₂)₅C=CH–OH (enol of cyclohexanecarbaldehyde)\n(IV) tetrahydro-2H-pyran-2-ol (cyclic hemiacetal)","options":["(I), (III) and (IV) only","Only (IV)","Only (II)","(III) and (IV) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for students who think only II reacts (reverse logic); (B)/(D) for those who think a ring hemiacetal or an enol \"has no CHO\" and cannot react.\nWorking: (I) is an aldehyde; (III) tautomerises to cyclohexanecarbaldehyde; (IV) is a cyclic hemiacetal in equilibrium with open-chain 5-hydroxypentanal – all three give the silver mirror. (II) tautomerises to acetone, a ketone, so it does not.\nKey idea: Rewrite every enol as its keto/aldehyde tautomer and every hemiacetal as the open-chain carbonyl before applying the test.","traps":["Enol not converted: Tautomerise: II → ketone, III → aldehyde.","Hemiacetal treated as ether: Cyclic hemiacetals open to aldehydes, like glucose."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ammonia-derivative reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of nitrogen atoms in a semicarbazone molecule of acetone is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (confusing with hydrazone/oxime-type products); 1 (counting only the imine N).\nWorking: acetone semicarbazone is (CH₃)₂C=N–NH–CO–NH₂; semicarbazide NH₂NHCONH₂ brings all three N atoms into the product.\nKey idea: Semicarbazide has 3 N; condensation loses only H₂O.","traps":["Semicarbazide formula: H₂N–NH–CO–NH₂ has 3 N."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"To synthesise 1.0 mole of 2-methylpropan-2-ol from Ethylethanoate ________ equivalents of CH₃MgBr reagent will be required. (Integer value)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (treating the ester like a ketone); 3 (counting the ethoxy group as needing a Grignard).\nWorking: CH₃COOC₂H₅ + CH₃MgBr → (CH₃)₂C=O (after loss of EtOMgBr); acetone + CH₃MgBr → (CH₃)₃COMgBr → (CH₃)₃COH on hydrolysis. Two equivalents per mole of alcohol.\nKey idea: Ester → ketone (1 eq) → 3° alcohol (2nd eq); two R groups added are the same.","traps":["Ester treated as ketone: The ketone intermediate reacts again."],"topics":["Alcohol preparation","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The major product (P) in the following reaction is : 2-oxo-1-(3-oxobutyl)cyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde [a cyclohexanone whose α-carbon carries both CHO and CH₂CH₂COCH₃] —(i) KOH (alc.) (ii) H⁺, Δ→ P (major product).","options":["bridged bicyclic enal: bicyclo[3.3.1]-type ring with a methyl-substituted C=C, CHO and ketone at the bridgehead region (from ring-enolate attack on the side-chain ketone)","spiro[5.5]undecane: cyclohexanone spiro-fused to cyclohex-2-en-1-one (spiro carbon α to the ring C=O, the new C=C adjacent to the spiro carbon)","octalone (4a-formyl-4,4a,5,6,7,8-hexahydronaphthalen-2(3H)-one): fused bicyclic enone with CHO at the ring junction","spiro[3.5] compound: cyclohexanone spiro-fused to a cyclobutene bearing an acetyl group"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) the classic Robinson-annulation/Wieland–Miescher pattern – attack on the ring ketone – is the most tempting; (D) four-membered ring from the internal CH₂ enolate onto CHO; (A) bridged product from the ring enolate onto the side-chain ketone.\nWorking: the least hindered enolate (the CH₃ of the methyl ketone) attacks the most electrophilic carbonyl, the aldehyde; the new ring contains CH₃-carbon, C=O, two CH₂ and the quaternary carbon plus CHO carbon = six-membered; acid/heat dehydrates the β-hydroxy ketone to the conjugated enone, giving the spiro[5.5] enone (B). (C) would need attack on the less reactive ring ketone (Robinson-type), (D) needs a strained 4-ring, (A) needs attack on the hindered ketone forming a bridge.\nKey idea: In intramolecular aldols pick the pathway giving a 5/6-membered ring with the most electrophilic carbonyl (aldehyde) as acceptor.","traps":["Robinson annulation pattern recalled: Check which carbonyl is most electrophilic: an aldehyde beats the ring ketone when both give six-membered rings.","Ring size ignored: Count atoms in the new ring; reject 3/4-membered rings when a 6-ring is available."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"(A) R–CO–Cl (B) R–CO–O–CO–R (C) R–CO–O–R (D) R–CO–NH₂. The correct order of their reactivity towards hydrolysis at room temperature is :","options":["(A) > (B) > (C) > (D)","(D) > (A) > (B) > (C)","(D) > (B) > (A) > (C)","(A) > (C) > (B) > (D)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts ester above anhydride; (B)/(C) put amide first (confusing basicity or H-bonding with reactivity).\nWorking: reactivity of acyl derivatives to nucleophilic acyl substitution: acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide (leaving-group ability Cl⁻ > RCOO⁻ > RO⁻ > NH₂⁻; resonance donation increases in the reverse order).\nKey idea: Better leaving group and weaker resonance donation → faster hydrolysis: Cl > OCOR > OR > NH₂.","traps":["Anhydride vs ester: Carboxylate is a weaker base (better leaving group) than alkoxide.","Amide placed first: N lone pair donation makes amides the least reactive."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following compounds will provide a tertiary alcohol on reaction with excess of CH₃MgBr followed by hydrolysis?","options":["Phenyl acetate, CH₃COOC₆H₅ (structure: Ph–O–C(=O)–CH₃)","4-(Ethoxymethyl)-2-ethynylbenzonitrile: benzene ring bearing –CN, –C≡CH ortho to CN and –CH₂OCH₂CH₃ para to CN","3-Hydroxyacetophenone (m-HO–C₆H₄–COCH₃)","1,4-Naphthoquinone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) –C≡CH acidic H and –CN → ketone; (C) phenolic OH seen as \"killing\" the Grignard (but excess is given); (D) quinone C=O often not recognised as ketone.\nWorking: the ester gives 2-methylpropan-2-ol (Me₂C(OH)Me, via acetone, two Me added) plus phenol – the textbook route to a tertiary alcohol. Caveat: with EXCESS CH₃MgBr, (C) also gives a tertiary alcohol after the phenolic OH consumes one equivalent (2-(3-hydroxyphenyl)propan-2-ol), and (D) can add Me to both C=O giving a di-tertiary diol; the intended answer is (A). In (B) the terminal alkyne H destroys the reagent and –CN gives a ketone after hydrolysis.\nKey idea: RCOOR' + 2 R''MgX → tertiary alcohol R(R'')₂C–OH; acidic H only consumes one extra equivalent.","traps":["Ester double addition: Ester + 2 RMgX → 3° alcohol.","Acidic H with excess reagent: With excess RMgX the ketone still reacts; choose the textbook (ester) case."],"topics":["Alcohol preparation","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"An organic compound A (C₆H₆O) gives dark green colouration with ferric chloride. On treatment with CHCl₃ and KOH, followed by acidification gives compound B. Compound B can also be obtained from compound C on reaction with pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC). Identify A, B and C.","options":["A = phenol; B = 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde); C = 2-hydroxybenzyl alcohol (o-HOC₆H₄CH₂OH)","A = phenol; B = 2-hydroxybenzyl alcohol; C = 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde","A = 2-hydroxybenzyl alcohol; B = 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde; C = phenol","A = 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde; B = 2-hydroxybenzyl alcohol; C = phenol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps B and C (PCC would oxidise, not reduce); (C)/(D) misassign A despite the C₆H₆O formula.\nWorking: C₆H₆O with FeCl₃ colour = phenol; Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/KOH, then H⁺) gives salicylaldehyde; PCC oxidises the 1° alcohol o-hydroxybenzyl alcohol to the same aldehyde.\nKey idea: C₆H₆O must be phenol; PCC goes alcohol → aldehyde.","traps":["B and C swapped: PCC is an oxidant: CH₂OH → CHO.","Formula ignored: C₆H₆O fixes A = phenol."],"topics":["Phenol substitution","Aldehyde & ketone preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"2,2-Dimethylcyclopentanone —[CH₃CHO, NaOH]→ 'P' (major product) —[(i) I₂/NaOH, filter; (ii) filtrate + HCl]→ 'X'. Consider the given reaction, the product 'X' is:","options":["5-(1-hydroxyethyl)-2,2-dimethylcyclopentan-1-one","5-acetyl-2,2-dimethylcyclopentan-1-one","2-(1-hydroxy-2,2-dimethylcyclopentyl)ethanal (ring carbon carries OH and CH₂CHO; adjacent ring carbon gem-dimethyl)","3,3-dimethyl-2-oxocyclopentane-1-carboxylic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is P itself (stops before the haloform step); (B) is the oxidised but not cleaved methyl ketone; (C) wrong aldol direction (ethanal enolate attacking the hindered ketone C=O) and no haloform step.\nWorking: the ketone enolate (only C-5 has α-H) adds to CH₃CHO (crossed aldol) giving P = 5-(1-hydroxyethyl)-2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone with a CH(OH)CH₃ group; I₂/NaOH converts CH(OH)CH₃ to –COO⁻ with CHI₃ (filtered off); acidifying the filtrate gives the β-keto acid (D).\nKey idea: CH(OH)CH₃ is iodoform-positive: it becomes –COOH (after acidification) and loses one carbon as CHI₃.","traps":["Stopping at P: Apply every reagent in the scheme.","Which partner forms the enolate: Ketone enolate + more electrophilic aldehyde gives the ketol shown in (A)."],"topics":["Haloform reaction","Enolates & aldol reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"A reaction of benzonitrile with one equivalent CH₃MgBr followed by hydrolysis produces a yellow liquid \"P\". The compound \"P\" will give positive ______.","options":["Iodoform test","Schiff's test","Ninhydrin's test","Tollen's test"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) and (B) suit aldehydes – tempting if the student thinks nitrile gives an aldehyde (that is Stephen reduction/DIBAL); (C) is for amino acids/amines.\nWorking: PhC≡N + CH₃MgBr → imine salt → hydrolysis → acetophenone C₆H₅COCH₃, a methyl ketone, which gives the iodoform test but not Schiff's/Tollens (not an aldehyde) nor ninhydrin (not an amino acid).\nKey idea: RCN + R'MgX → R–CO–R'; CH₃CO– group → iodoform.","traps":["Aldehyde vs ketone from nitrile: Grignard adds a carbon → ketone."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Haloform reaction"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Maleic anhydride can be prepared by:","options":["Heating trans-but-2-enedioic acid","Heating cis-but-2-enedioic acid","Treating cis-but-2-enedioic acid with alcohol and acid","Treating trans-but-2-enedioic acid with alcohol and acid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) heating the trans acid – no cyclisation; (C)/(D) Fischer esterification gives esters, not anhydrides.\nWorking: in cis-butenedioic (maleic) acid both COOH groups are on the same side, so heating removes water to give the cyclic anhydride; the trans (fumaric) acid cannot cyclise; alcohol + acid gives esters.\nKey idea: Only the cis diacid can close a five-membered anhydride ring on heating.","traps":["cis vs trans geometry: Cyclic anhydride needs both COOH on the same side.","Esterification vs dehydration: Alcohol + acid → ester."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Br–CH₂–CHO —(EtOH excess, dry HCl gas)→ \"A\" (major product) —(ᵗBuO⁻K⁺)→ \"B\" (major product) [where Et = –C₂H₅, ᵗBu = (CH₃)₃C–]. Consider the above reaction sequence, Product \"A\" and Product \"B\" formed respectively are:","options":["BrCH₂CH(OEt)₂ , H₂C=C(OEt)₂","EtOCH₂CHO , EtOCH₂CH(OH)(OᵗBu)","EtOCH₂CH(OEt)₂ , H₂C=C(OEt)₂","BrCH₂CH(OEt)₂ , ᵗBuOCH₂CH(OEt)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) SN2 by ᵗBuO⁻ (hindered nucleophile – minor); (B)/(C) replace Br by OEt in step 1 (EtOH is too weak a nucleophile); (B) also misses acetal formation.\nWorking: excess dry EtOH/HCl converts the aldehyde to the diethyl acetal BrCH₂CH(OEt)₂ (C–Br untouched); bulky ᵗBuO⁻ acts as a base (E2) and removes HBr, giving the ketene acetal CH₂=C(OEt)₂ rather than SN2 substitution.\nKey idea: Dry HCl/ROH protects CHO as acetal; ᵗBuOK is a bulky base → elimination.","traps":["Substitution vs elimination with ᵗBuO⁻: Bulky alkoxide → E2.","Br replaced in step 1: Dry HCl/EtOH acts only on the carbonyl."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ammonia-derivative reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which one of the following compounds will give orange precipitate when treated with 2,4-dinitrophenyl hydrazine?","options":["Ethyl salicylate (o-HOC₆H₄COOCH₂CH₃)","Ethyl benzoate (C₆H₅COOCH₂CH₃)","Salicylic acid (o-HOC₆H₄COOH)","Acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ester + phenol – the C=O is an ester carbonyl; (B) plain ester – its C=O can be mistaken for a ketone C=O; (C) carboxylic acid – C=O present but resonance-stabilised.\nWorking: 2,4-DNP forms coloured hydrazones only with aldehydes and ketones; esters and acids have a C=O made unreactive by resonance with –OR/–OH, so only acetophenone (D) gives the orange 2,4-DNP derivative.\nKey idea: Only aldehyde/ketone carbonyls undergo addition-elimination with 2,4-DNP; acid derivatives do not.","traps":["Any C=O does not mean 2,4-DNP positive: Check what is attached to the C=O: only H/C allowed.","Phenolic OH is a distractor: Ignore the OH; test is for aldehyde/ketone."],"topics":["Ammonia-derivative reactions","Tests for oxygen compounds"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Cyclohexylmethanol (C₆H₁₁–CH₂OH) —[H₃PO₄, 120 °C]→ A (major product) —[(BH₃)₂; H₂O₂/OH⁻, H₂O]→ P (major product). Consider the above reaction and identify the Product P: [Options: (1) cyclohexylmethanol (C₆H₁₁CH₂OH); (2) 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CH₃ and OH on the same ring carbon); (3) 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol (CH₂OH and OH on adjacent ring carbons); (4) 2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CH₃ and OH on adjacent ring carbons)]","options":["Cyclohexylmethanol","1-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol","2-(Hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol","2-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes no shift: methylenecyclohexane + hydroboration returns the start alcohol; (B) Markovnikov hydration of 1-methylcyclohexene (acid hydration, not hydroboration–oxidation, HBO); (C) treats HBO as dihydroxylation.\nWorking: acid dehydration gives a 1° carbocation that undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the 3° ring cation, then loses H⁺ to give the more substituted 1-methylcyclohexene (A). Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH anti-Markovnikov (syn) → trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol, option (D).\nKey idea: Primary cation next to a ring CH shifts H to become tertiary; HBO puts OH on the less substituted alkene carbon.","traps":["1,2-H shift to 3° cation: Always check if the cation can rearrange.","HBO is anti-Markovnikov: B goes to less hindered C; OH replaces B."],"topics":["Dehydration","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"R–CN —(i) DIBAL-H; (ii) H₂O→ R–Y. Consider the above reaction and identify \"Y\".","options":["–CH₂NH₂","–CONH₂","–CHO","–COOH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the LiAlH₄/Na-alcohol product; (B)/(D) are partial/complete hydrolysis products.\nWorking: DIBAL-H delivers one hydride to give an aluminium imine salt; hydrolysis gives the aldehyde.\nKey idea: DIBAL-H stops at the aldehyde oxidation level.","traps":["DIBAL-H vs LiAlH₄: DIBAL-H = one hydride, aldehyde after hydrolysis."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"C₆H₅CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ —[conc. H₂SO₄, Δ]→ A + B\n(The reactant, A and B are shown below.)\nConsider the above reaction, and choose the correct statement:","options":["The reaction is not possible in acidic medium","Both compounds A and B are formed equally","Compound A will be the major product","Compound B will be the major product"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) says acid can't dehydrate alcohols; (B) assumes E and Z form equally since they have the same constitution; (D) picks the cis isomer, ignoring Ph/CH₃ steric strain.\nWorking: E1 via the benzylic carbocation C₆H₅C⁺HCH₂CH₃; loss of H from CH₂ gives the conjugated 1-phenylprop-1-ene. A (trans/E) and B (cis/Z) are geometrical isomers of the same alkene; the trans isomer avoids Ph/CH₃ steric clash and keeps the ring coplanar for conjugation, so A is the major product.\nKey idea: E1 gives the thermodynamically more stable trans (E) alkene as the major product.","traps":["A and B are E/Z isomers, not regioisomers: Compare steric strain: trans keeps Ph and CH₃ apart.","Cis alkene less stable: Remember trans alkenes are more stable in E1 products."],"img":["img/jee/JM21Jul27S2C13-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A bicyclic lactone — a 1,2-dihydronaphthalene in which C1 (benzylic carbon) carries the ring oxygen and C2 carries a CH₂–C(=O) unit, closing a five-membered γ-lactone (O–C(=O)–CH₂ fused across C1–C2) — is treated with i) DIBAL-H, toluene, −78 °C; ii) H₃O⁺ to give \"P\" (major product). The product \"P\" in the above reaction is:","options":["2-(1,2-dihydronaphthalen-2-yl)acetic acid (ring CH₂ at C1, CH₂COOH at C2)","2-(1-hydroxy-1,2-dihydronaphthalen-2-yl)acetaldehyde (OH at C1, CH₂CHO at C2)","(2-methyl-1,2-dihydronaphthalen-1-yl) formate (O–CHO at C1, CH₃ at C2)","2-(1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalen-2-yl)acetaldehyde (CH₂CHO on saturated ring, no OH)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – no reduction (or C–O hydrogenolysis) and acid kept; (C) – wrong C–O bond cleaved, formate ester; (D) – loses the OH as if the alkoxy part were removed and also reduces the ring C=C.\nWorking: one equivalent of DIBAL-H at −78 °C stops at the aldehyde oxidation level; the lactone gives a lactol which opens to the hydroxy-aldehyde (OH at C1, CH₂CHO at C2).\nKey idea: Lactone + DIBAL-H (−78 °C) → lactol ⇌ hydroxy-aldehyde: the ring O ends as OH on the same carbon.","traps":["Lost the ring oxygen: In a lactone the 'alcohol' stays attached: expect OH + CHO.","DIBAL-H as full reducer: DIBAL-H at −78 °C stops at aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"In the reaction 3-methoxytoluene (benzene ring with CH₃ and OCH₃ meta to each other) –[\"A\"]→ 3-methoxybenzoic acid (COOH in place of CH₃), the reagent \"A\" is:","options":["NaBH₄, H₃O⁺","LiAlH₄","Alkaline KMnO₄, H⁺","HCl, Zn-Hg"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NaBH₄ and (B) LiAlH₄ are hydride reducing agents; (D) Zn-Hg/HCl is Clemmensen reduction – all go the wrong direction.\nWorking: side-chain oxidation of an alkylbenzene with alkaline KMnO₄ followed by acidification gives the benzoic acid; the other three are reducing agents.\nKey idea: CH₃ → COOH is an oxidation; only KMnO₄ in the list is an oxidant.","traps":["Reducing agents offered for an oxidation: Count C–O bonds at the reacting carbon: 0 → 3 means oxidation."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"2-(1-Methoxyethyl)benzonitrile (benzene ring with CN and, ortho to it, CH(CH₃)OCH₃) is treated with (i) C₆H₅MgBr (1.0 equivalent), dry ether, (ii) H₃O⁺ → X (major product). The structure of X is:","options":["ring bearing CH₂NH₂ and, ortho, CH(CH₃)C₆H₅","ring bearing CH₂NH₂ and, ortho, CH(CH₃)OCH₃","ring bearing C(=O)C₆H₅ and, ortho, CH(CH₃)C₆H₅","ring bearing C(=O)C₆H₅ and, ortho, CH(CH₃)OCH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) CH₂NH₂ is the LiAlH₄ reduction product of a nitrile, not a Grignard product; (C) assumes the Grignard also displaces OCH₃ (ethers do not react with RMgX).\nWorking: PhMgBr adds once to C≡N to give the imine salt, which is hydrolysed to the ketone, (2-(1-methoxyethyl)phenyl)(phenyl)methanone; the ether is untouched.\nKey idea: R–C≡N + R'MgX → R–C(=NMgX)R' → (H₃O⁺) R–CO–R'.","traps":["Nitrile reduction confused with Grignard addition: Grignard adds a carbon group; only hydrides give amines.","Ether cleaved by Grignard: Ethers are Grignard solvents – they do not react."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"CH₃COCH₂COOC₂H₅ (ethyl acetoacetate) + ethylene glycol, H⁺ → A (major product). The product \"A\" in the above reaction is:","options":["open hemiketal: CH₃C(OH)(OCH₂CH₂OH)CH₂COOC₂H₅","cyclic ketal (1,3-dioxolane) at the ketone, ester intact: 2-methyl-1,3-dioxolan-2-yl–CH₂COOC₂H₅","3-ethoxy-3-hydroxycyclobutanone","cyclic ketal at the ketone with the ester hydrolysed: 2-methyl-1,3-dioxolan-2-yl–CH₂COOH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) stops at the hemiketal stage (not the major isolated product); (C) an invented ring closure; (D) assumes the ester is hydrolysed too.\nWorking: the ketone carbonyl is far more electrophilic than the ester carbonyl, so ethylene glycol/H⁺ gives the cyclic ketal at the ketone; the ester survives under anhydrous acid.\nKey idea: Ketone carbonyl is protected as a 1,3-dioxolane; the ester carbonyl is less electrophilic (resonance from OR).","traps":["Hemiketal treated as final: With a diol and acid the ring closes to the ketal.","Ester also attacked: Ester C=O is resonance-stabilised and less reactive."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (Sucrose) + H₂O —Enzyme A→ C₆H₁₂O₆ (Glucose) + C₆H₁₂O₆ (Fructose)\nC₆H₁₂O₆ (Glucose) —Enzyme B→ 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂\nIn the above reactions, the enzyme A and enzyme B respectively are :-","options":["Amylase and Invertase","Invertase and Amylase","Invertase and Zymase","Zymase and Invertase"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) amylase acts on starch, not sucrose; (D) reverses the order.\nWorking: invertase hydrolyses sucrose to glucose + fructose; zymase (yeast) ferments the hexoses to ethanol and CO₂.\nKey idea: Invertase inverts (hydrolyses) cane sugar; zymase ferments.","traps":["Amylase vs invertase: Amylase – starch; invertase – sucrose.","Order reversed: Map A → first step (hydrolysis), B → fermentation."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Reaction of Grignard reagent, C₂H₅MgBr with C₈H₈O followed by hydrolysis gives compound \"A\" which reacts instantly with Lucas reagent to give compound B, C₁₀H₁₃Cl. The Compound B is:","options":["PhCH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂Cl (1-chloro-3-phenylbutane; primary chloride)","PhCH(CH₂Cl)CH₂CH₃ (1-chloro-2-phenylbutane; primary chloride)","PhC(Cl)(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (2-chloro-2-phenylbutane; tertiary benzylic chloride)","PhCH(CH₃)CH(Cl)CH₃ (2-chloro-3-phenylbutane; secondary chloride)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B) primary chlorides – would come from primary alcohols that do not react instantly; (D) secondary chloride – would form slowly and needs a different skeleton.\nWorking: C₈H₈O with a ketone/aldehyde able to react with RMgX = acetophenone (PhCOCH₃); EtMgBr then H₃O⁺ gives PhC(OH)(CH₃)C₂H₅ (C₁₀H₁₄O), a tertiary alcohol, which reacts instantly with Lucas reagent to give PhCCl(CH₃)C₂H₅ (C₁₀H₁₃Cl).\nKey idea: \"Instant\" Lucas reaction = tertiary (or benzylic tertiary) alcohol, so the Grignard must have added to a ketone.","traps":["Benzaldehyde-type isomer of C₈H₈O: Instant Lucas test needs a tertiary alcohol → ketone (acetophenone).","Carbon count of the chloride: Track which carbon bore the C=O; Cl sits there."],"topics":["Alcohol preparation","Alcohol reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Cyclohexanecarbonitrile (C₆H₁₁–C≡N) —(H₂O, H⁺)→ \"A\" (Major Product) —(H₂O, H⁺, Δ)→ cyclohexanecarboxylic acid (C₆H₁₁–COOH). Consider the above chemical reaction and identify product \"A\".","options":["C₆H₁₁–CH₂NH₂ (cyclohexylmethanamine)","C₆H₁₁–CH₂NO₂ (nitromethylcyclohexane)","C₆H₁₁–CONH₂ (cyclohexanecarboxamide)","C₆H₁₁–CH=N–OH (cyclohexanecarbaldehyde oxime)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) amine – product of nitrile reduction, not hydrolysis; (B) nitro compound – unrelated; (D) oxime – from aldehyde + NH₂OH.\nWorking: acid hydrolysis of a nitrile goes via the amide; mild hydrolysis stops at RCONH₂, which on further heating with aqueous acid gives RCOOH.\nKey idea: Hydrolysis adds water across C≡N: nitrile → amide → acid; no change in carbon count.","traps":["Hydrolysis vs reduction of nitrile: H₂O/H⁺ hydrolyses; LiAlH₄ or Na/ethanol reduces."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"4-Methoxytoluene (p-CH₃–C₆H₄–OCH₃) —(Alkaline KMnO₄, then H⁺)→ \"X\". Considering the above chemical reaction, identify the product \"X\":","options":["X = 4-methoxybenzaldehyde (p-OHC–C₆H₄–OCH₃)","X = 4-methoxybenzyl alcohol (p-HOCH₂–C₆H₄–OCH₃)","X = 4-methoxybenzoic acid (p-HOOC–C₆H₄–OCH₃)","X = 4-methylphenol (p-CH₃–C₆H₄–OH)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) aldehyde – needs controlled oxidation (Etard/CrO₂Cl₂); (B) alcohol – partial; (D) ether cleavage – needs HI, not KMnO₄.\nWorking: alkaline KMnO₄ oxidises a benzylic CH₃ completely to –COO⁻, acidification gives –COOH; the aryl methyl ether is untouched: 4-methoxybenzoic acid.\nKey idea: KMnO₄ takes any benzylic carbon with an H all the way to COOH.","traps":["Aldehyde vs acid stage: Strong KMnO₄ goes to acid; CrO₂Cl₂ or CrO₃/Ac₂O stop at aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Main Products formed during a reaction of 1-methoxy naphthalene with hydroiodic acid are:","options":["1-iodonaphthalene and CH₃OH","1-naphthol (OH at C-1) and CH₃I","1-naphthol (OH at C-1) and CH₃OH","1-iodonaphthalene and CH₃I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) break the aryl–O bond (iodo-naphthalene); (C) gives the right naphthol but stops at CH₃OH, ignoring that HI converts the methyl to CH₃I.\nWorking: alkyl aryl ethers are cleaved at the alkyl–O bond (aryl–O has partial double-bond character and SN on sp² carbon does not occur), giving the naphthol and methyl iodide.\nKey idea: Aryl–O stays; methyl leaves as CH₃I.","traps":["Aryl–O bond broken: SN does not occur at sp² aryl carbon.","Methanol vs methyl iodide: I⁻ attacks the methyl of the protonated ether."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"2 Cyclopentanone —(dil. NaOH)→ \"X\" —(H⁺, Heat)→ \"Y\"\nConsider the above reaction, the product 'X' and 'Y' respectively are :","options":["X = 2-(2-hydroxycyclopentyl)cyclopentan-1-one (OH on a ring CH adjacent to the ring link); Y = 2-(cyclopent-1-en-1-yl)cyclopentan-1-one","X = 2-(1-hydroxycyclopentyl)cyclopentan-1-one; Y = 2-(cyclopent-2-en-1-ylidene)cyclopentan-1-one (extra C=C in the second ring)","X = 2-(1-hydroxycyclopentyl)cyclopentan-1-one; Y = 2-cyclopentylidenecyclopentan-1-one","X = 2-(1-hydroxycyclopentyl)cyclopentan-1-one; Y = 2-(cyclopent-1-en-1-yl)cyclopentan-1-one"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts OH on a secondary carbon (wrong connectivity – reduction-type product); (D) gives the non-conjugated endocyclic alkene; (B) adds an extra C=C not possible from one dehydration.\nWorking: the enolate of one cyclopentanone adds to the C=O of the other giving the β-hydroxy ketone with a tertiary OH (X); acid/heat dehydrates it to the conjugated α,β-unsaturated ketone (Y).\nKey idea: Dehydration gives the α,β-unsaturated (conjugated) ketone: exocyclic C=C to the carbonyl α-carbon.","traps":["Endocyclic vs exocyclic double bond: Pick the C=C conjugated with C=O.","OH carbon in the aldol: Nucleophile = α-C; electrophile = C=O carbon gets OH."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"In Tollen's test for aldehyde, the overall number of electron(s) transferred to the Tollen's reagent formula [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ per aldehyde group to form silver mirror is_______ (Round off to the Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (per Ag⁺ ion), 4 (if HCHO assumed fully oxidised), 3 (counting OH⁻).\nWorking: RCHO + 2[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ + 3OH⁻ → RCOO⁻ + 2Ag + 2H₂O + 4NH₃; the aldehyde carbon goes from +1 to +3, releasing 2 electrons per CHO, taken up by two Ag⁺ ions.\nKey idea: –CHO → –COO⁻ is a 2-electron oxidation.","traps":["Per Ag⁺ instead of per CHO: Count electrons lost by one aldehyde group."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Oxidation of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions a compound A (molecular formula C₆H₁₂O₂) with a straight chain structure gives a C₄ carboxylic acid. A is: A —(i) LiAlH₄, (ii) H₃O⁺→ B —Oxidation→ C₄ carboxylic acid","options":["CH₃–CH₂–COO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (propyl propanoate)","CH₃–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₂–O–CH=CH₂","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–COO–CH₂–CH₃ (ethyl butanoate)","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–O–CH=CH–CH₂–OH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) propyl propanoate gives two propan-1-ol → C₃ acid only; (B) and (D) enol-ether/alcohol isomers – LiAlH₄ leaves the ether intact, so no C₄ acid arises cleanly.\nWorking: ethyl butanoate is reduced by LiAlH₄ to butan-1-ol + ethanol; oxidation of butan-1-ol gives butanoic acid (C₄). Propyl propanoate would give only C₃ acid.\nKey idea: Ethyl butanoate → butan-1-ol + ethanol → butanoic acid (C₄).","traps":["Wrong acyl/alkyl part counted: LiAlH₄ splits RCOOR′ into RCH₂OH + R′OH; the C₄ acid must come from a C₄ acyl (or C₄ alkoxy) part.","Ethers expected to be reduced: LiAlH₄ does not cleave C–O–C ethers/enol ethers; only the ester gives two alcohols."],"topics":["Carboxylic acid preparation","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions, C₃H₆ —H⁺/H₂O→ A —KIO, dil KOH→ B + C. The compounds B and C respectively are:","options":["CI₃COOK, HCOOH","CI₃COOK, CH₃I","CH₃I, HCOOK","CHI₃, CH₃COOK"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CI₃COOK/HCOOH and (B) CI₃COOK/CH₃I – stop at a triiodinated fragment and misplace the carbons; (C) CH₃I/HCOOK – wrong cleavage.\nWorking: propene + H⁺/H₂O → propan-2-ol (A); KIO/KOH oxidises it to acetone and then gives iodoform CHI₃ (B) and CH₃COOK (C).\nKey idea: CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ → CH₃COCH₃ → CHI₃ + CH₃COOK.","traps":["Markovnikov product missed: Acid hydration of propene gives propan-2-ol, which has the CH₃CH(OH)– unit needed for iodoform.","Triiodo intermediate taken as product: The triiodomethyl ketone is cleaved by OH⁻ to CHI₃ + carboxylate."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The nucleophilic addition of sodium hydrogen sulphite to an aldehyde or a ketone involves proton transfer to form a stable ion. Statement II: The nucleophilic addition of hydrogen cyanide to an aldehyde or a ketone yields amine as final product. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – accepts \"amine\"; (C) – rejects the correct bisulphite mechanism and accepts amine; (D) – doubts Statement I.\nWorking: Statement I is true (bisulphite addition proceeds via proton transfer to the stable sulphonate ion); Statement II is false (HCN gives a cyanohydrin, not an amine).\nKey idea: HCN + C=O → cyanohydrin; the CN is reduced to amine only in a separate step.","traps":["Cyanohydrin confused with amine: HCN addition gives a cyanohydrin (2-hydroxy nitrile), not an amine.","Proton transfer in bisulphite step doubted: NCERT shows the bisulphite adduct forming by proton transfer to give the stable sulphonate ion."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"1-methyl ethylene oxide when treated with an excess of HBr produces :","options":["CH₂=C(Br)CH₃ (2-bromopropene)","CH₃CHBr₂ (1,1-dibromoethane; a carbon bearing two Br and a CH₃ stub)","Br–CH=CH–CH₃ (1-bromoprop-1-ene, drawn trans)","BrCH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃ (1,2-dibromopropane)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C): assume elimination to a vinyl bromide. (B): wrongly puts both Br on one carbon (geminal) and loses a carbon.\nWorking: 1-methyl ethylene oxide is propylene oxide (methyloxirane). HBr opens the protonated epoxide to a bromohydrin, and excess HBr then converts the remaining OH to Br, giving 1,2-dibromopropane.\nKey idea: Both C–O bonds of the epoxide break; each carbon that held O gets a Br, so the dibromide is vicinal (1,2).","traps":["Common name of epoxide not decoded: Draw ethylene oxide (three-membered C–C–O ring) and put CH₃ on one carbon.","Geminal vs vicinal dibromide: Each Br goes where the oxygen was bonded – the two adjacent ring carbons."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"What is the product of following reaction ? Hex-3-ynal —(i) NaBH₄; (ii) PBr₃; (iii) Mg/ether; (iv) CO₂/H₃O⁺→ ?","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH (hexanoic acid; saturated zigzag, 5 chain C + COOH)","CH₃CH₂CH=CHCH₂COOH (hex-3-enoic acid; C=C at the third bond)","CH₃CH₂C≡CCH₂CH₂COOH (hept-4-ynoic acid)","CH₃CH₂CH=CHCH₂CH₂COOH (hept-4-enoic acid; COOH drawn hanging down at the chain end)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hexanoic acid: assumes NaBH₄ also fully reduces C≡C and forgets that CO₂ adds a carbon. (B) hex-3-enoic acid: partial reduction of C≡C to C=C plus no added carbon. (D) hept-4-enoic acid: correct C₇ count but triple bond wrongly reduced to C=C.\nWorking: CH₃CH₂C≡CCH₂CHO → (NaBH₄, C=O only) CH₃CH₂C≡CCH₂CH₂OH → (PBr₃) bromide → Grignard (internal alkyne has no acidic H) → +CO₂/H₃O⁺ adds one carbon: CH₃CH₂C≡CCH₂CH₂COOH.\nKey idea: NaBH₄ touches only C=O; CO₂ adds exactly one carbon at the carbon that bore Br.","traps":["NaBH₄ thought to reduce C≡C: NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor: it reduces C=O, not isolated C=C/C≡C.","Carbon count after carboxylation: Count: C₆ aldehyde → C₆ bromide → C₇ acid."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In the following reaction sequence, structures of A and B, respectively will be :\nChromane (3,4-dihydro-2H-1-benzopyran) bearing CH₂Br at C-5 (peri to C-4) —HBr, Δ→ A —Na, ether→ (intramolecular product) B","options":["A: 2-(3-bromopropyl)-3-(bromomethyl)phenol; B: 2-methyl-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-4-ol (indane with OH on the benzene ring next to the ring fusion and CH₃ at C-2 of the five-membered ring)","A: 3-[2-bromo-6-(bromomethyl)phenyl]propan-1-ol (Br on the ring, OH on the chain end); B: chromane with a CH₂Br group on the benzene ring","A: 3-[2-bromo-6-(bromomethyl)phenyl]propan-1-ol (Br on the ring, OH on the chain end); B: 5-bromoisochromane (3,4-dihydro-1H-2-benzopyran, Br on the benzene ring next to C-4a; ring O not attached to the benzene ring)","A: 2-(3-bromopropyl)-3-(bromomethyl)phenol; B: 5,6,7,8-tetrahydronaphthalen-1-ol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) break the aryl–O bond (putting Br on the ring), which does not happen; (A) closes a five-membered ring by miscounting carbons.\nWorking: HBr cleaves the alkyl aryl ether at the O–alkyl bond, giving a phenol and a primary alkyl bromide (–CH₂CH₂CH₂Br); intramolecular Wurtz between this and the neighbouring ArCH₂Br closes a six-membered carbocycle, giving tetrahydronaphthol.\nKey idea: Ar–O survives as phenol; ring size = 3 chain C + CH₂ + 2 ring C = 6.","traps":["Aryl–O bond cleaved: Aryl C–O has partial double-bond character; alkyl C–O breaks.","Ring size miscounted: Count atoms between the two reacting carbons."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The most suitable reagent for the given conversion is: a decalin (bicyclic) skeleton carrying –CONH₂, –COCH₃ (acetyl), –CO₂H and –CN groups is converted into the same skeleton in which only –CO₂H has become –CH₂OH (HOH₂C–); –CONH₂, –COCH₃ and –CN are unchanged.","options":["LiAlH₄","NaBH₄","H₂/Pd","B₂H₆"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) LiAlH₄ also reduces ketone, amide and nitrile; (B) NaBH₄ reduces the ketone but not the acid; (C) H₂/Pd may reduce nitrile/ketone and does not touch COOH.\nWorking: diborane is an electrophilic reducing agent that rapidly reduces –COOH to –CH₂OH and leaves amide, nitrile and (comparatively) the ketone intact; LiAlH₄ would reduce all, NaBH₄ would reduce the ketone but not the acid.\nKey idea: B₂H₆ is electrophilic, so it attacks the electron-rich carboxyl first.","traps":["Picking LiAlH₄ as the 'acid to alcohol' reagent: Check every other group in the molecule.","NaBH₄ assumed to reduce acids: NaBH₄ reduces aldehydes/ketones, not acids."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂CH₂–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₂OH (3,7-dimethylocta-2,6-dien-1-ol; as drawn the C2=C3 bond is Z, i.e. nerol – the CH₂CH₂ chain and CH₂OH are on the same side) —dil. H₂SO₄→","options":["Acyclic triol: (CH₃)₂C(OH)–CH₂CH₂CH₂–C(OH)(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂OH","α-Terpineol: 1-methylcyclohexene ring with a C(CH₃)₂OH group at C-4","Cyclopentane bearing CH₃, vinyl and C(CH₃)₂OH groups on three adjacent ring carbons","Acyclic triol: (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₂–C(OH)(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂OH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) simple hydration products; (C) five-membered ring from wrong attack.\nWorking: protonation/loss of water gives an allylic cation, which is attacked intramolecularly by the remote C=C forming a six-membered ring and a tertiary cation; water capture and proton loss give α-terpineol.\nKey idea: Allylic cation + internal C=C → six-membered ring → tertiary cation → water.","traps":["Stopping at hydration: Look for intramolecular nucleophilic C=C.","Wrong ring size: Count atoms between cation and attacking C=C."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol physical properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Among the compounds A and B with molecular formula C₉H₁₈O₃, A is having higher boiling point than B. The possible structures of A and B are :","options":["A = 1,3,5-tris(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexane; B = 1,3,5-trimethoxycyclohexane","A = 1,3,5-trimethoxycyclohexane; B = 5-(3-hydroxypropyl)cyclohexane-1,3-diol","A = 1,3,5-trimethoxycyclohexane; B = 1,3,5-tris(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexane","A = 1,3,5-tris(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexane; B = 5-(3-hydroxypropyl)cyclohexane-1,3-diol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C) reverse the order (ether higher); (D) pairs two triols so the H-bonding argument cannot decide, tempting those who only check that A is an alcohol.\nWorking: the triol (three O–H groups, intermolecular H-bonding) boils far higher than the isomeric triether, which has no O–H. (B) and (C) put the ether as the higher-boiling one; (D) compares two triols with no clear-cut difference.\nKey idea: Same formula: the isomer with O–H groups boils higher than the one with only C–O–C.","traps":["Only checking A is an alcohol: Check that B lacks O–H, so the comparison is decisive.","Methoxy groups thought to raise boiling point: Ethers cannot donate H-bonds."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product [B] in the following sequence of reactions is :- CH₃–C(CH(CH₃)₂)=CH–CH₂CH₃ —(i) B₂H₆, (ii) H₂O₂, OH⁻→ [A] —dil. H₂SO₄, Δ→ [B]","options":["CH₃–C(CH₂CH₂CH₃)=C(CH₃)₂","CH₂=C(CH(CH₃)₂)–CH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃–CH(CH(CH₃)₂)–CH=CH–CH₃","CH₃–C(CH(CH₃)₂)=CH–CH₂CH₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the starting alkene (assumes hydration then dehydration just reverses); (C) is the unrearranged Saytzeff/Hofmann alkene from the 2° cation; (B) a terminal (least substituted) alkene from the rearranged cation.\nWorking: hydroboration–oxidation puts OH on the less substituted alkene carbon: A = CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂ (4,5-dimethylhexan-3-ol). Acid dehydration gives a 2° cation at C-3; a 1,2-hydride shift gives the 3° cation CH₃CH₂CH₂–C⁺(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂, and loss of the isopropyl C–H (Saytzeff) gives the tetrasubstituted alkene (CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃ (2,3-dimethylhex-2-ene).\nKey idea: Dehydration goes through a cation that shifts to the most stable 3° site, so the final alkene is tetrasubstituted, not the one you started from.","traps":["Dehydration reverses hydroboration: Follow the cation: it rearranges to a 3° centre.","No hydride shift considered: Check neighbouring C–H for a shift to a more stable cation."],"topics":["Dehydration","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Identify (A) in the following reaction sequence: (A) [gives positive iodoform test] —(i) CH₃MgBr (ii) H⁺, H₂O (iii) conc. H₂SO₄/Δ→ (B) —O₃/Zn, H₂O→ product shown: benzene ring bearing –CHO and, on the adjacent (ortho) carbon, –C(CH₃)₂–C(=O)CH₃ (2-(2-methyl-3-oxobutan-2-yl)benzaldehyde).","options":["PhC(CH₃)=CH–COCH₃ (4-phenylpent-3-en-2-one)","PhCH=CH–COCH₃ (4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one, benzalacetone)","PhC(CH₃)=C(CH₃)–COCH₃ (3-methyl-4-phenylpent-3-en-2-one)","PhCH=C(CH₃)–COCH₃ (3-methyl-4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) put a CH₃ on the ring-attached alkene carbon, so ozonolysis would give an acetyl, not CHO; (B) lacks the α-methyl, so the product would lack the C(CH₃)–C=O unit next to C(CH₃)₂.\nWorking: the ozonolysis product carries both carbonyls in one molecule, so B is a ring alkene: Ar–CH=C(CH₃)–C(CH₃)₂–Ar(ortho), i.e. 1,1,2-trimethyl-1H-indene. That indene forms when MeMgBr turns the methyl ketone of PhCH=C(CH₃)COCH₃ into PhCH=C(CH₃)C(OH)(CH₃)₂ and acid gives the allylic tertiary cation that attacks the ortho ring carbon. The ring-attached CH (giving –CHO) rules out (A) and (C); the C(CH₃) next to C(CH₃)₂ rules out (B).\nKey idea: Read the ozonolysis product backwards: CHO-carbon was a ring-attached CH=, the C=O(CH₃) carbon was the other alkene carbon, and the gem-dimethyl carbon closed the ring.","traps":["Missing the ring closure step: Notice the ozonolysis product is one molecule, so B must be cyclic.","Which alkene carbon carried H: CHO comes only from a =CH– carbon."],"topics":["Nucleophilic addition","Haloform reaction"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In the following reaction A is: A —(i) Br₂, hν; (ii) KOH (alc.); (iii) O₃; (iv) (CH₃)₂S; (v) NaOH (aq) + Δ→ cyclopent-1-ene-1-carbaldehyde (five-membered ring with CHO on a C=C carbon).","options":["1,2-dimethylcyclobutane","ethylcyclopentane","cyclohexane","methylcyclopentane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) methylcyclopentane chosen by students who keep the ring size and see an extra carbon in CHO; (B) ethylcyclopentane similar ring-size error; (A) 1,2-dimethylcyclobutane would give hexane-2,5-dione and a cyclopentenone, not an aldehyde.\nWorking: cyclohexane → bromocyclohexane → cyclohexene → hexane-1,6-dial → intramolecular aldol condensation (5-membered ring) → cyclopent-1-ene-1-carbaldehyde.\nKey idea: Six ring carbons → C₆ dialdehyde → 5-ring + exocyclic CHO; the CHO carbon came from the ring.","traps":["Ring size assumed unchanged: Count all carbons: C₆H₈O product ⇒ six-carbon starting ring.","Intramolecular aldol not recognised: Dialdehydes with 1,6-relationship close to 5-membered rings."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Consider the following reactions: A —(i) CH₃MgBr; (ii) H₃O⁺→ B —Cu, 573 K→ 2-methyl-2-butene. The mass percentage of carbon in A is ____.","type":"num","answer":"66.65 to 66.7","range":[66.65,66.7],"explain":"Common slips: 62.07 (acetone, C₃H₆O, if the Grignard is wrongly taken as ethyl), 58.82 (ethyl propanoate route), 68.18 (%C of B, C₅H₁₂O), 85.71 (%C of the alkene C₅H₁₀).\nWorking: Cu at 573 K dehydrates a tertiary alcohol, so B = 2-methylbutan-2-ol, formed from butan-2-one (A, C₄H₈O) + CH₃MgBr; %C = 48/72 × 100 = 66.67. (Ethyl propanoate + 2 MeMgBr would also give B, but the single Grignard step points to the ketone.)\nKey idea: B = 2-methylbutan-2-ol ⇒ A = butanone; %C = 66.67.","traps":["Cu/573 K thought to dehydrogenate all alcohols: Tertiary alcohols are dehydrated by Cu at 573 K.","%C calculated for B instead of A: Question asks for A (C₄H₈O)."],"topics":["Alcohol preparation","Dehydration"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The major aromatic product C in the following reaction sequence will be: 2-methyl-2H-chromene (benzene fused to a pyran ring O–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–) —HBr (excess), Δ→ (A) —(i) KOH (alc.), (ii) H⁺→ B —O₃, Zn/H₃O⁺→ C","options":["2-hydroxyphenylacetic acid (o-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂CO₂H)","2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde, o-HO–C₆H₄–CHO)","2-bromobenzaldehyde (o-Br–C₆H₄–CHO)","2-bromophenylacetic acid (o-Br–C₆H₄–CH₂CO₂H)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) assume the aryl–O bond breaks and Br ends on the ring; (A)/(D) assume oxidative cleavage or keep an extra CH₂ next to the ring.\nWorking: excess HBr cleaves the alkyl aryl ether at the O–alkyl bond (phenol OH stays on the ring) and adds HBr to the benzylic C=C, giving o-HO–C₆H₄–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃; alcoholic KOH removes two HBr to give the conjugated diene o-HO–C₆H₄–CH=CH–CH=CH–CH₃ (H⁺ re-protonates phenoxide); reductive ozonolysis gives salicylaldehyde + glyoxal + ethanal.\nKey idea: In aryl alkyl ethers the phenolic O stays on the ring; ozonolysis cuts at the ring-adjacent C=C to give ArCHO.","traps":["Aryl–O bond assumed to break: Aryl–O bond has partial double-bond character; phenol is formed.","Zn/H₃O⁺ ozonolysis gives aldehyde, not acid: Reductive work-up stops at aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The increasing order of the following compounds towards HCN addition is: (i) 3-methoxybenzaldehyde; (ii) 2-nitrobenzaldehyde; (iii) 2-methoxybenzaldehyde; (iv) 3-nitrobenzaldehyde","options":["(iii) < (iv) < (ii) < (i)","(iii) < (iv) < (i) < (ii)","(iii) < (i) < (iv) < (ii)","(i) < (iii) < (iv) < (ii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts m-OCH₃ most reactive (treating –I of OMe as stronger than NO₂); (B) places m-OMe above m-NO₂; (D) ranks m-OMe below o-OMe, ignoring that +R works only at o/p.\nWorking: electron-withdrawal raises carbonyl electrophilicity. o-OCH₃ (+R) deactivates most; m-OCH₃ acts only by –I (slightly activating vs o); m-NO₂ –I; o-NO₂ –R and –I strongest. Order (iii) < (i) < (iv) < (ii).\nKey idea: Resonance effects act only from ortho/para; at meta OCH₃ is electron-withdrawing.","traps":["OCH₃ treated as donating at meta: At meta only –I operates.","Nitro position strength: Ortho NO₂ adds –R; it is strongest."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 2-methyl-5-nitrophenol [benzene ring with OH at C1, CH₃ at C2 (ortho to OH), NO₂ at C5 (meta to OH, para to CH₃)] treated with conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄ → ?","options":["2-methyl-3,5-dinitrophenol [new NO₂ ortho to CH₃, meta to OH]","2-methyl-5,6-dinitrophenol [new NO₂ ortho to OH, between OH and the old NO₂]","2-methyl-4,5-dinitrophenol [new NO₂ para to OH, next to the old NO₂]","2-methyl-4,5,6-trinitrophenol [two new NO₂ groups, ortho and para to OH]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) follows CH₃ (ortho to it) instead of the stronger OH director; (B) picks the ortho-to-OH site that is crowded between OH and NO₂; (D) assumes picric-acid-style exhaustive nitration because of 'conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄'.\nWorking: OH is the strongest activator and directs o/p. Of its free o/p positions, the para position (C4) is open, while the free ortho position (C6) is wedged between OH and the existing NO₂, so mononitration para to OH gives (C). The ring already carries a deactivating NO₂, so a single further nitration is intended rather than trinitration.\nKey idea: The strongest activator (OH) controls orientation; between its open o/p positions the less crowded para site wins.","traps":["Weaker director (CH₃) chosen over OH: Rank activators: –OH ≫ –CH₃.","Crowded ortho site taken as major: Check steric crowding between two substituents."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Two compounds A and B with same molecular formula (C₃H₆O) undergo Grignard's reaction with methylmagnesium bromide to give products C and D. Products C and D show following chemical tests.\nTest | C | D\nCeric ammonium nitrate test | Positive | Positive\nLucas test | Turbidity obtained after five minutes | Turbidity obtained immediately\nIodoform test | Positive | Negative\nC and D respectively are:","options":["C = (CH₃)₃C–OH ; D = CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃","C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH ; D = (CH₃)₃C–OH","C = CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃ ; D = (CH₃)₃C–OH","C = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH ; D = CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps C and D; (B) and (D) contain butan-1-ol, which cannot form from a C₃H₆O carbonyl + CH₃MgBr and gives no Lucas turbidity at room temperature.\nWorking: C₃H₆O carbonyls are propanal and propanone. Propanal + CH₃MgBr → butan-2-ol (2°: Lucas turbidity in ~5 min; CH₃CH(OH)– gives iodoform). Propanone + CH₃MgBr → 2-methylpropan-2-ol (3°: Lucas immediate; no α-H on carbinol C, iodoform negative).\nKey idea: Aldehyde + RMgX → 2°; ketone + RMgX → 3°; 3° alcohols give instant Lucas turbidity; only CH₃CH(OH)– alcohols give iodoform.","traps":["C and D swapped: Use Lucas timing: immediate = 3°.","Iodoform needs CH₃CH(OH)– group: Iodoform needs CH₃–CH(OH)– with an H."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"An organic compound 'A' (C₉H₁₀O) when treated with conc. HI undergoes cleavage to yield compounds 'B' and 'C'. 'B' gives yellow precipitate with AgNO₃ where as 'C' tautomerizes to 'D'. 'D' gives positive iodoform test. 'A' could be :","options":["C₆H₅–O–CH=CH–CH₃","C₆H₅–CH₂–O–CH=CH₂","C₆H₅–O–CH₂–CH=CH₂","p-CH₃–C₆H₄–O–CH=CH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives phenol (no AgNO₃ ppt) and propanal (iodoform negative); (C) gives phenol and allyl iodide/alcohol (no enol); (D) gives p-cresol (no AgNO₃ ppt).\nWorking: HI cleaves the benzyl–O bond (stable benzyl cation) giving C₆H₅CH₂I (B; AgI yellow ppt) and CH₂=CH–OH (C), which tautomerises to CH₃CHO (D; iodoform positive). Aryl ethers (A), (C), (D) give phenols, which do not precipitate with AgNO₃; (A) would also give propanal (iodoform negative).\nKey idea: B must be an alkyl/benzyl iodide, so the O must not be bonded to the ring; C must be vinyl alcohol → acetaldehyde.","traps":["Aryl–O bond assumed to cleave: Ar–O bond has partial double-bond character; phenol forms.","Enol of propanal taken as iodoform positive: Tautomerise first: propanal has no CH₃CO–."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The increasing order of the reactivity of the following compound in nucleophilic addition reaction is :\nPropanal, Benzaldehyde, Propanone, Butanone","options":["Butanone < Propanone < Benzaldehyde < Propanal","Benzaldehyde < Butanone < Propanone < Propanal","Propanal < Propanone < Butanone < Benzaldehyde","Benzaldehyde < Propanal < Propanone < Butanone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts benzaldehyde below ketones – over-weights resonance; (C) and (D) are reversed orders (decreasing written as increasing).\nWorking: ketones < aldehydes (steric and +I); butanone (ethyl) < propanone; benzaldehyde is less reactive than propanal (resonance) but more than ketones in the standard JEE ordering.\nKey idea: Aldehyde > ketone first; then resonance lowers ArCHO below RCHO; bigger alkyl lowers ketone reactivity.","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing reversed: Read 'increasing' and place least reactive first.","Benzaldehyde put below ketones: Standard order keeps all aldehydes above ketones."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The compound A in the following reaction is :\nA —(i) CH₃MgBr/H₂O; (ii) Conc. H₂SO₄/Δ→ B\nB —(i) O₃; (ii) Zn/H₂O→ C + D\nC —(i) Conc. KOH; (ii) Δ→ C₆H₅COO⁻K⁺ + C₆H₅CH₂OH\nD —(Ba(OH)₂, Δ)→ H₃C–C(CH₃)=CH–CO–CH₃","options":["C₆H₅–CO–CH(CH₃)₂","C₆H₅–CO–CH₂CH₃","C₆H₅–CH₂–CO–CH₃","C₆H₅–CO–CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives PhC(CH₃)=C(CH₃)₂ – ozonolysis would give acetophenone, not PhCHO; (B) gives PhC(CH₃)=CHCH₃; (D) gives PhC(CH₃)=CH₂ – yields HCHO and acetophenone.\nWorking: C gives Cannizzaro products → C = benzaldehyde; D gives mesityl oxide by aldol condensation → D = acetone. So B = C₆H₅CH=C(CH₃)₂. A = C₆H₅CH₂COCH₃: CH₃MgBr → C₆H₅CH₂C(OH)(CH₃)₂, dehydration → conjugated C₆H₅CH=C(CH₃)₂.\nKey idea: Ozonolysis fragments (PhCHO + acetone) fix B; the alkene carbon bearing H must come from the CH₂ next to Ph.","traps":["Wrong dehydration direction: Check which ketone makes PhCH= after dehydration (conjugated alkene).","Ba(OH)₂ product mis-identified: Mesityl oxide = self-aldol product of acetone."],"topics":["Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","Enolates & aldol reactions"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Oxidation of alcohols","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Consider the following reaction :\nA tricyclic (hexahydroanthracene-type) compound – aromatic ring fused to two saturated six-membered rings – with four –OH groups: d = phenolic –OH on the aromatic ring; c = secondary –OH on a ring CH; b = tertiary –OH on a ring carbon also bearing CH₃; a = primary –OH on CH₂ at the end of an allylic –CH=CH–CH₂OH side chain —(Chromic anhydride)→ 'P'\nThe product 'P' gives positive ceric ammonium nitrate test. This is because of the presence of which of these –OH group(s) ?","options":["(c) and (d)","(b) only","(d) only","(b) and (d)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) includes (c) – the 2° OH is oxidised to a ketone; (C) phenol only – phenols do not give the red alcohol colour and ignore the surviving 3° OH; (D) b and d – counts the phenol's brown/green colour change as a positive CAN test.\nWorking: CrO₃ oxidises the 1° allylic –OH (a) to –CHO and the 2° ring –OH (c) to C=O; the 3° –OH (b) is not oxidised and the phenolic –OH (d) is not an alcohol. The 'positive CAN test' is the alcohol test (yellow → red colour), which only (b) can give; phenols give a different brown/green-black response, not the red alcohol result. So (B).\nKey idea: Only the tertiary –OH survives CrO₃ as an alcohol; the CAN red colour is an alcohol test, so the phenol does not count.","traps":["Secondary alcohol thought unaffected: CrO₃ converts 2° alcohols to ketones.","Phenol response to CAN: CAN red colour is the alcohol test; phenols give a different colour."],"topics":["Oxidation of alcohols","Tests for oxygen compounds"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"When neopentyl alcohol is heated with an acid, it slowly converted into an 85 : 15 mixture of alkenes A and B, respectively. What are these alkenes ?","options":["(CH₃)₂C=CHCH₃ (2-methylbut-2-ene) and (CH₃)₂CHCH=CH₂ (3-methylbut-1-ene)","CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (2-methylbut-1-ene) and (CH₃)₂CHCH=CH₂ (3-methylbut-1-ene)","(CH₃)₂CHCH=CH₂ (3-methylbut-1-ene) and CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (2-methylbut-1-ene)","(CH₃)₂C=CHCH₃ (2-methylbut-2-ene) and CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (2-methylbut-1-ene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) include 3-methylbut-1-ene, which needs the unrearranged 1° cation; (C) also reverses major/minor.\nWorking: (CH₃)₃CCH₂OH → protonation/loss of water with 1,2-methyl shift gives the 3° cation (CH₃)₂C⁺CH₂CH₃; loss of H⁺ gives more substituted 2-methylbut-2-ene (major, 85) and 2-methylbut-1-ene (minor, 15). 3-Methylbut-1-ene cannot form from the 3° cation.\nKey idea: Neopentyl gives 3° tert-amyl cation by methyl shift; Saytzeff alkene is major.","traps":["Unrearranged alkene expected: Neopentyl C has no β-H; a methyl shift must occur first.","Major/minor reversed: Major = more substituted (trisubstituted) alkene."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"An organic compound (A) (molecular formula C₆H₁₂O₂) was hydrolysed with dil. H₂SO₄ to give a carboxylic acid (B) and an alcohol (C). 'C' give white turbidity immediately when treated with anhydrous ZnCl₂ and conc. HCl. The organic compound (A) is :","options":["CH₃COOC(CH₃)₃ (tert-butyl acetate)","CH₃COOCH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ (sec-butyl acetate)","CH₃CH₂COOCH₂CH₂CH₃ (propyl propanoate)","CH₃COOCH₂CH(CH₃)₂ (isobutyl acetate)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) gives butan-2-ol – turbidity in about 5 min; (C) and (D) give 1° alcohols – no turbidity at room temperature.\nWorking: immediate Lucas turbidity means a 3° alcohol; only tert-butyl acetate gives tert-butanol on hydrolysis. Others give 2° (slow) or 1° alcohols.\nKey idea: Immediate turbidity = 3° alcohol.","traps":["2° alcohol mistaken for immediate: 3° immediate, 2° in ~5 min, 1° none at RT."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The number of chiral centres present in [B] is ________. 2-(3-Methylcyclohexyl)propanenitrile [cyclohexane ring with CH(CH₃)–C≡N on C-1 and CH₃ on C-3] –(i) C₂H₅MgBr, (ii) H₃O⁺→ [A] –(i) CH₃MgBr, (ii) H₂O→ [B]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 – missing the new carbinol centre (CH₃ vs C₂H₅ different); 2 – ignoring ring carbons; 5 – counting ring CH₂ or CH₃-bearing carbinol wrongly.\nWorking: A = 2-(3-methylcyclohexyl)pentan-3-one (Grignard on nitrile → ketone); B = 3-methyl-2-(3-methylcyclohexyl)pentan-3-ol. Chiral centres: ring C-1, ring C-3 (1,3-disubstituted ring, both are stereocentres), side-chain CH(CH₃), and C(OH)(CH₃)(C₂H₅) – total 4.\nKey idea: Ring C-1 and C-3 are both stereocentres in a 1,3-disubstituted cyclohexane (the two ring paths between them differ).","traps":["Ring stereocentres missed: In a 1,3-disubstituted cyclohexane both substituted carbons are chiral (the two ring paths between them differ).","Carbinol carbon missed: OH, CH₃, C₂H₅ and the CH(CH₃)R group are all different."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Nucleophilic addition"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The major product [B] in the following reactions is : CH₃–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–O–CH₂–CH₃ —HI, heat→ [A] alcohol —H₂SO₄, Δ→ [B]","options":["CH₃–CH₂–C(CH₃)=CH₂","CH₃–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₃","CH₂=CH₂","CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) ethene – from wrongly taking ethanol as [A]; (A) unrearranged/less substituted alkene; (B) wrong skeleton.\nWorking: HI cleaves the ether by SN2 at the less hindered ethyl carbon → EtI + 2-methylbutan-1-ol [A]; acid dehydration via 1° → 3° cation (hydride shift) and Saytzeff elimination → 2-methylbut-2-ene.\nKey idea: I⁻ attacks the less hindered group (ethyl), leaving the larger alcohol, which dehydrates to the most substituted alkene.","traps":["Wrong alcohol from cleavage: SN2 at less hindered carbon: ethyl → EtI.","No rearrangement in 1° alcohol dehydration: Hydride shift to the tertiary carbon, then Saytzeff."],"topics":["Ether reactions","Dehydration"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"In the following reaction sequence the major products A and B are :\no-xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene) + succinic anhydride –(anhydrous AlCl₃)→ A –(1. Zn–Hg/HCl; 2. H₃PO₄)→ B","options":["A = 4-(3,4-dimethylphenyl)-4-oxobutanoic acid; B = 6,7-dimethyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one (6,7-dimethyl-1-tetralone)","A = 4-(3,4-dimethylphenyl)-4-oxobutanoic acid; B = 6,7-dimethyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (no C=O)","A = 4-(2,3-dimethylphenyl)-4-oxobutanoic acid; B = 5,6-dimethyl-1-tetralone","A = 4-(2,3-dimethylphenyl)-4-oxobutanoic acid; B = 5,6-dimethyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1-one with an extra ring C=C (5,6-dimethylnaphthalen-1(2H)-one)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) forgets that H₃PO₄ cyclisation forms a new ketone (treats B as fully reduced); (C) acylation at the hindered position ortho to a methyl; (D) same wrong regiochemistry plus an unwarranted ring C=C.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation of o-xylene goes para to one methyl (position 4, less hindered) giving the keto acid; Clemmensen reduces the aryl C=O to CH₂ (4-arylbutanoic acid); H₃PO₄ effects intramolecular Friedel–Crafts acylation to the tetralone, so B keeps a C=O.\nKey idea: Each acylation adds a C=O; only the first is reduced by Clemmensen, so the final product is a tetralone.","traps":["Final ketone omitted: Order matters: reduction comes before the ring-closing acylation.","Acylation ortho to methyl: Position 4 of o-xylene is para to one CH₃ and unhindered."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which of the following derivatives of alcohols is unstable in an aqueous base ?","options":["RO–CMe₃ (tert-butyl ether)","RO–C(=O)Me (acetate ester)","RO–CH₂Ph (benzyl ether)","RO–(tetrahydropyran-2-yl) (THP acetal)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tert-butyl ether – no electrophilic site for OH⁻; (C) benzyl ether – stable to base (cleaved by hydrogenolysis); (D) THP acetal – cleaved by aqueous acid, not base.\nWorking: esters undergo base hydrolysis (saponification) via OH⁻ attack at C=O; ethers (t-butyl, benzyl) and the THP acetal are stable to base (acetals hydrolyse only in acid).\nKey idea: Base attacks an electrophilic C=O; only the ester has one.","traps":["Acetal assumed base-labile: Acetals/ketals are stable in base, labile in acid."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dehydration","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n[Reactant: 3-ethyl-3-hydroxycyclohexan-1-one (cyclohexanone with OH and CH₂CH₃ on C-3)] —H₂SO₄→","options":["3-ethylidenecyclohexan-1-one (exocyclic C=CHCH₃ at C-3)","3-ethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (ring C2=C3 double bond, conjugated with C=O)","3-ethylcyclohex-3-en-1-one (ring C3=C4 double bond, not conjugated)","3-vinylcyclohexan-1-one (CH=CH₂ on C-3)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) exocyclic alkene – Saytzeff-type but not conjugated, less stable; (C) endocyclic but β,γ – trisubstituted yet not conjugated; (D) vinyl – needs loss of H from the CH₃ end, impossible from a C-3 cation without rearrangement.\nWorking: acid protonates OH, loss of water gives the tertiary carbocation at C-3; loss of H from C-2 (α to C=O, most acidic) gives the conjugated, more substituted enone 3-ethylcyclohex-2-enone.\nKey idea: When a carbonyl is next door, elimination goes toward the α-carbon to give the conjugated enone.","traps":["Conjugation decides the alkene: Prefer the alkene conjugated with C=O (α-H is also the most acidic).","Exocyclic alkene picked: Endocyclic conjugated alkene is more stable than exocyclic."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The correct match between Item-I (starting material) and Item-II (reagent) for the preparation of benzaldehyde is: Item-I: (I) Benzene, (II) Benzonitrile, (III) Benzoyl chloride. Item-II: (P) HCl and SnCl₂, H₃O⁺; (Q) H₂, Pd-BaSO₄, S and quinoline; (R) CO, HCl and AlCl₃.","options":["(I)-(Q), (II)-(R) and (III)-(P)","(I)-(R), (II)-(Q) and (III)-(P)","(I)-(R), (II)-(P) and (III)-(Q)","(I)-(P), (II)-(Q) and (III)-(R)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps Stephen and Rosenmund reagents; (A) and (D) assign H₂/Pd-BaSO₄ or SnCl₂ to benzene, which cannot react by reduction.\nWorking: benzene + CO/HCl/AlCl₃ – Gattermann–Koch; benzonitrile + SnCl₂/HCl then H₃O⁺ – Stephen; benzoyl chloride + H₂/Pd-BaSO₄ (poisoned) – Rosenmund.\nKey idea: Benzene needs formylation (CO/HCl); nitrile needs SnCl₂/HCl; acid chloride needs poisoned Pd.","traps":["Stephen and Rosenmund reagents swapped: Pd-BaSO₄ for acyl chloride; SnCl₂/HCl for nitrile."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Named reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A solution of phenol in chloroform when treated with aqueous NaOH gives compound P as a major product. The mass percentage of carbon in P is ______. (to the nearest integer) (Atomic mass: C = 12; H = 1; O = 16)","type":"num","answer":"69","range":[69.0,69.0],"explain":"Common slips: 58 (taking the sodium phenoxide salt C₇H₅O₂Na, 84/144 = 58.3) or 77 (phenol C₆H₆O, 72/94 = 76.6), 61 (salicylic acid C₇H₆O₃, 84/138 = 60.9 – Kolbe product confused).\nWorking: Reimer–Tiemann gives o-hydroxybenzaldehyde (after acidification) C₇H₆O₂, M = 122; %C = 68.85 → 69.\nKey idea: Reimer–Tiemann (CHCl₃/NaOH) gives aldehyde; Kolbe (CO₂/NaOH) gives acid.","traps":["Kolbe product taken: CHCl₃ → –CHO; CO₂ → –COOH.","Salt form used for P: P is taken as neutral salicylaldehyde (after acidification)."],"topics":["Named reactions","Phenol substitution"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"An organic compound neither reacts with neutral ferric chloride solution nor with Fehling solution. It however, reacts with Grignard reagent and gives positive iodoform test. The compound is –","options":["1-[2-(1-hydroxyethyl)phenyl]propan-1-one [ortho: –CH(OH)CH₃ and –COC₂H₅]","2-methoxybenzaldehyde [ortho: –OCH₃ and –CHO]","1-(2-hydroxyphenyl)ethan-1-one [ortho: –COCH₃ and phenolic –OH]","1-(2-methoxyphenyl)propan-1-one [ortho: –COC₂H₅ and –OCH₃]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aromatic aldehyde – no CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)– group, so no iodoform; (C) phenol gives FeCl₃ colour; (D) ethyl ketone and ether give no iodoform.\nWorking: (A) has no phenol (FeCl₃ –ve), no CHO (Fehling –ve), an acidic OH and C=O (reacts with RMgX) and a CH(OH)CH₃ group (iodoform +ve). (B) aldehyde; (C) phenol; (D) ethyl ketone/no CH₃CO or CH₃CHOH – iodoform –ve.\nKey idea: Iodoform also works for CH₃CH(OH)– (oxidised in situ).","traps":["Overlooking CH₃CH(OH)– as iodoform-positive: Secondary alcohol CH₃CHOH– oxidises to methyl ketone.","Missing the phenol in (C): Phenolic OH gives violet colour with neutral FeCl₃."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Haloform reaction"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The major product of the following reactions: [3-methoxystyrene (m-CH₂=CH–C₆H₄–OCH₃), conc. HBr (excess), heat]","options":["3-(2-bromoethyl)phenol","1-bromo-3-(2-bromoethyl)benzene","1-bromo-3-(1-bromoethyl)benzene","3-(1-bromoethyl)phenol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) anti-Markovnikov (peroxide-type) addition; (B)/(C) aryl–O bond wrongly cleaved to aryl bromide.\nWorking: HBr cleaves the alkyl–O bond of the aryl methyl ether → ArOH + CH₃Br; HBr adds Markovnikov to the vinyl group via benzylic cation → –CH(Br)CH₃.\nKey idea: Aryl–O bond survives; benzylic cation directs Br.","traps":["Aryl bromide formation: sp² C–O has partial double-bond character; never cleaved.","Anti-Markovnikov product: No peroxide – Markovnikov, benzylic cation."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: [PhCOCH₂Br, NaBH₄, MeOH, 25 °C]","options":["2-methoxy-1-phenylethan-1-ol","2-bromo-1-phenylethan-1-ol","(2-methoxyvinyl)benzene [PhCH=CH–OMe]","2-phenyloxirane (styrene oxide)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) stops at the bromohydrin (the obvious NCERT product); (A) MeO⁻ substitution; (C) elimination product.\nWorking: NaBH₄ reduces C=O to the alkoxide, which displaces the adjacent Br intramolecularly (SN2) giving styrene oxide (D). (In practice the bromohydrin (B) is often isolated after a neutral work-up.)\nKey idea: The alkoxide formed is a nucleophile next to C–Br.","traps":["Stopping at the bromohydrin: Look for an internal nucleophile next to a leaving group."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 4-chlorotoluene —(1) Cl₂/hν; (2) H₂O, Δ→ ?","options":["4-ClC₆H₄CH₂OH","4-ClC₆H₄CHCl₂","4-ClC₆H₄CO₂H","4-ClC₆H₄CHO"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes monochlorination; (B) stops before hydrolysis; (C) assumes trichlorination to ArCCl₃.\nWorking: side-chain chlorination to the benzal chloride ArCHCl₂, then hydrolysis gives the aldehyde 4-ClC₆H₄CHO – the NCERT route. The amount of Cl₂ is not stated, so (A) or (C) could arise with 1 or 3 equivalents.\nKey idea: ArCHCl₂ + H₂O → ArCHO (gem-diol loses water).","traps":["Hydrolysis step ignored: Apply step (2): gem-dihalide hydrolyses to C=O.","Extent of chlorination guessed: Follow the NCERT benzal chloride route."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The major product obtained in the following reaction is: 2-methyl-6-oxoheptanal [OHC–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–C(=O)–CH₃] —NaOH, Δ→ ?","options":["1-(3-Methylcyclopent-2-en-1-yl)ethan-1-one: acetyl on an sp³ ring carbon, CH₃ on a ring alkene carbon","1,2-Dimethylcyclopent-2-ene-1-carbaldehyde: CHO and CH₃ on a quaternary ring carbon, CH₃ on the adjacent alkene carbon","1-Methyl-2-methylidenecyclopentane-1-carbaldehyde: CHO and CH₃ on a quaternary carbon, exocyclic =CH₂ on the next carbon","1-(3-Methylcyclopent-1-en-1-yl)ethan-1-one: acetyl on the ring alkene carbon, CH₃ on the allylic carbon next to the other alkene carbon"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) come from the aldehyde enolate (C2) attacking the ketone – less electrophilic acceptor; (A) has the C=C not conjugated with C=O.\nWorking: the ketone α-CH₂ (C5) enolate attacks the more electrophilic aldehyde C=O, closing a five-membered ring; dehydration gives the conjugated enone with C=C between the former aldehyde carbon and the acetyl-bearing carbon, CH₃ on the neighbouring sp³ carbon.\nKey idea: Aldehyde is the acceptor; five-membered ring; dehydration toward conjugation.","traps":["Ketone chosen as electrophile: Aldehydes are better electrophiles.","Non-conjugated alkene product: Dehydration gives α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : ethylbenzene (C₆H₅CH₂CH₃) → (i) alkaline KMnO₄ ; (ii) H₃O⁺","options":["acetophenone (C₆H₅COCH₃)","benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH)","phenylacetic acid (C₆H₅CH₂COOH)","phenylacetaldehyde (C₆H₅CH₂CHO)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) partial oxidation at benzylic carbon; (C) keeps the CH₂ (oxidising only the terminal CH₃); (D) aldehyde stage.\nWorking: vigorous oxidation of any alkyl side chain with a benzylic H gives benzoate (all extra carbons lost); acidification gives benzoic acid.\nKey idea: Any length side chain with benzylic H → C₆H₅COOH.","traps":["Only terminal carbon oxidised: Oxidation starts at benzylic carbon and cleaves the chain.","Stopping at ketone: Hot alkaline KMnO₄ is vigorous; goes to acid."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : CH₃CH=CHCO₂CH₃ —LiAlH₄→","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO","CH₃CH=CHCH₂OH","CH₃CH₂CH₂CO₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) stops at aldehyde; (C) reduces only C=C (catalytic-hydrogenation type); (D) reduces both (as with H₂/Ni excess).\nWorking: LiAlH₄ reduces the ester to the primary alcohol (with CH₃OH released) and, under normal conditions, leaves the isolated/crotyl C=C intact: but-2-en-1-ol.\nKey idea: Hydride donors attack polar C=O; non-polar C=C is normally untouched.","traps":["LiAlH₄ assumed to reduce C=C too: Hydride reagents attack polar multiple bonds.","Aldehyde taken as product: LiAlH₄ goes all the way to alcohol."],"topics":["Reduction of carbonyl compounds","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In the following reaction: carbonyl compound + MeOH ⇌(HCl) acetal. Rate of the reaction is the highest for :","options":["Acetone as substrate and methanol in stoichiometric amount","Propanal as substrate and methanol in stoichiometric amount.","Acetone as substrate and methanol in excess","Propanal as substrate and methanol in excess"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) choose acetone; (B) right substrate but ignores concentration effect.\nWorking: aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards nucleophilic addition (less steric hindrance, less +I), and excess methanol raises the forward rate (and shifts the equilibrium).\nKey idea: Aldehyde + excess alcohol = fastest acetalisation.","traps":["Excess reagent irrelevant to rate: Higher [MeOH] increases rate."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: ethyl 3-hydroxy-2-(2-hydroxyethyl)benzoate [benzene ring with OH at C-3, CH₂CH₂OH at C-2 and CO₂Et at C-1] —H₂SO₄ (cat.), CHCl₃→ ?","options":["dehydration product with vinyl group and ethyl ester (ring drawn non-aromatic)","2,3-dihydrobenzofuran-4-carboxylic acid","5-hydroxy-3,4-dihydroisocoumarin (5-hydroxyisochroman-1-one) (six-membered lactone, OH retained)","ethyl 2,3-dihydrobenzofuran-4-carboxylate"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) assumes phenol–alcohol ether formation (cyclic ether, 5-ring); (B) also hydrolyses the ester; (A) assumes alcohol dehydration and breaks aromaticity.\nWorking: acid-catalysed intramolecular transesterification — the primary CH₂CH₂OH attacks the adjacent CO₂Et, expelling EtOH and closing a stable six-membered lactone; the phenolic OH stays. Ether formation between phenol and a primary alcohol under catalytic acid is not favoured, and dehydration of a primary alcohol to a vinyl group needs harsh conditions.\nKey idea: The ester and the CH₂CH₂OH are ortho, so a 6-membered lactone closes easily with loss of EtOH.","traps":["Cyclic ether instead of lactone: Phenols do not form ethers by acid dehydration; ester exchange is easier.","H₂SO₄ means dehydration: Only catalytic acid in CHCl₃; primary alcohol not dehydrated."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"p-Hydroxybenzophenone upon reaction with bromine in carbon tetrachloride gives:","options":["(3-bromophenyl)(4-hydroxyphenyl)methanone – Br at meta position of the unsubstituted ring","(2-bromo-4-hydroxyphenyl)(phenyl)methanone – Br ortho to C=O on the phenol ring","(4-bromophenyl)(4-hydroxyphenyl)methanone – Br at para position of the unsubstituted ring","(3-bromo-4-hydroxyphenyl)(phenyl)methanone – Br ortho to OH on the phenol ring"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) meta on the other ring follows C=O as a meta director but that ring is deactivated; (B) ortho to C=O ignores OH directing; (C) para on the unactivated ring.\nWorking: OH strongly activates its ring (o/p-director, para blocked by C=O) while the benzoyl ring is deactivated; in non-polar CCl₄ mono-bromination occurs ortho to OH → (D).\nKey idea: The most activated ring wins: substitute ortho to OH.","traps":["Meta director of the ketone dominates: Ring with OH is far more activated.","Polysubstitution expected: Non-polar CCl₄ gives monobromination."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Major products of the following reaction are : C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde) + HCHO —[(i) 50% NaOH; (ii) H₃O⁺]→","options":["CH₃OH and HCO₂H","C₆H₅CH₂OH and C₆H₅COOH","CH₃OH and C₆H₅COOH","HCOOH and C₆H₅CH₂OH"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is self-Cannizzaro of HCHO; (B) is self-Cannizzaro of benzaldehyde; (C) reverses the roles.\nWorking: crossed Cannizzaro – HCHO, the more reactive aldehyde, is attacked by OH⁻ and transfers hydride, so it is oxidised to formate (HCOOH after acidification) and benzaldehyde is reduced to benzyl alcohol.\nKey idea: Formaldehyde always acts as the hydride donor (reductant) in crossed Cannizzaro.","traps":["Roles of the two aldehydes reversed: HCHO is more electrophilic, takes OH⁻ and donates hydride.","Self-Cannizzaro products chosen: In a crossed reaction each aldehyde gives one product."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : [Benzene ring bearing –OCH₃ and, meta to it, a –CH(CN)–O–CH(CH₃)₂ group (2-isopropoxy-2-(3-methoxyphenyl)acetonitrile)] —[HI (excess), Δ]→ ?","options":["3-HO–C₆H₄–CH(CN)I (phenolic OH; benzylic C bears CN and I)","3-I–C₆H₄–CH(CN)OH (aryl iodide; benzylic C bears CN and OH)","3-HO–C₆H₄–CH(CN)OH (phenol; benzylic C bears CN and OH)","3-I–C₆H₄–CH(CN)I (aryl iodide; benzylic C bears CN and I)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) put I on the ring (aryl–O cleavage impossible); (C) forgets that excess HI converts the benzylic OH to iodide.\nWorking: Ar–OCH₃ is cleaved at the methyl side (Ar–O bond is strong) → phenol + CH₃I; aryl–O never becomes aryl–I, ruling out (B), (D). The benzylic ether C–O is cleaved by excess HI at the benzylic carbon (and any benzylic OH formed is converted to iodide by excess HI), giving ArCH(CN)I + isopropyl iodide/alcohol.\nKey idea: Phenol C–O survives; alkyl/benzylic C–O becomes C–I with excess HI.","traps":["Aryl–O bond cleaved to aryl iodide: Ar–O has partial double-bond character; phenol is not converted to ArI.","Benzylic OH left unconverted: Excess HI and heat: alcohol → iodide."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Compound A (C₉H₁₀O) shows positive iodoform test. Oxidation of A with KMnO₄/KOH gives acid B (C₈H₆O₄). Anhydride of B is used for the preparation of phenolphthalein. Compound A is:","options":["2-methylacetophenone (1-(2-methylphenyl)ethan-1-one)","3-methylacetophenone","(2-methylphenyl)acetaldehyde","4-methylacetophenone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) are methyl ketones but give meta/para diacids; (C) gives phthalic acid but fails iodoform (no CH₃CO).\nWorking: iodoform positive → CH₃CO–; B = phthalic acid (anhydride used for phenolphthalein) → ortho-disubstituted; so A = o-methylacetophenone. (C) is ortho but not a methyl ketone; (B)/(D) give iso/terephthalic acid.\nKey idea: Phenolphthalein ⇒ phthalic anhydride ⇒ ortho.","traps":["Iodoform test ignored for ortho aldehyde: Iodoform needs CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)–.","Positional isomer of diacid: Phthalic acid is the 1,2-diacid."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Haloform reaction"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product 'Y' in the following reaction is: Ph–CO–CH₃ —(NaOCl)→ X —((i) SOCl₂, (ii) aniline)→ Y","options":["N-phenylbenzamide (benzanilide), PhCONHPh","PhN=CH–CO–Ph (imine of phenylglyoxal with aniline)","4-aminobenzophenone","2-aminobenzophenone"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) keeps the methyl ketone carbon and condenses aniline; (C)/(D) Friedel-Crafts-type aryl ketones from acylation on ring carbon instead of N.\nWorking: haloform reaction gives PhCOOH (X); SOCl₂ → PhCOCl; aniline → benzanilide.\nKey idea: NaOCl removes CH₃ as CHCl₃; acyl chloride acylates N of aniline.","traps":["C-acylation instead of N-acylation: Free NH₂ is acylated first (benzoylation)."],"topics":["Haloform reaction","Acid derivatives"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The major products of the following reaction are : 4-chlorophenol —(1) CHCl₃/aq. NaOH; (2) HCHO, NaOH (conc.); (3) H₃O⁺→","options":["2-(hydroxymethyl)benzene-1,4-diol and formic acid","5-chloro-2-hydroxybenzoic acid (5-chlorosalicylic acid) and methanol","4-chloro-2-(hydroxymethyl)phenol and formic acid","2,5-dihydroxybenzoic acid and methanol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) oxidise the aryl aldehyde and reduce HCHO (wrong crossed-Cannizzaro partner); (A)/(D) replace Cl by OH with conc. NaOH (aryl Cl does not undergo SNAr here).\nWorking: Reimer–Tiemann puts –CHO ortho to OH → 5-chloro-2-hydroxybenzaldehyde; crossed Cannizzaro with HCHO: HCHO is oxidised to formate (→ HCOOH on acidification) and the aryl aldehyde is reduced to CH₂OH; aryl Cl is untouched.\nKey idea: In crossed Cannizzaro HCHO is always the one oxidised (to formate).","traps":["Wrong partner oxidised in crossed Cannizzaro: HCHO → HCOO⁻; ArCHO → ArCH₂OH.","Aryl Cl replaced by OH: Haloarenes need harsh conditions or activation for SNAr."],"topics":["Cannizzaro & benzoin reactions","Named reactions"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product of the following reaction : 3-(3-hydroxypropyl)phenol —(1) CrO₃; (2) SOCl₂/Δ; (3) Δ→","options":["7-hydroxy-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-1-one (7-hydroxyindan-1-one)","7-chloroindan-1-one","5-chloroindan-1-one (Cl para to the C=O ring junction position)","5-hydroxyindan-1-one"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) assume SOCl₂ replaces phenolic OH by Cl; (A) closes at the hindered position between OH and chain (ortho to OH).\nWorking: CrO₃ (Jones) oxidises the primary CH₂OH to COOH; SOCl₂ converts the acid to the acyl chloride (phenolic OH is not replaced); heating gives intramolecular Friedel–Crafts acylation ortho to the chain. Of the two ortho positions, the one para to OH (less hindered, activated) closes, giving 5-hydroxyindan-1-one.\nKey idea: SOCl₂ does not convert phenols to aryl chlorides; ring closure goes para to the activating OH.","traps":["SOCl₂ on phenolic OH: Phenol C–O has partial double-bond character; not replaced by SOCl₂.","Which ortho position closes: Choose the position para to OH and away from crowding."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Carboxylic acid reactions"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Consider the following reactions: A + Ag₂O → ppt; A + Hg²⁺/H⁺ → B; B + NaBH₄ → C; C + ZnCl₂/conc. HCl → turbidity within 5 minutes. 'A' is :","options":["CH≡CH","CH₃–C≡CH","CH₂=CH₂","CH₃–C≡C–CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ethyne passes the Ag test but leads to a 1° alcohol; (D) 2-butyne gives a 2° alcohol but no Ag ppt (internal alkyne); (C) ethene gives neither.\nWorking: ppt with Ag₂O needs a terminal alkyne; Lucas turbidity in ~5 min means C is a 2° alcohol, so B is a ketone: propyne → acetone → propan-2-ol. Ethyne would give acetaldehyde → ethanol (1°, no turbidity).\nKey idea: Ag₂O ppt → terminal; 5-minute turbidity → 2° alcohol → methyl ketone → propyne.","traps":["Ethyne chosen because it is the classic acetylide: Ethyne → acetaldehyde → ethanol (1°), no quick turbidity.","Internal alkyne also forms ketone: Use the first clue: no acidic ≡C–H, no ppt."],"topics":["Tests for oxygen compounds","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"What will be the major product when m-cresol is reacted with propargyl bromide (HC≡C–CH₂Br) in presence of K₂CO₃ in acetone ?","options":["2-propargyl-5-methylphenol (C-alkylation ortho to OH, para to CH₃)","2-propargyl-3-methylphenol (C-alkylation between OH and CH₃)","4-propargyl-3-methylphenol (C-alkylation para to OH)","1-methyl-3-(prop-2-yn-1-yloxy)benzene, m-CH₃C₆H₄–O–CH₂C≡CH (O-alkylation)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)–(C) ring C-alkylation products that look like Friedel–Crafts/EAS products of an activated ring.\nWorking: K₂CO₃ deprotonates the phenol; the phenoxide O attacks the primary propargylic C–Br by SN2 (Williamson) to give the aryl propargyl ether.\nKey idea: Weak base + phenol + 1° halide in a polar aprotic solvent = aryl ether (O-alkylation).","traps":["Ring alkylation instead of ether formation: No Lewis acid present; phenoxide O is the nucleophile."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The major product of following reaction is :\nR–C≡N —(1) AlH(i-Bu)₂, (2) H₂O→ ?","options":["RCHO","RCOOH","RCH₂NH₂","RCONH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): full reduction as with LiAlH₄. (B)/(D): nitrile hydrolysis products.\nWorking: DIBAL-H reduces the nitrile to an imine (aluminium imide), which is hydrolysed to the aldehyde RCHO.\nKey idea: DIBAL-H = partial reduction to aldehyde.","traps":["DIBAL-H treated like LiAlH₄: DIBAL-H stops at the imine stage; hydrolysis gives aldehyde.","Hydrolysis of nitrile assumed: Water only hydrolyses the imine formed in step 1."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for oxygen compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The tests performed on compound X and their inferences are: (a) 2,4-DNP test – coloured precipitate; (b) Iodoform test – yellow precipitate; (c) Azo-dye test – no dye formation. Compound 'X' is:","options":["1-(2-aminophenyl)ethan-1-ol: o-H₂N–C₆H₄–CH(OH)CH₃","1-[2-(dimethylamino)phenyl]ethan-1-one: o-(CH₃)₂N–C₆H₄–COCH₃","2-(2-aminophenyl)ethanal: o-H₂N–C₆H₄–CH₂CHO","2-[2-(dimethylamino)phenyl]ethanal: o-(CH₃)₂N–C₆H₄–CH₂CHO"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) No C=O, so 2,4-DNP negative; primary ArNH₂ gives azo dye. (C) Aldehyde CH₂CHO gives no iodoform; ArNH₂ gives azo dye. (D) Iodoform negative (no CH₃CO).\nWorking: needs a C=O (2,4-DNP +), a CH₃CO– or CH₃CH(OH)– unit (iodoform +), and no primary aromatic NH₂ (no diazotisation/azo dye). Only the N,N-dimethylamino acetophenone fits.\nKey idea: Azo-dye negative rules out primary aromatic amine; iodoform needs CH₃CO–; 2,4-DNP needs C=O.","traps":["CH₃CH(OH)– gives iodoform but not 2,4-DNP: 2,4-DNP needs a free carbonyl.","Tertiary aromatic amine and azo test: Only primary aromatic amines diazotise; N,N-dimethyl cannot."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is: CH₃CHO + C₆H₅COCH₃ —dil. NaOH→ ?","options":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂COC₆H₅ (3-hydroxy-1-phenylbutan-1-one)","C₆H₅COCH₂C(OH)(CH₃)C₆H₅ (3-hydroxy-1,3-diphenylbutan-1-one)","CH₃CH(OH)CH₂COC₆H₅ (3-hydroxy-1-phenylbutan-1-one; same structure as option A)","OHC–CH₂–C(OH)(CH₃)C₆H₅ (3-hydroxy-3-phenylbutanal)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Self-aldol of acetophenone – ketone as electrophile is less reactive. (D) Acetaldehyde enolate adding to the ketone C=O – wrong electrophile.\nWorking: the acetophenone enolate attacks the more electrophilic, unhindered aldehyde C=O, giving CH₃CH(OH)CH₂COPh. Note: options (A) and (C) show the same structure.\nKey idea: Aldehyde C=O is the better electrophile; product is CH₃CH(OH)CH₂COPh.","traps":["Ketone taken as electrophile: Aldehydes are more electrophilic and less hindered than ketones.","Self-condensation chosen: Crossed product with aldehyde acceptor is intended major."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Phenol preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The product formed in the reaction of cumene with O₂ followed by treatment with dil. HCl are:","options":["Phenol and propan-2-ol ((CH₃)₂CHOH)","Acetophenone and methanol","Phenol and acetone","Benzene and acetone"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Propan-2-ol: reduced form of acetone. (B) Acetophenone + methanol: wrong migration. (D) Benzene: no C–O retained.\nWorking: cumene → cumene hydroperoxide (air oxidation) → acid-catalysed rearrangement to phenol + acetone.\nKey idea: Phenyl migrates to O in the hydroperoxide; products phenol + acetone.","traps":["Isopropanol vs acetone: The isopropyl carbon ends as a ketone after rearrangement."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which dicarboxylic acid in presence of a dehydrating agent is least reactive to give an anhydride :","options":["Benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid (phthalic acid)","HOOC–CH₂–CH₂–COOH (succinic acid)","cis-Cyclopentane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid (both COOH on hashed bonds, same face of the ring)","HOOC–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–COOH (adipic acid)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) phthalic and (B) succinic both close 5-membered rings easily. (C) cis ring diacid: the wedge/hash drawing looks like a hurdle, but both COOH are on the same face, so a cis-fused 5-membered anhydride forms easily (a student who misreads it as trans may pick it).\nWorking: adipic acid would need a 7-membered cyclic anhydride, which is unfavourable (on strong heating it tends to give cyclopentanone instead); phthalic and succinic acids give 5-membered anhydrides easily, and option (C) is drawn as the cis diacid (both COOH hashed), which closes a cis-fused 5-membered anhydride readily.\nKey idea: Five- and six-membered cyclic anhydrides form easily; the C₆ diacid would need a 7-membered ring.","traps":["Stereo drawing of ring diacid misread as obstacle: Both bonds hashed = cis (same face), which favours cyclisation; then compare ring sizes: adipic acid needs a 7-membered ring.","Chain length counting: Count carbons between the two COOH groups."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of phenols","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The increasing order of the pKa values of the following compounds is : A = phenol; B = 4-nitrophenol; C = 3-nitrophenol; D = 4-methoxyphenol","options":["D < A < C < B","B < C < D < A","C < B < A < D","B < C < A < D"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the order of decreasing pKa (reversed). (B) puts D before A (treats OMe as −I). (C) puts m-NO₂ ahead of p-NO₂.\nWorking: lower pKa = stronger acid. p-nitrophenol (−R, −I; ≈7.1) < m-nitrophenol (−I only; ≈8.3) < phenol (10.0) < p-methoxyphenol (+R; ≈10.2) → B < C < A < D.\nKey idea: Increasing pKa = decreasing acidity; −R at para beats −I at meta; +R of OMe at para makes it weaker than phenol.","traps":["pKa order reversed with acidity order: Strongest acid has the smallest pKa – put it first.","OMe treated as electron-withdrawing: At para, +R of OMe dominates → less acidic than phenol."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The decreasing order of ease of alkaline hydrolysis for the following esters is : I = C₆H₅COOC₂H₅; II = p-Cl–C₆H₄–COOC₂H₅; III = p-O₂N–C₆H₄–COOC₂H₅; IV = p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–COOC₂H₅","options":["IV > II > III > I","III > II > I > IV","III > II > IV > I","II > III > I > IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the order. (C) puts OMe above H. (D) puts Cl above NO₂.\nWorking: alkaline hydrolysis is speeded by electron-withdrawing para groups: NO₂ (III) > Cl (II) > H (I) > OCH₃ (IV).\nKey idea: Rate follows electron withdrawal at the carbonyl: NO₂ > Cl > H > OMe.","traps":["Cl as donor: For halogens −I outweighs +R."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product 'X' formed in the following reaction is : methyl (6-oxocyclohex-2-en-1-yl)acetate (cyclohexanone; the α-carbon carries –CH₂–CO–OCH₃ and is allylic to a ring C=C that is not conjugated with C=O) —[NaBH₄, MeOH]→ X","options":["2-(2-Hydroxyethyl)cyclohexan-1-ol (C=C and ester both reduced)","(6-Oxocyclohex-2-en-1-yl)acetaldehyde: ring ketone and C=C kept, CH₂–CHO side chain (ester reduced to aldehyde)","2-(2-Hydroxyethyl)cyclohex-3-en-1-ol (ketone and ester both reduced, C=C kept)","Methyl (6-hydroxycyclohex-2-en-1-yl)acetate (only the ketone reduced; ring C=C and CH₂CO₂CH₃ kept)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): treats NaBH₄ like H₂/catalyst + LiAlH₄. (B): reduces ester to aldehyde (DIBAL-H behaviour). (C): LiAlH₄-type result (ester also reduced).\nWorking: NaBH₄ in MeOH reduces the ketone to a secondary alcohol; it does not reduce the ester or the isolated C=C.\nKey idea: NaBH₄ is mild: C=O of aldehyde/ketone only.","traps":["NaBH₄ treated as LiAlH₄: Esters need LiAlH₄; NaBH₄ leaves them."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: CH₃N=CH–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–CO–CH₃ —NaBH₄→","options":["CH₃N=CH–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (only C=O reduced)","CH₃N=CH–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (C=O and C=C reduced, imine kept)","CH₃NH–CH₂–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (C=O and C=N reduced, C=C kept)","CH₃NH–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (everything reduced)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes NaBH₄ reduces only C=O (imine kept). (B) reduces C=C, which hydride reagents do not do for isolated alkenes. (D) over-reduction as if catalytic hydrogenation.\nWorking: NaBH₄ delivers hydride to polar C=O and C=N bonds (ketone → 2° alcohol, imine → 2° amine) but does not reduce an isolated, non-conjugated C=C.\nKey idea: Hydride reagents attack polar π bonds (C=O, C=N), not non-polar C=C.","traps":["NaBH₄ thought to reduce only carbonyls: Hydride adds to any polarised π bond: C=N is reduced too.","Hydride reagent vs catalytic hydrogenation: NaBH₄ leaves isolated C=C; H₂/Pd reduces it."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ether preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: o-cresol (2-methylphenol) —(i) aq. NaOH, (ii) CH₃I→","options":["2,6-dimethylphenol (CH₃ and OH adjacent, second CH₃ on the other ortho position)","2,4-dimethylphenol (OH, with CH₃ ortho and CH₃ para)","2,5-dimethylphenol (OH with ortho CH₃ and a CH₃ para to the first CH₃)","2-methylanisole (1-methoxy-2-methylbenzene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)–(C): ring C-methylation at ortho/para positions (as if Friedel–Crafts) — not what CH₃I does with phenoxide in water.\nWorking: NaOH converts the phenol to phenoxide; phenoxide O attacks CH₃I by SN2 (Williamson) giving the methyl aryl ether; ring C-methylation does not occur under these conditions.\nKey idea: Phenoxide is an O-nucleophile toward CH₃I.","traps":["Phenoxide ring activation: CH₃I is an SN2 substrate; O attacks, giving ether."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 2-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl acetate [benzene ring with –OCOCH₃, an adjacent (ortho) –OH, and –OCH₃ para to the acetate] —(i) dil. HCl/Δ; (ii) (COOH)₂ / Polymerisation→","options":["Polyester –[O–Ar–O–CO–CO]ₙ– with the ring still carrying –OCOCH₃ (one ring O from the methoxy position, acetate retained)","Polyester –[O–Ar–O–CO–CO]ₙ– of 4-methoxybenzene-1,2-diol (ring O atoms at the former OAc and OH positions, –OCH₃ retained)","Polyester through ring oxygens with a free –OH (methoxy cleaved to OH)","Polyester –[O–Ar–O–CO–CO]ₙ– through two ring O atoms meta to each other (former OH and former OCH₃ positions), with a free –OH at the former acetate position (ortho to one chain O, para to the other)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes the ester survives dil. HCl and the aryl methyl ether is cleaved instead, so the chain runs through the former OH and OCH₃ oxygens. (C) and (D) assume dil. HCl cleaves the aryl methyl ether (needs HI/HBr); (D) also puts the chain through the former OH/OCH₃ oxygens and leaves the hydrolysed acetate O as a free OH.\nWorking: dil. HCl/Δ hydrolyses only the ester (aryl acetate → phenol + CH₃COOH); the aryl methyl ether survives. The resulting diol (two ortho OH) condenses with oxalic acid to a polyester carrying –OCH₃.\nKey idea: Dilute acid cleaves esters, not aryl alkyl ethers; the two freed OH groups build the polyester.","traps":["Ether cleaved by dilute acid: Aryl methyl ethers need conc. HI/HBr; dil. HCl only hydrolyses esters.","Which OH groups enter the chain: Only free OH groups (from OH and hydrolysed OAc) esterify with oxalic acid."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The major product obtained in the following reaction is : 2-substituted cyclopentanone with side chain –CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO₂Et at C-2 [ethyl 4-oxo-5-(2-oxocyclopentyl)pentanoate] —NaOEt/Δ→","options":["Bicyclo[3.3.0] (5–5 fused) cyclopentenone with C=C between the two ring-fusion carbons; ketone in new ring and CH₂CO₂Et on the carbon next to it","5–6 fused (hexahydroindenone-type) with C=C between the fusion carbons, ketone in 6-ring and CO₂Et on the 6-ring","5–6 fused enone with C=C from a fusion carbon to the ring carbon bearing CO₂Et, ketone in the 6-ring","Bicyclo[3.3.0] (5–5 fused) enone with C=C from the fusion carbon to the carbon bearing CH₂CO₂Et, conjugated with the new-ring ketone"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) correct ring but unconjugated C=C at the fusion (not the dehydration product). (B) and (C) 6-ring from the ester α-carbanion (Claisen-like thinking) – less acidic site; (B) also has the double bond misplaced.\nWorking: the most acidic CH₂ is the ketone α-CH₂ next to CH₂CH₂CO₂Et; its enolate attacks the cyclopentanone C=O intramolecularly forming a 5-membered ring (aldol); dehydration gives the conjugated enone with C=C at the ring fusion carbon and the CH₂CO₂Et on the α,β-carbon. Ester α-carbanion route (6-ring, options B/C) uses a less acidic H.\nKey idea: Use the ketone α-CH₂ enolate and count ring atoms: attack on ring C=O closes a five-membered ring.","traps":["Ester α-H vs ketone α-H: Ketone α-H (pKa ≈ 19–20) is more acidic than ester α-H (≈ 25).","Position of C=C after dehydration: Dehydration gives the alkene conjugated with C=O."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloform reaction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Which is the most suitable reagent for the following transformation ? CH₃–CH=CH–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ → CH₃–CH=CH–CH₂–CO₂H","options":["alkaline KMnO₄","I₂/NaOH","Tollens' reagent","CrO₃/CS₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) alkaline KMnO₄ would also cleave/hydroxylate the C=C. (C) Tollens' oxidises only aldehydes. (D) CrO₃ would give the ketone, not the acid with one carbon fewer.\nWorking: CH₃CH(OH)– is oxidised by NaOI to CH₃CO–, then haloform cleavage gives CHI₃ and the carboxylate with one carbon fewer; C=C is untouched.\nKey idea: Count carbons: product has one fewer C → haloform.","traps":["Strong oxidant chosen for alcohol → acid: Check C count and the C=C: KMnO₄ attacks C=C; product lost one C."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nEthyl 2-(cyanomethyl)benzoate [benzene ring with –COOEt and ortho –CH₂CN] treated with (i) Ni/H₂, (ii) DIBAL-H.","options":["2-(2-oxoethyl)benzaldehyde [benzene with –CHO and ortho –CH₂CHO]","3,4-dihydroisoquinoline [benzene fused to six-membered ring with C=N]","1,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquinoline [fused ring with NH]","2-(2-aminoethyl)benzyl alcohol [benzene with –CH₂OH and ortho –CH₂CH₂NH₂]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats DIBAL-H as reducing both CN and ester to CHO (ignores step i). (C) over-reduced amine – would need hydride on the imine. (D) full reduction of ester to alcohol, missing that DIBAL-H stops at aldehyde and the amine then cyclises.\nWorking: Ni/H₂ reduces –C≡N to –CH₂NH₂ (ester untouched); DIBAL-H takes the ester (or the lactam formed by intramolecular aminolysis) to the aldehyde level; the primary amine condenses intramolecularly with the CHO to give the cyclic imine 3,4-dihydroisoquinoline.\nKey idea: NH₂ and CHO held ortho on one ring form a six-membered cyclic imine spontaneously.","traps":["Missing intramolecular cyclisation: Check whether new NH₂ and C=O are positioned to close a 5/6-ring.","DIBAL-H strength: DIBAL-H (1 equiv, low T) stops at aldehyde/imine level."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carboxylic acid preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n4-Methylacetophenone [p-CH₃C₆H₄COCH₃] treated with (i) KMnO₄/KOH, Δ; (ii) H₂SO₄ (dil).","options":["HOOC–C₆H₄–COCOOH (para)","HOOC–C₆H₄–COOH (terephthalic acid)","OHC–C₆H₄–COOH (para)","HOOC–C₆H₄–COCH₃ (para)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) only CH₃ oxidised – treats ketone as resistant. (A) acetyl stopped at aryl-glyoxylic acid. (C) aldehyde would be oxidised further.\nWorking: hot alkaline KMnO₄ oxidises every benzylic side chain (CH₃ and COCH₃) to –COO⁻; acidification gives terephthalic acid.\nKey idea: Any benzylic carbon bearing H or a carbonyl ends as Ar–COOH under hot KMnO₄.","traps":["Ketone assumed inert: Under hot KMnO₄ the whole side chain goes to COOH.","Partial oxidation of acetyl: Vigorous conditions cleave to ArCOOH."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Phenol substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n4-Hydroxybenzenesulfonic acid [p-HO–C₆H₄–SO₃H] + Br₂ (excess).","options":["2,4,6-Tribromophenol","3-Bromo-4-hydroxybenzenesulfonic acid (one Br ortho to OH)","3,4,5-Tribromophenol (Br meta/para to OH)","3,5-Dibromo-4-hydroxybenzenesulfonic acid"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) stops after ortho bromination, keeping SO₃H. (B) mono-bromination despite excess Br₂. (C) wrong positions (meta to OH).\nWorking: OH strongly activates ortho/para; excess Br₂ brominates both ortho positions and replaces the para –SO₃H by ipso substitution, giving 2,4,6-tribromophenol.\nKey idea: SO₃H is a good ipso leaving group (desulfonation); OH directs Br into the para site too.","traps":["Blocking group seen as permanent: With excess Br₂ and strongly activating OH, SO₃H is replaced.","Positions relative to OH: Br goes ortho/para to OH."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The major product obtained in the following reaction is :- Substrate: cyclohexane ring bearing –COOH; on the adjacent carbon a vinyl group (–CH=CH₂); on the next carbon a –C(=O)CH₃ group; and an –NO₂ group on the next carbon (C4, para to the COOH-bearing C1 and adjacent to the acetyl-bearing C3); ring numbering C1 COOH, C2 CH=CH₂, C3 COCH₃, C4 NO₂; reagent LiAlH₄ (excess).","options":["Ring with –COOH, vinyl, –CH(OH)CH₃ and –NO₂ (only the ketone reduced)","Ring with –CH₂OH, vinyl, –CH(OH)CH₃ and –NH₂","Ring with –CH₂OH, ethyl (C=C reduced), –CH(OH)CH₃ and –NH₂","Ring with –CH₂OH, vinyl, –CH(OH)CH₃ and –NO₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reduces only the ketone (NaBH₄-like). (C) also reduces the C=C – LiAlH₄ does not reduce isolated alkenes. (D) leaves NO₂ unchanged; excess LiAlH₄ reduces aliphatic nitro to amine.\nWorking: excess LiAlH₄ reduces –COOH → –CH₂OH, ketone → sec-alcohol and aliphatic –NO₂ → –NH₂; an isolated C=C is not reduced by LiAlH₄.\nKey idea: LiAlH₄: polar multiple bonds yes, isolated C=C no.","traps":["Alkene reduced by hydride: Hydride attacks polar π bonds only.","Nitro left untouched: Excess LiAlH₄ reduces aliphatic NO₂ to NH₂."],"topics":["Reduction of carbonyl compounds","Alcohol preparation"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aldehyde & ketone preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: [Substrate: benzofuran-2(3H)-one (a five-membered benzo-lactone, Ar–O–C(=O)–CH₂–Ar) carrying a CN group on the benzene carbon next to the ring O (i.e. 7-cyanobenzofuran-2(3H)-one)] treated with (i) DIBAL-H, (ii) H₃O⁺.","options":["2,3-dihydrobenzofuran with CHO at C-7 (lactone C=O removed, cyclic ether retained)","Benzofuran-2(3H)-one with CHO at C-7 (only CN reduced, lactone intact)","2-hydroxy-3-(2-oxoethyl)benzaldehyde: benzene bearing CHO, OH (ortho to CHO) and CH₂CHO (ortho to OH)","Benzene bearing CH=NH, OH and CH₂CH₂OH (imine + phenol + primary alcohol)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) over-reduction of lactone to cyclic ether – DIBAL-H does not remove the C=O oxygen. (B) assumes the lactone is untouched. (D) keeps the imine (forgets H₃O⁺ hydrolysis) and over-reduces to alcohol.\nWorking: DIBAL-H reduces –CN to the imine-aluminium complex (→ CHO on hydrolysis) and the lactone (ester) to the aldehyde level; acid work-up frees phenol OH and CH₂CHO.\nKey idea: DIBAL-H stops at the aldehyde stage for both nitriles and esters; a lactone opens to give OH + CHO.","traps":["Lactone thought inert to DIBAL-H: Treat a lactone as an ester: DIBAL-H opens it to aldehyde + alcohol/phenol.","Imine left un-hydrolysed: After DIBAL-H on nitrile, aqueous acid converts imine to CHO."],"topics":["Aldehyde & ketone preparation","Reduction of carbonyl compounds"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alcohol preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"CH₃CH₂–C(OH)(Ph)–CH₃ (2-phenylbutan-2-ol) cannot be prepared by :","options":["HCHO + PhCH(CH₃)CH₂MgX","PhCOCH₂CH₃ + CH₃MgX","PhCOCH₃ + CH₃CH₂MgX","CH₃CH₂COCH₃ + PhMgX"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B),(C),(D) are all valid ketone + Grignard routes, so they are traps only if a student miscounts carbons.\nWorking: HCHO + RMgX gives a primary alcohol PhCH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂OH; (B)–(D) each combine two of the three groups on a ketone with the third as Grignard to give the tertiary alcohol.\nKey idea: Formaldehyde always gives a 1° alcohol – cannot give a 3° alcohol.","traps":["Choosing a valid route: Underline \"cannot\"; find the route giving wrong alcohol class."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Enolates & aldol reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In the following reactions, products A and B are : CH₃–CO–C(CH₃)₂–CH₂–CH₂–CHO (4,4-dimethyl-5-oxohexanal) —dil NaOH→ [A]; [A] —H₃O⁺, Δ→ [B]","options":["A = 2-hydroxy-2,3,3-trimethylcyclobutane-1-carbaldehyde (4-ring aldol: OH and CH₃ on one ring carbon, gem-dimethyl on the next, CHO on the other neighbour); B = 3,3-dimethyl-2-methylidenecyclobutane-1-carbaldehyde (exocyclic C=CH₂)","A = same 4-membered β-hydroxy aldehyde as in (A); B = 2,3,3-trimethylcyclobut-1-ene-1-carbaldehyde (endocyclic C=C conjugated with CHO)","A = 5-hydroxy-2,2-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one; B = 6,6-dimethylcyclohex-3-en-1-one (non-conjugated C=C)","A = 5-hydroxy-2,2-dimethylcyclohexan-1-one; B = 6,6-dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (C=C conjugated with C=O)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) four-membered ring from aldehyde enolate attacking ketone (strained). (C) correct A but dehydration to a non-conjugated alkene.\nWorking: the methyl ketone enolate (CH₃ side; the other α-carbon is quaternary) attacks the CHO, closing a six-membered ring (β-hydroxy ketone A); acid/heat dehydrates to the conjugated enone B.\nKey idea: Six-membered ring wins; dehydration gives α,β-unsaturated ketone.","traps":["Wrong enolate / ring size: Count ring atoms; prefer 5/6-membered rings.","Non-conjugated alkene after dehydration: E1cb dehydration places C=C between α and β carbons."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"In the following reaction, Aldehyde + Alcohol —HCl→ Acetal. Aldehyde: HCHO, CH₃CHO; Alcohol: ᵗBuOH, MeOH. The best combination is :","options":["HCHO and MeOH","HCHO and ᵗBuOH","CH₃CHO and MeOH","CH₃CHO and ᵗBuOH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) tert-butanol is a bulky, poor nucleophile. (C) CH₃CHO less electrophilic than HCHO.\nWorking: least hindered, most electrophilic aldehyde (HCHO) and least hindered alcohol (MeOH) give acetal most readily.\nKey idea: Smallest carbonyl + smallest alcohol.","traps":["Bulky alcohol chosen: Nucleophilic attack needs small alcohol."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid derivatives","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The increasing order of the reactivity of the following with LiAlH₄ is :\n(A) C₂H₅CONH₂ (B) C₂H₅COOCH₃ (C) C₂H₅COCl (D) C₂H₅CO–O–COC₂H₅","options":["(A) < (B) < (D) < (C)","(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) swap anhydride and acid chloride. (C) and (D) place ester below amide, ignoring strong N lone-pair resonance in amides.\nWorking: reactivity of acyl derivatives toward nucleophilic (hydride) attack follows leaving-group ability: amide < ester < anhydride < acid chloride.\nKey idea: Better leaving group and weaker resonance donation to C=O → faster: Cl⁻ > RCOO⁻ > RO⁻ > NH₂⁻.","traps":["Anhydride ranked above acid chloride: Rank by leaving group: Cl⁻ is best.","Ester vs amide order reversed: N donates more strongly by resonance, so amide is least reactive."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: cyclopent-3-en-1-one [five-membered ring, C=O at C1, C=C between C3 and C4, i.e. not conjugated with C=O] —NaBH₄/EtOH→ ?","options":["3-ethoxycyclopentan-1-ol","cyclopentanol","cyclopentanone","cyclopent-3-en-1-ol [OH at C1, C=C between C3 and C4]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) cyclopentanol: both C=O and C=C reduced (H₂/Ni-type thinking). (C) cyclopentanone: only C=C reduced – reverses NaBH₄ chemoselectivity. (A) 3-ethoxy alcohol: ethanol solvent wrongly treated as a nucleophile adding across the C=C.\nWorking: the drawn ketone is cyclopent-3-en-1-one (C=C isolated, not conjugated with C=O). NaBH₄ in EtOH reduces only the C=O, leaving the isolated C=C, so the product is cyclopent-3-en-1-ol (D). No conjugate (1,4) reduction or ethoxide addition is possible because the C=C is not conjugated.\nKey idea: NaBH₄ is a hydride donor for C=O; it does not hydrogenate C=C.","traps":["NaBH₄ treated like catalytic hydrogenation: NaBH₄ leaves C=C untouched.","Solvent taken as reagent: EtOH is only the protic solvent."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ether reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The major product in the following conversion is :\nCH₃O–C₆H₄–CH=CH–CH₃ (para) —HBr (excess), Heat→ ?","options":["HO–C₆H₄–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃","HO–C₆H₄–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃","CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃","CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) phenol formed but Br at the non-benzylic carbon. (C) ether intact and wrong regiochemistry. (D) correct regiochemistry but ether not cleaved (ignores excess HBr/heat).\nWorking: excess HBr with heat cleaves the aryl methyl ether at the O–CH₃ bond to give the phenol; HBr adds to the alkene via the more stable benzylic (p-hydroxy/methoxy-stabilised) carbocation, so Br goes on the benzylic carbon.\nKey idea: Excess HBr/Δ cleaves Ar–O–CH₃ to ArOH; H⁺ adds to give the benzylic cation.","traps":["Ether cleavage missed: 'Excess HBr, heat' means the ether is also cleaved.","Wrong carbocation chosen: Benzylic cation is resonance-stabilised by the ring and para-OH/OMe."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The aldehydes which will not form Grignard product with one equivalent Grignard reagents are :\n(A) C₆H₅CHO (B) p-HO₂C–C₆H₄–CHO (C) p-H₃CO–C₆H₄–CHO (D) p-HOH₂C–C₆H₄–CHO","options":["(B), (C), (D)","(B), (D)","(B), (C)","(C), (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) include (C): –OCH₃ looks like an –OH but has no acidic H. (D) misses (B) and includes (C).\nWorking: (B) has acidic COOH and (D) has an alcoholic OH; one equivalent of RMgX is destroyed by these acidic protons, so no addition product forms. (A) and (C) have no acidic H.\nKey idea: Any O–H (acid, alcohol) quenches one equivalent of RMgX before carbonyl addition.","traps":["Methoxy confused with hydroxy: Ethers have no acidic H (ether is the Grignard solvent)."]}],"m02-atom":[{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":52,"q":"The visible spectrum is shown below. Which of the following statements are correct, if the threshold frequency of caesium is 5.16 × 10¹⁴ Hz?\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:\nA. When Cs is placed inside a vacuum chamber with an ammeter connected to it and yellow light is focused on Cs, the ammeter shows the presence of current.\nB. When the brightness of the yellow light is dimmed, the value of the current in the ammeter is reduced.\nC. When a red light is used instead of the yellow light, the current produced is higher with respect to the yellow light.\nD. When a blue light is used, the ammeter shows the formation of current.\nE. When a white light is used, the ammeter shows formation of current.","options":["B, C and D Only","A, C, D and E Only","A, D and E Only","A, B, D and E Only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A true (yellow just above threshold); B true (dimmer light, fewer photons, lower current); C false (red below threshold, no current); D true (blue above threshold); E true (white has blue). (A) includes C and drops A; (B) includes C; (C) misses B (intensity effect); (D) A, B, D, E correct.\nWorking: λ₀ = c/ν₀ = 3 × 10⁸ / 5.16 × 10¹⁴ ≈ 581 nm; yellow (≈ 570 nm), blue and white light (which contains blue) have λ < 581 nm and eject electrons; red (> 600 nm) does not.\nKey idea: Emission needs ν > ν₀; once emitted, the current scales with brightness.","traps":["Threshold frequency converted to wavelength cutoff: λ₀ = c/ν₀ = 581 nm; yellow at about 570 nm has ν above 5.16 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","Photoelectric current set by intensity, emission set by frequency: Red (>600 nm) is below the threshold, so no current; current rises with intensity only above threshold."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C02-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The de-Broglie's wavelength of an electron in the 4ᵗʰ orbit is ______ πa₀. (a₀ = Bohr's radius)","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 32 (forgets to divide by n), 2 (uses r = na₀ = 4a₀).\nWorking: 2πrₙ = nλ; r₄ = 16a₀ → λ = 2π(16a₀)/4 = 8πa₀.\nKey idea: λₙ = 2πrₙ/n = 2πn a₀ → 8πa₀ for n = 4.","traps":["Radius scaling: rₙ = 0.529 n² Å.","Division by n forgotten: 2πr = nλ."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The maximum number of orbitals which can be identified with n = 4 and mₗ = 0 is ________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 16 (all orbitals of n = 4, n²); 8 (electrons in those 4 orbitals); 3 (forgetting l = 0 or l = 3).\nWorking: for n = 4, l = 0, 1, 2, 3; each subshell has exactly one orbital with mₗ = 0 → 4 orbitals.\nKey idea: Each l from 0 to n − 1 contributes one orbital with mₗ = 0.","traps":["Orbitals vs electrons: Question asks orbitals, not electrons.","Total orbitals counted: Fix mₗ = 0: one per subshell."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The value of Rydberg constant (R_H) is 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. The velocity of electron having mass 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg in Bohr's first orbit of hydrogen atom = ____ ×10⁵ m s⁻¹ (nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 15 if KE taken as R_H/2 (v = √(R_H/m)); 31 if KE taken as 2R_H; arithmetic slips with √.\nWorking: KE in first orbit = −E₁ = R_H = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J; v = √(2KE/m) = √(2 × 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ / 9.1 × 10⁻³¹) = √(4.79 × 10¹²) = 2.19 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ = 21.9 ≈ 22 ×10⁵.\nKey idea: In Bohr orbit KE = |Eₙ|; for n = 1, KE = R_H.","traps":["KE taken as half of |E|: Total E = −KE; PE = 2E.","Square-root arithmetic: Write 4.79×10¹² → 2.19×10⁶."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In an atom, total number of electrons having quantum numbers n = 4, |mₗ| = 1 and mₛ = −½ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (only mₗ = +1), 12 (ignoring mₛ), 8 (including l = 0 wrongly).\nWorking: for n = 4, l = 1, 2, 3 each have mₗ = +1 and −1 → 6 orbitals; one electron with mₛ = −½ in each → 6.\nKey idea: |mₗ| = 1 → two orbitals per l ≥ 1; three such l values for n = 4.","traps":["Absolute value ignored: Count both +1 and −1.","Spin restriction dropped: Only one electron per orbital has mₛ = −½."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Frequency of the de-Broglie wave of electron in Bohr's first orbit of hydrogen atom is ______ × 10¹³ Hz (nearest integer).\n[Given : R_H (Rydberg constant) = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, h (Planck's constant) = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J.s.]","type":"num","answer":"658 or 661","ranges":[[658.0,658.0],[661.0,661.0]],"range":[658.0,658.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 330 (using ν = E/h with 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J), 105 (using ν = v/2πr wrongly as orbital frequency with mismatched units), 1320 (4KE/h).\nWorking: ν = v/λ = mv²/h = 2KE/h; KE in n = 1 equals 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J → ν = 2 × 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ / 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ = 6.61 × 10¹⁵ Hz = 661 × 10¹³ Hz (658 if v and r are taken from rounded Bohr values).\nKey idea: ν = v/λ = mv²/h = 2KE/h, and KE₁ = R_H.","traps":["ν = E/h used: For matter waves use ν = v/λ.","KE vs total energy sign/size: KE = −E in Bohr atom."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"For hydrogen atom, energy of an electron in first excited state is −3.4 eV. K.E. of the same electron of hydrogen atom is x eV. Value of x is ______ × 10⁻¹ eV. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"34","range":[34.0,34.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 68 (taking PE magnitude), 136 (ground state), −34 (sign kept).\nWorking: in the Bohr model KE = −E (total) = +3.4 eV = 34 × 10⁻¹ eV.\nKey idea: KE is positive and equal to |E|.","traps":["PE magnitude used: KE = −E; PE = 2E.","Ground-state energy used: First excited = n = 2 (given −3.4 eV)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electromagnetic radiation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A hypothetical electromagnetic wave is shown below.\nThe frequency of the wave is x × 10¹⁹ Hz.\nx = ________ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 20 (taking 1.5 pm as λ), 10 (taking it as λ/2), 50 (pm→m slip).\nWorking: crest-to-node = λ/4 = 1.5 pm → λ = 6 pm = 6 × 10⁻¹² m; ν = c/λ = 3 × 10⁸ / 6 × 10⁻¹² = 5 × 10¹⁹ Hz.\nKey idea: Crest to adjacent node is a quarter wavelength.","traps":["Marked distance taken as λ: Identify what fraction of a cycle the arrow spans.","pm conversion: 1 pm = 10⁻¹² m."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr08S1C24-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electromagnetic radiation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Wavenumber for a radiation having 5800 Å wavelength is x × 10 cm⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"1724","range":[1724.0,1724.0],"explain":"Common slips: 17241 (forgetting ×10 scaling), 172 (wrong power of ten).\nWorking: 1/(5800 × 10⁻⁸ cm) = 17241 cm⁻¹ = 1724 × 10 cm⁻¹.\nKey idea: 1 Å = 10⁻⁸ cm.","traps":["Å to cm power: 1 Å = 10⁻⁸ cm."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Compare the energies of following sets of quantum numbers for multielectron system.\n(A) n = 4, l = 1\n(B) n = 4, l = 2\n(C) n = 3, l = 1\n(D) n = 3, l = 2\n(E) n = 4, l = 0\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(E) > (C) > (A) > (D) > (B)","(B) > (A) > (C) > (E) > (D)","(C) < (E) < (D) < (A) < (B)","(E) < (C) < (D) < (A) < (B)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts 4s below 3p (ignores n+l). (A)/(B) are decreasing orders with misplaced entries.\nWorking: 3p (n+l=4) < 4s (4, higher n) < 3d (5) < 4p (5, higher n) < 4d (6).\nKey idea: Convert to 3p, 3d, 4s, 4p, 4d, then apply n + l with lower-n tie-break.","traps":["Tie-break for equal n + l: Lower n first: 3p < 4s, 3d < 4p."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Element): A. N B. S C. Br D. Kr\nLIST II (Electronic Configuration): I. [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁵ II. [Ne]3s²3p⁴ III. [He]2s²2p³ IV. [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Wrong options pair Br with the 4p⁶ (Kr) configuration or N/S with the other period.\nWorking: N 1s²2s²2p³, S [Ne]3s²3p⁴, Br [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁵, Kr [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶.\nKey idea: Count valence electrons from group number: 15 → p³, 16 → p⁴, 17 → p⁵, 18 → p⁶.","traps":["Br vs Kr 4p count: Group 17 → p⁵, group 18 → p⁶."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Heisenberg uncertainty","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Based on Heisenberg's uncertainty principle, the uncertainty in the velocity of the electron to be found within an atomic nucleus of diameter 10⁻¹⁵ m is ______ × 10⁹ m s⁻¹ (nearest integer)\n[Given : mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, Planck's constant (h) = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s] (Value of π = 3.14)","type":"num","answer":"58","range":[58.0,58.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using h/2π, or the radius (0.5 × 10⁻¹⁵ m) as Δx, gives 116; using h/π gives 232; using h/(4πm) without π = 3.14 changes nothing to the nearest integer.\nWorking: Δv = h/(4π m Δx) = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ / (4 × 3.14 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 10⁻¹⁵) = 5.80 × 10¹⁰ m s⁻¹ = 58 × 10⁹.\nKey idea: Δv = h/(4πmΔx); take Δx as the full diameter given.","traps":["Wrong form of the relation: Use Δx·Δp ≥ h/4π.","Radius vs diameter: Use Δx = 10⁻¹⁵ m as given."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"According to the wave-particle duality of matter by de-Broglie, which of the following graph plot presents most appropriate relationship between wavelength of electron (λ) and momentum of electron (p)?\n(A) λ (y-axis) vs 1/p (x-axis): a falling hyperbola-like curve\n(B) p (y-axis) vs λ (x-axis): straight line through origin\n(C) λ (y-axis) vs p (x-axis): straight line through origin\n(D) λ (y-axis) vs p (x-axis): falling rectangular-hyperbola curve","options":["λ vs 1/p: decreasing curve","p vs λ: straight line through origin","λ vs p: straight line through origin","λ vs p: decreasing hyperbolic curve"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): correct shape but wrong axis (λ vs 1/p should be linear). (B),(C): treat λ as directly proportional to p.\nWorking: λ = h/p, so λ vs p is a rectangular hyperbola; λ vs 1/p would be a straight line (so (A) is wrongly drawn), and p ∝ 1/λ, not ∝ λ.\nKey idea: Inverse proportion → hyperbola; with 1/p on x-axis → straight line.","traps":["Hyperbola drawn against 1/p: Read the x-axis: against 1/p the plot must be a straight line."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of radial node/s for 3p orbital is :","options":["3","2","1","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2 = total nodes (n – 1). (A) 3 = n. (D) no basis.\nWorking: radial nodes = n – l – 1 = 3 – 1 – 1 = 1.\nKey idea: Radial = n – l – 1; total = n – 1.","traps":["Total vs radial nodes: Subtract angular nodes (l)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of electrons present in all the completely filled subshells having n = 4 and s = +½ is _______.\n(Where n = principal quantum number and s = spin quantum number)","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 32 (ignoring the spin condition), 9 (stopping at 4s, 4p, 4d as in real atoms up to Xe), 4 (one per subshell).\nWorking: n = 4 has 16 orbitals (4s, 4p, 4d, 4f = 1+3+5+7), each holding one electron with s = +½, giving 16.\nKey idea: Electrons with one spin value in shell n = n² (one per orbital).","traps":["Spin condition ignored: One spin value = one electron per orbital = n².","4f excluded as \"not filled\" in real atoms: The question intends all subshells of n = 4 filled."],"topics":["Quantum numbers","Pauli principle"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Total number of ions from the following with noble gas configuration is ________. Sr²⁺ (z = 38), Cs⁺ (z = 55), La²⁺ (z = 57), Pb²⁺ (z = 82), Yb²⁺ (z = 70) and Fe²⁺ (z = 26)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (Yb²⁺ 4f¹⁴ treated as noble-gas like), 4 (also La²⁺ or Pb²⁺), 1 (missing Cs⁺).\nWorking: Sr²⁺ = [Kr] and Cs⁺ = [Xe]; La²⁺ = [Xe]5d¹, Pb²⁺ = [Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰6s², Yb²⁺ = [Xe]4f¹⁴, Fe²⁺ = [Ar]3d⁶ are not noble-gas configurations.\nKey idea: Noble gas configuration means no electrons beyond the noble-gas core; filled 4f¹⁴ or 6s² do not count.","traps":["Filled 4f¹⁴ counted as noble gas: Filled f or d subshells beyond the core are not noble-gas configurations.","Pb²⁺ taken as stable noble-gas type: 6s² remains outside [Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The correct set of four quantum numbers for the valence electron of rubidium atom (Z = 37) is :","options":["5, 1, 1, +½","5, 0, 0, +½","5, 0, 1, +½","5, 1, 0, +½"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) take valence electron as 5p; (C) mₗ = 1 is not allowed for l = 0.\nWorking: Rb = [Kr]5s¹ → n = 5, l = 0, mₗ = 0, s = +½.\nKey idea: Alkali metal valence electron is ns¹: l = 0, mₗ = 0.","traps":["mₗ ≠ 0 for an s electron: mₗ ranges −l…+l; for l = 0 it must be 0.","Valence electron placed in p: Group 1 → ns¹."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList-I (Spectral Series for Hydrogen): (A) Lyman (B) Balmer (C) Paschen (D) Pfund\nList-II (Spectral Region/Higher Energy State): (I) Infrared region (II) UV region (III) Infrared region (IV) Visible region\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) put Lyman in IR. (C) puts Balmer in IR and Pfund in visible.\nWorking: Lyman UV, Balmer visible, Paschen and Pfund infrared; only option (A) has Lyman–UV and Balmer–visible (I and III are both \"Infrared region\", so C-III, D-I is acceptable).\nKey idea: Fix Lyman = UV and Balmer = visible; the rest are IR.","traps":["Duplicate List-II label: Decide from the unique entries (UV, visible) first."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : The orbitals having same energy are called as degenerate orbitals.\nStatement (II) : In hydrogen atom, 3p and 3d orbitals are not degenerate orbitals.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) chosen by applying the multi-electron (n+l) order to hydrogen. (B)/(C) wrongly reject the definition.\nWorking: I is the NCERT definition; in the one-electron H atom energy depends only on n, so 3s, 3p, 3d are degenerate and II is false.\nKey idea: Hydrogen (one electron): E depends on n alone.","traps":["n+l rule applied to H atom: For one-electron species all orbitals of a shell are degenerate."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Number of spectral lines obtained in He⁺ spectra, when an electron makes transition from fifth excited state to first excited state will be","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 15 (n = 6 → 1), 6 (n = 5 → 2 or 5 → 1 counting), 4 (only direct one-step jumps), 21 (n = 7 → 1).\nWorking: fifth excited state n = 6, first excited state n = 2; lines = Δn(Δn+1)/2 = 4 × 5/2 = 10 (independent of Z).\nKey idea: n-th excited state = level n + 1; count lines with Δn(Δn+1)/2; Z is irrelevant.","traps":["Excited state number taken as n: Ground state is n = 1, so fifth excited = n = 6.","He⁺ thought to change the count: Number of lines depends only on levels involved."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The ionization energy of sodium in kJ mol⁻¹, if electromagnetic radiation of wavelength 242 nm is just sufficient to ionize sodium atom is _______.","type":"num","answer":"494","range":[494.0,494.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8.2×10⁻¹⁹ (per atom), 494000 (J not kJ).\nWorking: E = hcN_A/λ = (6.626×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸ × 6.022×10²³)/(242×10⁻⁹) = 4.946 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹ = 494.6 kJ mol⁻¹, taken as 494 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Multiply per-photon energy by N_A and convert to kJ.","traps":["Per atom vs per mole: Multiply by N_A."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The four quantum numbers for the electron in the outermost orbital of potassium (atomic no. 19) are","options":["n = 3, l = 0, m = 1, s = +1/2","n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = +1/2","n = 2, l = 0, m = 0, s = +1/2","n = 4, l = 2, m = −1, s = +1/2"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) impossible (m = 1 with l = 0). (C) wrong shell. (D) 3d-type numbers with n = 4 (4d).\nWorking: K = [Ar]4s¹; 4s → n = 4, l = 0, m = 0, s = +½.\nKey idea: K fills 4s before 3d.","traps":["4s vs 3d filling: Aufbau: 4s before 3d."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The wavelength of an electron of kinetic energy 4.50 × 10⁻²⁹ J is ______ × 10⁻⁵ m. (Nearest integer)\nGiven : mass of electron is 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg, h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 10: drops the factor 2 (p = √(mK)) giving 10.4 × 10⁻⁵ m. 73 or 0.7: mishandles √(10⁻⁵⁹) and shifts the decimal.\nWorking: λ = h/√(2mK) = 6.6×10⁻³⁴/√(2×9×10⁻³¹×4.5×10⁻²⁹) = 6.6×10⁻³⁴/√(8.1×10⁻⁵⁹) = 6.6×10⁻³⁴/9×10⁻³⁰ = 7.33×10⁻⁵ m → 7.\nKey idea: Write 8.1×10⁻⁵⁹ as 81×10⁻⁶⁰ so the root is 9×10⁻³⁰.","traps":["Factor 2 in p = √(2mK) dropped: p² = 2mK.","Odd exponent under square root: Rewrite as 81 × 10⁻⁶⁰."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"If the radius of the first orbit of hydrogen atom is α₀, then de Broglie's wavelength of electron in 3rd orbit is :","options":["πα₀/6","3πα₀","6πα₀","πα₀/3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 3πα₀ uses r₃ = 3α₀ (r ∝ n) or forgets the factor 2; (A)/(D) invert the relation (λ = n/2πr type slips).\nWorking: r₃ = 9α₀; 2πr₃ = 3λ ⇒ λ = 2π(9α₀)/3 = 6πα₀.\nKey idea: λₙ = 2πrₙ/n = 2πα₀n for hydrogen, so λ₃ = 6πα₀.","traps":["Radius taken proportional to n: rₙ = α₀n²/Z.","Quantisation condition inverted: Standing wave: whole number of wavelengths fit the circumference, nλ = 2πr."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":81,"q":"The number of following statement/s which is/are incorrect is ______\n(A) Line emission spectra are used to study the electronic structure\n(B) The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet\n(C) An absorption spectrum is like the photographic negative of an emission spectrum\n(D) The element helium was discovered in the sun by spectroscopic method","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (accepts B, mixing up continuous spectrum of white light), 2 (rejects A or D as unfamiliar).\nWorking: only (B) is incorrect – gaseous atoms give line spectra, not a continuous spread (that describes white light). A, C, D are NCERT statements. Count = 1.\nKey idea: Atoms in gas phase emit only discrete wavelengths → line spectra.","traps":["White light continuum transferred to atoms: Gaseous atoms → line (discontinuous) spectra.","'Incorrect' count reversed: Count false ones only."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The number of atomic orbitals from the following having 5 radial nodes is ______. 7s, 7p, 6s, 8p, 8d","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (using n − l or total nodes n − 1), 1 (only 6s), 4 (counting 7s as 5 using n − 2).\nWorking: radial nodes = n − l − 1: 7s 6, 7p 5, 6s 5, 8p 6, 8d 5 → three orbitals (7p, 6s, 8d).\nKey idea: Radial nodes = n − l − 1; total nodes = n − 1.","traps":["Total nodes used instead of radial: Subtract l for angular nodes."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The energy of an electron in the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. Its energy in the third Bohr orbit is ________.","options":["One third of this value","Three times of this value","1/9 th of this value","1/27 of this value"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): uses 1/n (velocity-type dependence). (B): uses n (or confuses with radius ratio thinking). (D): uses 1/n³.\nWorking: Eₙ = E₁/n², so E₃ = E₁/9 = −2.42 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.\nKey idea: Energy ∝ 1/n²; radius ∝ n²; velocity ∝ 1/n.","traps":["Wrong power of n: Energy ∝ Z²/n²."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Electrons in a cathode ray tube have been emitted with a velocity of 1000 m s⁻¹. The number of following statements which is/are true about the emitted radiation is ______.\nGiven : h = 6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, mₑ = 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg.\n(A) The de-Broglie wavelength of the electron emitted is 666.67 nm.\n(B) The characteristic of electrons emitted depend upon the material of the electrodes of the cathode ray tube.\n(C) The cathode rays start from cathode and move towards anode.\n(D) The nature of the emitted electrons depends on the nature of the gas present in cathode ray tube.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: A: λ = 6×10⁻³⁴/(9×10⁻³¹×10³) = 6.67×10⁻⁷ m = 666.67 nm, true. B false, D false (NCERT says independent). C true. Likely wrong answers: 3 (treating D or B as true), 1 (slip in nm conversion).\nWorking: (A) true – λ = h/(mv) = 6 × 10⁻³⁴/(9 × 10⁻³¹ × 10³) = 6.67 × 10⁻⁷ m = 666.67 nm; (B) and (D) false – cathode-ray electrons are the same whatever the electrode material or gas; (C) true. Count = 2.\nKey idea: Electrons are the same whatever the electrode material or gas, so only A and C are true.","traps":["Canal-ray property transferred to cathode rays: Cathode rays: independent; anode rays: depend on gas.","Unit conversion of wavelength: 6.67×10⁻⁷ m = 666.7 nm."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Subatomic particles"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"If wavelength of the first line of the Paschen series of hydrogen atom is 720 nm, then the wavelength of the second line of this series is _________ nm. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"492","range":[492.0,492.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1054 (ratio inverted), 576 or 540 (using Balmer n₁ = 2), 506 (rounding of fractions early).\nWorking: 1/λ ∝ (1/9 − 1/n²). First line (4→3): 7/144; second (5→3): 16/225. λ₂ = 720 × (7/144)/(16/225) = 492.2 → 492 nm.\nKey idea: λ₂/λ₁ = (7/144)/(16/225) = 0.6836.","traps":["Inverting wavelength ratio: Higher-energy line → shorter λ, so λ₂ < 720.","Wrong lower level: Paschen: n₁ = 3."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The number of s-electrons present in an ion with 55 protons in its unipositive state is","options":["10","9","12","8"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 9: miscounting one shell or removing an s electron from an inner shell. (C) 12: counting the 6s¹ plus an extra pair or using neutral Cs with a wrong count. (D) 8: forgetting 1s² or a shell.\nWorking: Cs⁺ (54 e⁻) = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p⁶ 4d¹⁰ 5s² 5p⁶; s electrons = 2 × 5 = 10.\nKey idea: Unipositive Cs loses its only 6s electron, leaving five filled s subshells (n = 1–5) = 10 s electrons.","traps":["Neutral Cs vs Cs⁺: Remove the outermost (6s) electron first."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Following figure shows spectrum of an ideal black body at four different temperatures. The number of correct statement/s from the following is ________. Graph: energy distribution vs wavelength; curves T₁ (highest peak) > T₂ > T₃ > T₄ (lowest peak), peaks shifting to longer wavelength for lower curves. A. T₄ > T₃ > T₂ > T₁ B. The black body consists of particles performing simple harmonic motion. C. The peak of the spectrum shifts to shorter wavelength as temperature increases. D. T₁/ν₁ = T₂/ν₂ = T₃/ν₃ ≠ constant E. The given spectrum could be explained using quantisation of energy.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: A: reversed temperature order. B: oscillator model wording – counting it gives 3. D: 'not constant' flips Wien's law. Likely wrong totals: 1, 3.\nWorking: C and E correct; A reversed (T₁ highest); D false (T/ν_max is constant by Wien's law); B is taken as false (the curve is explained by quantisation of energy, not by classical oscillators).\nKey idea: Higher curve = higher T and peak at shorter λ; Planck quantisation explains the curve.","traps":["Temperature order: Higher intensity curve = hotter body.","Oscillator statement: Statement B is taken as false; quantisation (E), not classical oscillation, explains the curve."],"topics":["Planck's quantum theory","Electromagnetic radiation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The radius of the 2nd orbit of Li²⁺ is x. The expected radius of the 3rd orbit of Be³⁺ is","options":["16x/27","4x/9","9x/4","27x/16"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the inverted ratio. (C) ignores the Z change (n² ratio only). (B) is the inverse of (C).\nWorking: r ∝ n²/Z; r(Li²⁺, n = 2) ∝ 4/3, r(Be³⁺, n = 3) ∝ 9/4, so the ratio is (9/4)/(4/3) = 27/16.\nKey idea: r(Be³⁺, 3)/r(Li²⁺, 2) = (9/4)/(4/3) = 27/16.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Larger n increases r; check that the answer is > x when n goes 2→3.","Z ignored: r ∝ n²/Z – Z changes from 3 to 4."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Shapes of orbitals","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The number of given orbitals which have electron density along the axis is ______: pₓ, p_y, p_z, d_xy, d_yz, d_xz, d_z², d_x²–y²","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (only p orbitals counted); 4 (d_z² missed because of its torus); 8 (assuming all lobes are along axes).\nWorking: pₓ, p_y, p_z, d_z² and d_x²–y² have lobes along the axes → 5.\nKey idea: Axial: all three p, d_z² and d_x²–y²; the three 'two-letter' d orbitals point between axes.","traps":["d_z² with torus: The main lobes of d_z² lie along z.","Subscript letters vs axes: Two-letter subscripts mean lobes in that plane but between the axes."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The shortest wavelength of hydrogen atom in Lyman series is λ. The longest wavelength in Balmer series of He⁺ is","options":["36λ/5","5/(9λ)","9λ/5","5λ/9"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 36λ/5 omits Z² = 4 (hydrogen Balmer longest line); (B) inverts the ratio; (D) 5λ/9 inverts 9/5.\nWorking: H Lyman shortest: 1/λ = R. He⁺ Balmer longest (3→2): 1/λ′ = 4R(1/4 − 1/9) = 5R/9, so λ′ = 9/(5R) = 9λ/5.\nKey idea: Shortest = series limit; longest = adjacent levels; multiply wavenumber by Z².","traps":["Forgetting Z² for He⁺: Multiply 1/λ by Z².","Shortest vs longest line: Shortest λ = n₂→∞; longest λ = n₂ = n₁+1."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Assume that the radius of the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is 0.6 Å. The radius of the third Bohr orbit of He⁺ is __________ picometer. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"270","range":[270.0,270.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2.7 or 3 (left in Å); 238 (using 52.9 pm instead of the given 0.6 Å); 540 (forgetting Z); 90 (using n instead of n²).\nWorking: r = 0.6 Å × 3²/2 = 2.7 Å = 270 pm.\nKey idea: r ∝ n²/Z; 1 Å = 100 pm.","traps":["Use given a₀, not 52.9 pm: Use the assumed 0.6 Å.","Å to pm: Multiply Å by 100."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The energy of one mole of photons of radiation of frequency 2 × 10¹² Hz in J mol⁻¹ is ______. (Nearest integer) [Given: h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, N_A = 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"798","range":[798.0,798.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (giving kJ mol⁻¹ rounded), 0 (energy of one photon), 399 (dropping the 2).\nWorking: E = N_A hν = 6.022×10²³ × 6.626×10⁻³⁴ × 2×10¹² = 798.0 J mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Multiply per-photon energy hν by N_A: 6.022 × 6.626 × 2 × 10¹ = 798.","traps":["Per photon vs per mole: Multiply by N_A for one mole of photons.","J vs kJ: The blank is in J mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in shell with n = 4 are:","options":["50","32","72","16"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 16 = n² (orbitals, not electrons); (A) 50 = 2×5²; (C) 72 = 2×6².\nWorking: 2n² = 2 × 4² = 32.\nKey idea: Electrons = 2n² = 32.","traps":["Orbitals vs electrons: Multiply by 2 for spin."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The wave function (Ψ) of 2s is given by Ψ₂ₛ = [1/(2√(2π))]·(1/a₀)^(1/2)·(2 − r/a₀)·e^(−r/2a₀). At r = r₀, radial node is formed. Thus, r₀ in terms of a₀","options":["r₀ = a₀","r₀ = 4a₀","r₀ = 2a₀","r₀ = a₀/2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) a₀: Bohr radius reflex; (B) 4a₀: from exponent 2a₀ doubled; (D) a₀/2: algebra slip.\nWorking: Ψ = 0 when (2 − r₀/a₀) = 0, so r₀ = 2a₀.\nKey idea: Node where 2 − r/a₀ = 0 ⇒ r₀ = 2a₀ (the exponential never vanishes).","traps":["Exponent term confused with node: Only the polynomial factor can be zero."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which transition in the hydrogen spectrum would have the same wavelength as the Balmer type transition from n = 4 to n = 2 of He⁺ spectrum","options":["n = 3 to n = 4","n = 2 to n = 1","n = 1 to n = 2","n = 1 to n = 3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3→4 is an absorption with a much smaller energy. (C) 1→2 has the same |ΔE| but is an absorption, while the given He⁺ line is an emission. (D) 1→3 gives 8/9, not 3/4.\nWorking: He⁺ 4→2 gives 4(1/4 − 1/16) = 3/4 = H 2→1 (1 − 1/4). Since the He⁺ line is an emission, the matching H line is the emission 2 → 1 (B); 1 → 2 (C) is the absorption of the same wavelength.\nKey idea: Z² = 4 for He⁺ cancels the factor 4 in n: He⁺ (n₁,n₂) = H (n₁/2, n₂/2), so 4→2 matches 2→1.","traps":["Direction of transition ignored: Match emission with emission: higher n to lower n.","Forgetting Z² for He⁺: Multiply by Z² = 4, then halve each n."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Arrange the following orbitals in decreasing order of energy. A. n = 3, l = 0, m = 0 B. n = 4, l = 0, m = 0 C. n = 3, l = 1, m = 0 D. n = 3, l = 2, m = 1. The correct option for the order is:","options":["B > D > C > A","A > C > B > D","D > B > A > C","D > B > C > A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts 4s above 3d (pure n ordering). (B) is the increasing order reversed wrongly. (C) puts 3s above 3p.\nWorking: D (3d, n + l = 5) > B (4s, n + l = 4, higher n) > C (3p, n + l = 4) > A (3s, n + l = 3).\nKey idea: 3d > 4s > 3p > 3s for multi-electron atoms by (n + l).","traps":["4s vs 3d: Use n + l: 3d = 5, 4s = 4.","Decreasing vs increasing: Reread 'decreasing' before matching."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following sets of quantum numbers is not allowed?","options":["n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = 0, s = +½","n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = −2, s = +½","n = 3, l = 3, mₗ = −3, s = −½","n = 3, l = 0, mₗ = 0, s = −½"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are all valid sets (l ≤ n − 1, |mₗ| ≤ l); a student unsure about mₗ = −2 may pick (B).\nWorking: l can only be 0 to n−1, so l = 3 is impossible for n = 3.\nKey idea: l ≤ n − 1.","traps":["l allowed up to n: l runs from 0 to n − 1 only."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"When the excited electron of a H atom from n = 5 drops to the ground state, the maximum number of emission lines observed are _____.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answer: 10 (applying n(n−1)/2 for a large sample); 1 (direct 5→1 only).\nWorking: a single electron in one H atom can at most step 5→4→3→2→1, giving 4 lines; the formula n(n−1)/2 = 10 applies to a sample of many atoms.\nKey idea: One electron can emit at most (n₂ − n₁) photons on the way down.","traps":["n(n−1)/2 used for one atom: \"An electron of a H atom\" = one atom: max n₂ − n₁ lines."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The wavelength of an electron and a neutron will become equal when the velocity of the electron is x times the velocity of neutron. The value of x is ______. (Nearest Integer)\n(Mass of electron is 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg and mass of neutron is 1.6 × 10⁻²⁷ kg)","type":"num","answer":"1758","range":[1758.0,1758.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 5.7 × 10⁻⁴ (ratio inverted), 1759 (rounding error).\nWorking: λ = h/mv equal → m_e v_e = m_n v_n → x = 1.6 × 10⁻²⁷ / 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ = 1758.2 → 1758.\nKey idea: Same λ ⇔ same momentum.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Lighter particle must move faster."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Subatomic particles","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider an imaginary ion ⁴⁸₂₂X³⁻. The nucleus contains 'a'% more neutrons than the number of electrons in the ion. The value of 'a' is ____. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 19 electrons (subtracting charge) gives 37%; using neutrons as the base gives 3.8 ≈ 4 as well, but 26/22 errors give 18%.\nWorking: neutrons = 48 − 22 = 26; electrons = 22 + 3 = 25; a = (26 − 25)/25 × 100 = 4.\nKey idea: Anion X³⁻ has Z + 3 electrons.","traps":["Anion charge direction: X³⁻ → add 3 electrons.","Percentage base: Divide by the number being compared against (25)."],"topics":["Subatomic particles","Isotopes & isobars"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Energy of 2s orbital of hydrogen atom is greater than that of 2s orbital of lithium.\nReason R : Energies of the orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increase in the atomic number.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): student thinks the rule is unrelated; (D): student thinks H 2s is lower because H has only one electron (no repulsion); (C): student believes energies depend only on n.\nWorking: a larger nuclear charge (higher effective Z) lowers orbital energy, so E₂ₛ(H) > E₂ₛ(Li); R states this general rule and explains A.\nKey idea: Same n and l, higher Z → more negative energy.","traps":["Hydrogen orbitals assumed lowest: Energy depends on nuclear attraction, which grows with Z.","R true but not the reason: R is the general rule; A is its instance."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct decreasing order of energy, for the orbitals having following set of quantum numbers: (A) n = 3, l = 0, m = 0; (B) n = 4, l = 0, m = 0; (C) n = 3, l = 1, m = 0; (D) n = 3, l = 2, m = 1.","options":["(D) > (B) > (C) > (A)","(B) > (D) > (C) > (A)","(C) > (B) > (D) > (A)","(B) > (C) > (D) > (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts 4s above 3d (ignores n + l = 4 vs 5); (C) puts 3p above 4s (ignores tie-break by n); (D) puts 4s above 3d and 3p above 3d.\nWorking: the sets are 3s, 4s, 3p and 3d. By the (n + l) rule: 3s = 3, 3p = 4, 4s = 4 (higher n, so above 3p), 3d = 5. Order 3d > 4s > 3p > 3s = (D) > (B) > (C) > (A).\nKey idea: 3d (n + l = 5) lies above 4s (n + l = 4), which lies above 3p (n + l = 4, lower n).","traps":["4s placed above 3d: Use n + l first; only break ties with n.","Tie between 3p and 4s: For equal n + l, larger n is higher in energy."],"topics":["Aufbau principle","Quantum numbers"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Shapes of orbitals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Identify the incorrect statement from the following.","options":["A circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves is proposed as Bohr’s orbit.","An orbital is the one electron wave function (Ψ) in an atom.","The existence of Bohr’s orbits is supported by hydrogen spectrum.","Atomic orbital is characterised by the quantum numbers n and l only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are near-verbatim NCERT lines; (C) may look doubtful because NCERT says Bohr orbits cannot be demonstrated, but the hydrogen line spectrum is the evidence Bohr used.\nWorking: an atomic orbital needs three quantum numbers n, l and mₗ, so (D) is false; (A), (B) and (C) are standard statements.\nKey idea: n and l fix a subshell; mₗ is needed to fix one orbital.","traps":["Subshell vs orbital labels: Remember 2p has three orbitals that differ only in mₗ."],"topics":["Shapes of orbitals","Quantum numbers"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"If the wavelength for an electron emitted from H-atom is 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m, then energy absorbed by the electron in its ground state compared to minimum energy required for its escape from the atom, is ____ times. (Nearest integer) [Given: h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, Mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 1 (KE/IE only), 3 (rounding errors), 0 (using eV/J mix).\nWorking: KE = h²/(2mλ²) = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴)²/(2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × (3.3 × 10⁻¹⁰)²) = 2.215 × 10⁻¹⁸ J (13.8 eV). Energy absorbed = IE + KE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ + 2.215 × 10⁻¹⁸ J; ratio to IE (2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, 13.6 eV) = 2.02 ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Energy absorbed = ionisation energy + KE of ejected electron; KE ≈ IE here.","traps":["Energy absorbed vs KE: Absorbed energy = IE + KE.","KE from wavelength: For a particle use KE = h²/(2mλ²)."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Heisenberg uncertainty","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The minimum uncertainty in the speed of an electron in an one dimensional region of length 2a₀ (Where a₀ = Bohr radius 52.9 pm) is _____ km s⁻¹. (Given : Mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, Planck's constant h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)","type":"num","answer":"548","range":[548.0,548.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1096 (Δx taken as a₀); 274 (Δx taken as 4a₀); 548000 (left in m s⁻¹); 1096 also from using h/2π; 6886 (h/mΔx, 4π dropped).\nWorking: Δv = h/(4π m Δx) = 6.63×10⁻³⁴ / (4π × 9.1×10⁻³¹ × 1.058×10⁻¹⁰) = 5.48 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ = 548 km s⁻¹.\nKey idea: Use h/4π, Δx = 105.8 pm, convert m s⁻¹ to km s⁻¹.","traps":["km/s conversion missed: Divide by 1000.","Factor 4π dropped: Use h/4π."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are the quantum numbers for 4 electrons. A. n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = 1, mₛ = +1/2; B. n = 4, l = 1, mₗ = 0, mₛ = +1/2; C. n = 4, l = 2, mₗ = −2, mₛ = −1/2; D. n = 3, l = 1, mₗ = −1, mₛ = +1/2. The correct order of increasing energy is:","options":["D < B < A < C","D < A < B < C","B < D < A < C","B < D < C < A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts 4p below 3d (ignores tie-break by n); (C) and (D) put 4p lowest (treat l as the only factor or misread n).\nWorking: A = 3d (n + l = 5), B = 4p (5), C = 4d (6), D = 3p (4). For A vs B (both 5) lower n is lower energy, so 3p < 3d < 4p < 4d = D < A < B < C.\nKey idea: 3d and 4p tie at n + l = 5; 3d is lower.","traps":["3d vs 4p tie: Equal n + l: smaller n is lower energy.","mₗ and mₛ treated as relevant: Energy (multi-electron atom) depends only on n and l."],"topics":["Aufbau principle","Quantum numbers"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Consider the following pairs of electrons: (A) (a) n = 3, l = 1, mₗ = 1, mₛ = +½; (b) n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = 1, mₛ = +½. (B) (a) n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = −2, mₛ = −½; (b) n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = −1, mₛ = −½. (C) (a) n = 4, l = 2, mₗ = 2, mₛ = +½; (b) n = 3, l = 2, mₗ = 2, mₛ = +½. The pairs of electrons present in degenerate orbitals is/are:","options":["Only A","Only B","Only C","(B) and (C)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (D): pair C matches l, mₗ and mₛ, tempting students who ignore n; (A): pair A shares n but differs in l (3p vs 3d, degenerate only in hydrogen).\nWorking: degenerate orbitals need the same n and l (free atom). A: 3p vs 3d – no; B: two 3d orbitals – yes; C: 4d vs 3d – no. Only B.\nKey idea: Degenerate = same subshell (same n and l); mₗ and mₛ do not matter.","traps":["Same mₗ taken as degenerate: Check n and l first; only these fix the energy of a subshell.","Hydrogen-atom degeneracy carried over: For multi-electron atoms energy depends on n + l; 3p < 3d."],"topics":["Quantum numbers","Aufbau principle"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The energy of one mole of photons of radiation of wavelength 300 nm is (Given: h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","options":["235 kJ mol⁻¹","325 kJ mol⁻¹","399 kJ mol⁻¹","435 kJ mol⁻¹"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) – arithmetic slips with powers of ten or not multiplying by N_A (gives 6.6×10⁻¹⁹ J, not listed).\nWorking: E = N_A·hc/λ = 6.02×10²³ × 6.63×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸ / 3×10⁻⁷ m = 3.99×10⁵ J = 399 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: N_A·hc = 0.1197 J m mol⁻¹; divide by λ in metres.","traps":["nm not converted to metres: Write 300 nm = 3 × 10⁻⁷ m before substituting.","Energy per photon instead of per mole: Multiply by N_A for per-mole energy."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The longest wavelength of light that can be used for the ionisation of lithium atom (Li) in its ground state is x × 10⁻⁸ m. The value of x is ____. (Nearest Integer) (Given: Energy of the electron in the first shell of the hydrogen atom is −2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ J; h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 – uses n = 1 and Z = 3 (E = 1.98 × 10⁻¹⁷ J, λ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁸ m); 9 – uses Z = 1, n = 1 (hydrogen); 36 – Z = 1, n = 2; 23 – using the real Li IE (5.39 eV).\nWorking: valence electron of Li is in n = 2; E = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ × Z²/n² = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 9/4 = 4.95 × 10⁻¹⁸ J; λ = hc/E = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/4.95 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 4.02 × 10⁻⁸ m → 4. Caveat: Bohr's formula is valid only for one-electron species; for neutral Li the real IE (5.39 eV) gives λ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁷ m. The official answer is 4, based on the Bohr scaling the question intends.\nKey idea: Take Li's valence electron in n = 2 with Z = 3 as the question intends.","traps":["Shell of the valence electron: Li ground state valence electron is 2s (n = 2).","Bohr model for multi-electron atom: Use the question's intended Z²/n² scaling; note it is only approximate."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The minimum energy that must be possessed by photons in order to produce the photoelectric effect with platinum metal is: [Given: The threshold frequency of platinum is 1.3 × 10¹⁵ s⁻¹ and h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.]","options":["3.21 × 10⁻¹⁴ J","6.24 × 10⁻¹⁶ J","8.58 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","9.76 × 10⁻²⁰ J"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are powers-of-ten slips or random values; no common wrong formula leads to them except mishandling the exponent (10⁻³⁴ × 10¹⁵ = 10⁻¹⁹).\nWorking: minimum photon energy = work function = hν₀ = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 1.3 × 10¹⁵ = 8.58 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.\nKey idea: Minimum photon energy for photoemission equals the work function hν₀.","traps":["Exponent arithmetic: Add exponents: −34 + 15 = −19."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"If the radius of the 3rd Bohr's orbit of hydrogen atom is r₃ and the radius of 4th Bohr's orbit is r₄. Then :","options":["r₄ = (9/16) r₃","r₄ = (16/9) r₃","r₄ = (3/4) r₃","r₄ = (4/3) r₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) inverts the ratio; (C)/(D) use r ∝ n instead of n² (velocity/other dependence).\nWorking: rₙ = n²a₀, so r₄/r₃ = 16/9 and r₄ = (16/9) r₃ = (B).\nKey idea: r ∝ n²/Z: the outer orbit is larger by (4/3)².","traps":["r ∝ n used instead of n²: Recall rₙ = 52.9 n²/Z pm.","Ratio inverted: Outer orbit must be larger."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The number of radial and angular nodes in 4d orbital are, respectively","options":["1 and 2","3 and 2","1 and 0","2 and 1"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 3 and 2 – uses n – 1 for radial nodes; (C) 1 and 0 – ignores angular nodes; (D) 2 and 1 – swaps the formulas or uses n – 2 and l – 1.\nWorking: radial nodes = n – l – 1 = 4 – 2 – 1 = 1; angular nodes = l = 2.\nKey idea: Radial = n – l – 1, angular = l, total = n – 1 = 3.","traps":["Total nodes taken as radial nodes: Total nodes = n – 1; subtract l angular nodes to get radial nodes.","Radial and angular counts swapped: Angular nodes always equal l (2 for d); radial = n – l – 1."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Heisenberg uncertainty","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"If the uncertainty in velocity and position of a minute particle in space are 2.4 × 10⁻²⁶ m s⁻¹ and 10⁻⁷ m respectively, the mass of the particle in g is ______ (Nearest integer). (Given: h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.022 or 0 – leaves the answer in kg; 88 – uses h/π or forgets the 4 differently; 276 – uses h/(Δx·Δv) without 4π.\nWorking: m = h/(4π·Δx·Δv) = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴/(4π × 10⁻⁷ × 2.4 × 10⁻²⁶) = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴/3.016 × 10⁻³² = 0.02197 kg = 21.97 g ≈ 22 g.\nKey idea: Answer comes out in kg; multiply by 1000 for grams.","traps":["kg left unconverted: Convert to g as asked.","4π dropped: Use h/4π (NCERT form)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider the following set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ): A. 3, 3, −3; B. 3, 2, −2; C. 2, 1, +1; D. 2, 2, +2. The number of correct sets of quantum numbers is ______","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 3 or 4 (accepting l = n); 1 (rejecting C by misreading mₗ sign).\nWorking: l must be 0 to n − 1 and mₗ from −l to +l. A (l = n) and D (l = n) are not allowed; B and C are allowed. Count = 2.\nKey idea: l < n always; |mₗ| ≤ l.","traps":["l = n accepted: l runs 0 to n − 1."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Element \"E\" belongs to the period 4 and group 16 of the periodic table. The valence shell electron configuration of the element, which is just above 'E' in the group is","options":["3s², 3p⁴","3d¹⁰, 4s², 4p⁴","4d¹⁰, 5s², 5p⁴","2s², 2p⁴"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is Se itself (E, not the one above); (C) is Te (below); (D) is O, two periods up.\nWorking: E = Se (period 4, group 16); the element just above it is S, valence configuration 3s²3p⁴.\nKey idea: Group 16 → ns²np⁴; one period above period 4 is n = 3.","traps":["Answering for E instead of the element above: Underline \"just above\" and drop n by one."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"If the work function of a metal is 6.63 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, the maximum wavelength of the photon required to remove a photoelectron from the metal is ______ nm. (Nearest integer) [Given: h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, and c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"300","range":[300.0,300.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3000 (Å confusion), 0.3 (µm), 3 (dropping powers).\nWorking: λ₀ = hc/W₀ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸)/(6.63 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 3 × 10⁻⁷ m = 300 nm.\nKey idea: Maximum wavelength ↔ minimum energy = W₀.","traps":["Metres to nanometres: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Consider the following statements: (A) The principal quantum number 'n' is a positive integer with values of 'n' = 1, 2, 3, …. (B) The azimuthal quantum number 'l' for a given 'n' (principal quantum number) can have values as 'l' = 0, 1, 2, …, n. (C) Magnetic orbital quantum number 'mₗ' for a particular 'l' (azimuthal quantum number) has (2l + 1) values. (D) ±1/2 are the two possible orientations of electron spin. (E) For l = 5, there will be a total of 9 orbitals. Which of the above statements are correct?","options":["(A), (B) and (C)","(A), (C), (D) and (E)","(A), (C) and (D)","(A), (B), (C) and (D)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (D) includes false statement (B) (l up to n); option (B) includes false statement (E) (9 orbitals – 2l − 1 instead of 2l + 1); option (A) includes (B) and drops (D).\nWorking: (B) is false – l runs from 0 to n − 1; (E) is false – l = 5 gives 2l + 1 = 11 orbitals; (A), (C), (D) are true.\nKey idea: l ≤ n − 1 and the number of orbitals in a subshell is 2l + 1.","traps":["l maximum value n − 1: For n = 1 only l = 0 exists.","2l + 1 vs 2l − 1: Compute 2×5 + 1 = 11."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following statements are correct? (A) The electronic configuration of Cr is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹. (B) The magnetic quantum number may have a negative value. (C) In the ground state of an atom, the orbitals are filled in order of their increasing energies. (D) The total number of nodes are given by n – 2. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (C) and (D) only","(A) and (B) only","(A) and (C) only","(A), (B) and (C) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) option includes false (D); (B) misses true (C); (C) misses true (B) – students who think mₗ is only positive or confuse aufbau with (n+l) wording drop a true statement.\nWorking: (A) true (half-filled 3d stability); (B) true, mₗ runs from –l to +l; (C) true, aufbau principle; (D) false, total nodes = n – 1.\nKey idea: Total nodes = n – 1 (radial n – l – 1 plus angular l).","traps":["Total vs radial nodes: Total = n – 1; radial = n – l – 1.","Options differ by one statement: mₗ = –l … +l includes negatives."],"topics":["Aufbau principle","Half/fully-filled stability"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following is the correct plot for the probability density ψ²(r) as a function of distance 'r' of the electron from the nucleus for 2s orbital?","options":["ψ²(r) maximum at r = 0 and decreasing smoothly to zero (no node)","ψ²(r) maximum at r = 0, falling to zero at one node, then a small maximum and decay to zero","ψ²(r) falling from a maximum at r = 0, crossing below zero (negative region) and returning to zero","ψ²(r) maximum at r = 0 with two nodes and two smaller maxima"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the 1s plot (no node); (C) shows negative ψ², impossible for a square; (D) has two nodes (3s). Students who think of 4πr²ψ² (zero at r = 0) may reject (B).\nWorking: 2s has n − l − 1 = 1 radial node; ψ² is largest at the nucleus, becomes zero at the node, rises to a smaller hump and decays; ψ² can never be negative (rules out C), (A) is 1s, (D) is 3s.\nKey idea: Radial nodes = n − l − 1; ψ² (not 4πr²ψ²) is maximum at r = 0 for s orbitals.","traps":["ψ² vs radial distribution function: ψ² is non-zero at r = 0 for s orbitals; RDF is zero there.","Sign of ψ vs ψ²: A square is never negative."],"topics":["Nodes","Shapes of orbitals"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: According to Bohr's model of an atom, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with decrease in positive charges on the nucleus as there is no strong hold on the electron by the nucleus. Statement II: According to Bohr's model of an atom, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with decrease in principal quantum number. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) rejects II by mixing v with r; (B)/(D) accept the faulty 'weak hold' argument.\nWorking: in the Bohr model v ∝ Z/n, so v decreases as nuclear charge decreases (I false) and increases as n decreases (II true).\nKey idea: v ∝ Z/n: more charge or inner orbit → faster.","traps":["v ∝ Z/n: Larger Z pulls harder → faster electron; v = 2.18×10⁶ Z/n m s⁻¹.","Lower n → faster: Inner orbits: smaller radius and higher speed."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A metal surface is exposed to 500 nm radiation. The threshold frequency of the metal for photoelectric current is 4.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz. The velocity of ejected electron is ____ × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ (Nearest integer) [Use: h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, mₑ = 9.0 × 10⁻³¹ kg]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 or 8 (forgetting to subtract hν₀), 3 or 4 (dropping the factor 2), 25 (reporting v² scale).\nWorking: ν = c/λ = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz; KE = h(ν – ν₀) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 1.7 × 10¹⁴ = 1.127 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; v = √(2KE/m) = √(2.504 × 10¹¹) = 5.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹.\nKey idea: Convert λ to ν first: ν – ν₀ = 1.7 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","traps":["Subtract threshold energy: KE = h(ν – ν₀).","Factor 2 and square root: v = √(2KE/m)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The kinetic energy of an electron in the second Bohr orbit of a hydrogen atom is equal to h²/(x m a₀²). The value of 10x is ______. (a₀ is radius of Bohr's orbit) (Nearest integer) [Given: π = 3.14]","type":"num","answer":"3155","range":[3155.0,3155.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 789 (n not squared in r: x = 8π²); 12620 (n = 1 used in mvr but r = 4a₀: x = 128π²); 315 (forgetting the ×10).\nWorking: r₂ = 4a₀; mvr = 2h/2π gives v = h/(4π m a₀); KE = ½mv² = h²/(32π² m a₀²); x = 32π² = 32 × 9.8596 = 315.5; 10x = 3155.\nKey idea: v ∝ n/r and r ∝ n², so KE ∝ 1/n²: KE₂ = h²/(8π² m a₀²) ÷ 4.","traps":["Radius not scaled: Use r₂ = 4a₀.","Final ×10 forgotten: Multiply by 10 at the end."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of photons emitted by a monochromatic (single frequency) infrared range finder of power 1 mW and wavelength of 1000 nm, in 0.1 second is x × 10¹³. The value of x is _____. (Nearest integer) (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 503 (forgetting the 0.1 s); 5 (wrong power conversion of mW); 5 × 10⁻⁴ magnitude errors from nm → m.\nWorking: E(photon) = hc/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/10⁻⁶ = 1.989 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; energy in 0.1 s = 10⁻³ × 0.1 = 10⁻⁴ J; N = 10⁻⁴/1.989 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 5.03 × 10¹⁴ = 50 × 10¹³.\nKey idea: N = Pt λ/(hc).","traps":["Time factor dropped: Energy = power × time = 10⁻⁴ J.","nm and mW conversions: 1000 nm = 10⁻⁶ m; 1 mW = 10⁻³ W."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Ge (Z = 32) in its ground state electronic configuration has x completely filled orbitals with mₗ = 0. The value of x is _______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 – counts a 4p orbital with mₗ = 0 as filled; 6 – forgets the 3d mₗ = 0 orbital; 4 – counts only s orbitals.\nWorking: Ge = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p². Filled orbitals with mₗ = 0: 1s, 2s, 3s, 4s (four s orbitals), one each from 2p and 3p, and one from 3d = 7. The 4p² electrons occupy two different 4p orbitals singly (Hund), so no completely filled 4p orbital. x = 7.\nKey idea: One mₗ = 0 orbital per filled subshell: 1s,2s,2p,3s,3p,3d,4s = 7.","traps":["4p counted as filled: Hund’s rule: 4p electrons unpaired in separate orbitals.","3d forgotten: Include 3d¹⁰ – one mₗ = 0 orbital."],"topics":["Quantum numbers","Aufbau principle"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The value of magnetic quantum number of the outermost electron of Zn⁺ ion is _______.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: +2/−2 (taking a 3d electron as outermost), 1 (confusing m with l or n-related value).\nWorking: Zn⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹; the outermost electron is 4s (l = 0), so m_l = 0.\nKey idea: Zn⁺ keeps one 4s electron; s orbital → m_l = 0.","traps":["3d taken as outermost: Outermost = highest n: 4s."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"A proton and a Li³⁺ nucleus are accelerated by the same potential. If λ_Li and λ_P denote the de Broglie wavelengths of Li³⁺ and proton respectively, then the value of λ_Li/λ_P is x × 10⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) (Mass of Li³⁺ = 8.3 mass of proton)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (charge ignored: √(1/8.3) = 0.35); 0 (square root not taken: 1/24.9 = 0.04); 50 (ratio inverted: √24.9 = 5.0).\nWorking: λ = h/√(2mqV); λ_Li/λ_P = √(m_p q_p /(m_Li q_Li)) = √(1/(8.3 × 3)) = √(1/24.9) = 0.200 → x = 2.\nKey idea: λ ∝ 1/√(mq) at fixed V.","traps":["Charge ignored: KE = qV; Li³⁺ gains 3× energy."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"According to Bohr's atomic theory: (A) Kinetic energy of electron is ∝ Z²/n². (B) The product of velocity (v) of electron and principal quantum number (n), 'vn' ∝ Z². (C) Frequency of revolution of electron in an orbit is ∝ Z³/n³. (D) Coulombic force of attraction on the electron is ∝ Z³/n⁴. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(C) Only","(A) Only","(A), (C) and (D) only","(A) and (D) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (A) and (C) accept statement (C) (Z³/n³) – confusing frequency with something like v³; option sets with B accept vn ∝ Z² (squaring Z by mistake).\nWorking: KE ∝ Z²/n² (A true); v ∝ Z/n so vn ∝ Z (B false); frequency = v/2πr ∝ (Z/n)/(n²/Z) = Z²/n³ (C false); F ∝ Z/r² ∝ Z·Z²/n⁴ = Z³/n⁴ (D true).\nKey idea: Write r ∝ n²/Z and v ∝ Z/n; everything else follows by combination.","traps":["Frequency exponent of Z: Frequency = v/(2πr) = (Z/n)·(Z/n²).","vn depends on Z, not Z²: Derive v from mvr = nh/2π with r ∝ n²/Z."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The plots of radial distribution functions (4πr²R²ₙ,ₗ(r) vs r/Å) for various orbitals of hydrogen atom are given: (A) a single peak near 1 Å; (B) a small inner peak then a larger peak near 3 Å (two maxima, one node); (C) a single peak near 2 Å; (D) two small inner peaks and a large peak near 7 Å (three maxima, two nodes). The correct plot for 3s orbital is:","options":["(B)","(A)","(D)","(C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (A), plot (B), is 2s (one node); option (B), plot (A), is 1s; option (D), plot (C), is 2p – single peak like 1s but farther out.\nWorking: 3s has n − l − 1 = 2 radial nodes, so its radial distribution plot shows three maxima with the largest farthest out. (A) one peak = 1s; (B) two peaks = 2s; (C) one peak at larger r = 2p; (D) three peaks = 3s.\nKey idea: Number of peaks in the RDF = n − l; 3s → 3 peaks.","traps":["Counting peaks as nodes: Nodes = peaks − 1.","Single-peak 2p taken for s: 1s and 2p both show one peak; 3s must show three."],"topics":["Nodes","Shapes of orbitals"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Electromagnetic radiation of wavelength 663 nm is just sufficient to ionise the atom of metal A. The ionization energy of metal A in kJ mol⁻¹ is ______. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"181","range":[181.0,181.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3×10⁻¹⁹ (per atom); 180 (rounded down); nm not converted.\nWorking: E = hc/λ = (6.63×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸)/(663×10⁻⁹) = 3.00×10⁻¹⁹ J per atom; × N_A = 1.806×10⁵ J mol⁻¹ = 180.6 → 181 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Multiply per-photon energy by N_A and divide by 1000.","traps":["Forgot N_A: Unit asked is kJ mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The orbital having two radial as well as two angular nodes is –","options":["3p","4f","4d","5d"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 4d has 1 radial, 2 angular; (B) 4f has 0 radial, 3 angular; (A) 3p has 1 radial, 1 angular.\nWorking: angular nodes = l = 2 → d; radial = n − l − 1 = 2 → n = 5; 5d.\nKey idea: Fix l from angular nodes first, then n = radial + l + 1.","traps":["Radial nodes taken as n – l: Radial = n – l – 1."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Heisenberg uncertainty","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A ball weighing 10 g is moving with a velocity of 90 m s⁻¹. If the uncertainty in its velocity is 5%, then the uncertainty in its position is _______ × 10⁻³³ m. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1173 or 0.001 – mass kept in grams; 0 – using full 90 m/s as Δv; 15 – forgetting 4π.\nWorking: Δv = 4.5 m s⁻¹; Δx = h/(4π m Δv) = 6.63×10⁻³⁴/(4π × 0.010 × 4.5) = 1.17 × 10⁻³³ m ≈ 1.\nKey idea: Use Δv = 5% of 90 = 4.5 m s⁻¹ and m in kg.","traps":["Mass left in grams: Convert 10 g to 0.010 kg.","4π omitted: Use h/4π."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The Azimuthal quantum number for the valence electrons of Ga⁺ ion is ________. (Atomic number of Ga = 31)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (taking the neutral atom’s 4p electron as valence); 2 (taking 3d as valence).\nWorking: Ga = [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p¹; Ga⁺ loses the 4p electron, giving [Ar]3d¹⁰4s². The valence electrons are 4s, so l = 0.\nKey idea: Ga⁺: 4p¹ removed → valence shell is 4s² → l = 0.","traps":["Neutral-atom valence used: Remove the 4p electron first for Ga⁺.","3d counted as valence: For Ga, 3d¹⁰ is a core shell."],"topics":["Quantum numbers","Aufbau principle"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The wavelength of electrons accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 40 kV is x × 10⁻¹² m. The value of x is _____. (Nearest integer) Given : Mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, Charge on an electron = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, Planck's constant = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 194 (taking 40 V), 9 (dropping the factor 2 under the root, λ ≈ 8.7 pm), 61 or 0.6 (power-of-ten slips).\nWorking: λ = h/√(2meV) = 6.63×10⁻³⁴/√(2 × 9.1×10⁻³¹ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 4×10⁴) = 6.63×10⁻³⁴/1.079×10⁻²² = 6.14×10⁻¹² m → 6.\nKey idea: λ(Å) ≈ 12.27/√V for electrons: 12.27/200 ≈ 0.061 Å = 6.1 pm.","traps":["kV not converted: Use V = 4 × 10⁴ V.","Factor 2 dropped: KE = p²/2m → p = √(2m·KE)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Number of electrons that Vanadium (Z = 23) has in p-orbitals is equal to ______","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (only 3p counted), 15 (d counted as p).\nWorking: V = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d³ 4s²; p electrons = 6 + 6 = 12.\nKey idea: 2p⁶ + 3p⁶ = 12.","traps":["Only valence p counted: Count all p subshells (2p and 3p)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A source of monochromatic radiation of wavelength 400 nm provides 1000 J of energy in 10 seconds. When this radiation falls on the surface of sodium, x × 10²⁰ electrons are ejected per second. Assume that wavelength 400 nm is sufficient for ejection of electron from the surface of sodium metal. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) (h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 20 (using 1000 J instead of 100 J s⁻¹), 0.2 (wavelength unit slip).\nWorking: E(photon) = hc/λ = 6.626×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸ / 400×10⁻⁹ = 4.97×10⁻¹⁹ J; power = 100 J s⁻¹; photons (= electrons) per second = 100/4.97×10⁻¹⁹ = 2.01×10²⁰ → 2.\nKey idea: Electrons per second = photons per second = power/(hc/λ).","traps":["Energy per 10 s vs per second: Convert to power (J s⁻¹) first."],"topics":["Photoelectric effect","Planck's quantum theory"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Heisenberg uncertainty","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"An accelerated electron has a speed of 5 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ with an uncertainty of 0.02%. The uncertainty in finding its location while in motion is x × 10⁻⁹ m. The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer) [Use mass of electron = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, π = 3.14]","type":"num","answer":"58","range":[58.0,58.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.058 or 5.8 (using v instead of Δv or slipping powers of ten); 116 (using h/2π instead of h/4π); 729 (using h/(mΔv) without 4π).\nWorking: Δv = 0.0002 × 5 × 10⁶ = 1000 m s⁻¹; Δx = h/(4πmΔv) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/(4 × 3.14 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1000) = 5.80 × 10⁻⁸ m = 58 × 10⁻⁹ m.\nKey idea: Convert % to absolute Δv first, then Δx = h/(4πmΔv).","traps":["Using v instead of Δv: Δv = % × v.","Power-of-ten bookkeeping: Carry exponents separately."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Rutherford's gold foil experiment cannot explain the line spectrum of hydrogen atom.\nStatement II: Bohr's model of hydrogen atom contradicts Heisenberg's uncertainty principle.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Statement I is true but statement II is false.","Both statement I and statement II are false.","Both statement I and statement II are true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrongly thinks Rutherford explained spectra; (B) misses that Bohr's orbits conflict with uncertainty; (C) rejects both.\nWorking: Rutherford's nuclear model says nothing about quantised energies, so it cannot explain the line spectrum (true); Bohr assigns electrons fixed orbits with definite position and momentum, which violates the uncertainty principle (true). Option (D).\nKey idea: Rutherford fails on stability and spectra; Bohr fails on uncertainty and multi-electron atoms.","traps":["Bohr model vs uncertainty: Definite orbit = definite r and v → violates Δx·Δp."],"topics":["Bohr model","Thomson & Rutherford models","Heisenberg uncertainty"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Thomson & Rutherford models","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"If the Thompson model of the atom was correct, then the result of Rutherford's gold foil experiment would have been:","options":["All of the α-particles pass through the gold foil without decrease in speed.","α-Particles are deflected over a wide range of angles.","All α-particles get bounced back by 180°","α-Particles pass through the gold foil deflected by small angles and with reduced speed."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) describe what a nuclear atom would give; (A) is tempting as 'no deflection at all'.\nWorking: with charge spread uniformly, no strong local field exists, so α-particles would pass with only small deflections; (B) and (C) need a concentrated nucleus. The 'reduced speed' part is debatable (energy loss would be small), so (A) is not absurd, but (D) is the intended best fit.\nKey idea: Diffuse positive charge → only small deflections, no back-scattering.","traps":["Thomson vs Rutherford prediction: Large deflection needs a concentrated nucleus.","Small deflection vs none: Some small deflection is expected."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"When light of wavelength 248 nm falls on a metal of threshold energy 3.0 eV, the de-Broglie wavelength of emitted electrons is _______ Å. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Use: √3 = 1.73, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, mₑ = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg; c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹; 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J]","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (using photon energy 5 eV as KE), 12 (dropping the factor 2 in √2mKE), answer in nm (0.87 → 1).\nWorking: E = hc/λ = 8.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 5.0 eV; KE = 2.0 eV = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; λ = h/√(2mKE) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/7.63 × 10⁻²⁵ = 8.7 × 10⁻¹⁰ m ≈ 9 Å.\nKey idea: λ = h/√(2m·KE), KE = E_photon − work function.","traps":["Photon energy used as KE: KE = 5.0 − 3.0 = 2.0 eV.","m to Å conversion: 1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Photoelectric effect"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of orbitals with n = 5, mₗ = +2 is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (stopping at f, forgetting 5g), 25 (all orbitals of n = 5), 5 (values of l).\nWorking: for n = 5, l = 0–4; mₗ = +2 requires l ≥ 2, i.e. 5d, 5f, 5g → 3 orbitals.\nKey idea: Count l values ≥ |mₗ| up to n − 1.","traps":["5g subshell forgotten: Allowed by rules even if not occupied."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A certain orbital has n = 4 and mₗ = −3. The number of radial nodes in this orbital is ________. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (taking l = 0, n − 1), 1 (using |mₗ| wrongly), 3 angular nodes confused with radial.\nWorking: |mₗ| = 3 requires l ≥ 3; with n = 4, l = 3 (4f). Radial nodes = n − l − 1 = 0.\nKey idea: l ≥ |mₗ| and l ≤ n − 1 fixes l = 3.","traps":["Radial vs angular nodes swapped: Radial = n − l − 1."],"topics":["Nodes","Quantum numbers"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"A certain orbital has no angular nodes and two radial nodes. The orbital is:","options":["2s","3s","3p","2p"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2s has one radial node; (C) 3p has one radial and one angular; (D) 2p has one angular node, no radial.\nWorking: no angular nodes → l = 0 (s); radial nodes n − l − 1 = 2 → n = 3: 3s.\nKey idea: l = angular nodes; n = radial + l + 1.","traps":["Total vs radial nodes: Angular = 0 forces s orbital."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Bohr's theory accounts for the stability and line spectrum of Li⁺ ion.\nStatement II : Bohr's theory was unable to explain the splitting of spectral lines in the presence of a magnetic field.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both statement I and statement II are true.","Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Both statement I and statement II are false.","Statement I is true but statement II is false."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chosen by students who skim Li⁺ as a hydrogen-like ion; (C)/(D) by those unsure about the Zeeman limitation.\nWorking: Li⁺ has two electrons, so Bohr's one-electron model does not apply (it works for Li²⁺); Bohr could not explain the Zeeman effect – Statement II is true.\nKey idea: Hydrogen-like = one electron: Li²⁺, not Li⁺.","traps":["Li⁺ read as hydrogen-like: Count electrons: Li⁺ has 2."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"A 50 watt bulb emits monochromatic red light of wavelength of 795 nm. The number of photons emitted per second by the bulb is x × 10²⁰. The value of x is ______. [Given: h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (using 400 nm habit or doubling); 20 (power-of-ten slip).\nWorking: E = hc/λ = 1.989 × 10⁻²⁵/7.95 × 10⁻⁷ = 2.50 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; N = 50/2.50 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.0 × 10²⁰ s⁻¹; x = 2.\nKey idea: E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/7.95 × 10⁻⁷ = 2.50 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; N = 50/2.50 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.0 × 10²⁰.","traps":["nm not converted: 795 nm = 7.95 × 10⁻⁷ m.","Energy per photon inverted: Photons per second = power ÷ energy per photon."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of orbitals associated with quantum numbers n = 5, mₛ = +1/2 is :","options":["11","25","15","50"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 50: counts all electrons (2n²). (A)/(C): counts orbitals in a single subshell or sum of l values.\nWorking: n = 5 shell has n² = 25 orbitals; each can hold one electron with mₛ = +1/2, so 25. (Strictly mₛ does not label orbitals; the intended reading is number of electrons/orbitals with mₛ = +1/2.)\nKey idea: Electrons with one fixed mₛ in shell n = n².","traps":["2n² used instead of n²: Fixing mₛ halves 2n² to n².","mₛ applied to orbitals: Read as electrons with mₛ = +½, one per orbital."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"For the Balmer series in the spectrum of H atom, ν̄ = R_H {1/n₁² − 1/n₂²}, the correct statements among (I) to (IV) are : (I) As wavelength decreases, the lines in the series converge (II) The integer n₁ is equal to 2 (III) The lines of longest wavelength corresponds to n₂ = 3 (IV) The ionization energy of hydrogen can be calculated from wave number of these lines","options":["(II), (III), (IV)","(I), (II), (III)","(I), (III), (IV)","(I), (II), (IV)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C),(D) include (IV), which needs the Lyman series limit.\nWorking: (I) lines crowd together towards the series limit (shorter λ) – true; (II) n₁ = 2 – true; (III) longest λ is 3→2 – true; (IV) Balmer lines end at n = 2, so they give the energy to ionise from n = 2, not the ground-state IE – false.\nKey idea: IE of H (from n = 1) comes from the Lyman series limit.","traps":["Any series gives IE: IE needs transitions ending at n = 1."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The radius of the second Bohr orbit, in terms of the Bohr radius, a₀, in Li²⁺ is :","options":["4a₀/9","2a₀/9","2a₀/3","4a₀/3"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4a₀/9 uses Z² (energy-type dependence); (B) 2a₀/9 uses n/Z²; (C) 2a₀/3 uses n instead of n².\nWorking: rₙ = a₀n²/Z = a₀ × 4/3 for n = 2, Z = 3.\nKey idea: r ∝ n²/Z (while E ∝ Z²/n²).","traps":["Z² used instead of Z: Radius ∝ n²/Z; energy ∝ Z²/n²."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron in the 4th Bohr orbit is:","options":["8πa₀","2πa₀","4πa₀","6πa₀"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2πa₀ takes r = a₀ (n = 1 value); (C) 4πa₀ uses r = 4a₀ (r ∝ n instead of n²) or divides by n twice; (D) 6πa₀ is an arithmetic slip.\nWorking: 2πrₙ = nλ with r₄ = 16a₀ → λ = 2π(16a₀)/4 = 8πa₀.\nKey idea: rₙ = n²a₀, so λₙ = 2πna₀.","traps":["Radius not scaled by n²: rₙ = n²a₀."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Planck's quantum theory","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The figure that is not a direct manifestation of the quantum nature of atoms is: (1) graph of internal energy of Ar rising linearly with temperature (300–600 K); (2) kinetic energy of photoelectrons vs frequency of incident radiation: three parallel straight lines for Rb, K, Na; (3) an absorption spectrum strip with discrete lines (increasing wavelength →); (4) intensity of black-body radiation vs wavelength at T₁ and T₂ (> T₁), the T₂ curve higher with its peak shifted to shorter wavelength.","options":["Internal energy of Ar vs temperature (linear)","Kinetic energy of photoelectrons vs frequency for Rb, K, Na","Absorption spectrum (line spectrum)","Intensity of black-body radiation vs wavelength at T₁ and T₂ > T₁"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are the three NCERT examples of quantum behaviour; a student unsure of the classical Ar graph may doubt the black-body graph instead.\nWorking: U of a monatomic ideal gas (3/2 RT) rising linearly with T is classical; the photoelectric, line-spectrum and black-body graphs all need quantisation.\nKey idea: Linear U–T for a monatomic gas follows from classical kinetic theory, not from quantisation.","traps":["Graph unfamiliar so assumed quantum: List the three NCERT quantum phenomena and eliminate them.","NOT-type wording: Mark each graph quantum/classical before choosing."],"topics":["Planck's quantum theory","Photoelectric effect","Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantum numbers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The number of subshells associated with n = 4 and m = –2 quantum numbers is :","options":["4","8","16","2"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4 counts all subshells of n = 4; (B) 8 / (C) 16 count electrons or orbitals of n = 4.\nWorking: for n = 4, l = 0–3; m = –2 needs l ≥ 2, i.e. 4d and 4f → 2 subshells.\nKey idea: m = –2 exists only when l ≥ 2.","traps":["Subshell vs orbital vs electron: One mₗ value per subshell."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The work function of sodium metal is 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. If the photons of wavelength 300 nm are incident on the metal, the kinetic energy of the ejected electrons will be ________ × 10⁻²¹ J. (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js; c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s)","type":"num","answer":"222","range":[222.0,222.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2.22 or 22.2 (scaling slip); 663 (photon energy without subtracting W₀).\nWorking: E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸)/(3 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.63 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; KE = 6.63 – 4.41 = 2.22 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 222 × 10⁻²¹ J.\nKey idea: KE = hc/λ – W₀, then rescale.","traps":["Scaling to 10⁻²¹: Convert 2.22 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 222 × 10⁻²¹."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The region in the electromagnetic spectrum where the Balmer series lines appear is","options":["Visible","Microwave","Ultraviolet","Infrared"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Lyman; (D) Paschen/Brackett/Pfund; (B) not a hydrogen series region.\nWorking: Balmer lines (n → 2) lie in the visible region (656–365 nm).\nKey idea: Balmer = visible.","traps":["Lyman/Balmer swapped: Lyman UV, Balmer visible, rest IR."],"topics":["Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","Electromagnetic radiation"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The shortest wavelength of H atom in the Lyman series is λ₁. The longest wavelength in the Balmer series of He⁺ is :","options":["5λ₁/9","27λ₁/5","9λ₁/5","36λ₁/5"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 36λ₁/5 – forgets Z² = 4; (A) inverted ratio; (B) wrong transition.\nWorking: 1/λ₁ = R (∞→1, H). He⁺ Balmer longest (3→2): 1/λ = 4R(1/4 − 1/9) = 5R/9 → λ = 9/(5R) = 9λ₁/5.\nKey idea: Shortest λ = series limit; longest λ = first line; multiply 1/λ by Z².","traps":["Forgetting Z²: For hydrogen-like ions 1/λ = RZ²(…).","Shortest vs longest line: Longest wavelength = smallest energy gap (n=3→2)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The difference between the radii of 3rd and 4th orbits of Li²⁺ is ΔR₁. The difference between the radii of 3rd and 4th orbits of He⁺ is ΔR₂. Ratio ΔR₁ : ΔR₂ is :","options":["8 : 3","3 : 2","3 : 8","2 : 3"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 3:2 uses r ∝ Z (inverted); (A)/(C) mix squares of Z or n incorrectly.\nWorking: rₙ = 52.9 n²/Z pm, so ΔR = 52.9(16 – 9)/Z; ratio = Z(He)/Z(Li) = 2/3.\nKey idea: r ∝ n²/Z; same n-gap cancels.","traps":["Ratio inverted: r ∝ 1/Z: larger Z, smaller orbit."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"In the sixth period, the orbitals that are filled are","options":["6s, 5f, 6d, 6p","6s, 6p, 6d, 6f","6s, 5d, 5f, 6p","6s, 4f, 5d, 6p"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5f/6d belong to period 7; (B) 6d/6f never in period 6; (C) 5f belongs to period 7.\nWorking: by (n + l) rule period 6 fills 6s, 4f, 5d, 6p (32 elements).\nKey idea: Period 6: 6s → 4f → 5d → 6p.","traps":["Period number used for all subshells: f and d lag by 2 and 1 shells."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nodes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct statement about probability density (except at infinite distance from nucleus) is:","options":["It can be negative for 2p orbital","It can be zero for 3p orbital","It can be zero for 1s orbital","It can never be zero for 2s orbital"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ψ can be negative but ψ² cannot; (C) 1s has no node except at infinity; (D) 2s has one radial node, so ψ² is zero there.\nWorking: ψ² is never negative; 3p has n−l−1 = 1 radial node (and an angular nodal plane) where ψ² = 0; 1s has no node at finite r; 2s has one radial node so ψ² is zero there.\nKey idea: ψ² ≥ 0 always; it is zero exactly at nodes, which exist for every orbital except 1s.","traps":["ψ and ψ² mixed up: ψ² is a square – never negative.","2s node forgotten: Radial nodes = n – l – 1 = 1 for 2s."],"topics":["Nodes","Shapes of orbitals"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The quantum numbers of four electrons are given below – I. n = 4, l = 2, m_l = –2, m_s = –½; II. n = 3, l = 2, m_l = 1, m_s = +½; III. n = 4, l = 1, m_l = 0, m_s = +½; IV. n = 3, l = 1, m_l = 1, m_s = –½. The correct order of their increasing energies will be –","options":["IV < III < II < I","IV < II < III < I","I < II < III < IV","I < III < II < IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts 4p below 3d (ignores tie rule); (C)/(D) reverse order.\nWorking: I = 4d (n+l = 6), II = 3d (5), III = 4p (5), IV = 3p (4); equal n+l → lower n first: 3p < 3d < 4p < 4d.\nKey idea: Equal n + l: smaller n is lower.","traps":["4p placed below 3d: Apply 'lower n first' for equal n + l."],"topics":["Aufbau principle","Quantum numbers"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"If p is the momentum of the fastest electron ejected from a metal surface after the irradiation of light having wavelength λ, then for 1.5 p momentum of the photoelectron, the wavelength of the light should be: (Assume kinetic energy of ejected photoelectron to be very high in comparison to work function)","options":["(1/2)λ","(3/4)λ","(2/3)λ","(4/9)λ"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) (2/3)λ assumes λ ∝ 1/p (de Broglie thinking); (A)/(B) arbitrary scalings.\nWorking: neglecting W₀, hc/λ = p²/2m, so λ ∝ 1/p²; λ′ = λ/(1.5)² = 4λ/9.\nKey idea: E_photon ≈ KE = p²/2m ⇒ λ ∝ 1/p².","traps":["de Broglie relation applied to the photon: Photon energy hc/λ equals electron KE p²/2m."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For any given series of spectral lines of atomic hydrogen, let Δν̄ = ν̄_max − ν̄_min be the difference in maximum and minimum frequencies in cm⁻¹. The ratio Δν̄_Lyman / Δν̄_Balmer is :","options":["27 : 5","4 : 1","5 : 4","9 : 4"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 27 : 5 = ratio of first-line wavenumbers (3/4 ÷ 5/36); (B) 4 : 1 = ratio of series limits; (C) 5 : 4 from a slip in Balmer minimum.\nWorking: Lyman: ν̄_max = R, ν̄_min = 3R/4 ⇒ Δ = R/4. Balmer: ν̄_max = R/4, ν̄_min = 5R/36 ⇒ Δ = R/9. Ratio = 9 : 4.\nKey idea: Max = series limit (n₂ = ∞); min = first line (n₂ = n₁ + 1).","traps":["Ratio of first lines or limits used: Compute both ends of each series.","Minimum frequency taken as n₂ = ∞: Series limit is the maximum wavenumber."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Shapes of orbitals","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which one of the following about an electron occupying the 1s orbital in a hydrogen atom is incorrect? (The Bohr radius is represented by a₀)","options":["The electron can be found at a distance 2a₀ from the nucleus","The probability density of finding the electron is maximum at the nucleus.","The magnitude of potential energy is double that of its kinetic energy on an average.","The total energy of the electron is maximum when it is at a distance a₀ from the nucleus."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks false if Bohr orbit is fixed at a₀; (B) looks false if radial probability (max at a₀) is confused with density; (C) virial theorem unfamiliar to some.\nWorking: total energy of a stationary state is fixed (–13.6 eV), it does not vary with distance, so (D) is incorrect; (A) true (ψ non-zero at 2a₀), (B) true (|ψ|² max at r = 0), (C) true by virial theorem |PE| = 2KE.\nKey idea: Energy belongs to the state, not to a position; a₀ is where radial probability, not energy, peaks.","traps":["Radial probability vs probability density: |ψ|² peaks at r = 0; 4πr²|ψ|² peaks at a₀.","Bohr orbit fixed at a₀: QM electron can be at any r."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Shapes of orbitals","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The graph between |ψ|² and r (radial distance) is shown below. This represents : [Plot: |ψ|² is maximum at r = 0, falls to zero at one r value, rises to a small second maximum, then decays to zero]","options":["3s orbital","1s orbital","2p orbital","2s orbital"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1s has no node; (A) 3s has two nodes; (C) 2p has |ψ|² = 0 at r = 0.\nWorking: non-zero |ψ|² at r = 0 ⇒ s orbital; one radial node (one zero crossing) ⇒ n – l – 1 = 1 ⇒ n = 2 ⇒ 2s.\nKey idea: One node + non-zero at r = 0 ⇒ 2s.","traps":["Probability density confused with radial probability: |ψ|² (not 4πr²ψ²) is maximum at nucleus for s.","Node count off by one: Count zeros of the curve: radial nodes = n – l – 1."],"topics":["Shapes of orbitals","Nodes"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The ratio of the shortest wavelength of two spectral series of hydrogen spectrum is found to be about 9. The spectral series are:","options":["Paschen and Pfund","Lyman and Paschen","Brackett and Pfund","Balmer and Brackett"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 25/9 = 2.8; (C) 25/16; (D) 16/4 = 4; error if longest wavelength used instead of shortest.\nWorking: shortest λ (series limit) = n₁²/R_H; ratio 9 → n₁² ratio 9 → n₁ = 1 and 3: Lyman and Paschen.\nKey idea: Shortest λ ∝ n₁².","traps":["Longest instead of shortest wavelength: Shortest λ corresponds to n₂ = ∞."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Shapes of orbitals","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The graph of ψ against x is shown below. The electrons are more likely to be found :","options":["in the region a and b","in the region a and c","only in the region c","only in the region a"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) counts only positive ψ; (C) only the visually \"lower\" lobe; (A) includes the node b where ψ = 0.\nWorking: probability density ∝ ψ²; ψ² is large at both a (large positive ψ) and c (large negative ψ) and zero at the node b.\nKey idea: Sign of ψ is irrelevant to probability; squaring makes both lobes equal.","traps":["Negative ψ read as low probability: Square ψ before judging likelihood.","Node mistaken for high probability: ψ = 0 at a node ⇒ zero probability."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Apr12S1C25-p.webp"],"topics":["Shapes of orbitals","Nodes"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aufbau principle","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Among the following, the energy of 2s orbital is lowest in :","options":["K","Na","Li","H"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) H: picking the highest instead of the lowest, or thinking a valence 2s lies lower; (B)/(C) intermediate positions in the NCERT sequence.\nWorking: NCERT states energies of orbitals in the same subshell decrease with increasing atomic number: E2s(H) > E2s(Li) > E2s(Na) > E2s(K), so 2s is lowest in K.\nKey idea: Greater Z pulls the same orbital lower: 2s lowest in K.","traps":["Highest and lowest reversed: Higher Z means lower (more negative) orbital energy.","Energy linked to valence shell, not nucleus: Same n,l orbital: energy falls with nuclear charge."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"For emission line of atomic hydrogen from nᵢ = 8 to n_f = n the plot of wave number (ν̄) against (1/n²) will be (The Rydberg constant, R_H is in wave number unit).","options":["Linear with slope −R_H","Linear with intercept −R_H","Non linear","Linear with slope R_H"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): wrong sign of slope. (B): intercept confused with R_H. (C): thinks 1/n² dependence is curved.\nWorking: ν̄ = R_H(1/n² − 1/8²) = R_H(1/n²) − R_H/64, a straight line of slope +R_H and intercept −R_H/64.\nKey idea: With nᵢ fixed, ν̄ is linear in 1/n_f² with slope R_H.","traps":["Sign of slope reversed: ν̄ = R_H(1/n² − 1/64): coefficient of 1/n² is +R_H.","Intercept taken as −R_H: Intercept is −R_H/64."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Which of the graphs shown below does not represent the relationship between incident light and the electron ejected from metal surface ?","options":["Number of e⁻ vs frequency of light: zero up to a threshold, then a constant horizontal line","K.E. of e⁻ vs intensity of light: horizontal line (constant)","K.E. of e⁻ vs frequency of light: straight line through the origin","K.E. of e⁻ vs energy of light: straight line starting from a positive threshold on the x-axis"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) correct: no emission below ν₀, then number fixed by intensity. (B) correct: KE independent of intensity. (D) correct: threshold then linear rise.\nWorking: KE = hν − hν₀, so KE vs ν is a straight line with a non-zero x-intercept at ν₀, not through the origin; the other three plots are correct.\nKey idea: Any KE–frequency line must cut the x-axis at ν₀ > 0.","traps":["Linear means correct: Check the intercept: KE = 0 at ν = ν₀, not at ν = 0."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bohr model","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is −13.6 eV. The energy of second excited state of He⁺ ion in eV is :","options":["−6.04","−27.2","−54.4","−3.4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) −27.2: divides by n instead of n² for n = 2 (−13.6 × 4/2), i.e. misreads the state. (C) −54.4: ground state of He⁺ (n = 1). (D) −3.4: hydrogen at n = 2 (forgets Z²).\nWorking: second excited state n = 3; E = −13.6 × Z²/n² = −13.6 × 4/9 = −6.04 eV.\nKey idea: Second excited state means n = 3.","traps":["Excited state numbering: Ground n=1, first excited n=2, second excited n=3."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic spectra & hydrogen spectrum","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Heat treatment of muscular pain involves radiation of wavelength of about 900 nm. Which spectral line of H-atom is suitable for this purpose?\n[R_H = 1 × 10⁵ cm⁻¹, h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹]","options":["Paschen, 5 → 3","Paschen, ∞ → 3","Lyman, ∞ → 1","Balmer, ∞ → 2"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Paschen 5→3 gives ~1400 nm with the given R_H. (C) Lyman limit 91 nm (UV). (D) Balmer limit 365 nm. h and c are not needed.\nWorking: 1/λ = 1/(9 × 10⁻⁵ cm) = 1.11 × 10⁴ cm⁻¹ = R_H/9 → n₁ = 3, n₂ = ∞ (Paschen series limit).\nKey idea: 1/λ = R/9 exactly → series limit of Paschen.","traps":["Unit conversion nm→cm: 900 nm = 9 × 10⁻⁵ cm.","Using h and c: Wavenumber route needs only R."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The de Broglie wavelength (λ) associated with a photoelectron varies with the frequency (ν) of the incident radiation as, [ν₀ is threshold frequency] :","options":["λ ∝ 1/(ν – ν₀)^(3/2)","λ ∝ 1/(ν – ν₀)^(1/2)","λ ∝ 1/(ν – ν₀)^(1/4)","λ ∝ 1/(ν – ν₀)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): treats λ ∝ 1/KE (confuses p with KE). (A)/(C): wrong power from algebra slips.\nWorking: KE = h(ν – ν₀); p = √(2m·KE); λ = h/p ∝ (ν – ν₀)^(–1/2).\nKey idea: λ ∝ 1/√KE for a particle.","traps":["λ taken ∝ 1/KE: For matter: λ = h/√(2mKE)."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Photoelectric effect"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Photoelectric effect","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"What is the work function of the metal if the light of wavelength 4000 Å generates photoelectrons of velocity 6 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ from it ? (Mass of electron = 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg; Velocity of light = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹; Planck's constant = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s; Charge of electron = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J eV⁻¹)","options":["0.9 eV","4.0 eV","2.1 eV","3.1 eV"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 3.1 eV: photon energy reported as W₀. (A) 0.9 / (B) 4.0: KE subtracted wrongly or added.\nWorking: E = hc/λ = 4.97 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 3.1 eV; KE = ½mv² = 1.62 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 1.0 eV; W₀ = 2.1 eV.\nKey idea: W₀ = hc/λ − ½mv².","traps":["Photon energy taken as work function: Subtract ½mv².","Å/eV conversion slip: Convert units before computing."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"de Broglie wavelength","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"If the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in nth Bohr orbit in a hydrogenic atom is equal to 1.5πa₀ (a₀ is Bohr radius), then the value of n/z is :","options":["1.0","0.75","0.40","1.50"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 1.50: forgets the factor 2 in 2πr. (A) 1.0 or (C) 0.40: uses r ∝ n/Z or λ ∝ n²/Z.\nWorking: nλ = 2πr with r = a₀n²/Z → λ = 2πa₀n/Z = 1.5πa₀ → n/Z = 0.75.\nKey idea: λₙ = 2πrₙ/n = 2πa₀ n/Z.","traps":["Dropping 2 in 2πr: Circumference = nλ."],"topics":["de Broglie wavelength","Bohr model"]}],"mx-env":[{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The F⁻ ions make the enamel on teeth much harder by converting hydroxyapatite (the enamel on the surface of teeth) into much harder fluoroapatite having the formula.","options":["[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂)·CaF₂]","[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₂)·Ca(OH)₂]","[3(Ca₃(PO₄)₃)·CaF₂]","[3(Ca₂(PO₄)₂)·Ca(OH)₂]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is hydroxyapatite (the starting enamel). (C) and (D) alter the phosphate formula subscripts.\nWorking: fluoroapatite is 3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·CaF₂; (B) is hydroxyapatite.\nKey idea: Replace Ca(OH)₂ in hydroxyapatite by CaF₂; keep Ca₃(PO₄)₂ intact.","traps":["Hydroxyapatite vs fluoroapatite: Fluoro- means F replaces OH.","Wrong calcium phosphate formula: Calcium phosphate is Ca₃(PO₄)₂."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"The possibility of photochemical smog formation is more at :","options":["Industrial areas","Himalayan villages in winter","The places with healthy vegetation","Marshy lands"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) cold winter suggests classical smog/fog, not photochemical. (D) marshy lands suggest methane. (C) vegetation is a distractor.\nWorking: photochemical smog needs NOₓ and hydrocarbons (from vehicles/industry) plus sunlight – found in urban/industrial areas.\nKey idea: NOₓ + hydrocarbons + sunlight → industrial/urban.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: Photochemical smog is oxidising, needs sunlight and NOₓ."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Soil pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"The group of chemicals used as pesticide is :","options":["Dieldrin, Sodium arsinite, Tetrachloroethene","Aldrin, Sodium chlorate, Sodium arsinite","DDT, Aldrin","Sodium chlorate, DDT, PAN"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) dieldrin is a pesticide but sodium arsenite (herbicide) and tetrachloroethene (solvent) are not; (B) aldrin with two herbicides; (D) DDT with herbicide and PAN.\nWorking: DDT and aldrin are organochlorine insecticides (pesticides). Sodium chlorate and sodium arsenite are herbicides, tetrachloroethene is a dry-cleaning solvent and PAN is a smog component.\nKey idea: Every member of the chosen group must be a pesticide; only (C) is clean.","traps":["Herbicides counted as pesticides: Sodium chlorate and sodium arsenite are weed killers."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Match List I with List II:\nLIST I (Species): A. F⁻; B. SO₄²⁻; C. NO₃⁻; D. Zn\nLIST II (Maximum allowed concentration in ppm in drinking water): I. < 50 ppm; II. < 5 ppm; III. < 2 ppm; IV. < 500 ppm\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C): permutations from guessing that the most toxic-sounding ion has the lowest limit (e.g. sulphate low, fluoride high).\nWorking: old NCERT water-quality standards – fluoride above 2 ppm causes mottling (III), sulphate above 500 ppm laxative (IV), nitrate max 50 ppm (I), zinc 5 ppm (II).\nKey idea: F⁻ < 2, Zn < 5, NO₃⁻ < 50, SO₄²⁻ < 500 ppm.","traps":["Toxicity guessed from familiarity: Learn the four numbers: F 2, Zn 5, NO₃ 50, SO₄ 500."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"Which of these reactions is not a part of breakdown of ozone in stratosphere?","options":["CF₂Cl₂(g) —uv→ Cl•(g) + •CF₂Cl(g)","Cl•(g) + O₃(g) → ClO•(g) + O₂(g)","2ClO• → ClO₂(g) + Cl•(g)","ClO•(g) + O(g) → Cl•(g) + O₂(g)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are the three NCERT steps; a student who half-remembers may reject (D) because it uses atomic O.\nWorking: the NCERT chain is CF₂Cl₂ photolysis, Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂, and ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂; the ClO dimerisation to ClO₂ + Cl is not part of it.\nKey idea: The chain regenerates Cl• via ClO• + O; no ClO₂ is formed.","traps":["Atomic oxygen step rejected: O atoms form by O₃/O₂ photolysis in the stratosphere and regenerate Cl•."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"The radical which mainly causes ozone depletion in the presence of UV radiations is:","options":["NO•","CH₃•","Cl•","•OH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): NO also destroys ozone (supersonic jets) – tempting as a real but secondary cause. (B)/(D): radicals present in the atmosphere but not the main ozone-depleting agents.\nWorking: UV photolysis of CFCs releases Cl• which catalytically destroys O₃ (Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂).\nKey idea: CFC + UV → Cl•, a catalytic ozone destroyer.","traps":["NO chosen as main culprit: NCERT story of ozone hole centres on CFC-derived Cl•."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"How can photochemical smog be controlled?","options":["By using catalytic convertors in the automobiles/industry.","By complete combustion of fuel.","By using tall chimneys.","By using catalyst."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) complete combustion still gives NOₓ. (C) tall chimneys only disperse pollutants. (D) \"catalyst\" is vague; the specific device is the catalytic converter.\nWorking: photochemical smog forms from NOₓ and hydrocarbons in sunlight; catalytic converters in automobiles convert these precursors into harmless products (N₂, CO₂, H₂O), so (A) is the effective control measure.\nKey idea: Catalytic converters remove NOₓ and hydrocarbons, the precursors of photochemical smog.","traps":["Generic \"catalyst\" option: Choose the specific NCERT measure."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"The industrial activity held least responsible for global warming is :","options":["manufacturing of cement","Electricity generation in thermal power plants","steel manufacturing","Industrial production of urea"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) cement may be thought minor, but CaCO₃ calcination plus fuel releases much CO₂. (B) obvious emitter. (C) coke reduction of iron ore releases CO₂.\nWorking: cement, thermal power and steel release large amounts of CO₂; urea synthesis (2NH₃ + CO₂ → NH₂CONH₂ + H₂O) actually consumes CO₂, so it is the least responsible.\nKey idea: Urea plants take in CO₂ (from the ammonia plant) rather than emit it as the main product.","traps":["Cement underestimated as emitter: Calcination CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ is a big CO₂ source."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Correct statement is :","options":["An average human being consumes more food than air","An average human being consumes 100 times more air than food","An average human being consumes nearly 15 times more air than food","An average human being consumes equal amount of food and air"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 100 times: overestimate. (A)/(D): intuitive guesses.\nWorking: old NCERT notes that an average human being consumes nearly 12–15 times more air than food (by mass), so (C).\nKey idea: Recall the 12–15 times figure.","traps":["Number recall: Remember 12–15."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Some reactions of NO₂ relevant to photochemical smog formation are: NO₂ —sunlight→ X + Y; X —A→ B. Identify A, B, X and Y :","options":["X = [O], Y = NO, A = O₂, B = O₃","X = N₂O, Y = [O], A = O₃, B = NO","X = ½O₂, Y = NO₂, A = O₃, B = O₂","X = NO, Y = [O], A = O₂, B = N₂O₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) has the right photolysis products but a wrong follow-up (N₂O₃). (B) invents N₂O. (C) has NO₂ regenerate itself.\nWorking: NO₂ + sunlight → NO + [O]; the atomic oxygen then combines with O₂ to form O₃. So X = [O], Y = NO, A = O₂, B = O₃ = (A).\nKey idea: NO₂ + hν → NO + O; O + O₂ → O₃ (X is the species that goes on to react).","traps":["X and Y labels swapped: The species that reacts with O₂ to give O₃ must be atomic O, so X = [O]."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"A. Ammonium salts produce haze in atmosphere. B. Ozone gets produced when atmospheric oxygen reacts with chlorine radicals. C. Polychlorinated biphenyls act as cleansing solvents. D. 'Blue baby' syndrome occurs due to the presence of excess of sulphate ions in water. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B and C only","A and D only","A, B and C only","A and C only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) accept the ozone statement; (B) accepts sulphate for blue baby.\nWorking: A true – ammonium salts are particulates that produce haze; C true – PCBs are used as cleansing solvents (old NCERT); B false – Cl radicals destroy ozone; D false – blue baby syndrome is due to excess nitrate. So A and C only.\nKey idea: Cl· destroys O₃; blue baby = nitrate (methaemoglobinaemia).","traps":["Sulphate vs nitrate: Blue baby syndrome = nitrate.","Cl radicals and ozone: Cl· catalyses ozone destruction, not formation."],"topics":["Air pollution","Water pollution"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Correct statement about smog is :","options":["NO₂ is present in classical smog","Both NO₂ and SO₂ are present in classical smog","Photochemical smog has high concentration of oxidizing agents","Classical smog also has high concentration of oxidizing agents"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) put NO₂ in classical smog; (D) gives classical smog oxidising nature.\nWorking: classical smog (smoke, fog, SO₂) is reducing; photochemical smog contains O₃, NO₂, PAN and aldehydes, i.e. a high concentration of oxidising agents → (C).\nKey idea: Classical = reducing smog; photochemical = oxidising smog.","traps":["Which smog is oxidising: Classical: SO₂ + soot + fog (reducing)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"The concentration of dissolved Oxygen in water for growth of fish should be more than X ppm and Biochemical Oxygen Demand in clean water should be less than Y ppm. X and Y in ppm are, respectively.","options":["X = 4, Y = 15","X = 4, Y = 8","X = 6, Y = 12","X = 6, Y = 5"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) use 4 ppm (often mixed with cold-water DO 10 ppm); (C) 12 ppm BOD is polluted range; (A) 15 close to polluted 17 ppm.\nWorking: fish growth is inhibited if dissolved oxygen falls below 6 ppm; clean water has BOD below 5 ppm → X = 6, Y = 5.\nKey idea: 6 ppm DO, 5 ppm BOD.","traps":["Swapping DO and BOD limits: DO must be high (>6); BOD must be low (<5)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"Formation of photochemical smog involves the following reaction in which A, B and C are respectively. i. NO₂ —[hν]→ A + B; ii. B + O₂ → C; iii. A + C → NO₂ + O₂. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["NO, O & O₃","N, O₂ & O₃","O, N₂O & NO","O, NO & NO₃⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B: N atom from NO₂ is not formed. C and D: order wrong or nonsensical species (N₂O, NO₃⁻).\nWorking: NO₂ → NO + O (A = NO, B = O); O + O₂ → O₃ (C = O₃); NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂ → (A).\nKey idea: NO₂ → NO + O; O + O₂ → O₃; NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂.","traps":["Letter order: B must react with O₂ to give C (O₃), so B is atomic O."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"The water quality of a pond was analyzed and its BOD was found to be 4. The pond has :","options":["Very clean water","Highly polluted water","Water has high amount of fluoride compounds","Slightly polluted water"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) slightly polluted: 4 seems non-zero so 'some pollution'; (B) highly polluted: confusing BOD with DO scale; (C) unrelated fluoride.\nWorking: clean water has BOD below 5 ppm, so a BOD of 4 ppm means very clean water → (A).\nKey idea: BOD < 5 ppm ⇒ clean water.","traps":["BOD threshold 5 ppm: Recall: <5 ppm clean, ≥17 ppm highly polluted."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Identify X, Y and Z in the following reaction. (Equation not balanced) ClO• + NO₂ → X —[H₂O]→ Y + Z","options":["X = ClONO₂, Y = HOCl, Z = NO₂","X = ClNO₃, Y = Cl₂, Z = NO₂","X = ClONO₂, Y = HOCl, Z = HNO₃","X = ClNO₂, Y = HCl, Z = HNO₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) right X and Y but Z = NO₂ is not a hydrolysis product. (B) Cl₂ is not formed in hydrolysis. (D) ClNO₂ has one O too few and HCl is from a different reaction (with ClONO₂ + HCl → Cl₂ + HNO₃).\nWorking: ClO• + NO₂ → ClONO₂ (X); ClONO₂ + H₂O → HOCl (Y) + HNO₃ (Z) → (C).\nKey idea: Hydrolysis of ClONO₂ gives HOCl + HNO₃.","traps":["Hydrolysis vs reaction with HCl: With water: HOCl + HNO₃; with HCl: Cl₂ + HNO₃."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"The normal rain water is slightly acidic and its pH value is 5.6 because of which one of the following?","options":["N₂O₅ + H₂O → 2HNO₃","2SO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 2H₂SO₄","4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃","CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) produce strong acids responsible for acid rain (pH < 5.6), not normal rain.\nWorking: CO₂ dissolves in rain water to form the weak acid H₂CO₃ (CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃), which gives normal rain a pH of about 5.6 → (D).\nKey idea: Normal rain is acidic only from dissolved CO₂; SOₓ/NOₓ cause acid rain.","traps":["Normal rain vs acid rain: pH 5.6 is the CO₂-equilibrium value."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The photochemical smog does not generally contain:","options":["NO","NO₂","SO₂","HCHO"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"NO, NO₂ and HCHO are all present in photochemical smog; a student unsure of HCHO may pick (D).\nWorking: photochemical smog contains NO, NO₂, O₃, PAN, HCHO, acrolein; SO₂ is a component of classical (reducing) smog.\nKey idea: SO₂ → classical smog; NOₓ/hydrocarbons/O₃ → photochemical.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: Classical: SO₂ + smoke + fog (reducing); photochemical: NOₓ, O₃, PAN, aldehydes (oxidising)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List I with List II. List-I: A. Sulphate, B. Fluoride, C. Nicotine, D. Sodium arsinite. List-II: I. Pesticide, II. Bending of bones, III. Laxative effect, IV. Herbicide. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps nicotine and sodium arsinite (pesticide vs herbicide); (A)/(B) misplace sulphate and fluoride.\nWorking: excess sulphate in water has a laxative effect; excess fluoride causes hardening/bending of bones; nicotine was an early pesticide; sodium arsinite is a herbicide.\nKey idea: Nicotine kills insects (pesticide); arsenite kills weeds (herbicide).","traps":["Pesticide vs herbicide swap: Nicotine = insecticide (plant alkaloid); arsenite = weed killer."],"topics":["Water pollution","Soil pollution"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Pollutant): A. Microorganisms; B. Plant nutrients; C. Toxic heavy metals; D. Sediment\nList II (Source): I. Strip mining; II. Domestic sewage; III. Chemical fertilizer; IV. Chemical factory\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the sediment and nutrient sources; (C)/(D) attach microorganisms to strip mining.\nWorking: microorganisms – domestic sewage; plant nutrients – chemical fertilisers; toxic heavy metals – chemical factories; sediments – strip mining/erosion (old NCERT Table 14.1).\nKey idea: Each pollutant has an obvious source; sediment ↔ mining/erosion.","traps":["Sediment vs nutrients: Fertiliser → nutrients; mining/erosion → sediment."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Pollutant): A. Sulphate (>500 ppm); B. Nitrate (>50 ppm); C. Lead (>50 ppb); D. Fluoride (>2 ppm)\nList II (Disease/sickness): I. Methemoglobinemia; II. Brown mottling of teeth; III. Laxative effect; IV. Kidney damage\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): sulphate and fluoride effects swapped; (C)/(D): nitrate linked to kidney instead of blue-baby syndrome.\nWorking: excess sulphate → laxative effect; nitrate → methemoglobinemia (blue baby); lead → kidney (and nervous) damage; fluoride > 2 ppm → brown mottling of teeth: A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.\nKey idea: Nitrate → blood (methemoglobin); fluoride → teeth; lead → kidney; sulphate → gut.","traps":["Sulphate vs lead effects: Sulphate is a laxative; lead harms kidney.","Nitrate vs lead: Nitrate causes methemoglobinemia."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Soil pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: The non bio-degradable fly ash and slag from steel industry can be used by cement industry. Statement II: The fuel obtained from plastic waste is lead free. Choose the most appropriate answer.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) reject one or both true facts, usually doubting the 'lead free' claim.\nWorking: old NCERT (waste management) says fly ash and steel slag are utilised by the cement industry, and that plastic waste can be converted into high-octane fuel that contains no lead. Both correct.\nKey idea: Both are textbook lines from waste-management in old Environmental Chemistry.","traps":["Doubting \"lead free\" fuel: Recall the old NCERT line: plastic-derived fuel contains no lead."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: In polluted water values of both dissolved oxygen and BOD are very low. Statement II: Eutrophication results in decrease in the amount of dissolved oxygen. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts Statement I because DO is indeed low; (C) reverses both.\nWorking: polluted water has low dissolved oxygen but HIGH BOD (much organic matter to oxidise), so I is false; eutrophication (algal bloom and decay) depletes dissolved oxygen, so II is true.\nKey idea: Low DO and high BOD go together in polluted water.","traps":["BOD direction: BOD = oxygen demanded by microbes; more organic waste → higher BOD."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Soil pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which among the following pairs has only herbicides ?","options":["Aldrin and Dieldrin","Sodium chlorate and Aldrin","Sodium arsinate and Dieldrin","Sodium chlorate and sodium arsinite."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) both insecticides; (B) and (C) mix one herbicide-like name with an insecticide ('sodium arsinate' in (C) is also a near-spelling of arsinite).\nWorking: sodium chlorate (NaClO₃) and sodium arsinite (Na₃AsO₃) are the herbicides named in NCERT; aldrin and dieldrin are organochlorine insecticides.\nKey idea: Herbicides: NaClO₃, Na₃AsO₃; insecticides: aldrin, dieldrin, DDT.","traps":["Arsinate vs arsinite: Read the anion name carefully; NCERT says arsinite."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Dinitrogen is a robust compound, but reacts at high altitude to form oxides. The oxide of nitrogen that can damage plant leaves and retard photosynthesis is:","options":["NO","NO₃⁻","NO₂","NO₂⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NO is the primary oxide formed but the damage is attributed to NO₂; (B)/(D) are ions, not oxides.\nWorking: higher concentrations of NO₂ damage leaves of plants and retard photosynthesis (old NCERT environmental chemistry).\nKey idea: NO₂ is the damaging oxide.","traps":["NO vs NO₂: The NCERT line names NO₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which one of the following elemental forms is not present in the enamel of the teeth?","options":["Ca²⁺","P³⁺","F⁻","P⁵⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) P⁵⁺ looks odd as an \"ion\" but represents phosphate phosphorus; (C) F⁻ may be thought absent if fluorapatite is forgotten.\nWorking: enamel is hydroxyapatite Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂, converted by fluoride to fluorapatite Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆F₂. It contains Ca²⁺, F⁻ and phosphorus in the +5 state (phosphate); P(+3) is absent.\nKey idea: Phosphate PO₄³⁻ has P in +5; nothing in apatite has P in +3.","traps":["\"P⁵⁺\" read as a free ion: Read P⁵⁺/P³⁺ as oxidation states; phosphate = +5.","Fluoride forgotten: NCERT: F⁻ converts enamel to fluorapatite."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Some gases are responsible for heating of atmosphere (green house effect). Identify from the following the gaseous species which does not cause it.","options":["CH₄","O₃","H₂O","N₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) O₃ – known for UV shielding, so students think it is not a greenhouse gas; (C) H₂O – not seen as a pollutant, yet it is the largest natural greenhouse gas; (A) CH₄ – well known, rarely chosen.\nWorking: CH₄, O₃ and water vapour absorb infrared radiation and are greenhouse gases; N₂ is a homonuclear diatomic with no dipole change on vibration and does not absorb IR.\nKey idea: IR-active molecules (polar bonds that change dipole on vibration) trap heat; N₂ and O₂ do not.","traps":["Ozone thought to be only a UV shield: Tropospheric O₃ also absorbs IR and is a greenhouse gas.","Water vapour not counted: Water vapour is the most abundant natural greenhouse gas."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The eutrophication of water body results in:","options":["loss of Biodiversity","breakdown of organic matter","increase in biodiversity","decrease in BOD."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – more algae read as more biodiversity; (D) – BOD actually rises; (B) – a process, not the result asked.\nWorking: nutrient enrichment (phosphates, nitrates) causes algal blooms that use up dissolved oxygen; fish and other aquatic life die, so biodiversity falls and BOD rises.\nKey idea: Eutrophication → oxygen depletion → loss of aquatic life.","traps":["Algal growth read as higher biodiversity: One species blooms; others die from O₂ depletion.","BOD direction: Dissolved O₂ falls, BOD rises."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R\nAssertion A: Polluted water may have a value of BOD of the order of 17 ppm.\nReason R: BOD is a measure of oxygen required to oxidise both the biodegradable and non-biodegradable organic material in water.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) accept the altered definition; (D) doubts the 17 ppm figure.\nWorking: clean water has BOD < 5 ppm, highly polluted water ≥ 17 ppm, so A is correct; BOD measures oxygen used by bacteria to break down biodegradable organic matter only (non-biodegradable matter is covered by COD), so R is false.\nKey idea: BOD = biological oxidation of biodegradable organic matter; COD includes non-biodegradable matter.","traps":["BOD vs COD: Bacteria cannot oxidise non-biodegradable matter; that is COD.","BOD threshold values: Clean < 5 ppm; highly polluted ≥ 17 ppm."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Polar stratospheric clouds facilitate the formation of :","options":["ClONO₂","HOCl","ClO","CH₄"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ClONO₂ – forms in the gas phase (summer), it is the reactant on PSCs; (C) ClO – from Cl + O₃, not PSC-specific; (D) CH₄ – unrelated sink molecule.\nWorking: on the surfaces of polar stratospheric clouds, chlorine nitrate is hydrolysed to HOCl (and reacts with HCl to give Cl₂); so the clouds facilitate HOCl formation = (B). ClONO₂ forms in the gas phase from ClO + NO₂.\nKey idea: PSCs: ClONO₂ + H₂O → HOCl + HNO₃; ClONO₂ + HCl → Cl₂ + HNO₃.","traps":["Reactant chosen as product: Ask what happens on the cloud surface.","General ozone-depletion species: PSC role is hydrolysis to HOCl/Cl₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The measured BOD values for four different water samples (A–D) are as follows: A = 3 ppm; B = 18 ppm; C = 21 ppm; D = 4 ppm. The water samples which can be called as highly polluted with organic wastes, are","options":["A and B","A and D","B and C","B and D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) A and D – the clean samples (reversed scale); (A), (D) mix one clean and one polluted sample.\nWorking: clean water has BOD < 5 ppm; highly polluted water has BOD ≥ 17 ppm, so B (18) and C (21).\nKey idea: High BOD = high organic load; ≥ 17 ppm is highly polluted.","traps":["Lower BOD read as polluted: BOD is oxygen demanded by microbes; large BOD means much organic waste."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Statement I: Classical smog occurs in cool humid climate. It is a reducing mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide. Statement II: Photochemical smog has components ozone, nitric oxide, acrolein, formaldehyde, PAN etc.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(B) – think classical smog is oxidising or that NO is not a photochemical smog component; (D) – reverses climate (thinks classical smog needs warm sunny weather).\nWorking: old NCERT – classical smog occurs in cool humid climate and is a mixture of smoke, fog and SO₂, chemically reducing; photochemical smog contains O₃, NO, acrolein, formaldehyde and PAN → both correct, (A).\nKey idea: Classical = cool, humid, SO₂, reducing; photochemical = warm, sunny, NOₓ/O₃/PAN, oxidising.","traps":["Reducing vs oxidising smog: Classical smog has SO₂ (reducing); photochemical has O₃/PAN (oxidising)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"On the surface of polar stratospheric clouds, hydrolysis of chlorine nitrate gives A and B while its reaction with HCl produces B and C. A, B and C are, respectively","options":["HOCl, HNO₃, Cl₂","Cl₂, HNO₃, HOCl","HClO₂, HNO₂, HOCl","HOCl, HNO₂, Cl₂O"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps A and C; (C) and (D) use wrong oxidation levels (HClO₂, HNO₂, Cl₂O).\nWorking: ClONO₂ + H₂O → HOCl + HNO₃; ClONO₂ + HCl → Cl₂ + HNO₃. Common product B = HNO₃; A = HOCl; C = Cl₂.\nKey idea: ClONO₂ splits as Cl⁺ + NO₃⁻: water gives HOCl, HCl gives Cl₂; nitrate becomes HNO₃ both times.","traps":["A and C swapped: H₂O supplies OH to Cl → HOCl; HCl supplies Cl → Cl₂.","Nitrate vs nitrite: Chlorine nitrate contains NO₃, so the acid is HNO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Soil pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which amongst the following is not a pesticide?","options":["DDT","Organophosphates","Dieldrin","Sodium arsenite"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) organophosphates may be thought of as nerve agents rather than pesticides; (C) dieldrin less familiar than DDT.\nWorking: in the old NCERT environmental chemistry text, DDT, aldrin, dieldrin and organophosphates are listed as pesticides (insecticides), while sodium chlorate and sodium arsenite are listed as herbicides.\nKey idea: NCERT groups sodium arsenite with herbicides, not pesticides.","traps":["Herbicide counted as pesticide: Follow NCERT classification: insecticide list vs herbicide list."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Correct statement about photochemical smog is:","options":["It occurs in humid climate.","It is a mixture of smoke, fog and SO₂","It is reducing smog.","It results from reaction of unsaturated hydrocarbons."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are true of classical (London) smog, not photochemical smog.\nWorking: photochemical smog forms in warm, dry, sunny climates from NOₓ and unsaturated hydrocarbons; it is oxidising. (A), (B), (C) describe classical smog.\nKey idea: Photochemical = sunlight + NOₓ + hydrocarbons → oxidising.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: Photochemical smog is oxidising and dry."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The acid that is believed to be mainly responsible for the damage of Taj Mahal is","options":["Sulfuric acid","Hydrofluoric acid","Phosphoric acid","Hydrochloric acid"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) HF etches glass (silicates) not marble specifically; (C), (D) are not acid-rain acids.\nWorking: SO₂ from Mathura refinery and other sources forms H₂SO₄ in acid rain, which attacks the marble (CaCO₃).\nKey idea: Acid rain is mainly H₂SO₄ (and HNO₃) from SO₂/NOₓ.","traps":["HF and etching: HF etches glass; acid rain is H₂SO₄/HNO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Sulphur dioxide is one of the components of polluted air. SO₂ is also a major contributor to acid rain. The correct and complete reaction to represent acid rain caused by SO₂ is:","options":["2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃","SO₂ + O₃ → SO₃ + O₂","SO₂ + H₂O₂ → H₂SO₄","2SO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 2H₂SO₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) are true intermediate oxidations but produce no acid; (C) gives H₂SO₄ but with H₂O₂, not the atmospheric route asked as \"correct and complete\".\nWorking: acid rain needs SO₂ oxidation by O₂ and dissolution in water to give H₂SO₄; only (D) is complete; (A) and (B) stop at SO₃, (C) uses H₂O₂.\nKey idea: \"Complete\" means the acid must appear, from SO₂ + O₂ + H₂O.","traps":["Intermediate step chosen: The acid must be formed with water.","H₂O₂ route: Choose the textbook complete equation with O₂ and water."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The conversion of hydroxyapatite occurs due to presence of F⁻ ions in water. The correct formula of hydroxyapatite is:","options":["[3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·Ca(OH)₂]","[3Ca(OH)₂·CaF₂]","[Ca₃(PO₄)₂·CaF₂]","[3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·CaF₂]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is fluorapatite (the product); (C) wrong ratio; (B) no phosphate.\nWorking: hydroxyapatite (tooth enamel) is Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂ = [3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·Ca(OH)₂]; F⁻ converts it to fluorapatite [3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·CaF₂].\nKey idea: Hydroxy-apatite has OH; fluor-apatite has F – the 3Ca₃(PO₄)₂ part is common.","traps":["Hydroxy- vs fluoro-apatite: Hydroxyapatite contains Ca(OH)₂; fluorapatite (formed from it by F⁻) contains CaF₂.","Ratio 3:1: Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂ = 3Ca₃(PO₄)₂·Ca(OH)₂."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Photochemical smog causes cracking of rubber.\nReason (R): Presence of ozone, nitric oxide, acrolein, formaldehyde and peroxyacetyl nitrate in photochemical smog makes it oxidizing.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the true explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true.","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the true explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tempting if the student sees the causes and effects as separate facts; (B)/(C) for those unsure that smog cracks rubber or that it is oxidising (classical smog is reducing).\nWorking: photochemical smog is oxidising because of O₃, NO, acrolein, HCHO and PAN; the oxidants (chiefly ozone) attack the C=C bonds of rubber and crack it, so R explains A.\nKey idea: Photochemical smog is oxidising; oxidants like ozone crack rubber – the cause–effect link is direct.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog nature: Photochemical = sunlight + NOx + hydrocarbons → oxidants.","Explanation link: Ask: does the oxidising nature cause the cracking? Yes."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In stratosphere most of the ozone formation is assisted by:","options":["cosmic rays","γ-rays.","ultraviolet radiation","visible radiations"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) high-energy radiations sound plausible but are not the main driver; (D) visible light lacks energy to break O=O.\nWorking: UV radiation splits O₂ into O atoms (O₂ → 2O), which combine with O₂ to give O₃.\nKey idea: O=O photolysis needs UV; the same UV is absorbed by ozone (protective layer).","traps":["Higher energy = more likely: Stratospheric chemistry is driven by solar UV, which is abundant."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"BOD values (in ppm) for clean water (A) and polluted water (B) are expected respectively:","options":["A > 50, B < 27","A > 25, B < 17","A < 5, B > 17","A > 15, B > 47"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B) reverse the sense (clean water with high BOD); (D) both high.\nWorking: clean water has BOD below 5 ppm, highly polluted water 17 ppm or more – option (C).\nKey idea: Low BOD = clean; ≥ 17 ppm = highly polluted.","traps":["High BOD read as clean: BOD measures oxygen used by microbes – high means more organic waste."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The deposition of X and Y on ground surfaces is referred as wet and dry depositions, respectively. X and Y are:","options":["X = Ammonium salts, Y = CO₂","X = SO₂, Y = Ammonium salts","X = Ammonium salts, Y = SO₂","X = CO₂, Y = SO₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the pair (SO₂ intuitively linked to acid rain); (A)/(D) bring in CO₂, which is not the deposited species named.\nWorking: in the old NCERT environmental chemistry text, ammonium salts carried in raindrops give wet deposition, while SO₂ absorbed on solid particles settles as dry deposition.\nKey idea: Wet = dissolved salts in rain (ammonium salts); dry = SO₂ on particulates.","traps":["SO₂ assumed wet (acid rain): Follow the NCERT wording: SO₂ on solid particles = dry deposition.","CO₂ as acid deposition: CO₂ is not the species NCERT names for either deposition."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The gas released during anaerobic degradation of vegetation may lead to:","options":["Ozone hole","Acid rain","Corrosion of metals","Global warming and cancer"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – ozone hole is due to CFCs/NO; (B) – acid rain is due to SO₂/NOₓ; (C) – corrosion links to acidic gases.\nWorking: anaerobic decay of vegetation (paddy fields, marshes) releases methane, a greenhouse gas → global warming. The 'cancer' part is loosely worded (it echoes the old NCERT line linking global warming to diseases), but (D) is the only option involving the greenhouse effect.\nKey idea: Anaerobic decay → CH₄ → greenhouse effect.","traps":["Gas misidentified: No oxygen → CH₄ (marsh gas), not SO₂/NO₂."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: The value of the parameter \"Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD)\" is important for survival of aquatic life. Statement II: The optimum value of BOD is 6.5 ppm. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts 6.5 ppm as BOD; (A) and (D) reject the true Statement I.\nWorking: BOD indicates organic pollution and so matters for aquatic life (I true); 6.5 ppm is close to the dissolved-oxygen value for healthy water, while clean water has BOD below ~5 ppm (II false).\nKey idea: BOD should be low (<5 ppm) in clean water; a fixed \"optimum\" BOD is not a valid idea.","traps":["BOD number swapped with DO: Low BOD = clean; DO is the oxygen present."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: An allotrope of oxygen is an important intermediate in the formation of reducing smog. Statement II: Gases such as oxides of nitrogen and sulphur present in troposphere contribute to the formation of photochemical smog. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) needs ozone in reducing smog; (C) accepts both false statements; (D) accepts SO₂ as a photochemical smog cause.\nWorking: Statement I false – ozone (allotrope of O) is a product/intermediate in photochemical (oxidising) smog, not classical (reducing) smog. Statement II false – photochemical smog arises from NOₓ and hydrocarbons in sunlight; SO₂ contributes to classical smog. Hence both statements are false.\nKey idea: Ozone and NOₓ → photochemical (oxidising) smog; SO₂ + smoke + fog → classical (reducing) smog.","traps":["SO₂ added to photochemical smog: Photochemical smog needs NOₓ + hydrocarbons + sunlight.","Ozone placed in wrong smog: Ozone is an oxidant, so it belongs to oxidising smog."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The pH of rain water is normally ~5.6. Statement II: If the pH of rain water drops below 5.6, it is called acid rain. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(B) – result from thinking normal rain is neutral (pH 7) or that acid rain starts at pH 7.\nWorking: dissolved CO₂ (H₂CO₃) makes normal rain pH ≈ 5.6; rain with pH below 5.6 is called acid rain.\nKey idea: Pure rain is already slightly acidic (pH 5.6) due to CO₂.","traps":["Rain assumed neutral: CO₂ dissolution gives pH 5.6."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The presence of ozone in troposphere","options":["Protects us from the UV radiation","Protects us from the X-ray radiation","Protects us from greenhouse effect","generates photochemical smog"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) UV protection – true only of the stratospheric ozone layer; (B) X-rays – not filtered by ozone; (C) ozone is itself a greenhouse gas, it does not protect from the effect.\nWorking: ozone in the troposphere is a pollutant and a component of photochemical smog; UV protection is by stratospheric ozone.\nKey idea: \"Good up high, bad nearby\": stratospheric ozone shields UV; tropospheric ozone is smog.","traps":["Ozone layer location confused: UV shield is stratospheric; troposphere O₃ is a pollutant.","Ozone thought anti-greenhouse: O₃ is a greenhouse gas."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Green chemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Green chemistry in day-to-day life is in the use of:","options":["Chlorine for bleaching of paper","Large amount of water alone for washing clothes","Tetrachloroethene for laundry","Liquified CO₂ for dry cleaning of clothes"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) are the very practices green chemistry replaces; (B) sounds eco-friendly but is not the NCERT example and wastes water.\nWorking: replacing chlorinated solvents (tetrachloroethene) by liquefied CO₂ with a suitable detergent is the standard green-chemistry example for dry cleaning; chlorine bleaching is replaced by H₂O₂, and using large amounts of water alone is wasteful, not a green practice.\nKey idea: Green dry cleaning = liquefied CO₂ + detergent instead of chlorinated solvents.","traps":["Polluting practice offered as option: Remember which solvent is replaced, not just which is mentioned.","Eco-sounding distractor: Green chemistry here means a substitute reagent, not more water."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which one of the following gases is reported to retard photosynthesis ?","options":["CO","CFCs","CO₂","NO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) CO₂ is a photosynthesis reactant – tempting by association; (A) CO binds haemoglobin; (B) CFCs deplete ozone.\nWorking: high concentrations of NO₂ damage leaves and retard photosynthesis (old NCERT environmental chemistry).\nKey idea: NO₂ → leaf damage and reduced photosynthesis.","traps":["Association with photosynthesis: CO₂ is a reactant; it does not retard the process."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Green chemistry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which one of the following chemical agent is not being used for dry–cleaning of clothes?","options":["H₂O₂","CCl₄","Liquid CO₂","Cl₂C = CCl₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) H₂O₂ looks out of place (not a solvent) and is the commonest pick; (B) CCl₄ is a known older dry-cleaning solvent; (C) liquid CO₂ is the green replacement.\nWorking: the old NCERT green-chemistry box says tetrachloroethene \"was earlier used\" for dry cleaning and is being replaced by liquid CO₂ with a detergent, and H₂O₂ is used for bleaching in laundry, so Cl₂C=CCl₂ is taken as \"not being used\". Outside the NCERT framing the item is ambiguous – tetrachloroethene is still widely used, and H₂O₂ is a laundry bleach rather than a dry-cleaning solvent.\nKey idea: The answer follows NCERT's narrative: Cl₂C=CCl₂ is the agent being phased out; H₂O₂ is listed under laundry bleaching.","traps":["Tense in NCERT box: Recall the NCERT story: tetrachloroethene → liquid CO₂; H₂O₂ for laundry bleaching.","NOT-type stem: Check each against the NCERT box."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Chlorofluoro carbons breakdown by radiation in the visible energy region and release chlorine gas in the atmosphere which then reacts with stratospheric ozone.\nStatement II: Atmospheric ozone reacts with nitric oxide to give nitrogen and oxygen gases, which add to the atmosphere.\nFor the above statements choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true","Both statement I and II are false","Statement I is correct but statement II is false","Both statement I and II are correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) accepts Statement I by missing 'visible'/'chlorine gas'; (A) accepts II if NO + O₃ products are not recalled.\nWorking: CFCs are broken by UV radiation (not visible) into chlorine free radicals (not Cl₂ gas) – Statement I false; NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂, not N₂ + O₂ – Statement II false.\nKey idea: UV + Cl radical; NO + O₃ → NO₂ + O₂.","traps":["UV vs visible: CFC photolysis needs UV.","NO + O₃ products: NO is oxidised to NO₂."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?","options":["Eutrophication indicates that water body is polluted","The dissolved oxygen concentration below 6 ppm inhibits fish growth","Eutrophication leads to increase in the oxygen level in water","Eutrophication leads to anaerobic conditions"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are true facts from the old chapter; a student unsure of the 6 ppm figure might wrongly mark (B).\nWorking: eutrophication (nutrient-driven algal bloom and decay) depletes dissolved oxygen, so (C) is the incorrect statement; DO below 6 ppm inhibits fish growth (true).\nKey idea: Algal bloom decay consumes oxygen; eutrophic water becomes anaerobic.","traps":["Bloom means less, not more, oxygen: Decomposition of dead algae uses O₂.","Pick the false one: Mark T/F against each."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Match List-I (compound) with List-II (effect/affected species):\n(a) Carbon monoxide (b) Sulphur dioxide (c) Polychlorinated biphenyls (d) Oxides of nitrogen\n(i) Carcinogenic (ii) Metabolized by pyrus plants (iii) Haemoglobin (iv) Stiffness of flower buds\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps PCB and NOₓ; (B)/(C) mismatch CO with flower buds or carcinogenic.\nWorking: CO binds haemoglobin (carboxyhaemoglobin); high SO₂ causes stiffness of flower buds; PCBs are carcinogenic; NOₓ then takes (ii) by elimination (some plants such as Pyrus can metabolise NOₓ).\nKey idea: Fix the three sure pairs; the odd item falls out.","traps":["Unfamiliar List-II item: Match the three NCERT facts first, eliminate."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The type of pollution that gets increased during the day time and in the presence of O₃ is:","options":["Reducing smog","Oxidising smog","Global warming","Acid rain"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – classical smog (cool humid, SO₂, reducing); (C)/(D) – other pollution types not tied to daytime O₃.\nWorking: photochemical (oxidising) smog forms in warm sunny conditions from NOₓ and hydrocarbons; O₃ and other oxidants build up during the day.\nKey idea: Sunlight + NOₓ + hydrocarbons → O₃, PAN → oxidising (photochemical) smog.","traps":["Reducing vs oxidising smog: Daytime sunlight and O₃ → photochemical, oxidising."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The green house gas/es is (are): (A) Carbon dioxide (B) Oxygen (C) Water vapour (D) Methane. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) and (C) only","(A) only","(A), (C) and (D) only","(A) and (B) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses methane; (B) only the best-known gas CO₂; (D) includes O₂, which is not IR-active.\nWorking: CO₂, water vapour and CH₄ all absorb outgoing infrared radiation and are greenhouse gases; O₂ (a homonuclear diatomic with no dipole change on vibration) is not.\nKey idea: Greenhouse gases are IR-absorbing gases: CO₂, CH₄, H₂O(g), N₂O, CFCs, O₃ – not O₂ or N₂.","traps":["Water vapour forgotten as greenhouse gas: Recall the full old-NCERT list; water vapour is the largest natural contributor."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Reducing smog is a mixture of:","options":["Smoke, fog and O₃","Smoke, fog and SO₂","Smoke, fog and CH₂=CH–CHO","Smoke, fog and N₂O₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) – O₃ and acrolein are components of photochemical (oxidising) smog; (D) – N₂O₃ is not a component of classical smog (nitrogen oxides belong with photochemical smog).\nWorking: classical (London) smog – smoke, fog and SO₂ – is chemically reducing.\nKey idea: Reducing smog = SO₂ smog; oxidising = photochemical.","traps":["Photochemical components chosen: Reducing ↔ SO₂; oxidising ↔ O₃, PAN, acrolein."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following statement(s) is (are) incorrect reason for eutrophication ?\n(A) excess usage of fertilisers\n(B) excess usage of detergents\n(C) dense plant population in water bodies\n(D) lack of nutrients in water bodies that prevent plant growth\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) only","(C) only","(B) and (D) only","(D) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): a student may reject (C) because dense plants are a consequence; (C): detergents (phosphates) are a genuine cause.\nWorking: eutrophication is caused by excess nutrients (nitrate, phosphate) from fertilisers and detergents, leading to dense plant/algal growth; \"lack of nutrients\" is the opposite, so (D) is the incorrect reason. (C) is really an effect rather than a cause, which makes the item slightly loose, but it is accepted here as part of the process.\nKey idea: Eutrophication = nutrient ENRICHMENT; anything saying \"lack of nutrients\" is wrong.","traps":["Effect vs cause: Pick the statement that contradicts the process outright.","Detergent link forgotten: Detergents add phosphate → nutrient enrichment."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The statements that are TRUE: (A) Methane leads to both global warming and photochemical smog (B) Methane is generated from paddy fields (C) Methane is a stronger global warming gas than CO₂ (D) Methane is a part of reducing smog. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (B), (C) only","(A) and (B) only","(B), (C), (D) only","(A), (B), (D) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) drops C (methane is more potent than CO₂); (C) includes D and drops A; (D) includes D (reducing smog is SO₂-based).\nWorking: methane is a greenhouse gas (per molecule stronger than CO₂), is released from paddy fields, and as a hydrocarbon contributes to photochemical (oxidising) smog; reducing (classical) smog is smoke + fog + SO₂, not methane.\nKey idea: Reducing smog = classical (SO₂, smoke); oxidising smog = photochemical (hydrocarbons + NOₓ + sunlight).","traps":["Reducing vs photochemical smog: Classical smog is reducing (SO₂); hydrocarbons go with photochemical smog.","Methane potency vs CO₂: Per molecule methane traps far more heat than CO₂."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Non-biodegradable wastes are generated by the thermal power plants.\nStatement II : Bio-degradable detergents leads to eutrophication.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the option given below :","options":["Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is true but statement II is false","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) chosen by students who think only non-biodegradable detergents pollute; (A)/(C) by those unaware of fly ash.\nWorking: thermal power plants produce fly ash (non-biodegradable waste); detergents, even biodegradable ones, carry phosphate builders that cause eutrophication. Statement II is loosely worded (it is the phosphate, not biodegradability, that causes it).\nKey idea: Eutrophication comes from phosphates in detergents regardless of biodegradability.","traps":["Biodegradable assumed harmless: Eutrophication is a nutrient (phosphate) effect."],"topics":["Water pollution","Soil pollution"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Water sample is called cleanest on the basis of which one of the BOD values given below?","options":["11 ppm","15 ppm","3 ppm","21 ppm"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 11 ppm and (B) 15 ppm – moderately polluted values; (D) 21 ppm – picked by students who think a larger oxygen number means healthier water.\nWorking: BOD measures the oxygen consumed by microbes decomposing organic matter, so the lowest value, 3 ppm, marks the cleanest sample.\nKey idea: BOD rises with organic load; the smallest value is the cleanest water.","traps":["Higher BOD read as cleaner water: BOD is oxygen demanded by microbes; more organic waste means higher BOD, so the cleanest water has the lowest BOD.","BOD confused with dissolved oxygen: High DO = clean; high BOD = polluted; clean water has BOD below about 5 ppm."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Among the gases (a)–(e), the gases that cause greenhouse effect are : (a) CO₂ (b) H₂O (c) CFCs (d) O₂ (e) O₃","options":["(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a), (c), (d) and (e)","(a) and (d)","(a), (b), (c) and (e)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(C) include O₂, which is not a greenhouse gas.\nWorking: CO₂, water vapour, CFCs and ozone are greenhouse gases; O₂ is not IR-active.\nKey idea: Greenhouse gases absorb IR; homonuclear O₂ does not.","traps":["O₂ included: Only IR-absorbing gases count."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) is the amount of oxygen required (in ppm):","options":["by anaerobic bacteria to breakdown inorganic waste present in a water body.","for the photochemical breakdown of waste present in 1 m³ volume of a water body.","by bacteria to break-down organic waste in a certain volume of a water sample.","for sustaining life in a water body."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps organic→inorganic and adds anaerobic; (B) photochemical breakdown; (D) is dissolved oxygen, not BOD.\nWorking: BOD is the oxygen used by (aerobic) bacteria to decompose organic matter in a fixed volume of water.\nKey idea: BOD = O₂ used by bacteria for organic waste.","traps":["BOD confused with dissolved oxygen: DO sustains life; BOD measures organic load."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The statement that is not true about ozone is:","options":["in the stratosphere, it forms a protective shield against UV radiation.","it is a toxic gas and its reaction with NO gives NO₂.","in the atmosphere, it is depleted by CFCs.","in the stratosphere, CFCs release chlorine free radicals (Cl•) which reacts with O₃ to give chlorine dioxide radicals."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are textbook-true statements; (B) may look wrong to students who forget O₃ oxidises NO to NO₂.\nWorking: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• (chlorine monoxide radical) + O₂, not chlorine dioxide; the other three statements are true.\nKey idea: Cl• + O₃ gives ClO•, a single oxygen on chlorine.","traps":["ClO vs ClO₂ radical: Write Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂ and count oxygens.","NOT-type wording: Mark each T/F before answering."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Thermal power plants can lead to:","options":["Ozone layer depletion","Eutrophication","Acid rain","Blue baby syndrome"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) linked to CFCs; (B) to phosphates/nitrates in water; (D) to nitrate in drinking water.\nWorking: coal-fired plants emit SO₂ and NOₓ which form H₂SO₄/HNO₃ in rain.\nKey idea: SO₂/NOₓ from fuel burning → acid rain.","traps":["Mixing pollutant–effect pairs: Match each effect to its pollutant."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The condition that indicates a polluted environment is","options":["BOD value of 5 ppm","eutrophication","0.03% of CO₂ in the atmosphere","pH of rain water to be 5.6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5 ppm is the clean-water boundary (polluted ≥ 17 ppm); (C) normal atmospheric CO₂; (D) normal rain (acid rain < 5.6).\nWorking: eutrophication (nutrient-enriched water, algal blooms, O₂ depletion) signals pollution; BOD < 5 ppm is clean water, ~0.03–0.04% CO₂ is normal air, pH 5.6 is normal rain.\nKey idea: Every distractor is a normal reference value.","traps":["Normal rain pH taken as acid rain: Acid rain is pH < 5.6.","BOD 5 ppm taken as polluted: Clean water BOD < 5 ppm; polluted ≥ 17 ppm."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The presence of soluble fluoride ion upto 1 ppm concentration in drinking water, is :","options":["harmful to bones","harmful for teeth","safe for teeth","harmful to skin"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (A) describe effects at >2 ppm and >10 ppm respectively; (D) not linked to fluoride.\nWorking: up to 1 ppm F⁻ hardens enamel (fluorapatite) and is safe/beneficial; above ~2 ppm causes mottling of teeth, above ~10 ppm harms bones.\nKey idea: 1 ppm F⁻ is the recommended, safe level.","traps":["Threshold levels mixed up: 1 ppm safe; >2 ppm mottling; >10 ppm bones."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Assertion : Ozone is destroyed by CFCs in the upper stratosphere. Reason : Ozone holes increase the amount of UV radiation reaching the earth.","options":["Assertion and reason are correct, but the reason is not the explanation for the assertion","Assertion is false, but the reason is correct","Assertion and reason are both incorrect","Assertion and reason are both correct and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) treats consequence as explanation; (B)/(C) doubt true facts.\nWorking: both true; the reason is a consequence of ozone depletion, not its cause, so not the explanation.\nKey idea: Reason must explain why the assertion happens, not what follows.","traps":["Effect mistaken for explanation: Ask: does R answer 'why A'?."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Green chemistry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which is wrong with respect to our responsibility as a human being to protect our environment ?","options":["Avoiding the use of floodlighted facilities","Restricting the use of vehicles","Using plastic bags","Setting up compost tin in gardens"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Other three are correct practices.\nWorking: using plastic bags harms the environment; the others are protective actions.\nKey idea: Pick the harmful habit.","traps":["Reading 'wrong' as 'right': Underline 'wrong'."],"topics":["Green chemistry","Soil pollution"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The maximum prescribed concentration of copper in drinking water is:","options":["5 ppm","0.5 ppm","0.05 ppm","3 ppm"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5 ppm is the Zn limit; (C) 0.05 ppm is the Mn limit; (B) 0.5 ppm is far below the Cu limit of 3 ppm.\nWorking: old NCERT table of drinking-water standards gives Cu maximum 3.0 ppm.\nKey idea: Cu: 3.0 ppm.","traps":["Values of neighbouring entries swapped: Memorise Zn 5, Cu 3."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Excessive release of CO₂ into the atmosphere results in :","options":["polar vortex","depletion of ozone","formation of smog","global warming"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) polar vortex is a weather pattern; (B) ozone depletion is caused by CFCs/NO; (C) smog by SO₂/NOx/hydrocarbons.\nWorking: CO₂ is a greenhouse gas; excess leads to global warming.\nKey idea: CO₂ ⇒ greenhouse effect ⇒ global warming.","traps":["Ozone depletion linked to CO₂: Ozone depletion ← CFCs/NOx, not CO₂."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The layer of atmosphere between 10 km to 50 km above the sea level is called as :","options":["troposphere","mesosphere","stratosphere","thermosphere"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) troposphere is below 10 km; (B) mesosphere and (D) thermosphere are higher layers.\nWorking: troposphere is up to ~10 km; the stratosphere lies between ~10 and 50 km.\nKey idea: Ozone layer (stratosphere) = 10–50 km.","traps":["Layer order: Tropo < strato < meso < thermo."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The regions of the atmosphere, where clouds form and where we live, respectively, are :","options":["Stratosphere and Troposphere","Troposphere and Stratosphere","Troposphere and Troposphere","Stratosphere and Stratosphere"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) place one or both in the stratosphere (ozone layer).\nWorking: the troposphere is the lowest layer, holding water vapour/clouds and all life.\nKey idea: Clouds and life: troposphere.","traps":["Expecting two different layers: Both can be the same region."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Air pollution that occurs in sunlight is:","options":["oxidising smog","acid rain","reducing smog","fog"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) reducing (classical) smog is in cool humid climate; (B)/(D) not sunlight-dependent.\nWorking: photochemical smog forms in warm sunny conditions and is oxidising (O₃, NO₂, PAN).\nKey idea: Photochemical = oxidising.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: Sunlight → photochemical → oxidising."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The correct set of species responsible for the photochemical smog is :","options":["NO, NO₂, O₃ and hydrocarbons","N₂, O₂, O₃ and hydrocarbons","N₂, NO₂ and hydrocarbons","CO₂, NO₂, SO₂ and hydrocarbons"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) SO₂ is classical smog; (B)/(C) include inert N₂/O₂.\nWorking: photochemical smog forms from NOₓ and hydrocarbons in sunlight, producing O₃ (and PAN, aldehydes). N₂/O₂ are inert air components; SO₂ belongs to classical (reducing) smog.\nKey idea: Photochemical smog = NOₓ + hydrocarbons + sunlight → O₃, PAN.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: SO₂ + smoke = classical (reducing) smog."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The primary pollutant that leads to photochemical smog is :","options":["sulphur dioxide","acrolein","ozone","nitrogen oxides"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SO₂ belongs to classical (reducing) smog; (B) acrolein and (C) ozone are secondary products of smog.\nWorking: photochemical smog starts from NOₓ and hydrocarbons (primary pollutants); O₃, acrolein, PAN are secondary.\nKey idea: Primary pollutant = emitted directly: NOₓ (with hydrocarbons).","traps":["Secondary product taken as primary: Ozone forms from NO₂ photolysis: it is secondary.","Classical smog mixed with photochemical smog: SO₂ = classical/reducing smog."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"A water sample has ppm level concentration of the following metals: Fe = 0.2; Mn = 5.0; Cu = 3.0; Zn = 5.0. The metal that makes the water sample unsuitable for drinking is :","options":["Zn","Fe","Mn","Cu"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): Zn at 5.0 seems high but equals its limit. (B)/(D): at their limits.\nWorking: drinking-water maximum limits are Fe 0.2, Mn 0.05, Cu 3.0, Zn 5.0 ppm; only Mn exceeds its limit.\nKey idea: Mn limit is 0.05 ppm – 100× lower than the given value.","traps":["Highest number picked: Compare each value with its own limit: Zn 5 ppm allowed, Mn only 0.05 ppm."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following conditions in drinking water causes methemoglobinemia?","options":["> 50 ppm of lead","> 100 ppm of sulphate","> 50 ppm of chloride","> 50 ppm of nitrate"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Lead: kidney/nervous damage (limit 50 ppb). (B) Sulphate >500 ppm: laxative effect. (C) Chloride: taste only.\nWorking: nitrate above 50 ppm in drinking water causes methaemoglobinaemia ('blue baby' syndrome).\nKey idea: Nitrate → blue baby syndrome.","traps":["Pollutant–effect swap: Link nitrate to haemoglobin oxidation (methaemoglobin)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The pH of rain water, is approximately:","options":["6.5","7.5","5.6","7.0"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 7.0: assuming pure water. (A) 6.5: guess near neutral. (B) 7.5: basic.\nWorking: normal rain water has pH ≈ 5.6 from dissolved CO₂ (carbonic acid).\nKey idea: Dissolved CO₂ makes even clean rain slightly acidic (5.6); acid rain < 5.6.","traps":["Rain taken as neutral: CO₂ dissolves forming H₂CO₃: pH ≈ 5.6."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Water filled in two glasses A and B have BOD values of 10 and 20, respectively. The correct statement regarding them, is :","options":["A is more polluted than B","A is suitable for drinking, whereas B is not","B is more polluted than A","Both A and B are suitable for drinking"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the BOD–pollution link. (B)/(D) ignore that 10 ppm is already above the 5 ppm clean-water limit.\nWorking: higher BOD means more organic pollution, so B (20 ppm) is more polluted; clean water has BOD < 5 ppm, so neither is drinkable.\nKey idea: BOD ∝ organic load; < 5 ppm for clean water.","traps":["BOD 10 judged drinkable: Compare with 5 ppm limit, not with each other."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The reaction that is NOT involved in the ozone layer depletion mechanism in the stratosphere is:","options":["HOCl(g) —hν→ •OH(g) + •Cl(g)","CF₂Cl₂(g) —UV→ •Cl(g) + •CF₂Cl(g)","CH₄ + 2O₃ → 3CH₂=O + 3H₂O","ClO•(g) + O(g) → •Cl(g) + O₂(g)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HOCl photolysis: from the polar stratospheric cloud mechanism. (B) Freon photolysis: initiation. (D) ClO + O: regenerates Cl•. All three are in NCERT's scheme.\nWorking: (A), (B), (D) are steps of the chlorine radical cycle (photolysis of freons/HOCl, regeneration of Cl•); methane oxidation to formaldehyde by O₃ is not part of it (and the printed equation is not even balanced).\nKey idea: Ozone depletion is a Cl•/ClO• chain started by UV; methane oxidation is not part of it.","traps":["HOCl photolysis less familiar: Recall: HOCl and Cl₂ from PSCs photolyse to give Cl•."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The concentration of dissolved oxygen (DO) in cold water can go upto:","options":["10 ppm","14 ppm","16 ppm","8 ppm"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 8 ppm – near the typical DO of fresh water; (B) 14 ppm – saturation at 0 °C from other sources; (C) 16 ppm – no basis.\nWorking: old NCERT Environmental Chemistry states cold water can hold DO up to about 10 ppm (clean water ~5–6 ppm needed for aquatic life).\nKey idea: NCERT figure: DO up to 10 ppm in cold water; below 6 ppm harms fish.","traps":["Mixing up DO thresholds: Remember the NCERT number: 10 ppm max in cold water."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN), an eye irritant, is produced by:","options":["Acid rain","Photochemical smog","Classical smog","Organic waste"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) classical (reducing) smog has SO₂ and smoke, not PAN.\nWorking: PAN forms in photochemical smog from hydrocarbons, NOₓ and O₃ in sunlight.\nKey idea: PAN is an oxidising, photochemical product.","traps":["Classical vs photochemical smog: Classical = reducing (SO₂); photochemical = oxidising (O₃, PAN)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Taj Mahal is being slowly disfigured and discoloured. This is primarily due to :-","options":["Water pollution","Global warming","Soil pollution","Acid rain"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Water pollution and (C) soil pollution do not reach the monument surface. (B) Global warming raises temperature; does not dissolve marble.\nWorking: SO₂/NOₓ from nearby industries (Mathura refinery) form acid rain; H₂SO₄/HNO₃ attack CaCO₃ of marble (marble cancer).\nKey idea: Marble = CaCO₃; acids from SO₂/NO₂ dissolve it.","traps":["Global warming confused with acid rain: Ask which one chemically attacks CaCO₃."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The higher concentration of which gas in air can cause stiffness of flower buds ?","options":["SO₂","NO₂","CO₂","CO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NO₂: damages leaves, retards photosynthesis (old NCERT). (C) CO₂: greenhouse gas. (D) CO: binds haemoglobin – human toxicity.\nWorking: old NCERT states that a high concentration of SO₂ leads to stiffness of flower buds, which eventually fall off.\nKey idea: SO₂ → flower bud stiffness; NO₂ → leaf damage/retarded photosynthesis.","traps":["SO₂ vs NO₂ plant effects mixed: Memorise pairs: SO₂–flower buds; NO₂–leaves."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The molecule that has minimum/no role in the formation of photochemical smog, is :","options":["CH₂ = O","N₂","O₃","NO"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HCHO is a smog product. (C) O₃ is central. (D) NO starts the cycle.\nWorking: photochemical smog involves NO/NO₂, O₃, hydrocarbons and products like HCHO, PAN; inert N₂ plays no direct role.\nKey idea: N₂ is unreactive; NO/NO₂ (not N₂) drive smog.","traps":["NO and N₂ confused: Only oxides of N take part."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Water pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Water samples with BOD values of 4 ppm and 18 ppm, respectively, are :","options":["Highly polluted and Clean","Highly polluted and Highly polluted","Clean and Highly polluted","Clean and Clean"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) order reversed. (B)/(D): one value misclassified.\nWorking: clean water BOD < 5 ppm; highly polluted ≥ 17 ppm.\nKey idea: BOD < 5 clean, ≥ 17 highly polluted.","traps":["Order of the pair reversed: Match values in order."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The compound that is NOT a common component of photochemical smog is :","options":["O₃","CH₂=CHCHO","CF₂Cl₂","H₃C–CO–OONO₂ (PAN)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) O₃, (B) acrolein, (D) PAN are all in the NCERT smog list; students who associate O₃ only with the protective layer may pick (A).\nWorking: ozone, acrolein and peroxyacetyl nitrate are photochemical-smog oxidants/irritants; CF₂Cl₂ (freon) is a stratospheric ozone-depleting CFC, not a smog component.\nKey idea: Smog = NOx + hydrocarbons + sunlight → O₃, PAN, acrolein, HCHO; CFCs act in the stratosphere.","traps":["Ozone thought to be only protective: Tropospheric O₃ is a smog oxidant."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Air pollution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The upper stratosphere consisting of the ozone layer protects us from the sun's radiation that falls in the wavelength region of :","options":["600–750 nm","0.8–1.5 nm","400–550 nm","200–315 nm"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) red visible light, (C) blue–green visible light, (D) correct UV; (B) 0.8–1.5 nm is X-ray region – tempts students who equate 'harmful' with 'very short wavelength'.\nWorking: ozone absorbs harmful UV (UV-B/UV-C), about 200–315 nm; the other ranges are visible light or X-rays.\nKey idea: Ozone absorbs UV, i.e. roughly 200–315 nm.","traps":["Harmful radiation taken as X-ray range: UV is 10–400 nm; ozone works in the 200–315 nm part."]}],"m09-period":[{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The correct order of first ionization enthalpy values of the following elements is :\n(A) O (B) N (C) Be (D) F (E) B\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A < B < D < C < E","C < E < A < B < D","E < C < A < B < D","B < D < C < E < A"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): Be < B (misses s vs p penetration). (A)/(D): random/reversed.\nWorking: B (801) < Be (899) < O (1314) < N (1402) < F (1681) kJ mol⁻¹ → E < C < A < B < D.\nKey idea: B < Be and O < N; F highest.","traps":["Be vs B anomaly missed: 2p electron of B is easier to remove than 2s of Be.","Letters vs elements confusion: Rewrite order in symbols first, then map letters."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Valence & oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Number of elements from the following that CANNOT form compounds with valencies which match with their respective group valencies is ______.\nB, C, N, S, O, F, P, Al, Si","options":["7","3","5","6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 6 or (A) 7: treat 'group valency' as 8 − group number style and over-count. (C) 5: counts B and C wrongly.\nWorking: group valency (= number of valence electrons: 3 for group 13 up to 7 for group 17) is shown by B (3, BF₃), C (4, CH₄), S (6, SF₆), P (5, PCl₅), Al (3, AlCl₃), Si (4, SiCl₄); N cannot show 5 (no d orbitals), O cannot show 6, F cannot show 7 → 3.\nKey idea: Second-period N, O, F cannot expand octet to reach group valency 5, 6, 7.","traps":["Meaning of group valency: Group valency here = highest valency (number of valence electrons).","Boron/carbon wrongly excluded: B (valency 3) and C (valency 4) need only 4 orbitals – no conflict."],"topics":["Valence & oxidation states","Anomalous second-period behaviour"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct order of the first ionization enthalpy is","options":["Ga > Al > B","Tl > Ga > Al","Al > Ga > Tl","B > Al > Ga"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) B > Al > Ga follows the simple down-the-group decrease. (C) Al > Ga > Tl is the full reverse of the anomaly. (A) Ga > Al > B gets Ga > Al right but forgets B is highest.\nWorking: ΔiH₁ (kJ mol⁻¹): B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589, so Tl > Ga > Al; Ga > Al > B is wrong (B highest); B > Al > Ga is wrong (Ga slightly > Al).\nKey idea: Group 13 IE: B ≫ Tl > Ga > Al > In.","traps":["Simple downward decrease assumed: Ga and Tl are raised by poor d/f shielding."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The correct order of first ionization enthalpy values of Li, Na, F and Cl is Na < Li < Cl < F.\nStatement II: The correct order of negative electron gain enthalpy values of Li, Na, F and Cl is Na < Li < F < Cl.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chosen by students who expect F to have the most negative ΔegH. (C) chosen if Na < Li in IE is doubted. (B) combines both slips.\nWorking: IE: Na (496) < Li (520) < Cl (1256) < F (1681) – true. Negative ΔegH: Na (53) < Li (60) < F (328) < Cl (349) – true.\nKey idea: Cl, not F, has the most negative ΔegH (small F crowds incoming electron).","traps":["F assumed most negative ΔegH: Small 2p shell repels incoming electron; Cl > F.","Na/Li electron gain order: Values close: Li −60, Na −53."],"topics":["Electron gain enthalpy","Ionisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The metallic radius of Na is 1.86 Å and the ionic radius of Na⁺ is lesser than 1.86 Å\nStatement II: Ions are always smaller in size than the corresponding elements.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): accepts 'always smaller'. (B): rejects Statement I by confusing metallic and ionic radius. (C): marks Statement I false, e.g. by thinking an ion of Na is larger.\nWorking: Na⁺ 95 pm < 186 pm, so Statement I true; Statement II false because anions are larger than their parent atoms → (D).\nKey idea: Cations shrink, anions expand relative to the atom.","traps":["'Always' overgeneralisation: Anions are larger than parent atoms."],"topics":["Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","Atomic radius"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The electron affinity values are negative for\nA. Be → Be⁻\nB. N → N⁻\nC. O → O²⁻\nD. Na → Na⁻\nE. Al → Al⁻\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["A, B, D and E only","D and E only","A and D only","A, B and C only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C): student treats 'negative EA' as 'negative ΔegH' (exothermic) and picks Na, Al; or misses Be/N exceptions.\nWorking: electron affinity negative ⇔ electron gain enthalpy positive (energy absorbed): Be (filled 2s²), N (half-filled 2p³) and O → O²⁻ (second electron added to anion, overall endothermic). Na and Al release energy on gaining an electron (positive EA).\nKey idea: EA = −ΔegH; negative EA means energy must be supplied: filled/half-filled subshells and adding to an anion.","traps":["EA sign taken same as ΔegH: EA = −ΔegH; negative EA = endothermic.","Be and N assumed to gain electrons exothermically: Filled 2s and half-filled 2p resist extra electron."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Elements): A. Cl, S B. Ge, As C. Fr, Ra D. F, O\nLIST II (Properties in their respective groups): I. Elements with highest electronegativity II. Elements with largest atomic size III. Elements which show properties of both metals and non-metal IV. Elements with highest negative electron gain enthalpy\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) put F,O against highest negative Δ_egH or misplace metalloids; tempting because F is \"most reactive\".\nWorking: Cl and S have the most negative Δ_egH in their groups (F and O are less negative due to small size); Ge, As are metalloids; Fr, Ra are largest; F, O most electronegative.\nKey idea: Highest EN = F, O but most negative Δ_egH = Cl, S.","traps":["F/O given highest negative Δ_egH: Small 2p shell repels incoming electron; Cl, S win."],"topics":["Electron gain enthalpy","Electronegativity","Atomic radius"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Identify the correct statements about p-block elements and their compounds.\n(A) Non metals have higher electronegativity than metals.\n(B) Non metals have lower ionisation enthalpy than metals.\n(C) Compounds formed between highly reactive nonmetals and highly reactive metals are generally ionic.\n(D) The non-metal oxides are generally basic in nature.\n(E) The metal oxides are generally acidic or neutral in nature.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(B) and (D) only","(B) and (E) only","(D) and (E) only","(A) and (C) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) all include at least one reversed statement (B, D or E) – each tempting only through careless reading.\nWorking: A and C true; non-metals have higher IE (B false); non-metal oxides are acidic (D false); metal oxides are basic (E false).\nKey idea: Non-metals: high EN, high IE, acidic oxides; metals the opposite.","traps":["Oxide acidity reversed: Metal oxides basic, non-metal oxides acidic."],"topics":["s, p, d, f blocks","Nature of oxides","Electronegativity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Valence & oxidation states","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement (I): The oxidation state of an element in a particular compound is the charge acquired by its atom on the basis of electron gain enthalpy consideration from other atoms in the molecule.\nStatement (II): pπ–pπ bond formation is more prevalent in second period elements over other periods.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts Statement I because the sentence reads almost verbatim. (A)/(D) reject Statement II through confusion with dπ–pπ bonding of heavier elements.\nWorking: oxidation state is defined on electronegativity, not electron gain enthalpy, so I is false; second-period elements form pπ–pπ multiple bonds more readily, so II is true.\nKey idea: Electronegativity (not electron gain enthalpy) decides which atom gets the negative oxidation state.","traps":["Electronegativity vs electron gain enthalpy: Recall the NCERT wording: electronegative consideration."],"topics":["Valence & oxidation states","Anomalous second-period behaviour"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"In case of isoelectronic species the size of F⁻, Ne and Na⁺ is affected by :","options":["Nuclear charge (z)","None of the factors because their size is the same","Electron-electron interaction in the outer orbitals","Principal quantum number (n)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): assumes equal electrons means equal size. (C): electron–electron repulsion is the same for all three. (D): n is the same (n = 2) for all.\nWorking: Same number of electrons and same n; only Z differs (9, 10, 11), so size falls F⁻ > Ne > Na⁺.\nKey idea: Isoelectronic → only Z differs → higher Z, smaller size.","traps":["Equal electrons taken as equal size: Different Z pulls the same electron cloud differently."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Both metals and non-metals exist in p and d-block elements.\nStatement (II) : Non-metals have higher ionisation enthalpy and higher electronegativity than the metals.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) misses that d-block has no non-metals. (D) rejects II, perhaps thinking of exceptions like noble gases or Hg. (A) rejects both.\nWorking: all d-block elements are metals; only the p-block contains both metals and non-metals, so I is false. Non-metals generally have higher IE and EN than metals, so II is true.\nKey idea: d-block = transition metals only.","traps":["d-block slipped in: All d-block elements are metals."],"topics":["s, p, d, f blocks","Ionisation enthalpy","Electronegativity"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : The 4f and 5f - series of elements are placed separately in the Periodic table to preserve the principle of classification.\nStatement (II) : S-block elements can be found in pure form in nature.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): reject a near-verbatim NCERT line. (B): accepts that reactive alkali/alkaline-earth metals occur native.\nWorking: I is the NCERT reason for placing lanthanoids/actinoids separately; s-block metals are too reactive to occur free in nature.\nKey idea: Highly electropositive s-block metals exist only as compounds in nature.","traps":["s-block metals assumed to occur native: Very reactive metals (Na, K, Ca) are never found free."],"topics":["s, p, d, f blocks","Modern periodic table"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :\nAssertion A : The first ionisation enthalpy decreases across a period.\nReason R : The increasing nuclear charge outweighs the shielding across the period.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below :","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) accept the reversed assertion; (D) treats the correct reason as false.\nWorking: across a period first IE generally increases (A false) because increasing nuclear charge outweighs the poor shielding by same-shell electrons (R true).\nKey idea: Rising Z_eff across a period raises IE; the reason actually explains the opposite of the assertion.","traps":["'decreases' read as 'increases': Read the direction word in the assertion before judging the reason.","Correct reason assumed to explain A: A false assertion cannot be explained; first judge A alone."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Fluorine has most negative electron gain enthalpy in its group.\nStatement II : Oxygen has least negative electron gain enthalpy in its group.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) believes F is most negative (simple trend). (B) thinks O is not the least because Po should be lowest down the group. (D) both errors together.\nWorking: Cl (−349) is more negative than F (−328), so I is false; in group 16 O is −141 vs S −200, Se −195, Te −190, Po −174, so O is least negative — II true.\nKey idea: The second-period anomaly makes O and F less negative than all heavier members (Po −174 still beats O −141).","traps":["F assumed most negative: ΔegH: Cl > F because of 2p electron repulsion.","Po expected least negative: Check table: Po −174 is still more negative than O −141."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The element having the highest first ionization enthalpy is","options":["C","Al","Si","N"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C: lower Z in period 2. (B) Al: lowest, period 3 group 13. (C) Si: period 3, below C.\nWorking: N (1402 kJ/mol) > C (1086) > Si (786) > Al (577); period-2, rightmost, half-filled 2p.\nKey idea: Up and right in the table gives highest IE; N wins.","traps":["Period-3 elements overrated: Down a group IE falls because of size and shielding."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous second-period behaviour","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Anomalous behavior of oxygen is due to its","options":["large size and low electronegativity","small size and high electronegativity","small size and low electronegativity","large size and high electronegativity"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) each flip one or both of size/electronegativity.\nWorking: second-period members (here O) differ from heavier congeners because of small size, high electronegativity and no d orbitals.\nKey idea: First member of a group: small and highly electronegative.","traps":["One property flipped: Both properties are extreme for O: small size, high EN."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Naming elements with Z > 100","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"If IUPAC name of an element is \"Ununnunium\" then the element belongs to nᵗʰ group of Periodic table. The value of n is _______.","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 111 (reporting Z), 1 (reading only \"un\"), 7 (period), 12 (miscount).\nWorking: the name \"Ununnunium\" in the question is a misprint; the intended name is Unununium (un-un-un = 111, roentgenium, Rg): [Rn]5f¹⁴6d⁹7s² → group 11 (below Au).\nKey idea: 111 = Cu, Ag, Au, Rg group → 11.","traps":["Z reported instead of group: Place Z = 111 in the 7th period d-block: group 11."],"topics":["Naming elements with Z > 100","s, p, d, f blocks"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement - I: Along the period, the chemical reactivity of the elements gradually increases from group 1 to group 18 .\nStatement - II: The nature of oxides formed by group 1 elements is basic while that of group 17 elements is acidic.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is False but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are False","Statement I is True But Statement II is False","Both Statement I and Statement II are True"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) accept a steady rise in reactivity. (B) rejects II by thinking group 17 oxides are amphoteric.\nWorking: reactivity is highest at the two extremes of a period (alkali metals and halogens) and least in the centre, with noble gases nearly inert, so I is false; oxides on the extreme left are basic (Na₂O) and on the right acidic (Cl₂O₇), so II is true.\nKey idea: Reactivity is high at both ends of a period, minimum in the middle.","traps":["Monotonic trend assumed: Reactivity peaks at both ends; noble gases are unreactive."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The correct sequence of electron gain enthalpy of the elements listed below is\nA. Ar\nB. Br\nC. F\nD. S\nChoose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["A > D > C > B","A > D > B > C","D > C > B > A","C > B > D > A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): order by magnitude (most negative first). (A): F placed less negative than Br (overcorrecting the F anomaly, which applies to Cl). (C): Ar placed last (sign ignored).\nWorking: algebraic ΔegH (kJ/mol): Ar +96, S −200, Br −325, F −328 → A > D > B > C.\nKey idea: Order signed values: positive Ar highest; F slightly more negative than Br.","traps":["Magnitude vs algebraic order: Use signed values unless 'magnitude' is said.","F vs Br: Table: F −328 vs Br −325."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic radius","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Consider the following elements, placed in the periodic table as shown:\nPeriod →\nA′  B′\nC′  D′\n(Group ↓: C′ is directly below A′ and D′ is directly below B′)\nWhich of the following is/are true about A′, B′, C′ and D′?\nA. Order of atomic radii: B′ < A′ < D′ < C′\nB. Order of metallic character: B′ < A′ < D′ < C′\nC. Size of the element: D′ < C′ < B′ < A′\nD. Order of ionic radii: B′⁺ < A′⁺ < D′⁺ < C′⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A only","B, C and D only","A and B only","A, B and D only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses that metallic character and ionic radii follow size. (B) includes C (reverse of A). (C) misses D.\nWorking: radius and metallic character decrease across a period and increase down a group, so B′ smallest, C′ largest; D (ionic radii) follows the same trend; C is the reverse.\nKey idea: Size, metallic character and cation size all rise down and leftwards.","traps":["Reading statement C as true: Compare each statement with the A order.","Ionic radius trend doubted: Same charge isoelectronic logic: larger atom → larger cation."],"topics":["Atomic radius","Ionic radius & isoelectronic species"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The difference between electron gain enthalpies will be maximum between :","options":["Ar and Cl","Ne and Cl","Ne and F","Ar and F"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Ne and F assumes F has the most negative value. (D) Ar and F gets both exceptions wrong. (A) Ar and Cl assumes Ar is more positive than Ne.\nWorking: Δ_egH (kJ mol⁻¹): Cl −349, F −328, Ne +116, Ar +96. Ne–Cl difference = 465 is the largest.\nKey idea: Pick the most negative halogen (Cl) and the most positive noble gas (Ne).","traps":["F assumed most negative: Recall Cl −349 > F −328 due to small-size repulsion in F.","Ar assumed more positive than Ne: Ne +116 > Ar +96 (smaller atom, compact shell)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electronegativity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct order of electronegativity for given elements is:","options":["C > P > At > Br","Br > C > At > P","Br > P > At > C","P > Br > C > At"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student puts C highest, forgetting halogens. (C): student puts P above At and C, treating P as more non-metallic. (D): student ranks P first – no basis.\nWorking: Pauling values Br 2.8, C 2.5, At 2.2, P 2.1 → Br > C > At > P.\nKey idea: At (2.2) is still slightly more electronegative than P (2.1); C (2.5) lies below Br (2.8).","traps":["At vs P close values: Memorise Table 3.8 values for heavy halogens.","C vs Br order: Halogen Br (2.8) beats C (2.5)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Modern periodic table","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Henry Moseley studied characteristic X-ray spectra of elements. The graph which represents his observation correctly is. Given ν = frequency of X-ray emitted, Z = atomic number.","options":["Straight line through origin: ν² (y-axis) vs Z (x-axis)","Straight line through origin: √ν (y-axis) vs Z (x-axis)","Straight line through origin: ν (y-axis) vs Z (x-axis)","Straight line through origin: √Z (y-axis) vs ν (x-axis)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses ν² and (C) uses ν (both curve in reality); (D) puts √Z against ν, inverting which quantity is square-rooted.\nWorking: Moseley found √ν proportional to Z (√ν = a(Z – b)), so √ν vs Z is linear; the drawn line in (B) starts near the origin, which ignores the screening constant b, but it is the only correct choice.\nKey idea: Square root goes on the frequency, not on Z.","traps":["Square root placed on Z: Moseley: √ν = a(Z − b).","Linear ν vs Z chosen: ν ∝ (Z − b)², so only √ν is linear."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Chlorine can easily combine with oxygen to form oxides; and the product has a tendency to explode.\nStatement II : Chemical reactivity of an element can be determined by its reaction with oxygen and halogens.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both the Statements I and II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both the Statements I and II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) is tempting because chlorine oxides are actually made indirectly; but the item follows the NCERT generalisation. (A), (B) reject the near-verbatim Statement II.\nWorking: Statement I – chlorine forms oxides such as ClO₂ and Cl₂O₇, which are unstable and tend to explode; Statement II – NCERT uses reaction with oxygen and halogens as a measure of an element's chemical reactivity. Both are true.\nKey idea: Both statements come from the NCERT Class XI text on periodic trends in chemical reactivity; chlorine oxides (ClO₂, Cl₂O₇) are unstable and explosive.","traps":["Descriptive chemistry vs NCERT generalisation: Match statements to the NCERT periodicity text."],"topics":["Nature of oxides","Valence & oxidation states"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which one of the following sets of ions represents a collection of isoelectronic species? (Given : Atomic Number : F : 9, Cl : 17, Na = 11, Mg = 12, Al = 13, K = 19, Ca = 20, Sc = 21)","options":["N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, S²⁻","Ba²⁺, Sr²⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺","Li⁺, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺","K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) S²⁻ has 18 while the rest have 10. (B) members of one group, not isoelectronic. (C) same charge-pattern ions of a group (2, 10, 10, 18).\nWorking: K⁺ (19 − 1), Cl⁻ (17 + 1), Ca²⁺ (20 − 2) and Sc³⁺ (21 − 3) all have 18 electrons.\nKey idea: Electrons = Z − charge; K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, Sc³⁺ all have 18.","traps":["One odd ion in a near-isoelectronic set: Count every member; S²⁻ has 18."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"For electron gain enthalpies of the elements denoted as Δ_egH, the incorrect option is :","options":["Δ_egH(I) < Δ_egH(At)","Δ_egH(Te) < Δ_egH(Po)","Δ_egH(Cl) < Δ_egH(F)","Δ_egH(Se) < Δ_egH(S)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) looks wrong to students who think F is most electronegative so most negative ΔegH; but Cl is more negative so Cl < F is true. (A)/(B) are normal down-group trends (heavier element less negative). Sign misreading flips all.\nWorking: I −295, At ≈ −270; Te −190, Po ≈ −174; Cl −349, F −328; Se −195, S −200, so Se < S is false.\nKey idea: Write signed values: S (−200) is more negative than Se (−195), so ΔegH(Se) > ΔegH(S).","traps":["Inequality read on magnitudes instead of signed values: Write the signed numbers before comparing.","F assumed to have most negative ΔegH: Remember Cl has the most negative ΔegH (small F suffers repulsion)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Modern periodic table","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"It is observed that characteristic X-ray spectra of elements show regularity. When frequency to the power \"n\" i.e. νⁿ of X-rays emitted is plotted against atomic number \"Z\", following graph is obtained (shown below).\nThe value of 'n' is :","options":["1","1/2","2","3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) n = 1: assumes ν ∝ Z directly. (C) n = 2: inverts the relation (ν ∝ Z²). (D) 3: random power.\nWorking: Moseley's law √ν = a(Z − b) gives a straight line of √ν vs Z, so n = 1/2.\nKey idea: √ν (not ν) is linear in Z.","traps":["ν ∝ Z² confused with √ν ∝ Z: The plotted quantity that is linear in Z is √ν (n = ½)."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan24S1C12-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Inert gases have positive electron gain enthalpy. Its correct order is :","options":["He < Ne < Kr < Xe","He < Xe < Kr < Ne","Xe < Kr < Ne < He","He < Kr < Xe < Ne"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes ΔegH rises steadily down the group. (C) assumes it falls steadily down the group with He largest (size logic). (D) puts Kr below Xe – reverses the 96 vs 77 values.\nWorking: ΔegH (kJ mol⁻¹): He +48, Ne +116, Kr +96, Xe +77, so He < Xe < Kr < Ne.\nKey idea: He (+48) is smallest and Ne (+116) largest; after Ne the value falls down the group, so He < Xe (77) < Kr (96) < Ne (116).","traps":["He assumed to have the largest positive ΔegH: Remember the Table 3.7 numbers: He is only +48, the lowest of the group.","Monotonic trend applied down Group 18: Ne is the peak; from Ne down the values fall (116, 96, 96, 77, 68)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Which of the following represents the correct order of metallic character of the given elements?","options":["K < Mg < Be < Si","Si < Be < Mg < K","Be < Si < Mg < K","Be < Si < K < Mg"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the order; (C) puts Be below Si; (D) puts K below Mg.\nWorking: K (group 1, period 4) is the most metallic; down group 2, Mg > Be; Be is a metal while Si is a metalloid, so Si < Be < Mg < K.\nKey idea: Metallic character rises down a group and falls across a period; Si is a metalloid.","traps":["Be vs Si: Group position matters more: Be (group 2) is a metal; Si is a metalloid."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : The decrease in first ionization enthalpy from B to Al is much larger than that from Al to Ga.\nStatement II : The d orbitals in Ga are completely filled.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct","Both the statements I and II are correct","Both the statements I and II are incorrect"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for students who think Ga has no d electrons or that d filling is irrelevant; (B) for students expecting a smooth, equal decrease down the group; (D) for those who note Ga IE is actually slightly higher than Al and call Statement I wrong.\nWorking: IE₁ (kJ mol⁻¹): B 801, Al 577, Ga 579. The B → Al drop (224) is far larger than the Al → Ga change (almost zero), because Ga ([Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p¹) has a completely filled, poorly shielding 3d subshell. Both statements are correct.\nKey idea: Filled 3d¹⁰ in Ga gives poor shielding, so the extra nuclear charge cancels the size increase and IE stays almost the same as Al.","traps":["Al→Ga IE actually rises slightly: Read Statement I as a comparison of drops; a near-zero change is still much smaller than the B→Al drop.","Statement II taken as unrelated: Ga is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p¹; its 3d is full."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Total number of acidic oxides among N₂O₃, NO₂, N₂O, Cl₂O₇, SO₂, CO, CaO, Na₂O and NO is ____________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (counting all non-metal oxides as acidic, missing neutral N₂O, CO, NO); 5 or 6 (missing one or two neutral ones).\nWorking: acidic – N₂O₃, NO₂, Cl₂O₇, SO₂ (4); neutral – N₂O, CO, NO; basic – CaO, Na₂O. Answer 4.\nKey idea: CO, NO, N₂O are neutral — exactly NCERT's examples.","traps":["Non-metal oxide ≠ always acidic: Learn the neutral trio."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List I (Atomic number) with List II (Block of periodic table). List I: A. 37; B. 78; C. 52; D. 65. List II: I. p-block; II. d-block; III. f-block; IV. s-block. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III","A - IV, B - III, C - II, D - I","A - IV, B - II, C - I, D - III","A - I, B - III, C - IV, D - II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (B): puts Pt in the f-block because it follows the lanthanoids (and also misplaces Te and Tb). Option (A): swaps the s- and d-block elements Rb and Pt. Option (D): misassigns all four.\nWorking: 37 (Rb, 5s¹) s-block; 78 (Pt, 5d) d-block; 52 (Te, 5p⁴) p-block; 65 (Tb, 4f) f-block → A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III.\nKey idea: Count from noble gases: 37 = [Kr]5s¹; 52 = [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p⁴; 65 = [Xe]4f⁹6s²; 78 = [Xe]4f¹⁴5d⁹6s¹.","traps":["Post-lanthanoid element put in f-block: The 4f series ends at Lu (71); 72–80 are 5d."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The correct increasing order of the ionic radii is","options":["Cl⁻ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < S²⁻","K⁺ < S²⁻ < Ca²⁺ < Cl⁻","Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻","S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Cl⁻ smallest, ignoring that cations are smaller. (B) random mix. (D) is the decreasing order (reverse).\nWorking: S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ are isoelectronic (18 e⁻); higher Z gives a smaller ion: Ca²⁺ (20) < K⁺ (19) < Cl⁻ (17) < S²⁻ (16).\nKey idea: All have 18 electrons; Z = 16, 17, 19, 20, so radius falls S²⁻ > Cl⁻ > K⁺ > Ca²⁺.","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing order reversed: Write the order first, then check the word “increasing”."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming elements with Z > 100","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The IUPAC nomenclature of an element with electronic configuration [Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹7s² is:","options":["Unnilbium","Unnilunium","Unnilquadium","Unniltrium"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 101, (A) 102, (C) 104 – off-by-one counting of electrons.\nWorking: [Rn] = 86 + 14 + 1 + 2 = 103 (lawrencium) → un-nil-tri-ium.\nKey idea: 86 + 17 = 103 → nil = 0, tri = 3.","traps":["Electron count from noble-gas core: 86 + 14 + 1 + 2 = 103."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The first ionization enthalpies of Be, B, N and O follow the order","options":["O < N < B < Be","Be < B < N < O","B < Be < N < O","B < Be < O < N"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the simple increasing trend; (C) handles Be/B but misses N > O; (A) reverses everything.\nWorking: B (2p¹ electron, shielded by 2s²) < Be (2s²) and O (paired 2p⁴) < N (half-filled 2p³); values ≈ 801, 899, 1314, 1402 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Penetration (s > p) and half-filled stability give B < Be and O < N.","traps":["Only one anomaly applied: Check both pairs: 2s²/2p¹ and 2p³/2p⁴.","Plain left-to-right trend: Recall the two NCERT exceptions."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The total number of acidic oxides from the following list is: NO, N₂O, B₂O₃, N₂O₅, CO, SO₃, P₄O₁₀","options":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) count N₂O, NO or CO as acidic because they are non-metal oxides; (A) misses B₂O₃ (boron near the metal border).\nWorking: acidic – B₂O₃, N₂O₅, SO₃, P₄O₁₀ (4 oxides); NO, N₂O and CO are neutral.\nKey idea: Non-metal oxides are acidic except the neutral trio CO, NO, N₂O.","traps":["Every non-metal oxide taken as acidic: Exclude CO, NO, N₂O.","B₂O₃ doubted: B₂O₃ is acidic (forms boric acid)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Atomic radius","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Given two statements below:\nStatement I: In Cl₂ molecule the covalent radius is double of the atomic radius of chlorine.\nStatement II: Radius of anionic species is always greater than their parent atomic radius.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): reads Statement I loosely (bond length = double the radius); (B)/(C): doubts the word \"always\" in Statement II.\nWorking: covalent radius is half the Cl–Cl internuclear distance (it is the atomic radius in a covalent molecule), so \"double\" is wrong; an anion has more electrons for the same nuclear charge, so it is always larger than its atom – II correct.\nKey idea: Covalent radius = ½ × internuclear distance; added electron → bigger anion.","traps":["Covalent radius vs bond length: Bond length = 2 × covalent radius, not the reverse.","\"Always\" makes Statement II look false: Check the physics: extra electron, same Z, always larger."],"topics":["Atomic radius","Ionic radius & isoelectronic species"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The incorrect statement is","options":["The first ionization enthalpy of K is less than that of Na and Li","Xe does not have the lowest first ionization enthalpy in its group","The first ionization enthalpy of element with atomic number 37 is lower than that of the element with atomic number 38.","The first ionization enthalpy of Ga is higher than that of the d-block element with atomic number 30."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true trend; (B) tempting as false if student thinks Xe is the lowest (forgets Rn); (C) tempting as false if Rb/Sr are not identified; (D) is the false one because Ga's 4p electron is easy to remove.\nWorking: (A) true (IE falls down Group 1); (B) true (Rn is lower); (C) true, Rb < Sr; (D) false — Ga (≈579 kJ/mol, 4p¹ electron) has lower IE than Zn (Z = 30, ≈906 kJ/mol, full 3d¹⁰4s²).\nKey idea: Removing a lone p electron is easier than breaking a filled s² subshell, so Ga < Zn.","traps":["Ga assumed higher than Zn: Filled ns²(n−1)d¹⁰ → p¹ drop, like Be → B.","Xe vs Rn: Rn below Xe has lower IE."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Outermost electronic configurations of four elements A, B, C, D are given below: (A) 3s²; (B) 3s²3p¹; (C) 3s²3p³; (D) 3s²3p⁴. The correct order of first ionization enthalpy for them is:","options":["(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (D) < (A) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the simple increasing trend; (C) puts S below Mg; (D) gets Mg/Al right but misses P > S.\nWorking: Mg (738) , Al (577), P (1012), S (1000 kJ/mol). Al < Mg because 3p is easier to remove than paired 3s; S < P because P has a stable half-filled 3p³ and S loses a paired 3p electron. Order B < A < D < C.\nKey idea: Filled 3s² and half-filled 3p³ give extra-high IE relative to the next element.","traps":["Simple left-to-right increase: Check s² → s²p¹ and p³ → p⁴ steps for drops.","Missing the P > S exception: Half-filled p³ is more stable; p⁴ loses a paired electron."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"In which of the following pairs, electron gain enthalpies of constituent elements are nearly the same or identical? (A) Rb and Cs (B) Na and K (C) Ar and Kr (D) I and At. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) and (B) only","(B) and (C) only","(A) and (C) only","(C) and (D) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts Na/K (5 kJ difference, close but not as close as Rb/Cs); (D) accepts I/At; (B) drops Rb/Cs.\nWorking: NCERT Table 3.7 gives Rb −47, Cs −46 (nearly same) and Ar +96, Kr +96 (identical); Na −53 vs K −48 and I −295 vs At −270 differ more.\nKey idea: Noble gases Ar and Kr share +96 kJ mol⁻¹ in Table 3.7; Rb and Cs differ by only 1.","traps":["Na–K looks close too: Compare differences: Rb/Cs 1, Na/K 5.","Noble gases assumed different: Recall Ar = Kr = +96 in Table 3.7."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct decreasing order for metallic character is","options":["Na > Mg > Be > Si > P","P > Si > Be > Mg > Na","Si > P > Be > Na > Mg","Be > Na > Mg > Si > P"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the reverse (increasing) order; (C) puts non-metals first; (D) puts Be above Na and Mg, ignoring both trends.\nWorking: metallic character falls across a period (Na > Mg > Si > P) and down a group it rises, so Mg > Be; Be (a metal) is still more metallic than the metalloid Si. Order Na > Mg > Be > Si > P.\nKey idea: Left of a period and lower in a group means more metallic.","traps":["Be vs Mg position: Down a group metallic character increases, so Mg > Be.","Increasing vs decreasing: Re-read \"decreasing\"."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The first ionization enthalpy of Na, Mg and Si, respectively, are: 496, 737 and 786 kJ mol⁻¹. The first ionization enthalpy (kJ mol⁻¹) of Al is:","options":["487","768","577","856"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 487 puts Al below Na; (B) 768 follows the simple across-period rise between Mg and Si; (D) 856 above Si.\nWorking: Al (3s²3p¹) loses a 3p electron that is shielded by 3s², so its IE₁ is below Mg (737) but above Na (496); 577 is the only value in that window.\nKey idea: Al < Mg because the 3p electron is easier to remove than a 3s electron.","traps":["Simple increasing trend applied: Remember IE₁: Mg > Al and P > S."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are the oxides: Na₂O, As₂O₃, N₂O, NO and Cl₂O₇. Number of amphoteric oxides is:","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D): neutral oxides NO and N₂O counted as amphoteric; (A): As₂O₃ treated as acidic (non-metal oxide).\nWorking: Na₂O basic; As₂O₃ amphoteric; N₂O and NO neutral; Cl₂O₇ acidic → 1.\nKey idea: NCERT names As₂O₃ as amphoteric and NO, N₂O as neutral in the same sentence.","traps":["Neutral counted as amphoteric: Neutral = reacts with neither; amphoteric = reacts with both."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The pair, in which ions are isoelectronic with Al³⁺ is:","options":["Br⁻ and Be²⁺","Cl⁻ and Li⁺","S²⁻ and K⁺","O²⁻ and Mg²⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) S²⁻/K⁺ are isoelectronic with each other (18 e⁻) but not with Al³⁺; (A), (B) mix unrelated counts.\nWorking: Al³⁺ has 13 − 3 = 10 electrons. O²⁻ (8 + 2) and Mg²⁺ (12 − 2) both have 10. Br⁻ 36, Be²⁺ 2; Cl⁻ 18, Li⁺ 2; S²⁻ and K⁺ 18.\nKey idea: Al³⁺ = Ne configuration (10 e⁻).","traps":["Pair isoelectronic with each other only: Compare each ion with Al³⁺'s 10 electrons."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following elements is considered as a metalloid?","options":["Sc","Pb","Bi","Te"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Pb and (C) Bi – heavy p-block elements near the boundary but metallic; (A) Sc – d-block metal.\nWorking: Te (with Si, Ge, As, Sb) is a metalloid at the metal/non-metal boundary; Sc is a d-block metal, Pb and Bi are metals.\nKey idea: Metalloids per NCERT: Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te.","traps":["Bi taken as metalloid: Metallic character rises down the group; Bi is a metal, only As and Sb are metalloids in Group 15."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Assertion (A): The ionic radii of O²⁻ and Mg²⁺ are same. Reason (R): Both O²⁻ and Mg²⁺ are isoelectronic species.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – accept that isoelectronic ions are equal in size; (C) – thinks O²⁻ and Mg²⁺ are not isoelectronic.\nWorking: O²⁻ and Mg²⁺ both have 10 electrons (isoelectronic, R true), but Mg²⁺ has 12 protons vs 8 for O²⁻, so Mg²⁺ (~72 pm) is much smaller than O²⁻ (~140 pm); A false → (D).\nKey idea: Same electron count, different nuclear charge: more protons pull the cloud in, so Mg²⁺ ≪ O²⁻.","traps":["Isoelectronic taken as equal size: Compare nuclear charges of isoelectronic ions."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct order of increasing ionic radii is","options":["Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻","N³⁻ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺","F⁻ < Na⁺ < O²⁻ < Mg²⁺ < N³⁻","Na⁺ < F⁻ < Mg²⁺ < O²⁻ < N³⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the decreasing order (direction trap); (C) and (D) mix cations among anions.\nWorking: all are isoelectronic (10 e⁻); radius falls as nuclear charge rises, so N³⁻ (Z = 7) largest and Mg²⁺ (Z = 12) smallest.\nKey idea: Same electrons, more protons → smaller ion.","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing: Smallest first: highest Z (Mg²⁺)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Oxide): (A) Cl₂O₇, (B) Na₂O, (C) Al₂O₃, (D) N₂O. List-II (Nature): (I) Amphoteric, (II) Basic, (III) Neutral, (IV) Acidic. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)–(IV), (B)–(III), (C)–(I), (D)–(II)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(II), (C)–(I), (D)–(III)","(A)–(II), (B)–(IV), (C)–(III), (D)–(I)","(A)–(I), (B)–(II), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) labels Na₂O neutral and N₂O basic; (C) and (D) misplace Cl₂O₇.\nWorking: Cl₂O₇ acidic, Na₂O basic, Al₂O₃ amphoteric, N₂O neutral.\nKey idea: N₂O is a neutral oxide – the only non-trivial pair.","traps":["N₂O as a neutral oxide: Remember CO, NO, N₂O as neutral."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Among the following, the basic oxide is:","options":["SO₃","SiO₂","CaO","Al₂O₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Al₂O₃ is amphoteric (has basic character too); (A), (B) acidic.\nWorking: CaO is a metal (s-block) oxide and is basic; SO₃ and SiO₂ are acidic, Al₂O₃ amphoteric.\nKey idea: Metallic oxides on the left of the table are basic.","traps":["Amphoteric vs basic: Choose the purely basic oxide."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: The first ionization enthalpy for oxygen is lower than that of nitrogen.\nReason R: The four electrons in 2p orbitals of oxygen experience more electron-electron repulsion.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who think half-filled stability of N (not repulsion in O) is the only valid reason; (C)/(D) for those who forget the anomaly.\nWorking: IE₁(O) < IE₁(N); in O two of the four 2p electrons share one orbital, raising inter-electronic repulsion, which makes removal easier – this is the explanation.\nKey idea: Both views (N half-filled, O paired 2p) describe the same effect; NCERT states the repulsion in O as the cause.","traps":["Half-filled stability vs pairing repulsion: NCERT gives repulsion in O as the reason; R explains A.","Expecting IE to rise across period: N→O is a known exception."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of ionic radii for the ions, P³⁻, S²⁻, Ca²⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ is:","options":["P³⁻ > S²⁻ > Cl⁻ > K⁺ > Ca²⁺","Cl⁻ > S²⁻ > P³⁻ > Ca²⁺ > K⁺","P³⁻ > S²⁻ > Cl⁻ > Ca²⁺ > K⁺","K⁺ > Ca²⁺ > P³⁻ > S²⁻ > Cl⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps K⁺ and Ca²⁺ (Ca²⁺ has more protons, so smaller); (B) reverses the anion order; (D) puts cations above anions.\nWorking: all five are isoelectronic (18 e⁻); radius falls as nuclear charge rises: P (15) > S (16) > Cl (17) > K (19) > Ca (20).\nKey idea: Same electron count → more protons, smaller ion.","traps":["Ca²⁺ vs K⁺: Higher Z with same electrons → stronger pull → smaller."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Assertion (A): Metallic character decreases and non-metallic character increases on moving from left to right in a period. Reason (R): It is due to increase in ionisation enthalpy and decrease in electron gain enthalpy, when one moves from left to right in a period. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true.","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) accepts \"decrease\" as \"more negative\" (not the intended reading); (D) accepts R but doubts the causal link; (A) rejects the NCERT trend in A.\nWorking: (A) is true. In the standard (NCERT/JEE) reading, electron gain enthalpy becomes more negative across a period, i.e. the tendency to gain an electron increases; R's 'decrease in electron gain enthalpy' therefore states the trend wrongly, so R is false and the answer is (B). Reading the signed value algebraically (more negative = 'decrease') would lead to (C), but that is not the intended reading.\nKey idea: Electron gain enthalpy increases in magnitude (becomes more negative) across a period, so \"decrease\" made R false.","traps":["Decrease vs more negative: In JEE questions, electron gain enthalpy is taken to increase in magnitude across a period, so \"decrease\" is treated as false.","IE trend exceptions: General trend still increases across a period."],"topics":["Electron gain enthalpy","Ionisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Consider the elements Mg, Al, S, P and Si. The correct increasing order of their first ionization enthalpy is:","options":["Mg < Al < Si < S < P","Al < Mg < Si < S < P","Mg < Al < Si < P < S","Al < Mg < S < Si < P"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) – miss the Mg > Al anomaly; (C) also misses P > S; (D) – places S below Si.\nWorking: Al (3p¹, electron removed from higher-energy 3p) < Mg (3s² filled); S < P because P has half-filled 3p³ and S loses a paired 3p electron. Order Al 577 < Mg 738 < Si 786 < S 1000 < P 1012 kJ/mol.\nKey idea: Group 2 > group 13 and group 15 > group 16 in the same period.","traps":["Mg–Al anomaly missed: s² filled subshell vs single p electron: Mg > Al.","P–S anomaly missed: Half-filled p³ more stable: P > S."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (electronic configuration of elements): (a) 1s²2s² (b) 1s²2s²2p⁴ (c) 1s²2s²2p³ (d) 1s²2s²2p¹. List-II (ΔᵢH in kJ mol⁻¹): (i) 801 (ii) 899 (iii) 1314 (iv) 1402. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["(a)→(ii), (b)→(iii), (c)→(iv), (d)→(i)","(a)→(i), (b)→(iv), (c)→(iii), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(i), (b)→(iii), (c)→(iv), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(iv), (b)→(i), (c)→(ii), (d)→(iii)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) give Be 801 (taking B>Be by trend); (B) also swaps N and O; (D) assigns largest value to Be.\nWorking: Be 899, O 1314, N 1402, B 801 kJ mol⁻¹ (Be > B and N > O anomalies).\nKey idea: Order: N (1402) > O (1314) > Be (899) > B (801).","traps":["Be and B swapped: 2p electron of B is less penetrating than 2s of Be.","N and O swapped: Half-filled 2p³ of N; paired 2p⁴ in O is easier to ionise."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct order of electron gain enthalpy is","options":["S > Se > Te > O","Te > Se > S > O","O > S > Se > Te","S > O > Se > Te"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – simple down-the-group decrease; (D) – O placed second; (B) – reversed order.\nWorking: in magnitude (negative values) S (–200) > Se (–195) > Te (–190) > O (–141 kJ/mol); O is small, so added electron feels strong repulsion.\nKey idea: Second-period anomaly: O < S in negative ΔegH.","traps":["Simple group trend applied: Recall: S and Cl have the most negative values in their groups.","Sign of enthalpy vs magnitude: Compare numerical magnitudes of the negative values."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which pair of oxides is acidic in nature?","options":["B₂O₃, CaO","B₂O₃, SiO₂","N₂O, BaO","CaO, SiO₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – N₂O assumed acidic like other N oxides; (A)/(D) – one basic oxide (CaO) paired with an acidic one.\nWorking: B₂O₃ (→ H₃BO₃) and SiO₂ (reacts with bases) are acidic; CaO, BaO are basic; N₂O is neutral.\nKey idea: N₂O, NO, CO are neutral; group 2 oxides basic.","traps":["N₂O taken as acidic: Remember neutral oxides: CO, NO, N₂O."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Outermost electronic configuration of a group 13 element, E, is 4s², 4p¹. The electronic configuration of an element of p-block period-five placed diagonally to element, E is :","options":["[Kr] 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p²","[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s² 4p²","[Xe] 5d¹⁰ 6s² 6p²","[Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s² 5p²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) impossible core ([Kr] with 3d/4s/4p); (B) Ge – same period, next group; (C) Pb – period 6.\nWorking: E = Ga (group 13, period 4); the diagonal neighbour in period 5 is group 14, Sn: [Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s² 5p².\nKey idea: Diagonal = one period down, one group right: Ga (13, 4) → Sn (14, 5).","traps":["Wrong noble-gas core: Core noble gas belongs to the previous period; period 5 → [Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5s² 5p².","Diagonal mistaken for same period: Diagonal moves both group and period."],"topics":["s, p, d, f blocks","Modern periodic table"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Modern periodic table","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which one of the following statements for D.I. Mendeleeff, is incorrect?","options":["He authored the textbook – Principles of Chemistry.","At the time, he proposed Periodic Table of elements structure of atom was known.","Element with atomic number 101 is named after him.","He invented accurate barometer."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) are obscure but true biography facts – a student who does not recall them may mark one as incorrect; (C) true (mendelevium).\nWorking: Mendeleev's table (1869) predates the discovery of the electron (1897) and nucleus (1911), so the structure of the atom was not known; he did write \"Principles of Chemistry\", element 101 is mendelevium, and he designed an accurate barometer.\nKey idea: Mendeleev used atomic masses because atomic structure (electrons, protons, atomic number) was not yet known.","traps":["Obscure true facts look false: Anchor on the one checkable fact: atomic structure came after 1869.","Incorrect-statement stem: Mark each statement T/F first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The ionic radii of K⁺, Na⁺, Al³⁺ and Mg²⁺ are in the order:","options":["Na⁺ < K⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺","Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < K⁺ < Na⁺","Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < K⁺","K⁺ < Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts K⁺ below Na⁺ (reverses group trend); (A) and (D) reverse the isoelectronic order.\nWorking: Al³⁺, Mg²⁺, Na⁺ are isoelectronic (10 e⁻), size falls as nuclear charge rises (Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺); K⁺ has an extra shell and is largest.\nKey idea: More positive charge on the same electron count = smaller ion; new shell = bigger.","traps":["Na⁺ vs K⁺: Down a group ionic radius increases."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The ionic radii of F⁻ and O²⁻ respectively are 1.33 Å and 1.4 Å, while the covalent radius of N is 0.74 Å. The correct statement for the ionic radius of N³⁻ from the following is:","options":["It is smaller than F⁻ and N","It is bigger than O²⁻ and F⁻","It is bigger than F⁻ and N, but smaller than of O²⁻","It is smaller than O²⁻ and F⁻, but bigger than of N"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (D) use the data to place N³⁻ between values – the numbers tempt interpolation; (A) ignores that anions are larger than atoms.\nWorking: N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻ are isoelectronic (10 e⁻); lowest nuclear charge gives the largest ion, so N³⁻ (~1.71 Å) > O²⁻ > F⁻, and an anion is bigger than its atom.\nKey idea: Isoelectronic: fewer protons → bigger ion.","traps":["Data invites interpolation: Use the isoelectronic rule, not the numbers."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The CORRECT order of first ionisation enthalpy is:","options":["Mg < S < Al < P","Mg < Al < S < P","Al < Mg < S < P","Mg < Al < P < S"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) jumbles order; (B) follows Z for Mg/Al (misses first anomaly); (D) misses the P > S anomaly.\nWorking: Al (577) < Mg (738) < S (1000) < P (1012 kJ mol⁻¹); Al's 3p electron is shielded by 3s², and S loses a paired 3p electron while P has a stable half-filled 3p³.\nKey idea: Group 2 > group 13 and group 15 > group 16 within a period.","traps":["Mg above Al: s-electron penetrates more than p.","P above S: Paired electron repulsion makes S easier to ionise."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Identify the elements X and Y using the ionisation energy values given below (kJ/mol): X – 1st 495, 2nd 4563; Y – 1st 731, 2nd 1450.","options":["X = Na ; Y = Mg","X = Mg ; Y = F","X = Mg ; Y = Na","X = F ; Y = Mg"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) bring in F, whose first IE (~1680 kJ/mol) is far higher than either value; (C) swaps Na and Mg – picked by those who think the larger second IE belongs to the element with two valence electrons.\nWorking: X has a low first IE and a huge jump to the second (removal from a noble-gas core) → Na; Y has a moderate first IE and a modest second IE (both 3s electrons) → Mg.\nKey idea: A very large IE₂/IE₁ ratio means one valence electron (group 1).","traps":["Na and Mg swapped: Look for the jump: Na's 2nd electron comes from the 2p core."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The characteristics of elements X, Y and Z with atomic numbers, respectively, 33, 53 and 83 are:","options":["X and Y are metalloids and Z is a metal.","X is a metalloid, Y is a non-metal and Z is a metal.","X, Y and Z are metals.","X and Z are non-metals and Y is a metalloid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) calls iodine a metalloid (lustre misleads); (C) calls As and I metals; (D) calls Bi a non-metal.\nWorking: Z = 33 is As (metalloid), 53 is I (non-metal, halogen), 83 is Bi (metal).\nKey idea: As – metalloid; I – non-metal despite lustre; Bi – metal (group 15 metallic character increases down).","traps":["Iodine called metalloid: Halogens are non-metals; lustre is not the criterion."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The absolute value of the electron gain enthalpy of halogens satisfies:","options":["I > Br > Cl > F","Cl > Br > F > I","Cl > F > Br > I","F > Cl > Br > I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – straight group trend ignoring the F anomaly; (B) – F placed too low; (A) – reversed trend.\nWorking: Cl has the most negative ΔegH (−349); F (−328) is less negative than Cl because of electron-electron repulsion in the small 2p subshell; then Br (−325), I (−295).\nKey idea: F < Cl in magnitude due to compact 2p repulsion.","traps":["Simple group trend applied: Remember the second-period anomaly.","F placed after Br: Only F/Cl swap; Br and I follow the trend."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous second-period behaviour","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The set of elements that differ in mutual relationship from those of the other sets is :","options":["Li – Mg","B – Si","Be – Al","Li – Na"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are all diagonal pairs; a student who thinks Li–Na \"similar\" or misremembers B–Si may pick one of them.\nWorking: Li–Mg, Be–Al and B–Si are diagonal pairs (period 2 element with the period 3 element one group to the right); Li–Na are in the same group, so that pair is the odd one out.\nKey idea: Diagonal = period 2, group n with period 3, group n+1: Li/Mg, Be/Al, B/Si.","traps":["Odd-one-out wording: Classify each pair first: diagonal or same group.","B–Si not remembered as diagonal: Check positions: B (2nd period, group 13), Si (3rd period, group 14)."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The ionic radius of Na⁺ ions is 1.02 Å. The ionic radii (in Å) of Mg²⁺ and Al³⁺, respectively, are-","options":["1.05 and 0.99","0.72 and 0.54","0.85 and 0.99","0.68 and 0.72"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) both larger than Na⁺ – reverses the trend; (C) Al³⁺ larger than Mg²⁺; (D) Al³⁺ (0.72) larger than Mg²⁺ (0.68) – order reversed.\nWorking: Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺ are isoelectronic (10 e⁻); radius falls as nuclear charge rises, so both values must be below 1.02 Å and Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺: 0.72 and 0.54 Å.\nKey idea: Isoelectronic: more protons → smaller ion, so Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Al³⁺.","traps":["Order of Mg²⁺ and Al³⁺ reversed: Higher charge, same electrons → smaller."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The first ionization energy of magnesium is smaller as compared to that of elements X and Y, but higher than that of Z. The elements X, Y and Z, respectively, are :","options":["chlorine, lithium and sodium","argon, lithium and sodium","argon, chlorine and sodium","neon, sodium and chlorine"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) need Li > Mg, but Li (520) < Mg (738) – diagonal-relation intuition misleads; (D) puts Na as higher and Cl as lower.\nWorking: IE₁ (kJ mol⁻¹) Mg 738; Ar 1521 and Cl 1251 are higher; Na 496 is lower. Li (520) is lower than Mg, so (A) and (B) fail; in (D) Na is lower and Cl higher, the wrong way round.\nKey idea: Li's IE₁ (520) is lower than Mg's (738); group 1 metals are below Mg.","traps":["Li assumed higher than Mg: Compare actual values: alkali metals have the lowest IE₁.","Order of X, Y, Z misread: Check each element against the condition separately."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The electron gain enthalpy (in kJ/mol) of fluorine, chlorine, bromine and iodine, respectively are :","options":["–333, –349, –325 and –296","–296, –325, –333 and –349","–333, –325, –349 and –296","–349, –333, –325 and –296"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): reverse order. (C): swaps Cl and Br. (D): assumes F most negative (simple trend).\nWorking: Cl has the most negative electron gain enthalpy; F is less negative than Cl because of its small, crowded 2p shell; then Br, I. Order Cl > F > Br > I in magnitude matches (A).\nKey idea: Most negative ΔegH is Cl, not F.","traps":["F assumed most negative: Small 2p shell of F → electron-electron repulsion → less negative than Cl.","F value differs from NCERT table: Rank by order, not exact number."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Within each pair of elements of F & Cl, S & Se, and Li & Na, respectively, the elements that release more energy upon an electron gain are-","options":["F, Se and Na","F, S and Li","Cl, S and Li","Cl, Se and Na"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) pick the heavier element in the S/Se and Li/Na pairs (trend reversed); (B) applies the normal trend to F/Cl, missing the anomaly; (D) also takes Cl correctly but reverses the others.\nWorking: Cl > F (added electron in compact 2p of F suffers more repulsion); S > Se and Li > Na (electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group).\nKey idea: Cl, not F, has the most negative electron gain enthalpy; for the other pairs the lighter element releases more energy.","traps":["F assumed more negative than Cl: Recall the 2p-repulsion exception: Cl has the most negative value.","Trend reversed for S/Se and Li/Na: Electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The first ionization energy (in kJ/mol) of Na, Mg, Al and Si respectively, are :","options":["496, 737, 577, 786","786, 737, 577, 496","496, 577, 737, 786","496, 577, 786, 737"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the whole order; (C) follows the simple increasing trend and misses Mg > Al; (D) swaps Mg and Si.\nWorking: Na lowest; Mg (3s² filled) > Al (3p¹ easily removed); Si highest: 496 < 577 (Al) < 737 (Mg) < 786 (Si).\nKey idea: Removing a 3p electron from Al is easier than a paired 3s electron from Mg, so Al < Mg.","traps":["Monotonic increase assumed across period: Remember s² → p¹ drop (Mg > Al)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic radius","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The increasing order of the atomic radii of the following elements is :- (a) C (b) O (c) F (d) Cl (e) Br","options":["(b) < (c) < (d) < (a) < (e)","(a) < (b) < (c) < (d) < (e)","(d) < (c) < (b) < (a) < (e)","(c) < (b) < (a) < (d) < (e)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts O < F and Cl < C; (B) gives increasing order in the alphabetical/period direction (C smallest); (C) puts Cl smallest.\nWorking: across period 2 radius falls C > O > F; down group 17 it rises F < Cl < Br; Cl (99 pm) > C (77 pm). So F < O < C < Cl < Br.\nKey idea: Second-period atoms are all smaller than any third-period atom of the right side, so F < O < C < Cl < Br.","traps":["Radius increasing across a period: Nuclear charge rises across a period, radius falls."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"B has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than Be. Consider the following statements: (I) It is easier to remove 2p electron than 2s electron. (II) 2p electron of B is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2s electrons of Be. (III) 2s electron has more penetration power than 2p electron. (IV) Atomic radius of B is more than Be. (Atomic number B = 5, Be = 4). The correct statements are:","options":["(I), (II) and (III)","(II), (III) and (IV)","(I), (III) and (IV)","(I), (II) and (IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) all include IV (B larger than Be), which is false.\nWorking: I, II, III are the NCERT reasons; IV is false because B is smaller than Be (radius falls across a period).\nKey idea: Penetration/shielding explain the anomaly, not size; B is smaller than Be.","traps":["Size explanation for the anomaly: Radius decreases across the period."],"topics":["Ionisation enthalpy","Atomic radius"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The acidic, basic and amphoteric oxides, respectively, are:","options":["MgO, Cl₂O, Al₂O₃","Cl₂O, CaO, P₄O₁₀","Na₂O, SO₃, Al₂O₃","N₂O₃, Li₂O, Al₂O₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) list basic oxide first; (B) gives P₄O₁₀ (acidic) as the amphoteric one.\nWorking: N₂O₃ acidic, Li₂O basic, Al₂O₃ amphoteric. (A) and (C) swap acidic/basic order; (B) ends with acidic P₄O₁₀.\nKey idea: Non-metal oxide acidic, alkali-metal oxide basic, Al₂O₃ amphoteric.","traps":["Order of the triple ignored: Match each position to the asked property."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The first and second ionisation enthalpies of a metal are 496 and 4560 kJ mol⁻¹, respectively. How many moles of HCl and H₂SO₄, respectively, will be needed to react completely with 1 mole of the metal hydroxide?","options":["1 and 0.5","2 and 0.5","1 and 1","1 and 2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats the metal as divalent but halves H₂SO₄ wrongly; (C) forgets H₂SO₄ is dibasic; (D) inverts the H₂SO₄ ratio.\nWorking: the huge jump from IE₁ to IE₂ means one valence electron (alkali metal, Na), so hydroxide is MOH: 1 mol HCl or 0.5 mol H₂SO₄.\nKey idea: IE₂ ≫ IE₁ ⇒ M⁺ ⇒ MOH; H₂SO₄ is dibasic so 0.5 mol.","traps":["Dibasic H₂SO₄ factor missed: H₂SO₄ gives 2 H⁺, so half a mole per OH⁻.","Valency misread from IE data: A ~9-fold jump at IE₂ means only one valence electron."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic radius","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"In general, the property (magnitudes only) that shows an opposite trend in comparison to other properties across a period is","options":["Electronegativity","Electron gain enthalpy","Ionization enthalpy","Atomic radius"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A student worried about the sign of electron gain enthalpy might pick (B); the phrase \"magnitudes only\" removes that trap.\nWorking: across a period atomic radius decreases while electronegativity, |electron gain enthalpy| and ionisation enthalpy generally increase.\nKey idea: Size falls across a period because Z_eff rises; the other three rise for the same reason.","traps":["Sign of electron gain enthalpy: Read \"magnitudes only\"."],"topics":["Atomic radius","Ionisation enthalpy","Electronegativity"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nature of oxides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Three elements X, Y and Z are in the 3rd period of the periodic table. The oxides of X, Y and Z, respectively, are basic, amphoteric and acidic. The correct order of the atomic numbers of X, Y and Z is :","options":["Z < Y < X","X < Z < Y","X < Y < Z","Y < X < Z"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the trend; (B) and (D) misplace the amphoteric element.\nWorking: across period 3 oxides go basic (Na, Mg) → amphoteric (Al) → acidic (Si–Cl), so atomic number rises X < Y < Z.\nKey idea: Basic → amphoteric → acidic is the left-to-right order.","traps":["Trend direction reversed: Na₂O basic is on the left."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming elements with Z > 100","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The atomic number of the element unnilennium is:","options":["119","108","102","109"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 119 reads \"un-un-enn\"; (B) 108 confuses \"enn\" with \"oct\"; (C) 102.\nWorking: un-nil-enn = 1-0-9 → Z = 109.\nKey idea: nil = 0, un = 1, enn = 9.","traps":["Digit order read wrongly: Read roots left to right as digits."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous second-period behaviour","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Among the statements (I – IV), the correct ones are:\n(I) Be has smaller atomic radius compared to Mg.\n(II) Be has higher ionization enthalpy than Al.\n(III) Charge/radius ratio of Be is greater than that of Al.\n(IV) Both Be and Al form mainly covalent compounds.","options":["(I), (II) and (IV)","(II), (III) and (IV)","(I), (II) and (III)","(I), (III) and (IV)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) include (IV) – Al forms many ionic compounds; (B) drops (I), which is plainly true.\nWorking: (I) true (Be 112 pm < Mg 160 pm); (II) true (Be 899 > Al 577 kJ/mol); (III) true on ionic radii – Be²⁺ 2/31 pm ≈ 0.065 vs Al³⁺ 3/53.5 pm ≈ 0.056; (IV) taken as false because Al also forms ionic compounds (AlF₃, Al₂O₃), though Be–Al diagonal-relationship notes often say both are largely covalent, so (A) was a tempting alternative.\nKey idea: Compute the ratios from ionic radii (Be²⁺ 31 pm, Al³⁺ 53.5 pm) instead of relying on \"nearly the same\".","traps":["Diagonal similarity read as equality: Use actual radii; Be²⁺ is slightly higher.","Al taken as mainly covalent: Al forms ionic AlF₃ and Al₂O₃; only Be is predominantly covalent."],"topics":["Anomalous second-period behaviour","Atomic radius","Ionisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The five successive ionization enthalpies of an element are 800, 2427, 3658, 25024 and 32824 kJ mol⁻¹. The number of valence electrons in the element is :","options":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2: reading the 800→2427 rise as the jump; (C) 4: counting the jump position wrongly (IE₄ value); (D) 5: total data points.\nWorking: the large jump is between IE₃ (3658) and IE₄ (25024), so three electrons are in the valence shell (boron-like).\nKey idea: Count electrons removed before the steep jump.","traps":["Wrong jump chosen: Compare ratios; 3658→25024 is ~7×."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"s, p, d, f blocks","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The elements with atomic numbers 101 and 104 belong to, respectively :","options":["Group 11 and Group 4","Actinoids and Group 4","Actinoids and Group 6","Group 6 and Actinoids"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) takes 101 as group 11 by its last digits; (C) puts 104 in group 6 by miscounting; (D) reversed.\nWorking: Z = 101 (Md) is an actinoid (Z 90–103); Z = 104 (Rf) is the first 6d element, in group 4.\nKey idea: Actinoids end at 103 (Lr); 104 starts the 6d series in group 4.","traps":["Group from last digits: Count from Rn (86): 87–88 s-block, 89–103 actinoids, 104 group 4."],"topics":["s, p, d, f blocks","Modern periodic table"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The ionic radii of O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ are in the order :","options":["F⁻ > O²⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺","Mg²⁺ > Na⁺ > F⁻ > O²⁻","O²⁻ > F⁻ > Mg²⁺ > Na⁺","O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps O²⁻/F⁻; (B) reversed; (C) swaps Na⁺/Mg²⁺.\nWorking: isoelectronic (10 e⁻) series – radius falls as nuclear charge rises: O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺.\nKey idea: More protons, same electrons → smaller.","traps":["Anion order swapped: O²⁻ has fewer protons than F⁻ → larger."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The process that is NOT endothermic in nature is :","options":["Ar(g) + e⁻ → Ar⁻(g)","H(g) + e⁻ → H⁻(g)","Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","O⁻(g) + e⁻ → O²⁻(g)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) noble gas – positive ΔegH (stable configuration); (C) ionisation always endothermic; (D) second electron gain is endothermic due to repulsion with O⁻.\nWorking: H has negative electron gain enthalpy (≈ −73 kJ/mol), so H → H⁻ releases energy; electron addition to a noble gas, removal of an electron (ionisation) and second electron gain by O⁻ all need energy.\nKey idea: Only first electron gain for most non-noble atoms (like H) is exothermic here.","traps":["Second electron gain thought exothermic: First ΔegH of O is negative; second is positive (repulsion).","NOT wording: Mark each as endo/exo before choosing."],"topics":["Electron gain enthalpy","Ionisation enthalpy"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct order of the ionic radii of O²⁻, N³⁻, F⁻, Mg²⁺, Na⁺ and Al³⁺ is:","options":["Al³⁺ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < N³⁻","N³⁻ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺","Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻","N³⁻ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < Al³⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) – reversed direction; (A) Na⁺/Mg²⁺ and O²⁻/F⁻ swapped (one pair each).\nWorking: all are isoelectronic (10 e⁻); radius falls as nuclear charge rises: N³⁻ (Z=7) > O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > Al³⁺ (Z=13).\nKey idea: Same electrons → more protons → smaller ion.","traps":["Pairs swapped within cations/anions: Order strictly by Z: 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13.","Increasing order read as decreasing: Check which end the smallest ion (Al³⁺) sits."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming elements with Z > 100","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The atomic number of Unnilunium is ______.","type":"num","answer":"101","range":[101.0,101.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 110 (reading un-nil-un as 1-1-0), 111 (unununium), 100.\nWorking: un = 1, nil = 0, un = 1 → 101.\nKey idea: Digit roots: nil 0, un 1, bi 2, tri 3, quad 4 …, read in order.","traps":["Unnilunium vs unununium: Spell out root by root: un-nil-un = 1-0-1."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The size of the iso-electronic species Cl⁻, Ar and Ca²⁺ is affected by –","options":["Principal quantum number of valence shell","Nuclear charge","Azimuthal quantum number of valence shell","Electron-electron interaction in the outer orbitals"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) are identical for all three; (D) e–e repulsion is the same for equal electron counts.\nWorking: same electron count and shells; size decreases as nuclear charge (Z = 17, 18, 20) increases.\nKey idea: Same electrons, different Z → Z decides size.","traps":["Choosing electron–electron repulsion: Repulsion is equal when electron counts are equal."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming elements with Z > 100","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The IUPAC symbol for the element with atomic number 119 would be:","options":["unh","uun","une","uue"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) unh = 116 (un-un-hex); (B) uun = 110 (un-un-nil); (C) une = 109 (un-nil-enn).\nWorking: 1-1-9 = un-un-enn ⇒ Ununennium, symbol Uue.\nKey idea: Each digit → its root initial: 1=u, 9=e.","traps":["Wrong root for a digit: Write the three digits then their roots in order."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The element having greatest difference between its first and second ionization energies, is :","options":["Ca","K","Ba","Sc"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) group-2 metals: second electron still a valence electron; (D) Sc loses 4s² before 3d.\nWorking: K (4s¹) – second electron is removed from the [Ar] noble-gas core, giving a very large IE₂ − IE₁; Ca, Ba, Sc lose a second valence electron.\nKey idea: Largest jump comes when the second electron must come from a noble-gas core – group 1.","traps":["Choosing heaviest / largest metal: Look for configuration jumping to a core, not size."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionic radius & isoelectronic species","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The isoelectronic set of ions is :","options":["N³⁻, Li⁺, Mg²⁺ and O²⁻","Li⁺, Na⁺, O²⁻ and F⁻","F⁻, Li⁺, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺","N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻ and Na⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) each include Li⁺ (2 e⁻) among 10-electron ions.\nWorking: N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ each have 10 electrons; Li⁺ has 2, so every other set fails.\nKey idea: Li⁺ is He-like, not Ne-like.","traps":["Li⁺ counted as 10-electron: Count Z – charge for each ion."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Valence & oxidation states","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The group number, number of valence electrons, and valency of an element with atomic number 15, respectively, are","options":["16, 5 and 2","16, 6 and 3","15, 5 and 3","15, 6 and 2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) wrong group (16); (D) 6 valence electrons and valency 2 belong to group 16.\nWorking: Z = 15 is P, [Ne]3s²3p³: group 15, 5 valence electrons, valency 8 − 5 = 3.\nKey idea: P: 2,8,5 → group 15, valency 3.","traps":["Group vs valence electrons mix-up: Count valence electrons from the configuration."],"topics":["Valence & oxidation states","s, p, d, f blocks"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ionisation enthalpy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In comparison to boron, beryllium has :","options":["lesser nuclear charge and greater first ionisation enthalpy","lesser nuclear charge and lesser first ionisation enthalpy","greater nuclear charge and greater first ionisation enthalpy","greater nuclear charge and lesser first ionisation enthalpy"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) follows the general trend; (C)/(D) reverse the nuclear charge.\nWorking: Be (Z = 4) < B (Z = 5) in nuclear charge, but Be loses a penetrating 2s electron while B loses a shielded 2p electron, so IE₁(Be) > IE₁(B).\nKey idea: s-electron penetrates more than p-electron of the same shell.","traps":["IE assumed to rise smoothly across period: Recall the Be > B and N > O exceptions."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electronegativity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"In general, the properties that decrease and increase down a group in the periodic table, respectively, are :","options":["electronegativity and electron gain enthalpy.","electronegativity and atomic radius.","atomic radius and electronegativity.","electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): electron gain enthalpy, whose sign makes 'increase' ambiguous. (C): order reversed.\nWorking: down a group electronegativity decreases and atomic radius increases.\nKey idea: Down a group: size up, electronegativity down.","traps":["Electron gain enthalpy direction: Pick the option that is unambiguous: radius clearly increases.","Order of 'decrease, increase' swapped: Match 'respectively' carefully."],"topics":["Electronegativity","Atomic radius"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electron gain enthalpy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"When the first electron gain enthalpy (ΔegH) of oxygen is –141 kJ/mol, its second electron gain enthalpy is:","options":["almost the same as that of the first","negative, but less negative than the first","a positive value","a more negative value than the first"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) More negative: thinking O²⁻ is stable, so formation exothermic. (B) less negative: partial reasoning. (A) same.\nWorking: adding an electron to O⁻ requires work against repulsion, so ΔegH₂ is positive (about +780 kJ/mol).\nKey idea: Any second electron gain is endothermic; O²⁻ exists in solids only due to lattice energy.","traps":["O²⁻ stability mistaken for exothermic gain: Stability of O²⁻ comes from lattice/hydration energy, not ΔegH₂."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electronegativity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The electronegativity of aluminium is similar to :","options":["Boron","Carbon","Lithium","Beryllium"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) boron: same group, but EN 2.0. (C) lithium: diagonal partner of Mg, not Al. (B) carbon: EN 2.5.\nWorking: diagonal relationship – Be and Al have similar electronegativity (≈1.5 on the Pauling scale).\nKey idea: Be–Al diagonal pair: similar charge/size ratio and EN.","traps":["Group neighbour chosen over diagonal one: Similar EN comes from diagonal pairs (Li–Mg, Be–Al, B–Si)."],"topics":["Electronegativity","Anomalous second-period behaviour"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Atomic radius","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct order of the atomic radii of C, Cs, Al and S is:","options":["S < C < Al < Cs","S < C < Cs < Al","C < S < Cs < Al","C < S < Al < Cs"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts S below C (period ignored). (B)/(C) put Cs below Al.\nWorking: C (period 2) smallest; S < Al across period 3; Cs (period 6, group 1) largest: C < S < Al < Cs.\nKey idea: New shell outweighs across-period contraction.","traps":["S vs C: S is a period lower – larger than C."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electronegativity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct option with respect to the Pauling electronegativity values of the elements is :-","options":["Ga < Ge","Si < Al","P > S","Te > Se"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Si < Al: reverses the across-period rise (Al 1.5 < Si 1.8). (C) P > S: reverses trend; P 2.1 < S 2.5. (D) Te > Se: reverses the down-group fall.\nWorking: Pauling values Ga ≈ 1.8 < Ge ≈ 2.0 (rises across period 4 p-block). Si (1.8) > Al (1.5), P (2.1) < S (2.5), Te (2.1) < Se (2.4), so (B), (C), (D) are reversed.\nKey idea: EN increases left to right in a period and decreases down a group; every wrong option is a simple reversal.","traps":["Ga–Ge suspected as d-block anomaly: Ga/Ge differ in the right direction (1.8 vs 2.0); only Al/Ga and B/Al show EN irregularity.","Trend direction reversed: Write positions in the periodic table and apply 'right and up = more electronegative'."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Modern periodic table","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The element with Z = 120 (not yet discovered) will be an/a :","options":["transition metal","inner-transition metal","alkaline earth metal","alkali metal"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) alkali metal: off-by-one (that is Z = 119). (A)/(B): assuming superheavy means d/f block.\nWorking: period 7 ends at Z = 118; Z = 119 starts period 8 in group 1, Z = 120 is group 2 (8s²).\nKey idea: After Og (118), 8s fills first: 119 group 1, 120 group 2.","traps":["Off-by-one counting: 118 is noble gas; +1 alkali, +2 alkaline earth."],"topics":["Modern periodic table","s, p, d, f blocks"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Anomalous second-period behaviour","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The element that shows greater ability to form pπ–pπ multiple bonds, is :","options":["Si","Ge","Sn","C"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C): heavier Group 14 elements with diffuse orbitals and poor sideways overlap.\nWorking: the second-period element C (small size, good 2p–2p overlap) forms pπ–pπ multiple bonds far more readily than Si, Ge, Sn.\nKey idea: First member of each p-block group forms strong pπ–pπ bonds.","traps":["Picking a heavier Group 14 element: recall C=C and C≡C; Si, Ge and Sn rarely form stable pπ–pπ multiple bonds."]}],"m19-bio":[{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Fructose","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"Which of the following is the correct structure of L−Fructose?\n(Each option is a Fischer projection, C-1 at top: C-1 = CH₂OH, C-2 = C=O, C-6 = CH₂OH at bottom. The side of the OH at C-3, C-4 and C-5 is given.)","options":["C-3: OH left; C-4: OH right; C-5: OH left","C-3: OH right; C-4: OH left; C-5: OH left","C-3: OH right; C-4: OH right; C-5: OH left","C-3: OH left; C-4: OH left; C-5: OH left"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C3 L, C4 R, C5 L: only C5 inverted, a diastereomer. (B) C3 R, C4 L, C5 L: correct full mirror. (C) C3 R, C4 R, C5 L: C3 and C5 inverted only. (D) all left: C3 retained, C4 and C5 inverted.\nWorking: D-fructose has OH at C3 left, C4 right, C5 right; its mirror image L-fructose has C3 right, C4 left, C5 left – option (B).\nKey idea: An enantiomer is obtained by mirroring all stereocentres, not just the one that names D/L.","traps":["L-enantiomer made by inverting only the lowest stereocentre: An enantiomer inverts every stereocentre: write D-fructose (L,R,R at C3,C4,C5) and reverse all to (R,L,L).","D/L assigned from lowest stereocentre OH in Fischer: Use the D/L rule to screen, then match C3 and C4 to the mirror of D-fructose."]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"A dipeptide, \"x\" on complete hydrolysis gives \"y\" and \"z\". \"y\" on treatment with aq. HNO₂ produces lactic acid. On the other hand \"z\" on heating gives the following cyclic molecule (shown below).\nBased on the information given, the dipeptide X is:","options":["alanine-glycine","alanine-alanine","valine-glycine","valine-leucine"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ala-Gly correct. (B) Ala-Ala: z misread as alanine. (C) Val-Gly: y misread (valine gives 2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid). (D) Val-Leu: neither fits.\nWorking: y + HNO₂ → lactic acid CH₃CH(OH)COOH, so y = alanine CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH. The ring formed by z on heating has no substituents, so z = glycine. The only option containing alanine and glycine is (A) alanine-glycine.\nKey idea: HNO₂ swaps NH₂ for OH, and a substituent-free diketopiperazine can only come from glycine.","traps":["Amino acid identified from HNO₂ deamination product: Replace OH by NH₂: CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH = alanine.","Diketopiperazine ring read as derived from the wrong amino acid: Ring carbons between N and C=O are CH₂ with no substituent: glycine."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C20-s.webp"],"topics":["Amino acids","Peptide bond"]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"H₂N—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COOH —[pH = 2]→ A\nH₂N—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COOH —[pH = 10]→ B\nChoose the correct option for structures of A and B, respectively","options":["H₃N⁺—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COO⁻ and H₃N⁺—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COOH","H₃N⁺—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COOH and H₂N—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COO⁻","H₂N—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COO⁻ and H₃N⁺—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COOH","H₂N—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COO⁻ and H₃N⁺—CH(CH(CH₃)₂)—COO⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) zwitterion at pH 2 (wrong), cation at pH 10 (wrong). (B) correct. (C) fully reversed. (D) anion at pH 2 (wrong), zwitterion at pH 10 (wrong).\nWorking: at pH 2 both groups are protonated: H₃N⁺–CH(R)–COOH (A); at pH 10 both lose H⁺: H₂N–CH(R)–COO⁻ (B) – option (B).\nKey idea: Low pH protonates everything, high pH deprotonates everything.","traps":["Charge form of amino acid at low vs high pH: Acid (pH 2) protonates both groups: COOH and NH₃⁺; base (pH 10) removes both protons: COO⁻ and NH₂.","Zwitterion assumed to persist at every pH: Valine pI is about 6; pH 2 and 10 lie well on either side."]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"Identify the number of structure/s from the following which can be correlated to D-glyceraldehyde.\n(Fischer projections, written top to bottom)\n(A) CHO | H–C–OH | H–C–OH | CH₂OH\n(B) CHO | H–C–OH | HO–C–H | H–C–OH | CH₂OH\n(C) CHO | H–C–OH | HO–C–H | HO–C–H | CH₂OH\n(D) CH₂OH | HO–C–H | H–C–OH | HO–C–H | CHO","options":["one","two","three","four"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A D (tetrose, OH right). B D (lowest OH right). C L (lowest OH left). D D after 180 degree rotation. Count = 3. Wrong counts: 1 (only all-right structure), 2 (D rejected because inverted), 4 (C counted).\nWorking: look only at the stereocentre next to CH₂OH: A – OH right (D); B – OH right (D); C – OH left (L); D – after a 180° in-plane rotation the OH is on the right (D). So three structures (A, B, D) are D.\nKey idea: D/L depends only on the highest-numbered stereocentre, and an in-plane 180 degree rotation is allowed.","traps":["D/L assigned from lowest stereocentre OH in Fischer: Look only at the stereocentre next to the terminal CH₂OH.","Fischer projection drawn upside down: A Fischer projection may be rotated 180 degrees in plane; after rotation the centre next to CH₂OH has OH on the right: D."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which of the following is the correct structure of L-Glucose? (Fischer projections, CHO at top, CH₂OH at bottom; OH positions on C2, C3, C4, C5 listed top to bottom)","options":["C2 right, C3 left, C4 right, C5 left","C2 left, C3 left, C4 left, C5 left","C2 left, C3 left, C4 right, C5 right","C2 left, C3 right, C4 left, C5 left"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): D-glucose with only C5 flipped (an L-sugar but not glucose – L-idose). (B): all-left. (C): unrelated pattern.\nWorking: D-glucose has OH R, L, R, R at C2–C5; its mirror image L-glucose has L, R, L, L – option (D).\nKey idea: Enantiomer = invert every stereocentre; checking C5 alone is not enough.","traps":["Only C5 inverted: L-glucose is the full mirror image of D-glucose."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomers & glycosides","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I: A. α-Glucose and α-Galactose; B. α-Glucose and β-Glucose; C. α-Glucose and α-Fructose; D. α-Glucose and α-Ribose\nList II: I. Functional isomers; II. Homologous; III. Anomers; IV. Epimers\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) swap A and B: students confuse epimer (any one carbon, here C-4) with anomer (C-1 only). (A)/(D) put fructose as 'homologous' and ribose as 'functional isomer', ignoring that ribose has a different formula (C₅H₁₀O₅).\nWorking: glucose and galactose differ only at C-4 (epimers, IV); α and β glucose differ at C-1 (anomers, III); glucose (aldose) and fructose (ketose) share C₆H₁₂O₆ (functional isomers, I); glucose (aldohexose) and ribose (aldopentose) differ by one CHOH unit – loosely 'homologous' (II), the only label left.\nKey idea: Anomers differ at the anomeric C-1; epimers at one other stereocentre; same formula + different group = functional isomers.","traps":["Epimers vs anomers swapped: Anomer = C-1 of the cyclic form only.","Ribose called functional isomer: Check molecular formula: C₅ vs C₆."],"topics":["Anomers & glycosides","Glucose structure"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following gives a positive test with ninhydrin?","options":["Polyvinyl chloride","Cellulose","Egg albumin","Starch"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): carbohydrates (Molisch/iodine tests), no N. (A): synthetic polymer, no amino group.\nWorking: ninhydrin reacts with free α-amino groups of amino acids/proteins (purple colour); egg albumin is a protein; PVC, cellulose and starch have no amino groups.\nKey idea: Ninhydrin → free –NH₂ of amino acids/proteins → purple (Ruhemann’s purple).","traps":["Biomolecule tests mixed up: Ninhydrin = amino acids/proteins; iodine = starch."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Denaturation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Coagulation of egg, on heating is because of:","options":["Denaturation of protein occurs","Biological property of protein remains unchanged","Breaking of the peptide linkage in the primary structure of protein occurs","The secondary structure of protein remains unchanged"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) peptide bonds are not broken; (B)/(D) contradict denaturation.\nWorking: heat unfolds secondary/tertiary structure (denaturation), primary structure intact, biological activity lost.\nKey idea: Denaturation keeps primary structure, destroys 2°/3°.","traps":["Peptide bonds thought broken: Only H-bonds etc. break; primary structure stays."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"DNA molecule contains 4 bases whose structure are shown below. One of the structures is not correct, identify the incorrect base structure.","options":["5-methylpyrimidine-2,4-dione [thymine: C=O at C2 and C4, CH₃ at C5]","4-amino-pyrimidin-2-one [cytosine]","6-aminopurine [adenine]","purine with C=O at C6, NH₂ at C2 and a CH₃ on C8 of the imidazole ring [guanine drawn with an extra methyl]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Thymine's methyl tempts students to mark it wrong (confusing with uracil). (B)/(C) correct structures; unfamiliar drawings tempt random choice.\nWorking: thymine, cytosine and adenine are drawn correctly; guanine has H (not CH₃) at C8, so (D) is wrong.\nKey idea: Only thymine carries a methyl among DNA bases.","traps":["Thymine's methyl taken as the error: Thymine = 5-methyluracil.","Extra methyl on guanine missed: Check each substituent against NCERT figure."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enzymes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The incorrect statements regarding enzymes are :\n(A) Enzymes are biocatalysts.\n(B) Enzymes are non-specific and can catalyse different kinds of reactions.\n(C) Most Enzymes are globular proteins.\n(D) Enzyme - oxidase catalyses the hydrolysis of maltose into glucose.\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below :","options":["(B) and (D)","(A), (B) and (C)","(B) and (C)","(B), (C) and (D)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (B), (C) and (D) each include statement (A) or (C), which are true NCERT statements.\nWorking: enzymes are very specific (B false); maltose → glucose is catalysed by maltase, not oxidase (D false); A and C are NCERT statements.\nKey idea: Read each NCERT line for the changed word.","traps":["Oxidase accepted for maltose hydrolysis: Enzymes are named after substrate: maltose → maltase.","Selecting true statements in an \"incorrect\" question: Mark each as T/F, then choose the Fs."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"[Fischer projection: CHO at top; C2 H–OH (OH right); C3 HO–H (OH left); C4 H–OH; C5 H–OH; CH₂OH at bottom – D-glucose]\nThe incorrect statement regarding the given structure is","options":["can be oxidized to a dicarboxylic acid with Br₂ water","despite the presence of –CHO does not give Schiff's test","will coexist in equilibrium with 2 other cyclic structures","has 4 asymmetric carbon atoms"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) are NCERT \"limitations of open chain\" facts that look false at first; (D) true for the open chain (4 chiral C).\nWorking: Br₂ water gives gluconic acid (monocarboxylic); HNO₃ is needed for saccharic acid. B, C, D are true NCERT facts for D-glucose (open chain has C2–C5 chiral).\nKey idea: Br₂ water → gluconic (mono-acid); HNO₃ → saccharic (di-acid).","traps":["Br₂ water confused with HNO₃: Mild oxidant attacks only CHO.","Schiff's test statement thought false: NCERT lists this as an anomaly of glucose."],"topics":["Glucose structure","Glucose reactions"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The total number of carbon atoms present in tyrosine, an amino acid, is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 if only side chain counted; 8 forgetting the α-carbon or COOH.\nWorking: tyrosine HO-C₆H₄-CH₂-CH(NH₂)-COOH = C₉H₁₁NO₃, 9 carbons.\nKey idea: Backbone gives 2 C + side chain 7 C.","traps":["Only side chain counted: Add α-C and COOH."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Total number of essential amino acid among the given list of amino acids is ____.\nArginine, Phenylalanine, Aspartic acid, Cysteine, Histidine, Valine, Proline","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if arginine and histidine are omitted (often called semi-essential); 3 if one is dropped; 5 if cysteine is added.\nWorking: The NCERT amino-acid table marks arginine, phenylalanine, histidine and valine as essential = 4.\nKey idea: Go by NCERT asterisks: Arg and His are marked essential.","traps":["Arginine/histidine status: NCERT table marks both with *."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The incorrect statement about Glucose is :","options":["Glucose remains in multiple isomeric form in its aqueous solution","Glucose is soluble in water because of having aldehyde functional group","Glucose is an aldohexose","Glucose is one of the monomer unit in sucrose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks doubtful but anomers/open chain co-exist. (C)/(D) are direct NCERT facts.\nWorking: solubility comes from five –OH groups hydrogen bonding with water; the other three statements are true (α/β anomers and open chain in water).\nKey idea: Water solubility of sugars is due to many –OH groups (H-bonding).","traps":["Wrong reason for solubility: Hydrogen bonding through –OH groups.","Multiple forms in solution doubted: Mutarotation: α, β and open chain."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"If one strand of a DNA has the sequence ATGCTTCA, sequence of the bases in complementary strand is :","options":["CATTAGCT","TACGAAGT","ATGCGACT","GTACTTAC"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): pairs some bases wrongly / reads in reverse. (A),(C): random sequences; (C) partly copies the original strand.\nWorking: A↔T, G↔C pairing: ATGCTTCA → TACGAAGT.\nKey idea: A–T, G–C, letter by letter.","traps":["Copying instead of complementing: Replace each base with its partner."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of tripeptides formed by three different amino acids using each amino acid once is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (treating composition only), 3 (choosing positions), 27 (repeats allowed).\nWorking: sequences of three different residues used once each = 3! = 6; peptides are directional (N→C).\nKey idea: Peptides have N- and C-terminus, so every permutation is distinct: 3! = 6.","traps":["Order of residues ignored: Ala-Gly-Val and Val-Gly-Ala differ (N- and C-ends).","Repetition allowed: Use permutations without repetition."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Two nucleotides are joined together by a linkage known as :","options":["Phosphodiester linkage","Glycosidic linkage","Disulphide linkage","Peptide linkage"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Glycosidic: joins the base to the sugar inside one nucleotide (or monosaccharides in carbohydrates). (C) Disulphide: cysteine–cysteine link in proteins. (D) Peptide: amide link between amino acids.\nWorking: the phosphate links C-5′ of one nucleotide to C-3′ of the next sugar through two ester bonds (phosphodiester).\nKey idea: Base–sugar = glycosidic; nucleotide–nucleotide = phosphodiester.","traps":["Glycosidic bond mistaken as inter-nucleotide link: Remember: glycosidic is within the nucleoside; phosphate bridges join nucleotides."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Denaturation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which structure of protein remains intact after coagulation of egg white on boiling?","options":["Quaternary","Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are higher-order structures held by H-bonds/other weak forces that are disrupted on heating.\nWorking: coagulation of egg white is denaturation; secondary and tertiary structures are destroyed, primary structure (peptide sequence) remains.\nKey idea: Denaturation breaks only non-covalent interactions; peptide bonds survive.","traps":["Secondary structure thought to survive: Only covalent peptide sequence (primary) survives denaturation."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Type of amino acids obtained by hydrolysis of proteins is :","options":["γ","β","α","δ"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) are other positional classes NCERT names alongside α.\nWorking: proteins are polymers of α-amino acids.\nKey idea: Proteins → α-amino acids only.","traps":["Other Greek-letter classes: Only α-amino acids are obtained from proteins."],"topics":["Amino acids","Protein structure"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList-I (Bio Polymer): (A) Starch (B) Cellulose (C) Nucleic acid (D) Protein\nList-II (Monomer): (I) nucleotide (II) α-glucose (III) β-glucose (IV) α-amino acid\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong at A, B and D (gives α-glucose to cellulose). (B) gives nucleotide to starch. (C) swaps cellulose and nucleic acid monomers.\nWorking: starch – α-D-glucose; cellulose – β-D-glucose; nucleic acid – nucleotides; protein – α-amino acids.\nKey idea: Starch α, cellulose β; nucleic acid = polynucleotide; protein = poly-α-amino acid.","traps":["Starch vs cellulose anomer: Cellulose = β-1,4 links (indigestible to humans); starch = α."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Sugar which does not give reddish brown precipitate with Fehling's reagent, is :","options":["Glucose","Sucrose","Lactose","Maltose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Glucose: free aldehyde → reducing. (C) Lactose and (D) Maltose: one free hemiacetal → reducing.\nWorking: in sucrose both anomeric carbons (C1 of glucose, C2 of fructose) are in the glycosidic bond, so there is no free hemiacetal – it is non-reducing.\nKey idea: Sucrose is the standard non-reducing sugar.","traps":["All disaccharides assumed non-reducing: Only sucrose has both anomeric carbons locked."],"topics":["Disaccharides","Tests for carbohydrates"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleic acid functions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The total number of correct statements, regarding the nucleic acids is _________.\nA. RNA is regarded as the reserve of genetic information\nB. DNA molecule self-duplicates during cell division\nC. DNA synthesizes proteins in the cell\nD. The message for the synthesis of particular proteins is present in DNA\nE. Identical DNA strands are transferred to daughter cells.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (accepting C because 'protein synthesis is a function of nucleic acids'), 2 (rejecting D), 5 (accepting A).\nWorking: B, D and E are correct (NCERT wording); A is false (DNA, not RNA, is the reserve of genetic information); C is false (proteins are synthesised by RNA molecules; DNA carries the message).\nKey idea: DNA stores and carries the message; RNA makes the proteins.","traps":["DNA 'synthesizes' proteins: Proteins are made by RNA; DNA holds the message.","RNA as reserve of genetic information: Reserve of genetic information = DNA."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I: (A) Glucose/NaHCO₃/Δ (B) Glucose/HNO₃ (C) Glucose/HI/Δ (D) Glucose/Bromine water\nList-II: (I) Gluconic acid (II) No reaction (III) n-hexane (IV) Saccharic acid\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) give HI → gluconic acid or NaHCO₃ → n-hexane/saccharic – pure misreads. (D) swaps A and D: a student who thinks NaHCO₃ test is positive (as for acids) puts gluconic acid against it and 'no reaction' against bromine water.\nWorking: glucose has no –COOH, so NaHCO₃ gives no reaction (II); HNO₃ oxidises both ends → saccharic acid (IV); prolonged HI/Δ → n-hexane (III); bromine water (mild) oxidises only –CHO → gluconic acid (I).\nKey idea: Br₂ water oxidises only CHO (gluconic); HNO₃ oxidises CHO and CH₂OH (saccharic); NaHCO₃ needs a free –COOH.","traps":["Gluconic vs saccharic acid swapped: Mild oxidant → mono-acid; HNO₃ → di-acid.","Glucose assumed to react with NaHCO₃: Glucose has no –COOH; NaHCO₃ test is for acids."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"From the vitamins A, B₁, B₆, B₁₂, C, D, E and K, the number of vitamins that can be stored in our body is ________.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (forgets B₁₂ exception); 3 (also drops one fat-soluble); 8 (assumes all stored).\nWorking: fat-soluble A, D, E, K are stored in liver and adipose tissue, and B₁₂ is the stated exception among water-soluble vitamins → 5.\nKey idea: Fat-soluble (A, D, E, K) + B₁₂ = 5.","traps":["B₁₂ exception: Recall NCERT parenthesis: except vitamin B₁₂."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enzymes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Enzymatic reaction): A. Sucrose → Glucose and Fructose B. Glucose → ethyl alcohol and CO₂ C. Starch → Maltose D. Proteins → Amino acids\nLIST II (Enzyme): I. Zymase II. Pepsin III. Invertase IV. Diastase\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps diastase and pepsin (C/D shifted). (C)/(D) put zymase on sucrose, mixing the two fermentation enzymes.\nWorking: invertase hydrolyses sucrose; zymase ferments glucose to ethanol + CO₂; diastase converts starch to maltose; pepsin hydrolyses proteins.\nKey idea: Invertase → sucrose; zymase → glucose to ethanol; diastase → starch; pepsin → protein.","traps":["Invertase vs zymase order: Invertase splits sucrose first; zymase then ferments.","Diastase vs pepsin swap: Pepsin = stomach protease; diastase = starch (malt) enzyme."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Vitamin): A. Vitamin A B. Thiamine C. Ascorbic acid D. Riboflavin\nLIST II (Deficiency disease): I. Beri-Beri II. Cheilosis III. Xerophthalmia IV. Scurvy\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps thiamine and riboflavin (B₁/B₂). (B)/(C) swap vitamin A and C diseases.\nWorking: vitamin A – xerophthalmia; B₁ thiamine – beri-beri; C ascorbic acid – scurvy; B₂ riboflavin – cheilosis.\nKey idea: Thiamine = B₁ (beri-beri), riboflavin = B₂ (cheilosis).","traps":["Thiamine vs riboflavin: B₁ thiamine → beri-beri; B₂ riboflavin → cheilosis."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Natural Amino Acid): A. Arginine; B. Aspartic acid; C. Asparagine; D. Alanine\nLIST II (One Letter Code): I. D; II. N; III. A; IV. R\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) - IV, (B) - I, (C) - III, (D) - II","(A) - III, (B) - I, (C) - II, (D) - IV","(A) - IV, (B) - I, (C) - II, (D) - III","(A) - I, (B) - III, (C) - IV, (D) - II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps asparagine and alanine (Asparagine → A by first letter); (B) gives Arginine A by first letter; (D) assigns D to arginine.\nWorking: Arginine R, Aspartic acid D, Asparagine N, Alanine A.\nKey idea: Only alanine uses its first letter; arginine R, aspartate D, asparagine N must be memorised.","traps":["First-letter guessing of codes: Learn exceptions: R (aRginine), D (asparDic), N (asparagiNe).","Asparagine vs aspartic acid: Asparagine (amide) = N, aspartic acid = D."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Sulphur (S) containing amino acids from the following are:\n(a) isoleucine (b) cysteine (c) lysine (d) methionine (e) glutamic acid","options":["b, d","a, d","b, c, e","a, b, c"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): isoleucine confused with methionine (both essential, similar names ending -ine). (C)/(D): lysine/glutamic acid wrongly assumed to carry S.\nWorking: cysteine (HS–CH₂–) and methionine (CH₃–S–CH₂CH₂–) contain sulphur; isoleucine, lysine, glutamic acid do not.\nKey idea: Only Cys and Met have S in the side chain.","traps":["Isoleucine mistaken for S-amino acid: Recall Met = CH₃–S–CH₂CH₂–."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I (Natural amino acid): A. Glutamic acid; B. Glutamine; C. Tyrosine; D. Tryptophan. List II (One letter code): I. Q; II. W; III. E; IV. Y. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives Glutamine Y and Tyrosine Q; (B) gives glutamic acid Y, glutamine E and tyrosine Q; (C) gives glutamic acid W and tryptophan E (initial-letter guesses fail).\nWorking: Glu = E, Gln = Q, Tyr = Y, Trp = W.\nKey idea: Glu/Gln pair: the acid is E, the amide is Q; Trp is W.","traps":["Glu and Gln codes confused: Acid = E (\"glu-E-tamic\"), amide = Q (\"Q-tamine\").","Initial-letter guess: T is threonine; Tyr = Y, Trp = W."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct representation in six membered pyranose form for the following sugar [X] is\nSugar [X] (Fischer projection, top to bottom):\nCHO\nHO–C–H\nHO–C–H\nH–C–OH\nH–C–OH\nCH₂OH","options":["Haworth (ring O at upper right, CH₂OH up at C-5): C-1 OH down; C-2 OH up; C-3 OH down; C-4 OH down","Haworth: C-1 OH down; C-2 OH up; C-3 OH down; C-4 OH up","Haworth: C-1 OH down; C-2 OH down; C-3 OH up; C-4 OH down","Haworth (CH₂OH up at C-5): C-1 OH down; C-2 OH up; C-3 OH up; C-4 OH down"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C3-OH down: wrong at C3 (that pattern is neither glucose nor mannose). (B) C4-OH up: galactose-like error at C4 and C3 down. (C) C2-OH down, C3-OH up, C4 down: this is glucose, the familiar NCERT pattern, not mannose.\nWorking: D-mannose has OH on the left at C2 and C3 and on the right at C4 and C5 (C5-O forms the ring). Left → up and right → down in the Haworth form (CH₂OH up at C5), so C2-OH up, C3-OH up, C4-OH down. Only (D) has this pattern.\nKey idea: Left in Fischer = up in Haworth. Mannose has OH left at C2 and C3, so both point up; C4 OH (right) points down.","traps":["Glucose pattern recalled instead: Translate each carbon of the given Fischer projection separately.","Left/right to up/down rule reversed: Use the rule: right = down, left = up (D-sugar, CH₂OH up)."],"topics":["Glucose structure","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Total number of tripeptides possible by mixing of valine and proline is ________","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (only tripeptides containing both amino acids, excluding Val-Val-Val and Pro-Pro-Pro); 2 or 3 (ignoring sequence); 4 (dipeptide count).\nWorking: each of 3 positions can be Val or Pro → 2³ = 8 (sequence matters; repetition allowed).\nKey idea: Order matters (N→C direction) and residues may repeat: 2 × 2 × 2.","traps":["Repetition allowed: Homo-tripeptides also form on mixing.","Sequence matters: N-terminus to C-terminus is directional."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Anomers & glycosides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match items of Row I with those of Row II. Row I (Haworth structures): A – glucopyranose ring, CH₂OH up at C5, anomeric C1 with OH down (H up); B – glucopyranose ring, CH₂OH up, anomeric C1 with OH up (H down); C – fructofuranose ring, HOCH₂ (C6) on left, anomeric C2 with CH₂OH up and OH down; D – fructofuranose ring, HOCH₂ on left, anomeric C2 with OH up and CH₂OH down. Row II: (i) α-D-(−)-Fructofuranose (ii) β-D-(−)-Fructofuranose (iii) α-D-(−)-Glucopyranose (iv) β-D-(−)-Glucopyranose. Correct match is","options":["A → iii, B → iv, C → i, D → ii","A → iv, B → iii, C → i, D → ii","A → i, B → ii, C → iii, D → iv","A → iii, B → iv, C → ii, D → i"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps α/β for glucose (reads OH-down as β). (C) ignores ring size and matches in drawing order. (D) gets glucose right but swaps α/β for fructose, where the anomeric C carries both OH and CH₂OH.\nWorking: A: glucopyranose with C1-OH down (trans to CH₂OH) = α → (iii); B: C1-OH up = β → (iv); C: fructofuranose with anomeric OH down = α → (i); D: anomeric OH up = β → (ii). (Row II labels glucopyranose as (−); glucose is actually dextrorotatory, D-(+), but this does not affect the match.)\nKey idea: In a D-sugar Haworth: anomeric OH trans to the terminal CH₂OH (down) = α; same side (up) = β. For fructose look at the OH on C2, not the CH₂OH on C2.","traps":["α/β swapped for fructofuranose: Judge α/β from the anomeric OH: OH down (opposite the C6 CH₂OH) = α.","α/β swapped for glucopyranose: α-D-glucopyranose has the C1 OH down, trans to CH₂OH."],"topics":["Anomers & glycosides","Fructose"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Number of cyclic tripeptides formed with 2 amino acids A and B is :","options":["2","3","5","4"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2 counts only mixed rings (AAB, ABB) excluding homo-tripeptides; (B) 3 drops one homo-ring or counts one mixed; (C) 5 or higher from treating rotations/directions as different or counting linear chains (8 linear).\nWorking: with two amino acids the possible compositions are AAA, BBB, AAB and ABB; in a three-membered ring all rotations (AAB = ABA = BAA) are the same ring, so each composition gives one cyclic tripeptide → 4.\nKey idea: In a 3-membered ring every rotation of AAB is the same ring, so each composition gives one ring.","traps":["Whether AAA and BBB count: The accepted answer counts all compositions including homo-peptides.","Rotations in a ring: For a 3-ring, rotations are identical."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Following tetrapeptide can be represented as\n[Structure: tetrapeptide drawn N→C left to right. Residue 1: side chain CH₂Ph (phenylalanine); the N-terminal part of this residue is drawn incompletely (the H₂N and its C=O are not clearly drawn, only a zig-zag with a wedge to CH₂Ph). Residue 2: –NH–CH(CH₂CH(CH₃)₂)–C(=O)– (leucine, isobutyl side chain on hashed bond). Residue 3: –N–CH(CH₂COOH)–C(=O)– (aspartic acid, CH₂COOH on wedge). Residue 4: –NH–CH(CH₂-C₆H₄-OH(p))–COOH (tyrosine, C-terminal). Intended: Phe-Leu-Asp-Tyr.]\n(F, L, D, Y, I, Q, P are one letter codes for amino acids)","options":["YQLF","FIQY","PLDY","FLDY"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) YQLF reads C→N and mislabels Asp as Q; (B) FIQY confuses leucine with isoleucine and Asp with Gln; (C) PLDY reads the benzyl residue as proline (P).\nWorking: reading N → C: residue 1 CH₂Ph = Phe (F); residue 2 isobutyl = Leu (L); residue 3 CH₂COOH = Asp (D); residue 4 p-hydroxybenzyl = Tyr (Y) → FLDY.\nKey idea: Isobutyl (CH₂CH(CH₃)₂) = Leu, sec-butyl = Ile; CH₂COOH = Asp (D), CH₂CH₂CONH₂ = Gln (Q).","traps":["Leucine vs isoleucine: Isobutyl branch at the far carbon = Leu.","Asp (D) vs Gln (Q): Asp side chain is CH₂COOH; Gln is CH₂CH₂CONH₂."],"topics":["Peptide bond","Amino acids"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Assertion (A): Ketoses give Seliwanoff's test faster than Aldoses. Reason (R): Ketoses undergo β-elimination followed by formation of furfural. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"D: accepts 'furfural' as the product; for hexoses the product is 5-hydroxymethylfurfural. B: both true but unlinked. A: treats the Assertion (well-known fact) as false.\nWorking: ketohexoses dehydrate faster to 5-hydroxymethylfurfural, not furfural, which comes from pentoses; this wording makes R false. A reader who accepts 'furfural derivative' as loose wording would pick (D).\nKey idea: Ketoses dehydrate faster (Assertion true); the dehydration product of a ketohexose is 5-HMF, so the Reason as written is false.","traps":["Furfural vs hydroxymethylfurfural: Hexoses give 5-hydroxymethylfurfural; pentoses give furfural.","Test outside NCERT: Learn the standard carbohydrate colour tests (Molisch, Seliwanoff, Benedict) as a set."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"A short peptide on complete hydrolysis produces 3 moles of glycine (G), two moles of leucine (L) and two moles of valine (V) per mole of peptide. The number of peptide linkages in it are ____.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 (counting residues), 3 (distinct amino acids), 2.\nWorking: 3 + 2 + 2 = 7 residues in a linear peptide ⇒ 7 − 1 = 6 peptide bonds.\nKey idea: Linear peptide of n amino acids has n − 1 peptide bonds: 3 + 2 + 2 = 7 ⇒ 6.","traps":["Residues vs linkages: Subtract 1 for a linear chain."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"A protein 'X' with molecular weight of 70,000 u, on hydrolysis gives amino acids. One of these amino acids is","options":["NH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂COOH (5-amino-4-methylpentanoic acid, a δ-amino acid)","CH₃–C(CH₃)(NH₂)–CH₂–CH₂COOH (4-amino-4-methylpentanoic acid, a γ-amino acid)","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH(NH₂)–COOH (leucine, an α-amino acid)","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(NH₂)–CH₂–COOH (3-amino-4-methylpentanoic acid, a β-amino acid)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) δ-amino acid. (B) γ-amino acid with tertiary-carbon NH₂. (D) β-amino acid (β-homovaline type). All are C₆H₁₃NO₂ isomers of leucine, so formula cannot separate them.\nWorking: proteins are polymers of α-amino acids. Only in (C), leucine, is NH₂ on the carbon next to COOH (α); in (A), (B) and (D) it is on the δ, γ and β carbon.\nKey idea: Only α-amino acids (NH₂ on the carbon next to COOH) come from proteins.","traps":["Molar mass 70,000 u looks important: Recognise it as decoration; the point is α-position of NH₂.","All options are isomers: Check which carbon bears NH₂: must be adjacent to COOH."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Compound A, C₅H₁₀O₅, gives a tetraacetate with Ac₂O and oxidation of A with Br₂–H₂O gives an acid, C₅H₁₀O₆. Reduction of A with HI gives isopentane. The possible structure of A is: [options are structures]","options":["HOH₂C–CO–CH(CH₂OH)–CH(OH)₂ (ketone with branched carbon; drawn as HOH₂C–C(=O)–CH(–CH(OH)OH)(–CH₂OH))","OHC–CH(OH)–C(OH)(CH₂OH)₂ (branched aldopentose: CHO, CHOH, then a carbon bearing OH and two CH₂OH)","OHC–(CHOH)₃–CH₂OH (straight-chain aldopentose)","HOCH₂–CO–C(OH)(CH₂OH)₂ (branched ketose: CH₂OH, C=O, then a carbon bearing OH and two CH₂OH)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ketone, and only 3 free OH groups plus a gem-diol — fails Br₂ water test. (C) straight chain gives n-pentane, not isopentane. (D) ketose is not oxidised by bromine water to an acid with same carbons.\nWorking: tetraacetate → 4 OH; Br₂ water oxidises only –CHO → aldose; HI gives isopentane → branched carbon skeleton; only (B) fits.\nKey idea: Isopentane from HI means the carbon chain is branched; Br₂ water acid means an aldehyde.","traps":["Straight-chain aldopentose chosen by habit: Check the HI product: isopentane needs a branch.","Ketose vs aldose with bromine water: Bromine water oxidises only aldoses."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Denaturation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"During the denaturation of proteins, which of these structures will remain intact?","options":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Quaternary"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are disrupted by denaturation.\nWorking: denaturation disrupts H-bonds etc.; secondary and tertiary structures are destroyed but the primary structure (peptide sequence) remains intact.\nKey idea: Peptide bonds survive denaturation.","traps":["Which level survives: Only non-covalent interactions break; peptide bonds stay."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Glycosidic linkage between C1 of α-glucose and C2 of β-fructose is found in","options":["maltose","sucrose","lactose","amylose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) maltose α(1→4) glucose–glucose; (C) lactose β-galactose C1–glucose C4; (D) amylose α(1→4) glucose chain – none contain fructose.\nWorking: sucrose = α-D-glucose C1 – β-D-fructose C2 (both reducing ends tied up, non-reducing).\nKey idea: Only sucrose contains fructose among the options.","traps":["Disaccharide linkages mixed: Fructose present → sucrose."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Anomers & glycosides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following is reducing sugar?\n(A) Cyclic (pyranose) form drawn in Fischer-type projection with a free hemiacetal –OH on C-1 and ring O joining C-1 and C-5\n(B) Methyl furanoside (Haworth): CH₂OH-bearing furanose with OCH₃ on the anomeric carbon\n(C) Two furanose rings joined through an O bridge between their anomeric carbons\n(D) Open-chain Fischer projection with C-1 as CH(OCH₃)₂ (dimethyl acetal), H/OH pattern of glucose, CH₂OH at the bottom","options":["Cyclic hemiacetal (free anomeric OH)","Methyl glycoside (furanoside)","Disaccharide linked through both anomeric carbons","Open-chain dimethyl acetal of glucose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) the OCH₃ on C-1 looks like a simple substituent; (C) disaccharides are assumed reducing (like maltose); (D) open-chain sugar looks like it has an aldehyde.\nWorking: only (A) has a free hemiacetal OH that opens to a –CHO and reduces Tollens/Fehling. (B) and (D) are acetals (glycoside / dimethyl acetal); (C) has both anomeric carbons tied up in the glycosidic link.\nKey idea: Reducing = anomeric carbon with free OH (hemiacetal).","traps":["Open chain assumed reducing: C-1 bears two OCH₃ – it is an acetal, no free CHO.","All disaccharides reducing: Check whether both anomeric carbons are in the link."],"topics":["Anomers & glycosides","Disaccharides"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Animal starch is the other name of :","options":["amylose","maltose","glycogen","amylopectin"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) amylopectin is structurally similar to glycogen and tempts; (A) amylose is the other starch component; (B) maltose is a disaccharide.\nWorking: glycogen, the storage carbohydrate of animals, is called animal starch.\nKey idea: Glycogen = animal starch (structure like amylopectin, more branched).","traps":["Glycogen vs amylopectin: Similar structure ≠ same name; animal starch is glycogen."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"A sugar 'X' dehydrates very slowly under acidic condition to give furfural which on further reaction with resorcinol gives the coloured product after sometime. Sugar 'X' is","options":["Aldopentose","Aldotetrose","Oxalic acid","Ketotetrose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) ketotetrose: \"furfural\" suggests ketose test; (B) tetroses cannot form a furan aldehyde; (C) oxalic acid is not a sugar.\nWorking: furfural (not hydroxymethylfurfural) comes from a pentose; slow dehydration and slow colour with resorcinol (Seliwanoff) indicate an aldose rather than a ketose → aldopentose.\nKey idea: Pentose → furfural (C5); hexose → HMF; ketoses react fast, aldoses slowly.","traps":["Seliwanoff = ketose only: Slow reaction points to aldose; furfural points to pentose.","Carbon count of furfural: Furan ring + CHO needs a C5 sugar."],"topics":["Tests for carbohydrates","Carbohydrate classification"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Glucose + HI; (B) Glucose + Br₂ water; (C) Glucose + acetic anhydride; (D) Glucose + HNO₃. List-II: (I) Gluconic acid; (II) Glucose pentaacetate; (III) Saccharic acid; (IV) Hexane.","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the two oxidation products (gluconic vs saccharic acid); (C)/(D) shuffle further.\nWorking: prolonged heating with HI gives n-hexane; bromine water oxidises –CHO to gluconic acid; acetic anhydride gives glucose pentaacetate; HNO₃ oxidises both ends to saccharic acid.\nKey idea: Mild oxidant (Br₂ water) oxidises only CHO; strong HNO₃ oxidises CHO and CH₂OH.","traps":["Gluconic vs saccharic acid: Br₂ water: monocarboxylic; HNO₃: dicarboxylic."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"For the below given cyclic hemiacetal (X), the correct pyranose structure is: (X) Fischer projection of a cyclic aldohexose with ring O between C1 and C5: C1 (H, OH on left; ring O to the right), C2 HO–C–H (OH left), C3 H–C–OH (OH right), C4 HO–C–H (OH left), C5 H–C (ring O on the right), C6 CH₂OH (i.e. β-D-idopyranose).","options":["Haworth pyranose with CH₂OH up at C5; C4-OH down, C3-OH up, C2-OH down, C1-OH up","Haworth pyranose with CH₂OH up; C4-OH down, C3-OH down, C2-OH up, C1-OH up","Haworth pyranose with CH₂OH up; C4-OH down, C3-OH up, C2-OH down, C1-OH down","Haworth pyranose with CH₂OH up; C4-OH up, C3-OH down, C2-OH up, C1-OH up"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) invert C2–C4 (the glucose-like pattern); (B) inverts C4 only; (C) also makes C1 α.\nWorking: Fischer-left groups go up and right groups go down in the Haworth form (D-sugar, CH₂OH up). X has OH left at C1, C2, C4 and right at C3, so C1, C2, C4 OH up and C3 OH down → (D).\nKey idea: Left = up, right = down, checked at each carbon.","traps":["Left/right to up/down rule: Apply left → up, right → down carbon by carbon.","Numbering around the ring: Start at the carbon next to ring O on the right (C1) and move clockwise."],"topics":["Glucose structure","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Fructose","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The formulas of A and B for the following reaction sequence are: Fructose → (HCN, H₃O⁺) → A; Fructose → ((i) NaBH₄, (ii) HI/P) → B.","options":["A = C₇H₁₄O₈, B = C₆H₁₄","A = C₇H₁₃O₇, B = C₇H₁₄O","A = C₇H₁₂O₈, B = C₆H₁₄","A = C₇H₁₄O₈, B = C₆H₁₄O₆"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) has an impossible odd-H formula; (C) has two H too few (miscounted hydrolysis); (D) stops at the hexitol (ignores HI/P).\nWorking: C₆H₁₂O₆ + HCN → cyanohydrin C₇H₁₃NO₆; hydrolysis (–CN → –COOH: +2H₂O, –NH₃) gives C₇H₁₄O₈. NaBH₄ reduces C=O to give hexitol C₆H₁₄O₆; HI/P reduces all OH to give n-hexane C₆H₁₄.\nKey idea: Cyanohydrin hydrolysis adds one C and converts CN to COOH: C₇H₁₄O₈.","traps":["Stopping at hexitol: HI/P removes all OH → hydrocarbon.","Atom counting after hydrolysis: CN → COOH: add 2H₂O, remove NH₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"In a linear tetrapeptide (Constituted with different amino acids), (number of amino acids) − (number of peptide bonds) is ______.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (assuming 4 bonds, cyclic); 3 (number of bonds reported).\nWorking: a linear peptide of n amino acids has n − 1 peptide bonds; 4 − 3 = 1.\nKey idea: Linear: bonds = n − 1.","traps":["Cyclic count: Linear peptides have one fewer bond."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Amylose is insoluble in water. Reason R: Amylose is a long linear molecule with more than 200 glucose units. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) accept the false assertion (amylopectin is the insoluble one); (C) rejects the true reason.\nWorking: NCERT – amylose is the water-soluble component of starch (15–20%); it is a long unbranched chain of 200–1000 α-D-glucose units. A false, R true.\nKey idea: Amylose soluble; amylopectin insoluble.","traps":["Amylose vs amylopectin: Amylose = soluble, linear; amylopectin = insoluble, branched."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"A polysaccharide 'X' on boiling with dil H₂SO₄ at 393 K under 2–3 atm pressure yields 'Y'. 'Y' on treatment with bromine water gives gluconic acid. 'X' contains β-glycosidic linkages only. Compound 'X' is:","options":["starch","cellulose","amylose","amylopectin"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D) are all α-glucans; the hydrolysis conditions (from NCERT preparation of glucose from starch) tempt (A) starch.\nWorking: hydrolysis gives glucose (Y; Br₂ water → gluconic acid). A glucose polymer with only β-glycosidic links is cellulose; starch, amylose and amylopectin have α-links.\nKey idea: Only β-glycosidic links → cellulose.","traps":["Starch recalled from hydrolysis conditions: Use the decisive clue: β-linkages only."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In alanylglycylleucylalanylvaline, the number of peptide linkages is ____.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 – counting residues instead of linkages; 3 – missing one \"-yl\" unit while parsing.\nWorking: Ala-Gly-Leu-Ala-Val is a pentapeptide (5 residues), so 5 − 1 = 4 peptide (–CO–NH–) linkages.\nKey idea: Each \"-yl\" prefix marks one peptide bond: alanyl, glycyl, leucyl, alanyl → 4.","traps":["Residues counted instead of bonds: n residues give n − 1 peptide bonds; count the \"-yl\" prefixes."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enzymes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: A. Zymase; B. Diastase; C. Urease; D. Pepsin\nList II: I. Stomach; II. Yeast; III. Malt; IV. Soyabean\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) swap urease and pepsin (soyabean vs stomach); (C) and (D) swap zymase and diastase (yeast vs malt).\nWorking: zymase – yeast; diastase – malt; urease – soyabean; pepsin – stomach.\nKey idea: Fermentation pairs: diastase from malt (starch → maltose), zymase from yeast (glucose → ethanol); urease from soyabean.","traps":["Zymase vs diastase source: Malt → diastase (starch to maltose); yeast → zymase.","Urease vs pepsin: Pepsin is the stomach protease; urease from soyabean."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The number of oxygens present in a nucleotide formed from a base, that is present only in RNA is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 11 – adding base + ribose + H₃PO₄ with no water removed; 10 – removing water at only one link; 8 – using deoxyribose (DNA sugar).\nWorking: base only in RNA = uracil (2 O). Uridine (uracil + ribose, loss of water) has 2 + 4 = 6 O; the 5′-phosphate ester adds 3 more O (–O–PO₃H₂). Uridine monophosphate C₉H₁₃N₂O₉P has 9 O.\nKey idea: Base(2) + ribose(5) + H₃PO₄(4) – 2 H₂O lost = 9 O.","traps":["Water loss ignored: Each linkage removes one H₂O (one O).","Deoxyribose used: RNA sugar is ribose (2′-OH present)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Enzymes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Enzyme): A. Invertase; B. Zymase; C. Diastase; D. Maltase\nList II (Conversion of): I. Starch into maltose; II. Maltose into glucose; III. Glucose into ethanol; IV. Cane sugar into glucose\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) give invertase III – confuse invertase with zymase (both in yeast fermentation); (D) B-II, C-III swaps zymase and diastase roles.\nWorking: invertase hydrolyses sucrose (cane sugar) to glucose + fructose (A-IV); zymase ferments glucose to ethanol (B-III); diastase converts starch to maltose (C-I); maltase converts maltose to glucose (D-II).\nKey idea: Name hints: invert sugar from sucrose (invertase), maltose by maltase, diastase on starch, zymase for fermentation.","traps":["Invertase confused with zymase: Invertase only splits sucrose; zymase does the fermentation of glucose to ethanol.","Diastase and maltase order: Diastase acts on starch (gives maltose); maltase acts on maltose (gives glucose)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which one of the following is a water soluble vitamin, that is not excreted easily?","options":["Vitamin B₂","Vitamin B₁","Vitamin B₆","Vitamin B₁₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) – other B vitamins, all readily excreted.\nWorking: water-soluble vitamins are readily excreted and must be supplied regularly, except vitamin B₁₂, which is stored (in the liver).\nKey idea: B₁₂ is the exception among water-soluble vitamins.","traps":["Any B vitamin assumed stored: Remember the NCERT parenthesis: only B₁₂ is stored."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"L-isomer of a compound 'A' (C₄H₈O₄) gives a positive test with [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. Treatment of 'A' with acetic anhydride yields a triacetate derivative. Compound 'A' produces an optically active compound (B) and an optically inactive compound (C) on treatment with bromine water and HNO₃ respectively. Compound (A) is: [four Fischer projections, CHO at top, CH₂OH at bottom] (A) C2: HO–H, C3: HO–H; (B) C2: H–OH, C3: HO–H; (C) C2: H–OH, C3: H–OH; (D) C2: HO–H, C3: H–OH.","options":["Fischer: CHO / HO–C–H / HO–C–H / CH₂OH (L-erythrose)","Fischer: CHO / H–C–OH / HO–C–H / CH₂OH (L-threose)","Fischer: CHO / H–C–OH / H–C–OH / CH₂OH (D-erythrose)","Fischer: CHO / HO–C–H / H–C–OH / CH₂OH (D-threose)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – L-threose gives optically active tartaric acid with HNO₃; (C) – erythrose but D (OH right at C3); (D) – D-threose, both wrong.\nWorking: Tollens positive + three OH → aldotetrose. HNO₃ gives the dicarboxylic acid; it is optically inactive only if meso, i.e. both OH on the same side (erythrose). Bromine water gives the chiral erythronic acid (active). L means the OH on the last chiral carbon (C3) is on the left → both OH left = (A).\nKey idea: Optically inactive aldaric acid → meso → erythro configuration; then pick L (C3-OH on left).","traps":["D/L read from C2: D/L is set by the chiral carbon farthest from CHO.","Meso product not checked: Draw the diacid: same-side OH gives a mirror plane (meso)."],"topics":["Glucose structure","Glucose reactions"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: Maltose has two α-D-glucose units linked at C₁ and C₄ and is a reducing sugar. Statement II: Maltose has two monosaccharides: α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose linked at C₁ and C₆ and it is a non-reducing sugar. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts II; (D) rejects the true NCERT line; (B) doubts the reducing nature.\nWorking: maltose = two α-D-glucose units joined C1–C4 (α-glycosidic); the free hemiacetal at C1 of the second unit makes it reducing. Statement II is wrong on linkage, anomer and reducing nature.\nKey idea: Maltose: α(1→4), free anomeric OH, reducing.","traps":["C1–C4 vs C1–C6 linkage: Maltose is α(1→4); 1→6 is the amylopectin branch point."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Protein structure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Stability of α-Helix structure of proteins depends upon","options":["dipolar interaction","H-bonding interaction","van der Waals forces","π-stacking interaction"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C) are weaker general forces; (D) π-stacking belongs to nucleic acid bases.\nWorking: the α-helix is held by intramolecular hydrogen bonds between the C=O of one residue and the N–H of the residue four places along.\nKey idea: Secondary structure (α-helix, β-sheet) = backbone H-bonding.","traps":["Tertiary-structure forces confused: Secondary structure → H-bonds only."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"When sugar 'X' is boiled with dilute H₂SO₄ in alcoholic solution, two isomers 'A' and 'B' are formed. 'A' on oxidation with HNO₃ yields saccharic acid whereas 'B' is laevorotatory. The compound 'X' is:","options":["Maltose","Sucrose","Lactose","Starch"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) give only glucose (no laevorotatory isomer); (C) gives glucose + galactose.\nWorking: sucrose hydrolyses to two isomers, D-glucose (→ saccharic acid with HNO₃) and D-fructose (laevorotatory). Maltose and starch give only glucose; lactose gives glucose + galactose (galactose is dextrorotatory).\nKey idea: Laevorotatory hexose isomer of glucose = fructose → sucrose.","traps":["Lactose also gives two isomers: Galactose is dextrorotatory."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Sugar moiety in DNA and RNA molecules respectively are","options":["β-D-2-deoxyribose, β-D-deoxyribose","β-D-2-deoxyribose, β-D-ribose","β-D-ribose, β-D-2-deoxyribose","β-D-deoxyribose, β-D-2-deoxyribose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) reverses order; (A), (D) give deoxyribose for both.\nWorking: NCERT – in DNA the sugar is β-D-2-deoxyribose, in RNA β-D-ribose.\nKey idea: D in DNA = deoxy (no 2′-OH).","traps":["Order reversed: Read order DNA first, RNA second."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Denaturation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The structure of protein that is unaffected by heating is:","options":["secondary structure","tertiary structure","primary structure","quaternary structure"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Other levels depend on weak interactions destroyed on heating.\nWorking: denaturation by heat disrupts H-bonds etc., uncoiling secondary and tertiary (and quaternary) structure, while the peptide-bond sequence (primary structure) stays intact.\nKey idea: Covalent peptide bonds survive denaturation.","traps":["Secondary structure thought stable: H-bonds break on heating."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The total number of negative charge in the tetrapeptide, Gly-Glu-Asp-Tyr, at pH 12.5 will be ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (ignoring Tyr phenol); 5 (counting a COO⁻ for every residue); 2 (counting only side chains).\nWorking: at pH 12.5 the C-terminal (Tyr) COOH → COO⁻, Glu and Asp side-chain COOH → COO⁻, Tyr phenolic OH (pKa ≈ 10.1) → O⁻; the N-terminal amine is neutral. Total 4.\nKey idea: At very high pH every acidic H (COOH and phenolic OH) is gone; peptide-bonded COOH groups do not count.","traps":["Tyr phenol ionises at pH 12.5: Phenolic OH (pKa ≈ 10) is deprotonated above pH 10.","Count only free carboxyls: Charges: C-terminal COO⁻ + Glu and Asp side chains + Tyr O⁻ = 4; N-terminal NH₂ neutral."],"topics":["Amino acids","Peptide bond"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Sucrose is a disaccharide and a non-reducing sugar.\nReason (R): Sucrose involves glycosidic linkage between C₁ of β-glucose and C₂ of α-fructose.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the true explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true.","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the true explanation of (A)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) chosen by those who read the familiar C1–C2 linkage and miss the α/β swap; (A) also needs R true; (B) for those who forget sucrose is non-reducing.\nWorking: A is true. R is false: the linkage is between C1 of α-D-glucose and C2 of β-D-fructose (anomers swapped in R).\nKey idea: Sucrose = α-D-glucopyranose C1 – β-D-fructofuranose C2.","traps":["α-glucose, β-fructose in sucrose: Memorise: α-Glc(1→2)β-Fru."],"topics":["Disaccharides","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Out of following isomeric forms of uracil, which one is present in RNA? (Four tautomers of the pyrimidine ring are drawn.)","options":["2,4-dihydroxypyrimidine (lactim–lactim: OH at C-2 and C-4, no N–H)","4-hydroxy-pyrimidin-2(1H)-one (C-2 C=O with N1–H, OH at C-4)","2-hydroxy-pyrimidin-4(3H)-one (C-4 C=O with N3–H, OH at C-2)","pyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione (both C=O, N1–H and N3–H; diketo/lactam form)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) aromatic dihydroxy (lactim) form – looks 'more aromatic'; (B) and (C) mixed lactam–lactim forms, each one C=O away from the true structure.\nWorking: uracil exists in nucleic acids in the diketo (lactam) form, pyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione, with N1 bonded to ribose and N3–H available for base pairing with adenine.\nKey idea: Nucleobases exist as lactams (C=O and N–H), which is what allows Watson–Crick H-bonding.","traps":["Aromatic OH form preferred: Bases in DNA/RNA are in keto (lactam) form.","Mixed tautomers: Uracil has two C=O and two ring N–H (one becomes N–C1′ in nucleoside)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Hydrolysis of sucrose gives:","options":["α-D-(−)-Glucose and β-D-(−)-Fructose","α-D-(+)-Glucose and α-D-(−)-Fructose","α-D-(−)-Glucose and α-D-(+)-Fructose","α-D-(+)-Glucose and β-D-(−)-Fructose"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) give glucose as (−); (B)/(C) give fructose as α instead of β.\nWorking: sucrose is α-D-glucopyranose C1 linked to β-D-fructofuranose C2; hydrolysis releases dextrorotatory D-(+)-glucose and laevorotatory D-(−)-fructose (invert sugar).\nKey idea: Glucose α and (+); fructose β and (−).","traps":["α/β of fructose unit: Sucrose: α-glucose C1 – β-fructose C2.","Sign of rotation: D-glucose is dextrorotatory (+); D-fructose laevorotatory (−)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which one of the following compounds contains β-C₁–C₄ glycosidic linkage?","options":["Lactose","Sucrose","Maltose","Amylose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) sucrose – C1–C2 linkage; (C) maltose – α-1,4 (correct positions, wrong anomer); (D) amylose – α-1,4 chain.\nWorking: lactose = β-D-galactose C1 linked to C4 of D-glucose (β-1,4). Sucrose is α-C1–β-C2; maltose and amylose are α-1,4. Answer (A).\nKey idea: Only lactose (and cellulose) use β-1,4; maltose/starch use α-1,4.","traps":["α vs β 1,4: Check anomer: maltose α, lactose β.","Amylose linkage: Amylose (starch) is α-1,4; cellulose is β-1,4."],"topics":["Disaccharides","Polysaccharides"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Protein structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of fibrous protein?","options":["Keratin","Albumin","Collagen","Myosin"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Keratin and myosin are NCERT fibrous examples; collagen is fibrous though not named in NCERT, so an unsure student may pick it.\nWorking: albumin is a globular protein; keratin, collagen and myosin are fibrous.\nKey idea: Albumin and insulin are the NCERT globular examples.","traps":["Collagen unfamiliar: Collagen (connective tissue) is a classic fibrous protein."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Protein structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Out of the following, which type of interaction is responsible for the stabilisation of α-helix structure of proteins?","options":["Ionic bonding","Hydrogen bonding","Covalent bonding","van der Waals forces"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – covalent peptide/disulphide bonds belong to primary/tertiary structure; (A)/(D) – tertiary-structure forces.\nWorking: the α-helix is held by N–H···O=C hydrogen bonds between residues in adjacent turns.\nKey idea: Secondary structure = hydrogen bonds.","traps":["Structure levels mixed: Primary – peptide; secondary – H-bond; tertiary – H-bond, disulphide, ionic, van der Waals."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the glycosidic linkage between galactose and glucose is present in lactose?","options":["C-1 of galactose and C-4 of glucose","C-1 of glucose and C-6 of galactose","C-1 of glucose and C-4 of galactose","C-1 of galactose and C-6 of glucose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps the sugars; (B)/(D) use C-6 (as in amylopectin branches).\nWorking: lactose = β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose joined C1 (galactose) – O – C4 (glucose).\nKey idea: Galactose supplies the anomeric C1; glucose gives C4 and keeps a free anomeric C (reducing).","traps":["Sugars swapped: Lactose is reducing via glucose; galactose C1 is tied up.","C-6 linkage: Disaccharides in NCERT are 1→4 (maltose, lactose) or 1→2 (sucrose)."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following is the correct structure of α-anomer of maltose? (Four Haworth-type structures of two linked glucopyranose rings, C1 of the left ring joined through O to C4 of the right ring.)","options":["Both rings: C3–OH and C2–OH drawn down (C3 configuration wrong for glucose); reducing-end C1: OH up, H down (β)","Both rings: C3–OH up, C2–OH down (correct D-glucose); reducing-end C1: OH up, H down (β)","Both rings: C3–OH and C2–OH drawn down (C3 wrong); reducing-end C1: H up, OH down (α)","Both rings: C3–OH up, C2–OH down (correct D-glucose); reducing-end C1: H up, OH down (α)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) wrong C3 + β; (B) correct glucose but β at reducing end; (C) α at reducing end but C3–OH down (not glucose).\nWorking: maltose is α-D-Glc-(1→4)-D-Glc. In the Haworth form D-glucose has C2-OH down, C3-OH up, C4-OH down; the α-anomer of maltose has the anomeric OH of the second (reducing) unit down (trans to CH₂OH). Only option (D) has both correct ring configuration and α at the free C1.\nKey idea: Check two things: glucose ring pattern (down-up-down at C2-C3-C4) and the anomeric OH down for α.","traps":["β drawn instead of α: α = anomeric OH trans to CH₂OH (down in Haworth).","C3 configuration ignored: Verify every ring carbon against D-glucose."],"topics":["Disaccharides","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which of the following vitamin is helpful in delaying the blood clotting –","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin B","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Vitamin E – literal reading (anticoagulant effect) or its \"fragility of RBCs\" entry; (A) C – scurvy/bleeding gums; (B) B – no clotting role.\nWorking: vitamin K promotes clotting (its deficiency increases blood-clotting time, NCERT table). The question evidently means \"the vitamin related to blood clotting\". Vitamin E (mild anticoagulant) would fit the literal wording better.\nKey idea: Vitamin K ↔ blood clotting in every NCERT-level question.","traps":["Literal wording followed: Match the NCERT keyword \"blood clotting\" to vitamin K.","Bleeding linked to vitamin C: C → scurvy; K → clotting."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Sucrose (b) Lactose (c) Maltose. List-II: (i) β-D-Galactose and β-D-Glucose (ii) α-D-Glucose and β-D-Fructose (iii) α-D-Glucose and α-D-Glucose.","options":["(a) → (i), (b) → (iii), (c) → (ii)","(a) → (iii), (b) → (i), (c) → (ii)","(a) → (ii), (b) → (i), (c) → (iii)","(a) → (iii), (b) → (ii), (c) → (i)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) give sucrose as two α-glucose – confuses sucrose with maltose; (A) gives lactose as α-glucose units.\nWorking: sucrose = α-D-glucose + β-D-fructose; lactose = β-D-galactose + β-D-glucose; maltose = two α-D-glucose units.\nKey idea: Maltose = only glucose (α); lactose is the only one with galactose.","traps":["Sucrose and maltose swapped: Fructose is present only in sucrose."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct structure of Ruhemann's Purple, the compound formed in the reaction of ninhydrin with proteins is:","options":["Two indane rings joined through =N–; left ring: C1–O⁻, C3=O, C2=N; right ring: C1–O⁻, C3=O with sp³ C2 attached to N (dianion, no ring C=C)","Two indane rings joined through =N–; left ring: C1–O⁻, C3=O, C2=N; right ring: C1–O⁻ with ring C1=C2 double bond, C3=O (dianion)","Two phthalimide-type units (isoindoline-1,3-dione) joined by an N=N–N⁺ chain, with one O⁻ on the left unit","2-[(1,3-dioxo-1H-inden-2(3H)-ylidene)amino]-3-oxo-3H-inden-1-olate: left ring with two C=O and C2=N; right ring with C1–O⁻, C1=C2 double bond and C3=O (monoanion)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) look right at a glance but are dianions (two O⁻); (A) also lacks the ring C=C so C2 has wrong valence; (C) replaces the single =N– bridge by an N=N–N⁺ chain on phthalimide rings – a look-alike skeleton.\nWorking: Ruhemann's purple is the conjugated monoanion of 2-(1,3-dioxoindan-2-ylideneamino)indene-1,3-dione: one 1,3-dioxoindan-2-ylidene half (=N–) and one enolate half (O⁻–C=C–N). Options (A) and (B) carry two negative charges (and (A) has an impossible valence pattern at C2); (C) has an azo/triazene chain that is not formed.\nKey idea: Only one N (from the amino acid) bridges two ninhydrin-derived units; the product is a monoanion with one enolate half and one dione half.","traps":["Charge count on the dye: Count charges: one N links two units, overall −1.","Look-alike skeleton: Ninhydrin is indane-1,2,3-trione; the bridge is a single N."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Identify the incorrect statement from the following:","options":["Amylose is a branched chain polymer of glucose","Starch is a polymer of α-D glucose","β-Glycosidic linkage makes cellulose polymer","Glycogen is called as animal starch"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are near-verbatim NCERT facts; (C) may look wrong to students who forget that cellulose uses β-links.\nWorking: amylose is a long unbranched chain of α-D-glucose (C1–C4 links); amylopectin is the branched component. (B), (C), (D) are correct.\nKey idea: Amylose = linear (α-1,4); amylopectin and glycogen = branched.","traps":["Amylose vs amylopectin: Amylose linear, amylopectin branched.","Incorrect-statement stem: Mark T/F for each."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Enzymes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which one of the following statements is not true about enzymes ?","options":["Enzymes are non-specific for a reaction and substrate.","Almost all enzymes are proteins.","Enzymes work as catalysts by lowering the activation energy of a biochemical reaction.","The action of enzymes is temperature and pH specific"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)–(D) are true NCERT/biology facts; (D) 'temperature and pH specific' may look odd in wording but is true.\nWorking: enzymes are highly specific for a particular reaction and substrate, so (A) is false; (B)–(D) are standard true facts.\nKey idea: Specificity is the defining feature of enzymes.","traps":["Odd wording of a true statement: Enzymes have optimum T and pH – the statement is true."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Thiamine and pyridoxine are also known respectively as :","options":["Vitamin B₂ and Vitamin E","Vitamin E and Vitamin B₂","Vitamin B₆ and Vitamin B₂","Vitamin B₁ and Vitamin B₆"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"B₂ is riboflavin; E is tocopherol – distractors for weak recall.\nWorking: thiamine = vitamin B₁, pyridoxine = vitamin B₆.\nKey idea: Thiamine B₁, riboflavin B₂, pyridoxine B₆, cobalamin B₁₂.","traps":["B-vitamin names mixed: Learn B₁/B₂/B₆/B₁₂ in order."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Protein structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The water soluble protein is:","options":["Fibrin","Albumin","Myosin","Collagen"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are fibrous proteins (NCERT lists keratin and myosin; fibrin and collagen are other fibrous examples).\nWorking: albumin is a globular protein and globular proteins are usually water soluble; fibrin, myosin and collagen are fibrous and insoluble.\nKey idea: Globular = water soluble (albumin, insulin); fibrous = insoluble (keratin, myosin, collagen).","traps":["Fibrin vs fibrous: Fibrin is the insoluble clot protein; fibrinogen is the soluble precursor."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which one of the following is correct structure for cytosine?","options":["6-methylpyrimidin-2(1H)-one (CH₃ on C-6, next to N1–H; C-2 carbonyl)","thymine: 5-methylpyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione","4-aminopyrimidin-2(1H)-one (NH₂ on C-4, C=O at C-2, N1–H)","5-aminopyrimidin-2(1H)-one (NH₂ on C-5)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is thymine (another pyrimidine base); (D) is a positional isomer (NH₂ at C-5); (A) swaps NH₂ for CH₃.\nWorking: cytosine is 4-amino-pyrimidin-2(1H)-one; (B) is thymine, (D) puts NH₂ at C-5, (A) has CH₃ instead of NH₂.\nKey idea: Cytosine = pyrimidin-2-one with NH₂ at C-4 (next to the ring N3).","traps":["Thymine vs cytosine: Cytosine has NH₂, thymine has CH₃ and two C=O.","Position of NH₂: NH₂ sits on the carbon next to N3."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which one among the following chemical tests is used to distinguish monosaccharide from disaccharide?","options":["Seliwanoff's test","Iodine test","Barfoed test","Tollen's test"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Seliwanoff – ketose vs aldose; (B) iodine – starch; (D) Tollens – reducing vs non-reducing sugars (both mono and many disaccharides positive).\nWorking: Barfoed's reagent (Cu²⁺ acetate in dilute acetic acid) is reduced quickly by monosaccharides but only slowly by reducing disaccharides, so it separates them. Option (C).\nKey idea: Barfoed = mono vs di (rate of Cu₂O formation under mildly acidic conditions).","traps":["Tollens does not separate mono from di: Tollens tests reducing ends, not size.","Seliwanoff is aldose vs ketose: Map each test to its purpose."],"topics":["Tests for carbohydrates","Carbohydrate classification"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"[Structure A: pyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione ring with CH₃ at C-5 and H at C-6, N1–H and N3–H]\nThe compound 'A' is a complementary base of __________ in DNA strands.","options":["Uracil","Guanine","Adenine","Cytosine"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uracil – the base A resembles (minus CH₃) and replaces thymine in RNA; (B) guanine pairs with cytosine; (D) cytosine – another pyrimidine.\nWorking: A is 5-methyluracil = thymine, which pairs with adenine in DNA (two H-bonds). Option (C).\nKey idea: Methyl on the uracil ring = thymine → pairs with adenine.","traps":["Thymine vs uracil: Spot the 5-CH₃.","Partner, not identity: Read \"complementary base of\"."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Compound A gives D-Galactose and D-Glucose on hydrolysis. The compound A is:","options":["Amylose","Sucrose","Maltose","Lactose"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) sucrose for those who mix fructose with galactose; (A)/(C) give glucose only.\nWorking: lactose = β-D-galactose + β-D-glucose (β-1,4); sucrose gives glucose + fructose; maltose and amylose give only glucose.\nKey idea: Galactose appears only in lactose among these.","traps":["Galactose vs fructose: Sucrose = glucose + fructose; lactose = glucose + galactose."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which among the following pairs of Vitamins is stored in our body relatively for longer duration?","options":["Thiamine and Vitamin A","Vitamin A and Vitamin D","Thiamine and Ascorbic acid","Ascorbic acid and Vitamin D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) – each pairs one fat-soluble with one water-soluble vitamin; (C) – both water-soluble (thiamine B₁, ascorbic acid C).\nWorking: fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored in liver and adipose tissue; water-soluble B and C are excreted in urine and must be taken regularly.\nKey idea: Stored = fat-soluble = A, D, E, K.","traps":["Half-right pairs: Both members must be from A, D, E, K."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Protein structure","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The secondary structure of protein is stabilised by:","options":["Peptide bond","glycosidic bond","Hydrogen bonding","van der Waals forces"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) peptide bond defines primary structure; (B) glycosidic bond links sugars, not amino acids; (D) van der Waals forces contribute to tertiary structure.\nWorking: α-helix and β-pleated sheet are held by C=O···H–N hydrogen bonds between peptide groups.\nKey idea: Primary = peptide bonds; secondary = H-bonds; tertiary = H-bond, disulphide, van der Waals, electrostatic.","traps":["Peptide bond picked for secondary structure: Peptide bonds = primary sequence only."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following is correct structure of tyrosine? (Fischer-type α-carbon: COOH on top, H₂N on left, H on right, side chain below.)","options":["side chain –CH₂CH₂–C₆H₄–OH (para)","side chain –CH₂–C₆H₄–OH (meta)","side chain –CH₂CH₂–C₆H₄–OH (meta)","side chain –CH₂–C₆H₄–OH (para)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) one extra CH₂ (homotyrosine); (B) OH meta; (C) both errors.\nWorking: tyrosine side chain is p-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂– (one CH₂, OH para).\nKey idea: Tyrosine = phenylalanine with a para-OH.","traps":["Extra CH₂ in side chain: Tyrosine = Phe + p-OH; one CH₂.","OH position meta: Remember \"(p)\" in the NCERT table."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Fructose","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Fructose is an example of :-","options":["Pyranose","Ketohexose","Aldohexose","Heptose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Pyranose: glucose forms a pyranose ring; fructose forms a furanose. (C) Aldohexose: glucose, not fructose. (D) Heptose: wrong carbon count.\nWorking: fructose, C₆H₁₂O₆, has six carbons and a ketone (C-2) carbonyl, so it is a ketohexose; its ring form is a five-membered furanose, not a pyranose.\nKey idea: Fructose = keto group at C-2 + six carbons = ketohexose.","traps":["Glucose vs fructose classification: Glucose is the aldose; fructose is the ketose.","Ring size of fructose: Fructose cyclises to a furanose (5-membered) ring."],"topics":["Fructose","Carbohydrate classification"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"A non-reducing sugar \"A\" hydrolyses to give two reducing mono saccharides. Sugar A is-","options":["Fructose","Galactose","Glucose","Sucrose"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are monosaccharides and reducing; they do not hydrolyse further.\nWorking: sucrose is non-reducing (C1 of glucose and C2 of fructose both in the glycosidic link) and hydrolyses to glucose + fructose, both reducing; the other three are monosaccharides.\nKey idea: Only a disaccharide can hydrolyse to two monosaccharides.","traps":["Fructose as a non-reducing ketose: Fructose reduces Tollens/Fehling in base; it is a monosaccharide."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Deficiency of vitamin K causes :","options":["Increase in blood clotting time","Increase in fragility of RBC's","Cheilosis","Decrease in blood clotting time"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reverses the direction; (B) is vitamin E deficiency; (C) cheilosis is vitamin B₂ deficiency.\nWorking: vitamin K deficiency increases blood clotting time (NCERT vitamin table); RBC fragility is vitamin E, cheilosis is vitamin B₂.\nKey idea: Vitamin K is needed for clotting, so its lack lengthens clotting time.","traps":["Direction of clotting change: No vitamin K → blood clots slowly → time increases.","Other vitamins' deficiencies: Learn the table row by row."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A peptide synthesized by the reactions of one molecule each of Glycine, Leucine, Aspartic acid and Histidine will have ______ peptide linkages.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (one per amino acid); 5 (counting side-chain amide formation).\nWorking: four amino acids joined linearly form a tetrapeptide with 4 – 1 = 3 peptide linkages.\nKey idea: Tetrapeptide → 3 peptide bonds.","traps":["Number of amino acids taken as number of bonds: A linear peptide of n residues has n – 1 peptide bonds.","Side-chain COOH/NH counted: Peptide linkages are α-CO–NH bonds of the backbone; one per condensation."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Vitamins","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match the following : (i) Riboflavin (ii) Thiamine (iii) Pyridoxine (iv) Ascorbic acid with (a) Beriberi (b) Scurvy (c) Cheilosis (d) Convulsions","options":["(i)-(c), (ii)-(a), (iii)-(d), (iv)-(b)","(i)-(c), (ii)-(d), (iii)-(a), (iv)-(b)","(i)-(d), (ii)-(b), (iii)-(a), (iv)-(c)","(i)-(a), (ii)-(d), (iii)-(c), (iv)-(b)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): swaps thiamine and pyridoxine. (C)/(D): mix up B₂ and B₁.\nWorking: B₂ riboflavin – cheilosis; B₁ thiamine – beriberi; B₆ pyridoxine – convulsions; C ascorbic acid – scurvy.\nKey idea: Thiamine → beriberi; riboflavin → cheilosis (mouth corners).","traps":["B₁ and B₆ diseases swapped: Thiamine–beriberi; pyridoxine–convulsions."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following statements is correct-","options":["Gluconic acid can form cyclic (acetal/hemiacetal) structure","Gluconic acid is a partial oxidation product of glucose","Gluconic acid is obtained by oxidation of glucose with HNO₃","Gluconic acid is a dicarboxylic acid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) transfers glucose's hemiacetal ring to gluconic acid (it has no free CHO); (C) confuses with saccharic acid from HNO₃; (D) describes saccharic acid.\nWorking: mild oxidation (Br₂ water) converts only CHO to COOH, giving gluconic acid; it has no carbonyl to form a hemiacetal, HNO₃ gives saccharic acid, and it is monocarboxylic.\nKey idea: Br₂ water oxidises only the aldehyde; HNO₃ oxidises both ends.","traps":["Gluconic and saccharic acids confused: Br₂ water: one COOH; HNO₃: two COOH."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following statement is not true for glucose?","options":["The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine to give oxime","Glucose gives Schiff's test for aldehyde","Glucose exists in two crystalline forms α and β","Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form oxime"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C),(D) are true NCERT facts; (D) and (A) look contradictory but refer to glucose vs its pentaacetate.\nWorking: despite the CHO group glucose does not give Schiff's test (open-chain form is negligible); the other three are true.\nKey idea: Glucose: no Schiff's test, no NaHSO₃ adduct; pentaacetate: no oxime.","traps":["(A) and (D) seem contradictory: One is glucose, the other its pentaacetate."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Two monomers in maltose are :","options":["α-D-glucose and β-D-glucose","α-D-glucose and α-D-Fructose","α-D-glucose and α-D-glucose","α-D-glucose and α-D-galactose"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) α + β glucose suggests lactose/cellobiose-type confusion; (B) glucose + fructose is sucrose; (D) glucose + galactose is lactose.\nWorking: maltose is two α-D-glucose units joined C1–C4 (α-glycosidic).\nKey idea: Maltose = α-glucose + α-glucose; sucrose = glucose + fructose; lactose = galactose + glucose.","traps":["Disaccharide compositions swapped: Tie each name to its monomer pair."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"A, B and C are three biomolecules. The results of the tests performed on them are given below: Molisch's test – A positive, B positive, C negative; Barfoed test – A negative, B positive, C negative; Biuret test – A negative, B negative, C positive. A, B and C are respectively:","options":["A = Glucose, B = Fructose, C = Albumin","A = Lactose, B = Fructose, C = Alanine","A = Lactose, B = Glucose, C = Alanine","A = Lactose, B = Glucose, C = Albumin"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) glucose would be Barfoed positive, so A ≠ glucose; (B), (C) alanine has no peptide bond → biuret negative; B = fructose in (B) is also Barfoed positive, but the option fails on C.\nWorking: Molisch + for all carbohydrates; Barfoed + only for (reducing) monosaccharides, so A is a disaccharide (lactose) and B a monosaccharide; biuret + needs peptide bonds (protein albumin), not a free amino acid (alanine).\nKey idea: Biuret needs ≥2 peptide bonds → albumin; Barfoed negative carbohydrate → disaccharide.","traps":["Amino acid taken to give biuret test: Biuret detects peptide linkages, absent in a single amino acid.","Barfoed test unknown: Barfoed: Cu(II) acetate in acetic acid reduced quickly only by monosaccharides."],"topics":["Tests for carbohydrates","Disaccharides"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Glucose reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the following reactions: (i) Glucose + ROH —dry HCl→ Acetal —x eq. of (CH₃CO)₂O→ acetyl derivative; (ii) Glucose —Ni/H₂→ A —y eq. of (CH₃CO)₂O→ acetyl derivative; (iii) Glucose —z eq. of (CH₃CO)₂O→ acetyl derivative. 'x', 'y' and 'z' in these reactions are respectively.","options":["5, 6 & 5","4, 5 & 5","5, 4 & 5","4, 6 & 5"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) forgets the anomeric OH is used in the acetal; (B) forgets reduction adds an OH; (C) mixes both errors.\nWorking: the glycoside (acetal) has 4 free OH → x = 4; Ni/H₂ gives sorbitol (glucitol) with 6 OH → y = 6; glucose itself gives the pentaacetate → z = 5.\nKey idea: Acetal uses one OH; reduction converts CHO to CH₂OH, adding one.","traps":["Anomeric OH counted in glycoside: Glycoside has OR at C1, leaving 4 OH.","Sorbitol OH count: CHO → CH₂OH gives a sixth OH."],"topics":["Glucose reactions","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The correct observation in the following reactions is : Sucrose →(Glycosidic bond cleavage (Hydrolysis)) A + B →(Seliwanoff's reagent) ?","options":["Formation of blue colour","Formation of violet colour","Formation of red colour","Gives no colour"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) blue and (B) violet are colours of other tests (Benedict/Molisch); (D) assumes neither monosaccharide reacts.\nWorking: hydrolysis of sucrose gives glucose + fructose; fructose (a ketose) gives a cherry-red colour quickly with Seliwanoff's reagent (resorcinol/HCl).\nKey idea: Fructose is a ketose → Seliwanoff red.","traps":["Molisch violet colour recalled instead: Seliwanoff = ketose → red."],"topics":["Tests for carbohydrates","Disaccharides"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of >C=O groups present in a tripeptide Asp – Glu – Lys is ______.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3: only peptide + terminal C=O; 4: missing one acidic side chain; 6: counting a Lys side chain C=O (it has –NH₂ only).\nWorking: two peptide C=O + C-terminal COOH of Lys + side-chain COOH of Asp + side-chain COOH of Glu = 5.\nKey idea: Asp and Glu are acidic amino acids (extra COOH); Lys is basic (extra NH₂).","traps":["Side-chain COOH missed: Write each side chain: Asp CH₂COOH, Glu (CH₂)₂COOH.","Lys side chain given a C=O: Lys side chain is (CH₂)₄NH₂."],"topics":["Peptide bond","Amino acids"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"What are the functional groups present in the structure of maltose ?","options":["One ketal and one hemiketal","One acetal and one hemiacetal","Two acetals","One acetal and one ketal"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) 'ketal' – no ketose unit in maltose; (C) two acetals = sucrose-like non-reducing.\nWorking: maltose = α-D-glucose C1 – O – C4 glucose; C1 of the first unit is an acetal (glycosidic), the second unit has a free anomeric C1 (hemiacetal) – hence reducing.\nKey idea: Glucose units → acetal/hemiacetal; free anomeric carbon → hemiacetal.","traps":["Two acetals (non-reducing): Maltose is reducing → one free hemiacetal.","Ketal used for glucose: Aldose C1 gives acetal/hemiacetal."],"topics":["Disaccharides","Anomers & glycosides"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of chiral centres present in threonine is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 – only α-carbon counted; 0 or 3 – miscount.\nWorking: threonine CH₃–CH(OH)–CH(NH₂)–COOH has two stereocentres, C-2 (α-carbon) and C-3 (carbinol carbon).\nKey idea: Threonine and isoleucine are the amino acids with two stereocentres.","traps":["Only α-carbon counted: Check side chain: CH(OH)CH₃ carbon is also chiral."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following is not an essential amino acid :","options":["Valine","Leucine","Lysine","Tyrosine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are all essential (marked essential in the NCERT amino-acid table).\nWorking: tyrosine is made in the body from phenylalanine; valine, leucine and lysine are essential.\nKey idea: Tyrosine (from phenylalanine) is non-essential.","traps":["Lysine thought non-essential: Recall the asterisked list: V, L, I, K, T, M, F, W, R, H."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The number of chiral carbon(s) present in peptide, Ile-Arg-Pro, is ______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (one per residue – missing Ile Cβ), 2 (ignoring proline as a ring), 5 (counting a guanidine or ring C).\nWorking: isoleucine has two stereocentres (Cα and Cβ), arginine one (Cα), proline one (Cα) → 4; peptide formation creates no new stereocentres.\nKey idea: Isoleucine (and threonine) carry two stereocentres.","traps":["Ile side-chain stereocentre missed: Check Cβ of Ile: H, CH₃, C₂H₅, Cα.","Proline Cα ignored: Proline Cα has H, COOH/CO, N and CH₂ – four different groups."],"topics":["Amino acids","Peptide bond"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of chiral carbons present in sucrose is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 10 (counting fructose C-1 or C-6 CH₂OH, or treating glucose C-6 as chiral), 8 (missing anomeric C-1 of glucose or C-2 of fructose because they are involved in the glycosidic bond), 4/5 (counting one ring only).\nWorking: glucose unit (pyranose) C-1 to C-5 = 5 stereocentres; fructose unit (furanose) C-2, C-3, C-4, C-5 = 4 (C-1 and C-6 are CH₂OH) → 9.\nKey idea: Every ring carbon carrying H/OH/O-link and two different ring paths is chiral; CH₂OH carbons are not.","traps":["Anomeric carbons skipped: Anomeric C still has four different groups – count it.","CH₂OH carbons counted: CH₂OH has two H atoms – not chiral."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which one of the following statements not true?","options":["Lactose contains α-glycosidic linkage between C₁ of galactose and C₄ of glucose.","Lactose (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) is a disaccharide and it contains 8 hydroxyl groups.","On acid hydrolysis, lactose gives one molecule of D(+)-glucose and one molecule of D(+)-galactose.","Lactose is a reducing sugar and it gives Fehling's test."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) looks false if OH groups counted as 10; (C) galactose sign may be doubted; (D) students may think all disaccharides are non-reducing like sucrose.\nWorking: the linkage is β (β-D-galactose C1 to C4 of β-D-glucose). (B) 10 OH of two hexoses minus 2 used in the glycosidic link = 8; (C) and (D) true (free hemiacetal at C1 of glucose).\nKey idea: Lactose and cellulose-type links are β; maltose is α.","traps":["α vs β linkage: Remember: maltose α, lactose β.","OH count in lactose: Two OH are consumed when the glycosidic bond forms (one H₂O lost, one ring O–C link)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Maltose on treatment with dilute HCl gives :","options":["D-Galactose","D-Glucose","D-Glucose and D-Fructose","D-Fructose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) from lactose; (C) from sucrose; (D) fructose part of sucrose.\nWorking: maltose = two α-D-glucose units (α-1,4); hydrolysis gives only D-glucose.\nKey idea: Maltose = glucose + glucose.","traps":["Confusing disaccharides: Sucrose → Glc + Fru; lactose → Gal + Glc; maltose → 2 Glc."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for carbohydrates","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Fructose and glucose can be distinguished by:","options":["Fehling's test","Barfoed's test","Benedict's test","Seliwanoff's test"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) Fehling's and Benedict's are positive for both reducing sugars; (B) Barfoed's distinguishes mono- from disaccharides, positive for both.\nWorking: Seliwanoff's test (resorcinol/HCl) gives a rapid red colour with ketoses such as fructose and only slowly with aldoses; both sugars reduce Fehling's and Benedict's, and both are monosaccharides for Barfoed's.\nKey idea: Seliwanoff: ketohexoses dehydrate faster ⇒ quick red colour.","traps":["Reducing-sugar test chosen: Both glucose and fructose reduce Fehling's."],"topics":["Tests for carbohydrates","Fructose"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Disaccharides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about sucrose?","options":["On hydrolysis, it produces glucose and fructose","The glycosidic linkage is present between C₁ of α-glucose and C₁ of β-fructose","It is also named as invert sugar","It is a non reducing sugar"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true; (C) sloppy but intended true by setters (sucrose 'gives' invert sugar); (D) true – both anomeric carbons are tied up.\nWorking: (C) is also strictly not true – NCERT names the hydrolysis product (glucose + fructose mixture) invert sugar, not sucrose itself; (B) is the intended answer because it is clearly false.\nKey idea: Sucrose link is C1(α-Glc)–C2(β-Fru); fructose's anomeric carbon is C2.","traps":["C1–C2 changed to C1–C1: Recall fructose anomeric carbon is C2.","'Invert sugar' attached to sucrose itself: Invert sugar = hydrolysed mixture; pick the clearly wrong fact."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Amylopectin is composed of :","options":["α-D-glucose, C₁–C₄ and C₁–C₆ linkages","α-D-glucose, C₁–C₄ and C₂–C₆ linkages","β-D-glucose, C₁–C₄ and C₂–C₆ linkages","β-D-glucose, C₁–C₄ and C₁–C₆ linkages"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) invent a C₂–C₆ link; (D) uses β-glucose (cellulose monomer).\nWorking: amylopectin is a branched polymer of α-D-glucose with C1–C4 glycosidic links in chains and C1–C6 links at branch points.\nKey idea: Starch (amylose, amylopectin) = α-glucose; cellulose = β-glucose; branches via C1–C6.","traps":["β-glucose taken for starch: Starch/glycogen α; cellulose β.","Branch linkage misnumbered: Glycosidic bonds always start at anomeric C1."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Number of stereo centers present in linear and cyclic structures of glucose are respectively:","options":["4 & 5","5 & 5","4 & 4","5 & 4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reverses order; (B) counts C1 in open chain (CHO is not chiral); (C) forgets anomeric carbon.\nWorking: open-chain glucose has chiral C2–C5 (D); in the pyranose ring C1 becomes the anomeric stereocentre too (5).\nKey idea: C1 becomes chiral only after cyclisation.","traps":["Anomeric carbon missed: Hemiacetal C1 has four different groups."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleic acid functions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about RNA ?","options":["It has always double stranded α-helix structure","It usually does not replicate","It is present in the nucleus of the cell","It controls the synthesis of protein"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are true facts; (C) may look doubtful as students associate RNA with cytoplasm only.\nWorking: RNA is single stranded (secondary structure single-stranded helices); DNA is the double helix. RNA usually does not replicate, is found in the nucleus (and cytoplasm) and directs protein synthesis.\nKey idea: RNA is single stranded.","traps":["RNA location: RNA is synthesised in the nucleus."],"topics":["Nucleic acid functions","DNA & RNA structure"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Glucose structure","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Glucose and Galactose are having identical configuration in all the positions except position.","options":["C–3","C–2","C–4","C–5"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) C-2 is the glucose–mannose epimer; (A) C-3 is allose; (D) C-5 sets D/L.\nWorking: galactose is the C-4 epimer of glucose.\nKey idea: Galactose = C-4 epimer; mannose = C-2 epimer.","traps":["Mannose vs galactose epimer: Galactose ↔ C4; mannose ↔ C2."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Polysaccharides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Which of the given statements is INCORRECT about glycogen ?","options":["It is a straight chain polymer similar to amylose","Only α-linkages are present in the molecule","It is present in animal cells","It is present in some yeast and fungi"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) true (α-1,4 and α-1,6); (C) true; (D) true per NCERT.\nWorking: glycogen is similar to amylopectin and even more highly branched, not linear like amylose.\nKey idea: Glycogen = more branched amylopectin.","traps":["Amylose and amylopectin swapped: Amylose is linear; glycogen is branched.","Yeast/fungi line thought false: NCERT states glycogen is found in yeast and fungi."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The increasing order of pKa of the following amino acids in aqueous solution is :\nGly Asp Lys Arg","options":["Asp < Gly < Arg < Lys","Arg < Lys < Gly < Asp","Gly < Asp < Arg < Lys","Asp < Gly < Lys < Arg"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): Lys put above Arg. (B): order reversed. (C): Gly below Asp.\nWorking: Asp (acidic, ≈2.8) < Gly (neutral, ≈6.0) < Lys (basic, ≈9.7) < Arg (most basic guanidino, ≈10.8).\nKey idea: Arg's guanidino group is the strongest base among common side chains.","traps":["Lys vs Arg order: Guanidine of Arg is a far stronger base than ε-NH₂ of Lys.","pKa read as acid-strength order: Most acidic (Asp) has the lowest value."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct sequence of amino acids present in the tripeptide given below is: H₂N–CH(CH(CH₃)₂)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂OH)–CO–NH–CH(CH(CH₃)OH)–COOH (wedge/hash stereo shown; side chains: isopropyl, hydroxymethyl, 1-hydroxyethyl).","options":["Leu – Ser – Thr","Thr – Ser – Leu","Thr – Ser – Val","Val – Ser – Thr"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Leu has isobutyl CH₂CH(CH₃)₂, not isopropyl. (B) Leu and reversed order. (C) Order read C→N.\nWorking: N-terminal residue has an isopropyl side chain (Val), middle has CH₂OH (Ser), C-terminal has CH(OH)CH₃ (Thr); read N→C.\nKey idea: Isopropyl = Val, isobutyl = Leu; sequence written from free NH₂ end.","traps":["Val vs Leu side chain: Val: CH directly on α-carbon carries two Me; Leu has an extra CH₂.","Reading direction: Always N-terminus (free NH₂) first."],"topics":["Peptide bond","Amino acids"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Peptide bond","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The correct structure of product 'P' in the following reaction is : Asn-Ser + (CH₃CO)₂O (excess) —NEt₃→ P","options":["CH₃CO–NH–CH(CH₂CONH₂)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂OCOCH₃)–COOH (N-acetyl, O-acetyl; side-chain amide free)","CH₃CO–NH–CH(CH₂CONHCOCH₃)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂OCOCH₃)–COOH (side-chain amide also acetylated)","CH₃CO–NH–CH(CH₂OCOCH₃)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂CONHCOCH₃)–COOH (Ser at N-terminus; amide acetylated)","CH₃CO–NH–CH(CH₂OCOCH₃)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂CONH₂)–COOH (Ser at N-terminus)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): acetylates the amide NH₂ too. (C)/(D): reverse the sequence (Ser-Asn).\nWorking: Asn-Ser has Asn at the N-terminus; excess Ac₂O/Et₃N acetylates the free α-NH₂ and the Ser –OH; the side-chain amide NH₂ (non-nucleophilic) and COOH are not acetylated.\nKey idea: Amide N lone pair is delocalised → not acetylated; the first-named residue carries the free NH₂.","traps":["Sequence reversed: Peptides are written from N-terminus (left) to C-terminus.","Amide NH₂ acetylated: Amide nitrogen is non-nucleophilic due to resonance."],"topics":["Peptide bond","Amino acids"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following tests cannot be used for identifying amino acids ?","options":["Biuret test","Xanthoproteic test","Barfoed test","Ninhydrin test"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Biuret: protein/peptide test (strictly needs ≥2 peptide bonds, so a careful student may hesitate). (B) Xanthoproteic: aromatic amino acids. (D) Ninhydrin: classic α-amino-acid test.\nWorking: Barfoed test (Cu²⁺ acetate in acetic acid) distinguishes reducing monosaccharides from disaccharides; ninhydrin (α-amino acids), xanthoproteic (aromatic amino acids) and biuret (peptide links in proteins) are protein/amino-acid tests.\nKey idea: Barfoed is a sugar test.","traps":["Biuret does not detect free amino acids: Pick the test that is for a different class (sugars)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"DNA & RNA structure","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Among the following compounds which one is found in RNA?\n(Four pyrimidine structures drawn.)","options":["Thymine (5-methylpyrimidine-2,4-dione)","6-Amino-5,6-dihydropyrimidin-4(3H)-one [ring N3H–C4(=O)–C5H₂–C6H(NH₂)–N1=C2H; non-aromatic, not cytosine]","Uracil (pyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione)","1,3-Dimethyluracil (N-Me at both ring N)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) thymine – differs from uracil only by the 5-CH₃. (D) N-methylated uracil – looks like uracil. (B) has an amino group like cytosine but is a non-aromatic dihydropyrimidinone, not a nucleobase.\nWorking: uracil (C) is the RNA-specific base; thymine is DNA's; the N,N-dimethyl compound is not a nucleobase. Option (B) is not cytosine (C=O at C4 next to an sp³ CH₂, NH₂ on an sp³ carbon), so (C) is the single answer.\nKey idea: Uracil = thymine without the methyl; RNA uses U.","traps":["Thymine vs uracil: U has H at C5; T has CH₃.","Cytosine also in RNA: Inspect the drawing: option B has an sp³ carbon, not cytosine."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Among the following compounds most basic amino acid is :","options":["Lysine","Asparagine","Serine","Histidine"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Histidine: also basic but imidazole much weaker. (B) Asparagine: amide N not basic. (C) Serine: neutral.\nWorking: lysine has a side-chain primary amine (pKa ≈ 10.5); histidine imidazole is weakly basic (≈ 6); asparagine (amide) and serine (OH) are neutral.\nKey idea: Aliphatic NH₂ in side chain ⇒ strongest base.","traps":["Histidine chosen: Aromatic imidazole lone pair is less basic than an alkylamine.","Amide treated as basic: Amide N lone pair is delocalised; not basic."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Amino acids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct structure of histidine in a strongly acidic solution (pH = 2) is","options":["H₃N⁺–CH(COOH)–CH₂–(imidazolium ring, protonated on the ring N=, i.e. both ring N bearing H, ring positive)","H₃N⁺–CH(COOH)–CH₂–(imidazole ring with the N–H nitrogen drawn as NH₂⁺ and the other ring N unprotonated)","H₃N–CH(COO⁻)–CH₂–(imidazole with the N–H nitrogen drawn as NH₂⁺, other ring N unprotonated)","H₃N⁺–CH(COO⁻)–CH₂–(imidazolium ring, both ring N bearing H, ring positive)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) protonates the N–H (pyrrole-type) nitrogen, whose lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet. (C) zwitter-ion carboxylate plus wrong ring protonation and a neutral amino group. (D) correct ring but carboxylate – the zwitter ion form at near-neutral pH.\nWorking: at pH 2 the carboxyl group (pKa ≈ 1.8, so at pH 2 it is mostly un-ionised) stays as COOH, the α-amino group is NH₃⁺, and the imidazole is protonated at its pyridine-type N giving an aromatic imidazolium ion – structure (A).\nKey idea: Strong acid: every basic site protonated, COOH not ionised; imidazole takes H⁺ on its =N–, not on its N–H.","traps":["Zwitter ion assumed at all pH: At pH 2 the carboxylate is protonated back to COOH.","Wrong ring nitrogen protonated: Lone pair in the aromatic sextet is not basic; protonate the =N–."]}],"m15-hc":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Designate whether each of the following compounds (a)–(h) (shown below) is aromatic or not aromatic.","options":["b, e, f, g aromatic and a, c, d, h not aromatic","a, c, d, e, h aromatic and b, f, g not aromatic","a, b, c, d aromatic and e, f, g, h not aromatic","e, g aromatic and a, b, c, d, f, h not aromatic"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the cyclopentadienyl ions and adds f, g as aromatic. (C) takes all five- and four-membered ions as aromatic and drops tropylium and cyclopropenyl. (D) keeps only tropylium and cyclobutadiene, the latter being antiaromatic.\nWorking: π electrons: (a) C₅H₅⁻ 6, aromatic; (b) C₅H₅⁺ 4, antiaromatic; (c) C₄H₄²⁺ 2, aromatic; (d) C₄H₄²⁻ 6, aromatic; (e) C₇H₇⁺ 6, aromatic; (f) cyclooctatetraene 8, tub-shaped, non-aromatic; (g) cyclobutadiene 4, antiaromatic; (h) C₃H₃⁺ 2, aromatic. Aromatic: a, c, d, e, h.\nKey idea: Recount π electrons including the charge on each ring and test 4n + 2 (n = 0 allowed).","traps":["Charge changes π count in small charged rings: Count electrons: anion carbon adds 2 π electrons, cation carbon adds 0; then test 4n + 2.","Antiaromatic or non-planar ring counted as aromatic: 4 and 8 π electrons are 4n; COT is tub-shaped and non-planar, cyclobutadiene is antiaromatic."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C15-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Which compound would give 3-methyl-6-oxoheptanal upon ozonolysis?","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1,6-dimethyl gives 5-methyl-6-oxoheptanal (methyl α to the ketone). (B) 1,5-dimethyl gives 4-methyl-6-oxoheptanal. (D) 1,3-dimethyl gives 2-methyl-6-oxoheptanal (methyl α to CHO).\nWorking: 1,4-dimethylcyclohexene: ring C2 (=CH) becomes CHO (C1 of the chain), C3 → C2, C4 (bearing CH₃) → C3, C5 → C4, C6 → C5, and C1 (bearing CH₃) becomes the CO of CH₃CO– (C6). Product: OHC–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃ = 3-methyl-6-oxoheptanal.\nKey idea: Ozonolysis opens the ring into one chain whose two carbonyl carbons were the alkene carbons; renumber from the CHO end.","traps":["Cleavage fragments reassembled into parent alkene: CHO carbon = ring CH of the alkene, keto carbon = methyl-bearing alkene carbon; count along the chain from the CHO end: C3 of heptanal is ring C4.","IUPAC numbering of product misapplied to ring numbering: Translate each chain carbon to its ring carbon explicitly."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S1C15-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"In the following series of reactions identify the major products A & B respectively\nBromobenzene —[SO₃, H₂SO₄]→ A (Major product) —[Br₂, Fe]→ B (Major product)","options":["A: 2-bromobenzenesulfonic acid; B: 2,6-dibromobenzenesulfonic acid","A: 2-bromobenzenesulfonic acid; B: 2,4-dibromobenzenesulfonic acid","A: 4-bromobenzenesulfonic acid; B: 2,4-dibromobenzenesulfonic acid","A: 4-bromobenzenesulfonic acid; B: 3,4-dibromobenzenesulfonic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ortho A and Br flanking SO₃H: ignores para preference and SO₃H meta direction. (B) ortho A. (C) correct A but second Br ortho to SO₃H (against meta direction).\nWorking: Br is o/p-directing and the bulky SO₃H group enters para, so A = 4-bromobenzenesulfonic acid. In A, Br directs to its ortho positions and SO₃H directs meta – these are the same positions, so the second Br enters ortho to the first Br: B = 3,4-dibromobenzenesulfonic acid.\nKey idea: Put the second electrophile where the o/p director and the meta director agree.","traps":["Ortho product chosen where para dominates: The bulky SO₃H electrophile goes para; para is the major product for o/p directors.","Second substituent placed by only one director: Choose the position that is ortho to Br and meta to SO₃H; both groups agree there."]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Consider the following molecule (X) (shown below). The major product formed when the given molecule (X) is treated with HBr (1 eq) is:","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) adds to the cyclohexene CH=CH giving a secondary bromide. (C) and (D) add to the upper cyclopentene, secondary bromides differing only in which CH gets Br. (A) correct: tertiary bromide.\nWorking: of the three C=C, only the ring-fusion alkene is trisubstituted; protonating its CH end puts the positive charge on the ring-fusion carbon (tertiary), the most stable cation. Br⁻ adds there, giving the tertiary bromide (A). The other two C=C would give only secondary cations.\nKey idea: Find the alkene whose protonation gives a tertiary carbocation.","traps":["Most substituted alkene reacts first with limited HX: Rate follows carbocation stability: the trisubstituted alkene gives a tertiary carbocation.","Complex polycyclic drawing hides simple Markovnikov rule: Classify each C=C carbon as CH or C before anything else."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C15-s.webp","img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C15-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene isomerism","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I): cis-1,2-Dichloroethene is more polar than cis-1,2-dibromoethene.\nStatement (II): Boiling point of trans-1,2-dibromoethene is lower than cis-1,2-dibromoethene but it is more polar than cis-1,2-dibromoethene.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I: true (cis-ClCH=CHCl about 1.9 D vs cis-BrCH=CHBr about 1.4 D). Statement II: b.p. part true, 'more polar' part false (trans has μ = 0).\nWorking: C–Cl is more polar than C–Br, so cis-1,2-dichloroethene has the larger dipole moment (I correct). trans-1,2-Dibromoethene has μ = 0 (bond dipoles cancel), so it is less polar than the cis isomer and has the lower b.p.; II is incorrect. Answer: (C).\nKey idea: Trans-symmetrical dihaloethenes have zero dipole; for cis isomers dipole follows C–X bond polarity.","traps":["True clause and false clause joined in one statement: Judge each clause separately; trans-1,2-dihaloethene has zero dipole moment.","Larger halogen assumed to give larger dipole: Electronegativity dominates: C–Cl bond moment exceeds C–Br, so cis-dichloro is more polar."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S2C15-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"The number of optically active products obtained from the complete ozonolysis of the given compound (shown below) is :","options":["0","1","2","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1 and (C) 2 come from carrying stereocentres through; (D) 4 counts all fragments. Ethanal and 2-methylpropanedial are both achiral.\nWorking: each C=C is cleaved: the compound CH₃CH=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–CH₃ gives 2 CH₃CHO + 2 OHC–CH(CH₃)–CHO. In each 2-methylpropanedial the former stereocentre now carries two identical CHO groups, so no product is optically active: 0.\nKey idea: A carbon flanked by two cleaved C=C bonds ends up with two identical CHO groups and loses chirality.","traps":["Stereocentre in reactant not carried into product: After cleavage each former stereocentre bears two CHO groups: CH(CH₃)(CHO)₂ is achiral.","Chirality of ozonolysis products judged wrongly: Draw each fragment and test four different groups."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S2C16-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Identify the structure of the final product (D) in the following sequence of the reactions :\nPh−CO−CH₃ —[PCl₅, Δ]→ A —[3 eq. NaNH₂/NH₃]→ B —[Acidify]→ C —[1. B₂H₆; 2. H₂O₂/OH⁻]→ D\nTotal number of sp² hybridised carbon atoms in product D is ______.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (counting the enol PhCH=CH–OH, or counting the sp³ CH₂ of PhCH₂CHO as sp²), 6 (alkyne stage, sp carbons excluded).\nWorking: PCl₅ gives PhCCl₂CH₃ (A); 3 NaNH₂ remove 2 HCl and deprotonate the terminal alkyne (B = PhC≡C⁻Na⁺); acid gives PhC≡CH (C); hydroboration–oxidation gives PhCH₂CHO (D). sp² carbons: 6 ring C + 1 CHO = 7.\nKey idea: Gem-dichloride → terminal alkyne → anti-Markovnikov aldehyde; only ring carbons and the CHO carbon are sp².","traps":["Third equivalent of NaNH₂ forms acetylide: Terminal alkynes are acidic; NaNH₂ converts them to RC≡C⁻Na⁺, which acid restores.","Hydroboration of terminal alkyne gives aldehyde: B₂H₆/H₂O₂ adds anti-Markovnikov; the enol tautomerises to PhCH₂CHO."],"topics":["Alkyne preparation","Alkyne addition"]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne preparation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"1,2-Dibromocyclooctane —[(i) KOH (alc.); (ii) NaNH₂; (iii) Hg²⁺/H⁺; (iv) Zn–Hg/H⁺]→ P (major product)\n'P' is","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ketone stops before step iv. (B) alcohol misreads Clemmensen. (D) cyclooctyne stops after step ii.\nWorking: alc. KOH then NaNH₂ remove two HBr → cyclooctyne; Hg²⁺/H⁺ hydrates it → cyclooctanone; Zn–Hg/HCl (Clemmensen) reduces C=O to CH₂ → cyclooctane.\nKey idea: Track every reagent: double dehydrohalogenation, hydration, then complete deoxygenation.","traps":["Hidden last step in reagent sequence ignored: Zn–Hg/HCl reduces C=O fully to CH₂.","Clemmensen confused with reduction to alcohol: Clemmensen converts C=O to CH₂, not CHOH."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr08S2C15-o.webp"],"lettered":true,"topics":["Alkyne preparation","Alkyne addition"]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Benzene – preparation and reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"Choose the correct set of reagents for the following conversion.\nEthyl benzene → product shown below","options":["Cl₂/anhy.AlCl₃ ; Br₂/Fe ; alc. KOH","Br₂/Fe ; Cl₂, Δ ; alc.KOH","Br₂/anhy.AlCl₃ ; Cl₂, Δ ; aq. KOH","Cl₂/Fe ; Br₂/anhy.AlCl₃ ; aq. KOH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cl₂/AlCl₃ puts Cl on the ring, never on the side chain. (C) Br₂/AlCl₃ fine but aq. KOH gives alcohol. (D) Cl₂/Fe on ring and aq. KOH.\nWorking: Br₂/Fe brominates the ring para to the ethyl group; Cl₂ with heat chlorinates the benzylic carbon → 4-Br–C₆H₄–CHCl–CH₃; alc. KOH removes HCl → 4-bromostyrene.\nKey idea: Lewis acid for ring, heat for side chain, alcoholic base for elimination.","traps":["Ring vs side-chain halogenation conditions: Lewis acid = ring (electrophilic); heat/light = benzylic (free radical).","Alcoholic vs aqueous KOH: elimination vs substitution: Only alcoholic KOH dehydrohalogenates to the vinyl group."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr08S2C16-s.webp"],"topics":["Benzene – preparation and reactions","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"When sec-butylcyclohexane reacts with bromine in the presence of sunlight, the major product is :","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ring tertiary bromide, the closest competitor. (C) and (D) secondary bromides, ignore bromine selectivity.\nWorking: Br• is highly selective for the most stable (tertiary) radical. There are two tertiary C–H: the ring CH and the side-chain CH. The side-chain tertiary radical has more α-H (6 vs 5) for hyperconjugation, so Br ends up on the side-chain carbon: (2-bromobutan-2-yl)cyclohexane.\nKey idea: Bromine radicals pick the most stable radical; among two tertiary sites, the one with more α-H is chosen.","traps":["Two tertiary positions compared by hyperconjugation: Count α-H: side-chain CH has CH₃, CH₂ and ring CH neighbours (6 H); ring CH has two CH₂ and side-chain CH (5 H).","Bromination selectivity vs chlorination: Bromination is highly selective for tertiary H."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan22S2C14-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"Match List - I with List - II.\nList - I (Isomers of C₁₀H₁₄): A, B, C, D (shown below)\nList - II (Ozonolysis product):\nI. OHC−CH(CH₃)−CH₂−CO−(CH₂)₃−CO−CHO\nII. CH₃−CO−CO−(CH₂)₄−CO−CH₂−CHO\nIII. OHC−CH₂−CH₂−CO−CH₂−CH(CH₃)−CH₂−CO−CHO\nIV. OHC−CO−(CH₂)₄−CO−CH₂−CO−CH₃\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A-III correct but B-II, D-IV swapped (II and IV differ in where CH₃CO sits). (B) A-II/B-III swaps vinylic and sp³ methyl compounds. (C) A-I/C-III swap the two sp³-methyl compounds and B/D.\nWorking: A and C carry CH₃ on an sp³ carbon, so their products keep a CH(CH₃) unit (I, III); B and D carry CH₃ on an alkene carbon, so their products contain CH₃CO– (II, IV). Cleaving both C=C of each and writing the chain gives A–III, B–IV, C–I, D–II.\nKey idea: Methyl on an alkene carbon gives a methyl ketone; methyl on an sp³ carbon gives a branched CH(CH₃).","traps":["Cleavage fragments reassembled into parent alkene: Cleave each C=C, keep every ring σ bond, then write the chain from one end.","Vinylic vs sp³ methyl decides ketone vs CH(CH₃): CH₃ on a cleaved carbon gives a methyl ketone (II, IV); CH₃ on an sp³ carbon stays as CH(CH₃) (I, III)."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan23S2C16-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"A compound 'X' absorbs 2 moles of hydrogen and 'X' upon oxidation with KMnO₄|H⁺ gives CH₃−CO−CH₃, CH₃−CO−OH and CH₃−CO−CH₂CH₂−CO−OH.\nThe total number of σ bonds present in the compound 'X' is ______.","type":"num","answer":"27","range":[27.0,27.0],"explain":"Common slips: 29 (double bonds counted as 2 σ), 25 (forgetting 2 C–H by using C₁₀H₁₆), 28/26 (miscounted H).\nWorking: joining the fragments at the C=O carbons gives an acyclic diene C₁₀H₁₈, e.g. (CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂–CH=CH–CH₃ (the other possible arrangement gives the same count); it takes up 2 H₂. σ bonds = 9 C–C + 18 C–H = 27.\nKey idea: For any open-chain hydrocarbon CₓHᵧ, σ bonds = (x − 1) + y.","traps":["Cleavage fragments reassembled into parent alkene: Acid carbon came from =CH–, ketone carbon from =C(R)–; join C=O ends pairwise.","Double bond counted as two sigma bonds: Each double bond has one σ and one π bond; acyclic C₁₀H₁₈ has 9 C–C σ + 18 C–H."]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"The product B formed in the following reaction sequence is :\nCompound shown below (4-methyl-1-(prop-1-enyl)benzene) —[HCl]→ (A) (Major) —[AgCN]→ (B) (Major)","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) are the same non-benzylic isocyanide (wrong regiochemistry). (C) nitrile with lost carbon.\nWorking: H⁺ adds to the C=C so that the cation forms at the benzylic carbon (stabilised by the p-tolyl ring); Cl⁻ gives Ar–CHCl–CH₂CH₃ (A). AgCN (covalent, attacks through N) gives the isocyanide Ar–CH(NC)–CH₂CH₃ (B).\nKey idea: When both alkene carbons are equally substituted, the carbocation next to the aryl ring decides.","traps":["Markovnikov decided by carbocation stability not H count: Protonate to give the benzylic carbocation; Cl goes to the benzylic carbon.","Ambident nucleophile: silver salt vs alkali salt: AgCN gives isocyanides."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C16-s.webp","img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C16-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene —'A'→ 6-oxoheptanal (OHC–(CH₂)₄–CO–CH₃) —'B'→ sodium 6-oxohexanoate, OHC–(CH₂)₄–COO⁻Na⁺ (the methyl ketone converted to the sodium carboxylate, aldehyde retained).\nIn the above chemical reaction sequence 'A' and 'B' respectively are","options":["H₂O, H⁺ and NaOH(alc)/I₂","O₃, Zn/H₂O and KMnO₄","H₂O, H⁺ and KMnO₄","O₃, Zn/H₂O and NaOH(alc)/I₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) uses KMnO₄, which would oxidise the CHO too. (A)/(C) use H₂O/H⁺, which only hydrates to an alcohol and cannot cleave the ring.\nWorking: ring-opening to a keto-aldehyde needs reductive ozonolysis (O₃, Zn/H₂O); converting CH₃CO– into –COO⁻Na⁺ while keeping –CHO is the iodoform (haloform) reaction with I₂/NaOH. KMnO₄ would also oxidise the aldehyde.\nKey idea: Haloform affects only CH₃CO–; ozonolysis + Zn keeps aldehydes.","traps":["KMnO₄ thought selective: Check that CHO survives in product.","Hydration mistaken for cleavage: Ring opening needs C=C cleavage (O₃)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Nitration of benzene involves the following step –\nH–O⁺(H)–NO₂ ⇌ H₂O + ⁺NO₂ [protonated nitric acid losing water to give nitronium ion]\nStatement II: Use of Lewis base promotes the electrophilic substitution of benzene.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): student skims \"Lewis base\" as \"Lewis acid\". (A)/(C): student thinks NO₂⁺ forms directly from HNO₃ without protonation.\nWorking: protonated HNO₃ loses H₂O to form NO₂⁺ (NCERT shows this); electrophile generation needs Lewis/Brønsted acids, not Lewis bases.\nKey idea: Electrophiles are generated by acids; a Lewis base would quench them.","traps":["Lewis base read as Lewis acid: Read the qualifier: acid catalysts create E⁺."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Identify A and B in the given chemical reaction sequence:\nBenzene + succinic anhydride —[AlCl₃]→ A —[Zn–Hg / HCl]→ B —[H⁺]→ α-tetralone (3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one)","options":["A = naphthalene-1,4-dione type bicyclic diketone (1,4-dioxotetralin); B = naphthalene","A = 4-oxo-4-phenylbutanoic acid (PhCOCH₂CH₂COOH); B = 4-phenylbutanoic acid (PhCH₂CH₂CH₂COOH)","A = bicyclic 1,4-diketone (1,4-dioxotetralin); B = corresponding 1,4-diol","A = 4-phenylbutanoic acid; B = 4-phenylbutan-1-ol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) assume cyclisation happens in step 1. (D) skips the keto group and has Clemmensen reduce COOH to CH₂OH.\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation by the anhydride gives the keto-acid; Clemmensen reduces only the aryl ketone C=O; acid then does intramolecular acylation to α-tetralone.\nKey idea: Clemmensen reduces ketone C=O to CH₂ but leaves COOH; cyclisation needs the final acid step.","traps":["Clemmensen reducing COOH: Clemmensen acts on aldehyde/ketone C=O only.","Cyclisation placed too early: Work forward step by step; ring closes only with H⁺ at the end."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is P.\nCH₃C≡C–CH₃ —[(i) Na/liq. NH₃; (ii) dil. KMnO₄, 273 K]→ 'P'\nNumber of oxygen atoms present in product 'P' is ______. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (assuming cleavage to two CH₃COOH molecules, total O), 1 (ketone/aldehyde), 0 (stopping at alkene).\nWorking: Na/liq. NH₃ gives trans-but-2-ene; cold dilute KMnO₄ (Baeyer's reagent) gives butane-2,3-diol (C₄H₁₀O₂) → 2 O atoms.\nKey idea: Cold dilute KMnO₄ only adds two OH; it does not cleave the C=C.","traps":["Cold dilute KMnO₄ treated as cleaving oxidant: 273 K, dilute → diol only."],"topics":["Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","Alkene oxidation"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Four compounds: (I) toluene (C₆H₅CH₃), (II) benzene, (III) anisole (C₆H₅OCH₃), (IV) benzotrifluoride (C₆H₅CF₃).\nThe correct arrangement for decreasing order of electrophilic substitution for above compounds is :","options":["(III) > (IV) > (II) > (I)","(III) > (I) > (II) > (IV)","(II) > (IV) > (III) > (I)","(IV) > (I) > (II) > (III)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): CF₃ treated as an alkyl-like activating group. (C): ignores substituent effects altogether.\nWorking: –OCH₃ (+R) activates most, –CH₃ (+I/hyperconjugation) activates, benzene is the reference, –CF₃ (strong –I) deactivates: III > I > II > IV.\nKey idea: CF₃ is strongly electron-withdrawing by –I; OCH₃ is the strongest activator here through +R.","traps":["CF₃ mistaken for an activating alkyl group: Three F atoms make CF₃ strongly –I, a deactivator.","OCH₃ inductive withdrawal over-weighted: For EAS, +R of OCH₃ dominates over –I."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The incorrect statement regarding the geometrical isomers of 2-butene is :","options":["cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene are not interconvertible at room temperature.","trans-2-butene is more stable than cis-2-butene.","cis-2-butene has less dipole moment than trans-2-butene.","cis-2-butene and trans-2-butene are stereoisomers."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are true; choosing them means reading \"incorrect\" as \"correct\".\nWorking: cis-but-2-ene is polar (≈0.33 D) while trans is non-polar (0), so (C) is false.\nKey idea: Trans: bond dipoles cancel → zero.","traps":["Dipole order reversed: Trans symmetric → μ ≈ 0."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following are aromatic?\nA. [bicyclic C₁₀ structure drawn like naphthalene but with only four C=C (two in the left ring, two in the right ring incl. one at the ring fusion carbon), leaving one ring-fusion carbon and one CH₂ sp³ – a dihydronaphthalene]\nB. styrene (vinylbenzene)\nC. [10]annulene drawn as a 10-membered ring with five alternating C=C (naphthalene outline without the central bond)\nD. [14]annulene (14-membered ring, seven alternating C=C)","options":["B and D only","A and C only","C and D only","A and B only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) A and C – treats the naphthalene-like A as naphthalene and [10]annulene as aromatic on electron count alone; (C) C and D – Hückel count only, ignoring planarity; (D) A and B – misreads A.\nWorking: B has a benzene ring; D has 14 π electrons (4n+2, n=3) and is treated as aromatic; C, [10]annulene, cannot be planar (H–H clash) so is non-aromatic; A as printed has sp³ carbons in both rings so neither ring is fully conjugated.\nKey idea: 10π [10]annulene fails planarity; check every ring atom is sp² before counting.","traps":["Naphthalene look-alike assumed aromatic: Count double bonds; any sp³ ring carbon breaks conjugation.","[10]annulene judged by 4n+2 only: Planarity is required; [10]annulene is non-planar."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Total number of aromatic compounds among the following compounds is ____.\n(1) Fused 6-6 bicyclic C₁₀ triene: one ring has two C=C plus an sp³ CH₂, the fusion carbon at the top carries a C=C into the second ring, which has three sp³ CH₂ (cross-conjugated triene; not a benzene ring). (2) Two five-membered rings, each with one ring C=C, joined by an exocyclic C=C between ring carbons (bi(cyclopentenylidene)-type). (3) Cyclopentadienyl cation (C₅H₅⁺). (4) Cyclooctatetraene. (5) Pyridine (lone pair shown on N). (6) Cycloheptatriene (neutral, with an sp³ CH₂).","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if the first bicyclic is taken as having a benzene ring; 2 if C₅H₅⁺ is miscounted as 6π; 2–3 if cycloheptatriene/COT counted.\nWorking: only pyridine is aromatic; C₅H₅⁺ is antiaromatic (4π), COT is non-planar non-aromatic, cycloheptatriene has an sp³ CH₂; in the fused bicyclic triene neither ring has three endocyclic C=C (each ring has an sp³ CH₂), and the bis(cyclopentenylidene) has sp³ carbons in both rings.\nKey idea: Every ring atom must be sp² in a cyclic conjugated (4n+2)π system.","traps":["Partly saturated bicyclic read as naphthalene: Check every ring carbon for sp².","Cyclopentadienyl cation vs anion: Cation has 4π → antiaromatic."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkynes – structure, preparation and acidity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The incorrect statement regarding ethyne is","options":["The carbon-carbon bond in ethyne is weaker than that in ethene","Both carbons are sp hybridised","The C–C bond in ethyne is shorter than that in ethene","Ethyne is linear"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are true NCERT statements.\nWorking: C≡C (bond enthalpy ~823 kJ mol⁻¹) is stronger and shorter than C=C (~681 kJ mol⁻¹); ethyne is linear with sp carbons.\nKey idea: Triple bond is more reactive to addition (π bonds) but overall stronger.","traps":["Reactivity vs bond strength: Total C≡C bond enthalpy > C=C."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Number of compounds from the following which cannot undergo Friedel-Crafts reactions is: ______\ntoluene, nitrobenzene, xylene, cumene, aniline, chlorobenzene, m-nitroaniline, m-dinitrobenzene","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if aniline-type are counted only once or m-nitroaniline missed; 5 if chlorobenzene counted as unreactive.\nWorking: nitrobenzene, m-dinitrobenzene (strongly deactivated), aniline and m-nitroaniline (NH₂ complexes AlCl₃) fail; toluene, xylene, cumene and chlorobenzene react.\nKey idea: FC fails for –NO₂ rings and for –NH₂ (Lewis base) rings; halobenzenes still react.","traps":["Chlorobenzene assumed unreactive: Haloarenes still undergo Friedel–Crafts reactions.","Amino group counted as activating: –NH₂ forms a complex with AlCl₃."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which of the following compound will most easily be attacked by an electrophile?\n(A) chlorobenzene (C₆H₅Cl)\n(B) phenol (C₆H₅OH)\n(C) benzene (C₆H₆)\n(D) toluene (C₆H₅CH₃)","options":["C₆H₅Cl (chlorobenzene)","C₆H₅OH (phenol)","C₆H₆ (benzene)","C₆H₅CH₃ (toluene)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): CH₃ activates but weaker than OH. (A): Cl is o/p directing but deactivating – student equates o/p with activation. (C): unsubstituted reference.\nWorking: –OH is a strong +R activator, stronger than CH₃ (+I/hyperconjugation); Cl deactivates.\nKey idea: Resonance donation by O lone pair outweighs hyperconjugation.","traps":["o/p director assumed to be activator: Halogens: −I > +R → deactivating though o/p directing."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Total number of deactivating groups in aromatic electrophilic substitution reaction among the following is _______ .\n–C(=O)OCH₃ (ring bonded to carbonyl C), –NH–C(=O)CH₃, –NH–CH₃, –C≡N, –OCH₃","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counts –NHCOCH₃ as deactivating because of the C=O), 1 (counts only –CN).\nWorking: Deactivating – –COOCH₃ and –CN (−R/−I). Activating – –NHCOCH₃ (moderate, N lone pair), –NHCH₃, –OCH₃. Count 2.\nKey idea: Atom attached to the ring decides: N/O with lone pair → activating; C of C=O or C≡N → deactivating.","traps":["Acetamido counted as deactivating: Look at the atom bonded to the ring (N with lone pair).","Ester orientation (–COOR vs –OCOR): –C(=O)OR bonded through C deactivates; –OC(=O)R activates."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"In the given reactions identify A and B.\nH₂ + A —[Pd/C]→ cis-alkene drawn as CH₃(H)C=C(H)C₂H₅ with CH₃ and C₂H₅ on the same side (cis-pent-2-ene)\nCH₃–C≡C–CH₃ + H₂ —[Na/Liquid NH₃]→ \"B\"","options":["A : n-Pentane, B : cis-2-butene","A : 2-Pentyne, B : cis-2-butene","A : n-Pentane, B : trans-2-butene","A : 2-Pentyne, B : trans-2-butene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) n-pentane cannot give an alkene on hydrogenation and B wrongly cis. (B) gets A right but takes Na/NH₃ as cis-selective. (C) right B but takes the alkene as coming from an alkane.\nWorking: partial hydrogenation of pent-2-yne on Pd gives cis-pent-2-ene, so A = 2-pentyne; Na/liq. NH₃ reduces but-2-yne to trans-but-2-ene.\nKey idea: Lindlar/Pd + H₂ = syn addition (cis); Na/liq. NH₃ = anti (trans).","traps":["Pd/C shown without poison: An alkane cannot give an alkene; work backwards from the cis alkene.","Na/liq. NH₃ geometry: Dissolving-metal reduction gives trans alkene."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The set of meta directing functional groups from the following sets is :","options":["–CN, –NH₂, –NHR, –OCH₃","–CN, –CHO, –NHCOCH₃, –COOR","–NO₂, –NH₂, –COOH, –COOR","–NO₂, –CHO, –SO₃H, –COR"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) –NH₂, –NHR, –OCH₃ are o/p. (B) –NHCOCH₃ is o/p (lone pair on N, though deactivating vs –NH₂). (C) –NH₂ is o/p.\nWorking: all four groups in (D) are –R/–I groups (meta directors); –NH₂, –NHR, –OCH₃ and –NHCOCH₃ are o/p directors.\nKey idea: Groups with a multiple bond to an electronegative atom attached directly to the ring are meta directors.","traps":["Acetamido group as meta: Atom attached to ring is N with lone pair → o/p director."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Peroxide effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"3-Methylhex-2-ene on reaction with HBr in presence of peroxide forms an addition product (A). The number of possible stereoisomers for 'A' is ________.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (Markovnikov product 3-bromo-3-methylhexane with one stereocentre; or seeing only one centre), 0 (missing chirality), 3 (assuming a meso form, not possible as ends differ).\nWorking: radical Br adds to C-2 to give the tertiary radical at C-3, so A = 2-bromo-3-methylhexane CH₃CHBr–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂CH₃; C-2 and C-3 are both stereocentres and dissimilar, so 2² = 4 stereoisomers.\nKey idea: Anti-Markovnikov places Br on C-2, creating a second stereocentre beside C-3.","traps":["Markovnikov product used: With peroxide, Br goes to the carbon that leaves the more stable (tertiary) radical.","Only one stereocentre counted: Check every sp³ carbon with four different groups."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Among the given organic compounds, the total number of aromatic compounds is\n(A) 4a,8a-dihydronaphthalene [two fused six-membered rings, each with two C=C; the two ring-fusion carbons are sp³ CH]\n(B) Benzyl-cyclohexene [benzene ring joined by CH₂ to a cyclohexene ring]\n(C) Indenyl anion [benzene fused to a five-membered ring with a carbanion lone pair shown]\n(D) Fluorenyl anion [two benzene rings fused to a central five-membered ring bearing a negative charge on the CH]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (reading (A) as naphthalene), 2 (excluding (B) because one ring is non-aromatic, or excluding an anion), 1 (only counting neutral benzenoids).\nWorking: (A) the sp³ fusion carbons break cyclic conjugation — non-aromatic; (B) contains a benzene ring — aromatic; (C) indenyl anion 10π planar — aromatic; (D) fluorenyl anion 14π, fully conjugated — aromatic. Total 3.\nKey idea: Check every ring atom is sp²; a single sp³ centre stops aromaticity, while a carbanion lone pair can complete it.","traps":["Dihydronaphthalene read as naphthalene: Count double bonds: naphthalene needs five; here fusion carbons are sp³.","Whole molecule must be aromatic: A compound containing a benzene ring counts as aromatic."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Among the following, total number of meta directing functional groups is (Integer based)\n–OCH₃, –NO₂, –CN, –CH₃, –NHCOCH₃, –COR, –OH, –COOH, –Cl","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (counting –Cl as meta because it deactivates), 6 (adding –NHCOCH₃ too, since the acyl part withdraws).\nWorking: meta directors are –NO₂, –CN, –COR, –COOH (4); –OCH₃, –CH₃, –NHCOCH₃, –OH and –Cl are ortho/para directors.\nKey idea: Deactivating ≠ meta directing: halogens are deactivating o/p directors.","traps":["Halogen counted as meta director: Halogens: –I deactivates, +R directs o/p.","Acetamido counted as meta: N lone pair attached to the ring makes it o/p directing."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The incorrect statement regarding conformations of ethane is :","options":["The dihedral angle in staggered conformation is 60°.","Ethane has infinite number of conformations.","Eclipsed conformation is the most stable conformation.","The conformations of ethane are inter-convertible to one-another."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true (60° between nearest H in Newman). (B) true. (D) true - low barrier allows free interconversion.\nWorking: staggered is most stable; eclipsed is least stable due to torsional strain.\nKey idea: Staggered = most stable.","traps":["Dihedral angle 60° doubted: Nearest C–H bonds in staggered ethane are 60° apart."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Identify product A and product B :\n[Scheme: cyclohexene + Cl₂ —hν→ Product A; cyclohexene + Cl₂ —CCl₄→ Product B]","options":["A: 3,6-dichlorocyclohex-1-ene (Cl on both allylic carbons, ring C=C retained); B: 1,2-dichlorocyclohexane","A: 1,2-dichlorocyclohex-1-ene (Cl on both alkene carbons); B: 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane","A: 1,2-dichlorocyclohexane; B: 1,2-dichlorocyclohexane","A: 3,6-dichlorocyclohex-1-ene; B: 3,6-dichlorocyclohex-1-ene"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) vinylic substitution and 1,4-addition – neither happens; (C) treats both conditions as addition; (D) treats both as substitution.\nWorking: with light (radical conditions) Cl substitutes at the allylic positions keeping the C=C; in CCl₄ (dark) Cl₂ adds across the C=C to give vicinal dichloride. Option A's drawing shows allylic dichlorination, which is the only option with the correct pattern.\nKey idea: hν → radicals → weakest C–H (allylic) is substituted; dark/CCl₄ → electrophilic addition to C=C.","traps":["Light also gives addition: Light means radical substitution at the allylic C–H.","Vinylic Cl assumed: Vinylic C–H is strong; allylic radical is resonance stabilised."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"[Scheme: trans-but-2-ene, CH₃(H)C=C(H)CH₃ drawn with CH₃ groups on opposite sides —(i) O₃; (ii) Zn + H₂O→ (P) product]\nConsider the given reaction. The total number of oxygen atom/s present per molecule of the product (P) is _________.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if both ethanal molecules counted together; 3 if ozonide counted; geometry (trans) is irrelevant.\nWorking: reductive ozonolysis splits but-2-ene into two molecules of ethanal (CH₃CHO); each has one O.\nKey idea: Symmetrical alkene → two identical aldehydes, each with one O.","traps":["Counting O over both product molecules: Question asks per molecule of P.","Trans geometry thought to matter: Ozonolysis destroys the C=C; cis/trans give same products."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following reaction is correct?","options":["C₂H₅CONH₂ + Br₂ + NaOH → C₂H₅CH₂NH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + NaBr + H₂O","cyclohexene + Br₂ —(Δ, UV light)→ 1,2-dibromocyclohexane","1-methylcyclohexene + HI → 1-iodo-1-methylcyclohexane","CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ —(HNO₂, 0 °C; H₂O)→ CH₃CH₂OH + N₂ + HCl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) carbon count kept in Hofmann product. (B) ionic addition product shown under radical (UV, heat) conditions. (D) chain shortened by one carbon in the alcohol.\nWorking: (C) Markovnikov addition — I goes to the tertiary ring carbon. (A) Hofmann gives C₂H₅NH₂ (one carbon fewer), not C₂H₅CH₂NH₂. (B) Br₂ with heat/UV gives allylic substitution (3-bromocyclohexene), not the 1,2-adduct. (D) propylamine gives propan-1-ol, not ethanol.\nKey idea: Check carbon count and conditions for each option; only the HI addition is right.","traps":["Hofmann product keeps all carbons: Hofmann bromamide loses the C=O carbon as CO₃²⁻.","UV/heat with Br₂ read as addition: High temperature/UV → allylic substitution."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Compound A formed in the following reaction reacts with B to give the product C. Find out A and B.\nCH₃–C≡CH + Na → A —B→ CH₃–C≡C–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃ (C) + NaBr","options":["A = CH₃–C≡C⁻ Na⁺, B = CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br","A = CH₃–CH=CH₂, B = CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br","A = CH₃–C≡C⁻ Na⁺, B = CH₃–CH₂–CH₃","A = CH₃–CH₂–CH₃, B = CH₃–C≡CH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Propene: Na does not reduce the alkyne here (Na/liq NH₃ would). (C) Propane cannot supply a carbon and NaBr. (D) Na does not hydrogenate to propane; B must contain Br to form NaBr.\nWorking: Na removes the terminal acidic H to give sodium propynide; SN2 alkylation with 1-bromopropane gives hex-2-yne and NaBr.\nKey idea: NaBr by-product shows B is an alkyl bromide; A must be the nucleophilic alkynide.","traps":["Na taken as reducing agent: Without liq. NH₃/proton source, Na just removes the acidic ≡C–H.","Ignoring NaBr by-product: Balance atoms: Br must come from B."],"topics":["Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","Acidity of alkynes"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following molecule/species is most stable?","options":["Cyclohexa-1,3-diene (neutral, six-membered ring with two C=C)","Cyclopropenyl cation (three-membered ring, one C=C, positive charge on the third carbon)","Cyclopentadienyl cation (five-membered ring, two C=C, positive charge on the sp² CH)","Three-membered ring with one C=C and two dots (lone pair) on the third carbon, no charge shown (cyclopropenylidene, a carbene)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Non-aromatic (sp³ CH₂ breaks conjugation) – neutral so tempting. (C) 4 π electrons → antiaromatic. (D) Neutral cyclopropenylidene: a carbene (electron-deficient divalent carbon, 6 valence electrons) – a highly reactive intermediate, far less stable than the aromatic cyclopropenyl cation.\nWorking: cyclopropenyl cation is planar, cyclic, fully conjugated with 2 π electrons (4n+2, n=0) → aromatic; cyclopentadienyl cation (4 π) is antiaromatic; cyclopropenylidene (D) is a reactive carbene; cyclohexa-1,3-diene is non-aromatic.\nKey idea: Count π electrons including the charge/lone pair: 2 → aromatic.","traps":["Neutral molecule assumed most stable: Aromatic stabilisation outweighs charge.","Carbene lone pair taken as extra stabilisation: Two dots with no charge = carbene (6 valence electrons on C), a reactive intermediate; only (B) is aromatic (2 π e⁻)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkane preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Number of alkanes obtained on electrolysis of a mixture of CH₃COONa and C₂H₅COONa is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (only symmetric coupling: ethane, butane), 1 (only one salt considered).\nWorking: Kolbe electrolysis gives CH₃• and C₂H₅• radicals; coupling gives ethane, propane and butane → 3.\nKey idea: Two radicals → three coupling products (R–R, R–R′, R′–R′).","traps":["Cross product missed: List all radical pairs."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Major product of the following reaction is – [Structure: 1-methylcyclopentene (cyclopentene ring with CH₃ on one alkene carbon)] —(D–Cl)→ ?","options":["[Cyclopentane; one ring carbon bears H and Cl, the adjacent carbon bears D and CH₃ (H and D drawn up, Cl and CH₃ down)]","[Cyclopentane; one ring carbon bears Cl and H, the adjacent carbon bears D and CH₃ (Cl and D drawn up, H and CH₃ down)]","[Cyclopentane; one ring carbon bears D and H, the adjacent carbon bears Cl and CH₃ (D and Cl drawn up, H and CH₃ down) = 1-chloro-2-deuterio-1-methylcyclopentane]","[Cyclopentane; one ring carbon bears D and H, the adjacent carbon bears CH₃ and Cl (D and CH₃ drawn up, H and Cl down)]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): Cl on the CH carbon and D on the CH₃-bearing carbon – anti-Markovnikov placement (as if D were the nucleophilic part or a peroxide-type pathway). (D): same constitution as (C) but drawn with Cl trans to D; a student fixated on stereochemistry may agonise between C and D.\nWorking: Markovnikov addition – D⁺ adds to the ring CH carbon to give the tertiary carbocation, Cl⁻ then bonds to the CH₃-bearing carbon. (D) has the same constitution (a diastereomer – both form via the planar carbocation), so only (A)/(B) are clearly wrong; (C) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: D⁺ is the electrophile – it goes where H⁺ would go; Cl ends on the tertiary carbon.","traps":["D treated as nucleophilic part: D–Cl polarises as Dᵟ⁺–Clᵟ⁻; D is the electrophile like H in HCl.","Stereo drawing of C vs D: Carbocation route is not stereospecific; pick the option with the correct connectivity."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct order of reactivity in electrophilic substitution reaction of the following compounds is : A: benzene, B: toluene (C₆H₅CH₃), C: chlorobenzene (C₆H₅Cl), D: nitrobenzene (C₆H₅NO₂)","options":["B > C > A > D","B > A > C > D","D > C > B > A","A > B > C > D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts chlorobenzene above benzene (treating o/p-director as activator). (C) reverses everything. (D) puts benzene above toluene.\nWorking: CH₃ activates; Cl is mildly deactivating (–I > +R); NO₂ strongly deactivating.\nKey idea: Cl directs o/p but deactivates – it is slower than benzene.","traps":["o/p director assumed activating: Direction and rate are separate: –I of Cl wins on rate."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Number of isomeric products formed by monochlorination of 2-methylbutane in presence of sunlight is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (constitutional isomers only); 5 (one chiral centre missed); 8 (double counts achiral products).\nWorking: four constitutional monochlorides (1-chloro-2-methylbutane*, 2-chloro-2-methylbutane, 2-chloro-3-methylbutane*, 1-chloro-3-methylbutane); the two starred ones are chiral (2 each) → 6 including stereoisomers.\nKey idea: Count structural products, then add enantiomers of each chiral product.","traps":["Stereoisomers ignored: Check each product for a stereocentre.","Chiral centre missed in 2-chloro-3-methylbutane: List four different groups at each CHCl."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Number of bromo derivatives obtained on treating ethane with excess of Br₂ in diffused sunlight is ______","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6: counts only one compound per formula. 8: misses one of the di/tri/tetrabromo isomers. 10: counts ethane or a duplicate (1,2,2-tribromo = 1,1,2).\nWorking: C₂H₅Br (1), C₂H₄Br₂ (1,1 and 1,2 = 2), C₂H₃Br₃ (1,1,1 and 1,1,2 = 2), C₂H₂Br₄ (1,1,1,2 and 1,1,2,2 = 2), C₂HBr₅ (1), C₂Br₆ (1) → 9.\nKey idea: Ethane's two carbons give 2 isomers for n = 2, 3, 4 and 1 each for n = 1, 5, 6.","traps":["One compound per formula: List positional isomers for each n.","Duplicate isomers counted: Renumber from the other end to merge duplicates."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Molar mass of the hydrocarbon (X) which on ozonolysis consumes one mole of O₃ per mole of (X) and gives one mole each of ethanal and propanone is ______ g mol⁻¹\n(Molar mass of C : 12 g mol⁻¹, H : 1 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"70","range":[70.0,70.0],"explain":"Common slips: 102 (adds O-containing fragments 44 + 58), 72 (alkane formula C₅H₁₂), 84 (adds an extra CH₂).\nWorking: one C=C; joining CH₃CHO and (CH₃)₂CO at carbonyl carbons → CH₃CH=C(CH₃)₂, 2-methylbut-2-ene, C₅H₁₀ = 60 + 10 = 70.\nKey idea: Replace both C=O by one C=C: C₂ + C₃ fragments → C₅H₁₀.","traps":["Fragments' masses added: Remove the O atoms and join the carbons.","Alkane formula used: One C=C → CₙH₂ₙ."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Major product 'P' formed in the following reaction is: [Scheme: pent-4-enoic acid, CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH₂–COOH, treated with Br₂ / NaHCO₃ → P (major product)]","options":["4,5-Dibromopentanoic acid, BrCH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₂–COOH","4-Bromopentanoic acid, CH₃–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₂–COOH","5-(Bromomethyl)oxolan-2-one (γ-lactone: five-membered ring O–C(=O)–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₂Br)–)","5-Bromopentanoic acid, BrCH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–COOH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the plain NCERT anti-addition dibromide (ignores NaHCO₃ and the internal nucleophile); (B) is HBr Markovnikov addition; (D) is anti-Markovnikov HBr addition—neither involves Br₂.\nWorking: NaHCO₃ converts the acid to carboxylate; Br₂ forms a bromonium ion on the terminal alkene and the carboxylate opens it intramolecularly at the internal carbon (5-exo), giving the γ-butyrolactone with a CH₂Br side chain (bromolactonisation).\nKey idea: NaHCO₃ is there to make the carboxylate, which is a better nucleophile than Br⁻ and is held close, so a 5-membered ring closes.","traps":["Plain dibromide chosen: Look for an internal nucleophile and ask why base is added.","Role of NaHCO₃ ignored: Base deprotonates COOH; the carboxylate traps the bromonium ion."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In the following reaction 'X' is\nCH₃(CH₂)₄CH₃ —(Anhy. AlCl₃, HCl, Δ)→ 'X' (major product)","options":["CH₃(CH₂)₄CH₂Cl","CH₃CH(CH₃)–(CH₂)₂CH₃ (2-methylpentane)","Cl–CH₂–(CH₂)₄–CH₂–Cl","cyclohexane (six CH₂ groups in a ring)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): HCl misread as a chlorinating reagent (mono- or dichlorination). (D): confusion with aromatisation/cyclisation of n-hexane (which needs Cr₂O₃/V₂O₅ at high T and gives benzene, not cyclohexane).\nWorking: n-alkanes heated with anhydrous AlCl₃/HCl isomerise to branched alkanes; n-hexane gives 2-methylpentane (and 3-methylpentane). HCl is a co-catalyst, not a chlorinating agent.\nKey idea: AlCl₃/HCl on an n-alkane = skeletal isomerisation to branched isomers.","traps":["HCl taken as chlorinating agent: AlCl₃/HCl with alkane and heat = isomerisation.","Confusion with aromatisation/cyclisation: Aromatisation needs Cr₂O₃/V₂O₅/Mo₂O₃ at 773 K."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Among the following compounds, the one which shows highest dipole moment is","options":["Pentafulvalene: two cyclopentadiene rings (each with two ring C=C) joined by an exocyclic C=C","Two six-membered rings, each with two ring C=C, joined by an exocyclic C=C (symmetric, as drawn)","Cyclopropanone (C=O on a cyclopropane ring)","Calicene (triapentafulvalene): cyclopropene ring (one ring C=C) joined by an exocyclic C=C to a cyclopentadiene ring (two ring C=C)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): symmetric molecules – charge separation would not make both rings aromatic and any polarisation cancels; (C): C=O dipole seems large since it has a heteroatom – the trap is to assume only heteroatoms create big dipoles.\nWorking: in calicene the polarised central C=C gives an aromatic cyclopropenyl cation (2π) and an aromatic cyclopentadienyl anion (6π); this charge separation gives a large dipole moment. (A) and (B) are symmetric (μ ≈ 0); cyclopropanone has a moderate C=O dipole (~2.7 D) only.\nKey idea: Polarise the exocyclic C=C so that both rings become aromatic: 3-ring⁺ (2π) and 5-ring⁻ (6π).","traps":["Heteroatom assumed to give the largest dipole: Check whether a hydrocarbon can gain aromatic ions by charge separation.","Ring sizes misread in the drawings: Count ring atoms and π electrons in each ring."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"But-2-yne is reacted separately with one mole of Hydrogen as shown below :\nB ←(Na / liq NH₃) CH₃−C≡C−CH₃ + H₂ →(Pd/C, Δ) A\nA. A is more soluble than B.\nB. The boiling point & melting point of A are higher and lower than B respectively.\nC. A is more polar than B because dipole moment of A is zero.\nD. Br₂ adds easily to B than A.\nIdentify the incorrect statements from the options given below :","options":["A and B only","A, C & D only","B, C & D only","C and D only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A is true (polar cis more soluble). B is true (cis: higher b.p., lower m.p.). C is incorrect: A is more polar but its dipole moment is not zero. D is incorrect: the less stable cis isomer adds Br₂ at least as readily as trans. Options (A), (B), (C) include true statements A or B.\nWorking: H₂ on Pd/C adds syn on the metal surface, so A = cis-but-2-ene; Na/liq. NH₃ gives B = trans-but-2-ene. Statements A and B are true; C and D are incorrect, so the answer is \"C and D only\".\nKey idea: A = cis-but-2-ene, B = trans-but-2-ene. Cis is polar (μ ≈ 0.33 D), so higher b.p. and solubility; trans packs better, so higher m.p.","traps":["A taken as trans: Catalytic syn addition on a metal surface gives cis.","B.p. and m.p. trends assumed parallel: Cis: higher b.p. (polar); trans: higher m.p. (symmetry, packing)."],"topics":["Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","Alkene isomerism"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"'X' is : Benzene + methylenecyclohexane (cyclohexane ring with exocyclic C=CH₂) —[HF, Δ]→ X (Major product)","options":["1-methyl-2-phenylcyclohexane (phenyl on C1, CH₃ on adjacent C2 of cyclohexane)","2-cyclohexyltoluene (cyclohexyl on benzene, CH₃ ortho on the benzene ring)","1-methyl-3-phenylcyclohexane (CH₃ on C3 of the cyclohexane, meta-like to the phenyl-bearing C1)","(1-methylcyclohexyl)benzene (phenyl and CH₃ on the same cyclohexane carbon)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses a secondary cation (phenyl at ring carbon next to C–CH₃) - anti-Markovnikov or hydride-shift error. (B) puts the methyl on the benzene ring (confuses which part is alkylated). (C) implies a remote secondary cation.\nWorking: HF protonates the =CH₂ end of methylenecyclohexane, giving the tertiary 1-methylcyclohexyl cation; benzene attacks it (Friedel–Crafts alkylation) to give (1-methylcyclohexyl)benzene.\nKey idea: Protonate the CH₂ end so the positive charge sits on the ring carbon (tertiary); benzene bonds there, so CH₃ and Ph share one carbon.","traps":["Primary cation from protonating ring carbon: Add H⁺ to CH₂ to make the tertiary ring cation (Markovnikov).","Methyl placed on the aromatic ring: Benzene is the nucleophile; the alkyl group from the alkene keeps its CH₃."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structure of benzene","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A : Benzene is more stable than hypothetical cyclohexatriene. Reason R : The delocalised π electron cloud is attracted more strongly by nuclei of carbon atoms. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is true but R is false","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): doubting the explanation link; (A)/(D): not recalling the NCERT line.\nWorking: in benzene the π electrons are delocalised over all six carbons and are held more strongly by the carbon nuclei than in a structure with three localised C=C; this lowers the energy (resonance stabilisation). So A is true and R explains it.\nKey idea: The NCERT paragraph states exactly this cause-and-effect.","traps":["Explanation link: If NCERT uses 'therefore' between them, R explains A."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Maximum number of isomeric monochloro derivatives which can be obtained from 2,2,5,5-tetramethylhexane by chlorination is ________","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (structural only; ignores chirality at C-3); 4 (counting quaternary C as substitutable or C-3/C-4 as different).\nWorking: (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–CH₂–C(CH₃)₃ has two H types → ClCH₂ product (achiral) and CH(Cl) at C-3 (chiral, R + S) = 3 counting stereoisomers.\nKey idea: Symmetry gives two structural products; the CH(Cl) carbon becomes a stereocentre so 'maximum' adds an enantiomer.","traps":["Missing new stereocentre: Check each product for a new chiral carbon.","Symmetry-equivalent H: Molecule is symmetric about C3–C4 bond."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following conformations will be the most stable? (Newman projections of butane about C2–C3)","options":["Staggered, Me groups at 180° (anti)","Eclipsed, each Me eclipsed with an H","Staggered, Me groups at 60° (gauche)","Eclipsed, Me eclipsed with Me (fully eclipsed)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) gauche is staggered but has Me/Me steric strain. (B) and (D) are eclipsed; (D) is the least stable.\nWorking: staggered forms have no torsional strain; of these, anti (A) keeps the two CH₃ groups 180° apart, avoiding the gauche CH₃/CH₃ strain of (C). Eclipsed forms (B) and (D) are higher in energy.\nKey idea: Staggered + bulky groups 180° apart = anti = lowest energy.","traps":["Gauche taken as most stable: Among staggered forms, keep the two Me groups 180° apart."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"17 mg of a hydrocarbon (M.F. C₁₀H₁₆) takes up 8.40 mL of the H₂ gas measured at 0 °C and 760 mm of Hg. Ozonolysis of the same hydrocarbon yields\nCH₃–CO–CH₃, H–CHO, H–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CHO (OHC–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CHO)\nThe number of double bond/s present in the hydrocarbon is ___________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (from degrees of unsaturation of C₁₀H₁₆ = 3, plus a ring assumed, or counting carbonyl ends/2 incorrectly); 2 (counting distinct products).\nWorking: moles of X = 17 mg ÷ 136 g mol⁻¹ = 0.125 mmol; moles of H₂ = 8.40 mL ÷ 22400 mL mol⁻¹ = 0.375 mmol. H₂ : X = 3 : 1, so X has 3 C=C (consistent with 6 carbonyl ends in the ozonolysis products).\nKey idea: H₂ uptake mole ratio gives the C=C count directly.","traps":["Counting C=C from products: Use H₂ mole ratio; check by carbonyl ends (6 → 3 C=C).","mg and mL scaling: Use mmol for both."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The major products 'A' and 'B', respectively, are: 'A' ←[cold H₂SO₄]— H₃C–C(CH₃)=CH₂ (2-methylpropene) —[H₂SO₄, 80 °C]→ 'B'","options":["A = (CH₃)₃C–OSO₃H (tert-butyl hydrogen sulphate); B = CH₃–C(CH₃)=CH–C(CH₃)₂–CH₃ (2,4,4-trimethylpent-2-ene)","A = (CH₃)₃C–OSO₃H; B = CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₃ (2,5-dimethylhexane)","A = CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₃ (2,5-dimethylhexane); B = (CH₃)₃C–OSO₃H","A = CH₃–C(CH₃)=CH–C(CH₃)₂–CH₃ (2,4,4-trimethylpent-2-ene); B = (CH₃)₃C–OSO₃H"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B/C: 2,5-dimethylhexane is a saturated head-to-head dimer, which cannot form by cationic dimerisation (needs reduction). C is option B's pair in reversed order; D has exactly the right two structures but in reversed order (A and B swapped).\nWorking: in cold H₂SO₄ the tert-butyl cation is trapped by HSO₄⁻, giving tert-butyl hydrogen sulphate (A); at 80 °C the tert-butyl cation adds to a second molecule of 2-methylpropene and the dimeric cation loses H⁺, giving 2,4,4-trimethylpentene (B).\nKey idea: At higher temperature the tert-butyl cation adds to another isobutylene; the dimeric cation loses H⁺ to give 2,4,4-trimethylpentene (shown in the options as the pent-2-ene isomer).","traps":["Products listed in reversed order: Check which arrow (cold vs 80 °C) leads to which letter.","Warm acid gives dimer, not sulphate: Cold conc. acid traps the cation as HSO₄⁻ adduct; warm acid lets the cation attack another alkene."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Choose the correct set of reagents for the following conversion. trans (Ph–CH=CH–CH₃) → cis (Ph–CH=CH–CH₃)","options":["Br₂, alc. KOH, NaNH₂, H₂ Lindlar Catalyst","Br₂, aq. KOH, NaNH₂, Na (Liq NH₃)","Br₂, alc. KOH, NaNH₂, Na (Liq NH₃)","Br₂, aq. KOH, NaNH₂, H₂ Lindlar Catalyst"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aq. KOH gives substitution, not elimination, and Na/liq NH₃ returns the trans alkene. (C) right up to the alkyne but Na/liq NH₃ gives trans. (D) Lindlar right but aq. KOH wrong.\nWorking: Br₂ adds to give Ph–CHBr–CHBr–CH₃; alc. KOH and then NaNH₂ remove two HBr to give Ph–C≡C–CH₃; H₂ with Lindlar catalyst adds syn, giving cis-Ph–CH=CH–CH₃.\nKey idea: Destroy the double bond (Br₂), make the alkyne (alc. KOH then NaNH₂), and rebuild it syn with Lindlar to get cis.","traps":["alc. KOH vs aq. KOH: alc. KOH = elimination; aq. KOH = substitution.","Lindlar vs Na/liq NH₃ stereochemistry: Lindlar = syn addition → cis; Na/NH₃ = anti → trans."],"topics":["Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","Alkyne preparation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"A hydrocarbon 'X' with formula C₆H₈ uses two moles of H₂ on catalytic hydrogenation of its one mole. On ozonolysis, 'X' yields two moles of methane dicarbaldehyde. The hydrocarbon 'X' is:","options":["hexa-1,3,5-triene","cyclohexa-1,4-diene","cyclohexa-1,3-diene","1-methylcyclopenta-1,4-diene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) triene uses 3 H₂ and gives HCHO + OHC–CHO. (C) 1,3-diene gives OHC–CHO + OHC–CH₂CH₂–CHO. (D) methylcyclopentadiene gives different, unsymmetrical fragments.\nWorking: C₆H₈ has 3 degrees of unsaturation; 2 H₂ taken up means 2 C=C + one ring. Two OHC–CH₂–CHO (propanedial) from a C₆ ring need a CH₂ between the two C=C on both sides → cyclohexa-1,4-diene.\nKey idea: Two identical C₃ dialdehydes from C₆ means the ring is cut symmetrically at two isolated C=C separated by CH₂.","traps":["Name 'methane dicarbaldehyde' not recognised: Methane + two CHO = propanedial.","Conjugated vs isolated diene: Cut each C=C and check fragment sizes."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"A compound 'A' on reaction with 'X' and 'Y' produces the same major product but different by-product 'a' and 'b'. Oxidation of 'a' gives a substance produced by ants. Compound 'A': H₂C=C(CH₃)–CH₂–C(CH₃)₃ (2,4,4-trimethylpent-1-ene). With X: a + O=C(CH₃)–CH₂–C(CH₃)₃; with Y: b + O=C(CH₃)–CH₂–C(CH₃)₃ (4,4-dimethylpentan-2-one). 'X' and 'Y' respectively are:","options":["KMnO₄/H⁺ and dil. KMnO₄, 273 K","KMnO₄ (dilute), 273 K and KMnO₄/H⁺","KMnO₄/H⁺ and O₃, H₂O/Zn","O₃, H₂O/Zn and KMnO₄/H⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) use cold dilute KMnO₄, which gives a vicinal diol (Baeyer test), not cleavage; (C) is the right pair in the wrong order (KMnO₄/H⁺ gives CO₂, whose \"oxidation\" cannot give formic acid).\nWorking: reductive ozonolysis cleaves =CH₂ to HCHO (a), which oxidises to formic acid (ant acid); acidic KMnO₄ cleaves =CH₂ to CO₂ + H₂O (b); both give the same methyl ketone. Cold dilute KMnO₄ would give a diol, not the ketone.\nKey idea: a = HCHO (oxidises to HCOOH) → X = O₃/Zn; b = CO₂ → Y = KMnO₄/H⁺.","traps":["Order of X and Y: Identify a from the ant clue: HCHO comes from ozonolysis.","Cold dilute KMnO₄ cleaves?: Cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ → diol only."],"topics":["Alkene oxidation","Ozonolysis"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Major product of the following reaction is: CH₂=CH–C(=O)–O–CH₂–CH=CH₂ (allyl acrylate, prop-2-en-1-yl prop-2-enoate) + 2 HBr → ?","options":["CH₃CH(Br)C(=O)OCH₂CH₂CH₂Br","CH₃CH(Br)C(=O)OCH₂CH(Br)CH₃","BrCH₂CH₂C(=O)OCH₂CH₂CH₂Br","BrCH₂CH₂C(=O)OCH₂CH(Br)CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) applies Markovnikov to both C=C (ignores conjugation with C=O); (C) applies anti-Markovnikov to both (as with peroxide); (A) reverses both.\nWorking: the acrylate C=C is conjugated with C=O; H⁺ adds to O and Br⁻ adds at the β-carbon (conjugate addition) giving BrCH₂CH₂CO–; the isolated allyl C=C follows Markovnikov (secondary cation) giving –OCH₂CH(Br)CH₃.\nKey idea: Electron-poor alkene next to C=O: the carbocation would sit α to C=O (unstable), so Br ends up β.","traps":["Markovnikov applied to the acrylate C=C: Check whether the C=C is conjugated with C=O; α-carbocation is destabilised.","Anti-Markovnikov everywhere: No peroxide given; isolated alkene follows Markovnikov."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The total number of monobromo derivatives formed by the alkanes with molecular formula C₅H₁₂ is (excluding stereo isomers) _____","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (only n-pentane), 7 (missing one site in 2-methylbutane), 9+ (counting stereoisomers or duplicates).\nWorking: pentane gives 3 (1-, 2-, 3-bromopentane), 2-methylbutane gives 4, 2,2-dimethylpropane gives 1; total 8.\nKey idea: Count non-equivalent H types per isomer: 3 + 4 + 1.","traps":["Only n-pentane considered: Include all three chain isomers.","Duplicate or chiral counting: Stereoisomers excluded; count each constitution once."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Cl• + CH₄ → A + B\nA and B in the above atmospheric reaction step are","options":["C₂H₆ and Cl₂","•CHCl₂ and H₂","•CH₃ and HCl","C₂H₆ and HCl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) give ethane – a termination product, not this step; (B) has a dichloromethyl radical and H₂ – atom balance fails.\nWorking: a chlorine radical abstracts H from methane: Cl• + CH₄ → •CH₃ + HCl (propagation step).\nKey idea: Radical + C–H → new radical + H–X.","traps":["Termination product chosen: One radical in, one radical out in a propagation step.","\"Atmospheric\" context distracts: Treat it as the textbook methane chlorination step."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following compounds is not aromatic?\n(A) cyclopentadienyl anion\n(B) cyclopropenyl cation\n(C) [10]annulene drawn with two hydrogens pointing into the ring (cyclodecapentaene)\n(D) naphthalene","options":["Cyclopentadienyl anion (C₅H₅⁻)","Cyclopropenyl cation (C₃H₃⁺)","[10]Annulene with two inward-pointing H atoms (C₁₀H₁₀)","Naphthalene (C₁₀H₈)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) look non-aromatic to students who forget charged rings can be aromatic; (D) fused rings may be doubted.\nWorking: [10]annulene has 10 π electrons but the two inner H atoms clash, so the ring cannot be planar – not aromatic. C₅H₅⁻ (6π), C₃H₃⁺ (2π) and naphthalene (10π, planar) are aromatic.\nKey idea: 10 π electrons are not enough – inner H–H clash makes [10]annulene non-planar.","traps":["Counting π electrons only: Check planarity: inward H atoms prevent it.","Charged rings dismissed: Count π electrons including the charge."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Benzene – preparation and reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is\n2-Nitroanisole (1-methoxy-2-nitrobenzene) —(i) Na/liq. NH₃ (ii) CH₃CH₂OH→","options":["Cyclohexa-1,4-diene with OCH₃ on a ring double-bond carbon and NO₂ on the adjacent sp³ carbon (C bearing H and NO₂)","Same skeleton as (A) but with NH₂ in place of NO₂ on the sp³ carbon","Cyclohexa-1,4-diene with OH (not OCH₃) and NO₂ on adjacent double-bond carbons","Cyclohexa-1,4-diene with OCH₃ on an sp³ carbon (bearing H) and NO₂ on the adjacent double-bond carbon"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes nitro reduced to amine (realistic but not the intended answer); (C) demethylates the ether; (D) reverses the rule (OCH₃ on sp³ carbon).\nWorking: the electron-withdrawing NO₂-bearing carbon becomes sp³ and the electron-donating OCH₃ stays on a double bond → (A). Note: in practice Na/liq. NH₃ also reduces an aromatic nitro group, so the question is chemically idealised; the intended answer is (A).\nKey idea: Birch: EWG carbon ends up sp³, EDG carbon stays sp².","traps":["EWG vs EDG position reversed: Anion forms first at the carbon that stabilises negative charge – the EWG carbon.","Nitro group reduction: Follow the intended Birch question; note the caveat."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Benzene – preparation and reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In the presence of sunlight, benzene reacts with Cl₂ to give product, X. The number of hydrogens in X is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (chlorobenzene by substitution), 0 (hexachlorobenzene C₆Cl₆), 12 (miscount).\nWorking: free-radical addition in UV/sunlight gives benzene hexachloride C₆H₆Cl₆ (BHC), which has 6 H.\nKey idea: Sunlight/UV → addition, H atoms stay: C₆H₆Cl₆.","traps":["Substitution assumed: Sunlight → radical addition.","BHC vs hexachlorobenzene: BHC keeps all six H."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Arrange the following in increasing order of reactivity towards nitration: A. p-xylene; B. bromobenzene; C. mesitylene; D. nitrobenzene; E. benzene. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["C < D < E < A < B","D < B < E < A < C","D < C < E < A < B","C < D < E < B < A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D) misplace mesitylene (most reactive) or bromobenzene (less reactive than benzene).\nWorking: NO₂ strongly deactivates; Br deactivates mildly (–I > +R); benzene reference; two CH₃ activate; three CH₃ (mesitylene) activate most. D < B < E < A < C.\nKey idea: More alkyl groups → faster EAS; halogens deactivate; nitro strongly deactivates.","traps":["Bromobenzene vs benzene: Halogens are o/p-directing yet deactivating.","Increasing vs decreasing order: Start the list with the least reactive."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structure of benzene","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which among the following pairs of the structures will give different products on ozonolysis? (Consider the double bonds in the structures are rigid and not delocalized.) Each option shows the two Kekulé structures of one compound: (A) m-xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene) ↔ its other Kekulé form; (B) p-xylene ↔ other Kekulé form; (C) o-xylene ↔ other Kekulé form; (D) toluene ↔ other Kekulé form.","options":["Kekulé structures of m-xylene","Kekulé structures of p-xylene","Kekulé structures of o-xylene","Kekulé structures of toluene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(D): both forms give identical fragments (e.g. m-xylene: 2 methylglyoxal + 1 glyoxal both ways).\nWorking: the o-xylene Kekulé form with a single bond between the two methyl-bearing carbons (C1–C2) gives 1 butane-2,3-dione + 2 glyoxal; the form with C1=C2 gives 2 methylglyoxal + 1 glyoxal – different. For m-xylene, p-xylene and toluene both Kekulé forms give the same set of fragments.\nKey idea: Only when two substituted carbons are adjacent can a C=C sit between them in one form and not the other.","traps":["Every unsymmetrical ring assumed to differ: Write the fragments for each form.","Missing dimethylglyoxal fragment: Check whether the two CH₃ carbons are directly bonded."],"topics":["Structure of benzene","Ozonolysis"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"In bromination of Propyne, with Bromine 1,1,2,2-tetrabromopropane is obtained in 27% yield. The amount of 1,1,2,2-tetrabromopropane obtained from 1 g of Bromine in this reaction is ______ × 10⁻¹ g. (Nearest integer) (Molar Mass : Bromine = 80 g/mol)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (forgetting 2 mol Br₂ per alkyne); 11 (ignoring the 27% yield: 1.125 g).\nWorking: CH₃C≡CH + 2Br₂ → CH₃CBr₂CHBr₂ (M = 360). 1 g Br₂ = 1/160 mol → 1/320 mol product theoretically = 1.125 g; 27% → 0.304 g = 3 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: 2 Br₂ per propyne; Br₂ = 160 g mol⁻¹.","traps":["Br₂ molar mass as 80: Br₂ = 160 g mol⁻¹.","Two moles of Br₂ needed: Tetrabromo product needs 2 Br₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"In the given reaction sequence, the major product 'C' is: C₈H₁₀ —[HNO₃/H₂SO₄]→ A —[Br₂, Δ]→ B —[alcoholic KOH]→ C.","options":["C₆H₅–C(NO₂)=CH₂","p-O₂N–C₆H₄–CH=CH₂","p-O₂N–C₆H₄–C(NO₂)=CH₂","o-O₂N–C₆H₄–CH=CH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) ortho product (minor, steric hindrance); (A) nitro on the side chain (nitration wrongly placed on the alkyl group); (C) double nitration with side-chain NO₂.\nWorking: C₈H₁₀ = ethylbenzene (consistent with the options). Nitration → mainly 4-nitroethylbenzene (ortho/para director, para favoured sterically). Br₂/heat → benzylic bromination, 1-bromo-1-(4-nitrophenyl)ethane. Alcoholic KOH → dehydrobromination → 4-nitrostyrene.\nKey idea: Br₂ with heat/light and no Lewis acid attacks the benzylic C–H, not the ring.","traps":["Ortho product picked: Para is major for a bulky alkyl group.","Ring vs side-chain bromination: Br₂/Δ without catalyst → free-radical benzylic substitution."],"topics":["Directive influence","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The major product 'A' of the following given reaction has ____ sp² hybridized carbon atoms. 2,7-Dimethylocta-2,6-diene —[H⁺]→ A (major product)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (no cyclisation – both C=C kept after simple protonation/isomerisation); 0 (cyclisation followed by capture of the cation by water, no alkene left); 3 (miscounting).\nWorking: protonation of C6=C7 at C6 gives a tertiary cation at C7; the C2=C3 double bond attacks through C3, closing a five-membered ring (C3–C7) and leaving a tertiary cation at C2; loss of H⁺ gives the tetrasubstituted alkene 1,1-dimethyl-2-(propan-2-ylidene)cyclopentane. One C=C → 2 sp² carbons.\nKey idea: The second alkene acts as an internal nucleophile; one C=C is consumed, one regenerated.","traps":["Cyclisation not recognised: With H⁺ and no external nucleophile, the other C=C traps the cation.","Final cation captured, not deprotonated: Major path is E1 loss of H⁺ to the more substituted alkene."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – structure and stability","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The presence of weaker π-bonds make alkenes less stable than alkanes. Statement II: The strength of the double bond is greater than that of carbon-carbon single bond. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – student feels \"weaker π bond\" contradicts \"stronger double bond\" and rejects II; (D) – rejects I by thinking a stronger double bond means more stable alkenes; (B) – rejects both.\nWorking: I is the NCERT sentence (weaker π bond makes alkenes less stable than alkanes); II is true – C=C bond enthalpy (≈681 kJ mol⁻¹) exceeds C–C (≈348 kJ mol⁻¹) even though the π part alone is weaker than a σ bond.\nKey idea: σ + π (double) > σ (single) in total strength, yet the π bond is the weak, reactive part.","traps":["\"Weaker π\" read as weaker double bond: Double bond = σ + π; total enthalpy is larger than single σ.","Stronger bond equated with greater molecular stability: Alkenes are less stable (more reactive) because the π bond breaks easily, despite total C=C strength."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"In the following structures, which one is having staggered conformation with maximum dihedral angle? [Four Newman projections of butane along C2–C3] (A) staggered: front CH₃ up, back CH₃ upper-right (gauche, 60°); (B) eclipsed-type drawing: front CH₃ up with back H beside it, back CH₃ lower right (eclipsed, ~120°); (C) staggered: front CH₃ up, back CH₃ down (anti, 180°); (D) eclipsed: front and back CH₃ both at top (0°).","options":["staggered, CH₃/CH₃ gauche (60°)","eclipsed, CH₃ eclipsed with H (120°)","staggered, CH₃/CH₃ anti (180°)","eclipsed, CH₃/CH₃ eclipsed (0°)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) staggered but gauche (60°); (B), (D) eclipsed.\nWorking: staggered forms are (A) and (C); the CH₃–C–C–CH₃ dihedral angle is 60° in (A) and 180° in (C) (anti). So (C).\nKey idea: Anti butane: CH₃ groups 180° apart, front up and back down.","traps":["Gauche taken for anti: Measure the CH₃–CH₃ angle: 60° vs 180°."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The products formed in the following reaction: (CH₃)₂C=CH₂ + H–C(CH₃)₃ —H⁺→ ? is:","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)₂","(CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–C(CH₃)₃","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂","(CH₃)₃C–C(CH₃)₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – direct coupling of two t-butyl groups (radical-style); (A) – anti-Markovnikov head-to-tail joining; (C) – rearranged skeleton.\nWorking: H⁺ protonates isobutylene to the t-butyl cation, which adds to a second isobutylene to give (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–C⁺(CH₃)₂; hydride transfer from isobutane gives 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (iso-octane) and regenerates t-butyl cation. Option (B).\nKey idea: t-Butyl cation adds at CH₂ of isobutylene to make a new 3° cation; isobutane supplies H⁻.","traps":["Simple combination of the two reactants: Follow the carbocation: addition at CH₂ gives a 3° cation, then H⁻ transfer.","Regiochemistry of cation addition: Attack at CH₂ gives the tertiary cation."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Amongst the following, the major product of the given chemical reaction is\n[Reactant: 3,4-dihydro-2H-pyran (six-membered ring with ring O; C=C between C2 and C3 adjacent to O)] —(Br₂, CH₃OH)→ Major Product","options":["tetrahydropyran ring with OCH₃ on C2 (next to ring O, bold wedge) and Br on C3 (hashed wedge) – trans-3-bromo-2-methoxyoxane","tetrahydropyran ring with OCH₃ on C2 (wedge) and Br on C3 (wedge) – cis-3-bromo-2-methoxyoxane","tetrahydropyran ring with Br on C2 (next to ring O, wedge) and OCH₃ on C3 (hashed) – trans-2-bromo-3-methoxyoxane","tetrahydropyran ring with Br on C2 (hashed) and OCH₃ on C3 (wedge) – trans-2-bromo-3-methoxyoxane (other drawing)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) cis product (syn addition); (C)/(D) reversed regiochemistry – methanol at C3 and Br at C2, as if Br⁺ added to the carbon next to O.\nWorking: Br₂ forms a bromonium ion across C2=C3; methanol (solvent) opens it at C2, where the positive charge is stabilised by the ring oxygen (oxocarbenium character), from the face opposite Br – anti addition gives trans-2-methoxy-3-bromotetrahydropyran (racemic; one enantiomer drawn).\nKey idea: Nucleophile goes to the carbon best able to carry positive charge (next to ring O); bromonium opening is anti.","traps":["Which carbon gets OCH₃: Ring O stabilises positive charge at C2, so MeOH attacks C2.","Anti vs syn addition: Bromonium ion is opened from the back face → trans."],"topics":["Alkenes – reactions","Markovnikov addition"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The major product in the following reaction: 3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene [(CH₃)₃C–CH=CH₂] → (i) Hg(OAc)₂, H₂O; (ii) NaBH₄ → ?","options":["3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃C–CH(OH)–CH₃","3,3-dimethylbutan-2-one, (CH₃)₃C–CO–CH₃","2,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol, (CH₃)₂C(OH)–CH(CH₃)₂","3,3-dimethylbutan-1-ol, (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–CH₂OH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – product of acid-catalysed hydration with 1,2-methyl shift; (D) – anti-Markovnikov (hydroboration) product; (B) – ketone, from Wacker-type or alkyne hydration thinking.\nWorking: oxymercuration–demercuration adds H and OH in Markovnikov fashion through a bridged mercurinium ion, so no methyl shift occurs; OH goes to C-2.\nKey idea: Mercurinium bridge → no free carbocation → Markovnikov alcohol without rearrangement.","traps":["Rearrangement applied to oxymercuration: Oxymercuration goes through a bridged mercurinium ion, not a free carbocation.","Markovnikov vs anti-Markovnikov: Hg(OAc)₂/NaBH₄ gives Markovnikov OH; BH₃/H₂O₂ gives anti-Markovnikov."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Halogenation of which one of the following will yield m-substituted product with respect to methyl group as a major product?","options":["3-methylbenzaldehyde (CH₃ and CHO meta)","2-nitrotoluene (CH₃ and NO₂ ortho)","2-methylphenol (o-cresol; CH₃ and OH ortho)","3-nitrotoluene (CH₃ and NO₂ meta)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (D) – meta-directing CHO/NO₂ point meta to CH₃, but the activating CH₃ wins (o/p); (B) – NO₂ directs to C-4/C-6, which are o/p to CH₃.\nWorking: in o-cresol the strongly activating OH controls; its free ortho (C-3 w.r.t. CH₃ = C1) and para (C-5) positions are both meta to CH₃. In (A) and (D) the activating CH₃ dominates the deactivating CHO/NO₂ and directs o/p; in (B) both groups direct to positions o/p to CH₃.\nKey idea: Strongest activator decides: OH ≫ CH₃, and both of OH's free o/p sites are meta to CH₃.","traps":["Meta director assumed to win: Activating groups dominate deactivating ones; the meta product here comes from OH directing.","Position numbering relative to CH₃: Number from CH₃ = C-1 and check where the controlling group sends the halogen."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"'A' and 'B' respectively are: A —(1) O₃, (2) Zn–H₂O→ Ethane-1,2-dicarbaldehyde + Glyoxal/Oxaldehyde; B —(1) O₃, (2) Zn–H₂O→ 5-oxohexanal.","options":["1-methylcyclohex-1,3-diene & cyclopentene","Cyclohex-1,3-diene & cyclopentene","1-methylcyclohex-1,4-diene & 1-methylcyclopent-1-ene","Cyclohex-1,3-diene & 1-methylcyclopent-1-ene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – extra methyl would give methylglyoxal or a keto-aldehyde, not glyoxal; cyclopentene gives pentanedial, not a keto-aldehyde; (B) – right diene but cyclopentene lacks the CH₃ needed for the ketone end; (C) – 1,4-diene gives malonaldehyde-type and keto fragments, not glyoxal.\nWorking: cyclohexa-1,3-diene splits at both C=C into OHC–CHO (C2–C3) and OHC–CH₂CH₂–CHO (ethane-1,2-dicarbaldehyde, from C4–C5–C6–C1). 1-Methylcyclopentene opens to CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CHO = 5-oxohexanal → (D).\nKey idea: A ketone end (CH₃CO–) means a methyl on the ring double-bond carbon; glyoxal means two C=C separated by one single bond (conjugated diene).","traps":["Ring opening gives one molecule: Cyclic alkene → single chain with carbonyls at both ends.","Methyl position from keto end: Ketone carbon carried the CH₃ substituent in the ring."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following is most stable? (A) cyclopropenyl cation (three-membered ring, one C=C, ⊕ on the sp² CH; C₃H₃⁺); (B) cyclopropenyl anion (three-membered ring, one C=C, lone pair on the third carbon; C₃H₃⁻); (C) cyclopentadienyl cation (five-membered ring, two C=C, ⊕; C₅H₅⁺); (D) cyclohexa-1,3-diene (six-membered ring with two conjugated C=C).","options":["Cyclopropenyl cation","Cyclopropenyl anion","Cyclopentadienyl cation","Cyclohexa-1,3-diene"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts the anion as stable by charge delocalisation (it has 4 π e⁻); (C) treats the 5-ring cation like the aromatic anion; (D) relies on neutrality, ignoring the aromatic option.\nWorking: the cyclopropenyl cation is planar, fully conjugated with 2 π electrons (4n + 2, n = 0) and aromatic; (B) has 4 π electrons and (C) has 4 π electrons (antiaromatic); (D) is non-aromatic. The intended comparison is aromatic stabilisation, although a neutral diene is not strictly comparable with ions.\nKey idea: Count π electrons including the charge: 2 (aromatic) beats 4 (antiaromatic).","traps":["Cyclopentadienyl cation vs anion: Cation has 4 π e⁻: antiaromatic.","Neutral species assumed most stable: The question intends aromatic stabilisation; (D) is non-aromatic."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"What will be the major product of following sequence of reactions? n-Bu–C≡CH (hex-1-yne) → (i) n-BuLi, n-C₅H₁₁Cl; (ii) Lindlar cat, H₂.","options":["H₁₁C₅–CH=C(n-Bu)₂","n-Bu–CH=C(n-Bu)(C₅H₁₁)","cis-H₁₁C₅–CH=CH–n-Bu [(Z)-undec-5-ene]","trans-C₅H₁₁–CH=CH–n-Bu [(E)-undec-5-ene]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the Na/liq NH₃ (trans) product; (A)/(B) put three substituents on the C=C, as if alkylation occurred twice or at the wrong carbon.\nWorking: n-BuLi deprotonates the terminal alkyne; the acetylide is alkylated by n-C₅H₁₁Cl to give n-Bu–C≡C–C₅H₁₁ (undec-5-yne); Lindlar H₂ gives syn addition → cis-(Z)-undec-5-ene.\nKey idea: Lindlar = syn addition = cis alkene.","traps":["Lindlar vs Birch reduction: Lindlar (Pd/BaSO₄, quinoline) = cis; Na/NH₃ = trans.","Trisubstituted alkene drawings: Only one alkyl adds, at the former terminal carbon."],"topics":["Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","Acidity of alkynes"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Benzene – preparation and reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Product 'A' of following sequence of reactions is: Ethylbenzene → (a) Br₂, Fe; (b) Cl₂, Δ; (c) alc. KOH → 'A' (Major product).","options":["α-Chlorostyrene, C₆H₅C(Cl)=CH₂","α-Bromostyrene, C₆H₅C(Br)=CH₂","1-Bromo-4-(chloroethynyl)benzene","4-Bromostyrene, 4-Br-C₆H₄CH=CH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) put the halogen on the vinyl carbon, missing the ring bromination; (C) invents an alkyne and keeps Cl, as if double elimination occurred.\nWorking: Br₂/Fe brominates the ring para to the o,p-directing ethyl group; Cl₂ with heat chlorinates the benzylic carbon (free radical) → 4-Br-C₆H₄CH(Cl)CH₃; alcoholic KOH dehydrochlorinates → 4-bromostyrene. Aryl C–Br is untouched.\nKey idea: Fe → ring; heat/light → benzylic side chain; alc. KOH → alkene.","traps":["Ring vs side-chain halogenation: Lewis acid (Fe) = ring; heat/hν without catalyst = benzylic.","Aryl halide assumed to eliminate: Aryl C–Br does not undergo elimination; only the side-chain C–Cl does."],"topics":["Benzene – preparation and reactions","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following structures are aromatic in nature? [Structures] A: cyclopentadienyl anion (five-membered ring, two C=C, negative charge on the fifth carbon); B: cycloheptatrienyl (tropylium) cation (seven-membered ring, three C=C, positive charge); C: two fused six-membered rings drawn with four C=C bonds, two in each ring, and no double bond at or from the ring-fusion carbons (i.e. 4a,8a-dihydronaphthalene, both fusion carbons sp³); D: cycloheptatrienyl anion (seven-membered ring, three C=C, negative charge).","options":["A, B, C and D","Only A and B","Only A and C","Only B, C and D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (A) and (D) accept the 8π anion (compound D); options (A), (C) and (D) treat compound C as naphthalene.\nWorking: A (6π, planar, cyclic) and B (6π) are aromatic; C as drawn has sp³ ring-fusion carbons, so it is not fully conjugated (not naphthalene); D has 8π electrons (antiaromatic). No option gives \"A, B and C\", which confirms C is meant as the dihydro structure.\nKey idea: Count π electrons only if every ring atom is sp²; a drawn structure missing the central double bond has sp³ carbons.","traps":["C mistaken for naphthalene: Count double bonds and check each fusion carbon for sp² character.","Charge counted wrongly in D: 7-ring anion = 8π → antiaromatic."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The major product (P) in the reaction Ph–CH=CH–CH₂–Br —HBr→ ? (P) [Ph is –C₆H₅] is","options":["Ph–CH(Br)–CH(Br)–CH₂Br (1,2,3-tribromo-1-phenylpropane)","Ph–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂Br (1,2-dibromo-3-phenylpropane)","Ph–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₂Br (1,3-dibromo-1-phenylpropane)","Ph–CH(Br)–CH=CH–Br (1,3-dibromo-3-phenylprop-1-ene)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts Br at C-2 (non-benzylic cation); (A) treats HBr as Br₂ (adds two Br); (D) keeps a double bond with Br on both ends (allylic substitution/rearrangement picture, not HBr addition).\nWorking: H⁺ adds to C-2 so that the cation forms at the benzylic C-1 (resonance-stabilised by Ph; a cation at C-2 would also be destabilised by the –I CH₂Br); Br⁻ then attacks C-1, giving PhCHBr–CH₂–CH₂Br.\nKey idea: Carbocation stability, not H-count, decides orientation: benzylic beats secondary next to CH₂Br.","traps":["Markovnikov by H-count only: Compare the two cations: benzylic is resonance-stabilised.","HBr treated as Br₂: HBr adds one H and one Br."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product of the following reaction contains _______ bromine atom(s). [Substrate: 4-(but-1-en-2-yl)-1-benzofuran, i.e. benzofuran with a CH₂=C(CH₂CH₃)– group at C4] —Br₂, hν→ Major product","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (Br₂ addition across the C=C or across the furan C2=C3); 3 (addition plus substitution).\nWorking: under Br₂/hν (low Br₂, radical conditions) the allylic C–H of the CH₂ of the ethyl group (secondary and allylic, also benzylic-adjacent) is substituted, giving one Br; the alkene and the benzofuran ring are retained.\nKey idea: hν (radical conditions) → radical substitution at the most stable (allylic) radical site.","traps":["Addition vs allylic substitution: hν signals a radical pathway; allylic radical is stabilised."],"topics":["Alkane reactions","Alkenes – reactions"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Two isomers 'A' and 'B' with molecular formula C₄H₈ give different products on oxidation with KMnO₄ in acidic medium. Isomer 'A' on reaction with KMnO₄/H⁺ results in effervescence of a gas and gives ketone. The compound 'A' is","options":["But-1-ene","cis-But-2-ene","trans-But-2-ene","2-methyl propene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives CO₂ but an acid not a ketone; (B), (C) give two acetic acid molecules, no gas.\nWorking: (CH₃)₂C=CH₂ + KMnO₄/H⁺ → (CH₃)₂C=O (acetone) + CO₂ (from the =CH₂ end, effervescence). But-1-ene gives propanoic acid + CO₂ (no ketone); but-2-enes give acetic acid.\nKey idea: Ketone needs a disubstituted alkene carbon; gas needs a terminal =CH₂.","traps":["Gas alone not enough: Check both clues: CO₂ and a ketone."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Benzene + CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl —(anhydrous AlCl₃)→ 'A' (major product). The stable carbocation formed in the above reaction is:","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂⁺","CH₃CH₂⁺","CH₃–CH⁺–CH₃","C₆H₅–CH⁺–CH₂CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the cation expected without rearrangement (gives n-propylbenzene); (D) is a benzylic cation that is not formed in this reaction; (B) has the wrong carbon count.\nWorking: the n-propyl cation (or its AlCl₃ complex) undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation, which alkylates benzene to give cumene as the major product.\nKey idea: Primary carbocations rearrange to more stable secondary ones before substitution.","traps":["No rearrangement: 1° → 2° by 1,2-H shift.","Most stable cation in the list: Ask which cation is actually formed in this reaction."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is: isoprene, CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH=CH₂ + HBr (excess) → major product","options":["1-bromo-3-methylbut-2-ene, (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂Br","2,3-dibromo-2-methylbutane, (CH₃)₂CBr–CHBr–CH₃","3-bromo-3-methylbut-1-ene, (CH₃)₂CBr–CH=CH₂","1,4-dibromo-2-methylbutane, BrCH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂Br"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) double Markovnikov addition by misreading 'excess'; (C) 1,2-kinetic adduct; (D) anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) double addition.\nWorking: protonation at C-1 gives the tertiary allylic cation; bromide at the far end gives the more substituted, thermodynamically favoured 1,4-adduct (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂Br. Strictly, truly excess HBr could add again to give a dibromide, but none of the dibromide options is that product (1,3-dibromo-3-methylbutane), so (A) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: An allylic cation reacts at either end; the 1,4-product with the more substituted C=C is the thermodynamic major product.","traps":["1,4- vs 1,2-addition: Excess HBr / equilibrating conditions favour the more substituted, thermodynamic 1,4-alkene.","'Excess' suggests a dibromide: The intended answer is one addition; (D) would need peroxide."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct sequential addition of reagents in the preparation of 3-nitrobenzoic acid from benzene is:","options":["Br₂/AlBr₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄, Mg/ether, CO₂, H₃O⁺","Br₂/AlBr₃, NaCN, H₃O⁺, HNO₃/H₂SO₄","Br₂/AlBr₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄, NaCN, H₃O⁺","HNO₃/H₂SO₄, Br₂/AlBr₃, Mg/ether, CO₂, H₃O⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) bromination first puts NO₂ o/p to Br; (B) NaCN cannot displace aryl Br; (C) wrong orientation and same NaCN problem.\nWorking: nitration first gives nitrobenzene; NO₂ is meta-directing, so Br₂/AlBr₃ gives 3-bromonitrobenzene; Grignard + CO₂ + H₃O⁺ gives 3-nitrobenzoic acid. Caveat: a Grignard reagent is not really compatible with a nitro group, and option (B) also reaches the meta product but uses an impossible SN on an aryl bromide; (D) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: For a meta-disubstituted product, introduce the meta-director first.","traps":["Order of introduction decides meta: Put a meta-director (NO₂) on first so that Br enters meta.","Aryl halides do not undergo SN with NaCN: Aryl C–Br is inert to cyanide without a catalyst."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Excess of isobutane on reaction with Br₂ in presence of light at 125 °C gives which one of the following, as the major product?","options":["(CH₃)₂CBr–CH₂Br (1,2-dibromo-2-methylpropane)","CH₃–CH(CH₂Br)–CH₂Br (1,3-dibromo-2-methylpropane)","(CH₃)₂CH–CH₂Br (1-bromo-2-methylpropane)","(CH₃)₃C–Br (2-bromo-2-methylpropane)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) dibromination despite excess alkane; (C) statistical primary product.\nWorking: free-radical bromination is highly selective for the tertiary C–H (most stable 3° radical); excess alkane limits it to monobromination, giving (CH₃)₃CBr.\nKey idea: Bromine radical picks the weakest (3°) C–H; excess alkane → mono product.","traps":["Bromination is highly selective: Br• is selective (~1600:1 for 3° vs 1° at 125 °C) → tertiary bromide.","Excess alkane means monobromination: Excess isobutane suppresses polybromination."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"[Scheme: valerophenone (1-phenylpentan-1-one), C₆H₅COCH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃ —Br₂, AlBr₃(C₂H₅)₂O→ 'A' (Major product)] Consider the given reaction, the product A is:","options":["5-bromo-1-phenylpentan-1-one (Br on terminal CH₂)","1-(4-bromophenyl)pentan-1-one (para-Br)","1-(3-bromophenyl)pentan-1-one (meta-Br)","4-bromo-1-phenylpentan-1-one (Br on C-4 of the chain)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) para – treating C=O as o/p-director; (A)/(D) side-chain bromination as if under light/radical conditions.\nWorking: with a Lewis-acid catalyst the acyl group (deactivating, meta-directing) sends Br to the meta position. (With only catalytic AlX₃ in ether, α-bromination of the ketone can occur in practice, but no α-bromo product is offered; (A) and (D) would need radical conditions.)\nKey idea: Br₂ + Lewis acid → ring substitution; C=O is meta-directing.","traps":["Acyl group is meta-directing: Electron-withdrawing by resonance → meta.","Lewis acid vs radical conditions: AlBr₃ = ionic ring substitution; hν = radical chain."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkynes – structure, preparation and acidity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions, the final product D is: CH₃–C≡C–H + NaNH₂ → A; A + Br–CH₂–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃ (4-bromobutan-2-ol) → B; B —H₂/Pd–C→ C; C —CrO₃→ D","options":["H₃C–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CHO (hexanal)","CH₃–CH=CH–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–COOH","H₃C–CH=CH–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃ (heptan-2-one)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hexanal – one carbon short and treats the alcohol as primary; (B) keeps a C=C and makes an acid – assumes partial hydrogenation and over-oxidation; (C) keeps C=C and the OH – stops after partial reduction and ignores CrO₃.\nWorking: NaNH₂ gives sodium propynide (A); SN2 on the primary C–Br of 4-bromobutan-2-ol gives B = CH₃C≡C–CH₂CH₂CH(OH)CH₃ (hept-5-yn-2-ol); H₂/Pd–C saturates the triple bond to heptan-2-ol (C); CrO₃ oxidises the 2° alcohol to heptan-2-one (D). (Strictly, the free OH would quench the acetylide; the question ignores this.)\nKey idea: Count carbons: 3 (propyne) + 4 (bromobutanol) = 7; the OH on C-2 of the chain ends as a methyl ketone.","traps":["H₂/Pd–C taken as Lindlar: Plain Pd–C with H₂ reduces fully to the alkane chain; Lindlar needs poisoned Pd.","Carbon count lost: Add carbons of each fragment: 3 + 4 = 7."],"topics":["Alkynes – structure, preparation and acidity","Reduction of alkenes & alkynes"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Assertion (A): Treatment of bromine water with propene yields 1-bromopropan-2-ol. Reason (R): Attack of water on bromonium ion follows Markovnikov rule and results in 1-bromopropan-2-ol. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true.","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(B) think water attacks the less hindered carbon (SN2-like) giving 2-bromopropan-1-ol; (A) accepts both but doubts the link because Markovnikov is taught only for HX.\nWorking: Br⁺ adds first to form a cyclic bromonium ion; water (the solvent, in large excess) opens it at the more substituted carbon (more positive character), the Markovnikov orientation, so OH goes to C-2 and Br to C-1: 1-bromopropan-2-ol. Both true and R explains A: (C).\nKey idea: The electrophile (Br) ends on the carbon with more H; the nucleophile (OH) on the more substituted carbon.","traps":["Water attacks less hindered carbon: Opening favours the carbon that better holds positive charge.","Markovnikov only for HX: Rule generalises: electrophile to C with more H."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Arrange the following conformational isomers of n-butane in order of their increasing potential energy: [Four Newman projections along C2–C3] I: staggered, front CH₃ up, rear CH₃ down (anti, CH₃/CH₃ dihedral 180°); II: eclipsed with front CH₃ and rear CH₃ eclipsing each other (fully eclipsed, syn, 0°); III: staggered with the two CH₃ groups at 60° (gauche); IV: eclipsed with each CH₃ eclipsing an H (partially eclipsed, CH₃/CH₃ dihedral 120°).","options":["II < III < IV < I","I < IV < III < II","II < IV < III < I","I < III < IV < II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) reverse the scale (decreasing energy); (B) places partially eclipsed below gauche, treating any staggered form as automatically higher than eclipsed CH₃/H.\nWorking: anti staggered (I) is lowest; gauche (III) is higher by CH₃/CH₃ gauche strain; partially eclipsed CH₃/H (IV) higher still; fully eclipsed CH₃/CH₃ (II) is the highest. Order I < III < IV < II.\nKey idea: Staggered < eclipsed always; within each pair, more CH₃–CH₃ proximity means higher energy.","traps":["Misreading front vs rear groups: Front bonds meet at the centre; rear bonds start at the circle edge.","Order reversed (increasing vs decreasing): Lowest energy = most stable = anti; check the direction asked."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Identify correct A, B and C in the reaction sequence given below: Benzene —conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄, Δ→ A —Cl₂, anhyd. AlCl₃→ B —Fe/HCl→ C","options":["A = nitrobenzene, B = 1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene, C = 3-chloroaniline","A = nitrobenzene, B = 1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene, C = 3-chlorophenol","A = nitrosobenzene (C₆H₅NO), B = 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene, C = 4-chloroaniline","A = nitrobenzene, B = 1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene, C = 3-aminophenol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) replace Cl or NH₂ with OH (no hydrolysis step exists); (C) para-chlorination as if NO₂ were o/p-directing.\nWorking: nitration gives nitrobenzene; NO₂ is meta-directing so chlorination gives m-chloronitrobenzene; Fe/HCl reduces NO₂ to NH₂ leaving Ar–Cl intact → m-chloroaniline.\nKey idea: Introduce NO₂ first → meta product; Fe/HCl reduces only NO₂.","traps":["Para chlorination assumed: The group already on the ring (NO₂) decides the position.","Fe/HCl replacing Cl by OH: Aryl C–Cl survives; only NO₂ is reduced."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"What is the major product formed by HI on reaction with (CH₃)₃C–CH=CH₂ (3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene)?","options":["CH₃–C(CH₃)₂–CH₂–CH₂I (1-iodo-3,3-dimethylbutane)","CH₃–C(CH₃)₂–CH(I)–CH₃ (2-iodo-3,3-dimethylbutane)","CH₃–C(CH₃)(I)–CH(CH₃)–CH₃ (2-iodo-2,3-dimethylbutane)","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(I)–CH₂–CH₃ (3-iodo-2-methylpentane)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – unrearranged Markovnikov product; (A) – anti-Markovnikov (peroxide effect does not apply to HI); (D) – wrong skeleton (chain lengthening).\nWorking: Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary carbocation next to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives the tertiary carbocation, which I⁻ traps to give 2-iodo-2,3-dimethylbutane.\nKey idea: Secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon → methyl shift → tertiary cation.","traps":["Rearrangement missed: Check adjacent carbon for a shift that gives 3° cation.","Peroxide effect applied: Peroxide effect is only for HBr."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following reagent is used for the following reaction? CH₃CH₂CH₃ —?→ CH₃CH₂CHO","options":["Manganese acetate","Copper at high temperature and pressure","Molybdenum oxide","Potassium permanganate"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Mn acetate – gives carboxylic acid; (B) Cu, high T and P – gives alcohol; (D) KMnO₄ – oxidises tertiary alkanes to tertiary alcohols.\nWorking: controlled oxidation of an alkane to an aldehyde uses Mo₂O₃ (NCERT: CH₄ + O₂ → HCHO over Mo₂O₃); Cu/523 K/100 atm gives alcohol, (CH₃COO)₂Mn gives carboxylic acid, KMnO₄ oxidises tertiary C–H to alcohol.\nKey idea: Mo₂O₃ → aldehyde; Cu → alcohol; Mn(OAc)₂ → acid.","traps":["Catalysts swapped: Link each catalyst to its product level."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Identify products A and B: 1-Methylcyclopentene —dil. KMnO₄, 273 K→ A —CrO₃→ B","options":["A: 1-methylcyclopentane-1,2-diol; B: 2-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one","A: 1-methylcyclopentane-1,2-diol; B: 2-hydroxy-2-methylcyclopentan-1-one","A: OHC–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CO–CH₃ (5-oxohexanal); B: HOOC–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CO–CH₃ (5-oxohexanoic acid)","A: 2-methylcyclopentan-1-ol; B: 2-methylcyclopentan-1-one"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – assumes dehydration to an enone; (C) – hot/acidic KMnO₄ cleavage (condition swap); (D) – treats KMnO₄ as hydration reagent.\nWorking: cold dilute KMnO₄ (Baeyer) gives the cis-1,2-diol; CrO₃ oxidises only the secondary OH to C=O and leaves the tertiary OH, giving the α-hydroxy ketone.\nKey idea: Cold dilute KMnO₄ → diol; CrO₃ touches only 1°/2° OH.","traps":["Cold vs hot KMnO₄: 273 K, dilute → diol only.","Tertiary OH oxidised: Tertiary alcohols resist CrO₃."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"What is the correct sequence of reagents used for converting nitrobenzene into m-dibromobenzene? (Scheme: nitrobenzene → → → → m-dibromobenzene, four steps.)","options":["NaNO₂ / HCl / KBr / H⁺","Br₂/Fe / Sn/HCl / NaNO₂/HCl / CuBr/HBr","Sn/HCl / KBr / Br₂ / H⁺","Sn/HCl / Br₂ / NaNO₂ / NaBr"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) diazotisation without first reducing NO₂ – impossible; (C) and (D) reduce first, giving an o/p-directing NH₂ so the second Br would not go meta, and KBr/NaBr cannot replace N₂⁺ (needs Cu(I)).\nWorking: NO₂ is meta-directing, so brominate first (Br₂/Fe → m-bromonitrobenzene), then reduce NO₂ to NH₂ (Sn/HCl), diazotise (NaNO₂/HCl, 0–5 °C) and replace N₂⁺ by Br (CuBr/HBr, Sandmeyer).\nKey idea: Use the meta-director (NO₂) for the first bromination, then convert NO₂ into Br via NH₂ → N₂⁺ → Br.","traps":["Reducing NO₂ before brominating: Brominate while the meta-director is still present.","KBr/NaBr as Sandmeyer reagent: Sandmeyer bromination needs CuBr/HBr."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which one of the following carbonyl compounds cannot be prepared by addition of water on an alkyne in the presence of HgSO₄ and H₂SO₄?","options":["CH₃–CHO","Cyclohexyl methyl ketone, C₆H₁₁–CO–CH₃","CH₃–CH₂–CHO","CH₃–CO–CH₂CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ethanal is from ethyne – valid; (B) from cyclohexylethyne; (D) from 1- or 2-butyne.\nWorking: only ethyne gives an aldehyde (ethanal); terminal alkynes give methyl ketones by Markovnikov addition, so propanal cannot form (propyne gives acetone).\nKey idea: Except ethyne, alkyne hydration never gives an aldehyde.","traps":["Expecting propanal from propyne: Markovnikov: OH on the internal carbon → ketone."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following compounds is non-aromatic? (1) cyclohepta-1,3,5-triene (one sp³ CH₂); (2) furan; (3) cyclobutadienyl dication (C₄H₄²⁺: four-membered ring, one C=C and two positive charges); (4) anthracene.","options":["Cycloheptatriene","Furan","Cyclobutadienyl dication","Anthracene"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – dication thought anti-aromatic (confused with neutral cyclobutadiene, 4π); (B) – O lone pair not counted; (D) – 14π fused ring thought non-planar.\nWorking: cycloheptatriene has an sp³ CH₂ that breaks the cyclic conjugation → non-aromatic; furan (6π), C₄H₄²⁺ (2π, n = 0) and anthracene (14π) are aromatic.\nKey idea: Look first for an sp³ ring carbon: it kills aromaticity.","traps":["C₄H₄²⁺ vs cyclobutadiene: Count π electrons: dication has 2.","Overlooking the sp³ CH₂: Check every ring atom is sp²."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Identify A in the given chemical reaction: CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₃ (2-methylhexane) —[Mo₂O₃, 773 K, 10–20 atm]→ 'A' (major product).","options":["Benzene","Cyclohexane","Methylcyclohexane","Toluene (methylbenzene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) benzene: forgetting the extra carbon (n-hexane example recalled); (B)/(C) cycloalkanes: stopping at cyclisation without dehydrogenation.\nWorking: the reactant is CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₃ (2-methylhexane, C₇H₁₆). Mo₂O₃ at 773 K, 10–20 atm causes aromatisation (reforming): the six-carbon chain cyclises and dehydrogenates, giving methylbenzene.\nKey idea: Aromatisation = cyclisation + dehydrogenation; carbon count is conserved, so C₇ → C₇H₈.","traps":["Benzene recalled from NCERT example: Count carbons: 7 carbons give toluene.","Stopping at cycloalkane: High T over Mo₂O₃ dehydrogenates fully to the aromatic ring."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Identify A and B in the chemical reaction: 3-methoxy-6-nitrocyclohex-1-ene (ring: C bearing OCH₃ at top, C=C on the right side, C bearing NO₂ at bottom) —HCl→ [A] (major) —NaI, dry acetone→ [B] (major).","options":["A = 2-chloro-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (Cl adjacent to C–OCH₃); B = 3-chloro-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (Cl adjacent to C–NO₂)","A = 3-chloro-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (Cl adjacent to C–NO₂); B = 2-chloro-4-iodo-1-nitrocyclohexane (I in place of OCH₃, Cl adjacent to C–NO₂)","A = 2-chloro-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (Cl adjacent to C–OCH₃); B = 2-chloro-1-iodo-4-nitrocyclohexane (I in place of OCH₃, Cl retained)","A = 2-chloro-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (Cl adjacent to C–OCH₃); B = 2-iodo-1-methoxy-4-nitrocyclohexane (I adjacent to C–OCH₃)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B keeps Cl but moves it (no exchange); (B) puts the cation next to C–NO₂ and replaces OCH₃ by I; (C) replaces the ether OCH₃ instead of Cl.\nWorking: the alkene is 3-methoxy-6-nitrocyclohexene (C=C between the CH next to C–OCH₃ and the CH next to C–NO₂). H⁺ adds so that the carbocation forms away from the strongly −I NO₂ group, i.e. on the carbon adjacent to C–OCH₃; Cl⁻ attacks there → A. NaI/dry acetone (Finkelstein) swaps Cl for I at the same carbon → B.\nKey idea: Carbocation forms farther from the stronger −I group (NO₂ ≫ OCH₃); NaI replaces only the alkyl halide, not the ether.","traps":["Cation placed next to C–NO₂: Compare β −I effects: NO₂ destabilises more than OCH₃.","NaI attacks the ether: Finkelstein exchanges halide only; CH₃O⁻ is a poor leaving group."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: benzene treated with CH₃–CH(NO₂)–CH=CH₂ (3-nitrobut-1-ene) in H₂SO₄.","options":["C₆H₅–C(CH₃)(NO₂)–CH₂CH₃ (drawn: Ph on a quaternary carbon bearing NO₂ and methyl, then ethyl)","C₆H₅–CH₂CH₂–CH(NO₂)–CH₃","C₆H₅–CH(CH₃)–CH(NO₂)–CH₃ (2-nitro-3-phenylbutane)","C₆H₅–CH(NO₂)–CH(CH₃)₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes a shift placing the cation on the NO₂ carbon (destabilised); (B) anti-Markovnikov protonation (primary cation); (D) Ph attached at the NO₂-bearing carbon after hydride shift – destabilised cation.\nWorking: H⁺ adds to the terminal CH₂ (Markovnikov) giving CH₃–CH(NO₂)–C⁺H–CH₃. A 1,2-hydride shift would put the positive charge on the carbon carrying the strongly electron-withdrawing NO₂ group, so no shift occurs; Friedel–Crafts alkylation of benzene gives Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH(NO₂)–CH₃ (C).\nKey idea: A carbocation next to C–NO₂ will not shift onto the NO₂-bearing carbon; the electrophile is the secondary cation at C-2.","traps":["Reflex hydride shift: Check what group sits on the carbon receiving the charge.","Anti-Markovnikov protonation: Protonate to give the more stable (secondary) cation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct sequence of reagents used in the preparation of 4-bromo-2-nitroethyl benzene from benzene is:","options":["HNO₃/H₂SO₄, Br₂/AlCl₃, CH₃COCl/AlCl₃, Zn-Hg/HCl","Br₂/AlBr₃, CH₃COCl/AlCl₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄, Zn/HCl","CH₃COCl/AlCl₃, Br₂/AlBr₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄, Zn/HCl","CH₃COCl/AlCl₃, Zn-Hg/HCl, Br₂/AlBr₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Friedel–Crafts acylation fails on deactivated nitro/bromonitrobenzene; Zn-Hg/HCl would reduce NO₂; (B) acetyl introduced after Br then nitration with acetyl present puts NO₂ meta to acyl – wrong pattern, and Zn/HCl reduces NO₂; (C) acetyl (meta director) makes Br go meta – wrong.\nWorking: acylation → acetophenone; Clemmensen → ethylbenzene (o/p director); bromination → 4-bromoethylbenzene; nitration goes ortho to the stronger activator ethyl (and meta to Br) → 4-bromo-2-nitroethylbenzene. Routes (A)–(C) either fail (Friedel–Crafts on nitrobenzene) or place groups meta via the acetyl group, and Zn/HCl would also reduce NO₂.\nKey idea: Convert the meta-directing acyl group into ethyl (o/p) before the other substitutions.","traps":["Acyl group left as meta director: Note the directing nature of every intermediate.","Friedel–Crafts on nitroarene: FC reactions fail on strongly deactivated rings."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"For the given reaction: CH₃–CH=CHBr (1-bromoprop-1-ene) —1. NaNH₂; 2. Red hot iron tube, 873 K→ A (major product). A is:","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂","CH₃–CH=CH–NH₂","benzene","1,3,5-trimethylbenzene (mesitylene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) assume NaNH₂ acts as an aminating agent (substitution); (C) benzene – applying the ethyne result without the methyl groups.\nWorking: NaNH₂ dehydrobrominates the vinyl bromide to propyne (CH₃C≡CH); cyclic trimerisation in a red-hot iron tube at 873 K gives 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene.\nKey idea: Three propyne units → three CH₃ groups on the ring, 1,3,5 pattern.","traps":["NaNH₂ taken as nucleophile: Vinyl halide + strong base → elimination to alkyne.","Methyl groups dropped: Trimer keeps all substituents: C₉H₁₂."],"topics":["Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","Alkyne preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"4-Ethylhexan-3-one (CH₃CH₂CH(C₂H₅)COCH₂CH₃) —[(1) Zn/HCl; (2) Cr₂O₃, 773 K, 10–20 atm]→ ; considering the above reaction, the major product among the following is:","options":["Ethylbenzene, C₆H₅CH₂CH₃","n-Propylbenzene, C₆H₅CH₂CH₂CH₃","o-Xylene, 1,2-(CH₃)₂C₆H₄","Propiophenone, C₆H₅COCH₂CH₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – Clemmensen ignored; (B) – total carbon count (C₈) not conserved / longest chain mis-identified; (C) – C₈ isomer from a different chain-closing assumption.\nWorking: Clemmensen (Zn–Hg/HCl; written as Zn/HCl in the question) gives 3-ethylhexane; Cr₂O₃ aromatisation of a C₆ chain (C1–C6 with ethyl on C3) closes the ring with the ethyl group as substituent → ethylbenzene.\nKey idea: The 6-carbon chain cyclises; remaining two carbons stay as one ethyl substituent.","traps":["Ketone left unreduced: Zn/HCl on a ketone = Clemmensen to CH₂.","Wrong six carbons cyclised: Draw the alkane; find a straight C₆ chain and see what remains as substituent."],"topics":["Alkane reactions","Alkane preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Cyclohexene —(KMnO₄, H₂SO₄, Δ)→ 'A' (major product)\nCyclohexene —(KMnO₄, H₂O, 273 K)→ 'B' (major product)\nFor above chemical reactions, identify the correct statement from the following:","options":["Both compound 'A' and compound 'B' are dicarboxylic acids","Both compound 'A' and compound 'B' are diols","Compound 'A' is diol and compound 'B' is dicarboxylic acid","Compound 'A' is dicarboxylic acid and compound 'B' is diol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps the conditions; (A) and (B) assume the same reaction under both conditions.\nWorking: hot acidic KMnO₄ cleaves the C=C of cyclohexene to give hexanedioic (adipic) acid – A is a dicarboxylic acid; cold dilute KMnO₄ (Baeyer reagent, 273 K) gives cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol – B is a diol.\nKey idea: Cold dilute KMnO₄ → diol (no cleavage); hot acidic → C=C cleavage; a ring alkene gives a single dicarboxylic acid.","traps":["Conditions swapped: Hot + acid = cleavage; cold + neutral = glycol."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of alkynes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Metallic sodium does not react normally with","options":["gaseous ammonia","But-2-yne","Ethyne","tert-butyl alcohol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) t-BuOH reacts slowly (weakest alcohol) and may look unreactive; (A) Na + NH₃(g) gives NaNH₂ + H₂ on heating; (C) ethyne reacts readily.\nWorking: Na liberates H₂ only from compounds with an acidic H: NH₃ (→ NaNH₂), terminal ethyne (→ sodium acetylide), t-BuOH (→ t-BuONa). But-2-yne has no acetylenic H.\nKey idea: Only H on sp carbon is acidic in alkynes; but-2-yne has none.","traps":["Slow reaction treated as no reaction: Slow but real H₂ evolution still counts as a reaction.","All alkynes taken as acidic: Look for ≡C–H, not just C≡C."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Benzene on nitration gives nitrobenzene in presence of HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ mixture, where :","options":["both H₂SO₄ and HNO₃ act as a bases","HNO₃ acts as an acid and H₂SO₄ acts as a base","both H₂SO₄ and HNO₃ act as an acids","HNO₃ acts as a base and H₂SO₄ acts as an acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) both are strong acids in water, so students say both act as acids; (B) reverses roles.\nWorking: H₂SO₄ protonates HNO₃ (HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂NO₃⁺ + HSO₄⁻ → NO₂⁺ + H₂O); HNO₃ is the proton acceptor (base).\nKey idea: The stronger acid (H₂SO₄) protonates the weaker (HNO₃), which then loses water to give NO₂⁺.","traps":["Both acids: In the mixture, the weaker acid HNO₃ accepts a proton – it is the base."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"An organic compound 'A' C₄H₈ on treatment with KMnO₄/H⁺ yields compound 'B' C₃H₆O. Compound 'A' also yields compound 'B' on ozonolysis. Compound 'A' is:","options":["2–Methylpropene","1–Methylcyclopropane","But–2–ene","Cyclobutane"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) but-2-ene gives acetaldehyde/acetic acid; (B) methylcyclopropane and (D) cyclobutane are C₄H₈ isomers without C=C; \"1-methylcyclopropane\" is a non-IUPAC name trap.\nWorking: (CH₃)₂C=CH₂ gives acetone (C₃H₆O) both on ozonolysis (with HCHO) and with KMnO₄/H⁺ (with CO₂ + H₂O); but-2-ene gives C₂ products, and cycloalkanes do not react.\nKey idea: A ketone C₃H₆O = acetone means a (CH₃)₂C= unit; the remaining CH₂ is lost as HCHO/CO₂.","traps":["C₄H₈ ring isomers: Ozonolysis requires a C=C.","Losing the CH₂ fragment: Terminal =CH₂ gives HCHO/CO₂, not a C₃ product."],"topics":["Alkene oxidation","Ozonolysis"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Presence of which reagent will affect the reversibility of the following reaction, and change it to a irreversible reaction:\nCH₄ + I₂ ⇌(hν; reversible) CH₃–I + HI","options":["HOCl","dilute HNO₂","Liquid NH₃","Concentrated HIO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HOCl – an oxidant, but not the reagent used to remove HI here (it could also chlorinate); (B) dilute HNO₂ – confused with HNO₃; (C) liquid NH₃ – would neutralise HI but is not the standard answer and reacts with CH₃I.\nWorking: HI formed reduces CH₃I back to CH₄; an oxidising agent such as HIO₃ destroys HI (HIO₃ + 5HI → 3I₂ + 3H₂O), driving the iodination forward. Option (D).\nKey idea: Remove HI by oxidising it back to I₂: HIO₃ or HNO₃.","traps":["HNO₂ vs HNO₃: NCERT names HNO₃ and HIO₃.","Base vs oxidant to remove HI: NH₃ also attacks CH₃I; NCERT uses oxidants."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Staggered and eclipsed conformers of ethane are:","options":["Polymers","Rotamers","Enantiomers","Mirror images"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) confuse conformers with stereoisomers of chiral molecules; (A) unrelated.\nWorking: conformations arising from rotation about the C–C σ bond are called conformers or rotamers. Option (B).\nKey idea: Rotation about σ bond → rotamers.","traps":["Conformers are not enantiomers: Enantiomers need chirality; conformers interconvert by rotation."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The dihedral angle in staggered form of Newman projection of 1,1,1-Trichloro ethane is ......... degree. (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"60","range":[60.0,60.0],"explain":"Common slips: 180 (anti Cl/H), 0 (eclipsed), 120.\nWorking: in any staggered ethane-type conformation the front and rear bonds are offset by 60°.\nKey idea: Staggered = 60° between adjacent front/back bonds.","traps":["Substituents don't change staggered angle: Dihedral angle in staggered form is 60°."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Among the following, the aromatic compounds are: (A) 5-methylidenecyclohexa-1,3-diene (six-membered ring with two ring C=C and an exocyclic =CH₂) (B) benzene (C) cyclopentadienyl anion (C₅H₅⁻, negative charge on the fifth ring carbon) (D) cyclopentadienyl cation (C₅H₅⁺). Choose the correct answer from the following options:","options":["(A) and (B) only","(B) and (C) only","(B), (C) and (D) only","(A), (B) and (C) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (A)/(D) take compound (A) as aromatic because it is a six-membered ring with three C=C; option (C) counts C₅H₅⁺ (compound D) as aromatic (forgetting it has 4π electrons).\nWorking: benzene (6π) and C₅H₅⁻ (6π, all sp²) obey Hückel's rule; C₅H₅⁺ has 4π (antiaromatic); (A) has an exocyclic C=C and an sp³ ring carbon, so the ring π system is not cyclic.\nKey idea: Count π electrons in a fully conjugated planar ring: 6 → aromatic, 4 → antiaromatic.","traps":["Exocyclic double bond counted in ring: Only π bonds inside the ring, on a fully sp² ring, count.","Cation vs anion of cyclopentadienyl: Anion 6π aromatic; cation 4π antiaromatic."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following is Lindlar catalyst?","options":["Zinc chloride and HCl","Cold dilute solution of KMnO₄","Sodium and Liquid NH₃","Partially deactivated palladised charcoal"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – Lucas reagent; (B) – Baeyer's reagent; (C) – Birch-type reduction giving trans-alkenes.\nWorking: NCERT defines Lindlar's catalyst as partially deactivated palladised charcoal (Pd poisoned with S compounds/quinoline), giving cis-alkenes from alkynes.\nKey idea: Lindlar = poisoned Pd → cis-alkene; Na/liq. NH₃ → trans-alkene.","traps":["Na/liq. NH₃ vs Lindlar: Lindlar → cis; Na/NH₃ → trans."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkynes – reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"An unsaturated hydrocarbon X on ozonolysis gives A. Compound A when warmed with ammoniacal silver nitrate forms a bright silver mirror along the sides of the test tube. The unsaturated hydrocarbon X is:","options":["CH₃–C(CH₃)=C(CH₃)–CH₃","(CH₃)₂C=C₃H₄ (isopropylidenecyclopropane)","HC≡C–CH₂–CH₃","CH₃–C≡C–CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tetramethylethylene → 2 acetone (no mirror); (B) → acetone + cyclopropanone (both ketones); (D) internal alkyne → 2 CH₃COOH (carboxylic acid, no mirror).\nWorking: only the terminal alkyne gives a Tollens-positive fragment – ozonolysis of but-1-yne gives HCOOH (or the α-keto-aldehyde CH₃CH₂COCHO before full cleavage), which reduces Tollens' reagent; (A) and (B) give only ketones (acetone, cyclopropanone) and (D) gives two molecules of acetic acid.\nKey idea: Terminal ≡CH ends up as HCOOH/CHO – both reduce Tollens' reagent.","traps":["Alkyne ozonolysis products unknown: Alkynes give acids / dicarbonyls; only a terminal carbon gives HCOOH.","Formic acid not seen as reducing: HCOOH has an aldehyde-like H–C=O and gives a silver mirror."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following is an aromatic compound?","options":["cyclopentadienyl anion (five-membered ring, two C=C, one carbanion carbon) – C₅H₅⁻","cyclopenta-1,3-diene (two C=C and one sp³ CH₂)","five-membered ring with one C=C and negative charges on two carbons, with an sp³ CH₂ between the charged carbons","cyclopentadienyl cation (two C=C and one carbocation carbon) – C₅H₅⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – neutral diene, sp³ CH₂ blocks cyclic conjugation; (C) – 6 electrons counted but an sp³ carbon breaks the ring conjugation; (D) – cation has 4 π electrons (antiaromatic).\nWorking: C₅H₅⁻ is planar, fully conjugated, 6 π electrons (4n+2, n=1). (B) has an sp³ CH₂ (not cyclic conjugation), (C) has an sp³ carbon breaking conjugation, (D) has 4 π electrons (antiaromatic).\nKey idea: A carbanion lone pair in a p orbital adds 2 π electrons; a carbocation adds 0.","traps":["Electron count only, ignoring sp³ carbon: Check that every ring atom is sp² before counting.","Cation thought aromatic: Carbocation = empty p orbital = 0 electrons."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"1-Methyl-1-vinylcyclohexane —HBr, CCl₄→ A (major product). The product A is:","options":["1-(2-bromoethyl)-1-methylcyclohexane","1-bromo-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane","1-(1-bromoethyl)-2-methylcyclohexane","1-bromo-1-(propan-2-yl)cyclohexane"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) product – no peroxide here; (C) unrearranged-like product with wrong skeleton; (B) ring-carbon skeleton mis-drawn (would need a different shift).\nWorking: Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation next to the quaternary ring carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives the tertiary 1-isopropylcyclohexyl cation, captured by Br⁻. (Ring expansion would give a cycloheptane, not offered.)\nKey idea: A secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon rearranges by alkyl shift to a tertiary cation.","traps":["Anti-Markovnikov product: No peroxide → Markovnikov.","Rearrangement missed: Look for a more stable cation one shift away."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkane reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement-I : 2-methylbutane on oxidation with KMnO₄ gives 2-methylbutan-2-ol.\nStatement-II : n-alkanes can be easily oxidised to corresponding alcohol with KMnO₄.\nChoose the correct option :","options":["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for students who think all alkanes oxidise; (D)/(B) for students who think KMnO₄ never oxidises alkanes.\nWorking: an alkane with a tertiary C–H is oxidised by KMnO₄ at that carbon, so 2-methylbutane → 2-methylbutan-2-ol (I correct); ordinary (n-) alkanes have no tertiary H and resist oxidation (II incorrect).\nKey idea: KMnO₄ oxidises an alkane only at a tertiary C–H, giving a tertiary alcohol.","traps":["Tertiary-H condition dropped: Check whether the alkane has a 3° C–H.","Belief that alkanes never oxidise: Remember the NCERT exception for tertiary H."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Chemicals): (a) Alcoholic potassium hydroxide (b) Pd/BaSO₄ (c) BHC (Benzene hexachloride) (d) Polyacetylene. List-II (Use / Preparation / Constituent): (i) Electrodes in batteries (ii) Obtained by addition reaction (iii) Used for β-elimination reaction (iv) Lindlar's catalyst. Choose the most appropriate match:","options":["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (D) put alc. KOH under addition reaction; (C) puts BHC as Lindlar catalyst and polyacetylene as addition product.\nWorking: alc. KOH – β-elimination (dehydrohalogenation); Pd/BaSO₄ – Lindlar catalyst; BHC – formed by addition of Cl₂ to benzene in UV light; polyacetylene – conducting polymer used for battery electrodes.\nKey idea: alc. KOH → elimination (aq. KOH → substitution); BHC is the addition (not substitution) product of benzene.","traps":["BHC from addition, not substitution: Benzene + Cl₂ under UV adds 3 Cl₂ → C₆H₆Cl₆.","Lindlar catalyst composition: Pd on BaSO₄, partially poisoned (e.g. with quinoline), acts as Lindlar's catalyst for alkyne → cis-alkene."],"topics":["Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reduction of alkenes & alkynes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Experimentally reducing a functional group cannot be done by which one of the following reagents?","options":["Pt-C/H₂","Na/H₂","Pd-C/H₂","Zn/H₂O"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) – standard catalytic hydrogenation; (D) Zn/H₂O – genuine reductive work-up in ozonolysis, often wrongly chosen by students who know Zn only as Zn/HCl.\nWorking: Pt-C/H₂ and Pd-C/H₂ are hydrogenation catalysts and Zn/H₂O is the reductive ozonolysis work-up; Na with H₂ gas is not a working reducing system (Na needs liq. NH₃ or alcohol).\nKey idea: Sodium needs liquid NH₃ or an alcohol as the proton/electron medium; it does not activate H₂ gas.","traps":["Na/H₂ mistaken for Na/liquid NH₃ or Na/alcohol: Sodium reduces with a proton/electron source (liq. NH₃, alcohol); \"Na/H₂\" is not a working reducing system.","Zn/H₂O not recognised as a reducing work-up: Zn–H₂O reduces the ozonide (removes H₂O₂); it is used experimentally."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which one of the following compounds is aromatic in nature?","options":["Acenaphthene: a naphthalene unit peri-fused to a saturated –CH₂–CH₂– bridge (five-membered ring)","Cyclobutadiene (C₄H₄)","1-Methylcyclopenta-2,4-dien-1-yl cation (CH₃ on the cationic carbon of a five-membered ring with two C=C)","Cyclopentadienyl anion (C₅H₅⁻)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) acenaphthene – intended as non-aromatic because of the sp³ CH₂–CH₂ bridge (though its naphthalene core is aromatic); (B) cyclobutadiene – 4π, antiaromatic; (C) cyclopentadienyl cation – 4π, antiaromatic.\nWorking: the cyclopentadienyl anion has 6π electrons and is aromatic; cyclobutadiene and the cyclopentadienyl cation have 4π. Caveat: (A) acenaphthene contains an aromatic naphthalene core; the intended answer treats only (D) as the aromatic species.\nKey idea: C₅H₅⁻: planar, cyclic, 6π electrons → aromatic.","traps":["Cyclopentadienyl anion lone pair not counted: The carbanion carbon is sp² and its lone pair completes 6π electrons.","Cyclopentadienyl cation thought aromatic: Cation has 4π electrons – antiaromatic."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Consider the following reactions :\n(a) Benzene + chlorobenzene —anhyd. AlCl₃→ biphenyl\n(b) Benzene + Cl₂ (excess) —anhyd. AlCl₃, dark→ hexachlorobenzene (C₆Cl₆)\n(c) Benzene + CH₂=CH–Cl —anhyd. AlCl₃→ styrene (C₆H₅–CH=CH₂)\n(d) Benzene + CH₂=CH–CH₂Cl —anhyd. AlCl₃→ allylbenzene (C₆H₅–CH₂–CH=CH₂)\nWhich of these reactions are possible ?","options":["(a) and (d)","(b) and (d)","(a) and (b)","(b), (c) and (d)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) accept chlorobenzene as an alkylating agent; (D) accepts vinyl chloride; (C) also drops the valid allyl case.\nWorking: aryl and vinyl halides do not form carbocations with AlCl₃ (partial C–Cl double-bond character), so (a) and (c) fail; excess Cl₂/AlCl₃ substitutes all six H (b); allyl chloride gives a resonance-stabilised allyl cation and alkylates benzene (d).\nKey idea: Only halides that give a stable carbocation (alkyl, allyl, benzyl) work; sp² C–Cl does not ionise.","traps":["Aryl/vinyl halide treated as alkyl halide: sp² C–Cl has partial double-bond character; no carbocation forms.","'Dark' read as addition condition: With AlCl₃ in the dark the reaction is substitution."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The major products A and B in the following reactions are : (CH₃)₂CH–CH(CH₃)–CN (2,3-dimethylbutanenitrile) —Peroxide/Heat→ [A]; [A] + CH₃CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂ (pent-1-ene) → B","options":["A = (CH₃)₂CH–C•(CH₃)–CN and B = CH₃(CH₂)₄–C(CH₃)(CH(CH₃)₂)–CN","A = (CH₃)₂C•–CH(CH₃)–CN and B = CH₃(CH₂)₄–C(CH₃)₂–CH(CH₃)–CN","A = (CH₃)₂CH–C•(CH₃)–CN and B = CH₃CH₂CH₂–CH(CH₃)–C(CN)(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂ (radical added at the internal alkene carbon)","A = (CH₃)₂C•–CH(CH₃)–CN and B = CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)–C(CH₃)₂–CH(CH₃)–CN"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) take the radical on the isopropyl tertiary C (not captodatively stabilised by CN); (C) adds at the internal alkene carbon.\nWorking: the peroxide abstracts the tertiary H α to CN, giving a tertiary radical stabilised by the nitrile; it adds to the terminal CH₂ of pent-1-ene (giving a 2° radical), which then abstracts H → B = 2-isopropyl-2-methylheptanenitrile.\nKey idea: Most stable radical: tertiary + α-CN; it attacks the terminal CH₂.","traps":["Wrong radical site: Prefer the radical α to CN (resonance).","Markovnikov-type addition at internal carbon: Radicals add to the terminal CH₂."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"An unsaturated hydrocarbon X absorbs two hydrogen molecules on catalytic hydrogenation, and also gives following reaction : X —(O₃, Zn/H₂O)→ A —([Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺)→ B (3-oxo-hexanedicarboxylic acid, HOOC–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₂–COOH). X will be :","options":["4-methylenecyclohex-1-ene (ring C=C not conjugated with exocyclic =CH₂)","1,3-dimethylenecyclohexane","3-methylenecyclohex-1-ene (exocyclic =CH₂ conjugated with ring C=C)","cyclohex-2-en-1-one"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) conjugated isomer gives the 2-oxo diacid (α-keto) – differs only in position; (B) two exocyclic C=C give cyclohexane-1,3-dione, no COOH; (D) a ketone, not a hydrocarbon, and absorbs one H₂.\nWorking: ozonolysis of 4-methylenecyclohexene gives HCHO + OHC–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CHO; Tollens oxidises both CHO to COOH giving HOOC–CH₂–CO–CH₂CH₂–COOH (3-oxohexanedioic acid). (C) would give 2-oxohexanedioic acid; (B) gives a diketone, no acid; (D) is not a hydrocarbon and takes only one H₂ at C=C.\nKey idea: The keto carbon comes from the exocyclic C=CH₂ carbon; the ring C=C must be one CH₂ away from it to give the 3-oxo (β-keto) chain.","traps":["Conjugated vs isolated diene position: Number the chain after cleavage and locate the C=O.","Exocyclic CH₂ forgotten as HCHO: Count carbons: C₇H₁₀ → C₆ diacid + HCHO."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The major product in the following reaction is: 2-methylcycloprop-2-en-1-one + H₃O⁺ →","options":["2-hydroxy-3-methylcyclopropan-1-one (ring C=C hydrated)","1-hydroxy-2-methylcyclopropenylium cation (O-protonated, ring C=C retained, positive charge shown on ring)","2-methylcycloprop-2-ene-1,1-diol (gem-diol)","2-hydroxy-2-methylcyclopropan-1-one"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) follow normal Markovnikov hydration of a C=C; (C) follows usual carbonyl hydration to a gem-diol – both ignore aromatic stabilisation of the protonated form.\nWorking: protonation of the carbonyl O gives a hydroxycyclopropenyl cation with 2 π electrons over three ring carbons (4n+2, n = 0) – aromatic and very stable, so the reaction stops there. Hydration products (A), (C), (D) lose this stabilisation.\nKey idea: Protonated cyclopropenone is a 2π aromatic cyclopropenyl cation; aromaticity makes it the stable product.","traps":["Routine addition chosen over aromatic cation: Check if protonation creates a (4n+2)π ring.","Cation thought too unstable to be the product: Aromatic ions can be isolable products."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions the maximum number of atoms present in molecule 'C' in one plane is _______. A —(Red hot Cu tube)→ B —(CH₃Cl (1 eq.), Anhydrous AlCl₃)→ C (A is a lowest molecular weight alkyne)","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: 12 (only the ring C/H and CH₃ carbon, missing the methyl H); 15 (all atoms, ignoring sp³ geometry); 14 (two methyl H in plane).\nWorking: A = ethyne → B = benzene (cyclic trimerisation) → C = toluene (Friedel–Crafts methylation). Planar: 6 ring C + 5 ring H + CH₃ carbon + one methyl H rotated into the ring plane = 13.\nKey idea: An sp³ CH₃ can place only one of its H atoms in the plane of the ring.","traps":["Methyl hydrogen not counted: Rotate CH₃ so one C–H lies in the ring plane.","All methyl H counted: Tetrahedral CH₃: at most one H coplanar."],"topics":["Metal acetylides & cyclic polymerisation","Electrophilic substitution"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which of these will produce the highest yield in Friedel Crafts reaction?","options":["Benzamide (C₆H₅CONH₂)","Aniline (C₆H₅NH₂)","Chlorobenzene (C₆H₅Cl)","Phenol (C₆H₅OH)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) aniline and (D) phenol look most activated but deactivate on complexing AlCl₃; (A) amide is deactivating.\nWorking: aniline and phenol lone pairs bind AlCl₃, converting the substituent into a strongly deactivating cationic/complexed group; benzamide is deactivated by –CONH₂ (and also complexes AlCl₃). Chlorobenzene is only mildly deactivated and undergoes Friedel–Crafts (NCERT gives its alkylation/acylation).\nKey idea: Friedel–Crafts fails or gives poor yield when the substituent coordinates to AlCl₃.","traps":["Most activated ring chosen: Check whether the group reacts with AlCl₃."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The major product (Y) in the following reactions is: (CH₃)₂CH–C≡CH —HgSO₄, H₂SO₄, H₂O→ X —(i) C₂H₅MgBr, H₂O (ii) conc. H₂SO₄/Δ→ Y","options":["H₃C–C(=CH₂)–CH(CH₃)–C₂H₅ (2,3-dimethylpent-1-ene)","(CH₃)₂CH–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₃ (3,4-dimethylpent-2-ene)","(CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)–CH₂CH₃ (2,3-dimethylpent-2-ene)","(CH₃)₂CH–C(=CH₂)–CH₂CH₃ (2-ethyl-3-methylbut-1-ene)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the trisubstituted alkene toward the CH₂ side; (A) and (D) are disubstituted Hofmann-type terminal alkenes.\nWorking: Markovnikov hydration gives 3-methylbutan-2-one (X); EtMgBr gives 2,3-dimethylpentan-3-ol; acid dehydration via the 3° cation gives the most substituted (tetrasubstituted) Saytzeff alkene 2,3-dimethylpent-2-ene.\nKey idea: The most substituted alkene is the major dehydration product.","traps":["Less substituted alkene chosen: Count alkyl groups on the C=C; maximise.","Anti-Markovnikov hydration of alkyne: HgSO₄ hydration is Markovnikov → methyl ketone."],"topics":["Alkyne addition","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of the following reactions will not produce a racemic product? (1) CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂ (3-methylbut-1-ene) + HCl; (2) CH₃COCH₂CH₃ (butan-2-one) + HCN; (3) 1,3-dimethylcyclohex-1-ene (ring with CH₃ on an alkene carbon and CH₃ on the allylic sp³ carbon) + HCl; (4) CH₃CH₂CH=CH₂ + HBr","options":["CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂ + HCl","CH₃COCH₂CH₃ + HCN","1,3-dimethylcyclohex-1-ene + HCl","CH₃CH₂CH=CH₂ + HBr"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) cyanohydrin of butanone is chiral and formed from a planar C=O → racemic; (D) 2-bromobutane via planar cation → racemic; (C) chiral substrate tempts \"diastereomers, not racemic\", but as written (no configuration given) the product mixture is racemic.\nWorking: the 2° carbocation from 3-methylbut-1-ene undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the 3° cation, giving achiral 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (no racemate). (B) gives a chiral cyanohydrin, (D) 2-bromobutane, (C) a new stereocentre at C-1 – all racemic from achiral/racemic substrates.\nKey idea: 3-methylbut-1-ene + HCl → 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (rearranged, achiral).","traps":["Rearrangement not considered: Check for 1,2-H/CH₃ shift to a more stable 3° carbocation.","Chiral substrate assumed to give non-racemic product: No configuration is specified, so the substrate itself is racemic."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"[P] on treatment with Br₂/FeBr₃ in CCl₄ produced a single isomer C₈H₇O₂Br while heating [P] with sodalime gave toluene. The compound [P] is :","options":["Phenylacetic acid (C₆H₅CH₂COOH)","m-Toluic acid (3-methylbenzoic acid)","o-Toluic acid (2-methylbenzoic acid)","p-Toluic acid (4-methylbenzoic acid)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) phenylacetic acid also gives toluene but brominates at o and p; (B)/(C) directing effects give two or more isomers.\nWorking: all four can give toluene on sodalime decarboxylation, but only in p-toluic acid do CH₃ (o/p) and COOH (m) both direct to the same equivalent positions (ortho to CH₃), giving one monobromo product. Phenylacetic acid gives o + p; m- and o-toluic acids give mixtures.\nKey idea: Single isomer means both groups point to the same equivalent positions – para arrangement.","traps":["Phenylacetic acid also gives toluene: Use the single-isomer clue: CH₂COOH is o/p directing → two products.","Ortho isomer thought single-product: o-Toluic acid has two reinforced positions (3 and 5) that are not equivalent."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The major product [R] in the following sequence of reactions is : HC≡CH —(i) LiNH₂/ether; (ii) (CH₃)₂CH–CH(CH₃)–Br→ [P] —(i) HgSO₄/H₂SO₄; (ii) NaBH₄→ [Q] —conc. H₂SO₄, Δ→ [R]","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₃","CH₃CH₂–C(CH₃)=C(CH₃)₂","CH₂=C(CH₂CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂","(CH₃)₂CH–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Saytzeff product from unrearranged 2° cation; (D) Hofmann-type terminal alkene; (C) wrong skeleton/less substituted alkene.\nWorking: P = HC≡C–CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂; Markovnikov hydration → methyl ketone; NaBH₄ → CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂ (Q); acid gives 2° cation → 1,2-H shift → 3° cation; Saytzeff elimination → tetrasubstituted CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)=C(CH₃)₂.\nKey idea: Let the cation shift to the more stable 3° site, then form the most substituted alkene.","traps":["Missing hydride shift: Check for an adjacent H that gives a 3° cation.","Ketone position on hydration: Terminal alkyne + Hg²⁺/H⁺ → methyl ketone."],"topics":["Alkyne addition","Alkene preparation"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The major product [C] of the following reaction sequence will be : CH₂=CH–CHO —(i) NaBH₄; (ii) SOCl₂→ [A] —benzene, anhyd. AlCl₃→ [B] —DBr→ [C]","options":["C₆H₅–CH₂–CHBr–CH₂D","C₆H₅–CH₂–CHD–CH₂Br","C₆H₅–CHBr–CH₂–CH₂D","C₆H₅–CHBr–CHD–CH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Markovnikov product without hydride shift; (B) anti-Markovnikov placement; (D) wrong D position.\nWorking: NaBH₄ reduces CHO → allyl alcohol; SOCl₂ → allyl chloride [A]; Friedel–Crafts → allylbenzene [B]; D⁺ adds to terminal CH₂ (Markovnikov) giving PhCH₂C⁺HCH₂D, which undergoes 1,2-H shift to the benzylic cation; Br⁻ then gives PhCHBr–CH₂–CH₂D.\nKey idea: After protonation (deuteration), check if a 1,2-H shift gives a benzylic cation.","traps":["Stopping at the secondary cation: Look for a neighbouring benzylic H shift.","NaBH₄ reducing C=C: NaBH₄ reduces C=O, not isolated C=C."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The major product formed in the following reaction is:\nCH₃CH=CHCH(CH₃)₂ —HBr→","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂C(Br)(CH₃)₂","Br(CH₂)₃CH(CH₃)₂","CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH(CH₃)₂","CH₃CH(Br)CH₂CH(CH₃)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) unrearranged product of the C-3 cation (hydride shift missed); (D) product of protonation at C-3 giving the C-2 cation, which has no tertiary neighbour to shift to – a secondary bromide, less favoured than the tertiary route; (B) primary bromide – no route without a terminal alkene and peroxide.\nWorking: protonation at C-2 gives the secondary cation at C-3 (next to the isopropyl CH); a 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary cation at C-4; Br⁻ capture → 2-bromo-2-methylpentane, CH₃CH₂CH₂C(Br)(CH₃)₂.\nKey idea: Always check whether a secondary cation next to a tertiary C–H can shift to a tertiary cation.","traps":["Hydride shift missed: Check the neighbouring carbon: a tertiary C–H next to C⁺ means a 1,2-H shift.","Proton added at wrong carbon: Follow both secondary cations; only the C-3 cation can become tertiary, and the tertiary bromide is the major product."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The major product obtained from the following reaction is -\nO₂N–C₆H₄–C≡C–C₆H₄–OCH₃ (both para-substituted) —Hg²⁺/H⁺, H₂O→\n[Substrate: 1-methoxy-4-((4-nitrophenyl)ethynyl)benzene]","options":["4-O₂N–C₆H₄–CH₂–CO–C₆H₄–OH-4 (phenol, carbonyl next to the hydroxyphenyl ring)","4-O₂N–C₆H₄–CO–CH₂–C₆H₄–OCH₃-4 (carbonyl next to the nitrophenyl ring)","4-O₂N–C₆H₄–CH₂–CO–C₆H₄–OCH₃-4 (carbonyl next to the methoxyphenyl ring)","4-O₂N–C₆H₄–CO–CH₂–C₆H₄–OH-4 (phenol, carbonyl next to the nitrophenyl ring)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts C=O beside the nitrophenyl ring (cation on the destabilised side); (A) and (D) also cleave the methyl ether to phenol, which does not happen under Hg²⁺/dil. H⁺.\nWorking: in Hg²⁺-catalysed hydration the positive charge builds on the alkyne carbon better stabilised by its ring; the p-anisyl (+M OCH₃) carbon takes OH, the enol tautomerises to the ketone ArCO–CH₂Ar' with C=O on the anisyl side. The ether is not cleaved under these conditions.\nKey idea: OH goes to the carbon whose developing cation is stabilised by the electron-donating ring.","traps":["Carbonyl placed on the wrong side: Ask which alkyne carbon's cation is stabilised: next to the +M ring.","Ether cleavage assumed in acid: Aryl methyl ethers need HI/HBr to cleave, not dil. acid/Hg²⁺."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Consider the following reactions :\n'A' (C₇H₁₄) —ozonolysis→ 'B' + 'C'\n'B' —(I₂ + NaOH), Δ→ yellow ppt; 'B' —Ag₂O, Δ→ silver mirror\n'C' —(I₂ + NaOH), Δ→ no yellow ppt; 'C' —LiAlH₄→ 'D' —anhydrous ZnCl₂/conc. HCl→ gives white turbidity within 5 minutes\n'A' is -","options":["CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂CH₃ (3-methylhex-2-ene)","CH₃–CH=C(C₂H₅)₂ (3-ethylpent-2-ene)","CH₃–CH=CH–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃ (hept-2-ene)","(CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃ (2-methylhept-2-ene)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives CH₃CHO + pentan-2-one, which WOULD give iodoform; (C) gives CH₃CHO + pentanal (aldehyde, primary alcohol on reduction – no quick Lucas turbidity); (D) gives acetone (iodoform positive) + an aldehyde.\nWorking: B gives iodoform and Tollens' → CH₃CHO; C gives no iodoform and is reduced to a secondary alcohol (Lucas turbidity within 5 min) → a non-methyl ketone with 5 C = pentan-3-one. So A = CH₃CH=C(C₂H₅)₂.\nKey idea: 'No iodoform' rules out the methyl ketone; secondary alcohol → C is a ketone → pentan-3-one.","traps":["Pentan-2-one accepted as C: Pentan-2-one is a methyl ketone and gives iodoform.","Lucas timing misread: 1° no turbidity at RT, 2° ~5 min, 3° immediate."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is\n1-methyl-4-nitrocyclohexa-1,4-diene —2HBr→","options":["1,3-dibromo-4-methyl-1-nitrocyclohexane (CH₃ carbon has no Br; Br on the next carbon; Br and NO₂ on the same carbon)","1,3-dibromo-1-methyl-4-nitrocyclohexane (Br and CH₃ on C1; NO₂ on C4; second Br on the carbon next to C4)","1,4-dibromo-1-methyl-4-nitrocyclohexane (Br with CH₃ on C1; Br with NO₂ on C4)","1,4-dibromo-2-methyl-5-nitrocyclohexane (Br next to the CH₃ carbon; Br next to the NO₂ carbon on the other side)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) applies simple 'fewer H' Markovnikov at the NO₂ alkene (Br on C–NO₂); (A) Br on C bearing NO₂ and wrong placement at the methyl alkene; (D) both anti-Markovnikov.\nWorking: at the CH₃-bearing C=C, H⁺ adds to give the tertiary cation, so Br goes to the carbon bearing CH₃; at the NO₂-bearing C=C, a cation next to NO₂ is destabilised, so H adds to C(NO₂) and Br goes to the adjacent CH carbon. Product: 1,3-dibromo-1-methyl-4-nitrocyclohexane, option (B).\nKey idea: Carbocation next to NO₂ is strongly destabilised; Br ends up β to NO₂.","traps":["Markovnikov rule applied by H-count only: Judge by carbocation stability; −I/−M NO₂ destabilises an adjacent cation.","Options differ by Br position only: Place each Br separately, one double bond at a time."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which of the following compound shows geometrical isomerism","options":["2-methylpent-2-ene","4-methylpent-1-ene","4-methylpent-2-ene","2-methylpent-1-ene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) internal alkene but two identical CH₃ on one carbon; (B)/(D) terminal =CH₂.\nWorking: CH₃CH=CHCH(CH₃)₂ has two different groups on each alkene carbon; (A) has two CH₃ on C2, (B) and (D) are terminal (=CH₂).\nKey idea: Each sp² carbon needs two different substituents.","traps":["Internal alkene assumed to show cis-trans: Check both carbons: C2 has two CH₃."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Peroxide effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The increasing order of the boiling points of the major products A, B and C of the following reactions will be: (a) but-1-ene (CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH₃) + HBr, (C₆H₅CO)₂O₂ → A; (b) 2-methylpropene ((CH₃)₂C=CH₂) + HBr → B; (c) but-2-ene (CH₃CH=CHCH₃) + HBr → C.","options":["C < A < B","B < C < A","A < B < C","A < C < B"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) A < B < C and (D) A < C < B put A lowest, ignoring the peroxide (anti-Markovnikov gives straight-chain 1-bromobutane); (A) C < A < B puts the tertiary bromide highest.\nWorking: A = 1-bromobutane (anti-Markovnikov, b.p. ≈ 101 °C), B = 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (≈ 73 °C), C = 2-bromobutane (≈ 91 °C); among isomers b.p. falls with branching: B < C < A.\nKey idea: All three products are C₄H₉Br isomers; straight chain (A) highest, tertiary (B) lowest.","traps":["Peroxide effect ignored: With peroxide, HBr adds anti-Markovnikov → 1-bromobutane.","Starting alkene miscounted: (a) is but-1-ene (4 C), so product A is a C₄H₉Br isomer of B and C."],"topics":["Peroxide effect","Markovnikov addition"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: [Phthalic anhydride + chlorobenzene, (i) AlCl₃, heat; (ii) H₂O]","options":["2-(3-chlorobenzoyl)benzoic acid","2-chloroanthraquinone","2-(4-chlorobenzoyl)benzoic acid","1-chloroanthraquinone"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) meta product – Cl is o/p director; (B)/(D) anthraquinones need a second intramolecular acylation (conc. H₂SO₄).\nWorking: Friedel–Crafts acylation of chlorobenzene by phthalic anhydride; Cl is o/p-directing and para is favoured sterically → 2-(4-chlorobenzoyl)benzoic acid. Cyclisation to anthraquinone needs conc. H₂SO₄, not given.\nKey idea: Halogen deactivates yet directs o/p; anhydride opens to keto-acid.","traps":["Assuming ring closure to anthraquinone: Only one step (AlCl₃) given; H₂O work-up gives keto-acid.","Meta substitution for deactivating Cl: Halogens are o/p directors."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 4-chloro-1-(3-isopropylphenyl)butan-2-one [Ar–CH₂–C(=O)–CH₂–CH₂–Cl, Ar = 3-isopropylphenyl] —(1) ᵗBuOK; (2) conc. H₂SO₄/Δ→ ?","options":["5-Isopropyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-2(1H)-one (isopropyl on the aromatic carbon next to the ring fusion)","1-Methyl-5-isopropyl-indan-2-one type (five-membered ketone ring with CH₃ on the ring carbon, isopropyl para-like on the benzene ring)","7-Isopropyl-1-methyl-indan-2-one type (five-membered ring, isopropyl next to the ring fusion)","7-Isopropyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-2(1H)-one (β-tetralone with isopropyl remote from the ring fusion)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) cyclises at the crowded position between the two substituents; (B)/(C) five-membered rings from wrong carbon attacking the ring (or a methyl-bearing 5-ring from Markovnikov cation) – ring size miscounted.\nWorking: ᵗBuOK eliminates HCl to give the vinyl ketone Ar–CH₂–CO–CH=CH₂; conc. H₂SO₄ protonates it and the resulting cation alkylates the ring intramolecularly (6-membered ring) at the less hindered ortho position, para to isopropyl, giving the 7-isopropyl-2-tetralone (7-isopropyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-2(1H)-one).\nKey idea: Count ring atoms: Ar–C1, CH₂, C=O, CH₂, CH₂, Ar–C2 ⇒ six-membered; attack para to the alkyl group is less hindered.","traps":["Ring size miscounted: Count atoms in the new ring for each cyclisation mode.","Crowded ortho site chosen: Cyclise at the position para to isopropyl (less hindered, activated)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which one of the following alkenes when treated with HCl yields majorly an anti Markovnikov product?","options":["F₃C–CH=CH₂","Cl–CH=CH₂","CH₃O–CH=CH₂","H₂N–CH=CH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Cl has +R stabilising the α-cation; (C) OCH₃ strong +R; (D) NH₂ strong +R – all Markovnikov.\nWorking: CF₃ strongly withdraws electrons by –I, destabilising the secondary cation next to it; protonation gives the primary cation CF₃CH₂CH₂⁺ and the product CF₃CH₂CH₂Cl. Cl, OCH₃ and NH₂ stabilise the adjacent cation by +R, giving Markovnikov products.\nKey idea: Most stable carbocation decides; CF₃ makes the α-cation the worst.","traps":["Peroxide effect recalled instead: Think carbocation stability with substituents.","Cl treated as only –I: Halogen lone pair stabilises adjacent cation by resonance."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Polysubstitution is a major drawback in:","options":["Reimer Tiemann reaction","Friedel Craft's acylation","Friedel Craft's alkylation","Acetylation of aniline"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) acylation deactivates the ring – monosubstitution; (A) Reimer–Tiemann gives mainly o-hydroxy aldehyde; (D) acetylation of aniline is used to prevent polysubstitution.\nWorking: the alkyl group introduced activates the ring, so the product alkylates further; acylation deactivates the ring and stops at one substitution.\nKey idea: Product more reactive than reactant ⇒ polysubstitution.","traps":["Acylation and alkylation swapped: Ask whether the new group activates or deactivates."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The increasing order of reactivity of the following compounds towards aromatic electrophilic substitution reaction is : A = chlorobenzene, B = anisole (OMe), C = toluene (Me), D = benzonitrile (CN)","options":["D < B < A < C","A < B < C < D","D < A < C < B","B < C < A < D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) places OMe below Cl; (B) and (D) reverse the whole order (decreasing).\nWorking: –CN strongly deactivating < –Cl weakly deactivating < –CH₃ activating (+I, hyperconjugation) < –OCH₃ strongly activating (+R).\nKey idea: Order by electron donation: CN < Cl < CH₃ < OCH₃.","traps":["Halogen treated as activating (o/p director): Halogens are deactivating (−I > +R) despite o/p direction.","Decreasing order chosen: Check direction of '<'."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : CH₃C≡CH —(i) DCl (1 equiv.); (ii) DI→","options":["CH₃CD(Cl)CHD(I)","CH₃CD₂CH(Cl)(I)","CH₃CD(I)CHD(Cl)","CH₃C(I)(Cl)CHD₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) are vicinal dihalides (anti-Markovnikov in the second step); (B) puts both D on C-2 (anti-Markovnikov throughout).\nWorking: Markovnikov addition of DCl gives CH₃C(Cl)=CHD; second Markovnikov addition of DI puts D on the CHD terminus and I on the Cl-bearing carbon (carbocation stabilised by Cl lone pair) – geminal dihalide CH₃C(I)(Cl)CHD₂.\nKey idea: Both halogens end on C-2; both D on C-1.","traps":["Second addition placed vicinally: Cl lone pair stabilises cation on its own carbon.","D placed on internal carbon: Electrophile (D⁺) goes to the terminal carbon."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The increasing order of the reactivity of the following compounds towards electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions is : (I) chlorobenzene C₆H₅Cl; (II) toluene C₆H₅CH₃; (III) acetophenone C₆H₅COCH₃","options":["I < III < II","II < I < III","III < I < II","III < II < I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Cl below COCH₃; (B) and (D) treat CH₃ as deactivating or Cl as activating.\nWorking: COCH₃ is strongly deactivating (–R, –I); Cl is weakly deactivating (–I > +R); CH₃ is activating.\nKey idea: Halogens deactivate but less than carbonyl groups.","traps":["Cl assumed more deactivating than COCH₃: Cl has a +R that partly offsets –I; C=O has –R.","o/p-director assumed activating: Directing effect ≠ activation."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which of these factors does not govern the stability of a conformation in acyclic compounds?","options":["Torsional strain","Angle strain","Steric interactions","Electrostatic forces of interaction"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) all operate in acyclic conformers; students who know only ethane may pick steric or electrostatic as 'not governing'.\nWorking: In open chains, rotation about σ bonds changes torsional strain, steric (gauche) interactions and dipole/electrostatic interactions; bond angles stay near tetrahedral, so angle strain (a ring effect) does not apply.\nKey idea: Angle strain arises from forcing bond angles away from 109.5° in rings, not from rotation.","traps":["Electrostatic forces dismissed as irrelevant: Dipole-dipole and H-bond interactions also shift conformer stability."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The major product obtained in the given reaction is: 1-(3-chlorobutoxy)-3-methylbenzene [m-CH₃C₆H₄–O–CH₂CH₂CH(Cl)CH₃] —(AlCl₃)→ Product","options":["m-CH₃C₆H₄–O–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₃ (but-2-enyl ether)","m-CH₃C₆H₄–O–CH₂–CH₂–CH=CH₂ (but-3-enyl ether)","4,5-dimethylchromane (ring closure at C2 of the aryl ring, between O and CH₃; CH₃ groups peri)","4,7-dimethylchromane (ring closure para to the aryl CH₃)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) elimination products (AlCl₃ not a base); (C) closure at the hindered ortho position between O and CH₃.\nWorking: AlCl₃ gives a secondary carbocation that attacks the ring ortho to the activating OR group (intramolecular Friedel–Crafts) forming a six-membered chromane; of the two ortho positions, the one para to CH₃ and not flanked by CH₃ is preferred → 4,7-dimethylchromane.\nKey idea: Six-membered ring forms ortho to O at the less hindered, CH₃-para site.","traps":["Elimination expected with AlCl₃: AlCl₃ makes a carbocation that is trapped by the activated ring.","Wrong ortho site chosen: Steric crowding between O and CH₃ disfavors that site; para to CH₃ is also activated."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The major product of the following addition reaction is : H₃C–CH=CH₂ —(Cl₂/H₂O)→","options":["CH₃–CH(Cl)–CH₂(OH) [2-chloropropan-1-ol]","H₃C–CH(OH)–CH₂Cl [1-chloropropan-2-ol]","2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide)","H₃C–CO–CH₃ (acetone)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverse regiochemistry (treating Cl as the \"negative part\"); (C) epoxide needs a further base step; (D) acetone would need oxidation/Wacker-type chemistry.\nWorking: chlorine water adds Cl⁺ first to give a chloronium ion; water attacks the more substituted carbon (Markovnikov-type opening), so OH goes to C-2 and Cl to C-1: 1-chloropropan-2-ol.\nKey idea: In HO–Cl the electrophile is Cl⁺, so the nucleophile OH⁻ ends up on the more substituted carbon.","traps":["Polarity of HO–Cl reversed: Identify electrophile (Cl⁺) and nucleophile (OH) before applying Markovnikov.","Halohydrin vs epoxide: No base given; stop at the halohydrin."],"topics":["Markovnikov addition","Alkenes – reactions"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkene oxidation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"But-2-ene on reaction with alkaline KMnO₄ at elevated temperature followed by acidification will give :","options":["one molecule of CH₃CHO and one molecule of CH₃COOH","CH₃–CH(OH)–CH(OH)–CH₃","2 molecules of CH₃COOH","2 molecules of CH₃CHO"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) diol is the cold dilute (Baeyer) product; (D) aldehydes would be over-oxidised; (A) mixes the two.\nWorking: hot alkaline KMnO₄ cleaves the C=C; each CH₃CH= end becomes CH₃COO⁻, acidified to CH₃COOH. Cold dilute KMnO₄ would give the diol.\nKey idea: Hot KMnO₄ takes each =CH– end to –COOH.","traps":["Hot vs cold KMnO₄: Cold dilute → diol; hot/acidified → cleavage to acids.","Stopping at aldehyde: KMnO₄ is not reductive work-up; aldehydes oxidise further."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ozonolysis","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The major product(s) obtained in the following reaction is/are : 3-bromocyclohex-1-ene —(i) KOᵗBu; (ii) O₃/Me₂S→","options":["OHC–CH₂–CH₂–CH=CH–CHO (hex-2-enedial)","OHC–CH₂–CH₂–CHO (butanedial) and OHC–CHO (glyoxal)","OHC–CH₂–CH(OᵗBu)–CH₂–CH₂–CHO","OHC–CH₂–CHO (propanedial)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) assumes SN2 substitution by bulky tBuO⁻; (A) ozonolysis of only one double bond / no elimination; (D) wrong fragment length from cyclohexa-1,4-diene thinking.\nWorking: bulky KOᵗBu effects E2 to give cyclohexa-1,3-diene (conjugated); ozonolysis with reductive work-up cleaves both C=C: the C2–C3 piece gives OHC–CHO and the C4–C5–C6–C1 piece gives OHC–CH₂–CH₂–CHO.\nKey idea: Bulky base → elimination; a cyclic diene gives as many fragments as double bonds.","traps":["Substitution by tert-butoxide: KOᵗBu is bulky: elimination dominates.","Counting fragments from a cyclic diene: Number ring carbons and cut each C=C."],"topics":["Ozonolysis","Alkenes – preparation"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Conformations","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In the following skew conformation of ethane, H′–C–C–H″ dihedral angle is : [Newman projection: front C bonds to H (upper left), H′ (upper right, ~30°) and H (bottom); back C is rotated 29° so its bonds lie at ~1° (H, right), ~121° (H, upper left) and ~241° (H″, lower left); the 29° arc is marked between H′ and the back H on the right]","options":["120°","58°","149°","151°"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 151° = 180° − 29°, from taking the wrong reference bond; (A) 120° ignores the 29° rotation; (B) 58° = 2 × 29°.\nWorking: front-carbon H′ sits at ~30°; the back carbon is rotated 29° clockwise, putting H″ at ~241°; 241° − 30° = 211°, so the dihedral angle is 360° − 211° = 149°.\nKey idea: Assign absolute angles to each bond, then subtract.","traps":["Misreading which carbon carries H′ and H″: Front bonds meet at centre; back bonds start at the circle.","Arithmetic from 29° done in wrong direction: Place bonds on a clock: H′ 30°, H″ 241°."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is :\n[Scheme: 1,2-dihydronaphthalene (benzene fused to a six-membered ring with C1=C2 double bond conjugated to the arene) —(i) Br₂, (ii) EtOH→ ?]","options":["1,2-diethoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (OEt on C1 and C2)","1-ethoxy-1,4-dihydronaphthalene (OEt at C1, double bond retained in the ring)","1-bromo-2-ethoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (Br at benzylic C1, OEt at C2)","2-bromo-1-ethoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (OEt at benzylic C1, Br at C2)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): both carbons etherified. (B): substitution with retention of C=C. (C): correct atoms, reversed regiochemistry.\nWorking: Br₂ forms a bromonium ion across C1=C2; EtOH attacks the benzylic C1 (greater positive character), giving trans-2-bromo-1-ethoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene = option (D).\nKey idea: Bromonium opening follows carbocation stability – nucleophile to the benzylic carbon.","traps":["Nucleophile placed at wrong carbon: Solvent opens bromonium at the carbon best able to hold + charge: benzylic C1.","Simple dibromide/diether expected: EtOH as solvent outcompetes Br⁻ (solvent in large excess)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n[Scheme: 1-bromo-3-(3-bromopropyl)benzene (aryl Br meta to a –CH₂CH₂CH₂Br chain) —(1) KOH (aqueous), (2) CrO₃/H⁺, (3) H₂SO₄/Δ→ ?]","options":["7-hydroxy-1-indanone (OH on the ring carbon next to the C=O-bearing ring-fusion carbon)","5-bromo-1-indanone (Br para to the C=O-bearing ring-fusion carbon)","5-hydroxy-1-indanone (OH para to the C=O-bearing ring-fusion carbon)","7-bromo-1-indanone (Br on the ring carbon next to the C=O-bearing ring-fusion carbon)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): aryl Br wrongly hydrolysed. (D): cyclisation between the substituents (ortho to Br), the more hindered site. (B): para to Br.\nWorking: aq. KOH hydrolyses only the primary alkyl C–Br (aryl Br inert); CrO₃/H⁺ oxidises the 1° alcohol to 3-(3-bromophenyl)propanoic acid; H₂SO₄/Δ gives intramolecular Friedel–Crafts acylation at a ring position ortho to the chain. Of the two, the one para to Br (Br is o/p-directing) and not flanked by both substituents is favoured, giving 5-bromo-1-indanone = option (B).\nKey idea: Only the alkyl Br reacts with aq. KOH; ring closure goes para to Br.","traps":["Aryl Br replaced by OH: Aryl C–Br is inert to aq. KOH; only the alkyl Br is hydrolysed.","Cyclisation site: between substituents vs para to Br: Br is o/p-directing; the position para to Br is less hindered than the one flanked by both groups."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: γ-butyrolactone (oxolan-2-one) + o-cresol (2-methylphenol) —AlCl₃, Δ→ ?","options":["7-hydroxy-6-methyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one (tetralone; OH and CH₃ adjacent; ring CH₂ attached para to OH, C=O carbon meta to OH)","Indan-1-one with 3-methyl group; OH and CH₃ on the benzene ring with C=O-bearing ring carbon para to OH (5-hydroxy-3,6-dimethylindan-1-one)","Indan-1-one with 3-methyl group; OH ortho to CH₃ and CH₃ next to the C=O ring junction (6-hydroxy-3,7-dimethylindan-1-one)","6-hydroxy-7-methyl-3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one (tetralone with C=O carbon para to OH)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): assume primary carbocation rearranges to secondary and a five-membered indanone forms. (D): acylation first, para to OH, then alkylation – wrong order of attachment.\nWorking: AlCl₃ opens the lactone; the CH₂–O end alkylates o-cresol para to OH (no rearrangement, as it is not a free primary cation), giving 4-aryl-butanoic acid; intramolecular Friedel–Crafts acylation closes a six-membered ring ortho to the chain → 7-hydroxy-6-methyl-1-tetralone.\nKey idea: Alkylating end (CH₂–O) bonds para to the strongest activator (OH); the acyl end then closes the six-membered ring.","traps":["Carbocation rearrangement assumed: AlCl₃–lactone complex alkylates without a free primary cation; ring size six.","Which end attaches first: The alkyl end attacks para to OH first; the C=O ends up meta to OH."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Which of the following compounds is not aromatic?","options":["Cyclopentadienyl anion (C₅H₅⁻)","Pyridine","Cyclopentadienyl cation (C₅H₅⁺)","Pyrrole"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Anion has 6π – aromatic. (B) Pyridine 6π (N lone pair not in ring). (D) Pyrrole: N lone pair joins ring → 6π.\nWorking: C₅H₅⁺ has 4 π electrons in a planar cyclic conjugated ring – antiaromatic; the anion (6π), pyridine (6π) and pyrrole (6π, using N lone pair) are aromatic.\nKey idea: Count π electrons: +ve charge removes 2, –ve adds 2.","traps":["Charge effect on π count: Anion carries the extra pair (6π); cation has 4π.","NOT wording: Mark the odd one out."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: m-CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₂Cl, i.e. 1-(4-chloro-3-methylbutyl)-3-methoxybenzene (methoxy meta to the side chain) —[(i) AlCl₃ (anhyd.), (ii) H₂O]→","options":["6-Methoxy-2-methyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (OMe para to one ring-fusion carbon, CH₃ on the saturated ring)","5-Methoxy-1,1-dimethylindane (= 6-methoxy-3,3-dimethylindane; gem-dimethyl five-membered ring, OMe para to the ring carbon bearing CMe₂)","7-Methoxy-1,1-dimethylindane (gem-dimethyl five-membered ring closed ortho to OMe)","8-Methoxy-2-methyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene (OMe ortho to ring fusion)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): six-membered ring from the unrearranged primary cation (no hydride shift). (C): correct rearranged cation but ring closed at the crowded position ortho to OMe.\nWorking: AlCl₃ ionises the primary chloride; 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary cation ArCH₂CH₂C⁺(CH₃)₂; intramolecular Friedel–Crafts closes a five-membered ring at the position para to OMe (less hindered than the ortho site between substituents) → 5-methoxy-1,1-dimethylindane (OMe para to the new C–C bond).\nKey idea: Primary cation → tertiary by H shift; the tertiary centre is four atoms from the ring, so a five-membered ring forms, para to OMe.","traps":["Missed hydride shift: Always check for 1,2-H/CH₃ shift to a more stable cation under FC conditions.","Ortho vs para to OMe: Choose the less hindered para site; the ortho site is flanked by two substituents."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Which hydrogen in compound (E) is easily replaceable during bromination reaction in presence of light : CH₃(δ)–CH₂(γ)–CH(β)=CH₂(α)","options":["β-hydrogen","γ-hydrogen","δ-hydrogen","α-hydrogen"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): vinylic H, strong C–H, radical not stabilised (and addition to C=C competes). (C): primary CH₃ H, less stable radical.\nWorking: light-induced bromination is a radical substitution at the allylic position; the γ-CH₂ (C3 of but-1-ene) gives the resonance-stabilised allylic radical.\nKey idea: Allylic C–H is the weakest; the allyl radical is resonance-stabilised.","traps":["Greek labels read as usual α-to-carbonyl sense: Find the CH₂ next to C=C – that is γ here."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Directive influence","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"What will be the major product in the following mononitration reaction ? [Substrate: benzanilide, C₆H₅–NH–CO–C₆H₅ (N-phenylbenzamide), reagent HNO₃ / conc. H₂SO₄]","options":["NO₂ ortho on the benzoyl (C=O) ring: N-phenyl-2-nitrobenzamide","NO₂ meta on the N-phenyl ring: N-(3-nitrophenyl)benzamide","NO₂ para on the N-phenyl ring: N-(4-nitrophenyl)benzamide","NO₂ para on the benzoyl ring: 4-nitro-N-phenylbenzamide"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ortho on benzoyl ring: that ring is deactivated by C=O and would be meta-directed. (B) meta on N-ring: would apply to anilinium, not to an amide. (D) para on benzoyl ring: wrong ring and wrong position.\nWorking: –NHCOPh activates its ring (N lone pair donates, though less than –NH₂) and is o/p-directing, para favoured sterically; –CONHPh deactivates the benzoyl ring (meta director).\nKey idea: The ring on N is activated (o/p, para major); the ring on C=O is deactivated.","traps":["Which ring is activated: N lone pair donates into its own ring; C=O withdraws from its ring.","Meta from protonation: Amide N is not appreciably protonated; stays o/p-directing."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which compound(s) out of the following is/are not aromatic?\n(A) cyclopropenyl cation; (B) cyclopentadienyl cation; (C) cycloheptatrienyl anion; (D) cycloheptatriene (C₇H₈, one sp³ CH₂)","options":["C and D","B, C and D","A and C","B"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (A) misses compound B (thinks a cation is always aromatic). Option (C) marks compound A as non-aromatic. Option (D) misses compounds C and D.\nWorking: A has 2π (4n+2, n=0) aromatic; B has 4π (antiaromatic); C has 8π (antiaromatic); D has an sp³ carbon (non-aromatic). Not aromatic: B, C, D.\nKey idea: Cation removes, anion adds two electrons to the π count; any sp³ ring carbon breaks delocalisation.","traps":["Charge sign and π count: Count: cation ring of n C has n−1 π e⁻, anion n+1.","Tropylium vs its anion: The anion has 8 π e⁻ (4n)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Alkenes – reactions","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The major product obtained in the following conversion is :- Substrate: benzene ring bearing an acetoxy group (–O–C(=O)CH₃) and, ortho to it, a –C(=O)–CH=CH–CH₃ group (1-(2-acetoxyphenyl)but-2-en-1-one); reagent Br₂ (1 eqv.), MeOH.","options":["Ring-brominated product: Br on the ring carbon next to OAc (ortho to OAc, position 3), side chain –CO–CH=CH–CH₃ unchanged","ArCO–CH(Br)–CH(OMe)–CH₃ (Br on the carbon α to C=O, OMe on the β-carbon bearing CH₃), OAc retained","ArCO–CH(OMe)–CH(Br)–CH₃ (OMe α to C=O, Br on the β-carbon), OAc retained","Ring-brominated product: Br para to the OAc group (ring position 5), side chain –CO–CH=CH–CH₃ unchanged"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) ring bromination: Br₂ in MeOH without Lewis acid prefers the C=C; the ring has an acyl group. (C) reverse regiochemistry: places the cation at the carbon next to C=O, which is destabilised.\nWorking: Br₂ adds to the C=C via a bromonium ion; solvent MeOH (in excess) opens it at the carbon that better carries δ+. The carbon α to C=O is destabilised for positive charge, so MeOH attacks the β-carbon (next to CH₃) and Br ends up α to the carbonyl. The ring is deactivated by the acyl group, so no ring bromination.\nKey idea: In bromonium opening, nucleophile goes to the carbon better able to bear δ+; a carbonyl-adjacent carbon is the worst.","traps":["Dibromide or solvent capture?: With solvent in large excess, solvent opens the bromonium ion.","Regiochemistry of MeOH attack: Compare δ+ stability: β-carbon (next to CH₃, away from C=O) is better."],"topics":["Alkenes – reactions","Markovnikov addition"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Aromaticity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Which of the following compounds will produce a precipitate with AgNO₃ ?","options":["3-Bromopyridine","Bromobenzene","Bromocyclohexane","7-Bromocyclohepta-1,3,5-triene (bromine on the sp³ carbon of cycloheptatriene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B): C(sp²)–Br, partial double-bond character, no ionisation. (C) secondary alkyl bromide: SN1 slow, no ready precipitate with aqueous AgNO₃.\nWorking: 7-bromocycloheptatriene ionises readily to the aromatic tropylium cation (6π, Hückel) and Br⁻, so it behaves as an ionic salt and gives AgBr at once. Aryl/heteroaryl bromides do not ionise; bromocyclohexane reacts only slowly (no ready precipitate).\nKey idea: Ionic character follows the stability of the cation formed: aromatic tropylium makes C–Br effectively ionic.","traps":["Alkyl halide assumed most reactive: Ask which cation is formed; aromatic tropylium is far more stable.","Cycloheptatriene thought antiaromatic/non-aromatic: Count π electrons in the cation: 6 → aromatic."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is :- Substrate: 3-(but-3-en-1-yl)phenol (benzene ring with OH and, meta to it, –CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂); reagents (1) HCl, (2) AlCl₃ (anhyd.).","options":["Chloro-substituted 1-methylindane (Cl on the ring in place of OH; ring closure para to Cl)","1-Methylindan-5-ol: OH on the benzo ring para to the ring-fusion carbon bearing CH(CH₃) (ring closure para to OH)","Chloro-substituted 1-methylindane with ring closure ortho to Cl (between the substituents)","1-Methylindan-7-ol type product: ring closure at the carbon ortho to OH (between OH and the chain), CH(CH₃) next to the OH-bearing carbon"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): replace OH by Cl – HCl does not convert a phenol to an aryl chloride. (D) ring closure ortho to OH, between two substituents – more hindered; para attack preferred.\nWorking: HCl adds to the terminal alkene by Markovnikov's rule → ArCH₂CH₂CH(Cl)CH₃. AlCl₃ generates the secondary carbocation, which alkylates the ring intramolecularly to give a five-membered ring (indane) with CH₃ on C1. Of the two positions ortho to the chain, the one para to the activating OH (less hindered) is attacked, giving 1-methyl-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-5-ol. The phenolic OH is not replaced by Cl.\nKey idea: Markovnikov cation at C3 of the chain → 5-membered ring; close para to OH.","traps":["Phenol OH converted to Cl: Phenolic C–O has partial double-bond character; HCl does not substitute it.","Closure position: Prefer the less hindered position para to OH."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence","Markovnikov addition"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : [Substrate: 1-methoxy-3-(but-3-en-1-yl)benzene, CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂ with OMe meta to the side chain] (1) Cl₂/CCl₄ (2) AlCl₃ (anhyd.)","options":["Indane with CH₂Cl on C-1 and OMe on the benzene carbon ortho to the ring-fusion carbon next to C-1 (1-(chloromethyl)-7-methoxyindane)","Tetralin with Cl on C-2 of the saturated ring (CH₂ between it and the ring-fusion carbon) and OMe on the aromatic carbon next to that ring-fusion carbon (2-chloro-8-methoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene)","Tetralin with Cl on C-2 of the saturated ring and OMe at C-6 of the aromatic ring (2-chloro-6-methoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene)","Indane with OMe on the aromatic ring para to the new C–C bond and CH₂Cl on C-1 (1-(chloromethyl)-5-methoxyindane)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5-ring closure ortho to OMe (crowded position between OMe and the chain). (B) six-membered ring closed ortho to OMe and (C) six-membered ring closed para to OMe – both need the unfavourable primary carbocation from the CH₂Cl end and leave Cl on C-2.\nWorking: Cl₂ adds to give ArCH₂CH₂CHCl–CH₂Cl; AlCl₃ ionises the secondary C–Cl; intramolecular Friedel–Crafts closes a 5-membered ring at the ring carbon para to OMe (activated, less hindered than the position between OMe and the chain).\nKey idea: Secondary carbocation forms; 5-ring closure para to OMe.","traps":["Primary chloride ionised: Secondary C–Cl ionises more easily; primary carbocation is not formed.","Cyclisation ortho to OMe: Prefer para to OMe; the ortho site is flanked by both groups."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acidity of alkynes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The correct order for acid strength of compounds CH≡CH, CH₃–C≡CH and CH₂=CH₂ is as follows :","options":["CH≡CH > CH₂=CH₂ > CH₃–C≡CH","HC≡CH > CH₃–C≡CH > CH₂=CH₂","CH₃–C≡CH > CH₂=CH₂ > HC≡CH","CH₃–C≡CH > CH≡CH > CH₂=CH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts ethene above propyne (ignores sp vs sp²). (D) +I effect reversed. (C) fully reversed.\nWorking: sp C–H more acidic than sp²; methyl (+I) reduces acidity of propyne slightly.\nKey idea: s-character: sp > sp²; +I of CH₃ lowers acidity.","traps":["+I effect reversed: Alkyl groups destabilise acetylide."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophilic substitution","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nm-(3-aminopropyl)phenyl acetate [CH₃COO–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, meta] —(i) NaNO₂/H⁺ (ii) CrO₃/H⁺ (iii) H₂SO₄ (conc.), Δ→ ?","options":["7-hydroxy-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-1-one (7-hydroxyindan-1-one)","1-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-5-yl acetate","1-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-7-yl acetate","5-hydroxy-2,3-dihydro-1H-inden-1-one (5-hydroxyindan-1-one)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ring closure between the two substituents (ortho to O, sterically crowded). (B) correct regiochemistry but acetate kept (hydrolysis in conc. H₂SO₄/aqueous acid missed). (C) both errors.\nWorking: (i) primary aliphatic amine + HNO₂ → alcohol (N₂ lost); (ii) CrO₃/H⁺ oxidises the primary alcohol to ArCH₂CH₂COOH; (iii) hot conc. H₂SO₄ hydrolyses the acetate and effects intramolecular Friedel–Crafts acylation para to the O-substituent (less hindered than the position between the two groups), giving 5-hydroxyindan-1-one.\nKey idea: Cyclise para to the oxygen substituent; the acetate does not survive hot conc. acid.","traps":["Aliphatic amine treated as aromatic diazonium: Primary aliphatic amine → alcohol + N₂.","Ring closure position: Para to OAc/OH and less hindered wins."],"topics":["Electrophilic substitution","Directive influence"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Markovnikov addition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n2-(prop-1-en-2-yl)octahydro-1H-indene [hydrindane whose five-membered ring C2 bears H and a C(CH₃)=CH₂ group] —HCl→ ?","options":["2-(2-chloropropan-2-yl)octahydro-1H-indene [ring CH–C(CH₃)₂Cl]","2-(chloromethyl)-2-methyldecahydronaphthalene [decalin C bearing CH₂Cl and CH₃]","2-(1-chloropropan-2-yl)octahydro-1H-indene [ring CH–CH(CH₃)CH₂Cl]","3-chloro-2,2-dimethyldecahydronaphthalene [decalin with CMe₂ next to CHCl]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) ring expansion (5 → 6) product – tempting because ring-expansion is a favourite trick, but it would go tertiary → secondary cation. (C) anti-Markovnikov (peroxide-type) product. (B) wrong ring expansion keeping a primary chloride.\nWorking: protonation of the =CH₂ gives a tertiary carbocation C⁺(CH₃)₂ on the side chain; Cl⁻ captures it (Markovnikov). A 1,2-ring shift would expand the five-membered ring but would turn a tertiary cation into a secondary one, so it is not favoured.\nKey idea: Tertiary cation already formed; no rearrangement that lowers cation stability.","traps":["Automatic ring expansion: Rearrange only if the new cation is at least as stable.","Anti-Markovnikov product: Peroxide effect applies to HBr only, and no peroxide is given."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Alkyne preparation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nCH₃CH₂CH(Br)–CH₂Br —(i) KOH alc. (ii) NaNH₂ in liq NH₃→ ?","options":["CH₃CH₂C≡CH","CH₃CH₂CH(NH₂)–CH₂NH₂","CH₃CH=C=CH₂","CH₃CH=CHCH₂NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) substitution by NH₂⁻ (amide as nucleophile). (C) allene – an isomeric C₄H₆ that is not the product. (D) substitution plus elimination mix-up.\nWorking: alcoholic KOH removes one HBr to give a vinyl bromide; NaNH₂ removes the second HBr giving but-1-yne (terminal alkyne, isolated after work-up; it first forms the sodium acetylide).\nKey idea: alc. KOH then NaNH₂ on vic-dihalide → alkyne.","traps":["NaNH₂ seen as nucleophile: NaNH₂ acts as a strong base here.","Allene as elimination product: Second HBr comes off the same carbons → C≡C."]}],"m12-coord":[{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Number of complexes from the following with even number of unpaired \"d\" electrons is ______\n[V(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺, [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Ni(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺\n[Given atomic numbers: V = 23, Cr = 24, Fe = 26, Ni = 28, Cu = 29]","options":["1","5","2","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1: student misses V³⁺ or Cr²⁺. (B) 5: counts all as 'having unpaired electrons'. (D) 4: miscounts Fe³⁺ or Ni³⁺ as even (e.g. takes Ni as +2, d⁸ → 2).\nWorking: V³⁺ d² (2 unpaired, even); Cr²⁺ d⁴ high spin (4, even); Fe³⁺ d⁵ high spin (5, odd); Ni³⁺ d⁷ (3 high spin or 1 low spin – odd either way); Cu²⁺ d⁹ (1, odd). Even count = 2.\nKey idea: Only d² and d⁴ (high spin) give even numbers; d⁵, d⁷, d⁹ are always odd whatever the spin state.","traps":["Ni taken as Ni²⁺ (d⁸, 2 unpaired): Compute oxidation state from the given charge every time.","Odd dⁿ can never give even unpaired count: Use parity: odd dⁿ → odd unpaired count."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct sequence of ligands in the order of decreasing field strength is :","options":["NCS⁻ > EDTA⁴⁻ > CN⁻ > CO","CO > H₂O > F⁻ > S²⁻","S²⁻ > ⁻OH > EDTA⁴⁻ > CO","⁻OH > F⁻ > NH₃ > CN⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): written in increasing order. (D): OH⁻ and F⁻ swapped and NH₃/CN⁻ reversed.\nWorking: spectrochemical series … S²⁻ < F⁻ < OH⁻ < … < H₂O < NCS⁻ < edta⁴⁻ < NH₃ < en < CN⁻ < CO; only (B) is in decreasing order.\nKey idea: Read the series backwards: CO strongest; S²⁻ weaker than F⁻.","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing: Check the first ligand: CO must lead a decreasing order.","S²⁻ vs F⁻ position: S²⁻ < F⁻ in NCERT."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"If an iron (III) complex with the formula [Fe(NH₃)ₓ(CN)ᵧ]⁻ has no electron in its e_g orbital, then the value of x + y is","options":["6","4","3","5"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 5 or (B) 4: students fix y = 4 from charge but then guess a tetrahedral/odd CN or take x + y = y only. (C) 3 arises from taking y = 2 with wrong oxidation-state arithmetic.\nWorking: Fe³⁺ with overall charge −1 needs y = 4 CN⁻ (3 − 4 = −1); no e_g electrons means low-spin t₂g⁵ octahedral, so coordination number 6 and x = 2; x + y = 6.\nKey idea: Charge gives y = 4; e_g-empty d⁵ needs octahedral strong field, so total ligands = 6.","traps":["Coordination number not fixed: e_g/t₂g labels imply octahedral, CN 6.","Charge balance slip: +3 − y = −1 → y = 4."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The number of unpaired d-electrons in [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is ________.","options":["0","2","1","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 4 is the high-spin t₂g⁴e_g² answer from treating H₂O as weak field. (B) 2 and (C) 1 come from miscounting d electrons (Co²⁺ d⁷ low spin = 1) or partial pairing.\nWorking: Co³⁺ is d⁶; for this high-charge 3d ion even H₂O gives Δo > P, so the complex is low spin t₂g⁶e_g⁰ and diamagnetic – 0 unpaired electrons.\nKey idea: Co³⁺ (d⁶) is low spin with almost all ligands except F⁻; [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is diamagnetic.","traps":["H₂O assumed weak field: For M³⁺ of Co, Δo with H₂O exceeds P.","Co oxidation state misread: Overall +3 with neutral H₂O → Co³⁺, d⁶."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct order of ligands arranged in increasing field strength.","options":["F⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻ < NH₃","H₂O < ⁻OH < CN⁻ < NH₃","Br⁻ < F⁻ < H₂O < NH₃","Cl⁻ < ⁻OH < Br⁻ < CN⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): halide order reversed (F⁻ placed weakest). (B): H₂O put below OH⁻ and NH₃ above CN⁻. (D): Br⁻ placed above OH⁻.\nWorking: spectrochemical series I⁻ < Br⁻ < … < Cl⁻ < … < F⁻ < OH⁻ < … < H₂O < … < NH₃ < … < CN⁻ < CO; only (C) is consistent.\nKey idea: Halides: I⁻ < Br⁻ < Cl⁻ < F⁻; then OH⁻ < H₂O < NH₃ < CN⁻.","traps":["Halide order reversed: Memorise I⁻ < Br⁻ < Cl⁻ < F⁻ at the weak end.","OH⁻ vs H₂O order: In the series OH⁻ < H₂O."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The number of complexes from the following with no electrons in the t₂ orbital is ______.\nTiCl₄, [MnO₄]⁻, [FeO₄]²⁻, [FeCl₄]⁻, [CoCl₄]²⁻","options":["4","2","3","1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"4: counts FeO₄²⁻ d² wrongly or treats it as d⁰-like plus one more. 2: puts the d² electrons of FeO₄²⁻ into t₂ (octahedral order). 1: counts only TiCl₄.\nWorking: all are tetrahedral (e below t₂). TiCl₄ Ti⁴⁺ d⁰, MnO₄⁻ Mn⁷⁺ d⁰, FeO₄²⁻ Fe⁶⁺ d² → e² t₂⁰: three have empty t₂. FeCl₄⁻ Fe³⁺ d⁵ (e²t₂³) and CoCl₄²⁻ Co²⁺ d⁷ (e⁴t₂³) have t₂ electrons.\nKey idea: In a tetrahedral field e is lower; d⁰, d¹ and d² ions keep t₂ empty.","traps":["Octahedral order used for tetrahedral: Tetrahedral: e (2 orbitals) lower, t₂ (3 orbitals) higher.","Fe(VI) in ferrate miscounted: Fe⁶⁺ = [Ar]3d²."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The metal atom present in the complex MABXL (where A, B, X and L are unidentate ligands and M is metal) involves sp³ hybridization. The number of geometrical isomers exhibited by the complex is:","options":["0","2","3","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 3 is the square-planar [MABCD] count (dsp²).\nWorking: sp³ → tetrahedral; all four positions mutually adjacent, so no cis/trans isomers (only optical).\nKey idea: sp³ = tetrahedral = no geometrical isomers.","traps":["Square-planar count applied: 3 isomers only for dsp² square planar."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Consider the following complexes\n(A) [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺, (B) [Co(CN)₆]³⁻, (C) [Co(NH₃)₅(H₂O)]³⁺, (D) [Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺\nThe correct order of A, B, C and D in terms of wavenumber of light absorbed is :","options":["D < A < C < B","B < C < A < D","C < D < A < B","A < C < B < D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) reverse the sense (wavenumber vs wavelength). (C): places the aqua-ammine complex lowest, ignoring Cl⁻ < H₂O.\nWorking: wavenumber absorbed ∝ Δ. Cu²⁺ aqua complex (lower charge, Jahn–Teller, weak H₂O) lowest; among Co³⁺: Cl⁻ < H₂O < CN⁻ in spectrochemical series → A < C < B. So D < A < C < B.\nKey idea: Higher Δ → higher energy → higher wavenumber absorbed; replace one NH₃ by Cl⁻ (weaker) vs H₂O (slightly stronger than Cl⁻).","traps":["Wavenumber confused with wavelength: Wavenumber ∝ energy ∝ Δ.","Cl⁻ vs H₂O position: Cl⁻ < H₂O in spectrochemical series."],"topics":["Colour of complexes","Spectrochemical series"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Tetrahedral Complex): A. TiCl₄; B. [FeO₄]²⁻; C. [FeCl₄]⁻; D. [CoCl₄]²⁻\nList II (Electronic configuration): I. e², t₂⁰; II. e⁴, t₂³; III. e⁰, t₂⁰; IV. e², t₂³\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives TiCl₄ five d electrons (e²t₂³), ferrate none and FeCl₄⁻ only two. (C) swaps TiCl₄ (taken as d²) and ferrate (taken as d⁰). (D) mixes up the d-counts of FeO₄²⁻, FeCl₄⁻ and CoCl₄²⁻.\nWorking: Ti(IV) d⁰ → e⁰t₂⁰; Fe(VI) in FeO₄²⁻ d² → e²t₂⁰; Fe(III) d⁵ high spin (tetrahedral) → e²t₂³; Co(II) d⁷ → e⁴t₂³.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral: fill e first (lower), then t₂, no pairing until both half-filled.","traps":["Fe oxidation state in FeO₄²⁻ misjudged: x − 8 = −2 → Fe(VI), d².","Tetrahedral order of e and t₂ reversed: In Td, e is lower than t₂."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The correct IUPAC name of [PtBr₂(PMe₃)₂] is :","options":["bis(trimethylphosphine)dibromoplatinum(II)","dibromodi(trimethylphosphine)platinum(II)","dibromobis(trimethylphosphine)platinum(II)","bis[bromo(trimethylphosphine)]platinum(II)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): multiplicative prefix used for alphabetising. (B): di used instead of bis for complex ligand name. (D): wrong grouping of ligands.\nWorking: ligands in alphabetical order (bromo before trimethylphosphine); bis used for ligand names containing numerical prefixes; Pt +2.\nKey idea: Alphabetise by ligand name ignoring prefixes; use bis for names with built-in numbers.","traps":["Alphabetised by \"bis\": Ignore multiplying prefixes when ordering.","\"di\" used with trimethylphosphine: Ligands with numbers in name take bis/tris."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Number of Complexes with even number of electrons in t₂g orbitals is -\n[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺, [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺, [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺, [Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺","options":["3","2","1","5"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2 – miscounts Co³⁺ or Cu²⁺; (C) 1; (D) 5 – assumes all even. Cr²⁺ taken low spin (t₂g⁴) would give 4, not offered.\nWorking: Fe²⁺ d⁶ (high spin) t₂g⁴ even; Co²⁺ d⁷ t₂g⁵ odd; Co³⁺ d⁶ t₂g⁶ (low spin; t₂g⁴ if high spin – even either way); Cu²⁺ d⁹ t₂g⁶ even; Cr²⁺ d⁴ high spin t₂g³ odd → 3.\nKey idea: Write d count, decide spin, fill t₂g then e_g.","traps":["Wrong d-count from oxidation state: Co³⁺ d⁶, Co²⁺ d⁷.","Spin state misjudged for H₂O: Co³⁺ d⁶ gives even t₂g in either spin state."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"An octahedral complex with the formula CoCl₃·nNH₃ upon reaction with excess of AgNO₃ solution gives 2 moles of AgCl. Consider the oxidation state of Co in the complex is 'x'. The value of \"x + n\" is ________.","options":["6","5","3","8"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 6 – takes n = 3 (number of Cl) +3; (B) 5 – n only; (C) 3 – x only.\nWorking: 2 ionisable Cl⁻ → [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂; octahedral needs 6 ligands so n = 5; x = +3 → 8.\nKey idea: 2 AgCl ⇒ one Cl inside; 6 − 1 = 5 NH₃.","traps":["Forgetting to add x and n: Read what the blank asks."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: N(CH₃)₃ and P(CH₃)₃ can act as ligands to form transition metal complexes.\nStatement II: As N and P are from same group, the nature of bonding of N(CH₃)₃ and P(CH₃)₃ is always same with transition metals.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts \"always same\" because N and P are in the same group; (A)/(C) doubt that NMe₃ is a ligand.\nWorking: both have a lone pair and can donate (I true); PMe₃ can also accept metal d-electron density into vacant orbitals (π-acceptor) while NMe₃ is only a σ-donor, so bonding differs (II false).\nKey idea: Phosphines have vacant orbitals for back bonding; amines do not.","traps":["Same group → same bonding: \"Always\" claims are usually false; P has π-acceptor ability."],"topics":["Ligands & denticity","Metal carbonyls"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Complex ion): A. [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ B. [NiCl₄]²⁻ C. [CoF₆]³⁻ D. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻\nLIST II (Spin only magnetic moment in B.M.): I. 4.90 II. 3.87 III. 0.0 IV. 2.83\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): [NiCl₄]²⁻ taken as square planar diamagnetic (strong-field confusion). (C): Cr³⁺ given 4.90 by miscounting d electrons.\nWorking: Cr³⁺ d³ → 3 unpaired, 3.87; [NiCl₄]²⁻ tetrahedral d⁸ → 2 unpaired, 2.83; [CoF₆]³⁻ high-spin d⁶ → 4 unpaired, 4.90; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ square planar → 0.\nKey idea: Cl⁻ weak → tetrahedral paramagnetic Ni(II); CN⁻ strong → square planar diamagnetic Ni(II).","traps":["[NiCl₄]²⁻ treated as diamagnetic: Cl⁻ is weak field → tetrahedral, 2 unpaired.","d count of Cr³⁺ or Co³⁺: Cr³⁺ d³, Co³⁺ d⁶."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Total number of unpaired electrons in the complex ions [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and [NiCl₄]²⁻ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (or 6) if Co³⁺ taken high spin; 2 vs 4 confusion; 0 if NiCl₄²⁻ taken square planar.\nWorking: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ low-spin d⁶ (d²sp³) → 0; [NiCl₄]²⁻ tetrahedral d⁸ (sp³) → 2; total 2.\nKey idea: NH₃ pairs Co³⁺ d⁶; Cl⁻ does not pair Ni²⁺.","traps":["Co(III) with NH₃ taken as high spin: Co³⁺ with NH₃ is low spin.","[NiCl₄]²⁻ assumed square planar: Cl⁻ weak field → tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): The total number of geometrical isomers shown by [Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ complex ion is three.\nReason (R): [Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ complex ion has an octahedral geometry.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) accept 3 by counting the cis enantiomers as geometrical isomers. (A) wrongly rejects the octahedral geometry.\nWorking: [Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ is octahedral with only two geometrical isomers (cis and trans); three is the total stereoisomer count (trans + d-cis + l-cis).\nKey idea: Geometrical: cis, trans = 2; optical isomers of cis are not extra geometrical isomers.","traps":["Geometrical vs total stereoisomers: Count only cis/trans arrangements for geometrical isomers."],"topics":["Geometrical isomerism","Optical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Number of ambidentate ligands among the following is ____.\nNO₂⁻, SCN⁻, C₂O₄²⁻, NH₃, CN⁻, SO₄²⁻, H₂O.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 if oxalate (two donor O) is counted; 2 if CN⁻ is missed; SO₄²⁻ sometimes wrongly counted.\nWorking: NO₂⁻ (N/O), SCN⁻ (S/N), CN⁻ (C/N) are ambidentate = 3; C₂O₄²⁻ is didentate, others unidentate.\nKey idea: Ambidentate = two different donor atoms, only one used at a time.","traps":["Didentate counted as ambidentate: Ambidentate needs two different donor atoms, one binding at a time."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I: A. K₂[Ni(CN)₄] B. [Ni(CO)₄] C. [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ D. Na₃[CoF₆]\nLIST II: I. sp³ II. sp³d² III. dsp² IV. d²sp³\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ni(CO)₄ to sp³d². (B)/(C) swap the two Co complexes (inner vs outer).\nWorking: [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ square planar dsp²; Ni(CO)₄ tetrahedral sp³; [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ inner-orbital d²sp³; [CoF₆]³⁻ outer-orbital sp³d².\nKey idea: NH₃ pairs Co³⁺ electrons → d²sp³; F⁻ does not → sp³d².","traps":["Inner vs outer orbital Co(III): Strong field NH₃ → d²sp³; weak field F⁻ → sp³d².","Ni(0) carbonyl geometry: Ni(0) d¹⁰ → sp³ tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The coordination environment of Ca²⁺ ion in its complex with EDTA⁴⁻ is :","options":["trigonal prismatic","octahedral","square planar","tetrahedral"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is an alternative six-coordinate geometry not used here. (C)/(D) assume four-coordination.\nWorking: EDTA⁴⁻ is hexadentate (2 N + 4 O), giving six-coordinate octahedral [Ca(EDTA)]²⁻.\nKey idea: Six donor atoms → CN 6 → octahedral.","traps":["Coordination number from charge: Count donor atoms of the ligand."],"topics":["Ligands & denticity","Chelation"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which of the following complex is homoleptic?","options":["[Ni(NH₃)₂Cl₂]","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺","[Fe(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺","[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)–(C): all mix NH₃ and Cl⁻; picked only if the definition is reversed or the charge distracts.\nWorking: Only [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ has one kind of ligand; the rest carry both NH₃ and Cl⁻ (heteroleptic).\nKey idea: Homoleptic = one type of ligand only.","traps":["Homoleptic vs heteroleptic definition swapped: homo = same ligand throughout."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : A solution of [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is green in colour.\nStatement (II) : A solution of [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is colourless.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): student knows d⁸ Ni²⁺ has d electrons so expects all Ni(II) complexes to be coloured. (A)/(C): misremembers aqua Ni(II) colour (blue for Cu²⁺).\nWorking: [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (d⁸, weak field, d–d transitions) is green; square planar [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is regarded as colourless in NCERT (strong-field CN⁻ – absorption shifted out of visible; actually pale yellow).\nKey idea: Strong field CN⁻ shifts absorption out of the visible region; aqua complex absorbs red, appears green.","traps":["All d⁸ complexes assumed coloured: Strong-field CN⁻ with large Δ can absorb outside visible."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and [CoF₆]³⁻ are respectively known as :","options":["Inner orbital Complex, Spin paired Complex","Spin paired Complex, Spin free Complex","Spin free Complex, Spin paired Complex","Outer orbital Complex, Inner orbital Complex"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) both labels describe [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ only. (C) reversed. (D) reversed inner/outer.\nWorking: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ d²sp³, diamagnetic, spin paired; [CoF₆]³⁻ sp³d², 4 unpaired, spin free.\nKey idea: NH₃ pairs Co³⁺ d⁶ electrons (inner d²sp³); F⁻ does not (outer sp³d², high spin).","traps":["Both labels for one complex: Read 'respectively': one label per complex.","Reversed assignment: F⁻ weak field → spin free/outer."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Consider the following complex ions\nP = [FeF₆]³⁻\nQ = [V(H₂O)₆]²⁺\nR = [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺\nThe correct order of the complex ions, according to their spin only magnetic moment values (in B.M.) is :","options":["R < Q < P","R < P < Q","Q < R < P","Q < P < R"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) R < Q < P: puts Fe²⁺ below V²⁺ (treats [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ as low spin, 0 unpaired). (B) R < P < Q: low-spin view for both iron complexes. (D) Q < P < R: swaps Fe³⁺ and Fe²⁺ d-counts.\nWorking: P Fe³⁺ d⁵ high spin (F⁻ weak) 5 unpaired, 5.92 BM; Q V²⁺ d³, 3 unpaired, 3.87 BM; R Fe²⁺ d⁶ high spin (H₂O weak for Fe²⁺) 4 unpaired, 4.90 BM.\nKey idea: All three ligands are weak field here, so count free-ion unpaired electrons: 3 < 4 < 5.","traps":["Treating H₂O as strong field for Fe²⁺: H₂O is weak field for M²⁺ 3d ions; [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is high spin.","Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ d-count swapped: Fe³⁺ = d⁵, Fe²⁺ = d⁶."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Identify from the following species in which d²sp³ hybridization is shown by central atom :","options":["[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","SF₆","[PtCl₄]²⁻","BrF₅"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) SF₆ octahedral but sp³d² (3d outer). (C) square planar dsp². (D) BrF₅ square pyramidal, sp³d² (6 electron pairs).\nWorking: Co³⁺ (3d⁶) pairs up with NH₃ and uses two inner 3d orbitals: d²sp³. SF₆ and BrF₅ are sp³d² (outer d); [PtCl₄]²⁻ is dsp².\nKey idea: Octahedral geometry alone does not mean d²sp³; only (n−1)d use counts.","traps":["Octahedral equated with d²sp³: Main-group atoms use outer nd: sp³d².","Pt(II) complex hybridisation: Four-coordinate d⁸ Pt(II) is dsp², not d²sp³."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The Spin only magnetic moment value of square planar complex [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl(NH₂CH₃)]Cl is ________ B.M. (Nearest integer) (Given atomic number for Pt = 78)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (2 unpaired, √8 ≈ 2.83 as for tetrahedral Ni²⁺ with weak ligands), 2 (rounding a d⁸ high-spin assumption), 4 or 5 (wrong oxidation state).\nWorking: Pt is +2 (5d⁸); square planar dsp² with all eight d electrons paired, so μ = 0.\nKey idea: Square planar d⁸ is always diamagnetic; 5d metals are low spin regardless of ligand.","traps":["NH₃/Cl⁻ treated as weak field → unpaired: Square planar d⁸ = dsp² with paired electrons; heavy metals are low spin.","Oxidation state miscounted: Charge: x + 0 + 0 − 1 = +1 → x = +2."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I (Substances): (A) Ziegler catalyst (B) Blood Pigment (C) Wilkinson catalyst (D) Vitamin B₁₂\nList-II (Element Present): (I) Rhodium (II) Cobalt (III) Iron (IV) Titanium\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps Fe/Ti and Co/Fe; (C) and (D) give Wilkinson as Ti and B₁₂ as Rh.\nWorking: Ziegler catalyst TiCl₄/Al(C₂H₅)₃ – Ti; haemoglobin – Fe; Wilkinson catalyst [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl] – Rh; vitamin B₁₂ (cyanocobalamin) – Co.\nKey idea: Wilkinson – Rh, B₁₂ – Co, blood – Fe; Ziegler–Natta – Ti.","traps":["Ziegler catalyst metal unknown: Eliminate using the three NCERT facts; Ti is left."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"In which one of the following metal carbonyls, CO forms a bridge between metal atoms?","options":["[Os₃(CO)₁₂]","[Ru₃(CO)₁₂]","[Mn₂(CO)₁₀]","[Co₂(CO)₈]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Mn₂(CO)₁₀ is dinuclear but unbridged; (A)/(B) trinuclear clusters look like they need bridges but have none.\nWorking: solid Co₂(CO)₈ has a Co–Co bond bridged by two CO; Mn₂(CO)₁₀, Ru₃(CO)₁₂ and Os₃(CO)₁₂ have only terminal CO.\nKey idea: Co₂(CO)₈ is the NCERT bridged carbonyl.","traps":["Any polynuclear carbonyl assumed bridged: Remember: Mn₂(CO)₁₀ has only M–M bond; Co₂(CO)₈ has two μ-CO."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The correct IUPAC name of K₂MnO₄ is","options":["Potassium tetraoxidomanganate (VI)","Dipotassium tetraoxidomanganate (VII)","Potassium tetraoxopermanganate (VI)","Potassium tetraoxidomanganese (VI)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) uses +7 (permanganate) and an unnecessary di- prefix. (C) old \"oxo\" ligand name and \"permanganate\". (D) \"manganese\" instead of \"manganate\" for an anion.\nWorking: Mn = +6 (2 + x − 8 = 0); anionic entity takes -ate; oxido ligand; cation number not prefixed.\nKey idea: K₂MnO₄ is manganate(VI); anionic complex → -ate.","traps":["Manganate vs permanganate: Compute Mn oxidation state: 2K⁺ means MnO₄²⁻, Mn +6.","Metal name in anion: Anionic entity → metal + ate."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Choose the correct statements from the following :\n(A) Ethane-1, 2-diamine is a chelating ligand.\n(B) Metallic aluminium is produced by electrolysis of aluminium oxide in presence of cryolite.\n(C) Cyanide ion is used as ligand for leaching of silver.\n(D) Phosphine acts as a ligand in Wilkinson catalyst.\n(E) The stability constants of Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ are similar with EDTA complexes.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(B), (C), (E) only","(A), (D), (E) only","(C), (D), (E) only","(A), (B), (C) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Wrong statement E (similar stability constants) is in options A, B, C – recalling the NCERT \"difference\" line eliminates them. Statement D: PPh₃ is a phosphine, so a student who marks D true looks for A, D together and is pulled to (B).\nWorking: A true (en is didentate, chelating); B true (Hall–Héroult electrolysis of Al₂O₃ in cryolite); C true (Ag/Au leached as [M(CN)₂]⁻); E false (NCERT: selective estimation of Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ is possible because their EDTA stability constants differ). Statement D is debatable (Wilkinson catalyst [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl] contains triphenylphosphine), but E being false already forces option (D).\nKey idea: NCERT says Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ can be estimated selectively because their EDTA stability constants differ.","traps":["EDTA stability constants taken as similar: NCERT stresses the difference that allows selective estimation.","Phosphine vs triphenylphosphine: Use elimination: E is false, so only (A),(B),(C) remains."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The coordination geometry around the manganese in decacarbonyldimanganese(0) is","options":["Trigonal bipyramidal","Square pyramidal","Square planar","Octahedral"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) taken straight from the NCERT description of the Mn(CO)₅ unit. (A) Fe(CO)₅ geometry carried over. (C) four-coordinate assumption.\nWorking: each Mn is bonded to five CO and to the other Mn (Mn–Mn bond), giving six-coordination in an octahedral arrangement; the Mn(CO)₅ fragment alone is square pyramidal.\nKey idea: Coordination geometry counts all neighbours: 5 CO + 1 Mn = 6 → octahedral.","traps":["Square pyramidal from NCERT wording: Add the Mn–Mn bond as the sixth position.","Fe(CO)₅ geometry carried over: Mn(0) d⁷ fragment pairs through Mn–Mn bond; not TBP."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Number of complexes which show optical isomerism among the following is ________.\ncis-[Cr(ox)₂Cl₂]³⁻, [Co(en)₃]³⁺, cis-[Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²⁺, cis-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺, trans-[Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²⁺, trans-[Cr(ox)₂Cl₂]³⁻","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (excluding the Pt(IV) one by treating Pt as square planar), 6 (counting trans isomers), 2.\nWorking: the three cis-M(AA)₂X₂ octahedral complexes and [Co(en)₃]³⁺ are chiral; the two trans isomers have a mirror plane. Count 4.\nKey idea: In M(AA)₂X₂, only the cis isomer is chiral; M(AA)₃ is always chiral.","traps":["Pt assumed square planar: Here Pt is +4 with six donor atoms: octahedral.","trans isomers counted: trans-M(AA)₂X₂ has a mirror plane."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct statements from following are:\nA. The strength of anionic ligands can be explained by crystal field theory.\nB. Valence bond theory does not give a quantitative interpretation of kinetic stability of coordination compounds.\nC. The hybridization involved in formation of [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ complex is dsp².\nD. The number of possible isomer(s) of cis-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺ is one\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B, C only","B, D only","A, C only","A, D only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D): Statement A sounds right because CFT 'explains' spectrochemical series. (B)/(D): counting cis-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺ as one isomer forgets its optical pair.\nWorking: A false – CFT (point charges) predicts anionic ligands should split most, yet they sit at the weak end; this is a CFT limitation. B true – NCERT VBT limitation. C true – square planar Ni(II) with CN⁻, dsp². D false – cis-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺ is chiral, so cis form has two (d, l) isomers.\nKey idea: CFT fails to explain why anionic ligands are weak-field; cis-[M(AA)₂X₂] is chiral → 2 isomers.","traps":["CFT limitation read as CFT success: Recall: point-charge model predicts anions strongest – contradicted.","cis isomer counted as one: cis-[M(AA)₂X₂] lacks a mirror plane → enantiomers."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","Optical isomerism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The 'Spin only' Magnetic moment for [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ is ________ × 10⁻¹ BM. (given = Atomic number of Ni : 28)","type":"num","answer":"28","range":[28.0,28.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (pairing assumed by NH₃ as strong ligand), 17 or 49 (wrong n), 29 (rounding up 2.83).\nWorking: Ni²⁺ is 3d⁸; octahedral (sp³d², outer orbital) with 2 unpaired electrons; μ = √(2×4) = 2.83 BM ≈ 28 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Octahedral d⁸ always has 2 unpaired electrons in e_g.","traps":["NH₃ assumed to pair electrons: In octahedral d⁸, t₂g⁶e_g² always.","Rounding 2.83: 2.83 → 28."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Select the option with correct property -","options":["[Ni(CO)₄] and [NiCl₄]²⁻ both Paramagnetic","[Ni(CO)₄] and [NiCl₄]²⁻ both Diamagnetic","[NiCl₄]²⁻ Diamagnetic, [Ni(CO)₄] Paramagnetic","[Ni(CO)₄] Diamagnetic, [NiCl₄]²⁻ Paramagnetic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) treat both alike since both are tetrahedral. (C) swaps them (assumes Cl⁻ pairs electrons and CO does not).\nWorking: Ni(0) d¹⁰ with CO (sp³) – no unpaired e⁻; Ni(II) d⁸ with weak-field Cl⁻, sp³ tetrahedral – 2 unpaired e⁻.\nKey idea: Same geometry, different d-count: Ni(0) d¹⁰ vs Ni(II) d⁸.","traps":["Same geometry assumed same magnetism: Find oxidation state: Ni⁰ d¹⁰ vs Ni²⁺ d⁸."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List I with List II. List-I (Complex ion): (A) [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (B) [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (C) [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (D) [V(H₂O)₆]³⁺. List-II (Electronic configuration): (I) t₂g² e_g⁰ (II) t₂g³ e_g⁰ (III) t₂g³ e_g² (IV) t₂g⁶ e_g². Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) gives Cr³⁺ as high-spin d⁵ pattern and Fe³⁺ as d³. (A)/(D) assign Ni²⁺ or Cr³⁺ to IV.\nWorking: Cr³⁺ d³ → t₂g³; Fe³⁺ d⁵ high spin (H₂O weak) → t₂g³e_g²; Ni²⁺ d⁸ → t₂g⁶e_g²; V³⁺ d² → t₂g².\nKey idea: Count d-electrons from oxidation state, then fill; H₂O is weak field for Fe³⁺.","traps":["d-count from oxidation state: Cr³⁺ d³, Fe³⁺ d⁵.","Spin state of Fe³⁺ aqua: H₂O weak field → t₂g³e_g²."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: The spin only magnetic moment value for [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ is 1.74 BM, whereas for [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is 5.92 BM.\nReason R: In both complexes, Fe is present in +3 oxidation state.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student sees both true and assumes the common +3 state is the reason. (B): student thinks Fe is +2 in the cyanide complex (confusing with [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻). (C): student computes 5.92 for both (ignores CN⁻ pairing) or doubts the 1.74 value (√3 = 1.73).\nWorking: Fe³⁺ is d⁵ in both. CN⁻ (strong field) pairs electrons → 1 unpaired, μ ≈ 1.73 BM; H₂O (weak field) leaves 5 unpaired, μ ≈ 5.92 BM. A true, R true, but the same oxidation state cannot explain the different moments – ligand field strength does.\nKey idea: Same d⁵ count, different ligand field strength: the explanation is CN⁻ vs H₂O, not the oxidation state.","traps":["Common oxidation state taken as the explanation: Ask whether R alone could predict two different moments – it cannot.","[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ confused with [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻: Compute charge: x − 6 = −3 → x = +3."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Number of ambidentate ligands in a representative metal complex [M(en)(SCN)₄] is ______.\n[en = ethylenediamine]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5: counts en (two donors) as ambidentate. 1: counts SCN as one type rather than four ligands. 8: counts donor atoms.\nWorking: each SCN⁻ can bind through S or N (ambidentate) → 4; en is didentate (both donors N), not ambidentate.\nKey idea: Ambidentate = one donor at a time, two possible atoms; en uses both N at once (didentate).","traps":["Didentate called ambidentate: Ambidentate donors must be different atoms used alternatively.","Counting ligand types instead of ligands: Multiply by the subscript 4."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The IUPAC name of K₃[Co(C₂O₄)₃] is:-","options":["Potassium tris(oxalato)cobalt(III)","Potassium tris(oxalato)cobaltate(III)","Potassium trioxalatocobaltate(III)","Potassium trioxalatocobalt(III)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) omit '-ate' for an anionic complex; (B) uses 'tris(oxalato)', which NCERT reserves for ligand names already containing a numerical prefix – partly defensible, so it is the closest distractor.\nWorking: anionic complex ⇒ 'cobaltate'; Co is +3; NCERT style uses 'trioxalato' (cf. potassium trioxalatoaluminate(III)). Note: (B) 'tris(oxalato)' is also seen in IUPAC 2005 practice, so (B)/(C) differ only in multiplying-prefix style; the NCERT-consistent answer is (C).\nKey idea: Complex anion ⇒ metal name ends in -ate; oxalato is a simple ligand name ⇒ tri.","traps":["'-ate' suffix forgotten: If the complex ion is negative, use -ate (cobaltate).","tri vs tris prefix: Follow NCERT: tri for simple ligand names like oxalato."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following complex is octahedral, diamagnetic and the most stable?","options":["Na₃[CoCl₆]","[Co(H₂O)₆]Cl₂","K₃[Co(CN)₆]","[Ni(NH₃)₆]Cl₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student counts Co³⁺ d⁶ as diamagnetic without checking that Cl⁻ is weak field. (B): student misses that Co is +2 (d⁷, odd electron count → always paramagnetic). (D): Ni²⁺ d⁸ octahedral always has 2 unpaired electrons whatever the ligand.\nWorking: [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ – Co³⁺ d⁶ with strong-field CN⁻ → t₂g⁶, d²sp³ inner-orbital, zero unpaired electrons, high charge on metal and strong ligand → most stable. [CoCl₆]³⁻ is high-spin d⁶ (4 unpaired); [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ d⁷ (3 unpaired); [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺ d⁸ (2 unpaired).\nKey idea: Only strong-field CN⁻ with Co³⁺ (d⁶) pairs all electrons; higher metal charge plus strong ligand also gives highest stability.","traps":["Co³⁺ d⁶ assumed always low-spin: Check ligand strength: Cl⁻ is weak field.","Oxidation state of Co misread: Compute charge from counter-ions: [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is Co²⁺, d⁷."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Stability of complexes"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct order of spin only magnetic moments for the following complex ions is","options":["[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ < [CoF₆]³⁻ < [MnBr₄]²⁻ < [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻","[MnBr₄]²⁻ < [CoF₆]³⁻ < [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ < [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻","[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ < [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ < [CoF₆]³⁻ < [MnBr₄]²⁻","[CoF₆]³⁻ < [MnBr₄]²⁻ < [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ < [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): places [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ highest by treating it as high spin d⁴ (4 unpaired). (B)/(D): place [MnBr₄]²⁻ low, treating it as square planar/low spin.\nWorking: [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ Fe³⁺ d⁵ low spin, 1 unpaired (1.73 BM); [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ Mn³⁺ d⁴ low spin, 2 unpaired (2.83 BM); [CoF₆]³⁻ Co³⁺ d⁶ high spin, 4 unpaired (4.90 BM); [MnBr₄]²⁻ Mn²⁺ d⁵ tetrahedral, 5 unpaired (5.92 BM).\nKey idea: Unpaired electrons: 1 < 2 < 4 < 5 – CN⁻ pairs, F⁻ and tetrahedral Br⁻ do not.","traps":["Mn(CN)₆³⁻ taken as high spin: CN⁻ is strong field: t₂g⁴ → 2 unpaired.","Oxidation state of Mn in each species: [MnBr₄]²⁻ is Mn²⁺ d⁵; [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ is Mn³⁺ d⁴."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I (Coordination Complex): A. [Cr(CN)₆]³⁻; B. [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺; C. [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺; D. [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺. List II (Number of unpaired electrons): I. 0; II. 3; III. 2; IV. 4. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats Cr(CN)₆³⁻ as d⁶-like and swaps Co/Ni; (B) makes Fe(H₂O)₆²⁺ low spin (0) and Co(NH₃)₆³⁺ high spin (4); (D) gives Cr³⁺ only 2 unpaired electrons and Ni²⁺ 3.\nWorking: Cr³⁺ d³ → 3 (t₂g³); Fe²⁺ d⁶ with weak-field H₂O, high spin → 4; Co³⁺ d⁶ with NH₃, low spin t₂g⁶ → 0; Ni²⁺ d⁸ octahedral → 2 regardless of ligand.\nKey idea: d³ and d⁸ octahedral counts do not depend on ligand strength; only the d⁶ pair needs the weak-field/strong-field call (H₂O high spin for Fe²⁺, NH₃ low spin for Co³⁺).","traps":["Fe(H₂O)₆²⁺ taken as low spin: H₂O is weak field and Fe²⁺ has low charge, so Δₒ < P: 4 unpaired.","Cyanide treated as changing Cr³⁺ count: With only three d electrons in t₂g there is nothing to pair."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The observed magnetic moment of the complex [Mn(NCS)₆]^x⁻ is 6.06 BM. The numerical value of x is ______. (NCS⁻ is N-bonded.)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (Mn³⁺ with 4 unpaired, μ 4.9 BM), 2 (taking Mn⁴⁺), 6 (adding ligand charges only).\nWorking: 6.06 BM ≈ √35 = 5.92 BM → 5 unpaired electrons → Mn²⁺ (d⁵ high spin with weak-field NCS⁻); charge = +2 − 6 = −4, x = 4.\nKey idea: ~6 BM means 5 unpaired → Mn²⁺; then add ligand charges.","traps":["6.06 BM taken as 6 unpaired: Use √n(n+2): n = 5 gives 5.92 BM."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The mismatched combinations are\nA. Chlorophyll – Co\nB. Water hardness – EDTA\nC. Photography – [Ag(CN)₂]⁻\nD. Wilkinson catalyst – [(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]\nE. Chelating ligand – D-Penicillamine\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and E Only","D and E Only","A and C Only","A, C, and E Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D): include E, mistaking D-penicillamine for a non-chelating drug; (B) also doubts the Wilkinson formula. (D) catches both real errors but adds E.\nWorking: chlorophyll contains Mg (not Co; Co is in vitamin B₁₂) → A mismatched. In photography the film is fixed as [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻ ([Ag(CN)₂]⁻ is used in silver extraction/electroplating) → C mismatched. B, D, E are correct (EDTA titration for hardness; Wilkinson catalyst (Ph₃P)₃RhCl; D-penicillamine chelation therapy).\nKey idea: Chlorophyll – Mg; photography fixing – [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻.","traps":["[Ag(CN)₂]⁻ accepted for photography: Fixing = hypo: [Ag(S₂O₃)₂]³⁻.","Co in chlorophyll accepted: Chlorophyll Mg, haemoglobin Fe, B₁₂ Co."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which of the following complexes will show largest splitting of d-orbitals?","options":["[Fe(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻","[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","[Fe(NH₃)₆]³⁺","[FeF₆]³⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) oxalate is weak-to-medium field. (C) NH₃ is stronger than oxalate but weaker than CN⁻. (D) F⁻ is the weakest ligand here, smallest Δₒ.\nWorking: Fe is +3 in all four complexes, so Δₒ depends only on the ligand. Spectrochemical series: F⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < NH₃ < CN⁻, so [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ has the largest splitting.\nKey idea: With the same metal and oxidation state, Δₒ follows the ligand order of the spectrochemical series; CN⁻ is the strongest of the four.","traps":["Charge of complex confused with field strength: Compare ligands only, using the spectrochemical series."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Double salts vs complexes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which of the following are the example of double salt?\nA. FeSO₄·(NH₄)₂SO₄·6H₂O\nB. CuSO₄·4NH₃·H₂O\nC. K₂SO₄·Al₂(SO₄)₃·24H₂O\nD. Fe(CN)₂·4KCN\nChoose the correct answer :","options":["A, B and D only","B and D only","A and B only","A and C only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"B is [Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄·H₂O, a complex. D is K₄[Fe(CN)₆], a complex. Options (A), (B), (C) all include at least one of these.\nWorking: A (Mohr's salt) and C (potash alum) dissociate completely into simple ions in water, so they are double salts; B is [Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄·H₂O and D is K₄[Fe(CN)₆], both complexes. Answer: A and C only.\nKey idea: Rewrite each formula: if it can be written with a stable complex ion in square brackets, it is a complex, not a double salt.","traps":["Adduct formula hides a complex ion: Rewrite as K₄[Fe(CN)₆] and [Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄·H₂O."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The complex cation which has two isomers is :","options":["[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]⁺","[Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Co(NH₃)₅NO₂]²⁺","[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) are MA₅B with monodentate Cl⁻ (only one arrangement); (A) also has a wrong charge for Co(III). (B) MA₆ has no isomer.\nWorking: nitro (N-bonded) and nitrito (O-bonded) linkage isomers; MA₅B has no geometrical isomers.\nKey idea: Only NO₂⁻ can bind via two different donor atoms, giving nitro and nitrito isomers.","traps":["MA₅B thought to show cis/trans: MA₅B has only one arrangement; need ambidentate ligand."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The spin only magnetic moment of [Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ complexes is ________ B.M. (Nearest integer) (Given : Atomic no. of Mn is 25)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (low spin d⁵, n = 1, μ = 1.73), 5 (reporting n instead of μ), 4 (d⁴ by wrong oxidation state).\nWorking: Mn²⁺ d⁵, H₂O weak field → high spin, n = 5, μ = √35 = 5.92 ≈ 6 BM.\nKey idea: Mn²⁺ is d⁵ and H₂O cannot pair electrons: five unpaired, 5.92 BM.","traps":["Number of unpaired electrons given instead of μ: Apply √n(n+2) and round.","Low-spin assumed: H₂O is weak field; Mn²⁺ d⁵ remains high spin."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The primary and secondary valencies of cobalt respectively in [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ are :","options":["2 and 8","2 and 6","3 and 5","3 and 6"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2 and 8: counts only the ionisable Cl⁻ as primary valence and adds all ligands+counter-ions for secondary. (B) 2 and 6: equates primary valence with the number of outer-sphere Cl⁻. (C) 3 and 5: counts only the five NH₃ and forgets coordinated Cl.\nWorking: Co is +3 (primary valence 3, ionisable); coordination number 6 = five NH₃ + one Cl (secondary valence 6).\nKey idea: Primary valence = oxidation state (+3), not the number of counter-ions; secondary valence = all donor atoms inside the bracket (6).","traps":["Primary valence equals counter-ion count: Primary valence = oxidation state of the metal; compute it from charges (+3).","Coordinated Cl⁻ ignored in coordination number: Count every ligand inside the square bracket."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The d-electronic configuration of [CoCl₄]²⁻ in tetrahedral crystal field is eᵐt₂ⁿ. Sum of \"m\" and \"number of unpaired electrons\" is ___________","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (filling t₂ first as in octahedral: t₂ order reversed gives m = 3 + unpaired 3), 5 (low spin e⁴t₂³ wrongly paired), 8 (d⁸ count for Co²⁺).\nWorking: Co²⁺ is d⁷; tetrahedral (Δt small, high spin): e⁴t₂³, m = 4, unpaired = 3; sum = 7.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral: e below t₂, always high spin → d⁷ = e⁴t₂³.","traps":["Octahedral order applied to tetrahedral: In Td the two-fold e set is lower.","d-count of Co²⁺: Remove 4s electrons first: Co²⁺ = 3d⁷."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The hybridization and magnetic behaviour of cobalt ion in [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ complex, respectively is :","options":["sp³d² and paramagnetic","sp³d² and diamagnetic","d²sp³ and diamagnetic","d²sp³ and paramagnetic"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): sp³d² is for [CoF₆]³⁻ (outer orbital, high spin). (D): mixes inner-orbital with unpaired electrons.\nWorking: Co³⁺ is 3d⁶; strong-field NH₃ pairs all six electrons into three 3d orbitals, leaving two inner 3d orbitals for d²sp³ hybridisation; no unpaired electrons, so the complex is diamagnetic.\nKey idea: NH₃ pairs the 3d⁶ electrons of Co³⁺, freeing two inner d orbitals.","traps":["Inner vs outer orbital: NH₃ with Co³⁺ acts as strong field."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following cannot be explained by crystal field theory?","options":["The order of spectrochemical series","Stability of metal complexes","Magnetic properties of transition metal complexes","Colour of metal complexes"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are all successes of CFT listed by NCERT.\nWorking: CFT treats ligands as point charges, so it predicts that anionic ligands should split the d orbitals most; in fact halides and other anions lie at the weak end of the spectrochemical series. CFT cannot explain this order, while it does account for stability (CFSE), magnetic properties and colour.\nKey idea: CFT's point-charge model fails to explain why anionic ligands like halides sit at the weak end of the spectrochemical series.","traps":["Negative wording: Read 'cannot'; recall the limitation list."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","Spectrochemical series"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of paramagnetic species from the following is ________. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, [Ni(CO)₄], [NiCl₄]²⁻, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺, [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ and [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ (d⁵ low-spin, 1 unpaired) is taken as diamagnetic; 5 if [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is taken as high-spin; 5 if [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is taken as tetrahedral.\nWorking: [NiCl₄]²⁻ 2 unpaired, [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ 1, [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ 1, [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ 4; diamagnetic: [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, [Ni(CO)₄], [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻. Paramagnetic count = 4.\nKey idea: Odd-electron d⁵ (low-spin) and d⁹ ions are always paramagnetic; CN⁻/CO pair up Ni(II), Ni(0) and Fe(II).","traps":["Low-spin d⁵ taken as diamagnetic: Fe³⁺ d⁵ low-spin still has 1 unpaired electron.","[NiCl₄]²⁻ vs [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻: Cl⁻ weak → tetrahedral sp³, 2 unpaired; CN⁻ strong → square planar dsp², 0."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I (Coordination entity) with List II (Wavelength of light absorbed in nm). List I: A. [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺; B. [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺; C. [Co(CN)₆]³⁻; D. [Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺. List II: I. 310; II. 475; III. 535; IV. 600. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives CN⁻ complex a visible wavelength and Co(NH₃)₆³⁺ the UV value; (C) swaps the hexaammine (475) and hexacyanido (310) values; (D) reverses the Δ–λ relation entirely.\nWorking: Δ increases [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺ < [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ < [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ (one NH₃ replaced by weaker Cl⁻; CN⁻ strongest), so the wavelength absorbed decreases: 535 nm, 475 nm, 310 nm; [Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺ (Cu²⁺ with weak-field H₂O, small Δ) absorbs at 600 nm. Answer: A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV.\nKey idea: Larger Δ → higher energy → shorter λ: CN⁻ (310, UV) < NH₃ (475) < NH₃/Cl⁻ (535); Cu²⁺ aqua 600.","traps":["Δ vs wavelength: E = hc/λ: bigger gap, smaller λ.","Replacing one NH₃ by Cl⁻: Cl⁻ is weaker-field than NH₃, so the chlorido complex absorbs at longer λ."],"topics":["Colour of complexes","Spectrochemical series"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Total number of moles of AgCl precipitated on addition of excess of AgNO₃ to one mole each of the following complexes [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl, [Ni(H₂O)₆]Cl₂, [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] and [Pd(NH₃)₄]Cl₂ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9 (all Cl counted); 3 (neutral Pt complex counted as giving 2 and others undercounted); 7 (coordinated Cl of Co complex included).\nWorking: 1 + 2 + 0 + 2 = 5.\nKey idea: Only Cl⁻ outside the square brackets precipitates.","traps":["Coordinated vs ionic chloride: Count only the chlorides written outside [ ]."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Chiral complex from the following is :\nHere en = ethylene diamine","options":["cis-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺","trans-[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺","trans-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺","cis-[PtCl₂(NH₃)₂]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) the trans isomer of the same complex has a mirror plane; (B) trans-MA₄B₂ achiral; (D) square-planar cis-[PtCl₂(NH₃)₂] lies in a mirror plane.\nWorking: cis-[PtCl₂(en)₂]²⁺ is an octahedral cis-[M(AA)₂B₂] complex with no plane or centre of symmetry, so it is not superimposable on its mirror image (chiral). The trans isomers and square-planar cis-[PtCl₂(NH₃)₂] all have a mirror plane.\nKey idea: cis-[M(AA)₂B₂] is the classic chiral octahedral type.","traps":["Square planar cis isomer is not chiral: Square planar complexes have the molecular plane as mirror."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The sum of bridging carbonyls in W(CO)₆ and Mn₂(CO)₁₀ is ____________.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (confusing Mn₂(CO)₁₀ with Co₂(CO)₈); 3 (Fe₂(CO)₉).\nWorking: W(CO)₆ is octahedral with six terminal CO; Mn₂(CO)₁₀ has ten terminal CO and one Mn–Mn bond. Bridging CO = 0 + 0 = 0.\nKey idea: Mn₂(CO)₁₀ is held only by a Mn–Mn bond.","traps":["Mn₂(CO)₁₀ vs Co₂(CO)₈: Only Co₂(CO)₈ (and Fe₂(CO)₉) have bridging CO."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Correct order of spin only magnetic moment of the following complex ions is :\n(Given At.no. Fe : 26, Co : 27)","options":["[CoF₆]³⁻ > [FeF₆]³⁻ > [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻","[FeF₆]³⁻ > [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ > [CoF₆]³⁻","[FeF₆]³⁻ > [CoF₆]³⁻ > [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻","[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ > [CoF₆]³⁻ > [FeF₆]³⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes d⁶ Co³⁺ has more unpaired electrons than d⁵ Fe³⁺; (B) treats oxalate as weak field (high spin) for Co³⁺; (D) reverses the whole order.\nWorking: [FeF₆]³⁻ Fe³⁺ d⁵ high spin, 5 unpaired (5.92 BM); [CoF₆]³⁻ Co³⁺ d⁶ high spin, 4 unpaired (4.90 BM); [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ Co³⁺ d⁶ low spin, 0 unpaired. Order: [FeF₆]³⁻ > [CoF₆]³⁻ > [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻.\nKey idea: Co³⁺ has a high charge, so even oxalate pairs the electrons; only F⁻ keeps it high spin.","traps":["Oxalate with Co(III): Use NCERT fact: it is diamagnetic (inner orbital d²sp³).","d⁵ vs d⁶ high spin: Count: d⁵ HS = 5, d⁶ HS = 4."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"To inhibit the growth of tumours, identify the compounds used from the following: A. EDTA B. Coordination compounds of Pt C. D-Penicillamine D. Cis-platin. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and B Only","C and D Only","B and D Only","A and C Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: EDTA is used for lead poisoning, not tumours. C: D-penicillamine removes excess copper/iron. Option (A) pairs Pt compounds with EDTA, option (B) pairs cis-platin with D-penicillamine, and option (D) has only the two chelating agents.\nWorking: cis-platin and related Pt coordination compounds inhibit tumour growth (B and D); EDTA is used for lead poisoning and D-penicillamine removes excess copper/iron – they are chelating agents, not anti-tumour drugs.\nKey idea: Cis-platin is itself a Pt coordination compound, so B and D are both correct; the other two are chelation-therapy agents.","traps":["Chelating agents mistaken for anti-tumour drugs: Link each agent to its specific use: EDTA–Pb, penicillamine–Cu/Fe, cis-platin–tumours.","B and D seem to overlap: Both statements are true; overlap does not make either false."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following is correct order of ligand field strength?","options":["S²⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < NH₃ < en < CO","CO < en < NH₃ < C₂O₄²⁻ < S²⁻","S²⁻ < NH₃ < en < CO < C₂O₄²⁻","NH₃ < en < CO < S²⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B: complete reversal (decreasing order). C: oxalate moved to the top. D: places anionic ligands above CO.\nWorking: NCERT spectrochemical series: … S²⁻ < … < C₂O₄²⁻ < H₂O < … < NH₃ < en < … < CO, so (A) is the correct increasing order.\nKey idea: Read off the NCERT series: S²⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < NH₃ < en < CO.","traps":["Reversed series: Check that weak-field halide/sulphide ligands come first.","Oxalate placed as strongest: Oxalate is a weak-field O-donor, just below H₂O."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List I (Complexes) with List II (Hybridisation): A. [Ni(CO)₄]; B. [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺; C. [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺; D. [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺. I. sp³; II. dsp²; III. sp³d²; IV. d²sp³.","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps the two Fe complexes (NH₃ as weak field); (B)/(D) swap Ni(CO)₄ and [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (treating Ni(0) as square planar like [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻).\nWorking: Ni(0) d¹⁰ → sp³; [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ square planar → dsp²; NH₃ taken as strong field → [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺ inner-orbital d²sp³; weak-field H₂O → [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ outer-orbital sp³d². (Strictly, [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺ is found to be high spin, but the one-to-one match needs NH₃ to be treated as strong field here.)\nKey idea: Ni(0) d¹⁰ → sp³; Cu(II) square planar → dsp²; NH₃ taken as stronger than H₂O so [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺ inner-orbital d²sp³ and [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ outer-orbital sp³d².","traps":["NH₃ field strength depends on metal: Follow exam convention: NH₃ > H₂O ⇒ inner orbital where options demand one-to-one.","[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ square planar not tetrahedral: Cu²⁺ d⁹ tetraammine is square planar (dsp²) by convention."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The Cl–Co–Cl bond angle values in a fac-[Co(NH₃)₃Cl₃] complex is/are :","options":["90° & 120°","90°","180°","90° & 180°"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 120°: treating the triangular face as a planar trigonal arrangement around Co; (C)/(D) 180°: describes mer, where two Cl are trans.\nWorking: in the fac isomer the three Cl occupy one triangular face of the octahedron, so every pair of Cl is cis: each Cl–Co–Cl angle is 90°.\nKey idea: On an octahedral face all three vertices are cis to each other, so every Cl–Co–Cl angle is 90°.","traps":["Face triangle is not 120° at the metal: Angles are measured at Co, along octahedral axes: cis = 90°, trans = 180°.","fac vs mer swap: fac = all cis; mer = two cis, one trans."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"1 L, 0.02 M solution of [Co(NH₃)₅SO₄]Br is mixed with 1 L, 0.02 M solution of [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄. The resulting solution is divided into two equal parts (X) and treated with excess of AgNO₃ solution and BaCl₂ solution respectively as shown below: 1 L Solution (X) + AgNO₃ solution (excess) → Y; 1 L Solution (X) + BaCl₂ solution (excess) → Z. The number of moles of Y and Z respectively are","options":["0.01, 0.01","0.01, 0.02","0.02, 0.01","0.02, 0.02"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D): forget that the 2 L mixture is halved (0.02 mol each complex total → 0.01 mol in each 1 L half), or count coordinated Br/SO₄ as precipitable.\nWorking: each solution contains 0.02 mol of complex; the 2 L mixture is split into two 1 L parts, each with 0.01 mol of each complex. With AgNO₃ only the free Br⁻ of [Co(NH₃)₅SO₄]Br precipitates: 0.01 mol AgBr (Y). With BaCl₂ only the free SO₄²⁻ of [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄ precipitates: 0.01 mol BaSO₄ (Z).\nKey idea: Each 1 L half has 0.01 mol of each isomer; only [Co(NH₃)₅SO₄]Br gives free Br⁻ (0.01 mol AgBr) and only [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄ gives free SO₄²⁻ (0.01 mol BaSO₄).","traps":["Dilution/halving ignored: Track moles, not molarity: 0.02 mol each in 2 L → 0.01 mol each per 1 L.","Coordinated ions do not precipitate: Only ions outside the square bracket ionise."],"topics":["Structural isomerism","Werner's theory"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Cobalt chloride when dissolved in water forms pink colored complex X which has octahedral geometry. This solution on treating with conc HCl forms deep blue complex, Y which has a Z geometry. X, Y and Z, respectively, are","options":["X = [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺, Y = [CoCl₄]²⁻, Z = Tetrahedral","X = [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺, Y = [CoCl₆]³⁻, Z = Octahedral","X = [Co(H₂O)₄Cl₂]⁺, Y = [CoCl₄]²⁻, Z = Tetrahedral","X = [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, Y = [CoCl₆]³⁻, Z = Octahedral"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) keeps CN 6 and changes the oxidation state to +3 in Y. (C) X written as a +1 chloro-aqua species, not the simple hexaaqua ion. (D) wrong oxidation state (+3) for both.\nWorking: CoCl₂ in water gives pink octahedral [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺; with excess Cl⁻ (conc. HCl) it changes to deep blue [CoCl₄]²⁻, which is tetrahedral.\nKey idea: CoCl₂ is Co(II): pink [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ⇌ blue tetrahedral [CoCl₄]²⁻ in excess Cl⁻.","traps":["Oxidation state drift to Co(III): Start from CoCl₂: cobalt is +2 throughout.","Coordination number kept at 6: Large Cl⁻ ligands and colour change signal change to 4-coordinate tetrahedral."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"If the CFSE of [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is −96.0 kJ/mol, this complex will absorb maximum at wavelength ________ nm. (nearest integer) Assume Planck's constant (h) = 6.4 × 10⁻³⁴ Js, Speed of light (c) = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s and Avogadro's Constant (N_A) = 6 × 10²³/mol","type":"num","answer":"480","range":[480.0,480.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1200 (using CFSE 96 kJ directly as Δo), 0.48 or 4.8 (unit slips), 498 (quoting NCERT table value).\nWorking: CFSE = −0.4Δo → Δo = 240 kJ mol⁻¹; per ion 4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; λ = hc/E = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m = 480 nm.\nKey idea: One t₂g electron: CFSE = −0.4Δo, so Δo = 96/0.4 = 240 kJ mol⁻¹.","traps":["CFSE taken as Δo: For d¹, Δo = |CFSE|/0.4.","Per mole vs per photon: Divide by N_A before E = hc/λ."],"topics":["CFSE","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the following metal complexes: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺, [Co(CN)₆]³⁻, [Co(NH₃)₅(H₂O)]³⁺. The spin-only magnetic moment value of the complex that absorbs light with shortest wavelength is ____ B.M. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4.9 → 5 (high-spin d⁶ counted); a complex picked by longest wavelength (Cl⁻ complex) still d⁶ low spin → 0 too, so only high-spin error changes the value.\nWorking: shortest wavelength absorbed = largest Δo = [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ (CN⁻ strongest field); Co³⁺ d⁶ low spin t₂g⁶ → 0 unpaired → 0 B.M.\nKey idea: All four are Co(III) d⁶ low spin with strong/intermediate ligands; the answer is 0 either way, so CN⁻ selection mainly tests the concept.","traps":["Shortest wavelength = largest Δo: E = hc/λ: shorter λ means larger Δo."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct order of energy of absorption for the following metal complexes is A: [Ni(en)₃]²⁺, B: [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺, C: [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺","options":["C < B < A","B < C < A","C < A < B","A < C < B"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) place en below NH₃ (counting en as one N donor rather than chelating); (B) puts NH₃ below H₂O.\nWorking: all are octahedral Ni²⁺ (d⁸); Δo follows ligand strength H₂O < NH₃ < en, so absorbed energy C < B < A.\nKey idea: Higher Δo → higher energy (shorter wavelength) absorbed.","traps":["Absorbed vs observed colour: Order by Δo, not by perceived colour.","en vs NH₃ order: en is a stronger-field (chelating) ligand than NH₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The difference between spin only magnetic moment values of [Co(H₂O)₆]Cl₂ and [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2.14 or 2 (taking Co²⁺ low spin, 1 unpaired: 3.87 − 1.73), 1 (counting difference of unpaired electrons wrongly).\nWorking: Co²⁺ d⁷ with weak-field H₂O is high spin, 3 unpaired; Cr³⁺ d³, 3 unpaired; both √15 = 3.87 BM → difference 0.\nKey idea: d⁷ high spin and d³ both have 3 unpaired electrons.","traps":["Co²⁺ taken as low spin: H₂O is weak field for Co²⁺ → high spin.","Wrong oxidation state: Charge balance: Co²⁺, Cr³⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The metal complex that is diamagnetic is (Atomic number : Fe, 26; Cu, 29)","options":["K₃[Cu(CN)₄]","K₂[Cu(CN)₄]","K₃[Fe(CN)₄]","K₄[FeCl₆]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Cu(II) d⁹ always has one unpaired electron even with CN⁻; (C) unusual Fe(I) d⁷ is odd-electron; (D) Cl⁻ is weak-field, Fe²⁺ high spin with 4 unpaired.\nWorking: K₃[Cu(CN)₄] has Cu(I), 3d¹⁰, no unpaired electrons; Cu(II) d⁹ in (B), Fe(I) d⁷ in (C) and high-spin Fe(II) d⁶ with Cl⁻ in (D) are paramagnetic.\nKey idea: d¹⁰ (Cu⁺) is diamagnetic whatever the ligand.","traps":["K count fixes oxidation state: Charge on complex = −(number of K⁺); Cu = charge + 4.","Strong field cannot pair an odd count: An odd number of electrons always leaves one unpaired."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Low oxidation state of metals in their complexes are common when ligands:","options":["have good π-accepting character","have good σ-donor character","are having good π-donating ability","are having poor σ-donating ability"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) strong σ-donation adds electron density (favours higher states); (C) π-donors stabilise high oxidation states; (D) poor σ-donation is irrelevant.\nWorking: π-acceptor ligands (CO, CN⁻, PR₃) withdraw electron density from the metal by back-bonding, stabilising low (zero or negative) oxidation states, as in metal carbonyls.\nKey idea: Back-bonding drains electron density from an electron-rich low-valent metal.","traps":["σ-donor vs π-acceptor: Low oxidation state metal is electron-rich, so it needs a ligand that accepts electrons."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The spin only magnetic moment of the complex present in Fehling's reagent is ______ B.M. (Nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (assuming Cu⁺ d¹⁰, the red Cu₂O product); 3 or 4 (miscounting unpaired electrons).\nWorking: Fehling's solution contains a Cu(II)–tartrate complex; Cu²⁺ is 3d⁹ with one unpaired electron, μ = √3 = 1.73 BM ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Cu²⁺, d⁹, n = 1, μ = 1.73 BM → 2.","traps":["Cu(I) vs Cu(II): The reagent itself is Cu(II) tartrate; Cu₂O is the product.","Rounding 1.73: 1.73 rounds to 2."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Applications"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Total number of relatively more stable isomer(s) possible for octahedral complex [Cu(en)₂(SCN)₂] will be ___________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if the cis enantiomers are not counted separately; 6–9 if S/N linkage combinations are added.\nWorking: for M(AA)₂B₂ the cis form is chiral (two enantiomers) and the trans form is achiral → 3 stereoisomers. \"Relatively more stable\" is read as excluding the less stable linkage (S- vs N-bonded) variants; counting linkage isomers as well would give more.\nKey idea: M(AA)₂B₂ → cis (d + l) + trans = 3.","traps":["Counting enantiomers: Cis M(AA)₂B₂ has no mirror plane → two enantiomers.","Linkage isomers of SCN: The phrase \"relatively more stable\" asks for one bonding mode only."],"topics":["Optical isomerism","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List I (Complex) with List II (Hybridization): A. Ni(CO)₄; B. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻; C. [Co(CN)₆]³⁻; D. [CoF₆]³⁻. I. sp³; II. sp³d²; III. d²sp³; IV. dsp². Choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) swap the two Ni complexes (Ni(CO)₄ is not square planar); (C)/(D) swap the Co complexes (F⁻ is weak field).\nWorking: Ni(0) d¹⁰ with CO → sp³ tetrahedral; Ni²⁺ d⁸ with strong CN⁻ → dsp² square planar; Co³⁺ d⁶ with CN⁻ (low spin, inner orbital) → d²sp³; Co³⁺ with weak F⁻ (high spin, outer orbital) → sp³d².\nKey idea: Strong field CN⁻ pairs electrons (inner d); weak F⁻ uses outer 4d; Ni(0) d¹⁰ can only be sp³.","traps":["Ni(CO)₄ vs [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻: Ni(0) is d¹⁰: no free 3d, so sp³.","[CoF₆]³⁻ inner vs outer: F⁻ is weak field: high spin, sp³d²."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ should be an inner orbital complex. Ignoring the pairing energy, the value of crystal field stabilization energy for this complex is (−) _________ Δo. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (high-spin d⁵); 1 or 3 (wrong per-electron factor).\nWorking: Fe³⁺ d⁵, CN⁻ strong field → low spin t₂g⁵ eg⁰; CFSE = 5 × (−0.4 Δo) = −2.0 Δo.\nKey idea: Low spin d⁵: t₂g⁵ → −2.0 Δo.","traps":["High-spin d⁵ CFSE: Inner orbital = low spin.","Fe oxidation state: Charge −3 with six CN⁻ → Fe³⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Octahedral complexes of copper(II) undergo structural distortion (Jahn–Teller). Which one of the given copper(II) complexes will show the maximum structural distortion? (en – ethylenediamine; H₂N–CH₂–CH₂–NH₂)","options":["[Cu(H₂O)₆]SO₄","[Cu(en)(H₂O)₄]SO₄","cis-[Cu(en)₂Cl₂]","trans-[Cu(en)₂Cl₂]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) one chelate ring partly restricts distortion; (C)/(D) two chelate rings plus mixed ligands – (D) tempting because trans-dichloro already looks tetragonal.\nWorking: in [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ all six ligands are identical weak-field monodentate ligands, so the d⁹ (e_g³) degeneracy is lifted only by a free Jahn–Teller elongation; chelating en rings restrict bond-length changes, so complexes containing en show less distortion. (Strictly, mixed-ligand trans complexes already have a ligand-imposed tetragonal distortion, but the expected answer follows this usual argument.)\nKey idea: Identical monodentate ligands allow the largest pure Jahn–Teller elongation.","traps":["Trans mixed-ligand taken as most distorted: JT distortion is about degenerate e_g³ in a symmetric field.","Chelate rigidity ignored: Chelates resist elongation along their bonds."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Sum of oxidation state (magnitude) and coordination number of cobalt in Na[Co(bpy)Cl₄] is ___. (Given bpy = 2,2'-bipyridine, two pyridine rings joined at C2–C2')","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (bpy counted as monodentate, CN 5); 10 (oxidation state taken as +4 or CN 7).\nWorking: bpy is neutral bidentate; +1 + x − 4 = 0 → x = +3; CN = 2 (bpy) + 4 (Cl) = 6; sum = 9.\nKey idea: bpy: neutral, two N donors.","traps":["bpy counted once: CN = number of donor atoms."],"topics":["Coordination number & oxidation state","Ligands & denticity"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) [PtCl₄]²⁻, (B) BrF₅, (C) PCl₅, (D) [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺. List-II (hybridisation): (I) sp³d, (II) d²sp³, (III) dsp², (IV) sp³d².","options":["(A)→(II), (B)→(IV), (C)→(I), (D)→(III)","(A)→(III), (B)→(IV), (C)→(I), (D)→(II)","(A)→(III), (B)→(I), (C)→(IV), (D)→(II)","(A)→(II), (B)→(I), (C)→(IV), (D)→(III)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) give [PtCl₄]²⁻ d²sp³ (ignoring square planar); (C)/(D) give BrF₅ sp³d by counting only the 5 bonds and missing the lone pair.\nWorking: [PtCl₄]²⁻ square planar d⁸ → dsp²; BrF₅ 5 bp + 1 lp → sp³d²; PCl₅ → sp³d; [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ low-spin d⁶ inner-orbital → d²sp³.\nKey idea: Count steric number (bonds + lone pairs) for BrF₅ = 6; d⁸ Pt(II) is always square planar.","traps":["BrF₅ lone pair ignored: Steric number = 5 bonds + 1 lone pair = 6 → sp³d².","Inner vs outer d-orbitals: Strong-field NH₃ with Co³⁺ (d⁶) pairs electrons → inner 3d used → d²sp³."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In the cobalt-carbonyl complex [Co₂(CO)₈], number of Co–Co bonds is \"X\" and terminal CO ligands is \"Y\". X + Y = ____.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9 (1 Co–Co + 8 CO, ignoring bridges); 8 (no Co–Co bond counted, or all CO terminal); 3 (counting bridging CO + Co–Co).\nWorking: the bridged isomer (as drawn in NCERT Fig. 5.13) has one Co–Co bond, two bridging CO and six terminal CO → X + Y = 1 + 6 = 7.\nKey idea: Co₂(CO)₈: 6 terminal + 2 bridging CO, plus one Co–Co bond.","traps":["All eight CO taken as terminal: Recall the two μ-CO bridges in the NCERT figure."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Transition metal complex with highest value of crystal field splitting (Δ₀) will be","options":["[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Mo(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Os(H₂O)₆]³⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Cr³⁺ / (C) Fe³⁺ – 3d metals, smallest Δo; students may pick Fe³⁺ thinking higher Z in the same row matters; (B) Mo³⁺ – 4d, larger than 3d but smaller than 5d.\nWorking: same ligand and same +3 charge, so Δo is decided by the metal row; Δo rises 3d < 4d < 5d (more diffuse d orbitals), so the 5d Os³⁺ complex has the largest Δo.\nKey idea: Same ligand, same charge → go down the group: 5d > 4d > 3d (≈ 30–50 % rise per row).","traps":["Only ligand strength considered: When ligand and charge are equal, compare the metal period: 5d largest.","Stronger field picked by d-electron count: CFSE ≠ Δo; Δo is set by ligand, charge and metal row."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Leaching of gold with dilute aqueous solution of NaCN in presence of oxygen gives complex [A], which on reaction with zinc forms the elemental gold and another complex [B]. [A] and [B], respectively are:","options":["[Au(CN)₄]⁻ and [Zn(CN)₂(OH)₂]²⁻","[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [Zn(OH)₄]²⁻","[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻","[Au(CN)₄]²⁻ and [Zn(CN)₆]⁴⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) use Au(III)-like [Au(CN)₄] – oxidation-state slip; (B) [Zn(OH)₄]²⁻ – thinks the OH⁻ formed in leaching binds zinc; (D) [Zn(CN)₆]⁴⁻ – assumes CN 6.\nWorking: 4Au + 8CN⁻ + 2H₂O + O₂ → 4[Au(CN)₂]⁻ + 4OH⁻ (Au(I), linear, CN 2); 2[Au(CN)₂]⁻ + Zn → [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻ + 2Au (Zn²⁺ tetrahedral, CN 4). Option (C).\nKey idea: Gold goes to +1 with two CN⁻; Zn²⁺ takes four CN⁻.","traps":["Au oxidation state in cyanide complex: Leaching gives Au(I): [Au(CN)₂]⁻.","Zn complex formula: Zinc displaces Au and picks up the freed CN⁻."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Amongst FeCl₃·3H₂O, K₃[Fe(CN)₆] and [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃, the spin-only magnetic moment value of the inner-orbital complex that absorbs light at shortest wavelength is ________ B.M. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (picking [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, diamagnetic), 6 (FeCl₃·3H₂O high-spin d⁵, μ 5.92), 4 (rounding errors).\nWorking: K₃[Fe(CN)₆] (Fe³⁺ d⁵, low spin, d²sp³) and [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ (Co³⁺ d⁶, low spin, d²sp³) are inner-orbital; CN⁻ is the stronger-field ligand, so [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ has the larger Δo and absorbs at the shorter wavelength. It has 1 unpaired electron: μ = √3 = 1.73 ≈ 2 BM.\nKey idea: Shortest wavelength absorbed = largest splitting = strongest-field ligand (CN⁻).","traps":["Shortest wavelength ↔ largest Δo: Higher Δo → higher energy → shorter λ.","Inner-orbital filter: FeCl₃·3H₂O is high spin/outer orbital."],"topics":["Colour of complexes","Spectrochemical series","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The spin–only magnetic moment value of an octahedral complex among CoCl₃·4NH₃, NiCl₂·6H₂O and PtCl₄·2HCl, which upon reaction with excess of AgNO₃ gives 2 moles of AgCl is ______ B.M. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 – choosing Co(III) (d⁶ low spin) or Pt(IV); 4 – Co³⁺ high-spin assumption; 2 – rounding 2.83 down.\nWorking: CoCl₃·4NH₃ = [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl (1 mol AgCl); NiCl₂·6H₂O = [Ni(H₂O)₆]Cl₂ (2 mol AgCl); PtCl₄·2HCl = H₂[PtCl₆] (0). Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ octahedral has 2 unpaired electrons, μ = √8 = 2.83 ≈ 3.\nKey idea: Six ligands fill the octahedron: count how many Cl⁻ are left outside.","traps":["Wrong complex identified: Only Cl⁻ outside the sphere precipitate; 4NH₃ + 2Cl fill Co.","Rounding 2.83: 2.83 rounds to 3."],"topics":["Werner's theory","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Reaction of [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ with excess ammonia and in the presence of oxygen results into a diamagnetic product. Number of electrons present in t₂g-orbitals of the product is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 or 7 (keeping Co²⁺ d⁷, high- or low-spin t₂g count); 4 (high-spin d⁶ t₂g⁴e_g²).\nWorking: air oxidises Co(II) to Co(III) in ammonia: [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, Co³⁺ d⁶ with strong-field NH₃ → t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ (diamagnetic); 6 electrons in t₂g.\nKey idea: \"Diamagnetic\" fixes the product as low-spin Co(III) d⁶.","traps":["Oxidation state not updated: O₂ oxidises Co(II) to Co(III); diamagnetism requires an even, paired count.","Spin state of d⁶: With Co³⁺, NH₃ is strong-field → low spin t₂g⁶."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which of the following will have maximum stabilization due to crystal field?","options":["[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Co(CN)₆]³⁻","[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – one t₂g electron only; (B) – weak-field H₂O keeps Co²⁺ high spin; (D) – NH₃ is stronger than H₂O but Cu²⁺ d⁹ gains little CFSE.\nWorking: [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ is Co³⁺ d⁶ with strong-field CN⁻ → low spin t₂g⁶, CFSE = −2.4Δo, and Δo itself is largest (strong ligand, +3 metal). Ti³⁺ d¹ (−0.4Δo), Co²⁺ d⁷ high spin with weak H₂O (−0.8Δo), Cu²⁺ d⁹ (−0.6Δo-type, moderate ligand) are all much smaller → (C).\nKey idea: Low-spin d⁶ with a strong ligand and a +3 metal maximises both the electron term (−2.4) and Δo.","traps":["Only ligand strength compared: Also count d-electrons and spin state.","Oxidation state of Co missed: Co³⁺ (d⁶) in [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ vs Co²⁺ (d⁷) in aqua complex."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Arrange the following coordination compounds in the increasing order of magnetic moments. (Atomic numbers: Mn = 25; Fe = 26) A. [FeF₆]³⁻ B. [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ C. [MnCl₆]³⁻ (high spin) D. [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻","options":["A < B < D < C","B < D < C < A","A < C < D < B","B < D < A < C"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) put [FeF₆]³⁻ lowest (treat F⁻ as strong field); (D) puts high-spin Fe(III) below high-spin Mn(III).\nWorking: unpaired electrons – [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ low-spin d⁵: 1; [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ low-spin d⁴: 2; [MnCl₆]³⁻ high-spin d⁴: 4; [FeF₆]³⁻ high-spin d⁵: 5. Increasing μ: B < D < C < A.\nKey idea: CN⁻ pairs electrons; F⁻ and Cl⁻ do not: 1 < 2 < 4 < 5 unpaired.","traps":["F⁻ treated as strong field: Use the spectrochemical series: F⁻ weak, CN⁻ strong.","d⁴ vs d⁵ counts: High-spin d⁵ (5) > high-spin d⁴ (4)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is square planar and diamagnetic complex, with dsp² hybridization for Ni but [Ni(CO)₄] is tetrahedral, paramagnetic and with sp³-hybridization for Ni. Statement II: [NiCl₄]²⁻ and [Ni(CO)₄] both have same d-electron configuration, have same geometry and are paramagnetic. In light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is correct but statement II is false.","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) accepts \"paramagnetic\" for Ni(CO)₄; (A)/(D) accept the same d-configuration claim for Ni(II) and Ni(0) complexes because both are tetrahedral.\nWorking: [Ni(CO)₄] is tetrahedral sp³ but diamagnetic (Ni(0), 3d¹⁰ after pairing), so I is false; [NiCl₄]²⁻ is Ni²⁺ d⁸ (2 unpaired, paramagnetic) while [Ni(CO)₄] is d¹⁰ diamagnetic, so II is false although both are tetrahedral.\nKey idea: Same geometry does not mean same d-count: Ni(0) d¹⁰ vs Ni(II) d⁸.","traps":["One wrong word in long statement: Check every adjective separately.","Same geometry, different d-count: Work out the oxidation state of Ni first."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","VBT: hybridisation & geometry"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of complexes which will exhibit synergic bonding amongst, [Cr(CO)₆], [Mn(CO)₅] and [Mn₂(CO)₁₀] is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (rejecting [Mn(CO)₅] as unstable/odd-electron).\nWorking: all three are metal carbonyls with M–CO σ donation and M→CO π back donation; [Mn(CO)₅] is a 17-electron radical but still has synergic M–CO bonding.\nKey idea: Any terminal M–CO bond in a low-valent carbonyl is synergic.","traps":["Odd-electron carbonyl excluded: Synergic bonding depends on M–CO, not on stability."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻, [Ti(CN)₆]³⁻, [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, [Co(CN)₆]³⁻. Among the given complexes, the number of paramagnetic complexes is _____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (forgetting Ti³⁺ d¹ cannot pair); 3 (treating [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ as tetrahedral); 4 (treating CN⁻ as weak field).\nWorking: [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ Fe²⁺ d⁶ low spin – 0 unpaired; [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ Fe³⁺ d⁵ low spin – 1; [Ti(CN)₆]³⁻ Ti³⁺ d¹ – 1; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ d⁸ square planar – 0; [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ Co³⁺ d⁶ low spin – 0. Paramagnetic: 2.\nKey idea: Strong field: odd d-count → paramagnetic; d⁶ low spin and square-planar d⁸ → diamagnetic.","traps":["d¹ always paramagnetic: One electron cannot pair.","Fe²⁺ vs Fe³⁺ in cyanides: Count d-electrons from the charge."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"(a) CoCl₃·4NH₃, (b) CoCl₃·5NH₃, (c) CoCl₃·6NH₃ and (d) CoCl(NO₃)₂·5NH₃. Number of complex(es) which will exist in cis-trans form is/are ____","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (thinking (d) MA₅B shows cis/trans, or counting ionisation isomers); 0 (missing (a)).\nWorking: (a) [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl is MA₄B₂ – cis/trans; (b) [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ MA₅B – none; (c) [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ – none; (d) [Co(NH₃)₅Cl](NO₃)₂ or [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₃)]Cl(NO₃), both MA₅B – none. Answer 1.\nKey idea: Octahedral MA₅B never shows cis-trans; MA₄B₂ does.","traps":["MA₅B has no cis/trans: Only one non-NH₃ ligand inside the sphere."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: In CuSO₄·5H₂O, Cu–O bonds are present.\nStatement II: In CuSO₄·5H₂O, ligands coordinating with Cu(II) ion are O- and S-based ligands.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for students who think sulphate (containing S) is an \"S-based\" ligand; (B)/(D) for those who think all five waters are lattice water.\nWorking: CuSO₄·5H₂O is [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O; four water O atoms (and sulphate O atoms weakly in axial positions) bind Cu²⁺, so Cu–O bonds exist; no ligand binds through S, so II is false.\nKey idea: Sulphate binds through O; the 5th water is H-bonded, not coordinated.","traps":["Sulphate taken as S-donor: Oxoanions coordinate through O.","All water treated as lattice water: Four H₂O are coordinated to Cu²⁺."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Spin only magnetic moment of [MnBr₆]⁴⁻ is ____ B.M. (round off to the closest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (low-spin d⁵, one unpaired) if Br⁻ is taken as strong field; wrong oxidation state (+4 → d³, 4 BM).\nWorking: Mn²⁺ (d⁵); Br⁻ weak field → high spin, 5 unpaired electrons; μ = √(5 × 7) = 5.92 ≈ 6 BM.\nKey idea: Halide ligands are weak field → high spin d⁵ → 5 unpaired.","traps":["Oxidation state of Mn: x − 6 = −4 → Mn²⁺.","Spin state: Br⁻ is weak field."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"For the reaction given below: CoCl₃·xNH₃ + AgNO₃(aq) → If two equivalents of AgCl precipitate out, then the value of x will be ____.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (if CN taken as 6 with two Cl inside), 6 (all Cl ionisable).\nWorking: two ionisable Cl⁻ means one Cl is coordinated: [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ with coordination number 6 → x = 5.\nKey idea: Precipitated Cl⁻ = counter ions; CN 6 fixed.","traps":["Coordination number not used: NH₃ + coordinated Cl = 6."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Indicate the complex/complex ion which did not show any geometrical isomerism:","options":["[CoCl₂(en)₂]","[Co(CN)₅(NC)]³⁻","[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃]","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) are the classic NCERT cis/trans examples; (C) fac/mer is sometimes forgotten as geometrical isomerism; (B) looks like it has two different ligands, but only one is unique.\nWorking: [Co(CN)₅(NC)]³⁻ is of type MA₅B, which has only one arrangement; [CoCl₂(en)₂]⁺ (cis/trans), [Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃] (fac/mer) and [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ (cis/trans) all show geometrical isomerism.\nKey idea: MA₅B and MA₆ have no geometrical isomers; the CN/NC pair signals linkage, not geometric, isomerism.","traps":["MA₅B has no geometrical isomers: Count how many of the odd ligand: one → no GI.","fac/mer is geometrical isomerism: fac/mer = geometric isomers."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"1 mol of an octahedral metal complex with formula MCl₃·2L on reaction with excess of AgNO₃ gives 1 mol of AgCl. The denticity of Ligand L is ______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (assuming L monodentate without checking CN); 3 (forgetting the two bound Cl); 4.\nWorking: 1 Cl⁻ is ionisable, so the complex is [MCl₂L₂]Cl; coordination number 6 = 2 (Cl) + 2 × denticity → denticity = 2.\nKey idea: CN 6 − 2 bound Cl = 4 sites for 2 L → each L is bidentate.","traps":["Ionised Cl counted as ligand: Only 2 Cl are inside; 1 precipitates."],"topics":["Ligands & denticity","Werner's theory"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The number of optical isomers possible for [Cr(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (counting the pair as one compound), 0 (assuming a symmetric ligand set is achiral), 3 or 4 (adding geometrical isomers that do not exist for M(AA)₃).\nWorking: a tris-chelate octahedral complex [M(AA)₃] is chiral and exists as one pair of enantiomers (Δ and Λ) – 2 optical isomers.\nKey idea: M(AA)₃ → no geometrical isomers, one enantiomeric pair.","traps":["Identical ligands thought achiral: Three chelate rings form a propeller – chiral."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The denticity of an organic ligand, biuret is:","options":["2","4","3","6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) counts all three N; (B) counts two O + two terminal N; (D) counts every lone-pair atom.\nWorking: biuret, H₂N–CO–NH–CO–NH₂, binds metal ions (e.g. Cu²⁺ in the biuret test) through two donor atoms (two O, or two N after deprotonation) to form a six-membered chelate – bidentate, 2.\nKey idea: Only two donors (O,O or N,N) can reach one metal to form a 6-membered chelate ring.","traps":["Counting all donor atoms: Denticity = donors bound to one metal simultaneously; geometry allows 2."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Spin only magnetic moment in BM of [Fe(CO)₄(C₂O₄)]⁺ is:","options":["5.92","0","1","1.73"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5.92 = high-spin d⁵ (ignores CO); (B) 0 = treats Fe as Fe(0)/18-electron carbonyl; (C) 1 = number of unpaired electrons given instead of μ.\nWorking: CO neutral, C₂O₄²⁻ = −2, overall +1 → Fe(III), d⁵. CO is a strong-field ligand, so low spin t₂g⁵: one unpaired electron, μ = √3 = 1.73 BM.\nKey idea: Fe(III) d⁵ + strong field CO → t₂g⁵, n = 1, μ = 1.73 BM.","traps":["Fe taken as zero-valent: Count charges: +1 overall, oxalate −2 → Fe +3.","High spin d⁵: Four CO ligands dominate; treat as strong field, low spin."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Coordination number & oxidation state"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The calculated magnetic moments (spin only value) for species [FeCl₄]²⁻, [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ and MnO₄²⁻ respectively are:","options":["5.82, 0 and 0 BM","4.90, 0 and 1.73 BM","5.92, 4.90 and 0 BM","4.90, 0 and 2.83 BM"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5.82 – mistakes Fe as Fe³⁺-like value and MnO₄²⁻ as d⁰; (C) 5.92 for Fe³⁺ and high-spin Co³⁺; (D) 2.83 – takes Mn as d² (confusing manganate with Mn(V)).\nWorking: Fe²⁺ d⁶ tetrahedral (high spin) 4 unpaired → 4.90 BM; Co³⁺ d⁶ with oxalate is low spin t₂g⁶ → 0; Mn(VI) in MnO₄²⁻ is d¹ → 1.73 BM.\nKey idea: Manganate is Mn(VI) d¹; permanganate is Mn(VII) d⁰.","traps":["MnO₄²⁻ taken as d⁰: Charge −2 → Mn +6 → d¹.","Oxalate Co(III) taken high spin: Co³⁺ octahedral complexes are low spin except with F⁻."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The hybridization and magnetic nature of [Mn(CN)₆]⁴⁻ and [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻, respectively are:","options":["d²sp³ and diamagnetic","sp³d² and diamagnetic","d²sp³ and paramagnetic","sp³d² and paramagnetic"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes pairing makes them diamagnetic (true for d⁶ [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻); (B)/(D) treat CN⁻ as weak-field outer-orbital.\nWorking: [Mn(CN)₆]⁴⁻ has Mn²⁺ d⁵; [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ has Fe³⁺ d⁵. CN⁻ is strong-field, so both are low-spin t₂g⁵ with one unpaired electron, inner-orbital d²sp³ and paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Same d⁵ low-spin count → same answer for both; odd d-count can never be diamagnetic.","traps":["Confusing with [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻: Find the oxidation state first: Mn(II), Fe(III) → d⁵.","CN⁻ strength ignored: CN⁻ forces pairing → inner d orbitals."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"In which of the following order the given complex ions are arranged correctly with respect to their decreasing spin only magnetic moment? (i) [FeF₆]³⁻ (ii) [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ (iii) [NiCl₄]²⁻ (iv) [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","options":["(i) > (iii) > (iv) > (ii)","(ii) > (iii) > (i) > (iv)","(iii) > (iv) > (ii) > (i)","(ii) > (i) > (iii) > (iv)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B),(D) treat [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ as high spin (4 unpaired); (C) treats [FeF₆]³⁻ as low spin.\nWorking: (i) Fe³⁺ d⁵ high spin, 5 unpaired (5.92 BM); (iii) Ni²⁺ d⁸ tetrahedral, 2 unpaired (2.83 BM); (iv) Cu²⁺ d⁹, 1 unpaired (1.73 BM); (ii) Co³⁺ d⁶ low spin with NH₃, 0 unpaired.\nKey idea: F⁻ weak field → high spin Fe³⁺; NH₃ with Co³⁺ → low spin, diamagnetic.","traps":["[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ taken as high spin: Co³⁺ + NH₃ pairs up: d²sp³, zero unpaired.","Wrong d-count for Cu²⁺/Ni²⁺: Write the d-count before counting unpaired electrons."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Number of bridging CO ligands in [Mn₂(CO)₁₀] is _______.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (carrying over Co₂(CO)₈, which has two bridging CO).\nWorking: [Mn₂(CO)₁₀] is two Mn(CO)₅ units joined by a Mn–Mn bond; all 10 CO are terminal.\nKey idea: Mn₂(CO)₁₀: Mn–Mn bond, no bridges; Co₂(CO)₈: two bridging CO.","traps":["Co₂(CO)₈ structure transferred: Only Co₂(CO)₈ has bridging CO."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of stereoisomers possible for [Co(ox)₂(Br)(NH₃)]²⁻ is _______. [ox = oxalate]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 – only cis/trans counted; 4 – trans also counted as chiral; 1 – no isomerism seen.\nWorking: octahedral [M(AA)₂BC]: trans (Br and NH₃ opposite) – achiral, 1; cis – chiral, pair of enantiomers, 2; total 3.\nKey idea: M(AA)₂B₂ and M(AA)₂BC both give 3 stereoisomers (trans + cis d/l).","traps":["Optical isomers of cis missed: Check cis form for a mirror plane – none.","Trans form taken as chiral: Trans has a mirror plane."],"topics":["Optical isomerism","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"According to the valence bond theory the hybridization of central metal atom is dsp² for which one of the following compounds?","options":["NiCl₂·6H₂O","K₂[Ni(CN)₄]","[Ni(CO)₄]","Na₂[NiCl₄]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is an octahedral aqua complex (sp³d²); (C) Ni(0) carbonyl is sp³ tetrahedral – tempting because CO is strong-field; (D) Cl⁻ is weak-field, so [NiCl₄]²⁻ is sp³ tetrahedral – same coordination number 4 as the answer.\nWorking: in [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ Ni²⁺ (3d⁸) pairs its electrons under the strong-field CN⁻ ligand, freeing one 3d orbital, so it is dsp² square planar. NiCl₂·6H₂O is [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (sp³d², octahedral), [Ni(CO)₄] is Ni(0) sp³, [NiCl₄]²⁻ is sp³ tetrahedral.\nKey idea: For Ni²⁺ with CN 4: strong-field CN⁻ gives dsp² square planar, weak-field Cl⁻ gives sp³ tetrahedral.","traps":["Four-coordinate Ni: tetrahedral or square planar: Check ligand field strength: CN⁻ pairs electrons, Cl⁻ does not.","Strong-field CO also present: Ni(0) is d¹⁰ after pairing – no empty 3d, so sp³."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct order of intensity of colors of the compounds is:","options":["[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ > [NiCl₄]²⁻ > [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ > [NiCl₄]²⁻ > [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻","[NiCl₄]²⁻ > [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ > [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻","[NiCl₄]²⁻ > [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ > [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) put [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ high because CN⁻ gives the largest Δ – confusing splitting size with intensity; (B) puts the octahedral aqua complex first because it is the familiar green colour.\nWorking: by the Laporte rule, tetrahedral [NiCl₄]²⁻ has no centre of symmetry, so its d–d bands are much more intense than those of centrosymmetric octahedral [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺; square planar diamagnetic [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is only pale yellow in the visible. (Intensity of colour goes slightly beyond NCERT.)\nKey idea: Intensity depends on how \"allowed\" the transition is: non-centrosymmetric tetrahedral complexes absorb far more strongly than octahedral ones.","traps":["Large Δ taken as intense colour: Δ sets the wavelength, not the intensity.","Geometry and symmetry ignored: Tetrahedral (no inversion centre) → intense; octahedral → weak."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The spin-only magnetic moment value for the complex [Co(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is ________ BM. [At. no. of Co = 27]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (3.87 rounded, high spin with 3 unpaired electrons); 1.73 not entered as integer; 0 if Co is taken as Co³⁺ d⁶.\nWorking: Co²⁺ is 3d⁷; CN⁻ is strong field, so low spin t₂g⁶e_g¹ with one unpaired electron; μ = √(1·3) = 1.73 BM ≈ 2 (nearest integer).\nKey idea: Oxidation state +2 (−4 = x − 6) → d⁷ → low spin in CN⁻ → 1 unpaired → 1.73 BM → 2.","traps":["Oxidation state misread: x − 6 = −4 → Co²⁺.","High-spin assumed: CN⁻ forces pairing."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Spin only magnetic moment of an octahedral complex of Fe²⁺ in the presence of a strong field ligand in BM is :","options":["4.89","2.82","0","3.46"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4.89 = high-spin d⁶ (4 unpaired); (B) 2.83 = 2 unpaired; (D) 3.87 would be 3 unpaired – 3.46 is a made-up value.\nWorking: Fe²⁺ is d⁶; with a strong field ligand Δo > P, giving t₂g⁶ eg⁰, no unpaired electrons, μ = 0.\nKey idea: d⁶ + strong field → t₂g⁶ → diamagnetic.","traps":["High-spin count used: Strong field d⁶ → all six electrons paired in t₂g."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"An aqueous solution of NiCl₂ was heated with excess sodium cyanide in presence of strong oxidizing agent to form [Ni(CN)₆]²⁻. The total change in number of unpaired electrons on metal centre is _______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (comparing [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ with the product), 4 (taking Ni(IV) as high spin and starting from 0), 1 (miscounting d⁷).\nWorking: aqueous Ni²⁺ ([Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺, d⁸) has 2 unpaired electrons; [Ni(CN)₆]²⁻ is Ni(IV), d⁶ low spin with CN⁻ (t₂g⁶), 0 unpaired; change = 2.\nKey idea: Start: Ni²⁺ d⁸ octahedral – always 2 unpaired; end: Ni⁴⁺ d⁶ + CN⁻ → 0.","traps":["Product oxidation state: Ox. state = −2 + 6 = +4.","Initial species: The solution starts as aqueous Ni²⁺ (d⁸, 2 unpaired)."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Coordination number & oxidation state"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which one of the following species responds to an external magnetic field?","options":["[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻","[Co(CN)₆]³⁻","[Ni(CO)₄]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Ni²⁺ d⁸ with CN⁻ is square planar dsp² and diamagnetic – picked if Ni²⁺ is assumed tetrahedral; (C) Co³⁺ d⁶ looks like it has 4 unpaired electrons if CN⁻ strength is ignored; (D) Ni(0) taken as d⁸ 4s² with unpaired electrons.\nWorking: Fe³⁺ (3d⁵) with weak-field H₂O is high spin with 5 unpaired electrons → paramagnetic; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ (d⁸, square planar), [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ (low-spin d⁶) and [Ni(CO)₄] (d¹⁰) are all diamagnetic.\nKey idea: Only Fe³⁺ with weak-field water keeps unpaired electrons; the other three are paired.","traps":["\"Responds to magnetic field\" means paramagnetic: Read it as \"attracted by a field\" = unpaired electrons.","[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ geometry: Ni²⁺ + CN⁻ → dsp², square planar, 0 unpaired."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Three moles of AgCl get precipitated when one mole of an octahedral co-ordination compound with empirical formula CrCl₃·3NH₃·3H₂O reacts with excess of silver nitrate. The number of chloride ions satisfying the secondary valency of the metal ion is ________.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (confusing precipitated Cl with coordinated Cl), 6 (coordination number).\nWorking: all three Cl⁻ are ionisable, so the complex is [Cr(NH₃)₃(H₂O)₃]Cl₃; the six coordination sites (secondary valency) are filled by 3 NH₃ and 3 H₂O, so 0 chlorides.\nKey idea: Precipitated Cl⁻ = counter-ions; coordinated Cl would not precipitate.","traps":["Primary vs secondary valency: Precipitated Cl⁻ satisfies primary valency only."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stability of complexes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which one of the following metal complexes is most stable?","options":["[Co(en)(NH₃)₄]Cl₂","[Co(en)₃]Cl₂","[Co(en)₂(NH₃)₂]Cl₂","[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (A) have fewer chelate rings; (D) has none – chosen if a student thinks NH₃ is stronger than en.\nWorking: all are Co(II) with six N donors; stability rises with the number of chelate rings (chelate effect), so tris(en) with three rings is the most stable.\nKey idea: Same metal, same donor atoms → more chelate rings, more stable.","traps":["Counting chelate rings: Count en ligands: more en = more rings = more stable."],"topics":["Stability of complexes","Chelation"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The type of hybridisation and magnetic property of the complex [MnCl₆]³⁻, respectively, are:","options":["sp³d² and diamagnetic","d²sp³ and diamagnetic","d²sp³ and paramagnetic","sp³d² and paramagnetic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) diamagnetic is impossible for d⁴ (even low-spin t₂g⁴ has 2 unpaired electrons); (C) inner-orbital d²sp³ assumes Cl⁻ forces pairing.\nWorking: Mn³⁺ is d⁴; Cl⁻ is weak-field, so no pairing – outer-orbital sp³d², 4 unpaired electrons, paramagnetic. Option (D).\nKey idea: d⁴ is paramagnetic in any octahedral field; weak-field Cl⁻ → outer orbital sp³d².","traps":["Cl⁻ is weak field: Chloride is low in the spectrochemical series.","d⁴ is never diamagnetic: Low-spin d⁴ still has 2 unpaired."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The number of geometrical isomers found in the metal complexes [PtCl₂(NH₃)₂], [Ni(CO)₄], [Ru(H₂O)₃Cl₃] and [CoCl₂(NH₃)₄]⁺ respectively, are:","options":["1, 1, 1, 1","2, 1, 2, 2","2, 0, 2, 2","2, 1, 2, 1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats every complex as one form; (B) counts the single form of Ni(CO)₄ as \"1 isomer\"; (D) also misses cis/trans of [CoCl₂(NH₃)₄]⁺.\nWorking: square-planar MA₂B₂ – cis/trans = 2; tetrahedral Ni(CO)₄ – no geometrical isomerism = 0; MA₃B₃ – fac/mer = 2; MA₄B₂ – cis/trans = 2. Option (C).\nKey idea: Homoleptic tetrahedral and MA₆ have no geometrical isomers – write 0, not 1.","traps":["Zero vs one for no isomerism: \"Geometrical isomers\" means ≥2 arrangements; none → 0.","fac/mer for MA₃B₃: Recall fac and mer."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of geometrical isomers possible in triamminetrinitrocobalt(III) is X and in trioxalatochromate(III) is Y. Then the value of X + Y is _______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (counting d/l of trioxalato as 2), 3 (adding optical isomers of mer/fac), 1 (missing fac/mer).\nWorking: [Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃] is MA₃B₃ → fac and mer, X = 2; [Cr(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ (M(AA)₃) has only optical isomers, Y = 0; X + Y = 2.\nKey idea: M(AA)₃ shows only optical isomerism.","traps":["Optical ≠ geometrical: Count only cis/trans or fac/mer.","Names to formulae: Write [Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃] first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻, [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ and [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ are d²sp³ hybridised.\nStatement II: [MnCl₆]³⁻ and [FeF₆]³⁻ are paramagnetic and have 4 and 5 unpaired electrons, respectively.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is correct but statement II is false","Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) for those who think oxalate is weak field so [Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ is sp³d²; (C) for those who count Mn³⁺ in CN⁻ as outer orbital.\nWorking: Mn³⁺ d⁴, Fe³⁺ d⁵ with CN⁻ and Co³⁺ d⁶ with oxalate pair up to leave two inner 3d orbitals → d²sp³ (I true); with Cl⁻/F⁻ Mn³⁺ (d⁴) and Fe³⁺ (d⁵) stay high spin with 4 and 5 unpaired electrons (II true).\nKey idea: Co³⁺ is low spin even with oxalate; Mn³⁺/Fe³⁺ high spin with halides.","traps":["Oxalate with Co³⁺ gives low spin: High charge Co³⁺ (d⁶) pairs even with moderate ligands; NCERT says diamagnetic.","Oxidation state first: Charge −3 with six −1 ligands → M³⁺."],"topics":["VBT: hybridisation & geometry","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"3 moles of metal complex with formula Co(en)₂Cl₃ gives 3 moles of silver chloride on treatment with excess of silver nitrate. The secondary valency of Co in the complex is _____. (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (en counted as monodentate), 3 (primary valency), 5.\nWorking: 1 AgCl per formula unit → [Co(en)₂Cl₂]Cl; 2 en (bidentate) + 2 Cl = coordination number 6 = secondary valency.\nKey idea: Secondary valency = CN; en is bidentate.","traps":["Secondary valency is CN, not oxidation state: Primary = oxidation state, secondary = CN.","en is bidentate: Each en gives 2 donor atoms."],"topics":["Werner's theory","Coordination number & oxidation state"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The equivalents of ethylene diamine required to replace the neutral ligands from the coordination sphere of the trans-complex of CoCl₃·4NH₃ is _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (one en per NH₃), 3 (counting a Cl⁻), 1.\nWorking: trans-[CoCl₂(NH₃)₄]Cl has 4 NH₃ (neutral); each en is didentate, so 2 en replace them (here 'equivalents' = moles of en per mole of complex).\nKey idea: Four monodentate NH₃ = four donor sites = two en.","traps":["'trans' as relevant data: Only the number of neutral donor sites matters.","One en per NH₃: en occupies two sites."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ absorbs light of wavelength 498 nm during a d – d transition. The octahedral splitting energy for the above complex is ______ × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ Js; c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.4 or 40 (nm conversion slip), 2 (dividing by 2 or using per-electron half), 240 (kJ mol⁻¹ value ~240 confused).\nWorking: d¹ ion, one d–d transition t₂g → eg, so Δo = hc/λ = 3.99 × 10⁻¹⁹ J → 4.\nKey idea: For a d¹ octahedral ion the single absorption energy equals Δo.","traps":["Δo per mole reported: Answer asked per ion in 10⁻¹⁹ J."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List-I with List-II :\nList-I: (a) [Co(NH₃)₆][Cr(CN)₆] (b) [Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃] (c) [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ (d) cis-[CrCl₂(ox)₂]³⁻\nList-II: (i) Linkage isomerism (ii) Solvate isomerism (iii) Co-ordination isomerism (iv) Optical isomerism\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) labels (a) solvate and (c) coordination – swapped; (D) labels (a) linkage – would need an ambidentate ligand; (B) shifts every pair.\nWorking: (a) cation and anion both complex → ligands can interchange (coordination); (b) NO₂⁻ can bind via N or O (linkage); (c) water inside/outside the sphere (solvate/hydrate); (d) cis bis-chelate octahedral complex is chiral (optical).\nKey idea: Two complex ions → coordination; NO₂/SCN → linkage; H₂O in/out → solvate; cis-M(AA)₂X₂ → optical.","traps":["Coordination vs solvate swapped: Solvate needs water able to move in/out; coordination needs both ions complex.","Optical activity of cis-bis(oxalato): cis-[M(AA)₂X₂] lacks a mirror plane → chiral."],"topics":["Structural isomerism","Optical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Werner's theory","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"On complete reaction of FeCl₃ with oxalic acid in aqueous solution containing KOH, resulted in the formation of product A. The secondary valency of Fe in the product A is ____. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (primary valency / oxidation state +3, or number of oxalate ligands), 4.\nWorking: product A is K₃[Fe(C₂O₄)₃]; three didentate oxalate ligands give coordination number (secondary valency) 6.\nKey idea: Secondary valency = coordination number; 3 didentate oxalates → 6.","traps":["Primary vs secondary valency: Secondary valency = number of donor atoms bound.","Ligand count vs donor atoms: Oxalate is didentate: 3 × 2 = 6."],"topics":["Werner's theory","Coordination number & oxidation state"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Chlorophyll (b) Vitamin-B₁₂ (c) Anticancer drug (d) Grubbs catalyst. List-II: (i) Ruthenium (ii) Platinum (iii) Cobalt (iv) Magnesium. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chlorophyll–Co and B₁₂–Pt swaps; (C) swaps Pt and Ru; (D) swaps B₁₂ and anticancer metals.\nWorking: chlorophyll – Mg; vitamin B₁₂ (cyanocobalamin) – Co; anticancer cis-platin – Pt; Grubbs olefin-metathesis catalyst – Ru.\nKey idea: Fix Mg (chlorophyll) and Co (B₁₂) from NCERT; Pt for cis-platin then leaves Ru for Grubbs.","traps":["Grubbs vs Wilkinson metal: Grubbs = Ru carbene; elimination of sure pairs settles it.","Chlorophyll vs haemoglobin metal: Chlorophyll Mg, haem Fe, B₁₂ Co."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The correct structures of trans-[NiBr₂(PPh₃)₂] and meridional-[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃], respectively, are (square-planar Ni drawings and octahedral Co drawings, ligand positions listed as top / upper-left / upper-right / lower-left / lower-right / bottom):","options":["Ni: Ph₃P and Br opposite pairs (Br trans to Br, PPh₃ trans to PPh₃) and Co: NO₂ at top, upper-right and lower-right; NH₃ at upper-left, lower-left and bottom (fac arrangement)","Ni: both PPh₃ on the left, both Br on the right (cis) and Co: NO₂ at top, upper-left, lower-left; NH₃ at upper-right, lower-right, bottom (fac)","Ni: both PPh₃ on the left, both Br on the right (cis) and Co: NH₃ at top, upper-right, bottom; NO₂ at upper-left, lower-left, lower-right (mer)","Ni: Br trans to Br, PPh₃ trans to PPh₃ and Co: NO₂ at top, upper-right and lower-left; NH₃ at upper-left, lower-right and bottom (mer)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) correct Ni but fac-Co; (B) cis-Ni and fac-Co; (C) cis-Ni with mer-Co.\nWorking: trans-square planar needs the two Br diagonal (options (A) and (D)). In the octahedral drawing upper-right and lower-left are mutually trans, so NO₂ at top + upper-right + lower-left lie on one meridian (mer) – option (D); option (A) puts all three NO₂ mutually cis (fac).\nKey idea: On the drawn octahedron, the diagonal equatorial positions (upper-left/lower-right, upper-right/lower-left) are trans.","traps":["Fac vs mer on perspective drawing: Mer = two of the three ligands trans to each other.","Only one complex correct in an option: Check both halves independently."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The total number of unpaired electrons present in the complex K₃[Cr(oxalate)₃] is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (Cr²⁺/d⁴), 6 (total Cr valence electrons), 0 (assuming pairing by chelate).\nWorking: Cr³⁺ is 3d³; in octahedral d²sp³ (t₂g³) the three electrons stay unpaired regardless of ligand strength: 3.\nKey idea: Charge balance: 3K⁺ + 3 C₂O₄²⁻ → Cr³⁺, d³; d³ never pairs in Oh.","traps":["Wrong oxidation state: 3K⁺ + Cr = 3×(−2) → Cr = +3."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The secondary valency and the number of hydrogen bonded water molecule(s) in CuSO₄·5H₂O, respectively, are :","options":["6 and 4","4 and 1","6 and 5","5 and 1"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) treats all five waters as coordinated (6 from octahedral habit); (A) assumes octahedral Cu with four H-bonded waters; (D) counts all five as coordinated but still one H-bonded.\nWorking: CuSO₄·5H₂O is written [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O – four water molecules on Cu²⁺ and one held by hydrogen bonds. (In the crystal Cu also has two weak contacts to sulphate O, giving distorted octahedral 6-coordination, but no option offers “6 and 1”, so the textbook value 4 is intended.)\nKey idea: Four waters coordinate Cu²⁺; the fifth bridges sulphate and coordinated water by H-bonds.","traps":["Assuming octahedral 6: Learn the hydrate formula [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O.","All water as ligand: One water is lattice water held by H-bonds."],"topics":["Coordination number & oxidation state","Werner's theory"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The Crystal Field Stabilization Energy (CFSE) and magnetic moment (spin-only) of an octahedral aqua complex of a metal ion (Mᶻ⁺) are –0.8 Δ₀ and 3.87 BM, respectively. Identify (Mᶻ⁺):","options":["V³⁺","Cr³⁺","Mn⁴⁺","Co²⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) V³⁺ d² – CFSE –0.8Δ₀ but μ 2.83 BM; (B) Cr³⁺ and (C) Mn⁴⁺ d³ – μ 3.87 BM but CFSE –1.2Δ₀.\nWorking: Co²⁺ is d⁷; in weak-field H₂O it is t₂g⁵e_g², CFSE = –2.0 + 1.2 = –0.8Δ₀ with 3 unpaired electrons (3.87 BM). V³⁺ fits CFSE only; Cr³⁺/Mn⁴⁺ fit μ only.\nKey idea: High-spin d⁷: 5(–0.4) + 2(+0.6) = –0.8Δ₀, three unpaired electrons → √15 = 3.87 BM.","traps":["d³ chosen from 3.87 BM alone: d³ has CFSE –1.2Δ₀; check both data; high-spin d⁷ (t₂g⁵e_g²) gives –0.8Δ₀ with 3 unpaired.","d² matches CFSE but not μ: d² has only 2 unpaired electrons (2.83 BM)."],"topics":["CFSE","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The sum of oxidation states of two silver ions in [Ag(NH₃)₂][Ag(CN)₂] complex is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (taking the whole as neutral Ag); 1 (counting only one Ag); 3 (Ag⁺ + Ag²⁺).\nWorking: [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ has Ag(+1) and [Ag(CN)₂]⁻ has Ag(+1); sum = 2.\nKey idea: Ag in [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ = +1; in [Ag(CN)₂]⁻ = +1; sum = 2.","traps":["Charges of the two ions not balanced: Cation [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ and anion [Ag(CN)₂]⁻; each Ag is +1.","Neutral salt read as Ag(0): The NH₃ complex is cationic and the CN⁻ complex anionic; both Ag are +1."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The theory that can completely/properly explain the nature of bonding in [Ni(CO)₄] is :","options":["Werner's theory","Crystal field theory","Valence bond theory","Molecular orbital theory"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) VBT: explains sp³ tetrahedral, diamagnetic Ni(0), so looks complete. (B) CFT: treats ligands as point charges – cannot handle neutral π-acceptor CO. (A) Werner: only primary/secondary valences.\nWorking: the synergic σ-donation/π-back-donation (stronger Ni–C bond, weaker and longer C–O bond) needs a covalent orbital-overlap treatment; MOT handles it, while CFT (purely ionic) and VBT (no back-bonding, no spectra) do not.\nKey idea: Back-bonding is orbital overlap in both directions – only MO (or ligand field) theory describes it.","traps":["VBT seen as sufficient: Ask whether the theory explains C–O bond weakening (back-bonding): VBT does not.","CFT applied to carbonyl: CFT is purely electrostatic; it cannot explain covalent π bonding with neutral CO."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The IUPAC name of the complex [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl(NH₂CH₃)]Cl is :","options":["Diammine (methanamine) chlorido platinum (II) chloride","Bisammine (methanamine) chlorido platinum (II) chloride","Diamminechlorido (aminomethane) platinum(II) chloride","Diamminechlorido (methanamine) platinum (II) chloride"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): alphabetises 'methanamine' before 'chlorido'. (B): uses 'bis' for a simple ligand. (C): uses old name 'aminomethane'.\nWorking: ligands alphabetically ignoring 'di' – ammine, chlorido, methanamine; Pt oxidation state +2 (one Cl⁻ inside, one outside).\nKey idea: a(mmine) < c(hlorido) < m(ethanamine); 'di' is the prefix for simple ligands.","traps":["Alphabetical order of ligands: Sort ligand names (without di/tri) alphabetically.","bis vs di: bis/tris only for ligands whose names already contain numbers or are complex."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Among the statements (a)-(d), the incorrect ones are-\n(a) Octahedral Co(III) complexes with strong field ligands have very high magnetic moments\n(b) When Δ₀ < P, the d-electron configuration of Co(III) in an octahedral complex is t₂g⁴ e_g²\n(c) Wavelength of light absorbed by [Co(en)₃]³⁺ is lower than that of [CoF₆]³⁻\n(d) If the Δ₀ for an octahedral complex of Co(III) is 18,000 cm⁻¹, the Δt for its tetrahedral complex with the same ligand will be 16,000 cm⁻¹","options":["(a) and (b) only","(c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only","(a) and (d) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) marks (b) wrong by confusing the high-spin filling; (B) marks (c) wrong by linking larger Δ with longer wavelength; (C) combines both slips.\nWorking: (a) false – strong-field Co(III) is low-spin t₂g⁶, diamagnetic; (b) true – high-spin d⁶ is t₂g⁴e_g²; (c) true – en is the stronger-field ligand so absorbs higher energy, shorter λ; (d) false – Δt = (4/9)Δ₀ = 8000 cm⁻¹.\nKey idea: Larger Δ means higher-energy, shorter-wavelength absorption; Δt is only 4/9 of Δ₀.","traps":["Larger Δ thought to absorb longer λ: E = hc/λ: bigger splitting, shorter absorbed λ.","Δt taken as slightly less than Δ₀: Use Δt = (4/9)Δ₀ = 8000 cm⁻¹."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","Spectrochemical series"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The number of possible optical isomers for the complexes MA₂B₂ with sp³ and dsp² hybridised metal atom, respectively, is :\nNote : A and B are unidentate neutral and unidentate monoanionic ligands, respectively","options":["0 and 0","0 and 2","0 and 1","2 and 2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts the two square-planar geometrical isomers (cis/trans) as optical; (C) counts one; (D) assumes tetrahedral MA₂B₂ is chiral like a carbon centre with four groups.\nWorking: tetrahedral MA₂B₂ has a mirror plane (only two kinds of ligand, no stereocentre); square-planar cis and trans MA₂B₂ both lie in a molecular plane of symmetry, so no optical isomers in either case.\nKey idea: A chiral tetrahedral centre needs four different ligands; any planar complex is superimposable on its mirror image.","traps":["Cis/trans counted as optical isomers: Ask whether the mirror image is superimposable; planar complexes always are."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The complex that can show fac- and mer-isomers is :","options":["[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺","[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃]","[CoCl₂(en)₂]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) square planar cis/trans only; (B) Ma₄b₂ cis/trans; (D) M(AA)₂b₂ cis/trans (+ optical).\nWorking: fac/mer isomerism needs octahedral [Ma₃b₃]; only [Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃] fits.\nKey idea: fac/mer ⇔ octahedral Ma₃b₃.","traps":["Any cis/trans complex taken as fac/mer: fac/mer only for Ma₃b₃."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Among (a) – (d) the complexes that can display geometrical isomerism are : (a) [Pt(NH₃)₃Cl]⁺ (b) [Pt(NH₃)Cl₅]⁻ (c) [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl(NO₂)] (d) [Pt(NH₃)₄ClBr]²⁺","options":["(d) and (a)","(a) and (b)","(b) and (c)","(c) and (d)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) each include an MA₃B or MA₅B complex, which cannot have isomers because every position of the single odd ligand is equivalent.\nWorking: (a) square planar MA₃B and (b) octahedral MA₅B have only one arrangement; (c) square planar MA₂BC has cis/trans (NH₃ groups adjacent or opposite); (d) octahedral MA₄BC has cis/trans (Cl and Br adjacent or opposite).\nKey idea: Geometrical isomers need at least two ligands of one kind that can be cis or trans to something else; MA₃B and MA₅B have only one isomer.","traps":["Any mixed-ligand complex assumed isomeric: Check whether two distinct arrangements actually exist."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The correct order of the calculated spin-only magnetic moments of complexes (A) to (D) is: (A) Ni(CO)₄ (B) [Ni(H₂O)₆]Cl₂ (C) Na₂[Ni(CN)₄] (D) PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂","options":["(A) ≈ (C) ≈ (D) < (B)","(A) ≈ (C) < (B) ≈ (D)","(C) < (D) < (B) < (A)","(C) ≈ (D) < (B) < (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats Pd(II) d⁸ like octahedral Ni²⁺ (2 unpaired); (C)/(D) give Ni(CO)₄ a moment by taking Ni²⁺ or ignoring CO pairing.\nWorking: Ni(0) d¹⁰ in Ni(CO)₄ – 0 BM; [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ d⁸ octahedral, 2 unpaired – 2.83 BM; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ d⁸ square planar – 0; PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂ Pd(II) 4d⁸ square planar – 0.\nKey idea: 4d/5d d⁸ ions are always low-spin square planar; Ni(0) d¹⁰ has no unpaired electrons.","traps":["Pd(II) given two unpaired electrons: 4d metals have large Δ – d⁸ is square planar diamagnetic.","Ni oxidation state in Ni(CO)₄ wrong: CO is neutral: Ni(0), d¹⁰."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"[Pd(F)(Cl)(Br)(I)]²⁻ has n number of geometrical isomers. Then, the spin-only magnetic moment and crystal field stabilisation energy [CFSE] of [Fe(CN)₆]ⁿ⁻⁶, respectively, are: [Note: Ignore the pairing energy]","options":["2.84 BM and −1.6 Δ₀","1.73 BM and −2.0 Δ₀","0 BM and −2.4 Δ₀","5.92 BM and 0"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) treats Fe³⁺ as high-spin; (C) assumes d⁶ ([Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, n = 2 isomers); (A) treats as d⁴/two unpaired.\nWorking: square-planar [MABCD] has 3 geometrical isomers, so n = 3 and the complex is [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻: Fe³⁺ d⁵ low-spin t₂g⁵, one unpaired electron → 1.73 BM, CFSE = 5 × (−0.4Δ₀) = −2.0Δ₀.\nKey idea: n = 3 → [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ → low-spin d⁵, one unpaired.","traps":["Miscounting isomers of MABCD: MABCD square planar has 3 isomers.","Taking cyanide complex as high spin: CN⁻ is strong field."],"topics":["CFSE","Geometrical isomerism","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Complex X of composition Cr(H₂O)₆Clₙ has a spin only magnetic moment of 3.83 BM. It reacts with AgNO₃ and shows geometrical isomerism. The IUPAC nomenclature of X is:","options":["Tetraaquadichlorido chromium (III) chloride dihydrate","Hexaaqua chromium (III) chloride","Dichloridotetraaqua chromium (IV) chloride dihydrate","Tetraaquadichlorido chromium(IV) chloride dihydrate"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ shows no geometrical isomerism; (C) and (D) give Cr(IV) (wrong oxidation state; (C) also wrong alphabetical order).\nWorking: 3.83 BM → 3 unpaired → Cr³⁺ (n = 3). AgNO₃ reaction needs ionisable Cl⁻; geometrical isomerism needs [Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]⁺ (cis/trans). So X = [Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]Cl·2H₂O, tetraaquadichloridochromium(III) chloride dihydrate.\nKey idea: 3.83 BM → Cr(III); cis/trans needs an MA₄B₂ ion.","traps":["Wrong oxidation state: μ = √15 = 3.87 → d³ → Cr³⁺.","Alphabetical order of ligands: Aqua precedes chlorido alphabetically."],"topics":["IUPAC naming","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct order of the spin-only magnetic moments of the following complexes is: (I) [Cr(H₂O)₆]Br₂ (II) Na₄[Fe(CN)₆] (III) Na₃[Fe(C₂O₄)₃] (Δ₀ > P) (IV) (Et₄N)₂[CoCl₄]","options":["(III) > (I) > (II) > (IV)","(I) > (IV) > (III) > (II)","(II) ≈ (I) > (IV) > (III)","(III) > (I) > (IV) > (II)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) treat the oxalato Fe³⁺ complex as high spin (5 unpaired) despite Δ₀ > P; (C) treats ferrocyanide as high spin like Cr²⁺ and ignores the strong CN⁻ field.\nWorking: Cr²⁺ d⁴ high spin (H₂O weak) 4 unpaired, 4.90 BM; [CoCl₄]²⁻ Co²⁺ d⁷ tetrahedral 3 unpaired, 3.87 BM; Fe³⁺ d⁵ low spin because Δ₀ > P, 1 unpaired, 1.73 BM; [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ Fe²⁺ d⁶ low spin, 0 BM.\nKey idea: Count unpaired electrons: I = 4, IV = 3, III = 1, II = 0.","traps":["Oxalate taken as weak field so Fe³⁺ high spin: Use the given Δ₀ > P: pairing occurs, t₂g⁵, 1 unpaired.","Wrong oxidation state from counter-ion count: Charge balance first: Cr²⁺, Fe²⁺, Fe³⁺, Co²⁺."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The isomer(s) of [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂] that has/have a Cl–Co–Cl angle of 90°, is/are:","options":["meridional and trans","cis and trans","trans only","cis only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) mer is not applicable to MA₄B₂; (B) trans has 180°; (C) reverse of the correct.\nWorking: in octahedral MA₄B₂ the cis isomer has adjacent Cl (90°), the trans isomer opposite Cl (180°); mer/fac apply only to MA₃B₃.\nKey idea: cis = 90°, trans = 180°.","traps":["mer/fac applied to MA₄B₂: fac/mer only for three identical ligands."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"For octahedral Mn(II) and tetrahedral Ni(II) complexes, consider the following statements: (I) both the complexes can be high spin. (II) Ni(II) complex can very rarely be low spin. (III) with strong field ligands, Mn(II) complexes can be low spin. (IV) aqueous solution of Mn(II) ions is yellow in color. The correct statements are:","options":["(I), (III) and (IV) only","(II), (III) and (IV) only","(I), (II) and (III) only","(I) and (II) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) contain (IV) — Mn²⁺(aq) is pink (NCERT table), not yellow; (D) omits (III), catching students who think d⁵ Mn(II) is always high spin.\nWorking: (I) true — tetrahedral complexes are high spin and octahedral Mn(II) d⁵ is high spin with weak ligands; (II) true — tetrahedral Ni(II) is essentially never low spin; (III) true — e.g. [Mn(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is low spin (1 unpaired e⁻); (IV) false — Mn²⁺(aq) is pale pink.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral → always high spin; octahedral d⁴–d⁷ can be low spin with strong field ligands.","traps":["Mn(II) always high spin: Strong ligands such as CN⁻ can pair d⁵ electrons.","Colour of Mn²⁺(aq): Mn²⁺ pale pink; Fe³⁺ yellow."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Consider that a d⁶ metal ion (M²⁺) forms a complex with aqua ligands, and the spin only magnetic moment of the complex is 4.90 BM. The geometry and the crystal field stabilization energy of the complex is:","options":["tetrahedral and –1.6 Δt + 1P","tetrahedral and –0.6 Δt","octahedral and –1.6 Δ₀","octahedral and –2.4 Δ₀ + 2P"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) –1.6 Δ₀ is octahedral d⁴ low spin/miscount; (D) –2.4 Δ₀ + 2P is low-spin d⁶ (0 unpaired) contradicting 4.90 BM; (A) adds a pairing term that is not needed for e³t₂³.\nWorking: 4.90 BM → 4 unpaired e⁻ (high spin d⁶). Octahedral high spin t₂g⁴e_g² gives –0.4 Δ₀ (not offered); tetrahedral e³t₂³ gives 3(–0.6) + 3(+0.4) = –0.6 Δt with no extra pairing beyond the free ion. Only (B) is consistent.\nKey idea: When the \"obvious\" octahedral value is missing, compute both geometries and match the magnetic moment.","traps":["Aqua complex assumed octahedral: Check whether any octahedral option equals –0.4 Δ₀; it does not.","Low-spin CFSE used despite 4 unpaired e⁻: Match unpaired electrons to the configuration first."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Simplified absorption spectra of three complexes ((i), (ii) and (iii)) of Mⁿ⁺ ion are provided below; their λmax values are marked as A, B and C respectively. The correct match between the complexes and their λmax values is :\n(i) [M(NCS)₆]⁽⁻⁶⁺ⁿ⁾\n(ii) [MF₆]⁽⁻⁶⁺ⁿ⁾\n(iii) [M(NH₃)₆]ⁿ⁺","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) matches λ directly with field strength (forgets inverse relation); (A) and (C) misplace NCS⁻ in the series.\nWorking: field strength F⁻ < NCS⁻ < NH₃, so Δo rises in that order and λmax falls: NH₃ shortest (A), NCS⁻ middle (B), F⁻ longest (C).\nKey idea: Stronger ligand → larger Δo → shorter λmax.","traps":["λ taken as proportional to Δo: E = hc/λ; larger Δo means smaller λ.","NCS⁻ placed wrongly: Memorise F⁻ < NCS⁻ < NH₃."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep02S2C10-p.webp"],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The one that is not expected to show isomerism is :","options":["[Ni(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺","[Ni(NH₃)₂Cl₂]","[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]","[Ni(en)₃]²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) looks the same as (B) but Pt(II) is square planar (cis/trans); (A) and (D) are octahedral with cis/trans and optical isomers.\nWorking: [Ni(NH₃)₂Cl₂] (Ni²⁺, d⁸, weak-field Cl⁻, sp³) is tetrahedral – no geometrical or optical isomers. (A) octahedral MA₄B₂ has cis/trans; (C) square planar Pt(II) MA₂B₂ has cis/trans; (D) M(AA)₃ is optically active.\nKey idea: 4d/5d d⁸ ions are square planar; Ni(II) with weak-field ligands is tetrahedral.","traps":["Ni(II) assumed square planar like Pt(II): Check metal row and ligand field.","'Not' wording: Mark each as shows / does not show."],"topics":["Geometrical isomerism","VBT: hybridisation & geometry"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The complex that can show optical activity is:","options":["trans-[Fe(NH₃)₂(CN)₄]⁻","cis-[Fe(NH₃)₂(CN)₄]⁻","cis-[CrCl₂(ox)₂]³⁻ (ox = oxalate)","trans-[Cr(Cl₂)(ox)₂]³⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) Ma₂b₄ complexes – both isomers have a mirror plane; picking cis-(B) assumes \"cis = chiral\"; (D) trans-[M(AA)₂X₂] has a mirror plane through both Cl.\nWorking: cis-[M(AA)₂X₂] has no mirror plane and is chiral; trans-[M(AA)₂X₂] has a mirror plane; [Ma₂b₄] (cis or trans) always has a mirror plane, so is achiral.\nKey idea: Chirality needs a didentate ligand pattern without a mirror plane: cis-bis(chelate) complexes.","traps":["Any cis complex taken as optically active: Look for a mirror plane; Ma₂b₄ always has one.","Trans bis-chelate thought chiral: In trans-[M(AA)₂X₂] the plane holding both chelate rings is a mirror plane."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The electronic spectrum of [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺ shows a single broad peak with a maximum at 20,300 cm⁻¹. The crystal field stabilization energy (CFSE) of the complex ion, in kJ mol⁻¹, is:","options":["242.5","83.7","145.5","97"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 242.5 = Δo itself; (C) 145.5 = 0.6Δo (e_g factor); (B) 83.7 = 1 kJ mol⁻¹ expressed in cm⁻¹ (conversion factor).\nWorking: Δo = 20300 cm⁻¹ × 11.96 J mol⁻¹ per cm⁻¹ ≈ 242.8 kJ mol⁻¹; d¹ → CFSE = −0.4Δo ≈ −97 kJ mol⁻¹ (magnitude 97).\nKey idea: Absorption maximum = Δo; d¹ CFSE = 0.4Δo.","traps":["Δo reported as CFSE: Multiply by 0.4 for t₂g¹.","Using 0.6 instead of 0.4: t₂g lowered by 0.4Δo."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The d-electron configuration of [Ru(en)₃]Cl₂ and [Fe(H₂O)₆]Cl₂, respectively are :","options":["t₂g⁴ e_g² and t₂g⁶ e_g⁰","t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ and t₂g⁶ e_g⁰","t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ and t₂g⁴ e_g²","t₂g⁴ e_g² and t₂g⁴ e_g²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ru high spin – treats 4d like 3d; (B) Fe²⁺ with H₂O low spin – H₂O is weak field; (D) both high spin.\nWorking: Ru²⁺ is 4d⁶; 4d metals have large Δₒ and en is strong, so low spin t₂g⁶ e_g⁰. Fe²⁺ 3d⁶ with weak-field H₂O → high spin t₂g⁴ e_g².\nKey idea: Second/third-row transition metals almost always form low-spin complexes.","traps":["4d metal treated as 3d: Δₒ grows ~50% from 3d to 4d; Ru²⁺ is low spin.","Water treated as strong field: H₂O is weak field for Fe²⁺: high spin."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Complex A has a composition of H₁₂O₆Cl₃Cr. If the complex on treatment with conc. H₂SO₄ loses 13.5% of its original mass, the correct molecular formula of A is :\n[Given : atomic mass of Cr = 52 amu and Cl = 35 amu]","options":["[Cr(H₂O)₅Cl]Cl₂·H₂O","[Cr(H₂O)₃Cl₃]·3H₂O","[Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]Cl·2H₂O","[Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1 H₂O → 6.8% loss; (B) 3 H₂O → 20.4%; (D) no lattice water → 0% (coordinated water not lost).\nWorking: M = 52 + 3×35 + 6×18 = 265; 13.5% of 265 = 35.8 ≈ 2 × 18, so two water molecules are outside the sphere (removed by conc. H₂SO₄) → [Cr(H₂O)₄Cl₂]Cl·2H₂O.\nKey idea: Only lattice water is removed by conc. H₂SO₄; mass loss counts it.","traps":["Coordinated water counted as lost: Dehydrating agent removes lattice water only."],"topics":["Structural isomerism","Werner's theory"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The number of isomers possible for [Pt(en)(NO₂)₂] is :","options":["3","2","1","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts only all-N vs all-O; (C) sees no isomerism (only geometric checked); (D) adds a non-existent cis/trans pair.\nWorking: square planar [M(AA)b₂] has no geometrical or optical isomers; ambidentate NO₂⁻ gives linkage isomers: (NO₂)₂, (NO₂)(ONO), (ONO)₂ = 3.\nKey idea: en must span cis positions; only linkage isomers remain.","traps":["Missing mixed NO₂/ONO isomer: Each NO₂ independently can bind N or O.","Geometric isomers assumed: Didentate en forces cis; no cis/trans."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The pair in which both the species have the same magnetic moment (spin only) is :","options":["[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [CoCl₄]²⁻","[Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Co(OH)₄]²⁻ and [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5 vs 4; (B) 4 vs 3; (D) 3 vs 4 (if [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺ wrongly taken low spin it becomes 0).\nWorking: Mn²⁺ d⁵ HS 5 unpaired; Cr²⁺ d⁴ HS 4; [CoCl₄]²⁻ d⁷ tetrahedral 3; Fe²⁺ d⁶ HS (H₂O) 4; [Co(OH)₄]²⁻ 3; [Fe(NH₃)₆]²⁺ HS 4. Only (C) is 4 and 4.\nKey idea: Cr²⁺ d⁴ and Fe²⁺ d⁶ high spin both have 4 unpaired.","traps":["d⁴ and d⁶ high spin not matched: d⁴ HS and d⁶ HS both have 4 unpaired.","NH₃ treated as strong field for Fe²⁺: Even so, (D) never matches: 3 vs 4 (or 0)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The crystal field stabilization energy (CFSE) of [CoF₃(H₂O)₃] (Δ₀ < P) is :","options":["−0.8 Δ₀","−0.4 Δ₀ + P","−0.8 Δ₀ + 2P","−0.4 Δ₀"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) low-spin-like or miscount (−0.8); (B) adds P although the pair exists in free ion too; (C) −0.8Δ₀ + 2P uses a miscounted CFSE and adds pairing energy that high-spin d⁶ does not need.\nWorking: Co³⁺ d⁶, Δ₀ < P → high spin t₂g⁴e_g²; CFSE = 4(−0.4) + 2(0.6) = −0.4 Δ₀. The single pair is also present in the free ion, so no extra P term.\nKey idea: Count only pairs created beyond those in the free ion; high-spin d⁶ has the same one pair as the free ion.","traps":["Adding P for the paired electron: Compare pairs with the free-ion configuration.","Treating as low spin: Given Δ₀ < P → high spin."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The one that can exhibit highest paramagnetic behaviour among the following is : gly = glycinato; bpy = 2,2'-bipyridine","options":["[Pd(gly)₂]","[Ti(NH₃)₆]³⁺","[Co(OX)₂(OH)₂]⁻ (Δ₀ > P)","[Fe(en)(bpy)(NH₃)₂]²⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) one unpaired electron – chosen if Co is taken as +3 (d⁶ low spin, 0); (A) Pd(II) square planar diamagnetic; (D) low-spin d⁶ diamagnetic.\nWorking: in [Co(ox)₂(OH)₂]⁻ Co is +5 (2×(−2) + 2×(−1) + x = −1), d⁴; low spin t₂g⁴ → 2 unpaired. Ti³⁺ d¹ → 1; Pd²⁺ d⁸ square planar → 0; Fe²⁺ d⁶ with en/bpy (strong field) → 0.\nKey idea: Always compute oxidation state from ligand charges: ox²⁻ and OH⁻ make Co +5.","traps":["Assuming Co(III): Sum ligand charges: −4 −2 + x = −1 → x = +5.","Pd(II) taken as tetrahedral: 4d/5d d⁸ are always square planar, diamagnetic."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Coordination number & oxidation state"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The molecule in which hybrid MOs involve only one d-orbital of the central atom is :","options":["[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻","[CrF₆]³⁻","BrF₅","XeF₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) d²sp³ (two inner d); (C)/(D) sp³d² (two outer d).\nWorking: [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is dsp² (one d orbital); [CrF₆]³⁻ d²sp³, BrF₅ and XeF₄ sp³d² – two d orbitals each.\nKey idea: dsp² uses one d orbital; sp³d one; sp³d² and d²sp³ two.","traps":["sp³d² counted as one d: sp³d² = two d orbitals."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The values of the crystal field stabilization energies for a high spin d⁶ metal ion in octahedral and tetrahedral fields, respectively, are :","options":["–0.4 Δ₀ and –0.27 Δt","–1.6 Δ₀ and –0.4 Δt","–0.4 Δ₀ and –0.6 Δt","–2.4 Δ₀ and –0.6 Δt"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) converts tetrahedral to Δ₀ units wrongly (–0.6 × 4/9 ≈ –0.27); (B) uses low-spin octahedral wrongly / mixed; (D) low-spin d⁶ octahedral value –2.4Δ₀.\nWorking: octahedral HS d⁶ = t₂g⁴eg²: 4(–0.4) + 2(+0.6) = –0.4Δ₀; tetrahedral d⁶ = e³t₂³: 3(–0.6) + 3(+0.4) = –0.6Δt (pairing energy ignored).\nKey idea: Use –0.4/+0.6 for octahedral and –0.6/+0.4 for tetrahedral levels.","traps":["Low-spin CFSE used for octahedral: Stem says high spin: t₂g⁴eg².","Δt expressed in Δ₀ units: Answer is asked in Δt; no conversion needed."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The total number of coordination sites in ethylenediaminetetraacetate (EDTA⁴⁻) is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (carboxylates only), 2 (amines only), 10 (all heteroatoms counted).\nWorking: EDTA⁴⁻ binds through two N and four carboxylate O atoms – hexadentate.\nKey idea: Two N + four O = 6.","traps":["Only carboxylates counted: Add the two amine N donors."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Consider the complex ions, trans-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ (A) and cis-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ (B). The correct statement regarding them is:","options":["both (A) and (B) can be optically active","both (A) and (B) cannot be optically active","(A) can be optically active, but (B) cannot be optically active","(A) cannot be optically active, but (B) can be optically active"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) trans thought chiral; (B) cis thought achiral; (C) reversed.\nWorking: trans-[Co(en)₂Cl₂]⁺ has a mirror plane (achiral); the cis isomer lacks one and exists as d/l enantiomers.\nKey idea: cis-bis(chelate) = chiral; trans = mirror plane.","traps":["Trans/cis reversed: Look for the mirror plane in the trans form."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Considering that Δ₀ > P, the magnetic moment (in BM) of [Ru(H₂O)₆]²⁺ would be ______.","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4.90 BM (high-spin d⁶ assumed because H₂O is a weak-field ligand), 2.83 BM (wrong d count, e.g. Ru²⁺ taken as d⁸ or d⁴ low spin miscounted), 5.92 (d⁵).\nWorking: Ru²⁺ is 4d⁶; with Δ₀ > P (also typical of 4d metals) the configuration is t₂g⁶ e_g⁰ – no unpaired electrons → μ = 0 BM.\nKey idea: Δ₀ > P means pair up: d⁶ → t₂g⁶, diamagnetic.","traps":["Water assumed weak field: Use the given Δ₀ > P, and recall 4d metals are low spin anyway.","Ru²⁺ d-count: Ru²⁺ = 4d⁶."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The species that has a spin only magnetic moment of 5.9 BM, is -","options":["Ni(CO)₄ (T_d)","[MnBr₄]²⁻ (T_d)","[NiCl₄]²⁻ (T_d)","[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ (square planar)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) tetrahedral Ni²⁺ d⁸ is paramagnetic but only 2 unpaired (2.83 BM); (A) Ni(0) d¹⁰ diamagnetic; (D) strong-field CN⁻ square planar, diamagnetic.\nWorking: 5.9 BM = √35 → 5 unpaired electrons; Mn²⁺ d⁵ with weak-field Br⁻ in tetrahedral geometry has 5 unpaired. Ni(CO)₄ (d¹⁰) 0; [NiCl₄]²⁻ d⁸ tetrahedral 2 (2.83 BM); [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ square planar 0.\nKey idea: 5.9 BM ↔ n = 5 ↔ high-spin d⁵ (Mn²⁺/Fe³⁺).","traps":["Any paramagnetic Ni complex chosen: Compute √(n(n+2)): n = 2 gives 2.83, not 5.9."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"For a d⁴ metal ion in an octahedral field, the correct electronic configuration is:","options":["t₂g⁴ e_g⁰ when Δo < P","e_g² t₂g² when Δo < P","t₂g³ e_g¹ when Δo < P","t₂g³ e_g¹ when Δo > P"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) swap the conditions; (B) puts e_g below t₂g (tetrahedral-type ordering) in an octahedral field.\nWorking: weak field (Δo < P) – fourth electron goes to e_g: t₂g³e_g¹; strong field (Δo > P) gives t₂g⁴e_g⁰.\nKey idea: Δo < P → high spin (spread out); Δo > P → low spin (pair in t₂g).","traps":["Condition swapped: Large Δo forces pairing; small Δo allows spreading.","Tetrahedral ordering used: In octahedral field t₂g is lower."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VBT: magnetism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The correct order of the spin-only magnetic moment of metal ions in the following low spin complexes, [V(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, [Ru(NH₃)₆]³⁺, and [Cr(NH₃)₆]²⁺, is :","options":["V²⁺ > Cr²⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Fe²⁺","V²⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Cr²⁺ > Fe²⁺","Cr²⁺ > V²⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Fe²⁺","Cr²⁺ > Ru³⁺ > Fe²⁺ > V²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) use high-spin Cr²⁺ (4 unpaired); (B) treats Ru³⁺ as having more unpaired than Cr²⁺.\nWorking: low spin – V²⁺ d³ (3 unpaired), Cr²⁺ d⁴ (2), Ru³⁺ d⁵ (1), Fe²⁺ d⁶ (0).\nKey idea: t₂g filling: d³ 3, d⁴ 2, d⁵ 1, d⁶ 0.","traps":["Cr²⁺ taken high spin: Stem says low spin; fill t₂g first.","Wrong oxidation state of metal: V in [V(CN)₆]⁴⁻ is +2."],"topics":["VBT: magnetism","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The compound that inhibits the growth of tumors is:","options":["cis-[Pd(Cl)₂(NH₃)₂]","cis-[Pt(Cl)₂(NH₃)₂]","trans-[Pt(Cl)₂(NH₃)₂]","trans-[Pd(Cl)₂(NH₃)₂]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) Pd analogues – wrong metal; (C) transplatin – inactive isomer.\nWorking: cisplatin, cis-[PtCl₂(NH₃)₂], is the antitumour drug.\nKey idea: Cis isomer of the Pt complex.","traps":["Trans isomer chosen: Remember the name: cis-platin.","Pd confused with Pt: Platinum is the drug metal."],"topics":["Applications","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The degenerate orbitals of [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ are :","options":["d_yz and d_z²","d_z² and d_xz","d_xz and d_yz","d_x²−y² and d_xy"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) mix one t₂g with one e_g orbital; (D) d_x²−y² (e_g) with d_xy (t₂g) – the two 'in-plane' orbitals look alike but split apart.\nWorking: in an octahedral field the d set splits into t₂g (d_xy, d_yz, d_xz) and e_g (d_x²−y², d_z²); only d_xz and d_yz are in the same set among the options.\nKey idea: Axial orbitals (d_x²−y², d_z²) form e_g; between-axes orbitals (d_xy, d_yz, d_xz) form t₂g.","traps":["d_x²−y² and d_xy treated as a pair: Classify by lobes on axes vs between axes.","Cr³⁺ d³ configuration distracts: Degeneracy depends on field symmetry, not electron count."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Optical isomerism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The one that will show optical activity is : (en = ethane-1,2-diamine)","options":["trans-[M(en)₂A₂]: two en spanning the equatorial plane, A above and below","mer-[MA₃B₃]: A at top, A and A trans in the plane, B trans in the plane, B at bottom","[MA₂B₂(en)]: en spanning two cis positions, the two A cis (top and in-plane) and the two B cis (bottom and in-plane)","trans-[MA₂B₄]: A above and below, four B in the plane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) trans-bis(en) – plane contains both A; (B) mer arrangement has a plane; (D) trans-MA₂B₄ highly symmetric.\nWorking: (C) cis,cis-[MA₂B₂(en)] has no mirror plane and is chiral; (A) trans-[M(en)₂A₂], (B) mer-MA₃B₃ and (D) trans-MA₂B₄ all have a mirror plane.\nKey idea: With en + two A cis + two B cis, every possible plane is broken.","traps":["Every en complex assumed chiral: Check for a mirror plane, not for en.","Misreading 3-D perspective drawing: Redraw with explicit trans pairs."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The number of water molecule(s) not coordinated to copper ion directly in CuSO₄·5H₂O, is :","options":["4","3","1","2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4 is the number coordinated, not uncoordinated; (B)/(D) arbitrary splits.\nWorking: CuSO₄·5H₂O = [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O; four waters coordinate Cu²⁺, the fifth is held by H-bonds to sulfate and coordinated water.\nKey idea: [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O – one lattice water.","traps":["Coordinated count given instead of non-coordinated: Underline 'not'.","Assuming all five waters bound to Cu: Sulfate O atoms occupy the axial sites; one water is lattice water."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ligands & denticity","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The maximum possible denticities of a ligand given below towards a common transition and inner-transition metal ion, respectively, are : [Ligand: diethylenetriaminepentaacetate (DTPA⁵⁻), (⁻OOCCH₂)₂N–CH₂CH₂–N(CH₂COO⁻)–CH₂CH₂–N(CH₂COO⁻)₂: 3 N donors and 5 carboxylate donors]","options":["6 and 8","8 and 6","8 and 8","6 and 6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 8 and 8 counts donor atoms only; (D) 6 and 6 treats f-block like d-block; (B) reverses the two.\nWorking: the ligand has 8 donor atoms (3 N + 5 O); a common transition metal ion usually has maximum coordination number 6, so denticity 6, while larger lanthanide/actinide ions accommodate CN 8, so 8.\nKey idea: Max denticity = min(donor atoms, metal coordination number): 6 for 3d ions, 8 for 4f/5f ions.","traps":["Counting all donor atoms only: Cap by metal coordination number.","Order of the pair reversed: Transition first, inner-transition second."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VBT: hybridisation & geometry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The correct statements among I to III are : (I) Valence bond theory cannot explain the color exhibited by transition metal complexes. (II) Valence bond theory can predict quantitatively the magnetic properties of transition metal complexes. (III) Valence bond theory cannot distinguish ligands as weak and strong field ones.","options":["(I) and (II) only","(I), (II) and (III)","(I) and (III) only","(II) and (III) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) accept II, which reverses an NCERT limitation; VBT does predict number of unpaired electrons qualitatively.\nWorking: NCERT limitations of VBT: no explanation of colour (I true), no quantitative interpretation of magnetic data (II false), does not distinguish weak and strong ligands (III true).\nKey idea: VBT gives qualitative magnetism (number of unpaired e⁻), not quantitative.","traps":["Qualitative vs quantitative magnetism: NCERT: no quantitative interpretation of magnetic data."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The species that can have a trans-isomer is : (en = ethane-1,2-diamine, ox = oxalate)","options":["[Pt(en)Cl₂]","[Cr(en)₂(ox)]⁺","[Zn(en)Cl₂]","[Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²⁺"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) square planar MA₂(AA): the chelate fixes the two Cl cis; (B) M(AA)₂(BB) gives only cis-like arrangement and optical isomers; (C) tetrahedral – no cis/trans.\nWorking: [Pt(en)₂Cl₂]²⁺ is octahedral M(AA)₂B₂ – Cl⁻ can be cis or trans. Square-planar [Pt(en)Cl₂] forces both Cl cis (en spans only cis sites); tetrahedral [Zn(en)Cl₂] has no geometrical isomers; [Cr(en)₂(ox)]⁺ has three bidentate ligands – only optical isomers.\nKey idea: Chelating ligands span adjacent sites, so trans needs two monodentate ligands across an M(AA)₂ frame.","traps":["Square-planar Pt(en)Cl₂ assumed to have trans form: The bidentate en takes two cis sites, leaving only cis Cl.","Tetrahedral Zn complex assumed to isomerise: d¹⁰ Zn²⁺ with 4 ligands is tetrahedral: no geometrical isomers."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Three complexes, [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺ (I), [Co(NH₃)₅H₂O]³⁺ (II) and [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ (III) absorb light in the visible region. The correct order of the wavelength of light absorbed by them is :","options":["(III) > (I) > (II)","(I) > (II) > (III)","(II) > (I) > (III)","(III) > (II) > (I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) gives the Δₒ order instead of λ order; (A), (C) misplace Cl⁻ relative to H₂O.\nWorking: field strength Cl⁻ < H₂O < NH₃, so Δₒ: I < II < III; λ absorbed ∝ 1/Δₒ ⇒ I > II > III.\nKey idea: Weaker field ligand ⇒ smaller Δ ⇒ longer λ absorbed.","traps":["Energy order given instead of wavelength order: λ = hc/Δ: invert the Δ order.","Cl⁻ placed above H₂O: Spectrochemical series: halides < H₂O < NH₃."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The INCORRECT statement is:","options":["the spin-only magnetic moments of [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [Cr(H₂O)₆]²⁺ are nearly similar.","the spin-only magnetic moment of [Ni(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ is 2.83 BM.","the gemstone, ruby, has Cr³⁺ ions occupying the octahedral sites of beryl.","the color of [CoCl(NH₃)₅]²⁺ is violet as it absorbs the yellow light."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks false because Fe and Cr differ, but both have 4 unpaired e⁻; (B) Ni(II) octahedral always 2 unpaired; (D) correct complementary colour.\nWorking: Ruby is Al₂O₃ with Cr³⁺ at Al³⁺ sites; Cr³⁺ in beryl is emerald. (A) Fe²⁺ d⁶ HS and Cr²⁺ d⁴ HS both 4 unpaired (4.90 BM); (B) Ni²⁺ d⁸ 2 unpaired = 2.83 BM; (D) violet is complementary to yellow.\nKey idea: Beryl hosts Cr³⁺ in emerald, not ruby.","traps":["Ruby and emerald hosts swapped: Ruby = Al₂O₃; emerald = beryl.","Different metals assumed to give different moments: Count unpaired electrons: both 4 (high spin)."],"topics":["Colour of complexes","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The crystal field stabilization energy (CFSE) of [Fe(H₂O)₆]Cl₂ and K₂[NiCl₄], respectively, are:","options":["–0.4Δₒ and –0.8Δₜ","–0.4Δₒ and –1.2Δₜ","–2.4Δₒ and –1.2Δₜ","–0.6Δₒ and –0.8Δₜ"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) –2.4Δₒ assumes low-spin Fe²⁺ (H₂O as strong field); (B) –1.2Δₜ from counting only e electrons or wrong t₂ weight; (D) –0.6Δₒ from miscounting eg electrons.\nWorking: Fe²⁺ d⁶ high spin t₂g⁴e_g²: 4(–0.4) + 2(0.6) = –0.4Δₒ. Ni²⁺ d⁸ tetrahedral e⁴t₂⁴: 4(–0.6) + 4(0.4) = –0.8Δₜ.\nKey idea: H₂O is weak field for Fe²⁺; tetrahedral e = –0.6Δₜ, t₂ = +0.4Δₜ.","traps":["Low spin assumed for aqua Fe²⁺: H₂O is weak field with Fe²⁺: high spin.","Tetrahedral weights mixed with octahedral: Tetrahedral: e at –0.6Δₜ, t₂ at +0.4Δₜ."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"CFSE","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The complex ion that will lose its crystal field stabilization energy upon oxidation of its metal to +3 state is (Phen = 1,10-phenanthroline, and ignore pairing energy)","options":["[Fe(phen)₃]²⁺","[Zn(phen)₃]²⁺","[Ni(phen)₃]²⁺","[Co(phen)₃]²⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Co²⁺→Co³⁺ actually gains CFSE (t₂g⁶); (C) Ni²⁺→Ni³⁺ gains; (B) Zn²⁺ d¹⁰ has no CFSE to lose and no +3 state.\nWorking: phen is strong field. Fe²⁺ d⁶ low spin t₂g⁶ CFSE −2.4Δo → Fe³⁺ d⁵ t₂g⁵ −2.0Δo (CFSE decreases). Co²⁺ d⁷ (−1.8Δo) → Co³⁺ d⁶ (−2.4Δo) gains; Ni²⁺ d⁸ (−1.2Δo) → Ni³⁺ d⁷ (−1.8Δo) gains; Zn²⁺ d¹⁰ has zero CFSE and no accessible +3. \"Lose\" is read as \"decrease\".\nKey idea: Low-spin d⁶ (t₂g⁶) is the CFSE maximum; any change from d⁶ reduces it.","traps":["Assuming Co²⁺ loses CFSE: Write t₂g/eg occupancies for both oxidation states.","Treating phen as weak field: Phen and bipy are strong-field N-donors."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Complete removal of both the axial ligands (along the z-axis) from an octahedral complex leads to which of the following splitting patterns? (relative orbital energies not on scale).","options":["d(x²−y²) > d(xy) > d(z²) > d(xz), d(yz)","d(z²) > d(x²−y²) > d(xz), d(yz) > d(xy)","d(x²−y²) > d(z²) > d(yz), d(xz) > d(xy)","d(x²−y²) > d(z²) > d(xy) > d(xz), d(yz)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) partial tetragonal elongation ordering with d(z²) still above d(xy); (C) puts d(xy) lowest; (B) puts d(z²) highest (z-compression).\nWorking: removing both z-ligands gives a square-planar field; d(x²−y²) (pointing at ligands) is highest, then d(xy) (in-plane), then d(z²), with d(xz)/d(yz) lowest.\nKey idea: With no z-ligands, in-plane orbitals rise and all z-component orbitals fall.","traps":["d(z²) vs d(xy) order: Remember square-planar: x²−y² ≫ xy > z² > xz,yz.","Elongation vs compression: Removing z-ligands lowers every orbital with z character."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The compound used in the treatment of lead poisoning is :","options":["EDTA","Cis-platin","D-penicillamine","desferrioxime B"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) cis-platin: anticancer; (C) D-penicillamine: excess copper; (D) desferrioxime B: excess iron.\nWorking: NCERT states EDTA is used in the treatment of lead poisoning; D-penicillamine and desferrioxime B remove excess Cu/Fe; cis-platin is anticancer.\nKey idea: Pb → EDTA; Cu/Fe → D-penicillamine/desferrioxime B.","traps":["Chelation drugs mixed up: Pair each drug with its metal: Pb–EDTA, Cu/Fe–penicillamine/desferrioxime."],"topics":["Applications","Chelation"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The coordination numbers of Co and Al in [Co(Cl)(en)₂]Cl and K₃[Al(C₂O₄)₃], respectively, are : (en = ethane-1,2-diamine)","options":["3 and 3","6 and 6","5 and 6","5 and 3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) count ligands instead of donor atoms; (B) assumes Co is always 6-coordinate.\nWorking: Co: 1 Cl + 2 bidentate en = 5; Al: 3 bidentate oxalate = 6.\nKey idea: Count donor atoms in the bracket only.","traps":["Counting ligands, not donor atoms: Multiply each ligand by its denticity.","Assuming Co(III) is always 6-coordinate: Count what is written; outer Cl⁻ is not coordinated."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colour of complexes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Two complexes [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ (A) and [Cr(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ (B) are violet and yellow coloured, respectively. The incorrect statement regarding them is :","options":["Δ₀ value of (A) is less than that of (B).","Δ₀ value of (A) and (B) are calculated from the energies of violet and yellow light, respectively","Both absorb energies corresponding to their complementary colors.","Both are paramagnetic with three unpaired electrons."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student thinks violet light is higher energy so A has larger Δ₀. (C): true. (D): student forgets Cr³⁺ is d³ in both (3 unpaired regardless of field).\nWorking: A (violet) absorbs yellow light and B (yellow) absorbs violet light, so Δ₀ of A comes from yellow and of B from violet – statement (B) has them reversed. Δ₀(A) < Δ₀(B) since NH₃ > H₂O; both Cr³⁺ d³ with 3 unpaired e⁻.\nKey idea: The colour you see is the complement of what is absorbed; Δ₀ follows the absorbed light.","traps":["Observed colour used as absorbed colour: Δ₀ comes from the absorbed (complementary) light: violet complex absorbs yellow.","Δ₀ order guessed from colours: Use spectrochemical series: NH₃ > H₂O."],"topics":["Colour of complexes","Spectrochemical series"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The complex that has highest crystal field splitting energy (Δ), is:","options":["K₃[Co(CN)₆]","[Co(NH₃)₅(H₂O)]Cl₃","K₂[CoCl₄]","[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NH₃/H₂O mixed – medium field, lower than CN⁻. (C) [CoCl₄]²⁻: weak Cl⁻, Co(II), tetrahedral – smallest. (D) NH₃ + Cl⁻ – Cl⁻ lowers Δ.\nWorking: all are Co(III) octahedral except tetrahedral [CoCl₄]²⁻ (Co²⁺, small Δt); CN⁻ is the strongest-field ligand, so [Co(CN)₆]³⁻ has the largest Δo.\nKey idea: Same metal and geometry: the ligand furthest along the spectrochemical series wins; CN⁻ ≫ NH₃ > H₂O > Cl⁻.","traps":["Tetrahedral Δt ignored: Tetrahedral, weak-field, Co(II): Δt is smallest.","NH₃ count mistaken for strength: Ligand identity matters more than number; CN⁻ is strongest."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","Crystal field splitting"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Homoleptic octahedral complexes of a metal ion 'M³⁺' with three monodentate ligands L₁, L₂, L₃ absorb wavelengths in the region of green, blue and red respectively. The increasing order of the ligand strength is:","options":["L₂ < L₁ < L₃","L₃ < L₂ < L₁","L₃ < L₁ < L₂","L₁ < L₂ < L₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Ordering by visible-spectrum order without inverting λ to energy. (A)/(B) Using observed (complementary) colours instead of absorbed.\nWorking: absorbed λ: red > green > blue, so Δo (∝ 1/λ): L₃ < L₁ < L₂; ligand strength follows Δo.\nKey idea: Absorbed blue = highest energy = strongest field; absorbed red = weakest.","traps":["Wavelength-energy inversion missed: E ∝ 1/λ: blue absorption means largest Δo.","Absorbed vs observed colour: The question gives absorbed colours; use them directly."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","Colour of complexes"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Wilkinson catalyst is : (Et = C₂H₅)","options":["[(Ph₃P)₃RhCl]","[(Et₃P)₃IrCl]","[(Et₃P)₃RhCl]","[(Ph₃P)₃IrCl]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) both metal and ligand wrong. (C) right metal, wrong phosphine (PEt₃). (D) right phosphine, wrong metal (Ir, as in Vaska's complex).\nWorking: Wilkinson's catalyst is chloridotris(triphenylphosphine)rhodium(I), [RhCl(PPh₃)₃].\nKey idea: Wilkinson = Rh + three PPh₃ + Cl.","traps":["Rh vs Ir confusion: Wilkinson: rhodium."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The total number of isomers for a square planar complex [M(F)(Cl)(SCN)(NO₂)] is :","options":["12","8","16","4"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 4: forgets one ambidentate ligand or geometric isomerism. (B) 8: 2 geometric × 4 linkage. (C) 16: adds optical isomers or 4 geometric.\nWorking: square planar [Mabcd] has 3 geometrical isomers; SCN (S/N) and NO₂ (N/O) are both ambidentate, so 3 × 2 × 2 = 12.\nKey idea: 3 geometric × 2 (SCN/NCS) × 2 (NO₂/ONO) = 12; square planar has no optical isomers.","traps":["Ambidentate ligands ignored: Check every ligand for two donor atoms: SCN⁻ and NO₂⁻.","Wrong count of Mabcd geometric isomers: Fix one ligand; three different trans partners → 3."],"topics":["Structural isomerism","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The difference in the number of unpaired electrons of a metal ion in its high-spin and low-spin octahedral complexes is two. The metal ion is :","options":["Fe²⁺","Co²⁺","Mn²⁺","Ni²⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Fe²⁺ and (C) Mn²⁺: difference 4. (D) Ni²⁺ d⁸: no HS/LS distinction in octahedral (both 2 unpaired).\nWorking: Fe²⁺ d⁶: HS 4, LS 0 (diff 4); Co²⁺ d⁷: HS 3 (t₂g⁵e_g²), LS 1 (t₂g⁶e_g¹) (diff 2); Mn²⁺ d⁵: 5 vs 1 (diff 4); Ni²⁺ d⁸: 2 vs 2 (diff 0).\nKey idea: Only d⁷ gives a difference of 2 (3 → 1).","traps":["d-electron count of ions: Remove 4s first: Fe²⁺ d⁶, Co²⁺ d⁷.","Low-spin d⁵ unpaired count: Fill t₂g with pairing: 5 electrons in 3 orbitals → 1 unpaired."],"topics":["Crystal field splitting","VBT: magnetism"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"A reaction of cobalt(III) chloride and ethylenediamine in a 1 : 2 mole ratio generates two isomeric products A (violet coloured) and B (green coloured). A can show optical activity, B is optically inactive. What type of isomers does A and B represent ?","options":["Geometrical isomers","Ionisation isomers","Coordination isomers","Linkage isomers"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Ionisation: would need exchange between inner and outer sphere ions. (C) Coordination: needs two metal centres. (D) Linkage: needs ambidentate ligand.\nWorking: product is [CoCl₂(en)₂]⁺; cis (violet, chiral) and trans (green, has mirror plane) are geometrical isomers.\nKey idea: Same ligands, different spatial arrangement → geometrical; optical activity of cis is extra information.","traps":["Different colours read as different ions: Both have the same formula [CoCl₂(en)₂]Cl; only arrangement differs (cis/trans)."],"topics":["Geometrical isomerism","Optical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The electrolytes usually used in the electroplating of gold and silver, respectively, are :","options":["[Au(OH)₄]⁻ and [Ag(OH)₂]⁻","[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [AgCl₂]⁻","[Au(NH₃)₂]⁺ and [Ag(CN)₂]⁻","[Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [Ag(CN)₂]⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hydroxido complexes not used. (B) [AgCl₂]⁻ – right gold, wrong silver. (C) ammine gold complex – wrong gold, right silver.\nWorking: smooth electroplating of Au and Ag uses the dicyanido complexes [Au(CN)₂]⁻ and [Ag(CN)₂]⁻.\nKey idea: Both use cyanide complexes.","traps":["Half-right options: Both metals use [M(CN)₂]⁻."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Applications","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match the metals (Column I) with the coordination compound(s)/enzyme(s) (Column II):\nColumn-I: (A) Co; (B) Zn; (C) Rh; (D) Mg\nColumn-II: (i) Wilkinson catalyst; (ii) Chlorophyll; (iii) Vitamin B₁₂; (iv) Carbonic anhydrase","options":["A-ii; B-i; C-iv; D-iii","A-iii; B-iv; C-i; D-ii","A-iv; B-iii; C-i; D-ii","A-i; B-ii; C-iii; D-iv"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps Co and Zn. (A)/(D) random pairings.\nWorking: Co – vitamin B₁₂; Zn – carbonic anhydrase; Rh – Wilkinson catalyst [RhCl(PPh₃)₃]; Mg – chlorophyll.\nKey idea: Only Zn–carbonic anhydrase is less familiar; elimination solves it.","traps":["Co vs Zn enzyme: B₁₂ = cobalamin (Co)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The number of bridging CO ligand(s) and Co–Co bond(s) in Co₂(CO)₈, respectively are :-","options":["0 and 2","2 and 0","4 and 0","2 and 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0 and 2: no bridges and a double count of Co–Co. (B) 2 and 0: bridges without a metal–metal bond (a bridged structure still has the Co–Co bond). (C) 4 and 0: treats all extra CO as bridging.\nWorking: solid Co₂(CO)₈ has six terminal CO, two bridging CO and one Co–Co bond (each Co reaches 18 e).\nKey idea: Co₂(CO)₈ = (CO)₃Co(μ-CO)₂Co(CO)₃ with one Co–Co bond.","traps":["Bridging CO assumed to replace M–M bond: Count electrons: each Co needs the Co–Co bond to reach 18 e."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Coordination number & oxidation state","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The coordination number of Th in K₄[Th(C₂O₄)₄(OH₂)₂] is :- (C₂O₄²⁻ = Oxalato)","options":["6","10","14","8"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 6: counts ligands, not donor atoms. (C) 14: overcount (e.g. 4 × 3 + 2) – oxalate is only didentate. (D) 8: forgets the two aqua ligands.\nWorking: 4 bidentate oxalato = 8 donor atoms + 2 aqua = 10.\nKey idea: CN = Σ(denticity × number of ligands).","traps":["Ligands counted instead of donor atoms: Oxalate is didentate.","Aqua ligands missed: Include every ligand inside the bracket."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Crystal field splitting","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The metal d-orbitals that are directly facing the ligands in K₃[Co(CN)₆] are :","options":["d_xz, d_yz and d_z²","d_xy, d_xz and d_yz","d_xy and d_x²−y²","d_x²−y² and d_z²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) t₂g set (between axes). (A)/(C) mixed sets – one orbital from each group.\nWorking: in an octahedral complex ligands lie on the axes; the e_g orbitals (d_x²−y², d_z²) point along the axes.\nKey idea: Octahedral: e_g along axes face ligands.","traps":["t₂g vs e_g swapped: Orbitals facing ligands are raised in energy (e_g)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Metal carbonyls","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Mn₂(CO)₁₀ is an organometallic compound due to the presence of :","options":["Mn – Mn bond","Mn – C bond","Mn – O bond","C – O bond"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Mn–Mn bond exists but does not make it organometallic. (C) CO binds via C not O. (D) C–O within ligand.\nWorking: organometallic compounds contain at least one metal–carbon bond; CO binds through C.\nKey idea: Metal–carbon bond ⇒ organometallic.","traps":["Mn–Mn bond chosen: Ask what defines the class: M–C bond."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Spectrochemical series","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The magnetic moment of an octahedral homoleptic Mn(II) complex is 5.9 BM. The suitable ligand for this complex is :","options":["CN⁻","NCS⁻","CO","ethylenediamine"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CN⁻ and (C) CO: strong-field ligands would pair electrons (low spin, μ ≈ 1.73 BM). (D) en: above NH₃ in the spectrochemical series, so the intended answer treats it as stronger field (in reality [Mn(en)₃]²⁺ is also high spin, so the item depends on series position).\nWorking: 5.9 BM = √35 → 5 unpaired electrons, so high-spin d⁵ Mn²⁺ needs a weak-field ligand; NCS⁻ is the only weak-field ligand offered (CN⁻ and CO are strong field; en is intermediate-strong in the series).\nKey idea: 5.9 BM means 5 unpaired → high spin → weakest-field ligand in the list.","traps":["Treating en as weak field: Place each ligand in the spectrochemical series; en lies above NH₃.","Spin-only value miscount: √(n(n+2)): n=5 gives 5.92 BM."],"topics":["Spectrochemical series","VBT: magnetism"]}],"mx-hyd":[{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Loss of electron from hydrogen atom results in nucleus of ~1.5 × 10⁻³ pm size.\nReason R : Proton (H⁺) always exists in combined form.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student sees two linked NCERT facts and accepts R as explanation, but R is the consequence of A. (B)/(C): doubts the 1.5 × 10⁻³ pm value or 'always'.\nWorking: both are NCERT facts; R is a consequence of A (tiny H⁺ cannot exist free), not its cause, so R does not explain A.\nKey idea: Small size causes the non-existence of free H⁺; the reverse is not an explanation.","traps":["Consequence offered as reason: Check direction: A → R, not R → A."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In an ice crystal, each water molecule is hydrogen bonded to ________ neighbouring molecules.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if only the molecule's own two O–H bonds are counted; 3 from confusion with the three-dimensional open-cage picture.\nWorking: in hexagonal ice each O is tetrahedrally surrounded by four other O atoms, two via its own H atoms and two via its lone pairs – 4 hydrogen bonds per molecule.\nKey idea: Two donors + two acceptors = 4 H-bonded neighbours.","traps":["Only donor H-bonds counted: Count both donated (via H) and accepted (via lone pairs) bonds."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The water gas on reacting with cobalt as a catalyst forms","options":["Methanoic acid","Methanol","Methanal","Ethanol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): oxidised C₁ products from CO thinking. (D): Fischer–Tropsch chain growth (cobalt also used there) suggests higher alcohols.\nWorking: CO(g) + 2H₂(g) —Co catalyst→ CH₃OH(l); the old NCERT Hydrogen chapter gives this use of syngas.\nKey idea: Water gas + Co catalyst → methanol (CO + 2H₂ → CH₃OH).","traps":["Fischer–Tropsch confusion: Old NCERT: CO + 2H₂ → CH₃OH with Co."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Which of the following can reduce decomposition of H₂O₂ on exposure to light:","options":["Urea","Glass containers","Alkali","Dust"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) glass containers seem protective but release alkali that catalyses decomposition (hence wax lining); (C) alkali and (D) dust promote decomposition.\nWorking: H₂O₂ is stored in wax-lined glass/plastic vessels in the dark with urea added as a stabiliser; glass (alkali traces), alkali and dust all catalyse decomposition.\nKey idea: Urea is the stabiliser; plain glass, alkali, metal surfaces and dust speed decomposition.","traps":["Glass container chosen: Glass leaches alkali which catalyses decomposition; wax-lined or plastic vessels are used."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Permutit process is more efficient compared to the synthetic resin method for the softening of water.\nStatement II: Synthetic resin method results in the formation of soluble sodium salts.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both the Statements I and II are correct","Both the Statements I and II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): accept one swapped statement. (B): accepts both – the sodium-salt property of permutit attached to resins.\nWorking: synthetic ion-exchange resins (cation exchanger RH + anion exchanger ROH) are more efficient than permutit and give deionised water, exchanging ions for H⁺ and OH⁻ – no soluble sodium salts. Permutit (sodium zeolite) is the method that leaves soluble sodium salts. Both statements are wrong.\nKey idea: Permutit leaves Na⁺ salts; resins give deionised water and are more efficient.","traps":["Permutit and resin properties swapped: Permutit: Na⁺ exchange; resins: H⁺/OH⁻ exchange."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R\nAssertion A : Hydrogen is an environment friendly fuel.\nReason R : Atomic number of hydrogen is 1 and it is a very light element.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) accepts R as explanation; lightness is not why it is clean. (B), (C) reject a true statement.\nWorking: H₂ burns to give only water, so it is a clean fuel (A true); hydrogen has Z = 1 and is the lightest element (R true), but lightness is not why it is pollution-free, so R does not explain A → option (A).\nKey idea: Clean because combustion product is only water; lightness is unrelated.","traps":["True reason taken as explanation: Ask whether R causes A."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"The starting material for convenient preparation of deuterated hydrogen peroxide (D₂O₂) is :","options":["BaO₂","2-ethylanthraquinol","BaO","K₂S₂O₈"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) BaO₂ + H₂SO₄ is the laboratory route for H₂O₂, not D₂O₂. (B) 2-ethylanthraquinol is the industrial H₂O₂ route. (C) BaO is not a peroxide.\nWorking: D₂O₂ is conveniently made by hydrolysing potassium peroxodisulphate with heavy water: K₂S₂O₈ + 2D₂O → 2KDSO₄ + D₂O₂; so option (D).\nKey idea: Peroxodisulphate hydrolysed in D₂O gives D₂O₂ directly.","traps":["H₂O₂ lab or industrial route chosen: Remember the deuterium-specific equation with K₂S₂O₈."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"'25 volume' hydrogen peroxide means","options":["1 L marketed solution contains 75 g of H₂O₂.","1 L marketed solution contains 250 g of H₂O₂.","1 L marketed solution contains 25 g of H₂O₂.","100 mL marketed solution contains 25 g of H₂O₂."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) and (D) read '25 volume' as 25 g (per L or per 100 mL). (B) is 10 × the figure, a unit misreading. Taking 1:1 ratio would give ~38 g, not offered.\nWorking: agrees (25 L O₂ at STP = 1.116 mol O₂ → 2.23 mol H₂O₂ × 34 = 75.9 g ≈ 75 g per litre; '75' is the nearest given value)\nKey idea: 1 L of x-volume H₂O₂ gives x L O₂ at STP; g/L ≈ x × 68/22.4 ≈ 3.04x → 25 × 3.04 ≈ 76 g.","traps":["Volume strength read as grams: Volume strength = litres of O₂ released per litre at STP, not mass.","2:1 mole ratio dropped: Write 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ before converting."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I: A. Cobalt catalyst; B. Syngas; C. Nickel catalyst; D. Brine solution. List II: I. (H₂ + Cl₂) production; II. Water gas production; III. Coal gasification; IV. Methanol production. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Ni and brine; (C) assigns cobalt to water gas; (D) assigns syngas to brine electrolysis.\nWorking: cobalt catalyst – methanol from CO + 2H₂ (IV); syngas – produced by coal gasification (III); nickel catalyst – water gas from hydrocarbons by steam reforming (II); brine electrolysis – H₂ + Cl₂ (I) → option (B).\nKey idea: Co → methanol; Ni → water gas from hydrocarbons; coal → syngas; brine → H₂ + Cl₂.","traps":["Co vs Ni catalyst: Ni: steam reforming (water gas); Co: methanol synthesis."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Which of the given compounds can enhance the efficiency of hydrogen storage tank?","options":["SiH₄","Li/P₄","NaNi₅","Di-isobutylaluminium hydride"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SiH₄ and (D) DIBAL-H are hydrides but not reversible storage media; (B) Li/P₄ irrelevant.\nWorking: alloys such as NaNi₅, Ti–TiH₂ and Mg–MgH₂ absorb hydrogen reversibly (metallic/interstitial hydrides) and are used to store dihydrogen; SiH₄ and di-isobutylaluminium hydride are molecular hydrides and Li/P₄ is unrelated → option (C).\nKey idea: Reversible interstitial absorption in metal alloys enables storage.","traps":["Any hydride stores hydrogen: Storage needs reversible uptake in metals/alloys."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Nickel is being used as the catalyst for producing syn gas and edible fats.\nStatement II : Silicon forms both electron rich and electron deficient hydrides.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Both the statements I and II are correct","Both the statements I and II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who think Si, being able to expand octet, forms both types; (C)/(D) for students unsure that Ni (not Fe or Pt) is the syn-gas/hydrogenation catalyst.\nWorking: Ni catalyses steam reforming of hydrocarbons to syn gas and the hydrogenation of vegetable oils to edible fats (I correct); Si is a group-14 element and forms only electron-precise hydrides such as SiH₄ (II incorrect) → option (A).\nKey idea: Group 13 → electron-deficient, Group 14 → electron-precise, Groups 15–17 → electron-rich hydrides.","traps":["Hydride class by group: Memorise: Gr 13 deficient, Gr 14 precise, Gr 15–17 rich.","Syn gas catalyst: Steam reforming and vanaspati hydrogenation both use Ni."],"topics":["Hydrides","Hydrogen & isotopes"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Lithium aluminium hydride can be prepared from the reaction of:","options":["LiCl and Al₂H₆","LiH and Al(OH)₃","LiH and Al₂Cl₆","LiCl, Al and H₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: Al₂H₆ is not a reagent used. B: Al(OH)₃ would destroy the hydride. D: direct synthesis from LiCl, Al, H₂ is not the textbook route.\nWorking: 8LiH + Al₂Cl₆ → 2LiAlH₄ + 6LiCl; LiH supplies the hydride ions and Al₂Cl₆ supplies aluminium → option (C).\nKey idea: Hydride source LiH plus Lewis-acidic Al₂Cl₆.","traps":["Hydride source must be LiH: The hydride ions come from LiH; Al₂Cl₆ supplies Al."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: H₂O₂ is used in the synthesis of Cephalosporin. Statement II: H₂O₂ is used for the restoration of aerobic conditions to sewage wastes. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) doubts the cephalosporin use (unfamiliar). (B) doubts sewage use. (C) both rejected.\nWorking: NCERT lists both among the uses of H₂O₂ – synthesis of drugs such as cephalosporin (I correct) and restoring aerobic conditions to sewage wastes (II correct) → option (D).\nKey idea: Old NCERT lists both uses explicitly.","traps":["Unfamiliar pharmaceutical use: Old NCERT lists cephalosporin synthesis among H₂O₂ uses."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"High purity (>99.95%) dihydrogen is obtained by","options":["reaction of zinc with aqueous alkali.","electrolysis of acidified water using platinum electrodes.","electrolysis of warm aqueous barium hydroxide solution between nickel electrodes.","reaction of zinc with dilute acid."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) are laboratory methods (impure H₂); (B) is the general electrolysis of acidified water, not the high-purity method.\nWorking: old NCERT states this exact method for >99.95% pure H₂.\nKey idea: Ba(OH)₂ electrolyte + Ni electrodes = high-purity H₂.","traps":["General vs high-purity electrolysis: Remember the specific electrolyte (warm Ba(OH)₂) and electrodes (Ni)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following can be used to prevent the decomposition of H₂O₂?","options":["Urea","Formaldehyde","Formic acid","Ethanol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are reducing organic compounds that would react with H₂O₂ rather than stabilise it.\nWorking: H₂O₂ is stored in wax-lined plastic or glass bottles in the dark with a little urea added as a stabiliser (negative catalyst).\nKey idea: Urea is the textbook H₂O₂ stabiliser.","traps":["Organic reducing agents as distractors: Recall the stabiliser line: urea, dark, wax-lined bottle."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Hydrogen peroxide can act as an oxidizing agent in both acidic and basic conditions.\nStatement II: Density of hydrogen peroxide at 298 K is lower than that of D₂O.\nIn the light of the above statements. Choose the correct answer from the options.","options":["Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student assumes D₂O is denser (heavy water); (B)/(D): student thinks H₂O₂ oxidises only in acid.\nWorking: H₂O₂ oxidises in acid (e.g. Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺) and in base (e.g. Cr³⁺ → CrO₄²⁻); its density (≈1.44 g cm⁻³ liquid) is higher than that of D₂O (≈1.11 g cm⁻³), so II is false.\nKey idea: H₂O₂ is denser than water by about 40%; D₂O only about 10%.","traps":["Heavy water assumed densest: Compare numbers: H₂O₂ 1.44 vs D₂O 1.11 g cm⁻³.","H₂O₂ oxidises only in acid: Recall basic examples: Cr³⁺ → CrO₄²⁻, Mn²⁺ → MnO₂."],"topics":["H₂O₂","Hydrogen & isotopes"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The metal salts formed during softening of hardwater using Clark's method are:","options":["Ca(OH)₂ and Mg(OH)₂","CaCO₃ and Mg(OH)₂","Ca(OH)₂ and MgCO₃","CaCO₃ and MgCO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) assumes both precipitate as carbonates (true for boiling Ca but not Mg); (A) takes the added lime itself; (C) mixes both errors.\nWorking: lime removes Ca(HCO₃)₂ as CaCO₃; Mg(HCO₃)₂ + 2Ca(OH)₂ → 2CaCO₃ + Mg(OH)₂ + 2H₂O, since Mg(OH)₂ is less soluble than MgCO₃.\nKey idea: Mg(OH)₂ has a lower Ksp than MgCO₃, so magnesium comes out as hydroxide.","traps":["Mg as carbonate: Recall that boiling and liming both give Mg(OH)₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The products obtained during treatment of hard water using Clark's method are:","options":["CaCO₃ and MgCO₃","Ca(OH)₂ and Mg(OH)₂","CaCO₃ and Mg(OH)₂","Ca(OH)₂ and MgCO₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes Mg precipitates as carbonate (as in boiling it is Mg(OH)₂ too); (B) and (D) list the added lime as a product.\nWorking: lime is added: Ca(HCO₃)₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → 2CaCO₃↓ + 2H₂O; Mg(HCO₃)₂ + 2Ca(OH)₂ → 2CaCO₃↓ + Mg(OH)₂↓ + 2H₂O. Mg precipitates as Mg(OH)₂ because it is less soluble than MgCO₃.\nKey idea: Mg(OH)₂ is less soluble than MgCO₃, so magnesium is removed as hydroxide.","traps":["MgCO₃ vs Mg(OH)₂: Mg(OH)₂ has the lower solubility product."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The reaction of zinc with excess of aqueous alkali, evolves hydrogen gas and gives :","options":["Zn(OH)₂","ZnO","[Zn(OH)₄]²⁻","[ZnO₂]²⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Zn(OH)₂ forms only with limited alkali and dissolves in excess; (B) ZnO is not formed in solution; (C) hydrated zincate – chemically reasonable but not the NCERT form used in the official answer.\nWorking: Zn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + H₂ (sodium zincate, ZnO₂²⁻), the form written in old NCERT (Hydrogen chapter, preparation of dihydrogen). Note: in water the zincate is better described as [Zn(OH)₄]²⁻, so (C) is also chemically reasonable, but the official answer is (D).\nKey idea: Excess alkali gives zincate; NCERT writes it as Na₂ZnO₂.","traps":["Zincate written two ways: Follow the NCERT equation form Na₂ZnO₂.","Hydroxide stops at Zn(OH)₂: Amphoteric Zn(OH)₂ dissolves in excess OH⁻."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: Stannane is an example of a molecular hydride. Statement II: Stannane is a planar molecule. In the light of the above statement, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes planar like BH₃; (B)/(D) misclassify SnH₄ as metallic/ionic hydride.\nWorking: SnH₄ is a covalent (molecular, electron-precise) hydride of a group-14 element; with four bond pairs on Sn it is tetrahedral, not planar.\nKey idea: SnH₄ is CH₄-like: molecular, tetrahedral.","traps":["Stannane not recognised: Stannane = SnH₄, analogue of methane.","Planar shape assumed: Four bond pairs → tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The highest industrial consumption of molecular hydrogen is to produce compounds of element:","options":["Carbon","Nitrogen","Oxygen","Chlorine"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): hydrogenation of oils and methanol synthesis are well known uses involving carbon; (D): HCl manufacture; (C): water/H₂O₂.\nWorking: the largest single use of H₂ is ammonia synthesis (Haber process), i.e. nitrogen compounds (NH₃ and then fertilisers).\nKey idea: Ammonia (Haber) consumes the most H₂ → nitrogen.","traps":["Hydrogenation of oils remembered first: NCERT: largest single use is NH₃ synthesis."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In the industrial production of which of the following, molecular hydrogen is obtained as a byproduct?","options":["NaOH","NaCl","Na metal","Na₂CO₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NaCl – mined/evaporated, no H₂; (C) Na metal – Downs process (molten NaCl) gives Cl₂, no H₂; (D) Na₂CO₃ – Solvay process, by-product CaCl₂.\nWorking: electrolysis of brine (Castner–Kellner / membrane cell) gives NaOH with H₂ at the cathode and Cl₂ at the anode.\nKey idea: Only aqueous electrolysis reduces water to H₂ at the cathode: brine → NaOH + H₂ + Cl₂.","traps":["Na metal electrolysis assumed to give H₂: Downs process uses molten NaCl – no water, so no H₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Consider the following reaction : 2HSO₄⁻(aq) —(1) Electrolysis; (2) Hydrolysis→ 2HSO₄⁻ + 2H⁺ + A. The dihedral angle in product A in its solid phase at 110 K is :","options":["104°","111.5°","90.2°","111.0°"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 111.5° – gas-phase value; (A) 104° – H–O–H angle of water; (D) 111.0° – near-copy of the gas value.\nWorking: electrolysis of HSO₄⁻ gives peroxodisulphate, whose hydrolysis gives back HSO₄⁻ and H₂O₂ (A). H₂O₂ has a dihedral angle of 111.5° in the gas phase but 90.2° in the solid at 110 K = (C).\nKey idea: Two angles in the NCERT figure: 111.5° gas, 90.2° solid (110 K).","traps":["Gas-phase angle given: Note the phase and temperature in the stem.","Water bond angle recalled: Dihedral angle ≠ bond angle."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Boiling of hard water is helpful in removing the temporary hardness by converting calcium hydrogen carbonate and magnesium hydrogen carbonate to","options":["CaCO₃ and Mg(OH)₂","CaCO₃ and MgCO₃","Ca(OH)₂ and MgCO₃","Ca(OH)₂ and Mg(OH)₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes both give carbonates by symmetry; (C) and (D) Ca(OH)₂ is wrong – calcium precipitates as carbonate.\nWorking: Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O + CO₂; Mg(HCO₃)₂ → Mg(OH)₂↓ + 2CO₂ (Mg(OH)₂ is less soluble than MgCO₃, so it precipitates).\nKey idea: Mg(OH)₂ (Ksp ~10⁻¹¹) is far less soluble than MgCO₃, so magnesium comes down as hydroxide.","traps":["Both assumed to give carbonates: Mg(HCO₃)₂ on boiling gives Mg(OH)₂ because it is less soluble than MgCO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Addition of H₂SO₄ to BaO₂ produces:","options":["BaO, SO₂ and H₂O","BaHSO₄ and O₂","BaSO₄, H₂ and O₂","BaSO₄ and H₂O₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – treats peroxide as oxide plus SO₂ (H₂SO₄ is not reduced here); (B) – O₂ evolution assumes decomposition; (C) – H₂ evolution as for metals with acid.\nWorking: BaO₂·8H₂O + H₂SO₄ → BaSO₄↓ + H₂O₂ + 8H₂O; a peroxide salt with acid gives hydrogen peroxide, and insoluble BaSO₄ is filtered off → (D).\nKey idea: Peroxide ion + H⁺ → H₂O₂; it is an acid–base (not redox) reaction.","traps":["Peroxide treated as redox: O₂²⁻ just picks up two H⁺ to give H₂O₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Dihydrogen reacts with CuO to give","options":["CuH₂","Cu","Cu₂O","Cu(OH)₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CuH₂ (hydride formation is not what happens); (C) Cu₂O (partial reduction); (D) Cu(OH)₂ (adds water instead of removing O).\nWorking: CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O on heating; H₂ reduces the oxide to the metal.\nKey idea: H₂ removes oxygen as water: metal oxide → metal.","traps":["Partial reduction product: Excess hot H₂ reduces fully to metal."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Hydrogen has three isotopes: protium (¹H), deuterium (²H or D) and tritium (³H or T). They have nearly the same chemical properties but different physical properties. They differ in","options":["number of protons","atomic number","electronic configuration","atomic mass"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are all identical for isotopes.\nWorking: isotopes differ only in the number of neutrons, hence in atomic mass.\nKey idea: Same Z, different neutron number → different mass.","traps":["Protons vs neutrons: Isotopes = same Z."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following methods is most suitable for preparing deionized water?","options":["Synthetic resin method","Clark's method","Calgon's method","Permutit method"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Clark's removes temporary hardness only; (C) Calgon sequesters Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺; (D) permutit swaps them for Na⁺ – water is soft, not deionised.\nWorking: synthetic cation-exchange (RH) and anion-exchange (R'OH) resins together remove all cations and anions, giving deionised water. Clark's, Calgon and permutit only remove or mask hardness.\nKey idea: Deionised = both cation and anion exchange resins.","traps":["Deionised needs both cation and anion removal: Permutit only swaps Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ for Na⁺; mixed cation/anion resins remove all ions.","Softening vs deionising: Ask: does the method remove anions too?"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Heavy water is used for the study of reaction mechanism.\nReason (R): The rate of reaction for the cleavage of O–H bond is slower than that of O–D bond.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the true explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the true explanation of (A).","(A) is false but (R) is true.","(A) is true but (R) is false."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who accept R because it sounds like the reason D₂O is useful; (A) also needs R true; (C) for those unaware of the use.\nWorking: D₂O is used in exchange reactions to study reaction mechanisms (true). The O–D bond is stronger (lower zero-point energy), so O–D cleavage is slower than O–H; R reverses this and is false.\nKey idea: Heavier isotope → stronger bond → slower cleavage (O–D slower than O–H).","traps":["O–D breaks slower than O–H: Heavier isotope lowers zero-point energy → stronger bond.","Plausible-sounding reason: Check R independently before linking."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Deuterium resembles hydrogen in properties but:","options":["reacts slower than hydrogen","reacts vigorously than hydrogen","reacts just as hydrogen","emits β⁺ particles"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses the direction; (C) ignores the isotope effect; (D) confuses with tritium, which is radioactive (β⁻ emitter, not β⁺).\nWorking: D has the same electronic configuration as H so the chemistry is the same, but its double mass (higher bond enthalpy, lower zero-point energy) makes its reactions slower; deuterium is stable and not radioactive.\nKey idea: Heavier isotope → stronger bond → slower reaction.","traps":["Tritium property given to deuterium: Only tritium is radioactive (β⁻).","Direction of isotope effect: Higher bond enthalpy for D bonds → slower."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The oxide that gives H₂O₂ most readily on treatment with H₂O is:","options":["PbO₂","Na₂O₂","SnO₂","BaO₂·8H₂O"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) BaO₂·8H₂O is the NCERT lab route but needs acid; (A)/(C) PbO₂, SnO₂ are MO₂ dioxides (M⁴⁺, O²⁻) – no peroxide link.\nWorking: Na₂O₂ is a true ionic peroxide (O₂²⁻) of a very strong base and is hydrolysed by (cold) water: Na₂O₂ + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂O₂. BaO₂ needs dilute acid (H₂SO₄) to liberate H₂O₂; PbO₂ and SnO₂ are dioxides (no O–O bond) and give no H₂O₂.\nKey idea: Only peroxides (O–O) give H₂O₂; with water alone the strongly basic Na₂O₂ hydrolyses most readily.","traps":["Dioxide mistaken for peroxide: Check oxidation state: Pb⁴⁺/Sn⁴⁺ with two O²⁻ – no O–O bond.","NCERT BaO₂ route remembered: That route uses dilute H₂SO₄, not water alone."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement-I: The process of producing syn-gas is called gasification of coal. Statement-II: The composition of syn-gas is CO + CO₂ + H₂ (1 : 1 : 1). In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) accepts the altered composition; (A) and (C) reject the verbatim NCERT definition.\nWorking: Statement-I is the old NCERT definition (producing syngas from coal is coal gasification) – true. Syngas (water gas) is a mixture of CO and H₂ only (C + H₂O → CO + H₂, 1 : 1); CO₂ is not a component, so Statement-II is false. Option (B).\nKey idea: Syngas = CO + H₂; CO₂ appears only after the water-gas shift.","traps":["CO₂ added to syngas: Syngas itself is only CO + H₂."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of hydrogen bonded water molecule(s) associated with stoichiometry CuSO₄·5H₂O is _______.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 – counts the coordinated waters; 5 – counts all; 0 – thinks all are coordinated.\nWorking: CuSO₄·5H₂O = [Cu(H₂O)₄]SO₄·H₂O; four water molecules coordinate Cu²⁺ and one is held by hydrogen bonds between the sulphate and coordinated water. Answer 1.\nKey idea: 4 coordinated + 1 hydrogen-bonded.","traps":["Coordinated vs H-bonded: Only the lattice water outside the coordination sphere is \"hydrogen-bonded water\"."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?","options":["Atomic hydrogen is produced when H₂ molecules at a high temperature are irradiated with UV radiation.","At around 2000 K, the dissociation of dihydrogen into its atoms is nearly 8.1%.","Bond dissociation enthalpy of H₂ is highest among diatomic gaseous molecules which contain a single bond.","Dihydrogen is produced on reacting zinc with HCl as well as NaOH(aq)."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) are true NCERT statements; (D) looks wrong to students who forget Zn is amphoteric.\nWorking: the old NCERT value is only about 0.081% dissociation at 2000 K; the other three statements are NCERT facts (UV/electric arc gives atomic H; H–H 435.9 kJ/mol is the highest single-bond diatomic value; Zn reacts with acid and with aqueous NaOH to give H₂).\nKey idea: H₂ is very hard to dissociate: only 0.081% at 2000 K.","traps":["Decimal place shift: Remember H₂ dissociation is tiny even at 2000 K.","Zn with NaOH doubted: Zn is amphoteric: Zn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + H₂."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the Universe, but it is not the most abundant gas in the troposphere. Reason R: Hydrogen is the lightest element. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – R true but student fails to link lightness with escape from gravity; (A)/(C) reject a true statement.\nWorking: A and R are both true, and the lightness of H₂ (light H₂ molecules easily reach escape velocity) is why it escaped Earth's gravity, so R explains A.\nKey idea: Light H₂ molecules escape Earth's gravity, so they are scarce in the troposphere.","traps":["R true but not seen as explanation: Ask \"why is it scarce?\" – because it is light and escapes."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Water does not produce CO on reacting with:","options":["CO₂","C","CH₄","C₃H₈"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) C – water-gas reaction gives CO; (C) CH₄ – steam reforming gives CO; (D) C₃H₈ – general CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ + nH₂O → nCO + (2n+1)H₂.\nWorking: C + H₂O → CO + H₂ (water gas); CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂ and C₃H₈ + 3H₂O → 3CO + 7H₂ (steam reforming, Ni, ~1270 K). CO₂ is already fully oxidised and gives no CO with water.\nKey idea: Only reduced carbon (C or hydrocarbons) can give CO with steam.","traps":["Propane not recognised as syngas source: Use the general steam-reforming equation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Statements about heavy water are given below. A. Heavy water is used in exchange reactions for the study of reaction mechanisms. B. Heavy water is prepared by exhaustive electrolysis of water. C. Heavy water has higher boiling point than ordinary water. D. Viscosity of H₂O is greater than D₂O.","options":["A, B and C only","A and B only","A and D only","A and C only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) omits C (b.p. thought lower); (C)/(D) include D or drop B – D reverses the viscosity order.\nWorking: A true (D₂O used as tracer in exchange reactions); B true (exhaustive electrolysis enriches D₂O); C true (b.p. 374.4 K vs 373 K); D false – D₂O is more viscous than H₂O.\nKey idea: Most physical constants of D₂O (m.p., b.p., density, viscosity) are higher than those of H₂O; dielectric constant and ionic product are lower.","traps":["Viscosity order reversed: D₂O has stronger effective H-bonding: higher b.p., m.p. and viscosity.","Preparation method doubted: H₂O is electrolysed preferentially, leaving D₂O-enriched residue."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which of the following forms of hydrogen emits low energy β⁻ particles?","options":["Deuterium ²₁H","Tritium ³₁H","Protium ¹₁H","Proton H⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) deuterium – heavy but stable isotope; (C) protium – stable common isotope; (D) H⁺ – a bare proton, not an isotope form that decays.\nWorking: tritium (1 p, 2 n) is the only radioactive hydrogen isotope; it decays by low-energy β⁻ emission to ³He.\nKey idea: Only ³H has an unstable n/p ratio (2:1) and undergoes β⁻ decay.","traps":["Deuterium taken as radioactive: Remember only tritium is radioactive among H isotopes."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Calgon is used for water treatment. Which of the following statement is NOT true about Calgon?","options":["Calgon contains the 2nd most abundant element by weight in the Earth's crust.","It is polymeric compound and is water soluble.","It is also known as Graham's salt","It does not remove Ca²⁺ ion by precipitation."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – sounds false because Calgon 'removes hardness', but it complexes rather than precipitates; (C) – Graham's salt name unfamiliar; (B) – polymeric metaphosphate, true.\nWorking: Calgon is sodium hexametaphosphate Na₆P₆O₁₈ (Graham's salt), a water-soluble polymeric phosphate that sequesters Ca²⁺ as a soluble complex. It contains Na, P, O – not Si, the 2nd most abundant element in the crust; so (A) is false.\nKey idea: Calgon = Na₆P₆O₁₈; Si (2nd most abundant) is absent.","traps":["'Does not precipitate' judged false: Calgon sequesters ions in solution.","Oxygen taken as 2nd most abundant: Crust order: O > Si > Al > Fe."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Assertion A: The dihedral angles in H₂O₂ in gaseous phase is 90.2° and in solid phase is 111.5°.\nReason R: The change in dihedral angle in solid and gaseous phase is due to the difference in the intermolecular forces.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below for A and R.","options":["A is correct but R is not correct.","Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) or (B) for students who remember both numbers but not which phase; (A) for those who doubt the H-bonding reason.\nWorking: the values are swapped – the dihedral angle of H₂O₂ is 111.5° in the gas phase and 90.2° in the solid. R is correct: hydrogen bonding in the solid changes the angle.\nKey idea: Gas phase (free molecule) has the larger angle 111.5°; H-bonding in the crystal closes it to 90.2°.","traps":["Gas/solid values swapped: Learn the pair: gas 111.5°, solid 90.2°."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The single largest industrial application of dihydrogen is :","options":["Manufacture of metal hydrides","Rocket fuel in space research","In the synthesis of ammonia","In the synthesis of nitric acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) nitric acid is made from ammonia (Ostwald), an indirect use; (B) rocket fuel is a well-known but minor use; (A) hydrides are a minor use.\nWorking: most hydrogen produced is consumed in the Haber process for ammonia; nitric acid is made from ammonia, not directly from H₂.\nKey idea: H₂ → NH₃ (Haber) is the dominant use; HNO₃ and fertilisers come downstream.","traps":["Downstream product chosen: Pick the direct use of H₂; HNO₃ is made from NH₃."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Isotope(s) of hydrogen which emits low energy β⁻ particles with t½ value > 12 years is/are","options":["Protium","Tritium","Deuterium","Deuterium and Tritium"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) tempting for students who think deuterium is also radioactive.\nWorking: tritium is the only radioactive hydrogen isotope; it is a weak β⁻ emitter with t½ ≈ 12.3 years.\nKey idea: Only ³H is radioactive.","traps":["Deuterium thought radioactive: Only tritium is radioactive."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"At 298.2 K the relationship between enthalpy of bond dissociation (in kJ mol⁻¹) for hydrogen (E_H) and its isotope, deuterium (E_D), is best described by:","options":["E_H = ½ E_D","E_H = E_D","E_H ≃ E_D − 7.5","E_H = 2E_D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes isotopes have identical chemistry; (A) and (D) wrongly scale the enthalpy with mass.\nWorking: bond dissociation enthalpy of H₂ ≈ 435.9 kJ mol⁻¹ and of D₂ ≈ 443.4 kJ mol⁻¹, so E_H ≈ E_D − 7.5 (heavier D₂ has lower zero-point energy, so a slightly stronger bond).\nKey idea: D–D is slightly stronger than H–H (≈7.5 kJ mol⁻¹) because of lower zero-point energy.","traps":["Isotopes have the same chemistry: Mass changes vibrational zero-point energy, so bond enthalpy differs slightly.","Scaling with mass: Mass ratio does not scale bond energy."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which one of the following metals forms interstitial hydride easily?","options":["Cr","Fe","Mn","Co"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) are in groups 7–9 – tempting because Fe/Co are familiar for interstitial carbides/nitrides.\nWorking: d- and f-block metals of groups 3–6 form (metallic/interstitial) hydrides; in group 6 only Cr forms CrH. Groups 7–9 (Mn, Fe, Co) show the 'hydride gap' and do not form hydrides.\nKey idea: Hydride gap: groups 7–9 form no hydrides; Cr is the group-6 exception that does.","traps":["Interstitial carbides vs hydrides: Recall the hydride gap specifically."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The number of neutrons and electrons, respectively, present in the radioactive isotope of hydrogen is:","options":["1 and 1","3 and 1","2 and 1","2 and 2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is deuterium; (B) takes mass number 3 as neutrons; (D) gives 2 electrons as if neutral atom had Z = 2.\nWorking: the radioactive isotope is tritium ³H: 1 proton, 2 neutrons, 1 electron.\nKey idea: Tritium: A = 3, Z = 1 → n = 2, e = 1.","traps":["Mass number is not neutron count: n = A − Z.","Which isotope is radioactive: Only tritium is radioactive."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: H₂O₂ can act as both oxidising and reducing agent in basic medium. Statement II: In the hydrogen economy, the energy is transmitted in the form of dihydrogen. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both statement I and statement II are false","Both statement I and statement II are true","Statement I is true but statement II is false","Statement I is false but statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – doubts 'transmitted in the form of dihydrogen' (thinking of electricity); (D)/(A) – believes H₂O₂ is only an oxidant in base.\nWorking: H₂O₂ oxidises (e.g. Mn²⁺ → Mn⁴⁺) and reduces (e.g. I₂ → I⁻, MnO₄⁻ → MnO₂) in basic medium; the hydrogen economy is based on transporting and storing energy as liquid or gaseous dihydrogen.\nKey idea: H₂O₂ (O in −1) can go up to O₂ or down to H₂O/OH⁻ in either medium.","traps":["H₂O₂ only oxidant in base: O in −1 state can be oxidised or reduced in any medium.","Energy carrier misread: Hydrogen economy = store/transport energy as H₂."],"topics":["H₂O₂","Hydrogen & isotopes"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Statement I: Sodium hydride can be used as an oxidising agent. Statement II: The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen in pyridine makes it basic. Choose the CORRECT answer from the options given below:","options":["Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is true but statement II is false","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(B) accept NaH as an oxidant (it is a reducing agent); (A) rejects pyridine's basicity, confusing it with pyrrole whose N lone pair is in the aromatic sextet.\nWorking: NaH is an ionic (saline) hydride containing H⁻, a strong reducing agent, not an oxidant; pyridine's sp² nitrogen lone pair is not part of the aromatic sextet, so it is available for protonation (pyridine is basic).\nKey idea: H⁻ in NaH donates electrons → reductant; pyridine N lone pair lies in the ring plane, outside the π-sextet.","traps":["NaH taken as oxidising agent: Look at the anion: H⁻ (oxidation state −1) can only be oxidised.","Pyridine confused with pyrrole: Pyridine lone pair is not used in aromaticity; pyrrole's is."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct statements about H₂O₂ are: (A) used in the treatment of effluents. (B) used as both oxidising and reducing agents. (C) the two hydroxyl groups lie in the same plane. (D) miscible with water. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (B), (C) and (D)","(A), (B) and (D) only","(B), (C) and (D) only","(A), (C) and (D) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts the planar structure; (C) drops the effluent-treatment use; (D) drops dual redox behaviour and accepts planarity.\nWorking: H₂O₂ is used to treat domestic and industrial effluents, acts as oxidant and reductant, and is miscible with water in all proportions; its open-book (skew) structure puts the two O–H bonds in different planes (dihedral ≈111° in gas), so (C) is false.\nKey idea: H₂O₂ is skew ('open book'), so the two OH groups are not coplanar.","traps":["Planar H₂O₂ accepted: Recall the open-book dihedral angle.","Effluent use not recalled: Learn the uses list along with structure."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The INCORRECT statement(s) about heavy water is (are): (A) used as a moderator in nuclear reactor (B) obtained as a by-product in fertilizer industry. (C) used for the study of reaction mechanism (D) has a higher dielectric constant than water. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(B) only","(C) only","(D) only","(B) and (D) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) – student unsure about the fertilizer by-product statement; (B) – tracer use unfamiliar.\nWorking: D₂O dielectric constant (≈78.06) is slightly lower than that of H₂O (≈78.39), so (D) is incorrect; (A), (B) (obtained as a by-product in some fertilizer industries) and (C) are correct per old NCERT.\nKey idea: Almost all physical constants of D₂O are higher than H₂O except dielectric constant (and ionic product).","traps":["Higher values assumed for all D₂O properties: Dielectric constant is the exception (lower).","By-product statement doubted: NCERT states it is a fertilizer-industry by-product."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The functional groups that are responsible for the ion-exchange property of cation and anion exchange resins, respectively, are :","options":["–SO₃H and –NH₂","–SO₃H and –COOH","–NH₂ and –COOH","–NH₂ and –SO₃H"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reverses the order; (B) gives two acidic groups (both cation exchangers); (C) reverses and uses –COOH.\nWorking: cation-exchange resins carry acidic –SO₃H groups whose H⁺ is exchanged for Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺; anion-exchange resins carry basic –NH₂ (amino/ammonium) groups that exchange OH⁻ for anions.\nKey idea: Acidic group (–SO₃H) releases H⁺ → exchanges cations; basic group (–NH₂/–NR₃⁺OH⁻) exchanges anions.","traps":["Order of the pair reversed: Write \"cation – acid group, anion – basic group\" before reading options.","–COOH as anion exchanger: –COOH is acidic, so it also exchanges cations."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In comparison to the zeolite process for the removal of permanent hardness, the synthetic resins method is :","options":["less efficient as it exchanges only anions","more efficient as it can exchange only cations","less efficient as the resins cannot be regenerated","more efficient as it can exchange both cations as well as anions"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): 'only anions' – reverses zeolite's role. (B): correct direction but wrong reason (only cations). (C): resins are regenerable.\nWorking: zeolite (permutit) exchanges only Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ for Na⁺; cation- plus anion-exchange resins remove both cations and anions (deionised water) and are regenerable.\nKey idea: Resins = cation exchanger (RH/RNa) + anion exchanger (R–NH₃⁺OH⁻) in series.","traps":["Right verdict, wrong reason: Check the reason: resins exchange anions too."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Among the statements (a) - (d), the correct ones are -\n(a) Decomposition of hydrogen peroxide gives dioxygen\n(b) Like hydrogen peroxide, compounds, such as KClO₃, Pb(NO₃)₂ and NaNO₃ when heated liberated dioxygen\n(c) 2-Ethylanthraquinone is useful for the industrial preparation of hydrogen peroxide.\n(d) Hydrogen peroxide is used for the manufacture of sodium perborate","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(a) and (c) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a), (c) and (d) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) doubts the perborate use; (B) doubts both (b) and (d); (D) doubts (b).\nWorking: all four are true – 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂; KClO₃, Pb(NO₃)₂, NaNO₃ give O₂ on heating; 2-ethylanthraquinol autoxidation is the industrial H₂O₂ route; H₂O₂ is used to make sodium perborate.\nKey idea: Every statement is a textbook fact; NaNO₃ gives O₂ on heating, unlike heavy-metal nitrates it gives no NO₂.","traps":["NaNO₃ decomposition doubted: Alkali-metal nitrates give nitrite + O₂.","Use of H₂O₂ in perborate unknown: Learn the uses list of H₂O₂."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Hydrogen has three isotopes (A), (B) and (C). If the number of neutron(s) in (A), (B) and (C) respectively, are (x), (y) and (z), the sum of (x), (y) and (z) is :","options":["4","3","2","1"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4 adds a neutron to protium; (C) 2 takes only tritium; (D) 1 takes only deuterium.\nWorking: protium 0, deuterium 1, tritium 2 neutrons; sum = 3.\nKey idea: ¹H, ²H, ³H have 0, 1, 2 neutrons.","traps":["Protium assumed to have a neutron: Neutrons = A − Z; protium A = 1, Z = 1."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The hardness of a water sample containing 10⁻³ M MgSO₄ expressed as CaCO₃ equivalents (in ppm) is ______. (molar mass of MgSO₄ is 120.37 g/mol)","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 120.37 gives 120 ppm (mass of MgSO₄, not CaCO₃); forgetting ×1000 conversion gives 0.1.\nWorking: 10⁻³ mol MgSO₄ per litre ≡ 10⁻³ mol CaCO₃ = 0.100 g per 1000 g water = 100 ppm.\nKey idea: 1 mol Mg²⁺ ≡ 1 mol CaCO₃ (100 g/mol); ppm = mg per litre.","traps":["Using MgSO₄ molar mass: Hardness is expressed as CaCO₃ (M = 100).","ppm conversion: ppm = mg L⁻¹ for dilute water."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The volume strength of 8.9 M H₂O₂ solution calculated at 273 K and 1 atm is ______. (R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹) (rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: 199 (forgetting the 1/2 for O₂), 50 (halving twice), 218 (using 298 K instead of 273 K and also missing the ½).\nWorking: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂; 1 L gives 4.45 mol O₂ × 22.41 L = 99.7 L → volume strength ≈ 100.\nKey idea: Vol. strength = 11.2 × M at STP.","traps":["Stoichiometric half missed: Write the decomposition equation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The strengths of 5.6 volume hydrogen peroxide (of density 1 g/mL) in terms of mass percentage and molarity (M), respectively, are:\n(Take molar mass of hydrogen peroxide as 34 g/mol)","options":["1.7 and 0.25","1.7 and 0.5","0.85 and 0.5","0.85 and 0.25"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) 0.25 M: using 22.4 instead of 11.2 (ignoring the 2:1 ratio); (C)/(D) 0.85%: halving the mass again; (B) correct.\nWorking: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂; 1 M H₂O₂ gives 11.2 L O₂ per L, so M = 5.6/11.2 = 0.5; mass = 0.5 × 34 = 17 g per 1000 g solution = 1.7%.\nKey idea: Volume strength ÷ 11.2 = molarity (at STP, 2 mol H₂O₂ give 22.4 L O₂).","traps":["22.4 used in place of 11.2: Write 2H₂O₂ → O₂ and use 11.2 L per mole of H₂O₂."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The equation that represents the water-gas shift reaction is :","options":["CO(g) + H₂O(g) –(673 K, catalyst)→ CO₂(g) + H₂(g)","CH₄(g) + H₂O(g) –(1270 K, Ni)→ CO(g) + 3H₂(g)","C(s) + H₂O(g) –(1270 K)→ CO(g) + H₂(g)","2C(s) + O₂(g) + 4N₂(g) –(1273 K)→ 2CO(g) + 4N₂(g)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) steam reforming of methane – makes syngas, not the shift; (C) coal gasification – makes water gas itself; (D) producer gas from air and coke.\nWorking: the shift reaction converts the CO of water gas into CO₂ with more H₂ using steam over an iron-chromate catalyst at ~673 K; (B) and (C) make water gas (syngas), (D) makes producer gas.\nKey idea: The \"shift\" starts from CO (a product of water gas) and gives CO₂ + H₂.","traps":["Water gas formation confused with shift reaction: Shift reaction uses CO, not C, as reactant."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The one that is NOT suitable for the removal of permanent hardness of water is:","options":["Treatment with sodium carbonate","Calgon's method","Clark's method","Ion-exchange method"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) washing soda, (B) Calgon, (D) ion exchange – all are NCERT methods for permanent hardness, so they are correct methods (not the answer).\nWorking: Clark's method (adding slaked lime) removes temporary hardness (bicarbonates) only; Na₂CO₃ (washing soda), Calgon and ion exchange remove permanent hardness.\nKey idea: Clark's method (lime) works only on bicarbonates – temporary hardness.","traps":["NOT wording missed: Ask which removes only Ca/Mg bicarbonates."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Hydrogen peroxide, in the pure state, is:","options":["non-planar and almost colorless","linear and almost colorless","planar and blue in color","linear and blue in color"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) linear – H–O–O–H is not linear; (C) planar and blue – planarity wrong, and 'very pale blue' exaggerated to blue; (D) both attributes wrong.\nWorking: H₂O₂ has an open-book (non-planar) structure with dihedral angle ~111° (gas); pure H₂O₂ is an almost colourless (very pale blue) liquid.\nKey idea: H₂O₂: open-book, non-planar; pure liquid almost colourless.","traps":["'Very pale blue' read as blue: NCERT's main word is 'almost colourless'.","H–O–O–H pictured flat: Recall the open-book dihedral structure."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Dihydrogen of high purity (> 99.95%) is obtained through:","options":["the electrolysis of warm Ba(OH)₂ solution using Ni electrodes.","the reaction of Zn with dilute HCl","the electrolysis of brine solution.","the electrolysis of acidified water using Pt electrodes."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Zn + dil HCl – laboratory method, not high purity; (C) brine electrolysis – H₂ is a by-product of chlor-alkali process; (D) acidified water with Pt – a commercial method but the purity figure belongs to Ba(OH)₂/Ni.\nWorking: old NCERT states high-purity (>99.95%) H₂ is made by electrolysing warm aqueous Ba(OH)₂ between Ni electrodes.\nKey idea: The >99.95% figure is tied to warm Ba(OH)₂ with Ni electrodes.","traps":["Any electrolysis assumed equally pure: Link the purity figure to the specific Ba(OH)₂/Ni method."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"100 mL of a water sample contains 0.81 g of calcium bicarbonate and 0.73 g of magnesium bicarbonate. The hardness of this water sample expressed in terms of equivalents of CaCO₃ is: (molar mass of calcium bicarbonate is 162 g mol⁻¹ and magnesium bicarbonate is 146 g mol⁻¹)","options":["1,000 ppm","10,000 ppm","100 ppm","5,000 ppm"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1000 ppm – taking 1 L instead of 100 mL; (C) 100 ppm – double unit slip; (D) 5000 ppm – counting only one salt.\nWorking: 0.81/162 = 0.005 mol and 0.73/146 = 0.005 mol ⇒ 0.010 mol CaCO₃ = 1.0 g per 100 g water = 10⁴ ppm.\nKey idea: Each bicarbonate mole = one CaCO₃ mole; ppm = g per 10⁶ g.","traps":["Volume basis slip: ppm = mass solute/mass solution × 10⁶.","Only one salt counted: Add Mg equivalents too."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The strength of 11.2 volume solution of H₂O₂ is: [Given that molar mass of H = 1 g mol⁻¹ and O = 16 g mol⁻¹]","options":["13.6%","3.4%","34%","1.7%"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 34% forgets dividing g L⁻¹ by 10; (D) 1.7% uses 1 mol O₂ per mol H₂O₂ (wrong stoichiometry); (A) 13.6% uses 4× factor.\nWorking: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂; 11.2 L O₂ at STP = 0.5 mol from 1 mol H₂O₂ per litre ⇒ 1 M = 34 g L⁻¹ = 3.4% (w/v).\nKey idea: Volume strength = 11.2 × M.","traps":["Stoichiometry of O₂ release: Write the balanced equation.","g/L read as %: % w/v = g per 100 mL."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The synonym for water gas when used in the production of methanol is :","options":["natural gas","laughing gas","syn gas","fuel gas"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) natural gas is CH₄; (B) laughing gas N₂O; (D) fuel gas is generic.\nWorking: water gas (CO + H₂) is called synthesis gas (\"syngas\") because it is used to synthesise methanol and other compounds.\nKey idea: Water gas = syngas.","traps":["Producer/fuel gas confused with water gas: Water gas = CO + H₂ = syngas."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The correct statements among (a) to (d) are: (a) saline hydrides produce H₂ gas when reacted with H₂O. (b) reaction of LiAlH₄ with BF₃ leads to B₂H₆. (c) PH₃ and CH₄ are electron-rich and electron-precise hydrides, respectively. (d) HF and CH₄ are called as molecular hydrides.","options":["(c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a), (c) and (d) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) reject (a) or (b): students may doubt saline hydrides react with water or forget LiAlH₄ + BF₃ diborane prep; (B) rejects (d) believing HF is ionic.\nWorking: (a) NaH + H₂O → NaOH + H₂; (b) 4BF₃ + 3LiAlH₄ → 2B₂H₆ + 3LiF + 3AlF₃; (c) PH₃ has a lone pair (electron-rich), CH₄ electron-precise; (d) both are covalent molecular hydrides. All four correct.\nKey idea: Every statement is a textbook line; none is altered.","traps":["HF thought ionic, not molecular: Molecular hydrides = covalent hydrides of p-block elements, including HF."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The metal that gives hydrogen gas upon treatment with both acid as well as base is :","options":["zinc","iron","magnesium","mercury"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Fe and (C) Mg give H₂ with acids only; (D) Hg is below H in the activity series.\nWorking: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ and Zn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + H₂ (amphoteric metal). Fe and Mg react only with acids; Hg with neither (non-oxidising acids).\nKey idea: Zn (like Al) is amphoteric and dissolves in NaOH forming zincate with H₂.","traps":["Reactive metal assumed to react with base: Reaction with alkali needs an amphoteric oxide/hydroxide, not just reactivity."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The temporary hardness of a water sample is due to compound X. Boiling this sample converts X to compound Y. X and Y, respectively, are :","options":["Ca(HCO₃)₂ and CaO","Mg(HCO₃)₂ and MgCO₃","Mg(HCO₃)₂ and Mg(OH)₂","Ca(HCO₃)₂ and Ca(OH)₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) MgCO₃ is the intuitive product but Mg(OH)₂ is less soluble; (A)/(D) Ca gives CaCO₃, not CaO or Ca(OH)₂.\nWorking: on boiling Mg(HCO₃)₂ → Mg(OH)₂ + 2CO₂ (Mg(OH)₂ is less soluble than MgCO₃); Ca(HCO₃)₂ gives CaCO₃.\nKey idea: Mg bicarbonate → Mg(OH)₂; Ca bicarbonate → CaCO₃.","traps":["MgCO₃ assumed by analogy with CaCO₃: Mg(OH)₂ has lower solubility, so it precipitates."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The isotopes of hydrogen are :","options":["Tritium and protium only","Deuterium and tritium only","Protium and deuterium only","Protium, deuterium and tritium"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C): one isotope dropped.\nWorking: hydrogen has three isotopes – protium, deuterium and tritium.\nKey idea: Three isotopes: ¹H, ²H, ³H.","traps":["Protium not counted as an isotope: All three nuclides ¹H, ²H, ³H are isotopes."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The temporary hardness of water is due to:","options":["Ca(HCO₃)₂","NaCl","Na₂SO₄","CaCl₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) CaCl₂: permanent hardness. (B) NaCl, (C) Na₂SO₄: sodium salts cause no hardness.\nWorking: temporary hardness comes from Mg/Ca hydrogencarbonates, removable by boiling.\nKey idea: Bicarbonate = temporary; chloride/sulphate of Ca/Mg = permanent.","traps":["Permanent vs temporary: Only hydrogencarbonates decompose on boiling."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The total number of isotopes of hydrogen and number of radioactive isotopes among them, respectively, are :","options":["2 and 0","3 and 2","3 and 1","2 and 1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) forget tritium. (B) counts deuterium as radioactive.\nWorking: protium, deuterium, tritium = 3 isotopes; only tritium is radioactive.\nKey idea: H, D, T; only T is a β-emitter.","traps":["Deuterium thought radioactive: Only tritium (half-life ≈ 12 y) is radioactive."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Among the following reactions of hydrogen with halogens, the one that requires a catalyst is :","options":["H₂ + I₂ → 2HI","H₂ + F₂ → 2HF","H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl","H₂ + Br₂ → 2HBr"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) H₂ + F₂: most vigorous, explodes even in the dark. (C) Cl₂ and (D) Br₂: proceed with light or heat, no catalyst needed.\nWorking: reactivity of halogens with H₂ falls F > Cl > Br > I; F₂ reacts even in the dark, I₂ needs a catalyst (e.g. Pt).\nKey idea: Reactivity with H₂ decreases down group 17; only the least reactive, I₂, needs a catalyst.","traps":["Fluorine thought to need activation: Remember: H₂ + F₂ reacts even in the dark."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen & isotopes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The correct statements among (a) to (d) regarding H₂ as a fuel are:\n(a) It produces less pollutant than petrol\n(b) A cylinder of compressed dihydrogen weighs ~30 times more than a petrol tank producing the same amount of energy\n(c) Dihydrogen is stored in tanks of metal alloys like NaNi₅\n(d) On combustion, values of energy released per gram of liquid dihydrogen and LPG are 50 and 142 kJ, respectively","options":["b and d only","a, b and c only","b, c and d only","a and c only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(d) swaps 142 and 50 – options (A) and (C) include it. (D) omits true statement (b), which sounds exaggerated.\nWorking: (a), (b), (c) are NCERT statements; (d) is reversed – liquid H₂ gives ~142 kJ g⁻¹ and LPG ~50 kJ g⁻¹.\nKey idea: Per gram, H₂ releases about three times the energy of LPG – 142 belongs to H₂.","traps":["Swapped energy values: H₂ has the highest energy per gram of all fuels.","'30 times heavier' looks false: NCERT states this for compressed gas cylinders."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"NaH is an example of:","options":["Electron-rich hydride","Molecular hydride","Saline hydride","Metallic hydride"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) electron-rich – covalent hydrides like NH₃. (D) metallic – d/f-block. (B) molecular – p-block.\nWorking: s-block metal hydrides such as NaH are ionic (saline) hydrides.\nKey idea: Ionic = saline hydrides for s-block.","traps":["Metallic vs saline: Metallic hydrides are d/f-block."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrides","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The hydride that is NOT electron deficient is :-","options":["B₂H₆","AlH₃","SiH₄","GaH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D): all Group 13 – electron deficient.\nWorking: Group 13 hydrides (B₂H₆, AlH₃, GaH₃) have fewer electrons than needed for normal Lewis structures (electron-deficient); SiH₄ (Group 14) is electron-precise.\nKey idea: Group 13 hydrides: deficient; Group 14: precise; Groups 15–17: rich.","traps":["Al/Ga hydrides thought electron-precise: Classify by group: any EH₃ of Group 13 is deficient."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hard water","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The hardness of a water sample (in terms of equivalents of CaCO₃) containing 10⁻³ M CaSO₄ is : (molar mass of CaSO₄ = 136 g mol⁻¹)","options":["100 ppm","50 ppm","10 ppm","90 ppm"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 50 ppm: using equivalent mass 50 per mole wrongly. (C) 10 ppm: a factor-of-10 slip in the mg conversion; (D) 90 ppm: an arithmetic slip (using the CaSO₄ mass, 136, would give 136 ppm, which is not an option).\nWorking: 10⁻³ mol CaSO₄ ≡ 10⁻³ mol CaCO₃ = 0.1 g per litre = 100 mg/L = 100 ppm.\nKey idea: Moles of Ca²⁺ × 100 g/mol (CaCO₃) × 1000 = ppm.","traps":["Using CaSO₄ molar mass: Hardness is expressed as CaCO₃ (100 g/mol).","Equivalent weight confusion: Mol CaSO₄ = mol CaCO₃ (both divalent)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"H₂O₂","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The volume strength of 1 M H₂O₂ is :\n(Molar mass of H₂O₂ = 34 g mol⁻¹)","options":["16.8","11.35","22.4","5.6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 16.8: 0.75 mol O₂ × 22.4 – wrong stoichiometry. (C) 22.4: 1 mol O₂ per mol H₂O₂ (ratio missed). (D) 5.6: 0.25 mol O₂ × 22.4. With 22.4 L the correct ratio gives 11.2, absent – so the 22.7 L (1 bar) STP is needed.\nWorking: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂, so 1 mol H₂O₂ gives 0.5 mol O₂; at STP (1 bar, 273 K, 22.7 L mol⁻¹) that is 11.35 L per litre of solution, i.e. volume strength 11.35.\nKey idea: Volume strength = 11.35 × molarity (STP = 1 bar, 273 K).","traps":["Old STP molar volume: Use 22.7 L at 1 bar; 0.5 mol O₂ per mol H₂O₂.","Mole ratio H₂O₂ : O₂ missed: 2H₂O₂ → O₂: ratio 2 : 1."]}],"mx-surf":[{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"The number of colloidal systems from the following, which will have 'liquid' as the dispersion medium, is ________.\nGem stones, paints, smoke, cheese, milk, hair cream, insecticide sprays, froth, soap lather","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 if insecticide spray (liquid dispersed phase) is counted; 4 if hair cream is missed; 6 if cheese (liquid in solid) is counted.\nWorking: liquid medium – paints (sol), milk and hair cream (emulsions), froth and soap lather (foams) = 5. Gem stones (solid sol), cheese (gel, solid medium), smoke (solid in gas) and insecticide sprays (liquid in gas, aerosol) have non-liquid media.\nKey idea: Medium is the continuous phase: sprays/aerosols have gas, gels have solid.","traps":["Dispersed phase confused with medium: Ask which phase is continuous."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Which of the following represent the Freundlich adsorption isotherms?\nA. x/m vs p: curve rising steeply then levelling off (from origin)\nB. log(x/m) vs log p: straight line with positive slope and positive intercept marked 'constant'\nC. x/m vs c: straight line with positive intercept marked 'constant'\nD. x/m vs p¹ᐟⁿ: straight line through the origin\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B, C, D only","A, B only","A, B, D only","A, C, D only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): accepts graph C because it is linear with an intercept like B. (B): rejects D, not recognising the p¹ᐟⁿ linearisation.\nWorking: x/m = k p¹ᐟⁿ → curved x/m vs p (A), linear log–log with intercept log k (B), linear x/m vs p¹ᐟⁿ through origin (D). C (linear x/m vs c with intercept) is not a Freundlich form.\nKey idea: Only the log–log plot has a non-zero intercept; x/m vs p¹ᐟⁿ passes through the origin.","traps":["Intercept graph copied onto linear plot: x/m = k p¹ᐟⁿ has no additive constant.","p¹ᐟⁿ linearisation missed: x/m ∝ p¹ᐟⁿ → straight line through origin."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Coagulating value of the electrolytes AlCl₃ and NaCl for As₂S₃ are 0.09 and 50.04 respectively. The coagulating power of AlCl₃ is x times the coagulating power of NaCl. The value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"556","range":[556.0,556.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 0.0018 (direct ratio 0.09/50.04), 27 (cube of charge ratio from Hardy–Schulze rule used instead of data).\nWorking: coagulating power ∝ 1/coagulating value, so x = 50.04/0.09 = 556.\nKey idea: Power = 1/value; data supplied override the rough Hardy–Schulze charge rule.","traps":["Direct ratio taken: Smaller coagulating value = stronger coagulant."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"What happens when a lyophilic sol is added to a lyophobic sol?","options":["Film of lyophobic sol is formed over lyophilic sol.","Lyophilic sol is dispersed in lyophobic sol.","Film of lyophilic sol is formed over lyophobic sol.","Lyophobic sol is coagulated"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): roles reversed. (B): vague mixing statement. (D): confuses mutual coagulation of oppositely charged sols with protection.\nWorking: lyophilic colloids act as protective colloids – they form a protective layer around lyophobic particles and stabilise them against coagulation.\nKey idea: Lyophilic sol coats and protects lyophobic sol.","traps":["Protection confused with mutual coagulation: Lyophilic + lyophobic → protection; oppositely charged lyophobic sols → coagulation."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R\nAssertion A: Amongst He, Ne, Ar and Kr; 1 g of activated charcoal adsorbs more of Kr.\nReason R: The critical volume V_c (cm³ mol⁻¹) and critical pressure P_c (atm) is highest for Krypton but the compressibility factor at critical point Z_c is lowest for Krypton.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) accept R; Kr does have the highest V_c and P_c, but Z_c is not uniquely lowest for Kr (Ar ≈ Kr ≈ 0.29), so R as a whole is taken as false. (B) rejects A, which is true.\nWorking: gases with higher critical temperature (more easily liquefied) are adsorbed more on charcoal; Kr has the highest T_c of the four, so A is true. In R, Z_c is about 0.29 for both Ar and Kr, so \"Z_c lowest for Kr\" is not correct and R is false.\nKey idea: Adsorption depends on critical temperature (T_c highest for Kr); the Z_c claim in R is not correct, so A true, R false. Caveat: depends on the Z_c data table used.","traps":["Partly-true reason accepted: A compound reason is false if any part is false.","Wrong property cited for adsorption: Link adsorption to T_c (ease of liquefaction)."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"In figure, a straight line is given for Freundlich Adsorption (y = 3x + 2.505). The value of 1/n and log K are respectively.","options":["0.3 and log 2.505","3 and 2.505","3 and 0.7033","0.3 and 0.7033"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) take 1/n as reciprocal of the slope (1/3 ≈ 0.3). (A) wrongly applies log to the intercept; (C)/(D) give 0.7033, which does not follow from the intercept 2.505.\nWorking: (log x/m = log K + (1/n) log P; slope 1/n = 3, intercept log K = 2.505; note 1/n = 3 is outside the usual 0–1 range, but the arithmetic is unambiguous)\nKey idea: Compare y = mx + c directly: m = 1/n, c = log K.","traps":["Slope taken as n instead of 1/n: Equation is log x/m = log K + (1/n) log P; slope itself is 1/n.","Log applied again to the intercept: Intercept is already log K."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S2C27-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Statement I : For colloidal particles, the values of colligative properties are of small order as compared to values shown by true solutions at same concentration.\nStatement II : For colloidal particles, the potential difference between the fixed layer and the diffused layer of same charges is called the electrokinetic potential or zeta potential.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true and Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) misses that \"same charges\" should read \"opposite charges\". (A)/(D) reject statement I by thinking colloids have large colligative effects.\nWorking: colloidal particles are large aggregates so the number of particles is small (I true); zeta potential is between the fixed layer and the diffused layer of opposite charges (II false).\nKey idea: Double layer = fixed layer + diffuse layer of opposite charge.","traps":["'same' vs 'opposite' charges: Read every qualifier in definitions."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"The number of statement/s which are the characteristics of physisorption is ________. A. It is highly specific in nature B. Enthalpy of adsorption is high C. It decreases with increase in temperature D. It results into unimolecular layer E. No activation energy is needed","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (counting D, unimolecular layer); 1 (missing E).\nWorking: physisorption is not specific (A false), has low enthalpy of adsorption (B false), decreases with rise in temperature (C true), forms multimolecular layers (D false) and needs no activation energy (E true) → 2 (C and E).\nKey idea: A, B, D describe chemisorption.","traps":["Layer type: Physisorption forms multilayers."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Catalysis","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"The variation of the rate of an enzyme catalyzed reaction with substrate concentration is correctly represented by graph : (a) straight line rising from origin; (b) straight line falling; (c) curve rising from origin and levelling off (saturation); (d) curve falling and levelling off.","options":["(a)","(b)","(d)","(c)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) graph (a) is simple first-order behaviour with no saturation. (B)/(C) show rate falling with substrate.\nWorking: at low substrate concentration the rate rises almost in proportion; as the active sites of the enzyme fill up, the rate levels off at a maximum – graph (c), option (D).\nKey idea: At high [S] all active sites are occupied, so rate reaches a maximum (zero order in S).","traps":["Option letters do not match graph letters: Read the label inside each option, not the option letter.","Linear rate assumed: Enzymes saturate – the curve flattens."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":81,"q":"The number of incorrect statement/s from the following is/are ______. A. Water vapours are adsorbed by anhydrous calcium chloride. B. There is a decrease in surface energy during adsorption. C. As the adsorption proceeds, ΔH becomes more and more negative. D. Adsorption is accompanied by decrease in entropy of the system.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (missing adsorbed/absorbed); 3 (rejecting the entropy decrease).\nWorking: A incorrect – CaCl₂ absorbs (not adsorbs) water; C incorrect – ΔH becomes less negative as adsorption proceeds; B and D are correct → 2 incorrect statements.\nKey idea: CaCl₂ absorbs; silica gel adsorbs. ΔH less negative as the surface fills.","traps":["Adsorb vs absorb: CaCl₂ is a bulk absorbent; silica gel is an adsorbent.","Counting incorrect statements: Mark T/F, then count the Fs."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"Which of the following salt solutions would coagulate the colloid solution formed when FeCl₃ is added to NaOH solution, at the fastest rate?","options":["10 mL of 0.15 mol dm⁻³ CaCl₂","10 mL of 0.2 mol dm⁻³ AlCl₃","10 mL of 0.1 mol dm⁻³ Na₂SO₄","10 mL of 0.1 mol dm⁻³ Ca₃(PO₄)₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Na₂SO₄ / (D) PO₄³⁻ chosen if sol taken as positive (FeCl₃ into water); (D) also insoluble salt.\nWorking: FeCl₃ added to NaOH gives Fe(OH)₃ that adsorbs OH⁻ (ion in excess), so the sol is negative and is coagulated by cations. By the Hardy–Schulze rule the highest-charge cation, Al³⁺ (also at the highest concentration), coagulates fastest – option (B).\nKey idea: Sol adsorbs the ion in excess: OH⁻ here.","traps":["Charge of Fe(OH)₃ sol: Check which ion is in excess."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"Match List I and List II\nList I: A. Osmosis; B. Reverse osmosis; C. Electro osmosis; D. Electrophoresis\nList II: I. Solvent molecules pass through semi permeable membrane towards solvent side. II. Movement of charged colloidal particles under the influence of applied electric potential towards oppositely charged electrodes. III. Solvent molecules pass through semi permeable membrane towards solution side. IV. Dispersion medium moves in an electric field.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps electro-osmosis and electrophoresis; (C)/(D) swap osmosis and reverse osmosis directions.\nWorking: osmosis – solvent passes through the membrane towards the solution side (A-III); reverse osmosis – solvent is forced towards the solvent side (B-I); electro-osmosis – the dispersion medium moves in an electric field (C-IV); electrophoresis – charged colloidal particles move to the oppositely charged electrode (D-II).\nKey idea: Electrophoresis moves the particles; electro-osmosis moves the medium.","traps":["Particles vs medium moving: Phoresis = particles carried.","Direction of osmotic flow: Osmosis: solvent → solution side."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Assertion (A): In expensive scientific instruments, silica gel is kept in watch-glasses or in semipermeable membrane bags. Reason (R): Silica gel adsorbs moisture from air via adsorption, thus protects the instrument from water corrosion (rusting) and / or prevents malfunctioning. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"D: treating R as unrelated. B/C: doubting the watch-glass/bag detail.\nWorking: silica gel adsorbs water vapour on its large surface and keeps the air around the instrument dry; that is exactly why it is kept in open watch-glasses or porous bags inside instruments. Both statements are true and R explains A.\nKey idea: Moisture removal by adsorption is exactly why silica gel is kept near instruments.","traps":["Unusual detail in Assertion: Judge the chemistry: silica gel is placed open near the instrument to adsorb moisture."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"The graph of log(x/m) vs log p for an adsorption process is a straight line inclined at an angle of 45° with intercept equal to 0.6020. The mass of gas adsorbed per unit mass of adsorbent at the pressure of 0.4 atm is ____ × 10⁻¹ (Nearest integer). Given: log 2 = 0.3010","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (forgetting to multiply by p), 6 (using intercept as k), 10 (using log form).\nWorking: slope 1/n = tan 45° = 1; log k = 0.602 ⇒ k = 4; x/m = 4 × 0.4 = 1.6 = 16 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: tan 45° = 1 ⇒ x/m = kp with k = antilog 0.602 = 4.","traps":["Intercept is log k: Take antilog: k = 4."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Adding surfactants in non polar solvent, the micelles structure will look like (○ polar head; ~ non-polar tail)","options":["d","a","c","b"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) b is the normal aqueous micelle, tempting from the NCERT figure. (C) c and (A) d are layered/unaggregated arrangements, not micelles.\nWorking: in a non-polar solvent the micelle is reversed – polar heads cluster inside and hydrocarbon tails face the solvent – picture (a), option (B).\nKey idea: Like faces like: in a non-polar solvent the tails face outward and the heads hide inside.","traps":["NCERT aqueous picture applied directly: Ask which part matches the solvent: non-polar tails face non-polar solvent."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan31S1C27-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I: A. Physisorption; B. Chemisorption; C. N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) (catalyst: Fe(s)); D. Analytical Application of Adsorption. List II: I. Single Layer Adsorption; II. 20–40 kJ mol⁻¹; III. Chromatography; IV. Heterogeneous catalysis. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) misplace physisorption. (B) gives chemisorption to chromatography and C to single layer.\nWorking: physisorption has enthalpy of about 20–40 kJ mol⁻¹ (A-II); chemisorption forms a single layer (B-I); the Haber process on solid Fe is heterogeneous catalysis (C-IV); chromatography is an analytical application of adsorption (D-III).\nKey idea: Physisorption ΔH 20–40 kJ mol⁻¹; chemisorption monolayer; Fe in Haber process is heterogeneous.","traps":["Single layer vs multilayer: Chemisorption is unimolecular; physisorption multimolecular."],"topics":["Adsorption","Catalysis"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Catalysis","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g); (B) CO(g) + 3H₂(g) → CH₄(g) + H₂O(g); (C) CO(g) + H₂(g) → HCHO(g); (D) CO(g) + 2H₂(g) → CH₃OH(g). List-II: (I) Cu; (II) Cu/ZnO–Cr₂O₃; (III) FeₓOᵧ + K₂O + Al₂O₃; (IV) Ni. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) give Haber the methanol catalyst; (D) swaps Ni and Cu for the CH₄/HCHO pair.\nWorking: Haber process uses promoted iron oxide (III); CO + 3H₂ → CH₄ over Ni (IV); CO + H₂ → HCHO over Cu (I); methanol over Cu/ZnO–Cr₂O₃ (II).\nKey idea: Same reactants (CO + H₂), different catalysts → different products; Fe-based for NH₃.","traps":["Ni vs Cu for CO + H₂: Ni → CH₄ (full hydrogenation); Cu → HCHO (mild)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"For micelle formation, which of the following statements are correct? (A) Micelle formation is an exothermic process. (B) Micelle formation is an endothermic process. (C) The entropy change is positive. (D) The entropy change is negative.","options":["A and D only","A and C only","B and C only","B and D only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A and D: exothermic with ΔS < 0 (treating aggregation as ordering); (C) B and C: endothermic, entropy-driven (arguable, but not the official answer); (D) B and D gives ΔG > 0 and could never be spontaneous.\nWorking: micellisation is entropy-driven (ΔS > 0, release of structured water around the hydrocarbon tails); for common ionic surfactants near room temperature ΔH is small and slightly negative, so A and C. Note: the sign of ΔH changes with temperature and surfactant, so \"B and C\" is also argued; the official answer is \"A and C\".\nKey idea: Aggregation of tails frees ordered water, so ΔS > 0 dominates; ΔH is small.","traps":["Aggregation assumed to lower entropy: Count the solvent: water released from around tails gains much more entropy.","Exothermic vs endothermic undecidable: The official answer takes micelle formation as exothermic (A and C)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Finest gold is red in colour, as the size of the particles increases, it appears purple then blue and finally gold.\nReason R : The colour of the colloidal solution depends on the wavelength of light scattered by the dispersed particles.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below;","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who think colour is due to absorption only; (C)/(D) for those unsure of the gold sol colour sequence.\nWorking: both are old-NCERT lines on the colour of colloids; particle size changes the wavelength scattered, which explains the gold sol colour change.\nKey idea: Particle size → scattered wavelength → colour; that is exactly the gold sol case.","traps":["Explanation link: Ask whether R is the cause of the colour change in A."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Activated charcoal adsorbs SO₂ more efficiently than CH₄.\nReason R : Gases with lower critical temperatures are readily adsorbed by activated charcoal.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): student accepts R without checking the direction; (D): student thinks CH₄ (smaller) is adsorbed more.\nWorking: SO₂ (Tc ≈ 430 K) is more easily liquefied than CH₄ (Tc ≈ 190 K), so it is adsorbed more; easily liquefiable gases with HIGHER critical temperatures are adsorbed more, so R is false.\nKey idea: Higher Tc → stronger van der Waals forces → more adsorption.","traps":["Lower vs higher critical temperature: Easily liquefied gases have high Tc and adsorb more.","Small molecule adsorbs more: Physisorption extent tracks intermolecular forces, not size."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Assertion (A): Dissolved substances can be removed from a colloidal solution by diffusion through a parchment paper. Reason (R): Particles in a true solution cannot pass through parchment paper but the colloidal particles can pass through the parchment paper.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) accept the reversed reason; (D) rejects the correct dialysis statement.\nWorking: (A) describes dialysis — true. (R) is reversed: true-solution particles (ions, small molecules) pass through parchment; colloidal particles do not. So A true, R false.\nKey idea: Parchment lets small solute particles through and holds back colloidal particles.","traps":["Membrane permeability reversed: Recall that dialysis keeps the sol inside the bag."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Among the following the number of curves not in accordance with Freundlich adsorption isotherm is ____. Graphs: (a) log(x/m) vs log P – a curve rising from the origin and levelling off; (b) log(x/m) vs P – a straight line through the origin; (c) log(x/m) vs P – a straight line with positive intercept; (d) log(x/m) vs log P – a straight line with positive intercept.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 2 (accepting (c) because it is a straight line), 1, 4.\nWorking: Freundlich: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log P, a straight line of positive intercept on log(x/m) vs log P axes. Only (d) fits; (a) is curved on log–log axes, (b) and (c) use P not log P. So 3 are not in accordance.\nKey idea: Both the axes (log–log) and linearity with intercept must match.","traps":["Straight line on wrong axes: Read the x-axis label: P vs log P."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"100 mL of 5% (w/v) solution of NaCl in water was prepared in 250 mL beaker. Albumin from the egg was poured into NaCl solution and stirred well. This resulted in a/an :","options":["Lyophilic sol","Lyophobic sol","Emulsion","Precipitate"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats albumin like a metal sol; (C) emulsion needs two liquids; (D) assumes NaCl salts out the protein.\nWorking: egg albumin (a protein) disperses spontaneously in water to give a lyophilic (macromolecular) sol; the NaCl solution is only the dispersion medium and dilute salt does not coagulate a lyophilic sol.\nKey idea: Proteins form lyophilic sols; these are stable to small amounts of electrolyte.","traps":["Salt assumed to coagulate: Lyophilic sols need large amounts of electrolyte to coagulate."],"topics":["Colloid types","Colloid properties"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are the critical temperatures of some of the gases: He 5.2 K; CH₄ 190 K; CO₂ 304.2 K; NH₃ 405.5 K. The gas showing least adsorption on a definite amount of charcoal is:","options":["He","CH₄","CO₂","NH₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) NH₃ is the most adsorbed (answers the reverse question); (B)/(C) intermediate.\nWorking: easily liquefiable gases (higher critical temperature, stronger van der Waals forces) are adsorbed more; He has the lowest Tc, so least adsorption.\nKey idea: Higher Tc → more adsorption; lowest Tc → least.","traps":["Most vs least: Underline \"least\"."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Emulsions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Emulsions of oil in water are unstable and sometimes they separate into two layers on standing. Statement II: For stabilisation of an emulsion, excess of electrolyte is added. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): accepts electrolyte as a stabiliser by analogy with peptisation; (B)/(D): doubt the instability of emulsions.\nWorking: oil-in-water emulsions are unstable and may separate into layers (true); emulsions are stabilised by emulsifying agents (soaps, proteins, gums), not by excess electrolyte, which actually helps break them (false).\nKey idea: Electrolytes coagulate/break colloids; emulsions need emulsifiers.","traps":["Electrolyte as stabiliser: Peptisation is a little electrolyte for a sol; emulsions need emulsifying agents; excess electrolyte destabilises."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"When 200 mL of 0.2 M acetic acid is shaken with 0.6 g of wood charcoal, the final concentration of acetic acid after adsorption is 0.1 M. The mass of acetic acid adsorbed per gram of carbon is ____ g.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 or 1.2 (forgot to divide by 0.6 g); 0.03 (moles not converted to mass); 20 (used 2 L instead of 0.2 L).\nWorking: moles adsorbed = 0.200 L × (0.2 − 0.1) M = 0.02 mol = 1.2 g; per gram charcoal = 1.2/0.6 = 2 g.\nKey idea: Δn = V × ΔM; x/m = Δn × 60 / 0.6.","traps":["Per gram of charcoal not applied: Divide total adsorbed mass by 0.6 g."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Incorrect statement for Tyndall effect is:","options":["The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium differ greatly in magnitude.","The diameter of the dispersed particles is much smaller than the wavelength of the light used.","During projection of movies in the cinemas hall, Tyndall effect is noticed.","It is used to distinguish a true solution from a colloidal solution."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is a true NCERT condition that sounds odd ('differ greatly'); (C) and (D) are true everyday/diagnostic uses.\nWorking: Tyndall scattering needs particles whose diameter is NOT much smaller than the wavelength of light, and a large refractive-index difference between phases; it is seen in a cinema beam and separates colloids from true solutions. So (B) is the incorrect statement.\nKey idea: Scattering needs particle size comparable to wavelength – true-solution particles are too small to scatter.","traps":["Size condition reversed: Remember: tiny solute particles do not scatter."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Using very little soap while washing clothes, does not serve the purpose of cleaning of clothes because","options":["soap particles remain floating in water as ions","the hydrophobic part of soap is not able to take away grease","the micelles are not formed due to concentration of soap, below its CMC value","colloidal structure of soap in water is completely disturbed."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) partly true (ions present) but not the reason; (B) hydrophobic part can bind grease – problem is no micelles; (D) vague.\nWorking: soap cleans by forming micelles that trap grease; micelles form only above the critical micelle concentration. Too little soap stays below CMC.\nKey idea: Below CMC no micelles, so no emulsification of grease.","traps":["True fact but not the reason: Ask what cleaning needs: micelles."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"2.0 g of H₂ gas is adsorbed on 2.5 g of platinum powder at 300 K and 1 bar pressure. The volume of the gas adsorbed per gram of the adsorbent is ______ mL. (Given : R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"9960","range":[9960.0,9960.0],"explain":"Common slips: 24900 – forgetting per gram; 19920 – using 2 mol H₂ (mass taken as moles); 22400-based values – using STP molar volume.\nWorking: n(H₂) = 1 mol; V = nRT/P = 0.083 × 300 = 24.9 L = 24900 mL; per gram Pt = 24900/2.5 = 9960 mL.\nKey idea: 2 g H₂ = 1 mol; at 300 K, 1 bar, 1 mol occupies 24.9 L.","traps":["Per gram division missed: Divide by 2.5 g Pt.","STP molar volume used: Use given T, P and R."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Lyophilic colloid, (B) Emulsion, (C) Positively charged colloid, (D) Negatively charged colloid. List-II: (I) Liquid–liquid colloid, (II) Protective colloid, (III) FeCl₃ + NaOH, (IV) FeCl₃ + hot water colloid.","options":["(A)–(II), (B)–(I), (C)–(IV), (D)–(III)","(A)–(III), (B)–(I), (C)–(IV), (D)–(II)","(A)–(II), (B)–(I), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(III), (B)–(II), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) – call the lyophilic colloid \"FeCl₃ + NaOH\" (that sol is lyophobic); (C) – swaps the charges of the two Fe(OH)₃ sols.\nWorking: lyophilic sols act as protective colloids; an emulsion is liquid-in-liquid; FeCl₃ hydrolysed in hot water gives a positive Fe(OH)₃ sol (adsorbs Fe³⁺); FeCl₃ added to NaOH (excess OH⁻) gives a negative sol (adsorbs OH⁻) → option (A).\nKey idea: The sol adsorbs the ion present in excess: Fe³⁺ (water method) → positive; OH⁻ (NaOH method) → negative.","traps":["Charges of the two Fe(OH)₃ sols swapped: Find the ion in excess; it is adsorbed and sets the charge.","Lyophilic vs lyophobic sol: Metal hydroxide sols are lyophobic; starch/gelatin type are lyophilic and protective."],"topics":["Colloid properties","Colloid types","Emulsions"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Graph: log(x/m) on the y-axis vs log p on the x-axis is a straight line with slope = 1 and intercept = 0.602. If the initial pressure of a gas is 0.03 atm, the mass of the gas adsorbed per gram of the adsorbent is ______ × 10⁻² g.","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 2 (k taken as the intercept 0.602, giving x/m ≈ 0.018 → 1.8 ≈ 2); 400 (forgetting to multiply by p, i.e. x/m = k = 4 g); 3 (using 1/n = 1 but adding log p linearly).\nWorking: Freundlich isotherm log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log p; slope 1/n = 1, log k = 0.602 → k = 4; x/m = 4 × 0.03 = 0.12 g = 12 × 10⁻² g.\nKey idea: Intercept is log k: k = antilog 0.602 = 4.","traps":["Intercept used as k: Intercept = log k, so take the antilog."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The Zeta potential is related to which property of colloids?","options":["Colour","Tyndall effect","Charge on the surface of colloidal particles","Brownian movement"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) colour and (B) Tyndall effect are optical properties; (D) Brownian movement is a kinetic property unrelated to the double layer.\nWorking: zeta (electrokinetic) potential is the potential difference across the fixed and diffuse layers of the double layer formed by the surface charge on colloidal particles.\nKey idea: Zeta potential = electrokinetic potential of the charged double layer.","traps":["Optical vs electrical properties: Potential is an electrical quantity, so pick the charge option."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Negatively charged sol, (B) Macromolecular colloid, (C) Positively charged sol, (D) Cheese. List-II: (I) Fe₂O₃·xH₂O, (II) CdS sol, (III) Starch, (IV) a gel. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(IV), (D)–(I)","(A)–(II), (B)–(I), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(I), (B)–(III), (C)–(II), (D)–(IV)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps the positive and negative sols; (A) pairs positive sol with gel and cheese with Fe₂O₃; (B) calls Fe₂O₃·xH₂O macromolecular.\nWorking: metal sulphide sols (CdS) are negative; starch is a macromolecular colloid; hydrated ferric oxide sol is positive; cheese is a gel (liquid dispersed in solid).\nKey idea: Sulphide sols negative, hydrated oxide sols positive.","traps":["Positive vs negative sols: Hydrated metal oxides positive, sulphides negative."],"topics":["Colloid properties","Colloid types"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following is a correct statement?","options":["Brownian motion destabilises sols.","Any amount of dispersed phase can be added to emulsion without destabilising it.","Mixing two oppositely charged sols in equal amount neutralises charges and stabilises colloids.","Presence of equal and similar charges on colloidal particles provides stability to the colloidal solution."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses effect of Brownian motion; (B) ignores emulsion breaking; (C) says mutual coagulation \"stabilises\".\nWorking: like charges repel and prevent coagulation; Brownian motion stabilises; emulsions break with excess dispersed phase; mixing opposite sols coagulates them.\nKey idea: Stability of a lyophobic sol = like charges + Brownian motion; neutralisation causes coagulation.","traps":["Brownian motion effect: Brownian motion prevents settling, so it stabilises.","Mutual coagulation: Neutralised particles coagulate; they do not stabilise."],"topics":["Colloid properties","Emulsions"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Which one of the following is correct for the adsorption of a gas at a given temperature on a solid surface?","options":["ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0","ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0","ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0","ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) endothermic adsorption; (D) entropy increase – picked by those who equate spontaneity with ΔS > 0.\nWorking: adsorption of a gas restricts its motion (ΔS < 0), so for ΔG < 0 the process must be exothermic (ΔH < 0).\nKey idea: Gas → surface: order increases (ΔS < 0), so ΔH must be negative for the process to occur.","traps":["Adsorption lowers entropy: Spontaneity here is enthalpy-driven: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS < 0 needs ΔH sufficiently negative.","Adsorption is exothermic: Recall heat of adsorption is always released for gases on solids."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The sol given below with negatively charged colloidal particles is:","options":["FeCl₃ added to hot water","KI added to AgNO₃ solution","AgNO₃ added to KI solution","Al₂O₃·xH₂O in water"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the mirror of the answer – which solution is in excess is easily reversed; (A) and (D) are standard positive sols that some students mark negative.\nWorking: when AgNO₃ is added to excess KI, AgI adsorbs the common ion I⁻ and the sol is AgI/I⁻ (negative). (A) Fe₂O₃·xH₂O/Fe³⁺ and (D) hydrated alumina are positive; (B) KI into excess AgNO₃ gives AgI/Ag⁺ (positive).\nKey idea: The precipitate adsorbs the common ion present in excess – the solution in the beaker, not the one added.","traps":["Which reagent is in excess: The ion of the beaker solution is adsorbed.","Hydrated oxides are positive sols: Metal hydroxide/oxide sols adsorb M³⁺ → positive."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Tyndall effect is more effectively shown by:","options":["true solution","lyophilic colloid","lyophobic colloid","suspension"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) suspension – larger particles thought to scatter more; (B) lyophilic – chosen by those who forget the refractive-index condition; (A) no scattering.\nWorking: Tyndall effect needs particles of colloidal size with a large refractive-index difference from the medium; lyophobic sols meet this better than lyophilic sols (whose solvated particles have refractive index close to the medium). True solutions do not scatter; suspensions are not transparent colloidal systems.\nKey idea: Large refractive-index difference (lyophobic) → strong Tyndall scattering.","traps":["Bigger particles scatter more: Tyndall effect is defined for colloids; suspensions are opaque and settle.","Lyophilic vs lyophobic: Solvation makes lyophilic particles optically similar to the medium."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Lyophilic sols are more stable than lyophobic sols because:","options":["there is a strong electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged colloidal particles.","the colloidal particles have positive charge.","the colloidal particles have no charge.","the colloidal particles are solvated."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) charge repulsion stabilises both sol types (and need not be negative), so it does not explain the difference; (B)/(C) sign or absence of charge is irrelevant.\nWorking: lyophilic sols are stabilised both by charge and by extensive solvation of the particles; solvation is the extra factor lyophobic sols lack, so it explains their greater stability.\nKey idea: Charge is common to both; solvation is what lyophobic sols lack.","traps":["Charge as the reason: Ask what differs between the two sol types – solvation."],"topics":["Colloid properties","Colloid types"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Select the graph that correctly describes the adsorption isotherms at two temperatures T₁ and T₂ (T₁ > T₂) for a gas: (x – mass of the gas adsorbed; m – mass of adsorbent; P – pressure). [Four x/m vs P graphs]","options":["Two straight lines from the origin, T₂ line steeper (above) than T₁","Two saturating curves, T₁ curve above T₂","Two straight lines from the origin, T₁ line steeper (above) than T₂","Two saturating curves, T₂ curve above T₁"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) right temperature order but linear (Henry-type) shape; (B) correct shape but higher temperature placed higher; (C) both wrong.\nWorking: adsorption is exothermic, so x/m falls as temperature rises – the lower-temperature (T₂) isotherm lies above. A Freundlich isotherm rises steeply and then levels off (x/m = kP¹ᐟⁿ), so curves, not straight lines: option (D).\nKey idea: Exothermic adsorption → lower T gives more adsorption; isotherm saturates at high P.","traps":["Higher T gives more adsorption: Physisorption is exothermic; Le Chatelier lowers x/m at higher T.","Linear vs curved isotherm: x/m vs P curves and levels off; log(x/m) vs log P is linear."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"CH₄ is adsorbed on 1 g charcoal at 0 °C following the Freundlich adsorption isotherm. 10.0 mL of CH₄ is adsorbed at 100 mm of Hg, whereas 15.0 mL is adsorbed at 200 mm of Hg. The volume of CH₄ adsorbed at 300 mm of Hg is 10ˣ mL. The value of x is ____ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer) [Use log₁₀2 = 0.3010, log₁₀3 = 0.4771]","type":"num","answer":"128","range":[128.0,128.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 148 (assuming adsorption linear in p: V = 30 mL, log 30 = 1.477), 100 (taking x = 1), 124 (assuming 1/n = 0.5).\nWorking: x/m = kp¹ᐟⁿ; 15/10 = 2¹ᐟⁿ → 1/n = log1.5/log2 = 0.1761/0.3010 = 0.585; V(300) = 10 × 3⁰·⁵⁸⁵ → log V = 1 + 0.585 × 0.4771 = 1.279; x = 1.279 = 128 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Take ratios to eliminate k, then work in logs throughout.","traps":["Assuming linear adsorption: Freundlich exponent must come from the data.","Answer format 10ˣ: The asked quantity is x = log₁₀V."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In Freundlich adsorption isotherm, slope of AB line is:","options":["log n with (n > 1)","n with (n, 0.1 to 0.5)","log(1/n) with (n < 1)","1/n with (1/n = 0 to 1)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) – put a logarithm on the slope (confusing slope with intercept log k); (B) – uses n instead of 1/n.\nWorking: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log P, so the slope is 1/n, which lies between 0 and 1.\nKey idea: Slope = 1/n (0 to 1); intercept = log k.","traps":["Slope vs intercept: Intercept is log k; slope is the exponent 1/n."],"img":["img/jee/JM21Feb24S1C09-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Most suitable salt which can be used for efficient clotting of blood will be:","options":["NaHCO₃","FeSO₄","Mg(HCO₃)₂","FeCl₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) FeSO₄ – Fe²⁺ has lower charge; (A) and (C) – Na⁺/Mg²⁺ low charge and bicarbonates are not used to stop bleeding.\nWorking: blood is a negatively charged colloid; the highly charged Fe³⁺ (Hardy–Schulze rule) coagulates it best, and FeCl₃ is used to stop bleeding.\nKey idea: Higher charge on the cation opposite to the sol charge → stronger coagulation; Fe³⁺ wins.","traps":["Fe²⁺ vs Fe³⁺: Compare ionic charge: Fe³⁺ > Fe²⁺."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In Freundlich adsorption isotherm at moderate pressure, the extent of adsorption (x/m) is directly proportional to Pˣ. The value of x is:","options":["zero","1/n","1","∞"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) zero is the high-pressure limit; (C) 1 is the low-pressure limit; (D) meaningless.\nWorking: Freundlich isotherm x/m = kP¹ᐟⁿ (n > 1) applies at intermediate pressure; at low P exponent → 1, at high P → 0.\nKey idea: Low P: 1; moderate P: 1/n; high P: 0.","traps":["Pressure regimes swapped: Tie \"moderate\" to the fractional exponent 1/n."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one of the following statements is FALSE for hydrophilic sols?","options":["Their viscosity is of the order of that of H₂O.","The sols cannot be easily coagulated.","They do not require electrolytes for stability.","These sols are reversible in nature."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) true – stabilised by solvation; (C) true – no electrolyte needed; (D) true – reversible. Students mistake them for hydrophobic-sol properties.\nWorking: hydrophilic (lyophilic) sols are heavily solvated, so their viscosity is much higher than that of water; (B)–(D) are true properties.\nKey idea: Hydrophilic sols: high viscosity, low surface tension, reversible, stable.","traps":["Viscosity property swapped: Lyophilic → strong solvation → higher viscosity."],"topics":["Colloid types","Colloid properties"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"3.12 g of oxygen is adsorbed on 1.2 g of platinum metal. The volume of oxygen adsorbed per gram of the adsorbent at 1 atm and 300 K in L is ______. [R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (using O = 16 as molar mass), 2.4 if reported without dividing by the 1.2 g of Pt, 1.8 (using 22.4 L mol⁻¹ instead of 300 K).\nWorking: n(O₂) = 3.12/32 = 0.0975 mol; V = nRT/P = 0.0975 × 0.0821 × 300 = 2.40 L; per gram = 2.40/1.2 = 2.0 L.\nKey idea: x/m here is volume per gram: nRT/P ÷ m.","traps":["Atomic mass of O used: O₂ molar mass = 32."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The nature of charge on resulting colloidal particles when FeCl₃ is added to excess of hot water is:","options":["Positive","Sometimes positive and sometimes negative","Neutral","Negative"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) – confuses with FeCl₃ added to NaOH (adsorbs OH⁻); (B) – conflates the two preparations; (C) – colloids assumed neutral.\nWorking: hydrolysis gives Fe₂O₃·xH₂O, which preferentially adsorbs Fe³⁺ (common ion) → positively charged sol.\nKey idea: Excess FeCl₃ side → Fe³⁺ adsorbed → positive.","traps":["Mixed up with FeCl₃ in NaOH: Ion in excess is adsorbed: here Fe³⁺."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The conditions given below are in the context of observing Tyndall effect in colloidal solutions:\n(A) The diameter of the colloidal particles is comparable to the wavelength of light used.\n(B) The diameter of the colloidal particles is much smaller than the wavelength of light used.\n(C) The diameter of the colloidal particles is much larger than the wavelength of light used.\n(D) The refractive indices of the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium are comparable.\n(E) The dispersed phase has a very different refractive index from the dispersion medium.\nChoose the most appropriate conditions from the options given below:","options":["(A) and (E) only","(C) and (D) only","(A) and (D) only","(B) and (E) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) keeps the size condition but reverses the refractive index condition; (D) reverses the size condition; (B) reverses both.\nWorking: Tyndall effect needs (i) particle diameter not much smaller than the wavelength of light and (ii) a large difference in refractive index between dispersed phase and medium – (A) and (E).\nKey idea: Scattering needs comparable size and a refractive index mismatch.","traps":["Refractive index condition reversed: No index difference → no scattering.","Size condition reversed: \"Not much smaller\" ≈ comparable."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"100 ml of 0.0018% (w/v) solution of Cl⁻ ion was the minimum concentration of Cl⁻ required to precipitate a negative sol in one h. The coagulating value of Cl⁻ ion is ______ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 0 (mol L⁻¹, 5×10⁻⁴ rounds to 0), 507 (µmol L⁻¹), 18 (mg L⁻¹), 5 (using 100 mL volume as litres).\nWorking: 0.0018 g per 100 mL = 0.018 g L⁻¹; 0.018/35.5 = 5.07 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹ = 0.507 mmol L⁻¹ → nearest integer 1 (coagulating value in millimoles per litre).\nKey idea: Coagulating value = millimoles of ion per litre; the 100 mL volume is irrelevant once concentration is known.","traps":["Unit of coagulating value: Express in millimoles per litre.","Volume given but irrelevant: Percentage (w/v) already fixes concentration."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"When silver nitrate solution is added to potassium iodide solution then the sol produced is :","options":["AgI / I⁻","AgI / Ag⁺","KI / NO₃⁻","AgNO₃ / NO₃⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for the reverse order of addition (KI added to AgNO₃); (C) and (D) are not colloids.\nWorking: AgNO₃ added to KI means KI is in excess; AgI particles adsorb the common ion I⁻, giving a negatively charged AgI/I⁻ sol.\nKey idea: The solution you add TO is in excess; its common ion is adsorbed.","traps":["Order of addition reversed: The receiving solution (KI) is in excess → I⁻ adsorbed."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Match List I (Example of colloids) with List II (Classification):\n(a) Cheese (b) Pumice stone (c) Hair cream (d) Cloud\n(i) dispersion of liquid in liquid (ii) dispersion of liquid in gas (iii) dispersion of gas in solid (iv) dispersion of liquid in solid\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps hair cream and cloud; (B) swaps pumice stone and hair cream; (C) swaps cheese and pumice stone.\nWorking: cheese – liquid in solid (gel); pumice – gas in solid (solid sol/foam); hair cream – liquid in liquid (emulsion); cloud – liquid in gas (aerosol).\nKey idea: Name dispersed phase first, then medium.","traps":["Phase order: Read \"dispersion of X in Y\" as X dispersed in Y."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"CO₂ gas adsorbs on charcoal following Freundlich adsorption isotherm. For a given amount of charcoal, the mass of CO₂ adsorbed becomes 64 times when the pressure of CO₂ is doubled. The value of n in the Freundlich isotherm equation is _________ × 10⁻². (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"17","range":[17.0,17.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 600 (reporting 1/n = 6 as ×10⁻²), 6, or 16 (truncating 16.67).\nWorking: x/m = kP¹ᐟⁿ; 64 = 2¹ᐟⁿ → 1/n = 6 → n = 0.1667 = 16.67 × 10⁻² → 17.\nKey idea: Ratio of masses = (P ratio)¹ᐟⁿ; here 1/n = 6, so n = 1/6.","traps":["n vs 1/n: Write 2¹ᐟⁿ = 2⁶ then invert.","Rounding 16.67: Round, do not truncate."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: SO₂(g) is adsorbed to a large extent than H₂(g) on activated charcoal.\nReason R: SO₂(g) has a higher critical temperature than H₂(g).\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct.","A is correct but R is not correct."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for those unsure that Tc is the cause; (C)/(D) for those who think the lighter gas adsorbs more or misjudge Tc.\nWorking: easily liquefiable gases (higher critical temperature, stronger van der Waals forces) are physisorbed more; SO₂ (Tc ≈ 430 K) ≫ H₂ (Tc ≈ 33 K).\nKey idea: Higher Tc → stronger intermolecular forces → more physisorption.","traps":["Critical temperature is the cause: Recall NCERT ties extent of adsorption to Tc directly."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The INCORRECT statements below regarding colloidal solutions is:","options":["A colloidal solution shows colligative properties.","An ordinary filter paper can stop the flow of colloidal particles.","The flocculating power of Al³⁺ is more than that of Na⁺.","A colloidal solution shows Brownian motion of colloidal particles."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true (values small but present); (C) true by Hardy–Schulze rule; (D) true.\nWorking: colloidal particles (1–1000 nm) pass through ordinary filter paper (only ultrafilters or parchment stop them); colligative properties (small), Hardy–Schulze (Al³⁺ > Na⁺) and Brownian motion are all correct.\nKey idea: Ordinary filter paper pores (>1 μm) let colloidal particles through.","traps":["Colligative property statement doubted: Small is not zero; the statement is true.","Incorrect-statement wording: Pick the false one."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A colloidal system consisting of a gas dispersed in a solid is called a/an:","options":["solid sol","gel","aerosol","foam"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) foam – gas in liquid; (C) aerosol – liquid/solid in gas; (B) gel – liquid in solid.\nWorking: by the old NCERT table, gas in solid = solid sol (pumice stone, foam rubber); foam = gas in liquid; aerosol = solid/liquid in gas; gel = liquid in solid.\nKey idea: Name follows the medium: solid medium → solid sol / gel.","traps":["Foam chosen: Foam = gas in liquid; solid foam is called solid sol in NCERT."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"For the coagulation of a negative sol, the species below, that has the highest flocculating power is :","options":["SO₄²⁻","Ba²⁺","Na⁺","PO₄³⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) PO₄³⁻ has the largest charge but the same sign as the sol; (A) likewise; (C) Na⁺ is a cation of lower charge.\nWorking: a negative sol is coagulated by cations; by the Hardy–Schulze rule, power rises with charge, so Ba²⁺ > Na⁺; the anions do not coagulate a negative sol.\nKey idea: First match the opposite sign, then pick the highest charge.","traps":["Highest charge regardless of sign: Only ions opposite in charge to the sol coagulate it."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The charges on the colloidal CdS sol and TiO₂ sol are, respectively :","options":["positive and positive","positive and negative","negative and negative","negative and positive"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses both; (A)/(C) assume both sols carry the same charge.\nWorking: metal sulphide sols (As₂S₃, CdS) are negatively charged; hydrated metal oxide sols such as TiO₂ are positively charged.\nKey idea: Sulphide sols negative; oxide/hydroxide sols positive.","traps":["Sulphide vs oxide charge swapped: Sulphide: negative (adsorbs S²⁻/HS⁻); oxide: positive."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List-I (Colloid Preparation Method) with List-II (Chemical Reaction). (a) Hydrolysis; (b) Reduction; (c) Oxidation; (d) Double decomposition. (i) 2AuCl₃ + 3HCHO + 3H₂O → 2Au(sol) + 3HCOOH + 6HCl; (ii) As₂O₃ + 3H₂S → As₂S₃(sol) + 3H₂O; (iii) SO₂ + 2H₂S → 3S(sol) + 2H₂O; (iv) FeCl₃ + 3H₂O → Fe(OH)₃(sol) + 3HCl. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps oxidation and double decomposition and puts reduction on the S sol; (C) gives double decomposition to the gold sol; (D) puts hydrolysis on gold and oxidation on Fe(OH)₃.\nWorking: FeCl₃ hydrolysis gives Fe(OH)₃ sol; HCHO reduces AuCl₃ to gold sol; H₂S is oxidised by SO₂ to sulphur sol; As₂O₃ + H₂S is double decomposition – (a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii).\nKey idea: Fe(OH)₃ sol = hydrolysis; Au sol by HCHO = reduction; S sol from H₂S = oxidation; As₂S₃ = double decomposition.","traps":["SO₂ + H₂S labelled reduction: The method is named after what happens to the species that forms the sol: H₂S is oxidised to S, so it is \"oxidation\".","As₂S₃ sol taken as reduction: As₂O₃ + H₂S is ion exchange (double decomposition); no change of oxidation state."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The flocculation value of HCl for arsenic sulphide sol. is 30 m mole L⁻¹. If H₂SO₄ is used for the flocculation of arsenic sulphide, the amount, in grams, of H₂SO₄ in 250 ml required for the above purpose is ______.\n(molecular mass of H₂SO₄ = 98 g/mol)","type":"num","answer":"0.36 to 0.38","range":[0.36,0.38],"explain":"Common slips: Using H₂SO₄ = HCl mole-for-mole gives 0.735 g; applying Hardy–Schulze to SO₄²⁻ (divalent anion) gives other values.\nWorking: As₂S₃ sol is negative, so H⁺ is the coagulating ion; 30 mmol L⁻¹ of H⁺ needs 15 mmol L⁻¹ H₂SO₄, i.e. 3.75 mmol in 250 mL = 0.3675 g.\nKey idea: Count the coagulating cation (H⁺): each H₂SO₄ supplies two.","traps":["Equal moles of acid assumed: Convert via moles of H⁺.","Divalent sulphate treated as active ion: Only the ion opposite to the sol's charge matters."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"As per Hardy-Schulze formulation, the flocculation values of the following for ferric hydroxide sol are in the order :","options":["AlCl₃ > K₃[Fe(CN)₆] > K₂CrO₄ > KBr = KNO₃","K₃[Fe(CN)₆] < K₂CrO₄ < AlCl₃ < KBr < KNO₃","K₃[Fe(CN)₆] > AlCl₃ > K₂CrO₄ > KBr > KNO₃","K₃[Fe(CN)₆] < K₂CrO₄ < KBr = KNO₃ = AlCl₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) count Al³⁺ as active (same charge as sol); (B) treats Br⁻ and NO₃⁻ as unequal.\nWorking: Fe(OH)₃ sol is positive, so anions coagulate; power [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ > CrO₄²⁻ > Cl⁻ = Br⁻ = NO₃⁻; flocculation value is inversely related.\nKey idea: Only the ion opposite to the sol charge matters; flocculation value ∝ 1/power.","traps":["Using cation charge for a positive sol: Positive sol → look at anions.","Power vs flocculation value: Flocculation value = min concentration; inverse of power."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Catalysis","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"For the following Assertion and Reason, the correct option is Assertion : For hydrogenation reactions, the catalytic activity increases from Group 5 to Group 11 metals with maximum activity shown by Group 7-9 elements. Reason : The reactants are most strongly adsorbed on group 7-9 elements.","options":["Both assertion and reason are true but the reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion.","Both assertion and reason are false.","Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is the correct explanation for the assertion.","The assertion is true, but the reason is false."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) accepts the plausible-sounding reason; (A) treats both as true; (B) rejects the NCERT assertion.\nWorking: the assertion is the old NCERT statement on catalyst activity. Activity depends on chemisorption being reasonably strong but not so strong that reactants are immobilised; groups 7–9 give optimum (not strongest) adsorption, so the reason is false.\nKey idea: Best catalysts adsorb reactants moderately – strong enough to activate, weak enough to release.","traps":["Strongest adsorption assumed best: Too-strong adsorption blocks the surface."],"topics":["Catalysis","Adsorption"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"A mixture of gases O₂, H₂ and CO are taken in a closed vessel containing charcoal. The graph that represents the correct behaviour of pressure with time is:","options":["pressure rises with time and levels off","pressure falls linearly with time to zero","pressure constant with time","pressure falls with time and levels off (decaying curve)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) linear drop to zero implies all gas adsorbed at constant rate; (A) rising pressure suggests desorption/reaction; (C) ignores adsorption.\nWorking: charcoal adsorbs the gases, so pressure drops and then stays constant when adsorption equilibrium is reached.\nKey idea: Adsorption is fast at first, then equilibrium – pressure decays to a plateau.","traps":["Linear drop chosen: Adsorption is an equilibrium; pressure levels off."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which of the following is used for the preparation of colloids?","options":["Ostwald Process","Van Arkel Method","Bredig's Arc Method","Mond Process"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Van Arkel and (D) Mond are refining methods (metallurgy); (A) Ostwald process is HNO₃ manufacture.\nWorking: Bredig's arc method disperses metals (Au, Ag, Pt) as colloidal sols; Ostwald process makes HNO₃, Van Arkel and Mond processes refine metals.\nKey idea: Bredig's arc = electrical disintegration to make metal sols.","traps":["Process names from other chapters: Link each name to its chapter: Van Arkel/Mond → refining."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The mass of gas adsorbed, x, per unit mass of adsorbate, m, was measured at various pressures, p. A graph between log (x/m) and log p gives a straight line with slope equal to 2 and the intercept equal to 0.4771. The value of x/m at a pressure of 4 atm is: (Given log 3 = 0.4771)","type":"num","answer":"48","range":[48.0,48.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (exponent taken as 1/2: 3 × √4 = 6), 12 (exponent taken as 1: 3 × 4 = 12).\nWorking: Freundlich: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log p; log k = 0.4771 → k = 3; 1/n = 2; x/m = 3 × 4² = 48.\nKey idea: Intercept → k, slope → exponent; then x/m = k p², since the slope is the exponent of p.","traps":["Slope taken as n instead of 1/n: Slope of the log plot is the exponent of p directly.","Intercept used as k: Intercept is log k; k = 3."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Amongst the following statements regarding adsorption, those that are valid are : (a) ΔH becomes less negative as adsorption proceeds. (b) On a given adsorbent, ammonia is adsorbed more than nitrogen gas. (c) On adsorption, the residual force acting along the surface of the adsorbent increases. (d) With increase in temperature, the equilibrium concentration of adsorbate increases.","options":["(b) and (c)","(a) and (b)","(d) and (a)","(c) and (d)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) accept (c), reversing the residual-force idea; (C) and (D) accept (d), ignoring that adsorption is exothermic.\nWorking: (a) true – as the surface fills, fewer active sites remain and less heat is released per mole; (b) true – NH₃ (higher critical temperature, more easily liquefied, polar) is adsorbed more than N₂; (c) false – residual forces decrease on adsorption; (d) false – adsorption is exothermic, so equilibrium adsorption falls with temperature.\nKey idea: Adsorption lowers surface energy (residual forces) and is exothermic.","traps":["Residual force direction: Adsorption decreases residual forces.","Temperature effect reversed: Equilibrium physisorption falls as T rises."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Tyndall effect is observed when:","options":["The diameter of dispersed particles is much smaller than the wavelength of light used","The diameter of dispersed particles is much larger than the wavelength of light used","The diameter of dispersed particles is similar to the wavelength of light used","The refractive index of dispersed phase is greater than that of the dispersion medium"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the true-solution case (no scattering); (B) is suspension-scale; (D) NCERT says the indices must differ greatly, not that one is greater.\nWorking: scattering (Tyndall effect) needs particle diameter not much smaller than the wavelength of light, and a large refractive-index difference (not specifically greater).\nKey idea: Colloid-size particles (comparable to λ) scatter light.","traps":["Refractive index condition twisted: Difference, not direction, matters."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"An ionic micelle is formed on the addition of :","options":["excess water to liquid 1,3-dimethylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate ([C₃H₃N₂(CH₃)₂]⁺ PF₆⁻)","excess water to liquid 1,3-dimethylimidazolium long-chain alkyl sulphate (imidazolium cation with a C₈ zig-zag chain–SO₄⁻ anion)","liquid diethyl ether to aqueous NaCl solution","sodium stearate to pure toluene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ionic liquid without a long tail – dissolves, no micelle; (C) ether–water: no surfactant; (D) soap in non-polar solvent – no ionic micelle.\nWorking: micelles need an ionic surfactant with a long hydrophobic tail in water above the CMC; option (B) has an alkyl sulphate (surfactant anion) and water is added in excess. (A) has no long chain; (C) no surfactant; (D) toluene is non-polar (only reverse micelles possible, not ionic micelles in water).\nKey idea: Ionic head + long hydrocarbon tail + water (above CMC) = micelle.","traps":["Soap chosen regardless of solvent: Micelles of soap form in water, not toluene.","Any ionic liquid taken as surfactant: Look for a long hydrocarbon chain."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match the following : (i) Foam (ii) Gel (iii) Aerosol (iv) Emulsion with (a) smoke (b) cell fluid (c) jellies (d) rubber (e) froth (f) milk","options":["(i)-(b), (ii)-(c), (iii)-(e), (iv)-(d)","(i)-(d), (ii)-(b), (iii)-(e), (iv)-(f)","(i)-(e), (ii)-(c), (iii)-(a), (iv)-(f)","(i)-(d), (ii)-(b), (iii)-(a), (iv)-(e)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) use rubber (solid sol/foam in old table) or cell fluid (sol) as distractors.\nWorking: foam (gas in liquid) – froth; gel (liquid in solid) – jellies; aerosol (solid in gas) – smoke; emulsion (liquid in liquid) – milk.\nKey idea: Phase combination fixes the example.","traps":["Rubber placed under foam: Foam = gas in liquid → froth."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"A sample of red ink (a colloidal suspension) is prepared by mixing eosin dye, egg white, HCHO and water. The component which ensures stability of the ink sample is :","options":["HCHO","Eosin dye","Egg white","Water"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HCHO is a preservative; (B) dye is the dispersed phase; (D) water is the medium.\nWorking: egg white (albumin) is a lyophilic protective colloid that stabilises the lyophobic dye sol; HCHO is only a preservative.\nKey idea: The protein is the lyophilic stabiliser.","traps":["Preservative mistaken for stabiliser: Colloid stability = protection against coagulation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Identify the correct molecular picture showing what happens at the critical micellar concentration (CMC) of an aqueous solution of a surfactant (○ polar head; ~ non-polar tail; • water).","options":["(B)","(A)","(D)","(C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (A) [picture (B)] and (D) [picture (C)]: tails dipping into water at the surface – wrong orientation; option (B) [picture (A)]: surface only, no micelles (below CMC).\nWorking: at CMC the surface is saturated with surfactant oriented heads in water and tails towards air, and micelles (tails inside, heads out) begin to form in the bulk – picture (D), option (C).\nKey idea: Tails avoid water both at the surface (point to air) and in the micelle (point inward).","traps":["Surface orientation reversed: Hydrophobic tail must point out of water at the surface.","Below-CMC picture chosen: At CMC micelles start appearing."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep05S1C14-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Adsorption of a gas follows Freundlich adsorption isotherm. If x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent, the correct plot of x/m versus p is:","options":["Linear plots; 200 K > 250 K > 270 K","Linear plots; 270 K > 250 K > 200 K","Saturating curves; 200 K > 250 K > 270 K","Saturating curves; 270 K > 250 K > 200 K"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) – straight lines would hold only if 1/n = 1 (or for log x/m vs log p); (D) – temperature order reversed (as if adsorption were endothermic).\nWorking: Freundlich x/m = kp¹ᐟⁿ (n > 1) gives curves that bend over toward saturation; physical adsorption is exothermic, so x/m decreases with rise in temperature – the lowest temperature curve is highest.\nKey idea: Physisorption: lower T, more adsorption; Freundlich curve saturates at high p.","traps":["Temperature order reversed: Physisorption is exothermic: Le Chatelier – less at high T.","Linear x/m vs p assumed: Only log(x/m) vs log p is linear."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Kraft temperature is the temperature","options":["below which the formation of micelles takes place.","below which the aqueous solution of detergents starts freezing.","above which the formation of micelles takes place.","above which the aqueous solution of detergents starts boiling."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses 'above' to 'below'; (B) and (D) link the term to freezing/boiling of detergent solutions.\nWorking: micelles form only above the Kraft temperature (and above the CMC).\nKey idea: Micelles need T > Tk and c > CMC.","traps":["Above/below reversed: Recall: micelles form above both Tk and CMC."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"For Freundlich adsorption isotherm, a plot of log (x/m) (y-axis) and log p (x-axis) gives a straight line. The intercept and slope for the line is 0.4771 and 2, respectively. The mass of gas, adsorbed per gram of adsorbent if the initial pressure is 0.04 atm, is ______ × 10⁻⁴ g. (log 3 = 0.4771)","type":"num","answer":"48","range":[48.0,48.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6000 (slope taken as n, x/m = 3 × √0.04 = 0.6 g); 0.0048 (not scaled to 10⁻⁴); 16 (intercept ignored, k taken as 1); 4.8 (decimal slip).\nWorking: log k = 0.4771 → k = 3; 1/n = 2; x/m = 3 × (0.04)² = 0.0048 g = 48 × 10⁻⁴ g. (Slope 2 means 1/n > 1, unusual for Freundlich, but the arithmetic is unambiguous.)\nKey idea: Intercept = log k, slope = 1/n.","traps":["Slope taken as n not 1/n: In log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log p the slope is 1/n.","Answer not scaled to 10⁻⁴: Divide the result by 10⁻⁴ before entering."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Adsorption of a gas follows Freundlich adsorption isotherm. x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent. The plot of log(x/m) versus log p is shown in the given graph. x/m is proportional to:","options":["p³ᐟ²","p³","p²ᐟ³","p²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) p³ᐟ² inverts the slope (run/rise = 3/2 instead of rise/run = 2/3); (B) p³ uses the run alone; (D) p² uses the rise alone.\nWorking: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log p; slope = 2/3 ⇒ x/m ∝ p²ᐟ³.\nKey idea: Slope = rise/run = 1/n.","traps":["Using rise or run instead of the slope: Slope = 2/3.","Inverting the slope as run/rise: Slope of log(x/m) vs log p equals 1/n = rise/run = 2/3, the power of p."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Apr08S1C25-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"0.27 g of a long chain fatty acid was dissolved in 100 cm³ of hexane. 10 mL of this solution was added dropwise to the surface of water in a round watch glass. Hexane evaporates and a monolayer is formed. The distance from edge to centre of the watch glass is 10 cm. What is the height of the monolayer? [Density of fatty acid = 0.9 g cm⁻³, π = 3]","options":["10⁻⁸ m","10⁻⁶ m","10⁻⁴ m","10⁻² m"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 10⁻⁴ m leaves the result in cm but labels it m; (A) 10⁻⁸ m from extra 100× factor; (D) ignores the 1/10 aliquot and unit conversion.\nWorking: 10 mL carries 0.027 g ⇒ volume 0.03 cm³; area = πr² = 3 × 100 = 300 cm²; height = 0.03/300 = 10⁻⁴ cm = 10⁻⁶ m.\nKey idea: Mass in aliquot → volume → divide by area → convert cm to m.","traps":["cm not converted to m: Convert: 10⁻⁴ cm = 10⁻⁶ m.","Aliquot fraction ignored: 10 mL of 100 mL carries 1/10 of the acid."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The aerosol is a kind of colloid in which :","options":["gas is dispersed in solid","solid is dispersed in gas","liquid is dispersed in water","gas is dispersed in liquid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) solid foam (pumice); (C) emulsion; (D) foam.\nWorking: aerosol – solid (smoke, dust) or liquid (fog) dispersed in gas; among options only (B).\nKey idea: Aerosol = dispersion medium is gas.","traps":["Dispersed phase and medium reversed: Aerosol's medium is air (gas)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"A gas undergoes physical adsorption on a surface and follows the given Freundlich adsorption isotherm equation x/m = kp⁰·⁵. Adsorption of the gas increases with :","options":["Decrease in p and decrease in T","Increase in p and increase in T","Increase in p and decrease in T","Decrease in p and increase in T"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) wrongly think heating helps physisorption (true only initially for chemisorption); (A) misreads the isotherm.\nWorking: x/m ∝ p⁰·⁵ rises with p; physisorption is exothermic, so it falls as T rises.\nKey idea: Physisorption: more at high p, low T.","traps":["Chemisorption temperature behaviour applied: Physisorption is exothermic with low Ea – falls with T."],"topics":["Isotherms","Adsorption"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The correct option among the following is:","options":["Colloidal particles in lyophobic sols can be precipitated by electrophoresis.","Brownian motion in colloidal solution is faster if the viscosity of the solution is very high.","Colloidal medicines are more effective because they have small surface area.","Addition of alum to water makes it unfit for drinking."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) reverses viscosity effect; (C) 'small' replaces 'large'; (D) reverses the purpose of alum.\nWorking: in electrophoresis charged lyophobic particles reach the oppositely charged electrode, lose charge and coagulate. (B) Brownian motion slows with high viscosity; (C) colloidal medicines have large surface area; (D) alum purifies water by coagulation.\nKey idea: Electrophoresis leads to coagulation at the electrode.","traps":["Surface area wording flipped: Colloids are effective because of large surface area."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Peptization is a :","options":["process of converting a colloidal solution into precipitate","process of converting precipitate into colloidal solution","process of converting soluble particles to form colloidal solution","process of bringing colloidal molecule into solution"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is coagulation; (C) describes condensation methods; (D) vague wording close to dissolution.\nWorking: peptization disperses a freshly formed precipitate into a colloidal sol by shaking with a small amount of electrolyte (peptizing agent).\nKey idea: Peptization is the reverse of coagulation: precipitate → sol.","traps":["Peptization vs coagulation: Remember peptization makes a sol; coagulation destroys it."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Among the following, the INCORRECT statement about colloids is :","options":["They can scatter light","They are larger than small molecules and have high molar mass","The range of diameters of colloidal particles is between 1 and 1000 nm","The osmotic pressure of a colloidal solution is of higher order than the true solution at the same concentration"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Tyndall effect is true; (B) high molar mass true; (C) 1–1000 nm true.\nWorking: colloidal particles have high molar mass, so for the same mass concentration there are far fewer particles and colligative properties (osmotic pressure) are much smaller.\nKey idea: Fewer, bigger particles → lower osmotic pressure.","traps":["Large particles assumed to give larger colligative effect: Colligative = number of particles.","Selecting a correct statement: Mark each statement T/F first."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Isotherms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Adsorption of a gas follows Freundlich adsorption isotherm. In the given plot, x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on mass m of the adsorbent at pressure p. x/m is proportional to","options":["P¹ᐟ⁴","P²","P","P¹ᐟ²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): uses rise 2 as exponent. (A): uses 1/4 from run. (C): assumes linear (n = 1).\nWorking: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log p; slope = 2/4 = 1/2, so x/m ∝ p¹ᐟ².\nKey idea: Slope of log–log plot = exponent of p.","traps":["Slope read as 2 instead of 2/4: Slope = rise/run = 2/4.","Exponent inverted: log(x/m) = log k + (1/n) log P; slope is the exponent."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Jan09S1C04-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"For coagulation of arsenious sulphide sol, which one of the following salt solution will be most effective?","options":["AlCl₃","NaCl","BaCl₂","Na₃PO₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Na₃PO₄: highest-charge anion – would be right only for a positive sol. (B) NaCl, (C) BaCl₂: lower cation charge.\nWorking: As₂S₃ sol is negatively charged; coagulating power rises with the charge of the cation (Hardy–Schulze), so Al³⁺ is best.\nKey idea: Negative sol → look for the highest-charge cation.","traps":["Sol charge reversed: Sulphide sols are negative; opposite-charge ions (cations) coagulate."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Catalysis","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Which of the following is not an example of heterogeneous catalytic reaction ?","options":["Ostwald's process","Haber's process","Combustion of coal","Hydrogenation of vegetable oils"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are all NCERT examples of solid catalyst with gas/liquid reactants.\nWorking: Ostwald (Pt), Haber (Fe) and hydrogenation of oils (Ni) are heterogeneous catalytic processes; combustion of coal needs no catalyst.\nKey idea: Coal combustion is not catalysed.","traps":["\"Not\" overlooked: Underline \"not\"; pick the reaction without a catalyst."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Haemoglobin and gold sol are examples of :","options":["negatively charged sols","positively charged sols","negatively and positively charged sols, respectively","positively and negatively charged sols, respectively"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): treat both as the same sign. (C): order reversed.\nWorking: haemoglobin (and other basic dye/hydroxide sols) is positively charged; metal sols such as gold are negatively charged.\nKey idea: Haemoglobin +, metal sols −.","traps":["Order of pair reversed: Match each item in order."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"An example of solid sol is:","options":["Butter","Gem stones","Paint","Hair cream"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) butter – liquid in solid (gel). (C) paint – solid in liquid (sol). (D) hair cream – emulsion.\nWorking: solid dispersed in solid = solid sol (gem stones, coloured glass); butter is a gel, paint a sol, hair cream an emulsion.\nKey idea: Name = phase of dispersion medium; solid sol has solid medium and solid phase.","traps":["Gel vs solid sol: Butter has liquid dispersed in solid (gel)."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Colloid types","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Among the colloids cheese (C), milk (M) and smoke (S), the correct combination of the dispersed phase and dispersion medium, respectively is :-","options":["C : solid in liquid; M : solid in liquid; S : solid in gas","C : solid in liquid; M : liquid in liquid; S : gas in solid","C : liquid in solid; M : liquid in solid; S : solid in gas","C : liquid in solid; M : liquid in liquid; S : solid in gas"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) cheese and milk as sols. (B) cheese as sol, smoke as 'gas in solid' (that is a solid foam). (C) milk as liquid in solid (gel).\nWorking: cheese is a gel (liquid dispersed in solid), milk an emulsion (liquid in liquid: fat in water), smoke an aerosol (solid in gas).\nKey idea: Name the type first: gel, emulsion, aerosol; then read the phases.","traps":["Order of phase and medium reversed: Smoke = solid particles in air: solid in gas.","Cheese taken as solid in liquid: Cheese is a gel: liquid trapped in a solid network."],"topics":["Colloid types","Emulsions"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Adsorption","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given: Gas: H₂, CH₄, CO₂, SO₂; Critical temperature/K: 33, 190, 304, 630. On the basis of data given above, predict which of the following gases shows least adsorption on a definite amount of charcoal ?","options":["H₂","CH₄","SO₂","CO₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) SO₂: most adsorbed (reverse reading). (B)/(D): middle values.\nWorking: easily liquefiable gases (high critical temperature) are adsorbed more; H₂ has the lowest Tc.\nKey idea: Lower Tc ⇒ less van der Waals attraction ⇒ less adsorption.","traps":["Least vs most reversed: Underline \"least\"."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Colloid properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Among the following, the false statement is :","options":["Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles which are positively charged","Tyndall effect can be used to distinguish between a colloidal solution and a true solution.","It is possible to cause artificial rain by throwing electrified sand carrying charge opposite to the one on clouds from an aeroplane.","Lyophilic sol can be coagulated by adding an electrolyte."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the strongest distractor: students recall 'lyophilic sols are stable' and think they cannot be coagulated at all.\nWorking: rubber particles in latex are negatively charged, so (A) is false. (D) is true in the sense that lyophilic sols are coagulated by electrolyte (in large amount, or with a desolvating solvent) – the item treats it as true.\nKey idea: Latex = negatively charged rubber particles.","traps":["Charge on latex sign flipped: Remember latex particles are negative.","Lyophilic sols thought never to coagulate: Large amounts of electrolyte or desolvation coagulate them."]}],"m08-kin":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"For the reaction A → products, the graph of t½ against [A]₀ is shown below.\nThe concentration of A at 10 minutes is ________ × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ (nearest integer).\nThe reaction was started with 2.5 mol L⁻¹ of A.","type":"num","answer":"2435","range":[2435.0,2435.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 2370 (slope taken as 1/k, forgetting the 2), and values near 2.5 e⁻ᵏᵗ from a first-order treatment.\nWorking: slope = 1/(2k) = 76.92 → k = 0.0065 mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, so [A] at 10 min = 2.5 − 0.065 = 2.435 M = 2435 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹.\nKey idea: For zero order t½ = [A]₀/2k, so the slope of t½ vs [A]₀ is 1/(2k).","traps":["Half-life proportional to initial concentration read as first order: t½ proportional to [A]₀ (line through the origin) is the signature of zero order; first order gives a horizontal line.","Graph slope taken directly as rate constant: t½ = [A]₀/(2k), so slope = 1/(2k) and k = 1/(2 × 76.92) = 0.0065 mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C22-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":55,"q":"Consider the following plots of log of rate constant k (log k) vs 1/T for three different reactions. The correct order of activation energies of these reactions is:","options":["Ea₂ > Ea₁ > Ea₃","Ea₁ > Ea₂ > Ea₃","Ea₃ > Ea₂ > Ea₁","Ea₁ > Ea₃ > Ea₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ea₂ > Ea₁ > Ea₃ is correct. (B) swaps 1 and 2 (judged by line length or height). (C) is the full reversal (sign misreading). (D) puts the flat line 3 above line 2.\nWorking: slope of log k vs 1/T = −Ea/(2.303R); line 2 is steepest, line 1 moderate, line 3 nearly flat, so Ea₂ > Ea₁ > Ea₃.\nKey idea: On a log k vs 1/T plot, steeper means a larger activation energy.","traps":["Arrhenius plot height mistaken for slope: Only the magnitude of the slope matters: steepest (line 2) > moderate (line 1) > nearly flat (line 3).","Arrhenius plot slope magnitude mapped wrongly to Ea: Ea = −2.303R × slope, so a more negative slope means a larger Ea."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C05-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":56,"q":"Reaction A(g) → 2B(g) + C(g) is a first order reaction. It was started with pure A\nt/min | Pressure of system at time t/mm Hg\n10 | 160\n∞ | 240\nWhich of the following option is incorrect?","options":["Initial pressure of A is 80 mm Hg","Partial pressure of A after 10 minute is 40 mm Hg","Rate constant of the reaction is 1.693 min⁻¹","The reaction never goes to completion"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) p₀ = 80 mm Hg is correct. (B) p_A = 40 mm Hg at 10 min is correct. (C) is the incorrect one: k = 0.693/10 = 0.0693 min⁻¹, not 1.693. (D) is correct in principle, since first-order decay approaches zero only asymptotically.\nKey idea: Use p∞ to get p₀, then the half-drop in p_A shows that 10 min is one half-life.","traps":["Final pressure taken equal to initial pressure: For A → 2B + C each A gives 3 gas molecules, so p∞ = 3p₀ and p₀ = 80 mm Hg.","Total pressure mistaken for reactant partial pressure: p_t = p₀ + 2x, so x = 40 and p_A = p₀ − x = 40 mm Hg."]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":57,"q":"A person's wound was exposed to some bacteria and then bacterial growth started to happen at the same place. The wound was later treated with some antibacterial medicine and the rate of bacterial decay(r) was found to be proportional with the square of the existing number of bacteria at any instance. Which of the following set of graphs correctly represents the 'before' and 'after' situation of the application of the medicine?\n[Given: N = No. of bacteria, t = time, bacterial growth follows 1st order kinetics.]","options":["Graph pair (A)","Graph pair (B)","Graph pair (C)","Graph pair (D)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): right before-curve, but after = falling curve (an r vs t shape). (B): after = straight line, which would be first-order decay. (C): before starts at the origin, which is impossible since N/N₀ = 1 at t = 0. (D) correct.\nKey idea: Check the intercept (N/N₀ = 1) and the power in r ∝ N² to pick each graph.","traps":["Exponential growth curve confused with decay or saturation curve: At t = 0, N = N₀, so N/N₀ = 1; first-order growth is concave-up exponential.","Rate vs concentration plot shape for given order: r = kN² is a parabola from the origin, rising with N."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C07-p.webp"],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":57,"q":"A(g) → B(g) + C(g) is a first order reaction.\nTime | t | ∞\nP_system | p_t | p_∞\nThe reaction was started with reactant A only. Which of the following expression is correct for rate constant k ?","options":["k = (1/t) ln (p_∞/p_t)","k = (1/t) ln [p_∞/(2(p_∞ − p_t))]","k = (1/t) ln [p_∞/(p_∞ − p_t)]","k = (1/t) ln [2(p_∞ − p_t)/p_t]"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses p_t as p_A. (B) correct. (C) drops the 1/2 (p∞ treated as p₀). (D) is an inverted, garbled ratio.\nWorking: p∞ = 2p₀ and p_A = 2p₀ − p_t = p∞ − p_t; k = (1/t) ln(p₀/p_A) = (1/t) ln[p∞/(2(p∞ − p_t))].\nKey idea: Express both p₀ and p_A in terms of p∞ using the 1 → 2 molecule stoichiometry.","traps":["Final pressure taken equal to initial pressure: For A → B + C, p∞ = 2p₀, so p₀ = p∞/2 appears in the numerator.","Total pressure mistaken for reactant partial pressure: p_A = 2p₀ − p_t = p∞ − p_t, not p_t."]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Activation energy","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":57,"q":"Consider the given figure (energy profile shown below) and choose the correct option :","options":["Activation energy of forward reaction is E₁ + E₂ and product is less stable than reactant.","Activation energy of forward reaction is E₁ + E₂ and product is more stable than reactant.","Activation energy of both forward and backward reaction is E₁ + E₂ and reactant is more stable than product.","Activation energy of backward reaction is E₁ and product is more stable than reactant."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) correct. (B) right Ea but wrong stability. (C) wrongly gives the backward Ea as E₁ + E₂ (it is E₁). (D) right backward Ea (E₁) but wrong stability.\nWorking: forward Ea = peak − reactant level = E₁ + E₂; backward Ea = E₁; the product lies E₂ above the reactant, so it is less stable.\nKey idea: Activation energy is always measured from the starting level of that direction to the peak.","traps":["Activation energy measured from wrong level in energy profile: Forward barrier is measured from the reactant (E₁ + E₂); backward from the product (E₁).","Stability inferred from wrong energy comparison: Lower energy means more stable; here the product is higher, so the reaction is endothermic and the product is less stable."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan22S2C07-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":57,"q":"Which of the following graphs most appropriately represents a zero order reaction?","options":["Rate vs time: a straight line decreasing with time","Reactant concentration vs time: a horizontal straight line","Rate vs time: a curve decaying exponentially","Reactant concentration vs time: a straight line decreasing linearly from a positive intercept"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) rate falling linearly (wrong axis). (B) [R] constant (that would mean no reaction). (C) exponential rate decay (first order). (D) [R] falling linearly is correct.\nWorking: for zero order, rate = k (constant), so [R] = [R]₀ − kt; a plot of [R] against t is a straight line falling with slope −k.\nKey idea: For zero order, concentration falls linearly while the rate stays constant.","traps":["Rate vs time confused with concentration vs time: For zero order the rate is constant (rate = k), so rate vs t is horizontal; it is [R] that falls linearly.","Zero order misread as zero change in concentration: Zero order means rate independent of concentration, not no reaction."]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":57,"q":"For a given reaction R → P, t½ is related to [A]₀ as given in table. Given: log 2 = 0.30. Which of the following is true?\n[A]₀ / mol L⁻¹ | t½ / min\n0.100 | 200\n0.025 | 100\nA. The order of the reaction is ½.\nB. If [A]₀ is 1M, then t½ is 200√10 min\nC. The order of the reaction changes to 1 if the concentration of reactant changes from 0.100 M to 0.500 M.\nD. t½ is 800 min for [A]₀ = 1.6 M\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A and B Only","A and C Only","A, B and D Only","C and D Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A true (n = ½). B true (200√10 min at 1 M). C false (order does not change with concentration). D true (800 min). Options (A) and (B) miss D; (B) and (D) include the false C. (C) is correct.\nWorking: t½ ∝ [A]₀¹⁻ⁿ. From the table, [A]₀ falls 4-fold (0.100 → 0.025) and t½ halves (200 → 100), so 4¹⁻ⁿ = 2 and n = ½, i.e. t½ ∝ √[A]₀. At 1 M (10 × 0.100 M): t½ = 200√10 min. At 1.6 M (16 × 0.100 M): t½ = 200 × 4 = 800 min.\nKey idea: For any order other than one, t½ scales as [A]₀¹⁻ⁿ; a 4-fold concentration change giving a 2-fold t½ change means n = ½.","traps":["Half-life exponent relation sign slip: t½ ∝ [A]₀¹⁻ⁿ: 2 = 4¹⁻ⁿ, so 1 − n = ½ and n = ½.","Order assumed to change with concentration: Order is a fixed property of the rate law over the range where the law holds; changing concentration does not change it."],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":56,"q":"For bacterial growth in a cell culture, growth law is very similar to the law of radioactive decay. Which of the following graphs is most suitable to represent bacterial colony growth?\nWhere N - Number of Bacteria at any time, N₀ - Initial number of Bacteria.","options":["N/N₀ vs time: a decreasing exponential-decay curve","N/N₀ vs time: an increasing exponential (concave-up) curve starting at a positive intercept","N/N₀ vs time: a straight line decreasing with time","N/N₀ vs time: a curve rising from the origin and levelling off"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) decay curve (pulled by 'radioactive decay'). (B) correct. (C) linear decrease (zero-order-like). (D) starts from the origin and saturates (logistic-like, wrong intercept).\nWorking: decay follows N = N₀e⁻ᵏᵗ; growth follows N = N₀eᵏᵗ, so N/N₀ = eᵏᵗ starts at 1 when t = 0 and rises ever more steeply (concave up).\nKey idea: 'Similar to radioactive decay' means the same first-order exponential form, with the sign of the exponent reversed for growth.","traps":["Exponential growth curve confused with decay or saturation curve: Same mathematical form, but growth has +k: N/N₀ = eᵏᵗ, starting at 1 and rising ever faster.","Initial value of normalised quantity: At t = 0, N/N₀ = 1, not 0."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Consider the following transformation involving first order elementary reaction in each step at constant temperature as shown below.\nA + B ⇌ C (Step 1 forward, Step 3 backward); C → P (Step 2)\nSome details of the above reactions are listed below.\nStep 1: rate constant k₁ (sec⁻¹), activation energy 300 kJ mol⁻¹\nStep 2: k₂, 200 kJ mol⁻¹\nStep 3: k₃, Ea₃\nIf the overall rate constant of the above transformation (k) is given as k = k₁k₂/k₃ and the overall activation energy (Eₐ) is 400 kJ mol⁻¹, then the value of Ea₃ is ______ kJ mol⁻¹ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: 900 if all added; 300 if signs mixed (Ea₃ = Ea₁ + Ea₂ + … ); 0.5-type ratios if Ea multiplied/divided like k.\nWorking: k = k₁k₂/k₃ with Arrhenius form for each → Eₐ = Ea₁ + Ea₂ − Ea₃ → 400 = 300 + 200 − Ea₃ → Ea₃ = 100. (Calling A + B → C 'first order' is loose, but irrelevant to the answer.)\nKey idea: Multiplying k's adds Ea's; dividing subtracts.","traps":["Ea treated like k (multiplied/divided): Take logs: ln k = ln k₁ + ln k₂ − ln k₃.","Reverse step added: Denominator → subtract."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the following reaction, the rate expression of which is given below\nA + B → C\nrate = k[A]¹ᐟ²[B]¹ᐟ²\nThe reaction is initiated by taking 1 M concentration of A and B each. If the rate constant (k) is 4.6 × 10⁻² s⁻¹, then the time taken for A to become 0.1 M is ______ sec. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using half-order integration for one reactant only, or treating the overall order as 1 with ln but wrong log base (e.g. 21.7 using log instead of 2.303 log).\nWorking: [A] = [B] at all times (1:1 consumption, equal start) so rate = k[A], first order. t = (2.303/k) log(1/0.1) = 2.303/0.046 = 50.07 s ≈ 50.\nKey idea: Equal initial concentrations + 1:1 stoichiometry ⇒ [A] = [B] ⇒ rate = k[A].","traps":["Half-order integration applied: [A]¹ᐟ²[B]¹ᐟ² = [A] when [A] = [B].","log vs ln: t = (2.303/k) log10 = ln10/k."],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"During kinetic study of reaction 2A + B → C + D, the following results were obtained:\nI: [A] = 0.1 M, [B] = 0.1 M, initial rate of formation of D = 6.0 × 10⁻³\nII: [A] = 0.3 M, [B] = 0.2 M, rate = 7.2 × 10⁻²\nIII: [A] = 0.3 M, [B] = 0.4 M, rate = 2.88 × 10⁻¹\nIV: [A] = 0.4 M, [B] = 0.1 M, rate = 2.40 × 10⁻²\nBased on above data, overall order of the reaction is ____.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: getting 3 from stoichiometry (2 + 1) matches only by coincidence (it implies order 2 in A and 1 in B, which the data contradict); 4 if B order taken 2 and A 2; 2 if B taken first order.\nWorking: I→IV ([A] ×4, [B] same) rate ×4 → first order in A; II→III ([B] ×2) rate ×4 → second order in B; check I→II: 3 × 2² = 12 ✓; overall 3.\nKey idea: Compare experiments where only one concentration changes.","traps":["Order read from coefficients: Orders come only from data.","Pair with both concentrations changing: Use I/IV for A and II/III for B."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Consider the following single step reaction in gas phase at constant temperature.\n2A(g) + B(g) → C(g)\nThe initial rate of the reaction is recorded as r₁ when the reaction starts with 1.5 atm pressure of A and 0.7 atm pressure of B. After some time, the rate r₂ is recorded when the pressure of C becomes 0.5 atm. The ratio r₁ : r₂ is ______ × 10⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"315","range":[315.0,315.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 79 (p_A taken as 1.0 instead of 0.5); 15 or 21 (first-order dependence on A assumed).\nWorking: elementary → r = k p_A² p_B. r₁ ∝ 1.5² × 0.7 = 1.575; at p_C = 0.5, p_A = 0.5, p_B = 0.2 → r₂ ∝ 0.05; r₁/r₂ = 31.5 = 315 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Single step → exponents = coefficients; A falls by twice the C formed.","traps":["A decrease taken as 0.5: p_A falls by 2 × p_C.","Order not linked to stoichiometry: 'Single step' means elementary: use coefficients."],"topics":["Rate law, rate constant & order","Molecularity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Time required for 99.9% completion of a first order reaction is ______ times the time required for completion of 90% reaction.(nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 10 (recalls NCERT's 99.9%-vs-half-life result), 1.11 (ratio of percentages).\nWorking: t = (2.303/k) log([R]₀/[R]); t₉₉.₉ ∝ log 1000 = 3, t₉₀ ∝ log 10 = 1 → ratio 3.\nKey idea: Ratio of times = ratio of log([R]₀/[R]) = 3/1.","traps":["NCERT's '10 times' recalled: Re-derive with 90% → log 10."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Consider the two different first order reactions given below\nA + B → C (Reaction 1)\nP → Q (Reaction 2)\nThe ratio of the half life of Reaction 1 : Reaction 2 is 5 : 2. If t₁ and t₂ represent the time taken to complete 2/3rd and 4/5th of Reaction 1 and Reaction 2, respectively, then the value of the ratio t₁ : t₂ is ______ × 10⁻¹ (nearest integer). [Given : log₁₀(3) = 0.477 and log₁₀(5) = 0.699]","type":"num","answer":"17","range":[17.0,17.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 or 5 (using fraction completed 2/3 and 4/5 in log instead of remaining), 7 (half-life ratio inverted), 25 (log terms ignored).\nWorking: t = (t½/ln2)·ln(a/(a−x)); t₁/t₂ = (5/2)·(log 3/log 5) = 2.5 × 0.477/0.699 = 1.706 ≈ 17 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Remaining fractions are 1/3 and 1/5 → log 3 and log 5.","traps":["Fraction completed used inside log: [R]₀/[R] = 3 and 5.","A + B → C taken as second order: Stem says first order."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider the following reaction\nA + B → C\nThe time taken for A to become 1/4th of its initial concentration is twice the time taken to become 1/2 of the same. Also, when the change of concentration of B is plotted against time, the resulting graph gives a straight line with a negative slope and a positive intercept on the concentration axis.\nThe overall order of the reaction is ________.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (taking both first order or reading t₁/₄ = 2t₁/₂ as second order); 0 (taking both zero order).\nWorking: t₃/₄ = 2t₁/₂ means constant half-life → first order in A; linear [B] vs t with negative slope → zero order in B; overall 1 + 0 = 1.\nKey idea: Two successive equal half-lives → first order.","traps":["Linear plot read as first order: Linear [R] vs t (not ln) → zero order."],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"For a reaction A —K₁→ B —K₂→ C. If the rate of formation of B is set to be zero then the concentration of B is given by :","options":["(K₁ − K₂)[A]","(K₁ + K₂)[A]","K₁K₂[A]","(K₁/K₂)[A]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): adding or subtracting rate constants as if parallel reactions. (C): multiplying constants.\nWorking: d[B]/dt = K₁[A] − K₂[B] = 0 gives [B] = (K₁/K₂)[A].\nKey idea: Net rate of B = formation − consumption = 0.","traps":["Consecutive vs parallel rate constants: Write d[B]/dt explicitly."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The rate law for the reaction A + B → C is rate (r) = k[A]²[B]. When the concentration of both A and B is doubled, the reaction rate is increased \"x\" times.\nStatement II: The figure shown below is showing \"the variation in concentration against time plot\" for a \"y\" order reaction.\nThe value of x + y is ____.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9 if the linear plot is read as first order; 4 or 6 if exponents are mishandled.\nWorking: x = 2²×2 = 8; linear [R] vs t with slope −k is zero order, y = 0; x + y = 8.\nKey idea: Linear fall of [R] with time ⇒ zero order (y = 0).","traps":["Linear plot read as first order: Check the y-axis: [R], not ln[R]."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr09S1C26-p.webp"],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the following first order gas phase reaction at constant temperature A(g) → 2B(g) + C(g)\nIf the total pressure of the gases is found to be 200 torr after 23 sec. and 300 torr upon the complete decomposition of A after a very long time, then the rate constant of the given reaction is ______ × 10⁻² s⁻¹ (nearest integer)\n[Given : log₁₀(2) = 0.301]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using P₀ = 200 or NCERT formula for 1 → 2 gives wrong x; using P∞ − Pt incorrectly gives k ≈ 1–2.\nWorking: P∞ = 3P₀ = 300 → P₀ = 100 torr; Pt = P₀ + 2x = 200 → x = 50, so half of A is gone at 23 s; k = 0.693/23 = 3.0 × 10⁻² s⁻¹.\nKey idea: P∞ = 3P₀; the reaction is at t½ when Pt = 200.","traps":["Copying NCERT 1→2 pressure formula: Rebuild the table for A → 2B + C.","Initial pressure not given: Find P₀ from P∞ = 3P₀."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The ratio of ¹⁴C/¹²C in a piece of wood is 1/8 part that of atmosphere. If half life of ¹⁴C is 5730 years, the age of wood sample is _______ years.","type":"num","answer":"17190","range":[17190.0,17190.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 5730 × 8 = 45840, 22920 (4 half-lives).\nWorking: 1/8 = (1/2)³ → 3 half-lives → 3 × 5730 = 17190 years.\nKey idea: Fraction left = (1/2)ⁿ.","traps":["Multiplying by 8 instead of 3: (1/2)³ = 1/8 → n = 3."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The following data were obtained during the first order thermal decomposition of a gas A at constant volume :\nA(g) → 2B(g) + C(g)\nS.No. | Time /s | Total pressure /(atm)\n1. | 0 | 0.1\n2. | 115 | 0.28\nThe rate constant of the reaction is ______ × 10⁻² s⁻¹ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: using pA = 2pᵢ – pt gives a negative pressure; using pt in place of pA gives a negative/meaningless k; ln-vs-log slip gives about 0.87.\nWorking: pt = p₀ + 2x → x = 0.09 atm; pA = 0.01 atm; k = (2.303/115) log(0.1/0.01) = 0.0200 s⁻¹ = 2 × 10⁻² s⁻¹\nKey idea: pA = (3p₀ – pt)/2 = 0.01 atm, a tenfold drop, so k = 2.303/115 ≈ 0.02 s⁻¹.","traps":["Reusing NCERT pA = 2pᵢ – pt: Derive pA from stoichiometry each time.","Scale ×10⁻²: Report 2."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider the following data for the given reaction\n2HI(g) → H₂(g) + I₂(g)\nExperiment: 1, 2, 3\nHI (mol L⁻¹): 0.005, 0.01, 0.02\nRate (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹): 7.5×10⁻⁴, 3.0×10⁻³, 1.2×10⁻²\nThe order of the reaction is _________.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (reading ratio as 2 instead of 4), 4 (taking ratio itself as order).\nWorking: doubling [HI] multiplies rate by 4 (7.5×10⁻⁴ → 3.0×10⁻³ → 1.2×10⁻²), so 2ⁿ = 4, n = 2.\nKey idea: Rate ratio 4 for concentration ratio 2 → second order.","traps":["Ratio of rates taken as the order: Solve 2ⁿ = 4 for n."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Time required for completion of 99.9% of a First order reaction is ________ times of half life (t₁/₂) of the reaction.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (using log 1000 without dividing by log 2), 9 or 9.97 entered without rounding, 1000 (ratio of concentrations).\nWorking: t₉₉.₉ = (2.303/k) log 1000 = 6.909/k; t₁/₂ = 0.693/k; ratio ≈ 9.97 ≈ 10.\nKey idea: t = n·t₁/₂ where n = log(1000)/log 2 ≈ 10.","traps":["Log ratio not divided by log 2: Ratio = log(1/0.001)/log 2."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"For a reaction taking place in three steps at same temperature, overall rate constant K = K₁K₂/K₃. If Ea₁, Ea₂ and Ea₃ are 40, 50 and 60 kJ/mol respectively, the overall Ea is _________ kJ/mol.","type":"num","answer":"30","range":[30.0,30.0],"explain":"Common slips: 150 if all added; −30 or 0 with wrong signs; 20 if quotient treated as ratio of energies.\nWorking: writing each K = A e^(−Ea/RT), overall Ea = Ea₁ + Ea₂ − Ea₃ = 40 + 50 − 60 = 30 kJ/mol.\nKey idea: Exponents add for products and subtract for quotients.","traps":["Adding all three Ea: Take ln of K: Ea combines with the same signs as the K's."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The half-life of radioisotope bromine-82 is 36 hours. The fraction which remains after one day is ________ × 10⁻².\n(Given antilog 0.2006 = 1.587)","type":"num","answer":"63","range":[63.0,63.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 67 (linear interpolation 1 − 24/72), 37 (fraction decayed), 50 (one half-life assumed).\nWorking: n = 24/36 = 2/3 half-lives; fraction = (1/2)^(2/3) = 1/2^(2/3) = 1/1.587 = 0.630 = 63 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: N/N₀ = 2^(−t/t½); log(2^(2/3)) = 0.2006 → 1.587.","traps":["Remaining vs decayed: Remaining = 1/1.587 = 0.63.","Linear decay assumed: Use exponential: 2^(−2/3)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The rate of a first order reaction is 0.04 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 10 minutes and 0.03 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 20 minutes after initiation. Half life of the reaction is _______ minutes.\n(Given log 2 = 0.3010, log 3 = 0.4771)","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 or 20 (misreading times), 0.04 (rate), 2.4 or 240 (decimal slip), 34 (using ln instead of log).\nWorking: rate ∝ [A]; k = (2.303/10) log(4/3) = 0.2303 × 0.1249 = 0.02877 min⁻¹; t½ = 0.693/0.02877 = 24.1 min ≈ 24.\nKey idea: For first order, rate ratio = concentration ratio; Δt = 10 min.","traps":["Using t = 20 min instead of Δt = 10 min: Use the interval between the two readings.","log vs ln slip: Keep 2.303 with log₁₀."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"NO₂ required for a reaction is produced by decomposition of N₂O₅ in CCl₄ as by equation\n2N₂O₅(g) → 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g)\nThe initial concentration of N₂O₅ is 3 mol L⁻¹ and it is 2.75 mol L⁻¹ after 30 minutes.\nThe rate of formation of NO₂ is x × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, value of x is _________. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"17","range":[17.0,17.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (rate of N₂O₅ loss only), 4 (rate of reaction, dividing by 2), 33 (multiplying by 4).\nWorking: −Δ[N₂O₅]/Δt = 0.25/30 = 8.33 × 10⁻³; rate of NO₂ formation = 2 × that = 16.7 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹ → 17.\nKey idea: Rate of NO₂ formation = (4/2) × rate of N₂O₅ loss.","traps":["Using rate of reaction instead of NO₂ formation: Read which species' rate is asked."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Integrated rate law equation for a first order gas phase reaction is given by (where Pᵢ is initial pressure and Pₜ is total pressure at time t)\n(Take the reaction to be of the type A(g) → B(g) + C(g).)","options":["k = (2.303/t) × log [Pᵢ/(2Pᵢ − Pₜ)]","k = (2.303/t) × log [(2Pᵢ − Pₜ)/Pᵢ]","k = (2.303/t) × Pᵢ/(2Pᵢ − Pₜ)","k = (2.303/t) × log [2Pᵢ/(2Pᵢ − Pₜ)]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): inverted ratio (gives negative k). (C): log dropped. (D): numerator 2Pᵢ – using final pressure instead of initial.\nWorking: Pₜ = Pᵢ + x so P_A = Pᵢ − x = 2Pᵢ − Pₜ; k = (2.303/t) log (Pᵢ/P_A).\nKey idea: Partial pressure of A at time t = 2Pᵢ − Pₜ.","traps":["Ratio inverted: k must be positive: initial/remaining > 1.","2Pᵢ used in numerator: Numerator is initial pressure of A, Pᵢ."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"r = k[A] for a reaction, 50% of A is decomposed in 120 minutes. The time taken for 90% decomposition of A is ______ minutes.","type":"num","answer":"399","range":[399.0,399.0],"explain":"Common slips: 216 (assumes 90% is 1.8 half-lives); 400 (rounding 3.33); 360 (3 half-lives).\nWorking: first order; k = 0.693/120; t₉₀ = 2.303 log10 / k = 120 × 3.32 = 398.6 ≈ 399 min.\nKey idea: t₉₀ ≈ 3.32 t½ for first order.","traps":["Linear time scaling: Use log form: t₉₀/t½ = log10/log2."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"For the adsorption of hydrogen on platinum, the activation energy is 30 kJ mol⁻¹ and for the adsorption of hydrogen on nickel, the activation energy is 41.4 kJ mol⁻¹. The logarithm of the ratio of the rates of chemisorption on equal areas of the metals at 300 K is ______ (Nearest integer)\nGiven: ln 10 = 2.3, R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (≈4.6): uses ln instead of log (forgets ln 10). 0 or negative: subtracts in the wrong order. Unit slip (kJ not converted) gives ≈0.002.\nWorking: assuming equal A, log(r_Pt/r_Ni) = (Ea,Ni − Ea,Pt)/(2.3RT) = 11400/(2.3 × 8.3 × 300) = 11400/5727 = 1.99 ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Same A and T → log(k₁/k₂) = ΔEa/2.3RT.","traps":["ln vs log: Divide by 2.3 to get log₁₀.","Adsorption context seems out of syllabus: Recognise a plain Arrhenius ratio."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molecularity","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Consider the following reaction that goes from A to B in three steps as shown below:\nChoose the correct option","options":["Number of intermediates 2; Number of activated complexes 3; Rate determining step III","Number of intermediates 2; Number of activated complexes 3; Rate determining step II","Number of intermediates 2; Number of activated complexes 3; Rate determining step I","Number of intermediates 3; Number of activated complexes 2; Rate determining step II"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) picks the last step as RDS; (C) picks the first step; (D) swaps the counts of intermediates and activated complexes.\nWorking: three maxima = 3 activated complexes; two minima between them = 2 intermediates; Step II has the highest barrier so it is rate determining.\nKey idea: Peaks = activated complexes, valleys between peaks = intermediates, tallest barrier = slowest step.","traps":["Peaks and valleys swapped: Count maxima for transition states, interior minima for intermediates.","RDS taken as first or last step: Compare barrier heights from each preceding minimum."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Apr06S2C03-p.webp"],"topics":["Molecularity","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of given statement/s which is/are correct is ______.\n(A) The stronger the temperature dependence of the rate constant, the higher is the activation energy.\n(B) If a reaction has zero activation energy, its rate is independent of temperature.\n(C) The stronger the temperature dependence of the rate constant, the smaller is the activation energy.\n(D) If there is no correlation between the temperature and the rate constant then it means that the reaction has negative activation energy.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Student counting 3 accepts (D), thinking 'no correlation' implies negative Ea; counting 1 rejects (B) believing all rates rise with T.\nWorking: k = A e^(−Ea/RT); d ln k/dT = Ea/RT². (A) true; (B) true (k = A, constant, if A taken T-independent); (C) false; (D) false – no correlation means Ea = 0, not negative. Count = 2.\nKey idea: Ea measures how strongly k depends on T; Ea = 0 → k independent of T.","traps":["No correlation read as negative Ea: Zero slope ↔ Ea = 0.","Zero-Ea reaction still assumed T-dependent: With Ea = 0, k = A."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Catalyst effect","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The correct reaction profile diagram for a positive catalyst reaction. [Each option is an energy vs reaction coordinate plot with curves labelled \"with catalyst\" (dashed) and \"without catalyst\" (solid)]","options":["Catalysed (dashed) curve has a higher peak, shifted along the reaction coordinate, and ends at a separately drawn product at about the same energy as the uncatalysed product","Catalysed curve peaks earlier along the reaction coordinate but its maximum is only slightly lower; curves shifted sideways","Catalysed curve has a higher peak than the uncatalysed curve; same reactant and product levels","Catalysed curve has a lower peak than the uncatalysed curve; same reactant and product levels"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) changes the product energy and raises the barrier; (B) shifts the curve sideways (implies a different path length, not a clearly lower barrier); (C) raises the barrier (negative catalyst).\nWorking: a positive catalyst gives an alternative path with lower activation energy and does not change the energies of reactants or products (ΔH unchanged).\nKey idea: Only the hump height changes; both ends stay put.","traps":["Barrier shift misread: Compare peak heights, not positions."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"t₈₇.₅ is the time required for the reaction to undergo 87.5% completion and t₅₀ is the time required for the reaction to undergo 50% completion. The relation between t₈₇.₅ and t₅₀ for a first order reaction is t₈₇.₅ = x × t₅₀. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.75: ratio of percentages (87.5/50). 7 or 8: uses 1/8 directly. 2: miscounts halvings.\nWorking: 87.5 % completion leaves 1/8 = (1/2)³, i.e. three half-lives for a first-order reaction.\nKey idea: 100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5 % remaining = three half-lives.","traps":["Percentages divided directly: Count halvings of the remaining amount."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"A and B are two substances undergoing radioactive decay in a container. The half life of A is 15 min and that of B is 5 min. If the initial concentration of B is 4 times that of A and they both start decaying at the same time, how much time will it take for the concentration of both of them to be same? ______ min.","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 (taking two half-lives of B only), 30 (two half-lives of A), 7.5.\nWorking: [A] = [A]₀(½)^(t/15) and [B] = 4[A]₀(½)^(t/5); they are equal when 2^(t/5 − t/15) = 4, i.e. 2t/15 = 2, so t = 15 min.\nKey idea: 4 = 2^(t/5 − t/15) = 2^(2t/15) → t = 15 min.","traps":["Only B's decay considered: Write both expressions and equate."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A → B. The above reaction is of zero order. Half life of this reaction is 50 min. The time taken for the concentration of A to reduce to one-fourth of its initial value is ________ min. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"75","range":[75.0,75.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 100 (two half-lives, first-order thinking), 25 (successive half-lives halving for zero order taken wrongly).\nWorking: t½ = [A]₀/2k → k = [A]₀/100 min⁻¹; for [A] = [A]₀/4, t = (3/4)[A]₀/k = 75 min\nKey idea: Zero order: consuming 3/4 takes 1.5 × the time to consume 1/2.","traps":["Two half-lives used: That works only for first order; zero order is linear in time."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The number of correct statement/s from the following is __________\nA. Larger the activation energy, smaller is the value of the rate constant.\nB. The higher is the activation energy, higher is the value of the temperature coefficient.\nC. At lower temperatures, increase in temperature causes more change in the value of k than at higher temperature\nD. A plot of ln k vs 1/T is a straight line with slope equal to −Eₐ/R","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (accepting A without considering the A-factor), 2 (rejecting C by thinking k changes more at high T).\nWorking: B (d ln k/dT = Eₐ/RT², larger for larger Eₐ), C (same expression larger at low T) and D are correct. A is not unconditionally true because k also depends on the pre-exponential factor A; with A rejected the count is 3 (if A were accepted the count would be 4).\nKey idea: d ln k/dT = Eₐ/RT²: bigger for larger Eₐ and for lower T; k itself also needs the A-factor.","traps":["Statement A is conditional: k depends on both A and Eₐ; without equal A the claim is not general.","Temperature sensitivity at low vs high T: Fractional change ∝ 1/T², so larger at low T."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"A student has studied the decomposition of a gas AB₃ at 25°C. He obtained the following data. Table: p (mm Hg) 50, 100, 200, 400; relative t₁/₂ (s) 4, 2, 1, 0.5. The order of the reaction is","options":["2","0.5","1","0 (zero)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): sees t₁/₂ change but defaults to first order. (D): zero order would need t₁/₂ ∝ p (increasing). (B): wrong exponent algebra.\nWorking: each doubling of p halves t₁/₂, so t₁/₂ ∝ p⁻¹; since t₁/₂ ∝ p^(1−n), 1 − n = −1 and n = 2.\nKey idea: t₁/₂ halves as p doubles → t₁/₂ ∝ 1/p → 1 − n = −1 → n = 2.","traps":["Direction of t₁/₂ change: Zero order: t₁/₂ rises with [A]₀; second order: falls."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For the first order reaction A → B, the half life is 30 min. The time taken for 75% completion of the reaction is ________ min. (Nearest integer) Given : log 2 = 0.3010, log 3 = 0.4771, log 5 = 0.6989","type":"num","answer":"60","range":[60.0,60.0],"explain":"Common slips: 45 if 75% is treated as 1.5 half-lives; 120 if 25% remaining is mistaken for 4 half-lives.\nWorking: 75% completion = two half-lives = 60 min\nKey idea: 100 → 50 → 25: two half-lives.","traps":["Linear scaling of time with % completion: First order: equal times halve the amount; 25% left = 2 t½.","Log values tempt full integrated-law calculation: Use the half-life shortcut; logs are not needed."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"A first order reaction has the rate constant, k = 4.6 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. The number of correct statement/s from the following is/are ______. Given: log 3 = 0.48. A. Reaction completes in 1000 s. B. The reaction has a half-life of 500 s. C. The time required for 10% completion is 25 times the time required for 90% completion. D. The degree of dissociation is equal to (1 – e^(–kt)). E. The rate and the rate constant have the same unit.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (accepting C because the number 25 matches log 3 = 0.48); 2 (taking 500 s as half-life via 2.303/k); 0 (thinking D refers to dissociation of a salt, not fraction reacted).\nWorking: A false – a first-order reaction never strictly completes; B false – t½ = 0.693/k ≈ 150 s; C false – it is reversed: t₉₀%/t₁₀% = 1/log(10/9) = 1/0.04 = 25; D true – fraction reacted = 1 − e^(−kt); E false – mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ vs s⁻¹. Count = 1.\nKey idea: log(10/9) = 1 – 2(0.48) = 0.04 so t₉₀%/t₁₀% = 25 – the given log 3 makes the reversed statement look right.","traps":["Reversed ratio: Always check which time is larger: more completion takes longer.","t½ from 2.303/k: t½ = 0.693/k ≈ 150 s; 2.303/k is the time for 90% completion."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"For certain chemical reaction X → Y, the rate of formation of product is plotted against the time as shown in the figure. The number of correct statement/s from the following is ___________.\n(A) Over all order of this reaction is one.\n(B) Order of this reaction can't be determined.\n(C) In region I and III, the reaction is of first and zero order respectively.\n(D) In region-II, the reaction is of first order.\n(E) In region-II, the order of reaction is in the range of 0.1 to 0.9.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: (A), (D) assume first order from a linear segment; (C) reads 'linear' as first order and 'constant' as zero order; (E) assigns fractional order to the curved region – the C+E reading gives 2.\nWorking: the graph is rate vs time, so it cannot give the order (that needs rate vs concentration); only (B) is correct, giving 1. (Some solutions also accept (C) and (E), giving 2; the official answer is 1.)\nKey idea: Order needs rate vs concentration; a rate–time graph alone does not fix it.","traps":["Linear rate–time segment taken as first order: Check the axes: time, not concentration, is on x.","Fractional order in curved region: Curvature vs time says nothing about order."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan29S1C14-p.webp"],"topics":["Rate law, rate constant & order","Rate of reaction"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"For conversion of compound A → B, the rate constant of the reaction was found to be 4.6 × 10⁻⁵ L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹. The order of the reaction is ____________.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 1 (reading order from the A → B stoichiometry); 0 (confusing with rate units).\nWorking: units of k = (mol L⁻¹)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹; L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ means 1 − n = −1, so n = 2.\nKey idea: Order comes from units of k, not from the equation.","traps":["Order from stoichiometry: Use k units: (mol L⁻¹)¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"If compound A reacts with B following first order kinetics with rate constant 2.011 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. The time taken by A (in seconds) to reduce from 7 g to 2 g will be ______. (Nearest Integer) [log 5 = 0.698, log 7 = 0.845, log 2 = 0.301]","type":"num","answer":"623","range":[623.0,623.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 271 (omitting 2.303), 347 (using log 5), 1145 (dropping the log term).\nWorking: t = (2.303/2.011×10⁻³) × log(7/2) = 1145.2 × 0.544 = 623\nKey idea: Mass ratio equals concentration ratio; t = 2.303/k × log 3.5.","traps":["2.303 factor dropped: With log₁₀ use 2.303; with ln use none.","Irrelevant reactant B and log 5: First order in A depends only on A's ratio."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"An organic compound undergoes first-order decomposition. If the time taken for the 60% decomposition is 540 s, then the time required for 90% decomposition will be ____ s. (Nearest integer). Given: ln 10 = 2.3; log 2 = 0.3","type":"num","answer":"1350","range":[1350.0,1350.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 810 (using t₉₀ = 1.5 t₆₀ by proportion), 2700 (using log 1.67 or 0.4 → 0.2), 900 (linear % scaling).\nWorking: t₉₀/t₆₀ = log10 / log2.5 = 1/0.4 ⇒ 540 × 2.5 = 1350 s\nKey idea: t₉₀/t₆₀ = log(10)/log(2.5) = 1/0.4 = 2.5.","traps":["Remaining vs decomposed fraction: Use [R]₀/[R] = 1/0.4.","Linear scaling: First-order time ∝ log of ratio, not % reacted."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A → B. The rate constants of the above reaction at 200 K and 300 K are 0.03 min⁻¹ and 0.05 min⁻¹ respectively. The activation energy for the reaction is ________ J (Nearest integer) (Given : ln10 = 2.3, R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, log 5 = 0.70, log 3 = 0.48, log 2 = 0.30)","type":"num","answer":"2520","range":[2520.0,2520.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1096 (using log without 2.303), 5803 or 5800 (using ln but then multiplying again by 2.3 wrongly), 420 (using 1/T difference wrongly).\nWorking: log(5/3) = 0.22; Ea = 2.3 × 8.3 × 0.22 × (200 × 300/100) = 2519.9 ≈ 2520 J\nKey idea: Ea = 2.303R log(k₂/k₁) T₁T₂/(T₂ − T₁).","traps":["ln vs log factor: Use 2.303 with log10 form, or ln form directly — not both or neither."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The rate constant for a first order reaction is 20 min⁻¹. The time required for the initial concentration of the reactant to reduce to its 1/32 level is ________ × 10⁻² min. (Nearest integer) (Given: ln 10 = 2.303 and log 2 = 0.3010)","type":"num","answer":"17","range":[17.0,17.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 or 4 (using log without 2.303 → 0.075), 173 (scaling error), 35 (using t½ only once ×10).\nWorking: t = (2.303/20) × log 32 = (2.303 × 1.505)/20 = 0.1733 min = 17.33 × 10⁻² min\nKey idea: 1/32 means 5 half-lives: t = 5 × 0.693/20 = 0.173 min.","traps":["Scaling to ×10⁻²: Multiply by 100 and round: 17."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The half life for the decomposition of gaseous compound A is 240 s when the gaseous pressure was 500 Torr initially. When the pressure was 250 Torr, the half life was found to be 4.0 min. The order of the reaction is ____. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (reading 4.0 as smaller than 240 and assuming t½ changes); 0.\nWorking: 4.0 min = 240 s, so t½ is independent of initial pressure → first order (t½ ∝ p₀^(1−n) gives n = 1).\nKey idea: Convert units first; equal t½ → first order.","traps":["Hidden unit change: Convert to the same unit before comparing."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"For the decomposition of azomethane, CH₃N₂CH₃(g) → CH₃CH₃(g) + N₂(g), a first order reaction, the variation in partial pressure with time at 600 K is given as shown below. The half life of the reaction is _____ × 10⁻⁵ s. [Nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 87 or similar (using log₁₀ and slope = −k/2.303); powers-of-ten slips (0.2, 20).\nWorking: slope = −k, so k = 3.465 × 10⁴ s⁻¹; t½ = 0.693/k = 0.693/(3.465 × 10⁴) = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ s.\nKey idea: ln plot slope is −k directly (no 2.303 factor).","traps":["2.303 factor misapplied: Axis is ln, so slope = −k."],"img":["img/jee/JM22Jul25S2C25-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For a reaction A → 2B + C the half lives are 100 s and 50 s when the concentration of reactant A is 0.5 and 1.0 mol L⁻¹ respectively. The order of the reaction is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 (thinks t½ ∝ [A]₀ – direction reversed), 1 (assumes first order), 3 (from stoichiometry 1+2).\nWorking: t½ ∝ [A]₀^(1−n); doubling [A]₀ halves t½ → 2^(1−n) = ½ → n = 2.\nKey idea: t½ inversely proportional to [A]₀ → second order.","traps":["Order from stoichiometry: Order comes from rate data only.","Direction of t½ change: Zero order: t½ rises with [A]₀; here it falls."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"At 30°C, the half life for the decomposition of AB₂ is 200 s and is independent of the initial concentration of AB₂. The time required for 80% of the AB₂ to decompose is (Given: log 2 = 0.30; log 3 = 0.48)","options":["200 s","323 s","467 s","532 s"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) = t½ itself; (B) 323 s from using log 3 or (1/0.8) style errors; (D) 532 s from misusing log values (e.g. log 6).\nWorking: half-life independent of [A]₀ → first order; t = (t½/log 2)·log 5 = 200 × 0.70/0.30 = 466.7 ≈ 467 s.\nKey idea: 80% decomposed → [A]/[A]₀ = 1/5; t = t½ × log5/log2.","traps":["Fraction left vs decomposed: Use [A]₀/[A] = 100/20.","Unused log 3: log 5 = 1 − log 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"2NO + 2H₂ → N₂ + 2H₂O\nThe above reaction has been studied at 800°C. The related data are given in the table below\nReaction serial number | Initial pressure of H₂ / kPa | Initial pressure of NO / kPa | Initial rate (−dp/dt)/(kPa/s)\n1 | 65.6 | 40.0 | 0.135\n2 | 65.6 | 20.1 | 0.033\n3 | 38.6 | 65.6 | 0.214\n4 | 19.2 | 65.6 | 0.106\nThe order of the reaction with respect to NO is____","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (using runs 3–4, which vary H₂), 3 (total order).\nWorking: runs 1 and 2 (same p(H₂)): p(NO) ratio 40.0/20.1 ≈ 2, rate ratio 0.135/0.033 ≈ 4.1 → order 2 in NO (runs 3, 4 give order 1 in H₂).\nKey idea: Pick runs where only NO changes: doubling NO quadruples rate.","traps":["Wrong pair of runs: Use runs 1–2 where p(H₂) is fixed.","Order from stoichiometry: Always get order from data."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"[A] (Reactant) → [B] (Product). If formation of compound [B] follows the first order of kinetics and after 70 minutes the concentration of [A] was found to be half of its initial concentration. Then the rate constant of the reaction is x × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹. The value of x is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"165","range":[165.0,165.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9900 (k in min⁻¹ ×10⁻⁶ mistake: 0.0099 min⁻¹); 0.165 (power error).\nWorking: t½ = 70 min = 4200 s; k = 0.693/4200 = 1.65 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ = 165 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹.\nKey idea: k = 0.693/t½ with t in seconds.","traps":["Time unit conversion: Convert 70 min to 4200 s first."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C26","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"For the given first order reaction A → B the half life of the reaction is 0.3010 min. The ratio of the initial concentration of reactant to the concentration of reactant at time 2.0 min will be equal to ___________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"100","range":[100.0,100.0],"explain":"Common slips: Counting half-lives (2/0.301 = 6.64, and 6.64 halvings give a factor ≈ 100) gives the same answer; mixing ln and log gives e² ≈ 7; dividing 2 min by t½ and stopping gives 7.\nWorking: k = 0.693/0.3010 = 2.303 min⁻¹; log([A]₀/[A]) = kt/2.303 = 2.303 × 2/2.303 = 2 → ratio = 100.\nKey idea: k = 2.303 min⁻¹, so log([A]₀/[A]) equals t in minutes = 2, ratio = 10².","traps":["ln vs log: Use 2.303 log consistently."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For a reaction, given below is the graph of ln k vs 1/T. The activation energy for the reaction is equal to ____ cal mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) (Given: R = 2 cal K⁻¹ mol⁻¹). Graph: straight line from ln k = 20 at 1/T = 0 down to ln k = 0 at 1/T = 5 K⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 4 (forgetting R), 18 or 9 (using log₁₀ factor 2.303), 40.\nWorking: slope = −20/5 = −4 K = −Ea/R → Ea = 4 × 2 = 8 cal mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Slope = −Ea/R with natural log.","traps":["ln vs log: Axis is ln k; no 2.303.","Multiplying by R: Ea = −slope × R."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The reaction between X and Y is first order with respect to X and zero order with respect to Y. Experiment / [X] mol L⁻¹ / [Y] mol L⁻¹ / Initial rate mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹: I. 0.1, 0.1, 2 × 10⁻³; II. L, 0.2, 4 × 10⁻³; III. 0.4, 0.4, M × 10⁻³; IV. 0.1, 0.2, 2 × 10⁻³. Examine the data of table and calculate ratio of numerical values of M and L. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"40","range":[40.0,40.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.025 (L/M inverted); 20 (M taken as 4 by doubling); 80 (using [Y] dependence).\nWorking: rate = k[X], k = 2×10⁻³/0.1 = 0.02 min⁻¹. II: 4×10⁻³ = 0.02 L → L = 0.2. III: rate = 0.02 × 0.4 = 8×10⁻³ → M = 8. M/L = 8/0.2 = 40.\nKey idea: Ignore [Y] completely; scale rate with [X] only.","traps":["Zero order ignored: Zero order: rate independent of [Y].","Ratio inverted: Read 'M and L' order."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Assuming 1 μg of trace radioactive element X with a half life of 30 years is absorbed by a growing tree. The amount of X remaining in the tree after 100 years is ___ × 10⁻¹ μg. [Given: ln 10 = 2.303; log 2 = 0.30]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.98 ≈ 1 if exact log used (rounding OK); 3 or 0.3 from linear decay assumption; 1.25 from integer 3 half-lives.\nWorking: N = N₀ (1/2)^(100/30); log(N/N₀) = −(10/3)(0.30) = −1.0 → N = 0.1 μg = 1 × 10⁻¹ μg.\nKey idea: (1/2)^(10/3) = 10⁻¹.","traps":["Integer half-lives used: Use the exponent 10/3 with logs."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The rate constants for decomposition of acetaldehyde have been measured over the temperature range 700–1000 K. The data has been analysed by plotting ln k vs 10³/T graph (shown below). The value of activation energy for the reaction is ____ kJ mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) (Given: R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"154","range":[154.0,154.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.154 (ignoring the 10³ factor); 354 (using log k slope × 2.303); 153735 (left in J).\nWorking: ln k = ln A − (Ea/R)(1/T) = ln A − [Ea/(1000R)](10³/T). Slope −18.5 = −Ea/(1000 × 8.31) → Ea = 153 735 J ≈ 154 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Slope against 10³/T = −Ea/(R × 10³), so Ea = 18.5 × 10³ × R.","traps":["Axis scaling ignored: Multiply the slope by 10³ before multiplying by R.","ln vs log: Here the plot is ln k: no 2.303."],"img":["img/jee/JM22Jun24S1C25-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"For a first order reaction, the time required for completion of 90% reaction is 'x' times the half life of the reaction. The value of 'x' is (Given: ln 10 = 2.303 and log 2 = 0.3010)","options":["1.12","2.43","3.32","33.31"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1.12 – using [R]₀/[R] = 1/0.9 (fraction reacted mistaken for remaining); (D) 33.31 – decimal slip.\nWorking: t₉₀ = (2.303/k) log 10 = 2.303/k; t½ = 0.693/k; x = 2.303/0.693 = 3.32.\nKey idea: 90 % complete means 10 % left, so [R]₀/[R] = 10 and t₉₀ = 1/log 2 half-lives = 3.32.","traps":["Fraction remaining taken as 0.9: 90 % completion leaves 10 %: ratio 10, not 1/0.9."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For a given chemical reaction γ₁A + γ₂B → γ₃C + γ₄D, concentration of C changes from 10 mmol dm⁻³ to 20 mmol dm⁻³ in 10 seconds. Rate of appearance of D is 1.5 times the rate of disappearance of B which is twice the rate of disappearance A. The rate of appearance of D has been experimentally determined to be 9 mmol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. Therefore the rate of reaction is ____ mmol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9 – reports rate of D; 3 or 6 – reports rate of A or B; also non-integer if coefficients not reduced to smallest integers.\nWorking: rate(C) = 10/10 = 1; rate(D) = 9, rate(B) = 9/1.5 = 6, rate(A) = 3. Coefficients ∝ rates: γ₁:γ₂:γ₃:γ₄ = 3:6:1:9. Rate of reaction = (1/γ₃) d[C]/dt = 1/1 = 1 mmol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.\nKey idea: Rates of species are proportional to coefficients; smallest set 3:6:1:9 gives rate = rate(C).","traps":["Species rate given as reaction rate: Divide each species rate by its coefficient.","Chain of ratios misread: Write D = 1.5B, B = 2A explicitly."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"At 345 K, the half life for the decomposition of a sample of a gaseous compound initially at 55.5 kPa was 340 s. When the pressure was 27.8 kPa, the half life was found to be 170 s. The order of the reaction is ________. [integer answer]","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 1 (half-life confused as always constant / proportional), 2 (thinking t½ ∝ 1/p₀).\nWorking: t½ ∝ [A]₀^(1−n); halving initial pressure (55.5 → 27.8 kPa) halves t½ (340 → 170 s), so t½ ∝ p₀ ⇒ 1 − n = 1 ⇒ n = 0.\nKey idea: t½ ∝ p₀ → zero order; t½ constant → first order; t½ ∝ 1/p₀ → second order.","traps":["Direction of t½ dependence: Use t½ ∝ [A]₀^(1−n) and plug the ratio.","Zero as an integer answer: Zero order is valid – common for surface decompositions."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A flask is filled with equal moles of A and B. The half lives of A and B are 100 s and 50 s respectively and are independent of the initial concentration. The time required for the concentration of A to be four times that of B is ______ s. (Given : ln 2 = 0.693)","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: 100 s – ratio of 2 instead of 4; 400 s – using ratio of half-lives incorrectly; 50 s – mixing up which decays faster.\nWorking: half-life independent of concentration → first order. [A]/[B] = e^{(k_B – k_A)t} = 4; k_B – k_A = 0.693/50 – 0.693/100 = 0.693/100 s⁻¹; t = ln 4 × 100/0.693 = 200 s. (Equivalently, after 200 s A has had 2 half-lives (1/4) and B 4 half-lives (1/16).)\nKey idea: Count half-lives: at t = 200 s, A = 1/4, B = 1/16 → ratio 4.","traps":["Order not identified: Concentration-independent t½ ⇒ first order.","Ratio direction: [A]/[B] = 2^(–t/100) ÷ 2^(–t/50) = 2^(t/100) = 4."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Catalyst effect","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Catalyst A reduces the activation energy for a reaction by 10 kJ mol⁻¹ at 300 K. The ratio of rate constants, k(T, catalysed)/k(T, uncatalysed), is eˣ. The value of x is ______. [nearest integer] [Assume that the pre-exponential factor is same in both the cases. Given R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (dividing by 2.303); 0 (kJ not converted, 10/2493); 1.\nWorking: ln(k_cat/k_uncat) = ΔEa/RT = 10000/(8.31 × 300) = 4.01 → x = 4.\nKey idea: Ratio = e^(ΔEa/RT) directly since the answer is in powers of e.","traps":["log vs ln: The answer is a power of e, so use ln: x = ΔEa/RT."],"topics":["Catalyst effect","Arrhenius equation"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The rate constant for a first order reaction is given by the following equation: ln k = 33.24 − (2.0 × 10⁴ K)/T. The activation energy for the reaction is given by ______ kJ mol⁻¹ (nearest integer). (Given: R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"166","range":[166.0,166.0],"explain":"Common slips: 382 – multiplies by 2.303 as if the equation were log₁₀; 166000 – leaves J; 20 – forgets R.\nWorking: ln k = ln A − Ea/RT, so Ea/R = 2.0 × 10⁴ K → Ea = 2.0 × 10⁴ × 8.3 = 1.66 × 10⁵ J mol⁻¹ = 166 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: The coefficient of 1/T in ln k is Ea/R – no 2.303 needed.","traps":["Spurious 2.303 factor: ln form: slope = −Ea/R directly."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"It has been found that for a chemical reaction with rise in temperature by 9 K the rate constant gets doubled. Assuming a reaction to be occurring at 300 K, the value of activation energy is found to be ______ kJ mol⁻¹. [nearest integer] (Given ln 10 = 2.3, R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, log 2 = 0.30)","type":"num","answer":"59","range":[59.0,59.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 58 or 60 (using 2.303 and 0.3010 instead of the given 2.3 and 0.30); 0.059 (J vs kJ); 57 (using 1/300 − 1/309 approximated as 9/90000).\nWorking: ln 2 = (Ea/R)(1/300 − 1/309); ln 2 = 2.3 × 0.30 = 0.69; 1/300 − 1/309 = 9/92700 = 9.709 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹; Ea = 0.69 × 8.3/(9.709 × 10⁻⁵) = 58 989 J ≈ 59 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Use the exact given constants; T₂ = 309 K.","traps":["Given constants ignored: Use ln 10 = 2.3 and log 2 = 0.30 as given."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"For a first order reaction A → B, the rate constant, k = 5.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ s⁻¹. The time required for 67% completion of reaction is x × 10⁻¹ times the half life of reaction. The value of x is ______ (Nearest integer) (Given: log 3 = 0.4771)","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (ignoring ×10⁻¹), 1 (log ratio of 67/33), 6 (using log(100/67)).\nWorking: t₆₇ = (2.303/k) log(100/33) ≈ (2.303/k) log 3; t½ = (2.303/k) log 2; ratio = 0.4771/0.3010 = 1.585 ≈ 15.85 × 10⁻¹ → 16 (k is irrelevant).\nKey idea: Remaining fraction = 1/3 → t = t½ × log 3/log 2.","traps":["Fraction remaining vs completed: Use [R]₀/[R] = 100/33 ≈ 3.","Scale factor 10⁻¹: Multiply ratio by 10 before rounding."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A radioactive element has a half-life of 200 days. The percentage of original activity remaining after 83 days is ______. (Nearest integer) (Given: antilog 0.125 = 1.333, antilog 0.693 = 4.93)","type":"num","answer":"75","range":[75.0,75.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25 (fraction decayed instead of remaining); 79 (linear interpolation 1 − 83/400).\nWorking: log(A₀/A) = 0.693 × 83/(2.303 × 200) = 0.125 → A₀/A = 1.333 → A/A₀ = 0.75 = 75%.\nKey idea: t/t½ = 0.415 → 0.415 half-lives leave 0.75 of the activity.","traps":["Remaining vs decayed: A/A₀ = 1/1.333.","Red-herring antilog 0.693: Use only the computed log value 0.125."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The activation energy of one of the reactions in a biochemical process is 532611 J mol⁻¹. When the temperature falls from 310 K to 300 K, the change in rate constant observed is k₃₀₀ = x × 10⁻³ k₃₁₀. The value of x is ________. [Given: ln 10 = 2.3, R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Sign slip gives 10³ instead of 10⁻³ (x = 10⁶); using log without 2.303 gives wrong exponent.\nWorking: ln(k₃₁₀/k₃₀₀) = (532611/8.3)(1/300 – 1/310) = 64170 × 1.0753 × 10⁻⁴ = 6.90 = 3 × 2.3, so k₃₀₀ = 10⁻³ k₃₁₀; x = 1.\nKey idea: Ea/R × ΔT/(T₁T₂) = 6.9 = 3 ln 10.","traps":["Direction of change: k₃₀₀ < k₃₁₀ so ratio 10⁻³.","ln vs log: Divide ln value by 2.3 to get powers of 10."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The equation k = (6.5 × 10¹² s⁻¹) e^(−26000 K/T) is followed for the decomposition of compound A. The activation energy for the reaction is ____ kJ mol⁻¹. [nearest integer] (Given: R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"216","range":[216.0,216.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 2.303 R (498) or giving J instead of kJ.\nWorking: Ea/R = 26000 K → Ea = 26000 × 8.314 = 216 164 J mol⁻¹ ≈ 216 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Exponent = −Ea/RT, so Ea = R × 26000 K.","traps":["2.303 introduced: The given form is natural exponential."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The following data was obtained for chemical reaction given below at 975 K. 2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). [NO] (mol L⁻¹), [H₂] (mol L⁻¹), Rate (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹): (A) 8×10⁻⁵, 8×10⁻⁵, 7×10⁻⁹; (B) 24×10⁻⁵, 8×10⁻⁵, 2.1×10⁻⁸; (C) 24×10⁻⁵, 32×10⁻⁵, 8.4×10⁻⁸. The order of the reaction with respect to NO is ______. [Integer answer]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (taking the order from the stoichiometric coefficient; the textbook rate law of this reaction is second order in NO, but these data give 1); 0 if wrong rows are compared.\nWorking: A→B, [NO] tripled at constant [H₂], rate 7×10⁻⁹ → 2.1×10⁻⁸ (×3) → first order in NO. (B→C gives first order in H₂ as well.)\nKey idea: Order = log(rate ratio)/log(conc ratio) with the other reactant fixed.","traps":["Order from experimental data, not stoichiometry: Compare A and B: [NO] ×3 at constant [H₂] → rate ×3 → order 1.","Pick the right pair of rows: Use only runs where the other concentration is fixed."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The reaction rate for the reaction [PtCl₄]²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ [Pt(H₂O)Cl₃]⁻ + Cl⁻ was measured as a function of concentrations of different species. It was observed that –d[[PtCl₄]²⁻]/dt = 4.8 × 10⁻⁵ [[PtCl₄]²⁻] – 2.4 × 10⁻³ [[Pt(H₂O)Cl₃]⁻][Cl⁻], where square brackets are used to denote molar concentrations. The equilibrium constant Kc = ____. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 50 (k_b/k_f, inverted), 2 (reporting 0.02 × 100), 1 (rounding up a small number).\nWorking: at equilibrium net rate = 0 → Kc = k_f/k_b = 4.8 × 10⁻⁵ / 2.4 × 10⁻³ = 0.02 → nearest integer 0.\nKey idea: At equilibrium the forward and backward terms are equal: Kc = k_f/k_b = 0.02 → 0.","traps":["Kc = k_f / k_b, not inverse: Products over reactants ↔ forward over backward.","Nearest integer of 0.02 is 0: Trust the rounding instruction."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The reaction that occurs in a breath analyser, a device used to determine the alcohol level in a person's blood stream is 2K₂Cr₂O₇ + 8H₂SO₄ + 3C₂H₆O → 2Cr₂(SO₄)₃ + 3C₂H₄O₂ + 2K₂SO₄ + 11H₂O. If the rate of appearance of Cr₂(SO₄)₃ is 2.67 mol min⁻¹ at a particular time, the rate of disappearance of C₂H₆O at the same time is ______ mol min⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (ratio inverted: 2.67 × 2/3 = 1.78); 3 (2.67 taken equal); 8 (multiplied by 3).\nWorking: rate = (1/2) d[Cr₂(SO₄)₃]/dt = −(1/3) d[C₂H₆O]/dt, so −d[C₂H₆O]/dt = (3/2) × 2.67 = 4.005 ≈ 4.\nKey idea: Rate of species ÷ its coefficient is the same for all species.","traps":["Coefficient ratio inverted: Ethanol coefficient 3 > Cr₂(SO₄)₃ coefficient 2, so its rate is larger.","Extra data distracts: Use only the two species asked."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The first order rate constant for the decomposition of CaCO₃ at 700 K is 6.36 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ and activation energy is 209 kJ mol⁻¹. Its rate constant (in s⁻¹) at 600 K is x × 10⁻⁶. The value of x is ___. (Nearest integer) [Given R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; log 6.36 × 10⁻³ = −2.19, 10⁻⁴·⁷⁹ = 1.62 × 10⁻⁵]","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 63.6 or similar if the sign of the temperature term is reversed (k increases instead of decreasing); ~0.16 or 162 from mishandling the ×10⁻⁶ scaling; using ln instead of log gives a wildly wrong exponent.\nWorking: log(k₆₀₀/k₇₀₀) = −(209000/(2.303 × 8.31))(1/600 − 1/700) = −10921 × 2.381 × 10⁻⁴ = −2.60; log k₆₀₀ = −2.19 − 2.60 = −4.79; k₆₀₀ = 1.62 × 10⁻⁵ = 16.2 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹ → x = 16.\nKey idea: Going down 100 K at Ea = 209 kJ drops k by ~10²·⁶.","traps":["Sign of (1/T₁ − 1/T₂): Lower T must give smaller k – check direction.","Scaling to ×10⁻⁶: 1.62 × 10⁻⁵ = 16.2 × 10⁻⁶."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"For a first order reaction, the ratio of the time for 75% completion of a reaction to the time for 50% completion is _______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.5 – linear thinking (75/50); 4 – squares instead of logs.\nWorking: t = (2.303/k) log([R]₀/[R]); t₇₅ = (2.303/k) log 4 = 2 × (2.303/k) log 2 = 2 t₅₀. Ratio = 2.\nKey idea: 75% = two half-lives.","traps":["Linear proportion: Use log: 25% left = two half-lives."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Activation energy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"According to the following figure, the magnitude of the enthalpy change of the reaction A + B → M + N in kJ mol⁻¹ is equal to _________. (Integer answer) [Energy vs reaction-coordinate profile: reactants A+B, a hump above them (x = 20 kJ mol⁻¹ from the A+B level to the peak), products M+N lower than A+B by y = 45 kJ mol⁻¹, and a further gap z = 15 kJ mol⁻¹ marked below the M+N level]","type":"num","answer":"45","range":[45.0,45.0],"explain":"Common slips: 20 – takes Ea; 65 – adds x + y (backward Ea); 60 – adds y + z; 15 – takes z.\nWorking: ΔH is the energy difference between the reactant (A+B) and product (M+N) levels, i.e. y = 45 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic, ΔH = −45). x is the activation energy of the forward reaction and z is unrelated to ΔH.\nKey idea: ΔH = product level − reactant level only.","traps":["Ea added to ΔH: ΔH ignores the hump; use reactant–product gap.","Sign vs magnitude: Question asks magnitude: 45."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"For the reaction A → B, the rate constant k (in s⁻¹) is given by log₁₀ k = 20.35 − (2.47 × 10³)/T. The energy of activation in kJ mol⁻¹ is _______. (Nearest integer) [Given: R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"47","range":[47.0,47.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 21 (forgets 2.303: 8.314 × 2470 = 20.5 kJ), 47293 (J instead of kJ).\nWorking: log k = log A − Ea/(2.303RT) → Ea = 2.303 × 8.314 × 2470 = 4.73 × 10⁴ J/mol = 47 kJ/mol.\nKey idea: Slope = −Ea/(2.303R).","traps":["Missing 2.303 with log₁₀: log₁₀ form carries 2.303."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Gaseous cyclobutene isomerizes to butadiene in a first order process which has a 'k' value of 3.3 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ at 153 °C. The time in minutes it takes for the isomerization to proceed 40% to completion at this temperature is ______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"26","range":[26.0,26.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 46 (40% taken as remaining: ln 2.5/k = 2777 s); 1548 (seconds not converted); 35 (half-life).\nWorking: t = (2.303/k) log(100/60) = 0.5108/(3.3 × 10⁻⁴) = 1548 s = 25.8 min ≈ 26.\nKey idea: 40% complete → [R]/[R]₀ = 0.6.","traps":["Fraction remaining vs reacted: Completion % reacted → remaining 60%.","Seconds not converted: Divide by 60."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Sucrose hydrolyses in acid solution into glucose and fructose following first order rate law with a half-life of 3.33 h at 25 °C. After 9 h, the fraction of sucrose remaining is f. The value of log₁₀(1/f) is ____ × 10⁻². (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Assume: ln 10 = 2.303, ln 2 = 0.693]","type":"num","answer":"81","range":[81.0,81.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 187 (ln instead of log), 15 (f itself ≈ 0.154).\nWorking: k = 0.693/3.33 = 0.208 h⁻¹; log(1/f) = kt/2.303 = 0.208 × 9/2.303 = 0.813 → 81.\nKey idea: log(1/f) = kt/2.303.","traps":["ln reported instead of log: Divide kt by 2.303."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"For the reaction aA + bB → cC + dD, the plot of log k vs 1/T is given below: [straight line, slope = −10000 K]. The temperature at which the rate constant of the reaction is 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ is ______ K. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [Given: The rate constant of the reaction is 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹ at 500 K.]","type":"num","answer":"526","range":[526.0,526.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 513 (using ln instead of log, i.e. dividing by 2.303 again), 476 (sign slip giving 1/T₂ = 0.0021), 5000 (misreading slope as Ea/R in ln form).\nWorking: slope = −Ea/(2.303R) = −10000 K. log(k₂/k₁) = 10000(1/T₁ − 1/T₂): log(10⁻⁴/10⁻⁵) = 1 = 10000(1/500 − 1/T₂) → 1/T₂ = 0.0019 → T₂ = 526.3 ≈ 526 K.\nKey idea: Slope already includes 2.303R, so use it directly with log.","traps":["Extra 2.303 factor: Slope is for log k, so no 2.303 needed.","Sign of 1/T term: k increases → T₂ > 500 K."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The rate constant of a reaction increases by five times on increase in temperature from 27 °C to 52 °C. The value of activation energy in kJ mol⁻¹ is ______. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer) [R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"52","range":[52.0,52.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: Celsius used in 1/T; log instead of ln without 2.303; J vs kJ (52186).\nWorking: ln 5 = (Ea/R)(1/300 − 1/325) = (Ea/R)(25/97500); Ea = 8.314 × 1.609 × 3900 = 52.2 kJ mol⁻¹ → 52.\nKey idea: Convert to kelvin; use ln 5 = 1.609.","traps":["Celsius in Arrhenius: Always add 273.","log vs ln: Match log₁₀ with 2.303R."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Activation energy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"An exothermic reaction X → Y has an activation energy 30 kJ mol⁻¹. If energy change ΔE during the reaction is –20 kJ, then the activation energy for the reverse reaction in kJ is _______. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 10 (subtracting 20 from 30 – sign trap).\nWorking: Ea(rev) = Ea(fwd) − ΔE = 30 − (−20) = 50 kJ.\nKey idea: For an exothermic reaction the reverse barrier is larger: Ea(f) + |ΔE|.","traps":["Sign of ΔE misused: Ea(rev) = Ea(fwd) − ΔH."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"If the activation energy of a reaction is 80.9 kJ mol⁻¹, the fraction of molecules at 700 K having enough energy to react to form products is e⁻ˣ. The value of x is _______. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Use R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.014 → 0 – Ea left in kJ; 6 – using log₁₀ (divide by 2.303).\nWorking: fraction = e^(–Ea/RT); x = 80900/(8.31 × 700) = 13.91 ≈ 14.\nKey idea: x = Ea/RT with Ea in J.","traps":["Ea not converted to J: Convert 80.9 kJ to 80900 J.","Divided by 2.303: Natural exponent – no 2.303."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Collision theory"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The inactivation rate of a viral preparation is proportional to the amount of virus. In the first minute after preparation, 10% of the virus is inactivated. The rate constant for viral inactivation is ________ × 10⁻³ min⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Use: ln 10 = 2.303; log₁₀ 3 = 0.477; property of logarithm: log xʸ = y log x]","type":"num","answer":"106","range":[106.0,106.0],"explain":"Common slips: 100 (using 0.1/1 as a linear rate); 46 (forgetting the 2.303 factor); 1000 or 10 (inverting 90/100 or scale slip).\nWorking: first order; k = (1/1 min) ln(100/90) = 2.303 × (1 − 2 × 0.477) = 2.303 × 0.046 = 0.1059 min⁻¹ = 106 × 10⁻³ min⁻¹.\nKey idea: Rate ∝ amount → first order; 10% decayed → [R]/[R]₀ = 0.9; log(10/9) = 1 − 2 log 3.","traps":["Zero-order shortcut: Rate ∝ amount means first order: use logs.","log vs ln: Multiply log value by 2.303."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"PCl₅(g) → PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g). In the above first order reaction the concentration of PCl₅ reduces from initial concentration 50 mol L⁻¹ to 10 mol L⁻¹ in 120 minutes at 300 K. The rate constant for the reaction at 300 K is x × 10⁻² min⁻¹. The value of x is _______. [Given log5 = 0.6989]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 33 (zero-order (50−10)/120, ×100); 13 (misplacing the power of ten); 0 or 2 (rounding errors with ln vs log).\nWorking: k = (2.303/120) log(50/10) = 2.303 × 0.6989/120 = 1.341 × 10⁻² min⁻¹ → x ≈ 1.\nKey idea: x × 10⁻² with k = 0.0134 → x = 1.34 ≈ 1.","traps":["Zero-order formula used: First order → use the log form.","Power of ten: k = 0.0134 = 1.34 × 10⁻²."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"N₂O₅(g) → 2NO₂(g) + ½O₂(g). In the above first order reaction the initial concentration of N₂O₅ is 2.40 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ at 318 K. The concentration of N₂O₅ after 1 hour was 1.60 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹. The rate constant of the reaction at 318 K is _________ × 10⁻³ min⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Given : log 3 = 0.477, log 5 = 0.699]","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 0.11 (t in hours, k = 0.405 h⁻¹), 3 (log₁₀ without 2.303).\nWorking: k = (2.303/60 min) log(2.40/1.60) = (2.303/60) × log 1.5; log 1.5 = log 3 − log 2 = 0.477 − (1 − 0.699) = 0.176; k = 2.303 × 0.176/60 = 6.76 × 10⁻³ min⁻¹ → 7.\nKey idea: log 2 = 1 − log 5; t = 60 min.","traps":["Time unit: Convert to 60 min.","log 2 not given: log 2 = log 10 − log 5."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"For the following graphs: (a) Rate vs Time – horizontal line; (b) t₁/₂ vs Initial concentration – straight line through origin rising; (c) Concentration vs Time – decaying curve (exponential); (d) Concentration vs Time – horizontal line; (e) Rate vs Concentration – straight line through origin rising. Choose from the options given below, the correct one regarding order of reaction is:","options":["(b) zero order (c) and (e) First order","(a) and (b) Zero order (e) First order","(b) and (d) Zero order (e) First order","(a) and (b) Zero order (c) and (e) First order"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) treats (d) flat concentration as zero order (confusing with flat rate); (A) and (B) are incomplete subsets.\nWorking: zero order – rate constant with time (a) and t₁/₂ ∝ [A]₀ (b); first order – exponential decay of concentration (c) and rate ∝ [A] (e); (d) constant concentration means no reaction, not zero order.\nKey idea: For zero order it is the RATE that is constant, not the concentration.","traps":["Flat concentration vs flat rate: Check axis labels: constant [R] means nothing is reacting.","Half-life dependence by order: Zero order t₁/₂ ∝ [A]₀; first order t₁/₂ constant."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"For a chemical reaction A → B, it was found that concentration of B is increased by 0.2 mol L⁻¹ in 30 min. The average rate of the reaction is ______ × 10⁻¹ mol L⁻¹ h⁻¹. (in nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.067 → '0' or 67 (using minutes), 1 (taking 0.2 × 0.5).\nWorking: rate = 0.2 mol L⁻¹ / 0.5 h = 0.4 mol L⁻¹ h⁻¹ = 4 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Convert 30 min to 0.5 h before dividing.","traps":["Time unit: Convert time first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"For a reaction of order n, the unit of the rate constant is:","options":["mol¹⁻ⁿ L¹⁻ⁿ s","mol¹⁻ⁿ L²ⁿ s⁻¹","mol¹⁻ⁿ Lⁿ⁻¹ s⁻¹","mol¹⁻ⁿ L¹⁻ⁿ s⁻¹"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) s instead of s⁻¹; (D) forgets that L sits in the denominator of concentration (L¹⁻ⁿ); (B) wrong exponent on L.\nWorking: k = rate/[A]ⁿ = (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹)(mol L⁻¹)⁻ⁿ = mol¹⁻ⁿ Lⁿ⁻¹ s⁻¹, option (C).\nKey idea: Expand (mol L⁻¹)¹⁻ⁿ: mol gets 1 − n, L gets −(1 − n) = n − 1.","traps":["L exponent sign when expanding: Check with n = 2: L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹.","Time unit inverse: k always has per time."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The decomposition of formic acid on gold surface follows first order kinetics. If the rate constant at 300 K is 1.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ and the activation energy Ea = 11.488 kJ mol⁻¹, the rate constant at 200 K is _______ × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). (Given: R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (answer given in ×10⁻⁴), 100 (k increased instead of decreased).\nWorking: ln(k₃₀₀/k₂₀₀) = (11488/8.314)(1/200 − 1/300) = 1381.8/600 = 2.303 → k₂₀₀ = k₃₀₀/10 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹.\nKey idea: Lower temperature → smaller k; ratio = e^(2.303) = 10.","traps":["Direction of change: Cooling lowers k.","Scaled answer format: 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 × 10⁻⁵."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A and B decompose via first order kinetics with half-lives 54.0 min and 18.0 min respectively. Starting from an equimolar non reactive mixture of A and B, the time taken for the concentration of A to become 16 times that of B is ______ min. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"108","range":[108.0,108.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 72 (4 half-lives of B), 216 (4 half-lives of A), 54 (ratio of half-lives misused), 36.\nWorking: [A]/[B] = 2^(t/t½B − t/t½A) = 2^(t(1/18 − 1/54)) = 2^(t/27); setting = 2⁴ gives t = 108 min.\nKey idea: Ratio grows as 2^(t/27) because 1/18 − 1/54 = 1/27.","traps":["Only B's decay considered: Both decay: use the difference of rate constants."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"For a certain first order reaction 32% of the reactant is left after 570 s. The rate constant of this reaction is _______ × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: log₁₀2 = 0.301, ln10 = 2.303]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (using 68% reacted as [R], log(100/68) ≈ 0.167 → 0.67), 0.87 (log₁₀ without 2.303).\nWorking: k = (2.303/570) log(100/32) = (2.303/570)(2 − 5 × 0.301) = (2.303 × 0.495)/570 = 2.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹.\nKey idea: 32 = 2⁵, so log(100/32) = 2 − 5 log 2.","traps":["Reacted fraction used as remaining: [R] = 32.","log vs ln confusion: Use 2.303 with log₁₀."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The reaction 2A + B₂ → 2AB is an elementary reaction.\nFor a certain quantity of reactants, if the volume of the reaction vessel is reduced by a factor of 3, the rate of the reaction increases by a factor of _____. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"27","range":[27.0,27.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 9 (forgetting B₂), 3 (taking order 1), 1/27 (thinking smaller volume lowers concentration).\nWorking: elementary → rate = k[A]²[B₂]; volume ÷3 multiplies each concentration by 3, so rate × 3² × 3 = 27.\nKey idea: Order = stoichiometric coefficients only because the reaction is elementary; total order 3 → factor 3³.","traps":["Overall order miscounted: Overall order = 2 + 1 = 3.","Volume vs concentration direction: Smaller volume → higher concentration."],"topics":["Rate law, rate constant & order","Molecularity"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"2NO(g) + Cl₂(g) ⇌ 2NOCl(s). This reaction was studied at −10 °C and the following data was obtained: run 1: [NO]₀ = 0.10, [Cl₂]₀ = 0.10, r₀ = 0.18; run 2: [NO]₀ = 0.10, [Cl₂]₀ = 0.20, r₀ = 0.35; run 3: [NO]₀ = 0.20, [Cl₂]₀ = 0.20, r₀ = 1.40. [NO]₀ and [Cl₂]₀ are the initial concentrations and r₀ is the initial reaction rate. The overall order of the reaction is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (reading order 1 for NO), 4, or 3 from stoichiometry by luck; misreading 1.40/0.18.\nWorking: runs 1→2 double [Cl₂], rate ×1.94 ≈ 2 → order 1 in Cl₂; runs 2→3 double [NO], rate ×4 → order 2 in NO; overall 3.\nKey idea: Compare runs that change only one concentration.","traps":["Order from stoichiometry: Always derive from data.","Comparing runs where both change: Pick pairs with one variable changed."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A reaction has a half life of 1 min. The time required for 99.9% completion of the reaction is ______ min. (Round off to the Nearest integer) [Use : ln 2 = 0.69, ln 10 = 2.3]","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (using log instead of ln mixing), 9.99 rounding, 3 (only log 1000), 6.9 (forgetting to divide by k).\nWorking: taking first order (constant half-life), t = (ln 1000)/k = 3 × 2.3/(0.69/1 min) = 10 min.\nKey idea: t₉₉.₉% = 10 t½ for first order.","traps":["Order not stated: Constant t½ ⇒ first order.","ln vs log mix-up: Use ln 1000 = 3 ln 10 = 6.9 with k = 0.69."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which one of the following given graphs represents the variation of rate constant (k) with temperature (T) for an endothermic reaction?","options":["k decreases with T along a falling concave-up curve","k increases linearly with T (straight line through the origin)","k increases exponentially with T (concave-up rising curve)","k increases with T but levels off (concave-down rising curve)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) decreasing k – thinks endothermic reactions slow down on heating; (B) linear rise – treats Arrhenius as linear in T; (D) saturation – confuses with the K vs T curve or a plot with an upper limit A shown over a wide range.\nWorking: by k = A e^(–Ea/RT), k rises exponentially with T over the practical range for any reaction with positive Ea; being endothermic does not change this, so curve (C).\nKey idea: k = A e^(–Ea/RT) rises exponentially with T over the usual range; ΔH of the reaction is irrelevant.","traps":["\"Endothermic\" taken to change the k–T trend: Rate constant rises with T for every reaction with Ea > 0, by k = A e^(–Ea/RT); ΔH does not matter.","Linear vs exponential rise: Arrhenius gives an exponential rise; only ln k vs 1/T is linear."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"During the nuclear explosion, one of the products is ⁹⁰Sr with half life of 6.93 years. if 1 μg of ⁹⁰Sr was absorbed in the bones of a newly born baby in place of Ca, how much time, in years, is required to reduce it by 90% if it is not lost metabolically ______ .","type":"num","answer":"23 to 23.03","range":[23.0,23.03],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1.05 y (uses 10% decay, log 10/9); 69.3 y; 6.93 × log 10 errors such as 6.93 y.\nWorking: k = 0.693/6.93 = 0.1 y⁻¹; 90% reduction leaves 10%: t = (2.303/0.1) log 10 = 23.03 y.\nKey idea: t₉₀% = 2.303/k = 3.32 half-lives.","traps":["'Reduce by 90%' read as 90% remaining: Reduced by 90% ⇒ [A]/[A]₀ = 0.1."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"For the reaction\n2H₂(g) + 2NO(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nthe observed rate expression is, rate = k_f[NO]²[H₂]. The rate expression of the reverse reaction is :","options":["k_b[N₂][H₂O]²/[NO]","k_b[N₂][H₂O]","k_b[N₂][H₂O]²","k_b[N₂][H₂O]²/[H₂]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) writes the reverse rate from the stoichiometry of products; (A) divides by the wrong species; (B) drops the square.\nWorking: at equilibrium forward rate = reverse rate and k_f/k_b = K = [N₂][H₂O]²/([NO]²[H₂]²); so the reverse rate law must satisfy k_f[NO]²[H₂] = k_b·X at equilibrium, giving X = [N₂][H₂O]²/[H₂].\nKey idea: Reverse rate law must make k_f/k_b equal the thermodynamic K.","traps":["Reverse rate from product stoichiometry: Impose k_f/k_b = K to find the leftover concentration term."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Catalyst effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The rate of a certain biochemical reaction at physiological temperature (T) occurs 10⁶ times faster with enzyme than without. The change in the activation energy upon adding enzyme is :","options":["–6RT","+6RT","+6(2.303)RT","–6(2.303)RT"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) uses log10 without 2.303; (B)/(C) wrong sign (catalyst lowers Ea).\nWorking: k′/k = 10⁶ = e^{(Ea−Ea′)/RT} → Ea′ − Ea = −RT ln10⁶ = −6(2.303)RT.\nKey idea: ΔEa = −RT ln(rate ratio) = −2.303RT log(ratio).","traps":["Dropping 2.303: ln 10⁶ = 6 × 2.303.","Sign of change: Catalyst lowers Ea → negative."],"topics":["Catalyst effect","Arrhenius equation"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Consider the following plots of rate constant versus 1/T for four different reactions. Which of the following orders is correct for the activation energies of these reactions?","options":["Eb > Ed > Ec > Ea","Ea > Ec > Ed > Eb","Ec > Ea > Ed > Eb","Eb > Ea > Ed > Ec"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) rank by the reverse of steepness (flattest = highest Ea); (B) ranks by height on the graph (a is highest line) rather than slope.\nWorking: log k = log A − Ea/(2.303RT), so slope = −Ea/2.303R; magnitude of slope read from the figure c > a > d > b, hence Ec > Ea > Ed > Eb. Position (height) of a line reflects A, not Ea.\nKey idea: Steeper descent of log k with 1/T means larger Ea; the intercept only reflects A.","traps":["Line height taken as Ea: Compare only slopes; height relates to A.","Flatter line read as larger Ea: Larger Ea = faster fall of k as T drops = steeper."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Jan08S2C03-p.webp"],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Activation energy"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Catalyst effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"For the following reactions: A —700 K→ Product; A —500 K, catalyst→ Product. It was found that Eₐ is decreased by 30 kJ/mol in the presence of catalyst. If the rate remains unchanged, the activation energy for the catalysed reaction is (assume pre-exponential factor is same):","options":["135 kJ/mol","105 kJ/mol","198 kJ/mol","75 kJ/mol"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 105 is the uncatalysed Eₐ (answers the wrong quantity); (A) 135 adds 30 instead of subtracting; (C) 198 comes from inverting the temperature ratio.\nWorking: equal k with equal A means Eₐ/700 = (Eₐ − 30)/500 → Eₐ = 105 kJ/mol uncatalysed, so catalysed Eₐ = 75 kJ/mol.\nKey idea: Same k and same A means Eₐ/T is the same for both runs.","traps":["Reporting uncatalysed Eₐ: Re-read which Eₐ is asked.","Temperatures paired wrongly: Catalysed reaction is at the lower temperature 500 K."],"topics":["Catalyst effect","Arrhenius equation"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A sample of milk splits after 60 min. at 300 K and after 40 min. at 400 K when the population of lactobacillus acidophilus in it doubles. The activation energy (in kJ/mol) for this process is closest to ____. (Given, R = 8.3 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, ln(2/3) = 0.4, e⁻³ = 4.0)","type":"num","answer":"3.98 to 4 or -4 to -3.98","ranges":[[3.98,4.0],[-4.0,-3.98]],"range":[3.98,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: −3.98 (using the given ln(2/3) = 0.4 at face value; this was also accepted), 0.4 × 8.3 × 1200 in J (3984), using e⁻³ value to get a spurious answer.\nWorking: rate ∝ 1/time, k₄₀₀/k₃₀₀ = 60/40 = 1.5; ln 1.5 = 0.4 = (Ea/8.3)(1/300 − 1/400) = Ea/(8.3 × 1200); Ea = 3984 J ≈ 3.98 kJ/mol. The negative range was also accepted because the given value ln(2/3) = 0.4 is a misprint (ln(3/2) ≈ 0.4).\nKey idea: k ∝ 1/t, so k₂/k₁ = t₁/t₂ = 1.5.","traps":["Time ratio inverted: Faster at higher T means shorter time: k₂/k₁ = 60/40.","Answer left in J/mol: Convert to kJ/mol as asked."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The results given in the below table were obtained during kinetic studies of the following reaction: 2A + B → C + D\nExperiment | [A]/mol L⁻¹ | [B]/mol L⁻¹ | Initial rate/mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹\nI | 0.1 | 0.1 | 6.00 × 10⁻³\nII | 0.1 | 0.2 | 2.40 × 10⁻²\nIII | 0.2 | 0.1 | 1.20 × 10⁻²\nIV | X | 0.2 | 7.20 × 10⁻²\nV | 0.3 | Y | 2.88 × 10⁻¹\nX and Y in the given table are respectively :","options":["0.3, 0.4","0.4, 0.3","0.4, 0.4","0.3, 0.3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps X and Y; (C) 0.4, 0.4 and (D) 0.3, 0.3 come from a wrong order (e.g. taking order from the stoichiometric coefficients 2A + B) for one of the reactants.\nWorking: I→II ([B] doubled, rate ×4) order in B = 2; I→III ([A] doubled, rate ×2) order in A = 1; k = 6 L² mol⁻² min⁻¹. IV: 7.2 × 10⁻² = 6·X·(0.2)² → X = 0.3. V: 0.288 = 6·0.3·Y² → Y = 0.4.\nKey idea: Get orders from pairs varying one reactant, then solve for unknowns.","traps":["Stoichiometric coefficient used as order: Orders come only from data.","X and Y swapped: Keep 'respectively' order."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molecularity","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"It is true that:","options":["A zero order reaction is a single step reaction","A second order reaction is always a multistep reaction","A first order reaction is always a single step reaction","A zero order reaction is a multistep reaction"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats zero order as simplest; (B) many second-order reactions are elementary; (C) first-order reactions can be complex (pseudo-first-order).\nWorking: for an elementary (single-step) reaction order = molecularity ≥ 1, so a zero-order reaction cannot be elementary; it must be complex (multistep, e.g. surface-catalysed).\nKey idea: Molecularity is never zero, so no elementary step can be zero order.","traps":["Zero order seen as simplest single step: Molecularity ≥ 1 for any step.","\"Always\" statements not tested: Find a counterexample for each \"always\"."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"For the reaction 2A + 3B + (3/2)C → 3P, which statement is correct ?","options":["dn_A/dt = dn_B/dt = dn_C/dt","dn_A/dt = (2/3) dn_B/dt = (3/4) dn_C/dt","dn_A/dt = (3/2) dn_B/dt = (3/4) dn_C/dt","dn_A/dt = (2/3) dn_B/dt = (4/3) dn_C/dt"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) uses 3/4 for C – inverts 4/3; (C) uses 3/2 for B – inverts 2/3; (A) ignores coefficients.\nWorking: rate = –(1/2)dn_A/dt = –(1/3)dn_B/dt = –(2/3)dn_C/dt, so dn_A/dt = (2/3)dn_B/dt = (4/3)dn_C/dt.\nKey idea: dn_A/dt : dn_B/dt : dn_C/dt = 2 : 3 : 3/2.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Write dn_A/dn_B = 2/3 directly from coefficients."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"If 75% of a first order reaction was completed in 90 minutes, 60% of the same reaction would be completed in approximately (in minutes) ________. (Take : log 2 = 0.30; log 2.5 = 0.40)","type":"num","answer":"60","range":[60.0,60.0],"explain":"Common slips: 72 (linear scaling 60/75 × 90, zero-order thinking); ≈33 (using ln(1/0.6), i.e. fraction reacted taken as fraction left); 45 (taking 60% as one half-life).\nWorking: t₇₅ = 2 t½ = 90 min → t½ = 45 min; k t = ln 2.5 → t = 45 × log 2.5/log 2 = 45 × 0.40/0.30 = 60 min.\nKey idea: Fraction remaining 0.4 → ln(1/0.4) = ln 2.5.","traps":["Linear scaling of time: First order: t ∝ log(1/fraction left).","Using fraction reacted in the log: [R]/[R]₀ = 0.4."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of molecules with energy greater than the threshold energy for a reaction increases five fold by a rise of temperature from 27 °C to 42 °C. Its energy of activation in J/mol is ______. (Take ln 5 = 1.6094; R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"84290 to 84300","range":[84290.0,84300.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using °C (27, 42) instead of K gives a huge error; using log₁₀ with ln 5 value; arithmetic slips in 300 × 315/15 = 6300.\nWorking: fraction of molecules above Ea ∝ e^(−Ea/RT), so ln 5 = (Ea/R)(1/300 − 1/315) = Ea/(R × 6300); Ea = 1.6094 × 8.314 × 6300 ≈ 84297.5 J/mol, within the accepted range.\nKey idea: Fraction above Ea ratio = k₂/k₁; use temperatures in kelvin.","traps":["Celsius temperatures used: Convert to 300 K and 315 K.","Mixing ln and log: Use natural log directly with given ln 5."],"topics":["Arrhenius equation","Collision theory"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"A flask contains a mixture of compounds A and B. Both compounds decompose by first-order kinetics. The half-lives for A and B are 300 s and 180 s, respectively. If the concentrations of A and B are equal initially, the time required for the concentration of A to be four times that of B (in s) : (Use ln 2 = 0.693)","options":["180","120","300","900"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) are just the given half-lives; (B) 120 is the difference of half-lives; 900 needs the exponent difference.\nWorking: [A]/[B] = e^{(k_B – k_A)t} = 4 → (ln2/180 – ln2/300)t = 2 ln2 → t(1/180 – 1/300) = 2 → t = 900 s.\nKey idea: Ratio grows as e^{Δk t}; ln 4 = 2 ln 2 makes ln 2 cancel.","traps":["Half-life numbers picked directly: Write the ratio expression before guessing.","Half-lives subtracted: k ∝ 1/t½; subtract rate constants, not half-lives."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The rate constant (k) of a reaction is measured at different temperatures (T), and the data are plotted in the given figure. The activation energy of the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹ is: (R is gas constant)","options":["2R","R","1/R","2/R"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) R – the 10³ factor of the axis and the J→kJ step are both dropped or mishandled; (C) 1/R and (D) 2/R – Ea divided by R instead of multiplied (slope = −Ea/R inverted).\nWorking: ln k = ln A − Ea/RT; slope against 10³/T is −10/5 = −2, so slope against 1/T = −2 × 10³ K; Ea = 2000R J mol⁻¹ = 2R kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Slope = −Ea/R; a 10³/T axis multiplies the slope by 10³, which exactly cancels the J→kJ conversion.","traps":["Slope relation inverted: Write slope = −Ea/R, then Ea = −slope × R.","10³ axis factor and kJ unit: Slope per (1/T) = −2000 K; Ea = 2000R J = 2R kJ."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep05S2C01-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Consider the following reactions :\nA → P1 ; B → P2 ; C → P3 ; D → P4\nThe order of the above reactions are a, b, c, and d, respectively. The following graph is obtained when log[rate] vs. log[conc] are plotted:\nAmong the following, the correct sequence for the order of the reactions is:","options":["a > b > c > d","c > a > b > d","d > b > a > c","d > a > b > c"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps A and B (lines labelled close together at the top); (A) alphabetical order; (B) reverses the reading.\nWorking: log rate = log k + n log[conc]; slope = order. From the graph the slopes are D > B > A > C, so d > b > a > c.\nKey idea: Slope of log rate vs log[conc] = order.","traps":["Line labels misread: Follow each line to compare steepness, not label height."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep06S1C19-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The rate of a reaction decreased by 3.555 times when the temperature was changed from 40°C to 30°C. The activation energy (in kJ mol⁻¹) of the reaction is ______. Take; R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, ln 3.555 = 1.268","type":"num","answer":"99.9 to 100.1","range":[99.9,100.1],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 43.4 (ln value divided by 2.303 – log bases mixed); 230 (ln value multiplied by 2.303); about 1.27 (°C used: 1.268 × 8.314 / (1/30 − 1/40) ≈ 1265 J); 99980 (left in J).\nWorking: ln(k₃₁₃/k₃₀₃) = 1.268 = (Ea/8.314)(1/303 − 1/313) = (Ea/8.314)(1.0544 × 10⁻⁴) → Ea ≈ 99 980 J ≈ 99.98 kJ mol⁻¹, inside the accepted range.\nKey idea: Use kelvin; with ln given, no 2.303 factor.","traps":["Log base mixed: With ln, use Ea/R directly.","Celsius temperatures used: Convert to 303 K and 313 K."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"For the reaction 2A + B → C, the values of initial rate at different reactant concentrations are given in the table below. The rate law for the reaction is : [A] (mol L⁻¹): 0.05, 0.10, 0.20; [B] (mol L⁻¹): 0.05, 0.05, 0.10; Initial rate (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹): 0.045, 0.090, 0.72","options":["Rate = k[A][B]","Rate = k[A]²[B]²","Rate = k[A][B]²","Rate = k[A]²[B]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) reads order from stoichiometry 2A + B; (B) assumes both second order; (A) ignores ×8.\nWorking: runs 1→2: [A] ×2, rate ×2 ⇒ order 1 in A; runs 2→3: [A] ×2 and [B] ×2, rate ×8 ⇒ 2 × 2ʸ = 8, y = 2.\nKey idea: Fix A's order first, then extract B.","traps":["Order from stoichiometric coefficients: Order is experimental only."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"For a reaction scheme A —k₁→ B —k₂→ C, if the rate of formation of B is set to be zero then the concentration of B is given by:","options":["(k₁/k₂)[A]","(k₁ + k₂)[A]","k₁k₂[A]","(k₁ − k₂)[A]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) add or subtract rate constants; (C) multiplies them – none come from a rate balance.\nWorking: d[B]/dt = k₁[A] − k₂[B] = 0 ⇒ [B] = (k₁/k₂)[A].\nKey idea: Set formation = consumption: k₁[A] = k₂[B].","traps":["Net rate written wrongly: Write d[B]/dt term by term."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The given plots represent the variation of the concentration of a reactant R with time for two different reactions (i) and (ii). The respective orders of the reactions are : (i) ln[R] vs time – straight line with negative slope; (ii) [R] vs time – straight line with negative slope.","options":["1, 0","1, 1","0, 1","0, 2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps the two; (B) assumes both straight lines mean same order; (D) invents second order.\nWorking: linear ln[R]–t ⇒ first order; linear [R]–t ⇒ zero order.\nKey idea: Look at the y-axis: ln[R] linear ⇒ 1st order, [R] linear ⇒ 0 order.","traps":["Both linear plots assumed same order: Read the axis label.","Orders swapped: Linear ln[R] is first order."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Activation energy","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Consider the given plot of enthalpy of the following reaction between A and B. A + B → C + D. Identify the incorrect statement. [Plot: enthalpy (kJ mol⁻¹) vs reaction coordinate, gridlines at 5, 10, 15, 20. A + B sits at 5; to its left a barrier peaking at 15 leads down to D at about 10–12; to its right a barrier peaking at 20 leads down to C, below 5 (≈0). The curve on the far left of D rises to 20.]","options":["C is the thermodynamically stable product.","Formation of A and B from C has highest enthalpy of activation.","D is kinetically stable product.","Activation enthalpy to form C is 5 kJ mol⁻¹ less than that to form D."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) true (C lowest); (B) true (~20 kJ mol⁻¹ from C, largest); (C) true (lower barrier to D) — students who misread barrier heights or the sign of the 5 kJ difference pick these.\nWorking: Ea(A+B→D) = 15 – 5 = 10 kJ mol⁻¹, Ea(A+B→C) = 20 – 5 = 15 kJ mol⁻¹, so forming C needs 5 kJ mol⁻¹ MORE, making (D) incorrect; C is lowest (thermodynamic product), D forms faster (kinetic product), C→A+B has the largest barrier (~20).\nKey idea: Measure each Ea from the A+B level, not from zero.","traps":["Ea read from axis zero: Ea = peak – starting level.","Direction of the 5 kJ difference: Compare 10 vs 15 explicitly."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"A bacterial infection in an internal wound grows as N′(t) = N₀ exp(t), where the time t is in hours. A dose of antibiotic, taken orally, needs 1 hour to reach the wound. Once it reaches there, the bacterial population goes down as dN/dt = –5N². What will be the plot of N₀/N vs. t after 1 hour? [Four sketches of N₀/N (y-axis) vs t(h) (x-axis)]","options":["Straight line rising from a positive intercept (N₀/N increases linearly with t)","Straight line falling with t","Nearly flat (slightly rising) segment followed by a steep straight rise after a break","Curve that falls to a minimum and then rises (U-shaped)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats the decline in N as a decline in N₀/N (inverse quantity missed); (C) tries to draw the 0–1 h growth phase, but the question asks only after 1 h; (D) mixes the exponential growth and decay phases into one curve.\nWorking: for t ≥ 1 h, ∫dN/N² = –5∫dt gives 1/N = 1/N(1) + 5(t – 1), so N₀/N = 1/e + 5N₀(t – 1) – a straight line with positive slope.\nKey idea: Second-order decay ⇒ 1/N linear in t ⇒ N₀/N rises linearly.","traps":["Plotting N instead of N₀/N: Note the y-axis is the reciprocal of N.","Including the growth phase before 1 h: Read \"after 1 hour\" – only the decay law applies."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"For the reaction of H₂ with I₂, the rate constant is 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 327 °C and 1.0 dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ at 527 °C. The activation energy for the reaction, in kJ mol⁻¹ is: (R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":["72","166","150","59"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 59 ≈ using 2.303 wrongly or °C temperatures; (A) 72 from log₁₀ without 2.303 (8.29/2.303 = 3.6 → 72); (C) 150 from rounding 1/T terms.\nWorking: ln(1/2.5×10⁻⁴) = ln 4000 = 8.29; (1/600 – 1/800) = 1/2400 K⁻¹; Ea = 8.29 × 8.314 × 2400 ≈ 165.5 kJ mol⁻¹ ≈ 166.\nKey idea: Convert to 600 K and 800 K; 1/600 – 1/800 = 1/2400.","traps":["Forgetting 2.303 with log₁₀: Use ln 4000 = 8.29 or 2.303 × 3.60.","Temperatures left in °C: Add 273 before taking reciprocals."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"In the following reaction; xA → yB, log₁₀[−d[A]/dt] = log₁₀[d[B]/dt] + 0.3010. 'A' and 'B' respectively can be :","options":["n-Butane and Iso-butane","C₂H₄ and C₄H₈","N₂O₄ and NO₂","C₂H₂ and C₆H₆"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 1:2 ratio (inverting the relation); (D) 3:1; (A) 1:1 isomerisation.\nWorking: −d[A]/dt = 2 d[B]/dt ⇒ x/y = 2, i.e. 2A → B; 2C₂H₄ → C₄H₈ fits. (A) is 1:1, (C) 1:2, (D) 3:1.\nKey idea: 0.3010 = log 2 ⇒ A disappears twice as fast as B forms ⇒ 2A → B.","traps":["Ratio inverted: If A is consumed faster, A has the bigger coefficient.","Not recognising log 2: Memorise log 2 = 0.3010."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"NO₂ required for a reaction is produced by the decomposition of N₂O₅ in CCl₄ as per the equation 2N₂O₅(g) → 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g). The initial concentration of N₂O₅ is 3.00 mol L⁻¹ and it is 2.75 mol L⁻¹ after 30 minutes. The rate of formation of NO₂ is :","options":["2.083 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹","4.167 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹","8.333 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹","1.667 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) rate of N₂O₅ loss only; (B) divided by 2; (A) divided by 4 (rate of reaction per O₂).\nWorking: −Δ[N₂O₅]/Δt = 0.25/30 = 8.333 × 10⁻³; d[NO₂]/dt = 2 × that = 1.667 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹.\nKey idea: d[NO₂]/dt = 2(−d[N₂O₅]/dt).","traps":["Rate of N₂O₅ disappearance reported: Multiply by 4/2 for NO₂.","Ratio inverted: Product with larger coefficient forms faster."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The following results were obtained during kinetic studies of the reaction : 2A + B → Products\nExperiment | [A] (mol L⁻¹) | [B] (mol L⁻¹) | Initial rate (mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹)\n(I) | 0.10 | 0.20 | 6.93 × 10⁻³\n(II) | 0.10 | 0.25 | 6.93 × 10⁻³\n(III) | 0.20 | 0.30 | 1.386 × 10⁻²\nThe time (in minutes) required to consume half of A is :","options":["10","5","100","1"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): 10 min if the factor 2 is ignored. (C)/(D): order or k wrong by a power of ten.\nWorking: I vs II → zero order in B; I vs III → rate doubles when [A] doubles → first order in A. k = 6.93×10⁻³/0.10 = 6.93×10⁻² min⁻¹ for the reaction rate; A is consumed at 2k, so t½(A) = 0.693/(2 × 0.0693) = 5 min.\nKey idea: Rate of disappearance of A = 2 × rate of reaction.","traps":["Rate of reaction used as rate of A: −d[A]/dt = 2 × rate, so k_A = 2k.","Order w.r.t. B misread: Compare I and II first: [B] changes, rate does not → zero order in B."],"topics":["Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rate law, rate constant & order","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"For the reaction, 2A + B → products, when the concentrations of A and B both were doubled, the rate of the reaction increased from 0.3 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ to 2.4 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. When the concentration of A alone is doubled, the rate increased from 0.3 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ to 0.6 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. Which one of the following statements is correct?","options":["Order of the reaction with respect to B is 2","Order of the reaction with respect to A is 2","Total order of the reaction is 4","Order of the reaction with respect to B is 1"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Taking order from stoichiometric coefficient 2 of A. (C) Adding 8-fold as order 4 or summing coefficients wrongly. (D) Assuming B order 1 from coefficient.\nWorking: 2ˣ⁺ʸ = 8 → x + y = 3; 2ˣ = 2 → x = 1; so y = 2.\nKey idea: Factor 8 on doubling both → total order 3; A alone ×2 → order 1 in A.","traps":["Order read from stoichiometry: Order comes from experiment, not coefficients."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Consider the given plots for a reaction obeying Arrhenius equation (0°C < T < 300°C) : (k and Ea are rate constant and activation energy, respectively). Plot I: k (y-axis) vs Ea (x-axis), a curve falling steeply then levelling towards zero. Plot II: k (y-axis) vs T(°C) (x-axis), starting at a positive value at 0 °C, almost flat over the low-temperature part, then rising steeply. Choose the correct option :","options":["Both I and II are wrong","I is wrong but II is right","Both I and II are correct","I is right but II is wrong"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): accepts II because it eventually rises. (B): thinks k vs Ea should increase. (A): rejects I misreading the exponential decay.\nWorking: k = Ae^(−Ea/RT) falls exponentially with Ea, so plot I is right. dk/dT = k·Ea/RT² > 0, so k must rise throughout; plot II is drawn nearly flat (zero slope) at low T before a sharp rise, which is taken to be wrong. Note: plot II has no visible minimum; the flaw is only its flat initial part, so (C) is a conceivable objection, but (D) is the intended answer.\nKey idea: dk/dT = k·Ea/RT² > 0 always, so k vs T can never decrease.","traps":["Overall rise accepted, flat start ignored: Check the slope over the whole range: dk/dT = k·Ea/RT² is never zero, so k cannot stay constant at low T."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rate of reaction","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"For an elementary chemical reaction, A₂ ⇌ 2A (forward k₁, backward k₋₁), the expression for d[A]/dt is :","options":["2k₁[A₂] − k₋₁[A]²","k₁[A₂] − k₋₁[A]²","2k₁[A₂] − 2k₋₁[A]²","k₁[A₂] + k₋₁[A]²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) applies factor 2 only to the forward term. (B) forgets the factor 2. (D) wrong sign for reverse reaction.\nWorking: rate of reaction r = k₁[A₂] − k₋₁[A]² = (1/2) d[A]/dt, so d[A]/dt = 2k₁[A₂] − 2k₋₁[A]².\nKey idea: Two A are formed per forward event and two consumed per reverse event.","traps":["Factor 2 on reverse term: Each reverse event removes 2 A."],"topics":["Rate of reaction","Rate law, rate constant & order"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"If a reaction follows the Arrhenius equation, the plot ln k vs 1/(RT) gives a straight line with a gradient (−y) unit. The energy required to activate the reactant is:","options":["y unit","−y unit","yR unit","y/R unit"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) yR – the usual 1/T plot answer (slope −Ea/R). (D) y/R – wrong algebra. (B) −y – keeps the negative sign; Ea must be positive.\nWorking: ln k = ln A − Ea·(1/RT); slope against 1/(RT) is −Ea = −y, so Ea = y.\nKey idea: With 1/(RT) on the x-axis, slope = −Ea directly.","traps":["Reflex multiply by R: Check the x-variable before converting slope.","Keeping the minus sign: Activation energy is positive; slope = −Ea."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The reaction 2X → B is a zeroth order reaction. If the initial concentration of X is 0.2 M, the half-life is 6 h. When the initial concentration of X is 0.5 M, the time required to reach its final concentration of 0.2 M will be :-","options":["18.0 h","7.2 h","9.0 h","12.0 h"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 7.2 h: scaling slip 6 × (0.3/0.25); first-order treatment gives ≈ 7.9 h, close to it. (C) 9 h: scales the half-life by 0.3/0.2. (D) 12 h: thinks t½ is constant and counts two half-lives.\nWorking: zero order – [X] falls linearly. 0.1 M consumed in 6 h → rate of fall 1/60 M h⁻¹. From 0.5 to 0.2 M is 0.3 M → 18 h. (The factor 2 in 2X cancels as long as the same k definition is used.)\nKey idea: Zero order: equal concentration drops take equal times.","traps":["Half-life treated as constant: Zero order t½ ∝ [R]₀; use k instead.","Coefficient 2 doubles the rate: Keep the same rate definition in both parts; the factor cancels."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Orders of reactions (zero, first, half-life, pseudo-first)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Decomposition of X exhibits a rate constant of 0.05 µg/year. How many years are required for the decomposition of 5 µg of X into 2.5 µg ?","options":["50","25","20","40"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(B): using first-order half-life 0.693/k-type reasoning or k×t slips. (C) 20: 1/k.\nWorking: units µg/year ⇒ zero order; t = ([R]₀ − [R])/k = 2.5/0.05 = 50 years.\nKey idea: k in amount/time means zero order: t = Δ[R]/k.","traps":["Assuming first order: Check units of k first."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Arrhenius equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For a reaction, consider the plot of ln k versus 1/T given in the figure (straight line, slope = −4606 K). If the rate constant of this reaction at 400 K is 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹, then the rate constant at 500 K is :","options":["2 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹","10⁻⁴ s⁻¹","10⁻⁶ s⁻¹","4 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 10⁻⁶: rate taken to fall with temperature (sign slip). (A)/(D): mixing ln and log (4606/2.303 = 2000) or temperature-ratio errors.\nWorking: ln(k₂/k₁) = 4606 (1/400 − 1/500) = 4606/2000 = 2.303 → k₂/k₁ = 10 → k₂ = 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹.\nKey idea: Slope × (1/T₁ − 1/T₂) = 2.303 = ln 10.","traps":["ln vs log confusion: Slope of ln k is −Ea/R; no 2.303 needed.","Sign of slope misused: k always increases with T for positive Ea."]}],"m14-goc":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Free radicals","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"Consider the following compound (X):\nH−C≡C−CH₂−CH(CH₃)−CH₃\nwith carbons labelled I (the ≡C−H carbon), II (CH₂), III (CH) and IV (terminal CH₃).\nThe most stable and least stable carbon radicals, respectively, produced by homolytic cleavage of corresponding C−H bond are :","options":["II, I","III, II","II, IV","I, IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) III, II: tertiary taken as most stable, resonance at II ignored, and II wrongly called least. (C) II, IV: correct most stable but primary IV chosen as least, ignoring sp radical. (D) I, IV: treats alkynyl radical as stabilised (s-character misread as stabilising). (A) II, I correct.\nWorking: breaking the C–H at II gives a propargylic radical, delocalised onto the C≡C – most stable (more than the 3° radical at III or the 1° radical at IV). Breaking the C–H at I gives an alkynyl radical on an sp carbon; the odd electron is in an sp orbital, not delocalised – least stable. So: II (most), I (least).\nKey idea: Resonance (propargylic) beats degree of substitution, and an sp-carbon radical is the least stable of all.","traps":["Tertiary centre offered against resonance-stabilised site: Propargylic/allylic radicals are delocalised over the π system and beat a simple tertiary alkyl radical.","Allylic or propargylic resonance stabilisation of intermediate: A radical on the carbon α to a π bond is conjugated with it (propargyl radical)."]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"The least acidic compound, among the following (A–D, shown below) is:","options":["B","D","C","A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B: benzenesulfonic acid is the strongest acid here (pKa < 0); chosen by misreading 'least'. (C) C: butanoic acid (pKa 4.8) chosen if aliphatic acids are thought weaker than phenols. (D) A: phenol with alkoxy group, chosen by donor-group reasoning. (B) D correct: terminal alkyne C-H.\nWorking: benzenesulfonic acid (pKa < 0) > butanoic acid (pKa ≈ 4.8) > 3-ethoxyphenol (pKa ≈ 10) ≫ ethyl propiolate, whose only acidic H is the sp C–H (pKa ≈ 20–25). The ester group makes this C–H more acidic than in a simple alkyne, but it is still far weaker than any O–H acid, so D is least acidic.\nKey idea: O-H and S-O-H acids all beat a C-H acid; the alkyne is least acidic despite its ester group.","traps":["Electron-withdrawing group overrates weak C-H acid: Even activated sp C-H acids (pKa ~ 20-25) are far weaker than phenols (pKa ~ 10); an EWG cannot bridge a 10+ unit gap.","Electron-donating substituent on phenol ranked as weakest acid: At meta position OEt acts mainly by -I; in any case a phenol is still much more acidic than an alkyne C-H."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S1C17-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr03S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"What is the correct IUPAC name of the compound shown below?","options":["2-Hydroxy-3-bromo-5-nitrobenzoic acid","5-Nitro-3-bromo-2-hydroxybenzoic acid","3-Bromo-4-hydroxy-1-nitrobenzoic acid","3-Bromo-2-hydroxy-5-nitrobenzoic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) right locants, prefixes in locant order not alphabetical. (B) nitro listed first, non-alphabetical. (C) nitro given locant 1, impossible for benzoic acid. (D) correct.\nWorking: the COOH carbon is C-1; numbering towards OH gives OH-2, Br-3, NO₂-5, i.e. {2,3,5}, lower than {3,5,6} the other way. Prefixes in alphabetical order (bromo, hydroxy, nitro): 3-bromo-2-hydroxy-5-nitrobenzoic acid.\nKey idea: Fix C-1 at COOH, minimise locants, then write prefixes alphabetically.","traps":["Substituent prefixes not in alphabetical order: Prefixes are arranged alphabetically (bromo < hydroxy < nitro), irrespective of locant numbers.","Principal group must take locant 1: In a benzoic acid the ring carbon bearing COOH is C-1 by definition."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S2C16-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":63,"q":"In which pairs, the first ion is more stable than the second?\n(Pairs (A)–(D) are shown below as first ion, second ion.)","options":["(A) & (C) only","(B) & (C) only","(A) & (B) only","(B) & (D) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A true (OMe +R). B true (nitroaryl stabilises carbanion, destabilises carbocation). C false (second, allylic, is more stable). D false (OMe-substituted cation more stable). (A) includes C; (B) includes C, drops A; (D) includes D. (C) correct.\nWorking: (A) OMe on C⁺ donates its lone pair (+R, oxocarbenium ion) – more stable than with CH₃ (hyperconjugation only): true. (B) the o-nitrophenyl group withdraws electrons, stabilising the carbanion and destabilising the carbocation: true. (C) the second cation is allylic (conjugated with the ring C=C), so it is the more stable one: false. (D) (CH₃)₂C⁺–OMe is stabilised by O lone-pair donation, more than (CH₃)₃C⁺: false. Hence (A) & (B) only.\nKey idea: Ask which way charge is delocalised: donors help cations, acceptors help anions, and conjugation beats alkyl substitution.","traps":["Resonance (+R) donor outweighs alkyl hyperconjugation in cation: Oxygen lone pair donation gives an oxocarbenium ion with full octets; +R dominates -I.","EWG stabilises carbanion but destabilises carbocation: -R nitro group delocalises negative charge (stabilises C⁻) and intensifies positive charge (destabilises C⁺)."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C13-s.webp"],"topics":["Carbocations","Carbanions"]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"The IUPAC name of the following compound is:\nHC≡C−CH₂−CH(OH)−CH₂−CH=CH₂","options":["Hept-1-en-6-yn-4-ol","Hept-6-en-1-yn-4-ol","4-Hydroxyhept-1-en-6-yne","4-Hydroxyhept-6-en-1-yne"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) alkyne given lower locant, tie-break wrong. (C) correct numbering but OH as prefix. (D) OH as prefix and alkyne-first numbering. (A) correct.\nWorking: the 7-carbon chain contains both multiple bonds; –OH is the principal group (suffix -ol) and gets locant 4 from either end, so the tie is broken in favour of the double bond: C1=C2 and C6≡C7 → hept-1-en-6-yn-4-ol.\nKey idea: Suffix -ol fixes the chain and locant 4; the tie goes to the double bond.","traps":["Ene gets lower locant only on a tie: When locants tie, the double bond gets the lower number.","Principal group cited as prefix instead of suffix: -OH outranks C=C and C≡C, so it must be the suffix '-ol'."]},{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"The correct order of basicity for the following molecules (P, Q, R shown below) is:","options":["P > Q > R","Q > P > R","R > Q > P","R > P > Q"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) P > Q > R: treats the bicyclic lactam as least basic, ignoring loss of amide resonance. (B) Q > P > R: same error for R. (D) R > P > Q: right about R, wrong about cross-conjugation in Q. (C) R > Q > P correct.\nWorking: in R the N is at the bridgehead of a small bicyclic ring, so it cannot become planar and its lone pair cannot overlap with the C=O π bond – it behaves like an amine and is the most basic. In Q the C=C conjugated with C=O donates electron density into the carbonyl, so the C=O draws less on the N lone pair than in P. P has full amide resonance and is least basic. Order: R > Q > P.\nKey idea: The less the N lone pair is delocalised into C=O, the more basic the amide nitrogen.","traps":["Bridgehead nitrogen cannot delocalise lone pair: Amide resonance needs N p-orbital parallel to the C=O π bond; a bridgehead N in a small bicycle is pyramidal, so its lone pair stays localised and basic.","Cross-conjugated carbonyl draws less on nitrogen lone pair: C=C conjugated with C=O donates into the carbonyl, reducing its demand for the N lone pair, so Q's N is more basic than P's."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C19-s.webp"],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"Which of the following is the correct IUPAC name of given organic compound (X) (shown below)?","options":["4-Bromo-3-methylbut-2-ene","1-Bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene","2-Bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene","3-Bromo-3-methylprop-2-ene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbered from the wrong end. (C) Br placed on the sp² C-2, impossible structure (pentavalent carbon). (D) wrong parent chain (prop) and impossible locant set. (B) correct.\nWorking: the longest chain through the C=C has 4 carbons and includes the CH₂Br carbon. The double bond gets locant 2 from either end, so number to give the substituents the lowest set: Br on C-1, CH₃ on C-2 ({1,2}, versus {3,4} the other way) → 1-bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene.\nKey idea: Include CH₂Br in the four-carbon chain; on a tie for C=C, the lower substituent set {1,2} wins.","traps":["Numbering from wrong end of chain: With the double bond tied, choose the direction giving lower locants to substituents: 1-bromo-2-methyl.","Main chain chosen without the substituted carbon: Br is a prefix only; the CH₂ carbon belongs to the longest chain (4 C) through the C=C."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C15-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"Which of the following compounds is least likely to give effervescence of CO₂ in presence of aq. NaHCO₃ ?","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 4-nitrobenzoic acid: a carboxylic acid, clearly reacts. (B) anilinium chloride: pKa 4.6, reacts; trap for students who see it as a base salt. (D) picric acid: pKa 0.4, reacts despite being a phenol. (C) 3-nitrophenol correct (pKa 8.4).\nWorking: only acids stronger than H₂CO₃ (pKa ≈ 6.4) liberate CO₂ from NaHCO₃. 4-Nitrobenzoic acid (pKa ≈ 3.4), the anilinium ion (pKa ≈ 4.6) and picric acid (pKa ≈ 0.4) are all stronger; 3-nitrophenol (pKa ≈ 8.4) is weaker, so it gives no effervescence.\nKey idea: Effervescence needs pKa below about 6.4, regardless of whether the acid is a phenol, a carboxylic acid or an ammonium ion.","traps":["Acid strength threshold for NaHCO₃ versus NaOH: Compare pKa to 6.4: 3-nitrophenol (8.4) is too weak; a single meta nitro acts only by -I.","Anilinium salt not recognised as an acid: PhNH₃⁺ has pKa about 4.6, stronger than carbonic acid, so it liberates CO₂ from bicarbonate."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C18-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":63,"q":"What is the correct IUPAC name of the compound shown below?","options":["1-Ethyl-3-hydroxycyclopent-2-ene","4-Ethyl-1-hydroxycyclopent-2-ene","4-Ethylcyclopent-2-en-1-ol","1-Ethylcyclopent-2-en-3-ol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) OH as prefix, ethyl given C-1. (B) OH as prefix with correct relative numbering. (D) -ol suffix but OH given locant 3. (C) correct.\nWorking: –OH is the principal group, so its carbon is C-1; numbering towards the double bond gives C2=C3, which puts the ethyl group on C-4: 4-ethylcyclopent-2-en-1-ol.\nKey idea: The suffix group takes C-1 in a ring, then the double bond, then substituents.","traps":["Principal group cited as prefix instead of suffix: -OH outranks C=C, so the name ends in -ol.","Principal group lowest locant, not alphabetical first: The carbon bearing the principal group is C-1; then the double bond gets the lowest locants (2,3)."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr08S2C13-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"The most stable carbocation from the following is :","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) unsubstituted benzyl: baseline, no extra donor. (C) p-methyl: weaker hyperconjugation. (D) m-methoxy: only -I, less stable than benzyl. (B) correct.\nWorking: p-OMe donates its lone pair (+R) directly to the benzylic carbon (oxocarbenium-type resonance form), the strongest stabilisation; p-CH₃ helps only by hyperconjugation/+I; m-OMe cannot donate by resonance to CH₂⁺ and acts by –I, so it is even less stable than benzyl. Order: p-OMe > p-CH₃ > benzyl > m-OMe.\nKey idea: An o/p lone-pair donor delocalises directly into the benzylic C⁺; meta cannot.","traps":["Meta substituent cannot donate by resonance: Resonance from meta does not reach the benzylic carbon; only -I acts, which destabilises.","Resonance (+R) donor outweighs alkyl hyperconjugation in cation: Lone-pair +R from O at para is stronger than CH₃ hyperconjugation."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan22S2C15-o.webp"],"lettered":true,"topics":["Carbocations","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"The correct order of stability of following carbocations (A–D, shown below) is :","options":["C > A > B > D","A > B > C > D","C > B > A > D","B > C > A > D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) trityl over tropylium (aromaticity ignored). (C) Ph₂CH⁺ above Ph₃C⁺ (steric twist overrated). (D) Ph₂CH⁺ first, inconsistent ranking. (A) correct.\nWorking: the tropylium ion (C) is aromatic (planar, cyclic, 6 π electrons) and is the most stable; Ph₃C⁺ (A) delocalises the charge into three rings and Ph₂CH⁺ (B) into two; the sec-butyl cation (D) has only hyperconjugation and +I. Order: C > A > B > D.\nKey idea: Aromaticity beats resonance count: tropylium is the most stable carbocation here.","traps":["Aromatic tropylium cation outranks triarylmethyl: Tropylium is aromatic (planar, cyclic, 6 π electrons); aromatic stabilisation beats resonance counting.","More aryl groups give more resonance stabilisation: Each extra phenyl adds delocalisation: Ph₃C⁺ > Ph₂CH⁺."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S1C14-s.webp"],"topics":["Carbocations","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electromeric effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I (Mechanism steps):\nA. Aniline: lone pair on N shown by curved arrows moving into the ring ⇌ resonance form with ⁺NH₂= on ring and a negative charge (carbanion) at the ortho position.\nB. Cyclohexene (π bond arrow towards attacking H⁺) + H⁺ → cyclohexyl cation with H added to one carbon and + on the adjacent carbon.\nC. Cyclohexene (π bond arrow) + ⁻CN → carbanion with CN on one carbon and − on the adjacent carbon.\nD. Nitrobenzene (O←N=O): ring π electrons shifted towards N=O, with O becoming O⁻ ⇌ resonance form with ring =N(O⁻)→O and + charge on the ring.\nList II (Effect):\nI. −E effect\nII. −R effect\nIII. +E effect\nIV. +R effect\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): swap of E and R labels. (B): +E/−E reversed (thinking H⁺ addition pulls electrons away).\nWorking: A NH₂ donates lone pair into ring → +R; B π electrons move to the carbon that receives H⁺ → +E; C π electrons move away from the carbon attacked by CN⁻ → −E; D nitro withdraws ring π density → −R.\nKey idea: +E: electrons move to the atom where the reagent attaches (electrophile); −E: electrons move away (nucleophile attacks).","traps":["+E vs −E reversed: Ask: does the reagent bond to the carbon receiving the π pair? Yes → +E.","Resonance step mistaken for electromeric: E effect needs an attacking reagent; R effect occurs in the isolated molecule."],"topics":["Electromeric effect","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electromeric effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which among the following is incorrect statement?","options":["The electromeric effect is a temporary effect","Hydrogen ion (H⁺) shows negative electromeric effect","The organic compound shows electromeric effect in the presence of the reagent only.","Electromeric effect dominates over inductive effect"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C),(D) are verbatim NCERT truths that look suspicious (e.g. 'dominates').\nWorking: with H⁺ the π electrons move to the carbon the reagent attaches to – a +E effect, so (B) is false. A, C, D are NCERT statements.\nKey idea: Electrophile (H⁺) → +E; nucleophile (CN⁻) → −E.","traps":["H⁺ example sign flipped: +E when the reagent attaches where electrons move.","'Dominates' sounds overstated: NCERT does say electromeric predominates over inductive when opposed."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carbanions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Correct order of stability of carbanions (a–d, shown below) is:","options":["d > c > b > a","c > b > d > a","d > a > c > b","a > b > c > d"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) puts a second – treating the cyclopropenyl anion as stabilised by the adjacent C=C (allylic) and missing antiaromaticity. (B) puts the saturated anions above d, ignoring aromatic stabilisation. (D) is the reverse (small-ring s-character argument).\nWorking: d has 6 π electrons (aromatic) – most stable; a has 4 π electrons in a planar ring (antiaromatic) – least stable; b and c are ordinary localised carbanions placed c > b (only option with d first and a last).\nKey idea: Cyclopentadienyl anion is aromatic (6π); cyclopropenyl anion is antiaromatic (4π) and least stable.","traps":["Cyclopropenyl anion seen as allylic-stabilised: Count π electrons: 4π planar cyclic → antiaromatic.","Aromatic anion not recognised: Lone pair in p orbital joins ring π system (6π)."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr04S2C05-s.webp"],"topics":["Carbanions","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Common name of Benzene-1,2-diol is -","options":["catechol","quinol","o-cresol","resorcinol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) resorcinol is the 1,3 isomer; (B) quinol the 1,4; (C) o-cresol is a methylphenol, not a diol.\nWorking: benzene-1,2-diol = catechol; 1,3 = resorcinol; 1,4 = hydroquinone/quinol; o-cresol is 2-methylphenol.\nKey idea: Catechol 1,2 – Resorcinol 1,3 – Quinol 1,4.","traps":["Dihydroxy isomers mixed: Order: C-R-Q for 1,2 / 1,3 / 1,4."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tetravalency & shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The total number of 'sigma' and 'pi' bonds in 2-oxohex-4-ynoic acid is _____.","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: 17 (missing the O–H σ bond); 14 or 4 (giving only one type); 20 (counting triple bond as 3 π or double bonds as 2 π each).\nWorking: structure HOOC–CO–CH₂–C≡C–CH₃ (C₆H₆O₃). σ bonds = atoms − 1 = 15 − 1 = 14 (acyclic); π bonds = 2 (two C=O) + 2 (C≡C) = 4; total 18.\nKey idea: For an acyclic molecule σ = (number of atoms − 1); π from each double (1) and triple (2) bond.","traps":["Missed O–H or C–H bonds: Use σ = atoms − 1 for acyclic.","Name mis-parsed: C1 is COOH; oxo at C2; yne at C4–C5."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural formulae","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Vanillin compound obtained from vanilla beans, has total sum of oxygen atoms and π electrons is ________.","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 (counting π bonds, 4, instead of π electrons); 13–15 (counting lone-pair electrons of O as π); 10 (missing methoxy O).\nWorking: vanillin = 4-hydroxy-3-methoxybenzaldehyde, C₈H₈O₃: 3 O atoms; π electrons = 6 (ring) + 2 (C=O) = 8; total 11.\nKey idea: π electrons = 2 × π bonds: ring 6 + CHO 2.","traps":["π bonds vs π electrons: Each π bond = 2 electrons.","Vanillin structure misremembered: Three O: CHO, OH, OCH₃."],"topics":["Structural formulae","Functional groups"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct nomenclature for the following compound is:\n[Structure: CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₂–CH(CHO)–COOH]","options":["2-carboxy-4-hydroxyhept-7-enal","2-formyl-4-hydroxyhept-7-enoic acid","2-carboxy-4-hydroxyhept-6-enal","2-formyl-4-hydroxyhept-6-enoic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) make CHO the principal group with 'carboxy' prefix. (B) numbers the alkene as 7-ene (end carbon) instead of the lower locant 6.\nWorking: –COOH is the principal group (C1); the 7-carbon chain includes it; CHO becomes 'formyl' at C2, OH at C4, double bond C6=C7.\nKey idea: Acid beats aldehyde; CHO on a branch is 'formyl'; alkene locant is the first carbon of the double bond.","traps":["Aldehyde taken as principal group: COOH has higher priority than CHO.","Double-bond locant 7 vs 6: Locant is the lower-numbered carbon of the C=C."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Functional groups","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Functional group present in sulphonic acids is :","options":["–SO₂","–S(=O)–OH [structure: S with one double-bonded O and an OH]","–SO₃H","–SO₄H"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) –SO₂: sulphonyl fragment without OH. (B) sulphinic acid –SO₂H drawn as S(=O)OH. (D) –SO₄H: hydrogen sulphate (sulphate ester) group.\nWorking: sulphonic acid group is –SO₃H (–S(=O)₂–OH). Option (B) is a sulphinic acid group.\nKey idea: Sulphonic acid = –SO₃H (S with two =O and one –OH).","traps":["Sulphinic vs sulphonic: Count O atoms: sulphonic has 3 O.","Sulphate vs sulphonate: Sulphonic: C–S bond, three O."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hyperconjugation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Number of carbocations from the following that are not stabilized by hyperconjugation is ______.\n(i) (CH₃)₂C⁺–CH₂CH₃ (2-methylbutan-2-yl cation); (ii) [(CH₃)₃C]₂CH⁺ (di-tert-butylmethyl cation); (iii) ⁺CH₃; (iv) cyclopentadienyl cation (C₅H₅⁺, all ring carbons sp²); (v) ⁺CH₂–OCH₃; (vi) (CH₃)₃C⁺ (tert-butyl cation); (vii) (CH₃)₂N–CH₂⁺","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counts (ii) as hyperconjugated because of many CH₃ groups), 3 (counts (v)/(vii) as hyperconjugated via C–H on CH₃ of O/N), 6 (excludes (i) by mistake).\nWorking: hyperconjugation needs α-C–H bonds on an sp³ carbon next to C⁺. (i) and (vi) have them. (ii) – adjacent carbons are quaternary, no α-H; (iii) no adjacent carbon; (iv) neighbours are sp² CH (no sp³ C–H); (v) and (vii) – C⁺ attached to O/N, stabilised by resonance, no α-C–H. Count = 5.\nKey idea: Look only at atoms directly attached to C⁺: they must be sp³ carbons bearing H.","traps":["Di-tert-butyl cation counted as hyperconjugated: The carbons next to C⁺ are quaternary – no α-H.","O/N-attached CH₂⁺ counted: Those H are β to C⁺ through a heteroatom; stabilisation is by lone-pair resonance."],"topics":["Hyperconjugation","Carbocations"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural formulae","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"In the given compound, the number of 2° carbon atom/s is ________.\nCH₃–C(CH₃)H–CH(H)–C(CH₃)H–CH₃ (i.e. CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH₃, 2,4-dimethylpentane; the H atoms are drawn below C2, C3, C4)","options":["Two","Three","Four","One"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Two – counts the two CH carbons (3°) as 2°; (B) Three – counts CH₂ and both CH; (C) Four.\nWorking: 2,4-dimethylpentane has four 1° (CH₃), two 3° (CH) and one 2° carbon (the CH₂).\nKey idea: Degree = number of C attached, not number of H.","traps":["Counting H instead of attached C: Count carbon neighbours: C2, C4 have three."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I: [benzene ring with Cl at one carbon, NO₂ at the adjacent (ortho) carbon and O₂N at the para-to-Cl carbon] IUPAC name of Compound A is 4-chloro-1,3-dinitrobenzene.\nStatement II: [benzene ring with NH₂, CH₃ ortho to it, and C₂H₅ para to NH₂] IUPAC name of Compound B is 4-ethyl-2-methylaniline.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) believes alphabetical order decides numbering in I; (A)/(B) misjudge II.\nWorking: A has locant set {1,2,4} (Cl-1, NO₂-2,4) vs {1,3,4}; lowest set wins → 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene, so I is wrong. B: aniline is parent, NH₂ = C1 → 4-ethyl-2-methylaniline (alphabetical), correct.\nKey idea: Lowest locant set first; alphabetical order only for ties.","traps":["Alphabetical order used for numbering: Alphabet breaks ties only after lowest-locant set."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The shape of carbocation is :","options":["tetrahedral","diagonal pyramidal","diagonal","trigonal planar"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tetrahedral: confuses with carbanion/sp³. (B)/(C): non-standard terms used as fillers.\nWorking: the carbocation carbon is sp² with an empty p orbital, so trigonal planar.\nKey idea: Carbocation = sp², planar, empty p orbital.","traps":["Carbocation vs carbanion geometry: Carbocation sp² planar; carbanion sp³ pyramidal."],"topics":["Carbocations","Hybridisation in organic molecules"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"IUPAC name of following hydrocarbon (X) is :\nCH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃ (X)","options":["2,5,6-Trimethyloctane","2-Ethyl-2,6-diethylheptane","3,4,7-Trimethyloctane","2-Ethyl-3,6-dimethylheptane"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): numbering from the wrong end (3,4,7 vs 2,5,6; first point of difference 2 < 3). (B)/(D): ethyl taken as a substituent, so the parent chain is not the longest.\nWorking: longest chain 8 C; numbering from left gives 2,5,6 vs 3,4,7 from right; first point of difference favours 2,5,6.\nKey idea: Choose longest chain, then first point of difference.","traps":["Locant set choice: Compare first point of difference: 2 < 3.","Shorter parent chain: An ethyl at C-2 means chain can be extended."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Relative stability of the contributing structures is:\n(I) CH₂=CH–CH=O (acrolein, neutral form, O with two lone pairs)\n(II) ⁺CH₂–CH=CH–O⁻ (positive on terminal carbon, negative on oxygen)\n(III) ⁻CH₂–CH=CH–O⁺ (negative on terminal carbon, positive on oxygen with one lone pair)\n(each drawn as CH₂…CH…C(H)…O with the aldehyde H on C1)","options":["(II) > (I) > (III)","(III) > (II) > (I)","(I) > (III) > (II)","(I) > (II) > (III)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) ranks III above II, ignoring electronegativity. (A)/(B) rank a charged structure above the neutral one.\nWorking: the neutral structure is most stable; charge-separated form with negative charge on the more electronegative O (II) beats the one with positive charge on O and negative on C (III).\nKey idea: Neutral > charges on correct atoms (− on O) > charges on wrong atoms (+ on O).","traps":["Charge on wrong atom: Negative charge should sit on the more electronegative atom."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hyperconjugation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"How many compounds among the following compounds show inductive, mesomeric as well as hyperconjugation effects?\n(1) Anisole (C₆H₅OCH₃)\n(2) 5-Methylhex-3-en-2-one ((CH₃)₂CH–CH=CH–CO–CH₃)\n(3) Benzene\n(4) Chlorocyclohexane\n(5) 1-Isopropyl-2-nitrobenzene (o-nitrocumene)\n(6) 1-(But-3-en-2-yl)-2-nitrobenzene (o-NO₂–C₆H₄–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂)\n(7) m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene)\n(8) 1-(2-Methylcyclohex-1-en-1-yl)ethan-1-one (ring C=C bearing COCH₃ and CH₃)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if anisole is counted (O–CH₃ is not C–H on a carbon attached to the ring π-system in the usual sense) or if m-xylene is counted (CH₃ gives +I and hyperconjugation but no mesomeric group); 3 if (6) or (8) is missed.\nWorking: (2), (5), (6), (8) have a polar group (I), a conjugated π-acceptor or donor (M) and α-C–H on an sp³ carbon attached to a π system (hyperconjugation) = 4.\nKey idea: Need all three: polar substituent, conjugated group with lone pair/π-bond, and sp³ C–H next to a π-system.","traps":["Hyperconjugation counted as mesomeric: CH₃ shows +I and hyperconjugation only.","Anisole hyperconjugation: α-H must be on carbon attached to the π system."],"topics":["Hyperconjugation","Inductive effect","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct stability order of the following resonance structures of CH₃–CH=CH–CHO is\nI: CH₃–C⁻H–CH=C(–O⁺)–H (carbanion on C-3, positive charge on oxygen bearing only a single bond)\nII: CH₃–C⁺H–CH=C(–O⁻)–H (carbocation on C-3, negative charge on oxygen)\nIII: CH₃–CH=CH–C(=O)–H (neutral structure)","options":["II > III > I","II > I > III","III > II > I","I > II > III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ranks II above neutral III. (B) ranks reverse-polarity I above III. (D) reverses the order fully.\nWorking: neutral III is most stable; II puts the negative charge on electronegative O; I puts + on O with an incomplete octet and – on carbon, so it is least stable.\nKey idea: No charge separation beats any charge separation; negative charge belongs on O, not C.","traps":["Charge-separated form ranked above neutral: Neutral, full-octet form is always the major contributor.","Wrong polarity ignored: Place – on the more electronegative atom."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bond fission","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Ionic reactions with organic compounds proceed through :\n(A) homolytic bond cleavage\n(B) heterolytic bond cleavage\n(C) free radical formation\n(D) primary free radical\n(E) secondary free radical\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) only","(B) only","(C) only","(D) and (E) only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C),(D): all describe radical (homolytic) pathways.\nWorking: Heterolysis gives ions (carbocations/carbanions); homolysis gives free radicals – those are radical, not ionic, reactions.\nKey idea: Ions come from heterolysis.","traps":["Homolytic and heterolytic swapped: hetero = unequal split → ions."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The functional group that shows negative resonance effect is :","options":["–OH","–OR","–COOH","–NH₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are +R groups (lone pair donors) though –I; a student mixing I and R effects may pick them.\nWorking: –COOH withdraws π-electrons from a conjugated system (–R); –OH, –OR and –NH₂ donate lone pairs (+R).\nKey idea: Lone-pair donors are +R; multiple-bonded electronegative groups are –R.","traps":["–I mistaken for –R: Resonance looks at lone pairs/π bonds, not electronegativity."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following has highly acidic hydrogen?","options":["CH₃–CO–CO–CH₂CH₂CH₃ (hexane-2,3-dione)","CH₃–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃ (hexane-2,5-dione)","CH₃CH₂CH₂–CO–CH₂CH₃ (hexan-3-one)","CH₃–CO–CH₂–CO–CH₂CH₃ (hexane-2,4-dione)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1,2-dione: two C=O but no CH₂ between them; α-H stabilised by one C=O only. (B) 1,4-dione: each CH₂ is α to one C=O only. (C) Simple ketone: one C=O, least acidic.\nWorking: the CH₂ flanked by two C=O groups (β-diketone) gives a carbanion delocalised over both carbonyl oxygens (pKa ≈ 9).\nKey idea: Doubly-activated CH₂ (between two C=O) gives the most stabilised enolate.","traps":["Any diketone treated as active methylene: Find a carbon that is α to BOTH carbonyls."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Cyclohexene [six-membered carbocyclic ring with one C=C] is ________ type of an organic compound.","options":["Benzenoid aromatic","Benzenoid non-aromatic","Acyclic","Alicyclic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Benzenoid aromatic: needs a benzene ring. (B) Benzenoid non-aromatic: hexagon drawing resembles benzene. (C) Acyclic: it is a ring.\nWorking: cyclohexene is a closed-chain carbocycle with aliphatic properties and no benzene ring.\nKey idea: A ring of carbons without aromaticity = alicyclic.","traps":["Hexagon drawing read as benzene: Benzenoid requires a benzene ring; one C=C does not make it."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"IUPAC name of following compound (P) is: [Bond-line cyclohexane with two methyl groups on one ring carbon (gem-dimethyl) and an ethyl group on the carbon two positions away (1,3-relationship)]","options":["1-Ethyl-5,5-dimethylcyclohexane","3-Ethyl-1,1-dimethylcyclohexane","1-Ethyl-3,3-dimethylcyclohexane","1,1-Dimethyl-3-ethylcyclohexane"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1,5,5: not the lowest set. (C) 1,3,3: gives ethyl locant 1 by alphabetical priority, but {1,1,3} is lower at first point of difference. (D) right numbers, wrong order of citation (prefixes must be alphabetical).\nWorking: lowest locant set {1,1,3} (numbering from the gem-dimethyl carbon) beats {1,3,3}; substituents are then cited alphabetically (ethyl before dimethyl).\nKey idea: Apply lowest locants first; alphabetical order decides numbering only when locant sets tie.","traps":["Alphabetical priority used before lowest-locant set: Compare locant sets first: 1,1,3 < 1,3,3.","Substituents not cited alphabetically: Cite prefixes alphabetically, ignoring \"di\"."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The order of relative stability of the contributing structure is : I: CH₂=CH–CH=O (neutral propenal, C=O with two lone pairs on O) ⟷ II: ⁺CH₂–CH=CH–O⁻ (positive charge on terminal CH₂, negative charge on single-bonded O with three lone pairs) ⟷ III: ⁻CH₂–CH=CH–O⁺ (carbanion on terminal CH₂, positive charge on single-bonded O). Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["I = II = III","I > II > III","III > II > I","II > I > III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats all contributors as equal (confusing contributors with the hybrid). (C) reverses the order. (D) ranks charge-separated II above neutral I.\nWorking: I is neutral with all octets (most stable); II separates charge but puts negative charge on the more electronegative O; III puts positive charge on O and negative on C (least stable).\nKey idea: Neutral > charge-separated with − on O > charge-separated with + on O.","traps":["Contributors thought equally stable: Equal contribution only for equivalent structures; these are not.","Charge placement ignored: Negative charge must sit on the more electronegative atom."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural formulae","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Bond line formula of HOCH(CN)₂ is :","options":["Structure written with all atoms: a C bearing H, CN, CN and HO (condensed/expanded drawing)","HO–CH with two CN groups on lines (partly condensed)","HO–CH with two C≡N groups on lines (partly condensed)","Skeletal drawing: a vertex bearing OH and two lines ending in CN"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) all write the central C and/or H explicitly - these are condensed or partly expanded forms, not bond-line.\nWorking: in a bond-line formula the C and H of CH are not written; only the vertex with OH and the two CN groups appear, as in (D).\nKey idea: If CH is written, it is not a bond-line formula.","traps":["Partly condensed form taken as bond-line: Bond-line formulas never show C or H on carbon."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The molecular formula of second homologue in the homologous series of mono carboxylic acids is","options":["C₂H₄O₂","C₂H₂O₂","CH₂O","C₃H₆O₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) wrong H count (glyoxal formula). (C) is formaldehyde formula, not HCOOH. (D) propanoic acid - third homologue (counting acetic acid as first).\nWorking: first member HCOOH (CH₂O₂), second CH₃COOH (C₂H₄O₂).\nKey idea: HCOOH is the first member.","traps":["Acetic acid taken as first member: Start counting from HCOOH."],"topics":["Classification","Functional groups"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure for a given compound is","options":["electromeric energy","resonance energy","ionization energy","hyperconjugation energy"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) are names of other electronic effects, not energy terms in NCERT; (C) is an atomic property.\nWorking: this is the NCERT definition of resonance (stabilisation) energy.\nKey idea: Resonance energy = E(most stable canonical form) − E(hybrid).","traps":["Other electronic-effect names: Only resonance is defined via canonical structures."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The interaction between π bond and lone pair of electrons present on an adjacent atom is responsible for","options":["Resonance effect","Electromeric effect","Hyperconjugation","Inductive effect"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) electromeric needs an attacking reagent; (C) hyperconjugation is σ(C–H)–π; (D) inductive is σ-bond polarisation.\nWorking: NCERT defines the resonance (mesomeric) effect as polarity from interaction of two π bonds or of a π bond with a lone pair on an adjacent atom.\nKey idea: π–lone pair conjugation = resonance (mesomeric) effect.","traps":["Hyperconjugation vs resonance: Hyperconjugation needs σ C–H, not a lone pair."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of compounds among the following which contain sulphur as heteroatom is ___________.\nFuran, Thiophene, Pyridine, Pyrrole, Cysteine, Tyrosine","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 if cysteine excluded (S not in a ring); 3 if tyrosine assumed to contain S (confused with methionine).\nWorking: thiophene (S in ring) and cysteine (–SH side chain) contain S; furan (O), pyridine and pyrrole (N), tyrosine (O, N) do not.\nKey idea: Thiophene = S-heterocycle; cysteine = thiol amino acid.","traps":["Heteroatom read as ring atom only: Any non-C/H atom is a heteroatom; cysteine's S counts.","Tyrosine confused with S-amino acid: S-containing amino acids: cysteine, methionine."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"According to IUPAC system, the compound [structure: cyclohexene ring with OH on the carbon next to the ring double bond] is named as","options":["Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol","1-Hydroxyhex-2-ene","Cyclohex-1-en-3-ol","Cyclohex-1-en-2-ol"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats the ring as an open hexene chain and uses hydroxy prefix. (C) gives the double bond priority (1-en-3-ol). (D) wrong ring positions and priority.\nWorking: the –OH (principal group) gets locant 1; numbering toward the double bond gives C2=C3, so cyclohex-2-en-1-ol.\nKey idea: The suffix group fixes C1; the double bond then takes the lowest possible locant.","traps":["Double bond numbered first: Suffix (-ol) outranks -ene for the lowest locant."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The ascending acidity order of the following H atoms is\n[structures: A = H–C≡C–(H) (ethyne terminal H, sp C–H); B = H₂C=CH–(H) (vinylic H of ethene); C = (CH₃)₃C–(H) (tertiary C–H of isobutane); D = H₃C–CH₂–(H) (primary C–H of ethane)]","options":["A < B < D < C","A < B < C < D","D < C < B < A","C < D < B < A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) written in descending order (reversal). (C) D < C: assumes tertiary H more acidic (confuses with carbocation/radical stability).\nWorking: acidity follows carbanion stability: sp (A) > sp² (B) > sp³ primary (D) > sp³ tertiary (C, +I of three CH₃ destabilises the carbanion).\nKey idea: Carbanion stability: sp > sp² > sp³, and among sp³ 1° > 2° > 3°.","traps":["Tertiary treated like carbocation: Carbanions are destabilised by +I alkyl groups: 3° least acidic.","Ascending vs descending: Write the weakest first for ascending."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Carbanions"]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tetravalency & shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The total number of 'Sigma' and 'Pi' bonds in 2-formylhex-4-enoic acid is _________.","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 19 or 3 (only σ or only π), 21 (misses one H or the formyl C=O π), 25 (counts double bonds as 2 σ).\nWorking: HOOC–CH(CHO)–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₃ = C₇H₁₀O₃ (20 atoms, acyclic) → 19 σ; π: 2 C=O + 1 C=C = 3; total 22.\nKey idea: For an acyclic molecule σ bonds = atoms − 1; add one π per double bond.","traps":["Formyl carbon or its H missed: Write C₇H₁₀O₃ and use atoms − 1 for σ.","Only one bond type counted: Add σ and π."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Structure of 4-Methylpent-2-enal is :","options":["H₂C=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH₂–CHO","CH₃–CH₂–C(CH₃)=CH–CHO","CH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–CHO","CH₃–CH₂–CH=C(CH₃)–CHO"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 3-methylpent-4-enal (C=C at the far end). (B) 3-methylpent-2-enal (methyl on C3). (D) 2-methylpent-2-enal (methyl on C2).\nWorking: CHO = C1, C=C between C2–C3, methyl on C4, five-carbon chain → (CH₃)₂CH–CH=CH–CHO.\nKey idea: Number from CHO; locants: ene at 2, methyl at 4.","traps":["Methyl locant misplaced: Count from the CHO carbon as C1."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct stability order of carbocations is","options":["(CH₃)₃C⁺ > CH₃–C⁺H₂ > (CH₃)₂C⁺H > C⁺H₃","(CH₃)₃C⁺ > (CH₃)₂C⁺H > CH₃–C⁺H₂ > C⁺H₃","C⁺H₃ > CH₃–C⁺H₂ > CH₃–C⁺H–CH₃ > (CH₃)₃C⁺","C⁺H₃ > (CH₃)₂C⁺H > CH₃–C⁺H₂ > (CH₃)₃C⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps 1° and 2°. (C) fully reversed (as for carbanions). (D) reversed with a 1°/2° swap.\nWorking: stability 3° > 2° > 1° > methyl, from the number of α-C–H bonds for hyperconjugation (9 > 6 > 3 > 0) plus +I effect.\nKey idea: More alkyl groups on C⁺ → more hyperconjugation → more stable.","traps":["Carbanion order reversed onto carbocations: Alkyl groups donate electrons: they stabilise C⁺, destabilise C⁻.","1° and 2° swapped: Count α-H: 2° has 6, 1° has 3."],"topics":["Carbocations","Hyperconjugation"]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"IUPAC name of following compound is :\nCH₃–CH(NH₂)–CH₂–CN","options":["2-Aminobutanenitrile","3-Aminopropanenitrile","2-Aminopentanenitrile","3-Aminobutanenitrile"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers from the wrong end. (B) leaves the CN carbon out of the parent chain (propane). (C) counts the chain as five carbons.\nWorking: –CN is the principal group and its carbon is C-1; chain C1(N)–C2H₂–C3H(NH₂)–C4H₃ = butanenitrile with NH₂ at C-3.\nKey idea: In '-nitrile' names the C of C≡N is C-1 of the chain; in '-carbonitrile' names it is not counted.","traps":["CN carbon left out of chain: Suffix -nitrile includes the C of CN in the parent.","Numbering from the methyl end: Principal group (CN) gets C-1."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"A species having carbon with sextet of electrons and can act as electrophile is called","options":["pentavalent carbon","carbon free radical","carbanion","carbocation"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): free radical has seven electrons (septet), not sextet. (C): carbanion has an octet with a lone pair (nucleophile). (A): pentavalent carbon is a transition-state idea, not an intermediate.\nWorking: a carbocation has six valence electrons on carbon and is electron-deficient, so it is an electrophile.\nKey idea: Sextet + positive charge = carbocation = electrophile.","traps":["Radical counted as sextet: Radical = 7 valence electrons."],"topics":["Carbocations","Electrophiles & nucleophiles"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: IUPAC name of HO–CH₂–(CH₂)₃–CH₂–COCH₃ is 7-hydroxyheptan-2-one.\nStatement II: 2-oxoheptan-7-ol is the correct IUPAC name for above compound. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): student treats alcohol as principal group because OH is at the chain end.\nWorking: 7-carbon chain; ketone outranks alcohol, so suffix '-one' at C-2 and OH as prefix 'hydroxy' at C-7: 7-hydroxyheptan-2-one. II wrongly makes the alcohol the principal group.\nKey idea: Ketone > alcohol: '-one' suffix, 'hydroxy' prefix.","traps":["Alcohol chosen as principal group: Use priority list: –COOH > ester > –CHO > C=O > –OH."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Identify structure of 2,3-dibromo-1-phenylpentane.","options":["[Ph–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃]","[Ph–CH(Br)–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₂–CH₃]","[Ph–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃]","[Ph–CH₂–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH(Br)–CH₃]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Br at C1 and C3 relative to phenyl (wrong locants). (B) 1,2-dibromo-1-phenylpentane (phenyl and Br on same carbon). (D) 3,4-dibromo-1-phenylpentane.\nWorking: pentane chain C1 bears phenyl, C2 and C3 bear Br: PhCH₂CHBrCHBrCH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: C1 is the carbon carrying phenyl; Br on the next two carbons.","traps":["Zig-zag carbons miscounted: Number vertices from the phenyl-bearing carbon."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"In the following reaction, 'B' is\n[Structure: a cyclohexene ring bearing two adjacent methyl groups on C4 and C5, away from the C=C (4,5-dimethylcyclohex-1-enyl), the alkene carbon C1 carrying a side chain –CH₂–CH₂–CH(OH)–C(CH₃)₃; i.e. 1-(4,5-dimethylcyclohex-1-en-1-yl)-4,4-dimethylpentan-3-ol] —[H₃O⁺]→ 'B' (major)","options":["A fused bicyclic ether: tetrahydro-chromene-type ring (O in a six-membered ring fused to the cyclohexene at the C=C), tert-butyl on the carbon next to O; the carbocyclic ring carries no methyl groups","A fused bicyclic hydrocarbon (octahydronaphthalene) with a tetrasubstituted ring-fusion C=C; one ring carries two adjacent methyls, the other ring carries a gem-dimethyl carbon next to the fusion carbon and an adjacent CH–CH₃","A cyclohexene fused to a cyclopentene (hydrindene) with ring-fusion C=C, two methyls on the six-membered ring and a tert-butyl on the five-membered ring","A spiro ether: 1-oxaspiro[4.5]decane with two methyls on the cyclohexane and a tert-butyl next to O"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) cyclises without the methyl shift and with the wrong ring size (five-membered) keeping t-Bu intact; (A) and (D) have the OH oxygen attacking (ether formation) although water is lost as leaving group – (D) as a spiro ether via attack at C1.\nWorking: protonation/loss of water gives a 2° cation next to C(CH₃)₃; a 1,2-methyl shift gives the 3° cation; the ring C=C attacks it forming a new six-membered ring (C1, CH₂, CH₂, CH(CH₃), C(CH₃)₂, C2); the resulting 3° cation at the ring-fusion carbon loses H⁺ to give the tetrasubstituted ring-fusion alkene.\nKey idea: After the methyl shift the 3° cation is exactly five atoms from the ring alkene carbon, so a six-membered ring forms; the final cation at the fusion carbon eliminates to the tetrasubstituted alkene.","traps":["Methyl shift missed: Check for 1,2-shift to a more stable 3° cation before further steps.","Ether formation chosen: In H₃O⁺, OH leaves as water; the nucleophile is the ring C=C."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct IUPAC nomenclature for the following compound is: [Structure: CH₃–C(=O)–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–COOH, i.e. a six-carbon chain with COOH at one end, a methyl on the carbon next to COOH and a ketone C=O on the fifth carbon]","options":["2-Formyl-5-methylhexan-6-oic acid","5-Methyl-2-oxohexan-6-oic acid","5-Formyl-2-methylhexanoic acid","2-Methyl-5-oxohexanoic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) call the ketone a \"formyl\" group (CHO is an aldehyde, not a ketone); (A)/(B) number from the wrong end giving \"hexan-6-oic\", which is never used since COOH carbon is C-1.\nWorking: COOH is the principal group (C-1); chain of six carbons includes the ketone carbon (C-5, oxo) and CH₃ branch at C-2 → 2-methyl-5-oxohexanoic acid.\nKey idea: COOH carbon is always C-1 and the ketone becomes \"oxo\" when a higher-priority group is present.","traps":["Ketone named as formyl: Formyl is –CHO only; an in-chain C=O is oxo.","Numbering from the methyl ketone end: The principal characteristic group gets the lowest locant (C-1 for COOH)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"In the reaction given below 'B' is\nSubstrate: a tetralin (1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalene) in which benzylic C-1 bears OH and a –CH₂CH₂NH₂ chain, and C-2 is a spiro carbon of a cyclopentane ring. Reagents: i) HCl; ii) KOH → 'B' (major product)","options":["Spiro compound: tetralin C-1 converted into a spiro-piperidine carbon (ring N–H formed by the chain amine), C-2 still spiro to cyclopentane","Tetracyclic saturated product: aromatic ring fused to a six-membered ring which is fused to a new cyclohexane ring; the ring-fusion benzylic carbon bears –CH₂CH₂NH₂; no C=C","Same skeleton as (B) (cyclopentane expanded to cyclohexane, fused at C-1/C-2) with a C=C in the middle ring (C-2=C-3) and –CH₂CH₂NH₂ on the benzylic ring-fusion carbon","Tetralin with exocyclic C=C at C-1 bearing CH₂NH₂ side chain (CH=CH–CH₂NH₂ type) and C-2 still spiro to cyclopentane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): intramolecular trapping by NH₂ – ignores that HCl protonates the amine. (B): ring expansion right but forgets the final elimination (or assumes substitution then loss). (D): elimination without rearrangement, with the side chain misplaced.\nWorking: HCl protonates the amine (no intramolecular trapping) and the tertiary benzylic OH leaves to give a carbocation at C-1. A ring bond of the adjacent spiro cyclopentane migrates to C-1 (ring expansion 5→6), placing the cation at old C-2 (now a tertiary ring-fusion carbon). KOH then removes a β-H from C-3 to give the C-2=C-3 alkene, and frees the amine: option (C).\nKey idea: Cation next to a spiro cyclopentane → ring expands to cyclohexane; then KOH gives the alkene.","traps":["Amine assumed to trap the carbocation: Under HCl the amine is protonated (–NH₃⁺) and not nucleophilic.","Final elimination missed: Step ii (KOH) removes a β-H to form C=C."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Reaction: cyclobutyl–CH(OH)–cyclopentyl —(H⁺, Δ)→ major product\nIn the above reaction, left hand side and right hand side rings are named as 'A' and 'B' respectively. They undergo ring expansion. The correct statement for this process is:","options":["Finally both rings will become six membered each.","Only A will become 6 membered.","Ring expansion can go upto seven membered rings","Finally both rings will become five membered each."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): stops after one expansion of the strained ring. (C): assumes expansion continues beyond six (larger rings gain nothing). (D): only the 4-ring expands to 5.\nWorking: the 2° cation at the carbinol carbon expands ring A (4→5) placing C⁺ in the new ring; a 1,2-H shift makes the carbinol carbon tertiary; ring B then expands (5→6) to give a spiro cation, and a further alkyl shift expands the 5-membered ring A to 6, giving a decalin-type (fused 6,6) tertiary cation that loses H⁺ to an octalin. Both rings end six-membered.\nKey idea: Rings expand to relieve strain until six-membered; each shift must also keep/produce a stable cation.","traps":["Only the strained four-membered ring expanded: Keep shifting while a more stable cation/less strained ring results.","Expansion assumed to continue beyond six: Six-membered rings are strain-free; no driving force to seven."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"In the following given reaction, 'A' is\nprop-1-en-2-ylcyclobutane [cyclobutane ring bearing C(CH₃)=CH₂] + HBr → 'A' (major product)","options":["1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane (Br and CH₃ on adjacent carbons of a cyclohexane ring)","1-bromo-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane (cyclopentane; one carbon bears Br and CH₃, the adjacent carbon bears CH₃)","1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (cyclohexane; one carbon bears both CH₃ and Br)","2-bromo-1,1-dimethylcyclopentane (cyclopentane; one carbon bears two CH₃, the adjacent carbon bears Br)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) stops after ring expansion (2° cation captured). (A)/(C) imagine expansion to a six-membered ring. A plain Markovnikov product (2-bromo-2-cyclobutylpropane) is not offered, which signals rearrangement.\nWorking: Markovnikov protonation gives a 3° cation next to the strained cyclobutane; ring expansion gives a 2° cyclopentyl cation, which undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift to a 3° cation; Br⁻ attack gives 1-bromo-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane.\nKey idea: After ring expansion, check again: a 2° cation next to a quaternary carbon will shift CH₃ to become 3°.","traps":["Stopping after ring expansion: Re-check the new cation's stability; shift CH₃ to give 3°.","Wrong ring size after expansion: Only the cation carbon joins the ring: 4 + 1 = 5."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which will undergo deprotonation most readily in the basic medium? a: CH₃COCH₂COCH₃ (pentane-2,4-dione); b: CH₃OOC–CH₂–COOCH₃ (dimethyl malonate); c: CH₃COCH₂COOCH₃ (methyl acetoacetate).","options":["a only","Both a and c","c only","b only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): treats ketone and ester C=O as equally withdrawing. (C)/(D): thinks –OMe increases acidity (ignores its +R donation).\nWorking: in (a) the CH₂ lies between two ketone C=O groups, so its enolate is the most stabilised (pKa ≈ 9). In (c) one of the two groups is an ester (pKa ≈ 11) and in (b) both are esters (pKa ≈ 13); the OCH₃ lone pair donates into the ester C=O, making it a weaker electron-withdrawer. So (a) is deprotonated most readily.\nKey idea: Ester C=O is less electron-withdrawing because OMe donates by resonance; diketone anion is best stabilised (pKa ≈ 9 < 11 < 13).","traps":["Ester vs ketone carbonyl: +R of OMe dominates; ester enolate less stable."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"What is the correct order of acidity of the protons marked A–D in the given compounds? [Structure: benzene ring with a CH₂ group bearing CO₂H (H_D on this CH₂, H_C on the CO₂H) at one carbon and, meta to it, a CH₂–C≡C–H group (H_B on the CH₂, H_A on the terminal alkyne carbon); 2-[3-(prop-2-yn-1-yl)phenyl]acetic acid]","options":["H_D > H_C > H_B > H_A","H_C > H_A > H_D > H_B","H_C > H_D > H_B > H_A","H_C > H_D > H_A > H_B"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: puts α-C–H above COOH. B: ranks alkyne C–H above the α-carbonyl/benzylic C–H. C: ranks the benzylic-propargylic CH₂ above the terminal alkyne.\nWorking: H_C (O–H of COOH) is by far the most acidic. H_D is on a CH₂ that is both α to C=O and benzylic, so its carbanion is delocalised into the carbonyl and the ring (pKa ≈ 22). H_A is on an sp carbon (pKa ≈ 25). H_B is on an sp³ CH₂ stabilised only by benzylic/propargylic delocalisation, so it is least acidic. Order: H_C > H_D > H_A > H_B.\nKey idea: O–H of COOH ≫ C–H α to C=O and benzylic (enolate + aryl) > sp C–H (pKa ≈ 25) > sp³ C–H stabilised only by benzyl/propargyl delocalisation.","traps":["Alkyne C–H over-ranked: Compare pKa: α-carbonyl benzylic C–H (~22) beats sp C–H (~25).","Benzylic-propargylic CH over-ranked: Delocalisation into a ring and alkyne still leaves an sp³ carbanion far weaker than sp C–H acid."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The most stable carbocation for the following is: four six-membered-ring cyclohexadienyl cations each bearing NH₂. (a) NH₂ on sp³ ring carbon C1; positive charge on adjacent C2; ring double bonds C3=C4 and C5=C6. (b) NH₂ on sp³ C1; double bonds C2=C3 and C5=C6; positive charge on C4 (para to C1). (c) NH₂ on sp² C1 which is part of C1=C2; second double bond C3=C4; positive charge on C5; C6 is CH₂ – i.e. the charge is conjugated through both double bonds to the NH₂-bearing carbon. (d) NH₂ on sp² C1 of C1=C2; double bond C3=C4; positive charge on C6 (adjacent to C1); C5 is CH₂.","options":["b","d","c","a"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) b and (D) a: NH₂ is on the sp³ carbon, so only –I acts; (B) d: NH₂ is on the central (non-charge-bearing) carbon of the conjugated chain, so +M cannot reach the charge and NH₂ even withdraws inductively.\nWorking: in c the positive charge on C5 is delocalised through C3=C4 and C1=C2 onto C3 and C1; at C1 the NH₂ lone pair donates (+R), giving an iminium form (C=N⁺H₂) with every atom having an octet. In a and b NH₂ is on an sp³ carbon (only –I). In d the charge is spread over C6, C2 and C4 and never reaches C1, so NH₂ cannot donate by resonance. Hence c is most stable.\nKey idea: In c the charge alternates C5→C3→C1, and C1 carries NH₂, giving an iminium resonance form with a complete octet on every atom.","traps":["NH₂ on sp³ carbon cannot donate by resonance: Check hybridisation of the carbon carrying NH₂ first.","Charge only at alternate carbons: Draw resonance structures and see whether + ever lands on the NH₂-bearing carbon."],"topics":["Carbocations","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation in organic molecules","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The number of sp³ hybridised carbons in an acyclic neutral compound with molecular formula C₄H₅N is ____.","type":"num","answer":"0 or 1","ranges":[[0.0,0.0],[1.0,1.0]],"range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (assuming a saturated chain portion); only one of 0/1 if a single isomer is fixed.\nWorking: C₄H₅N has 3 degrees of unsaturation and, being acyclic, needs triple/double bonds. Nitriles such as CH₂=CH–CH₂–CN, CH₃–CH=CH–CN, CH₂=C(CH₃)–CN have 1 sp³ C; structures such as HC≡C–CH=CH–NH₂ or HC≡C–NH–CH=CH₂ have 0. The question is ambiguous, so both 0 and 1 were accepted as correct.\nKey idea: DBE = 3 for C₄H₅N; acyclic forces a triple bond plus a double bond (or nitrile + C=C).","traps":["Ill-defined structure: Compute DBE, then draw the possible isomers; both 0 and 1 are possible."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The correct decreasing order of priority of functional groups in naming an organic compound as per IUPAC system of nomenclature is :","options":["—COOH > —CONH₂ > —COCl > —CHO","—SO₃H > —COCl > —CONH₂ > —CN","—COOR > —COCl > —NH₂ > >C=O","—COOH > —COOR > —CONH₂ > —COCl"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) put CONH₂ above COCl; (C) puts NH₂ above the ketone.\nWorking: priority COOH > SO₃H > COOR > COCl > CONH₂ > CN > CHO > >C=O > OH > NH₂; only (B) follows it.\nKey idea: Acid > sulphonic acid > ester > acid halide > amide > nitrile > aldehyde > ketone > alcohol > amine.","traps":["Acid halide vs amide: Remember \"ester, halide, amide\".","Ketone vs amine: Carbonyls outrank –OH and –NH₂."],"topics":["IUPAC naming","Functional groups"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (skeletal structures): (A) five-membered saturated ring with one O (tetrahydrofuran); (B) two triangles sharing one vertical edge, with the bonds extended past both outer ring carbons as line-ends (gem-dimethyl groups) – a bicyclo[1.1.0]butane core (2,2,4,4-tetramethylbicyclo[1.1.0]butane); (C) two triangles sharing one central vertex, with the bonds extended past the four outer ring carbons as line-ends (methyl groups) – a spiro[2.2]pentane core; (D) five-membered ring with one O and two C=C (furan). List-II: (I) Spiro compound; (II) Aromatic compound; (III) Non-planar Heterocyclic compound; (IV) Bicyclo compound. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) – (II), (B) – (I), (C) – (IV), (D) – (III)","(A) – (IV), (B) – (III), (C) – (I), (D) – (II)","(A) – (III), (B) – (IV), (C) – (I), (D) – (II)","(A) – (IV), (B) – (III), (C) – (II), (D) – (I)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) call THF bicyclic; (A) calls THF aromatic and furan a non-planar heterocycle; (D) also calls furan a spiro compound.\nWorking: THF is a saturated (puckered, non-planar) heterocycle; edge-fused triangles = bicyclo[1.1.0]butane; vertex-shared triangles = spiropentane; furan has 6 π electrons (two C=C + O lone pair) and is aromatic.\nKey idea: Shared bond = fused (bicyclo); shared atom = spiro.","traps":["Spiro vs bicyclo: Count shared atoms: one = spiro, two = fused bicyclo.","THF vs furan: Aromatic needs a fully conjugated planar ring."],"topics":["Classification","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Among the following marked proton of which compound shows lowest pKa value? (A) CH₃COOH – marked α-C–H of the CH₃ group; (B) CH₃COCH₃ – marked α-C–H; (C) PhCH₂COCH₃ (phenylacetone) – marked benzylic CH₂ proton between the phenyl ring and C=O; (D) PhCH₂COOH (phenylacetic acid) – marked benzylic CH₂ proton.","options":["H₂C(H)–COOH (marked α-H of acetic acid)","H₂C(H)–CO–CH₃ (marked α-H of acetone)","Ph–CH(H)–CO–CH₃ (marked benzylic α-H of phenylacetone)","Ph–CH(H)–COOH (marked benzylic α-H of phenylacetic acid)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) tempts because a carboxylic acid is \"more acidic\" — but the O–H, not the marked C–H; (A) same error; (B) lacks the phenyl stabilisation.\nWorking: the carbanion from (C) is stabilised by both the ketone C=O and the phenyl ring (pKa ≈ 16); acid α-H’s (A, D) are weakly acidic because the carboxyl (or carboxylate) stabilises a carbanion poorly; acetone α-H (B) ≈ 19–20 has only C=O stabilisation.\nKey idea: Ketone C=O stabilises an α-carbanion better than COOH; a phenyl ring adds further delocalisation.","traps":["Acidity of molecule vs marked proton: Look only at the conjugate base of the marked C–H.","Ketone vs acid α-H: COOH/COO⁻ is a weaker carbanion stabiliser than a ketone C=O."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Carbanions"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The major product in the given reaction is: substrate (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂CH₂CH₂–C(CH₃)₂–OH (2,7-dimethyloct-6-en-2-ol) treated with (1) H⁺, heat; (2) HBr.","options":["(CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₂CH₂CH₂–C(CH₃)₂Br (2-bromo-2,7-dimethyloct-6-ene)","cyclopentane bearing C(CH₃)Br on one ring carbon and a gem-dimethyl ring carbon next to it (1-bromo-1,2,2-trimethylcyclopentane)","cyclopentane with a 2-bromopropan-2-yl group on C-1 and gem-dimethyl at C-2: 1-(2-bromopropan-2-yl)-2,2-dimethylcyclopentane","(CH₃)₂CH–CHBr–CH₂CH₂CH₂–CH(CH₃)₂ (3-bromo-2,7-dimethyloctane)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is simple SN1 substitution of OH by Br without the H⁺/heat step; (B) has the wrong carbon count (8 C); (D) is a reduced skeleton with Br on a secondary carbon.\nWorking: H⁺/heat ionises the tertiary alcohol to a tertiary cation; the internal C=C attacks it (5-exo cyclisation) forming a cyclopentane with gem-dimethyl and a new tertiary cation on the exocyclic C(CH₃)₂; this loses H⁺ to an alkene (isopropylidene/isopropenyl), and HBr then adds by Markovnikov to put Br on that tertiary carbon. Option (C) fits all 10 carbons.\nKey idea: Count carbons: only (C) keeps C₁₀ with a five-membered ring formed from the cation and alkene.","traps":["Direct substitution vs cyclisation: Step (1), H⁺/heat, is separate: trace the cation first.","Carbon count of ring products: Count carbons in reactant and product."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which of the following is an example of conjugated diketone? (A) CH₃–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃ (heptane-2,6-dione); (B) CH₃–CO–CH₂– attached to cyclohexan-4-one ring (4-(2-oxopropyl)cyclohexanone); (C) cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione (p-benzoquinone, O=C₆H₄=O); (D) C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃ (1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione).","options":["CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₂COCH₃","CH₃COCH₂–(cyclohexan-4-one)","p-benzoquinone (O=C₆H₄=O)","C₆H₅COCH₂CH₂COCH₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): conjugation of the phenyl ring with one carbonyl is mistaken for conjugation between the two carbonyls; (B): ring ketone drawn alongside an enone-like line looks conjugated.\nWorking: in p-benzoquinone each C=O is joined to the other through alternating C=C bonds (O=C–C=C–C=O), a conjugated diketone. In (A), (B) and (D) the two carbonyls are separated by sp³ CH₂ groups; in (D) only the phenyl ring is conjugated with one C=O.\nKey idea: Conjugated diketone needs C=O–(C=C)ₙ–C=O with no sp³ break.","traps":["Aryl–C=O conjugation taken as diketone conjugation: Trace the path between the two C=O groups; CH₂ breaks conjugation."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Electrophiles & nucleophiles","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of electrophilic centre in the given compound is ____. [Structure: naphthalene bearing at C1 a –CH₂–CH=CH–CO–CH₃ chain (an α,β-unsaturated methyl ketone, i.e. 5-(naphthalen-1-yl)pent-3-en-2-one unit) and at C7 a –CH₂CN group.]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (missing the enone β-carbon); 4 (adding the CH₂ of CH₂CN or counting the carbonyl oxygen/ring carbons).\nWorking: electrophilic carbons are the carbonyl carbon, the β-carbon of the enone (conjugate-addition site) and the nitrile carbon → 3.\nKey idea: Conjugation relays the carbonyl δ+ to the β-carbon.","traps":["β-carbon of enone missed: Draw the resonance form with + on the β-carbon.","Acidic CH₂ counted: Electrophilic = accepts electrons; acidic H is a different property."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The IUPAC name of ethylidene chloride is:","options":["1-Chloroethene","1-Chloroethyne","1,2-Dichloroethane","1,1-Dichloroethane"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – ethylene dichloride (vic); (A) – vinyl chloride, 'ethylidene' read as alkene; (B) – nonsense alkyne.\nWorking: ethylidene = CH₃CH< (gem); ethylidene chloride = CH₃CHCl₂ = 1,1-dichloroethane. Ethylene dichloride is 1,2-dichloroethane.\nKey idea: -idene = both halogens on the same carbon (gem).","traps":["Ethylidene vs ethylene: Alkylidene = gem, alkylene = vic.","'-ene' sound read as double bond: Ethylidene is a divalent group, not an alkene."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Free radicals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"(C₇H₅O₂)₂ —hν→ [X] + 2 C₆H₅• + 2CO₂. Consider the above reaction and identify the intermediate X.","options":["C₆H₅–C(=O)⁺ (benzoyl/acylium cation)","C₆H₅–C(=O)–O⁻ (benzoate anion)","C₆H₅–C(=O)–Ö: (oxygen shown with two lone pairs, no charge or odd electron)","C₆H₅–C(=O)–O• (benzoyloxy radical, oxygen with an unpaired electron)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) acylium cation – heterolysis thinking; (B) benzoate anion – ionic cleavage; (C) oxygen drawn without the odd electron – miscounting electrons on a radical.\nWorking: (C₇H₅O₂)₂ is benzoyl peroxide; light breaks the weak O–O bond homolytically giving two C₆H₅COO• radicals, each losing CO₂ to give C₆H₅•. X is the benzoyloxy radical = (D).\nKey idea: Light + peroxide → homolysis of O–O → RCOO•, which loses CO₂ → R•.","traps":["Ionic cleavage assumed: hν on peroxide → homolysis → neutral radicals.","Radical vs neutral oxygen drawing: Count electrons on O: 7 for a radical."],"topics":["Free radicals","Bond fission"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrophiles & nucleophiles","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct order of nucleophilicity is","options":["F⁻ > OH⁻","H₂Ö > OH⁻ (H₂O with two lone pairs shown)","RÖH > RO⁻ (ROH with lone pairs shown)","NH₂⁻ > NH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – reverses period trend (F more electronegative holds its pair tighter); (B), (C) – neutral species placed above their anions.\nWorking: an anion is a better nucleophile than its neutral conjugate acid (NH₂⁻ > NH₃, OH⁻ > H₂O, RO⁻ > ROH); along a period nucleophilicity falls with electronegativity, so OH⁻ > F⁻.\nKey idea: Negative charge and lower electronegativity raise nucleophilicity: NH₂⁻ > NH₃.","traps":["Basicity of F⁻ confused with nucleophilicity: In a period, the less electronegative atom is the better nucleophile: OH⁻ > F⁻.","Lone pairs drawn on neutral species: Charge matters more than drawn lone pairs: conjugate base > neutral acid."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The correct IUPAC name of the following compound is: [structure: O₂N–CH(CH₃)–CH=C(CH₃)–C(=O)–CH₂–CHO]","options":["4-methyl-2-nitro-5-oxohept-3-enal","4-methyl-5-oxo-2-nitrohept-3-enal","4-methyl-6-nitro-3-oxohept-4-enal","6-formyl-4-methyl-2-nitrohex-3-enal"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers from the nitro end; (B) also non-alphabetical (oxo before nitro); (D) uses a six-carbon chain with CHO as formyl prefix.\nWorking: the seven-carbon chain is numbered from CHO (C1): C3 keto (oxo), C4 methyl, C4=C5 double bond, C6 nitro; substituents alphabetical: methyl, nitro, oxo.\nKey idea: -al takes C1, so numbering is fixed; everything else follows.","traps":["Numbering direction: Principal group (–CHO) is C1.","Alphabetical order of prefixes: methyl < nitro < oxo."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following carbocations is most stable:","options":["CH₂=C(OCH₃)–CH₂⁺ (2-methoxyallyl cation)","CH₂=C(OCH₃)–CH=CH–CH₂⁺ (OCH₃ on C-4 of the pentadienyl cation)","H₃CO–CH=CH–CH₂⁺ (3-methoxyallyl cation)","H₃CO–CH=CH–CH=CH–CH₂⁺ (5-methoxypentadienyl cation)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is tempting because it has the longest conjugation plus OCH₃, but OCH₃ is at a node of positive charge; (C) has proper +R donation but less delocalisation; (A) has OCH₃ only −I.\nWorking: in (D) the positive charge is delocalised over C1, C3, C5 and the OCH₃ on C5 gives an extra oxocarbenium form (+R); (C) has the same +R donation but shorter conjugation; in (A) and (B) OCH₃ sits on a carbon that carries no positive charge in any resonance form, so it only withdraws (−I).\nKey idea: +R of OCH₃ helps only when it is on a carbon that bears + in some resonance structure (odd positions of a polyenyl cation).","traps":["Conjugation length alone: Draw resonance forms; check where + appears.","OCH₃ on non-charged carbon: At even positions OCH₃ is only −I."],"topics":["Carbocations","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Choose the correct name for compound given below: [bond-line structure: CH₃–C≡C–CH=C(Br)–CH₃, with Br and the alkynyl group on opposite sides of the C=C]","options":["(4E)-5-Bromo-hex-4-en-2-yne","(2E)-2-Bromo-hex-4-yn-2-ene","(2E)-2-Bromo-hex-2-en-4-yne","(4E)-5-Bromo-hex-2-en-4-yne"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers from the alkyne end (gives yne lower locant – wrong tie-break); (B) writes \"-yn-…-ene\" – wrong suffix order; (D) wrong numbering with \"-en-4-yne\" locants mixed.\nWorking: six-carbon chain with C=C and C≡C; the multiple-bond locants are {2,4} from either end, so the tie is broken in favour of the double bond, numbering from the Br end: C2=C3, C4≡C5, Br on C2 → 2-bromohex-2-en-4-yne. At C2, Br > CH₃; at C3, C≡C–CH₃ > H; Br and the alkynyl group are on opposite sides → E. Answer (C).\nKey idea: On a locant tie, the double bond takes the lower number; name is always \"-en-…-yne\".","traps":["Tie-break for en-yne: Lower number to double bond on a tie.","Suffix order: Always \"-en\" before \"-yne\"."],"topics":["IUPAC naming","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Compound(s) which will liberate carbon dioxide with sodium bicarbonate solution is/are: A = 2,4,6-triaminophenol (phenol with NH₂ at both ortho and the para positions); B = benzoic acid; C = 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid).","options":["B only","C only","B and C only","A and B only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes no phenol reacts with NaHCO₃; (B) forgets benzoic acid; (D) thinks amino groups make phenol more acidic.\nWorking: benzoic acid (pKa ≈ 4.2) and picric acid, 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (pKa ≈ 0.4), are stronger acids than H₂CO₃ and liberate CO₂ from NaHCO₃. A, 2,4,6-triaminophenol, is a very weak acid (electron-donating NH₂) and does not.\nKey idea: Three o/p NO₂ groups make picric acid stronger than carbonic (and even benzoic) acid.","traps":["\"Phenols never react with NaHCO₃\": Strongly deactivated phenols (picric acid) are exceptions.","NH₂ effect reversed: –NH₂ is +R, lowering phenol acidity."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Functional groups","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The number of compound/s given below which contain/s –COOH group is ______. (A) Sulphanilic acid (B) Picric acid (C) Aspirin (D) Ascorbic acid","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (every 'acid' assumed –COOH); 2 (sulphanilic or ascorbic acid counted); 3.\nWorking: sulphanilic acid is p-H₂N–C₆H₄–SO₃H; picric acid is 2,4,6-trinitrophenol; ascorbic acid is an enediol lactone (no free COOH); only aspirin (2-acetoxybenzoic acid) has –COOH. Count = 1.\nKey idea: Acidic character can come from –SO₃H, phenolic OH or an enol, not only –COOH.","traps":["Name read as functional group: Draw each structure.","Ascorbic acid as carboxylic acid: Its acidity comes from an enediol in a lactone ring."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation in organic molecules","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"In CH₂=C=CH–CH₃ molecule (carbons numbered 1 to 4 from the CH₂ end), the hybridization of carbon 1, 2, 3 and 4 respectively are:","options":["sp³, sp, sp³, sp³","sp², sp², sp², sp³","sp², sp, sp², sp³","sp², sp³, sp², sp³"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – every double-bonded C taken as sp²; (D) – central C taken as sp³; (A) – terminal CH₂ taken as sp³.\nWorking: buta-1,2-diene – C1 (one π) sp², C2 (two π, cumulated) sp, C3 sp², C4 sp³.\nKey idea: A carbon with two π bonds is sp regardless of whether they are cumulated or a triple bond.","traps":["Central allene carbon taken as sp²: Count π bonds on each carbon: 2 π → sp."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Among the given species the Resonance stabilised carbocations are:\n(A) C₆H₅–CH₂⁺ (benzyl cation)\n(B) H₂C=CH–CH₂⁺ (allyl cation)\n(C) cyclohex-2-en-1-yl–CH₂⁺: CH₂⁺ attached to the ring carbon next to the C=C (homoallylic cation)\n(D) CH₃–CO–CH₂–CH=CH⁺: positive charge drawn on the terminal carbon of the C=C (vinylic cation)","options":["(C) and (D) only","(A), (B) and (D) only","(A) and (B) only","(A), (B) and (C) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) includes C – the ring C=C looks close but an sp³ carbon intervenes; (B) includes D – the C=C and C=O tempt, but the charge is vinylic; (A) is the reverse selection.\nWorking: benzyl (A) and allyl (B) cations have the empty p orbital conjugated with a π system. In (C) the CH₂⁺ is separated from the ring C=C by an sp³ carbon (homoallylic) – no resonance. In (D) the positive charge sits on a doubly bonded carbon (vinylic cation) and is not conjugated with the C=O, which is insulated by CH₂.\nKey idea: Resonance needs the cationic carbon to be directly attached to a π bond (allylic/benzylic), not one carbon further away or on the π bond itself.","traps":["Homoallylic mistaken for allylic: Count bonds: C⁺ must be adjacent to the C=C carbon.","Vinylic cation with nearby C=O: A charge on the alkene carbon itself is vinylic and unstable."],"topics":["Carbocations","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Which of the following compounds does not exhibit resonance?","options":["CH₃CH₂OCH=CH₂","Benzyl alcohol, C₆H₅CH₂OH","CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂","CH₃CH₂CH=CHCH₂NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) tempting because –CH₂OH is not conjugated, but the benzene ring itself has resonance; (A) enol ether conjugation missed.\nWorking: in (D) the –NH₂ lone pair is separated from C=C by an sp³ CH₂, so no conjugation; (A) O lone pair conjugates with C=C, (B) benzene ring itself shows resonance, (C) amide N lone pair conjugates with C=O.\nKey idea: An sp³ carbon between donor and π bond blocks resonance; any benzene ring always shows resonance.","traps":["Benzyl alcohol chosen: Benzene ring alone exhibits resonance.","Allylic amine looks conjugated: Count bonds: N–CH₂–CH= is not conjugation."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one among the following resonating structures is not correct? [Parent: 1-nitrobuta-1,3-diene, CH₂=CH–CH=CH–NO₂]","options":["CH₂=CH–C̄H–C⁺H–N⁺(=O)O⁻ (negative charge on C-3, positive charge on C-4 next to the nitro N⁺)","CH₂=CH–C⁺H–C̄H–N⁺(=O)O⁻ (positive charge on C-3, negative charge on C-4 next to the nitro N⁺)","⁺CH₂–CH=CH–CH=N⁺(O⁻)–O⁻ (positive charge on C-1, nitro group as the aci-nitronate form)","⁺CH₂–C̄H–CH=CH–N⁺(=O)O⁻ (positive charge on C-1, negative charge on C-2)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) looks odd (charges adjacent) but its negative charge sits next to N⁺ and is acceptable; (D) looks unrelated to NO₂ but is a valid alkene polarisation; (C) is the full conjugation form.\nWorking: the nitro group withdraws electrons, so acceptable charge-separated forms put negative charge towards N (B, C) or are simple alkene polarisations (D); structure (A) puts a positive charge on the carbon directly bonded to the positively charged N, opposite to the –M direction, so it is the \"not correct\" (unacceptable) structure.\nKey idea: With an electron-withdrawing group, electron density moves towards it; a cation next to N⁺ (like charges adjacent) is not an acceptable contributor.","traps":["Like charges on adjacent atoms: Check that no two adjacent atoms carry the same charge.","Option (C) looks over-charged: Count the charges – they add up to zero, so (C) is a valid fully conjugated (aci-nitro type) form."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which among the following is the strongest acid?","options":["CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","toluene (methylbenzene)","cyclopropene","cyclopenta-1,3-diene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) cyclopropenyl anion is antiaromatic (4π) – tempting if ring strain/sp² is taken as acidifying; (B) benzyl anion is resonance-stabilised but less than an aromatic anion; (A) alkane.\nWorking: deprotonating the CH₂ of cyclopentadiene gives the aromatic cyclopentadienyl anion (6π), so its pKa (~16) is far lower than toluene (~41), cyclopropene (anion antiaromatic) or butane (~50).\nKey idea: Most stable conjugate base → strongest acid; aromatic anion wins.","traps":["Aromatic vs antiaromatic anion: Count π electrons in the anion: 6 = aromatic, 4 = antiaromatic."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Carbocations: A = benzyl cation (C₆H₅CH₂⁺), B = vinyl cation (CH₂=CH⁺), C = ethyl cation (CH₃–CH₂⁺), D = ethynyl cation (HC≡C⁺). The correct order of stability of given carbocation is:","options":["A > C > B > D","D > B > C > A","D > B > A > C","C > A > D > B"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) put D first – reversed electronegativity logic (more s-character makes the cation less stable); (D) puts ethyl above benzyl, ignoring resonance.\nWorking: benzyl is resonance-stabilised (most stable); ethyl has hyperconjugation; vinyl (sp) and ethynyl (sp, very electronegative carbon) cations are increasingly unstable. A > C > B > D, option (A).\nKey idea: Positive charge on an sp carbon is very unstable; resonance > hyperconjugation.","traps":["s-character destabilises cations: More s-character holds electrons tighter, worse for a + charge.","Resonance beats hyperconjugation: Benzyl delocalises charge into the ring."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hyperconjugation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Hyperconjugation is a permanent effect.\nStatement II: Hyperconjugation in ethyl cation (CH₃–C⁺H₂) involves the overlapping of C(sp²)–H(1s) bond with empty 2p orbital of other carbon.\nChoose the correct option:","options":["Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true","Statement I is correct but statement II is false","Both Statement I and statement II are true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) for those who skim past the sp² label; (A)/(B) for those who confuse hyperconjugation with the temporary electromeric effect.\nWorking: NCERT calls hyperconjugation a permanent effect (I true); in CH₃–CH₂⁺ the C–H bonds that overlap with the empty p orbital belong to the sp³ CH₃ carbon, so 'C(sp²)–H' makes II false.\nKey idea: The donating C–H belongs to the carbon next to C⁺, which is sp³.","traps":["sp³, not sp², C–H donates: Ask which carbon carries the donating C–H: the CH₃ (sp³).","Permanent vs temporary effect: Only the electromeric effect needs an attacking reagent."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Mesityl oxide is a common name of:","options":["2,4-Dimethyl pentan-3-one","3-Methyl cyclohexane carbaldehyde","2-Methyl cyclohexanone","4-Methyl pent-3-en-2-one"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) diisopropyl ketone; (B), (C) cyclic carbonyls – plausible-sounding distractors.\nWorking: mesityl oxide = (CH₃)₂C=CHCOCH₃ = 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one.\nKey idea: Mesityl oxide is the aldol-condensation product of acetone.","traps":["Name recalled without unsaturation: Mesityl oxide has a C=C (acetone dimer minus water)."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Choose the correct statement regarding the formation of carbocations A and B given :-\nCH₃–CH₂–CH=CH₂ + HBr → CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂⁺ + Br⁻ (\"A\")\n            → CH₃–CH₂–CH⁺–CH₃ + Br⁻ (\"B\")","options":["Carbocation B is more stable and formed relatively at faster rate","Carbocation A is more stable and formed relatively at slow rate","Carbocation B is more stable and formed relatively at slow rate","Carbocation A is more stable and formed relatively at faster rate"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) separates stability from rate (more stable but slower); (B)/(D) call the primary cation more stable.\nWorking: B is a secondary carbocation (more hyperconjugation and +I), so it is more stable; by the Hammond postulate its transition state is lower, so it forms faster – the basis of Markovnikov addition.\nKey idea: More stable carbocation → lower-energy TS → forms faster.","traps":["Stable but slow: For carbocation formation, the more stable ion has the lower activation energy."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C24","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tetravalency & shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The total number of C–C sigma bond/s in mesityl oxide (C₆H₁₀O) is_____. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (counting the C=C as two σ, or counting C=O), 4 (missing the σ in C=C), 7 if C–H/C=O bonds mixed in.\nWorking: mesityl oxide = 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one, (CH₃)₂C=CH–CO–CH₃. C–C σ bonds: two CH₃–C, the σ part of C=C, CH–C(O), C(O)–CH₃ = 5.\nKey idea: Every double bond has exactly one σ; count only C–C links.","traps":["Structure of mesityl oxide unknown: Mesityl oxide = acetone aldol condensation product (CH₃)₂C=CHCOCH₃.","σ component of C=C missed: Each double bond = 1σ + 1π."],"topics":["Tetravalency & shapes","Structural formulae"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation in organic molecules","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In the following molecules, H₃C(a)–CH=C(b)H–O–C(c)₆H₅ [carbon a = CH₃; carbon b = the alkene carbon bonded to O; carbon c = the ring carbon bonded to O]\nHybridisation of carbon a, b and c respectively are :","options":["sp³, sp, sp","sp³, sp², sp","sp³, sp², sp²","sp³, sp, sp²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) put an sp label on b or c, possible if a student miscounts the O-attachment as an extra π bond.\nWorking: a is a CH₃ (four σ bonds, sp³); b is a C=C carbon (sp²); c is an aromatic ring carbon (sp²). The compound is prop-1-enyl phenyl ether.\nKey idea: Count σ bonds + lone pairs; one π bond → sp².","traps":["Ring carbon taken as sp: Aromatic carbons are sp²."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct order of stability for the following alkoxides is :\n(A) CH₃–CH(NO₂)–O⁻\n(B) CH₂=C(NO₂)–O⁻\n(C) O₂N–CH=CH–O⁻","options":["(C) > (B) > (A)","(C) > (A) > (B)","(B) > (C) > (A)","(B) > (A) > (C)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (B) ranks the saturated compound (A) above compound (B), ignoring O⁻/C=C delocalisation; options (C)/(D) think NO₂ directly on the O⁻-bearing carbon is best because –I is strongest at short distance.\nWorking: in (C) the negative charge is delocalised through C=C onto the NO₂ group (extended conjugation, –R); in (B) O⁻ is conjugated with C=C (charge onto CH₂) and NO₂ acts only by –I on the same carbon (cross-conjugated); (A) has only the –I effect.\nKey idea: Delocalisation onto NO₂ through a conjugated path beats any inductive effect.","traps":["Proximity of NO₂ over-valued: Check whether the charge can be delocalised onto NO₂ by resonance.","Enolate resonance in (B) missed: O⁻ next to C=C still delocalises into the alkene."],"topics":["Resonance effect","Inductive effect"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of C–OH bond length : methanol, phenol, p-ethoxyphenol","options":["phenol < methanol < p-ethoxyphenol","phenol < p-ethoxyphenol < methanol","methanol < p-ethoxyphenol < phenol","methanol < phenol < p-ethoxyphenol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts methanol in the middle; (C)/(D) reverse the order (taking methanol as shortest).\nWorking: phenol C–O has partial double-bond character (O lone pair conjugated); a p-OEt (+M) group competes and reduces this, lengthening C–O slightly; methanol has a pure single bond (longest).\nKey idea: More delocalisation of O lone pair into ring ⇒ shorter C–O; +M para group reduces it.","traps":["Ignoring competition from the p-OEt group: A +M substituent reduces ring acceptance of O lone pair."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carbanions","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The increasing order of basicity for the following intermediates is (from weak to strong): (i) (CH₃)₃C⁻ (tert-butyl carbanion); (ii) H₂C=CH–CH₂⁻ (allyl carbanion); (iii) HC≡C⁻; (iv) CH₃⁻; (v) ⁻CN","options":["(v) < (i) < (iv) < (ii) < (iii)","(iii) < (i) < (ii) < (iv) < (v)","(v) < (iii) < (ii) < (iv) < (i)","(iii) < (iv) < (ii) < (i) < (v)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) misplace tert-butyl (thinking +I stabilises the carbanion); (D) makes cyanide the strongest base.\nWorking: weaker base = more stable anion = stronger conjugate acid. HCN (pKa ≈ 9) > HC≡CH (≈ 25, sp carbon) > propene (allylic, resonance ≈ 43) > CH₄ (≈ 48) > isobutane (+I destabilises 3° carbanion ≈ 53).\nKey idea: Alkyl groups destabilise carbanions; s-character and resonance stabilise them; CN⁻ is barely basic.","traps":["Carbocation order applied to carbanions: Carbanion stability is the reverse: 3° least stable.","Cyanide taken as strong base: HCN is a weak acid (pKa ≈ 9), so CN⁻ is weak base."],"topics":["Carbanions","Acid & base strength"]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which of the following has the shortest C–Cl bond?","options":["Cl–CH=CH–OCH₃","Cl–CH=CH–CH₃","Cl–CH=CH₂","Cl–CH=CH–NO₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) OCH₃ looks electron-rich and \"strong\" but competes with Cl for conjugation; (C) unsubstituted reference; (B) CH₃ hyperconjugation also donates.\nWorking: Cl lone pair delocalises into C=C; a –M group (NO₂) at the other end pulls this push–pull conjugation further, raising C–Cl double-bond character; OCH₃ (+M) and CH₃ (+H) compete with Cl and lengthen the bond.\nKey idea: Acceptor at the other end of C=C maximises Cl→C=C→NO₂ delocalisation → shortest C–Cl.","traps":["Electron-withdrawing group thought to weaken C–Cl: Think resonance: acceptor pulls Cl lone pair into conjugation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The IUPAC name for the following compound is: [structure: H₃C–CH(CHO)–CH=CH–CH(CH₃)–COOH, i.e. numbered from the COOH carbon (C1), with a C3=C4 double bond, a CH₃ on C2, and C5 carrying both a CH₃ and a CHO group]","options":["2,5-dimethyl-6-carboxy-hex-3-enal","6-formyl-2-methyl-hex-3-enoic acid","2,5-dimethyl-5-carboxy-hex-3-enal","2,5-dimethyl-6-oxo-hex-3-enoic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) wrongly make the aldehyde the principal group (–COOH outranks –CHO); (B) treats CHO as a formyl branch but puts it on C6 instead of C5.\nWorking: COOH is the principal group (C1); the six-carbon chain may end in CH₃ or in CHO, and taking the CHO carbon as C6 gives 2,5-dimethyl-6-oxohex-3-enoic acid. (B) has the formyl locant wrong (it would be 5-formyl).\nKey idea: Higher-priority COOH sets the suffix; a terminal CHO included in the chain is named as an oxo prefix.","traps":["Aldehyde taken as principal group: Recall seniority order: carboxylic acid > aldehyde.","Formyl vs oxo for a chain-end CHO: If the CHO carbon is counted in the main chain, use oxo; formyl only for a CHO off the chain."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The major product in the following reaction is: 1-methyl-1-vinylcyclopentane (cyclopentane ring carbon bearing CH₃ and CH=CH₂) —H₃O⁺, Heat→","options":["3,3-dimethylcyclohexene (gem-dimethyl next to C=C in a six-membered ring)","1-(1-methylcyclopentyl)ethan-1-ol (ring carbon bearing CH₃ and a CH(OH)CH₃ group)","1,2-dimethylcyclohexene","1-isopropylcyclopentene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) stops at the alcohol from direct hydration (no rearrangement, wrong carbon skeleton); (D) comes from a methyl shift keeping the five-membered ring; (A) misplaces the methyl groups after expansion.\nWorking: protonation of the vinyl group gives a secondary cation next to the quaternary ring carbon; ring expansion (alkyl shift) gives a tertiary 1,2-dimethylcyclohexyl cation with less ring strain; loss of H⁺ gives the tetrasubstituted alkene 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene.\nKey idea: Cation next to a strained ring → ring expansion to a tertiary cyclohexyl cation, then the most substituted alkene.","traps":["No rearrangement considered: Heat with acid favours elimination; check for a better cation.","Methyl shift chosen over ring expansion: Ring expansion also relieves five-ring strain."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Arrange the following labelled hydrogens in decreasing order of acidity (compound shown below):\nHₐ = H of the C≡C–H group; H_b = H of the –COOH group; H_c = H of the O–H para to NO₂; H_d = H of the O–H meta to NO₂.","options":["b > c > d > a","c > b > a > d","b > a > c > d","c > b > d > a"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) put phenol above COOH; (B) also puts alkyne above phenol; (C) ranks the sp C–H above phenols.\nWorking: COOH (b) is the most acidic (carboxylate resonance over two O). Phenolic H(c) is para to NO₂, so its phenoxide is stabilised by –R of NO₂; H(d) is meta to NO₂ (only –I), so c > d. Terminal alkyne H (a, pKa ≈ 25) is least acidic.\nKey idea: COOH > phenol with para-NO₂ (–R) > phenol with meta-NO₂ (–I only) > ≡C–H.","traps":["Para vs meta NO₂ effect on phenol: Trace the ring positions before ranking.","sp C–H thought quite acidic: Terminal alkyne pKa ≈ 25 vs phenol ≈ 7–10."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep02S2C03-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one of the following compounds possesses the most acidic hydrogen?","options":["Malononitrile, N≡C–CH₂–C≡N","Propyne, H₃C–C≡C–H","Acetone, CH₃COCH₃","Trimethyl methanetricarboxylate, HC(COOMe)₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) malononitrile – strong but only two −R groups; (B) sp C–H acidity is weak; (C) one carbonyl.\nWorking: the C–H in HC(COOMe)₃ is flanked by three ester carbonyls; the carbanion is delocalised over three C=O groups (pKa ≈ 7–8), more acidic than malononitrile (≈11), acetone (≈19) and propyne (≈25).\nKey idea: More −R groups on the carbanion carbon → more acidic.","traps":["CN taken as stronger than three esters: Count the delocalising groups.","sp C–H acidity overrated: pKa ≈ 25 is weak."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Carbanions"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The IUPAC name of the following compound (shown below) is :","options":["4-Bromo-2-methylcyclopentane carboxylic acid","5-Bromo-3-methylcyclopentanoic acid","3-Bromo-5-methylcyclopentane carboxylic acid","3-Bromo-5-methylcyclopentanoic acid"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) use 'cyclopentanoic acid' – wrong suffix for a ring acid; (C) uses the higher locant set {3,5}.\nWorking: ring carboxylic acids are named ‘cyclopentane carboxylic acid’ with the COOH carbon as C-1; numbering toward CH₃ gives locants {2,4} vs {3,5} the other way, so 4-bromo-2-methylcyclopentane-1-carboxylic acid.\nKey idea: COOH carbon = C-1, then first-point-of-difference gives 2,4.","traps":["Wrong suffix 'cyclopentanoic acid': COOH carbon is outside the ring → name as cyclopentanecarboxylic acid.","Numbering toward Br: Lowest locant set decides first; alphabet only at a tie."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep04S1C06-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The decreasing order of reactivity of the following organic molecules towards AgNO₃ solution is : (A) 3-chlorocyclopropene; (B) 3-chloro-1-methoxycyclopropene (OMe on a ring double-bond carbon); (C) CH₃CHClCH₃; (D) CH₃CHClCH₂NO₂.","options":["(A) > (B) > (D) > (C)","(A) > (B) > (C) > (D)","(C) > (D) > (A) > (B)","(B) > (A) > (C) > (D)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) put A above B, ignoring OMe donation; (A) also places D above C, ignoring –I of NO₂; (C) treats cyclopropenyl halides as unreactive (ring strain).\nWorking: reactivity with AgNO₃ follows carbocation stability (SN1). A and B ionise to aromatic cyclopropenyl cations; OMe (+R) further stabilises B's cation; C gives a 2° cation; D's 2° cation is destabilised by –I of NO₂. B > A > C > D.\nKey idea: Cyclopropenyl cation (2π) is aromatic; donating OMe adds stabilisation.","traps":["Ring strain thought to block ionisation: Ionisation gives an aromatic 2π cation – very favourable.","OMe effect ignored: OMe on the ring carbon donates by +R into the cation."],"topics":["Carbocations","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocations","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following compounds will form the precipitate with aq. AgNO₃ solution most readily ?","options":["CH₃CH₂–O–CH₂Br (bromomethyl ethyl ether)","N-(bromomethyl)piperidine (piperidine ring with N–CH₂Br)","PhN(CH₂CH₃)(CH₂Br) (N-bromomethyl-N-ethylaniline)","4-CH₃O–C₆H₄–N(CH₂CH₃)(CH₂CH₂Br)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) oxocarbenium is stable but O is less donating than N; (C) aniline N lone pair partly delocalised into ring; (D) β-bromo – no resonance stabilisation of the cation (only remote).\nWorking: rate depends on ease of forming the cation (SN1). N-CH₂⁺ from the aliphatic amine gives a very stable iminium ion (N more electron-donating than O); in (C) the N lone pair is delocalised into the ring; (D) bromine is β to N, no direct resonance stabilisation.\nKey idea: Most readily ionising halide = most stable cation: R₂N=CH₂⁺ from a non-conjugated amine.","traps":["Oxygen thought more stabilising than nitrogen: N is the better π donor (less electronegative).","Aryl amine lone pair availability: N lone pair in aniline is shared with ring; (D) has Br on β carbon."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance effect","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Among the following compounds, which one has the shortest C—Cl bond ?","options":["H₃C–Cl","(CH₃)₃C–Cl","CH₂=CH–Cl","CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) allyl chloride – Cl on sp³ C, no resonance; (B) tertiary C–Cl longest (steric/inductive); (A) normal sp³ C–Cl.\nWorking: in vinyl chloride the Cl lone pair is in resonance with C=C, giving C–Cl partial double-bond character; the carbon is also sp² hybridised. Others are sp³ C–Cl.\nKey idea: Lone pair conjugation + sp² carbon → shortest C–X.","traps":["Allyl taken as vinyl: Check if Cl is on the sp² carbon."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The increasing order of the acidity of the α-hydrogen of the following compounds is :\n(A) CH₃COCH₃ (acetone) (B) PhCOCH₂COPh (dibenzoylmethane) (C) CH₃COOMe (methyl acetate) (D) CH₃CONMe₂ (N,N-dimethylacetamide)","options":["(C) < (A) < (B) < (D)","(B) < (C) < (A) < (D)","(A) < (C) < (D) < (B)","(D) < (C) < (A) < (B)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts amide most acidic (treats N as withdrawing); (B) puts the diketone least acidic; (C) places amide above ketone and ester.\nWorking: the 1,3-diketone CH₂ is flanked by two C=O (pKa ~9); ketone (~19-20) > ester (~25) > amide (~30), since N and O lone pairs donate into C=O and reduce enolate stabilisation (N more than O).\nKey idea: More donation from the heteroatom into C=O means a less stabilised enolate: amide < ester < ketone < β-diketone.","traps":["N and O treated only as electron-withdrawing: Resonance donation (+R) into C=O dominates for esters and amides.","Doubly activated CH₂ overlooked: Two flanking C=O groups give the most stable enolate."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The increasing order of basicity of the following compounds is\n(A) pyridine (B) pyrrole (N–H) (C) pyrrolidine (N–H) (D) imidazole (N1–H)","options":["(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)","(D) < (A) < (B) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) put imidazole above pyrrolidine; (C) makes pyrrole more basic than pyridine and imidazole least basic.\nWorking: pyrrole lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet (least basic); pyridine sp² N (pKaH ≈ 5.2); imidazole N3 (pKaH ≈ 7, cation resonance-stabilised by the second N); pyrrolidine sp³ N (pKaH ≈ 11.3) most basic.\nKey idea: Lone pair in aromatic sextet → non-basic; protonated imidazole is resonance-stabilised.","traps":["Imidazole judged less basic than pyridine: Imidazolium cation is symmetric and resonance-stabilised.","Pyrrole N treated like an amine N: Pyrrole lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Aromaticity"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The IUPAC name of the following compound is: [benzene ring bearing CHO at C1, NO₂ at C2, CH₂OH at C4 and NH₂ at C5; i.e. CH₂OH and NH₂ ortho to each other, NO₂ ortho to CHO and para to NH₂]","options":["3-amino-4-hydroxymethyl-5-nitrobenzaldehyde","2-nitro-4-hydroxymethyl-5-aminobenzaldehyde","4-amino-2-formyl-5-hydroxymethylnitrobenzene","5-amino-4-hydroxymethyl-2-nitrobenzaldehyde"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers the wrong way and misplaces groups (NH₂/NO₂ meta, not the drawn compound); (B) correct locants but prefixes not in alphabetical order; (C) uses nitrobenzene as parent and \"formyl\" prefix, ignoring CHO priority.\nWorking: CHO is the principal group (C1); numbering towards NO₂ gives locants {2,4,5} versus {3,4,6} the other way; prefixes in alphabetical order: amino, hydroxymethyl, nitro.\nKey idea: Aldehyde outranks –OH, –NH₂ and –NO₂, so the parent is benzaldehyde; then lowest locants, then alphabetical citation.","traps":["Prefixes cited in locant order: Cite prefixes alphabetically: amino < hydroxymethyl < nitro.","Nitrobenzene taken as parent: NO₂ is only a prefix; CHO is the principal group."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The IUPAC name of the following compound is : H₃C–CH(CH₃)–CH(OH)–CH₂–COOH","options":["2-Methyl-3-hydroxypentan-5-oic acid","4,4-Dimethyl-3-hydroxybutanoic acid","3-Hydroxy-4-methylpentanoic acid","4-Methyl-3-hydroxypentanoic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers from wrong end; (B) wrong chain; (D) non-alphabetical prefix order.\nWorking: C-1 = COOH; 5-carbon chain; OH on C-3, CH₃ on C-4; alphabetical: 3-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoic acid.\nKey idea: COOH is C-1; hydroxy before methyl alphabetically.","traps":["Prefixes not alphabetised: h before m."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The correct IUPAC name of the following compound is : benzene ring with NO₂ (top), Cl (meta to NO₂) and CH₃ (para to NO₂, ortho to Cl).","options":["5-chloro-4-methyl-1-nitrobenzene","2-methyl-5-nitro-1-chlorobenzene","3-chloro-4-methyl-1-nitrobenzene","2-chloro-1-methyl-4-nitrobenzene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) put NO₂ at 1 (locant sets {1,4,5} and {1,3,4}); (B) uses {1,2,5} and non-alphabetical order.\nWorking: lowest locant set is {1,2,4} (CH₃ = 1, Cl = 2, NO₂ = 4); prefixes cited alphabetically: 2-chloro-1-methyl-4-nitrobenzene.\nKey idea: No principal group here (nitro, chloro, methyl are all prefixes), so choose lowest locant set {1,2,4}.","traps":["Nitro given locant 1 as if principal group: Nitro is only a prefix; apply lowest locant rule to all.","Locants not compared as a set: Compare locant sets term by term."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Electrophiles & nucleophiles","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The increasing order of nucleophilicity of the following nucleophiles is: (a) CH₃CO₂⁻ (b) H₂O (c) CH₃SO₃⁻ (d) OH⁻","options":["(b) < (c) < (a) < (d)","(a) < (d) < (c) < (b)","(d) < (a) < (c) < (b)","(b) < (c) < (d) < (a)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts acetate above hydroxide (reverse); (C) is the decreasing order written as increasing; (B) puts acetate weakest and H₂O strongest.\nWorking: neutral H₂O is weakest; among anions with O as donor, more delocalised charge = weaker nucleophile: CH₃SO₃⁻ (3 O share charge, conjugate base of strong acid) < CH₃CO₂⁻ < OH⁻.\nKey idea: Same donor atom: nucleophilicity follows basicity, i.e. inversely with acid strength of conjugate acid (CH₃SO₃H > CH₃COOH > H₂O).","traps":["Increasing vs decreasing order: Read 'increasing' and start with the weakest.","Acetate vs hydroxide: Resonance delocalisation lowers nucleophilicity."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The IUPAC name of the following compound is : [structure: H₃C–CH₂–CH₂–CH< whose CH carries CH(CH₃)–C≡CH (up) and CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂ (down); i.e. HC≡C–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₂CH₂CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂]","options":["3,5-dimethyl-4-propylhept-6-en-1-yne","3-methyl-4-(3-methylprop-1-enyl)-1-heptyne","3-methyl-4-(1-methylprop-2-ynyl)-1-heptene","3,5-dimethyl-4-propylhept-1-en-6-yne"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) numbers from the yne end (hept-6-en-1-yne); (B)/(C) choose a chain that excludes one multiple bond.\nWorking: parent is the 7-C chain containing both C=C and C≡C; locants 1 and 6 either way, so the tie goes to the double bond (hept-1-en-6-yne); substituents 3,5-dimethyl-4-propyl.\nKey idea: When ene and yne would get the same lowest locants, give the double bond the lower number.","traps":["Yne given priority over ene in numbering: Tie at 1,6: double bond takes 1.","Parent chain missing a multiple bond: Parent must contain both multiple bonds."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct decreasing order for acid strength is :-","options":["NO₂CH₂COOH > NCCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH","FCH₂COOH > NCCH₂COOH > NO₂CH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH","NO₂CH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH > NCCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH","NCCH₂COOH > O₂NCH₂COOH > FCH₂COOH > ClCH₂COOH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): F put first by electronegativity. (C): F placed above CN. (D): CN placed above NO₂.\nWorking: acidity of substituted acetic acids follows the -I power of the α-substituent, NO₂ > CN > F > Cl, giving option (A).\nKey idea: Group -I strength (NO₂ > CN > F > Cl), not atom electronegativity, decides the order.","traps":["F assumed strongest EWG: Use NCERT order of -I strength: F < CN < NO₂.","CN vs NO₂ order swapped: Recall NO₂ > CN in the NCERT substituent list."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Inductive effect"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Which amongst the following is the strongest acid ?","options":["CHI₃","CHCl₃","CHBr₃","CH(CN)₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): heavier haloforms (d-orbital / polarisability arguments). (B): CHCl₃ by electronegativity.\nWorking: C(CN)₃⁻ is stabilised by delocalisation of the negative charge onto three nitrile N atoms (plus -I); trihalomethyl anions are only inductively stabilised.\nKey idea: Resonance-stabilised conjugate base wins.","traps":["Haloform with most electronegative halogen picked: CN stabilises the carbanion by resonance as well as -I."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Resonance effect"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"IUPAC naming","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"What is the IUPAC name of the following compound ? [CH₃–CH=C(CH₃)–CH(Br)–CH₃ drawn with H on C-2, CH₃ on C-3 and Br on C-4]","options":["3-Bromo-1,2-dimethylbut-1-ene","4-Bromo-3-methylpent-2-ene","2-Bromo-3-methylpent-3-ene","3-Bromo-3-methyl-1,2-dimethylprop-1-ene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D): chain chosen too short with methyls named as substituents at C-1 (not allowed). (C) numbers from the Br end: gives lower locant to Br, but C=C must get the lowest locant.\nWorking: longest chain containing C=C is 5 carbons; double bond gets lowest locant (B), so Br at 4 and CH₃ at 3.\nKey idea: Lowest locant goes to the double bond (pent-2-ene), not to Br.","traps":["Numbering from the halogen end: C=C (suffix -ene) gets lowest locant before prefixes.","Methyl at chain end counted as substituent: Terminal CH₃ groups extend the parent chain."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Acid & base strength","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"In the following compound, the favourable site/s for protonation is/are :- Compound: adenine (6-aminopurine) with sites labelled (a) exocyclic NH₂, (b) ring N1 (pyrimidine N next to C–NH₂), (c) ring N3 (pyrimidine N), (d) imidazole N7 (=N–), (e) imidazole N9–H.","options":["(b), (c) and (d)","(a)","(a) and (e)","(a) and (d)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) include (a): NH₂ looks like an amine but its lone pair is conjugated with the ring. (C) includes (e): pyrrole-type N–H lone pair is part of aromaticity; protonating it destroys aromaticity.\nWorking: protonation needs a localised lone pair. The pyridine-type ring nitrogens (b, c, d) have sp² lone pairs in the ring plane, not part of the aromatic π system. The NH₂ lone pair (a) is delocalised into the ring (amidine-like), and the N–H nitrogen (e) uses its lone pair in the aromatic sextet.\nKey idea: Basic site = lone pair in an sp² orbital perpendicular to the π system (pyridine-type N).","traps":["NH₂ assumed most basic: Check resonance: aryl/heteroaryl NH₂ lone pair is delocalised, as in aniline.","Pyrrole-type N protonated: If the lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet, protonation destroys aromaticity."],"topics":["Acid & base strength","Aromaticity"]}],"mx-ss":[{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"A compound is formed by two elements X and Y. The element Y forms cubic close packed arrangement and those of element X occupy one third of the tetrahedral voids. What is the formula of the compound?","options":["XY₃","X₃Y₂","X₃Y","X₂Y₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) XY₃: uses N tetrahedral voids (confusing with octahedral). (B) X₃Y₂: ratio inverted.\nWorking: N atoms of Y give 2N tetrahedral voids; X = 2N/3 → X : Y = 2 : 3 → X₂Y₃.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral voids = 2 × spheres.","traps":["Tetrahedral void count taken as N: Use 2N for tetrahedral voids."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Number of crystal systems from the following where body centred unit cell can be found, is ________.\nCubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, rhombohedral, monoclinic, triclinic","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 if monoclinic is counted (it has end-centred, not body-centred); 2 if orthorhombic is missed.\nWorking: body-centred Bravais lattices exist only for cubic, tetragonal and orthorhombic = 3.\nKey idea: Body-centred: cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic only; monoclinic has end-centred.","traps":["Monoclinic end-centred taken as body-centred: Monoclinic variations: primitive and end-centred."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":81,"q":"A metal M crystallizes into two lattices :- face centred cubic (fcc) and body centred cubic (bcc) with unit cell edge length of 2.0 and 2.5 Å respectively. The ratio of densities of lattices fcc to bcc for the metal M is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (forgetting a³, or using Z ratio only), 3 (rounding 2.5³ wrongly), 1 (using a not a³ with Z swapped).\nWorking: fcc 4/8 = 0.5; bcc 2/15.625 = 0.128; ratio = 3.906 ≈ 4\nKey idea: Ratio = (4/2)(2.5/2.0)³ = 2 × 1.953 = 3.9.","traps":["Z values swapped or ignored: fcc z = 4, bcc z = 2."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"A cubic solid is made up of two elements X and Y. Atoms of X are present on every alternate corner and one at the center of cube. Y is at 1/3rd of the total faces. The empirical formula of the compound is :","options":["X₂Y₁.₅","X₃Y₂","XY₂.₅","X₂.₅Y"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) X₂Y₁.₅ comes from counting all 8 corners + body centre for X and 3 faces (half of 6) for Y. (C) XY₂.₅ and (D) X₂.₅Y come from misweighting face or corner shares.\nWorking: X: 4 × 1/8 + 1 = 1.5; Y: 2 faces × 1/2 = 1 → X₁.₅Y₁ = X₃Y₂.\nKey idea: Alternate corners = 4 corners; 1/3 of 6 faces = 2 faces.","traps":["'Alternate corners' read as all corners: Alternate corners of a cube = 4 corners → 1/2 atom.","Formula left with decimals: Multiply to the smallest whole-number ratio: X₃Y₂."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"A metal M forms hexagonal close-packed structure. The total number of voids in 0.02 mol of it is ________ × 10²¹ (Nearest integer).\n(Given N_A = 6.02 × 10²³)","type":"num","answer":"36","range":[36.0,36.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 24 (tetrahedral only); 12 (octahedral only); 4 (forgetting the ×10²¹ scaling).\nWorking: atoms = 0.02 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.204 × 10²²; total voids = N octahedral + 2N tetrahedral = 3N = 3.612 × 10²² = 36.12 × 10²¹ → 36.\nKey idea: Total voids = 3 × number of atoms in any close packing.","traps":["Total voids = 3N: Add N octahedral + 2N tetrahedral.","Scaling to 10²¹: Express 3.612 × 10²² as 36.12 × 10²¹."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Iron oxide FeO, crystallises in a cubic lattice with a unit cell edge length of 5.0 Å. If density of the FeO in the crystal is 4.0 g cm⁻³, then the number of FeO units present per unit cell is ____. (Nearest integer). Given: Molar mass of Fe and O is 56 and 16 g mol⁻¹ respectively. N_A = 6.0 × 10²³ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0 or 4×10⁻³ (Å not converted), 8 (using M = 36), 3.\nWorking: Z = ρa³N_A/M = 4.0 × 1.25×10⁻²² × 6.0×10²³ / 72 = 4.17 ≈ 4.\nKey idea: a³ = (5×10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 1.25×10⁻²² cm³.","traps":["Å to cm: Convert a before cubing."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrical & magnetic properties","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I : O₂, Cu²⁺ and Fe³⁺ are weakly attracted by magnetic field and are magnetized in the same direction as magnetic field.\nStatement II : NaCl and H₂O are weakly magnetized in opposite direction to magnetic field.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): student forgets O₂ is paramagnetic (Lewis structure shows all pairs); (C): student thinks diamagnetic substances are unaffected rather than weakly repelled.\nWorking: O₂ (two unpaired π* electrons), Cu²⁺ (3d⁹) and Fe³⁺ (3d⁵) are paramagnetic — weakly attracted and magnetised along the field; NaCl and H₂O have all electrons paired — diamagnetic, weakly magnetised opposite to the field.\nKey idea: Unpaired electrons → paramagnetic (along the field); all paired → diamagnetic (against the field).","traps":["O₂ assumed diamagnetic: Use MO filling: two unpaired electrons in π* orbitals.","Diamagnetic = not affected by field: Diamagnetic substances are weakly repelled, magnetised opposite to field."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"An element M crystallises in a body centred cubic unit cell with a cell edge of 300 pm. The density of the element is 6.0 g cm⁻³. The number of atoms present in 180 g of the element is ______ × 10²³. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 11 if z = 1 is used; 44 if z = 4 (fcc); errors of 10³ from pm → cm conversion.\nWorking: a³ = (3 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ = 2.7 × 10⁻²³ cm³; volume of 180 g = 30 cm³ → 30/2.7 × 10⁻²³ = 1.111 × 10²⁴ cells × 2 atoms = 2.22 × 10²⁴ = 22.2 × 10²³ → 22.\nKey idea: Atoms = (mass/density) ÷ a³ × z, no Avogadro number needed.","traps":["z for bcc: bcc: 8 × 1/8 + 1 = 2.","pm to cm: Convert first: 300 pm = 3 × 10⁻⁸ cm."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Metal M crystallizes into a FCC lattice with the edge length of 4.0 × 10⁻⁸ cm. The atomic mass of the metal is ____ g/mol. (Nearest integer) (Use: N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, density of metal M = 9.03 g cm⁻³)","type":"num","answer":"87","range":[87.0,87.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 22 (Z = 1), 43 (Z = 2), 348 (Z omitted the wrong way).\nWorking: M = ρa³N_A/Z = 9.03 × (4.0 × 10⁻⁸)³ × 6.02 × 10²³ / 4 = 86.98 ≈ 87 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Z = 4 for fcc; a³ = 6.4 × 10⁻²³ cm³.","traps":["Z for fcc: fcc has 4 atoms per cell."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Atoms of element X form hcp lattice and those of element Y occupy 2/3 of its tetrahedral voids. The percentage of element X in the lattice is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"43","range":[43.0,43.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 60 (using N octahedral voids → X₃Y₂), 75 (using N voids and 1/3), 57 (giving % of Y).\nWorking: per N atoms of X there are 2N tetrahedral voids; Y = (2/3)(2N) = 4N/3. Formula X₃Y₄; % X = 3/7 × 100 = 42.9 ≈ 43.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral voids = 2 × atoms; X₃Y₄ → 3/7.","traps":["Tetrahedral voids taken as N: Tetrahedral voids are twice the number of atoms.","Percentage of the wrong element: X fraction = 3/(3+4)."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The distance between Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions in solid NaCl of density 43.1 g cm⁻³ is ____ × 10⁻¹⁰ m. (Nearest Integer) (Given: N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 – reports edge length a instead of a/2; wrong Z (1) gives a ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nWorking: NaCl is fcc with Z = 4. a³ = ZM/(ρN_A) = 4 × 58.5/(43.1 × 6.02 × 10²³) = 9.02 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³, a = 2.08 × 10⁻⁸ cm = 2.08 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. Na⁺–Cl⁻ distance = a/2 = 1.04 × 10⁻¹⁰ m → 1. (The density 43.1 g cm⁻³ is unrealistic – real NaCl is 2.17 – but chosen to give a clean answer.)\nKey idea: Nearest Na⁺–Cl⁻ distance is half the edge in rock salt.","traps":["Edge length reported instead of a/2: In rock salt, cation–anion distance = a/2.","Wrong Z for NaCl: fcc NaCl has 4 formula units per cell."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"In a solid AB, A atoms are in ccp arrangement and B atoms occupy all the octahedral sites. If two atoms from the opposite faces are removed, then the resultant stoichiometry of the compound is AₓB_y. The value of x is ______. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (removing 2 whole atoms instead of 2 halves); 4 (ignoring removal); 7 (reporting x + y).\nWorking: per fcc cell A = 4 (8 × 1/8 + 6 × 1/2), B = 4 (12 × 1/4 + 1). The face-centre atoms are A; removing two opposite face centres removes 2 × 1/2 = 1 A, giving A₃B₄, x = 3.\nKey idea: Face-centre atoms count 1/2 each; two removed = 1 atom.","traps":["Face atom counted as whole: Each face-centre atom is shared by 2 cells, so two contribute 1.","Which atoms sit on faces: In ccp A, face centres are A; octahedral B are at edge centres and body centre."],"topics":["Unit cells","Voids"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Defects","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Metal deficiency defect is shown by Fe₀.₉₃O. In the crystal, some Fe²⁺ cations are missing and loss of positive charge is compensated by the presence of Fe³⁺ ions. The percentage of Fe²⁺ ions in the Fe₀.₉₃O crystals is ______. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"85","range":[85.0,85.0],"explain":"Common slips: 79 – reports moles of Fe²⁺ per formula (0.79) instead of percentage of iron ions; 15 or 16 – gives the Fe³⁺ percentage; 93 – uses total Fe.\nWorking: let Fe²⁺ = x, Fe³⁺ = 0.93 − x; charge 2x + 3(0.93 − x) = 2 → x = 0.79. % Fe²⁺ = 0.79/0.93 × 100 = 84.9 ≈ 85.\nKey idea: Total positive charge must still be +2 per O: 2x + 3y = 2 with x + y = 0.93.","traps":["Wrong denominator: Divide by total Fe (0.93), not by 1.","Fe³⁺ fraction reported: Read which ion is asked."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Defects","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The incorrect statement about the imperfections in solids is :","options":["Schottky defect decreases the density of the substance.","Interstitial defect increases the density of the substance.","Frenkel defect does not alter the density of the substance.","Vacancy defect increases the density of the substance."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) are true old-NCERT statements; a student who confuses Frenkel (no change) with Schottky (decrease) may doubt (A) or (C).\nWorking: a vacancy removes mass without changing volume, so density falls; (D) is the false statement. Schottky lowers density, interstitial raises it, Frenkel leaves it unchanged.\nKey idea: Missing mass at the same volume lowers density; extra atoms in voids raise it; a displaced ion keeps mass and volume.","traps":["Vacancy vs interstitial density effect: Ask whether mass is lost or added at constant volume.","Frenkel vs Schottky: Frenkel = ion moves to interstitial site, nothing leaves."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Defects","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: Frenkel defects are vacancy as well as interstitial defects. Statement II: Frenkel defect leads to colour in ionic solids due to presence of F-centres. Choose the most appropriate answer for the statements from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) reject the correct Frenkel description; (B) accepts F-centres from Frenkel defects.\nWorking: a Frenkel defect creates a vacancy and an interstitial ion (I true). F-centres (electrons in anion vacancies) arise from metal-excess defects, not Frenkel defects (II false).\nKey idea: Colour (F-centres) → metal-excess defect; Frenkel → vacancy + interstitial, no colour.","traps":["F-centres come from metal-excess defects: F-centres = anion vacancies occupied by electrons (metal excess), e.g. NaCl heated in Na vapour.","Frenkel = dislocation: The ion leaves a vacancy and occupies an interstitial site."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrical & magnetic properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match items of List-I (Property) with those of List-II (Example): (a) Diamagnetism, (b) Ferrimagnetism, (c) Paramagnetism, (d) Antiferromagnetism; (i) MnO, (ii) O₂, (iii) NaCl, (iv) Fe₃O₄. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)–(ii), (b)–(i), (c)–(iii), (d)–(iv)","(a)–(i), (b)–(iii), (c)–(iv), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(iii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(ii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(ii), (c)–(i), (d)–(iii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) gives Fe₃O₄ diamagnetic and MnO paramagnetic; (A) swaps MnO and Fe₃O₄ between ferri- and antiferromagnetism (and O₂ and NaCl between dia- and paramagnetism); (B) makes MnO diamagnetic and NaCl ferrimagnetic.\nWorking: NaCl has no unpaired electrons (diamagnetic); Fe₃O₄ has unequal antiparallel moments (ferrimagnetic); O₂ has two unpaired electrons (paramagnetic); MnO has equal antiparallel moments (antiferromagnetic).\nKey idea: Fe₃O₄ (ferrite) = ferrimagnetic; MnO = antiferromagnetic.","traps":["MnO vs Fe₃O₄: Equal opposed moments (MnO) cancel; unequal (Fe₃O₄) leave net moment."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The empirical formula for a compound with a cubic close packed arrangement of anions and with cations occupying all the octahedral sites in AₓB. The value of x is _________ (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 2 (using tetrahedral voids).\nWorking: in ccp the number of octahedral voids equals the number of anions; all filled → cation : anion = 1 : 1 → AB, x = 1.\nKey idea: N octahedral voids per N packed ions.","traps":["Octahedral vs tetrahedral void count: Octahedral = N, tetrahedral = 2N."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The coordination number of an atom in a body-centered cubic structure is ______. [Assume that the lattice is made up of atoms.]","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 12 (ccp/hcp); 6 (simple cubic); 2 (atoms per bcc cell).\nWorking: in bcc each atom touches 8 nearest neighbours at the cube corners/centre.\nKey idea: bcc → CN 8.","traps":["Atoms per cell vs CN: CN = nearest neighbours, not Z."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The unit cell of copper corresponds to a face centered cube of edge length 3.596 Å with one copper atom at each lattice point. The calculated density of copper in kg/m³ is ______. [Molar mass of Cu: 63.54 g; Avogadro Number = 6.022 × 10²³]","type":"num","answer":"9076","range":[9076.0,9076.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 9.076 (g cm⁻³ given instead of kg m⁻³); 2269 (Z = 1); 4538 (Z = 2, bcc).\nWorking: ρ = Z·M/(N_A·a³) = 4 × 0.06354 kg mol⁻¹ / (6.022×10²³ × (3.596×10⁻¹⁰ m)³) = 0.25416/(2.8004×10⁻⁵) = 9076 kg m⁻³.\nKey idea: fcc → Z = 4; convert M to kg mol⁻¹ and a to m.","traps":["Wrong Z for fcc: fcc: 8×1/8 + 6×1/2 = 4.","Unit mismatch: Answer requested in kg m⁻³."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of octahedral voids per lattice site in a lattice is _____. (Rounded off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 – tetrahedral count; 4 – voids per fcc unit cell; 13 – octahedral sites in fcc cell counted as 12 edge + 1 body centre.\nWorking: in a close-packed lattice of N spheres there are N octahedral voids (and 2N tetrahedral), so 1 per lattice site.\nKey idea: N spheres → N octahedral, 2N tetrahedral voids.","traps":["Tetrahedral count used: Octahedral = N, tetrahedral = 2N.","Per unit cell instead of per site: Per lattice site means per sphere."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Diamond has a three dimensional structure of C atoms formed by covalent bonds. The structure of diamond has face centred cubic lattice where 50% of the tetrahedral voids are also occupied by carbon atoms. The number of carbon atoms present per unit cell of diamond is _______.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 4 (fcc lattice points only), 12 (all 8 tetrahedral voids filled), 6 (4 lattice atoms + half of the 4 octahedral voids).\nWorking: fcc gives 4 atoms per cell and 8 tetrahedral voids; half filled adds 4 → 8.\nKey idea: fcc: 4 atoms, 4 octahedral, 8 tetrahedral voids per cell.","traps":["Octahedral and tetrahedral void counts swapped: Tetrahedral voids = 2 × number of atoms."],"topics":["Voids","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"A copper complex crystallising in a CCP lattice with a cell edge of 0.4518 nm has been revealed by employing X-ray diffraction studies. The density of a copper complex is found to be 7.62 g cm⁻³. The molar mass of copper complex is ______ g mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) [Given : N_A = 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"106","range":[106.0,106.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 26 or 423 (Z taken as 1 or omitted), 53 (Z = 2, bcc), errors of 10³ from nm→cm.\nWorking: Z = 4 (ccp); M = ρa³N_A/Z = 7.62 × (4.518 × 10⁻⁸ cm)³ × 6.022 × 10²³ / 4 = 7.62 × 9.222 × 10⁻²³ × 6.022 × 10²³ / 4 = 105.8 → 106.\nKey idea: ccp ⇒ Z = 4; 1 nm = 10⁻⁷ cm.","traps":["Z for ccp: ccp = fcc → Z = 4.","nm to cm: 0.4518 nm = 4.518 × 10⁻⁸ cm."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The parameters of the unit cell of a substance are a = 2.5, b = 3.0, c = 4.0, α = 90°, β = 120°, γ = 90°. The crystal system of the substance is:","options":["Hexagonal","Orthorhombic","Monoclinic","Triclinic"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) hexagonal – lured by 120°, but needs a = b and γ = 120°; (B) orthorhombic – needs all angles 90°; (D) triclinic – needs all three angles ≠ 90°.\nWorking: a ≠ b ≠ c with two angles 90° and one (β) ≠ 90° defines the monoclinic system, option (C).\nKey idea: Count unequal edges and non-90° angles; one odd angle with three unequal edges = monoclinic.","traps":["120° is not automatically hexagonal: Hexagonal needs a = b ≠ c and γ = 120°."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Types of solids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Select the correct statements.\n(A) Crystalline solids have long range order.\n(B) Crystalline solids are isotropic.\n(C) Amorphous solid are sometimes called pseudo solids.\n(D) Amorphous solids soften over a range of temperatures.\n(E) Amorphous solids have a definite heat of fusion.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["(A), (B), (E) only","(B), (D) only","(C), (D) only","(A), (C), (D) only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (A) includes the false statements (B) and (E); option (B) includes the false statement (B) and omits (A) and (C); option (C) omits the true statement (A).\nWorking: A, C, D true; crystalline solids are anisotropic (B false) and amorphous solids have no definite heat of fusion (E false).\nKey idea: Crystalline: anisotropic, sharp m.p., definite ΔfusH.","traps":["Anisotropy belongs to crystals: Ordered = direction-dependent."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"A certain element crystallises in a bcc lattice of unit cell edge length 27 Å. If the same element under the same conditions crystallises in the fcc lattice, the edge length of the unit cell in Å will be ________. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Assume each lattice point has a single atom] [Assume √3 = 1.73, √2 = 1.41]","type":"num","answer":"33","range":[33.0,33.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 27 (no change), 22 (inverting the ratio), 38 (fcc as a = 4r/√2 with r misread).\nWorking: bcc r = √3a/4 = 1.73 × 27/4 = 11.68 Å; fcc a = 2√2 r = 2 × 1.41 × 11.68 = 32.9 ≈ 33 Å.\nKey idea: Keep r fixed: a_fcc = 2√2 × (√3 a_bcc/4) = (√6/2) a_bcc ≈ 1.22 a_bcc.","traps":["bcc and fcc formulas swapped: bcc: body diagonal √3a = 4r; fcc: face diagonal √2a = 4r."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Ga (atomic mass 70 u) crystallizes in a hexagonal close packed structure. The total number of voids in 0.581 g of Ga is ______ × 10²¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (atoms only), 10 (tetrahedral only), 2.5 or 7.5 (using 6 atoms per hcp cell wrongly).\nWorking: n = 0.581/70 = 8.30 × 10⁻³ mol → 5.00 × 10²¹ atoms; hcp has N octahedral + 2N tetrahedral voids = 3N = 15 × 10²¹.\nKey idea: Total voids = 3 × number of atoms (any close packing).","traps":["Only one kind of void counted: Add N (octahedral) + 2N (tetrahedral)."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Defects","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"KBr is doped with 10⁻⁵ mole percent of SrBr₂. The number of cationic vacancies in 1 g of KBr crystal is _____ × 10¹⁴. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n[Atomic Mass : K : 39.1 u, Br : 79.9 u, N_A = 6.023 × 10²³]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 506 (using 10⁻⁵ as a fraction, not a percent), 10 (two vacancies per Sr²⁺), 3 (using molar mass of SrBr₂).\nWorking: each Sr²⁺ replaces two K⁺, creating one cation vacancy. Moles of KBr in 1 g = 1/119.0 = 8.40 × 10⁻³; 10⁻⁵ mol % = 10⁻⁷ mol SrBr₂ per mol; vacancies = 8.40 × 10⁻³ × 10⁻⁷ × 6.023 × 10²³ = 5.06 × 10¹⁴ → 5.\nKey idea: One Sr²⁺ → one cation vacancy; mol % ÷ 100 = mole fraction.","traps":["Percent read as fraction: Divide percent by 100: 10⁻⁷.","Vacancies per dopant ion: Two K⁺ removed, one site filled by Sr²⁺ → one vacancy."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In a binary compound, atoms of element A form a hcp structure and those of element M occupy 2/3 of the tetrahedral voids of the hcp structure. The formula of the binary compound is:","options":["M₂A₃","M₄A₃","M₄A","MA₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) M₂A₃ – takes voids = atoms (octahedral); (D) MA₃ – inverts the ratio; (C) M₄A – arithmetic slip.\nWorking: tetrahedral voids = 2 per A atom; M = ⅔ × 2 = 4/3 per A → M : A = 4 : 3 → M₄A₃.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral voids = 2N, octahedral = N.","traps":["Tetrahedral vs octahedral void count: Tetrahedral = 2 per atom."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Types of solids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A hard substance melts at high temperature and is an insulator in both solid and in molten state. This solid is most likely to be a / an :","options":["Ionic solid","Molecular solid","Metallic solid","Covalent solid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ionic fits “hard, high m.p., insulator as solid” but conducts when molten; (B) molecular solids are soft and low-melting; (C) metals conduct.\nWorking: covalent network solids (diamond, SiO₂) are hard, high-melting and non-conducting even when molten; ionic solids conduct when molten, molecular solids are soft/low-melting, metallic solids conduct.\nKey idea: “Insulator even when molten” rules out ionic.","traps":["Ionic chosen: Check the molten-state clue: ionic melts conduct."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Defects","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Which of the following compounds is likely to show both Frenkel and Schottky defects in its crystalline form?","options":["AgBr","ZnS","KBr","CsCl"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ZnS is the textbook Frenkel example; (C) KBr and (D) CsCl are Schottky examples.\nWorking: AgBr shows both Frenkel (small Ag⁺ to interstitial sites) and Schottky defects; ZnS shows Frenkel only; KBr and CsCl show Schottky.\nKey idea: AgBr is the one example NCERT gives for both defects.","traps":["ZnS chosen as Frenkel example: Question needs both defects; only AgBr."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Types of solids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"'X' melts at low temperature and is a bad conductor of electricity in both liquid and solid state. X is:","options":["Carbon tetrachloride","Mercury","Silicon carbide","Zinc sulphide"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) mercury is a metallic conductor; (C) SiC is a network solid with very high m.p.; (D) ZnS is ionic and conducts in the molten state.\nWorking: low melting point and non-conducting in both states → non-polar molecular solid, CCl₄. Hg conducts (metal), SiC is covalent network (high m.p.), ZnS is ionic (conducts when molten).\nKey idea: Bad conductor in BOTH solid and liquid plus low m.p. → molecular solid.","traps":["Ionic solid thought non-conducting: Ionic solids conduct when molten."],"topics":["Types of solids","Electrical & magnetic properties"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"An element with molar mass 2.7 × 10⁻² kg mol⁻¹ forms a cubic unit cell with edge length 405 pm. If its density is 2.7 × 10³ kg m⁻³, the radius of the element is approximately ______ × 10⁻¹² m (to the nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"143","range":[143.0,143.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 175 (bcc relation √3a/4), 202 (simple cubic a/2), 286 (a/√2 i.e. diameter).\nWorking: z = ρa³N_A/M = 2.7×10³ × 6.64×10⁻²⁹ × 6.022×10²³ / 2.7×10⁻² ≈ 4 → fcc; r = a/(2√2) = 405/2.828 = 143.2 pm.\nKey idea: z ≈ 4 → fcc → r = 0.3536a.","traps":["Wrong lattice relation used: Compute z first.","Unit conversion of pm³: Convert a to m before cubing."],"topics":["Density","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A diatomic molecule X₂ has a body-centred cubic (bcc) structure with a cell edge of 300 pm. The density of the molecule is 6.17 g cm⁻³. The number of molecules present in 200 g of X₂ is (Avogadro constant (N_A) = 6 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","options":["8 N_A","40 N_A","4 N_A","2 N_A"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 8 N_A comes from Z = 1 (M = 25); (D) 2 N_A from Z = 4 (M = 100); (B) unit slip in a³.\nWorking: ρ = ZM/(N_A a³) with Z = 2: M = 6.17 × 6×10²³ × 2.7×10⁻²³ / 2 ≈ 50 g mol⁻¹; 200/50 = 4 mol = 4 N_A molecules.\nKey idea: Each bcc lattice point is one X₂ molecule, so M is the molar mass of X₂.","traps":["Wrong Z for bcc: bcc: 8 × 1/8 + 1 = 2.","pm to cm conversion slip: Cube carefully: 2.7 × 10⁻²³ cm³."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"An element crystallises in a face-centred cubic (fcc) unit cell with cell edge a. The distance between the centres of two nearest octahedral voids in the crystal lattice is","options":["a","√2 a","a/√2","a/2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) a/2 – taken as the edge-centre to corner (an atom, not a void) distance; (A) a – distance between two body centres of adjacent cells; (B) √2a – face-diagonal confusion.\nWorking: in fcc, octahedral voids sit at the body centre and edge centres; body centre to an edge centre (or two adjacent edge centres) = √((a/2)² + (a/2)²) = a/√2.\nKey idea: Octahedral voids form an fcc array of their own (shifted by a/2), so nearest-void distance equals nearest-atom distance a/√2.","traps":["Edge-centre to corner distance taken: Corners hold atoms, not voids; join body centre to edge centre.","Body-centre to body-centre distance: Edge centres are voids too, and are closer."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"A crystal is made up of metal ions 'M₁' and 'M₂' and oxide ions. Oxide ions form a ccp lattice structure. The cation 'M₁' occupies 50% of octahedral voids and the cation 'M₂' occupies 12.5% of tetrahedral voids of oxide lattice. The oxidation numbers of 'M₁' and 'M₂' are, respectively:","options":["+2, +4","+3, +1","+1, +3","+4, +2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) gives total charge +7 and (C) +5 (not neutral); (D) is (A) with the two charges swapped: 2(+4) + (+2) = +10, not neutral.\nWorking: per 4 O²⁻ (ccp): 4 octahedral voids → 2 M₁; 8 tetrahedral voids → 1 M₂; formula (M₁)₂(M₂)O₄; 2x + y = 8; only (+2, +4) fits.\nKey idea: ccp of N anions: N octahedral, 2N tetrahedral voids.","traps":["Tetrahedral voids counted as N: ccp: octahedral = N, tetrahedral = 2N.","Order of M₁, M₂ reversed: M₁ is the more numerous cation (2 per formula), so it carries the smaller charge."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Element 'B' forms ccp structure and 'A' occupies half of the octahedral voids, while oxygen atoms occupy all the tetrahedral voids. The structure of bimetallic oxide is :","options":["A₂BO₄","A₂B₂O","A₄B₂O","AB₂O₄"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps A and B; (B)/(C) ignore 2N tetrahedral voids.\nWorking: per ccp unit of 4 B: octahedral voids 4 → A = 2; tetrahedral voids 8 → O = 8; A₂B₄O₈ = AB₂O₄.\nKey idea: Octa = N, tetra = 2N.","traps":["Swapping A and B in the formula: Write ratio A:B:O = 2:4:8 carefully."],"topics":["Voids","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Packing efficiency","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Consider the bcc unit cells of the solids 1 and 2 with the position of atoms as given below. The radius of atom B is twice that of atom A. The unit cell edge length is 50% more in solid 2 than in 1. What is the approximate packing efficiency in solid 2?\nSolid 1: atoms A at all corners and atom A at the body centre.\nSolid 2: atoms A at all corners and atom B at the body centre.","options":["45%","65%","90%","75%"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 65% ≈ assumes ideal bcc 68%; (D) 75% from using contact 2(r_A + r_B) = √3a; (A) 45% from counting B volume as 2r³.\nWorking: solid 1 (pure bcc A): a₁ = 4r_A/√3; a₂ = 1.5a₁ = 2√3 r_A; atoms per cell: 1 A + 1 B; volume = (4/3)π(r³ + 8r³) = 12πr³ ≈ 37.7r³; a₂³ = 24√3 r³ ≈ 41.6r³ ⇒ ≈ 90%.\nKey idea: Use a₂ = 1.5 × (4r_A/√3) and r_B = 2r_A.","traps":["Standard bcc value used: Recompute with given edge and radii.","Radius ratio applied linearly to volume: Volume scales as r³ ⇒ 8×."],"topics":["Packing efficiency","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"An element has a face-centred cubic (fcc) structure with a cell edge of a. The distance between the centres of two nearest tetrahedral voids in the lattice is :","options":["a/2","a","(3/2)a","√2 a"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) a confuses with corner-to-corner; (D) √2a is face diagonal; (C) 3a/2 has no basis.\nWorking: the 8 tetrahedral voids sit at (¼,¼,¼)a type positions forming a cube of edge a/2 inside the cell; nearest void–void distance = a/2.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral voids form a simple cube of edge a/2.","traps":["Void distance vs atom distance: Write void coordinates explicitly."],"topics":["Voids","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The ratio of number of atoms present in a simple cubic, body centered cubic and face centered cubic structure are, respectively :","options":["1 : 2 : 4","8 : 1 : 6","4 : 2 : 1","4 : 2 : 3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts lattice points without sharing; (C) reversed order.\nWorking: per unit cell sc = 8 × 1/8 = 1, bcc = 1 + 1 = 2, fcc = 1 + 6 × 1/2 = 4.\nKey idea: Corner 1/8, face 1/2, body 1.","traps":["Counting positions without sharing: Apply 1/8 and 1/2 sharing."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Electrical & magnetic properties","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The one that is extensively used as a piezoelectric material is :","options":["Quartz","Amorphous silica","Mica","Tridymite"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): amorphous – no piezoelectricity. (C): mica is a dielectric. (D): tridymite, another silica form.\nWorking: quartz crystals are the common piezoelectric material (oscillators, transducers).\nKey idea: Piezoelectric = quartz.","traps":["Other silica form picked: Quartz is the standard piezoelectric crystal."],"topics":["Electrical & magnetic properties","Types of solids"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"At 100 °C, copper (Cu) has FCC unit cell structure with cell edge length of x Å. What is the approximate density of Cu (in g cm⁻³) at this temperature? [Atomic mass of Cu = 63.55 u]","options":["105/x³","211/x³","205/x³","422/x³"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 105/x³: Z = 1 (simple cubic). (B) 211/x³: Z = 2 (bcc). (C) 205/x³: arithmetic slip.\nWorking: d = ZM/(Nₐa³) = 4 × 63.55/(6.022 × 10²³ × x³ × 10⁻²⁴) ≈ 422/x³.\nKey idea: FCC Z = 4; 10⁻²⁴ from Å³ combines with Nₐ to give 4×63.55/0.6022.","traps":["Wrong Z for FCC: FCC (ccp): Z = 4.","100 °C taken as relevant: Edge length already fixes density."],"topics":["Density","Unit cells"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Unit cells","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Which primitive unit cell has unequal edge lengths (a ≠ b ≠ c) and all axial angles different from 90° ?","options":["Tetragonal","Hexagonal","Monoclinic","Triclinic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) tetragonal: a = b ≠ c, all 90°. (B) hexagonal: a = b ≠ c, γ = 120°. (C) monoclinic: a ≠ b ≠ c but two angles 90°.\nWorking: triclinic has a ≠ b ≠ c and α ≠ β ≠ γ ≠ 90°.\nKey idea: Only triclinic has no 90° angle at all.","traps":["Monoclinic vs triclinic: Monoclinic keeps α = γ = 90°; triclinic has none at 90°."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"A compound of formula A₂B₃ has the hcp lattice. Which atom forms the hcp lattice and what fraction of tetrahedral voids is occupied by the other atoms :","options":["hcp lattice-A, 2/3 Tetrahedral voids-B","hcp lattice-B, 1/3 Tetrahedral voids-A","hcp lattice-B, 2/3 Tetrahedral voids-A","hcp lattice-A, 1/3 Tetrahedral voids-B"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D): A forms lattice — needs 3/4 of the tetrahedral voids, so wrong. (C) 2/3: forgets that tetrahedral voids = 2N.\nWorking: if B (3 per formula) forms hcp there are 6 tetrahedral voids; 2 A occupy 2/6 = 1/3. If A formed hcp, B would need 3/4 of tetrahedral voids, not offered.\nKey idea: Tetrahedral voids = 2 × lattice atoms; A:B = 2:3 → B lattice, A in 1/3 of tetrahedral voids.","traps":["Voids counted as N instead of 2N: Tetrahedral voids are 2N."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Density","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A solid having density of 9 × 10³ kg m⁻³ forms face centred cubic crystals of edge length 200√2 pm. What is the molar mass of the solid? (Avogadro constant ≅ 6 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, π ≅ 3)","options":["0.0216 kg mol⁻¹","0.0305 kg mol⁻¹","0.4320 kg mol⁻¹","0.0432 kg mol⁻¹"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 0.0432 – √2 dropped or Z = 2 type slip. (A) 0.0216 – other factor-2 error. (C) unit slip by 10.\nWorking: M = ρa³N_A/Z = 9×10³ × (2.828×10⁻¹⁰)³ × 6×10²³ / 4 = 0.0305 kg mol⁻¹ (π is not needed).\nKey idea: (200√2)³ = 1.6√2×10⁷ pm³ ≈ 2.26×10⁻²⁹ m³.","traps":["Distractor π: Only ρ = ZM/(a³N_A) is needed.","pm³ to m³: Convert a to metres before cubing."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Voids","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The radius of the largest sphere which fits properly at the centre of the edge of body centred cubic unit cell is : (Edge length is represented by 'a') :-","options":["0.134 a","0.027 a","0.067 a","0.047 a"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 0.134a = 2 × 0.067a: diameter instead of radius. (B) 0.027a and (D) 0.047a: no standard slip gives these; they are near-value fillers that catch rounding guesses (using the body-centre atom instead gives 0.274a, not offered).\nWorking: in bcc, 4r = √3a so r = 0.433a. At an edge centre the nearest atoms are the two corner atoms at a/2: void radius = a/2 – 0.433a = 0.067a (the body-centre atom is a/√2 = 0.707a away, not limiting).\nKey idea: Void radius = distance to nearest atom centre – atomic radius.","traps":["Diameter reported instead of radius: Void radius = a/2 – r, single subtraction.","Wrong r–a relation: bcc: atoms touch along body diagonal, 4r = √3a."],"topics":["Voids","Unit cells"]}],"m03-bond":[{"id":"JM25Apr04S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":56,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement (I): For ClF₃, all three possible structures may be drawn as follows (structures I, II and III shown below; Cl has two lone pairs).\nStatement (II): Structure III is most stable, as the orbitals having the lone pairs are axial, where the lp−bp repulsion is minimum.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I correct: three arrangements can be drawn. Statement II incorrect: lone pairs axial is least stable; the T-shape with equatorial lone pairs is most stable. So (C).\nKey idea: In trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry lone pairs always go equatorial.","traps":["Lone pair placement in trigonal bipyramid (axial vs equatorial): Equatorial positions have only two 90° neighbours; lone pairs go equatorial, giving the T shape (structure I).","Symmetric drawing assumed most stable: Stability is decided by number of 90° lp-lp and lp-bp interactions, not by symmetry."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr04S2C06-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr08S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Resonance","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Resonance in X₂Y can be represented as\n⁻:X̤=X⁺=Y̤: ↔ :X≡X⁺–Y̤:⁻\nThe enthalpy of formation of X₂Y (X≡X(g) + 1/2 Y=Y(g) → X₂Y(g)) is 80 kJ mol⁻¹. The magnitude of resonance energy of X₂Y is ______ kJ mol⁻¹ (nearest integer value)\nGiven : Bond energies of X≡X, X=X, Y=Y and X=Y are 940, 410, 500 and 602 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively.\nvalence X: 3 , Y: 2","type":"num","answer":"98","range":[98.0,98.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 348 (full Y=Y, 1440 − 1012 − 80); 258 (adding 80 instead of subtracting); 178 (calculated ΔHf reported); −98 (sign). Correct 98.\nWorking: using X=X=Y (the only form whose bonds are all given): bonds broken = X≡X + ½ Y=Y = 940 + 250 = 1190 kJ; bonds formed = X=X + X=Y = 410 + 602 = 1012 kJ; calculated ΔfH = 1190 − 1012 = 178 kJ mol⁻¹; resonance energy = 178 − 80 = 98 kJ mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Resonance energy = ΔHf calculated from one canonical form − experimental ΔHf.","traps":["Wrong canonical structure used for bond energies: Use the structure X=X=Y whose bonds (X=X 410, X=Y 602) are all given.","Stoichiometric coefficient omitted in Hess's law sum: Break 1/2 Y=Y = 250 kJ."],"topics":["Resonance","Bond parameters"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Number of molecules/ions from the following in which the central atom is involved in sp³ hybridization is ______.\nNO₃⁻, BCl₃, ClO₂⁻, ClO₃⁻","options":["2","4","1","3"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 1: counts only ClO₃⁻ (misses 2 lp on ClO₂⁻). (D) 3: treats NO₃⁻ as sp³. (B) 4: thinks all are sp³.\nWorking: NO₃⁻ sp² (3 σ, 0 lp), BCl₃ sp² (3 σ, 0 lp), ClO₂⁻ sp³ (2 σ + 2 lp), ClO₃⁻ sp³ (3 σ + 1 lp). Count = 2.\nKey idea: Steric number 4 → sp³, including lone pairs.","traps":["Lone pairs ignored on Cl: Steric number = σ bonds + lone pairs."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which one of the following molecules has maximum dipole moment?","options":["CH₄","NF₃","NH₃","PF₅"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) NF₃: student assumes larger EN difference → larger μ.\nWorking: CH₄ and PF₅ are symmetric (μ = 0); NH₃ has a much larger moment than NF₃ because the lone-pair moment adds to the N–H bond moments but opposes the N–F moments.\nKey idea: Lone-pair moment reinforces in NH₃, cancels in NF₃.","traps":["Higher EN difference means higher μ: Include lone-pair contribution direction."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of molecules/species from the following having one unpaired electron is ______.\nO₂, O₂⁻, NO, CN⁻, O₂²⁻","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3: counts O₂ as one unpaired or CN⁻ as one (confusing with CN radical). 1: misses NO.\nWorking: O₂ (2 unpaired), O₂⁻ (1), NO (1), CN⁻ (0, 14 e⁻), O₂²⁻ (0). Count = 2.\nKey idea: Odd-electron species (O₂⁻ 17 e⁻, NO 15 e⁻) have one unpaired electron here.","traps":["CN⁻ vs CN radical: CN⁻ is isoelectronic with N₂ – diamagnetic.","O₂ unpaired count: O₂ has two unpaired π* electrons."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The correct statement/s about Hydrogen bonding is/are\nA. Hydrogen bonding exists when H is covalently bonded to the highly electronegative atom.\nB. Intermolecular H bonding is present in o-nitro phenol\nC. Intramolecular H bonding is present in HF.\nD. The magnitude of H bonding depends on the physical state of the compound.\nE. H-bonding has powerful effect on the structure and properties of compounds\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, B, C only","A only","A, D, E only","A, B, D only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) and (A) accept B (o-nitrophenol intermolecular) – the swapped example; (A) also accepts C. (B) rejects D because students think H-bond strength is fixed by the atoms only.\nWorking: A true (H bonded to F/O/N); B false – o-nitrophenol has intramolecular H-bond; C false – HF shows intermolecular H-bonding (zig-zag chains); D true – maximum in solid, minimum in gas; E true – strong influence on structure and properties.\nKey idea: o-Nitrophenol: intramolecular; HF, p-nitrophenol: intermolecular.","traps":["Intra/inter examples swapped: o-substituent next to OH → intramolecular chelate.","State dependence doubted: NCERT: maximum in solid, minimum in gas."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The number of species from the following that have pyramidal geometry around the central atom is _________\nS₂O₃²⁻, SO₄²⁻, SO₃²⁻, S₂O₇²⁻","options":["4","3","2","1"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 2 counts S₂O₃²⁻ as pyramidal (thinking terminal S has a lone pair on the central atom). (B)/(A) treat any species with S=O and negative charge as pyramidal.\nWorking: S₂O₃²⁻ – central S tetrahedral (4 σ, no lone pair); SO₄²⁻ tetrahedral; SO₃²⁻ – 3 σ + 1 lone pair → pyramidal; S₂O₇²⁻ – each S tetrahedral. Only 1.\nKey idea: Pyramidal needs AX₃E: only sulphite (S in +4 with one lone pair).","traps":["Thiosulphate called pyramidal: Central S has 4 σ bonds, no lone pair.","Lone pair on S not identified: Count S valence electrons: S(+4) keeps a lone pair; S(+6) none."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Number of compounds / species from the following with non-zero dipole moment is ________.\nBeCl₂, BCl₃, NF₃, XeF₄, CCl₄, H₂O, H₂S, HBr, CO₂, H₂, HCl","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (treating NF₃ as non-polar because its dipole is small); 6 (counting XeF₄ as polar because of lone pairs).\nWorking: polar – NF₃ (pyramidal), H₂O, H₂S (bent), HBr, HCl; non-polar – BeCl₂, CO₂ (linear), BCl₃ (trigonal planar), CCl₄ (tetrahedral), XeF₄ (square planar), H₂. Count = 5.\nKey idea: Symmetric shapes (linear AX₂, trigonal planar, tetrahedral AX₄, square planar AX₄E₂) cancel bond dipoles.","traps":["NF₃ taken as non-polar: Opposing ≠ cancelling: NF₃ μ = 0.23 D.","XeF₄ taken as polar: Two lone pairs trans → square planar, symmetric."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): Cis form of alkene is found to be more polar than the trans form.\nReason (R): Dipole moment of trans isomer of 2-butene is zero.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): student treats R as a single example rather than the explanation – but the cancellation of dipoles in the trans isomer is the reason. (A): student thinks trans-2-butene has a small dipole. (B): student believes trans is more polar.\nWorking: in cis-but-2-ene the two C–CH₃ bond dipoles add (μ ≈ 0.33 D); in trans they point opposite and cancel (μ = 0), which is exactly why cis is more polar.\nKey idea: Trans isomer: equal opposite C–C dipoles cancel → μ = 0; cis: they add → polar.","traps":["R seen as an example, not explanation: Ask whether R gives the mechanism (dipole cancellation) behind A – it does.","Generalisation \"cis always more polar\": Accept the NCERT generalisation for symmetric 1,2-disubstituted alkenes."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sigma & pi bonds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Number of σ and π bonds present in ethylene molecule is respectively:","options":["3 and 1","5 and 1","4 and 1","5 and 2"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 4 and 1: C–C σ forgotten. (D) 5 and 2: double bond counted as 2 π. (A): H counted wrongly.\nWorking: C₂H₄ has 4 C–H σ + 1 C–C σ = 5 σ, and 1 π in the C=C.\nKey idea: Every bond has one σ; a double bond adds one π.","traps":["Double bond counted as one π only, σ missed: A double bond = 1σ + 1π."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lewis structures","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"In the Lewis dot structure for NO₂⁻, total number of valence electrons around nitrogen is ____.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 18 (total valence electrons of the ion); 5 (N’s own valence electrons); 2 (lone pair only); 6 (bonds only).\nWorking: O=N–O⁻ with one lone pair on N: double bond (4 e⁻) + single bond (2 e⁻) + lone pair (2 e⁻) = 8 (octet).\nKey idea: Count shared + unshared electrons around N → octet 8.","traps":["Total ion electrons counted: Restrict to electrons around N (bond pairs + lone pairs).","N’s own valence electrons: Shared electrons count around both atoms."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: A. ICl B. ICl₃ C. ClF₅ D. IF₇\nList II: I. T-shape II. Square pyramidal III. Pentagonal bipyramidal IV. Linear\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) put T-shape against ICl (forgetting it is diatomic). (D) swaps T-shape and pentagonal bipyramidal between ICl₃ and IF₇.\nWorking: ICl diatomic → linear; ICl₃ 3 bp + 2 lp → T-shape; ClF₅ 5 bp + 1 lp → square pyramidal; IF₇ 7 bp → pentagonal bipyramidal.\nKey idea: Count bp + lp: 5 pairs with 2 lp → T; 6 pairs with 1 lp → square pyramid.","traps":["ICl₃ shape vs trigonal planar: Iodine has 2 lone pairs: sp³d, T-shape."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): NH₃ and NF₃ molecule have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. The resultant dipole moment of NH₃ is greater than that of NF₃.\nReason (R): In NH₃, the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N–H bonds. F is the most electronegative element.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) for students who feel 'F most electronegative' does not explain the NH₃ > NF₃ order.\nWorking: NH₃ 1.47 D vs NF₃ 0.23 D; lone-pair dipole adds to N–H bond dipoles but opposes N–F bond dipoles.\nKey idea: Direction of lone-pair dipole relative to bond dipoles decides the resultant.","traps":["R judged as not explaining A: R gives the vector reason for both molecules."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Number of compounds from the following with zero dipole moment is ______.\nHF, H₂, H₂S, CO₂, NH₃, BF₃, CH₄, CHCl₃, SiF₄, H₂O, BeF₂","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (H₂ forgotten), 7 (CHCl₃ treated as symmetric).\nWorking: zero dipole: H₂, CO₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CH₄ and SiF₄ (tetrahedral), BeF₂ (linear) = 6.\nKey idea: Symmetric shape with identical ligands → zero μ.","traps":["CHCl₃ assumed non-polar: One H breaks symmetry.","H₂ overlooked: Count it: μ = 0."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Compound/Species): A. SF₄ B. BrF₃ C. BrO₃⁻ D. NH₄⁺\nList II (Shape/Geometry): I. Tetrahedral II. Pyramidal III. See saw IV. Bent T-Shape\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps BrF₃ and BrO₃⁻ shapes. (C)/(D) give SF₄ as pyramidal, confusing with AB₃E.\nWorking: SF₄ (4 bp + 1 lp) see-saw; BrF₃ (3 bp + 2 lp) bent T; BrO₃⁻ (3 bp + 1 lp) pyramidal; NH₄⁺ tetrahedral.\nKey idea: Count bonding + lone pairs: AB₄E see-saw, AB₃E₂ T, AB₃E pyramidal, AB₄ tetrahedral.","traps":["BrF₃ and BrO₃⁻ shapes swapped: BrF₃ has 2 lp (T-shape), BrO₃⁻ has 1 lp (pyramidal)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Molecule/Species): A. SO₂Cl₂ B. NO C. NO₂⁻ D. I₃⁻\nList II (Property/Shape): I. Paramagnetic II. Diamagnetic III. Tetrahedral IV. Linear\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives SO₂Cl₂ as diamagnetic (true but not the intended unique match) and NO as tetrahedral. (D) calls NO linear (true for a diatomic) and I₃⁻ paramagnetic.\nWorking: SO₂Cl₂ tetrahedral; NO odd-electron (11 valence e⁻) paramagnetic; NO₂⁻ all paired, diamagnetic; I₃⁻ (AB₂E₃) linear.\nKey idea: Odd total electrons → paramagnetic (NO); NO₂⁻ has even electrons → diamagnetic; I₃⁻ has 3 lp in equatorial positions → linear.","traps":["Multiple true properties per species: Find the unique pairing set.","NO₂⁻ assumed paramagnetic like NO₂: Anion adds one electron → even count."],"topics":["MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","VSEPR shapes"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Hybridization): A. sp³ B. dsp² C. sp³d D. sp³d²\nList II (Orientation in Shape): I. Trigonal bipyramidal II. Octahedral III. Tetrahedral IV. Square planar\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps dsp² and sp³d (both 'd + p' combinations). (C) swaps sp³ and dsp² (both four-coordinate).\nWorking: sp³ tetrahedral, dsp² square planar, sp³d trigonal bipyramidal, sp³d² octahedral.\nKey idea: Four-coordinate: sp³ tetrahedral vs dsp² square planar.","traps":["sp³ vs dsp² for four-coordinate: d-inclusion gives square planar."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Number of molecules from the following which can exhibit hydrogen bonding is ______. (nearest integer)\nCH₃OH, H₂O, C₂H₆, C₆H₆, o-nitrophenol, HF, NH₃","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 4 (excluding o-nitrophenol as it lacks intermolecular H-bonding), 6 (including benzene for π-cloud).\nWorking: CH₃OH, H₂O, o-nitrophenol (intramolecular H-bond), HF and NH₃ → 5; C₂H₆ and C₆H₆ cannot.\nKey idea: Intramolecular H-bonding still counts as hydrogen bonding.","traps":["o-Nitrophenol excluded: Intramolecular H-bond counts."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : PF₅ and BrF₅ both exhibit sp³d hybridisation.\nStatement II : Both SF₆ and [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ exhibit sp³d² hybridisation.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): BrF₅ judged by \"5 F atoms\" as sp³d. (C): Co(III) ammine taken as outer-orbital sp³d². (D): both errors.\nWorking: PF₅ sp³d but BrF₅ sp³d² (6 electron pairs) → I false. SF₆ sp³d² but [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is inner-orbital d²sp³ → II false.\nKey idea: Count all electron pairs (BrF₅ has a lone pair → 6 pairs); NH₃ with Co³⁺ gives low-spin d²sp³.","traps":["BrF₅ given sp³d from five bonds: Steric number = bonds + lone pairs = 6.","sp³d² vs d²sp³ treated as same: Co³⁺ with NH₃ uses inner 3d orbitals: d²sp³."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Total number of species from the following with central atom utilising sp² hybrid orbitals for bonding is ______.\nNH₃, SO₂, SiO₂, BeCl₂, C₂H₂, C₂H₄, BCl₃, HCHO, C₆H₆, BF₃, C₂H₄Cl₂","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 (SiO₂ taken as sp² like CO₂ analogue or C₂H₄Cl₂ misread as alkene), 5 (SO₂ taken as sp³ or benzene skipped).\nWorking: sp² – SO₂, C₂H₄, BCl₃, HCHO, C₆H₆, BF₃ = 6. NH₃ sp³, SiO₂ sp³ (network), BeCl₂ sp, C₂H₂ sp, C₂H₄Cl₂ sp³.\nKey idea: Steric number 3 → sp²; SiO₂ is a network of SiO₄ tetrahedra.","traps":["SiO₂ treated like a discrete molecule: SiO₂ is a 3D network with sp³ Si.","C₂H₄Cl₂ read as an alkene: Dichloroethane is saturated (sp³)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Molecule): A. NH₃ B. BrF₅ C. PCl₅ D. CH₄\nLIST II (Shape): I. Square pyramid II. Tetrahedral III. Trigonal pyramidal IV. Trigonal bipyramidal\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps BrF₅ and PCl₅ (both \"5 atoms\"); (A) treats NH₃ as tetrahedral (electron geometry); (C) gives NH₃ trigonal bipyramidal.\nWorking: NH₃ 3bp+1lp trigonal pyramidal; BrF₅ 5bp+1lp square pyramidal; PCl₅ 5bp trigonal bipyramidal; CH₄ tetrahedral.\nKey idea: Count lone pairs: BrF₅ has one → square pyramid.","traps":["Five-coordinate shapes swapped: BrF₅ carries one lone pair on Br.","NH₃ called tetrahedral: Shape ignores lone pair positions."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Number of molecules from the following which are exceptions to octet rule is ________.\nCO₂, NO₂, H₂SO₄, BF₃, CH₄, SiF₄, ClO₂, PCl₅, BeF₂, C₂H₆, CHCl₃, CBr₄","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (missing ClO₂ or H₂SO₄), 4, 7 (adding SiF₄).\nWorking: odd-electron NO₂, ClO₂; expanded octet H₂SO₄ (S has 12 e⁻), PCl₅; incomplete octet BF₃, BeF₂ → 6.\nKey idea: Check central-atom electron count in each Lewis structure.","traps":["H₂SO₄ missed: NCERT lists H₂SO₄ as expanded octet.","ClO₂ missed: Count total valence electrons (19, odd)."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"When ψ_A and ψ_B are the wave functions of atomic orbitals, then σ* is represented by :","options":["ψ_A + 2ψ_B","ψ_A − ψ_B","ψ_A − 2ψ_B","ψ_A + ψ_B"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the bonding MO. (A)/(C) carry a factor of 2 that has no meaning for identical atoms.\nWorking: the antibonding MO is formed by subtraction (destructive interference) of the two atomic wave functions.\nKey idea: Addition → bonding, subtraction → antibonding.","traps":["Bonding vs antibonding combination: σ* has a node: subtraction."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Number of molecules having bond order 2 from the following molecules is ______. C₂, O₂, Be₂, Li₂, Ne₂, N₂, He₂","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (missing C₂ double bond of two π bonds); 3 (including N₂).\nWorking: C₂ (2), O₂ (2); Be₂ 0, Li₂ 1, Ne₂ 0, N₂ 3, He₂ 0.\nKey idea: C₂ has two π bonds, no σ.","traps":["C₂ bond order: C₂: σ2s²σ*2s²π2p⁴ → BO 2."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"In which one of the following pairs the central atoms exhibit sp² hybridization?","options":["BF₃ and NO₂⁻","NH₂⁻ and BF₃","NH₂⁻ and H₂O","H₂O and NO₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) treat bent NH₂⁻ as sp² (two lone pairs make it sp³). (D) H₂O is sp³; NO₂ is ~sp².\nWorking: BF₃ (3 bp) and NO₂⁻ (2 bp + 1 lp) are sp²; NH₂⁻ and H₂O are sp³.\nKey idea: Steric number 3 → sp²; bent shape alone does not tell the hybridisation.","traps":["Bent shape taken as sp²: Count lone pairs: 2 lp + 2 bp = sp³."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The total number of species from the following in which one unpaired electron is present, is ____.\nN₂, O₂, C₂⁻, O₂⁻, O₂²⁻, H₂⁺, CN⁻, He₂⁺","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 if O₂ is counted (it has two unpaired); 3 if C₂⁻ is missed by using wrong ordering; CN⁻ treated as odd-electron.\nWorking: C₂⁻ (13 e), O₂⁻ (17 e), H₂⁺ (1 e), He₂⁺ (3 e) each have one unpaired electron = 4; N₂, O₂²⁻, CN⁻ have none, O₂ has two.\nKey idea: Odd total electrons → one unpaired (for these); O₂ has two unpaired in π*.","traps":["O₂ counted as one unpaired: O₂ has two unpaired π* electrons.","Electron count of ions: Count electrons first: C₂⁻ 13, CN⁻ 14."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The correct increasing order for bond angles among BF₃, PF₃ and ClF₃ is :","options":["BF₃ = PF₃ < ClF₃","BF₃ < PF₃ < ClF₃","ClF₃ < PF₃ < BF₃","PF₃ < BF₃ < ClF₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats PF₃ as planar. (B)/(D) rank ClF₃ largest by ignoring its two lone pairs.\nWorking: ClF₃ bent-T (F–Cl–F ≈ 87.5°), PF₃ pyramidal (≈ 97–98°), BF₃ trigonal planar (120°).\nKey idea: More lone pairs on the central atom compress the bond angle; ClF₃ has 90°-type angles.","traps":["Same formula type, same angle: Count lone pairs: 0, 1, 2."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"Total number of electrons present in (π*) molecular orbitals of O₂, O₂⁺ and O₂⁻ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 if O₂⁺ is assumed to lose a bonding electron (π* count stays 2); 12 if the σ*2s electrons (2 in each species) are also counted.\nWorking: π*2p electrons: O₂ 2, O₂⁺ 1, O₂⁻ 3; total 6.\nKey idea: Adding/removing an electron changes the π* count by one.","traps":["Charge direction for ions: O₂⁺ loses from π*, O₂⁻ adds to π*."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Arrange the bonds in order of increasing ionic character in the molecules. LiF, K₂O, N₂, SO₂ and ClF₃ :","options":["N₂ < SO₂ < ClF₃ < K₂O < LiF","ClF₃ < N₂ < SO₂ < K₂O < LiF","LiF < K₂O < ClF₃ < SO₂ < N₂","N₂ < ClF₃ < SO₂ < K₂O < LiF"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): uses Pauling refined values and puts SO₂ above ClF₃ – the closest distractor. (B): thinks ClF₃ covalent between halogens is least ionic. (C): order reversed.\nWorking: N₂ (ΔEN 0) lowest; LiF (3.0) > K₂O (2.7) highest. SO₂ vs ClF₃ is not decidable from NCERT Table 3.8 (both ΔEN = 1.0); Pauling refined values (S–O 0.86, Cl–F 0.82) would even favour D, while Allred–Rochow values (S–O ≈1.06, Cl–F ≈1.27) support (A), the official answer; the question is ambiguous on this pair.\nKey idea: Ends are easy (N₂, LiF); the middle pair needs electronegativity values.","traps":["SO₂ and ClF₃ nearly equal ΔEN: Use F as the most electronegative: the official answer ranks Cl–F above S–O.","Order direction reversed: Start from N₂ (zero ionic character)."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of molecules/ion/s having trigonal bipyramidal shape is _______.\nPF₅, BrF₅, PCl₅, [PtCl₄]²⁻, BF₃, Fe(CO)₅","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 4 (counts BrF₅ as TBP because of 5 ligands), 2 (misses Fe(CO)₅).\nWorking: TBP – PF₅, PCl₅ (sp³d, no lone pair), Fe(CO)₅ (dsp³). BrF₅ square pyramidal, [PtCl₄]²⁻ square planar, BF₃ trigonal planar.\nKey idea: Five bond pairs and no lone pair → TBP.","traps":["BrF₅ counted as TBP: Br has a lone pair → octahedral electron geometry, square pyramidal shape."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : A π bonding MO has lower electron density above and below the inter-nuclear axis.\nStatement (II) : The π* antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses the altered qualifier. (B) thinks π* has no node between nuclei (confusing with π having a nodal plane along the axis). (C) reverses.\nWorking: π bonding MO has larger electron density above and below the axis; π* has a node between nuclei. So I is false and II is true.\nKey idea: π bonding: density above/below axis, nodal plane containing the axis; π*: additional node between nuclei.","traps":["'Lower' vs 'larger': Bonding MO always has more density where the AOs overlap.","Nodal plane along axis vs between nuclei: π* has both nodes; π has only the axial nodal plane."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Select the compound from the following that will show intramolecular hydrogen bonding.","options":["o-nitrophenol (–NO₂ and –OH on adjacent ring carbons)","H₂O","C₂H₅OH","NH₃"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) show only intermolecular H-bonding.\nWorking: in o-nitrophenol the ortho –OH and –NO₂ form a six-membered intramolecular O–H···O ring; H₂O, C₂H₅OH and NH₃ show only intermolecular H-bonding.\nKey idea: Intramolecular H-bond needs donor and acceptor in suitable positions in the same molecule.","traps":["Inter vs intra: Need donor and acceptor within one molecule."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Choose the polar molecule from the following:","options":["CCl₄","CO₂","CH₂=CH₂","CHCl₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CCl₄: polar bonds but tetrahedral symmetry → zero. (B) CO₂: linear, dipoles cancel. (C) Ethene: planar symmetric, non-polar.\nWorking: in CHCl₃ the C–H and C–Cl bond moments do not cancel in the tetrahedral geometry; CCl₄, CO₂ and ethene are symmetric with zero net dipole.\nKey idea: Polar bonds ≠ polar molecule; symmetry decides.","traps":["Polar bonds assumed to make a polar molecule: Add bond dipoles as vectors; tetrahedral identical groups cancel."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Sum of bond order of CO and NO⁺ is ________.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5.5 (taking NO⁺ as NO, 2.5), 5 (CO as double bond from Lewis C=O), 7.\nWorking: CO and NO⁺ are both 14-electron species isoelectronic with N₂; bond order = (10 − 4)/2 = 3 each; sum 6.\nKey idea: Same electron count → same bond order (14 e⁻ → 3).","traps":["NO⁺ treated as NO: Remove one electron from antibonding π* of NO → BO 3.","CO drawn as C=O: Count total electrons (14) and use MO filling."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of non-polar molecules from the following is ________. HF, H₂O, SO₂, H₂, CO₂, CH₄, NH₃, HCl, CHCl₃, BF₃","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (SO₂ taken as linear like CO₂), 3 (BF₃ thought polar due to B–F polarity), 5 (CHCl₃ thought symmetric).\nWorking: H₂, CO₂ (linear), CH₄ (tetrahedral) and BF₃ (trigonal planar) have zero dipole moment; the rest are polar.\nKey idea: Symmetric geometry with identical terminal atoms gives zero net dipole.","traps":["SO₂ taken as linear: SO₂ has a lone pair on S: bent, polar.","CHCl₃ treated like CH₄/CCl₄: Different substituents: dipoles do not cancel."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of species from the following which are paramagnetic and with bond order equal to one is _________.\nH₂, He₂⁺, O₂⁺, N₂²⁻, O₂²⁻, F₂, Ne₂⁺, B₂","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if N₂²⁻ is taken as BO 1 or O₂²⁻ counted as paramagnetic; 4 if magnetism ignored (H₂, O₂²⁻, F₂, B₂).\nWorking: only B₂ (BO 1, two unpaired π electrons). H₂, O₂²⁻, F₂ have BO 1 but are diamagnetic; He₂⁺ and Ne₂⁺ BO 0.5; O₂⁺ BO 2.5; N₂²⁻ BO 2 (paramagnetic).\nKey idea: B₂: σ2s²σ*2s²(π2p)² with s-p mixing – two unpaired electrons, BO 1.","traps":["Magnetism condition skipped: Apply both filters: BO = 1 AND unpaired electrons.","N₂²⁻ bond order misjudged: N₂²⁻ is isoelectronic with O₂: BO 2."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Number of compounds with one lone pair of electrons on central atom amongst following is _________.\nO₃, H₂O, SF₄, ClF₃, NH₃, BrF₅, XeF₄","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if O₃ missed (central O lone pair forgotten); 5 if ClF₃ taken as one lone pair.\nWorking: one lone pair – O₃, SF₄, NH₃, BrF₅ (4); two lone pairs – H₂O, ClF₃, XeF₄.\nKey idea: Lone pairs = (valence e⁻ − bonds used)/2 on central atom; O₃ central O has one lp.","traps":["O₃ central lone pair overlooked: Central O: 6 e⁻, 4 used in bonding (2 in the double bond, 2 in the dative bond) → one lone pair.","ClF₃ lone pairs undercounted: Cl: 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 lone pairs."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The total number of molecules with zero dipole moment among CH₄, BF₃, H₂O, HF, NH₃, CO₂ and SO₂ is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (SO₂ treated as linear like CO₂); 2 (BF₃ thought polar because B–F bonds are very polar).\nWorking: CH₄ (tetrahedral), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CO₂ (linear) are non-polar = 3; H₂O, HF, NH₃, SO₂ (bent) are polar.\nKey idea: Symmetric shapes (linear AB₂, trigonal planar AB₃, tetrahedral AB₄) cancel bond dipoles; lone pairs on S make SO₂ bent.","traps":["SO₂ taken as linear: Count lone pairs on S: one lone pair → bent → polar.","Polar bonds equated with polar molecule: Vector sum matters; trigonal planar cancels."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The total number of anti bonding molecular orbitals, formed from 2s and 2p atomic orbitals in a diatomic molecule is _______.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 3 (π* counted once), 8 (all MOs counted), 2 (only σ*).\nWorking: 2 × (one 2s + three 2p) = 8 AOs → 8 MOs: 4 bonding (σ2s, σ2pz, π2px, π2py) and 4 antibonding (σ*2s, σ*2pz, π*2px, π*2py).\nKey idea: AOs in = MOs out; half are antibonding: 8/2 = 4.","traps":["Degenerate π* counted once: Each degenerate MO is separate."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Molecule): (A) BrF₅ (B) H₂O (C) ClF₃ (D) SF₄\nList II (Shape): (I) T-Shape (II) See saw (III) Bent (IV) Square pyramidal\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(C),(D) each misplace several pairs; (C) differs from the correct option only in BrF₅/H₂O.\nWorking: BrF₅ 5bp+1lp → square pyramidal; H₂O 2bp+2lp → bent; ClF₃ 3bp+2lp → T-shape; SF₄ 4bp+1lp → see-saw.\nKey idea: Count bond pairs and lone pairs, then read the shape from the AB_xE_y type.","traps":["See-saw vs T-shape: 4bp+1lp = see-saw; 3bp+2lp = T-shape."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The total number of molecular orbitals formed from 2s and 2p atomic orbitals of a diatomic molecule is _______.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counting one atom only), 6 (forgetting 2s or π* set), 10 (adding 1s).\nWorking: each atom contributes 2s + three 2p = 4 AOs; 8 AOs combine to give 8 MOs (σ2s, σ*2s, σ2pz, σ*2pz, π2px, π2py, π*2px, π*2py).\nKey idea: n AOs → n MOs; 2 atoms × 4 = 8.","traps":["Counting AOs of one atom: Both atoms contribute orbitals."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement - I: Since Fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the net dipole moment of NF₃ is greater than NH₃.\nStatement - II: In NH₃, the orbital dipole due to lone pair and the dipole moment of NH bonds are in opposite direction, but in NF₃ the orbital dipole due to lone pair and dipole moments of N-F bonds are in same direction.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) accepts the electronegativity argument. (B) misses the swap in II. (D) catches I but misses the swap in II.\nWorking: μ(NH₃) = 1.47 D > μ(NF₃) = 0.23 D, so I is false; in NH₃ the lone-pair orbital dipole adds to the N–H bond dipoles and in NF₃ it opposes the N–F dipoles, so II has the directions reversed and is false.\nKey idea: F pulls electrons away from N, so N–F bond dipoles point away from the lone pair and partly cancel it.","traps":["Higher electronegativity ⇒ higher dipole: Add the lone-pair dipole vectorially; in NF₃ it opposes the bond dipoles.","'same' and 'opposite' swapped: Recall: NH₃ – same direction; NF₃ – opposite."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The molecule / ion with square pyramidal shape is","options":["PCl₅","[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻","PF₅","BrF₅"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) five bond pairs, no lone pair → trigonal bipyramidal. (B) dsp² square planar complex, 'square' word lure.\nWorking: BrF₅ has 5 bond pairs + 1 lone pair (sp³d², octahedral electron geometry) → square pyramidal; PCl₅ and PF₅ are trigonal bipyramidal; [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is square planar.\nKey idea: Five ligands + one lone pair on the central atom = square pyramidal.","traps":["AB₅ assumed square pyramidal: Count lone pairs: P has none → TBP.","'Square' word in square planar complex: Square planar ≠ square pyramidal."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The linear combination of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals takes place only when the combining atomic orbitals\nA. have the same energy\nB. have the minimum overlap\nC. have same symmetry about the molecular axis\nD. have different symmetry about the molecular axis\nChoose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["B, C, D only","A, B, C only","B and D only","A and C only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): 'minimum overlap' read as NCERT's 'maximum'. (A)/(C): D (different symmetry) accepted.\nWorking: NCERT conditions – same or nearly same energy, same symmetry about the molecular axis, maximum overlap. B (minimum) and D (different) are false.\nKey idea: Same energy, same symmetry, maximum overlap.","traps":["Minimum vs maximum overlap: Overlap must be maximum."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The number of species from the following in which the central atom uses sp³ hybrid orbitals in its bonding is __________.\nNH₃, SO₂, SiO₂, BeCl₂, CO₂, H₂O, CH₄, BF₃","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (SiO₂ treated as linear sp like CO₂), 5 (SO₂ taken sp³).\nWorking: sp³ – NH₃, H₂O, CH₄, SiO₂ (network solid, Si tetrahedral); SO₂ sp², BF₃ sp², BeCl₂ sp, CO₂ sp. Count = 4.\nKey idea: SiO₂ is a 3D network of SiO₄ tetrahedra.","traps":["SiO₂ treated like CO₂: Si cannot form pπ–pπ bonds; tetrahedral network."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Fajans' rules","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Which of the following is least ionic?","options":["CoCl₂","KCl","BaCl₂","AgCl"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CoCl₂ tempting because Co²⁺ has higher charge; (B)/(C) are clearly ionic s-block chlorides.\nWorking: Ag⁺ has an 18-electron (pseudo-noble gas) shell, high polarising power, so AgCl has most covalent character among the four.\nKey idea: 18-electron outer shell (Ag⁺) shields poorly → strong polarisation.","traps":["Charge vs electronic configuration: Ag⁺ (d¹⁰, 18-e shell) polarises strongly; AgCl insoluble, covalent."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A diatomic molecule has a dipole moment of 1.2 D. If the bond distance is 1 Å, then fractional charge on each atom is ______ ×10⁻¹ esu. (Given 1 D = 10⁻¹⁸ esu cm)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.2 (charge in 10⁻¹⁰ esu taken as answer); 12 (wrong power of ten); 2.5 (fraction of e).\nWorking: as worded, q = μ/d = 1.2×10⁻¹⁸ esu cm / 10⁻⁸ cm = 1.2×10⁻¹⁰ esu = 1.2×10⁻⁹ × 10⁻¹ esu, which is 0 to the nearest integer, so 0 is the answer as worded. The question is defective: the probable intent (fraction of e = 1.2/4.8 = 0.25 = 2.5×10⁻¹) is dimensionless and does not fit the 'esu' blank.\nKey idea: q = μ/d = 1.2×10⁻¹⁰ esu; in the asked unit (×10⁻¹ esu) this rounds to 0. (As a fraction of e it would be 0.25, but that is not in esu.)","traps":["Charge vs fraction of e: Answer in the unit the blank asks for (×10⁻¹ esu); 1.2×10⁻¹⁰ esu → 0.","Å to cm conversion: Keep CGS units throughout."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The number of species from the following which have square pyramidal structure is ______\nPF₅, BrF₄⁻, IF₅, BrF₅, XeOF₄, ICl₄⁻","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4–5: counts BrF₄⁻/ICl₄⁻ as square pyramidal ('square' + four ligands). 2: misses XeOF₄ because O is double-bonded. 4: counts PF₅ (5 ligands).\nWorking: IF₅, BrF₅ (AB₅E) and XeOF₄ (5 σ + 1 lp) are square pyramidal = 3. PF₅ trigonal bipyramidal; BrF₄⁻ and ICl₄⁻ (AB₄E₂) square planar.\nKey idea: Square pyramidal needs 5 bond pairs + 1 lone pair (or 5 σ + 1 lp counting multiple bonds once).","traps":["Square planar mistaken for square pyramidal: Count lone pairs: 2 lp → square planar.","XeOF₄ double bond miscounted: Treat a multiple bond as one electron domain."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The number of species having a square planar shape from the following is ________.\nXeF₄, SF₄, SiF₄, BF₄⁻, BrF₄⁻, [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺, [FeCl₄]²⁻, [PtCl₄]²⁻","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is taken as tetrahedral; 5 if SF₄ is called square planar or [FeCl₄]²⁻ counted; 2 if only xenon/halogen species are recognised.\nWorking: square planar – XeF₄ (4 bp + 2 lp), BrF₄⁻ (4 bp + 2 lp), [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ (d⁹, dsp²), [PtCl₄]²⁻ (d⁸ 5d, dsp²) = 4. SF₄ see-saw; SiF₄, BF₄⁻ and [FeCl₄]²⁻ tetrahedral.\nKey idea: AB₄E₂ ⇒ square planar; 4-coordinate Pt(II) and Cu(II) ammine are square planar, Fe(II) chloride is tetrahedral.","traps":["[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ taken as tetrahedral: [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ is square planar (Jahn–Teller distorted d⁹).","SF₄ counted as square planar: SF₄ has one lone pair (AB₄E) ⇒ see-saw."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Butan-1-ol has higher boiling point than ethoxyethane.\nReason R: Extensive hydrogen bonding leads to stronger association of molecules.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student thinks the larger ether molecule boils higher. (B): student judges R too general to be 'the' explanation. (D): student doubts that H-bonding causes 'association'.\nWorking: butan-1-ol (b.p. ≈ 390 K) and ethoxyethane (≈ 308 K) are isomers (C₄H₁₀O) of similar mass; the alcohol's O–H groups form intermolecular H-bonds that associate the molecules, ethers cannot. R is true and explains A.\nKey idea: Isomers of equal mass: only the alcohol has O–H, so H-bonding decides the boiling point.","traps":["Generic R doubted as the explanation: If the general principle is exactly the cause of A, R explains A.","Ether assumed higher boiling: Same molecular formula; compare intermolecular forces only."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Fajans' rules","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The number of following factors which affect the percent covalent character of the ionic bond is ______\n(A) Polarising power of cation\n(B) Extent of distortion of anion\n(C) Polarisability of the anion\n(D) Polarising power of anion","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (accepts polarising power of anion), 2 (treats B and C as the same factor).\nWorking: NCERT lists polarising power of cation, polarisability of anion and extent of distortion (polarisation) of anion; polarising power of the anion is not a factor. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Cation polarises, anion is polarised.","traps":["Cation/anion roles swapped: Polarising power belongs to cation; polarisability to anion.","B and C seen as duplicates: NCERT lists both separately."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"In which of the following processes, the bond order increases and paramagnetic character changes to diamagnetic one?","options":["O₂ → O₂²⁻","N₂ → N₂⁺","NO → NO⁺","O₂ → O₂⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): bond order falls (2→1) though O₂²⁻ is diamagnetic – picked if only the magnetic change is checked. (B): bond order falls and N₂⁺ becomes paramagnetic (reverse change). (D): bond order rises (2→2.5) but O₂⁺ still has one unpaired electron – picked if only bond order is checked.\nWorking: NO (15 e⁻) has BO 2.5 with one unpaired π* electron; removing it gives NO⁺ (14 e⁻, isoelectronic with N₂), BO 3, diamagnetic. O₂→O₂²⁻: BO 2→1 (decreases). N₂→N₂⁺: BO 3→2.5, becomes paramagnetic. O₂→O₂⁺: BO 2→2.5 but stays paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Removing the single antibonding electron of NO both raises bond order and pairs all electrons.","traps":["Only one of the two conditions checked: Check bond order AND magnetism for each option.","O₂⁺ assumed diamagnetic: O₂⁺ has 11 valence electrons: one unpaired π* electron remains."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Resonance","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Resonance in carbonate ion (CO₃²⁻) is shown below (three resonance structures, linked by ↔).\nWhich of the following is true?","options":["It is possible to identify each structure individually by some physical or chemical method.","All these structures are in dynamic equilibrium with each other.","Each structure exists for equal amount of time.","CO₃²⁻ has a single structure i.e., resonance hybrid of the above three structures."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) canonical forms cannot be isolated or detected. (B) there is no equilibrium between them (that would be tautomerism). (C) no time-sharing between forms.\nWorking: the canonical forms are not real; CO₃²⁻ exists only as one resonance hybrid with three equal C–O bonds, so only (D) is true.\nKey idea: Resonance is not an equilibrium; the carbonate ion is one hybrid with three equal C–O bonds.","traps":["Resonance confused with tautomerism: Resonance ↔ is not ⇌; only the hybrid exists."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S1C06-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Bond parameters","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"O–O bond length in H₂O₂ is X̲ than the O–O bond length in F₂O₂. The O–H bond length in H₂O₂ is Y̲ than that of the O–F bond in F₂O₂. Choose the correct option for X̲ and Y̲ from those given below :","options":["X - shorter, Y - longer","X - longer, Y - shorter","X - shorter, Y - shorter","X - longer, Y - longer"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses both comparisons: it assumes F lengthens the O–O bond in F₂O₂ and that O–H is longer than O–F. (C) gets Y right only if one thinks both bonds in H₂O₂ are short. (D) gets X right but assumes O–H longer than O–F by size of atom (F larger than H), missing that O–H is short anyway.\nWorking: O–O in H₂O₂ ≈ 147.5 pm vs F₂O₂ ≈ 121.7 pm, so X = longer; O–H ≈ 95 pm vs O–F in F₂O₂ ≈ 157.5 pm, so Y = shorter.\nKey idea: In F₂O₂ the O–O bond is almost as short as in O₂ (≈122 pm) and the O–F bonds are long (≈158 pm); in H₂O₂ O–O is a normal single bond (≈148 pm) and O–H is short (≈95 pm).","traps":["F₂O₂ assumed to have longer O–O than H₂O₂: Recall F₂O₂ O–O ≈ 122 pm, close to O₂; F withdraws antibonding density.","O–H vs O–F judged only by atomic radius: Compare actual values: O–H ~95 pm is the shortest bond to O; O–F in F₂O₂ is abnormally long."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Decreasing order of the hydrogen bonding in following forms of water is correctly represented by\nA. Liquid water\nB. Ice\nC. Impure water\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A = B > C","B > A > C","A > B > C","C > B > A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) A = B: assumes liquid water keeps the full ice network. (C) A > B: links stronger H-bonding with higher density of liquid water. (D) reverses the order, thinking impurities add H-bonding partners.\nWorking: ice has each H₂O in four H-bonds in an open tetrahedral lattice (maximum); liquid water has partly broken H-bonds; dissolved impurities disrupt the H-bond network further.\nKey idea: More order → more H-bonds: ice > liquid > impure liquid.","traps":["Density anomaly confused with H-bond extent: Ice is less dense precisely because it is fully H-bonded in an open cage.","Impurity assumed to add H-bonds: Solutes break the water–water H-bond network."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Fajans' rules","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Order of Covalent bond;\nA. KF > KI; LiF > KF\nB. KF < KI; LiF > KF\nC. SnCl₄ > SnCl₂; CuCl > NaCl\nD. LiF > KF; CuCl < NaCl\nE. KF < KI; CuCl > NaCl\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["B, C only","A, B only","C, E only","B, C, E only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: KF > KI is reversed (anion size). D: CuCl < NaCl ignores the 18-electron Cu⁺ effect. Options (A) and (C) each omit one correct statement; (B) includes A.\nWorking: larger anion I⁻ → KI more covalent than KF; smaller Li⁺ → LiF more covalent than KF; Sn⁴⁺ more polarising than Sn²⁺; Cu⁺ (18-electron shell) more polarising than Na⁺. So B, C, E correct; A and D each contain one wrong pair.\nKey idea: Covalent character: higher cation charge, smaller cation, larger anion, d¹⁰ cation all increase it.","traps":["Anion size effect reversed: Larger, softer anion (I⁻) is more polarisable → more covalent.","Cu⁺ vs Na⁺ of similar size: 18-electron shell shields poorly → Cu⁺ more polarising."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"What is the number of unpaired electron(s) in the highest occupied molecular orbital of the following species : N₂; N₂⁺; O₂; O₂⁺ ?","options":["0, 1, 0, 1","2, 1, 0, 1","0, 1, 2, 1","2, 1, 2, 1"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses the two unpaired π* electrons in O₂. (B)/(D) treat N₂ HOMO as degenerate π orbitals with 2 unpaired electrons.\nWorking: N₂ HOMO σ2pz² → 0; N₂⁺ σ2pz¹ → 1; O₂ π*2pₓ¹ π*2p_y¹ → 2; O₂⁺ π*2p¹ → 1.\nKey idea: For N₂ the HOMO is σ2pz (filled); for O₂ it is the degenerate π* pair, half-filled.","traps":["N₂ HOMO identity: For Z ≤ 7 σ2pz lies above π2p.","O₂ unpaired count: Hund's rule in degenerate π*."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Lewis structures","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The total number of lone pairs of electrons on oxygen atoms of ozone is ________.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 or 8 if each O is given 2–3 pairs without checking 18 electrons; 5 if the central O's lone pair is missed; 9 if three lone pairs put on every O.\nWorking: central O: 1 lone pair; O with double bond: 2; O with single bond: 3 → 6.\nKey idea: 18 valence electrons − 3 bonding pairs (6 e⁻) = 12 e⁻ = 6 lone pairs.","traps":["Central O lone pair missed: Count all valence electrons first: 18 total, 6 in bonds."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The magnetic behaviour of Li₂O, Na₂O₂ and KO₂, respectively, are :","options":["paramagnetic, paramagnetic and diamagnetic","paramagnetic, diamagnetic and paramagnetic","diamagnetic, paramagnetic and diamagnetic","diamagnetic, diamagnetic and paramagnetic"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) call Li₂O paramagnetic (confusing O²⁻ with an odd-electron ion); (C) calls peroxide paramagnetic and superoxide diamagnetic, i.e. swaps O₂²⁻ and O₂⁻.\nWorking: Li₂O contains O²⁻ (all paired) → diamagnetic; Na₂O₂ contains O₂²⁻ (π* full) → diamagnetic; KO₂ contains O₂⁻ (one unpaired π* electron) → paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Superoxide O₂⁻ has 13 valence electrons in O₂ MOs → one unpaired; O²⁻ and O₂²⁻ are fully paired.","traps":["Peroxide vs superoxide magnetism: Count electrons: O₂²⁻ has 18 e (π* full), O₂⁻ has 17 e (one unpaired)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The number of molecules or ions from the following, which do not have odd number of electrons are _________.\n(A) NO₂\n(B) ICl₄⁻\n(C) BrF₃\n(D) ClO₂\n(E) NO₂⁺\n(F) NO","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (forgetting the extra electron of ICl₄⁻ or treating it as odd); 4 (counting ClO₂ as even).\nWorking: valence electrons – NO₂ 17 (odd), ICl₄⁻ 36, BrF₃ 28, ClO₂ 19 (odd), NO₂⁺ 16, NO 11 (odd) → 3 even-electron species.\nKey idea: Add one electron for each negative charge, subtract for positive.","traps":["Charge changes electron parity: Adjust electron count for charge before checking parity.","Question asks for 'not odd': Count the even-electron species."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"According to MO theory the bond orders for O₂²⁻, CO and NO⁺ respectively, are","options":["1, 3 and 3","2, 3 and 3","1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) uses BO 2 for O₂²⁻ (that of neutral O₂); (C) gives CO bond order 2 (counting the drawn C=O of organic compounds); (D) gives NO⁺ BO 2 (confusing with NO⁻ or O₂).\nWorking: O₂²⁻ (18 e⁻) BO 1; CO (14 e⁻) BO 3; NO⁺ (14 e⁻) BO 3.\nKey idea: Isoelectronic species have the same bond order: 14 e⁻ → 3, 18 e⁻ → 1.","traps":["Charge changes electron count: Count total electrons first, then fill."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"For OF₂ molecule consider the following: A. Number of lone pairs on oxygen is 2. B. FOF angle is less than 104.5°. C. Oxidation state of O is −2. D. Molecule is bent 'V' shaped. E. Molecular geometry is linear. Correct options are:","options":["A, C, D only","A, B, D only","C, D, E only","B, E, A only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"C: default −2 for oxygen (false, it is +2). E: linear (ignores two lone pairs). Statement B tests that OF₂ (≈103°) is narrower than H₂O.\nWorking: O has 2 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs → bent (A, D true); F–O–F ≈ 103° < 104.5° (B true); O is +2 (C false); not linear (E false).\nKey idea: OF₂ is AB₂E₂: bent, two lone pairs on O, angle ≈103° (< 104.5°), O is +2 because F is more electronegative.","traps":["Oxygen always −2: Oxygen bonded to fluorine has a positive oxidation number (+2 in OF₂).","Bond angle vs water: Electronegative F pulls bonding pairs away, so lone pairs squeeze the angle to ≈103°."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List I (molecules/ions) with List II (No. of lone pairs of e⁻ on central atom). List I: A. IF₇; B. ICl₄⁻; C. XeF₆; D. XeF₂. List II: I. Three; II. One; III. Two; IV. Zero. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A - II, B - I, C - IV, D - III","A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III","A - IV, B - III, C - II, D - I","A - II, B - III, C - IV, D - I"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A/D: give IF₇ one lone pair (I has 7 valence electrons, all used). B: gives ICl₄⁻ three. Swapping XeF₂/XeF₆ counts.\nWorking: IF₇ 0 (IV), ICl₄⁻ 2 (III), XeF₆ 1 (II), XeF₂ 3 (I).\nKey idea: Lone pairs = (valence e⁻ ± charge − bonds)/2: IF₇ 0, ICl₄⁻ 2, XeF₆ 1, XeF₂ 3.","traps":["Charge forgotten in ICl₄⁻: Add the charge to I's 7 valence electrons: (8 − 4)/2 = 2.","XeF₂ vs XeF₆ lone pairs: Xe has 8 valence e⁻: XeF₂ leaves 6 (3 pairs), XeF₆ leaves 2 (1 pair)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Boric acid is solid, whereas BF₃ is gas at room temperature because of :","options":["Strong van der Waal's interaction in Boric acid","Strong covalent bond in BF₃","Strong hydrogen bond in Boric acid","Strong ionic bond in Boric acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): van der Waals – present but not dominant; (B): intramolecular bond strength does not decide physical state; (D): boric acid is covalent, not ionic.\nWorking: boric acid molecules are linked by intermolecular O–H···O hydrogen bonds into layers, so it is a solid; BF₃ has only weak dispersion forces.\nKey idea: B(OH)₃ units link by O–H···O hydrogen bonds into layers; BF₃ has only weak dispersion forces.","traps":["Intramolecular vs intermolecular forces: Physical state depends on forces between molecules."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I: A. XeF₄; B. SF₄; C. NH₄⁺; D. BrF₃. List II: I. See-saw; II. Square-planar; III. Bent T-shaped; IV. Tetrahedral. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A - II, B - I, C - III, D - IV","A - II, B - I, C - IV, D - III","A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III","A - IV, B - III, C - II, D - I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps C and D: NH₄⁺ is not bent T-shaped. (C) treats XeF₄ as tetrahedral (ignoring its two lone pairs) and NH₄⁺ as square planar. (D) shifts three pairs.\nWorking: XeF₄ square planar (II), SF₄ see-saw (I), NH₄⁺ tetrahedral (IV), BrF₃ bent T-shaped (III).\nKey idea: Count lone pairs: 2 lp on octahedral base → square planar; 1 lp on tbp → see-saw; 2 lp on tbp → T-shape.","traps":["Four-coordinate = tetrahedral: Count lone pairs before naming the shape.","One pair shifted: Check NH₄⁺ (no lone pair) last: tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Amongst the following, the number of species having the linear shape is ________. XeF₂, I₃⁺, C₃O₂, I₃⁻, CO₂, SO₂, BeCl₂ and BCl₂⁻","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (counting BCl₂⁻ as linear like BeCl₂), 7 (also counting I₃⁺ as linear like I₃⁻), 4 (missing C₃O₂ or XeF₂).\nWorking: linear – XeF₂, C₃O₂, I₃⁻, CO₂, BeCl₂ (5); bent – I₃⁺ (2 bp + 2 lp), SO₂, BCl₂⁻ (2 bp + 1 lp).\nKey idea: I₃⁻ and XeF₂ (AB₂E₃) are linear; I₃⁺ (AB₂E₂) and BCl₂⁻ (AB₂E) are bent.","traps":["I₃⁺ vs I₃⁻: Count lone pairs on central I: 2 in I₃⁺, 3 in I₃⁻.","BCl₂⁻ like BeCl₂: B⁻ is isoelectronic with C: one lone pair → bent."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Among the following species N₂, N₂⁺, N₂⁻, N₂²⁻, O₂, O₂⁺, O₂⁻, O₂²⁻ the number of species showing diamagnetism is ____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (counting N₂²⁻ as diamagnetic because even-electron); 1 (forgetting O₂²⁻); 4.\nWorking: N₂ (14 e⁻) and O₂²⁻ (18 e⁻) have all electrons paired; N₂⁺, N₂⁻, O₂⁺, O₂⁻ are odd-electron; N₂²⁻ (16 e⁻, isoelectronic with O₂) and O₂ have two unpaired π* electrons.\nKey idea: Even electron count is not enough: 16-electron species have two unpaired π* electrons.","traps":["Even electrons ≠ diamagnetic: 16 e⁻ species (O₂, N₂²⁻) have π*¹π*¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Compound): (A) BrF₅ (B) [CrF₆]³⁻ (C) O₃ (D) PCl₅\nList-II (Shape): (I) bent (II) square pyramidal (III) trigonal bipyramidal (IV) octahedral\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives BrF₅ bent and O₃ trigonal bipyramidal – random pairing; (B) gives BrF₅ octahedral (counting electron-pair geometry, not shape); (D) gives BrF₅ trigonal bipyramidal (ignoring the lone pair, treating it like PCl₅).\nWorking: BrF₅ has 5 bp + 1 lp → square pyramidal (II); [CrF₆]³⁻ has six ligands, d²sp³ → octahedral (IV); O₃ has 2 σ-bond domains + 1 lp on the central O → bent (I); PCl₅ has 5 bp → trigonal bipyramidal (III).\nKey idea: Shape counts atoms only: AB₅E → square pyramidal, AB₂E → bent.","traps":["BrF₅ taken as octahedral or trigonal bipyramidal: Name the shape from bonded atoms only.","O₃ lone pair missed: Central O: 2 σ domains + 1 lone pair → bent."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Fajans' rules","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Arrange the following in increasing order of their covalent character.\n(A) CaF₂ (B) CaCl₂ (C) CaBr₂ (D) CaI₂\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["B < A < C < D","A < B < C < D","A < B < D < C","A < C < B < D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts CaCl₂ below CaF₂; (C) and (D) swap one pair – partial ordering errors.\nWorking: same cation; covalent character rises with anion size/polarisability F⁻ < Cl⁻ < Br⁻ < I⁻ (Fajans).\nKey idea: Fixed cation → bigger anion = more covalent.","traps":["Compound letters vs option letters: Write the order with formulas first, then match.","Ionic vs covalent direction: Covalent character increases as anion size increases."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Amongst the following the number of oxide(s) which are paramagnetic in nature is\nNa₂O, KO₂, NO₂, N₂O, ClO₂, NO, SO₂, Cl₂O","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (missing KO₂ — treated as an ordinary ionic oxide), 2 (missing ClO₂ too), 5 (counting N₂O or SO₂).\nWorking: paramagnetic (odd electron): KO₂ (O₂⁻ has one unpaired e⁻), NO₂, ClO₂, NO → 4; Na₂O, N₂O, SO₂, Cl₂O have all electrons paired.\nKey idea: Count valence electrons: an odd total means paramagnetic; for KO₂ look at the O₂⁻ ion (13 valence electrons, one unpaired).","traps":["KO₂ missed: O₂⁻ superoxide has one unpaired electron.","ClO₂ missed: ClO₂ has 19 valence electrons."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"According to MO theory, number of species/ions from the following having identical bond order is_____: CN⁻, NO⁺, O₂, O₂⁺, O₂²⁺","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (missing O₂²⁺), 1, 5.\nWorking: CN⁻ (14 e⁻) 3, NO⁺ (14 e⁻) 3, O₂ 2, O₂⁺ 2.5, O₂²⁺ (14 e⁻) 3 → three species share bond order 3.\nKey idea: 14-electron diatomics (N₂, CO, CN⁻, NO⁺, O₂²⁺) all have bond order 3.","traps":["O₂²⁺ miscounted: O₂²⁺ has 14 e⁻ like N₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) Ψ_MO = Ψ_A − Ψ_B; (B) μ = Q × r; (C) (N_b − N_a)/2; (D) Ψ_MO = Ψ_A + Ψ_B. List-II: (I) Dipole moment; (II) Bonding molecular orbital; (III) Anti-bonding molecular orbital; (IV) Bond order.","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps bonding and antibonding; (B) and (D) pair μ with bond order and bond order with dipole moment.\nWorking: subtraction of wave functions gives the antibonding MO (III); Q × r is dipole moment (I); half the difference of bonding and antibonding electrons is bond order (IV); addition gives the bonding MO (II).\nKey idea: Addition of wave functions = bonding; subtraction = antibonding.","traps":["Plus/minus combination swapped: Constructive (+) overlap = bonding, (−) = antibonding with a node.","Dipole moment vs bond order formula: μ = charge × distance; bond order uses electron counts."],"topics":["MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","Dipole moment"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of molecule(s) or ion(s) from the following having non-planar structure is ______. NO₃⁻, H₂O₂, BF₃, PCl₃, XeF₄, SF₄, XeO₃, PH₄⁺, SO₃, [Al(OH)₄]⁻","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 5 (H₂O₂ taken as planar); 7 (XeF₄ taken as non-planar because of 6 electron pairs).\nWorking: planar – NO₃⁻ (trigonal planar), BF₃ (trigonal planar), XeF₄ (square planar), SO₃ (trigonal planar). Non-planar – H₂O₂ (open book), PCl₃ (pyramidal), SF₄ (see-saw), XeO₃ (pyramidal), PH₄⁺ (tetrahedral), [Al(OH)₄]⁻ (tetrahedral) = 6.\nKey idea: Lone pairs can make geometry planar (XeF₄) or non-planar (PCl₃, XeO₃).","traps":["XeF₄ taken as non-planar: Two lone pairs trans → square planar shape.","H₂O₂ open-book structure: Dihedral angle ~111° (gas) makes it non-planar."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of paramagnetic species among the following is ___________. B₂, Li₂, C₂, C₂⁻, O₂²⁻, O₂⁺ and He₂⁺","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 if B₂ is filled with the O₂-type order (σ2p before π2p, giving σ2p² diamagnetic); 5 if C₂ is taken as paramagnetic; odd-electron species are always paramagnetic.\nWorking: paramagnetic are B₂ (π2p¹ π2p¹), C₂⁻ (one unpaired σ2p electron), O₂⁺ (one π* electron), He₂⁺ (σ*1s¹) = 4; Li₂, C₂ and O₂²⁻ are diamagnetic.\nKey idea: Any odd-electron species is paramagnetic; for B₂ the π2p level lies below σ2p.","traps":["MO order for B₂ and C₂: Use π2p below σ2p up to N₂.","Odd electrons: Odd total electrons → at least one unpaired."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which of the following pair of molecules contain odd electron molecule and an expanded octet molecule?","options":["BCl₃ and SF₆","NO and H₂SO₄","SF₆ and H₂SO₄","BCl₃ and NO"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) BCl₃ is incomplete octet, not odd-electron; (C) both expanded octet, no odd-electron species; (D) BCl₃ is incomplete octet, not expanded.\nWorking: NO has 11 valence electrons (odd-electron molecule); S in H₂SO₄ has 12 electrons around it in the usual Lewis structure (expanded octet). BCl₃ is an incomplete octet; SF₆ is expanded but not odd-electron.\nKey idea: Odd-electron = odd total valence electrons (NO); expanded octet = central atom of period 3+ with more than 8 electrons (S in H₂SO₄).","traps":["Incomplete octet confused with odd electron: Count total valence electrons: BCl₃ has 24 (even)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider PF₅, BrF₅, PCl₃, SF₆, [ICl₄]⁻, ClF₃ and IF₅. Amongst the above molecule(s)/ion(s), the number of molecule(s)/ion(s) having sp³d² hybridisation is ____.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (missing [ICl₄]⁻ – lone pairs ignored); 5 (counting ClF₃ or PF₅ as sp³d²).\nWorking: sp³d² (6 electron pairs): BrF₅ (5 bp + 1 lp), SF₆ (6 bp), [ICl₄]⁻ (4 bp + 2 lp), IF₅ (5 bp + 1 lp) = 4. PF₅ and ClF₃ are sp³d; PCl₃ is sp³.\nKey idea: Steric number 6 ⇔ sp³d².","traps":["Lone pairs on [ICl₄]⁻ missed: Steric number = bp + lp = 6.","ClF₃ taken as sp³d²: ClF₃: 3 bp + 2 lp = 5 → sp³d."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct order of bond orders of C₂²⁻, N₂²⁻ and O₂²⁻ is, respectively,","options":["C₂²⁻ < N₂²⁻ < O₂²⁻","O₂²⁻ < N₂²⁻ < C₂²⁻","C₂²⁻ < O₂²⁻ < N₂²⁻","N₂²⁻ < C₂²⁻ < O₂²⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – reverse order (thinking more electrons = stronger bond); (C)/(D) – one species misplaced, usually N₂²⁻ treated as N₂ (BO 3).\nWorking: C₂²⁻ (14 e⁻, isoelectronic with N₂) BO 3; N₂²⁻ (16 e⁻, like O₂) BO 2; O₂²⁻ (18 e⁻) BO 1. So O₂²⁻ < N₂²⁻ < C₂²⁻.\nKey idea: Isoelectronic: C₂²⁻ ≈ N₂, N₂²⁻ ≈ O₂, O₂²⁻ ≈ F₂.","traps":["More electrons read as higher bond order: Extra electrons beyond 14 go to antibonding π*; bond order falls.","N₂²⁻ given bond order of N₂: Count electrons: N₂²⁻ has 16, same as O₂, BO 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Bonding in which of the following diatomic molecule(s) become(s) stronger, on the basis of MO Theory, by removal of an electron? (A) NO (B) N₂ (C) O₂ (D) C₂ (E) B₂. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (B), (C) only","(B), (C), (E) only","(A), (C) only","(D) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) includes N₂ – assumes all cations get stronger or forgets N₂ loses a bonding σ2p electron; (B) includes N₂ and B₂ – reverses the logic; (D) C₂ only – thinks loss from π2p is antibonding.\nWorking: removing an electron raises bond order only when the electron leaves an antibonding orbital. NO (π*¹, BO 2.5 → 3) and O₂ (π*², BO 2 → 2.5) get stronger; N₂ (3 → 2.5), C₂ (2 → 1.5) and B₂ (1 → 0.5) lose a bonding electron and get weaker. So (A) and (C) only = option (C).\nKey idea: Bond strengthens on ionisation only if the electron removed is antibonding: true for NO and O₂ (π* electrons).","traps":["NO HOMO treated as bonding: Count 15 electrons: last one enters π*2p.","C₂ and B₂ HOMO are bonding π orbitals: For B₂, C₂, N₂ the HOMO is bonding; removing it weakens the bond."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Number of electron deficient molecules among the following PH₃, B₂H₆, CCl₄, NH₃, LiH and BCl₃ is","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"1 – counts only BCl₃ (thinks B₂H₆ satisfies octets via bridges); 3 – adds LiH (Li has only 2 electrons) though it is ionic; 0 – overlooks boron.\nWorking: B₂H₆ (too few electrons for normal 2c-2e bonds; 3c-2e bridges) and BCl₃ (six electrons around B) are electron deficient. PH₃, NH₃ are electron-rich (lone pair), CCl₄ is electron-precise, LiH is ionic. Count = 2.\nKey idea: Electron deficient = group 13 central atom with too few valence electrons: B₂H₆ and BCl₃.","traps":["LiH counted as electron deficient: LiH is an ionic (saline) hydride, not a molecule with a deficient centre.","B₂H₆ bridges thought to fix deficiency: Bridges are 3c-2e bonds precisely because electrons are short."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Amongst BeF₂, BF₃, H₂O, NH₃, CCl₄ and HCl, the number of molecules with non-zero net dipole moment is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (counting BF₃ or BeF₂ because B–F/Be–F bonds are polar), 2 (forgetting HCl).\nWorking: BeF₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CCl₄ (tetrahedral) have μ = 0; H₂O (bent), NH₃ (pyramidal), HCl (polar diatomic) have μ ≠ 0 → 3.\nKey idea: Polar bonds cancel in symmetric shapes (linear AB₂, trigonal planar AB₃, tetrahedral AB₄).","traps":["Polar bonds but zero net dipole: Check symmetry of the shape."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Consider the ions/molecule O₂⁺, O₂, O₂⁻, O₂²⁻. For increasing bond order the correct option is :","options":["O₂²⁻ < O₂⁻ < O₂ < O₂⁺","O₂⁻ < O₂²⁻ < O₂ < O₂⁺","O₂⁻ < O₂²⁻ < O₂⁺ < O₂","O₂⁻ < O₂⁺ < O₂²⁻ < O₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) misplace O₂⁻ and O₂²⁻ (thinking more charge = more bonding) or place O₂⁺ below O₂ (removing an electron taken as weakening).\nWorking: MO bond orders O₂⁺ 2.5, O₂ 2, O₂⁻ 1.5, O₂²⁻ 1; increasing order O₂²⁻ < O₂⁻ < O₂ < O₂⁺ = (A).\nKey idea: Every electron removed from O₂ comes out of π*, raising bond order by 0.5; every electron added lowers it by 0.5.","traps":["Removing electron thought to weaken bond: O₂ loses a π* (antibonding) electron.","Peroxide vs superoxide order swapped: More added electrons → lower bond order."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Octet rule exceptions","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The oxide which contains an odd electron at the nitrogen atom is","options":["N₂O","NO₂","N₂O₃","N₂O₅"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) – even-electron oxides; N₂O₃ may tempt as the dimer of NO + NO₂.\nWorking: NO₂ has 5 + 12 = 17 valence electrons, an odd-electron molecule with the unpaired electron mainly on N; N₂O (16), N₂O₃ (28), N₂O₅ (40) are even-electron.\nKey idea: Odd total electron count → radical; only NO₂ among the options.","traps":["N₂O₃ thought to keep NO radical: Coupling of two radicals pairs the odd electrons; count total valence electrons."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Based upon VSEPR theory, match the shape (geometry) of the molecules in List-I with the molecules in List-II and select the most appropriate option. List-I (Shape): (A) T-shaped, (B) Trigonal planar, (C) Square planar, (D) See-saw. List-II (Molecules): (I) XeF₄, (II) SF₄, (III) ClF₃, (IV) BF₃.","options":["(A)–(I), (B)–(II), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(III), (B)–(IV), (C)–(I), (D)–(II)","(A)–(III), (B)–(IV), (C)–(II), (D)–(I)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(III), (C)–(I), (D)–(II)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – matches in the listed order with no chemistry; (C) – swaps XeF₄ and SF₄ (both AX₄ type, differ in lone pairs); (D) – gives BF₃ as T-shaped, ignoring that B has no lone pair.\nWorking: ClF₃ (3 bp + 2 lp) is T-shaped; BF₃ (3 bp) trigonal planar; XeF₄ (4 bp + 2 lp) square planar; SF₄ (4 bp + 1 lp) see-saw → A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II = option (B).\nKey idea: Count lone pairs on the central atom: 0 → planar BF₃, 1 → see-saw SF₄, 2 → T-shape (ClF₃) or square planar (XeF₄).","traps":["XeF₄ vs SF₄ shape: SF₄ has one lone pair (see-saw); XeF₄ has two (square planar).","Options differ in one pair: Check every pairing, not just the first two."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Identify the incorrect statement for PCl₅ from the following.","options":["In this molecule, orbitals of phosphorus are assumed to undergo sp³d hybridization.","The geometry of PCl₅ is trigonal bipyramidal.","PCl₅ has two axial bonds stronger than three equatorial bonds.","The three equatorial bonds of PCl₅ lie in a plane."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are true NCERT statements put in to be rejected; the trap is overlooking the flipped comparative in (C).\nWorking: axial P–Cl bonds suffer more repulsion from the equatorial bond pairs, so they are longer and weaker; (C) is false. sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal and a planar equatorial set are all correct.\nKey idea: Axial = more repulsion = longer = weaker.","traps":["\"Stronger\" read past: Check each comparative word against NCERT: axial bonds are weaker.","Asked for the incorrect statement: Underline \"incorrect\" before reading options."],"topics":["Hybridisation","VSEPR shapes","Bond parameters"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The correct order of increasing intermolecular hydrogen bond strength is","options":["HCN < H₂O < NH₃","HCN < CH₄ < NH₃","CH₄ < HCN < NH₃","CH₄ < NH₃ < HCN"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts H₂O below NH₃ (O–H···O is stronger); (B) puts CH₄ above HCN; (D) puts HCN above NH₃ by its higher polarity.\nWorking: CH₄ forms no hydrogen bond; HCN forms weak C–H···N hydrogen bonds (acidic C–H, sp carbon); N–H···N in NH₃ is stronger. (A) is wrong because H₂O > NH₃.\nKey idea: sp C–H in HCN is acidic enough to donate a weak H-bond to N; CH₄ cannot.","traps":["HCN assumed to have no H-bond: An sp C–H next to a nitrile is acidic and H-bonds weakly.","Polarity confused with H-bond strength: Rank by donor H–X polarity and acceptor lone pair, not overall dipole."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The hybridization of P exhibited in PF₅ is spˣdʸ. The value of y is ______.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (giving x instead of y), 2 (sp³d² confusion).\nWorking: P has 5 bond pairs, no lone pair → sp³d, so x = 3, y = 1.\nKey idea: Steric number 5 → sp³d.","traps":["x and y swapped: y is the d exponent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"In the structure of SF₄, the lone pair of electrons on S is in:","options":["equatorial position and there are two lone pair–bond pair repulsions at 90°","equatorial position and there are three lone pair–bond pair repulsions at 90°","axial position and there are three lone pair–bond pair repulsions at 90°","axial position and there are two lone pair–bond pair repulsions at 90°"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) right position, wrong count (three is for axial); (C) is the rejected axial structure; (D) mixes axial with the equatorial count.\nWorking: in a trigonal bipyramid an equatorial lone pair is at 90° to the two axial bonds only (2 lp–bp at 90°); an axial lone pair would have three, so the equatorial site (see-saw shape) is preferred.\nKey idea: Equatorial site: 90° to 2 axial positions; axial site: 90° to 3 equatorial positions.","traps":["Equatorial vs axial 90° neighbours: An equatorial site has only two 90° neighbours."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Fajans' rules","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Arrange the following in the decreasing order of their covalent character : (A) LiCl (B) NaCl (C) KCl (D) CsCl. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) > (C) > (B) > (D)","(B) > (A) > (C) > (D)","(A) > (B) > (C) > (D)","(A) > (B) > (D) > (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) swap one adjacent pair (Li/Na, Na/K or K/Cs) – partial knowledge of the trend.\nWorking: same anion; cation size Li⁺ < Na⁺ < K⁺ < Cs⁺, so polarising power and covalent character fall LiCl > NaCl > KCl > CsCl (Fajans).\nKey idea: Smaller cation, higher polarising power, more covalent character.","traps":["Adjacent pair swapped: Order follows cation radius strictly."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Consider the species CH₄, NH₄⁺ and BH₄⁻. Choose the correct option with respect to these species:","options":["They are isoelectronic and only two have tetrahedral structures","They are isoelectronic and all have tetrahedral structures","Only two are isoelectronic and all have tetrahedral structures","Only two are isoelectronic and only two have tetrahedral structures"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): thinking BH₄⁻ (electron-deficient boron) is not tetrahedral; (C)/(D): forgetting to add/subtract the ionic charge when counting electrons.\nWorking: each has 10 electrons (C 6+4, N 7+4−1, B 5+4+1) and four bond pairs on an sp³ centre, so all three are tetrahedral.\nKey idea: Add electrons for anionic charge, subtract for cationic; four bond pairs, no lone pair → tetrahedral.","traps":["Charge ignored in electron count: Total electrons = Σ Z − charge.","Boron assumed trigonal: BH₄⁻ has 4 bond pairs → sp³."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Number of lone pair(s) of electrons on central atom and the shape of BrF₃ molecule respectively, are:","options":["0, triangular planar.","1, pyramidal.","2, bent T-shape.","1, bent T-shape"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) right shape, wrong lone pair count; (A) treats BrF₃ like BF₃; (B) treats it like NH₃.\nWorking: Br has 7 valence electrons, 3 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs (sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal); lone pairs equatorial → bent T-shape.\nKey idea: Lone pairs = (valence e⁻ − bonds)/2 = (7 − 3)/2 = 2.","traps":["Lone pair count with right shape: Compute (V − B)/2.","Analogy with BF₃: Br has 7 valence electrons, B has 3."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The bond order and magnetic behaviour of O₂⁻ ion are, respectively:","options":["1.5 and paramagnetic","1.5 and diamagnetic","2 and diamagnetic","1 and paramagnetic"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) right bond order but forgets the odd electron; (C) neutral O₂ bond order with wrong magnetism; (D) peroxide bond order.\nWorking: O₂⁻ (17 e⁻) has π*2p³ → bond order (10 – 7)/2 = 1.5 and one unpaired electron, so paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Any odd-electron species is paramagnetic.","traps":["Odd electron count means paramagnetic: 17 electrons → at least one unpaired."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The compound/s which will show significant intermolecular H-bonding is/are: (a) o-nitrophenol (2-nitrophenol); (b) 4-hydroxyacetanilide (paracetamol), HO–C₆H₄–NH–CO–CH₃ (para); (c) 2,6-di-tert-butyl-4-methylphenol (BHT) – OH flanked by two tert-butyl groups, CH₃ para to OH","options":["(b) only","(c) only","(a) and (b) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(B) count (c) because it has an OH, ignoring the tert-butyl shielding; (C) counts o-nitrophenol, whose H-bond is intramolecular.\nWorking: (a) forms an intramolecular H-bond (OH···O–N) so intermolecular association is small; (b) has free para OH and N–H/C=O groups that link molecules intermolecularly; (c) the OH is shielded by two bulky ortho tert-butyl groups, so intermolecular H-bonding is sterically blocked.\nKey idea: An OH group is not enough: it must be free (not chelated) and accessible (not sterically buried).","traps":["Every OH counted as H-bonding: Check steric access: two ortho t-Bu groups block intermolecular H-bonds.","o-Nitrophenol intra vs inter: ortho-NO₂/OH forms a six-membered chelate – intramolecular."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The number of species having non-pyramidal shape among the following is _______. (A) SO₃ (B) NO₃⁻ (C) PCl₃ (D) CO₃²⁻","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (counting pyramidal instead of non-pyramidal); 2 (thinking SO₃ has a lone pair like SO₃²⁻); 4 (missing the P lone pair).\nWorking: SO₃, NO₃⁻ and CO₃²⁻ have three bond pairs and no lone pair on the central atom – trigonal planar; PCl₃ has 3 bp + 1 lp – trigonal pyramidal. Non-pyramidal = 3.\nKey idea: SO₃ (S⁶⁺, no lp) is planar; SO₃²⁻ would be pyramidal – read the charge.","traps":["SO₃ vs SO₃²⁻: Neutral SO₃ has no lone pair on S – planar.","Counting the wrong category: Count planar species, not pyramidal."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"According to molecular orbital theory, the number of unpaired electron(s) in O₂²⁻ is:","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (O₂ value), 1 (superoxide value).\nWorking: O₂²⁻ has 18 electrons; π*2p fully filled (4 e⁻), so no unpaired electrons (bond order 1, diamagnetic).\nKey idea: O₂ (2 unpaired) → O₂⁻ (1) → O₂²⁻ (0).","traps":["Using O₂ value: Add the two extra electrons into π*."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following are isostructural pairs? A. SO₄²⁻ and CrO₄²⁻ B. SiCl₄ and TiCl₄ C. NH₃ and NO₃⁻ D. BCl₃ and BrCl₃","options":["C and D only","A and B only","A and C only","B and C only"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"C – NH₃/NO₃⁻ look alike as AX₃ but differ (pyramidal vs planar); D – BCl₃/BrCl₃ differ (planar vs T-shaped); B may be doubted because Ti is a transition metal.\nWorking: SO₄²⁻, CrO₄²⁻, SiCl₄, TiCl₄ are all tetrahedral; NH₃ is trigonal pyramidal while NO₃⁻ is trigonal planar; BCl₃ is trigonal planar while BrCl₃ (3 bp + 2 lp) is T-shaped.\nKey idea: Count lone pairs on the central atom before calling two AX₃ species isostructural.","traps":["Same formula type assumed same shape: Count lone pairs: NH₃ 1 lp, BrCl₃ 2 lp.","Transition-metal species doubted: d⁰ centres with 4 σ-pairs are tetrahedral."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The correct shape and I–I–I bond angles respectively in I₃⁻ ion are:","options":["Distorted trigonal planar; 135° and 90°","T-shaped; 180° and 90°","Trigonal planar; 120°","Linear; 180°"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) T-shape is AB₃E₂ (e.g. ClF₃); (A)/(C) treat it as 3 bonded atoms around I.\nWorking: central I has 2 bond pairs + 3 lone pairs (sp³d, AB₂E₃); lone pairs equatorial → linear, 180°.\nKey idea: Three equatorial lone pairs leave the two bonds axial → linear.","traps":["Counting atoms not electron pairs: Count electron domains on the central I."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Lattice enthalpy","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The correct set from the following in which both pairs are in correct order of melting point is:","options":["LiF > LiCl ; MgO > NaCl","LiCl > LiF ; NaCl > MgO","LiF > LiCl ; NaCl > MgO","LiCl > LiF ; MgO > NaCl"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) – think NaCl > MgO by formula mass; (D) – think LiCl higher due to larger mass.\nWorking: LiF (smaller anion, higher lattice enthalpy, less covalent) melts above LiCl; MgO (2+/2− ions) has far higher lattice enthalpy than NaCl.\nKey idea: Higher charges and smaller ions → higher lattice enthalpy → higher m.p.","traps":["Using molar mass for m.p: For ionic solids compare charges and radii."],"topics":["Lattice enthalpy","Ionic bond"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"According to molecular orbital theory, the species among the following that does not exist is:","options":["He₂⁺","He₂⁻","Be₂","O₂²⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) He₂⁺ and He₂⁻ both have BO 0.5 (He₂⁻ has 5 electrons: σ1s²σ*1s²σ2s¹) – fractional BO is mistaken for non-existence; (D) peroxide ion is BO 1 and well known.\nWorking: bond orders He₂⁺ 0.5, He₂⁻ 0.5, Be₂ 0 (σ1s²σ*1s²σ2s²σ*2s²), O₂²⁻ 1. Only Be₂ (BO = 0) does not exist.\nKey idea: Any positive bond order (even ½) means the species can exist.","traps":["He₂⁻ thought to have BO 0: Count 5 electrons: BO = (3−2)/2 = ½.","Be₂ σ2s/σ*2s cancel missed: Write the configuration: 2 bonding, 2 antibonding in n = 2 → BO 0."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which among the following species has unequal bond lengths?","options":["BF₄⁻","XeF₄","SF₄","SiF₄"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) BF₄⁻ – regular tetrahedron, all B–F equal; (B) XeF₄ – square planar with two lone pairs trans, all Xe–F equal; (D) SiF₄ – regular tetrahedron.\nWorking: SF₄ has 4 bond pairs + 1 lone pair (sp³d, see-saw); the two axial S–F bonds are longer than the two equatorial ones. BF₄⁻ and SiF₄ are regular tetrahedra and XeF₄ is square planar, all with equal bonds.\nKey idea: Only a trigonal-bipyramid-based shape (here see-saw) has two kinds of positions and hence unequal bonds.","traps":["Square planar XeF₄ thought distorted: Lone pairs trans in an octahedron leave all four bonds equivalent.","All AB₄ species assumed tetrahedral: Count lone pairs on the central atom first."],"topics":["VSEPR shapes","Bond parameters"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements: Assertion A: Dipole-dipole interactions are the only non-covalent interactions, resulting in hydrogen bond formation. Reason R: Fluorine is the most electronegative element and hydrogen bonds in HF are symmetrical. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer.","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true R is false","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) accept A because H-bond is often described as strong dipole–dipole; (C) rejects R because H···F and H–F lengths differ in (HF)ₙ.\nWorking: A is false – hydrogen bonding is not \"only\" dipole–dipole (it has partial covalent/charge-transfer character and other contributions). R: F is most electronegative; the official answer treats the H-bond in HF (as in the symmetric F–H–F⁻ / zig-zag chain) as symmetrical – arguable, since in (HF)ₙ chains the covalent H–F and H···F distances differ.\nKey idea: The word \"only\" makes A false; R is taken as a true fact about F and HF.","traps":["\"Only\" overlooked in A: Treat absolute words (only, always) as red flags.","Symmetry of HF bond doubted: The official answer accepts R as true."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: o-Nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding. Statement II: o-Nitrophenol has high melting due to hydrogen bonding.","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are true","Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is true but statement II is false"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) accepts II because \"hydrogen bonding raises m.p.\" is generalised; (A)/(C) doubt the verbatim NCERT statement I.\nWorking: intramolecular H-bond prevents association, so o-nitrophenol is steam volatile and has a lower m.p. (≈318 K) than p-nitrophenol (≈387 K).\nKey idea: Intramolecular H-bond → lower m.p./b.p. and steam volatility.","traps":["All H-bonding raises m.p: Intramolecular chelation lowers m.p."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List-I (Molecule) with List-II (Bond order). List-I: (a) Ne₂ (b) N₂ (c) F₂ (d) O₂. List-II: (i) 1 (ii) 2 (iii) 0 (iv) 3.","options":["(a)→(iii), (b)→(iv), (c)→(i), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(i), (b)→(ii), (c)→(iii), (d)→(iv)","(a)→(ii), (b)→(i), (c)→(iv), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(iv), (b)→(iii), (c)→(ii), (d)→(i)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Options (B)–(D) are cyclic shifts; any one known pair (e.g. N₂ = 3) eliminates all of them.\nWorking: MO bond orders Ne₂ 0, N₂ 3, F₂ 1, O₂ 2.\nKey idea: Bond order = ½(Nb – Na): N₂ 3, O₂ 2, F₂ 1, Ne₂ 0.","traps":["O₂ and F₂ bond orders swapped: Count antibonding π* electrons."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of lone pairs of electrons on the central I atom in I₃⁻ is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (forgetting the −1 charge); 9 (counting all lone pairs on all three atoms); 4 (treating I as having an octet only).\nWorking: central I has 7 + 1 (charge) = 8 valence electrons; two are used in bonds to the terminal I atoms, leaving 6 electrons = 3 lone pairs (sp³d, linear, lone pairs equatorial).\nKey idea: Central I: 8 electrons with charge, 2 bonds → 3 lone pairs; shape linear.","traps":["Charge not added: Add 1 electron for the negative charge.","All lone pairs counted: Count only on the central I."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Which one of the following species doesn't have a magnetic moment of 1.73 BM, (spin only value) ?","options":["O₂⁺","CuI","[Cu(NH₃)₄]Cl₂","O₂⁻"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) O₂⁻ wrongly taken as diamagnetic (confused with peroxide O₂²⁻); (C) Cu²⁺ d⁹ is paramagnetic; (A) O₂⁺ has one π* electron.\nWorking: 1.73 BM = one unpaired electron. O₂⁺ (π*¹), O₂⁻ (π*³) and Cu²⁺ (d⁹) in [Cu(NH₃)₄]Cl₂ each have one; CuI has Cu⁺ (d¹⁰), zero.\nKey idea: 1.73 BM ↔ one unpaired electron; Cu⁺ is d¹⁰ so CuI is diamagnetic.","traps":["Superoxide vs peroxide: O₂⁻ has π*³ (one unpaired); O₂²⁻ has π*⁴ (none).","Copper oxidation state in CuI: Iodide is −1 so Cu is +1, d¹⁰."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The hybridisations of the atomic orbitals of nitrogen in NO₂⁻, NO₂⁺ and NH₄⁺ respectively are.","options":["sp³, sp² and sp","sp, sp² and sp³","sp³, sp and sp²","sp², sp and sp³"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps nitrite and nitronium; (A)/(C) treat NO₂⁻ as sp³ by counting its charge as a lone pair plus the odd electron.\nWorking: NO₂⁻ – 2 σ bonds + 1 lone pair = sp² (bent); NO₂⁺ – 2 σ, no lone pair = sp (linear); NH₄⁺ – 4 σ = sp³.\nKey idea: Steric number = σ bonds + lone pairs: NO₂⁻ 3, NO₂⁺ 2, NH₄⁺ 4.","traps":["Nitrite vs nitronium swapped: NO₂⁺ is isoelectronic with CO₂ (linear, sp); NO₂⁻ keeps one lone pair."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Species): (a) SF₄ (b) IF₅ (c) NO₂⁺ (d) NH₄⁺. List-II (Hybrid Orbitals): (i) sp³d² (ii) d²sp³ (iii) sp³d (iv) sp³ (v) sp. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(v) and (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv) and (d)-(v)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v) and (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii) and (d)-(v)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(ii) d²sp³ is an inner-orbital complex label, not for main-group IF₅; (v)/(iv) swap for NO₂⁺/NH₄⁺.\nWorking: SF₄ 4 bp + 1 lp → sp³d; IF₅ 5 bp + 1 lp → sp³d²; NO₂⁺ linear, 2 σ → sp; NH₄⁺ 4 bp → sp³.\nKey idea: Main-group expanded octets use outer d: sp³d²; d²sp³ is for transition-metal complexes.","traps":["d²sp³ vs sp³d²: Non-metals use outer (n)d → sp³d².","Lone pair ignored in SF₄: Steric number = bp + lp = 5."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Sigma & pi bonds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of sigma bonds in H₃C–CH=CH–C≡C–H is ________.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (C–C only), 12 or 13 (counting π bonds as σ), 9 (missing the terminal ≡C–H).\nWorking: C–C σ bonds 4 (one in each of the single, double and triple links plus the CH₃–C bond) and C–H σ bonds 6 (3 + 1 + 1 + 1) → 10.\nKey idea: Every bond between two atoms has exactly one σ component.","traps":["π bonds counted as σ: One σ per bonded pair regardless of multiplicity.","Missing terminal alkyne H: Count all H: 3+1+1+1 = 6."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"In the following the correct bond order sequence is:","options":["O₂²⁻ > O₂⁺ > O₂⁻ > O₂","O₂⁺ > O₂⁻ > O₂²⁻ > O₂","O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂⁻ > O₂²⁻","O₂ > O₂⁻ > O₂²⁻ > O₂⁺"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts the peroxide first (reversed); (B) and (D) misplace neutral O₂ – picked by someone who treats added electrons as bonding.\nWorking: MO filling of 15, 16, 17, 18 electrons gives bond orders O₂⁺ 2.5, O₂ 2, O₂⁻ 1.5, O₂²⁻ 1, so O₂⁺ > O₂ > O₂⁻ > O₂²⁻.\nKey idea: Each electron added to π* lowers bond order by 0.5; each removed raises it by 0.5.","traps":["Added electrons go into antibonding π*: Start from O₂ = 2 and step ±0.5 per electron.","Order direction: Check the first and last species only."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Resonance","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Identify the species having one π-bond and maximum number of canonical forms from the following:","options":["SO₃","O₂","SO₂","CO₃²⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) SO₃ also has 3 resonance forms but 3 π bonds in the expanded-octet picture; (C) SO₂ has 2 forms; (B) O₂ has one π bond but no canonical forms.\nWorking: CO₃²⁻ has one C=O π bond delocalised over three equivalent canonical forms. O₂ has one π bond but no resonance; SO₂ and SO₃ in the usual expanded-octet Lewis structures have 2 and 3 π bonds (only if strict octet structures are drawn does SO₃ also show one π bond with 3 forms ; the intended answer is CO₃²⁻).\nKey idea: Apply both conditions: exactly one π bond AND most canonical forms.","traps":["Two conditions in one stem: Check π-bond count separately.","Octet vs expanded-octet drawing: Use expanded-octet structures for S as NCERT does."],"topics":["Resonance","Sigma & pi bonds"]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The difference between bond orders of CO and NO⁺ is x/2 where x = _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"0","range":[0.0,0.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 1 (taking NO⁺ as 2.5), 5 or 6 (reporting bond order rather than difference), 2.\nWorking: CO and NO⁺ are isoelectronic (14 electrons), both bond order 3; difference 0 → x = 0.\nKey idea: Same electron count → same bond order.","traps":["Isoelectronic → same bond order: Count electrons: both 14.","NO vs NO⁺: Removing the π* electron raises it to 3."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The total number of electrons in all bonding molecular orbitals of O₂²⁻ is ........ . (Round off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (ignoring σ1s), 6 (valence only and missing σ2s), 18 (all electrons).\nWorking: O₂²⁻ has 18 electrons: σ1s² σ*1s² σ2s² σ*2s² σ2pz² π2px² π2py² π*2px² π*2py²; bonding = 2+2+2+4 = 10 (Nb − Na = 2, bond order 1).\nKey idea: Count core σ1s too unless told 'valence'.","traps":["Include σ1s: NCERT writes σ1s² in the configuration; 'all' bonding MOs."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: The H–O–H bond angle in water molecule is 104.5°. Reason R: The lone pair – lone pair repulsion of electrons is higher than the bond pair – bond pair repulsion.","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – thinks the angle is explained by sp³ hybridisation alone; (A)/(C) – misremember 104.5° (e.g. with NH₃ 107°).\nWorking: H₂O has 2 bp + 2 lp; lp–lp > lp–bp > bp–bp repulsion squeezes the angle from 109.5° to 104.5°.\nKey idea: Two lone pairs compress the tetrahedral angle to 104.5°.","traps":["R true but 'not the explanation': The lp–lp > bp–bp order is exactly why the angle shrinks."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A central atom in a molecule has two lone pairs of electrons and forms three single bonds. The shape of this molecule is:","options":["see-saw","planar triangular","T-shaped","trigonal pyramidal"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) see-saw is AB₄E; (B) planar triangular is AB₃; (D) trigonal pyramidal is AB₃E.\nWorking: 5 electron pairs (AB₃E₂) → trigonal bipyramidal arrangement with both lone pairs equatorial → T-shape (e.g. ClF₃).\nKey idea: Count all electron pairs (5) first, then place lone pairs equatorially.","traps":["Lone pairs ignored in arrangement: Total pairs = bonds + lone pairs.","One lone pair case recalled: Two lone pairs → 5 pairs → trigonal bipyramidal base."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Amongst the following, the linear species is :","options":["NO₂","Cl₂O","O₃","N₃⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NO₂ has one unpaired electron on N → bent (~134°); (B) Cl₂O AB₂E₂ → bent; (C) O₃ AB₂E → bent.\nWorking: N₃⁻ (16 valence electrons, isoelectronic with CO₂) has a central sp N with no lone pair – linear. NO₂ (odd electron), Cl₂O (two lone pairs on O) and O₃ (one lone pair on central O) are bent.\nKey idea: 16-electron triatomics (CO₂, N₃⁻, NO₂⁺, N₂O) are linear.","traps":["NO₂ thought linear like NO₂⁺: Count electrons: NO₂ has 17, an odd electron on N → bent.","Lone pair on central atom ignored: Draw the Lewis structure and count lone pairs on the central atom."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"AX is a covalent diatomic molecule where A and X are second row elements of periodic table. Based on Molecular orbital theory, the bond order of AX is 2.5. The total number of electrons in AX is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 13 (CN, also BO 2.5 – the question is ambiguous), 14 (BO 3, N₂/CO), 16 (BO 2, O₂).\nWorking: bond order 2.5 for a neutral diatomic of two second-row atoms corresponds to 15 electrons, e.g. NO (7 + 8): σ1s²σ*1s²σ2s²σ*2s²σ2p_z²π2p⁴π*2p¹ gives (10 − 5)/2 = 2.5. Note: 13 electrons (CN radical, 6 + 7) also gives bond order 2.5, so the item is ambiguous; the official answer is 15.\nKey idea: BO 2.5 arises for 13 or 15 electrons; a neutral pair of second-row atoms like NO gives 15.","traps":["Two electron counts give BO 2.5: Note the ambiguity; NO is the textbook odd-electron example."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of species below that have two lone pairs of electrons in their central atom is ____ (Round off to the Nearest integer)\nSF₄, BF₄⁻, ClF₃, AsF₃, PCl₅, BrF₅, XeF₄, SF₆","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (counting SF₄ or BrF₅ as two), 1 (missing XeF₄), 5 (counting all with any lone pair).\nWorking: lone pairs on central atom – SF₄ 1, BF₄⁻ 0, ClF₃ 2, AsF₃ 1, PCl₅ 0, BrF₅ 1, XeF₄ 2, SF₆ 0; so ClF₃ and XeF₄ → 2.\nKey idea: Lone pairs = (valence e⁻ − bonds − charge adjustment)/2.","traps":["Any lone pair counted: Count exactly two.","Miscounting BrF₅ / SF₄: Br: 7 − 5 = 2 e⁻ = 1 lp; S: 6 − 4 = 2 e⁻ = 1 lp."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The spin-only magnetic moment value of B₂⁺ species is ______ × 10⁻² BM. (Nearest integer) [Given: √3 = 1.73]","type":"num","answer":"173","range":[173.0,173.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 283 (two unpaired, as in B₂); 0 (σ2p order giving paired electrons); 387 (three unpaired).\nWorking: B₂⁺ (9 e⁻): σ1s² σ*1s² σ2s² σ*2s² π2p¹ → one unpaired electron, μ = √3 = 1.73 BM = 173 × 10⁻² BM.\nKey idea: B₂⁺: KK σ2s² σ*2s² π2p¹ → n = 1 → μ = √3 = 1.73 BM = 173 × 10⁻².","traps":["σ2p filled before π2p for boron: For B₂ (≤14 e⁻) π2p lies below σ2p; B₂ has two unpaired π electrons, B₂⁺ has one.","Electron removed from wrong MO: Removing one π electron leaves one unpaired: μ = √3 = 1.73 BM."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The dipole moments of CCl₄, CHCl₃ and CH₄ are in the order :","options":["CH₄ = CCl₄ < CHCl₃","CH₄ < CCl₄ < CHCl₃","CCl₄ < CH₄ < CHCl₃","CHCl₃ < CH₄ = CCl₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): think C–Cl bond polarity gives CCl₄ a dipole. (D): reverses order.\nWorking: CH₄ and CCl₄ are symmetrical tetrahedra with zero net dipole; CHCl₃ has a net dipole.\nKey idea: Symmetric tetrahedral CX₄ cancels; replacing one X breaks the symmetry.","traps":["Polar bonds equated with polar molecule: Add bond dipoles as vectors; regular tetrahedron sums to zero."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The bond order and the magnetic characteristics of CN⁻ are :","options":["3, diamagnetic","2½, paramagnetic","3, paramagnetic","2½, diamagnetic"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) use 13 electrons (neutral CN) giving 2½; (C) pairs the right bond order with wrong magnetism.\nWorking: CN⁻ has 14 electrons, isoelectronic with N₂: bond order (10 − 4)/2 = 3, all electrons paired.\nKey idea: CN⁻ is isoelectronic with N₂.","traps":["Extra electron from charge ignored: Add one electron for the negative charge."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Bond parameters","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Arrange the following bonds according to their average bond energies in descending order : C–Cl, C–Br, C–F, C–I","options":["C–I > C–Br > C–Cl > C–F","C–Br > C–I > C–Cl > C–F","C–F > C–Cl > C–Br > C–I","C–Cl > C–Br > C–I > C–F"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reversed order; (B) and (D) scrambled orders putting C–F lowest (confusing with F–F anomaly).\nWorking: bond length C–F < C–Cl < C–Br < C–I as halogen size grows, so bond energy falls F > Cl > Br > I.\nKey idea: C–F is the shortest and strongest C–X bond; no F–F type anomaly here.","traps":["F–F weak-bond anomaly carried to C–F: Lone-pair repulsion weakens F–F, not C–F."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Complexes (ML₅) of metals Ni and Fe have ideal square pyramidal and trigonal bipyramidal geometries, respectively. The sum of the 90°, 120° and 180° L–M–L angles in the two complexes is _______.","type":"num","answer":"20","range":[20.0,20.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 16 or 18 (missing the 4 basal–basal 90° angles or the 2 trans-basal 180° angles); 22 (counting apical–basal as non-90° or double counting).\nWorking: square pyramidal ML₅ – 8 angles of 90° (4 apical–basal + 4 adjacent basal) and 2 of 180° (trans basal) = 10; trigonal bipyramidal – 6 of 90° (axial–equatorial), 3 of 120° (equatorial–equatorial), 1 of 180° (axial–axial) = 10; total 20.\nKey idea: Each ML₅ has C(5,2) = 10 L–M–L angles; in ideal geometries all are 90°, 120° or 180°, so the sum is 2 × 10 = 20.","traps":["Basal–basal angles missed: List every ligand pair: 5C2 = 10 per complex."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"If the magnetic moment of a dioxygen species is 1.73 B.M., it may be:","options":["O₂⁻ or O₂⁺","O₂ or O₂⁺","O₂ or O₂⁻","O₂, O₂⁻ or O₂⁺"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C), (D) include O₂, which has two unpaired electrons (2.83 BM).\nWorking: 1.73 BM = one unpaired electron. O₂ has two unpaired (π*), O₂⁺ and O₂⁻ each one.\nKey idea: √[n(n+2)] = 1.73 → n = 1; only the singly charged ions qualify.","traps":["Including neutral O₂: Paramagnetic ≠ one unpaired; O₂ has two."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The number of sp² hybrid orbitals in a molecule of benzene is:","options":["24","6","12","18"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts sp² carbons; (C) counts the 12 σ bonds (6 C–C + 6 C–H) instead of orbitals; (A) counts all four valence orbitals per carbon.\nWorking: 6 sp² carbons × 3 hybrid orbitals each = 18 (H uses 1s).\nKey idea: Each sp² atom has 3 hybrid orbitals: 6 × 3 = 18.","traps":["Atoms counted instead of orbitals: Multiply by 3 hybrid orbitals per sp² atom.","All four valence orbitals counted: Unhybridised 2p is not an sp² orbital."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dipole moment","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"If AB₄ molecule is a polar molecule, a possible geometry of AB₄ is:","options":["Square pyramidal","Tetrahedral","Square planar","Rectangular planar"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) tetrahedral is the usual AB₄ answer but is non-polar; (C) and (D) are planar with opposite dipoles cancelling — rectangular planar tempts because sides are unequal, yet still centrosymmetric.\nWorking: tetrahedral, square planar and rectangular planar AB₄ arrangements are centrosymmetric or symmetric so bond dipoles cancel; a square-pyramidal arrangement (A above the plane of four B, with a lone pair) leaves a net dipole.\nKey idea: Any arrangement with a centre of symmetry or perfect symmetry cancels dipoles; square pyramidal does not.","traps":["Unequal sides assumed polar: Check for an inversion centre: rectangle still has one.","Square pyramidal with 4 atoms puzzling: Treat it as A raised above the B₄ plane — dipoles do not cancel."],"topics":["Dipole moment","VSEPR shapes"]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The molecular geometry of SF₆ is octahedral. What is the geometry of SF₄ (including lone pair(s) of electrons, if any) ?","options":["Trigonal bipyramidal","Square planar","Tetrahedral","Pyramidal"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) are shapes for other lone-pair counts; (C) ignores the lone pair.\nWorking: S in SF₄ has 4 bond pairs + 1 lone pair (steric number 5, sp³d) → electron-pair geometry trigonal bipyramidal (shape see-saw).\nKey idea: Including lone pairs → use steric number geometry (TBP for 5).","traps":["Shape given instead of geometry: Read 'including lone pair'."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"If the boiling point of H₂O is 373 K, the boiling point of H₂S will be:","options":["Greater than 300 K but less than 373 K","Less than 300 K","Equal to 373 K","More than 373 K"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes only a modest drop; (C)/(D) apply a molar-mass trend that ignores H-bonding.\nWorking: H₂S has no significant H-bonding; it is a gas at room temperature (b.p. ≈ 213 K).\nKey idea: H₂S is a gas at 298 K, so its b.p. must be below 300 K.","traps":["Molar mass trend applied: H-bonding dominates for H₂O.","Room-temperature state not used: Recall H₂S is a gas at room temperature."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Of the species, NO, NO⁺, NO²⁺, NO⁻, the one with minimum bond strength is:","options":["NO²⁺","NO⁺","NO","NO⁻"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NO²⁺ picked because highest charge \"pulls bond apart\"; (B) is the strongest; (C) 2.5.\nWorking: bond orders – NO⁺ (14 e⁻) 3, NO²⁺ (13 e⁻) 2.5, NO (15 e⁻) 2.5, NO⁻ (16 e⁻) 2; lowest bond order → weakest bond.\nKey idea: Adding electrons to π* lowers bond order.","traps":["Charge equated with weakness: Compute bond order from electron count."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Bond parameters","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The intermolecular potential energy for the molecules A, B, C and D given below suggests that : (graph of potential energy (kJ mol⁻¹, 0 to –600) vs interatomic distance (pm, ~50–200) with four wells: A–A (minimum ≈ –450 at ≈ 55 pm), A–B (deepest, ≈ –550 at ≈ 90 pm), A–C (≈ –480 at ≈ 115 pm), A–D (shallowest ≈ –350 at ≈ 165 pm))","options":["D is more electronegative than other atoms","A-D has the shortest bond length","A-B has the stiffest bond","A–A has the largest bond enthalpy"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) A–D minimum is farthest right – longest bond; (D) A–A minimum is shortest but shallower than A–B; (A) no electronegativity information.\nWorking: A–B has the deepest and narrow well (largest bond enthalpy, steep walls) so it is the stiffest; A–D has the longest bond, A–A the shortest but not the deepest; nothing supports (A). Accepted by elimination; 'stiffness' strictly depends on well curvature, which the sketch only shows qualitatively.\nKey idea: Deepest well = strongest, stiffest bond; leftmost minimum = shortest bond.","traps":["Shortest bond taken as strongest: Compare depths directly on the graph.","Reading x-axis backwards: Bond length is the x-value at the minimum."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The reaction in which the hybridisation of the underlined atom is affected is :","options":["N̲H₃ →(H⁺)","X̲eF₄ + SbF₅ →","H₂S̲O₄ + NaCl →(420 K)","H₃P̲O₂ →(disproportionation)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NH₃→NH₄⁺: lone pair becomes bond pair but steric number stays 4; (C) HCl formation leaves S sp³; (D) P stays sp³ in PH₃ and H₃PO₃.\nWorking: XeF₄ + SbF₅ → [XeF₃]⁺[SbF₆]⁻; Xe goes from sp³d² (4 bp + 2 lp) to sp³d (3 bp + 2 lp). NH₃→NH₄⁺ stays sp³; H₂SO₄→NaHSO₄ S stays sp³; H₃PO₂→PH₃ + H₃PO₃ P stays sp³.\nKey idea: Count steric number before and after; only Xe changes from 6 to 5.","traps":["Protonation thought to change hybridisation: Steric number counts lone pairs too: 4 before and after.","Products of XeF₄ + SbF₅ unknown: SbF₅ is a fluoride acceptor: XeF₄ → XeF₃⁺."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Valence bond theory","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The potential energy curve for the H₂ molecule as a function of internuclear distance is :","options":["Graph (A)","Graph (B)","Graph (C)","Graph (D)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) only attraction-free rise; (C) no repulsive wall or minimum shown correctly; (D) activation-barrier-like curve.\nWorking: energy falls as H atoms approach, reaches a minimum at bond length 74 pm, then rises steeply from internuclear repulsion.\nKey idea: A bond corresponds to an energy minimum.","traps":["Reaction-profile shape chosen: Bond formation lowers energy – look for a well."],"img":["img/jee/JM20Sep05S1C19-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The increasing order of boiling points of the following compounds is:\nI: p-cresol (4-methylphenol); II: p-nitrophenol (4-nitrophenol); III: p-aminophenol (4-aminophenol); IV: p-methoxyphenol (4-methoxyphenol)","options":["I < IV < III < II","IV < I < II < III","I < III < IV < II","III < I < II < IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) put p-methoxyphenol or p-aminophenol in the wrong place relative to p-cresol; (C) I < III < IV < II places p-aminophenol below p-methoxyphenol, ignoring the extra N–H hydrogen bonding of III.\nWorking: p-cresol (~202 °C) is lowest; IV (~243 °C) is next (polar OCH₃, but no extra H-bond donor); II and III both form extensive intermolecular H-bonds. Literature b.p. of p-aminophenol (~284 °C, decomposes) is close to/above p-nitrophenol (~279 °C, decomposes), so II vs III at the top is not cleanly decidable; the official answer places II highest on the basis of its larger polarity. Of the given options only (A) puts I < IV and II, III at the top, so (A) is the best answer.\nKey idea: More intermolecular H-bonding sites and higher polarity raise b.p.: CH₃ < OCH₃ < NH₂ ≈ NO₂.","traps":["–NH₂ H-bonding ignored: Count H-bond donors: NH₂ adds N–H donors; OCH₃ adds none.","NO₂ vs NH₂ at the top: Eliminate by the clear part (I lowest, IV below both II, III)."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The compound that has the largest H–M–H bond angle (M = N, O, S, C), is:","options":["H₂O","CH₄","NH₃","H₂S"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) H₂O – two lone pairs, 104.5°; (C) NH₃ – one lone pair, 107°; (D) H₂S – smallest (~92°).\nWorking: CH₄ has no lone pair – 109.5°; NH₃ 107°, H₂O 104.5°, H₂S ≈ 92°.\nKey idea: No lone pair → ideal tetrahedral angle, the largest of the set.","traps":["Lone pair effect reversed: lp–bp repulsion squeezes the H–M–H angle."],"topics":["VSEPR shapes","Bond parameters"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hybridisation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The ion that has sp³d² hybridization for the central atom, is:","options":["[ICl₂]⁻","[IF₆]⁻","[ICl₄]⁻","[BrF₂]⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) linear ions look 'simple' but have 5 domains (sp³d); (B) IF₆⁻ has 6 ligands so looks sp³d², but its extra lone pair makes 7 domains (sp³d³).\nWorking: ICl₄⁻ has 4 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs = 6 electron domains ⇒ sp³d² (square planar). ICl₂⁻ and BrF₂⁻: 2 bp + 3 lp ⇒ sp³d; IF₆⁻: 6 bp + 1 lp ⇒ sp³d³.\nKey idea: Hybridisation follows total domains (bp + lp), not number of ligands.","traps":["Six ligands taken as six domains: Count lone pairs: (7 + 6 + 1 − 12)/2 = 1.","Linear shape taken as sp: Linear with 3 lone pairs is sp³d."],"topics":["Hybridisation","VSEPR shapes"]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Among the following molecules/ions, C₂²⁻, N₂²⁻, O₂²⁻, O₂, which one is diamagnetic and has the shortest bond length?","options":["C₂²⁻","N₂²⁻","O₂","O₂²⁻"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) N₂²⁻ is isoelectronic with O₂ – paramagnetic; (C) O₂ paramagnetic; (D) O₂²⁻ diamagnetic but BO 1 (longest).\nWorking: C₂²⁻ (14 e⁻) BO 3, diamagnetic; N₂²⁻ (16 e⁻) BO 2, paramagnetic; O₂ BO 2, paramagnetic; O₂²⁻ BO 1, diamagnetic but long bond.\nKey idea: C₂²⁻ is isoelectronic with N₂: BO 3, no unpaired electrons.","traps":["Only one criterion checked: Check bond order too.","N₂²⁻ assumed like N₂: Count electrons: 16 = O₂-like."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"VSEPR shapes","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The correct statement about ICl₅ and ICl₄⁻ is:","options":["ICl₅ is trigonal bipyramidal and ICl₄⁻ is tetrahedral.","ICl₅ is square pyramidal and ICl₄⁻ is tetrahedral.","ICl₅ is square pyramidal and ICl₄⁻ is square planar.","Both are isostructural."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores the lone pair of ICl₅; (B) treats ICl₄⁻ like CCl₄ (no lone pairs); (D) same hybridisation mistaken for same shape.\nWorking: ICl₅: 5 bp + 1 lp (sp³d²) ⇒ square pyramidal; ICl₄⁻: 4 bp + 2 lp (sp³d²) ⇒ square planar.\nKey idea: Six domains each; lone pairs set the shape.","traps":["Four ligands ⇒ tetrahedral: Count domains: 4 bp + 2 lp.","Same hybridisation ⇒ same shape: Shape depends on number of lone pairs."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Among the following, the molecule expected to be stabilized by anion formation is : C₂, O₂, NO, F₂","options":["NO","C₂","F₂","O₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) NO⁻: electron into π* (BO 2.5 → 2); (C) F₂⁻: into σ* (1 → 0.5); (D) O₂⁻: into π* (2 → 1.5).\nWorking: in C₂ (12 e⁻) the next electron enters σ2p_z bonding MO, raising bond order 2 → 2.5; in NO, O₂, F₂ it enters an antibonding MO and lowers bond order.\nKey idea: Only a molecule whose next available MO is bonding is stabilised – C₂ (next orbital σ2p_z).","traps":["Assuming every anion is destabilised: Check HOMO/LUMO type for each molecule.","NO chosen because NO⁻ is familiar / odd electron: NO's unpaired electron is in π*; adding one more lowers bond order."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Among the following species, the diamagnetic molecule is","options":["O₂","NO","B₂","CO"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) O₂ may be thought diamagnetic from a Lewis structure; (C) B₂ diamagnetic if s–p mixing is ignored; (B) NO odd-electron.\nWorking: O₂ (2 unpaired π*), NO (1 unpaired, 15 e⁻), B₂ (2 unpaired π) are paramagnetic; CO (14 e⁻, isoelectronic with N₂) has all paired.\nKey idea: Count electrons: 14-electron species (CO) are diamagnetic like N₂.","traps":["B₂ thought diamagnetic: For B₂–N₂, π2p lies below σ2p.","O₂ from Lewis structure: Use MOT: two unpaired π* electrons."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"HF has highest boiling point among hydrogen halides, because it has :","options":["lowest dissociation enthalpy","strongest van der Waals' interactions","strongest hydrogen bonding","lowest ionic character"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HF has the HIGHEST bond enthalpy; (B) vdW strongest for HI; (D) HF is the most ionic/polar.\nWorking: strong intermolecular H-bonding in HF raises its boiling point above HI despite lower mass.\nKey idea: H-bonding overrides the molar-mass trend.","traps":["Mass-based vdW reasoning: HF is an exception due to H-bonding."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"During the change of O₂ to O₂⁻, the incoming electron goes to the orbital :","options":["σ*2p_z","π2p_y","π*2p_x","π2p_x"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) σ*2p_z is higher and empty; (B), (D) bonding π orbitals are already full.\nWorking: O₂ (16 e⁻) has two singly occupied π*2p orbitals; the added electron enters π*2p (π*2p_x or π*2p_y equivalently).\nKey idea: Extra electrons in O₂⁻ pair up in π*.","traps":["Bonding π chosen: Bonding π are full; next level is π*.","σ*2p_z chosen: σ*2p lies above π*2p."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"According to molecular orbital theory, which of the following is true with respect to Li₂⁺ and Li₂⁻ ?","options":["Both are unstable","Li₂⁺ is unstable and Li₂⁻ is stable","Li₂⁺ is stable and Li₂⁻ is unstable","Both are stable"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): student sees the anion's antibonding electron and calls it unstable (in finer treatments Li₂⁻ is less stable, but BO is still positive). (A)/(B): misjudge BO.\nWorking: Li₂⁺ (5 e⁻) σ1s² σ*1s² σ2s¹ → BO 0.5; Li₂⁻ (7 e⁻) σ1s² σ*1s² σ2s² σ*2s¹ → BO 0.5; both have N_b > N_a so both are stable.\nKey idea: Equal bond orders (0.5) – both stable by the MOT criterion.","traps":["Anion thought unstable: Bond order 0.5 > 0 still means stable.","Fractional bond order dismissed: NCERT: stable if N_b > N_a."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"In which of the following processes, the bond order has increased and paramagnetic character has changed to diamagnetic?","options":["N₂ → N₂⁺","NO → NO⁺","O₂ → O₂²⁻","O₂ → O₂⁺"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) N₂→N₂⁺: BO 3→2.5 and dia→para (reverse of both). (C) O₂→O₂²⁻: becomes diamagnetic but BO 2→1. (D) O₂→O₂⁺: BO 2→2.5 but still paramagnetic (one unpaired).\nWorking: NO (15 e⁻, BO 2.5, one unpaired) → NO⁺ (14 e⁻, BO 3, diamagnetic). N₂→N₂⁺: BO falls, dia→para; O₂→O₂²⁻: BO 2→1; O₂→O₂⁺: BO rises but stays paramagnetic.\nKey idea: Removing the lone antibonding π* electron from NO gives NO⁺, isoelectronic with N₂: both conditions met.","traps":["Only one condition checked: O₂⁺ still has one unpaired π* electron; check both conditions.","Diamagnetic change seen, bond order not: Adding electrons to π* lowers bond order."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Two pi and half sigma bonds are present in:","options":["N₂⁺","N₂","O₂⁺","O₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) N₂: 2 π + 1 σ (full triple bond). (C) O₂⁺: also bond order 2.5, but it is 1 σ + 1.5 π (one electron in π*), the main decoy. (D) O₂: 1 σ + 1 π.\nWorking: N₂⁺ (13 e⁻) = σ1s²σ*1s²σ2s²σ*2s²(π2p)⁴σ2p¹ → two full π bonds and a single electron in σ2p (half σ bond), bond order 2.5.\nKey idea: Same bond order 2.5 for N₂⁺ and O₂⁺, but the electron is removed from σ2p in N₂ and from π* in O₂, so only N₂⁺ has a \"half σ\".","traps":["Equal bond order taken as identical bonding: Check which MO loses or holds the odd electron: σ2p for N₂⁺, π* for O₂⁺.","Wrong MO energy order for N₂: For Z ≤ 7 (up to N₂) π2p lies below σ2p."],"topics":["MO theory (bond order & magnetism)","Sigma & pi bonds"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Hydrogen bonding","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Among the following four aromatic compounds, which one will have the lowest melting point ?","options":["Naphthalene","Phthalic acid (benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid)","2,3-Diacetylnaphthalene (two –COCH₃ groups on adjacent carbons of naphthalene)","2-Naphthol (naphthalen-2-ol)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) has strong carboxylic acid H-bonding (high m.p.). (C) larger polar diketone. (D) phenolic OH H-bonding.\nWorking: naphthalene (m.p. ≈ 80 °C) is non-polar with only dispersion forces; phthalic acid and 2-naphthol are H-bonded, the diketone is heavier and polar.\nKey idea: No polar group and no H-bonding ⇒ lowest m.p.","traps":["Larger molecule assumed lower: Polar groups and mass raise m.p.; parent hydrocarbon is lowest."]}],"mx-poly":[{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"Polymer used in orlon is :","options":["Polyethene","Polyacrylonitrile","Polycarbonate","Polyamide"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) polyamide links to nylon fibres. (A) polyethene to polythene. (C) polycarbonate to Lexan.\nWorking: orlon (acrilan) is polyacrylonitrile, –[CH₂–CH(CN)]ₙ–.\nKey idea: Orlon/acrilan = PAN, a wool substitute.","traps":["Fibre trade names mixed: Orlon = acrylic (PAN); nylon = polyamide."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Match the following\nColumn A: (a) Nylon 6; (b) Vulcanized Rubber; (c) cis-1,4-polyisoprene; (d) Polychloroprene\nColumn B: (I) Natural Rubber; (II) Cross Linked; (III) Caprolactam; (IV) Neoprene\nChoose the correct answer from options given below:","options":["a → III, b → II, c → I, d → IV","a → II, b → III, c → IV, d → I","a → IV, b → III, c → I, d → II","a → III, b → IV, c → I, d → II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): one pair shifted (b → IV, d → II) – catches students who confuse neoprene with a cross-linked rubber. (B)/(C): scrambled pairings.\nWorking: Nylon 6 from caprolactam; vulcanised rubber is sulphur cross-linked; natural rubber is cis-1,4-polyisoprene; polychloroprene is neoprene.\nKey idea: Neoprene = polychloroprene; vulcanised = cross-linked.","traps":["Neoprene and cross-linking swapped: Neoprene is the name of polychloroprene; cross-linking describes vulcanisation."],"topics":["Rubber","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":73,"q":"Match List I (Name of polymer) with List II (Uses). List I: A. Glyptal; B. Neoprene; C. Acrilan; D. LDP. List II: I. Flexible pipes; II. Synthetic wool; III. Paints and Lacquers; IV. Gaskets. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives neoprene flexible pipes and acrilan gaskets; (B) swaps the acrilan and LDP uses; (C) swaps the neoprene and acrilan uses; all keep glyptal correct so it cannot separate options.\nWorking: glyptal – paints and lacquers; neoprene – gaskets; acrilan – synthetic wool; LDP – flexible pipes → A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I = (D).\nKey idea: Neoprene → gaskets; acrilan → synthetic wool; LDP → flexible pipes.","traps":["Neoprene vs LDP uses: Neoprene is rubber (gaskets, hoses); LDP is plastic (pipes, squeeze bottles)."],"topics":["Addition polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I: A. Elastomeric polymer; B. Fibre Polymer; C. Thermosetting Polymer; D. Thermoplastic Polymer\nList II: I. Urea formaldehyde resin; II. Polystyrene; III. Polyester; IV. Neoprene\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) put polystyrene as elastomer; (B) puts urea-formaldehyde as fibre.\nWorking: neoprene – elastomer; polyester – fibre; urea-formaldehyde resin – thermosetting; polystyrene – thermoplastic → A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II = (C).\nKey idea: Neoprene = rubber-like elastomer; urea-formaldehyde = cross-linked thermoset.","traps":["Thermoplastic vs thermosetting example: Cross-linked resins (Bakelite, urea-formaldehyde) are thermosets."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":69,"q":"Caprolactam when heated at high temperature in presence of water, gives","options":["Dacron","Nylon 6","Teflon","Nylon 6,6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"D: nylon 6,6 is from hexamethylenediamine + adipic acid. A: Dacron from ethylene glycol + terephthalic acid. C: Teflon from tetrafluoroethene.\nWorking: caprolactam heated with water undergoes ring-opening polymerisation to give nylon 6, –[NH–(CH₂)₅–CO]ₙ– → (B).\nKey idea: Caprolactam ring-opens and polymerises to nylon 6 (one monomer, six carbons).","traps":["Nylon 6 vs nylon 6,6: Nylon 6 has one six-carbon monomer (caprolactam); 6,6 has two."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Melamine polymer is formed by the condensation of:","options":["2,4,6-triamino-1,3,5-triazine (three ring N) + HCHO","2,4,6-triaminopyrimidine (two ring N) + HCHO","benzene-1,3,5-triamine + HCHO","pyridine-2,4-diamine (one ring N; NH₂ at C2 and C4) + HCHO"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) pyrimidine (only two ring N) looks almost identical; (C) benzene triamine; (D) pyridine diamine.\nWorking: melamine is 1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6-triamine; it condenses with formaldehyde.\nKey idea: Melamine ring has alternating C and N: C₃N₃.","traps":["Count ring nitrogens: Melamine = triazine: 3 N in ring, 3 NH₂ outside."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Match List I with List II: List-I (Polymer): A. Nylon 6,6, B. Low density polythene, C. High density polythene, D. Teflon. List-II (used for items): I. Buckets, II. Non-stick utensils, III. Bristles of brushes, IV. Toys. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A–III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A–III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A–II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A–II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps LDPE and HDPE (buckets vs toys); (C)/(D) give nylon to non-stick utensils and Teflon to bristles.\nWorking: nylon-6,6 – bristles for brushes; LDPE – squeeze bottles, toys, flexible pipes; HDPE – buckets, dustbins; Teflon – non-stick coatings.\nKey idea: Rigid HDPE = buckets; flexible LDPE = toys/squeeze bottles.","traps":["LDPE and HDPE uses swapped: Tough, rigid HDPE for buckets; soft LDPE for toys."],"topics":["Addition polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Vulcanization of rubber is carried out by heating a mixture of :","options":["isoprene and styrene","neoprene and sulphur","isoprene and sulphur","neoprene and styrene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) neoprene is a synthetic rubber vulcanised differently (metal oxides); (A)/(D) styrene relates to Buna-S copolymer.\nWorking: natural rubber (cis-polyisoprene) is heated with sulphur (and additives) to form S cross-links. The option says \"isoprene\" for polyisoprene.\nKey idea: Natural rubber + S on heating → cross-linked vulcanised rubber.","traps":["Neoprene vs natural rubber: Vulcanisation in NCERT is of natural rubber (polyisoprene)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Polymers): A. Phenol-formaldehyde resin; B. Copolymer of 1,3-butadiene and styrene; C. Polyester of glycol and phthalic acid; D. Polyester of glycol and terephthalic acid\nList II (Commercial names): I. Glyptal; II. Novolac; III. Buna-S; IV. Dacron\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): Glyptal and Dacron swapped (phthalic vs terephthalic); (C)/(D): Buna-S or Novolac mismatched.\nWorking: phenol–formaldehyde → Novolac; butadiene–styrene → Buna-S; glycol + phthalic acid → Glyptal; glycol + terephthalic acid → Dacron (terylene): A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.\nKey idea: Terephthalic (para) → fibre Dacron; phthalic (ortho) → resin Glyptal.","traps":["Phthalic vs terephthalic: Tere = para → Dacron; ortho → Glyptal."],"topics":["Condensation polymers","Rubber"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match List-I (Polymer) with List-II (Monomer). List-I: (A) Neoprene; (B) Teflon; (C) Acrilan; (D) Natural rubber. List-II: (I) Acrylonitrile; (II) Chloroprene; (III) Tetrafluoroethene; (IV) Isoprene.","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) put Teflon with acrylonitrile; (D) swaps neoprene and Teflon monomers.\nWorking: neoprene = polychloroprene; Teflon = polytetrafluoroethene; Acrilan (orlon) = polyacrylonitrile; natural rubber = cis-polyisoprene.\nKey idea: Acrilan is polyacrylonitrile; neoprene is polychloroprene.","traps":["Trade name Acrilan: Acrilan/Orlon = polyacrylonitrile."],"topics":["Addition polymers","Rubber"]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Terylene polymer is obtained by condensation of:","options":["Ethane-1, 2-diol and Benzene-1, 3 dicarboxylic acid","Propane-1, 2-diol and Benzene-1, 4 dicarboxylic acid","Ethane-1, 2-diol and Benzene-1, 4 dicarboxylic acid","Ethane-1, 2-diol and Benzene-1, 2 dicarboxylic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) isophthalic acid; (D) phthalic acid (used in glyptal); (B) propylene glycol.\nWorking: Terylene (Dacron, PET) is the polyester of ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid (benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid).\nKey idea: Tere-phthalic = para = benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid.","traps":["Phthalic acid isomers: Terephthalic = 1,4; glyptal uses phthalic (1,2)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List I (Polymer) with List II (Nature): A. –[CH₂–C(Cl)=CH–CH₂]ₙ– (neoprene); B. –[NH–(CH₂)₆–NH–CO–(CH₂)₄–CO]ₙ– (nylon-6,6); C. –[CH₂–CH(Cl)]ₙ– (PVC); D. phenol–formaldehyde chain: o-hydroxyphenyl rings linked by –CH₂– bridges (novolac/Bakelite). I. Thermosetting polymer; II. Fibers; III. Elastomer; IV. Thermoplastic polymer. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps A and B; (C) and (D) call nylon or Bakelite by the wrong class.\nWorking: neoprene is an elastomer, nylon-6,6 a fibre, PVC a thermoplastic and phenol–formaldehyde (Bakelite) a thermosetting polymer.\nKey idea: Identify each repeat unit first; then recall NCERT's example for each class.","traps":["Recognising repeat units: Cl on a diene unit = neoprene; amide links = nylon."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a natural polymer?","options":["Protein","Starch","Rubber","Rayon"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) rubber could be thought synthetic (synthetic rubbers exist), but natural rubber is natural.\nWorking: rayon is a semi-synthetic (regenerated cellulose) polymer; proteins, starch and natural rubber are natural.\nKey idea: Rayon = semi-synthetic.","traps":["Rubber assumed synthetic: Unqualified \"rubber\" = natural rubber."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Biodegradable polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which of the following is an example of polyester?","options":["Butadiene-styrene copolymer","Melamine polymer","Neoprene","Poly-β-hydroxybutyrate-co-β-hydroxyvalerate"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) melamine polymer is a condensation polymer, tempting those who equate condensation with polyester; (A)/(C) are addition elastomers.\nWorking: PHBV is a copolyester of 3-hydroxybutanoic and 3-hydroxypentanoic acids (biodegradable polyester). Buna-S and neoprene are addition polymers; melamine–formaldehyde is a condensation polymer but not an ester.\nKey idea: Hydroxy-acid monomers condense through –COO– links → polyester.","traps":["Condensation polymer equated with polyester: Polyester needs ester (–COO–) links from acid + alcohol/hydroxy-acid."],"topics":["Biodegradable polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following is not an example of a condensation polymer?","options":["Nylon 6,6","Dacron","Buna-N","Silicone"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Silicone – chosen by students who do not know it forms by condensation of silanols; (A)/(B) – familiar condensation polymers, rarely chosen.\nWorking: Buna-N is an addition copolymer of buta-1,3-diene and acrylonitrile; nylon-6,6 (amide), Dacron (ester) and silicones (from R₂Si(OH)₂ losing water) are condensation polymers.\nKey idea: \"Buna\" rubbers come from butadiene by addition (chain-growth) polymerisation.","traps":["Silicone thought to be addition polymer: R₂SiCl₂ hydrolyses to R₂Si(OH)₂, which condenses with loss of water."],"topics":["Condensation polymers","Rubber"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The Novolac polymer has mass of 963 g. The number of monomer units present in it are ________.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 or 8 (dividing by 124 = phenol + HCHO without removing water), 10 (dividing by 94 phenol only).\nWorking: Novolac repeat unit = phenol + HCHO − H₂O = –C₆H₃(OH)–CH₂– (C₇H₆O, 106 g mol⁻¹); 963/106 ≈ 9.1 ≈ 9. (\"Mass of 963 g\" is meant as molar mass 963 g mol⁻¹.)\nKey idea: Each condensation step releases H₂O, so the repeat unit is 94 + 30 − 18 = 106.","traps":["Water lost in condensation: Subtract 18 for each linkage.","\"Mass\" means molar mass: Treat as 963 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following sets are correct regarding polymer ? (A) Copolymer : Buna–S (B) Condensation polymer : Nylon–6,6 (C) Fibre : Nylon–6,6 (D) Thermosetting polymer : Terylene (E) Homopolymer : Buna–N. Choose the correct answer from given options below:","options":["(A), (B) and (C) are correct","(B), (C) and (D) are correct","(A), (C) and (E) are correct","(A), (B) and (D) are correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) include Terylene as thermosetting (it is a fibre/thermoplastic); (C) includes Buna-N as homopolymer (it is a copolymer).\nWorking: Buna-S is a copolymer (butadiene + styrene) ✓; Nylon-6,6 is a condensation polymer ✓ and a fibre ✓; Terylene is a thermoplastic polyester fibre, not thermosetting ✗; Buna-N is a copolymer (butadiene + acrylonitrile), not a homopolymer ✗. Correct set (A),(B),(C) = option (A).\nKey idea: Buna-S and Buna-N are both copolymers; Terylene is a polyester fibre.","traps":["Terylene as thermosetting: Terylene = fibre (Dacron), thermoplastic.","Buna-N as homopolymer: Buna-N = butadiene + acrylonitrile."],"topics":["Classification","Rubber"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match List-I (Polymer) with List-II (Used in). List-I: (A) Bakelite, (B) Glyptal, (C) PVC, (D) Polystyrene. List-II: (I) Radio and television cabinets, (II) Electrical switches, (III) Paints and lacquers, (IV) Water pipes.","options":["(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(IV), (D)–(I)","(A)–(I), (B)–(II), (C)–(III), (D)–(IV)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(III), (C)–(II), (D)–(I)","(A)–(II), (B)–(III), (C)–(I), (D)–(IV)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – simply pairs the items in the order listed; (C) – Bakelite for water pipes; (D) – PVC and polystyrene uses swapped.\nWorking: Bakelite – electrical switches; glyptal – paints and lacquers; PVC – water pipes; polystyrene – radio and TV cabinets → (A).\nKey idea: Thermosetting Bakelite insulates (switches); PVC is rigid tubing; polystyrene moulds into cabinets.","traps":["PVC vs polystyrene uses: PVC → pipes; polystyrene → cabinets."],"topics":["Condensation polymers","Addition polymers"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which is true about Buna-N?","options":["It is a linear polymer of 1, 3-butadiene.","It is obtained by copolymerization of 1, 3-butadiene and styrene.","It is obtained by copolymerization of 1, 3-butadiene and acrylonitrile.","The suffix N in Buna-N stands for its natural occurrence"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is Buna-S; (A) is polybutadiene (Buna); (D) misreads N (nitrile, not natural).\nWorking: Buna-N (nitrile rubber) is the copolymer of buta-1,3-diene and acrylonitrile; N refers to the nitrile monomer. Butadiene + styrene is Buna-S.\nKey idea: Buna-N: N = nitrile (acrylonitrile); Buna-S: S = styrene.","traps":["Buna-N vs Buna-S: S = styrene, N = nitrile."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which one of the following is NOT a copolymer?","options":["Buna-S","Neoprene","PHBV","Butadiene-styrene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) are the same copolymer under two names; (C) PHBV is less familiar, so may be wrongly picked as a homopolymer.\nWorking: neoprene is a homopolymer of chloroprene (2-chloro-1,3-butadiene); Buna-S (= butadiene–styrene) and PHBV (3-hydroxybutanoic + 3-hydroxypentanoic acid) are copolymers.\nKey idea: Neoprene has one monomer (chloroprene).","traps":["Unfamiliar PHBV picked: PHBV = poly(β-hydroxybutyrate-co-β-hydroxyvalerate).","Duplicate options: Two identical options cannot both be the single answer."],"topics":["Classification","Rubber","Biodegradable polymers"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements, one is Assertion (A) and the other is Reason (R). Assertion (A): Natural rubber is a linear polymer of isoprene called cis-polyisoprene with elastic properties. Reason (R): The cis-polyisoprene molecules consist of various chains held together by strong polar interactions with coiled structure. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct one from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true but (R) is false.","(A) is false but (R) is true."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) accept the altered Reason; (D) rejects a correct Assertion.\nWorking: natural rubber is linear cis-1,4-polyisoprene and elastic; its chains are held by weak van der Waals forces (not strong polar interactions) and have a coiled structure.\nKey idea: Elastomers have the weakest intermolecular forces.","traps":["Weak vs strong forces in elastomers: Elasticity needs weak forces."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The polymer, which can be stretched and retains its original status on releasing the force is","options":["Bakelite","Nylon 6,6","Buna-N","Terylene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) thermosetting; (B), (D) fibres with strong H-bonding/dipole forces.\nWorking: elastomers (rubber-like, weak van der Waals forces, a few cross-links) stretch and recover; Buna-N is an elastomer; Bakelite thermoset, nylon-6,6 and terylene fibres.\nKey idea: Elastomer = weakest intermolecular forces + slight cross-links → Buna-S, Buna-N, neoprene.","traps":["Nylon as stretchable: Nylon is a fibre (strong H-bonds), not an elastomer."],"topics":["Classification","Rubber"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Dacron is an example of polyester polymer.\nReason R: Dacron is made up of ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid monomers.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who regard R as just a fact about monomers, not an explanation; (C)/(D) for those who confuse Dacron with nylon.\nWorking: Dacron (terylene) is a polyester formed by condensation of ethylene glycol (diol) and terephthalic acid (diacid); the diol + diacid monomers make ester links, so R explains A.\nKey idea: Diol + dicarboxylic acid → ester linkages → polyester; R explains A.","traps":["Monomer fact as explanation: Ester linkage arises from diol + diacid, so R explains A."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The polymer formed on heating Novolac with formaldehyde is:","options":["Bakelite","Polyester","Melamine","Nylon 6,6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) melamine is another formaldehyde resin; (B)/(D) condensation polymers unrelated to phenol.\nWorking: novolac (linear phenol–formaldehyde polymer) on heating with more formaldehyde cross-links to Bakelite.\nKey idea: Novolac + HCHO + heat = Bakelite (thermoset).","traps":["Novolac is the linear stage of Bakelite: Phenol + HCHO → novolac → (more HCHO, heat) cross-linked Bakelite."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Monomer of Novolac is:","options":["3-Hydroxybutanoic acid","phenol and melamine","o-Hydroxymethylphenol","1,3-Butadiene and styrene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) monomer of PHB/PHBV; (B) melamine pairs with formaldehyde, not phenol; (D) monomers of Buna-S.\nWorking: phenol + HCHO (acid or base) first gives o- and p-hydroxymethylphenol, which condense to the linear polymer Novolac; the monomer unit is o-hydroxymethylphenol (C).\nKey idea: Novolac = phenol + HCHO via o-hydroxymethylphenol.","traps":["Melamine vs phenol resin: Melamine–HCHO is a separate polymer; Novolac is phenol–HCHO."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which among the following is not a polyester?","options":["Novolac","PHBV","Dacron","Glyptal"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"PHBV (biodegradable) may not be recognised as a polyester; glyptal is less familiar than Dacron.\nWorking: novolac is a linear phenol–formaldehyde resin (CH₂-bridged phenols, no ester links); PHBV, Dacron (terylene) and glyptal are polyesters.\nKey idea: Ester links come from acid + alcohol (or hydroxy acid) monomers; phenol + HCHO gives CH₂ bridges.","traps":["PHBV not recognised as polyester: Hydroxy acids condense to esters."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Match List I (Monomer unit) with List II (Polymer): (a) Caprolactam – (i) Natural rubber; (b) 2-Chloro-1,3-butadiene – (ii) Buna-N; (c) Isoprene – (iii) Nylon 6; (d) Acrylonitrile – (iv) Neoprene. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)→(iv), (b)→(iii), (c)→(ii), (d)→(i)","(a)→(ii), (b)→(i), (c)→(iv), (d)→(iii)","(a)→(iii), (b)→(iv), (c)→(i), (d)→(ii)","(a)→(i), (b)→(ii), (c)→(iii), (d)→(iv)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) – random pairings; most likely slip is linking acrylonitrile to neoprene or chloroprene to Buna-N.\nWorking: caprolactam → nylon 6; chloroprene → neoprene; isoprene → natural rubber; acrylonitrile (with butadiene) → Buna-N.\nKey idea: Chloro-diene → neoprene; nitrile → Buna-N.","traps":["Chloroprene vs acrylonitrile: Neoprene = polychloroprene; Buna-N contains acrylonitrile."],"topics":["Rubber","Addition polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In polymer Buna-S: 'S' stands for:","options":["Sulphonation","Strength","Sulphur","Styrene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Sulphur – confusion with vulcanisation; (A) and (B) are word-association guesses.\nWorking: Buna-S is a copolymer of 1,3-butadiene and styrene.\nKey idea: Bu = butadiene, Na = sodium (catalyst), S = styrene.","traps":["S = sulphur: Recall the monomers, not processing."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which statement is correct?","options":["Synthesis of Buna-S needs nascent oxygen.","Neoprene is an addition copolymer used in plastic bucket manufacturing.","Buna-S is a synthetic and linear thermosetting polymer.","Buna-N is a natural polymer."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) neoprene is a homopolymer and an elastomer; (C) elastomers are not thermosetting; (D) \"Buna\" rubbers are synthetic.\nWorking: (B) neoprene is an addition homopolymer of chloroprene (an elastomer), not a copolymer for buckets; (C) Buna-S is an elastomer, not thermosetting; (D) Buna-N is synthetic. (A) refers loosely to the free-radical (peroxide-initiated) copolymerisation of 1,3-butadiene and styrene; this is the intended correct statement.\nKey idea: Buna-S/Buna-N/neoprene are synthetic elastomers made by free-radical addition polymerisation.","traps":["Rejecting the odd \"nascent oxygen\" wording: Eliminate the three clearly false statements.","Neoprene called copolymer: Neoprene = polychloroprene (single monomer)."],"topics":["Rubber","Classification"]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The structure of Neoprene is –","options":["–[CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–CH₂–CH(CN)]ₙ–","–[CH₂–CH(CN)]ₙ–","–[CH₂–C(Cl)=CH–CH₂]ₙ–","–[NH–(1,3,5-triazine-2,4-diyl, 6-NH–)–NH–CH₂]ₙ–"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Buna-N-like copolymer unit (butadiene + acrylonitrile); (B) polyacrylonitrile (orlon/acrilan); (D) melamine–formaldehyde type unit – all familiar polymer structures.\nWorking: neoprene is the addition polymer of chloroprene (2-chlorobuta-1,3-diene), repeat unit –CH₂–C(Cl)=CH–CH₂–.\nKey idea: Neoprene = polychloroprene; Cl sits on the double-bond carbon of each butadiene-derived unit.","traps":["Neoprene confused with Buna-N: Neoprene has Cl (chloroprene); Buna-N has CN (acrylonitrile).","PAN mistaken for a rubber: PAN is a fibre with no C=C left in the chain."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Orlon fibres are made up of:","options":["Polyacrylonitrile","Polyesters","Polyamide","Cellulose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) polyester = Terylene/Dacron; (C) polyamide = nylon; (D) cellulose = natural/rayon – all other fibre classes.\nWorking: Orlon (acrilan) is polyacrylonitrile, –[CH₂–CH(CN)]ₙ–, made by addition polymerisation of acrylonitrile.\nKey idea: Orlon = acrilan = polyacrylonitrile (wool substitute).","traps":["Fibre trade names mixed up: Pair names: nylon–polyamide, terylene–polyester, orlon–PAN."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Bakelite is a cross-linked polymer of formaldehyde and :","options":["PHBV","Buna-S","Novolac","Dacron"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) PHBV biodegradable polyester; (B) Buna-S copolymer of butadiene and styrene; (D) Dacron polyester – all unrelated condensation/addition polymers.\nWorking: phenol and formaldehyde first give the linear polymer novolac; heating novolac with more formaldehyde cross-links it to Bakelite.\nKey idea: Phenol + HCHO → novolac (linear) → + HCHO, heat → Bakelite (cross-linked).","traps":["Looking for 'phenol': Remember the intermediate stage: novolac."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Chloroprene (b) Neoprene (c) Acrylonitrile (d) Isoprene. List-II: (i) CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH=CH₂ (ii) CH₂=C(Cl)–CH=CH₂ (iii) –[CH₂–C(Cl)=CH–CH₂]ₙ– (iv) CH₂=CH–CN. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) put the polymer against chloroprene (monomer–polymer swap); (C) swaps isoprene and neoprene.\nWorking: chloroprene = 2-chlorobuta-1,3-diene (ii); neoprene = polychloroprene (iii); acrylonitrile = CH₂=CHCN (iv); isoprene = 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene (i).\nKey idea: -prene with Cl = chloroprene; with CH₃ = isoprene; bracket with n = polymer (neoprene).","traps":["Monomer vs polymer: The (…)ₙ structure is the polymer.","Cl vs CH₃ diene: Check the substituent at C2."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The structure shown below is a repeating unit for:","options":["Novolac","Buna–N","Acrilan","Neoprene"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Buna-N and (D) Neoprene are elastomers from dienes; (C) Acrilan is polyacrylonitrile – chosen only by students who do not link phenol rings to phenol–formaldehyde resins.\nWorking: phenol rings linked through ortho –CH₂– bridges is the linear phenol–formaldehyde polymer Novolac; Buna-N is butadiene–acrylonitrile, Acrilan is polyacrylonitrile and Neoprene is polychloroprene – none contain phenol units.\nKey idea: Phenol rings bridged by CH₂ groups = phenol–formaldehyde resin; the linear (uncross-linked) one is Novolac, the cross-linked one Bakelite.","traps":["Novolac vs Bakelite: Linear chain = Novolac; 3-D cross-linked = Bakelite."],"img":["img/jee/JM21Jul25S1C01-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Biodegradable polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"A biodegradable polyamide can be made from:","options":["Glycine and isoprene","Hexamethylene diamine and adipic acid","Glycine and aminocaproic acid","Styrene and caproic acid"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is a real polyamide (nylon-6,6) but not biodegradable – the main trap; (A) and (D) pair monomers that cannot form a polyamide.\nWorking: nylon-2-nylon-6 is the biodegradable polyamide copolymer of glycine (H₂N–CH₂–COOH) and 6-aminocaproic acid; hexamethylenediamine + adipic acid gives nylon-6,6, a non-biodegradable polyamide.\nKey idea: Biodegradable polyamide = nylon-2-nylon-6 = glycine + aminocaproic acid.","traps":["Polyamide but not biodegradable: Read the qualifier \"biodegradable\" first."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Which of the following polymer is used in the manufacture of wood laminates?","options":["cis-poly isoprene","Melamine formaldehyde resin","Urea formaldehyde resin","Phenol and formaldehyde resin"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) natural rubber – elastomer; (B) melamine-formaldehyde – unbreakable crockery; (D) phenol-formaldehyde (Bakelite/novolac) – switches, combs, binding glue for laminated wood in some texts.\nWorking: old NCERT gives urea-formaldehyde resin for unbreakable cups and laminated sheets. (In industry phenol-formaldehyde and melamine resins are also used in laminates, so the item rests on the NCERT list.)\nKey idea: NCERT pairing: UF – laminated sheets; MF – crockery; PF – Bakelite items.","traps":["Phenol-formaldehyde chosen: Answer from the NCERT use-table, not general knowledge."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Monomer units of Dacron polymer are:","options":["ethylene glycol and phthalic acid","ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid","glycerol and terephthalic acid","glycerol and phthalic acid"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ethylene glycol + phthalic acid – wrong isomer of the diacid; (C) glycerol + terephthalic acid – wrong alcohol; (D) glycerol + phthalic acid – glyptal.\nWorking: Dacron (terylene, PET) is the condensation polymer of ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic (terephthalic) acid.\nKey idea: Dacron = poly(ethylene terephthalate).","traps":["Phthalic vs terephthalic acid: Dacron/terylene is a linear polyester: needs para (terephthalic) acid.","Glycerol vs ethylene glycol: Glyptal = glycerol + phthalic acid; Dacron = ethylene glycol + terephthalic acid."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Preparation of Bakelite proceeds via reactions.","options":["Condensation and elimination","Electrophilic addition and dehydration","Electrophilic substitution and dehydration","Nucleophilic addition and dehydration"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) \"condensation and elimination\" is vague and repeats the same idea; (B) aromatic ring does not undergo addition; (D) describes HCHO as electrophile from the carbonyl point of view – tempting, but the ring step is substitution.\nWorking: HCHO (activated by acid/base) attacks the o/p positions of phenol (electrophilic aromatic substitution) giving hydroxymethylphenols, which then link to phenol rings by loss of water (dehydration/condensation) forming –CH₂– bridges. Note: option (A) is loosely true for a condensation polymer, and some sources call the first step nucleophilic addition of phenoxide to HCHO; the official answer is (C).\nKey idea: The phenol ring is substituted (o/p) by the CH₂OH group, and the polymer grows by losing water.","traps":["Reaction viewed from HCHO side: Name the step by what happens to the phenol ring."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Biodegradable polymers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which polymer has 'chiral' monomer(s)?","options":["Buna-N","Nylon 6,6","Neoprene","PHBV"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Buna-N, (B) Nylon 6,6, (C) Neoprene all have achiral monomers; a student unsure of monomers may guess Nylon.\nWorking: PHBV is made from 3-hydroxybutanoic acid and 3-hydroxypentanoic acid, both with a stereocentre at C-3; the monomers of the others (buta-1,3-diene, acrylonitrile, hexamethylenediamine, adipic acid, chloroprene) are achiral.\nKey idea: 3-hydroxy acids have CH(OH) with four different groups → chiral.","traps":["Monomer of PHBV not recalled: Learn PHBV = 3-hydroxybutanoic + 3-hydroxypentanoic acid."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which one of the following polymers is not obtained by condensation polymerisation?","options":["Buna-N","Bakelite","Nylon 6","Nylon 6, 6"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Nylon 6 – formed by ring-opening of caprolactam, which some students call addition, but NCERT classes it as condensation; (B) bakelite and (D) nylon 6,6 – clear condensation polymers.\nWorking: Buna-N is an addition copolymer of buta-1,3-diene and acrylonitrile; Bakelite (phenol–HCHO), nylon 6,6 (hexamethylenediamine + adipic acid) and nylon 6 (from caprolactam, classed in NCERT as condensation) are condensation polymers.\nKey idea: Rubbers from dienes (Buna-S, Buna-N, neoprene) are addition polymers.","traps":["Nylon 6 taken as addition polymer: Follow NCERT's classification: nylon 6 is listed as condensation (polyamide).","NOT wording: Pick the addition polymer."],"topics":["Addition polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C03","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Rubber","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct match between Item-I and Item-II: Item-I: (a) Natural rubber, (b) Neoprene, (c) Buna-N, (d) Buna-S. Item-II: (I) 1,3-butadiene + styrene, (II) 1,3-butadiene + acrylonitrile, (III) Chloroprene, (IV) Isoprene.","options":["(a)-(III), (b)-(IV), (c)-(I), (d)-(II)","(a)-(IV), (b)-(III), (c)-(II), (d)-(I)","(a)-(IV), (b)-(III), (c)-(I), (d)-(II)","(a)-(III), (b)-(IV), (c)-(II), (d)-(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) swaps Buna-N/Buna-S; (A) and (D) swap natural rubber/neoprene; (D) also correct for Buna pair, (C) correct for rubber pair – each distractor gets two of four right.\nWorking: natural rubber = cis-polyisoprene; neoprene = polychloroprene; Buna-N = butadiene + acrylonitrile (N for nitrile); Buna-S = butadiene + styrene.\nKey idea: Letter after Buna tells the comonomer: N = acrylonitrile, S = styrene.","traps":["Buna-N and Buna-S swapped: N → nitrile (acrylonitrile), S → styrene.","Isoprene vs chloroprene swapped: Neoprene has Cl (chloroprene); natural rubber is isoprene."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The structure of Nylon-6 is:","options":["–[(CH₂)₆–C(=O)–NH]ₙ–","–[(CH₂)₄–C(=O)–NH]ₙ–","–[C(=O)–(CH₂)₅–NH]ₙ–","–[C(=O)–(CH₂)₆–NH]ₙ–"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) have 7 carbons per unit (6 CH₂ + C=O); (B) has 5 carbons.\nWorking: Nylon-6 comes from caprolactam; repeat unit –CO–(CH₂)₅–NH– has six carbons.\nKey idea: The '6' counts all carbons in the repeat unit: 5 CH₂ + 1 C=O.","traps":["Six CH₂ instead of six carbons: Count the carbonyl carbon too."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Which of the following compounds is a constituent of the polymer –[HN–CO–NH–CH₂]ₙ– ?","options":["Formaldehyde","Ammonia","Methylamine","N-Methyl urea"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) N-methyl urea mistakes the NH–CH₂ link for an N-methyl group; (C) methylamine for the same reason; (B) ammonia from N–H atoms.\nWorking: repeat unit –NH–CO–NH–CH₂– is urea–formaldehyde resin; monomers urea and formaldehyde.\nKey idea: –CH₂– between two N comes from HCHO bridging urea units.","traps":["NH–CH₂ read as N-methyl: The CH₂ is a bridge from formaldehyde."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following is a condensation polymer ?","options":["Buna-S","Nylon 6,6","Teflon","Neoprene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Buna-S, (C) Teflon, (D) neoprene are addition polymers from alkenes/dienes – chosen if \"rubber\"/\"synthetic\" is linked to condensation.\nWorking: nylon 6,6 forms from hexamethylenediamine + adipic acid with loss of water (condensation); Buna-S, Teflon and neoprene are addition (chain-growth) polymers.\nKey idea: Polyamides and polyesters are condensation polymers.","traps":["Copolymer confused with condensation polymer: Two monomers ≠ condensation; check for loss of small molecule."],"topics":["Condensation polymers","Classification"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct match between Item-I and Item-II is: Item-I: (a) High density polythene (b) Polyacrylonitrile (c) Novolac (d) Nylon 6. Item-II: (I) Peroxide catalyst (II) Condensation at high temperature & pressure (III) Ziegler-Natta catalyst (IV) Acid or base catalyst.","options":["(a)→(III), (b)→(I), (c)→(II), (d)→(IV)","(a)→(IV), (b)→(II), (c)→(I), (d)→(III)","(a)→(II), (b)→(IV), (c)→(I), (d)→(III)","(a)→(III), (b)→(I), (c)→(IV), (d)→(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps novolac and nylon 6 conditions: nylon 6 is not acid/base-catalysed in the NCERT account; (B) gives HDPE acid/base catalysis and PAN high-T condensation; (C) gives HDPE condensation and novolac a peroxide catalyst – both miss that HDPE needs Ziegler-Natta.\nWorking: HDPE by Ziegler-Natta (Et₃Al/TiCl₄); PAN by peroxide-initiated addition; novolac is the acid/base-catalysed phenol-formaldehyde product; nylon 6 from caprolactam heated with water at high temperature (ring-opening condensation).\nKey idea: Ziegler-Natta = HDPE; peroxide = PAN; novolac = acid/base; nylon 6 = caprolactam at high T.","traps":["Novolac vs nylon 6 conditions swapped: Novolac is the phenol-formaldehyde product (acid/base catalysed); nylon 6 needs high temperature with water."],"topics":["Addition polymers","Condensation polymers"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Classification","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Which of the following is a thermosetting polymer?","options":["Buna–N","PVC","Bakelite","Nylon 6"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) elastomer; (B) and (D) thermoplastic/fibre.\nWorking: Bakelite (phenol–formaldehyde) is cross-linked on heating and cannot be remoulded; Buna-N is an elastomer, PVC and nylon 6 are thermoplastics.\nKey idea: Bakelite = thermoset.","traps":["Nylon as thermoset: Fibres/thermoplastics soften on heating."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Addition polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct name of the following polymer is: –[CH₂–C(CH₃)₂]ₙ–","options":["Polyisoprene","Polytert-butylene","Polyisobutane","Polyisobutylene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) isoprene would retain a C=C; (B) \"tert-butylene\" is not a real monomer; (C) isobutane is saturated and cannot polymerise.\nWorking: repeating unit –CH₂–C(CH₃)₂– comes from isobutylene (2-methylpropene), CH₂=C(CH₃)₂.\nKey idea: Restore the C=C between the two backbone carbons: CH₂=C(CH₃)₂ = isobutylene.","traps":["Alkane name used for polymer: Polymer name comes from the alkene monomer."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The polymer obtained from the following reactions is:\nHOOC–(CH₂)₄–NH₂ (5-aminopentanoic acid) treated with (i) NaNO₂/H₃O⁺, (ii) polymerisation.","options":["–[C(=O)–(CH₂)₄–NH]ₙ– (polyamide)","–[O–(CH₂)₄–C(=O)]ₙ– (polyester)","–[NH–C(=O)–(CH₂)₄–C(=O)–NH]ₙ–","–[O–C(=O)–(CH₂)₄–O]ₙ–"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) polyamide – ignores the NaNO₂ step. (C)/(D) wrong repeat units (two carbonyls or two O).\nWorking: HNO₂ converts the primary aliphatic –NH₂ to –OH (via diazonium, N₂ lost), giving 5-hydroxypentanoic acid; self-condensation gives the polyester –[O(CH₂)₄CO]ₙ–.\nKey idea: Nitrous acid removes the amine, so no amide links are possible.","traps":["Ignoring NaNO₂ step: Aliphatic RNH₂ + HNO₂ → ROH + N₂."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The homopolymer formed from 4-hydroxy-butanoic acid is :-","options":["–[C(=O)(CH₂)₃–O]–ₙ","–[OC(=O)(CH₂)₃–O]–ₙ","–[C(=O)(CH₂)₂C(=O)–O]–ₙ","–[C(=O)(CH₂)₂C(=O)]–ₙ"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) has an extra O at the left – repeat unit would have formula C₄H₆O₃, not that of monomer minus H₂O. (C) has two C=O per unit – would need a diacid (succinic). (D) has no ester O – a polyketone, impossible from condensation.\nWorking: HO–CH₂CH₂CH₂–COOH self-condenses (–OH of one with –COOH of next, loss of H₂O) to give the polyester repeat unit –[CO(CH₂)₃O]–ₙ (poly-4-hydroxybutyrate).\nKey idea: Repeat unit = monomer – H₂O: C₄H₈O₃ → C₄H₆O₂, i.e. –CO–(CH₂)₃–O–.","traps":["Extra atom in repeat unit: Check repeat unit formula = monomer minus water.","Confusing AB monomer with AA+BB: A homopolymer of a hydroxy acid has one C=O and one ester O per unit."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Biodegradable polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Poly-β-hydroxybutyrate-co-β-hydroxyvalerate (PHBV) is a copolymer of ___.","options":["3-hydroxybutanoic acid and 4-hydroxypentanoic acid","2-hydroxybutanoic acid and 3-hydroxypentanoic acid","3-hydroxybutanoic acid and 2-hydroxypentanoic acid","3-hydroxybutanoic acid and 3-hydroxypentanoic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(C) each shift one hydroxy position (2- or 4-) – partial recall cannot separate them.\nWorking: β-hydroxy = 3-hydroxy; butyrate = butanoic, valerate = pentanoic.\nKey idea: β-carbon = C-3 of the acid for both monomers.","traps":["β converted to wrong locant: α = C-2, β = C-3 counting the COOH carbon as C-1."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Condensation polymers","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The two monomers for the synthesis of Nylon 6,6 are :","options":["HOOC(CH₂)₆COOH, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂","HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, H₂N(CH₂)₄NH₂","HOOC(CH₂)₆COOH, H₂N(CH₂)₄NH₂","HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HOOC(CH₂)₆COOH has 8 C (suberic acid) – counts six CH₂ instead of six carbons. (B) and (C) use putrescine (4 C diamine).\nWorking: Nylon 6,6 = hexamethylenediamine (6 C) + adipic acid HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH (6 C including the two COOH carbons).\nKey idea: Both monomers have six carbons in total: (CH₂)₄ in the acid, (CH₂)₆ in the amine.","traps":["CH₂ count vs carbon count: Adipic acid: 4 CH₂ + 2 COOH = 6 C."]}],"m01-basic":[{"id":"JM25Apr02S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"0.1 mol of the following given antiviral compound (P) (shown below) will weigh ________ × 10⁻¹ g (nearest integer).\n(Given : molar mass in g mol⁻¹ H : 1, C : 12, N : 14, O : 16, F : 19, I : 127)","type":"num","answer":"372","range":[372.0,372.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 37 (forgot the × 10⁻¹ format); 370/371 (missed implicit H); 356 (missed ring O); 245 (left out I when reading the structure).\nWorking: P is C₉H₁₀FIN₂O₅, M = 108 + 10 + 19 + 127 + 28 + 80 = 372 g mol⁻¹; 0.1 mol weighs 37.2 g = 372 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: Count every atom including hidden H, find M = 372, then express 37.2 g in units of 10⁻¹ g.","traps":["Implicit atoms missed when counting formula from a structure: Count C, H, N, O, F, I systematically: C₉H₁₀FIN₂O₅ = 108 + 10 + 19 + 127 + 28 + 80 = 372.","Answer-box power-of-ten scaling in numerical answer: 0.1 × 372 = 37.2 g = 372 × 10⁻¹ g."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr02S1C25-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Thyroxine, the hormone has given below structure\nThe percentage of iodine in thyroxine is ________%. (nearest integer)\n(Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹ C:12, H:1, O:16, N:14, I:127)","type":"num","answer":"65","range":[65.0,65.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 58 (3 I); 48 (2 I); 127/777 (16) one iodine only.\nWorking: thyroxine is C₁₅H₁₁I₄NO₄, M = 180 + 11 + 508 + 14 + 64 = 777 g mol⁻¹; %I = 508/777 × 100 = 65.4 ≈ 65.\nKey idea: Get the full formula C₁₅H₁₁I₄NO₄ and the iodine share follows.","traps":["Implicit atoms missed when counting formula from a structure: Count: C₁₅H₁₁I₄NO₄; M = 180 + 11 + 508 + 14 + 64 = 777.","Iodine atom count from structure: Four I atoms, two on each ring."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr07S1C25-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's atomic theory","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Choose the Incorrect Statement about Dalton's Atomic Theory","options":["Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in any ratio.","Matter consists of indivisible atoms.","chemical reactions involve reorganization of atoms","All the atoms of a given element have identical properties including identical mass."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) tempt students who judge by modern knowledge (subatomic particles, isotopes) instead of what Dalton stated. (C) is a correct postulate.\nWorking: Dalton's postulate says atoms combine in a fixed ratio; 'any ratio' is wrong. B, C, D are Dalton's postulates as stated (whatever their modern validity).\nKey idea: Question asks about Dalton's theory as stated: only 'any ratio' misquotes it.","traps":["Modern correctness vs postulate: Judge against Dalton's own postulates.","'Fixed' changed to 'any': Recall: fixed (simple whole-number) ratio."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"From 6.55 g of aniline, the maximum amount of acetanilide that can be prepared will be ________ × 10⁻¹ g.","type":"num","answer":"95","range":[95.0,95.0],"explain":"Common slips: 70 (reporting moles × 1000); 66 or 65 (giving aniline mass); 95 vs 96 rounding (9.508 g).\nWorking: C₆H₅NH₂ (93 g mol⁻¹) → C₆H₅NHCOCH₃ (135 g mol⁻¹), 1:1. 6.55/93 = 0.0704 mol × 135 = 9.51 g = 95 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: 1 mol aniline → 1 mol acetanilide; mass ratio 135/93.","traps":["Molar mass of acetanilide: Aniline + CH₃CO − H: 93 + 42 = 135.","Power-of-ten format: 9.51 g = 95 × 10⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's atomic theory","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The incorrect postulates of the Dalton's atomic theory are:\n(A) Atoms of different elements differ in mass.\n(B) Matter consists of divisible atoms.\n(C) Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio.\n(D) All the atoms of given element have different properties including mass.\n(E) Chemical reactions involve reorganisation of atoms.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(B), (D) only","(A), (B), (D) only","(B), (D), (E) only","(C), (D), (E) only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (B) includes statement (A), a true postulate. Option (C) includes statement (E), and option (D) includes statements (C) and (E), which are true postulates; a student who mixes in modern corrections (isotopes) may doubt them.\nWorking: Dalton said atoms are indivisible (B wrong) and all atoms of one element are identical including mass (D wrong); A, C and E are his postulates.\nKey idea: Dalton: atoms indivisible, identical within an element; select only statements that contradict these.","traps":["Asked for incorrect postulates: Underline \"incorrect\" before judging each line.","Modern knowledge mixed with Dalton: Judge against Dalton’s original postulates, not modern theory."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"An organic compound has 42.1% carbon, 6.4% hydrogen and the remainder is oxygen. If its molecular weight is 342, then its molecular formula is:","options":["C₁₄H₂₀O₁₀","C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁","C₁₂H₂₀O₁₂","C₁₁H₁₈O₁₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C₁₄H₂₀O₁₀ has M = 348 and (C) C₁₂H₂₀O₁₂ has M = 356 – both fail the molar mass. (D) C₁₁H₁₈O₁₂ also has M = 342, but %C = 38.6%, so only a %C check rules it out.\nWorking: C = 0.421×342/12 ≈ 12; H = 0.064×342 ≈ 22; O = (342 − 144 − 22)/16 = 11 → C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (sucrose).\nKey idea: Use %C to get C count directly: 0.421×342/12 = 12.","traps":["Checking molar mass only: Verify %C and %H, not just total mass."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The number of moles of methane required to produce 11 g CO₂(g) after complete combustion is: (Given molar mass of methane in g mol⁻¹: 16)","options":["0.35","0.25","0.5","0.75"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 0.5 if 11/22 or using O₂ ratio; 0.6875 (11/16) not offered.\nWorking: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; 11/44 = 0.25 mol CO₂ = 0.25 mol CH₄.\nKey idea: Use molar mass of CO₂ (44), not methane's 16.","traps":["Given molar mass misused: Moles of CO₂ = 11/44."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"In the Claisen-Schmidt reaction to prepare 351 g of dibenzalacetone using 87 g of acetone, the amount of benzaldehyde required is ______ g. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"318","range":[318.0,318.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 159 (1:1 ratio), other values from a wrong product molar mass.\nWorking: 2C₆H₅CHO + CH₃COCH₃ → C₆H₅CH=CHCOCH=CHC₆H₅ (M = 234) + 2H₂O. 351/234 = 1.5 mol product = 87/58 = 1.5 mol acetone; benzaldehyde = 3 mol × 106 = 318 g.\nKey idea: Both α-CH₃ groups of acetone condense: 2 PhCHO per acetone.","traps":["1:1 ratio used: Dibenzal = two benzylidene units.","Product molar mass: 234 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Combustion of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) produces CO₂ and water. The amount of oxygen (in g) required for the complete combustion of 900 g of glucose is :\n[Molar mass of glucose in g mol⁻¹ = 180]","options":["32","480","800","960"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 32 = molar mass of O₂ only; (B) 480 = taking O atoms 6 × 16 × 5 (O instead of O₂); (C) 800 = slip in mole ratio (5 O₂ per glucose).\nWorking: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O; 900/180 = 5 mol glucose needs 30 mol O₂ = 30 × 32 = 960 g.\nKey idea: 1 glucose needs 6 O₂; 5 mol × 6 × 32 g.","traps":["Using atomic O mass instead of O₂: Count O₂ molecules and use 32 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C10","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Laws of chemical combination","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The candela is the luminous intensity, in a given direction, of a source that emits monochromatic radiation of frequency 'A' × 10¹² hertz and that has a radiant intensity in that direction of 1/'B', watt per steradian. 'A' and 'B' are respectively","options":["450 and 683","540 and 1/683","540 and 683","450 and 1/683"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) use 450 (digit swap of 540). (B)/(D) put 1/683 as B although the stem already writes 1/B.\nWorking: frequency 540 × 10¹² Hz; radiant intensity 1/683 W sr⁻¹, so B = 683.\nKey idea: Same two numbers in both definitions: 540 THz and 683.","traps":["Digit swap 540 vs 450: 540 × 10¹² Hz (green light, ~555 nm).","B already inverted in stem: Radiant intensity is 1/683, so B = 683."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Consider the following reaction :\n3PbCl₂ + 2(NH₄)₃PO₄ → Pb₃(PO₄)₂ + 6NH₄Cl\nIf 72 mmol of PbCl₂ is mixed with 50 mmol of (NH₄)₃PO₄, then the amount of Pb₃(PO₄)₂ formed is _______ mmol (nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"24","range":[24.0,24.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 25 (50/2, phosphate taken as limiting), 72 or 50 (ignores stoichiometry).\nWorking: 72 mmol PbCl₂ needs 48 mmol phosphate (< 50) → PbCl₂ limiting → 72/3 = 24 mmol.\nKey idea: Divide each amount by its coefficient: 72/3 = 24 < 50/2 = 25.","traps":["Phosphate taken as limiting: Compare moles ÷ coefficient."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"10 mL of gaseous hydrocarbon on combustion gives 40 mL of CO₂(g) and 50 mL of water vapour. Total number of carbon and hydrogen atoms in the hydrocarbon is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 9 (taking y = 5, forgetting 2 H per H₂O), 4 or 10 (reporting only one count).\nWorking: x = 40/10 = 4; y/2 = 50/10 → y = 10; C₄H₁₀ → 4 + 10 = 14.\nKey idea: CₓHᵧ + O₂ → x CO₂ + y/2 H₂O, volumes in same ratio as moles.","traps":["Two H per water: y = 2 × (V H₂O / V hydrocarbon)."],"topics":["Empirical & molecular formula","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Mass of methane required to produce 22 g of CO₂ after complete combustion is _______ g.\n(Given Molar mass in g mol⁻¹ C = 12.0, H = 1.0, O = 16.0)","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 16 (taking 1 mol), 4 (halving twice), 11 (mass-to-mass 1:1 without moles).\nWorking: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; 22 g CO₂ = 0.5 mol → 0.5 mol CH₄ = 8 g.\nKey idea: 1 mol CH₄ → 1 mol CO₂.","traps":["Mass ratio taken 1:1: Convert 22 g CO₂ to 0.5 mol first."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"9.3 g of aniline is subjected to reaction with excess of acetic anhydride to prepare acetanilide. The mass of acetanilide produced if the reaction is 100% completed is ________ ×10⁻¹ g. (Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹ N : 14, O : 16, C : 12, H : 1)","type":"num","answer":"135","range":[135.0,135.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 13.5 entered unscaled (14 after rounding), 93 or 177 (wrong product formula, e.g. diacetyl), 129 (wrong H count).\nWorking: aniline C₆H₇N = 93 g mol⁻¹, 9.3 g = 0.1 mol → 0.1 mol acetanilide C₈H₉NO (135 g mol⁻¹) = 13.5 g = 135 ×10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: 1 mol aniline → 1 mol acetanilide; 93 → 135 g mol⁻¹.","traps":["Answer not scaled by 10⁻¹: Divide by 10⁻¹: enter 135.","Product formula wrong: Count atoms: C₆H₅NHCOCH₃ = C₈H₉NO."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"If 50 mL of 0.5 M oxalic acid is required to neutralise 25 mL of NaOH solution, the amount of NaOH in 50 mL of given NaOH solution is ______ g.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 1 (forgets dibasic factor and keeps 25 mL), 2 (forgets factor 2, or gives mass in 25 mL), 8 (doubles twice).\nWorking: oxalic acid 25 mmol × 2 H⁺ = 50 mmol OH⁻ in 25 mL → 2 M NaOH; 50 mL contains 0.1 mol = 4 g.\nKey idea: (COOH)₂ supplies 2 H⁺; then rescale to 50 mL.","traps":["Oxalic acid treated as monobasic: H₂C₂O₄ + 2NaOH; n-factor 2.","Mass found for 25 mL: Read which volume is asked (50 mL)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"0.05 cm thick coating of silver is deposited on a plate of 0.05 m² area. The number of silver atoms deposited on plate are _______ × 10²³. (At mass Ag = 108, d = 7.9 g cm⁻³)","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 0.11 or 1.1 (taking 0.05 m² as 5 cm² or 50 cm²), 1.83 (reporting moles), 110 (exponent slip).\nWorking: V = 0.05 cm × 500 cm² = 25 cm³; m = 25 × 7.9 = 197.5 g; n = 197.5/108 = 1.829 mol; atoms = 1.829 × 6.022×10²³ = 11.0 × 10²³.\nKey idea: 1 m² = 10⁴ cm², so 0.05 m² = 500 cm².","traps":["Area conversion slip: Convert 0.05 m² = 500 cm² before multiplying."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Number of moles of methane required to produce 22 g CO₂(g) after combustion is x × 10⁻² moles. The value of x is ________.","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong value: 5 (x × 10⁻² format misread as 0.05).\nWorking: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; 22 g CO₂ = 0.5 mol → 0.5 mol CH₄ = 50 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: 1:1 CH₄:CO₂.","traps":["Scaling x × 10⁻²: 0.5 = 50 × 10⁻²."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"A sample of CaCO₃ and MgCO₃ weighed 2.21 g is ignited to constant weight of 1.152 g. The composition of mixture is : (Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹ CaCO₃ : 100, MgCO₃ : 84)","options":["1.187 g CaCO₃ + 1.187 g MgCO₃","1.187 g CaCO₃ + 1.023 g MgCO₃","1.023 g CaCO₃ + 1.187 g MgCO₃","1.023 g CaCO₃ + 1.023 g MgCO₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) total ≠ 2.21 g (quick reject). (C) swaps the two masses.\nWorking: x + y = 2.21; 0.56x + (40/84)y = 1.152 → x = 1.187 g CaCO₃, y = 1.023 g MgCO₃.\nKey idea: Residue = CaO + MgO; set up two equations.","traps":["Swapping components: Check: CaCO₃ loses 44% mass, MgCO₃ 52%."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Percentage composition"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"If 5 moles of BaCl₂ is mixed with 2 moles of Na₃PO₄, the maximum number of moles of Ba₃(PO₄)₂ formed is ______ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2: rounds 5/3 from BaCl₂, or takes 2 mol from Na₃PO₄ ignoring the 2 : 1 ratio. 5: takes BaCl₂ moles directly.\nWorking: 3BaCl₂ + 2Na₃PO₄ → Ba₃(PO₄)₂ + 6NaCl. 5 mol BaCl₂ could give 5/3 = 1.67; 2 mol Na₃PO₄ gives 1 → Na₃PO₄ limiting → 1 mol.\nKey idea: Divide each reactant's moles by its coefficient; the smaller quotient wins.","traps":["Smaller mole number taken as limiting: Compare n/coefficient: 5/3 vs 2/2."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The volume of 0.02 M aqueous HBr required to neutralize 10.0 mL of 0.01 M aqueous Ba(OH)₂ is (Assume complete neutralization)","options":["7.5 mL","5.0 mL","10.0 mL","2.5 mL"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 5.0 mL ignores the factor 2 for Ba(OH)₂; (D) 2.5 mL applies the factor the wrong way; (A) random.\nWorking: mmol OH⁻ = 10 × 0.01 × 2 = 0.2; V(HBr) = 0.2/0.02 = 10.0 mL.\nKey idea: n-factor of Ba(OH)₂ is 2.","traps":["Factor 2 of Ba(OH)₂ dropped: Write balanced equation 2HBr + Ba(OH)₂ → BaBr₂ + 2H₂O."],"topics":["Concentration terms","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Uracil is a base present in RNA with the following structure. % of N in uracil is ___________\n[Structure: pyrimidine-2,4(1H,3H)-dione – six-membered ring with two N–H, two C=O and one C=C]\nGiven:\nMolar mass N = 14 g mol⁻¹\nO = 16 g mol⁻¹\nC = 12 g mol⁻¹\nH = 1 g mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25.9 or 26 (all four H left out, M = 108), 12.5 (counting one N), 24.6 (adding extra H).\nWorking: uracil C₄H₄N₂O₂, M = 112 g mol⁻¹; %N = 28/112 × 100 = 25%.\nKey idea: Count implicit ring C–H: C₄H₄N₂O₂.","traps":["Hidden H atoms: Add one H on each of the two CH ring carbons."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The density of a monobasic strong acid (Molar mass 24.2 g/mol) is 1.21 kg/L. The volume of its solution required for the complete neutralization of 25 mL of 0.24 M NaOH is ________ × 10⁻² mL (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Numerical: 1.2 or 0.12 entered without the ×10⁻² scaling; 120 if mL/L slip; using 1.21 g/L (not kg/L) gives 50 mM and V = 120 mL.\nWorking: 1210 g/L ÷ 24.2 = 50 M; mol NaOH = 6 × 10⁻³; V = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ L = 0.12 mL = 12 × 10⁻² mL.\nKey idea: Treat 1.21 kg/L as 1210 g per litre of acid → 50 mol/L.","traps":["Missing the × 10⁻² scaling: Convert the final volume into the asked unit (×10⁻² mL) at the end.","kg/L read as g/mL wrongly: 1.21 kg/L = 1210 g/L = 1.21 g/mL."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Number of hydrogen atoms per molecule of a hydrocarbon A having 85.8% carbon is ______ (Given: Molar mass of A = 84 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (reporting empirical formula CH₂ ratio); 14 (assuming alkane C₆H₁₄ without calculating).\nWorking: 0.858×84 = 72 g → 6 C; H: 12 g → 12 H; C₆H₁₂.\nKey idea: Mass of C = 0.858 × 84 ≈ 72 g (6 C); remainder 12 g = 12 H.","traps":["Empirical vs molecular formula: Scale by molar mass to the molecular formula."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"When a hydrocarbon A undergoes combustion in the presence of air, it requires 9.5 equivalents of oxygen and produces 3 equivalents of water. What is the molecular formula of A?","options":["C₉H₆","C₆H₆","C₈H₆","C₉H₉"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C₉H₆ if O atoms (not O₂) miscounted (x = 9); (B) C₆H₆ if only water is used; (D) C₉H₉ if y taken as water count ×3.\nWorking: CₓHᵧ + (x + y/4)O₂ → xCO₂ + (y/2)H₂O; y/2 = 3 ⇒ y = 6; x + 6/4 = 9.5 ⇒ x = 8 ⇒ C₈H₆.\nKey idea: Moles O₂ = x + y/4; moles H₂O = y/2.","traps":["\"Equivalents\" meaning moles: Here equivalents = moles per mole of A.","O₂ vs O atoms: Write the balanced equation explicitly."],"topics":["Empirical & molecular formula","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The volume of HCl, containing 73 g L⁻¹, required to completely neutralise NaOH obtained by reacting 0.69 g of metallic sodium with water, is __________ mL. (Nearest Integer)\n(Given : molar masses of Na, Cl, O, H, are 23, 35.5, 16 and 1 g mol⁻¹ respectively.)","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 30 (using 1 M, i.e. dividing 73 by 73); 7.5 (halving moles, as if 2Na → 1 NaOH); 0.015 (answer left in litres).\nWorking: 0.69 g Na = 0.03 mol → 0.03 mol NaOH; HCl = 73/36.5 = 2 mol L⁻¹; V = 0.03/2 L = 0.015 L = 15 mL.\nKey idea: 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂: 1 mol NaOH per mol Na.","traps":["Na : NaOH ratio: Balance: 2Na → 2NaOH.","g L⁻¹ to mol L⁻¹: Divide by 36.5 g mol⁻¹."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"When 0.01 mol of an organic compound containing 60% carbon was burnt completely, 4.4 g of CO₂ was produced. The molar mass of compound is _____________ g mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"200","range":[200.0,200.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 20 (using 1.2 g C as molar mass basis without dividing by moles); 2 (dividing wrongly); 120 (forgetting the 60%).\nWorking: 4.4 g CO₂ = 0.1 mol C = 1.2 g C from 0.01 mol compound, i.e. 120 g C per mole; molar mass = 120/0.60 = 200 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: Mass of C per mole of compound ÷ fraction C = molar mass.","traps":["Per-sample vs per-mole: Scale to one mole before applying %."],"topics":["Molar mass","Percentage composition"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"A 300 mL bottle of soft drink has 0.2 M CO₂ dissolved in it. Assuming CO₂ behaves as an ideal gas, the volume of the dissolved CO₂ at STP is ______ mL. (Nearest integer) Given: At STP, molar volume of an ideal gas is 22.7 L mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"1362","range":[1362.0,1362.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1344 (using 22.4 L), 1 (answering in L), 60 (moles in mmol).\nWorking: n = 0.2 × 0.300 = 0.06 mol; V = 0.06 × 22.7 L = 1.362 L = 1362 mL.\nKey idea: n = MV = 0.06 mol; V = 0.06 × 22.7 L = 1362 mL.","traps":["22.4 vs 22.7 L: Use the value given (22.7 L at 1 bar, 273 K).","L vs mL: Convert L to mL."],"topics":["Mole concept","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The strength of 50 volume solution of hydrogen peroxide is ____ g/L (Nearest integer). Given: Molar mass of H₂O₂ is 34 g mol⁻¹; Molar volume of gas at STP = 22.7 L.","type":"num","answer":"150","range":[150.0,150.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 75 (forgetting 2:1 H₂O₂:O₂), 152 (using 22.4 L), 4.4 (giving molarity).\nWorking: 50 L O₂ = 50/22.7 mol O₂ ⇒ 100/22.7 mol H₂O₂ per L ⇒ ×34 = 149.8 ≈ 150 g/L.\nKey idea: 1 L of 'x volume' H₂O₂ releases x L O₂ at STP; moles H₂O₂ = 2x/22.7.","traps":["2 mol H₂O₂ per mol O₂: Write 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ first.","22.7 vs 22.4 L: Use the molar volume given."],"topics":["Concentration terms","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid to give hydrogen and zinc chloride. The volume of hydrogen gas produced at STP from the reaction of 11.5 g of zinc with excess HCl is ________ L (Nearest integer) (Given : Molar mass of Zn is 65.4 g mol⁻¹ and Molar volume of H₂ at STP = 22.7 L)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (taking 2 mol H₂ per Zn from the 2HCl coefficient); 2 (halving); 4 is robust even with 22.4 L (3.94).\nWorking: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂; 11.5/65.4 = 0.1758 mol H₂; 0.1758 × 22.7 = 3.99 L ≈ 4.\nKey idea: Zn : H₂ = 1 : 1.","traps":["Mole ratio from the wrong coefficient: Ratio Zn : H₂ is 1 : 1."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"When a hydrocarbon A undergoes complete combustion it requires 11 equivalents of oxygen and produces 4 equivalents of water. What is the molecular formula of A?","options":["C₉H₈","C₅H₈","C₁₁H₄","C₁₁H₈"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) C₅H₈ has 8 H but needs only 7 O₂. (C) C₁₁H₄ takes 11 as the carbon count and 4 as H. (D) C₁₁H₈ takes 11 O₂ as 11 C with correct H.\nWorking: y = 8; O balance 22 = 2x + 4 → x = 9; C₉H₈.\nKey idea: 11 O₂ = 22 O atoms; 4 H₂O uses 4, so 18 O go into 9 CO₂.","traps":["Moles of O₂ taken as number of C: Balance O atoms: 2×O₂ = 2x + y/2.","O atoms vs O₂ molecules: Count atoms, not molecules, in the balance."],"topics":["Empirical & molecular formula","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Assume carbon burns according to the following equation: 2C(s) + O₂(g) → 2CO(g). When 12 g carbon is burnt in 48 g of oxygen, the volume of carbon monoxide produced is ________ × 10⁻¹ L at STP [nearest integer]. [Given: Assume CO as ideal gas, Mass of C is 12 g mol⁻¹, Mass of O is 16 g mol⁻¹ and molar volume of an ideal gas at STP is 22.7 L mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"227","range":[227.0,227.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 681 (taking O₂ as limiting: 3 mol CO), 224 (22.4 L habit), 23 (forgetting ×10⁻¹ scaling).\nWorking: 12 g C = 1 mol and 48 g O₂ = 1.5 mol; 1 mol C needs only 0.5 mol O₂ → C limiting; 1 mol CO × 22.7 L = 22.7 L = 227 × 10⁻¹ L.\nKey idea: Compare mole ratio 2:1; carbon (1 mol) is limiting, giving 1 mol CO.","traps":["Oxygen taken as limiting: Compare moles against the 2:1 ratio.","22.4 vs 22.7 L: Use the given 22.7 L mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"SO₂Cl₂ on reaction with excess of water results into acidic mixture: SO₂Cl₂ + 2H₂O → H₂SO₄ + 2HCl. 16 moles of NaOH is required for the complete neutralisation of the resultant acidic mixture. The number of moles of SO₂Cl₂ used is:","options":["16","8","4","2"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 8 counts only H₂SO₄ as dibasic (2 H⁺ per SO₂Cl₂) or only HCl; (A) 16 takes a 1:1 ratio; (D) 2 takes 8 H⁺ per mole (double counting).\nWorking: 1 mol SO₂Cl₂ gives 1 H₂SO₄ + 2 HCl = 4 mol H⁺, needing 4 mol NaOH; 16/4 = 4 mol SO₂Cl₂.\nKey idea: Each SO₂Cl₂ yields 4 H⁺ in total.","traps":["Counting H⁺ from only one acid: Add 2 (H₂SO₄) + 2 (2 HCl) = 4 H⁺ per SO₂Cl₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"56.0 L of nitrogen gas is mixed with excess of hydrogen gas and it is found that 20 L of ammonia gas is produced. The volume of unused nitrogen gas is found to be ____ L.","type":"num","answer":"46","range":[46.0,46.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 36 (taking 1 : 1, i.e. 20 L N₂ used), 50 (using 3 : 1 H₂ ratio for N₂), 10 (giving N₂ used instead of unused).\nWorking: N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃; 20 L NH₃ needs 10 L N₂ (same T, p), so unused N₂ = 56 − 10 = 46 L.\nKey idea: Volumes of gases at same T, p follow the mole ratio.","traps":["Wrong N₂ : NH₃ ratio: N₂ used = ½ × V(NH₃).","Used vs unused: Subtract used from 56 L."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Hemoglobin contains 0.34% of iron by mass. The number of Fe atoms in 3.3 g of hemoglobin is : (Given : Atomic mass of Fe is 56 u, N_A is 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","options":["1.21 × 10⁵","12.0 × 10¹⁶","1.21 × 10²⁰","3.4 × 10²²"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) come from dropping or misplacing powers of ten; (B) 12.0 × 10¹⁶ results from using 0.34 instead of 0.0034 and a wrong exponent.\nWorking: mass of Fe = 3.3 × 0.0034 = 0.01122 g; moles = 0.01122/56 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴; atoms = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.21 × 10²⁰.\nKey idea: Fe mass = 0.34% of 3.3 g; then divide by 56 and multiply by Nₐ.","traps":["Percent to fraction: Divide the percentage by 100 before multiplying."],"topics":["Mole concept","Percentage composition"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The normality of H₂SO₄ in the solution obtained on mixing 100 mL of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ with 50 mL of 0.1 M NaOH is _______ × 10⁻¹ N. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.5 (reports molarity of leftover H₂SO₄, 0.05 M, instead of normality); 1.5 (ignores the final volume, 15 meq/100 mL); 2 (uses 0.1 M as 0.2 N but forgets the NaOH).\nWorking: H₂SO₄ gives 100 × 0.1 × 2 = 20 meq; NaOH 50 × 0.1 = 5 meq. Excess acid 15 meq in 150 mL → 0.1 N = 1 × 10⁻¹ N.\nKey idea: Work in milliequivalents and divide by total volume.","traps":["n-factor of H₂SO₄: H₂SO₄ is dibasic: N = 2M.","Final volume: Total volume after mixing is 150 mL."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"C₆H₅CH₃ (toluene, 5 g) → C₆H₅CHO (benzaldehyde, 92%). In the above reaction, 5 g of toluene is converted into benzaldehyde with 92% yield. The amount of benzaldehyde produced is ______ × 10⁻² g. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"530","range":[530.0,530.0],"explain":"Common slips: 576 (forgets the 92% yield); 460 (takes 92% of 5 g directly).\nWorking: moles toluene = 5/92 = 0.05435; theoretical benzaldehyde = 0.05435 × 106 = 5.761 g; at 92% = 5.30 g = 530 × 10⁻² g.\nKey idea: Mass product = (mass reactant/M reactant) × M product × yield.","traps":["Yield applied to wrong quantity: Convert through moles and molar mass of product.","Forgetting yield: Multiply theoretical mass by 0.92."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In the given reaction, X + Y + 3Z ⇌ XYZ₃, if one mole of each of X and Y with 0.05 mol of Z gives compound XYZ₃. (Given: Atomic masses of X, Y and Z are 10, 20 and 30 amu, respectively). The yield of XYZ₃ is __________ g. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 g if the 1/3 factor is forgotten (0.05 mol product); 120 g if X or Y is taken as limiting; the \"⇌\" may suggest an equilibrium calculation, but full conversion is assumed.\nWorking: Z is limiting (needs 3 per formula unit): 0.05/3 mol XYZ₃ × (10 + 20 + 90) = 120 g mol⁻¹ → 2 g.\nKey idea: Moles of product = moles of Z ÷ 3.","traps":["Ignoring coefficient 3: Divide each reactant’s moles by its coefficient.","Equilibrium arrow: Treat as complete yield based on limiting reagent."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"2 L of 0.2 M H₂SO₄ is reacted with 2 L of 0.1 M NaOH solution, the molarity of the resulting product Na₂SO₄ in the solution is ____ millimolar. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 50 (using 2 L volume), 100 (using H₂SO₄ as limiting), 200 (1:1 ratio with H₂SO₄ and 2 L).\nWorking: H₂SO₄ 0.4 mol, NaOH 0.2 mol; H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, NaOH limiting → 0.1 mol Na₂SO₄ in 4 L = 0.025 M = 25 mM.\nKey idea: NaOH is limiting (needs 2:1); final volume is 4 L.","traps":["Total volume after mixing: Add both volumes.","Limiting reagent: Check the 1:2 mole ratio."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g); 20 g N₂ and 5 g H₂ are taken. Consider the above reaction, the limiting reagent of the reaction and number of moles of NH₃ formed respectively are:","options":["H₂, 1.42 moles","H₂, 0.71 moles","N₂, 1.42 moles","N₂, 0.71 moles"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) choose H₂ because its mass is smaller; (B)/(D) 0.71 forgets the factor 2 for NH₃.\nWorking: n(N₂) = 20/28 = 0.714 mol; n(H₂) = 5/2 = 2.5 mol, needs only 3 × 0.714 = 2.14 mol, so N₂ is limiting; NH₃ = 2 × 0.714 = 1.43 mol (closest option: C, 1.42).\nKey idea: Compare available mole ratio (2.5/0.714 = 3.5) with required 3; N₂ runs out first.","traps":["Smaller mass taken as limiting: Convert to moles and divide by coefficients.","Product factor 2 dropped: Use 1 N₂ → 2 NH₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Consider the reaction 4HNO₃(l) + 3KCl(s) → Cl₂(g) + NOCl(g) + 2H₂O(g) + 3KNO₃(s). The amount of HNO₃ required to produce 110.0 g of KNO₃ is: (Given: atomic masses of H, O, N and K are 1, 16, 14 and 39, respectively.)","options":["32.2 g","69.4 g","91.5 g","162.5 g"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 69.4 g = 1 : 1 ratio (1.089 × 63 ≈ 68.6) or 3 : 4 inverted ratio; (A) 32.2 g inverted/partial ratio; (D) 162.5 g wrong molar mass or ratio slip.\nWorking: M(KNO₃) = 101, so 110/101 = 1.089 mol KNO₃. HNO₃ : KNO₃ = 4 : 3, so HNO₃ = 1.452 mol × 63 = 91.5 g.\nKey idea: n(HNO₃) = (4/3) n(KNO₃).","traps":["Mole ratio inverted: Write the ratio as wanted/given from coefficients."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"If a rocket runs on a fuel (C₁₅H₃₀) and liquid oxygen, the weight of oxygen required and CO₂ released for every litre of fuel respectively are: (Given: density of the fuel is 0.756 g/mL)","options":["1188 g and 1296 g","2376 g and 2592 g","2592 g and 2376 g","3429 g and 3142 g"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps the two values (CO₂ first); (A) is half of each value (using 11.25 O₂ or wrong mol count); (D) comes from wrong moles (e.g. density not applied correctly or M taken wrongly).\nWorking: 1 L fuel = 756 g = 3.6 mol C₁₅H₃₀ (M = 210). C₁₅H₃₀ + 22.5 O₂ → 15 CO₂ + 15 H₂O, so O₂ = 3.6 × 22.5 × 32 = 2592 g and CO₂ = 3.6 × 15 × 44 = 2376 g.\nKey idea: For CₙH₂ₙ, O₂ needed = 1.5n per mole; masses then follow from 3.6 mol.","traps":["Order of the two answers swapped: Write O₂ and CO₂ values separately and match the order in the stem.","Fractional O₂ coefficient: Balance O atoms: 30 (from CO₂) + 15 (from H₂O) = 45 O = 22.5 O₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"120 g of an organic compound that contains only carbon and hydrogen gives 330 g of CO₂ and 270 g of water on complete combustion. The percentage of carbon and hydrogen, respectively, are","options":["25 and 75","40 and 60","60 and 40","75 and 25"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 25 and 75 – C and H swapped; (B)/(C) 40/60 – taking mass of CO₂ vs H₂O ratio or using H = 1/18 instead of 2/18.\nWorking: C = 330 × 12/44 = 90 g; H = 270 × 2/18 = 30 g; 90 + 30 = 120 g, so C 75 %, H 25 %.\nKey idea: C from CO₂ × 12/44, H from H₂O × 2/18; check they add to the sample mass.","traps":["Hydrogen fraction taken as 1/18: Use 2/18 = 1/9 of the water mass for H.","Order of percentages swapped: Read \"carbon and hydrogen, respectively\" and match order."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of N atoms in 681 g of C₇H₅N₃O₆ is x × 10²¹. The value of x is ____. (N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹) (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"5418","range":[5418.0,5418.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1806 – forgets the 3 N per molecule; 5418 vs 541.8 – power-of-ten slip with ×10²¹.\nWorking: M(C₇H₅N₃O₆) = 84 + 5 + 42 + 96 = 227 g mol⁻¹; 681/227 = 3 mol; N atoms = 3 × 3 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 5.418 × 10²⁴ = 5418 × 10²¹.\nKey idea: 681 = 3 × 227; 9 mol N atoms.","traps":["Atoms per molecule omitted: Multiply by 3 N atoms per molecule.","Power of ten in answer: Write 5.418 × 10²⁴ as 5418 × 10²¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A protein 'A' contains 0.30% of glycine (molecular weight 75). The minimum molar mass of the protein 'A' is ________ × 10³ g mol⁻¹ [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 250 (0.30 % taken as 0.00030), 2.5 (0.30 % taken as 0.030), 19 if subtracting water of condensation (57/0.0030 = 19 × 10³) – the question uses molecular weight 75 directly.\nWorking: the protein must contain at least one glycine unit, so 0.30 % = 75/M ⇒ M = 75/0.0030 = 25 000 g mol⁻¹ = 25 × 10³.\nKey idea: Minimum molar mass = mass of one unit ÷ fraction.","traps":["Percent to fraction: 0.30 % = 0.0030.","Answer in ×10³ units: Divide final answer by 1000."],"topics":["Percentage composition","Molar mass"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The neutralization occurs when 10 mL of 0.1 M acid 'A' is allowed to react with 30 mL of 0.05 M base M(OH)₂. The basicity of the acid 'A' is ________. [M is a metal]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 1.5 → rounded 2 (forgetting the acidity 2 of M(OH)₂), 6 (multiplying wrongly).\nWorking: equivalents of acid = equivalents of base: 10 × 0.1 × n = 30 × 0.05 × 2 ⇒ n = 3/1 = 3.\nKey idea: n_acid × M_acid × V_acid = n_base × M_base × V_base.","traps":["Acidity of M(OH)₂: Multiply base moles by 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"CNG is an important transportation fuel. When 100 g CNG is mixed with 208 g oxygen in vehicles, it leads to the formation of CO₂ and H₂O and produces large quantity of heat during this combustion, then the amount of carbon dioxide, produced in grams is ______. [nearest integer] [Assume CNG to be methane]","type":"num","answer":"143","range":[143.0,143.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 275 (CH₄ taken as limiting, 6.25 × 44); 286 (O₂ moles used 1:1); 572 (mass ratio errors).\nWorking: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; CH₄ = 100/16 = 6.25 mol, O₂ = 208/32 = 6.5 mol; O₂ needed 12.5 mol, so O₂ is limiting; CO₂ = 6.5/2 = 3.25 mol × 44 = 143 g.\nKey idea: Compare mole ratio 6.5 : 6.25 against required 2 : 1 – oxygen runs out first.","traps":["Fuel assumed limiting: Compare moles divided by coefficients: CH₄ 6.25/1, O₂ 6.5/2 = 3.25 → O₂ limits."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The moles of methane required to produce 81 g of water after complete combustion is ______ × 10⁻² mol. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"225","range":[225.0,225.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 450 (ignoring the 1 : 2 ratio); 112 (halving twice).\nWorking: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O; 81 g H₂O = 4.5 mol; CH₄ = 4.5/2 = 2.25 mol = 225 × 10⁻² mol.\nKey idea: One CH₄ gives two H₂O.","traps":["Mole ratio ignored: Divide by 2: one CH₄ makes two H₂O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic & molecular mass","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Two elements A and B form 0.15 moles of A₂B and AB₃ type compounds. If both A₂B and AB₃ weigh equally, then the atomic weight of A is ______ times of atomic weight of B.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.5 – reports B/A; 3 or 1.5 – mis-sets the formula masses.\nWorking: equal moles and equal masses → equal molar masses: 2A + B = A + 3B → A = 2B, so the factor is 2.\nKey idea: Same moles + same mass → same molar mass; 0.15 is irrelevant.","traps":["Ratio inverted: A = 2B, so the factor is 2."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"116 g of a substance upon dissociation reaction, yields 7.5 g of hydrogen, 60 g of oxygen and 48.5 g of carbon. Given that the atomic masses of H, O and C are 1, 16 and 12 respectively. The data agrees with how many formulae of the following? (A) CH₃COOH (B) HCHO (C) CH₃OOCH₃ (D) CH₃CHO","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong answers: 1 (only HCHO counted, ignoring that CH₃COOH has the same empirical formula); 3 (CH₃OOCH₃ wrongly read as C₂H₄O₂); 0 (rejecting all because C does not divide exactly).\nWorking: moles H = 7.5, O = 3.75, C = 4.04; H : O = 2 : 1 and C ≈ O, so the empirical formula is CH₂O (the C mass is slightly off: exact CH₂O would need 45 g C, but the intended reading is CH₂O). CH₃COOH (C₂H₄O₂) and HCHO (CH₂O) fit; CH₃OOCH₃ (C₂H₆O₂) and CH₃CHO (C₂H₄O) do not. Count = 2.\nKey idea: Same empirical formula ≠ same molecular formula: CH₂O and C₂H₄O₂ both agree with the data.","traps":["Inexact carbon mass: Round to the closest simple ratio CH₂O; H : O is exact.","Only molecular formula CH₂O accepted: Mass composition fixes only the empirical formula."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Compound A contains 8.7% hydrogen, 74% carbon and 17.3% nitrogen. The molecular formula of the compound is (Given: atomic masses of C, H and N are 12, 1 and 14 amu respectively; the molar mass of compound A is 162 g mol⁻¹):","options":["C₄H₆N₂","C₂H₃N","C₅H₇N","C₁₀H₁₄N₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) C₅H₇N is the empirical formula (M = 81) – chosen if the molar mass step is skipped; (A) and (B) have the wrong C:H:N ratio (C₂H₃N is 2:3:1, not 5:7:1).\nWorking: in 162 g, C = 0.74×162 = 119.9 g → 10 C; H = 14.1 g → 14 H; N = 28.0 g → 2 N; C₁₀H₁₄N₂ (M = 162).\nKey idea: Multiply each mass fraction by 162 and divide by the atomic mass to get atoms per molecule.","traps":["Empirical vs molecular formula: Check the option mass against 162."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Production of iron in blast furnace follows the following equation: Fe₃O₄(s) + 4CO(g) → 3Fe(l) + 4CO₂(g). When 4.640 kg of Fe₃O₄ and 2.520 kg of CO are allowed to react, then the amount of iron (in g) produced is : [Given: Molar atomic mass (g mol⁻¹): Fe = 56, O = 16, C = 12]","options":["1400","2200","3360","4200"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"Taking CO as limiting gives 90 × ¾ × 56 = 3780 g (not offered), so the distractors catch other slips: (D) 4200 and (B) 2200 do not follow from either reactant correctly; (A) 1400 ≈ one-quarter-scale error. The only consistent value uses Fe₃O₄ as limiting (60 mol Fe).\nWorking: Fe₃O₄ = 4640/232 = 20 mol; CO = 2520/28 = 90 mol, needs only 80 mol, so Fe₃O₄ is limiting; Fe = 3 × 20 = 60 mol = 3360 g.\nKey idea: Compare moles available ÷ stoichiometric coefficient: 20/1 vs 90/4 = 22.5; the smaller is limiting.","traps":["Comparing raw moles instead of mole ratio: Divide moles by coefficients before comparing.","kg to g conversion: Convert to g first: 4640 g, 2520 g."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic & molecular mass","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Using the rules for significant figures, the correct answer for the expression (0.02858 × 0.112)/0.5702 will be:","options":["0.005613","0.00561","0.0056","0.006"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) keeps 4 s.f. (taken from 0.02858/0.5702); (C) 2 s.f. and (D) 1 s.f. from miscounting leading zeros of 0.112 or thinking decimal places matter.\nWorking: 0.02858 × 0.112 / 0.5702 = 0.005614; in multiplication/division the result keeps the fewest significant figures (0.112 has 3), so 0.00561.\nKey idea: Leading zeros are not significant; 0.112 has 3 s.f., so the answer has 3.","traps":["Decimal-place rule vs significant-figure rule: Multiply/divide → fewest s.f.; add/subtract → fewest decimal places.","Leading zeros counted as significant: Count from the first non-zero digit."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A chloro compound \"A\": (i) forms aldehydes on ozonolysis followed by the hydrolysis; (ii) when vaporized completely 1.53 g of A gives 448 mL of vapour at STP. The number of carbon atoms in a molecule of compound A is ____.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (assuming C₄H₉Cl ≈ 92.5 or misusing 22.7 L), 2 (vinyl chloride, M = 62.5), 76 (reporting M).\nWorking: 448 mL at STP = 0.02 mol → M = 1.53/0.02 = 76.5 g mol⁻¹ = C₃H₅Cl. An alkene that gives only aldehydes on ozonolysis: CH₂=CH–CH₂Cl (allyl chloride) → HCHO + ClCH₂CHO. Carbon atoms = 3.\nKey idea: 76.5 – 35.5 = 41 = C₃H₅ → allyl chloride.","traps":["Subtract Cl first: 76.5 – 35.5 = 41 → C₃H₅.","STP molar volume: Use 22.4 L at old STP as the data intend."],"topics":["Molar mass","Mole concept"]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"100 g of propane is completely reacted with 1000 g of oxygen. The mole fraction of carbon dioxide in the resulting mixture is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ______. (Nearest integer) [Atomic weight: H = 1.008; C = 12.00; O = 16.00]","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 25 (water left out of the mixture); 43 (excess O₂ left out, only CO₂ + H₂O counted); errors from taking O₂ as limiting.\nWorking: C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. n(C₃H₈) = 100/44.064 = 2.269 mol (limiting); n(O₂) = 31.25 mol. Products: CO₂ 6.808, H₂O 9.077, O₂ left 19.90 mol; total 35.79 mol. x(CO₂) = 0.190 → 19 (water counted as part of the gaseous mixture).\nKey idea: Total moles = CO₂ + H₂O + unreacted O₂.","traps":["Excess O₂ omitted: Resulting mixture includes leftover oxygen.","Water left out: No condensation stated – count H₂O in the total."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Sodium oxide reacts with water to produce sodium hydroxide. 20.0 g of sodium oxide is dissolved in 500 mL of water. Neglecting the change in volume, the concentration of the resulting NaOH solution is ____ × 10⁻¹ M. (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass: Na = 23.0, O = 16.0, H = 1.0]","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (forgets factor 2, or uses 1 L instead of 0.5 L: 0.645 M), 3 (both slips: 0.32 M).\nWorking: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH; 20/62 = 0.3226 mol Na₂O → 0.645 mol NaOH in 0.500 L → 1.29 M = 12.9 × 10⁻¹ ≈ 13.\nKey idea: One Na₂O gives two NaOH.","traps":["Mole ratio 1:1: Balance: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH."],"topics":["Concentration terms","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The formula of a gaseous hydrocarbon which requires 6 times of its own volume of O₂ for complete oxidation and produces 4 times its own volume of CO₂ is CₓHᵧ. The value of y is ____.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (y/2 confusion), 16 (using y/2 in O₂ term).\nWorking: CₓHᵧ + (x + y/4) O₂ → x CO₂ + y/2 H₂O; x = 4 and x + y/4 = 6 → y = 8.\nKey idea: O₂ needed = x + y/4.","traps":["O₂ term as x + y/2: Each H₂O needs ½ O₂ → y/4."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Empirical & molecular formula"]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"1.86 g of aniline completely reacts to form acetanilide. 10% of the product is lost during purification. Amount of acetanilide obtained after purification (in g) is ____ × 10⁻².","type":"num","answer":"243","range":[243.0,243.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 270 (loss not applied), 167 (using aniline mass × 0.9).\nWorking: 1.86/93 = 0.02 mol aniline → 0.02 mol acetanilide (M = 135) = 2.70 g; 90% kept = 2.43 g.\nKey idea: Moles of aniline = moles of acetanilide.","traps":["Loss not applied: Multiply by 0.9 at the end."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The NaNO₃ weighed out to make 50 mL of an aqueous solution containing 70.0 mg Na⁺ per mL is ______ g. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given: atomic weight in g mol⁻¹ – Na: 23; N: 14; O: 16]","type":"num","answer":"13","range":[13.0,13.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3.5 – mass of Na⁺ given as answer; 0 or 1 – 70 mg taken for whole 50 mL (0.26 g); 259 – g/L confusion multiplying by 1000/50 wrongly.\nWorking: Na⁺ = 70.0 mg/mL × 50 mL = 3.50 g = 0.1522 mol; NaNO₃ = 0.1522 × 85 = 12.93 g ≈ 13.\nKey idea: Mass of salt = mass of ion × (M salt / M ion) = 3.5 × 85/23.","traps":["Per-mL figure not scaled to 50 mL: Multiply concentration by volume first.","Na⁺ mass reported instead of NaNO₃: Convert via moles to the salt."],"topics":["Concentration terms","Mole concept"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"250 mL of 0.5 M NaOH was added to 500 mL of 1 M HCl. The number of unreacted HCl molecules in the solution after complete reaction is ________ × 10²¹. (Nearest integer) (N_A = 6.022 × 10²³)","type":"num","answer":"226","range":[226.0,226.0],"explain":"Common slips: 301 (using total HCl 0.5 mol); 75 (using NaOH moles); 2.26 or 22.6 (power-of-ten slip).\nWorking: n(NaOH) = 0.125 mol, n(HCl) = 0.500 mol; unreacted HCl = 0.375 mol × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.258 × 10²³ = 225.8 × 10²¹ ≈ 226.\nKey idea: Moles left = n(HCl) − n(NaOH) (1:1); multiply by N_A and divide by 10²¹.","traps":["Answer scaled to 10²¹: Write 2.258 × 10²³ = 225.8 × 10²¹.","Total instead of excess: Subtract the NaOH consumed."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Mole concept"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"4g equimolar mixture of NaOH and Na₂CO₃ contains x g of NaOH and y g of Na₂CO₃. The value of x is ______ g. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong values: 2 (equal masses), 3 (y = 2.9 g reported instead of x).\nWorking: n(40 + 106) = 4 → n = 0.0274 mol; x = 40 × 0.0274 = 1.10 g → 1.\nKey idea: Equimolar → masses in the ratio of molar masses 40 : 106.","traps":["Equimolar read as equal mass: Equal moles → mass ratio = molar mass ratio.","Reporting y: Answer x (NaOH)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Methylation of 10 g of benzene gave 9.2 g of toluene. Calculate the percentage yield of toluene ______. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"78","range":[78.0,78.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 92 (mass ratio 9.2/10 × 100), 85 (inverted 78/92).\nWorking: C₆H₆ (78) → C₆H₅CH₃ (92), 1:1. Theoretical = 10 × 92/78 = 11.79 g; yield = 9.2/11.79 × 100 = 78%.\nKey idea: % yield = actual/theoretical; theoretical = moles benzene × 92.","traps":["Mass ratio used directly: Convert to moles first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Consider the complete combustion of butane, the amount of butane utilized to produce 72.0 g of water is ________ × 10⁻¹ g. (in nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"464","range":[464.0,464.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 46 (forgetting ×10⁻¹ scaling), 580 or 232 (wrong mole ratio).\nWorking: C₄H₁₀ + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O; 72.0 g water = 4.00 mol → butane = 0.800 mol × 58 g mol⁻¹ = 46.4 g = 464 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: 1 mol butane gives 5 mol water.","traps":["Answer scale: Divide the mass by 10⁻¹ (multiply by 10)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g). The above reaction is carried out in a vessel starting with partial pressure p_SO₂ = 250 m bar, p_O₂ = 750 m bar and p_SO₃ = 0 bar. When the reaction is complete, the total pressure in the reaction vessel is _____ m bar. (Round off of the nearest integer).","type":"num","answer":"875","range":[875.0,875.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1000 (no change), 750 (taking O₂ limiting), 500.\nWorking: SO₂ is limiting; 250 SO₂ uses 125 O₂ and gives 250 SO₃; total = 0 + 625 + 250 = 875 mbar.\nKey idea: At constant V and T, p ∝ n.","traps":["SO₂ is limiting: Divide by coefficients: 250/2 < 750/1."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"When 35 mL of 0.15 M lead nitrate solution is mixed with 20 mL of 0.12 M chromic sulphate solution, ______ × 10⁻⁵ moles of lead sulphate precipitate out. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).","type":"num","answer":"525","range":[525.0,525.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 240 (moles of Cr₂(SO₄)₃ taken as sulphate and treated as limiting), 720 (sulphate taken as amount precipitated), 1050 (Pb²⁺ doubled).\nWorking: n(Pb²⁺) = 0.035 × 0.15 = 5.25 × 10⁻³ mol; n(SO₄²⁻) = 0.020 × 0.12 × 3 = 7.2 × 10⁻³ mol; PbSO₄ formed = 5.25 × 10⁻³ mol = 525 × 10⁻⁵.\nKey idea: Cr₂(SO₄)₃ supplies 3 SO₄²⁻; then compare with Pb²⁺.","traps":["Sulphate count per formula unit missed: Multiply by 3 for SO₄²⁻.","Wrong limiting reagent: Product follows the smaller ionic amount."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"C₆H₆ —HNO₃/H₂SO₄→ C₆H₅NO₂. In the above reaction, 3.9 g of benzene on nitration gives 4.92 g of nitrobenzene. The percentage yield of nitrobenzene in the above reaction is _______ %. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). (Given atomic mass: C: 12.0 u, H: 1.0 u, O: 16.0 u, N: 14.0 u)","type":"num","answer":"80","range":[80.0,80.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 126 (4.92/3.9 ×100 ignoring molar masses), 63, 100.\nWorking: 3.9/78 = 0.05 mol benzene → theoretical 0.05 × 123 = 6.15 g; yield = 4.92/6.15 × 100 = 80%.\nKey idea: Theoretical yield first, then actual/theoretical.","traps":["Mass ratio without moles: Convert to moles first."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The number of chlorine atoms in 20 mL of chlorine gas at STP is_____ × 10²¹. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n[Assume chlorine is an ideal gas at STP; R = 0.083 L bar mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, N_A = 6.023 × 10²³]","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1063 (mL not converted to L), 0.53 (molecules, not atoms – rounds the same here, so the trap matters only in working); using 22.4 L instead of PV/RT also rounds to 1.\nWorking: STP = 1 bar, 273.15 K; n(Cl₂) = PV/RT = (1 × 0.020)/(0.083 × 273.15) = 8.82 × 10⁻⁴ mol; atoms = 2 × 8.82 × 10⁻⁴ × 6.023 × 10²³ = 1.06 × 10²¹ → 1.\nKey idea: Cl₂ is diatomic: atoms = 2 × molecules.","traps":["Molecules counted as atoms: Multiply molecules by 2.","mL to L: Convert 20 mL = 0.020 L."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"C₆H₅COCl (benzoyl chloride, 0.140 g) + C₆H₅NHC₆H₅ (diphenylamine, 0.388 g) → C₆H₅–CO–N(C₆H₅)₂ (N,N-diphenylbenzamide, 0.210 g)\nConsider the above reaction. The percentage yield of amide product is _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).\n(Given : Atomic mass : C : 12.0 u, H : 1.0 u, N : 14.0 u, O : 16.0 u, Cl : 35.5 u)","type":"num","answer":"77","range":[77.0,77.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 33 (using diphenylamine as limiting), 82 (using M = 257 or similar wrong product mass), 54 (mass ratio 0.210/0.388).\nWorking: benzoyl chloride 0.140/140.5 = 9.96 × 10⁻⁴ mol; diphenylamine 0.388/169 = 2.30 × 10⁻³ mol (excess), so benzoyl chloride limits. Theoretical amide (M 273) = 9.96 × 10⁻⁴ × 273 = 0.272 g; yield = 0.210/0.272 × 100 = 77.2 → 77.\nKey idea: Compare moles, not masses: 0.140 g acid chloride (≈1.0 mmol) is limiting.","traps":["Limiting reagent by mass: Always convert to moles first.","Product molar mass: Count atoms: C 19, H 15, N 1, O 1 → 273."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"_____ grams of 3-Hydroxy propanal (MW = 74) must be dehydrated to produce 7.8 g of acrolein (MW = 56) (C₃H₄O) if the percentage yield is 64. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Atomic masses: C: 12.0 u, H: 1.0 u, O: 16.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"16","range":[16.0,16.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 10 (ignoring yield), 7 (multiplying by 0.64), 6.6 (mass-based without moles).\nWorking: HOCH₂CH₂CHO → CH₂=CHCHO + H₂O (1:1). Acrolein 7.8/56 = 0.1393 mol; at 64% yield need 0.1393/0.64 = 0.2176 mol × 74 = 16.1 g → 16.\nKey idea: Required = theoretical ÷ fractional yield.","traps":["Yield applied the wrong way: Lower yield means MORE starting material."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A reaction of 0.1 mole of Benzylamine with bromomethane gave 23 g of Benzyl trimethyl ammonium bromide. The number of moles of bromomethane consumed in this reaction are n × 10⁻¹, when n = _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). (Given: Atomic masses: C: 12.0 u, H: 1.0 u, N: 14.0 u, Br: 80.0 u)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1 (one CH₃Br per salt), 2 (only two methylations), 4.\nWorking: C₆H₅CH₂N(CH₃)₃⁺Br⁻ (C₁₀H₁₆NBr) M = 230; 23 g = 0.1 mol, formed by adding three CH₃ groups: C₆H₅CH₂NH₂ + 3CH₃Br → C₆H₅CH₂N(CH₃)₃⁺Br⁻ + 2HBr; CH₃Br = 0.3 mol → n = 3.\nKey idea: Primary amine → quaternary salt needs three alkyl groups, i.e. 3 mol CH₃Br per mol salt.","traps":["One alkyl halide per salt: Count CH₃ groups on N, not bromides."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Complete combustion of 3 g of ethane gives x × 10²² molecules of water. The value of x is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Use: N_A = 6.023 × 10²³; Atomic masses in u: C: 12.0; O: 16.0; H: 1.0]","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 6 (1:1 ratio), 12 (2 H₂O as for methane), 36 (counting H atoms).\nWorking: C₂H₆ (30 g mol⁻¹) 3 g = 0.1 mol; C₂H₆ + 7/2 O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O gives 0.3 mol H₂O = 0.3 × 6.023 × 10²³ = 18.07 × 10²² → 18.\nKey idea: C₂H₆ has 6 H → 3 H₂O per molecule.","traps":["Methane ratio carried over: Count H: 6 H → 3 H₂O."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Mole concept"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"Benzoic acid + Br₂ —(FeBr₃)→ m-bromobenzoic acid + HBr\nConsider the above reaction where 6.1 g of benzoic acid is used to get 7.8 g of m-bromo benzoic acid. The percentage yield of the product is _____. (Round off to the Nearest integer) [Given : Atomic masses : C = 12.0 u, H : 1.0 u, O : 16.0 u, Br = 80.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"78","range":[78.0,78.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 77 (truncating 77.6), 128 (inverting the ratio), 61 (taking theoretical mass from the wrong molar mass); note that the mass ratio 6.1/7.8 ≈ 78 % coincides with the right answer only by chance.\nWorking: benzoic acid 122 g mol⁻¹ → 0.05 mol; m-bromobenzoic acid C₇H₅BrO₂ = 201 g mol⁻¹ → theoretical 10.05 g; yield = 7.8/10.05 × 100 = 77.6 ≈ 78 %.\nKey idea: Convert to moles: 0.05 mol → 10.05 g theoretical.","traps":["Mass ratio shortcut: Always compare moles, not masses.","Molar mass of product: C₇H₅BrO₂ = 84 + 5 + 80 + 32 = 201."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of atoms in 8 g of sodium is x × 10²³. The value of x is ______ (Nearest integer). [Given: N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹; Atomic mass of Na = 23.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (rounding error); 48 (multiplying mass by N_A without dividing by 23).\nWorking: (8/23) × 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.09 × 10²³; x ≈ 2.\nKey idea: N = (8/23) × 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.09 × 10²³.","traps":["Rounding 2.09 upward: 2.09 rounds to 2."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's atomic theory","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Amongst the following statements, that which was not proposed by Dalton was :","options":["all the atoms of a given element have identical properties including identical mass. Atoms of different elements differ in mass.","chemical reactions involve reorganisation of atoms. These are neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.","when gases combine or reproduced in a chemical reaction they do so in a simple ratio by volume provided all gases are at the same T & P.","matter consists of indivisible atoms."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are NCERT postulates nearly word for word; a student unsure of (D)'s 'indivisible' may pick it.\nWorking: (C) is Gay-Lussac's law of gaseous volumes, which NCERT says Dalton's theory could not explain; (A), (B), (D) are Dalton's postulates.\nKey idea: Anything about gas volumes is Gay-Lussac (and Avogadro), not Dalton.","traps":["Gay-Lussac's law credited to Dalton: Remember NCERT: Dalton's theory could not explain the laws of gaseous volumes."],"topics":["Dalton's atomic theory","Laws of chemical combination"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The ammonia (NH₃) released on quantitative reaction of 0.6 g urea (NH₂CONH₂) with sodium hydroxide (NaOH) can be neutralized by :","options":["100 ml of 0.1 N HCl","200 ml of 0.4 N HCl","100 ml of 0.2 N HCl","200 ml of 0.2 N HCl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) takes 1 NH₃ per urea (10 meq); (D) doubles again (40 meq); (B) is 80 meq, a random multiple.\nWorking: 0.6 g urea = 0.01 mol → 0.02 mol NH₃ → needs 20 meq HCl = 100 mL × 0.2 N.\nKey idea: Each urea gives 2 NH₃.","traps":["One NH₃ per urea: Count N atoms in NH₂CONH₂."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The volume (in mL) of 0.125 M AgNO₃ required to quantitatively precipitate chloride ions in 0.3 g of [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ is ______. M[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ = 267.46 g/mol; M AgNO₃ = 169.87 g/mol","type":"num","answer":"26.8 to 27","range":[26.8,27.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8.97 mL (1 Cl⁻), 17.94 mL (2 Cl⁻), mass-based use of 169.87.\nWorking: n(complex) = 0.3/267.46 = 1.1217×10⁻³ mol; all 3 Cl⁻ are ionisable → 3.365×10⁻³ mol Ag⁺; V = 3.365×10⁻³/0.125 L = 26.92 mL. 26.92 lies within the accepted range 26.8 to 27.\nKey idea: All three Cl⁻ are outside the coordination sphere.","traps":["Wrong number of ionisable Cl⁻: Cl outside [ ] are ionisable.","Using M(AgNO₃): Volume follows from molarity alone."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"NaClO₃ is used, even in spacecrafts, to produce O₂. The daily consumption of pure O₂ by a person is 492 L at 1 atm, 300 K. How much amount of NaClO₃, in grams, is required to produce O₂ for the daily consumption of a person at 1 atm, 300 K? NaClO₃(s) + Fe(s) → O₂(g) + NaCl(s) + FeO(s); R = 0.082 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"2120 to 2140","range":[2120.0,2140.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 1420 (2:3 ratio from KClO₃ decomposition 2KClO₃ → 3O₂); 2350 (using 22.4 L at STP: 492/22.4 = 21.96 mol); 4260 (doubling).\nWorking: n(O₂) = PV/RT = 492/(0.082 × 300) = 20 mol; the equation is balanced 1:1 (O: 3 = 2 + 1), so 20 mol NaClO₃ × 106.5 g mol⁻¹ = 2130 g.\nKey idea: Fe takes one O, so each NaClO₃ gives exactly one O₂.","traps":["KClO₃ 2:3 ratio carried over: Balance the given equation; here 1 NaClO₃ → 1 O₂.","STP molar volume used: Use PV = nRT at the stated T."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The mass percentage of nitrogen in histamine is ______.","type":"num","answer":"37.8 to 38.2","range":[37.8,38.2],"explain":"Common slips: Wrong formula (e.g. counting 2 N) gives ≈ 25 %; using C₅H₁₁N₃ gives ≈ 37.1 % (outside range).\nWorking: histamine = 2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanamine, C₅H₉N₃ (M ≈ 111.15); %N = 42.02/111.15 × 100 = 37.8 %, within the accepted range 37.8 to 38.2.\nKey idea: Histamine = imidazole ring (2 N) + ethylamine side chain (1 N), C₅H₉N₃.","traps":["Not knowing histamine structure: Imidazole has 2 N plus the side-chain NH₂."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"5 g of zinc is treated separately with an excess of (a) dilute hydrochloric acid and (b) aqueous sodium hydroxide. The ratio of the volumes of H₂ evolved in these two reactions is:","options":["1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) arise if NaOH equation is balanced with different H₂ yield; (A) random.\nWorking: Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ and Zn + 2NaOH → Na₂ZnO₂ + H₂; one H₂ per Zn in both.\nKey idea: Zn → Zn²⁺ in both cases, two electrons → one H₂.","traps":["Zn assumed not to react with NaOH: Zn, Al, Sn, Pb are amphoteric and release H₂ with NaOH."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Empirical & molecular formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The ratio of the mass percentages of 'C & H' and 'C & O' of a saturated acyclic organic compound 'X' are 4 : 1 and 3 : 4 respectively. Then, the moles of oxygen gas required for complete combustion of two moles of organic compound 'X' is _______ .","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2.5 (per mole only), 3.5 (using CH₃O as molecular formula and doubling wrongly), 7 (wrong formula C₂H₆O).\nWorking: C : H : O by mass = 12 : 3 : 16 → mole ratio 1 : 3 : 1, empirical formula CH₃O. A saturated acyclic compound needs H ≤ 2C+2 → C₂H₆O₂ (ethane-1,2-diol). C₂H₆O₂ + 5/2 O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O, so 2 mol need 5 mol O₂.\nKey idea: CH₃O cannot exist alone; n = 2 is the only saturated acyclic possibility.","traps":["Per-mole answer given: Multiply by moles of X.","Empirical taken as molecular formula: Use valency/saturation to fix n."],"topics":["Empirical & molecular formula","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The mass of ammonia in grams produced when 2.8 kg of dinitrogen quantitatively reacts with 1 kg of dihydrogen is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3400","range":[3400.0,3400.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using H₂ as limiting gives 5667 g; forgetting kg → g gives 3.4; using 1 NH₃ per N₂ gives 1700.\nWorking: N₂ = 2800/28 = 100 mol; H₂ = 1000/2 = 500 mol (needs 300); N₂ limiting; NH₃ = 200 mol × 17 = 3400 g.\nKey idea: Moles ratio vs 1 : 3 decides limiting reagent.","traps":["H₂ taken as limiting: Compare moles with the 1:3 ratio.","Answer in kg: Answer asked in grams."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The minimum number of moles of O₂ required for complete combustion of 1 mole of propane and 2 moles of butane is ______.","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 11.5 (only 1 mol butane), 13 (butane only), 31 (using 13 per mol of butane from the doubled equation ×2).\nWorking: C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O (5 mol); C₄H₁₀ + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O, so 2 mol need 13; total 18.\nKey idea: O₂ per mole CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ = (3n + 1)/2.","traps":["Doubled butane equation counted twice: Check mole basis of the balanced equation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Atomic & molecular mass","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The average molar mass of chlorine is 35.5 g mol⁻¹. The ratio of ³⁵Cl to ³⁷Cl in naturally occurring chlorine is close to:","options":["4 : 1","1 : 1","2 : 1","3 : 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1:1 would give 36; (C) 2:1 gives 35.67; (A) 4:1 gives 35.4.\nWorking: 35x + 37(1−x) = 35.5 → x = 0.75 → 3 : 1.\nKey idea: 35.5 is one quarter of the way from 35 to 37, so ³⁵Cl is three quarters.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Average lies closer to the more abundant isotope."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"The percentage composition of carbon by mole in methane is:","options":["80%","25%","75%","20%"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 75% is the mass percent (12/16); (B) 25% = 1/4 counts only H atoms as denominator; (A) 80% is the hydrogen atom fraction.\nWorking: CH₄ has 1 C out of 5 atoms ⇒ 20% by moles of atoms.\nKey idea: Mole % uses atom counts: 1/(1+4).","traps":["Mass percent used: Read the basis: 'by mole'.","Wrong denominator: Denominator is total atoms (5)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"For a reaction, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) ; identify dihydrogen (H₂) as a limiting reagent in the following reaction mixtures.","options":["14 g of N₂ + 4 g of H₂","28 g of N₂ + 6 g of H₂","56 g of N₂ + 10 g of H₂","35 g of N₂ + 8 g of H₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) have H₂ in excess; (B) exactly stoichiometric (no limiting reagent) – tempting if 'smaller mass' is taken as limiting.\nWorking: required H₂ = 3 × mol N₂ × 2 g. (A) 0.5 mol N₂ needs 3 g, 4 g present; (B) 1 mol needs 6 g, exactly stoichiometric; (C) 2 mol needs 12 g, only 10 g → H₂ limiting; (D) 1.25 mol needs 7.5 g, 8 g present.\nKey idea: Compare n(H₂)/n(N₂) with 3: less than 3 ⇒ H₂ limiting.","traps":["Smaller mass taken as limiting: Convert to moles and use the 1:3 ratio.","Stoichiometric mixture chosen: Limiting means one is short, not both exhausted."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Mole concept","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"10 mL of 1 mM surfactant solution forms a monolayer covering 0.24 cm² on a polar substrate. If the polar head is approximated as cube, what is its edge length?","options":["2.0 pm","2.0 nm","1.0 pm","0.1 nm"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 2.0 nm from a cm→m slip of 10³; (C) 1.0 pm from halving instead of square root; (D) 0.1 nm picks a \"realistic\" atomic size.\nWorking: moles = 10⁻² L × 10⁻³ M = 10⁻⁵ mol → 6.022 × 10¹⁸ molecules; area per head = 0.24/(6.022 × 10¹⁸) ≈ 4 × 10⁻²⁰ cm², edge = 2 × 10⁻¹⁰ cm = 2 × 10⁻¹² m = 2.0 pm (physically unrealistic size, but it is the arithmetic the data give).\nKey idea: Edge = √(area per molecule); 1 cm = 10¹⁰ pm.","traps":["Square-root step mishandled: Edge = √(4 × 10⁻²⁰ cm²).","Unit conversion cm → pm/nm: Write 2 × 10⁻¹⁰ cm = 2 × 10⁻¹² m."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"At 300 K and 1 atmospheric pressure, 10 mL of a hydrocarbon required 55 mL of O₂ for complete combustion and 40 mL of CO₂ is formed. The formula of the hydrocarbon is :","options":["C₄H₈","C₄H₇Cl","C₄H₁₀","C₄H₆"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) C₄H₈ needs 60 mL O₂; (C) C₄H₁₀ needs 65 mL; (B) C₄H₇Cl also consumes 55 mL O₂ (Cl ends as HCl) but is not a hydrocarbon.\nWorking: CₓHᵧ + (x + y/4)O₂ → xCO₂ + y/2 H₂O; 10x = 40 ⇒ x = 4; 10(4 + y/4) = 55 ⇒ y = 6 ⇒ C₄H₆. (C₄H₇Cl → 4CO₂ + HCl + 3H₂O would also need 5.5 vol O₂, but it is not a hydrocarbon.)\nKey idea: CO₂ volume fixes x; O₂ volume then fixes y.","traps":["Chlorinated option fits the O₂ data: Re-read \"hydrocarbon\" – only C and H allowed.","Water volume counted as gas: Only O₂ and CO₂ data are needed here."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Empirical & molecular formula"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The minimum amount of O₂(g) consumed per gram of reactant is for the reaction: (Given atomic mass: Fe = 56, O = 16, Mg = 24, P = 31, C = 12, H = 1)","options":["C₃H₈(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 3 CO₂(g) + 4 H₂O(l)","P₄(s) + 5 O₂(g) → P₄O₁₀(s)","4 Fe(s) + 3 O₂(g) → 2 Fe₂O₃(s)","2 Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2 MgO(s)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Mg looks smallest because only 1 O₂ is used; (B) P₄ has large molar mass; students comparing moles of O₂ rather than per gram pick (D).\nWorking: mol O₂ per g of reactant: C₃H₈ 5/44 = 0.114; P₄ 5/124 = 0.040; Fe 3/224 = 0.0134; Mg 1/48 = 0.021. Minimum for Fe.\nKey idea: Divide moles of O₂ by total mass of the oxidised reactant.","traps":["Comparing O₂ coefficients only: Normalise to per gram: Mg 1/48 vs Fe 3/224."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Molar mass","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"5 moles of AB₂ weigh 125 × 10⁻³ kg and 10 moles of A₂B₂ weigh 300 × 10⁻³ kg. The molar mass of A (M_A) and molar mass of B (M_B) in kg mol⁻¹ are :","options":["M_A = 50 × 10⁻³ and M_B = 25 × 10⁻³","M_A = 25 × 10⁻³ and M_B = 50 × 10⁻³","M_A = 5 × 10⁻³ and M_B = 10 × 10⁻³","M_A = 10 × 10⁻³ and M_B = 5 × 10⁻³"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) swap/scale the true values (M_A and M_B interchanged or ×10); (B) is the swap of (A) – arises if equations are set up as 2M_A + M_B.\nWorking: M(AB₂) = 0.125/5 = 0.025 kg mol⁻¹ ⇒ M_A + 2M_B = 25 g; M(A₂B₂) = 0.300/10 = 0.030 ⇒ M_A + M_B = 15 g; so M_B = 10 g, M_A = 5 g.\nKey idea: Divide each mass by moles first: 25 g and 30 g per mole; subtract to get M_B.","traps":["Atom ratio in AB₂ misapplied: Write M_A + 2M_B explicitly for AB₂.","Factor of ten in kg vs g: Convert to g mol⁻¹ first, then back."],"topics":["Molar mass","Atomic & molecular mass"]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Percentage composition","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"25 g of an unknown hydrocarbon upon burning produces 88 g of CO₂ and 9 g of H₂O. This unknown hydrocarbon contains.","options":["20 g of carbon and 5 g of hydrogen","24 g of carbon and 1 g of hydrogen","18 g of carbon and 7 g of hydrogen","22 g of carbon and 3 g of hydrogen"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C)/(D) distribute 25 g arbitrarily; (D) 22 g C confuses with 44/2.\nWorking: C = 88 × 12/44 = 24 g; H = 9 × 2/18 = 1 g; sum 25 g.\nKey idea: C from CO₂ × 12/44, H from H₂O × 2/18.","traps":["Using 1/18 instead of 2/18 for H: H₂O carries 2 g H per 18 g."],"topics":["Percentage composition","Stoichiometry"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"For the following reaction, the mass of water produced from 445 g of C₅₇H₁₁₀O₆ is: 2C₅₇H₁₁₀O₆(s) + 163O₂(g) → 114CO₂(g) + 110H₂O(l)","options":["495 g","490 g","890 g","445 g"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Rounding 18 or molar mass. (C) 890 g – the molar mass of the fat, not the mass of water. (D) 445 g – mass conservation assumption.\nWorking: M = 890 g mol⁻¹ → 0.5 mol; H₂O = 0.5 × 110/2 = 27.5 mol × 18 = 495 g.\nKey idea: 445/890 = 0.5 mol fat; each mol gives 55 mol H₂O, so 27.5 mol = 495 g.","traps":["Coefficient 2 of fat ignored: Use 110/2 = 55 mol water per mol fat."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Limiting reagent","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"A mixture of 100 m mol of Ca(OH)₂ and 2 g of sodium sulphate was dissolved in water and the volume was made up to 100 mL. The mass of calcium sulphate formed and the concentration of OH⁻ in resulting solution, respectively, are : (Molar mass of Ca(OH)₂, Na₂SO₄ and CaSO₄ are 74, 143 and 136 g mol⁻¹, respectively; Ksp of Ca(OH)₂ is 5.5 × 10⁻⁶)","options":["1.9 g, 0.14 mol L⁻¹","13.6 g, 0.14 mol L⁻¹","1.9 g, 0.28 mol L⁻¹","13.6 g, 0.28 mol L⁻¹"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): count OH⁻ per Na₂SO₄ instead of 2 NaOH. (B)/(D) 13.6 g: assume all 100 mmol Ca(OH)₂ forms CaSO₄ (wrong limiting reagent).\nWorking: Na₂SO₄ = 2/143 ≈ 0.014 mol (limiting) → 0.014 mol CaSO₄ = 1.9 g and 0.028 mol NaOH in 0.1 L → [OH⁻] = 0.28 M; leftover Ca(OH)₂ adds negligibly (Ksp 5.5 × 10⁻⁶ under the common ion).\nKey idea: Na₂SO₄ (14 mmol) is limiting; 2 OH⁻ per SO₄²⁻ precipitated → 28 mmol in 100 mL.","traps":["Wrong limiting reagent: Convert 2 g Na₂SO₄ to mmol (14) and compare.","Ksp used for [OH⁻]: NaOH formed dominates OH⁻; common ion suppresses Ca(OH)₂."],"topics":["Limiting reagent","Concentration terms"]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S2C04","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"The amount of sugar (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) required to prepare 2 L of its 0.1 M aqueous solution is :","options":["68.4 g","17.1 g","34.2 g","136.8 g"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 17.1 g: divides by 2 twice (0.05 mol). (C) 34.2 g: forgets the 2 L. (D) 136.8 g: multiplies by 4 (0.4 mol).\nWorking: moles = 0.1 × 2 = 0.2 mol; M(C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁) = 342 g mol⁻¹; mass = 68.4 g.\nKey idea: Mass = M × V × molar mass = 0.1 × 2 × 342.","traps":["Volume factor ignored: Multiply molarity by the given volume."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stoichiometry","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"A 10 mg effervescent tablet containing sodium bicarbonate and oxalic acid releases 0.25 mL of CO₂ at T = 298.15 K and p = 1 bar. If molar volume of CO₂ is 25.0 L under such condition, what is the percentage of sodium bicarbonate in each tablet? [Molar mass of NaHCO₃ = 84 g mol⁻¹]","options":["16.8","8.4","0.84","33.6"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 0.84 – mass in mg mistaken for %. (A) 16.8 – uses 2 NaHCO₃ per CO₂. (D) 33.6 – further factor error.\nWorking: n(CO₂) = 0.25/25000 = 1 × 10⁻⁵ mol = n(NaHCO₃) (1:1); mass = 8.4 × 10⁻⁴ g = 0.84 mg; 0.84/10 × 100 = 8.4%.\nKey idea: Each NaHCO₃ releases one CO₂ regardless of which acid is used.","traps":["Ratio from oxalic acid equation: Equation gives 2 CO₂ per 2 NaHCO₃ – still 1:1.","Mass vs percentage: Divide by tablet mass 10 mg."],"topics":["Stoichiometry","Percentage composition"]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Concentration terms","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"25 ml of the given HCl solution requires 30 mL of 0.1 M sodium carbonate solution. What is the volume of this HCl solution required to titrate 30 mL of 0.2 M aqueous NaOH solution?","options":["25 mL","50 mL","12.5 mL","75 mL"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 12.5 mL: Na₂CO₃ taken 1:1 with HCl. (B) 50 mL: factor of 2 applied the wrong way. (D) 75 mL: ratio error.\nWorking: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂; 3 mmol Na₂CO₃ needs 6 mmol HCl in 25 mL (0.24 M). 30 mL × 0.2 M NaOH = 6 mmol → 25 mL HCl.\nKey idea: Both bases give 6 mmol of 'H⁺ demand'; same HCl volume.","traps":["Carbonate n-factor 1: Na₂CO₃ takes 2 H⁺ to CO₂."],"topics":["Concentration terms","Stoichiometry"]}],"m14-iso":[{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":64,"q":"Identify the correct statements from the following (structures shown below).\nA. A(i) and A(ii) are metamers\nB. B(i) and B(ii) are functional isomers\nC. C(i) and C(ii) are position isomers\nD. D(i) and D(ii) are homologous\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A,B & C Only","B & C Only","C & D Only","A & B Only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"A correct (NCERT metamer example). B correct (CH₃CH₂CH₂CN vs CH₃CH₂CH₂NC, functional isomers). C wrong: C₄ vs C₅ alcohols, homologues not position isomers. D wrong: C₄H₁₁N vs C₅H₁₃N and different amine class, not homologues. (A) adds C; (B) drops A, adds C; (C) C & D both wrong; (D) correct.\nWorking: A – pentan-3-one and pentan-2-one (both C₅H₁₀O) have different alkyl groups on either side of C=O: metamers (true). B – butanenitrile and propyl isocyanide (both C₄H₇N): functional isomers (true). C – butan-1-ol (C₄H₁₀O) and pentan-2-ol (C₅H₁₂O) have different formulae, so they are not isomers (false). D – butan-1-amine (1°) and N-ethylpropan-1-amine (2°) are different amine classes, not homologues (false). So A & B only.\nKey idea: Check molecular formula and functional class before applying any isomer label.","traps":["Different molecular formulae labelled as isomers: Count carbons first; isomers must have the same molecular formula.","Homologues must belong to same functional class: Homologous series members share the same functional group and general formula; a 1° and a 2° amine are different classes."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S1C14-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":63,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I): I(a) and I(b) (shown below) are isomeric compounds.\nStatement (II): II(a) and II(b) (shown below) are functional group isomers.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I true (both C₆H₁₂). Statement II true (1° vs 2° amine, same formula C₅H₁₃N). (B), (C), (D) each reject at least one by treating ring/chain or amine class as non-isomeric. (A) correct.\nWorking: methylcyclopentane and hex-3-ene are both C₆H₁₂ (one ring vs one C=C), so they are isomers – I is true. Pentan-1-amine (1°) and N-ethylpropan-1-amine (2°) are both C₅H₁₃N with different amine classes, so they are functional group isomers – II is true.\nKey idea: Same molecular formula plus different functional class (ring vs C=C counts by unsaturation; –NH₂ vs >NH) makes them isomers.","traps":["Cycloalkane and alkene recognised as isomers: One ring and one double bond each give one degree of unsaturation: both C₆H₁₂.","Primary vs secondary amine counted as functional isomers: 1°, 2° and 3° amines are treated as different functional groups (–NH₂ vs >NH), so C₅H₁₃N 1° and 2° amines are functional isomers."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C13-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Reaction scheme: A [1-bromo-4-(2-bromoethyl)benzene, Br–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂Br (para)] —NaOH(alc)→ B —HBr / ether→ C\nIdentify B and C and how are A and C related?","options":["B = 4-bromostyrene (Br–C₆H₄–CH=CH₂); C = 1-bromo-4-(1-bromoethyl)benzene (Br–C₆H₄–CHBr–CH₃); position isomers","B = 4-(2-bromoethyl)phenol (HO–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂Br, para); C = 4-(1-bromoethyl)phenol (HO–C₆H₄–CHBr–CH₃, para); Derivative","B = 4-(2-bromoethyl)phenol (HO–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂Br, para); C = 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)phenol (HO–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂OH, para); functional group isomers","B = 4-bromostyrene (Br–C₆H₄–CH=CH₂); C = 1-bromo-4-(2-bromoethyl)benzene (Br–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂Br, para; same as A); chain isomers"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): assume aryl Br is replaced by OH (aryl halides do not react with NaOH under these conditions). (D): anti-Markovnikov product (needs peroxide), which would regenerate A itself, and wrong isomer label.\nWorking: alc. NaOH dehydrohalogenates the side-chain CH₂CH₂Br (aryl Br untouched) to 4-bromostyrene; HBr without peroxide adds Markovnikov (benzylic cation) giving ArCHBrCH₃. A (ArCH₂CH₂Br) and C (ArCHBrCH₃) differ only in Br position → position isomers.\nKey idea: Aryl C–Br survives; HBr in ether is Markovnikov → benzylic bromide, which is a positional isomer of A.","traps":["Aryl bromide substituted by OH: Aryl halides need drastic conditions (623 K, high pressure) for OH substitution.","Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition: No peroxide → Markovnikov; benzylic cation is favoured."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Counting structural isomers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of different chain isomers for C₇H₁₆ is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8: misses 3-ethylpentane; 10–11: counts 3-methylhexane enantiomers or duplicates.\nWorking: heptane, 2- and 3-methylhexane, 2,2-, 2,3-, 2,4-, 3,3-dimethylpentane, 3-ethylpentane, 2,2,3-trimethylbutane = 9.\nKey idea: Count by longest chain: 7 (1), 6 (2), 5 (5), 4 (1).","traps":["3-Ethylpentane missed: Enumerate by main-chain length.","Stereoisomers counted: Chain isomers are structural only."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Pair of compounds): A. n-propanol and isopropanol B. Methoxypropane and ethoxyethane C. Propanone and propanal D. Neopentane and isopentane\nList-II (Isomerism): I. Metamerism II. Chain isomerism III. Position isomerism IV. Functional isomerism\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps chain and position (A↔D). (B) swaps functional and chain. (D) calls n/iso-propanol metamers.\nWorking: propan-1-ol/propan-2-ol position; CH₃OC₃H₇/C₂H₅OC₂H₅ metamers; ketone/aldehyde functional; neopentane/isopentane chain.\nKey idea: Same chain, different OH position = position; different alkyls around O = metamerism.","traps":["Position vs chain isomerism: The carbon skeleton of propanol is unchanged."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which of the following is metamer of the given compound (X) ?\n(X): C₆H₅–NH–C(=O)–C₆H₁₁ [phenyl on N, cyclohexyl on C=O; N-phenylcyclohexanecarboxamide]","options":["OHC–C₆H₄–NH–C₆H₁₁ [4-(cyclohexylamino)benzaldehyde]","C₆H₅–NH–C(=O)–C₆H₅ [benzanilide]","C₆H₁₁–NH–C(=O)–C₆H₅ [N-cyclohexylbenzamide]","C₆H₁₁–NH–C(=O)–C₆H₁₁ [N-cyclohexylcyclohexanecarboxamide]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) same formula but aldehyde + amine – functional isomer. (B)/(D) look like swaps but change H count (not isomers).\nWorking: (C) has the same formula C₁₃H₁₇NO and same amide group with the alkyl/aryl groups swapped across –NH–CO– → metamer. (A) is a functional isomer; (B) and (D) have different formulae.\nKey idea: First check formula, then the functional group, then the groups on each side.","traps":["Different formula mistaken for isomer: Count H in each ring.","Functional isomer taken as metamer: Metamers keep the same functional group."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of optical isomers in following compound is : ________.\n[Linear tricyclic saturated hydrocarbon skeleton (perhydroanthracene, three fused cyclohexane rings) with Br on one ring-fusion carbon of the left junction and CH₃ on the right ring carbon adjacent to the right junction]","type":"num","answer":"32","range":[32.0,32.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (only Br and CH₃ carbons), 8 or 16 (missing one or two fusion carbons), 16 (assuming symmetry).\nWorking: stereocentres – Br-bearing fusion C, the other fusion C of the left junction, both fusion C of the right junction, and the CH₃-bearing C = 5; molecule has no symmetry → 2⁵ = 32.\nKey idea: Every ring-fusion CH/CBr with different paths is a stereocentre.","traps":["Fusion carbons missed: Check each ring junction for four different paths.","Assuming meso reduction: Br and CH₃ make the molecule unsymmetrical."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Optical activity & symmetry","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of optically active compounds from the following is ______.\n(i) Fischer projection: CH₃ top and bottom, two C with H on left and OH on right (meso-butane-2,3-diol). (ii) Skeletal: heptane-3,4,5-triol, CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH(OH)CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (no stereochemistry shown). (iii) CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–OH. (iv) CH₃–CH₂–CH(Cl)–CH₃. (v) CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–Cl. (vi) (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–CH₂–Cl.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 if the triol is counted chiral; 2 if the Fischer diol is taken as chiral; 0 if 2-chlorobutane missed.\nWorking: only 2-chlorobutane is clearly optically active; meso-2,3-butanediol is inactive. Heptane-3,4,5-triol drawn without stereochemistry has chiral (3R,5R)/(3S,5S) forms as well as meso forms, so counting it would give 2; the official answer 1 assumes it is taken as the symmetric (meso) form.\nKey idea: Look for a mirror plane in symmetric polyols before counting stereocentres.","traps":["Meso Fischer projection: Same-side OH groups → mirror plane.","Symmetric triol without stereo shown: The official answer treats it as the meso (achiral) form."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Total number of stereo isomers possible for the given structure : [skeletal structure: CH₃–C(Br)=CH–CH₂–CH(Br)–C(CH₃)=C(Br)–CH₃, IUPAC: 2,4,7-tribromo-3-methylocta-2,6-diene]","options":["4","2","8","3"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses one stereo element (often the trisubstituted C=C). (B) counts only the chiral centre. (D) adds the elements instead of using 2ⁿ.\nWorking: two stereogenic C=C bonds (each carbon has two different groups) and one chiral centre (C-4, the CHBr carbon), no symmetry → 2³ = 8.\nKey idea: Count stereo units (n) and use 2ⁿ when the molecule has no symmetry.","traps":["Missing a stereogenic double bond: Check both ends of each C=C for two different groups.","Reading skeletal formula: Expand line ends into CH₃."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Number of optical isomers possible for 2-chlorobutane ________.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong: 3 (counts racemic mixture), 1.\nWorking: One chiral carbon (C-2), no symmetry → 2 enantiomers.\nKey idea: 2ⁿ with n = 1.","traps":["Racemic mixture counted as an isomer: Racemate is a mixture, not an isomer."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Total number of isomeric compounds (including stereoisomers) formed by monochlorination of 2-methylbutane is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (structural only, the NCERT answer), 5 (only one chiral product counted), 8 (treating every product as chiral).\nWorking: 1-chloro-2-methylbutane (chiral, 2), 2-chloro-2-methylbutane (1), 2-chloro-3-methylbutane (chiral, 2), 1-chloro-3-methylbutane (1) → 6\nKey idea: Two of the four products (C1 and C3 substitution) have a stereocentre, adding 2 more.","traps":["Stopping at the NCERT count of 4: Check each product for a stereocentre.","Missing chirality of 1-chloro-2-methylbutane: Inspect the branched carbon after C1 chlorination."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tautomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Highest enol content will be shown by:","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 1,3-dione: high enol content (conjugation + H-bond) but no aromatic gain, chosen by students who stop at \"active methylene\". (C) 1,4-dione: no active methylene, low enol. (D) 1,2,3-trione: adjacent C=O; looks most carbonyl-rich but enolisation does not give an aromatic ring.\nWorking: full enolisation of the 1,3,5-trione gives benzene-1,3,5-triol (phloroglucinol), an aromatic ring, so the enol form dominates almost completely.\nKey idea: An enol that is aromatic (phenol form) wins over every other stabilising factor.","traps":["Stopping at 1,3-dione active methylene: Check whether complete enolisation makes a 4n+2 aromatic ring.","More C=O groups means more enol: Count active CH₂ between two C=O and check the enol product, not the number of C=O."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Jan27S1C02-o.webp"],"lettered":true},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Total number of compounds with Chiral carbon atoms from following is ________. (i) 2-methylcyclohexan-1-one (cyclohexanone with CH₃ on the carbon next to C=O); (ii) cyclopentane-1,3-dione; (iii) CH₃–CH₂–CH(NO₂)–COOH; (iv) CH₃–CH₂–CHBr–CH₂–CH₃; (v) CH₃–CH(I)–CH₂–NO₂; (vi) CH₃–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₂OH; (vii) CH₃–CH(I)–CH(I)–C₂H₅.","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (missing the ring stereocentre in 2-methylcyclohexanone), 6 (counting 3-bromopentane), 6 (counting chiral carbons in vii as 2 instead of compounds).\nWorking: chiral C present in (i) C2 of 2-methylcyclohexanone, (iii), (v), (vi) and (vii) (both CHI carbons); (ii) has none and (iv) 3-bromopentane is symmetric. Count = 5.\nKey idea: In rings, compare the two ring paths; in 3-bromopentane both sides are ethyl.","traps":["Ring carbon stereocentre missed: Trace both ring directions: C=O on one side, CH₂ on the other.","Symmetric halide counted: Two identical ethyl groups mean not chiral."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which one of the following will show geometrical isomerism?\n[structures: (A) cyclohexane ring with exocyclic =CHBr at C1 and Br at C3 (3-bromo-1-(bromomethylidene)cyclohexane); (B) exocyclic =CH₂ at C1, Br at C3; (C) exocyclic =CH₂ at C1, Br at C4; (D) exocyclic =CHBr at C1, Br at C4]","options":["3-bromo-1-(bromomethylidene)cyclohexane","3-bromo-1-methylidenecyclohexane","4-bromo-1-methylidenecyclohexane","4-bromo-1-(bromomethylidene)cyclohexane"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C): =CH₂ terminal, no isomers. (D): looks like (A) but C4-Br makes the two ring arms identical — no E/Z (students ignore this symmetry).\nWorking: geometrical isomers need two different groups on each sp² carbon. Exocyclic CH₂ (B, C) fails; in (D) the two ring arms from C1 are identical (Br at C4 is symmetric); in (A) the ring arms differ (C2 leads to C3–Br, C6 does not) and the other carbon has H and Br, so E/Z exist.\nKey idea: For the ring carbon, compare the two ring paths: they differ only if the substituent breaks the symmetry (C3, not C4).","traps":["Symmetric ring arms treated as different: Trace both ring paths from the sp² carbon; para (C4) substituent keeps them identical.","Any CHBr double bond assumed sufficient: Check both ends of the double bond."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Number of geometrical isomers possible for the given structure is/are _________.\n[Structure: DHC=C(CH₃)–CH₂–C(=CH–CH₃)–CH₂–C(CH₃)=CHD; i.e. a hepta-1,6-diene-type skeleton with a central exocyclic ethylidene: C4 carries =CH–CH₃, C2 and C6 each carry CH₃, and each terminal =CHD carbon bears one H and one D.]","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 8 (2³, treating all three as independent), 6 (removing duplicates incorrectly), 3 (ignoring the central bond), 2.\nWorking: both terminal C=CHD bonds are stereogenic (H vs D; CH₃ vs CH₂R). The central C=CH–CH₃ bond is stereogenic only when the two arms differ: EE → 1, ZZ → 1, EZ → 2 (central E/Z). Total 4.\nKey idea: The central double bond's two substituents are constitutionally the same arm; it is stereogenic only if the arms have opposite configurations.","traps":["2ⁿ formula applied blindly: Check symmetry: identical arms make some isomers coincide.","H vs D not seen as different: H and D are different groups for stereo purposes."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"2-chlorobutane + Cl₂ → C₄H₈Cl₂ (isomers)\nTotal number of optically active isomers shown by C₄H₈Cl₂, obtained in the above reaction is _________.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 7 or 8 (counting meso-2,3 or 2,2 as optically active), 3 (counting constitutional chiral products only), 4 (missing 1,3 or 1,2).\nWorking: monochlorination of 2-chlorobutane gives 1,2- (chiral: 2 enantiomers), 2,2- (achiral), 2,3- (two stereocentres: RR, SS optically active + meso) and 1,3-dichlorobutane (chiral: 2). Optically active = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.\nKey idea: Enumerate all C₄H₈Cl₂ from 2-chlorobutane: 1,2; 2,2; 2,3; 1,3 – then count chiral stereoisomers, excluding meso.","traps":["Meso-2,3-dichlorobutane counted: Meso form has a mirror plane.","Constitutional products counted instead of stereoisomers: Each chiral product counts as two enantiomers."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"D-(+)-Glyceraldehyde —(i) HCN; ii) H₂O/H⁺; iii) HNO₃)→\nThe products formed in the above reaction are","options":["Two optically active products","One optically active and one meso product","Two optically inactive products","One optically inactive and one meso product."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): both tartaric acids assumed chiral (meso form missed). (C): assumes racemic mixtures from HCN addition and calls both inactive. (D): 'inactive' and 'meso' overlap – would need both to be achiral.\nWorking: HCN adds to CHO creating a new stereocentre (two diastereomeric cyanohydrins); hydrolysis gives the two 2,3,4-trihydroxybutanoic acids; HNO₃ oxidises the terminal CH₂OH to COOH giving 2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acids: meso-tartaric acid (optically inactive) and the optically active (D-/L-threo) tartaric acid.\nKey idea: Existing D-stereocentre is retained; new centre forms both ways → diastereomers; symmetric diacid → one is meso.","traps":["Meso form missed: After oxidation both ends are COOH – check for internal mirror plane.","HCN addition assumed to give a racemate: The original D-centre stays fixed: products are diastereomers, not enantiomers."],"topics":["Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Counting structural isomers","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Number of isomeric compounds with molecular formula C₉H₁₀O which (i) do not dissolve in NaOH (ii) do not dissolve in HCl. (iii) do not give orange precipitate with 2,4-DNP (iv) on hydrogenation give identical compound with molecular formula C₉H₁₂O is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Counting all non-phenolic alkenyl alcohols/ethers gives large numbers; counting only one isomer gives 1; including enols gives 3+.\nWorking: C₉H₁₀O has DBE 5 (benzene ring + one C=C). Insoluble in NaOH → not a phenol; insoluble in HCl → not an amine; no 2,4-DNP test → not an aldehyde/ketone. So the compounds are aryl-substituted alkenyl alcohols/ethers. (E)- and (Z)-PhCH=CHCH₂OH both hydrogenate to the same compound, PhCH₂CH₂CH₂OH (C₉H₁₂O) → 2.\nKey idea: DBE 5 = benzene + one C=C; hydrogenation of one H₂ removes the C=C; isomers giving the same product are cis/trans pairs such as (E)- and (Z)-PhCH=CHCH₂OH, so 2.","traps":["Phrase \"identical compound\" misread: Look for a set of isomers converging on one product: E/Z pair.","Enol forms counted: Enols tautomerise to carbonyls (which give 2,4-DNP test)."],"topics":["Counting structural isomers","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Optical activity & symmetry","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The total number of chiral compound/s from the following (1)–(5) (shown below) is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Answers 1 (missing axial chirality of compound 1), 3 (counting cis cyclopropane or the inositol as chiral), 4 (counting every drawn stereocentre).\nWorking: (1) the benzylidene group and the C1 substituents lie in perpendicular planes, like an allene, so there is no mirror plane – chiral (axial chirality). (2) the CH(OH) carbon carries two different aryloxymethyl arms – one stereocentre, chiral. (3) meso-tartaric acid, (4) the inositol drawn and (5) the cis-cyclopropane diacid each have an internal mirror plane – achiral. Total = 2.\nKey idea: Compound 1 is chiral without a stereocentre (like an allene); compound 2 has one stereocentre; 3, 4, 5 each have a mirror plane.","traps":["No stereocentre so assumed achiral: Check overall symmetry: alkylidenecyclohexane behaves like an allene.","Meso forms counted as chiral: Look for an internal mirror plane."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S1C19-s.webp"],"topics":["Optical activity & symmetry","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tautomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"All structures given below are of vitamin C. Most stable of them is :","options":["γ-Lactone ring: O1–C1(=O)–C2H(OH)–C3(=O)–C4H(side chain) (3-keto tautomer); side chain C4–CH(OH)–CH₂OH","γ-Lactone ring: O1–C1(=O)–C2(OH)=C3(OH)–C4H(side chain) (2,3-enediol); side chain C4–CH(OH)–CH₂OH","γ-Lactone ring: O1–C1(=O)–C2(=O)–C3H(OH)–C4H(side chain) (2-keto tautomer); side chain C4–CH(OH)–CH₂OH","Ring: O1–C1(OH)=C2(OH)–C3(=O)–C4H(side chain) (enol of the lactone carbonyl); side chain C4–CH(OH)–CH₂OH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) are keto forms; students who memorise 'keto is more stable than enol' pick them. (D) is an enol of the ester carbonyl, which loses the lactone C=O resonance and is unstable.\nWorking: the 2,3-enediol form (B) is the most stable: its C=C is conjugated with the lactone C=O, and the enol OH groups are further stabilised by intramolecular H-bonding.\nKey idea: Enols are favoured when the C=C is conjugated with a carbonyl and H-bonded; ascorbic acid exists as the enediol (B).","traps":["Keto form always more stable than enol: Check conjugation and H-bonding: conjugated enediol with C=O wins here.","Enolising the lactone C=O: Enolising an ester carbonyl destroys the stabilised O–C=O; keep the lactone C=O intact."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C17","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Testosterone, which is a steroidal hormone, has the following structure (shown below). The total number of asymmetric carbon atom/s in testosterone is ________.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 or 5 (missing quaternary C10/C13 because they have no H, or missing C8), 7 (counting sp² C5 or a CH₂).\nWorking: the stereocentres are C8, C9, C10, C13, C14 and C17 = 6.\nKey idea: Quaternary ring-fusion carbons with methyl groups (C10, C13) are stereocentres; sp² C5 is not.","traps":["Quaternary carbons skipped: A carbon with four different ring paths is chiral even without H.","sp² C5 counted as a stereocentre: Only sp³ carbons with four different groups count."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Feb01S2C17-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The isomeric deuterated bromide with molecular formula C₄H₈DBr having two chiral carbon atoms is","options":["2-Bromo-1-deuterobutane","2-Bromo-1-deutero-2-methylpropane","2-Bromo-3-deuterobutane","2-Bromo-2-deuterobutane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) only C-2 is chiral (CH₂D is not a stereocentre); (B) C-2 bears two CH₃ groups – no stereocentre; (D) C-2 is chiral (D, Br, CH₃, C₂H₅) but there is only one.\nWorking: C-2 has H, Br, CH₃, CHDCH₃; C-3 has H, D, CH₃, CHBrCH₃ – both chiral, so 2-bromo-3-deuterobutane has two chiral carbons.\nKey idea: D ≠ H for chirality: CHD becomes a stereocentre only if its other two groups also differ – true at C-3 of 2-bromo-3-deuterobutane.","traps":["Isotope as a distinct group: Treat D as a different substituent when checking four different groups.","CH₂D is not a stereocentre: A carbon with two H atoms cannot be chiral even if one neighbour is D."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List I (Isomeric pairs) with List II (Type of isomers). List I: A. Propanamine and N-Methylethanamine; B. Hexan-2-one and Hexan-3-one; C. Ethanamide and Hydroxyethanimine; D. o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol. List II: I. Metamers; II. Positional isomers; III. Functional isomers; IV. Tautomers. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) call the hexanones tautomers or functional isomers; (C) calls the amines positional and the nitrophenols tautomers; only (A) separates metamers from position isomers correctly.\nWorking: A – propanamine (1° amine) and N-methylethanamine (2° amine) are functional isomers (III). B – hexan-2-one and hexan-3-one have different alkyl groups on either side of C=O: metamers (I). C – ethanamide ⇌ hydroxyethanimine is amide–imidic acid tautomerism (IV). D – o- and p-nitrophenol are position isomers (II).\nKey idea: If the functional group is in the chain and only the alkyl groups on each side change, call it metamerism (hexan-2-one: CH₃/C₄H₉; hexan-3-one: C₂H₅/C₃H₇).","traps":["Metamer vs position isomer: For divalent groups (–O–, –CO–, –NH–) inside a chain, different flanking alkyls = metamers.","Amide–imidic acid tautomerism: H shifts between N and O with a C=O ⇌ C=N switch – same pattern as keto–enol."],"topics":["Structural isomerism","Tautomerism"]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Total number of isomers (including stereoisomers) obtained on monochlorination of methylcyclohexane is ________.","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: Counting only constitutional isomers gives 5; ignoring enantiomers gives 8; treating 1,4-isomers as chiral gives 14.\nWorking: 1-chloro-1-methyl (1, achiral) + chloromethylcyclohexane (1) + 1-chloro-2-methyl (2 stereocentres: cis pair + trans pair = 4) + 1-chloro-3-methyl (4) + 1-chloro-4-methyl (cis + trans, both achiral = 2) = 12.\nKey idea: 1,2 and 1,3 → 4 each; 1,4 → 2 (cis/trans, achiral via mirror plane); 1,1 and CH₂Cl → 1 each.","traps":["1,4-isomer chirality: A plane through C1–C4 makes both cis and trans achiral.","Forgetting the CH₂Cl isomer: Consider every distinct H type including CH₃."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Counting structural isomers"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Optical activity & symmetry","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Optical activity of an enantiomeric mixture is +12.6° and the specific rotation of (+) isomer is +30°. The optical purity is____%","type":"num","answer":"42","range":[42.0,42.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 71 (% of (+) isomer = (100+42)/2), 58 or 29 (% of (−) isomer).\nWorking: optical purity = observed rotation / rotation of pure enantiomer × 100 = 12.6/30 × 100 = 42%.\nKey idea: Optical purity = enantiomeric excess = α(obs)/α(pure) × 100.","traps":["Optical purity vs % major enantiomer: Optical purity equals ee, not the mole % of (+)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Projections & configuration","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements. Statement I: The compound [stereocentre bearing NO₂ (wedge), H (hash), a (Z)-prop-1-enyl group and an (E)-prop-1-enyl group, i.e. (2Z,5E)-4-nitrohepta-2,5-diene as drawn] is optically active. Statement II: [the same constitution drawn with NO₂ (wedge) and H (hash) on the left, (E)-propenyl up-right and (Z)-propenyl down] is mirror image of above compound A. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) takes the redrawn molecule as the enantiomer from a quick glance; (B)/(D) think two propenyl groups are identical so the centre is not chiral.\nWorking: C4 carries NO₂, H, (Z)-CH=CHCH₃ and (E)-CH=CHCH₃ – four different groups (Z ≠ E), so the compound is chiral and optically active. CIP (NO₂ > Z-propenyl > E-propenyl > H, Z ranked above E) gives S for both drawings, so the Statement II structure is the same compound, not its mirror image.\nKey idea: Z-propenyl ≠ E-propenyl; assign R/S to both drawings – both S.","traps":["Two propenyl groups taken as identical: Compare alkene geometry of each branch.","Redrawn molecule taken as mirror image: Assign R/S to both before deciding."],"topics":["Projections & configuration","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The number of stereoisomers formed in a reaction of (±) Ph(C=O)C(OH)(CN)Ph with HCN is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (2² counted, meso missed); 2 (only the new centre considered); 1.\nWorking: HCN adds to C=O giving PhC(OH)(CN)–C(OH)(CN)Ph, with two identical stereocentres (each Ph, OH, CN, other half) → (R,R), (S,S) and meso = 3 stereoisomers; racemic start and non-selective addition give all three.\nKey idea: Two like stereocentres → 3 stereoisomers.","traps":["Meso form missed: Check for symmetry between the two centres."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations"]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tautomerism","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which will have the highest enol content ?","options":["cyclohexane-1,2-dione","cyclohexane-1,3-dione","cyclohexane-1,3,5-trione","cyclohexane-1,4-dione"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) 1,3-dione – the classic high-enol β-diketone, but not aromatic; (A) 1,2-dione – enolises (diosphenol) but less; (D) 1,4-dione – no 1,3 relationship, little enol.\nWorking: cyclohexane-1,3,5-trione can enolise all three C=O groups to give 1,3,5-trihydroxybenzene (phloroglucinol), an aromatic enol, so its enol content is highest = (C).\nKey idea: If the enol form is aromatic, enolisation is essentially complete.","traps":["β-diketone rule applied blindly: Check whether the full enol is aromatic.","Counting enolisable C=O only: Enol stability decides, not number of C=O."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Among the following structures, which will show the most stable enamine formation ? (Where Me is –CH₃) [All four are N-alkenyl derivatives of piperidine-2-carboxylic acid with COOH on a wedge at C2; the alkenyl group on N is –C(CH₃)=CH–OCH₃ in different geometries/orientations.]","options":["N–C(Me)=CH–OMe with OMe trans to N; the =CH(OMe) arm points toward the COOH side of the ring and Me toward the C6 side","N–C(Me)=CH–OMe with OMe cis to N; the =CH(OMe) arm points toward the COOH side and Me toward the C6 side","N–C(Me)=CH–OMe with OMe trans to N; the =CH(OMe) arm points toward the C6 (unsubstituted) side and Me toward the COOH side","N–C(Me)=CH–OMe with OMe cis to N; the =CH(OMe) arm points toward the C6 side and Me toward the COOH side"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – right geometry (OMe trans) but wrong orientation relative to COOH; (B)/(D) – OMe cis to N, chosen if only electronic donation is considered.\nWorking: for the N lone pair to conjugate with C=C the enamine must be planar. OMe cis to N (B, D) clashes with the ring CH₂ groups next to N (A(1,3)-type strain), so B and D are less stable. Between A and C, the longer planar =CH–OMe arm placed next to the substituted C2 (bearing COOH) in A causes more strain than a methyl there, so C is most stable. The reasoning is beyond JEE Main/NCERT level.\nKey idea: Decide by sterics: keep the bulky/long vinyl arm away from N-ring substituents and the substituted C2.","traps":["Geometry about C=C ignored: Mark whether OMe is cis or trans to N first.","Orientation relative to COOH ignored: Check which arm sits next to the substituted ring carbon."],"topics":["Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"Observe structures of the following compounds: 2-chlorobutane (CH₃CHClCH₂CH₃), glycine (H₂NCH₂COOH), alanine (CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH), styrene (C₆H₅CH=CH₂), and menthol (2-isopropyl-5-methylcyclohexan-1-ol). The total number of structures/compounds which possess asymmetric carbon atoms is ________.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (missing menthol ring stereocentres); 4 (counting glycine).\nWorking: 2-chlorobutane (C2), alanine (Cα) and menthol (three stereocentres) have asymmetric carbons; glycine (CH₂) and styrene have none → 3.\nKey idea: Asymmetric C has four different groups; ring carbons with different paths count.","traps":["Ring stereocentres: Check each substituted ring carbon.","Glycine: Glycine Cα has two H."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The number of chiral alcohol(s) with molecular formula C₄H₁₀O is ____.","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (counting R and S of butan-2-ol); including ethers (not alcohols).\nWorking: the four C₄H₉OH alcohols are butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol; only butan-2-ol has a stereocentre → 1 (counting its two enantiomers separately would give 2).\nKey idea: Count structures with an asymmetric carbon; only butan-2-ol.","traps":["Enantiomers counted separately: The question counts distinct alcohols, not enantiomers.","Ethers included: Only –OH compounds asked."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The number of stereoisomers possible for 1,2-dimethyl cyclopropane is:","options":["One","Four","Two","Three"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) Four = 2ⁿ with two stereocentres, ignoring the meso form; (C) Two = cis + trans, ignoring trans enantiomers; (A) treats the ring as achiral and single.\nWorking: cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopropane is meso (one isomer, mirror plane); trans exists as a pair of enantiomers (1R,2R)/(1S,2S). Total 3.\nKey idea: Two identical stereocentres → 2ⁿ minus duplicates: cis meso + trans pair = 3.","traps":["cis isomer is meso: Look for a mirror plane in the cis form.","trans is chiral: Draw the mirror image of trans – non-superimposable."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which of the following molecules does not show stereo isomerism ?","options":["3,4-Dimethylhex-3-ene","3-Methylhex-1-ene","3-Ethylhex-3-ene","4-Methylhex-1-ene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) are terminal alkenes (no E/Z) – tempting as \"no stereoisomerism\" if chirality is overlooked; (A) symmetric-looking but has E/Z.\nWorking: 3-ethylhex-3-ene is (C₂H₅)₂C=CH–C₂H₅; C3 carries two identical ethyl groups (no cis/trans) and there is no stereocentre. (A) shows E/Z (each alkene C has Me and Et); (B) C3 is chiral (H, CH₃, CH=CH₂, C₃H₇); (D) C4 is chiral (H, CH₃, C₂H₅, CH₂CH=CH₂).\nKey idea: Check both types: no E/Z and no stereocentre is needed for \"no stereoisomerism\".","traps":["Terminal alkene ruled out too early: Also look for a carbon with four different groups.","Symmetric-looking alkene: Each alkene C has Me ≠ Et → E/Z exists."],"topics":["Geometrical isomerism","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Counting structural isomers","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of acyclic structural isomers (including geometrical isomers) for pentene are ____","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 5 (forgetting cis/trans), 7 (giving 2-methylbut-2-ene cis/trans), 10+ (including cyclic).\nWorking: pent-1-ene, (E)- and (Z)-pent-2-ene, 2-methylbut-1-ene, 3-methylbut-1-ene, 2-methylbut-2-ene = 6.\nKey idea: 5 constitutional alkenes + 1 extra for cis/trans pent-2-ene.","traps":["Geometrical isomers missed: Add cis and trans for pent-2-ene.","False E/Z for trisubstituted alkene: A carbon with two identical groups gives no cis/trans."],"topics":["Counting structural isomers","Geometrical isomerism"]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"[cis-but-2-ene: H₃C(H)C=C(H)CH₃ with both CH₃ on the same side] + Br₂ —(CCl₄)→ Product \"P\". Consider the above chemical reaction. The total number of stereoisomers possible for Product 'P' is ______.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (all stereoisomers of 2,3-dibromobutane counted), 1 (meso, from syn addition or confusing cis/trans), 4 (2ⁿ with n = 2 ignoring meso).\nWorking: Br₂ adds anti via a bromonium ion; anti addition to cis-but-2-ene gives the racemic pair (2R,3R)- and (2S,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane – two stereoisomers formed (the meso isomer comes from the trans-alkene). Note: 2,3-dibromobutane itself has 3 stereoisomers; the question counts those actually formed.\nKey idea: cis + anti → racemic (2 isomers); trans + anti → meso (1).","traps":["Possible vs formed: Count the stereoisomers produced by anti addition.","cis/trans → meso/racemic: Anti + cis = racemic; anti + trans = meso."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tautomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Assertion A: Enol form of acetone [CH₃COCH₃] exists in < 0.1% quantity. However, the enol form of acetyl acetone [CH₃COCH₂COCH₃] exists in approximately 15% quantity. Reason R: enol form of acetyl acetone is stabilized by intramolecular hydrogen bonding, which is not possible in enol form of acetone. Choose the correct statement:","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – feels R is a side fact (conjugation also matters); (A)/(D) – doubt the enol percentages.\nWorking: the enol of acetylacetone is stabilised by conjugation and a six-membered intramolecular O–H···O hydrogen bond, absent in acetone's enol. (Its enol content is actually far above 15% in the neat liquid/non-polar solvents, about 15% only in water; the paper's figure is accepted as given.)\nKey idea: Intramolecular H-bond (six-membered chelate) + conjugation stabilise the enol of 1,3-diketones.","traps":["Conjugation vs H-bond as the reason: R gives a real difference between the two enols → it explains A.","Acetylacetone structure: CH₃COCH₂COCH₃ – the CH₂ between the two C=O groups is what enolises."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Structural isomerism","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Compound with molecular formula C₃H₆O can show:","options":["Positional isomerism","Both positional isomerism and metamerism","Metamerism","Functional group isomerism"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) positional – one could argue for unsaturated alcohols, but the classic carbonyl isomers differ in functional group; (C) metamerism – needs at least C₄ (e.g. C₄H₁₀O ethers).\nWorking: C₃H₆O includes propanal (CH₃CH₂CHO) and propanone (CH₃COCH₃) – functional group isomers (also allyl alcohol, methyl vinyl ether, cyclic ethers). Metamerism needs alkyl groups of different size on either side of a polyvalent group, impossible with three carbons in a saturated ether/ketone; the intended answer is functional isomerism (NCERT example).\nKey idea: C₃H₆O is NCERT's own example of aldehyde–ketone functional isomerism.","traps":["Metamerism needs larger groups: Metamers need different alkyl groups on each side; C₃ allows only CH₃/C₂H₅ arrangements that are identical or change the functional group."]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The stereoisomers that are formed by electrophilic addition of bromine to trans-but-2-ene is/are:","options":["2 enantiomers and 2 mesomers","2 identical mesomers","2 enantiomers","1 racemic and 2 enantiomers"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) mixes meso and chiral products – no such mixture forms; (C) 2 enantiomers – result for cis-but-2-ene (or syn addition to trans); (D) racemic + enantiomers – double counts the racemate.\nWorking: anti addition of Br₂ to trans-but-2-ene gives only meso-2,3-dibromobutane; attack at either carbon gives the same meso compound (\"2 identical mesomers\").\nKey idea: trans + anti → meso; cis + anti → racemic.","traps":["Syn addition assumed: Br₂ adds anti via the bromonium ion; anti addition to a trans alkene gives the meso dibromide.","cis/trans results swapped: trans + anti → meso; cis + anti → (±)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of chiral carbons in chloramphenicol is ______ .","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 3 (counts the CHCl₂ carbon as chiral); 1 (misses the C–NH carbon); 0 (structure not known).\nWorking: chloramphenicol is p-O₂N–C₆H₄–CH(OH)–CH(NHCOCHCl₂)–CH₂OH; the CH(OH) and CH(NH) carbons are stereocentres; CHCl₂ has two identical Cl and CH₂OH has two H.\nKey idea: Only the two adjacent carbons bearing OH and NHCOCHCl₂ are stereocentres.","traps":["CHCl₂ carbon counted as chiral: Two identical Cl on one carbon → not a stereocentre.","Structure must be recalled: Learn: p-nitrophenyl–CH(OH)–CH(NHCOCHCl₂)–CH₂OH."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"The number of chiral centres in penicillin is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 2 (missing C-2 with COOH), 4 (counting the gem-dimethyl C or N).\nWorking: penicillin core (6-acylamino-penam-3-carboxylic acid) has stereocentres at C-2 (COOH-bearing), C-5 (bridgehead CH–S) and C-6 (CH–NHCOR) = 3.\nKey idea: Look at the β-lactam–thiazolidine fused core: 3 stereocentres.","traps":["Structure not given: Recall the penam core: C-2, C-5, C-6.","Counting gem-dimethyl carbon: Two identical groups ⇒ not chiral."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The number of chiral carbons present in the molecule given below is ____ . [structure: a quinine-like molecule — a quinuclidine (1-azabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) cage bearing an ethyl group, attached at the carbon next to N to a CH that carries an O–(4-methylphenyl) ether and the C4 of a quinoline ring; the quinoline carries a CH(OH)CH₃ group at C6 (H, H₃C, HO drawn on that carbon) on the benzo ring]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (missing bridgehead C4), 3 (missing ring carbons), 6 (counting N or the tolyl CH₃).\nWorking: stereocentres are C3 (CH–ethyl) and C4 (bridgehead CH) of the quinuclidine, C8 (CH next to N), C9 (CH bearing O-aryl and quinolyl) and the CH(OH)CH₃ carbon = 5 (N is not counted as carbon).\nKey idea: A bridgehead CH in an unsymmetrically substituted cage is a stereocentre.","traps":["Bridgehead carbon ignored: Trace the three paths from the bridgehead; they differ because of the ethyl branch.","Nitrogen counted: Question asks only chiral carbons."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Chirality","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which of the following compounds produces an optically inactive compound on hydrogenation?","options":["Chiral alkene: stereocentre bearing H (wedge), CH₃ (hash), n-propyl and CH=C(CH₃)₂ – (CH₃)₂C=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₂CH₃, 2,4-dimethylhept-2-ene, one enantiomer","Chiral alkene: stereocentre bearing H, CH₃, isopropyl and C(CH₃)=CH₂ – CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂, 2,3,4-trimethylpent-1-ene, one enantiomer","Chiral alkene: stereocentre bearing H, CH₃, ethyl and CH=CH–CH₃ – CH₃CH=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH₂CH₃ (E double bond as drawn), (E)-4-methylhex-2-ene, one enantiomer","Chiral alkene: stereocentre bearing H, CH₃, isopropyl and CH=CH–CH₃ – CH₃CH=CH–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)₂ (E double bond as drawn), (E)-4,5-dimethylhex-2-ene, one enantiomer"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D): after hydrogenation the two carbon chains still differ (propyl/isobutyl, ethyl/propyl, isopropyl/propyl).\nWorking: H₂ turns C(CH₃)=CH₂ into CH(CH₃)₂, which equals the other isopropyl group, so the product 2,3,4-trimethylpentane is achiral. In (A) propyl ≠ isobutyl, in (C) ethyl ≠ propyl, in (D) isopropyl ≠ propyl – all stay chiral.\nKey idea: Hydrogenate in your head, then look at the four groups on the stereocentre.","traps":["Assuming any alkene loses chirality on hydrogenation: Write the saturated group and compare with the other chain.","Misreading skeletal branches: Count carbons in each arm."],"topics":["Chirality","Stereochemistry in reactions & conformations"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The total number of monohalogenated organic products in the following (including stereoisomers) reaction is ______.\nA (simplest optically active alkene) –(i) H₂/Ni/Δ; (ii) X₂/Δ→","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Likely wrong values: 4 (structural isomers only), 6 (missing the second stereocentre in 2-halo product), 7 (ignoring chirality at C3 in the 1-halo product).\nWorking: A = 3-methylpent-1-ene; H₂ → 3-methylpentane. Monohalogenation: 1-halo-3-methylpentane (1 stereocentre, 2), 2-halo-3-methylpentane (2 stereocentres, 4), 3-halo-3-methylpentane (achiral, 1), 1-halo-2-ethylbutane (achiral, 1) → 8.\nKey idea: C₆H₁₂ 3-methylpent-1-ene is the smallest chiral alkene; count stereocentres in each product.","traps":["Wrong simplest chiral alkene: Need four different groups: H, CH₃, C₂H₅, CH=CH₂.","Stereocentre created at C3 missed: Re-check C3 after halogenating C1."],"topics":["Relationships, meso & counting stereoisomers","Chirality"]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Geometrical isomerism","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Among the following compounds, geometrical isomerism is exhibited by:\n(1) 4-chloro-1-methylidenecyclohexane (=CH₂ exocyclic at C-1, Cl at C-4)\n(2) 1-(chloromethylidene)-3-methylcyclohexane (=CHCl exocyclic at C-1, CH₃ at C-3)\n(3) (chloromethylidene)cyclohexane (=CHCl exocyclic)\n(4) 1-(chloromethylidene)-3,5-dimethylcyclohexane (=CHCl exocyclic, CH₃ at C-3 and C-5)","options":["4-chloro-1-methylidenecyclohexane","1-(chloromethylidene)-3-methylcyclohexane","(chloromethylidene)cyclohexane","1-(chloromethylidene)-3,5-dimethylcyclohexane"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) =CH₂ end has two identical H; (C) unsubstituted ring – both ring arms identical; (D) 3,5-dimethyl symmetric – both arms identical.\nWorking: for E/Z each sp² carbon needs two different groups. (A) =CH₂ has two H; (C) ring carbons C-2 and C-6 are equivalent; (D) the 3,5-dimethyl ring (cis-dimethyl as drawn) is symmetric, so C-2 and C-6 are equivalent; (B) only 3-methyl makes the two ring arms different, and =CHCl has H ≠ Cl → E/Z exists.\nKey idea: Walk round the ring both ways from the sp² ring carbon; if the two paths differ, E/Z is possible.","traps":["Symmetric disubstituted ring counted: Check symmetry: 3,5-substitution makes C-2 and C-6 equivalent.","Ring substituent position ignored: A substituent on the axis through the double bond does not break symmetry; =CH₂ fails anyway."]},{"id":"JM19Apr08S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tautomerism","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Which of the following compounds will show the maximum enol content?","options":["CH₃COCH₂COCH₃","CH₃COCH₃","CH₃COCH₂CONH₂","CH₃COCH₂COOC₂H₅"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) acetone has no second carbonyl; (C) amide N lone pair reduces CH₂ acidity and enol stability; (D) ester OEt does the same, less strongly.\nWorking: acetylacetone's enol is a conjugated, intramolecularly H-bonded 1,3-diketone; amide and ester carbonyls are less able to pull electron density (resonance with N/O), so their enols are less favoured; acetone is almost entirely keto.\nKey idea: Two ketone C=O groups give the most acidic CH₂ and the most stable chelated enol.","traps":["Ester taken as most enolised: Compare electron-withdrawing power of COCH₃ vs COOEt.","H-bonded enol overlooked: Look for conjugation + six-membered H-bond chelate."]}],"m16-halo":[{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"The reactions which cannot be applied to prepare an alkene by elimination, are\nA. 1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane —[NaOEt]→\nB. CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃ —[KOH (aq)]→\nC. (CH₃)₃C–Br —[NaOMe]→\nD. Phenol (C₆H₅–OH) —[Na₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄]→\nE. (CH₃)₃C–OH —[Cu, 573 K]→\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, C & D Only","B & E Only","B & D Only","B, C & D Only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane + NaOEt gives alkene (can). B: aq. KOH gives alcohol (cannot). C: t-BuBr + NaOMe gives isobutylene (can). D: phenol + Na₂Cr₂O₇/H₂SO₄ gives benzoquinone (cannot). E: t-BuOH + Cu, 573 K gives isobutylene (can). Option (A) A, C, D treats alkoxide as non-eliminating; (B) B, E misreads the Cu exception; (D) adds C. (C) correct.\nWorking: an alkene forms only by β-elimination with an alkoxide/alcoholic base (A, C) or by dehydration of the tertiary alcohol (E); aqueous KOH gives substitution (B) and phenol is oxidised to benzoquinone (D), so B and D cannot give alkenes.\nKey idea: Only alkoxide/alcoholic base and tertiary-alcohol dehydration give alkenes here; aqueous KOH and phenol oxidation do not.","traps":["Alcoholic vs aqueous KOH: elimination vs substitution: KOH(aq) substitutes; only alcoholic KOH or alkoxide eliminates.","Tertiary alcohol with Cu at 573 K dehydrates: NCERT: tertiary alcohols with Cu at 573 K undergo dehydration to alkenes (2-methylpropene)."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM25Apr07S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"Match List - I with List - II.\nList - I: Conversion | List - II: Reagents, Conditions used\nA. Chlorobenzene → Phenol | I. Warm, H₂O\nB. 4-Nitrochlorobenzene → 4-Nitrophenol | II. (a) NaOH, 368 K; (b) H₃O⁺\nC. 2,4-Dinitrochlorobenzene → 2,4-Dinitrophenol | III. (a) NaOH, 443 K; (b) H₃O⁺\nD. 2,4,6-Trinitrochlorobenzene → 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol | IV. (a) NaOH, 623 K, 300 atm; (b) H₃O⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses A/B. (B) swaps C and D. (C) gives chlorobenzene the mild 368 K condition and trinitro the harshest. (D) correct.\nWorking: chlorobenzene needs 623 K and 300 atm (IV); one p-NO₂ lowers this to 443 K (III); two o/p-NO₂ groups to 368 K (II); three NO₂ groups react with warm water (I).\nKey idea: More o/p nitro groups stabilise the Meisenheimer intermediate, so milder conditions suffice.","traps":["Graded haloarene hydrolysis conditions swapped: Each extra o/p NO₂ lowers the temperature: 623 K, 443 K, 368 K, warm water."],"topics":["Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","Haloarene reactions"]},{"id":"JM25Jan22S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"RBr —[(i) Mg, dry ether; (ii) H₂O]→ 2-Methylbutane\nThe maximum number of RBr producing 2-methylbutane by above sequence of reactions is ______. (Consider the structural isomers only)","options":["5","4","3","1"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 5 adds a non-existent fifth position or an enantiomer; (C) 3 merges the C4 methyl with the C1 methyls; (D) 1 assumes only one RBr. (B) 4 correct.\nWorking: putting Br at each distinct carbon of 2-methylbutane gives four structural isomers – 1-bromo-2-methylbutane, 2-bromo-2-methylbutane, 2-bromo-3-methylbutane and 1-bromo-3-methylbutane – and each gives 2-methylbutane after Grignard formation and hydrolysis.\nKey idea: The number of RBr equals the number of chemically distinct H positions in the alkane formed.","traps":["Distinct hydrogen environments counted for isomer number: 2-methylbutane has four distinct H-bearing carbons: two equivalent CH₃ on C2, CH, CH₂, terminal CH₃.","Stereoisomers counted when only structural isomers asked: The stem says structural isomers only."]},{"id":"JM25Jan23S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 23 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"The ascending order of relative rate of solvolysis of following compounds is:\n(Compounds (A)–(D) are shown below.)","options":["(D) < (B) < (A) < (C)","(C) < (B) < (A) < (D)","(C) < (D) < (B) < (A)","(D) < (A) < (B) < (C)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts tertiary above secondary benzylic. (B) and (C) are reversed/descending. (D) correct.\nWorking: solvolysis is SN1, so rate follows carbocation stability: Ph₂CH⁺ (C, two rings) > secondary benzylic cation (B) > tertiary cation (A) > secondary cation (D). Ascending: (D) < (A) < (B) < (C).\nKey idea: Rank by cation stability: two aryl rings > one aryl ring > tertiary alkyl > secondary alkyl.","traps":["Benzylic carbocation beats tertiary alkyl carbocation: Resonance delocalisation into a benzene ring outweighs hyperconjugation of a tertiary cation.","Ascending order asked, options mix both directions: Write slowest first: secondary alkyl first, diphenylmethyl last."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan23S2C17-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":63,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Et₂N–CH₂CH₂–Cl will undergo alkaline hydrolysis at a faster rate than Et₂CH–CH₂CH₂–Cl\nStatement II: In Et₂N–CH₂CH₂–Cl, intramolecular substitution takes place first by involving lone pair of electrons on nitrogen.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Statement I: true. Statement II: true (intramolecular substitution first). Option (A).\nWorking: the N lone pair displaces Cl⁻ intramolecularly (neighbouring-group participation) to give a cyclic aziridinium ion, which OH⁻ opens rapidly; the carbon analogue has no lone pair and reacts by slow ordinary SN2. Both statements are correct.\nKey idea: A lone pair two carbons from the leaving group participates internally and accelerates substitution.","traps":["Neighbouring group participation speeds substitution: A beta lone pair forms a three-membered aziridinium ion, a much better leaving situation."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S1C13-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":66,"q":"The products A and B in the following reactions, respectively are:\nA ←(AgNO₂)— CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br —(AgCN)→ B","options":["CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NO₂ , CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CN","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–ONO , CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CN","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–ONO , CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NC","CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NO₂ , CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–NC"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives nitrile (KCN product). (B) gives nitrite and nitrile (both alkali-salt products). (C) gives nitrite with isocyanide. (D) correct.\nWorking: silver salts react through nitrogen: AgNO₂ gives 1-nitropropane, CH₃CH₂CH₂NO₂ (A), and AgCN gives propyl isocyanide, CH₃CH₂CH₂NC (B).\nKey idea: AgCN gives isocyanides and AgNO₂ gives nitroalkanes; the potassium salts give the opposite linkage.","traps":["Ambident nucleophile: silver salt vs alkali salt: Silver salts: AgCN attacks via N (isocyanide), AgNO₂ via N (nitroalkane); alkali salts: KCN via C (nitrile), KNO₂ via O (nitrite)."]},{"id":"JM25Jan28S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2025,"paper":"Main Jan 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":65,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nCompound (shown below) —[KOH/EtOH (excess), Δ]→ Major product","options":["6-Phenylhepta-2,4-diene","2-Phenylhepta-2,5-diene","6-Phenylhepta-3,5-diene","2-Phenylhepta-2,4-diene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 6-phenylhepta-2,4-diene: conjugated diene but phenyl on sp³ carbon. (B) 2-phenylhepta-2,5-diene: non-conjugated diene. (C) 6-phenylhepta-3,5-diene: incorrect numbering (should be lowest locants) and not the conjugation-favoured product. (D) correct.\nWorking: excess hot alcoholic KOH removes both HBr. Loss of the benzylic H at C2 (next to C3–Br) gives C2=C3, conjugated with the ring; the second elimination from C5–Br towards C4 gives C4=C5, conjugated with C2=C3: Ph–C(CH₃)=CH–CH=CH–CH₂CH₃, 2-phenylhepta-2,4-diene.\nKey idea: With excess base, choose the most extended conjugation: aryl-C=C-C=C.","traps":["Conjugated diene favoured in double elimination: Prefer the C=C conjugated with the phenyl ring and with the other C=C.","IUPAC numbering of product misapplied to ring numbering: Draw each named diene and check whether the phenyl carbon is part of a C=C."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Jan28S2C15-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Identify the correct set of reagents or reaction conditions 'X' and 'Y' in the following set of transformation.\nCH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br —'X'→ Product —'Y'→ CH₃–CH(Br)–CH₃","options":["X = dil.aq. NaOH, 20°C, Y = HBr/acetic acid","X = conc.alc. NaOH, 80°C, Y = Br₂/CHCl₃","X = dil.aq. NaOH, 20°C, Y = Br₂/CHCl₃","X = conc.alc. NaOH, 80°C, Y = HBr/acetic acid"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): aq. NaOH gives propan-1-ol (substitution), not the alkene. (B): Br₂/CHCl₃ gives 1,2-dibromopropane.\nWorking: hot conc. alcoholic NaOH eliminates HBr → propene; HBr (no peroxide) adds Markovnikov → 2-bromopropane.\nKey idea: Shift a halogen along the chain: eliminate (alc. base, heat), then re-add HX Markovnikov.","traps":["Aqueous vs alcoholic base: aq. → substitution (alcohol); alc. + heat → elimination.","Br₂ vs HBr addition: Br₂ gives vicinal dibromide; only HBr gives monobromide."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"PhCH₂–CH(Br)–CH(CH₃)₂ (2-bromo-3-methyl-1-phenylbutane) —KOH(alc), Δ→ major product 'P'. Product P is","options":["PhCH₂–CH=C(CH₃)₂ (3-methyl-1-phenylbut-2-ene)","Ph–C(=CH₂)–CH(CH₃)₂ (3-methyl-2-phenylbut-1-ene)","PhCH₂CH₂–C(=CH₂)CH₃ (2-methyl-4-phenylbut-1-ene)","Ph–CH=CH–CH(CH₃)₂ (3-methyl-1-phenylbut-1-ene)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the 'more substituted' Zaitsev alkene and traps rule-followers. (B) and (C) need H or bond shifts not available from this substrate (misreading carbon skeleton).\nWorking: β-H can be lost from the benzylic CH₂ or the isopropyl CH. Losing the benzylic H gives a C=C conjugated with the ring (styrene-type), which is more stable than the trisubstituted but unconjugated alkene (A), so D is major. B and C are not possible from this skeleton by single β-elimination.\nKey idea: Alkene stability: conjugation with benzene ring outweighs extra alkyl substitution.","traps":["Zaitsev applied blindly: Check for conjugation with aromatic ring first.","Skeleton misread: Number carbons of starting halide before drawing products."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Find out the major product formed from the following reaction. [Me: –CH₃]\n3,5-Dibromocyclopent-1-ene (both Br on the allylic carbons flanking the CH₂) —Me₂NH (2 equiv)→ ?","options":["cyclopentene with NMe₂ on two adjacent sp³ carbons, one of them allylic (N,N,N′,N′-tetramethylcyclopent-2-ene-1,5-diamine; i.e. 3,4-bis(dimethylamino)cyclopentene)","cyclopentene with NMe₂ on two carbons 1,3 to each other, one of them on a C=C carbon (enamine-type, C=C on the side of the ring)","cyclopentene with NMe₂ on adjacent carbons, one of them on a C=C carbon (enamine-type)","3,5-bis(dimethylamino)cyclopent-1-ene (direct substitution of both Br)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the naive double direct substitution. (B) keeps the 1,3 N positions but puts one N on a C=C carbon (enamine), which substitution cannot give. (C) puts NMe₂ on an sp² carbon (enamine) – not formed by substitution.\nWorking: first Br leaves easily from its allylic carbon; amine attacks at the other end of the allyl cation (allylic shift/SN2′), placing NMe₂ next to the remaining C–Br and moving the C=C. Second substitution at that C–Br gives the vicinal diamine with the C=C next to one N-bearing carbon – structure A. Direct double SN2 would give D, but the accepted answer follows the allylic-shift route.\nKey idea: Allyl cation can be attacked at either terminus; the shift changes which carbon carries the next leaving group.","traps":["Direct substitution assumed: Allylic cation reacts at its other terminus.","Position of C=C after shift: Number ring carbons and track C=C step by step."],"topics":["SN1","SN2"]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Identify the major product in the following reaction.\n1-Bromo-1-methylcyclopentane —[OH⁻ / C₂H₅OH]→ Major product","options":["Methylcyclopentane","1-Methylcyclopentene (ring C=C at the methyl-bearing carbon)","Methylenecyclopentane (exocyclic C=CH₂)","1-Bromocyclopentene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Hofmann-type exocyclic alkene (less substituted). (A) reduction product, not possible. (D) loses CH₃ – absurd.\nWorking: 3° bromide with alkoxide/hydroxide in ethanol undergoes β-elimination; Saytzeff (more substituted, endocyclic trisubstituted) alkene dominates over exocyclic disubstituted.\nKey idea: Trisubstituted ring alkene > disubstituted exocyclic alkene.","traps":["Exocyclic alkene chosen: Major product = more substituted alkene."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polyhalogen compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I (Compound): A. Iodoform B. Carbon tetrachloride C. CFC D. DDT\nList II (Uses): I. Fire extinguisher II. Insecticide III. Antiseptic IV. Refrigerants\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) jumble uses; (C) swaps CCl₄ and DDT (insecticide vs fire extinguisher) – the only pair a student could hesitate on.\nWorking: iodoform – antiseptic; CCl₄ – fire extinguisher; CFCs (freons) – refrigerants; DDT – insecticide.\nKey idea: Each compound has one standard NCERT use; anchor on iodoform = antiseptic and CFC = refrigerant.","traps":["CCl₄ and DDT uses swapped: CCl₄ is non-flammable vapour → fire extinguisher; DDT → insects."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The major products from the following reaction sequence are product A and product B.\nB ←[(i) Br₂; (ii) alc. KOH (3 eq.)]— cyclohexene —[(i) Br₂; (ii) HC≡C–CH₂–O⁻Na⁺ (1.0 eq.)]→ A\nThe total sum of π electrons in product A and product B are ______ (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (counting only one π bond in the alkyne), 10 (assuming cyclohexyne/benzene-type B or a diene in A too), 4 (only one product).\nWorking: Br₂ gives trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. Excess alc. KOH → double dehydrohalogenation to cyclohexa-1,3-diene (B, 4 π e⁻). One equivalent of sodium propargyloxide → Williamson substitution of one Br, giving 1-bromo-2-(prop-2-ynyloxy)cyclohexane (A, C≡C has 4 π e⁻). Total 8.\nKey idea: An alkyne contributes two π bonds (4 π e⁻); a 1,3-diene has 4 π e⁻.","traps":["Alkyne counted as one π bond: Triple bond = 1 σ + 2 π.","Elimination vs substitution with alkoxide: 1 eq. alkoxide → substitution as intended."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocation rearrangements","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"(1-Chloropropyl)cyclohexane [cyclohexyl–CH(Cl)–CH₂CH₃] + NaOH →(H₂O) Major Product \"A\".\nConsider the above chemical reaction. Product \"A\" is :","options":["1-propylcyclohexan-1-ol (OH and n-propyl on the same ring carbon)","1-cyclohexylpropan-2-ol [cyclohexyl–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃]","1-cyclohexylpropan-1-ol [cyclohexyl–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃]","2-propylcyclohexan-1-ol (OH on ring carbon next to the propyl-bearing carbon)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C): direct substitution without rearrangement (SN2 thinking). (B) and (D): wrong shifts or OH placed on a carbon that never carries the positive charge.\nWorking: in water the secondary carbocation forms and a 1,2-hydride shift from the ring carbon gives the more stable tertiary ring carbocation, which is captured by water/OH⁻ to give 1-propylcyclohexan-1-ol. (Direct SN2 would give (C); the expected answer involves ionisation with rearrangement.)\nKey idea: Secondary cation next to a ring CH shifts H⁻ to give a tertiary cation; OH ends up on that ring carbon.","traps":["Direct substitution chosen: In water, a 2° halide next to a 3° C–H can ionise and rearrange.","Wrong migrating group or position: Only a 1,2-shift from the adjacent carbon, ending at a 3° centre, is favoured."],"topics":["Carbocation rearrangements","SN1"]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which among the following compounds will undergo fastest S_N2 reaction.\n(A) 1-bromo-1-cyclobutylethane (cyclobutyl–CH(CH₃)Br, secondary)\n(B) 2-bromo-2-cyclobutylpropane (cyclobutyl–C(CH₃)₂Br, tertiary)\n(C) (bromomethyl)cyclobutane (cyclobutyl–CH₂Br, primary)\n(D) 1-bromo-1-methylcyclobutane (tertiary ring carbon)","options":["cyclobutyl–CH(CH₃)Br","cyclobutyl–C(CH₃)₂Br","cyclobutyl–CH₂Br","1-bromo-1-methylcyclobutane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) secondary; (B) and (D) tertiary – slower; (D) may tempt as the \"ring\" carbon looks small.\nWorking: S_N2 rate falls with steric crowding at the reacting carbon, 1° > 2° > 3°; only (C) is primary.\nKey idea: S_N2: least substituted carbon wins.","traps":["Confusing S_N1 and S_N2 order: Back-side attack needs an uncrowded carbon."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry of substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : S_N2 reactions are 'stereospecific', indicating that they result in the formation of only one stereo-isomer as the product.\nStatement (II) : S_N1 reactions generally result in formation of product as racemic mixtures.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): student doubts \"stereospecific\" term. (C): student thinks SN1 gives retention.\nWorking: SN2 gives complete inversion (one stereoisomer); SN1 via planar carbocation gives racemisation.\nKey idea: SN2 = inversion; SN1 = racemisation (mostly).","traps":["Unfamiliar term stereospecific: One stereoisomer from inversion = stereospecific."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A): SN2 reaction of C₆H₅CH₂Br occurs more readily than the SN2 reaction of CH₃CH₂Br.\nReason (R): The partially bonded unhybridized p-orbital that develops in the trigonal bipyramidal transition state is stabilized by conjugation with the phenyl ring.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C)/(D) reject one statement because students associate benzylic stabilisation only with carbocations (SN1). (A) accepts both but doubts the causal link.\nWorking: benzylic halides react faster by SN2 because the p-orbital at the TS carbon overlaps with the ring π-system, lowering the TS energy.\nKey idea: In the SN2 TS the central carbon is sp²-like with a p-orbital that conjugates with the phenyl ring.","traps":["Benzylic effect only for SN1: Conjugation also stabilises the SN2 transition state.","Causal link: R describes the exact cause of the rate increase."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions, the major products B and C respectively are:\nCl–(cyclobutane ring, drawn as a diamond)–Br [1-bromo-3-chlorocyclobutane] —Na/Et₂O→ A; A —(i) Mg/Et₂O (ii) D₂O→ B; A —CoF₂→ C","options":["B: bicyclo[1.1.0]butane drawn with D on both outer ring carbons (D–◇(with cross bond)–D); C: F–◇–◇–F (3,3'-difluoro-1,1'-bi(cyclobutyl))","B: D–◇–◇–D (3,3'-dideuterio-1,1'-bi(cyclobutyl)); C: F–◇–◇–F (3,3'-difluoro-1,1'-bi(cyclobutyl))","B: D–◇–D (1,3-dideuteriocyclobutane); C: F–◇–F (1,3-difluorocyclobutane)","B: bicyclo[1.1.0]butane (diamond with cross bond); C: F–◇–F (1,3-difluorocyclobutane)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) assume intramolecular coupling to bicyclobutane. (C) skips the Wurtz dimerisation and replaces both original halogens.\nWorking: Na couples the more reactive C–Br ends (Wurtz) to give A = 3,3'-dichloro-1,1'-bi(cyclobutyl); Grignard/D₂O replaces each Cl by D (B); CoF₂ exchanges Cl for F (Swarts) (C). Note: in practice 1-bromo-3-chlorocyclobutane with Na gives bicyclo[1.1.0]butane by intramolecular coupling, which would leave no halogen for steps 2–3.\nKey idea: C–Br reacts with Na first; both remaining C–Cl groups are then converted (D via RMgCl/D₂O, F via CoF₂).","traps":["Intramolecular vs intermolecular Wurtz: In this question Br couples intermolecularly and Cl survives.","Skipping the dimer: Apply steps 2 and 3 to the Wurtz dimer."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH(Br)–OCH₃ (1-bromo-1-methoxy-2-phenylpropane; benzene ring bearing CH(CH₃) whose next carbon carries both Br and OCH₃) —KCN (alc), Δ→ Major Product 'P'. In the above reaction product 'P' is","options":["Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH(OCH₃)–CN (2-methoxy-3-phenylbutanenitrile)","Ph–CH(CN)–CH(CH₃)–OCH₃ (3-methoxy-2-phenylbutanenitrile)","Ph–C(CH₃)(CN)–CH₂–OCH₃ (3-methoxy-2-methyl-2-phenylpropanenitrile)","Ph–CH(OCH₃)–CH(CH₃)–CN (3-methoxy-2-methyl-3-phenylpropanenitrile)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes CN ends up on the benzylic carbon after a shift. (C) assumes a hydride shift to a tertiary benzylic cation. (D) assumes OCH₃ migrates to the benzylic carbon.\nWorking: the C–Br carbon also carries OCH₃, so ionisation gives a resonance-stabilised oxocarbenium ion (CH=O⁺CH₃); it needs no shift, and CN⁻ adds at that carbon, giving direct substitution product (A).\nKey idea: Lone-pair donation from OCH₃ makes the cation at the C–Br carbon more stable than any benzylic or tertiary cation, so substitution is direct.","traps":["Over-predicting carbocation rearrangement: Check whether the first cation is already stabilised by a lone-pair donor.","α-Alkoxy cation stability underrated: O lone pair gives a full octet resonance form (oxocarbenium)."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : Haloalkanes react with KCN to form alkyl cyanides as a main product while with AgCN form isocyanide as the main product.\nReason (R) : KCN and AgCN both are highly ionic compounds.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): does not know AgCN is covalent. (C): doubts the AgCN–isocyanide statement.\nWorking: KCN is ionic (free CN⁻ attacks through C), AgCN is mainly covalent (N lone pair attacks) → isocyanide. R is false.\nKey idea: Covalent Ag–C bond leaves only N free to attack.","traps":["AgCN taken as ionic like KCN: Ag–C bond is covalent; that is why N attacks."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Polyhalogen compounds","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Match List - I with List - II.\nList I (Compound): (A) Carbon tetrachloride (B) Methylene chloride (C) DDT (D) Freons\nList II (Use): (I) Paint remover (II) Refrigerators and air conditioners (III) Fire extinguisher (IV) Non Biodegradable insecticide\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) pair CCl₄ or CH₂Cl₂ with refrigerant/paint remover wrongly; DDT and freons clearly fixed so they eliminate options quickly.\nWorking: CCl₄ – fire extinguisher; CH₂Cl₂ – paint remover; DDT – persistent insecticide; freons – refrigerants.\nKey idea: DDT (IV) and freons (II) alone single out option B.","traps":["CCl₄ vs CH₂Cl₂ uses: CH₂Cl₂ = paint remover; CCl₄ = fire extinguisher."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry of substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The correct statement regarding nucleophilic substitution reaction in a chiral alkyl halide is ;","options":["Retention occurs in SN1 reaction and inversion occurs in SN2 reaction.","Racemisation occurs in SN1 reaction and retention occurs in SN2 reaction.","Racemisation occurs in both SN1 and SN2 reactions.","Racemisation occurs in SN1 reaction and inversion occurs in SN2 reaction."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) retention in SN1 — wrong (planar cation). (B) retention in SN2 — reverses backside attack. (C) racemisation in SN2 — ignores Walden inversion.\nWorking: SN1 goes through a planar carbocation attacked from both faces (racemisation); SN2 is backside attack (inversion).\nKey idea: Planar intermediate → racemisation; concerted backside attack → inversion.","traps":["Retention confused with inversion: SN2 = Walden inversion, never retention."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which among the following halide/s will not show S_N1 reaction: (A) H₂C=CH–CH₂Cl (B) CH₃–CH=CH–Cl (C) C₆H₅–CH₂–Cl (benzyl chloride) (D) (CH₃)₂CH–Cl. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(B) and (C) only","(A), (B) and (D) only","(B) only","(A) and (B) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) excludes benzyl chloride (wrong, very fast SN1). (B) excludes allyl and isopropyl (2° can do SN1). (D) excludes allyl chloride - confuses allylic with vinylic.\nWorking: allyl (A) and benzyl (C) chlorides form resonance-stabilised cations; isopropyl chloride (D) forms a 2° cation and can react by SN1; only the vinylic chloride (B) cannot (vinyl cation unstable, C–Cl has partial double-bond character).\nKey idea: Halogen on sp² carbon (vinylic/aryl) never gives SN1; on sp³ next to C=C it is favoured.","traps":["Allylic confused with vinylic: Check whether Cl is on the sp² carbon (vinylic) or the next sp³ carbon (allylic).","Secondary halide said not to do SN1: 2° halides can follow SN1; the question asks which cannot."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloarene preparation","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :\nAssertion A : Aryl halides cannot be prepared by replacement of hydroxyl group of phenol by halogen atom.\nReason R : Phenols react with halogen acids violently.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below :","options":["A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) accept the invented reason; (C) rejects a true NCERT statement.\nWorking: A true – the phenolic C–O bond has partial double-bond character and is hard to break; R false – phenols do not react with halogen acids (let alone violently).\nKey idea: C–O bond in phenol is strengthened by resonance; phenol is unreactive to HX.","traps":["Plausible-sounding reason accepted: Recall the real reason: partial double bond C–O; phenols are inert to HX."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Alkyl halide is converted into alkyl isocyanide by reaction with","options":["KCN","NH₄CN","NaCN","AgCN"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C): ionic cyanides — C attacks → alkyl cyanide (nitrile).\nWorking: AgCN is mainly covalent, so attack occurs through N, giving R–NC; ionic cyanides give R–CN.\nKey idea: Covalent AgCN → N-attack → isocyanide.","traps":["Ionic cyanide chosen: Ionic CN⁻ → nitrile; covalent AgCN → isonitrile."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C02","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Example of vinylic halide is :","options":["Cyclohexene ring with X on the sp³ ring carbon adjacent to the C=C (3-halocyclohex-1-ene, allylic)","Cyclohexene ring with CH₂X on a doubly bonded ring carbon (1-(halomethyl)cyclohex-1-ene, allylic)","Benzene ring with X directly on the ring (halobenzene, aryl halide)","Cyclohexane ring carrying –CH=CH–X (2-cyclohexylvinyl halide; X on sp² C=C carbon)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) X on the sp³ ring carbon next to the C=C → allylic halide. (B) CH₂X attached to a C=C carbon → allylic (X on sp³ CH₂). (C) X on a benzene ring → aryl halide, which is also X on sp² carbon but classed separately.\nWorking: only in (D) is X bonded directly to an sp² carbon of a non-aromatic C=C; (A) and (B) are allylic, (C) is aryl.\nKey idea: Vinylic = X on C=C carbon (non-aromatic); aryl = X on benzene ring; allylic = X on carbon next to C=C.","traps":["Aryl halide taken as vinylic: NCERT keeps aryl halides (aromatic ring) separate from vinylic.","Allylic CH₂X next to C=C taken as vinylic: Check the hybridisation of the very carbon that carries X."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Naming","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl is an example of allyl halide.\nReason (R) : Allyl halides are the compounds in which the halogen atom is attached to sp² hybridised carbon atom.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D)/(C) chosen if sp² is accepted (the molecule contains sp² carbons). (B) chosen if allyl chloride is mistaken for vinylic.\nWorking: allyl chloride is the parent allylic halide; in allylic halides X is on an sp³ carbon next to C=C, so R (sp² carbon) describes vinylic halides and is false.\nKey idea: Allylic C is sp³; vinylic C is sp².","traps":["sp² vs sp³ for allylic carbon: The carbon carrying X is CH₂ (sp³)."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement - I: High concentration of strong nucleophilic reagent with secondary alkyl halides which do not have bulky substituents will follow S_N2 mechanism.\nStatement - II: A secondary alkyl halide when treated with a large excess of ethanol follows S_N1 mechanism.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate from the options given below:","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) assumes 2° halides never go SN1. (C)/(D) think 2° halides never go SN2 or that a strong nucleophile does not matter.\nWorking: with an unhindered 2° halide a high concentration of a strong nucleophile favours bimolecular SN2; a large excess of ethanol (weak nucleophile, polar protic solvent) gives solvolysis by SN1.\nKey idea: For 2° substrates, the nucleophile/solvent decides: strong Nu at high concentration → SN2; weak Nu as solvent (solvolysis) → SN1.","traps":["Secondary halide assigned one fixed mechanism: For 2° halides read the nucleophile and solvent."],"topics":["SN2","SN1"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C09","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The product (C) in the below mentioned reaction is :\nCH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br —[KOH(alc), Δ]→ A —[HBr]→ B —[Δ, KOH(aq)]→ C","options":["Propan-1-ol","Propyne","Propan-2-ol","Propene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student forgets the OH position shifts (or uses peroxide effect). (D): stops at first step or thinks aq. KOH also eliminates. (B): double elimination assumed.\nWorking: alc. KOH eliminates → propene (A); HBr adds Markovnikov → 2-bromopropane (B); aq. KOH substitutes → propan-2-ol (C).\nKey idea: Elimination then Markovnikov addition moves the functional group to the secondary carbon.","traps":["OH placed back on C-1: HBr without peroxide adds Br to C-2.","aq. vs alc. KOH confused: alc. → elimination; aq. → substitution."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"CH₃CH₂Br + NaOH —[C₂H₅OH]→ Product A\nCH₃CH₂Br + NaOH —[H₂O]→ Product B\nThe total number of hydrogen atoms in product A and product B is _________.","type":"num","answer":"10","range":[10.0,10.0],"explain":"Common slips: 12 (both ethanol), 16 (ethoxyethane via Williamson thought for A), 8.\nWorking: in ethanol (alcoholic NaOH) elimination → ethene CH₂=CH₂ (4 H); in water substitution → ethanol C₂H₅OH (6 H); total 10.\nKey idea: Alcoholic medium → elimination; aqueous → substitution.","traps":["Williamson ether assumed in ethanol: Standard answer: alc. alkali → alkene.","Same product in both: Note the solvent."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Identify A and B in the following reaction sequence. Bromobenzene —(Conc HNO₃)→ A —(i) NaOH (ii) HCl→ B","options":["A = nitrobenzene; B = 3-nitrophenol","A = 1-bromo-2,4-dinitrobenzene; B = 4-bromobenzene-1,3-diol (Br kept, OH at the positions of the two NO₂ groups)","A = 2-bromo-1,3,5-trinitrobenzene (picryl bromide); B = 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid)","A = 1-bromo-4-nitrobenzene; B = 2-bromo-5-nitrophenol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) replaces Br by NO₂ (ipso nitration). (B) replaces NO₂ groups by OH instead of Br. (D) substitutes ring H by OH while keeping Br – not a known NaOH reaction.\nWorking: only (C) has B formed by replacement of Br by OH, which is easy when ortho/para NO₂ groups activate the ring (SNAr), then HCl protonates phenoxide. (Realistically conc. HNO₃ alone gives mainly mono-nitration, but (C) is the only chemically consistent option.)\nKey idea: NaOH displaces the halide, not H or NO₂; activated by o/p NO₂.","traps":["Which group NaOH displaces: Nucleophile replaces the leaving group (Br).","Extent of nitration: Pick the option consistent with the second step."],"topics":["Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","Haloarene reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"For the reaction\nRCH₂Br + I⁻ —(Acetone)→ RCH₂I (major) + Br⁻\nThe correct statement is","options":["The solvent used in the reaction solvates the ions formed in rate determining step.","Br⁻ can act as competing nucleophile.","The reaction can occur in acetic acid also.","The transition state formed in the above reaction is less polar than the localised anion."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) carries over the SN1 idea of a solvated ionic intermediate. (B) looks right because Br⁻ is a nucleophile, but it is removed as NaBr precipitate. (C) assumes any polar solvent works; acetic acid is protic and does not precipitate NaBr.\nWorking: Finkelstein SN2 on a 1° bromide. The charge in the [I···C···Br]⁻ transition state is spread over two atoms, so it is less polar than the localised I⁻. (A) no ions are formed in a concerted SN2 step; (B) NaBr precipitates in dry acetone so Br⁻ is removed and does not compete; (C) acetic acid is protic and would solvate I⁻ and dissolve NaBr, so the Finkelstein drive is lost.\nKey idea: SN2 is concerted – no ions are formed in the RDS; the TS charge is delocalised and NaBr precipitation removes Br⁻.","traps":["SN1 solvation language applied to SN2: Ask whether the mechanism forms ions at all – SN2 does not.","Br⁻ as competing nucleophile: Remember NaBr precipitates in dry acetone (Le Chatelier)."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Choose the halogen which is most reactive towards S_N1 reaction in the given compounds (A, B, C & D)\nA. PhCH(Br_(a))CH(Br_(b))CH₃ – Br_(a) benzylic (secondary), Br_(b) on adjacent secondary carbon (1,2-dibromo-1-phenylpropane)\nB. I_(a)–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₂–CH₂–I_(b) (E-1,5-diiodopent-2-ene): I_(a) allylic primary, I_(b) homoallylic primary\nC. Bicyclo[2.2.1]heptane with Br_(a) on a bridgehead carbon and Br_(b) on the adjacent ring carbon which also carries Me (tertiary, non-bridgehead)\nD. (CH₃)₂C(Br_(a))–CH₂–CH₂–Br_(b): Br_(a) tertiary, Br_(b) primary","options":["A − Br_(b); B − I_(a); C − Br_(a); D − Br_(a)","A − Br_(a); B − I_(a); C − Br_(a); D − Br_(a)","A − Br_(b); B − I_(b); C − Br_(b); D − Br_(b)","A − Br_(a); B − I_(a); C − Br_(b); D − Br_(a)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): picks the secondary non-benzylic Br in A and the bridgehead Br in C. (B): only error is C – treats tertiary bridgehead as the best. (C): picks the 'other' halogen everywhere.\nWorking: A benzylic Br_(a) (resonance-stabilised cation); B allylic I_(a); C bridgehead cation cannot become planar (Bredt), so the tertiary non-bridgehead Br_(b) ionises; D tertiary Br_(a).\nKey idea: Bridgehead carbocation cannot be planar, so it is the worst SN1 site despite being tertiary.","traps":["Bridgehead tertiary treated as best: SN1 needs planar cation; bridgehead cannot flatten.","Benzylic vs adjacent secondary: Br_(a) is benzylic → resonance-stabilised cation."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct order of reactivity of following haloarenes towards nucleophilic substitution with aqueous NaOH is: A. chlorobenzene; B. 1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene (Cl with NO₂ ortho); C. 1-chloro-2-methoxybenzene (Cl with OMe ortho); D. 2-chloro-1,3,5-trinitrobenzene (Cl with NO₂ at both ortho positions and para). Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["C > A > D > B","D > B > A > C","A > B > D > C","D > C > B > A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverses the trend (treats EDG as activating as in electrophilic substitution); (C) puts plain chlorobenzene above nitro compounds; (D) ranks the OMe compound above mono-nitro.\nWorking: SNAr is accelerated by o/p electron-withdrawing NO₂ groups (three > one) and slowed by electron-donating OMe; so D > B > A > C.\nKey idea: For nucleophilic attack on the ring, electron-poor rings react faster: more o/p-NO₂ → faster; OMe slows it.","traps":["EAS reasoning used for SNAr: Nucleophiles need an electron-poor ring; EDGs slow SNAr."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"2-Methyl propyl bromide reacts with C₂H₅O⁻ and gives 'A' whereas on reaction with C₂H₅OH it gives 'B'. The mechanism followed in these reactions and the products 'A' and 'B' respectively are:","options":["S_N1, A = tert-butyl ethyl ether; S_N2, B = iso-butyl ethyl ether","S_N1, A = tert-butyl ethyl ether; S_N1, B = 2-butyl ethyl ether","S_N2, A = iso-butyl ethyl ether; S_N1, B = tert-butyl ethyl ether","S_N2, A = 2-butyl ethyl ether; S_N2, B = iso-butyl ethyl ether"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): mechanisms swapped. (B): both S_N1, and 2-butyl product from a wrong (1,2-methyl) shift. (D): 2-butyl ether from S_N2 is impossible (S_N2 never rearranges).\nWorking: (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br is primary; strong nucleophile C₂H₅O⁻ → S_N2 → (CH₃)₂CHCH₂OC₂H₅. With weak nucleophile/polar protic C₂H₅OH, ionisation gives a primary carbocation (or concerted shift) that rearranges by 1,2-H shift to (CH₃)₃C⁺ → (CH₃)₃COC₂H₅ (S_N1).\nKey idea: Strong base/nucleophile → S_N2 without rearrangement; solvolysis → carbocation → 1,2-H shift to tertiary.","traps":["Rearrangement forgotten in solvolysis: Weak nucleophile + protic solvent → think carbocation and 1,2-shift.","Mechanism labels swapped: Anion = strong Nu⁻ = S_N2; neutral solvent = S_N1."],"topics":["SN1","SN2","Carbocation rearrangements"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Identify the incorrect option from the following\n(A) Chlorobenzene + H₃C–CO–Cl →(anhyd AlCl₃) 2-chloroacetophenone (Cl and COCH₃ ortho) + HCl\n(B) Chlorobenzene →(i) NaOH, 623 K, 300 atm; (ii) HCl → phenol\n(C) (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–Br + KOH (alc) → (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–OH + KBr\n(D) CH₃CH₂CH₂–Br + KOH (aq) → CH₃CH₂CH₂–OH + KBr","options":["Friedel-Crafts acetylation of chlorobenzene giving ortho product","Dow process: chlorobenzene to phenol","neopentyl bromide + alc. KOH → neopentyl alcohol","1-bromopropane + aq. KOH → propan-1-ol"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ortho-chloroacetophenone is a real (minor) product; NCERT shows both o and p. (B) and (D) are correct NCERT reactions. Caveat: neopentyl bromide has no β-H, so alc. KOH cannot actually eliminate; the item treats \"alc. KOH gives alcohol\" as the error by convention.\nWorking: alcoholic KOH is the reagent for dehydrohalogenation, while aqueous KOH gives substitution; writing an alcohol as the product of (CH₃)₃C–CH₂–Br with alcoholic KOH is therefore the incorrect statement (C). (A) is a real Friedel–Crafts acylation (o- and p-products), (B) is the Dow process and (D) is normal substitution by aqueous KOH.\nKey idea: Alc. KOH is the elimination reagent; writing an alcohol as its product is the intended error.","traps":["Ortho-only product looks wrong: Ortho product is still formed; look for a clear rule violation.","Alc. vs aq. KOH condition: Alcoholic → elimination; aqueous → substitution."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Haloarene reactions"]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The graph which represents the following reaction is : (C₆H₅)₃C–Cl —[OH⁻, Pyridine]→ (C₆H₅)₃C–OH","options":["rate vs [(C₆H₅)₃C–Cl]: straight line through the origin","rate vs [OH⁻]: straight line through the origin","rate vs [Pyridine]: straight line through the origin","rate vs [(C₆H₅)₃C–Cl]: horizontal line (rate independent)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is SN2 thinking (rate depends on nucleophile). (C) treats pyridine (solvent/base) as a reactant. (D) is zero order in substrate.\nWorking: trityl chloride reacts by SN1 via the very stable triphenylmethyl cation; rate = k[(C₆H₅)₃CCl], first order in substrate, zero order in OH⁻.\nKey idea: Triphenylmethyl cation is extremely stable, so SN1; rate = k[R–Cl].","traps":["SN2 kinetics assumed because OH⁻ is strong nucleophile: Check carbocation stability first: 3° triaryl → SN1.","Pyridine treated as a reactant: Pyridine is the solvent/acid scavenger, not in the rate law."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C04","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Assertion A : Hydrolysis of an alkyl chloride is a slow reaction but in the presence of NaI, the rate of the hydrolysis increases.\nReason R : I⁻ is a good nucleophile as well as a good leaving group.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) R false: thinks a good nucleophile cannot also be a good leaving group. (B) A false: believes NaI just competes and slows hydrolysis. (D) treats R as a true but unrelated fact.\nWorking: I⁻ (good nucleophile) quickly displaces Cl⁻ to form RI; RI then hydrolyses quickly because I⁻ is a good leaving group, regenerating I⁻ (nucleophilic catalysis). R explains A.\nKey idea: Both properties of I⁻ are needed: it enters fast and leaves fast, so it shuttles the substrate to a more reactive RI.","traps":["Good nucleophile vs good leaving group seen as opposites: Large, polarisable I⁻ is both (weak base, high polarisability).","R judged as not explaining A: Write the two steps: RCl + I⁻ → RI; RI + H₂O → ROH + I⁻."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Number of moles of AgCl formed in the following reaction is _____________\n[Substrate: benzene ring with three substituents, 1,3,5: (i) –CH=CH–Cl (vinylic Cl), (ii) –Cl directly on the ring (aryl Cl), (iii) –CH(Cl)–CH₂–C(CH₃)₂Cl (benzylic secondary Cl and a tertiary Cl)] —AgNO₃→ (A) + X AgCl↓","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (tertiary Cl ignored), 3 (vinylic Cl counted as allylic-like), 4 (all Cl counted).\nWorking: only the benzylic 2° C–Cl and the 3° C–Cl ionise (SN1) to give AgCl; vinylic and aryl C–Cl do not react.\nKey idea: AgCl only from Cl that can leave as a stabilised carbocation (3°, benzylic, allylic).","traps":["Vinylic Cl mistaken for allylic: Cl on the sp² carbon = vinylic, unreactive.","Tertiary Cl overlooked: Check every C–Cl: 3° alkyl Cl ionises easily."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Polyhalogen compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"Which of the following is true about freons?","options":["These are radicals of chlorine and chlorine monoxide","These are chemicals causing skin cancer","All radicals are called freons","These are chlorofluorocarbon compounds"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) confuses freons with the Cl·/ClO· radicals they release. (B) confuses cause (ozone depletion → UV → skin cancer) with identity. (C) meaningless generalisation.\nWorking: freons are chlorofluorocarbons of methane and ethane (e.g. CCl₂F₂).\nKey idea: Freon = CFC.","traps":["Effect confused with identity: Ask what the substance is, not what it causes."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The compound which will have the lowest rate towards nucleophilic aromatic substitution on treatment with OH⁻ is","options":["1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene (o-nitrochlorobenzene)","1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene (m-nitrochlorobenzene)","1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene (p-nitrochlorobenzene)","1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the most reactive (two o/p nitro groups) – picked by students who invert the question. (A) and (C) are activated by o/p resonance.\nWorking: o- and p-NO₂ groups stabilise the negative charge of the Meisenheimer intermediate by resonance, so (A) and (C) are activated and (D), with two such groups, is fastest. In 1-chloro-3-nitrobenzene the NO₂ is meta and cannot delocalise the charge, so (B) reacts slowest.\nKey idea: Only o/p –NO₂ can carry the negative charge of the Meisenheimer intermediate; m-NO₂ cannot, so it is slowest.","traps":["Most reactive picked instead of least: Underline 'lowest'; rank all four first.","–I of meta NO₂ assumed to activate strongly: In SNAr the resonance stabilisation of the carbanion matters; meta gives none."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"Identify the correct order for the given property for following compounds.\n(A) Boiling point: CH₃CH₂Cl < CH₃CH₂CH₂Cl < CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Cl\n(B) Density: CH₃CH₂Br < CH₃CH₂Cl < CH₃CH₂I (bromoethane, chloroethane, iodoethane)\n(C) Boiling point: CH₃CH₂Br < CH₃CHBr₂ < CH₃CBr₃\n(D) Density: CH₃CH₂CH(Br)I < CH₃CH₂CH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)Cl (1-bromo-1-iodopropane, 1-bromopropane, 1-bromo-1-chloropropane)\n(E) Boiling point: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Cl > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Cl > (CH₃)₃CCl\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below :","options":["(A), (B) and (E) only","(A), (C) and (D) only","(A), (C) and (E) only","(B), (C) and (D) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is wrong: chloroethane is the least dense of the three ethyl halides (density rises Cl < Br < I), so CH₃CH₂Br < CH₃CH₂Cl is false. (D) is wrong: the true density order is 1-bromopropane < 1-bromo-1-chloropropane < 1-bromo-1-iodopropane, the reverse of the one written. Options (A), (B) and (D) each include (B) or (D).\nWorking: (A) b.p. rises with chain length – true. (B) density rises Cl < Br < I, so the order written is false. (C) b.p. rises with the number of Br atoms – true. (D) the density order written is reversed – false. (E) b.p. falls with branching – true. So (A), (C) and (E) are correct.\nKey idea: For a fixed carbon chain, density follows halogen mass and number of halogens; b.p. follows molar mass and surface area (falls with branching).","traps":["Density trend for haloalkanes: Density rises with heavier halogen and with more halogen atoms (e.g. n-C₃H₇Cl < n-C₃H₇Br < n-C₃H₇I).","Reading skeletal halides: Write each formula before comparing."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The one giving maximum number of isomeric alkenes on dehydrohalogenation reaction is (excluding rearrangement)","options":["2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylpentane","2-Bromopropane","1-Bromo-2-methylbutane","2-Bromopentane"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) looks largest/most branched but its C3 has no H; (C) primary halide has only one β-carbon; (B) symmetric gives one alkene.\nWorking: 2-bromopentane has H on both β-carbons, giving pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene (cis and trans) = 3 alkenes. 2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylpentane (only C1 has β-H) and 1-bromo-2-methylbutane (one β-carbon) each give one alkene; 2-bromopropane gives only propene.\nKey idea: Count β-carbons bearing H, then check each alkene for cis/trans.","traps":["Quaternary β-carbon has no H: Check H on each β-carbon.","Forgetting cis/trans pent-2-ene: Ask for geometrical isomers of each alkene."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I: A. C₆H₅Cl + CH₃Cl —[Na]→ C₆H₅CH₃; B. C₆H₅Cl + 2Na → C₆H₅–C₆H₅; C. C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ —[Cu₂Cl₂]→ C₆H₅Cl + N₂; D. C₂H₅Cl + NaI → C₂H₅I + NaCl. List II: I. Fittig reaction; II. Wurtz Fittig reaction; III. Finkelstein reaction; IV. Sandmeyer reaction. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A - IV, B - II, C - III, D - I","A - II, B - I, C - IV, D - III","A - II, B - I, C - III, D - IV","A - III, B - II, C - IV, D - I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"A: Sandmeyer/Fittig mislabelled. C: Finkelstein and Sandmeyer swapped. D: Finkelstein put on A.\nWorking: A joins an aryl and an alkyl group with Na → Wurtz–Fittig (II); B joins two aryl groups with Na → Fittig (I); C is a diazonium salt with Cu₂Cl₂ → Sandmeyer (IV); D is halide exchange with NaI → Finkelstein (III).\nKey idea: Aryl + alkyl halide/Na = Wurtz-Fittig; aryl + aryl/Na = Fittig; ArN₂⁺/Cu(I) = Sandmeyer; halide exchange with NaI = Finkelstein.","traps":["Fittig vs Wurtz-Fittig: Wurtz-Fittig joins aryl to alkyl; Fittig joins two aryls.","Finkelstein vs Sandmeyer: Sandmeyer starts from diazonium with Cu(I); Finkelstein is halide exchange with NaI."],"topics":["Reactions with metals","Haloarene preparation","Haloalkane preparation"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Decreasing order towards SN1 reaction for the following compounds is: (a) 4-nitrobenzyl chloride; (b) 4-methoxybenzyl chloride; (c) 4-chlorobenzyl chloride; (d) benzyl chloride.","options":["a > c > d > b","d > b > c > a","a > b > c > d","b > d > c > a"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): reverse logic (EWG helps SN1, as in SNAr/SN2 thinking); (B): ranks H above OMe, ignoring +M of OMe.\nWorking: SN1 rate follows benzyl cation stability. p-OCH₃ (+M) stabilises it most, then H; p-Cl (–I > +M) destabilises it slightly and p-NO₂ (–M, –I) most: b > d > c > a.\nKey idea: +M of OMe stabilises the cation most; Cl is net electron-withdrawing (–I > +M); NO₂ strongly destabilises.","traps":["Cl: –I outweighs +M: Halogens deactivate overall; put p-Cl below H.","EWG order reversed: SN1 needs a stable cation: donors speed it, acceptors slow it."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct order of melting points of dichlorobenzenes is","options":["1,4-dichlorobenzene > 1,2-dichlorobenzene > 1,3-dichlorobenzene (p > o > m)","p > m > o","m > p > o","o > m > p"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) para first is right but m > o is wrong: meta melts lowest. (C) and (D) put para below other isomers, against the symmetry rule.\nWorking: m.p. of p-dichlorobenzene ≈ 53 °C, o- ≈ −17 °C, m- ≈ −25 °C, so p > o > m.\nKey idea: Para highest by lattice packing; ortho (≈ −17 °C) is above meta (≈ −25 °C).","traps":["Assuming meta beats ortho by symmetry: Only the para rule is general; for dichlorobenzenes o > m in m.p. — learn the data.","Melting vs boiling point: Melting point = lattice packing (para best); boiling point = dipole/surface."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Polyhalogen compounds","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"In the following halogenated organic compounds, the one with the maximum number of chlorine atoms in its structure is:","options":["Gammaxene","Chloropicrin","Chloral","Freon-12"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) both CCl₃ – tempting as 'tri-chloro' compounds. (D) Freon-12 has only 2 Cl.\nWorking: gammaxene C₆H₆Cl₆ – 6 Cl; chloropicrin CCl₃NO₂ – 3; chloral CCl₃CHO – 3; Freon-12 CCl₂F₂ – 2.\nKey idea: Gammaxene is γ-hexachlorocyclohexane (BHC/lindane), C₆H₆Cl₆.","traps":["Trivial names unknown: Gammaxene = benzene hexachloride C₆H₆Cl₆."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry of substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Most stable product of the following reaction is: substrate 3-methylpentan-2-ol drawn as a zig-zag with OH on a wedge at C2 and the ethyl group on a wedge at C3; reagents (i) p-H₃C–C₆H₄–SO₂Cl (TsCl), pyridine; (ii) NaCN, DMF.","options":["2-cyano product drawn in the same orientation as the substrate: CN wedge at C2, ethyl wedge at C3 (both centres retained)","drawing flipped left–right: ethyl on hash at C3, CN on wedge at C2 (equivalent to CN hash at C2, ethyl wedge at C3 – inversion at C2 only)","flipped drawing: CN wedge at C2, ethyl wedge at C3 (equivalent to inversion at both centres, i.e. enantiomer of A)","same orientation as substrate: CN hash at C2, ethyl hash at C3 (inversion at both centres)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) assumes overall retention (forgets SN2 inversion, or counts tosylation as an inversion too); (D) inverts both centres; (C) is the flipped drawing of the doubly inverted product – catches students who only check wedge/hash at the CN carbon.\nWorking: tosylation keeps C–O intact (retention); CN⁻ in DMF does SN2 at C2 with inversion; C3 is untouched. Rotating (B) 180° about the vertical in-plane axis gives CN hash at C2 and ethyl wedge at C3 = inversion at C2 only.\nKey idea: TsCl: retention; SN2: inversion at the reacting carbon only; flipping a drawing in the page plane swaps wedge and hash.","traps":["Tosylation does not invert: Only the C–O–S bond forms in step (i); the C–O bond is not broken.","Inverting the untouched centre: Only the carbon attacked by CN⁻ inverts."],"topics":["Stereochemistry of substitution","SN2"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"A sample of 4.5 mg of an unknown monohydric alcohol, R–OH was added to methylmagnesium iodide. A gas is evolved and is collected and its volume measured to be 3.1 mL. The molecular weight of the unknown alcohol is ____ g/mol. [Nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"33","range":[33.0,33.0],"explain":"Common slips: 16 or 66 (assuming 2 : 1 or 1 : 2 gas ratio); 32 (taking methanol by guess and rounding down); errors of 10³ from mg/mL.\nWorking: ROH + CH₃MgI → CH₄ + ROMgI (1 mol CH₄ per mol ROH). At STP, n(CH₄) = 3.1/22400 = 1.384 × 10⁻⁴ mol (or 3.1/22700 = 1.366 × 10⁻⁴); M = 4.5 × 10⁻³/n = 32.5 (or 32.9) ≈ 33. STP is not stated; both molar volumes give 33.\nKey idea: One acidic H per monohydric alcohol → one CH₄.","traps":["Gas identity / ratio: CH₃MgI + ROH gives one CH₄ per OH.","Guessing methanol: Report the computed nearest integer (32.5–32.9 → 33)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"A 100 mL solution of CH₃CH₂MgBr on treatment with methanol produces 2.24 mL of a gas at STP. The weight of gas produced is _______ mg. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: Taking the gas as CH₄ gives 1.6 ≈ 2 mg; using 22.7 L gives 2.96 ≈ 3 anyway; mL/L slip gives 3000.\nWorking: CH₃CH₂MgBr + CH₃OH → C₂H₆ + Mg(OCH₃)Br; 2.24 mL/22400 mL = 1 × 10⁻⁴ mol C₂H₆ × 30 g mol⁻¹ = 3 × 10⁻³ g = 3 mg.\nKey idea: Alkyl of the Grignard becomes the alkane: C₂H₆, 30 g mol⁻¹.","traps":["Which alkane forms: R of RMgX becomes R–H.","mL vs L: 2.24 mL = 10⁻⁴ mol."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloalkane preparation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"4-(Hydroxymethyl)phenol (p-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂OH) + HCl, Δ → A; A + NaI → B. In the above reaction product B is:","options":["4-(iodomethyl)phenol (p-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂I)","1-iodo-4-(iodomethyl)benzene (p-I–C₆H₄–CH₂I)","4-(chloromethyl)phenol (p-HO–C₆H₄–CH₂Cl)","1-(chloromethyl)-4-iodobenzene (p-I–C₆H₄–CH₂Cl)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D): assume phenolic OH is also replaced by halogen; (C): stops after the first step (ignores NaI).\nWorking: HCl replaces only the benzylic alcohol OH (phenolic C–O has partial double-bond character) → p-HOC₆H₄CH₂Cl; NaI (Finkelstein) exchanges the benzylic Cl → p-HOC₆H₄CH₂I.\nKey idea: Aryl C–O and aryl C–X bonds resist substitution; benzylic ones react easily.","traps":["Phenolic OH replaced by halogen: Phenol C–O has partial double-bond character; no SN reaction.","Second step ignored: NaI exchanges benzylic Cl to I."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Identify the correct statement for the below given transformation. CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–CH(N⁺(CH₃)₃)–CH₃ —[C₂H₅ONa, C₂H₅OH]→ A (Major) + B (Minor)","options":["A – CH₃CH₂CH=CH–CH₃, B – CH₃CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂, Saytzeff products","A – CH₃CH₂CH=CH–CH₃, B – CH₃CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂, Hofmann products","A – CH₃CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂, B – CH₃CH₂CH=CHCH₃, Hofmann products","A – CH₃CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂, B – CH₃CH₂CH=CHCH₃, Saytzeff products"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) applies Zaitsev blindly; (B) right alkenes for Zaitsev but wrong label; (D) right structures but wrong label.\nWorking: E2 from a quaternary ammonium ion (bulky, poor leaving group) removes the more accessible, more acidic CH₃ hydrogen, so pent-1-ene (Hofmann alkene) is major and pent-2-ene minor.\nKey idea: –N⁺R₃ leaving groups favour the less substituted (Hofmann) alkene.","traps":["Zaitsev reflex: Check the leaving group: bulky charged –NMe₃⁺ → Hofmann.","Label vs structure mismatch: Less substituted alkene = Hofmann product."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"'A' (Major Product) ←[AgCN, C₂H₅OH–H₂O]— CH₃CH₂Cl —[NaCN, C₂H₅OH–H₂O]→ 'B' (Major Product). Considering the above reactions, the compound 'A' and compound 'B' respectively are :","options":["CH₃CH₂–N⁺≡C⁻, CH₃CH₂–N⁺≡C⁻","CH₃CH₂–C≡N, CH₃CH₂–C≡N","CH₃CH₂–N⁺≡C⁻, CH₃CH₂–C≡N","CH₃CH₂–C≡N, CH₃CH₂–N⁺≡C⁻"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) same product for both; (D) reversed assignment.\nWorking: AgCN is mainly covalent, so attack is through N → ethyl isocyanide (A); NaCN is ionic, attack through the more nucleophilic C → propanenitrile (B).\nKey idea: Covalent AgCN → R–NC; ionic NaCN/KCN → R–CN.","traps":["AgCN/KCN swapped: Ag–C bond covalent → only N lone pair free."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C14","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Compound 'A' undergoes following sequence of reactions to give compound 'B'. The correct structure and chirality of compound 'B' is: [where Et is –C₂H₅] Compound A = 2-bromo-3-methylbutane, (CH₃)₂CH–CH(Br)–CH₃; A —(i) Mg, Et₂O; (ii) D₂O→ B.","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–CHD–CH₃ (2-deuterio-3-methylbutane), Achiral","(CH₃)₂CH–CH(OD)–CH₃, Chiral","(CH₃)₂CH–CHD–CH₃ (2-deuterio-3-methylbutane), Chiral","isopropyl-substituted cyclopropane bearing OD, Achiral"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) right structure but treats H and D as same; (B) assumes alcohol formation; (D) absurd cyclisation.\nWorking: RMgBr + D₂O → R–D. In (CH₃)₂CH–CHD–CH₃ the C2 carries H, D, CH₃ and isopropyl – four different groups, so the molecule is chiral (formed as a racemate, but each molecule is chiral).\nKey idea: H and D are different substituents → C2 is a stereocentre.","traps":["H and D treated as identical: Isotopes count as different groups for chirality.","Alcohol expected from D₂O: RMgX + D₂O → RD + Mg(OD)X."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C29","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"[Reaction: 4-chloro-3-methylpent-1-ene, CH₃–CHCl–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH₂, treated with NaOH/H₂O → P (major product)]. Consider the above reaction. The number of π electrons present in the product 'P' is ____.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 – elimination to a conjugated diene assumed (alc. KOH product); 0 – thinking OH adds across C=C; 6 – counting O lone pairs as π electrons.\nWorking: aqueous NaOH substitutes Cl by OH (via the secondary cation, which can undergo 1,2-H shift to a tertiary allylic cation; either way the product is an unsaturated alcohol such as 3-methylpent-1-en-3-ol / 3-methylpent-2-en-1-ol). One C=C remains → 2 π electrons.\nKey idea: Aqueous NaOH → substitution (alcohol); the C=C (2 π electrons) survives any shift.","traps":["Elimination product assumed: Aqueous NaOH favours substitution; alcoholic KOH gives elimination.","Oxygen lone pairs counted: Count only electrons in π bonds (C=C)."],"topics":["SN1","Carbocation rearrangements"]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The major product in the reaction (CH₃)₃C–Cl + K⁺ ⁻O–C(CH₃)₃ → ? is","options":["t-Butyl ethyl ether","2,2-Dimethyl butane","2-Methyl pent-1-ene","2-Methyl prop-1-ene"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Williamson thinking (and even the wrong alkyl group); (B), (C) carbon-skeleton coupling products that cannot form.\nWorking: a tertiary halide with a bulky strong base (t-BuO⁻) undergoes E2 elimination only; the product is (CH₃)₂C=CH₂, 2-methylprop-1-ene. No ether forms.\nKey idea: 3° halide + alkoxide → alkene only.","traps":["Williamson ether expected: Tertiary halide → elimination only."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry of substitution","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nCompound shown below —[PhS⁻Na⁺, DMF]→","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – assumes SNAr at Ar–F, but NO₂ is meta to F; (C) – retention (SN1-type or ignoring inversion); (D) – double substitution.\nWorking: PhS⁻ is a strong, soft nucleophile in polar aprotic DMF → SN2 at the secondary benzylic C–I with Walden inversion. Ring F is meta to NO₂, so it is not activated for SNAr, and Ar–F is not displaced → (A).\nKey idea: Check where NO₂ sits relative to F: meta gives no activation, so only the alkyl iodide reacts – by SN2 with inversion.","traps":["Retention vs inversion: Strong nucleophile + aprotic solvent → SN2 → inversion.","SNAr assumed at Ar–F: SNAr needs the EWG ortho/para to the halogen."],"img":["img/jee/JM22Jun27S1C15-s.webp","img/jee/JM22Jun27S1C15-o.webp"],"lettered":true,"topics":["Stereochemistry of substitution","SN2","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution"]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which one of the following compounds is inactive towards SN1 reaction?","options":["(CH₃)₃C–Cl (tert-butyl chloride)","CH₂=CH–CH₂–Cl (allyl chloride)","1-chlorobicyclo[2.2.1]heptane (Cl on the bridgehead carbon of norbornane)","C₆H₅–CH(CH₃)–Cl (1-chloro-1-phenylethane)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) allyl chloride looks primary, so may be thought inactive; (C) looks tertiary, so is easily thought reactive.\nWorking: a bridgehead carbocation in norbornane cannot become planar (Bredt-type strain), so SN1 does not occur; tert-butyl, allyl and benzylic chlorides all form stabilised cations.\nKey idea: A carbocation must be planar sp²; rigid bridgehead positions cannot flatten.","traps":["Tertiary assumed reactive: Check whether the cation can become planar.","Allyl chloride treated as primary: Allylic cation is resonance-stabilised."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"2-Bromophenol —[(CH₃)₃CLi]→ (A) —[(i) CO₂, (ii) H₃O⁺]→ 2-hydroxybenzoic acid (salicylic acid). In the given conversion the compound A is:","options":["2-lithiophenol (Li ortho to OH)","dilithium species: Li ortho to OLi (2-lithiophenoxide lithium salt)","1-bromo-2-tert-butoxybenzene (Br ortho to OC(CH₃)₃)","2-tert-butylphenol (C(CH₃)₃ ortho to OH)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ignores that acidic OH is deprotonated first by RLi; (C) treats t-BuLi as an alkoxide/alkylating agent; (D) treats t-BuLi as an alkyl nucleophile on the ring.\nWorking: t-BuLi, a strong base, first deprotonates the phenolic OH (→ OLi), then a second equivalent does Br–Li exchange to give the aryllithium; CO₂ then H₃O⁺ gives salicylic acid.\nKey idea: Organometallics destroy acidic O–H first; the phenoxide survives as OLi until H₃O⁺ work-up.","traps":["Acidic OH overlooked: RLi deprotonates OH before anything else.","t-BuLi as alkylating agent: t-BuLi acts as base / Li donor, not t-Bu donor."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Two isomers (A) and (B) with molar mass 184 g/mol and elemental composition C, 52.2%; H, 4.9% and Br 42.9% gave benzoic acid and p-bromobenzoic acid, respectively on oxidation with KMnO₄. Isomer 'A' is optically active and gives a pale yellow precipitate when warmed with alcoholic AgNO₃. Isomer 'A' and 'B' are, respectively:","options":["H₃C–CHBr–C₆H₅ and 2-methylbenzyl bromide (o-CH₃C₆H₄CH₂Br)","2-methylbenzyl bromide (o-CH₃C₆H₄CH₂Br) and 1-bromo-4-ethylbenzene (p-BrC₆H₄CH₂CH₃)","H₃C–CHBr–C₆H₅ and 1-bromo-4-ethylbenzene (p-BrC₆H₄CH₂CH₃)","1-bromo-4-ethylbenzene (p-BrC₆H₄CH₂CH₃) and H₃C–CHBr–C₆H₅"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): 2-methylbenzyl bromide oxidises to phthalic acid, not benzoic or p-bromobenzoic acid, and is achiral; (D) swaps A and B (the aryl bromide is achiral and inert to AgNO₃).\nWorking: C 96/12 = 8, H 9, Br 79 → C₈H₉Br. A: oxidises to benzoic acid (monosubstituted ring), chiral, reactive benzylic C–Br (AgBr) → 1-bromo-1-phenylethane. B: gives p-bromobenzoic acid → ring Br para to an alkyl side chain → 4-bromoethylbenzene.\nKey idea: Side-chain oxidation keeps only ring substituents; aryl C–Br survives, benzylic C–Br reacts with AgNO₃.","traps":["Order of A and B: A must be chiral and react with AgNO₃.","Oxidation product of o-xylyl bromide: Two side chains → dicarboxylic acid."],"topics":["SN1","Haloarene reactions"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S1C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloarene reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Among the following compounds I–IV, which one forms a yellow precipitate on reacting sequentially with (i) NaOH (ii) dil. HNO₃ (iii) AgNO₃? I: 4-chlorotoluene (p-CH₃C₆H₄Cl); II: 3-chlorotoluene (m-CH₃C₆H₄Cl); III: 2-bromotoluene (o-CH₃C₆H₄Br); IV: 2-chlorobenzyl iodide (o-ClC₆H₄CH₂I).","options":["II","IV","I","III"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) aryl chlorides – no reaction; (D) aryl bromide – tempting because AgBr is pale yellow, but no Br⁻ is released.\nWorking: only IV has a halogen on an sp³ (benzylic) carbon; NaOH hydrolyses C–I to give I⁻, which after acidification gives yellow AgI. The aryl C–Cl/C–Br bonds in I–III (and in IV) are inert to aqueous NaOH.\nKey idea: Halide ion is freed only from sp³ C–X; aryl C–X survives NaOH.","traps":["Only the benzylic C–I hydrolyses: Aryl C–X does not react with aqueous NaOH; benzylic CH₂–I does.","Yellow ppt = AgI: AgCl white, AgBr pale yellow, AgI yellow."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Polyhalogen compounds","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The gas 'A' is having very low reactivity reaches to stratosphere. It is non-toxic and non-flammable but dissociated by UV-radiations in stratosphere. The intermediates formed initially from the gas 'A' are:","options":["ClO• + •CF₂Cl","ClO• + •CH₃","•CH₃ + •CF₂Cl","Cl• + •CF₂Cl"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) ClO• – formed in the second step (Cl• + O₃), not initially; (B) and (C) •CH₃ – no C–H bonds in CF₂Cl₂.\nWorking: gas A is a CFC such as freon-12, CF₂Cl₂; UV homolyses the weakest C–Cl bond: CF₂Cl₂ → Cl• + •CF₂Cl. ClO• forms only later when Cl• attacks O₃.\nKey idea: UV breaks the weakest bond (C–Cl), giving Cl• and the rest of the molecule as a radical.","traps":["ClO• taken as first radical: ClO• needs O₃; the first step is pure photolysis of CFC.","CH₃ radical assumed: Freon-12 is CF₂Cl₂ – no hydrogen."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"In the following sequence of reactions the P is: chlorocyclopentane + Mg —dry ether→ [A] —ethanol→ P (Major Product)","options":["cyclopentane","ethoxycyclopentane (cyclopentyl ethyl ether)","ethylcyclopentane","cyclopentene"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ether – assumes ethoxide substitution; (C) ethyl transfer – treats ethanol as an electrophile; (D) alkene – assumes elimination.\nWorking: A is cyclopentylmagnesium chloride; ethanol is a proton source and protonates the Grignard reagent to cyclopentane (with Mg(OEt)Cl).\nKey idea: RMgX + R′OH → RH; the alcohol only supplies H⁺.","traps":["Ethanol as electrophile: Acidic O–H reacts first and destroys RMgX."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The major product (A) formed in the reaction given below is: CH₃–CH₂–CH(C₆H₅)–CH₂–Br (1-bromo-2-phenylbutane) + CH₃O⁻ / CH₃OH → A (Major product)","options":["CH₃–CH₂–CH(C₆H₄-p-OCH₃)–CH₂Br (ring methoxylated at para position)","CH₃–CH₂–C(C₆H₅)=CH₂ (2-phenylbut-1-ene)","CH₃–CH₂–CH(C₆H₅)–CH₂–OH","CH₃–CH₂–CH(C₆H₅)–CH₂–OCH₃"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) SN2 product – the reflex answer for a 1° halide with CH₃O⁻; (C) assumes hydrolysis though no water/OH⁻ is present; (A) treats methoxide as attacking the ring (no SNAr on unactivated benzene).\nWorking: methoxide is a strong base; the β-H on C-2 is benzylic (acidified by the phenyl) and the primary carbon is β-branched (hindered for SN2), so E2 dominates, giving the conjugated alkene CH₂=C(Ph)CH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: Benzylic β-H + strong base + β-branching → E2 to the conjugated styrene-type alkene.","traps":["Primary halide reflex → SN2: Check β-H acidity and β-branching; benzylic β-H favours E2.","Methoxide treated as hydroxide: No water or OH⁻ present – OH product impossible."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The correct order of reactivity of the given chlorides with acetate in acetic acid is: [four chlorides drawn on a cyclohexene ring] P = 3-chloro-1-methylcyclohex-1-ene (Cl on the allylic carbon, CH₃ on the far alkene carbon); Q = 3-chloro-4-methylcyclohex-1-ene (allylic Cl, CH₃ on the neighbouring sp³ carbon); R = 5-chloro-3-methylcyclohex-1-ene (Cl on a homoallylic, non-allylic secondary carbon; CH₃ on the allylic carbon); S = 4-(chloromethyl)cyclohex-1-ene (primary CH₂Cl).","options":["P (3-chloro-1-methylcyclohexene) > Q (3-chloro-4-methylcyclohexene) > R (non-allylic secondary chloride with CH₃) > S (CH₂Cl, primary)","S (CH₂Cl) > Q (3-chloro-4-methylcyclohexene) > P (3-chloro-1-methylcyclohexene) > R (non-allylic secondary chloride)","P (3-chloro-1-methylcyclohexene) > S (CH₂Cl) > R (non-allylic secondary chloride) > Q (3-chloro-4-methylcyclohexene)","Q (3-chloro-4-methylcyclohexene) > P (3-chloro-1-methylcyclohexene) > S (CH₂Cl) > R (non-allylic secondary chloride)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) puts CH₂Cl first – SN2 thinking (least hindered); (C) places primary CH₂Cl above two secondary chlorides – ignores cation stability; (D) swaps P and Q – misses that CH₃ on the alkene carbon makes the resonance form tertiary.\nWorking: acetate in acetic acid (weak nucleophile, polar protic solvent) means SN1 solvolysis, so rate follows carbocation stability. P ionises to an allylic cation whose other end is tertiary (CH₃ on that carbon) – most stable; Q gives a secondary allylic cation with only an extra alkyl neighbour; R gives a plain secondary (non-allylic) cation; S would give a primary cation – slowest. Order P > Q > R > S = option (A).\nKey idea: Draw the allylic resonance form: a CH₃ on the far alkene carbon makes one contributor tertiary, so that chloride ionises fastest.","traps":["Solvolysis taken as SN2: Weak nucleophile in acetic acid → SN1, rank by cation stability.","Methyl position on allylic cation: Check whether CH₃ sits on a resonance terminus (makes it 3°)."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 3-chloro-4-methylcyclopentene (Cl on the allylic ring carbon next to the C=C, CH₃ on the adjacent sp³ ring carbon) —NaOH, C₂H₅OH→ Major Product","options":["4-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-ol (allylic-shifted alcohol, OH and CH₃ 1,3 across the ring)","1-methylcyclopent-3-en-1-ol (tertiary alcohol)","5-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-ol (direct substitution product: OH on the former C–Cl carbon, CH₃ on the adjacent carbon)","1-methylcyclopenta-1,3-diene (conjugated diene, CH₃ on a ring double-bond carbon next to the CH₂)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) direct SN2/SN1 alcohol; (A) allylic-rearranged alcohol (SN1′); (B) tertiary alcohol that would need hydride shift and ignores elimination.\nWorking: alcoholic NaOH favours elimination; loss of HCl using the H on the CH₃-bearing carbon gives a conjugated (and more substituted) diene, 1-methylcyclopenta-1,3-diene.\nKey idea: Alcoholic base → elimination; conjugated diene with more substituted C=C is the Zaitsev product.","traps":["Aqueous vs alcoholic NaOH: Alcoholic medium favours elimination.","Allylic cation rearrangement: Rearranged alcohols need aqueous SN1; here the stabilised conjugated diene wins."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"For the following: Benzene —1. Br₂/Fe/Δ; 2. Mg/dry ether; 3. CH₃OH→ Products","options":["Anisole (C₆H₅OCH₃) + HMgBr","Benzene (C₆H₅–H) + Mg(OCH₃)Br","Toluene (C₆H₅CH₃) + Mg(OH)Br","Bromobenzene (C₆H₅Br) + Mg(H)(OCH₃)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats CH₃OH as a methoxylating agent; (C) treats it as a methylating agent; (D) reverses the reaction.\nWorking: Br₂/Fe gives bromobenzene; Mg/ether gives PhMgBr; methanol protonates the Grignard reagent: PhMgBr + CH₃OH → C₆H₆ + Mg(OCH₃)Br.\nKey idea: R–MgX + H–OR′ → R–H + Mg(OR′)X.","traps":["Methanol as methylating agent: Grignard is a strong base; it takes the O–H proton."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"The product formed in the first step of the reaction of CH₃–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₃ (2,4-dibromohexane) with excess Mg/Et₂O (Et = C₂H₅) is:","options":["Dimer: CH₃–CH₂–CH–CH₂–CH–CH₃ drawn over CH₃–CH–CH₂–CH–CH₂–CH₃ (second chain reversed), joined by two vertical C–C bonds – upper C-3 to lower C-5 and upper C-5 to lower C-3 (head-to-tail Wurtz-type coupled product)","Two CH₃–CH₂–CH–CH₂–CH–CH₃ chains joined by two vertical C–C bonds at C-3 and C-5 (head-to-head coupled dimer)","CH₃–CH< bonded into a three-membered ring with CH₂ and CH–CH₃ (1,2-dimethylcyclopropane as drawn)","CH₃CH₂–CH(MgBr)–CH₂–CH(MgBr)–CH₃ (bis-Grignard reagent)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – Wurtz-type dimers (that needs Na, not Mg, and is not a first step); (C) – intramolecular cyclisation (a later step for 1,3-dihalides, and the drawn ring has the wrong carbon count).\nWorking: with excess Mg in dry ether each C–Br inserts Mg, so the first-formed species is the di-Grignard reagent. 1,3-Dihalides can later close to a cyclopropane, but option (C) as drawn has only five carbons (1,2-dimethylcyclopropane), not the C₆ ring product (1-ethyl-2-methylcyclopropane), so it cannot be the product from this substrate anyway.\nKey idea: Excess Mg + each C–X → each C–MgX; 'first step' rules out follow-up coupling or ring closure.","traps":["Wurtz coupling assumed: Wurtz uses Na; Mg in ether gives RMgX.","Cyclisation taken as first step: Read 'first step' and 'excess Mg'; check carbon count of the ring option."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The correct order of the following compounds showing increasing tendency towards nucleophilic substitution reaction is: (i) chlorobenzene; (ii) 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene; (iii) 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene; (iv) 1-chloro-2,4,6-trinitrobenzene (picryl chloride).","options":["(iv) < (iii) < (ii) < (i)","(iv) < (i) < (ii) < (iii)","(iv) < (i) < (iii) < (ii)","(i) < (ii) < (iii) < (iv)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) reverse order (sign/direction slip); (B) and (C) misplace (iv) as least reactive by treating NO₂ as deactivating, as in electrophilic substitution.\nWorking: each o/p-NO₂ stabilises the Meisenheimer carbanion, so reactivity rises with the number of o/p nitro groups: (i) < (ii) < (iii) < (iv).\nKey idea: NO₂ deactivates the ring to electrophiles but activates it to nucleophiles.","traps":["Treating NO₂ as deactivating here: Ask whether the attacking species is a nucleophile.","Increasing vs decreasing: Read \"increasing\" and start from the least reactive."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloalkane preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"For the given reaction: 3-ethylbenzonitrile (CH₂CH₃ and CN meta on benzene) —Br₂, UV light→ 'A' (major product, monobrominated). What is 'A'?","options":["2-bromo-3-ethylbenzonitrile (ring Br between the ethyl and CN groups)","2-bromo-5-ethylbenzonitrile (ring Br para to ethyl, ortho to CN)","3-(1-bromoethyl)benzonitrile (side chain –CH(Br)CH₃, meta to CN)","3-bromo-5-ethylbenzonitrile (ring Br meta to both ethyl and CN)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(D) ring bromination – needs FeBr₃ (electrophilic), not UV; primary (–CH₂CH₂Br) would be minor.\nWorking: Br₂/hν brominates by a free-radical path at the benzylic carbon (most stable radical); ring substitution needs a Lewis acid.\nKey idea: UV/heat → side chain; Lewis acid/dark → ring.","traps":["Ring substitution chosen: UV light signals a radical chain at the benzylic C–H."],"topics":["Haloalkane preparation","Haloarene preparation"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocation rearrangements","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"In the given reaction 3-Bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane —(C₂H₅OH)→ 'A' (Major Product). Product A is:","options":["2-Ethoxy-3,3-dimethylbutane","1-Ethoxy-3,3-dimethylbutane","2-Ethoxy-2,3-dimethylbutane","2-Hydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the unrearranged SN1/SN2 product; (B) wrong carbon; (D) assumes water as nucleophile instead of ethanol.\nWorking: in ethanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile) the secondary bromide ionises (SN1); the 2° carbocation undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift to the 3° cation (CH₃)₂C⁺–CH(CH₃)₂, which is trapped by ethanol to give (CH₃)₂C(OEt)–CH(CH₃)₂, 2-ethoxy-2,3-dimethylbutane.\nKey idea: Secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon → methyl shift to the tertiary cation before solvent attack.","traps":["Rearrangement missed: Always check if a 1,2-shift gives a more stable cation.","Solvent as nucleophile: Solvolysis in EtOH gives the ethyl ether."],"topics":["Carbocation rearrangements","SN1"]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C16","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Haloalkane preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"1-Allyl-4-bromobenzene (p-Br–C₆H₄–CH₂–CH=CH₂) —(i) (C₆H₅CO)₂O₂, HBr (ii) CoF₂→ P (major product). Major product P of above reaction, is :","options":["p-Br–C₆H₄–CHF–CH=CH₂","p-F–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂CH₂F","p-Br–C₆H₄–CH₂–CHF–CH₃","p-Br–C₆H₄–CH₂CH₂CH₂F"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) Markovnikov addition; (B) exchanges the aryl Br too; (A) allylic substitution (as if NBS-type).\nWorking: HBr with benzoyl peroxide adds anti-Markovnikov → ArCH₂CH₂CH₂Br; CoF₂ (Swarts) exchanges the alkyl Br for F; the aryl Br is not exchanged.\nKey idea: Peroxide → 1-bromo (terminal); Swarts acts only on alkyl halides.","traps":["Markovnikov product: Peroxide + HBr → anti-Markovnikov (free radical).","Aryl halide exchanged: Aryl C–Br has partial double-bond character and does not undergo exchange."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Physical properties","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct decreasing order of densities of the following compounds is:\n(A) benzene; (B) chlorobenzene; (C) 1,3-dichlorobenzene (Cl at C-1 and C-3); (D) 1-bromo-3-chlorobenzene (Br at C-1, Cl at C-3)","options":["(D) > (C) > (B) > (A)","(C) > (D) > (A) > (B)","(C) > (B) > (A) > (D)","(A) > (B) > (C) > (D)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option D is the full reversal; options B and C misplace the bromo compound (Br heavier than Cl).\nWorking: density rises with number and atomic mass of halogens: C₆H₄BrCl (~1.63) > C₆H₄Cl₂ (~1.29) > C₆H₅Cl (~1.11) > C₆H₆ (~0.88).\nKey idea: More/heavier halogen → denser.","traps":["Reversed order: Note \"decreasing\" in the stem."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloalkane preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Methyl 3-hydroxy-2-methylidenebutanoate (shown below) is treated with conc. HBr to give \"P\" (major product). Consider the above reaction, the major product \"P\" formed is:","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) direct substitution without rearrangement – the trap for those who ignore the cation's resonance; (C) nonsense O–Br product; (D) assumes HBr also converts the ester to acyl bromide.\nWorking: protonated OH leaves to give a secondary allylic cation delocalised onto the terminal CH₂; Br⁻ attacks the unhindered primary end (SN1′), giving the more substituted C=C conjugated with C=O.\nKey idea: Allylic cation reacts at the end that gives the more stable (conjugated, more substituted) alkene.","traps":["Allylic cation has two reactive ends: Draw both resonance forms and pick the product with conjugated C=C.","Ester survives conc. HBr: HBr substitutes alcohol OH, not the ester OCH₃ under these conditions."],"img":["img/jee/JM21Jul27S2C02-s.webp","img/jee/JM21Jul27S2C02-o.webp"],"lettered":true,"topics":["Haloalkane preparation","Carbocation rearrangements"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol —[20% H₃PO₄, 358 K]→ \"A\" (major product); 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane —[(CH₃)₃CO⁻K⁺]→ \"B\" (major product). The products \"A\" and \"B\" formed in above reactions are:","options":["A – methylenecyclohexane; B – methylenecyclohexane","A – 1-methylcyclohexene; B – 1-methylcyclohexene","A – 1-methylcyclohexene; B – methylenecyclohexane","A – methylenecyclohexane; B – 1-methylcyclohexene"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) – applies Saytzeff to both; (A) – thinks the exocyclic alkene forms in both; (D) – reverses the two.\nWorking: acid-catalysed E1 dehydration gives the more substituted (Saytzeff) endocyclic alkene; the bulky tert-butoxide removes the less hindered CH₃ hydrogen (E2, Hofmann), giving the exocyclic alkene.\nKey idea: Small base/acid → more substituted alkene; bulky t-BuO⁻ → less hindered (Hofmann) alkene.","traps":["Saytzeff applied to bulky base: t-BuOK abstracts the most accessible H → exocyclic C=C.","Endo vs exo alkene drawn: Check where the C=C is: in ring (CH₃ shown) or exocyclic (=CH₂)."],"topics":["Elimination & Saytzeff","Substitution vs elimination"]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Haloalkane preparation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Cyclohexene –[A]→ 3,6-dichlorocyclohexene (ring with one C=C and a Cl on each of the two allylic carbons). Identify the reagent(s) 'A' and condition(s) for the reaction:","options":["A = HCl; Anhydrous AlCl₃","A = HCl, ZnCl₂","A = Cl₂; UV light","A = Cl₂; dark, Anhydrous AlCl₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) HCl adds across C=C (chlorocyclohexane), no dichloride, double bond lost; (D) Cl₂ in the dark adds ionically to give trans-1,2-dichlorocyclohexane; AlCl₃ is for aromatic substitution.\nWorking: the C=C survives and Cl appears on both allylic carbons, so this is free-radical allylic substitution (Cl₂, hν, low Cl₂ concentration/high temperature); HCl would add across the double bond and Cl₂ in the dark gives the vicinal dichloride.\nKey idea: Double bond retained + Cl on allylic C → radical (hν) substitution.","traps":["Electrophilic addition chosen: Check whether the product still has the C=C.","Product position ignored: 1,2-dichloride = addition; allylic dichloride = substitution."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Haloarene reactions","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"C₆H₅Cl + NaOH → C₆H₅O⁻Na⁺. The above reaction requires which of the following reaction conditions?","options":["573 K, Cu, 300 atm","623 K, Cu, 300 atm","573 K, 300 atm","623 K, 300 atm"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) – Cu catalyst borrowed from aniline-from-chlorobenzene conditions; (C) – wrong temperature.\nWorking: NCERT (Dow process) – chlorobenzene with aqueous NaOH at 623 K and 300 atm gives sodium phenoxide; no Cu catalyst is needed (Cu is used for ammonolysis of haloarenes).\nKey idea: Dow process: 623 K, 300 atm, no catalyst.","traps":["Cu catalyst added: Cu only for NH₃ substitution.","Temperature misremembered: Memorise 623 K."],"topics":["Haloarene reactions","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution"]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The correct pair(s) of the ambident nucleophiles is (are) :\n(A) AgCN/KCN\n(B) RCOOAg/RCOOK\n(C) AgNO₂/KNO₂\n(D) AgI/KI","options":["(B) and (C) only","(A) only","(A) and (C) only","(B) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): carboxylate has two O atoms but they are equivalent by resonance, so it gives only the ester – not ambident. (B) omits nitrite.\nWorking: CN⁻ (attack through C or N) and NO₂⁻ (attack through N or O) are ambident; the AgX/KX pairs are the classic way of showing the two modes (isocyanide/nitroalkane with Ag salts, cyanide/nitrite with K salts). RCOO⁻ attacks only through O (the two O atoms are equivalent), and I⁻ has one site.\nKey idea: Ambident = two DIFFERENT donor atoms (C/N in CN⁻, N/O in NO₂⁻).","traps":["Carboxylate thought ambident: Ambident needs two different atoms giving different products.","Nitrite forgotten: Recall the nitrite/nitro pair: KNO₂ → R–ONO, AgNO₂ → R–NO₂."]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : C₂H₅OH and AgCN both can generate nucleophile.\nStatement II : KCN and AgCN both will generate nitrile nucleophile with all reaction conditions.\nChoose the most appropriate option :","options":["Statement I is true but statement II is false","Both statement I and statement II are true","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both statement I and statement II are false"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) for students who miss AgCN → isocyanide; (C)/(D) for students who think ethanol is not a nucleophile source.\nWorking: ethanol (or ethoxide from it) and AgCN (cyanide, ambident) both supply nucleophiles; but AgCN is largely covalent and attacks through N to give isocyanides, so KCN and AgCN do not both give nitriles.\nKey idea: Ionic KCN → C-attack (nitrile); covalent AgCN → N-attack (isocyanide).","traps":["AgCN assumed to give nitrile: AgCN → isocyanide (Ag–C bond covalent).","Absolute “all conditions”: Treat “always/all” statements with suspicion."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Consider the following reactions : (a) (CH₃)₃CCH(OH)CH₃ —conc. H₂SO₄→ (b) (CH₃)₂CHCH(Br)CH₃ —alc. KOH→ (c) (CH₃)₂CHCH(Br)CH₃ —(CH₃)₃CO⁻K⁺→ (d) (CH₃)₂C(OH)–CH₂–CHO —Δ→ Which of these reaction(s) will not produce Saytzeff product ?","options":["(c) only","(a), (c) and (d)","(d) only","(b) and (d)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): counts (a) as non-Saytzeff by missing the methyl shift, and (d) as non-Saytzeff because of E1cB. (C): treats (d) as the only odd case. (D): thinks alc. KOH gives the terminal alkene.\nWorking: (a) methyl shift gives tertiary cation → 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene (Saytzeff); (b) alc. KOH → 2-methylbut-2-ene (Saytzeff); (c) bulky tert-butoxide → 3-methylbut-1-ene (Hofmann); (d) dehydration of the β-hydroxy aldehyde gives conjugated (CH₃)₂C=CH–CHO, also the more substituted alkene. Only (c) fails.\nKey idea: Bulky base removes the most accessible (terminal) H → Hofmann; all others give the most substituted (and here conjugated) alkene.","traps":["Missed methyl shift in (a): 2° cation next to a quaternary carbon rearranges to 3° by 1,2-methyl shift, then Saytzeff.","Bulky base selectivity: Bulky base → less substituted (Hofmann) alkene."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"For the following reactions :\nCH₃CH₂CH₂Br + Z⁻ —k_s (substitution)→ CH₃CH₂CH₂Z + Br⁻\nCH₃CH₂CH₂Br + Z⁻ —k_e (elimination)→ CH₃CH=CH₂ + HZ + Br⁻\nwhere Z⁻ = CH₃CH₂O⁻ (A) or (CH₃)₃C–O⁻ (B), k_s and k_e, are, respectively, the rate constants for the substitution and elimination, and μ = k_s/k_e, the correct options is -","options":["μ_B > μ_A and k_e(B) > k_e(A)","μ_B > μ_A and k_e(A) > k_e(B)","μ_A > μ_B and k_e(B) > k_e(A)","μ_A > μ_B and k_e(A) > k_e(B)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) pairs the right k_e order with the wrong ratio; (B)/(D) assume ethoxide, the less hindered base, eliminates faster.\nWorking: bulky tert-butoxide is a poor nucleophile but strong base, so with a 1° halide it favours E2: k_e(B) > k_e(A) and k_s/k_e is smaller for B, i.e. μ_A > μ_B.\nKey idea: Bulk hinders nucleophilic attack at carbon more than removal of an exposed β-H.","traps":["Ratio direction confused: Smaller μ means more elimination – that is B.","Ethoxide assumed stronger eliminator: Steric bulk, not just basicity, decides E2 vs SN2."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The decreasing order of reactivity towards dehydrohalogenation (E₁) reaction of the following compounds is : (A) 1-chlorobutane, Cl–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃; (B) 4-chlorobut-1-ene, Cl–CH₂CH₂CH=CH₂ (as drawn); (C) 2-chlorobutane, CH₃CHClCH₂CH₃; (D) 3-chlorobut-1-ene, CH₃CHClCH=CH₂","options":["B > D > A > C","B > D > C > A","D > B > C > A","B > A > D > C"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(D) put B first, overrating the homoallylic chloride; (A)/(D) also misplace A or C.\nWorking: E1 rate follows carbocation stability; D gives a secondary allylic cation (most stable) and A a primary cation (least), so D first and A last – only (C) fits. Caveat: B as drawn is homoallylic (primary cation, not resonance-stabilised), so strictly C > B; the key treats B as allylic-type stabilised.\nKey idea: E1 ∝ stability of carbocation: 2° allylic > others > 1°.","traps":["Homoallylic taken as allylic: Check whether C=C is adjacent to the C⁺.","Primary allylic vs secondary ranking: Count resonance and alkyl stabilisation."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C15","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Stereochemistry of substitution","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Which of the following compounds will show retention in configuration on nucleophilic substitution by OH⁻ ion?","options":["CH₃–CH(C₂H₅)–CH₂Br (1-bromo-2-methylbutane)","CH₃–CH(Br)–C₆H₅ (1-bromo-1-phenylethane)","CH₃–CH(Br)–CH₃ (2-bromopropane)","CH₃–CH(Br)–C₆H₁₃ (2-bromooctane)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) benzylic halide racemises via SN1; (D) inverts by SN2; (C) has no stereocentre, so \"retention\" is meaningless.\nWorking: in (A) substitution takes place at the CH₂Br carbon; no bond to the stereocentre (C2) is broken, so its configuration is retained. (B) benzylic → SN1, racemisation; (C) achiral; (D) secondary, SN2 → inversion.\nKey idea: Retention is automatic when the substitution site is not the chiral centre.","traps":["Retention linked only to SN1: SN1 gives racemisation, not retention.","Achiral substrate: Check for a stereocentre first."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product obtained from E₂-elimination of 3-bromo-2-fluoropentane is:","options":["CH₃CH₂–CH(Br)–CH=CH₂","CH₃–CH₂–C(Br)=CH–CH₃","CH₃–CH=CH–CH(F)–CH₃","CH₃CH₂CH=C(F)–CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) lose HF instead of HBr; (C) is the less substituted alkene from the C4–H.\nWorking: Br⁻ is the better leaving group, so HBr is lost. β-H can come from C2 (bearing F) or C4. The C2–H is made more acidic by the –I effect of F, and removal gives the more substituted (trisubstituted) alkene CH₃CH₂CH=C(F)CH₃, so (D) is the Saytzeff/major product.\nKey idea: Br leaves; F stays and acidifies the H on its carbon, giving the more substituted fluoroalkene.","traps":["F taken as leaving group: C–F bond is strongest; Br⁻ leaves.","Less substituted alkene chosen: Apply Saytzeff and the –I of F on β-H."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Consider the reaction sequence given below : (CH₃)₃C–Br →(OH⁻/H₂O) (CH₃)₃C–OH + Br⁻ ……(1), rate = k[t-BuBr]; (CH₃)₃C–Br →(OH⁻/C₂H₅OH) CH₂=C(CH₃)₂ + HOH + Br⁻ ……(2), rate = k[t-BuBr][OH⁻]. Which of the following statements is true :","options":["Changing the concentration of base will have no effect on reaction (1)","Changing the concentration of base will have no effect on reaction (2)","Changing the base from OH⁻ to ⁻OR will have no effect on reaction (2)","Doubling the concentration of base will double the rate of both the reactions."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) contradicts the E2 rate law; (C) ignores that base strength/identity affects E2; (D) wrongly applies base dependence to the SN1 reaction.\nWorking: reaction (1) is SN1 with rate = k[t-BuBr], independent of [OH⁻]; reaction (2) is E2 with rate depending on [OH⁻] and on the base used. So only (A) is true.\nKey idea: Only species in the rate law change the rate.","traps":["Base dependence assigned to both: Check each rate law separately."],"topics":["Substitution vs elimination","SN1"]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Carbocation rearrangements","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The major product in the following reaction is :\n2-iodo-3,4-dimethylpentane, CH₃CH(I)CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂ —(t-BuOH, Heat)→","options":["tert-butyl ether at C-2: CH₃CH(Ot-Bu)CH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂","3,4-dimethylpent-1-ene, CH₂=CHCH(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂","3,4-dimethylpent-2-ene, CH₃CH=C(CH₃)CH(CH₃)₂","2,3-dimethylpent-2-ene, (CH₃)₂C=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) S_N1 ether – t-BuOH is too hindered a nucleophile, and heat favours elimination; (B) Hofmann alkene – needs t-BuOK; (C) Saytzeff alkene without rearrangement.\nWorking: t-BuOH (neutral, polar protic, heat) favours E1: the 2° carbocation undergoes 1,2-H shifts to a 3° cation, and loss of H⁺ gives the most substituted (tetrasubstituted) alkene, 2,3-dimethylpent-2-ene. (B) would need bulky t-BuO⁻ (E2, Hofmann).\nKey idea: Reagent is t-BuOH (not t-BuOK): carbocation pathway → rearrange → most substituted alkene.","traps":["t-BuOH read as t-BuOK: Neutral alcohol gives a carbocation (E1), not E2.","Rearrangement missed: Check for a 3° centre one carbon away; shift H."],"topics":["Carbocation rearrangements","Elimination & Saytzeff"]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Reactions with metals","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"In the following reaction sequence, [C] is : p-toluidine (4-methylaniline) —(i) NaNO₂ + HCl, 0–5 °C; (ii) Cu₂Cl₂ + HCl→ [A] —Cl₂/hν→ [B] —Na + dry ether→ [C] (major product)","options":["ClCH₂–C₆H₄–C₆H₄–CH₂Cl (4,4′-bis(chloromethyl)biphenyl)","CH₃–C₆H₄–C₆H₄–CH₃ (4,4′-dimethylbiphenyl)","Cl–C₆H₄–CH₂–CH₂–C₆H₄–Cl (1,2-bis(4-chlorophenyl)ethane)","Cl–C₆H₄–CH₂–C₆H₄–CH₂Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) aryl–aryl coupling (Fittig type) assumed; (D) mixed benzylic–aryl coupling.\nWorking: Sandmeyer gives 4-chlorotoluene [A]; side-chain chlorination gives 4-ClC₆H₄CH₂Cl [B]; Wurtz coupling occurs at the reactive benzylic C–Cl (aryl C–Cl inert) → ClC₆H₄CH₂CH₂C₆H₄Cl.\nKey idea: Under Na/ether the benzylic halide couples; the aryl chloride survives (partial double-bond character).","traps":["Aryl chloride assumed to couple: Aryl C–Cl is much less reactive; benzylic couples.","Cl₂/hν position: hν → free radical, at CH₃ side chain."],"topics":["Reactions with metals","Haloarene preparation"]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"The major products of the following reaction are :\nCH₃–CH(CH₃)–CH(OSO₂CH₃)–CH₃ —(i) KOtBu/Δ; (ii) O₃/H₂O₂→\n[Substrate: 3-methylbutan-2-yl methanesulfonate]","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–COOH (2-methylpropanoic acid) + HCOOH","(CH₃)₂CH–CHO (2-methylpropanal) + HCHO","CH₃–CO–CH₃ (acetone) + CH₃CHO","CH₃–CO–CH₃ (acetone) + CH₃COOH"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) right alkene but reductive-ozonolysis products (ignores H₂O₂); (C) Zaitsev alkene (CH₃)₂C=CHCH₃ with reductive work-up; (D) Zaitsev alkene with oxidative work-up.\nWorking: bulky KOtBu removes the less hindered CH₃ hydrogen (Hofmann elimination) → 3-methylbut-1-ene, (CH₃)₂CH–CH=CH₂; oxidative ozonolysis (H₂O₂) converts the fragments to (CH₃)₂CHCOOH and HCOOH.\nKey idea: KOtBu → less substituted alkene; O₃/H₂O₂ → acids (HCHO → HCOOH).","traps":["Zaitsev applied with a bulky base: Bulky t-butoxide abstracts the most accessible H → Hofmann alkene.","Work-up of ozonide ignored: H₂O₂ = oxidative work-up → carboxylic acids."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : 3-hydroxycyclohexan-1-one (cyclohexanone ring with OH at C-3) → 1. PBr₃ ; 2. KOH (alc.)","options":["cyclohex-3-en-1-one (C=C between C-3 and C-4)","4-hydroxycyclohex-2-en-1-one (C=C at C-2/C-3, OH retained at C-4)","phenol (C₆H₅OH)","cyclohex-2-en-1-one (conjugated enone)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) β,γ-unsaturated ketone (elimination toward C-4); (B) keeps OH (as if PBr₃ step ignored / misplaced); (C) phenol (over-aromatisation, also loses C=O).\nWorking: PBr₃ converts the 3-OH to 3-Br; alcoholic KOH eliminates HBr. β-H at C-2 is activated by the C=O, so the conjugated (more stable) cyclohex-2-enone forms.\nKey idea: The C-2 H is α to C=O (acidic) and gives a conjugated enone, so elimination goes toward C-2.","traps":["Non-conjugated alkene chosen: Prefer the alkene conjugated with C=O.","PBr₃ step overlooked: Track every reagent: PBr₃ replaces OH by Br first."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : 1-chloro-4-(2-chloropropyl)benzene (p-Cl-C₆H₄-CH₂-CHCl-CH₃) → (1) KOH (alc.) ; (2) Free radical polymerisation","options":["poly[1-(4-chlorophenyl)prop-1-ene]: repeat unit –CH(CH₃)–CH(C₆H₄-p-Cl)–","repeat unit –CH₂–CH(CH₂-C₆H₄-p-OH)– (poly-allyl phenol)","repeat unit –CH(CH₃)–CH(C₆H₄-p-OH)–","repeat unit –CH₂–CH(CH₂-C₆H₄-p-Cl)– (poly-allyl chlorobenzene)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) assume aryl Cl replaced by OH (KOH as nucleophile on haloarene); (D) Hofmann/terminal alkene (allylbenzene) polymer.\nWorking: alcoholic KOH removes HCl from the side chain only (aryl C–Cl inert) giving the conjugated Zaitsev alkene p-Cl-C₆H₄-CH=CH-CH₃; addition polymerisation gives –[CH(Ar)–CH(CH₃)]ₙ– with Ar = 4-chlorophenyl.\nKey idea: Aryl C–Cl survives; elimination gives the styrene-type conjugated alkene.","traps":["Aryl chloride assumed to react with KOH: Haloarenes need drastic conditions (623 K, 300 atm) for OH substitution.","Terminal alkene product chosen: Zaitsev and conjugation with ring favour internal alkene."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Increasing order of reactivity of the following compounds for S_N1 substitution is: (A) (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂–Cl (1-chloro-2-methylpropane); (B) CH₃CH₂Cl (chloroethane); (C) p-CH₃O–C₆H₄–CH₂Cl (4-methoxybenzyl chloride); (D) C₆H₅CH₂Cl (benzyl chloride).","options":["(B) < (C) < (D) < (A)","(A) < (B) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (C) < (A) < (D)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) put the p-methoxybenzyl halide low, treating OMe as electron-withdrawing (–I); (B) puts the isobutyl halide below ethyl, ignoring extra hyperconjugative/inductive help (and possible rearrangement).\nWorking: S_N1 rate follows carbocation stability; the two primary halides are slowest, isobutyl (more alkyl/hyperconjugation, can rearrange) slightly above ethyl; benzyl is resonance-stabilised and p-OMe adds +R, so B < A < D < C.\nKey idea: p-OCH₃ donates by resonance straight into the benzylic carbocation, so C is fastest.","traps":["OMe treated as electron-withdrawing: At para position, +R of OMe dominates for a cation.","Order of two primary halides: More alkyl branching near C⁺ gives more stabilisation."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following potential energy (PE) diagrams represents the S_N1 reaction? [(1) two humps, second hump higher than the first; (2) a single hump; (3) two humps of equal height; (4) two humps, first hump higher than the second, with an intermediate valley between them; products lower than reactants in the two-hump plots.]","options":["Two-step profile, second transition state higher","One-step profile (single transition state)","Two-step profile, both transition states equal","Two-step profile, first transition state higher"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is S_N2 (one step); (A) makes the nucleophile attack rate-determining; (C) shows no distinct slow step.\nWorking: S_N1 is two-step via a carbocation intermediate; the first step (ionisation) is slow/rate-determining so the first transition state is the higher one, i.e. (D).\nKey idea: Slowest step = highest-energy transition state = the first hump.","traps":["Which hump is higher: Rate-determining ionisation = higher first TS.","S_N1 drawn as one step: S_N1 has a carbocation intermediate (valley)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Increasing rate of S_N1 reaction in the following compounds is : (A) C₆H₅–CH(I)–CH₃ (1-iodo-1-phenylethane); (B) 3-MeO–C₆H₄–CH(I)CH₃ (meta-methoxy); (C) 4-H₃C–C₆H₄–CH(I)CH₃ (para-methyl); (D) 4-H₃CO–C₆H₄–CH(I)CH₃ (para-methoxy)","options":["(A) < (B) < (C) < (D)","(B) < (A) < (D) < (C)","(B) < (A) < (C) < (D)","(A) < (B) < (D) < (C)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) treats m-OMe as activating; (B), (D) rank p-CH₃ above p-OMe (hyperconjugation over resonance).\nWorking: SN1 rate follows benzylic cation stability: p-OMe (+R) > p-CH₃ (hyperconjugation/+I) > H > m-OMe (only –I at meta).\nKey idea: OMe donates by resonance only from o/p; at meta it withdraws inductively.","traps":["m-OMe treated as electron-donating: Resonance does not reach the benzylic C from meta.","CH₃ ranked above OMe at para: +R of OMe at para outweighs +H of CH₃."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Carbocation rearrangements","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is : (CH₃)₂CH–CH(Br)–CH₃ (2-bromo-3-methylbutane) —[CH₃OH]→ ?","options":["(CH₃)₂CH–CH(OCH₃)–CH₃ (2-methoxy-3-methylbutane)","(CH₃)₂CH–CH=CH₂ (3-methylbut-1-ene)","(CH₃)₂C(OCH₃)–CH₂CH₃ (2-methoxy-2-methylbutane)","(CH₃)₂C=CH–CH₃ (2-methylbut-2-ene)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) unrearranged SN1/SN2 product; (B) Hofmann alkene; (D) Saytzeff alkene (E1 minor in neutral methanol).\nWorking: methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile) ⇒ SN1 solvolysis; the 2° carbocation undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the 3° cation, which is captured by CH₃OH ⇒ tertiary methyl ether.\nKey idea: 2° cation next to a 3° C–H rearranges before capture.","traps":["Rearrangement missed: Check if a hydride/methyl shift gives a more stable cation.","Elimination product chosen: No strong base: solvolysis (substitution) dominates."],"topics":["Carbocation rearrangements","SN1"]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The major product 'Y' in the following reaction is: 2-chloro-3-methylbutane [CH₃CH(Cl)CH(CH₃)₂] —(EtONa, heat)→ X —(HBr)→ Y","options":["1-bromo-3-methylbutane [BrCH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)₂]","3-methylbutan-2-ol [CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂]","2-bromo-2-methylbutane [CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)₂Br]","2-bromo-3-methylbutane [CH₃CHBrCH(CH₃)₂]"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) returns the starting skeleton (substitution or Hofmann alkene + Markovnikov); (A) anti-Markovnikov on terminal alkene; (B) SN2 substitution/hydration product.\nWorking: E2 with EtONa gives Saytzeff alkene 2-methylbut-2-ene (X); HBr adds Markovnikov to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane.\nKey idea: Tri-substituted alkene then tertiary carbocation.","traps":["Hofmann alkene chosen: Small base EtONa gives Saytzeff product.","Substitution instead of elimination: Heat with alkoxide favours elimination."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN1","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which one of the following is likely to give a precipitate with AgNO₃ solution ?","options":["(CH₃)₃CCl","CHCl₃","CH₂=CH–Cl","CCl₄"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) polyhalides look \"chlorine-rich\" but do not ionise; (C) vinyl C–Cl has partial double-bond character.\nWorking: tert-butyl chloride ionises (SN1) to a stable 3° carbocation and Cl⁻, giving AgCl; CHCl₃, CCl₄ and vinyl chloride do not release Cl⁻.\nKey idea: Only a C–Cl that ionises readily (3° alkyl) gives free Cl⁻ for AgCl.","traps":["More Cl atoms means more AgCl: Ask which C–Cl breaks heterolytically in water.","Vinyl chloride treated like alkyl chloride: Vinyl C–Cl has partial double-bond character."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"C–X bond","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Assertion (A): Vinyl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution easily. Reason (R): Even though the intermediate carbocation is stabilized by loosely held π-electrons, the cleavage is difficult because of strong bonding.","options":["Both (A) and (R) are wrong statements","Both (A) and (R) are correct statements and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct statements but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is a correct statement but (R) is a wrong statement"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) accept the claim that the vinyl cation is stabilised; (A) rejects the true Assertion.\nWorking: A is true (C–X has partial double-bond character, sp² C). R is false: a vinyl cation is not stabilised by the π electrons (the empty sp/p orbital is orthogonal to the π bond), so its first clause is wrong.\nKey idea: Vinyl cations are unstable; the true reason is C–X partial double bond and sp² carbon.","traps":["Reason judged true because second half is correct: Check every clause of R separately.","Vinyl cation assumed resonance-stabilised: Vinylic (on C=C) ≠ allylic (next to C=C)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"Heating of 2-chloro-1-phenylbutane with EtOK/EtOH gives X as the major product. Reaction of X with Hg(OAc)₂/H₂O followed by NaBH₄ gives Y as the major product. Y is :","options":["4-phenylbutan-2-ol, Ph–CH₂CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₃","butylbenzene, Ph–CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃","1-phenylbutan-2-ol, Ph–CH₂–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃","1-phenylbutan-1-ol, Ph–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₂CH₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) assumes OH returns to the original C–Cl carbon; (A) assumes alkene at C3–C4; (B) treats NaBH₄ as removing the OH.\nWorking: E2 with ethoxide gives the conjugated, more substituted alkene Ph–CH=CH–CH₂CH₃ (X); oxymercuration–demercuration puts OH on the carbon better able to bear positive charge (benzylic), giving PhCH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: Conjugation with Ph makes X a styrene; benzylic carbon carries OH.","traps":["Alkene placed away from phenyl: Conjugated alkene (styrene-type) is the most stable.","OH placed at the original Cl carbon: Track the alkene: both alkene C are secondary, benzylic wins."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The compounds A and B in the following reaction are, respectively:\nBenzene —HCHO + HCl→ A —AgCN→ B","options":["A = Benzyl alcohol, B = Benzyl isocyanide","A = Benzyl alcohol, B = Benzyl cyanide","A = Benzyl chloride, B = Benzyl cyanide","A = Benzyl chloride, B = Benzyl isocyanide"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): benzyl alcohol as A. (C): cyanide instead of isocyanide.\nWorking: chloromethylation of benzene (HCHO/HCl) gives benzyl chloride; covalent AgCN reacts through N to give benzyl isocyanide.\nKey idea: AgCN → isocyanide; KCN → cyanide.","traps":["AgCN treated like KCN: AgCN is covalent: N end attacks → isocyanide.","HCHO + HCl seen as giving alcohol: HCl converts the hydroxymethyl intermediate to CH₂Cl (chloromethylation)."],"topics":["SN2","Haloalkane preparation"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"SN2","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: 2-ethylbenzamide (o-C₂H₅–C₆H₄–CONH₂) —(i) Br₂/hν, (ii) KOH (dil)→ ?","options":["2-methyl-1,2-dihydrobenzazete (benzo-fused four-membered ring: N–H and CH(CH₃)) – benzazetidine","Indoline (2,3-dihydro-1H-indole; benzo-fused five-membered ring with NH, no C=O)","3-methylisoindolin-1-one (benzo-fused five-membered lactam: C=O, NH, CH(CH₃))","3,4-dihydroisoquinolin-1(2H)-one (benzo-fused six-membered lactam: C=O, NH, CH₂, CH₂)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Four-membered ring – benzylic carbon to N directly with Hofmann-style loss of C=O. (B) Hofmann bromamide (Br₂/KOH) thinking – loses C=O. (D) Six-membered lactam – bromination at the terminal CH₃ instead of benzylic CH₂.\nWorking: Br₂/hν brominates the benzylic CH₂ → ArCH(Br)CH₃; KOH deprotonates the amide N, which displaces Br intramolecularly forming the five-membered lactam 3-methylisoindolin-1-one.\nKey idea: Br₂/hν picks the benzylic H; ring formed is five-membered with the methyl on the ring carbon.","traps":["Hofmann bromamide reflex: Br₂/hν then dil. KOH separately: radical bromination, not Hofmann.","Wrong bromination site: Benzylic radical is most stable; Br goes on CH₂ next to ring."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C07","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is: Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH(Br)–CH₂–CH₃ (3,5-dibromo-2-phenylheptane) —[KOH alc (excess), Δ]→","options":["Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–CH=CH–CH₃ (first C=C drawn Z, second E)","Ph–C(CH₃)=CH–CH₂–CH=CH–CH₃ (isolated diene)","Ph–C(CH₃)=CH–CH=CH–CH₂–CH₃ (diene conjugated with phenyl)","Ph–CH(CH₃)–CH=CH–CH=CH–CH₃ (E,E)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): both C=C away from the ring (no conjugation with Ph; less substituted). (B): first C=C conjugated with Ph but the second placed C5=C6, giving an isolated (non-conjugated) diene.\nWorking: double E2 on Ph–CH(CH₃)–CHBr–CH₂–CHBr–CH₂CH₃; the C3–Br loses the benzylic H to give a trisubstituted alkene conjugated with Ph, and the C5–Br loses H from C4 to extend conjugation → Ph–C(CH₃)=CH–CH=CH–CH₂CH₃.\nKey idea: The most stable product is the conjugated diene that is also conjugated with the phenyl ring.","traps":["Isolated vs conjugated diene: After the first elimination, prefer the H that puts the new C=C in conjugation.","E/Z drawing distracts: Check connectivity first, geometry second."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Elimination & Saytzeff","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\n5-Chlorocyclohex-3-en-1-one [ring: C1=O; C2 CH₂; C3=C4 double bond; C5 bears Cl; C6 CH₂ – the C=C is not conjugated with C=O] treated with (i) HBr, (ii) alc. KOH.","options":["Phenol","3-Chlorocyclohex-3-en-1-one [Cl on a ring C=C carbon; C3=C4, not conjugated with C=O]","5-Chlorocyclohex-2-en-1-one [conjugated enone, Cl on C5]","5-Hydroxycyclohex-2-en-1-one [conjugated enone, OH on C5]"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C) keep Cl – assume only one elimination (HBr) occurs, (C) also moving the C=C into conjugation. (D) treats KOH as aqueous (substitution of Cl by OH) plus conjugation of the C=C.\nWorking: HBr adds across C=C; alcoholic KOH removes HBr and HCl (two eliminations) giving a cyclohexadienone, which tautomerises to aromatic phenol.\nKey idea: Two eliminations on a six-ring ketone give a dienone that tautomerises to phenol – aromaticity is the sink.","traps":["Stopping after one elimination: Excess alc. KOH removes every β-H/halogen pair it can.","alc. vs aq. KOH: Alcoholic KOH → elimination."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Substitution vs elimination","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The major product of the following reaction is:\nH₃C–C(Cl)(CH₂CH₃)(COOCH₂CH₃) (drawn as a wedge/dash stereocentre) —NaOEt, Δ→ ?","options":["H₃C–C(OCH₂CH₃)(CH₂CH₃)(COOCH₂CH₃) with the same configuration (retention, substitution)","H₃CH₂C–C(OCH₂CH₃)(CH₃)(CO₂CH₂CH₃) with inverted configuration (SN2-type substitution)","CH₃C(CO₂CH₂CH₃)=CHCH₃","CH₃CH₂C(CO₂CH₂CH₃)=CH₂"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) retention substitution – impossible by SN2 and unlikely at a tertiary centre with strong base. (B) inversion substitution – SN2 is blocked at a tertiary carbon. (D) Hofmann (terminal) alkene from the CH₃ hydrogens – less substituted.\nWorking: tertiary chloride + strong base NaOEt + heat → E2 elimination; removing H from the CH₂ of the ethyl group gives the more substituted, ester-conjugated alkene ethyl 2-methylbut-2-enoate (Saytzeff).\nKey idea: Tertiary halide + strong bulky-enough base + heat = elimination; pick the more substituted (and here conjugated) alkene.","traps":["Wedge-dash drawing invites SN2 thinking: Tertiary carbon: no SN2; heat + alkoxide → E2.","Terminal alkene chosen: Saytzeff: more substituted alkene is major."]}],"mx-som":[{"id":"JM25Apr07S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":51,"q":"At the sea level, the dry air mass percentage composition is given as nitrogen gas: 70.0, oxygen gas: 27.0 and argon gas: 3.0. If total pressure is 1.15 atm, then calculate the ratio of following respectively:\n(i) partial pressure of nitrogen gas to partial pressure of oxygen gas\n(ii) partial pressure of oxygen gas to partial pressure of argon gas\n(Given: Molar mass of N, O and Ar are 14, 16 and 40 g mol⁻¹ respectively.)","options":["2.59, 11.85","2.96, 11.2","4.26, 19.3","5.46, 17.8"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 2.59, 11.85: first value is the mass ratio; (B) 2.96, 11.2 correct (11.25 truncated); (C) 4.26, 19.3 and (D) 5.46, 17.8: atomic/diatomic molar-mass mix-ups.\nWorking: per 100 g of air, n(N₂) = 70/28 = 2.5 mol, n(O₂) = 27/32 = 0.844 mol, n(Ar) = 3/40 = 0.075 mol. Partial pressures are in the mole ratio, so p(N₂)/p(O₂) = 2.5/0.844 = 2.96 and p(O₂)/p(Ar) = 0.844/0.075 = 11.25 ≈ 11.2 → option (B); the total pressure is not needed.\nKey idea: Partial pressure ratio = mole ratio, found from mass/molar mass with diatomic N₂ and O₂.","traps":["Mass percentage used directly as mole fraction: Convert each mass to moles before taking ratios.","Irrelevant given data invites unnecessary use: Ratios of partial pressures equal ratios of moles; total pressure cancels."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":83,"q":"Three bulbs are filled with CH₄, CO₂ and Ne as shown below:\nBulb 1: CH₄(g), P = 2 atm, V = 2 L\nBulb 2: CO₂(g), P = 4 atm, V = 3 L\nBulb 3: Ne(g), P = 3 atm, V = 4 L\nThe bulbs are connected through pipes of zero volume. When the stopcocks are opened and the temperature is kept constant throughout, the pressure of the system is found to be ______ atm. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 9 (adds pressures), 3 obtained by averaging pressures is coincidental, 28 (forgets to divide by total V).\nWorking: total PV = 2×2 + 4×3 + 3×4 = 28 L atm; total V = 9 L; P = 28/9 = 3.11 → 3 atm.\nKey idea: Σ(PV) is conserved at constant T; divide by total volume.","traps":["Pressures added directly: Each gas expands to 9 L first: p_i = P_iV_i/9.","Average of pressures: Weight by volume: ΣPV/ΣV."],"topics":["Dalton's partial pressures","Gas laws"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":67,"q":"Which of the following have same number of significant figures? A. 0.00253 B. 1.0003 C. 15.0 D. 163. Choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["A, C and D only","B and C only","A, B and C only","C and D only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) counts 1.0003 as 3; (C) counts 1.0003 as 3 too; (D) drops 0.00253 by counting leading zeros.\nWorking: 0.00253 → 3 (leading zeros not significant), 1.0003 → 5, 15.0 → 3 (trailing zero after decimal significant), 163 → 3.\nKey idea: Leading zeros never count; embedded zeros always count; trailing zeros after a decimal count.","traps":["Leading zeros counted: Leading zeros only fix the decimal place.","Embedded zeros ignored: Zeros between non-zero digits are significant."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":68,"q":"Arrange the following gases in increasing order of van der Waals constant 'a'. A. Ar B. CH₄ C. H₂O D. C₆H₆. Choose the correct option from the following.","options":["D, C, B and A","A, B, C and D","B, C, D and A","C, D, B and A"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts H₂O highest (H-bonding assumed to dominate); (A) is the reverse order; (C) places Ar last.\nWorking: a (L² bar mol⁻²) ≈ Ar 1.36 < CH₄ 2.25 < H₂O 5.46 < C₆H₆ 18.2; larger, more polarisable or H-bonded molecules attract more strongly.\nKey idea: a grows with molecular size/polarisability; large benzene beats water despite H-bonding.","traps":["Water placed above benzene: Benzene's large dispersion forces give a ≈ 18 vs 5.5 for water."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":81,"q":"The number of statement/s, which are correct with respect to the compression of carbon dioxide from point (a) in the Andrews isotherm from the following is ________. Graph: Andrews isotherms of CO₂ (pressure vs volume) with dome; on a sub-critical isotherm, point a (gas region, right), b (right edge of dome, where liquefaction starts), c (left edge of dome), d (liquid region, steep left). A. Carbon dioxide remains as a gas upto point (b) B. Liquid carbon dioxide appears at point (c) C. Liquid and gaseous carbon dioxide coexist between points (b) and (c) D. As the volume decreases from (b) and (c), the amount of liquid decreases","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (accepting B: liquid at c); 1 (missing A or C).\nWorking: A and C true; liquid first appears at b, not c (B false); liquid amount increases from b to c (D false).\nKey idea: Liquefaction starts at b and finishes at c.","traps":["Where liquid first appears: Liquid appears at start of flat line (b).","Direction of liquid amount: Compression converts gas to liquid."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"Based on the given figure, the number of correct statement/s is/are ______.\nA. Surface tension is the outcome of equal attractive and repulsive forces acting on the liquid molecule in bulk.\nB. Surface tension is due to uneven forces acting on the molecules present on the surface.\nC. The molecule in the bulk can never come to the liquid surface.\nD. The molecules on the surface are responsible for vapour pressure if the system is a closed system.","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (rejecting D); 3 (accepting A because bulk forces are 'balanced').\nWorking: B and D are true; A is false – forces on a bulk molecule are balanced and do not cause surface tension; C is false – molecules move freely between the bulk and the surface. So 2 statements are correct.\nKey idea: Surface tension comes from unbalanced forces on surface molecules, not bulk ones.","traps":["Bulk vs surface molecules: Surface tension comes from the unbalanced surface molecules."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan25S2C29-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C29","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":81,"q":"For 1 mol of gas, the plot of pV vs. p is shown below. p is the pressure and V is the volume of the gas\nWhat is the value of compressibility factor at point A?","options":["1 + b/V","1 − a/(RTV)","1 + a/(RTV)","1 − b/V"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the high-pressure limit (point B side); (C) wrong sign of a-term; (D) wrong sign of b-term.\nWorking: at point A (low pressure) pV is below the ideal line, so attraction dominates and the b-term can be neglected: (p + a/V²)V = RT → pV = RT − a/V → Z = pV/RT = 1 − a/(RTV) → option (B).\nKey idea: Below the ideal line = attraction-dominated = a-term.","traps":["Which vdW term dominates at low p: Low p: Z < 1 from attraction (a)."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan29S1C29-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":80,"q":"The total pressure of a mixture of non-reacting gases X (0.6 g) and Y (0.45 g) in a vessel is 740 mm of Hg. The partial pressure of the gas X is ________ mm of Hg. (Nearest Integer) (Given : molar mass X = 20 and Y = 45 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"555","range":[555.0,555.0],"explain":"Common slips: 423 (using mass fraction: 0.6/1.05 × 740 = 423), 185 (partial pressure of Y).\nWorking: n(X) = 0.6/20 = 0.03 mol, n(Y) = 0.45/45 = 0.01 mol; x(X) = 0.03/0.04 = 0.75; p(X) = 0.75 × 740 = 555 mm Hg.\nKey idea: Use mole fraction, not mass fraction.","traps":["Mass fraction used for mole fraction: Convert to moles first."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"The pressure of a moist gas at 27 °C is 4 atm. The volume of the container is doubled at the same temperature. The new pressure of the moist gas is ____ × 10⁻¹ atm. (Nearest integer) (Given: The vapour pressure of water at 27 °C is 0.4 atm)","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 20 (halving total pressure); 18 (dropping water vapour); 16 (subtracting vapour after halving).\nWorking: dry gas p = 4 − 0.4 = 3.6 atm → 1.8 atm after doubling V; water vapour stays at 0.4 atm (saturated, liquid present) → total 2.2 atm = 22 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Vapour pressure depends only on temperature.","traps":["Halving vapour pressure: Only the dry gas obeys Boyle; water vapour stays 0.4 atm."],"topics":["Dalton's partial pressures","Gas laws"]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A sealed flask with a capacity of 2 dm³ contains 11 g of propane gas. The flask is so weak that it will burst if the pressure becomes 2 MPa. The minimum temperature at which the flask will burst is _______ °C. [Nearest integer] (Given: R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Atomic masses of C and H are 12u and 1u respectively.) (Assume that propane behaves as an ideal gas.)","type":"num","answer":"1655","range":[1655.0,1655.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1928 (leaving the answer in kelvin); unit slips with dm³ or MPa giving values off by 10³.\nWorking: n = 11/44 = 0.25 mol; T = pV/nR = (2×10⁶ Pa)(2×10⁻³ m³)/(0.25 × 8.3) = 4000/2.075 = 1927.7 K = 1654.7 °C ≈ 1655 °C.\nKey idea: Pa × m³ = J, so with R in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ the units must be SI; then subtract 273.","traps":["Answer left in kelvin: Answer asked in °C: subtract 273 (273.15).","Mixed units: Convert to Pa and m³."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C22","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A mixture of hydrogen and oxygen contains 40% hydrogen by mass when the pressure is 2.2 bar. The partial pressure of hydrogen is ______ bar. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (using mass fraction 0.4 × 2.2 = 0.88), 0 (rounding 0.88 down), 3 (arithmetic slip).\nWorking: per 100 g: H₂ 40/2 = 20 mol, O₂ 60/32 = 1.875 mol; x(H₂) = 20/21.875 = 0.914; p(H₂) = 0.914 × 2.2 = 2.01 bar → 2.\nKey idea: Partial pressure follows mole fraction, not mass fraction.","traps":["Mass fraction used as mole fraction: Convert masses to moles first."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A 10 g mixture of hydrogen and helium is contained in a vessel of capacity 0.0125 m³ at 6 bar and 27°C. The mass of helium in the mixture is _______ g. (nearest integer) Given : R = 8.3 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (Atomic masses of H and He are 1u and 4u, respectively)","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 1 g/mol for hydrogen gives an impossible/negative split; using 1 bar = 1 atm or litres gives wrong n; 2 g (the H₂ mass) may be entered instead of He.\nWorking: n = PV/RT = (6 × 10⁵ × 0.0125)/(8.3 × 300) = 3.01 mol; x/4 + (10 − x)/2 = 3.01 → x = 7.95 ≈ 8 g He.\nKey idea: n_total ≈ 3 mol; with 10 g total, He ≈ 8 g and H₂ ≈ 2 g.","traps":["H atom vs H₂ molecule: Hydrogen gas is H₂, M = 2.","SI units: 6 bar = 6 × 10⁵ Pa; V in m³."],"topics":["Ideal gas equation","Dalton's partial pressures"]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"For a real gas at 25°C temperature and high pressure (99 bar) the value of compressibility factor is 2, so the value of Van der Waal's constant 'b' should be ______ × 10⁻² L mol⁻¹ (Nearest integer). (Given R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"25","range":[25.0,25.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50 (uses Z = pb/RT without the 1); 2 or 0.25 (unit/power slip); a value using T = 25 K.\nWorking: at high pressure the a-term is negligible, Z = 1 + pb/RT. 2 = 1 + 99b/(0.083 × 298) → b = 24.73/99 = 0.2498 L mol⁻¹ ≈ 25 × 10⁻² L mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: At high p, Z − 1 = pb/RT.","traps":["Limiting form of van der Waals: Neglect a at high p: p(V − b) = RT → Z = 1 + pb/RT.","Temperature in kelvin: Use T = 298 K."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"'x' g of molecular oxygen (O₂) is mixed with 200 g of neon (Ne). The total pressure of the non-reactive mixture of O₂ and Ne in the cylinder is 25 bar. The partial pressure of Ne is 20 bar at the same temperature and volume. The value of 'x' is ______. [Given: Molar mass of O₂ = 32 g mol⁻¹; molar mass of Ne = 20 g mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"80","range":[80.0,80.0],"explain":"Common slips: 40 (taking O₂ moles = 5 / atomic mass 16 slip); 50 (taking pressure ratio 5:20 as mass ratio); 160 (O₂ partial pressure set to 20 bar).\nWorking: n(Ne) = 10 mol; x(Ne) = 20/25 = 0.8 → total 12.5 mol, n(O₂) = 2.5 mol = 80 g.\nKey idea: p_i/p_total = n_i/n_total.","traps":["Mass ratio used for pressure ratio: Convert to moles first."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"At 300 K, a sample of 3.0 g of gas A occupies the same volume as 0.2 g of hydrogen at 200 K at the same pressure. The molar mass of gas A is ____ g mol⁻¹ (nearest integer). Assume that the behaviour of gases as ideal. (Given: The molar mass of hydrogen (H₂) gas is 2.0 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"45","range":[45.0,45.0],"explain":"Common slips: 30 – temperatures ignored (equal moles, 3.0/M = 0.1); 20 – temperatures inverted (n₁/T₁ = n₂/T₂ used).\nWorking: same p and V, so n₁T₁ = n₂T₂: (3.0/M)(300) = (0.2/2.0)(200) = 20 → M = 900/20 = 45 g mol⁻¹.\nKey idea: pV = nRT with p, V fixed → nT = constant.","traps":["Temperatures ignored: Equal moles only at equal T; here nT is equal.","n/T taken as constant: At fixed p, V: n ∝ 1/T, so n₁T₁ = n₂T₂."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"A rigid nitrogen tank stored inside a laboratory has a pressure of 30 atm at 06:00 am when the temperature is 27 °C. At 03:00 pm, when the temperature is 45 °C, the pressure in the tank will be ________ atm. [nearest integer]","type":"num","answer":"32","range":[32.0,32.0],"explain":"Common slips: 50 atm from using °C (30 × 45/27); times of day are irrelevant.\nWorking: rigid tank ⇒ constant V and n; p₂ = p₁T₂/T₁ = 30 × 318/300 = 31.8 ≈ 32 atm.\nKey idea: Always convert to kelvin before using gas laws.","traps":["Celsius instead of kelvin: Add 273.","Clock times as data: Times are irrelevant – only T matters."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"An evacuated glass vessel weighs 40.0 g when empty, 135.0 g when filled with a liquid of density 0.95 g mL⁻¹ and 40.5 g when filled with an ideal gas at 0.82 atm at 250 K. The molar mass of the gas in g mol⁻¹ is : (Given : R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":["35","50","75","125"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) 35 / (B) 50 – using the full filled mass (40.5 g) or mL instead of L; (C) 75 – volume error or wrong temperature handling.\nWorking: liquid mass 95.0 g ÷ 0.95 g/mL gives V = 100 mL = 0.1 L; gas mass = 0.5 g; n = PV/RT = 0.82 × 0.1/(0.082 × 250) = 0.004 mol; M = 0.5/0.004 = 125 g/mol = (D).\nKey idea: Volume of vessel = mass of liquid / density; gas mass is the small difference 40.5 − 40.0.","traps":["Gross mass used as gas mass: Subtract empty vessel mass every time.","Volume left in mL: Convert to L because R is in L atm."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which amongst the given plots is the correct plot for pressure (p) vs density (d) for an ideal gas? [Four graphs, y-axis p (atm), x-axis d (g cm⁻³), each labelled T₃ > T₂ > T₁.] (A) three hyperbola-like curves falling with d, T₁ outermost and T₃ innermost; (B) three straight lines through the origin, T₃ steepest, then T₂, then T₁ least steep; (C) three straight lines through the origin, T₁ steepest, then T₂, T₃ least steep; (D) three hyperbola-like curves, T₃ outermost and T₁ innermost.","options":["Falling hyperbola-like curves; T₁ outermost, T₃ innermost","Straight lines through origin; slope order T₃ > T₂ > T₁","Straight lines through origin; slope order T₁ > T₂ > T₃","Falling hyperbola-like curves; T₃ outermost, T₁ innermost"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) are p–V type hyperbolas (Boyle plot shape confused with p–d); (C) has the right shape but reverses the temperature order of slopes.\nWorking: p = dRT/M, so at fixed T, p is directly proportional to d (straight line through origin) with slope RT/M; the highest temperature T₃ gives the steepest line.\nKey idea: Slope of p vs d = RT/M, so higher T means a steeper straight line.","traps":["p–d mistaken for p–V hyperbola: Density is proportional to 1/V, so p vs d is linear, not hyperbolic.","Slope order reversed: Slope = RT/M: largest T, steepest line."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"100 g of an ideal gas is kept in a cylinder of 416 L volume at 27 °C under 1.5 bar pressure. The molar mass of the gas is ______ g mol⁻¹. (Nearest integer) (Given: R = 0.083 L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: using 27 instead of 300 K (≈ 0.36); forgetting the 1.5 bar (≈ 6).\nWorking: M = mRT/(PV) = 100 × 0.083 × 300 / (1.5 × 416) = 2490/624 = 3.99 ≈ 4 g mol⁻¹ (helium-like).\nKey idea: M = mRT/PV.","traps":["Celsius instead of kelvin: Always convert to 300 K."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Geraniol, a volatile organic compound, is a component of rose oil. The density of the vapour is 0.46 g L⁻¹ at 257°C and 100 mm Hg. The molar mass of geraniol is ________ (Nearest Integer) [Given R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"152","range":[152.0,152.0],"explain":"Common slips: Leaving P in mm Hg gives 0.2; using 257 K instead of 530 K gives ≈ 74; using 1 atm = 750 mm gives ≈ 150.\nWorking: M = dRT/P = 0.46 × 0.082 × 530 / (100/760) = 19.99/0.1316 ≈ 152 g mol⁻¹ (geraniol C₁₀H₁₈O = 154).\nKey idea: Convert mm Hg → atm (÷760) and °C → K (+273) before M = dRT/P.","traps":["Pressure unit: Convert to 0.1316 atm.","Temperature in °C: Use 530 K."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"A box contains 0.90 g of liquid water in equilibrium with water vapour at 27°C. The equilibrium vapour pressure of water at 27°C is 32.0 Torr. When the volume of the box is increased, some of the liquid water evaporates to maintain the equilibrium pressure. If all the liquid water evaporates, then the volume of the box must be ____ litre. [nearest integer] (Given: R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹) (Ignore the volume of the liquid water and assume water vapours behave as an ideal gas.)","type":"num","answer":"29","range":[29.0,29.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 32 directly as atm (≈0.04 L) or forgetting to convert °C to K (≈3 L); using 1 atm gives ≈1 L.\nWorking: n = 0.90/18 = 0.050 mol; P = 32.0/760 = 0.04211 atm; V = nRT/P = 0.050 × 0.082 × 300/0.04211 = 29.2 L → 29 (the small amount of vapour already in the box is neglected).\nKey idea: While liquid remains, pressure stays at the vapour pressure; last drop evaporates when nRT/V = p_vap.","traps":["Torr not converted: Divide Torr by 760.","Pressure assumed to fall on expansion: Equilibrium vapour pressure is constant at fixed T."],"topics":["Liquids","Ideal gas equation"]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The interaction energy of London forces between two particles is proportional to rˣ, where r is the distance between the particles. The value of x is:","options":["3","–3","–6","6"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 6 is the sign slip (power of r in the denominator); (B) –3 is the dipole–dipole (stationary polar molecules) dependence; (A) 3 is that with the sign lost.\nWorking: London (dispersion) interaction energy varies as 1/r⁶, so x = –6.\nKey idea: London ∝ 1/r⁶, dipole–dipole (stationary) ∝ 1/r³, rotating dipoles ∝ 1/r⁶.","traps":["Exponent sign: Inverse power → negative exponent.","London vs dipole–dipole: London/dipole-induced dipole are 1/r⁶; stationary dipole–dipole 1/r³."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"The unit of the van der Waals gas equation parameter 'a' in (P + an²/V²)(V − nb) = nRT is:","options":["kg m s⁻²","dm³ mol⁻¹","kg m s⁻¹","atm dm⁶ mol⁻²"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the unit of b; (A) is force (N); (C) is momentum.\nWorking: an²/V² has units of pressure, so a = P·V²/n² → atm dm⁶ mol⁻² (SI: Pa m⁶ mol⁻² = kg m⁵ s⁻² mol⁻²). dm³ mol⁻¹ is the unit of b.\nKey idea: an²/V² must be a pressure.","traps":["a and b units swapped: b has volume per mole; a has pressure × volume² per mole²."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C23","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Two flasks I and II shown below are connected by a valve of negligible volume. Flask I: 2.8 g N₂, 300 K, 1 L; Flask II: 0.2 g N₂, 60 K, 2 L. When the valve is opened, the final pressure of the system in bar is x × 10⁻². The value of x is ______. (Integer answer) [Assume ideal gas; 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa; Molar mass of N₂ = 28.0 g mol⁻¹; R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"84","range":[84.0,84.0],"explain":"Common slips: 24 (each flask held at its own temperature); 89 (using 300 K for the whole mixture); errors from forgetting the total 3 L volume.\nWorking: n = 0.1 + 0.00714 = 0.10714 mol. If the gases mix and reach a common temperature with no heat exchange to the surroundings (same Cv for the same gas), T_f = (0.1 × 300 + 0.00714 × 60)/0.10714 = 284 K and P = nRT/V = 0.10714 × 8.31 × 284/(3 × 10⁻³) = 8.43 × 10⁴ Pa = 84 × 10⁻² bar. If instead each flask were kept at its own temperature, P = 0.243 bar (x = 24). The question does not state the thermal condition.\nKey idea: Same gas, insulated mixing → T_f is the mole-weighted mean temperature; then pV = nRT over 3 L.","traps":["Final temperature not stated: Assume insulated mixing: T_f = Σn_iT_i/Σn_i for the same gas.","Unit conversion L → m³ and Pa → bar: Use V = 3 × 10⁻³ m³ with R = 8.31, then divide Pa by 10⁵."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Which one of the following is the correct PV vs P plot at constant temperature for an ideal gas? (P and V stand for pressure and volume of the gas respectively)","options":["Horizontal straight line (PV constant)","Curve falling steeply then flattening (hyperbola-like)","Straight line rising through the origin","Straight line falling with P"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is the V vs P (or P vs V) hyperbola; (C) is a PV vs T-type rising line; (D) mimics a real gas at low pressure.\nWorking: Boyle’s law, PV = nRT = constant at fixed T, so PV vs P is a horizontal line (A).\nKey idea: PV is constant for an ideal gas at fixed T – flat line.","traps":["PV vs P confused with V vs P: Check the axis label: PV, not V."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"The volume occupied by 4.75 g of acetylene gas at 50 °C and 740 mmHg pressure is ____ L. (Rounded off to the nearest integer) [Given R = 0.0826 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]","type":"num","answer":"5","range":[5.0,5.0],"explain":"Common slips: 4 (P in mmHg not converted properly / T in °C).\nWorking: n = 4.75/26 = 0.1827 mol; P = 740/760 = 0.974 atm; T = 323 K; V = nRT/P = 0.1827 × 0.0826 × 323 / 0.974 ≈ 5.0 L.\nKey idea: Convert mmHg → atm and °C → K before substituting.","traps":["Unit conversion slip: P/760 and T + 273."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C24","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"A car tyre is filled with nitrogen gas at 35 psi at 27 °C. It will burst if pressure exceeds 40 psi. The temperature in °C at which the car tyre will burst is ______. (Rounded-off to the nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"70","range":[70.0,70.0],"explain":"Common slips: 31 (using °C directly: 27 × 40/35 = 30.9), 343 (reporting kelvin), 69 (truncation).\nWorking: at constant volume p/T is constant: T₂ = 300 × 40/35 = 342.86 K = 69.7 °C ≈ 70.\nKey idea: Use absolute temperature for gas laws, then convert back to °C.","traps":["Celsius used in ratio: Convert to K before using p/T.","Answer left in kelvin: Subtract 273 at the end."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"The number of significant figures in 50000.020 × 10⁻³ is _____.","type":"num","answer":"8","range":[8.0,8.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 (dropping the final zero), 2 or 5 (treating zeros as placeholders), 6.\nWorking: all digits of 50000.020 are significant (zeros between non-zero digits and trailing zeros after the decimal point); the power of ten does not count → 8.\nKey idea: Trailing zeros after a decimal point count; the ×10ⁿ factor does not.","traps":["Trailing zero after decimal dropped: After a decimal point they are significant."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"A certain gas obeys P(Vm – b) = RT. The value of (∂Z/∂P)_T is xb/RT. The value of x is _____. (Integer answer) (Z: compressibility factor)","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0 (treating Z as constant), −1 (sign error from the attraction term), 2.\nWorking: Z = PVm/RT = 1 + Pb/RT, so (∂Z/∂P)_T = b/RT → x = 1.\nKey idea: Z = 1 + bP/RT is linear in P with slope b/RT.","traps":["Calculus form intimidates: Write Z explicitly in P first."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Assertion A: Sharp glass edge becomes smooth on heating it upto its melting point.\nReason R: The viscosity of glass decreases on melting.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) is the trap – viscosity falling seems to explain flow and rounding; (A) and (C) for students unsure whether glass \"melts\" or whether viscosity falls.\nWorking: A is true (fire polishing). R is also true – molten glass is less viscous than solid glass – but the smoothing is caused by surface tension, which makes the melted edge take a rounded shape of minimum surface area; so R does not explain A.\nKey idea: Rounding of a liquid edge minimises surface area – a surface-tension effect.","traps":["Viscosity mistaken as cause: Ask what drives the shape change: surface tension minimises area."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"A home owner uses 4.00 × 10³ m³ of methane (CH₄) gas, (assume CH₄ is an ideal gas) in a year to heat his home. Under the pressure of 1.0 atm and 300 K, mass of gas used is x × 10⁵ g. The value of x is ________. (Nearest integer) (Given R = 0.083 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"26","range":[26.0,26.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.026 or 0 (not converting m³ to L), 2.6 or 3 (wrong power of ten), 160 (stopping at moles).\nWorking: V = 4.00×10⁶ L; n = PV/RT = 4.00×10⁶/(0.083×300) = 1.606×10⁵ mol; mass = 16 × 1.606×10⁵ = 2.57×10⁶ g = 25.7×10⁵ → 26.\nKey idea: 1 m³ = 1000 L; then n = PV/RT and m = nM.","traps":["Volume units: Convert m³ to L (×1000).","Stopping at moles: Multiply by molar mass 16 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of significant figures in 0.00340 is ______.","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (ignoring the trailing zero), 5 or 6 (counting leading zeros).\nWorking: leading zeros are not significant; the trailing zero after the decimal point is significant → 3, 4, 0 = 3 significant figures.\nKey idea: Leading zeros never count; trailing zeros after a decimal point do.","traps":["Trailing zero after decimal: Trailing zeros right of the decimal point are significant."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"An LPG cylinder contains gas at a pressure of 300 kPa at 27 °C. The cylinder can withstand the pressure of 1.2 × 10⁶ Pa. The room in which the cylinder is kept catches fire. The minimum temperature at which the bursting of cylinder will take place is ______ °C. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"927","range":[927.0,927.0],"explain":"Common slips: 108 (using °C directly: 27 × 4), 1200 (leaving in K), 0.3 or 3000-scale slips from kPa/Pa.\nWorking: at constant volume P/T is constant: T₂ = 300 K × (1200 kPa/300 kPa) = 1200 K = 927 °C.\nKey idea: Use kelvin in the law; convert back to °C at the end.","traps":["Celsius in gas law: Always convert to K.","Answer requested in °C: Subtract 273 at the end."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The pressure exerted by a non-reactive gaseous mixture of 6.4 g of methane and 8.8 g of carbon dioxide in a 10 L vessel at 27°C is _______ kPa. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Assume gases are ideal, R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Atomic masses: C: 12.0 u, H: 1.0 u, O: 16.0 u]","type":"num","answer":"150","range":[150.0,150.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.15 or 150000 (unit slips), 100 (only CH₄), 50 (only CO₂).\nWorking: n = 6.4/16 + 8.8/44 = 0.4 + 0.2 = 0.6 mol; P = nRT/V = 0.6 × 8.314 × 300 / (10 × 10⁻³ m³) = 1.497 × 10⁵ Pa ≈ 150 kPa.\nKey idea: Use total moles; V in m³ with R = 8.314 gives Pa.","traps":["Volume not converted to m³: Convert L → m³ and Pa → kPa."],"topics":["Dalton's partial pressures","Ideal gas equation"]},{"id":"JM21Sep01S2C21","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2021,"paper":"Main Sep 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"An empty LPG cylinder weighs 14.8 kg. When full, it weighs 29.0 kg and shows a pressure of 3.47 atm. In the course of use at ambient temperature, the mass of the cylinder is reduced to 23.0 kg. The final pressure inside of the cylinder is ______ atm. (Nearest integer) (Assume LPG to be an ideal gas)","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3 (3.47 × 23/29 ≈ 2.75 rounded); 1 (using 6.0 kg used); 4 (inverting the ratio).\nWorking: gas mass full = 29.0 – 14.8 = 14.2 kg; after use = 23.0 – 14.8 = 8.2 kg; p = 3.47 × 8.2/14.2 = 2.004 ≈ 2 atm.\nKey idea: p₂ = 3.47 × 8.2/14.2 = 2.00 atm.","traps":["Cylinder mass not subtracted: Use gas mass only: 14.2 kg full, 8.2 kg after use.","Used mass taken instead of remaining: Pressure ∝ moles remaining (8.2 kg)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C10","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"The relative strength of interionic/intermolecular forces in decreasing order is :","options":["ion-dipole > ion-ion > dipole-dipole","dipole-dipole > ion-dipole > ion-ion","ion-dipole > dipole-dipole > ion-ion","ion-ion > ion-dipole > dipole-dipole"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): puts ion-dipole above ion-ion (thinks of hydration energies). (B)/(C): put dipole forces above ionic.\nWorking: interaction energy falls as full charges are replaced by partial charges: ion-ion > ion-dipole > dipole-dipole.\nKey idea: Full charge-full charge beats charge-partial charge, which beats partial-partial.","traps":["Hydration energy makes ion-dipole seem strongest: Compare per-pair interaction: ion-ion (∝1/r) > ion-dipole (∝1/r²) > dipole-dipole (∝1/r³)."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The predominant intermolecular forces present in ethyl acetate, a liquid, are :","options":["hydrogen bonding and London dispersion","Dipole-dipole and hydrogen bonding","London dispersion and dipole-dipole","London dispersion, dipole-dipole and hydrogen bonding"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A),(B),(D) include hydrogen bonding, which needs an H on O/N/F in the molecule itself.\nWorking: CH₃COOC₂H₅ is polar (dipole–dipole) and all molecules have London forces; it has no O–H/N–H, so no H-bonding between its own molecules.\nKey idea: Esters accept H-bonds but cannot donate them, so pure ethyl acetate has no H-bonding.","traps":["Oxygen present taken to mean H-bonding: H-bond needs H attached to O/N/F."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"Which one of the following graphs is not correct for ideal gas? (I) d vs T: decreasing curve (hyperbola); (II) d vs T: straight line with positive slope (a segment not drawn through the origin); (III) d vs 1/T: straight line rising; (IV) d vs P: straight line rising. d = Density, P = Pressure, T = Temperature","options":["II","III","I","IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) label correct graphs; a student who forgets the hyperbola shape may reject (I).\nWorking: d = PM/RT, so d ∝ 1/T (graph I hyperbola and graph III straight line correct) and d ∝ P (graph IV correct); d increasing linearly with T (graph II) is wrong.\nKey idea: d ∝ P/T: inverse with T, linear with 1/T and with P.","traps":["Hyperbola mistaken for wrong graph: Write d = PM/RT and test each axis pair.","NOT-type wording: Mark each graph T/F first."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Liquids","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match the type of interaction in Column A with the distance dependence of their interaction energy in Column B : Column A: (I) ion - ion, (II) dipole - dipole, (III) London dispersion. Column B: (a) 1/r, (b) 1/r², (c) 1/r³, (d) 1/r⁶.","options":["(I)-(a), (II)-(b), (III)-(c)","(I)-(a), (II)-(c), (III)-(d)","(I)-(a), (II)-(b), (III)-(d)","(I)-(b), (II)-(d), (III)-(c)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (C) put dipole–dipole as 1/r² (that is the force-like ion–dipole slip); (D) shifts ion–ion to 1/r².\nWorking: ion–ion energy ∝ 1/r, dipole–dipole (stationary polar molecules) ∝ 1/r³, London dispersion ∝ 1/r⁶ → option (B).\nKey idea: Exponents 1, 3, 6 for ion–ion, dipole–dipole, dispersion.","traps":["Dipole–dipole taken as 1/r²: Energy: stationary dipoles 1/r³, rotating 1/r⁶."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Dalton's partial pressures","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"A mixture of one mole each of H₂, He and O₂ each are enclosed in a cylinder of volume V at temperature T. If the partial pressure of H₂ is 2 atm, the total pressure of the gases in the cylinder is :","options":["14 atm","22 atm","6 atm","38 atm"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 38: weighting by mass fraction (H₂ = 2/38 of the mass, so 2 × 38/2); (A) 14 and (B) 22: other mass-based or arithmetic slips; (C) 6 correct (mole fraction 1/3).\nWorking: Dalton's law, x(H₂) = 1/3, so P_total = 2 × 3 = 6 atm.\nKey idea: Partial pressure depends on moles, not masses.","traps":["Mass used instead of moles: Use mole fraction: 1/3 each."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Gas laws","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"A spherical balloon of radius 3 cm containing helium gas has a pressure of 48 × 10⁻³ bar. At the same temperature, the pressure, of a spherical balloon of radius 12 cm containing the same amount of gas will be _____ × 10⁻⁶ bar.","type":"num","answer":"750","range":[750.0,750.0],"explain":"Common slips: 12000 (radius ratio 4 used as the volume ratio: 48 × 10⁻³/4 = 12000 × 10⁻⁶), 3000 (r² scaling), 0.75 (unit scaling slip).\nWorking: PV constant; V ∝ r³ so V₂/V₁ = 64; P₂ = 48 × 10⁻³/64 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁴ bar = 750 × 10⁻⁶ bar.\nKey idea: Radius ×4 → volume ×64 → pressure ÷64.","traps":["Radius ratio used as volume ratio: V ∝ r³."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S1C21","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"Consider the van der Waals constants, a and b, for the following gases. Gas: Ar, Ne, Kr, Xe; a/(atm dm⁶ mol⁻²): 1.3, 0.2, 5.1, 4.1; b/(10⁻² dm³ mol⁻¹): 3.2, 1.7, 1.0, 5.0. Which gas is expected to have the highest critical temperature?","options":["Kr","Ne","Ar","Xe"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) Xe chosen from real-world trend (heaviest noble gas) or largest b; (C) Ar mid values; (B) Ne lowest.\nWorking: T_c = 8a/27Rb ∝ a/b: Ar 0.41, Ne 0.12, Kr 5.1, Xe 0.82 → Kr highest (using the data given in the question).\nKey idea: Compute a/b for each; largest ratio ⇒ highest T_c.","traps":["Using a alone or periodic trend: Use T_c = 8a/27Rb with the given numbers.","Unit factor 10⁻² on b worried over: Common factor cancels in comparison."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"At a given temperature T, gases Ne, Ar, Xe and Kr are found to deviate from ideal gas behaviour. Their equation of state is given as p = RT/(V – b) at T. Here, b is the van der Waals constant. Which gas will exhibit steepest increase in the plot of Z (compression factor) vs p?","options":["Ne","Ar","Xe","Kr"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ne chosen if b thought inversely related to size or a-term reasoning used; (B)/(D) partial size order.\nWorking: Z = pV/RT = 1 + pb/RT, so slope = b/RT, largest for the largest atom, Xe.\nKey idea: With a = 0, Z = 1 + pb/RT is linear in p; slope ∝ b ∝ atomic volume.","traps":["Mixing up a and b effects: Equation has no a term; only b matters."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Consider the following table : Gas | a/(kPa dm⁶ mol⁻¹) | b/(dm³ mol⁻¹): A | 642.32 | 0.05196; B | 155.21 | 0.04136; C | 431.91 | 0.05196; D | 155.21 | 0.4382. a and b are van der Waals constants. The correct statement about the gases is :","options":["Gas C will occupy lesser volume than gas A; gas B will be lesser compressible than gas D","Gas C will occupy more volume than gas A; gas B will be lesser compressible than gas D","Gas C will occupy more volume than gas A; gas B will be more compressible than gas D","Gas C will occupy lesser volume than gas A; gas B will be more compressible than gas D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (D) think larger a means larger volume; (A), (B) think larger b means more compressible.\nWorking: A and C share b; A has larger a (stronger attraction) so A is pulled into a smaller volume ⇒ C occupies more volume. B and D share a; D has a much larger b (bigger excluded volume, repulsion dominant) ⇒ D less compressible, B more compressible.\nKey idea: Large a ⇒ smaller volume (more compressible); large b ⇒ less compressible.","traps":["Effect of a on volume reversed: a = attraction ⇒ molecules pulled together.","Effect of b on compressibility reversed: b = excluded volume ⇒ harder to compress."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Kinetic theory","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Points I, II and III in the following plot respectively correspond to (V_mp: most probable velocity)","options":["V_mp of N₂ (300 K); V_mp of H₂ (300 K); V_mp of O₂ (400 K)","V_mp of H₂ (300 K); V_mp of N₂ (300 K); V_mp of O₂ (400 K)","V_mp of O₂ (400 K); V_mp of N₂ (300 K); V_mp of H₂ (300 K)","V_mp of N₂ (300 K); V_mp of O₂ (400 K); V_mp of H₂ (300 K)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) reverses order; (A)/(B) place H₂ before O₂ by ignoring its tiny molar mass, or ignore T for O₂.\nWorking: V_mp ∝ √(T/M): N₂ 300/28 = 10.7, O₂ 400/32 = 12.5, H₂ 300/2 = 150, so N₂ < O₂(400 K) < H₂ → I, II, III.\nKey idea: Compare T/M: N₂ 10.7 < O₂ 12.5 < H₂ 150.","traps":["Higher T assumed to dominate over M: Compare T/M ratio, not T alone."],"img":["img/jee/JM19Apr10S2C13-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"0.5 moles of gas A and x moles of gas B exert a pressure of 200 Pa in a container of volume 10 m³ at 1000 K. Given R is the gas constant in JK⁻¹ mol⁻¹, x is :","options":["2R/(4+R)","2R/(4−R)","(4−R)/(2R)","(4+R)/(2R)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): sign slip (+R). (A)/(B): reciprocal forms.\nWorking: (0.5 + x) = PV/RT = 200×10/(1000R) = 2/R ⇒ x = (4 − R)/(2R).\nKey idea: Use total moles in pV = nRT.","traps":["Total moles equated to x: n_total = 0.5 + x.","Algebra inverted: x = 2/R − 1/2 = (4 − R)/2R."],"topics":["Ideal gas equation","Dalton's partial pressures"]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Real gases","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The volume of gas A is twice than that of gas B. The compressibility factor of gas A is thrice than that of gas B at same temperature. The pressures of the gases for equal number of moles are :","options":["2P_A = 3P_B","P_A = 3P_B","P_A = 2P_B","3P_A = 2P_B"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) 3P_A = 2P_B: ratio inverted. (B)/(C): one factor (volume or Z) ignored.\nWorking: Z = PV/nRT; Z_A/Z_B = P_A·2V_B/(P_B·V_B) = 3 ⇒ 2P_A = 3P_B.\nKey idea: Z ∝ PV at same n and T.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Write Z_A/Z_B = (P_A V_A)/(P_B V_B) and substitute."]},{"id":"JM19Jan12S2C12","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Ideal gas equation","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 12 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"An open vessel at 27°C is heated until two fifth of the air (assumed as an ideal gas) in it has escaped from the vessel. Assuming that the volume of the vessel remains constant, the temperature at which the vessel has been heated is :","options":["750°C","500°C","750 K","500 K"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) 750 K: takes the escaped 2/5 as the fraction remaining (300 × 5/2 = 750 K). (A) and (B) same numbers in °C – unit trap.\nWorking: P and V constant, so n₁T₁ = n₂T₂; n₂ = 3/5 n₁ → T₂ = 300 × 5/3 = 500 K.\nKey idea: Use the fraction remaining (3/5), and keep T in kelvin.","traps":["Escaped fraction used as remaining fraction: n₂ = (1 − 2/5) n₁ = 3/5 n₁.","Kelvin answer labelled °C: Gas-law temperatures are absolute."]}],"mx-evd":[{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Morphine is a narcotic analgesic. It helps in relieving pain without producing sleep.\nStatement II: Morphine and its derivatives are obtained from opium poppy.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for students who read 'narcotic analgesic' and accept the rest; (B)/(C) if the opium source is doubted.\nWorking: narcotic analgesics relieve pain AND produce sleep (non-narcotic ones do not), so I is false; morphine and its derivatives come from opium poppy, so II is true.\nKey idea: Narcotic = sleep-inducing; the qualifier makes I false.","traps":["Narcotic vs non-narcotic property swapped: Narcotics relieve pain and produce sleep."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C23","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Match List I with List II:\nLIST I: A. Saccharin; B. Aspartame; C. Alitame; D. Sucralose\nLIST II: I. High potency sweetener; II. First artificial sweetening agent; III. Stable at cooking temperature; IV. Unstable at cooking temperature\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): aspartame and sucralose stability swapped. (C): alitame and sucralose swapped.\nWorking: saccharin – first popular artificial sweetener (II); aspartame – unstable at cooking temperature (IV); alitame – high potency (I); sucralose – stable at cooking temperature (III).\nKey idea: Aspartame breaks down on cooking; sucralose (chlorinated sucrose) is stable.","traps":["Heat stability swapped: Aspartame (peptide ester) decomposes on heating; sucralose stable."]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S2C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":70,"q":"The statement/s which are true about antagonists from the following is/are: A. They bind to the receptor site. B. Get transferred inside the cell for their action. C. Inhibit the natural communication of the body. D. Mimic the natural messenger. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B only","A and B","A and C","A, C and D"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) adds the agonist property; (A)/(B) include the false claim that the drug enters the cell.\nWorking: antagonists bind to the receptor site and block (inhibit) the natural message; mimicking the messenger describes agonists, and they do not enter the cell.\nKey idea: Antagonist = binds and blocks; agonist = binds and mimics.","traps":["Agonist property included: Only agonists mimic the messenger."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S1C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList I: A. Tranquilizers; B. Aspirin; C. Antibiotic; D. Antiseptic\nList II: I. Anti blood clotting; II. Salvarsan; III. antidepressant drugs; IV. soframicine\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) - II, (B) - I, (C) - III, (D) - IV","(A) - IV, (B) - II, (C) - I, (D) - III","(A) - III, (B) - I, (C) - II, (D) - IV","(A) - II, (B) - IV, (C) - I, (D) - III"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts salvarsan under tranquilisers. (B) and (D) put soframicine under tranquilisers or aspirin, mixing antiseptic and antibiotic.\nWorking: tranquilizers are antidepressant drugs (III); aspirin prevents blood clotting (I); salvarsan is an antibiotic (II); soframicine is an antiseptic (IV) → A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV = (C).\nKey idea: Aspirin's anti-clotting action and salvarsan as an antibiotic (arsphenamine) are the key recalls.","traps":["Antiseptic vs antibiotic: Soframicine is applied on skin: antiseptic."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Among the following, the number of tranquilizer/s is/are ________. A. Chlordiazepoxide B. Veronal C. Valium D. Salvarsan","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (veronal omitted because it is a 'barbiturate'/hypnotic), 4 (salvarsan included).\nWorking: chlordiazepoxide, veronal (a barbiturate) and valium are tranquilizers; salvarsan is an arsenic antimicrobial for syphilis. Count = 3.\nKey idea: Barbiturates such as veronal are counted as tranquilizers in NCERT; salvarsan is an antimicrobial.","traps":["Barbiturate not counted as tranquilizer: NCERT lists barbiturates under tranquilizers."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":76,"q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I : Noradrenaline is a neurotransmitter.\nStatement II : Low level of noradrenaline is not the cause of depression in human.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) misses the 'not'. (A)/(D) doubt that noradrenaline is a neurotransmitter.\nWorking: noradrenaline is a neurotransmitter; a low level of noradrenaline is (per NCERT) a cause of depression, so II is false.\nKey idea: Low noradrenaline → depression; antidepressants raise its level.","traps":["Inserted negative: Look for 'not' added to a familiar sentence."]},{"id":"JM23Jan24S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Match List I with List II. List-I (Type): A. Antifertility drug; B. Tranquilizer; C. Antihistamine; D. Antibiotic. List-II (Name): i) Norethindrone; ii) Meprobomate; iii) Seldane; iv) Ampicillin. Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): swaps tranquilizer/antihistamine. (C): swaps antifertility/tranquilizer. (D): several shifted.\nWorking: norethindrone – antifertility; meprobamate – tranquilizer; Seldane (terfenadine) – antihistamine; ampicillin – antibiotic → A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV = (B).\nKey idea: Each name is the standard NCERT example for its class.","traps":["Meprobamate vs seldane: Seldane = terfenadine (antihistamine)."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect for antibiotics?","options":["An antibiotic should promote the growth or survival of microorganisms.","An antibiotic must be a product of metabolism.","An antibiotic should be effective in low concentrations.","An antibiotic is a synthetic substance produced as a structural analogue of naturally occurring antibiotic."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) sounds wrong under the modern definition, tempting students who know synthetic antibiotics exist. (C) and (D) are definition phrases.\nWorking: antibiotics inhibit or kill microorganisms, so (A) is incorrect. (B) 'must be a product of metabolism' fits only the older definition – the modern definition includes wholly synthetic substances – but (A) is the clearly wrong statement.\nKey idea: Antibiotics inhibit/kill microbes – 'promote growth' is the reversed qualifier.","traps":["Old vs modern definition of antibiotic: Pick the statement that is plainly opposite to an antibiotic's purpose."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S2C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":78,"q":"Assertion A: Butylated hydroxy anisole when added to butter increases its shelf life. Reason R: Butylated hydroxy anisole is more reactive towards oxygen than food. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","options":["A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","A is not correct but R is correct"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) treats R as true but unrelated.\nWorking: BHA is an antioxidant; it reacts with oxygen more readily than the food does, so the butter is protected from oxidation and keeps longer. Both A and R are true and R explains A.\nKey idea: Antioxidants are oxidised preferentially, sparing the food.","traps":["Explanation link: R states the mechanism of A."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":74,"q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (Antimicrobials): (A) Narrow Spectrum Antibiotic; (B) Antiseptic; (C) Disinfectants; (D) Broad spectrum antibiotic\nList II (Names): (I) Furacin; (II) Sulphur dioxide; (III) Penicillin G; (IV) Chloramphenicol\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) - III, (B) - I, (C) - II, (D) - IV","(A) - II, (B) - I, (C) - IV, (D) - III","(A) - I, (B) - II, (C) - IV, (D) - III","(A) - III, (B) - I, (C) - IV, (D) - II"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps disinfectant and broad-spectrum (SO₂ ↔ chloramphenicol); (B) makes SO₂ a narrow-spectrum antibiotic; (C) swaps furacin and penicillin.\nWorking: penicillin G – narrow-spectrum antibiotic; furacin – antiseptic; SO₂ (in low concentration) – disinfectant; chloramphenicol – broad-spectrum antibiotic → A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV = (A).\nKey idea: SO₂ is a disinfectant; chloramphenicol is broad spectrum.","traps":["Antiseptic vs disinfectant: Antiseptic on living tissue; disinfectant on objects."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":71,"q":"A doctor prescribed the drug Equanil to a patient. The patient was likely to have symptoms of which disease?","options":["Stomach ulcers","Depression and hypertension","Anxiety and stress","Hyperacidity"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) anxiety and stress is true of tranquillisers in general, so it competes with the NCERT wording; (A)/(D) belong to antacids such as ranitidine.\nWorking: Equanil is used in controlling depression and hypertension; (C) is a close distractor because it is a tranquilliser.\nKey idea: Follow the NCERT phrase: equanil – depression and hypertension.","traps":["Tranquilliser general vs specific use: Recall the NCERT line for equanil."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":72,"q":"Amongst the following compounds, which one is an antacid?","options":["Ranitidine","Brompheniramine","Terfenadine","Meprobamate"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B, C: antihistamines (same NCERT paragraph). D: meprobamate is a tranquilliser.\nWorking: ranitidine blocks H₂ receptors and reduces acid secretion, so it is an antacid; brompheniramine and terfenadine are antihistamines and meprobamate is a tranquilliser → (A).\nKey idea: Ranitidine is an H₂-receptor antagonist used as an antacid.","traps":["Antihistamine vs antacid: Antacid H₂ blockers: cimetidine, ranitidine; antihistamines: brompheniramine, terfenadine."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C27","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"Assertion A: Antihistamines do not affect the secretion of acid in stomach. Reason R: Antiallergic and antacid drugs work on different receptors. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): R seen as true but unrelated; (A): believing antihistamines reduce acid (confusing with cimetidine/ranitidine); (D): denying receptor difference.\nWorking: antiallergic antihistamines (brompheniramine, terfenadine) act on different receptors from those controlling acid secretion (blocked by cimetidine, ranitidine), so they do not affect stomach acid. Both A and R are true and R explains A.\nKey idea: Antiallergics act on H₁ receptors, acid-secretion drugs on H₂ receptors.","traps":["Histamine and acid secretion: Antihistamines (antiallergic) bind different receptors than cimetidine-type drugs."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":79,"q":"Which of the following artificial sweeteners has the highest sweetness value in comparison to cane sugar ?","options":["Alitame","Sucralose","Saccharin","Aspartame"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) sucralose 600 and (C) saccharin 550 are close and often confused as highest. (D) aspartame only 100.\nWorking: alitame ≈ 2000, sucralose 600, saccharin 550, aspartame 100 times cane sugar.\nKey idea: Alitame is the most potent (~2000×).","traps":["Saccharin remembered as sweetest: Recall alitame 2000 > sucralose 600 > saccharin 550 > aspartame 100."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":77,"q":"Which of the following compounds are not used as disinfectants? A. Chloroxylenol B. Bithional C. Veronal D. Prontosil E. Terpineol. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A, B","C, D","B, D, E","A, B, E"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) include Dettol ingredients. (C) includes bithional and terpineol, which are antiseptic.\nWorking: Veronal is a barbiturate tranquilliser and Prontosil a sulpha antibacterial; chloroxylenol and terpineol (Dettol) and bithional (in soaps) are antiseptic/disinfectant agents. So C and D are not disinfectants.\nKey idea: Veronal = barbiturate (tranquilliser); Prontosil = first sulpha drug (antibacterial).","traps":["Antiseptic vs disinfectant boundary: Treat the antiseptic/disinfectant group together vs other drug classes.","Prontosil as antiseptic: Prontosil is a systemic sulpha drug."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Drugs used to bind to receptors, inhibiting its natural function and blocking a message are called:","options":["Agonists","Antagonists","Allosterists","Anti histaminists"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) agonists do the opposite (mimic the messenger); (C) invented term from allosteric site; (D) a drug class, not a mechanism term.\nWorking: antagonists bind to receptor sites and block the message; agonists mimic the natural messenger.\nKey idea: Anti-agonist = blocks.","traps":["Agonist vs antagonist: Antagonist blocks; agonist mimics."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Some drugs bind to a site other than the active site of an enzyme. This site is known as","options":["non-active site","allosteric site","competitive site","therapeutic site"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) \"competitive\" describes inhibitors that bind at the active site; (A)/(D) are invented terms.\nWorking: old NCERT – some drugs bind to a different site of the enzyme called the allosteric site, changing the shape of the active site.\nKey idea: Allosteric = \"other site\".","traps":["Competitive vs allosteric: Competitive inhibitors bind at the active site itself."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Soaps & detergents","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Stearic acid and polyethylene glycol react to form which one of the following soap/s detergents?","options":["Cationic detergent","Soap","Anionic detergent","Non-ionic detergent"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) soap would need a metal salt of stearic acid; (C) anionic detergents are sulphonates/sulphates; (A) cationic detergents are quaternary ammonium salts.\nWorking: esterification of stearic acid with polyethylene glycol gives an ester with no ionic group – a non-ionic detergent (used in liquid dishwashing detergents).\nKey idea: Ester of fatty acid + PEG has no charge → non-ionic.","traps":["Stearic acid suggests soap: PEG forms an ester, no metal ion."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S2C30","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"How many of the following drugs is/are example(s) of broad spectrum antibiotic ? Ofloxacin, Penicillin G, Terpineol, Salvarsan","type":"num","answer":"1","range":[1.0,1.0],"explain":"Common slips: Counting penicillin G gives 2; counting salvarsan as broad gives 2 or 3; terpineol is an antiseptic, not an antibiotic.\nWorking: only ofloxacin is broad spectrum; penicillin G is narrow spectrum, terpineol is an antiseptic (in Dettol), salvarsan is an arsenical used against syphilis (narrow).\nKey idea: Old NCERT lists ofloxacin as broad spectrum and penicillin G as narrow.","traps":["Penicillin G spectrum: NCERT: penicillin G is narrow spectrum.","Antiseptic vs antibiotic: Terpineol is an antiseptic component of Dettol."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C18","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Soaps & detergents","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Which of the following enhances the lathering property of soap?","options":["Sodium stearate","Sodium carbonate","Sodium rosinate","Trisodium phosphate"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is the soap itself; (B)/(D) are builders/fillers that soften water but are remembered as 'improve washing'.\nWorking: old NCERT notes that sodium rosinate is added to soaps (e.g. laundry soaps) to make them lather well; sodium stearate is the soap itself, sodium carbonate and trisodium phosphate are builders.\nKey idea: Sodium rosinate = lathering agent in laundry soaps.","traps":["Builders vs lathering agent: Remember the specific old NCERT line on sodium rosinate."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Statements about Enzyme Inhibitor Drugs are given below: (A) There are Competitive and Non-competitive inhibitor drugs. (B) These can bind at the active sites and allosteric sites. (C) Competitive Drugs are allosteric site blocking drugs. (D) Non-competitive Drugs are active site blocking drugs. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["(A), (D) only","(A), (C) only","(A), (B) only","(A), (B), (C) only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (B) each accept one swapped statement; (D) adds C.\nWorking: competitive inhibitors compete with the substrate at the active site; non-competitive inhibitors bind at an allosteric site. So (A) and (B) are true and (C), (D) have the sites swapped.\nKey idea: Competitive = active site; non-competitive = allosteric site.","traps":["Swapped binding sites: \"Competitive\" means competing with substrate for the same (active) site."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Two statements in respect of drug-enzyme interaction are given below. Statement I: Action of an enzyme can be blocked only when an inhibitor blocks the active site of the enzyme. Statement II: An inhibitor can form a strong covalent bond with the enzyme. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) misses \"only\"; (B) also rejects the covalent-inhibitor fact; (C) reverses both.\nWorking: inhibitors can also bind at an allosteric site and change the active site shape, so \"only\" makes Statement I false; some inhibitors bind irreversibly through a strong covalent bond, so Statement II is true.\nKey idea: Allosteric binding also inhibits; covalent binding gives irreversible inhibition.","traps":["\"Only\" in Statement I: Recall allosteric inhibition."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following compounds is an example of hypnotic drug ?","options":["Seldane","Amytal","Aspartame","Prontosil"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) antihistamine; (C) sweetener; (D) antibacterial.\nWorking: Amytal (amobarbital) is a barbiturate – a hypnotic (sleep-producing) tranquilliser. Seldane is an antihistamine, aspartame an artificial sweetener, Prontosil an antibacterial (sulpha drug).\nKey idea: Barbiturates (veronal, amytal…) are hypnotics.","traps":["Drug names mixed up: Learn the NCERT examples by class."]},{"id":"JM22Jul29S2C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The number of chlorine atoms in bithionol is ____.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 2 (one dichlorophenol unit only); 6 (confused with hexachlorophene).\nWorking: bithionol is 2,2'-thiobis(4,6-dichlorophenol), C₁₂H₆Cl₄O₂S – 4 Cl atoms.\nKey idea: Two 4,6-dichlorophenol rings bridged by S.","traps":["Bithionol vs hexachlorophene: Bithionol: S bridge, 4 Cl."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following is not a broad spectrum antibiotic?","options":["Vancomycin","Ampicillin","Ofloxacin","Penicillin G"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) vancomycin may be chosen by students who know its real Gram-positive spectrum; (B) ampicillin may be thought narrow because it is a penicillin.\nWorking: NCERT lists ampicillin, amoxycillin, vancomycin, ofloxacin and chloramphenicol as broad-spectrum; penicillin G has a narrow spectrum. (Pharmacologically vancomycin is mainly Gram-positive, but the question follows NCERT.)\nKey idea: NCERT: penicillin G = narrow spectrum.","traps":["All penicillins thought alike: Ampicillin is a semi-synthetic broad-spectrum penicillin; penicillin G is narrow."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The structure shown below is of which well-known drug molecule? [Structure: 5-methyl-1H-imidazol-4-yl–CH₂–S–CH₂CH₂–NH–C(=N–CN)–NHCH₃]","options":["Ranitidine","Seldane","Cimetidine","Codeine"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ranitidine – same drug class and similar side chain, but contains a furan ring and a nitro group; (B) Seldane (terfenadine) – an antihistamine, not antacid; (D) Codeine – opiate analgesic, polycyclic.\nWorking: imidazole ring + thioether chain + N-cyanoguanidine is cimetidine (Tagamet), an H₂-receptor antagonist antacid. Ranitidine has a furan ring and a nitroethenediamine group.\nKey idea: Imidazole + cyanoguanidine = cimetidine; furan + nitroethene = ranitidine.","traps":["Ranitidine and cimetidine confused: Look at the ring: imidazole (two N) → cimetidine; furan (O) → ranitidine."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Which one of the following is an example of artificial sweetener?","options":["Bithional","Alitame","Salvarsan","Lactose"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) lactose – sweet but natural; (A) antiseptic; (C) antimicrobial drug.\nWorking: alitame is a high-potency artificial sweetener; bithional is an antiseptic, salvarsan an arsenical antimicrobial, lactose a natural sugar.\nKey idea: Artificial sweeteners: saccharin, aspartame, sucralose, alitame.","traps":["Natural sugar chosen: 'Artificial' excludes natural sugars."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"An antiseptic dettol is a mixture of two compounds 'A' and 'B' where A has 6π electrons and B has 2π electrons. What is 'B'?","options":["Bithionol","Terpineol","Chloroxylenol","Chloramphenicol"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) chloroxylenol is component A (6π); (A) bithionol is the soap antiseptic; (D) chloramphenicol is an antibiotic.\nWorking: Dettol = chloroxylenol (aromatic ring, 6π) + α-terpineol (one ring C=C, 2π). B = terpineol.\nKey idea: Terpineol is a monoterpene alcohol with one C=C.","traps":["Which component is B: 6π = aromatic ring (chloroxylenol); 2π = one C=C (terpineol).","Antiseptic vs antibiotic names: Chloramphenicol is a broad-spectrum antibiotic."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"A chemical which stimulates the secretion of pepsin is :","options":["Anti histamine","Cimetidine","Histamine","Zantac"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) cimetidine and Zantac – antacids that block histamine, appear in the same NCERT paragraph; (A) anti-histamine – opposes histamine.\nWorking: histamine stimulates the secretion of pepsin and HCl in the stomach; cimetidine and Zantac (ranitidine) block this action, and antihistamines block histamine receptors = (C).\nKey idea: Histamine stimulates acid/pepsin; cimetidine and ranitidine (Zantac) inhibit.","traps":["Antacid drug chosen: Stimulator vs blocker."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Soaps & detergents","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of synthetic detergent?","options":["CH₃–(CH₂)₁₁–C₆H₄–SO₃⁻Na⁺ (sodium dodecylbenzenesulphonate)","CH₃–(CH₂)₁₆–COO⁻Na⁺","[CH₃–(CH₂)₁₅–N⁺(CH₃)₃]Br⁻ (cetyltrimethylammonium bromide)","CH₃(CH₂)₁₆COO(CH₂CH₂O)ₙCH₂CH₂OH"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (C), (D) – detergent examples straight from old NCERT; (D) looks like an ester/soap and may tempt.\nWorking: sodium stearate is a soap (sodium salt of a fatty acid); (A) anionic, (C) cationic and (D) non-ionic are synthetic detergents.\nKey idea: Carboxylate sodium salt of a fatty acid = soap.","traps":["Non-ionic detergent taken as soap: Esterified with polyethylene glycol – no carboxylate salt, so a non-ionic detergent."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Soaps & detergents","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (A) [CH₃(CH₂)₁₅–N⁺(CH₃)₃] Br⁻ (cetyltrimethylammonium bromide); (B) CH₃–(CH₂)₁₁–C₆H₄–SO₃⁻Na⁺ (sodium dodecylbenzenesulphonate); (C) C₁₇H₃₅COO⁻Na⁺ + Na₂CO₃ + rosinate; (D) CH₃(CH₂)₁₆COO(CH₂CH₂O)ₙCH₂CH₂OH. List-II: (I) Dishwashing powder, (II) Toothpaste, (III) Laundry soap, (IV) Hair conditioner.","options":["(A)–(III), (B)–(II), (C)–(IV), (D)–(I)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(II), (C)–(III), (D)–(I)","(A)–(IV), (B)–(III), (C)–(II), (D)–(I)","(A)–(III), (B)–(IV), (C)–(I), (D)–(II)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) – cationic salt taken as laundry soap; (C) – swaps anionic detergent (toothpaste) and laundry soap.\nWorking: cationic detergent (CTAB) – hair conditioners; anionic alkylbenzenesulphonate – household detergents/toothpaste; sodium stearate with Na₂CO₃ and sodium rosinate fillers – laundry soap; non-ionic polyethylene glycol stearate – liquid dishwashing detergents → (B).\nKey idea: Charge on the head group tells the class: N⁺ = cationic (conditioner), SO₃⁻ = anionic (toothpaste/household), PEG ester = non-ionic (dishwashing).","traps":["Rosinate mixture vs detergent: Na₂CO₃ + rosinate are laundry-soap fillers.","Non-ionic ester not recognised: Polyethylene glycol ester = non-ionic → dishwashing liquid."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Match List I with List II. List I: A. Antipyretic; B. Analgesic; C. Tranquilizer; D. Antacid. List II: I. Reduces pain; II. Reduces stress; III. Reduces fever; IV. Reduces acidity (Stomach). Choose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) swaps antipyretic and analgesic; (B) swaps tranquilizer and antacid; (C) shifts three pairs.\nWorking: antipyretic lowers fever, analgesic relieves pain, tranquilizer relieves stress/anxiety, antacid neutralises excess stomach acid.\nKey idea: Pyretic = fever; algesia = pain.","traps":["Antipyretic vs analgesic: Pyrexia = fever; analgesia = pain relief."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Which of the following compound does not contain sulphur atom ?","options":["Cimetidine","Ranitidine","Histamine","Saccharin"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B) both have a –CH₂SCH₂– link; (D) has SO₂ in the ring – students may forget one of them.\nWorking: histamine is 2-(imidazol-4-yl)ethanamine (C, H, N only); cimetidine (thioether), ranitidine (thioether + nitro), saccharin (sulfonyl imide) contain S.\nKey idea: Histamine is an imidazole + ethylamine – no S.","traps":["Recall of all four structures: Saccharin = o-sulfobenzimide."],"topics":["Drugs","Food chemicals"]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The mixture of chloroxylenol and terpineol is an example of:","options":["antiseptic","pesticide","disinfectant","narcotic analgesic"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) disinfectant is the closest distractor (same chemicals at higher concentration can disinfect); (B), (D) unrelated.\nWorking: chloroxylenol + terpineol is Dettol, an antiseptic.\nKey idea: Dettol = chloroxylenol + terpineol = antiseptic.","traps":["Antiseptic vs disinfectant: Antiseptics are applied to living tissue; Dettol is listed as antiseptic."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C08","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Structures (A)–(D) are shown: (A) morphine (N-methyl morphinan with phenolic OH, 4,5-epoxy bridge and allylic OH); (B) diazepam (7-chloro-1-methyl-5-phenyl-1,3-dihydro-2H-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one); (C) serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine: 3-(2-aminoethyl)-1H-indol-5-ol); (D) codeine (morphine with the phenolic OH replaced by OCH₃). The correct statement about (A), (B), (C) and (D) is:","options":["(A), (B) and (C) are narcotic analgesics","(B), (C) and (D) are tranquillizers","(A) and (D) are tranquillizers","(B) and (C) are tranquillizers"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts diazepam and serotonin with narcotics; (B) adds codeine to tranquillizers; (C) calls the opioids tranquillizers.\nWorking: morphine (A) and codeine (D) are narcotic analgesics; diazepam/valium (B) and serotonin (C) are listed by old NCERT as tranquillizers.\nKey idea: The morphinan skeleton (A, D) = opioid analgesics; benzodiazepine and indole-amine = tranquillizer group.","traps":["Codeine recognised as different drug: Same morphinan skeleton = narcotic analgesic.","Serotonin as neurotransmitter only: Old NCERT lists serotonin among tranquilizers."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S2C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Which one of the following chemicals is responsible for the production of HCl in the stomach leading to irritation and pain?","options":["Saccharin (1,2-benzisothiazol-3(2H)-one 1,1-dioxide; benzene fused to a five-membered ring with –CO–NH–SO₂–)","Histamine, 2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanamine","Serotonin, 5-hydroxytryptamine","Phenelzine, (2-phenylethyl)hydrazine, C₆H₅CH₂CH₂NHNH₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) saccharin – artificial sweetener; (C) serotonin-type – neurotransmitter, confused with \"body amine\"; (D) phenelzine – antidepressant (hydrazine).\nWorking: histamine stimulates the secretion of pepsin and HCl in the stomach (antacids like cimetidine/ranitidine block its receptors).\nKey idea: Imidazole ring + ethylamine side chain = histamine.","traps":["Similar ethylamine side chains: Look at the ring: imidazole (two N, five-membered) = histamine.","Serotonin vs histamine: Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is a neurotransmitter; histamine triggers acid secretion in the stomach."]},{"id":"JM21Feb24S2C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Match List-I and List-II. List-I: (a) Valium; (b) Morphine; (c) Norethindrone; (d) Vitamin B₁₂. List-II: (i) Antifertility drug; (ii) Pernicious anaemia; (iii) Analgesic; (iv) Tranquilizer.","options":["(a)–(iv), (b)–(iii), (c)–(ii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(iii), (c)–(i), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(iii), (d)–(i)","(a)–(i), (b)–(iii), (c)–(iv), (d)–(ii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps (c) and (d); (C) and (D) misplace Valium.\nWorking: Valium tranquilizer, morphine narcotic analgesic, norethindrone antifertility, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency → pernicious anaemia.\nKey idea: Norethindrone is a synthetic progesterone derivative → antifertility.","traps":["Norethindrone vs B₁₂: Remember B₁₂ ↔ pernicious anaemia; the rest follows."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Drug): (a) Furacin (b) Arsphenamine (c) Dimetone (d) Valium\nList-II (Class of Drug): (i) Antibiotic (ii) Tranquilizers (iii) Antiseptic (iv) Synthetic antihistamines\nChoose the most appropriate match:","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps Furacin and arsphenamine (antibiotic vs antiseptic); (B) shifts three pairs; (C) Furacin as tranquilizer.\nWorking: Furacin – antiseptic; arsphenamine (salvarsan) – antimicrobial/antibiotic (Ehrlich); dimetone – synthetic antihistamine; Valium – tranquilliser. Option (D).\nKey idea: Furacin and Dettol-type = antiseptic; salvarsan = Ehrlich's antimicrobial; Valium = tranquilizer.","traps":["Antiseptic vs antibiotic: Furacin is applied to skin wounds = antiseptic."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The functions of antihistamine are:","options":["Antiallergic and Analgesic","Antacid and antiallergic","Analgesic and antacid","Antiallergic and antidepressant"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) – analgesic is a separate drug class; (D) – antidepressants are tranquilisers.\nWorking: antihistamines (e.g. brompheniramine, terfenadine) block H₁ receptors – antiallergic; H₂ blockers (cimetidine, ranitidine) cut gastric acid secretion – antacid.\nKey idea: Histamine causes both allergy symptoms and acid secretion, so its blockers are antiallergic and antacid.","traps":["Antacid role forgotten: Cimetidine/ranitidine are antihistamines used as antacids."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C01","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"With respect to drug-enzyme interaction, identify the wrong statement:","options":["Non-Competitive inhibitor binds to the allosteric site","Allosteric inhibitor changes the enzyme's active site","Allosteric inhibitor competes with the enzyme's active site","Competitive inhibitor binds to the enzyme's active site"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are correct NCERT statements and act as distractors for a student who reads \"allosteric\" and \"active site\" loosely.\nWorking: an allosteric (non-competitive) inhibitor binds at a different site and changes the shape of the active site; it does not compete for the active site – only a competitive inhibitor does.\nKey idea: Only a competitive inhibitor competes for the active site; an allosteric one binds elsewhere and distorts it.","traps":["Allosteric vs competitive swapped: Competes = same site; allosteric = other site that changes the active site.","Asked for the wrong statement: Mark each statement T/F before choosing."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Match List-I with List-II :\nList-I (Chemical Compound): (a) Sucralose (b) Glyceryl ester of stearic acid (c) Sodium benzoate (d) Bithionol\nList-II (Used as): (i) Synthetic detergent (ii) Artificial sweetener (iii) Antiseptic (iv) Food preservative\nChoose the correct match :","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) puts sucralose as detergent and the ester as sweetener; (A) and (C) shift sodium benzoate or bithionol.\nWorking: sucralose – artificial sweetener; sodium benzoate – food preservative; bithionol – antiseptic (added to soaps); the glyceryl/polyethylene-glycol ester of stearic acid is the old-NCERT example of a non-ionic synthetic detergent, so (b)-(i) by elimination.\nKey idea: Anchor on sucralose (sweetener) and sodium benzoate (preservative); the rest falls out.","traps":["Glyceryl stearate seen as a fat, not detergent: Use elimination: the only remaining use is synthetic detergent (non-ionic).","Bithionol use: Bithionol = antiseptic added to soap."],"topics":["Food chemicals","Drugs","Soaps & detergents"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S1C06","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Match the List-I with List-II. List-I (Class of Drug): (a) Antacid (b) Artificial sweetener (c) Antifertility (d) Tranquilizers. List-II (Example): (i) Novestrol (ii) Cimetidine (iii) Valium (iv) Alitame","options":["(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(i), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(i), (c)–(ii), (d)–(iii)","(a)–(iv), (b)–(iii), (c)–(i), (d)–(ii)","(a)–(ii), (b)–(iv), (c)–(iii), (d)–(i)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B), (C) put alitame under antacid; (D) swaps novestrol and valium (antifertility vs tranquilizer).\nWorking: cimetidine – antacid (H₂ blocker); alitame – artificial sweetener; novestrol (ethynylestradiol) – antifertility; valium – tranquilizer.\nKey idea: Anchor on the sure pair (cimetidine–antacid), then alitame–sweetener; only (A) and (D) remain.","traps":["Novestrol vs valium: Novestrol = synthetic oestrogen (birth control); valium = diazepam (tranquilizer)."],"topics":["Drugs","Food chemicals"]},{"id":"JM21Mar18S2C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 18 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List - I with List - II :\nList - I (Class of Chemicals): (a) Antifertility drug (b) Antibiotic (c) Tranquilizer (d) Artificial Sweetener\nList - II (Example): (i) Meprobamate (ii) Alitame (iii) Norethindrone (iv) Salvarsan","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) differs from the correct match by swapping (a) and (d) (alitame vs norethindrone); (A) and (B) mismatch salvarsan and meprobamate.\nWorking: norethindrone – antifertility; salvarsan – antibiotic (Ehrlich's arsphenamine); meprobamate – tranquilizer; alitame – artificial sweetener.\nKey idea: Learn one signature example per drug class.","traps":["Alitame vs norethindrone swapped: Alitame (like aspartame) = sweetener."],"topics":["Drugs","Food chemicals"]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"The number of sp² hybridised carbons present in \"Aspartame\" is ______.","type":"num","answer":"9","range":[9.0,9.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (missing the ester or acid C=O); 6 (only ring carbons); 10 (counting the OCH₃ or α-carbon as sp²).\nWorking: aspartame = HOOC–CH₂–CH(NH₂)–CO–NH–CH(CH₂C₆H₅)–COOCH₃ (Asp-Phe methyl ester): 6 aromatic C + 3 carbonyl C (acid, amide, ester) = 9.\nKey idea: Count ring carbons plus every C=O carbon.","traps":["A carbonyl carbon missed: List each functional group before counting.","Aspartame structure not known: Learn: Asp–Phe–OMe."]},{"id":"JM20Jan09S1C04","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Food chemicals","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":4,"q":"A chemist has 4 samples of artificial sweetener A, B, C and D. To identify these samples, he performed certain experiments and noted the following observations: (i) A and D both form blue-violet colour with ninhydrin. (ii) Lassaigne extract of C gives positive AgNO₃ test and negative Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ test. (iii) Lassaigne extract of B and D gives positive sodium nitroprusside test. Based on these observations which option is correct?","options":["A : Aspartame; B : Saccharin; C : Sucralose; D : Alitame","A : Alitame; B : Saccharin; C : Aspartame; D : Sucralose","A : Saccharin; B : Alitame; C : Sucralose; D : Aspartame","A : Aspartame; B : Alitame; C : Saccharin; D : Sucralose"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) put sucralose as D though D must contain S and N; (C) assigns ninhydrin-positive A to saccharin, which has no free amino group.\nWorking: ninhydrin-positive (free α-amino group, dipeptide-derived) → aspartame and alitame; halogen but no N → sucralose (C); sulfur (nitroprusside) → saccharin and alitame (thietane S). D is both ninhydrin- and S-positive → alitame; so A aspartame, B saccharin.\nKey idea: Alitame is the only sweetener with N, S and a free amine; sucralose is the only one with Cl and no N.","traps":["Saccharin taken as ninhydrin-positive: Ninhydrin needs a free α-amino acid unit; saccharin is a sulfonimide.","Forgetting S in alitame: Alitame has a thietane (S) ring."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S2C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Soaps & detergents","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"If you spill a chemical toilet cleaning liquid on your hand, your first aid would be :","options":["aqueous NH₃","vinegar","aqueous NaHCO₃","aqueous NaOH"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) aqueous NH₃ is basic but irritant/volatile; (B) vinegar is an acid and adds to the problem; (D) NaOH neutralises but is strongly corrosive to skin.\nWorking: toilet cleaners are acidic (HCl-based); a mild base such as NaHCO₃ neutralises the acid safely, while NaOH and NH₃ are themselves corrosive/irritant and vinegar is an acid.\nKey idea: Neutralise a spilt acid on skin with a weak base (NaHCO₃), never a strong alkali.","traps":["Any base taken as safe: Choose a weak base for skin contact.","Cleaner assumed basic: Toilet bowl cleaners are HCl-based."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The antifertility drug \"Novestrol\" can react with:","options":["Br₂/water; ZnCl₂/HCl; FeCl₃","Alcoholic HCN; NaOCl; ZnCl₂/HCl","Br₂/water; ZnCl₂/HCl; NaOCl","ZnCl₂/HCl; FeCl₃; Alcoholic HCN"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) include alcoholic HCN (needs C=O); (C) includes NaOCl (haloform needs CH₃CO–).\nWorking: novestrol (ethynylestradiol) has a phenol (FeCl₃ colour, Br₂-water substitution and also adds to C≡C), a 3° alcohol (Lucas, ZnCl₂/HCl) and a terminal alkyne; no carbonyl, so no HCN; NaOCl (haloform) needs a methyl ketone/CH₃CH(OH)–.\nKey idea: Phenol + 3° OH + alkyne; no carbonyl.","traps":["Steroid assumed to have a ketone: Novestrol is estradiol-based: phenol, no ketone."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S2C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Match the following drugs with their therapeutic actions :\n(i) Ranitidine (a) Antidepressant\n(ii) Nardil (Phenelzine) (b) Antibiotic\n(iii) Chloramphenicol (c) Antihistamine\n(iv) Dimetane (Brompheniramine) (d) Antacid\n (e) Analgesic","options":["(i)-(a); (ii)-(c); (iii)-(b); (iv)-(e)","(i)-(e); (ii)-(a); (iii)-(c); (iv)-(d)","(i)-(d); (ii)-(a); (iii)-(b); (iv)-(c)","(i)-(d); (ii)-(c); (iii)-(a); (iv)-(e)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Ranitidine as antidepressant; (B) Ranitidine as analgesic, chloramphenicol antihistamine; (D) chloramphenicol antidepressant.\nWorking: ranitidine – antacid (H₂-blocker); Nardil – antidepressant (MAO inhibitor); chloramphenicol – broad-spectrum antibiotic; brompheniramine (Dimetane) – antihistamine.\nKey idea: Ranitidine (Zantac) is an H₂-receptor antacid; Dimetane is an antihistamine.","traps":["Ranitidine thought antihistamine: NCERT places ranitidine under antacids."]},{"id":"JM20Sep04S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The mechanism of action of \"Terfenadine\" (Seldane) is :","options":["Activates the histamine receptor","Inhibits the secretion of histamine","Inhibits the action of histamine receptor","Helps in the secretion of histamine"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) agonist instead of antagonist; (B) and (D) confuse action on receptor with histamine secretion (antacid/secretion confusion).\nWorking: terfenadine is an antihistamine; it competes with histamine for its receptor and blocks its action.\nKey idea: Antihistamines are receptor antagonists, not secretion inhibitors.","traps":["Blocking receptor vs stopping secretion: Antihistamine = receptor blocker."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"If a person is suffering from the deficiency of nor-adrenaline, what kind of drug can be suggested ?","options":["Anti-inflammatory","Analgesic","Antihistamine","Antidepressant"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (C) treat inflammation, pain and allergy respectively – unrelated to neurotransmitter levels.\nWorking: low noradrenaline lowers signal transmission and causes depression; antidepressants inhibit its metabolising enzymes.\nKey idea: Noradrenaline deficiency → depression → antidepressant.","traps":["Drug classes mixed up: Link neurotransmitter to mood."]},{"id":"JM20Sep05S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"The following molecule acts as an:\nStructure: (CH₃)₂N–(CH₂)₂–CH(2-pyridyl)(4-bromophenyl)","options":["Antiseptic","Anti-bacterial","Anti-histamine","Anti-depressant"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) antiseptic, (B) antibacterial, (D) antidepressant – other drug classes from the same chapter; the amine side chain may suggest a neurologically active (antidepressant) drug.\nWorking: brompheniramine (Dimetapp) is a classic antihistamine (H₁ blocker).\nKey idea: Brompheniramine is listed in NCERT as an antihistamine (Dimetapp).","traps":["Amine drug read as antidepressant: Recall the NCERT antihistamine list by name."]},{"id":"JM19Apr09S2C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Noradrenaline is a/an","options":["Neurotransmitter","Antidepressant","Antihistamine","Antacid"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) antidepressant: NCERT links noradrenaline to depression and antidepressant drugs, so it is tempting; (C)/(D) unrelated drug classes.\nWorking: noradrenaline (norepinephrine) is a neurotransmitter; drugs that act on its levels are antidepressants, but it is not one itself.\nKey idea: Noradrenaline is the messenger; antidepressants modify its level.","traps":["Messenger vs drug acting on it: Noradrenaline is the neurotransmitter itself."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S1C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"The correct match between Item-I and Item-II is :\nItem-I (drug): (A) Chloroxylenol (B) Norethindrone (C) Sulphapyridine (D) Penicillin\nItem-II (test): (P) Carbylamine test (Q) Sodium hydrogen carbonate test (R) Ferric chloride test (S) Baeyer's test","options":["A→Q; B→P; C→S; D→R","A→R; B→P; C→S; D→Q","A→R; B→S; C→P; D→Q","A→Q; B→S; C→P; D→R"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D): chloroxylenol with NaHCO₃. (B): norethindrone with carbylamine, sulphapyridine with Baeyer's.\nWorking: chloroxylenol is a phenol (FeCl₃, R); norethindrone has C≡CH/C=C (Baeyer's, S); sulphapyridine has a free aromatic –NH₂ (carbylamine, P); penicillin has –COOH (NaHCO₃, Q) → option (C).\nKey idea: Phenol–FeCl₃, alkyne–Baeyer, ArNH₂–carbylamine, COOH–NaHCO₃.","traps":["Chloroxylenol matched to NaHCO₃: Phenols do not liberate CO₂ from NaHCO₃; use FeCl₃.","Norethindrone structure unknown: Norethindrone has C≡CH and C=C: Baeyer's test."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"The correct match between Item(I) and Item(II) is:\nItem-I: (A) Norethindrone; (B) Ofloxacin; (C) Equanil\nItem-II: (P) Anti-biotic; (Q) Anti-fertility; (R) Hypertension; (S) Analgesics","options":["A-R, B-P, C-S","A-Q, B-P, C-R","A-R, B-P, C-R","A-Q, B-R, C-S"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) equanil as analgesic. (A)/(C) norethindrone as hypertension drug.\nWorking: norethindrone – antifertility; ofloxacin – antibiotic; equanil (meprobamate) – tranquilizer used for hypertension.\nKey idea: Equanil (meprobamate) is the NCERT tranquilizer for hypertension.","traps":["Equanil category: Recall NCERT phrase 'depression and hypertension'."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S2C09","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Drugs","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"The correct match between item I and item II is :- Item I: (A) Allosteric effect, (B) Competitive inhibitor, (C) Receptor, (D) Poison. Item II: (P) Molecule binding to the active site of enzyme, (Q) Molecule crucial for communication in the body, (R) Molecule binding to a site other than the active site of enzyme, (S) Molecule binding to the enzyme covalently.","options":["(A)→(P); (B)→(R); (C)→(S); (D)→(Q)","(A)→(R); (B)→(P); (C)→(S); (D)→(Q)","(A)→(P); (B)→(R); (C)→(Q); (D)→(S)","(A)→(R); (B)→(P); (C)→(Q); (D)→(S)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps A/B and C/D. (B) swaps C and D (receptor vs poison). (C) swaps A and B (allosteric vs competitive).\nWorking: allosteric site = site other than active site (R); competitive inhibitor competes for the active site (P); receptors are proteins crucial to body communication (Q); a poison binds the enzyme irreversibly/covalently (S).\nKey idea: Allosteric = 'other site'; competitive = 'same (active) site'; poison = covalent/irreversible.","traps":["Allosteric and competitive swapped: Competitive inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site.","Receptor vs poison: Poison = irreversible covalent binding."]}],"m13-purif":[{"id":"JM25Apr03S1C25","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2025,"paper":"Main Apr 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":75,"q":"During estimation of nitrogen by Dumas' method of compound X (0.42 g) (shown below), ________ mL of N₂ gas will be liberated at STP. (nearest integer)\n(Given molar mass in g mol⁻¹ : C : 12, H : 1, N : 14)","type":"num","answer":"109","range":[109.0,109.0],"explain":"Common slips: wrong values: 219 (2 N₂ per molecule), 55 (N atoms halved wrongly), 111 (22.7 L molar volume), 112/107 (wrong molar mass from H miscount).\nWorking: piperazine is C₄H₁₀N₂ (M = 86 g mol⁻¹). Its two N atoms give one N₂ molecule, so n(N₂) = n(X) = 0.42/86 mol and V(N₂) = (0.42/86) × 22400 mL ≈ 109 mL.\nKey idea: Piperazine C₄H₁₀N₂ gives one N₂ per molecule: (0.42/86) × 22400 ≈ 109 mL.","traps":["Molecular formula read wrongly from skeletal ring: Saturated ring with two NH: C₄H₁₀N₂, M = 86.","One N₂ per two N atoms in Dumas: Two N atoms make exactly one N₂ molecule: n(N₂) = n(X) = 0.42/86."],"img":["img/jee/JM25Apr03S1C25-s.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Which of the following nitrogen containing compound does not give Lassaigne's test ?","options":["Glycine","Phenyl hydrazine","Urea","Hydrazine"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) glycine, (B) phenylhydrazine, (C) urea all contain C and N; a student may pick urea (decomposes) or phenyl hydrazine (–NH–NH₂ like hydrazine).\nWorking: Lassaigne's test for N needs C and N together to form NaCN on fusion; hydrazine (N₂H₄) has no carbon.\nKey idea: No carbon → no NaCN → no Prussian blue.","traps":["Phenyl hydrazine linked to hydrazine: Phenyl ring provides carbon.","Diazo/urea exceptions recalled: Urea has C and N together, gives the test."]},{"id":"JM24Apr04S2C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 4 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"The adsorbent used in adsorption chromatography is/are -\nA. silica gel\nB. alumina\nC. quick lime\nD. magnesia\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["C and D only","A only","B only","A and B only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (C) pick only one of the two. (A) picks the metal oxides that sound like alumina analogues.\nWorking: NCERT names silica gel and alumina as the commonly used adsorbents; quick lime and magnesia are not.\nKey idea: Silica gel and alumina are the standard adsorbents.","traps":["Only one adsorbent picked: NCERT names two adsorbents."]},{"id":"JM24Apr05S2C23","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 5 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":23,"q":"Using the given figure (shown below), the ratio of R_f values of sample A and sample C is x × 10⁻². Value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: 200 (C/A), 77 (A/B).\nWorking: Rf(A) = 5.0/12.5 = 0.40; Rf(C) = 10.0/12.5 = 0.80; ratio 0.5 = 50 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Solvent front cancels in a ratio: 5/10.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Rf(A)/Rf(C) = 5/10."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr05S2C23-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S1C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following statements are correct?\nA. Glycerol is purified by vacuum distillation because it decomposes at its normal boiling point.\nB. Aniline can be purified by steam distillation as aniline is miscible in water.\nC. Ethanol can be separated from ethanol water mixture by azeotropic distillation because it forms azeotrope.\nD. An organic compound is pure, if mixed M.P. remains the same.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["A, C, D only","B, C, D only","A, B, D only","A, B, C only"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(C)/(D) all include B, which fails only because 'immiscible' was changed to 'miscible'.\nWorking: A true (distillation under reduced pressure for glycerol); B false – aniline is steam distilled because it is steam-volatile and immiscible with water; C true (ethanol–water azeotrope broken by azeotropic distillation); D true (unchanged mixed m.p. shows purity/identity).\nKey idea: Steam distillation needs the substance to be immiscible with water.","traps":["Miscible vs immiscible in steam distillation: Recall: immiscible, steam-volatile."]},{"id":"JM24Apr06S2C07","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"The correct statement among the following, for a \"chromatography\" purification method is :","options":["R_f of a polar compound is smaller than that of a non-polar compound.","Non-polar compounds are retained at top and polar compounds come down in column chromatography.","Organic compounds run faster than solvent in the thin layer chromatographic plate.","R_f is an integral value."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B): polarity roles reversed. (C): ignores that R_f < 1. (D): R_f wrongly assumed to be a whole number.\nWorking: on polar silica/alumina the polar compound is adsorbed more and moves less, so lower R_f; (B) reversed; (C) solvent front always leads; (D) R_f is a fraction ≤ 1.\nKey idea: More strongly adsorbed (polar) = moves less = smaller R_f.","traps":["Column elution order reversed: Polar compounds stick to polar adsorbent; non-polar elute first.","R_f thought to be a count: R_f is a ratio of distances, between 0 and 1."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S1C20","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Compound): A. Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃·xH₂O B. [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻ C. [Fe(SCN)]²⁺ D. (NH₄)₃PO₄·12MoO₃\nLIST II (Colour): I. Violet II. Blood Red III. Prussian Blue IV. Yellow\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) puts Prussian blue as yellow; (B)/(C) shifted pairings.\nWorking: Prussian blue (N test), violet thionitroprusside complex (S test with nitroprusside), blood-red ferric thiocyanate (N + S together), yellow ammonium phosphomolybdate (P test).\nKey idea: Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ = Prussian blue; Fe(SCN)²⁺ = blood red.","traps":["Violet vs blood red mixed: Nitroprusside → violet; thiocyanate → blood red."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement (I) : Kjeldahl method is applicable to estimate nitrogen in pyridine.\nStatement (II) : The nitrogen present in pyridine can easily be converted into ammonium sulphate in Kjeldahl method.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): student unaware of the exception. (C)/(D): mixed judgement on cause and effect.\nWorking: ring nitrogen (pyridine) is not converted to (NH₄)₂SO₄, so Kjeldahl fails for it; both false.\nKey idea: Kjeldahl fails for nitro, azo, ring N.","traps":["Kjeldahl exceptions: Remember nitro, azo, pyridine exceptions."]},{"id":"JM24Apr08S2C20","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"In qualitative test for identification of presence of phosphorus, the compound is heated with an oxidising agent. Which is further treated with nitric acid and ammonium molybdate respectively. The yellow coloured precipitate obtained is :","options":["Na₃PO₄·12MoO₃","(NH₄)₃PO₄·12(NH₄)₂MoO₄","MoPO₄·21NH₄NO₃","(NH₄)₃PO₄·12MoO₃"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): sodium instead of ammonium. (B): molybdate instead of MoO₃. (C): invented.\nWorking: P oxidised to phosphate gives yellow ammonium phosphomolybdate (NH₄)₃PO₄·12MoO₃.\nKey idea: Ammonium phosphomolybdate = (NH₄)₃PO₄·12MoO₃.","traps":["MoO₃ vs (NH₄)₂MoO₄: Precipitate contains MoO₃ units."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S1C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Methods used for purification of organic compounds are based on:","options":["neither on nature of compound nor on the impurity present.","presence of impurity only.","nature of compound and presence of impurity.","nature of compound only."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D) keep only one factor. (A) negates both.\nWorking: NCERT states the choice of method depends on the nature of the compound and the impurity present.\nKey idea: Both the compound and the impurity decide the method.","traps":["Partial basis: Remember both factors."]},{"id":"JM24Apr09S2C22","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Apr 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"In the given TLC (shown below), the distance of spot A & B are 5 cm & 7 cm, from the bottom of TLC plate, respectively.\nR_f value of B is x × 10⁻¹ times more than A. The value of x is ______.","type":"num","answer":"15","range":[15.0,15.0],"explain":"Common slips: Measuring from the plate bottom gives Rf 0.5 and 0.7 (ratio 1.4 → 14). Ratio of distances from bottom 7/5 = 1.4 → 14. Difference 0.25 → 2.5 (if \"more than\" read as excess).\nWorking: solvent travels 10 − 1 − 1 = 8 cm; A moves 4 cm (Rf 0.5), B moves 6 cm (Rf 0.75); ratio 0.75/0.5 = 1.5 = 15 × 10⁻¹. (\"times more than\" read as ratio.)\nKey idea: Subtract the 1 cm baseline from spot and solvent distances first.","traps":["Distances from plate bottom: Measure all distances from the baseline.","\"Times more than\" wording: The intended reading is the ratio."],"img":["img/jee/JM24Apr09S2C22-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S1C05","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"In Kjeldahl's method for estimation of nitrogen, CuSO₄ acts as :","options":["catalytic agent","hydrolysis agent","reducing agent","oxidising agent"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): conc. H₂SO₄ is the oxidising agent, so student attributes oxidation to CuSO₄. (B): digestion is loosely called hydrolysis. (C): no basis; Cu²⁺ is not a reducing agent.\nWorking: CuSO₄ (with K₂SO₄) catalyses the digestion of the organic compound by conc. H₂SO₄; it is not consumed.\nKey idea: CuSO₄ is a catalyst for digestion; H₂SO₄ does the oxidation.","traps":["Role of H₂SO₄ transferred to CuSO₄: H₂SO₄ oxidises; CuSO₄ only speeds digestion."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C11","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Lassaigne's test is used for detection of :","options":["Phosphorous and halogens only","Nitrogen, Sulphur and Phosphorous only","Nitrogen, Sulphur, phosphorous and halogens","Nitrogen and Sulphur only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) omits N and S. (B) omits halogens. (D) is the common shortened belief (N and S only).\nWorking: NCERT groups N, S, halogens and P under Lassaigne's test; P is actually tested by oxidising with Na₂O₂ and ammonium molybdate, but the NCERT wording is followed.\nKey idea: NCERT lists four: N, S, halogens and P.","traps":["Phosphorus included or not: Follow NCERT wording, which lists all four."]},{"id":"JM24Feb01S2C27","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2024,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"Following Kjeldahl's method, 1 g of organic compound released ammonia, that neutralised 10 mL of 2 M H₂SO₄. The percentage of nitrogen in the compound is ______ %.","type":"num","answer":"56","range":[56.0,56.0],"explain":"Common slips: 28 (H₂SO₄ taken as monoprotic), 112 (doubling twice).\nWorking: H₂SO₄ = 20 mmol → NH₃ = 40 mmol → N = 0.56 g in 1 g → 56%.\nKey idea: 1 mol H₂SO₄ neutralises 2 mol NH₃.","traps":["Basicity of H₂SO₄: Multiply by 2 for H₂SO₄."]},{"id":"JM24Jan27S2C05","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"The technique used for purification of steam volatile water immiscible substances is :","options":["Fractional distillation","Distillation","Fractional distillation under reduced pressure","Steam distillation"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) for liquids with close boiling points. (B) simple distillation for large b.p. difference. (C) reduced pressure for liquids that decompose at their b.p.\nWorking: steam distillation is used for substances that are steam volatile and immiscible with water (e.g. aniline).\nKey idea: The question wording itself defines steam distillation.","traps":["Reduced-pressure distillation chosen: Steam distillation needs immiscibility with water; reduced pressure is for decomposing liquids."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S1C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Appearance of blood red colour, on treatment of the sodium fusion extract of an organic compound with FeSO₄ in presence of concentrated H₂SO₄ indicates the presence of element/s","options":["Br","S","N and S","N"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) N alone gives Prussian blue; (B) S alone gives black PbS or violet nitroprusside colour; (A) Br is tested with AgNO₃.\nWorking: when N and S are both present NaSCN forms; Fe³⁺ (from aerial oxidation of Fe²⁺ in the test) gives blood-red [Fe(SCN)]²⁺ instead of Prussian blue.\nKey idea: Blood red = Fe(SCN)²⁺ = N and S together.","traps":["FeSO₄ test linked only to N: Colour decides: blue = N only, blood red = N + S."]},{"id":"JM24Jan29S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Chromatographic technique/s based on the principle of differential adsorption is / are\nA. Column chromatography\nB. Thin layer chromatography\nC. Paper chromatography\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["B only","A only","A & B only","C only"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B only / (B) A only: half the adsorption pair. (D) C only: assumes paper (cellulose) adsorbs.\nWorking: column and TLC are adsorption chromatography; paper chromatography is partition chromatography.\nKey idea: Paper chromatography works by partition between water held on paper and the mobile phase.","traps":["Paper chromatography seen as adsorption: Paper = stationary water phase → partition."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"The Lassaigne's extract is boiled with dil HNO₃ before testing for halogens because,","options":["Silver halides are soluble in HNO₃.","AgCN is soluble in HNO₃.","Na₂S and NaCN are decomposed by HNO₃.","Ag₂S is soluble in HNO₃."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) False: silver halides are insoluble in HNO₃ – that is why the test works. (B) AgCN would precipitate; the aim is to remove CN⁻ before AgNO₃ is added, not dissolve AgCN. (D) Ag₂S is not dissolved; S²⁻ is expelled as H₂S beforehand.\nWorking: boiling with dil. HNO₃ expels HCN and H₂S from NaCN and Na₂S, so they cannot give AgCN/Ag₂S precipitates that would interfere with the AgNO₃ halide test.\nKey idea: Remove interfering CN⁻ and S²⁻ (as HCN, H₂S) before adding Ag⁺.","traps":["Solubility reason chosen instead of removal: The acid acts before AgNO₃ is added – it destroys CN⁻/S²⁻."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S1C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"On a thin layer chromatographic plate, an organic compound moved by 3.5 cm, while the solvent moved by 5 cm. The retardation factor of the organic compound is _______ × 10⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 14 (inverting ratio 5/3.5 = 1.43), 0.7 (ignoring ×10⁻¹ scaling).\nWorking: Rf = 3.5/5 = 0.7 = 7 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Rf = distance of spot / distance of solvent front (always < 1).","traps":["Ratio inverted: Rf must be less than 1."]},{"id":"JM24Jan30S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which among the following purification methods is based on the principle of \"Solubility\" in two different solvents?","options":["Distillation","Sublimation","Column Chromatography","Differential Extraction"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) based on boiling point difference. (B) based on direct solid → vapour change. (C) based on differential adsorption (column), tempting because a solvent (eluent) is used.\nWorking: differential extraction uses the different solubility of the compound in two immiscible solvents (e.g. water and an organic solvent).\nKey idea: 'Two solvents' + 'solubility' = extraction; chromatography is about adsorption/partition between phases.","traps":["Chromatography mistaken for solubility-based: Column chromatography separates by differential adsorption."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I (Technique): (A) Distillation (B) Fractional distillation (C) Steam distillation (D) Distillation under reduced pressure\nList-II (Application): (I) Separation of glycerol from spent-lye (II) Aniline - Water mixture (III) Separation of crude oil fractions (IV) Chloroform - Aniline\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"Option (C): crude oil and glycerol swapped. Options (A)/(B): aniline–water put under simple or fractional distillation.\nWorking: CHCl₃ (b.p. 334 K) and aniline (457 K) differ widely → simple distillation; crude oil fractions → fractional; aniline–water → steam distillation; glycerol from spent-lye → reduced pressure.\nKey idea: Large b.p. gap → simple; small gap → fractional; steam-volatile immiscible → steam; decomposes at b.p. → reduced pressure.","traps":["Reduced pressure vs fractional: Glycerol decomposes at its b.p. → reduced pressure."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"'Adsorption' principle is used for which of the following purification method?","options":["Chromatography","Sublimation","Distillation","Extraction"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D): extraction relies on differential solubility; (B)/(C): phase changes.\nWorking: adsorption chromatography (column/TLC) relies on differential adsorption on a stationary phase.\nKey idea: Differential adsorption = chromatography.","traps":["Extraction vs adsorption: Extraction = partition by solubility."]},{"id":"JM24Jan31S2C16","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2024,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"The fragrance of flowers is due to the presence of some steam volatile organic compounds called essential oils. These are generally insoluble in water at room temperature but are miscible with water vapour in vapour phase. A suitable method for the extraction of these oils from the flowers is -","options":["distillation","steam distillation","distillation under reduced pressure","crystallisation"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) plain distillation would decompose/needs high temperature. (C) used for liquids decomposing at bp (glycerol). (D) is for solids.\nWorking: steam-volatile, water-immiscible compounds are separated by steam distillation.\nKey idea: 'Steam volatile and water-immiscible' = steam distillation.","traps":["Reduced-pressure vs steam distillation: Water-immiscible + steam volatile → steam distillation."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Match List I with List II\nLIST I (Element detected): A. Nitrogen B. Sulphur C. Phosphorous D. Halogen\nLIST II (Reagent used / Product formed): I. Na₂[Fe(CN)₅NO] II. AgNO₃ III. Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃ IV. (NH₄)₂MoO₄\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) swaps N and halogen (AgNO₃ vs Prussian blue). (C)/(D) attach nitroprusside to phosphorus and molybdate to nitrogen/sulphur.\nWorking: N → Prussian blue Fe₄[Fe(CN)₆]₃; S → violet colour with sodium nitroprusside; P → yellow ammonium phosphomolybdate with ammonium molybdate; halogen → AgX with AgNO₃.\nKey idea: Prussian blue is a product (N); the other three are reagents.","traps":["Product vs reagent mixed in List II: Prussian blue forms only from cyanide (N) in the extract."]},{"id":"JM23Apr06S2C14","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"From the figure of column chromatography given below, identify incorrect statements.\nA. Compound 'c' is more polar than 'a' and 'b'\nB. Compound 'a' is least polar\nC. Compound 'b' comes out of the column before 'c' and after 'a'\nD. Compound 'a' spends more time in the column\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","options":["B and D only","B, C and D only","A, B and D only","A, B and C only"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B)/(C) all include D, which is a correct statement; students who mix 'more polar = moves faster' pick (C) or (B).\nWorking: 'c' has moved farthest so it is least strongly adsorbed (least polar) and elutes first; 'a' is most polar and stays longest. A false, B false, C false (b elutes after c, before a), D true. Incorrect = A, B, C.\nKey idea: Lowest band = least adsorbed = least polar = first out; top band stays longest.","traps":["Polarity–mobility relation reversed: On polar adsorbent (silica/alumina), polar compounds are held back.","Choosing correct instead of incorrect statements: Mark each statement T/F first, then pick the F set."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Apr06S2C14-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Apr08S1C17","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"0.5 g of an organic compound (X) with 60% carbon will produce ______ × 10⁻¹ g of CO₂ on complete combustion.","type":"num","answer":"11","range":[11.0,11.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1 (answers 1.1 g without converting to ×10⁻¹ → 1), 3 (reports 0.3 g carbon), 8 (uses 12/44 inverted → 0.08 g).\nWorking: mass C = 0.5 × 0.60 = 0.30 g; CO₂ = 0.30 × 44/12 = 1.1 g = 11 × 10⁻¹ g.\nKey idea: m(CO₂) = m(C) × 44/12.","traps":["×10⁻¹ answer format: Divide grams by 10⁻¹: 1.1 g → 11.","Mass ratio inverted: CO₂ heavier than C contained: multiply by 44/12."]},{"id":"JM23Apr13S1C15","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Apr 13 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"An organic compound gives 0.220 g of CO₂ and 0.126 g of H₂O on complete combustion. If the % of carbon is 24 then the % of hydrogen is ________ × 10⁻¹. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"56","range":[56.0,56.0],"explain":"Common slips: 6 (or 5.6): forgets the ×10⁻¹ scaling. 28: takes H mass as 0.126/18 (forgets 2 H per H₂O). 14: assumes a 1 g sample instead of finding 0.25 g from %C.\nWorking: C = 0.220 × 12/44 = 0.060 g; since %C = 24, sample mass = 0.25 g. H = 0.126 × 2/18 = 0.014 g; %H = 0.014/0.25 × 100 = 5.6 % = 56 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Use %C to recover sample mass (0.25 g), then apply the H formula.","traps":["Sample mass missing: m = mass of C/(%C/100).","×10⁻¹ answer scaling: Report 5.6 % as 56."]},{"id":"JM23Feb01S2C05","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Feb 1 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":5,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement I : Sulphanilic acid gives esterification test for carboxyl group. Statement II : Sulphanilic acid gives red colour in Lassaigne's test for extra element detection. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) assume 'acid' means carboxylic acid. (C) misses that N and S together give SCN⁻ (thinks only Prussian blue or violet tests apply).\nWorking: sulphanilic acid (p-H₂N–C₆H₄–SO₃H) has no –COOH group, so it does not give the esterification test for carboxyl (I false); it contains both N and S, so the sodium fusion extract contains NaSCN, which gives blood-red Fe(SCN)²⁺ with Fe³⁺ (II true).\nKey idea: Sulphanilic acid = H₂N–C₆H₄–SO₃H: sulphonic acid, not carboxylic; N + S → SCN⁻ → blood-red Fe(SCN)²⁺.","traps":["'Acid' read as carboxylic acid: Recall: sulphanilic acid is p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid.","Red colour linked to wrong element test: N and S together → NaSCN → blood red with Fe³⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Jan25S1C18","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"In sulphur estimation, 0.471 g of an organic compound gave 1.4439 g of barium sulphate. The percentage of sulphur in the compound is ________ (Nearest Integer) (Given : Atomic mass Ba: 137 u, S: 32 u, O: 16 u)","type":"num","answer":"42","range":[42.0,42.0],"explain":"Common slips: 307 if the 32/233 factor is omitted; 45 if BaSO₄ is taken as 217 (three O); 4 if the compound and precipitate masses are swapped.\nWorking: %S = 32/233 × 1.4439/0.471 × 100 = 42.1 → 42.\nKey idea: Mass fraction of S in BaSO₄ is 32/233.","traps":["Wrong molar mass of BaSO₄: BaSO₄ = 137 + 32 + 4×16 = 233."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C03","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Compound that will give positive Lassaigne's test for both nitrogen and halogen is :","options":["NH₂OH·HCl","N₂H₄·HCl","CH₃NH₂·HCl","NH₄Cl"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) contain N and Cl but no carbon, so no NaCN forms and the N test fails though the Cl test works.\nWorking: nitrogen is detected as NaCN, which forms on fusion only when both C and N are present. CH₃NH₂·HCl contains C, N and Cl, so it gives both the Prussian-blue test (N) and the AgCl test (Cl); the other three contain no carbon, so no NaCN forms.\nKey idea: No carbon → no cyanide → no Prussian blue.","traps":["N detection needs carbon: Check that the compound has C to form NaCN."]},{"id":"JM23Jan29S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Following chromatogram (shown below) was developed by adsorption of compound 'A' on a 6 cm TLC glass plate. Retardation factor of the compound 'A' is _________ × 10⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"6","range":[6.0,6.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 (3.5/5, not subtracting base line offset); 5.8 (3.5/6); 6 is correct only after subtracting 0.5 cm from the spot distance.\nWorking: solvent run = 6 − 0.5 − 0.5 = 5 cm; spot distance from base line = 3.5 − 0.5 = 3.0 cm; Rf = 3.0/5 = 0.6 = 6 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Both distances are measured from the base line where the sample was applied.","traps":["Spot distance measured from plate edge: Subtract the 0.5 cm base-line offset.","Solvent distance not whole plate: Solvent run = plate − top gap − bottom gap."],"img":["img/jee/JM23Jan29S1C12-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S1C07","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":7,"q":"Which of the following compounds would give the following set of qualitative analysis? (i) Fehling's Test: Positive. (ii) Na fusion extract upon treatment with sodium nitroprusside gives a blood red colour but not prussian blue.","options":["3-(1,3-thiazol-2-yl)propanal: thiazole ring with –CH₂CH₂CHO at C-2","1,3-oxazole-2-carbaldehyde: oxazole ring with CHO at C-2","imidazole-2-carbaldehyde: imidazole ring with CHO at C-2","1,3-thiazole-2-carbaldehyde: thiazole ring with CHO at C-2"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"B (O and N, no S) and C (N only) would give Prussian blue, not red. D has N and S but its CHO is on an aromatic ring, so Fehling's test is negative.\nWorking: Fehling's test is positive only for an aliphatic aldehyde, and a blood-red colour without Prussian blue means N and S are both present (SCN⁻ + Fe³⁺). Only A has a thiazole ring (N and S) and a –CH₂CH₂CHO group. (Strictly, sodium nitroprusside gives a violet colour with S²⁻; the blood-red colour comes from Fe³⁺ with SCN⁻.)\nKey idea: Need N and S (thiazole) and a CHO not attached to an aromatic ring: only A.","traps":["Aromatic aldehyde fails Fehling: Check where the CHO sits: on an aromatic ring Fehling's test is negative.","N + S gives red, not blue: When N and S are both present, SCN⁻ forms and gives blood red Fe(SCN)²⁺."]},{"id":"JM23Jan30S2C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 30 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"Match List I (Mixture) with List II (Separation Technique): A. CHCl₃ + C₆H₅NH₂; B. C₆H₁₄ + C₅H₁₂; C. C₆H₅NH₂ + H₂O; D. Organic compound in H₂O. I. Steam distillation; II. Differential extraction; III. Distillation; IV. Fractional distillation.","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C): put hexane–pentane under steam distillation or aniline–water under fractional distillation; (B): swaps differential extraction to A.\nWorking: CHCl₃ (b.p. 334 K) and aniline (b.p. 457 K) differ widely → distillation (III); hexane and pentane boil close together → fractional distillation (IV); aniline + water → steam distillation (I); an organic compound dissolved in water → differential extraction (II).\nKey idea: Large b.p. difference → simple distillation; small → fractional; water-immiscible volatile organics → steam; solute in water → extraction.","traps":["Hexane/pentane need fractional distillation: Close boiling points (36 vs 69 °C) → fractional."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C02","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"Match items of column I and II. Column I (Mixture of compounds): A. H₂O/CH₂Cl₂; B. α-tetralone (3,4-dihydronaphthalen-1(2H)-one) / 4-nitrophenol; C. Kerosene / Naphthalene; D. C₆H₁₂O₆/NaCl. Column II (Separation Technique): i. Crystallization; ii. Differential solvent extraction; iii. Column chromatography; iv. Fractional Distillation. Correct match is","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) B-(iv) and D-(iii): two solids are not separated by fractional distillation; glucose/NaCl not by column. (B) A-(iii) and C-(ii) misplace two pairs. (C) A-(i): H₂O/CH₂Cl₂ is a liquid pair, not crystallisation; D-(iv) distilling sugar/salt is absurd.\nWorking: H₂O/CH₂Cl₂ are immiscible liquids → differential solvent extraction (ii); α-tetralone/4-nitrophenol, two organic compounds of different polarity → column chromatography (iii); kerosene/naphthalene → fractional distillation (iv); glucose/NaCl → crystallisation (i).\nKey idea: Liquid–liquid immiscible → extraction; two organic solids → chromatography; miscible liquids with different b.p. → fractional distillation; sugar/salt → crystallisation.","traps":["Naphthalene solid suggests crystallisation: Kerosene is a liquid mixture; separate kerosene/naphthalene by b.p. difference.","Extraction vs chromatography for organic pairs: Extraction needs two immiscible liquid phases; two dissolved organic solids need chromatography."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"On complete combustion, 0.492 g of an organic compound gave 0.792 g of CO₂. The % of carbon in the organic compound is ________ (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"44","range":[44.0,44.0],"explain":"Common slips: 161 (inverting ratio), 12 or 27 (using 12/44 wrongly on the compound mass).\nWorking: C = 0.792 × 12/44 = 0.216 g; 0.216/0.492 × 100 = 43.9 ≈ 44.\nKey idea: %C = (12/44)(m_CO₂/m)×100.","traps":["Inverting the mass ratio: Carbon mass must be less than the compound mass."]},{"id":"JM23Jan31S2C08","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2023,"paper":"Main Jan 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"In Dumas method for the estimation of N₂, the sample is heated with copper oxide and the gas evolved is passed over:","options":["Copper oxide","Copper gauze","Pd","Ni"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) CuO is what the sample is heated with, not what the gas passes over. (C)/(D) Pd and Ni are hydrogenation catalysts, unrelated.\nWorking: the nitrogen oxides formed during combustion are passed over heated copper gauze, which reduces them to N₂; the N₂ is then collected over KOH solution.\nKey idea: Cu gauze reduces NO/NO₂ formed during combustion back to N₂.","traps":["CuO vs Cu gauze: CuO oxidises the compound; Cu gauze reduces N oxides."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"While estimating the nitrogen present in an organic compound by Kjeldahl's method, the ammonia evolved from 0.25 g of the compound neutralized 2.5 mL of 2 M H₂SO₄. The percentage of nitrogen present in organic compound is ____.","type":"num","answer":"56","range":[56.0,56.0],"explain":"Common slips: 28 (forgetting H₂SO₄ is dibasic); 112.\nWorking: H₂SO₄ = 5 mmol → NH₃ = 10 mmol → N = 140 mg; 140/250 × 100 = 56%.\nKey idea: 1 mol H₂SO₄ neutralises 2 mol NH₃.","traps":["Dibasic acid factor: mmol NH₃ = 2 × mmol H₂SO₄."]},{"id":"JM22Jul25S2C29","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":29,"q":"The separation of two coloured substances was done by paper chromatography. The distances travelled by solvent front, substance A and substance B from the base line are 3.25 cm, 2.08 cm and 1.05 cm, respectively. The ratio of Rf values of A to B is ______","type":"num","answer":"2","range":[2.0,2.0],"explain":"Common slips: 0.5 (B to A); 0.64 (Rf of A only).\nWorking: Rf(A) = 2.08/3.25 = 0.64, Rf(B) = 1.05/3.25 = 0.323; ratio = 2.08/1.05 = 1.98 ≈ 2.\nKey idea: Ratio of Rf values = ratio of spot distances.","traps":["Ratio inverted: Rf(A)/Rf(B) = 2.08/1.05."]},{"id":"JM22Jul26S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which technique among the following, is most appropriate in separation of a mixture of 100 mg of p-nitrophenol and picric acid?","options":["Steam distillation","2-5 ft long column of silica gel","Sublimation","Preparative TLC (Thin Layer Chromatography)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) steam distillation works for o-nitrophenol, not p-nitrophenol or picric acid; (B) column chromatography works in principle but the column size is for gram scale; (C) sublimation needs a sublimable component.\nWorking: both are non-volatile, polar solids differing in polarity; for only 100 mg, preparative TLC is the practical separation. Neither is steam-volatile (intermolecular H-bonding), neither sublimes usefully, and a 2–5 ft column is far too large for 100 mg.\nKey idea: Small (mg) quantity + polarity difference → preparative TLC.","traps":["Steam distillation for nitrophenols: Only the ortho isomer is steam volatile; here there is no ortho isomer.","Column vs TLC: Match the method to the 100 mg scale."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S1C19","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In Carius method of estimation of halogen, 0.45 g of an organic compound gave 0.36 g of AgBr. Find out the percentage of bromine in the compound. (Molar masses : AgBr = 188 g mol⁻¹ : Br = 80 g mol⁻¹)","options":["34.04%","40.04%","36.03%","38.04%"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B)/(D)/(C): arithmetic slips or using 108/188 (Ag fraction) or dividing the wrong way.\nWorking: %Br = (80/188) × (0.36/0.45) × 100 = 0.4255 × 0.8 × 100 = 34.04%.\nKey idea: Mass fraction of Br in AgBr is 80/188.","traps":["Ag fraction used for Br: Use 80/188 for bromine.","Close distractors: Compute 0.36/0.45 = 0.8 exactly first."]},{"id":"JM22Jul27S2C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Match List-I (Mixture) with List-II (Purification Process). List-I: (A) Chloroform & Aniline; (B) Benzoic acid & Napthalene; (C) Water & Aniline; (D) Napthalene & Sodium chloride. List-II: (I) Steam distillation; (II) Sublimation; (III) Distillation; (IV) Crystallisation.","options":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) gives crystallisation to the liquid pair; (B)/(C) swap steam distillation with crystallisation/sublimation.\nWorking: chloroform (bp 334 K) and aniline (bp 457 K) separate by simple distillation; benzoic acid and naphthalene by crystallisation (different solubilities, e.g. from hot water); aniline–water by steam distillation; naphthalene (sublimes) from NaCl by sublimation.\nKey idea: Liquids with large bp gap: distillation; steam-volatile, immiscible with water: steam distillation; one component sublimes: sublimation.","traps":["Benzoic acid and naphthalene both sublime: When both sublime, use solubility difference (crystallisation)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S1C30","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"On complete combustion of 0.492 g of an organic compound containing C, H and O, 0.7938 g of CO₂ and 0.4428 g of H₂O was produced. The % composition of oxygen in the compound is _____.","type":"num","answer":"46","range":[46.0,46.0],"explain":"Common slips: Using 1/18 instead of 2/18 for H gives 51; using CO₂ mass ratio 12/32 or omitting H gives wrong O%.\nWorking: C = 0.7938 × 12/44 = 0.2165 g (44.0 %); H = 0.4428 × 2/18 = 0.0492 g (10.0 %); O = 100 − 44.0 − 10.0 = 46.0 %.\nKey idea: O % = 100 − (C % + H %).","traps":["H factor in water: Mass of H = 2/18 × m(H₂O)."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R. Assertion A: Thin layer chromatography is an adsorption chromatography. Reason R: A thin layer of silica gel is spread over a glass plate of suitable size in thin layer chromatography which acts as an adsorbent. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) denies the link (some students think TLC is partition); (C)/(D) reject a true line.\nWorking: TLC is listed by NCERT as a type of adsorption chromatography, the silica gel (or alumina) layer being the adsorbent (stationary phase); R explains A.\nKey idea: TLC separates by differential adsorption on silica gel.","traps":["TLC as partition chromatography: NCERT: column and TLC = adsorption; paper = partition."]},{"id":"JM22Jul28S2C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jul 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"A sample of 0.125 g of an organic compound when analysed by Duma's method yields 22.78 mL of nitrogen gas collected over KOH solution at 280 K and 759 mm Hg. The percentage of nitrogen in the given organic compound is ____. (Nearest integer) (a) The vapour pressure of water at 280 K is 14.2 mm Hg; (b) R = 0.082 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","type":"num","answer":"22","range":[22.0,22.0],"explain":"Common slips: 11 (using N instead of N₂ mass); skipping the aqueous-tension correction gives 22.2, which happens to round to the same answer.\nWorking: dry N₂ pressure = 759 − 14.2 = 744.8 mm Hg; n = (744.8/760)(0.02278)/(0.082 × 280) = 9.72 × 10⁻⁴ mol; mass = 0.0272 g; % N = 21.8 ≈ 22.\nKey idea: Subtract aqueous tension; moles N₂ × 28 g.","traps":["Aqueous tension: Subtract vapour pressure of water.","N vs N₂ mass: Gas collected is N₂: 28 g mol⁻¹."]},{"id":"JM22Jun24S2C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 24 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"0.2 g of an organic compound was subjected to estimation of nitrogen by Dumas method in which volume of N₂ evolved (at STP) was found to be 22.400 mL. The percentage of nitrogen in the compound is ____ [nearest integer]. (Given: Molar mass of N₂ is 28 g mol⁻¹. Molar volume of N₂ at STP: 22.4 L)","type":"num","answer":"14","range":[14.0,14.0],"explain":"Common slips: 7 – using 14 g mol⁻¹ (atomic N) instead of 28; 28 – mass not divided properly.\nWorking: 22.4 mL = 1.0 × 10⁻³ mol N₂ = 0.028 g; %N = 0.028/0.2 × 100 = 14.\nKey idea: 22.4 mL at STP = 1 mmol N₂ = 28 mg.","traps":["Atomic mass of N used for N₂: N₂ has molar mass 28."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Phenol on reaction with dilute nitric acid, gives two products. Which method will be most effective for large scale separation?","options":["Chromatographic separation","Fractional Crystallisation","Steam distillation","Sublimation"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) chromatography works but is not for large scale; (B) fractional crystallisation – plausible for solids but not the NCERT method; (D) sublimation – neither isomer is chosen for it.\nWorking: dilute HNO₃ gives o- and p-nitrophenol. o-Nitrophenol is intramolecularly H-bonded and steam volatile; p-nitrophenol is intermolecularly H-bonded and not. Steam distillation separates them on a large scale.\nKey idea: Intramolecular H-bond → o-nitrophenol steam volatile.","traps":["Chromatography chosen as 'best' technique: 'Large scale' points to steam distillation.","Which isomer is volatile: Intramolecular H-bond lowers b.p. of o-isomer."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C13","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: A mixture contains benzoic acid and napthalene. The pure benzoic acid can be separated out by the use of benzene.\nReason R: Benzoic acid is soluble in hot water.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) assume \"like dissolves like\" picks benzoic acid out of benzene; (C) doubts benzoic acid solubility in hot water.\nWorking: both benzoic acid and naphthalene dissolve in benzene, so benzene cannot separate them (A false); benzoic acid dissolves in hot water while naphthalene does not, which is the basis of separation (R true).\nKey idea: A separating solvent must dissolve one component and not the other; non-polar benzene dissolves both.","traps":["Solvent dissolves both components: Check solubility of each component in the proposed solvent.","Hot-water solubility of benzoic acid: Benzoic acid is recrystallised from hot water."]},{"id":"JM22Jun25S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"During halogen test, sodium fusion extract is boiled with concentrated HNO₃ to","options":["remove unreacted sodium","decompose cyanide or sulphide of sodium","extract halogen from organic compound","maintain the pH of extract"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) sodium is already consumed during fusion; (C) halogen is already converted to NaX in fusion; (D) acidification is incidental, not the stated purpose.\nWorking: NaCN and Na₂S would give AgCN/Ag₂S precipitates with AgNO₃ and interfere, so they are destroyed by boiling with conc. HNO₃.\nKey idea: CN⁻ and S²⁻ would also precipitate with Ag⁺ – they must be removed first.","traps":["Purpose of HNO₃ boiling: Recall the interference of CN⁻/S²⁻ with AgNO₃."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C13","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Given below are two statements : Statement I : In 'Lassaigne's Test', when both nitrogen and sulphur are present in an organic compound, sodium thiocyanate is formed. Statement II : If both nitrogen and sulphur are present in an organic compound, then the excess of sodium used in sodium fusion will decompose the sodium thiocyanate formed to give NaCN and Na₂S. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – student doubts the excess-sodium decomposition; (D) – thinks NaCN and Na₂S form separately; (B) – unfamiliar with either line.\nWorking: Na + C + N + S → NaSCN (blood-red with Fe³⁺); with excess sodium NaSCN + 2Na → NaCN + Na₂S. Both statements true = (A).\nKey idea: NaSCN with little Na; NaCN + Na₂S with excess Na.","traps":["Excess sodium line not recalled: Learn both NaSCN formation and its decomposition."]},{"id":"JM22Jun26S1C28","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"On complete combustion 0.30 g of an organic compound gave 0.20 g of carbon dioxide and 0.10 g of water. The percentage of carbon in the given organic compound is ______. (Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"18","range":[18.0,18.0],"explain":"Common slips: 67 – taking 0.20 g CO₂ as carbon; 4 – using water data.\nWorking: C = 0.20 × 12/44 = 0.0545 g; %C = 0.0545/0.30 × 100 = 18.2 ≈ 18.\nKey idea: Carbon fraction of CO₂ is 12/44.","traps":["Water data used: Only CO₂ carries carbon."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following is structure of a separating funnel?","options":["Pear-shaped funnel with stopper and stopcock","Simple conical funnel","Funnel with a perforated (filter) plate","Stopcock/tap piece"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) ordinary filter funnel; (C) Büchner/sintered funnel used in suction filtration; (D) a detached stopcock.\nWorking: a separating funnel is a stoppered pear-shaped vessel with a stopcock to drain the lower liquid layer – option (A). (B) is an ordinary funnel, (C) a filter (Büchner/sintered) funnel, (D) only a stopcock.\nKey idea: Stopper on top + stopcock below = separating funnel (for immiscible liquids).","traps":["Filter funnel vs separating funnel: Separating funnel must hold and drain two liquid layers – needs stopper and stopcock."]},{"id":"JM22Jun27S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"0.25 g of an organic compound containing chlorine gave 0.40 g of silver chloride in Carius estimation. The percentage of chlorine present in the compound is ______. [in nearest integer] (Given: Molar mass of Ag is 108 g mol⁻¹ and that of Cl is 35.5 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"40","range":[40.0,40.0],"explain":"Common slips: 160 (mass ratio only, no Cl fraction); 120 (Ag fraction 108/143.5 used instead of Cl); 39 (truncating instead of rounding).\nWorking: % Cl = (35.5/143.5) × (0.40/0.25) × 100 = 0.2474 × 160 = 39.6 → 40.\nKey idea: Fraction of Cl in AgCl = 35.5/143.5.","traps":["Wrong mass fraction: Use 35.5/143.5, not 108/143.5."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"Which one of the following techniques is not used to spot components of a mixture separated on thin layer chromatographic plate?","options":["I₂ (Solid)","U.V. Light","Visualisation agent as a component of mobile phase","Spraying of an appropriate reagent"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A), (B), (D) are the three NCERT methods; a student who has not read the TLC paragraph may doubt I₂ (solid).\nWorking: spots are located by UV light, by an iodine-crystal chamber, or by spraying a reagent (e.g. ninhydrin) on the developed plate; a visualising agent is not added to the mobile phase.\nKey idea: Detection is done after development, on the plate, never through the eluent.","traps":["Iodine crystals as detector: Recall the covered jar with iodine crystals."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C20","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":20,"q":"The formula of the purple colour formed in Lassaigne's test for sulphur using sodium nitroprusside is","options":["NaFe[Fe(CN)₆]","Na[Cr(NH₃)₂(NCS)₄]","Na₂[Fe(CN)₅(NO)]","Na₄[Fe(CN)₅(NOS)]"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) is the reagent (sodium nitroprusside) itself; (A) is Prussian-blue type (nitrogen test); (B) Reinecke-type salt, unrelated.\nWorking: S²⁻ + [Fe(CN)₅NO]²⁻ → [Fe(CN)₅NOS]⁴⁻ (violet/purple), i.e. Na₄[Fe(CN)₅NOS].\nKey idea: Product = nitroprusside + S²⁻ → NOS ligand, charge 4−.","traps":["Reagent given as product: The product contains S: look for NOS.","Nitrogen-test complex: Prussian blue is the N test."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S1C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"In the estimation of bromine, 0.5 g of an organic compound gave 0.40 g of silver bromide. The percentage of bromine in the given compound is ______% (nearest integer) (Relative atomic masses of Ag and Br are 108 u and 80 u, respectively).","type":"num","answer":"34","range":[34.0,34.0],"explain":"Common slips: 80 (forgetting AgBr fraction), 43 (using 80/188 × 100 only).\nWorking: % Br = (80/188) × (0.40/0.5) × 100 = 0.4255 × 80 = 34.04 ≈ 34.\nKey idea: Mass fraction of Br in AgBr = 80/188.","traps":["Molar mass of AgBr: AgBr = 108 + 80 = 188."]},{"id":"JM22Jun28S2C28","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 28 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"The complete combustion of 0.492 g of an organic compound containing 'C', 'H' and 'O' gives 0.793 g of CO₂ and 0.442 g of H₂O. The percentage of oxygen composition in the organic compound is ____________. (nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"46","range":[46.0,46.0],"explain":"Common slips: 44 (C %), 10 (H %), 54 (C + H %).\nWorking: C = 0.793 × 12/44 = 0.2163 g; H = 0.442 × 2/18 = 0.0491 g; O = 0.492 − 0.2654 = 0.2266 g → 46.06% ≈ 46.\nKey idea: O % = 100 − (C % + H %) = 100 − 43.96 − 9.98.","traps":["Reporting C % instead of O %: Subtract C and H from 100."]},{"id":"JM22Jun29S1C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2022,"paper":"Main Jun 29 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"Kjeldahl's method was used for the estimation of nitrogen in an organic compound. The ammonia evolved from 0.55 g of the compound neutralised 12.5 mL of 1 M H₂SO₄ solution. The percentage of nitrogen in the compound is ________. (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"64","range":[64.0,64.0],"explain":"Common slips: 32 (forgetting the factor 2 for H₂SO₄).\nWorking: H₂SO₄ = 12.5 mmol neutralises 25 mmol NH₃ → N = 25 × 14 = 350 mg; % N = 350/550 × 100 = 63.6 ≈ 64.\nKey idea: Each H₂SO₄ neutralises 2 NH₃.","traps":["Basicity of H₂SO₄: mol NH₃ = 2 × mol H₂SO₄."]},{"id":"JM21Aug26S2C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 26 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In the sulphur estimation, 0.471 g of an organic compound gave 1.44 g of barium sulphate. The percentage of sulphur in the compound is ____ %. (Nearest integer) (Atomic Mass of Ba = 137 u)","type":"num","answer":"42","range":[42.0,42.0],"explain":"Common slips: using a wrong molar mass for BaSO₄, or inverting the mass ratio.\nWorking: % S = (32/233) × (1.44/0.471) × 100 = 0.13734 × 3.0573 × 100 = 41.99 ≈ 42.\nKey idea: %S = 32 × m(BaSO₄) × 100 / (233 × m(compound)).","traps":["Molar mass of BaSO₄ = 233: Count four O atoms."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In Carius method for estimation of halogens, 0.2 g of an organic compound gave 0.188 g of AgBr. The percentage of bromine in the compound is ______. (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass: Ag = 108, Br = 80]","type":"num","answer":"40","range":[40.0,40.0],"explain":"Common slips: 94 (mass of AgBr/compound ×100 without Br fraction); 54 (using Ag fraction 108/188 instead of Br).\nWorking: M(AgBr) = 188; mass of Br = 0.188 × 80/188 = 0.080 g; % Br = 0.080/0.2 × 100 = 40.\nKey idea: Br fraction in AgBr = 80/188.","traps":["Ag fraction used instead of Br: Multiply by Br/AgBr = 80/188."]},{"id":"JM21Aug27S1C26","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"The number of moles of CuO, that will be utilized in Dumas method for estimation of nitrogen in a sample of 57.5 g of N,N-dimethylaminopentane is ______ × 10⁻². (Nearest integer)","type":"num","answer":"1125","range":[1125.0,1125.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1075 (C₇H₁₅N, two H short); 700 (only carbon counted, 2x); 2250 (moles per mole, not for 0.5 mol).\nWorking: N,N-dimethylpentan-1-amine C₅H₁₁N(CH₃)₂ = C₇H₁₇N, M = 115; 57.5 g = 0.5 mol. CₓHᵧN_z + (2x + y/2) CuO → x CO₂ + y/2 H₂O + z/2 N₂ + (2x + y/2) Cu; 2(7) + 17/2 = 22.5 mol CuO per mol; 0.5 × 22.5 = 11.25 = 1125 × 10⁻².\nKey idea: Each C needs 2 O and each 2 H need 1 O, all from CuO.","traps":["Formula from name: Draw C₅H₁₁–N(CH₃)₂ and count H.","Hydrogen oxygen demand forgotten: Add y/2 for water."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S1C17","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":17,"q":"Assertion (A): A simple distillation can be used to separate a mixture of propanol and propanone. Reason (R): Two liquids with a difference of more than 20°C in their boiling points can be separated by simple distillations. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true.","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) thinks propanol/propanone need fractional distillation; (B) doubts the causal link; (C) rejects the numerical rule.\nWorking: propan-1-ol (b.p. ~97 °C) and propanone (b.p. 56 °C) differ by ~41 °C; simple distillation separates liquids with a sufficient difference in boiling point (rule of thumb > 20–25 °C). Both true and R explains A: (D).\nKey idea: ~41 °C gap is well above the ~20 °C threshold.","traps":["Fractional needed?: H-bonded propanol boils ~40 °C higher than acetone.","20 °C threshold not in NCERT: Accept the standard rule of thumb."]},{"id":"JM21Aug31S2C30","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Aug 31 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":30,"q":"The transformation occurring in Duma's method is given below: C₂H₇N + (2x + y/2) CuO → x CO₂ + (y/2) H₂O + (z/2) N₂ + (2x + y/2) Cu. The value of y is ________. (Integer answer)","type":"num","answer":"7","range":[7.0,7.0],"explain":"Common slips: 3.5 (y/2), 2 (x), 11 (2x + y/2 coefficient of CuO).\nWorking: the equation is NCERT's general CₓHᵧN_z equation written with C₂H₇N, so x = 2, y = 7, z = 1.\nKey idea: y is simply the number of H atoms in C₂H₇N.","traps":["Reporting a coefficient instead of y: y is the subscript of H in the formula."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C06","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Complete combustion of 1.80 g of an oxygen containing compound (CₓHᵧO_z) gave 2.64 g of CO₂ and 1.08 g of H₂O. The percentage of oxygen in the organic compound is:","options":["51.63","63.53","53.33","50.33"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(D) arithmetic slips (e.g. H from 1.08/18 without ×2 gives O = 1.02 g, 56.7%); (B) a near-miss decoy.\nWorking: C = 2.64 × 12/44 = 0.72 g; H = 1.08 × 2/18 = 0.12 g; O = 1.80 − 0.84 = 0.96 g; %O = 0.96/1.80 × 100 = 53.33.\nKey idea: O% = 100 − C% − H% = 100 − 40 − 6.67.","traps":["Hydrogen factor 2 dropped: Use 2/18 for H in water.","Percentage of C reported: Re-read which element is asked."]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S1C26","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":26,"q":"Using the provided information in the following paper chromatogram (shown below), the calculated Rf value of A ______ × 10⁻¹.","type":"num","answer":"4","range":[4.0,4.0],"explain":"Common slips: 8 (using B: 4/5), 5 (using A/B ratio 2/4), 2 (reporting distance only).\nWorking: Rf = distance of A from base line / distance of solvent front = 2 cm / 5 cm = 0.4 = 4 × 10⁻¹.\nKey idea: Both distances are measured from the base line.","traps":["B used instead of A: A is 2 cm from base line.","Distance from spot to solvent front misread: Solvent front distance is from base line."],"img":["img/jee/JM21Feb25S1C26-p.webp"]},{"id":"JM21Feb25S2C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following compound is added to the sodium extract before addition of silver nitrate for testing of halogens?","options":["Nitric acid","Ammonia","Hydrochloric acid","Sodium hydroxide"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) HCl adds chloride itself; (B) NH₃ is used later to dissolve AgCl; (D) NaOH would precipitate Ag₂O.\nWorking: the extract is boiled with HNO₃ to decompose NaCN and Na₂S, which would otherwise give precipitates with AgNO₃; HCl would itself add Cl⁻.\nKey idea: Acidify with HNO₃ to destroy CN⁻ and S²⁻ before AgNO₃.","traps":["HCl chosen as the acid: HCl introduces Cl⁻ – false positive.","Ammonia step misplaced: NH₃ is a confirmatory step after precipitation."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C10","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":10,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement I: A mixture of chloroform and aniline can be separated by simple distillation. Statement II: When separating aniline from a mixture of aniline and water by steam distillation aniline boils below its boiling point.","options":["Statement-I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement-I and Statement II are false","Statement-I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement-I and Statement II are true"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B): think aniline–chloroform needs fractional distillation; (C): forget that steam distillation lowers the boiling temperature.\nWorking: CHCl₃ (334 K) and aniline (457 K) differ widely in b.p.; in steam distillation the mixture boils when p(aniline) + p(water) = atmospheric pressure, i.e. below aniline's b.p.\nKey idea: Large b.p. gap → simple distillation; steam distillation boils below the substance's own b.p.","traps":["Fractional distillation assumed: b.p. difference of 123 K is large → simple distillation.","Steam distillation temperature: Total vapour pressure reaches 1 atm earlier."]},{"id":"JM21Feb26S1C13","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Feb 26 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":13,"q":"Which of the following is 'a' FALSE statement?","options":["Carius tube is used in the estimation of sulphur in an organic compound","Carius method is used for the estimation of nitrogen in an organic compound","Phosphoric acid produced on oxidation of phosphorus present in an organic compound is precipitated as Mg₂P₂O₇ by adding magnesia mixture.","Kjeldahl's method is used for the estimation of nitrogen in an organic compound"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) looks wrong because the first precipitate is MgNH₄PO₄ (Mg₂P₂O₇ forms on ignition); (A) Carius tube for S is true; (D) Kjeldahl for N is true.\nWorking: Carius method estimates halogens (and S in a Carius tube); N is estimated by Dumas or Kjeldahl. (C) is accepted as true: magnesia mixture gives MgNH₄PO₄, ignited to Mg₂P₂O₇.\nKey idea: Carius = halogens and S; N = Dumas/Kjeldahl.","traps":["Option (C) judged false: Ignition gives Mg₂P₂O₇; statement is loosely true.","Carius linked to N: N methods are Dumas and Kjeldahl only."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C18","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":18,"q":"In Carius method, halogen containing organic compound is heated with fuming nitric acid in the presence of :","options":["HNO₃","AgNO₃","CuSO₄","BaSO₄"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) HNO₃ is already named in the stem; (D) BaSO₄ is the weighed form in sulphur estimation (BaCl₂ added); (C) CuSO₄ is the Kjeldahl catalyst.\nWorking: the compound is heated with fuming HNO₃ and AgNO₃ in a Carius tube; halogen is weighed as AgX.\nKey idea: Halogen → AgX (AgNO₃); sulphur → BaSO₄ (BaCl₂).","traps":["Sulphur estimation reagent confused: Halogens precipitate as silver halides."]},{"id":"JM21Jul20S2C25","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 20 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"When 0.15 g of an organic compound was analyzed using Carius method for estimation of bromine, 0.2397 g of AgBr was obtained. The percentage of bromine in the organic compound is _______. (Nearest integer) [Atomic mass : Silver = 108, Bromine = 80]","type":"num","answer":"68","range":[68.0,68.0],"explain":"Common slips: 160 (forgetting the 80/188 factor), 42 (using 80/188 alone ×100).\nWorking: %Br = (80/188) × (0.2397/0.15) × 100 = 0.4255 × 1.598 × 100 = 68.0 → 68.\nKey idea: Mass fraction of Br in AgBr = 80/188.","traps":["Mass fraction omitted: Multiply by 80/188."]},{"id":"JM21Jul22S2C03","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 22 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":3,"q":"Which purification technique is used for high boiling organic liquid compound (decomposes near its boiling point)?","options":["Simple distillation","Steam distillation","Fractional distillation","Reduced pressure distillation"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) steam distillation also lowers the effective boiling temperature but needs a steam-volatile, water-immiscible compound; (C) fractional is for close-boiling mixtures; (A) simple would decompose the compound.\nWorking: lowering the external pressure lowers the boiling point, so the liquid distils below its decomposition temperature.\nKey idea: Decomposes near b.p. + high b.p. → lower the pressure.","traps":["Steam distillation picked: Steam distillation needs steam-volatility and water immiscibility; the stem gives neither."]},{"id":"JM21Jul25S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 25 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"0.8 g of an organic compound was analysed by Kjeldahl's method for the estimation of nitrogen. If the percentage of nitrogen in the compound was found to be 42%, then ______ mL of 1 M H₂SO₄ would have been neutralized by the ammonia evolved during the analysis.","type":"num","answer":"12","range":[12.0,12.0],"explain":"Common slips: 24 (forgetting H₂SO₄ is diprotic), 6 (doubling twice).\nWorking: mass N = 0.42 × 0.8 = 0.336 g = 0.024 mol NH₃; 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄ needs 0.012 mol H₂SO₄ = 12 mL of 1 M.\nKey idea: mol NH₃ = 2 × mol H₂SO₄.","traps":["Diprotic acid factor: Write 2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ before calculating."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S1C25","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":25,"q":"An organic compound is subjected to chlorination to get compound A using 5.0 g of chlorine. When 0.5 g of compound A is reacted with AgNO₃ [Carius Method], the percentage of chlorine in compound A is _______ when it forms 0.3849 g of AgCl. (Round off to the Nearest Integer) (Atomic masses of Ag and Cl are 107.87 and 35.5 respectively)","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: using 5.0 g in the calculation, or using 108 for Ag giving the same 19 – the irrelevant chlorine mass is the main lure.\nWorking: %Cl = (35.5/143.37) × (0.3849/0.5) × 100 = 19.06 → 19.\nKey idea: Only the mass of compound and of AgCl matter.","traps":["Irrelevant chlorine mass: Carius uses mass of compound taken (0.5 g)."]},{"id":"JM21Jul27S2C01","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Jul 27 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"Which one of the following set of elements can be detected using sodium fusion extract?","options":["Sulfur, Nitrogen, Phosphorous, Halogens","Phosphorous, Oxygen, Nitrogen, Halogens","Nitrogen, Phosphorous, Carbon, Sulfur","Halogens, Nitrogen, Oxygen, Sulfur"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) and (D) include oxygen, which has no Lassaigne test; (C) includes carbon, which is detected by CuO heating, not by the fusion extract.\nWorking: fusion with Na converts covalent N, S, X and P into NaCN, Na₂S, NaX and Na₃PO₄, which are tested in the aqueous extract; C and O are not detected this way.\nKey idea: Lassaigne = N, S, X, P only; C/H by CuO, O by difference.","traps":["Carbon and oxygen are not Lassaigne elements: Remember C and H are tested by heating with CuO."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C19","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"In chromatography technique, the purification of compound is independent of:","options":["Mobility or flow of solvent system","Solubility of the compound","Length of the column or TLC plate","Physical state of the pure compound"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) – length seems 'only geometry', but a longer column improves separation; (B) – solubility in eluent is central to partition.\nWorking: separation depends on differential adsorption/partition – solvent flow, solubility in the mobile phase and column/plate length all affect it; whether the pure compound is a solid or a liquid does not.\nKey idea: Separation depends on adsorption/partition between phases, not on the state of the isolated pure substance.","traps":["Column length thought irrelevant: Separation (resolution) still depends on path length.","'Independent of' reversed: Pick the factor with no role."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S1C22","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":22,"q":"Complete combustion of 750 g of an organic compound provides 420 g of CO₂ and 210 g of H₂O. The percentage composition of carbon and hydrogen in organic compound is 15.3 and ____ respectively. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)","type":"num","answer":"3","range":[3.0,3.0],"explain":"Common slips: 1.6 → 2 (using 1/18 instead of 2/18), 28 (210/750 directly).\nWorking: H = 210 × 2/18 = 23.3 g → 23.3/750 × 100 = 3.11% ≈ 3 (check: C = 420 × 12/44 = 114.5 g = 15.3%).\nKey idea: %H = (2/18) × m(H₂O)/m(compound) × 100.","traps":["Factor of 2 for H: Use 2/18, not 1/18."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C16","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":16,"q":"Match List-I (Test/Reagents/Observation(s)) with List-II (Species detected): (a) Lassaigne's Test; (b) Cu(II) oxide; (c) Silver nitrate; (d) The sodium fusion extract gives black precipitate with acetic acid and lead acetate. List-II: (i) Carbon; (ii) Sulphur; (iii) N, S, P, and halogen; (iv) Halogen specifically. The correct match is:","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) swaps (c) and (d) – AgNO₃ with sulphur; (B) makes Lassaigne's a carbon test; (D) makes CuO a sulphur test and lead acetate a general test.\nWorking: Lassaigne's test detects N, S, P and halogens; heating with CuO detects C (as CO₂, lime water); AgNO₃ (after HNO₃) gives halide precipitates; black PbS with lead acetate shows sulphur.\nKey idea: Lassaigne = general (N, S, P, X); CuO = C; AgNO₃ = X; Pb(OAc)₂ black = S.","traps":["AgNO₃ matched to sulphur: In Lassaigne scheme AgNO₃ (after HNO₃ boiling) is the halogen test."]},{"id":"JM21Mar16S2C24","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 16 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":24,"q":"In Duma's method of estimation of nitrogen, 0.1840 g of an organic compound gave 30 mL of nitrogen collected at 287 K and 758 mm of Hg pressure. The percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound is ______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer). [Given: Aqueous tension at 287 K = 14 mm of Hg]","type":"num","answer":"19","range":[19.0,19.0],"explain":"Common slips: ignoring aqueous tension gives 19.3, which still rounds to 19; 20 (no temperature correction, ~19.9); 9 (14 g used for 22400 mL N₂ instead of 28 g).\nWorking: dry gas pressure 758 − 14 = 744 mm Hg; V₀ = 27.94 mL at STP; m(N₂) = 0.03492 g; %N = 0.03492/0.1840 × 100 = 18.98 → 19.\nKey idea: %N = (28 × V_STP mL)/(22400 × m) × 100.","traps":["Aqueous tension ignored: Gas collected over water: subtract vapour pressure.","Atomic mass 14 used for N₂: N₂ gas: 28 g per 22400 mL."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S1C19","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":19,"q":"Given below are two statements: Statement-I: Retardation factor (Rf) can be measured in meter/centimeter. Statement-II: Rf value of a compound remains constant in all solvents. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement-I is true but statement-II is false","Both statement-I and statement-II are true","Both statement-I and statement-II are false","Statement-I is false but statement-II is true"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) – Rf thought to be a distance; (D) – Rf thought to be a fixed property; (B) – both.\nWorking: Rf is a ratio of two distances (distance moved by substance / by solvent front), so it has no unit – I false; Rf depends on the solvent (mobile phase) – II false.\nKey idea: Rf is dimensionless and solvent-specific.","traps":["Rf thought to have units: Ratio of two lengths.","Rf thought constant: Constant only for a given solvent system."]},{"id":"JM21Mar17S2C12","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2021,"paper":"Main Mar 17 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"Nitrogen can be estimated by Kjeldahl's method for which of the following compound ?","options":["Benzenediazonium chloride (C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻)","Benzylamine (C₆H₅CH₂–NH₂)","Pyridine","Nitrobenzene (C₆H₅NO₂)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) diazonium N is lost as N₂ / behaves like azo N; (C) ring N (named in NCERT); (D) nitro N (named in NCERT).\nWorking: Kjeldahl fails for N in nitro and azo/diazo groups and N in a ring (pyridine), because that N is not converted to (NH₄)₂SO₄; the amine N of benzylamine is converted, so (B).\nKey idea: Kjeldahl works only for N that can become NH₄⁺ – amines and amides, not nitro, azo/diazo or ring N.","traps":["Diazonium not seen as an exception: Any –N=N– nitrogen escapes as N₂ and is not trapped as NH₄⁺.","Positive vs negative question: Re-read: pick the compound that works."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"A solution of m-chloroaniline, m-chlorophenol and m-chlorobenzoic acid in ethyl acetate was extracted initially with a saturated solution of NaHCO₃ to give fraction A. The left over organic phase was extracted with dilute NaOH solution to give fraction B. The final organic layer was labelled as fraction C. Fractions A, B and C, contain respectively :","options":["m-chlorobenzoic acid, m-chloroaniline and m-chlorophenol","m-chloroaniline, m-chlorobenzoic acid and m-chlorophenol","m-chlorobenzoic acid, m-chlorophenol and m-chloroaniline","m-chlorophenol, m-chlorobenzoic acid and m-chloroaniline"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A): puts the amine in NaOH – forgets amines are bases, not acids. (B): thinks the amine reacts with NaHCO₃. (D): thinks phenol (pKa≈9) is acidic enough to react with NaHCO₃.\nWorking: NaHCO₃ (weak base) deprotonates only the carboxylic acid (A); NaOH then takes the phenol as phenoxide (B); the basic aniline stays in ethyl acetate (C).\nKey idea: NaHCO₃ only removes acids stronger than H₂CO₃ (carboxylic acids); NaOH also removes phenols; bases stay behind.","traps":["Phenol thought to dissolve in NaHCO₃: Only acids stronger than carbonic acid (pKa 6.4) liberate CO₂ with NaHCO₃; phenol pKa ≈ 10.","Amine placed in NaOH fraction: Aniline is a base: it is extracted only by aqueous acid, so it stays in the organic layer."]},{"id":"JM20Jan07S2C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 7 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"A chromatography column, packed with silica gel as stationary phase, was used to separate a mixture of compounds consisting of (A) benzanilide (B) aniline and (C) acetophenone. When the column is eluted with a mixture of solvents, hexane : ethyl acetate (20 : 80), the sequence of obtained compounds :","options":["(B), (C) and (A)","(C), (A) and (B)","(A), (B) and (C)","(B), (A) and (C)"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) and (D) let aniline elute first (judging by small size or low molar mass); (C) orders by molar mass with the largest first.\nWorking: on polar, acidic silica the least strongly adsorbed compound elutes first. Acetophenone (no N–H donor) comes first; aniline's basic –NH₂ binds most strongly to Si–OH and comes last; benzanilide (amide N–H, but lone pair delocalised) is intermediate.\nKey idea: Weakest adsorbed (least polar, no H-bond donor/basic site) elutes first; basic amine is held longest on acidic silica.","traps":["Smaller molecule assumed faster: Elution follows adsorption strength (polarity), not mass.","Elution order reversed: On silica, most strongly adsorbed comes out last."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S1C01","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":1,"q":"A flask contains a mixture of isohexane and 3-methylpentane. One of the liquids boils at 63°C while the other boils at 60°C. What is the best way to separate the two liquids and which one will be distilled out first?","options":["simple distillation, 3-methylpentane","simple distillation, isohexane","fractional distillation, isohexane","fractional distillation, 3-methylpentane"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(B) simple distillation fails for a 3 °C gap; (D) picks the higher-boiling 3-methylpentane as the first fraction.\nWorking: boiling points differ by only 3 °C, so fractional distillation is needed; isohexane (2-methylpentane, bp ≈ 60 °C, more branched) is the lower-boiling liquid and distils first.\nKey idea: Close bp → fractional; more branched isomer (2-methylpentane) has the lower bp and comes over first.","traps":["Not knowing isohexane is 2-methylpentane: Isohexane = 2-methylpentane; more branching, lower bp.","Simple distillation chosen for a small bp gap: Use simple distillation only for large bp differences."]},{"id":"JM20Jan08S2C06","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Jan 8 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":6,"q":"Kjeldahl's method cannot be used to estimate nitrogen for which of the following compounds?","options":["C₆H₅NO₂","C₆H₅NH₂","CH₃CH₂–C≡N","NH₂–CO–NH₂"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) nitrile looks unusual but its N hydrolyses to NH₄⁺; (D) urea is a classic Kjeldahl example; (B) aniline is an amine – fine.\nWorking: nitro (and azo, ring) nitrogen is not converted to (NH₄)₂SO₄ on digestion with conc. H₂SO₄, so nitrobenzene fails; aniline, propanenitrile and urea all give ammonium sulphate.\nKey idea: Nitro, azo and ring nitrogen escape conversion to ammonium sulphate.","traps":["Nitrile N thought unreactive: Recall only nitro, azo and ring N are excluded."]},{"id":"JM20Sep02S1C11","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 2 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"In Carius method of estimation of halogen, 0.172 g of an organic compound showed presence of 0.08 g of bromine. Which of these is the correct structure of the compound?","options":["4-bromoaniline (NH₂ and Br para on benzene)","H₃C–CH₂–Br","2,4-dibromoaniline (NH₂ with Br at ortho and para)","H₃C–Br"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) has two Br (wrong %); (B) and (D) are small molecules with much higher %Br. 0.172 g hints at M = 172.\nWorking: %Br = 0.08/0.172 × 100 = 46.5 %. C₆H₆BrN (M = 172) has 80/172 = 46.5 %; C₂H₅Br 73.4 %, C₆H₅Br₂N 63.7 %, CH₃Br 84.2 %.\nKey idea: Mass ratio 0.08/0.172 = 80/172 → one Br in a molecule of molar mass 172.","traps":["Counting Br wrongly in dibromo compound: Use 160/251 for the dibromo compound."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C12","level":"hard","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":12,"q":"The Kjeldahl method of Nitrogen estimation fails for which of the following reaction products?\n(a) Nitrobenzene –(Sn/HCl)→\n(b) Benzonitrile (C₆H₅CN) –(LiAlH₄)→\n(c) Phenylacetonitrile (C₆H₅CH₂CN) –(i) SnCl₂ + HCl; (ii) H₂O→\n(d) Aniline –(NaNO₂/HCl)→","options":["a and d","c and d","a, c and d","b and c"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A)/(C) treat Sn/HCl product as still a nitro compound; (D) miss that LiAlH₄ gives an amine.\nWorking: (a) aniline and (b) benzylamine contain amino N – Kjeldahl works; (c) Stephen reduction gives phenylacetaldehyde, which has no N; (d) benzenediazonium chloride – azo/diazo N is not converted to (NH₄)₂SO₄.\nKey idea: Judge the product: amine → works; diazonium or N-free aldehyde → fails.","traps":["Judging the reactant instead of product: Sn/HCl reduces NO₂ to NH₂.","Stephen reaction product overlooked: SnCl₂/HCl then H₂O gives aldehyde."]},{"id":"JM20Sep03S1C15","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 3 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":15,"q":"Glycerol is separated in soap industries by:","options":["Steam distillation","Differential extraction","Distillation under reduced pressure","Fractional distillation"],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) is for steam-volatile water-immiscible liquids (aniline); (B) for extraction; (D) for close-boiling liquids.\nWorking: glycerol decomposes near its b.p., so it is distilled under reduced pressure from spent lye.\nKey idea: High-boiling, heat-sensitive liquid → reduced pressure.","traps":["Mixing distillation variants: Link each method to its NCERT example."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S1C21","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":21,"q":"In an estimation of bromine by Carius method, 1.6 g of an organic compound gave 1.88 g of AgBr. The mass percentage of bromine in the compound is _______\n(Atomic mass, Ag = 108, Br = 80 g mol⁻¹)","type":"num","answer":"50","range":[50.0,50.0],"explain":"Common slips: 117.5 (inverting ratio 1.88/1.6), 42.6 (using Ag fraction 108/188 inverted), 57.4 (% Ag instead of % Br).\nWorking: Br = 1.88 × 80/188 = 0.80 g; % Br = 0.80/1.6 × 100 = 50.\nKey idea: % X = (at. mass X / M of AgX) × (mass AgX / mass compound) × 100.","traps":["Ag fraction used instead of Br: Br fraction of AgBr is 80/188."]},{"id":"JM20Sep06S2C08","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2020,"paper":"Main Sep 6 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":8,"q":"Match the following: Test/Method: (i) Lucas Test, (ii) Dumas method, (iii) Kjeldahl's method, (iv) Hinsberg Test. Reagent: (a) C₆H₅SO₂Cl/aq. KOH, (b) HNO₃/AgNO₃, (c) CuO/CO₂, (d) Conc. HCl and ZnCl₂, (e) H₂SO₄.","options":["(i)-(d), (ii)-(c), (iii)-(e), (iv)-(a)","(i)-(b), (ii)-(d), (iii)-(e), (iv)-(a)","(i)-(d), (ii)-(c), (iii)-(b), (iv)-(e)","(i)-(b), (ii)-(a), (iii)-(c), (iv)-(d)"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(b) HNO₃/AgNO₃ belongs to Carius method; (B) and (D) give it to Lucas; (C) gives H₂SO₄ to Hinsberg and Carius reagent to Kjeldahl.\nWorking: Lucas = conc. HCl + ZnCl₂; Dumas = heating with CuO in CO₂ atmosphere; Kjeldahl = digestion with conc. H₂SO₄; Hinsberg = benzenesulphonyl chloride with aqueous KOH.\nKey idea: Dumas = CuO (oxidises to N₂); Kjeldahl = H₂SO₄ (converts N to (NH₄)₂SO₄).","traps":["Carius reagent placed in the match: HNO₃/AgNO₃ is for halogens (Carius), not nitrogen.","Dumas vs Kjeldahl reagents swapped: Dumas measures N₂ gas (CuO); Kjeldahl measures NH₃ (H₂SO₄ digestion)."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S1C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The principle of column chromatography is :","options":["Capillary action.","Gravitational force.","Differential adsorption of the substances on the solid phase.","Differential absorption of the substances on the solid phase."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(D) absorption is a bulk phenomenon; (A) capillary action drives paper/TLC solvent flow; (B) gravity just moves the eluent.\nWorking: column chromatography is adsorption chromatography – components are adsorbed to different extents on the stationary solid (silica/alumina).\nKey idea: Separation comes from differential adsorption on the stationary phase.","traps":["Absorption read as adsorption: Adsorption = surface; absorption = bulk.","Mechanism of solvent flow taken as principle: Ask what separates components, not what moves the solvent."]},{"id":"JM19Apr10S2C28","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 10 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":28,"q":"In chromatography, which of the following statements is INCORRECT for R_f?","options":["R_f value depends on the type of chromatography.","The value of R_f can not be more than one.","Higher R_f value means higher adsorption.","R_f value is dependent on the mobile phase."],"answer":2,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) is true (substance cannot outrun solvent front); (A)/(D) true since R_f depends on stationary and mobile phase.\nWorking: R_f = distance by substance / distance by solvent; a strongly adsorbed component moves less, giving lower R_f.\nKey idea: More adsorption → less movement → smaller R_f.","traps":["Higher R_f linked to stronger adsorption: R_f measures movement, the inverse of adsorption."]},{"id":"JM19Apr12S1C27","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Tests for elements (N, S, P, halogens)","year":2019,"paper":"Main Apr 12 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":27,"q":"An organic compound 'A' is oxidized with Na₂O₂ followed by boiling with HNO₃. The resultant solution is then treated with ammonium molybdate to yield a yellow precipitate. Based on above observation, the element present in the given compound is :","options":["Sulphur","Nitrogen","Fluorine","Phosphorus"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) S gives black PbS / violet nitroprusside; (B) N gives Prussian blue; (C) F is not tested this way.\nWorking: Na₂O₂ oxidises P to phosphate; with HNO₃ and ammonium molybdate it gives yellow ammonium phosphomolybdate.\nKey idea: Ammonium molybdate yellow ppt = phosphate.","traps":["Yellow precipitate linked to sulphur: Molybdate test is specific to phosphate."]},{"id":"JM19Jan09S2C14","level":"easy","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 9 · Shift 2 · Chemistry","qno":14,"q":"The correct match between Item I and Item II is: Item I: (A) Benzaldehyde (B) Alumina (C) Acetonitrile; Item II: (P) Mobile phase (Q) Adsorbent (R) Adsorbate","options":["(A)→(Q); (B)→(R); (C)→(P)","(A)→(P); (B)→(R); (C)→(Q)","(A)→(Q); (B)→(P); (C)→(R)","(A)→(R); (B)→(Q); (C)→(P)"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(A) Benzaldehyde as adsorbent and alumina as adsorbate – reversed. (B) Benzaldehyde as mobile phase. (C) Alumina as mobile phase.\nWorking: benzaldehyde is the compound being adsorbed (adsorbate), alumina the adsorbent, acetonitrile the solvent/mobile phase.\nKey idea: Solid support = adsorbent; solvent = mobile phase; sample = adsorbate.","traps":["Adsorbate vs adsorbent: -ent = the surface (alumina); -ate = the substance held."]},{"id":"JM19Jan10S1C11","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 10 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":11,"q":"If dichloromethane (DCM) and water (H₂O) are used for differential extraction, which one of the following statements is correct ?","options":["DCM and H₂O would stay as lower and upper layer respectively in the S.F.","DCM and H₂O will be miscible clearly","DCM and H₂O would stay as upper and lower layer respectively in the separating funnel (S.F.)","DCM and H₂O will make turbid/colloidal mixture"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) assumes organic layer is always on top (true for ether, not DCM). (B) and (D) deny immiscibility.\nWorking: CH₂Cl₂ is immiscible with water and denser (≈1.33 g cm⁻³), so it forms the lower layer.\nKey idea: Chlorinated solvents (CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃, CCl₄) are denser than water and sink.","traps":["Organic layer assumed on top: Compare densities: halogenated solvents > 1 g cm⁻³."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C02","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Purification methods","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":2,"q":"The correct match between Items-I and II is:\nItem-I (Mixture): (A) H₂O : Sugar; (B) H₂O : Aniline; (C) H₂O : Toluene\nItem-II (Separation method): (P) Sublimation; (Q) Recrystallization; (R) Steam distillation; (S) Differential extraction","options":["A-Q, B-R, C-S","A-R, B-P, C-S","A-S, B-R, C-P","A-Q, B-R, C-P"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"explain":"(B) sugar by steam distillation and aniline by sublimation – both impossible. (C) sugar by extraction and toluene by sublimation. (D) differs from the correct match only in C – toluene by sublimation (liquids do not sublime).\nWorking: sugar crystallises from water (Q); aniline is steam-volatile and immiscible with water (R); toluene and water are immiscible liquids separated in a separating funnel (S).\nKey idea: Solid soluble in water → crystallisation; steam-volatile immiscible liquid → steam distillation; immiscible liquid pair → separating funnel/extraction.","traps":["Toluene with sublimation: Only solids sublime; toluene is a liquid.","Aniline vs toluene method: Aniline is the NCERT steam-distillation example."]},{"id":"JM19Jan11S1C09","level":"medium","time":120,"topic":"Quantitative analysis & formula","year":2019,"paper":"Main Jan 11 · Shift 1 · Chemistry","qno":9,"q":"An organic compound is estimated through Dumas method and was found to evolve 6 moles of CO₂, 4 moles of H₂O and 1 mole of nitrogen gas. The formula of the compound is:","options":["C₁₂H₈N","C₁₂H₈N₂","C₆H₈N","C₆H₈N₂"],"answer":3,"fixed":true,"explain":"(C) C₆H₈N – forgets N₂ has two N. (A)/(B) double the carbon (counting O-based factor) – C₁₂.\nWorking: 6 CO₂ → 6 C; 4 H₂O → 8 H; 1 N₂ → 2 N: C₆H₈N₂.\nKey idea: Each H₂O carries 2 H and each N₂ carries 2 N; CO₂ carries 1 C.","traps":["N₂ counted as one N: N₂ → 2 N atoms.","Doubling C: Only H₂O and N₂ need the factor 2."]}]};
